window.IELTS_DATA={"build":"2026.10.09-v112-ieltsquirrel","items":[{"section":"writing-general","title":"Application for an internship","prompt":"You have recently learned about an opening for an internship at a local company that you are interested in.\n\nWrite a letter to the director of the company. In your letter,\n- introduce yourself\n- explain how you learned about the internship\n- explain why you would be suitable for the internship","answer":"Dear Ms Carter,\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">I am writing to apply for</strong> the marketing internship recently advertised on your company's careers page. I am currently completing the final year of a business degree at Hanoi University, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with a particular focus on</strong> digital communication and consumer behaviour.\n\nI <strong class=\"key-phrase\">learned about the position through</strong> a presentation given by one of your former interns at a university careers event. Her description of the company's practical training and collaborative culture <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strongly encouraged me to apply</strong>.\n\nI believe <strong class=\"key-phrase\">I would be a suitable candidate for three reasons</strong>. First, I have managed social-media content for a student organisation and increased event registrations through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a targeted campaign</strong>. Second, my coursework has given me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a solid foundation</strong> in market research and data analysis. Finally, I am <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comfortable working in English</strong> and have experience presenting ideas to both small groups and large audiences.\n\nI have attached my CV and would welcome the opportunity to discuss how I could contribute to your team. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Thank you for considering my application</strong>.\n\nYours sincerely,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Formal","phrases":[{"en":"I am writing to apply for","context":"I am writing to apply for the marketing internship recently advertised on your company's careers page. I am currently completing the final year of a business degree at Hanoi University, with a particular focus on digital communication and consumer behaviour."},{"en":"with a particular focus on","context":"I am writing to apply for the marketing internship recently advertised on your company's careers page. I am currently completing the final year of a business degree at Hanoi University, with a particular focus on digital communication and consumer behaviour."},{"en":"learned about the position through","context":"I learned about the position through a presentation given by one of your former interns at a university careers event. Her description of the company's practical training and collaborative culture strongly encouraged me to apply."},{"en":"strongly encouraged me to apply","context":"I learned about the position through a presentation given by one of your former interns at a university careers event. Her description of the company's practical training and collaborative culture strongly encouraged me to apply."},{"en":"a targeted campaign","context":"First, I have managed social-media content for a student organisation and increased event registrations through a targeted campaign."},{"en":"a solid foundation","context":"Second, my coursework has given me a solid foundation in market research and data analysis."},{"en":"Thank you for considering my application","context":"I have attached my CV and would welcome the opportunity to discuss how I could contribute to your team. Thank you for considering my application."},{"en":"I would be a suitable candidate for three reasons","context":"I believe I would be a suitable candidate for three reasons."},{"en":"comfortable working in English","context":"Finally, I am comfortable working in English and have experience presenting ideas to both small groups and large audiences."}],"subtype":"Formal","summary":"Application for an internship","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-001","_displayNo":"#001"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Applying for a part-time restaurant job","prompt":"A local restaurant is recruiting a part-time employee.\n\nWrite to the restaurant manager. In your letter:\n- apply for the position\n- describe relevant experience\n- state the days and hours you can work","answer":"Dear Sir or Madam,\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">I am writing to apply for</strong> the part-time front-of-house position advertised in the window of Green Table Restaurant.\n\nI have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">eighteen months of relevant customer-service experience</strong> from working at a busy cafe near my university. My duties included taking orders, handling card payments, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">resolving minor complaints</strong> and preparing the dining area between sittings. The role taught me to remain polite and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work efficiently under pressure</strong>, even during crowded weekend shifts. I also hold a current food-safety certificate and speak both English and Vietnamese fluently.\n\nI am available from 5 p.m. until closing on Monday, Wednesday and Friday, and at any time on Saturday or Sunday. During university holidays, I could take additional daytime shifts if required.\n\nI would welcome the opportunity to discuss the position and am <strong class=\"key-phrase\">available for interview</strong> this week. My CV and the contact details of my previous supervisor are attached.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Thank you for considering my application</strong>.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Formal","phrases":[{"en":"I am writing to apply for","context":"I am writing to apply for the part-time front-of-house position advertised in the window of Green Table Restaurant."},{"en":"work efficiently under pressure","context":"The role taught me to remain polite and work efficiently under pressure, even during crowded weekend shifts."},{"en":"available for interview","context":"I would welcome the opportunity to discuss the position and am available for interview this week. My CV and the contact details of my previous supervisor are attached."},{"en":"Thank you for considering my application","context":"Thank you for considering my application."},{"en":"eighteen months of relevant customer-service experience","context":"I have eighteen months of relevant customer-service experience from working at a busy cafe near my university."},{"en":"resolving minor complaints","context":"My duties included taking orders, handling card payments, resolving minor complaints and preparing the dining area between sittings."}],"subtype":"Formal","summary":"Applying for a part-time restaurant job","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-002","_displayNo":"#002"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Complaint about a faulty camera","prompt":"You bought a camera that was faulty, returned it to the shop and were promised a repair, but a week later it is still not ready.\n\nWrite to the company. In your letter:\n- introduce yourself and the purchase\n- explain what has happened\n- state what action you expect","answer":"Dear Sir or Madam,\n\nMy name is Alex Morgan, and I am writing regarding a Lumix G90 camera purchased from your King Street branch on 2 June, receipt number KS-7714.\n\nThe camera appeared normal in the shop, but at home the shutter repeatedly froze and several photographs were saved with a black band across the image. I returned it the following day, and your representative assured me that the repair would take no more than three working days.\n\nIt has now been eight days, and staff cannot tell me when the camera will be ready. This delay is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no longer acceptable</strong> because I bought the equipment for a paid photography assignment next weekend. I have already given the shop <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ample opportunity</strong> to resolve the fault.\n\nI would therefore like either a new replacement of the same specification or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a full refund</strong>. If neither can be arranged by Friday, I will have to hire another camera and may seek reimbursement for that cost. I hope the matter can be brought to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a prompt resolution</strong>.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Formal","phrases":[{"en":"no longer acceptable","context":"This delay is no longer acceptable because I bought the equipment for a paid photography assignment next weekend."},{"en":"ample opportunity","context":"I have already given the shop ample opportunity to resolve the fault."},{"en":"a full refund","context":"I would therefore like either a new replacement of the same specification or a full refund."},{"en":"a prompt resolution","context":"I hope the matter can be brought to a prompt resolution."}],"subtype":"Formal","summary":"Complaint about a faulty camera","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-003","_displayNo":"#003"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Complaint about a theatre visit","prompt":"You attended a good play at a theatre but could not enjoy it because of problems at the venue.\n\nWrite to the theatre manager. In your letter:\n- identify the performance and date\n- describe the problems\n- suggest what should be done","answer":"Dear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am writing about the evening performance of The Last Station at the Grand Theatre on Friday, 5 June.\n\nThe play itself was excellent, but the experience was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">seriously undermined by several problems</strong>. When my partner and I arrived, another couple had been allocated the same seats. An usher moved us to the front row, where a stage light shone directly into our eyes throughout the first act. In addition, the air-conditioning was not working, and the auditorium became uncomfortably hot.\n\nI appreciate that isolated technical problems occur, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a double-booking error should have been resolved</strong> before the performance began. The replacement seats were clearly unsuitable, yet staff did not offer us another option or explain how to make a complaint.\n\nI suggest reviewing the booking system, checking lighting positions before each show and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">training ushers to handle seating errors consistently</strong>. Given the disruption, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">I would also consider a partial refund</strong> or replacement tickets to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a reasonable remedy</strong>.\n\nI look forward to your response.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Formal","phrases":[{"en":"seriously undermined by several problems","context":"The play itself was excellent, but the experience was seriously undermined by several problems."},{"en":"a reasonable remedy","context":"I suggest reviewing the booking system, checking lighting positions before each show and training ushers to handle seating errors consistently. Given the disruption, I would also consider a partial refund or replacement tickets to be a reasonable remedy."},{"en":"a double-booking error should have been resolved","context":"I appreciate that isolated technical problems occur, but a double-booking error should have been resolved before the performance began. The replacement seats were clearly unsuitable, yet staff did not offer us another option or explain how to make a complaint."},{"en":"training ushers to handle seating errors consistently","context":"I suggest reviewing the booking system, checking lighting positions before each show and training ushers to handle seating errors consistently. Given the disruption, I would also consider a partial refund or replacement tickets to be a reasonable remedy."},{"en":"I would also consider a partial refund","context":"I suggest reviewing the booking system, checking lighting positions before each show and training ushers to handle seating errors consistently. Given the disruption, I would also consider a partial refund or replacement tickets to be a reasonable remedy."}],"subtype":"Formal","summary":"Complaint about a theatre visit","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-004","_displayNo":"#004"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Complaint about noise at a recreation ground","prompt":"A school regularly brings pupils to a recreation ground near your home, and recent visits have caused problems.\n\nWrite to the head teacher. In your letter:\n- explain why you enjoy living near the ground\n- describe the recent problems\n- suggest what the school should do","answer":"Dear Sir or Madam,\n\nI live opposite Maple Recreation Ground and am writing about the sports sessions held there by pupils from your school.\n\nResidents generally value the open space because it provides trees, walking paths and a safe place for local children to play. We also understand that school sport is valuable and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have never objected to normal daytime activity</strong>.\n\nHowever, during the past three weeks, several groups have left plastic bottles and food packaging behind. Footballs have repeatedly struck parked cars, and pupils have gathered outside nearby houses shouting for up to thirty minutes after the sessions ended. These incidents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disrupt local residents</strong>, particularly older people and parents with young children.\n\nCould teachers please <strong class=\"key-phrase\">supervise pupils more closely</strong>, ensure all rubbish is collected and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep pupils within the marked playing area</strong>? It would also help if groups left promptly once the lesson had finished. These simple measures would allow the school to continue using the ground <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without creating unnecessary conflict</strong>.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Formal","phrases":[{"en":"disrupt local residents","context":"These incidents disrupt local residents, particularly older people and parents with young children."},{"en":"supervise pupils more closely","context":"Could teachers please supervise pupils more closely, ensure all rubbish is collected and keep pupils within the marked playing area?"},{"en":"have never objected to normal daytime activity","context":"Residents generally value the open space because it provides trees, walking paths and a safe place for local children to play. We also understand that school sport is valuable and have never objected to normal daytime activity."},{"en":"keep pupils within the marked playing area","context":"Could teachers please supervise pupils more closely, ensure all rubbish is collected and keep pupils within the marked playing area?"},{"en":"without creating unnecessary conflict","context":"These simple measures would allow the school to continue using the ground without creating unnecessary conflict."}],"subtype":"Formal","summary":"Complaint about noise at a recreation ground","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-005","_displayNo":"#005"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Complaint about noisy neighbours","prompt":"You live in an apartment building but are having trouble with the behaviour of your neighbours.\n\nWrite a letter to inform the building manager of the situation. In your letter,\n- explain who you are\n- describe the situation concerning your neighbours' behaviour\n- suggest how the situation could be dealt with","answer":"Dear Building Manager,\n\nI am a resident of apartment 8B and am writing to report repeated late-night noise from apartment 8C. The problem has continued for nearly three weeks and is now seriously affecting my sleep and ability to work.\n\nThe occupants frequently <strong class=\"key-phrase\">play loud music</strong> and move furniture between midnight and 2 a.m. Last Saturday, a large gathering continued until almost 3 a.m., despite two polite requests from nearby residents to reduce the noise. I fully understand that people should be able to enjoy their homes, but the current situation is unreasonable in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a shared building</strong>.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">I would be grateful if you could</strong> remind the residents of the building's quiet-hours policy and speak to them directly. If the disturbance continues, a written warning may be necessary. It may also be useful to circulate the policy to all residents so that expectations are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">clear and consistent</strong>.\n\nPlease let me know what action will be taken. I am available to provide a dated record of the incidents if required.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Formal","phrases":[{"en":"play loud music","context":"The occupants frequently play loud music and move furniture between midnight and 2 a.m."},{"en":"a shared building","context":"I fully understand that people should be able to enjoy their homes, but the current situation is unreasonable in a shared building."},{"en":"I would be grateful if you could","context":"I would be grateful if you could remind the residents of the building's quiet-hours policy and speak to them directly."},{"en":"clear and consistent","context":"It may also be useful to circulate the policy to all residents so that expectations are clear and consistent."}],"subtype":"Formal","summary":"Complaint about noisy neighbours","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-006","_displayNo":"#006"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Complaint about sports-centre changing rooms","prompt":"The changing rooms at a sports centre are in poor condition. You have complained before but nothing has changed.\n\nWrite to the manager. In your letter:\n- describe the problems\n- explain what happened after earlier complaints\n- state what action you expect","answer":"Dear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am writing to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make a formal complaint</strong> about the changing rooms at Greenfield Sports Centre.\n\nSeveral lockers no longer close properly, two showers have been leaking for weeks, and the floor is frequently covered in water. The broken ventilation system has also created a strong smell and visible mould near the ceiling. These conditions are unpleasant and, more importantly, pose a safety risk.\n\nI have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raised this issue repeatedly</strong> at reception during the past month. Each time, I was told that maintenance had been informed, yet no repairs have been completed and no warning signs have been placed near the wet floor.\n\nI would like the centre to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take immediate corrective action</strong> by repairing the showers and lockers, cleaning the affected areas thoroughly and publishing a clear timetable for the ventilation work. Until the floor is safe, staff should inspect it regularly and place appropriate signs where necessary.\n\nPlease respond within seven days with details of the action you intend to take.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Formal","phrases":[{"en":"make a formal complaint","context":"I am writing to make a formal complaint about the changing rooms at Greenfield Sports Centre."},{"en":"raised this issue repeatedly","context":"I have raised this issue repeatedly at reception during the past month. Each time, I was told that maintenance had been informed, yet no repairs have been completed and no warning signs have been placed near the wet floor."},{"en":"take immediate corrective action","context":"I would like the centre to take immediate corrective action by repairing the showers and lockers, cleaning the affected areas thoroughly and publishing a clear timetable for the ventilation work."}],"subtype":"Formal","summary":"Complaint about sports-centre changing rooms","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-007","_displayNo":"#007"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Disputing an increase in car insurance","prompt":"Your insurer has informed you that your monthly car-insurance payment will increase, and you disagree with the decision.\n\nWrite to the insurer. In your letter:\n- explain why you are writing\n- state why the increase seems unjustified\n- say what you want the company to do","answer":"Dear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am writing regarding your notice dated 10 June, which states that my monthly car-insurance premium will rise by 28 per cent from August.\n\nI would ask you to review this decision because my circumstances <strong class=\"key-phrase\">do not appear to justify such a substantial increase</strong>. I have held policy number MC-44821 for six years and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">maintained a completely claim-free record</strong>. My vehicle, annual mileage and home address have not changed, and the car is parked overnight in a locked garage. I have also completed an approved defensive-driving course since my last renewal.\n\nPlease provide a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">clear breakdown</strong> of the factors used to calculate the new premium. If the increase resulted from incorrect information, I would like the policy amended immediately. If it reflects a general pricing change, please reconsider the quotation <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in light of</strong> my history and confirm whether <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a higher excess or revised level of cover</strong> would reduce the cost.\n\nI look forward to receiving a written response before the current policy expires.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Formal","phrases":[{"en":"clear breakdown","context":"Please provide a clear breakdown of the factors used to calculate the new premium."},{"en":"in light of","context":"If it reflects a general pricing change, please reconsider the quotation in light of my history and confirm whether a higher excess or revised level of cover would reduce the cost."},{"en":"maintained a completely claim-free record","context":"I have held policy number MC-44821 for six years and maintained a completely claim-free record."},{"en":"do not appear to justify such a substantial increase","context":"I would ask you to review this decision because my circumstances do not appear to justify such a substantial increase."},{"en":"a higher excess or revised level of cover","context":"If it reflects a general pricing change, please reconsider the quotation in light of my history and confirm whether a higher excess or revised level of cover would reduce the cost."}],"subtype":"Formal","summary":"Disputing an increase in car insurance","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-008","_displayNo":"#008"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Documents left in a taxi","prompt":"You accidentally left important papers in a taxi.\n\nWrite to the taxi company. In your letter:\n- give details of the journey\n- explain why the papers matter\n- request specific help from the company","answer":"Dear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am writing to report a blue document case that I left in one of your taxis yesterday evening.\n\nI was collected outside City Hospital at approximately 6.20 p.m. and travelled to 18 King Street. I paid by card at 6.45 p.m., and the receipt shows journey reference TX-80417. I was seated in the rear left-hand seat and believe the case slipped onto the floor when I got out.\n\nIt contains signed employment forms, certified copies of my qualifications and other time-sensitive documents required for a visa appointment on Thursday. Some of these documents would take several weeks to replace.\n\nCould you please use the payment reference to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trace the vehicle</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">contact the driver directly</strong>? I would be happy to collect the case from your office or meet the driver at a convenient location. A member of your staff can reach me on 07700 123456 at any time today.\n\nThank you for treating this matter as urgent.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Formal","phrases":[{"en":"trace the vehicle","context":"Could you please use the payment reference to trace the vehicle and contact the driver directly?"},{"en":"contact the driver directly","context":"Could you please use the payment reference to trace the vehicle and contact the driver directly?"}],"subtype":"Formal","summary":"Documents left in a taxi","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-009","_displayNo":"#009"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Enquiring about a conference centre","prompt":"Your employer is planning a training course and has asked you to check whether a local conference centre is suitable.\n\nWrite to the centre manager. In your letter:\n- describe the course\n- list the equipment and facilities required\n- ask further questions about the venue","answer":"Dear Sir or Madam,\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">I am writing to enquire about</strong> hiring the Lakeside Conference Centre for a two-day staff training course in October.\n\nThe programme will focus on customer-service skills and is expected to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accommodate approximately</strong> forty-five participants. We would require one main room <strong class=\"key-phrase\">arranged in cabaret style</strong> and two smaller rooms for group exercises. The course would run from 9 a.m. to 4.30 p.m. on both days.\n\nPlease confirm whether the venue provides reliable Wi-Fi, a projector, wireless microphones and other audio-visual equipment. We would also need flip charts, movable tables and access to printing facilities. Tea, coffee and a light lunch should be available for all participants, including several people with dietary restrictions.\n\nCould you let me know whether parking is included, whether the building is fully accessible and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what cancellation terms apply</strong>? I would also appreciate an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">itemised quotation</strong> covering room hire, equipment and catering.\n\nIf the centre is available on 15–16 October, I would be pleased to arrange a site visit next week.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Formal","phrases":[{"en":"I am writing to enquire about","context":"I am writing to enquire about hiring the Lakeside Conference Centre for a two-day staff training course in October."},{"en":"accommodate approximately","context":"The programme will focus on customer-service skills and is expected to accommodate approximately forty-five participants."},{"en":"itemised quotation","context":"Could you let me know whether parking is included, whether the building is fully accessible and what cancellation terms apply? I would also appreciate an itemised quotation covering room hire, equipment and catering."},{"en":"arranged in cabaret style","context":"We would require one main room arranged in cabaret style and two smaller rooms for group exercises."},{"en":"what cancellation terms apply","context":"Could you let me know whether parking is included, whether the building is fully accessible and what cancellation terms apply? I would also appreciate an itemised quotation covering room hire, equipment and catering."}],"subtype":"Formal","summary":"Enquiring about a conference centre","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-010","_displayNo":"#010"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Important papers left at a hotel","prompt":"After attending a meeting at a hotel, you realised that you had left important documents behind.\n\nWrite to the hotel manager. In your letter:\n- state where you may have left the documents\n- explain why they are important\n- say what you would like the manager to do","answer":"Dear Sir or Madam,\n\nI attended a business meeting at the Riverside Hotel yesterday and believe I left a folder containing important documents in the building.\n\nThe meeting took place in the Oak Conference Room from 2 p.m. to 5 p.m. I was seated near the window and may have left the folder either under my chair or on the table beside the projector. It is dark grey and marked “Northgate Project”.\n\nThe folder contains signed contracts and confidential documents relating to a client tender that closes on Friday. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Replacing the originals would be difficult</strong> and could delay the submission, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">I would be grateful if</strong> a member of staff could check the meeting room as soon as possible. Please also ask the reception desk and housekeeping team whether it has been handed in.\n\nIf the folder is found, could you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep it securely at reception</strong>? I can collect it this afternoon. Alternatively, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">I will cover the cost</strong> if you need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">send it by registered post</strong> to my office.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Formal","phrases":[{"en":"I would be grateful if","context":"Replacing the originals would be difficult and could delay the submission, so I would be grateful if a member of staff could check the meeting room as soon as possible."},{"en":"send it by registered post","context":"If the folder is found, could you keep it securely at reception? I can collect it this afternoon. Alternatively, I will cover the cost if you need to send it by registered post to my office."},{"en":"Replacing the originals would be difficult","context":"Replacing the originals would be difficult and could delay the submission, so I would be grateful if a member of staff could check the meeting room as soon as possible."},{"en":"keep it securely at reception","context":"If the folder is found, could you keep it securely at reception? I can collect it this afternoon. Alternatively, I will cover the cost if you need to send it by registered post to my office."},{"en":"I will cover the cost","context":"If the folder is found, could you keep it securely at reception? I can collect it this afternoon. Alternatively, I will cover the cost if you need to send it by registered post to my office."}],"subtype":"Formal","summary":"Important papers left at a hotel","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-011","_displayNo":"#011"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Improving parking at an office building","prompt":"Your office building has no adequate parking for employees.\n\nWrite to the building manager. In your letter:\n- explain the current problem\n- suggest a solution\n- describe how better parking would help staff","answer":"Dear Mr Lewis,\n\nI am writing on behalf of several employees regarding the lack of parking at Westgate Business Centre.\n\nAt present, only twelve spaces are available for more than eighty staff members. The surrounding streets have strict time limits, and the nearest public car park is almost twenty minutes away on foot. This has become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a daily source of frustration</strong>, particularly for employees who begin early shifts or need to transport equipment.\n\nOne practical solution would be to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lease part of the unused land behind the building</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">introduce a permit system</strong> for staff. If this is not immediately possible, the centre could negotiate discounted monthly passes with the Riverside car park and reserve a few existing spaces for employees with mobility needs.\n\nImproved parking would save staff time at the start of the day, make the building more accessible and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improve punctuality</strong>. It would also prevent staff from moving their cars during working hours to avoid penalties.\n\nI would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">welcome the opportunity to discuss</strong> these options with you and provide a list of employees who would use the scheme.\n\nYours sincerely,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Formal","phrases":[{"en":"a daily source of frustration","context":"This has become a daily source of frustration, particularly for employees who begin early shifts or need to transport equipment."},{"en":"introduce a permit system","context":"One practical solution would be to lease part of the unused land behind the building and introduce a permit system for staff."},{"en":"improve punctuality","context":"Improved parking would save staff time at the start of the day, make the building more accessible and improve punctuality. It would also prevent staff from moving their cars during working hours to avoid penalties."},{"en":"welcome the opportunity to discuss","context":"I would welcome the opportunity to discuss these options with you and provide a list of employees who would use the scheme."},{"en":"lease part of the unused land behind the building","context":"One practical solution would be to lease part of the unused land behind the building and introduce a permit system for staff."}],"subtype":"Formal","summary":"Improving parking at an office building","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-012","_displayNo":"#012"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Lost watch on a train","prompt":"You left a valuable item on a train.\n\nWrite to the railway company. In your letter:\n- describe the item\n- state where and when you lost it\n- explain what you would like the company to do","answer":"Dear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am writing to report a watch that I believe I left on one of your trains on Monday, 8 June.\n\nThe item is a silver Seiko wristwatch with a dark-blue face and a narrow metal strap. There is a small scratch beside the number six, and the initials “A.M.” are engraved on the back. Although the watch is not especially expensive, it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">of considerable sentimental value</strong> because it was a graduation gift from my grandfather.\n\nI travelled on the 18:10 service from Manchester Piccadilly to Leeds and was seated in Coach C, seat 24A. I removed the watch while working on my laptop and probably left it on the fold-down table when I got off at Leeds at approximately 19:05.\n\nCould you please check the lost-property records for that service and ask the cleaning team whether the watch was found? If it has been handed in, I can collect it from Leeds station or pay any reasonable postage costs to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">arrange for its return</strong>.\n\nI have attached a photograph of the watch and a copy of my ticket.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Formal","phrases":[{"en":"of considerable sentimental value","context":"Although the watch is not especially expensive, it is of considerable sentimental value because it was a graduation gift from my grandfather."},{"en":"arrange for its return","context":"Could you please check the lost-property records for that service and ask the cleaning team whether the watch was found? If it has been handed in, I can collect it from Leeds station or pay any reasonable postage costs to arrange for its return."}],"subtype":"Formal","summary":"Lost watch on a train","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-013","_displayNo":"#013"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Offering a talk for an international day","prompt":"A local college is holding an international day, and you would like to give a presentation about your country.\n\nWrite to the college principal. In your letter:\n- describe what you would talk about\n- explain why the students would find it useful\n- ask about the arrangements for speakers","answer":"Dear Principal Harris,\n\nI am writing to offer a short presentation about Vietnam for the college's international day in September.\n\nMy talk would cover three areas: regional food traditions, the importance of family celebrations, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the rapid changes taking place in major cities</strong> such as Hanoi and Ho Chi Minh City. I would use photographs, a short video and a few everyday objects to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bring the subject to life</strong> rather than presenting a list of facts.\n\nI believe the session would be useful because it would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give students a balanced picture</strong> of a country that is often discussed mainly as a tourist destination. It could also encourage students from different backgrounds to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compare customs and ask questions about cultural similarities</strong>.\n\nCould you please tell me how long each presentation should be and how many students are expected? I would also like to know the technical requirements, particularly whether a projector, speakers and internet access will be available.\n\nI would be pleased to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">send a brief outline in advance</strong>.\n\nYours sincerely,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Formal","phrases":[{"en":"bring the subject to life","context":"I would use photographs, a short video and a few everyday objects to bring the subject to life rather than presenting a list of facts."},{"en":"give students a balanced picture","context":"I believe the session would be useful because it would give students a balanced picture of a country that is often discussed mainly as a tourist destination."},{"en":"the rapid changes taking place in major cities","context":"My talk would cover three areas: regional food traditions, the importance of family celebrations, and the rapid changes taking place in major cities such as Hanoi and Ho Chi Minh City."},{"en":"compare customs and ask questions about cultural similarities","context":"It could also encourage students from different backgrounds to compare customs and ask questions about cultural similarities."},{"en":"send a brief outline in advance","context":"I would be pleased to send a brief outline in advance."}],"subtype":"Formal","summary":"Offering a talk for an international day","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-014","_displayNo":"#014"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Problems with shared college accommodation","prompt":"You share a college room with another student, but the arrangement is making it difficult for you to study.\n\nWrite to the college accommodation officer. In your letter:\n- describe the current situation\n- explain why it is affecting your work\n- state what type of accommodation you would prefer","answer":"Dear Sir or Madam,\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">I am writing to request</strong> a change to my current accommodation in West Hall, where I share Room 214 with another student.\n\nAlthough my roommate is friendly, our routines are completely incompatible. He regularly invites several friends into the room late in the evening and often plays online games without headphones. In addition, he works night shifts and returns at around 2 a.m., which wakes me almost every day.\n\nThis situation is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">seriously affecting my ability to study</strong> because I am preparing for final examinations and need several uninterrupted hours of study each evening. I have tried discussing the matter with him, but the improvements have only been temporary. As a result, I am sleeping poorly and have already missed two morning lectures.\n\nI would therefore be grateful if I could move to a single room, ideally in a quieter part of the residence. If no single room is available, I would be willing to share with a student who follows a more regular daytime schedule. I hope the college can help me find <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a quieter environment</strong> <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at your earliest convenience</strong>.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Formal","phrases":[{"en":"I am writing to request","context":"I am writing to request a change to my current accommodation in West Hall, where I share Room 214 with another student."},{"en":"seriously affecting my ability to study","context":"This situation is seriously affecting my ability to study because I am preparing for final examinations and need several uninterrupted hours of study each evening."},{"en":"a quieter environment","context":"I hope the college can help me find a quieter environment at your earliest convenience."},{"en":"at your earliest convenience","context":"I hope the college can help me find a quieter environment at your earliest convenience."}],"subtype":"Formal","summary":"Problems with shared college accommodation","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-015","_displayNo":"#015"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Requesting help from a language tutor","prompt":"Your children are learning a foreign language at school but require extra support. A friend has recommended a private tutor.\n\nWrite to the tutor. In your letter:\n- explain why you are contacting them\n- describe the areas your children need help with\n- ask about their experience and teaching suggestions","answer":"Dear Ms Patel,\n\nI am writing on the recommendation of my colleague, Sarah Nguyen, to ask whether you could provide additional French tuition for my two children, aged eleven and thirteen.\n\nBoth children understand basic classroom vocabulary, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">they are falling behind in speaking and listening</strong>. My younger son is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hesitant to answer questions aloud</strong>, while my daughter finds it difficult to follow normal-speed recordings and often confuses verb endings. Their school teacher believes that regular one-to-one practice would give them the targeted support they need.\n\nCould you please <strong class=\"key-phrase\">outline your experience</strong> of teaching learners in this age group and explain how you normally assess a new student? I would also appreciate your view on whether they should study together or have separate lessons. Ideally, we are looking for one or two sessions each week after 5 p.m.\n\nPlease let me know your fees, availability and any materials the children would need. I would be happy to arrange an introductory meeting <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at your convenience</strong>.\n\nYours sincerely,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Formal","phrases":[{"en":"outline your experience","context":"Could you please outline your experience of teaching learners in this age group and explain how you normally assess a new student?"},{"en":"they are falling behind in speaking and listening","context":"Both children understand basic classroom vocabulary, but they are falling behind in speaking and listening."},{"en":"hesitant to answer questions aloud","context":"My younger son is hesitant to answer questions aloud, while my daughter finds it difficult to follow normal-speed recordings and often confuses verb endings."},{"en":"at your convenience","context":"Please let me know your fees, availability and any materials the children would need. I would be happy to arrange an introductory meeting at your convenience."}],"subtype":"Formal","summary":"Requesting help from a language tutor","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-016","_displayNo":"#016"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Suggestions for improving a public library","prompt":"Your local public library has asked users to suggest improvements to its services and facilities.\n\nWrite to the librarian. In your letter:\n- describe what you value about the library\n- explain what could be improved\n- suggest practical changes","answer":"Dear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am writing in response to your request for suggestions about the future development of Central Library.\n\nThe library is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a valuable community resource</strong>. I particularly value the knowledgeable staff, the quiet study area on the first floor and the wide selection of newspapers and reference books. The online reservation system is also straightforward and saves users a great deal of time.\n\nHowever, some facilities currently fall short of users’ needs. There are too few power sockets for laptop users, several chairs are worn and uncomfortable, and the building closes at 6 p.m. on weekdays, which is inconvenient for people who work full time. The children's section also becomes extremely crowded at weekends.\n\nI would recommend installing additional charging points and replacing the oldest furniture. It would also be helpful to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">extend the opening hours</strong> until at least 8 p.m. on two or three weekdays. Finally, a small booking system for children's activities could prevent overcrowding and allow staff to plan sessions more effectively.\n\nThank you for inviting public feedback. I hope these suggestions are useful.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Formal","phrases":[{"en":"a valuable community resource","context":"The library is a valuable community resource."},{"en":"extend the opening hours","context":"It would also be helpful to extend the opening hours until at least 8 p.m."}],"subtype":"Formal","summary":"Suggestions for improving a public library","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-017","_displayNo":"#017"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Trying to reconnect with a childhood friend","prompt":"You saw a newspaper article about a childhood friend whom you have not contacted for many years.\n\nWrite to the editor. In your letter:\n- identify the article and edition\n- explain how you lost contact\n- ask for help reconnecting with your friend","answer":"Dear Editor,\n\nI am writing about the profile of Dr Linh Tran published in the Sunday edition of the City Herald on 14 June.\n\nLinh and I were close friends at Nguyen Du Secondary School, but we lost touch after her family moved overseas in 2008. At the time, neither of us used social media regularly, and the email address I had for her later stopped working. I have tried searching professional networks, but I have not been able to confirm whether the person I found is the same Linh.\n\nThe details in your article, particularly the name of her school and her interest in environmental science, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strongly suggest that she is my former classmate</strong>. I understand that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">you cannot disclose private contact information</strong>. However, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">I would be grateful if you could</strong> <strong class=\"key-phrase\">forward my details</strong> to her on my behalf. She could then decide whether she wishes to respond.\n\nYour assistance could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put us back in contact</strong> after nearly two decades, and I would greatly appreciate it.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Formal","phrases":[{"en":"I would be grateful if you could","context":"However, I would be grateful if you could forward my details to her on my behalf."},{"en":"forward my details","context":"However, I would be grateful if you could forward my details to her on my behalf."},{"en":"put us back in contact","context":"Your assistance could put us back in contact after nearly two decades, and I would greatly appreciate it."},{"en":"strongly suggest that she is my former classmate","context":"The details in your article, particularly the name of her school and her interest in environmental science, strongly suggest that she is my former classmate."},{"en":"you cannot disclose private contact information","context":"I understand that you cannot disclose private contact information."}],"subtype":"Formal","summary":"Trying to reconnect with a childhood friend","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-018","_displayNo":"#018"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Advice about choosing work instead of college","prompt":"A friend has asked whether they should attend college or start working. You think employment would suit them better.\n\nWrite to your friend. In your letter:\n- explain why college may not suit them\n- describe the advantages of getting a job\n- suggest suitable kinds of work","answer":"Hi Jenny,\n\nIt was great to hear from you. I've thought carefully about your choice, and I honestly think starting work would suit you better than going <strong class=\"key-phrase\">straight to college</strong>.\n\nYou have always been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a practical, hands-on learner</strong>, whereas most college courses involve long lectures, extensive reading and several years of theoretical study. I'm not saying you couldn't succeed, but I suspect you'd <strong class=\"key-phrase\">find that environment frustrating</strong> and lose motivation quite quickly.\n\nA job would allow you to gain real-world experience, earn your own money and discover which type of work you genuinely enjoy. It would also give you more independence, and you could still take a professional course later once you have a clearer goal.\n\nBecause you're confident, organised and good with people, I'd look at customer service, hotel reception or event support. An apprenticeship in sales or digital marketing might also be worth considering because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">combines training with paid work</strong>.\n\nOf course, the final choice is yours, but there's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no one-size-fits-all answer</strong>. Starting work now doesn't close the door to education in the future.\n\nTake care,\nAlex","topic":"Informal","phrases":[{"en":"straight to college","context":"It was great to hear from you. I've thought carefully about your choice, and I honestly think starting work would suit you better than going straight to college."},{"en":"a practical, hands-on learner","context":"You have always been a practical, hands-on learner, whereas most college courses involve long lectures, extensive reading and several years of theoretical study."},{"en":"find that environment frustrating","context":"I'm not saying you couldn't succeed, but I suspect you'd find that environment frustrating and lose motivation quite quickly."},{"en":"combines training with paid work","context":"Because you're confident, organised and good with people, I'd look at customer service, hotel reception or event support. An apprenticeship in sales or digital marketing might also be worth considering because it combines training with paid work."},{"en":"no one-size-fits-all answer","context":"Of course, the final choice is yours, but there's no one-size-fits-all answer. Starting work now doesn't close the door to education in the future."}],"subtype":"Informal","summary":"Advice about choosing work instead of college","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-019","_displayNo":"#019"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Changing plans for a cinema trip","prompt":"You arranged to see a film with a friend, but you can no longer go. Another friend can take your place.\n\nWrite to your friend. In your letter:\n- explain why you cannot attend\n- say who can go instead\n- explain why they would be good company","answer":"Hi Ben,\n\nI'm really sorry, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">something urgent has come up</strong> and I won't be able to see the film with you on Saturday evening. My manager has asked me to cover a client presentation at short notice, and the meeting cannot be moved because the client is flying home on Sunday.\n\nThe good news is that Minh can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">step in at short notice</strong>. I checked with him before writing, and he's free at 7 p.m. He's already seen the first film in the series, so you won't have to explain the story, and he has offered to collect the tickets from the machine before you arrive.\n\nI think you two will <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get on really well</strong>. You both love science-fiction films, follow the same football team and have a very similar sense of humour. I've given Minh your number, and he'll message you tomorrow to confirm where to meet.\n\nI'm disappointed to miss it, but let's arrange another evening next week. Dinner is on me to make up for changing the plan.\n\nAll the best,\nAlex","topic":"Informal","phrases":[{"en":"something urgent has come up","context":"I'm really sorry, but something urgent has come up and I won't be able to see the film with you on Saturday evening."},{"en":"step in at short notice","context":"The good news is that Minh can step in at short notice."},{"en":"get on really well","context":"I think you two will get on really well. You both love science-fiction films, follow the same football team and have a very similar sense of humour. I've given Minh your number, and he'll message you tomorrow to confirm where to meet."}],"subtype":"Informal","summary":"Changing plans for a cinema trip","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-020","_displayNo":"#020"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Dog care while on holiday","prompt":"You are going on holiday next week and you need to ask your friend to watch your dog while you are away.\n\nWrite a letter to your friend. In your letter,\n- state how long you will be away\n- tell them what needs to be done\n- state how you will compensate them","answer":"Hi Daniel,\n\nI hope you're well. I'm writing to ask whether you could look after my dog, Milo, while I'm away in Da Nang from 12 to 19 July. You know him well, so I think staying with you would be far less stressful for him than going to a kennel.\n\nHis routine is simple. He needs a thirty-minute walk every morning and evening, fresh water throughout the day, and one cup of dry food at 7 a.m. and another at 6 p.m. I've left his food, lead and vet's number in a labelled box by the front door. Please also give him his allergy tablet every second evening.\n\nI know this is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a significant favour</strong>, and I don't want you to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">out of pocket</strong>. I'll cover all expenses and take you out for dinner when I return. I'd also be happy to help with your house move next month.\n\nThanks for considering it. Please let me know whether this arrangement would work for you.\n\nBest,\nAlex","topic":"Informal","phrases":[{"en":"a significant favour","context":"I know this is a significant favour, and I don't want you to be out of pocket. I'll cover all expenses and take you out for dinner when I return. I'd also be happy to help with your house move next month."},{"en":"out of pocket","context":"I know this is a significant favour, and I don't want you to be out of pocket. I'll cover all expenses and take you out for dinner when I return. I'd also be happy to help with your house move next month."}],"subtype":"Informal","summary":"Dog care while on holiday","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-021","_displayNo":"#021"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Friend visiting from overseas","prompt":"Your friend from overseas is coming to visit you next month.\n\nWrite a letter to your friend. In your letter,\n- tell them what they need to bring\n- tell them what they will be doing during the time they are visiting\n- warn them about some things that might be challenging","answer":"Hi Maya,\n\nIt's great news that you'll be visiting Hanoi next month. The weather will probably be hot and humid, so bring light clothes, comfortable walking shoes and a compact rain jacket. You should also install a local transport app before you arrive because it makes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">getting around much easier</strong>.\n\nI've planned <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a balanced programme</strong> for your stay. We can spend the first day exploring the Old Quarter, visit the Museum of Ethnology on Saturday, and take a day trip to Ninh Binh if the weather is clear. I've also booked a small street-food tour, which should give you a good introduction to Vietnamese cuisine.\n\nThere are two things to be prepared for. Traffic can seem chaotic at first, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">crossing busy roads requires patience</strong>. In addition, some smaller shops accept cash only, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">it's sensible to carry a modest amount</strong> of Vietnamese currency.\n\nI'm really looking forward to seeing you and showing you around. Send me your flight details when they are confirmed.\n\nTake care,\nAlex","topic":"Informal","phrases":[{"en":"a balanced programme","context":"I've planned a balanced programme for your stay."},{"en":"getting around much easier","context":"You should also install a local transport app before you arrive because it makes getting around much easier."},{"en":"crossing busy roads requires patience","context":"There are two things to be prepared for. Traffic can seem chaotic at first, and crossing busy roads requires patience. In addition, some smaller shops accept cash only, so it's sensible to carry a modest amount of Vietnamese currency."},{"en":"it's sensible to carry a modest amount","context":"There are two things to be prepared for. Traffic can seem chaotic at first, and crossing busy roads requires patience. In addition, some smaller shops accept cash only, so it's sensible to carry a modest amount of Vietnamese currency."}],"subtype":"Informal","summary":"Friend visiting from overseas","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-022","_displayNo":"#022"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"House and pet care during a holiday","prompt":"A friend has agreed to look after your home and pet while you are away.\n\nWrite to your friend. In your letter:\n- give contact details for your trip\n- explain how to care for your pet\n- describe any other household tasks","answer":"Hi Daniel,\n\nThanks again for agreeing to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep an eye on</strong> the flat and Milo while I'm in Da Nang from 12 to 19 July. I will be staying at the Seaview Hotel, and you can reach me on my usual mobile number. If I'm unavailable, please contact my sister Lan on 0903 555 812.\n\nMilo needs two small meals a day, at about 7 a.m. and 6 p.m. His food is in the lower kitchen cupboard. Please take him for a short walk each evening and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stick to his usual routine</strong>, as sudden changes tend to upset him. He's not allowed on the balcony unless someone is with him.\n\nCould you also water the plants in the living room twice during the week and collect any post from the box downstairs? The rubbish is collected on Tuesday evening, so it'd be great if you could leave the bin outside then.\n\nPlease <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make yourself at home</strong>; there's plenty of food in the fridge, and you're welcome to use the television and coffee machine.\n\nAll the best,\nAlex","topic":"Informal","phrases":[{"en":"keep an eye on","context":"Thanks again for agreeing to keep an eye on the flat and Milo while I'm in Da Nang from 12 to 19 July. I will be staying at the Seaview Hotel, and you can reach me on my usual mobile number. If I'm unavailable, please contact my sister Lan on 0903 555 812."},{"en":"stick to his usual routine","context":"Please take him for a short walk each evening and stick to his usual routine, as sudden changes tend to upset him."},{"en":"make yourself at home","context":"Please make yourself at home; there's plenty of food in the fridge, and you're welcome to use the television and coffee machine."}],"subtype":"Informal","summary":"House and pet care during a holiday","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-023","_displayNo":"#023"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Instructions for looking after a flat","prompt":"A friend will stay in your apartment and look after it while you are away.\n\nWrite to your friend. In your letter:\n- give useful information about the apartment\n- provide important instructions\n- explain what to do if a problem occurs","answer":"Hi Sophie,\n\nThanks for staying in my flat while I'm away. I thought I'd <strong class=\"key-phrase\">run through the essentials</strong> before I leave on Friday.\n\nThe spare key is in the lockbox beside the front door; the code is 4821. The hot-water switch is inside the kitchen cupboard, and the Wi-Fi password is written underneath the router. Please use anything in the kitchen and feel free to invite Mark over for dinner.\n\nThe main things are to water the balcony plants every three days, collect the post and keep the place secure whenever you go out. The windows in the bedroom can look closed even when they're not locked, so please check them carefully. The rubbish bins are taken downstairs on Wednesday evening.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">In the unlikely event</strong> of a leak or electrical fault, turn off the main switch beside the fridge and call the building caretaker, Mr Hoang, on 0908 222 410. For anything less urgent, just message me.\n\nHave a comfortable stay, and thanks again for helping.\n\nBest,\nAlex","topic":"Informal","phrases":[{"en":"run through the essentials","context":"Thanks for staying in my flat while I'm away. I thought I'd run through the essentials before I leave on Friday."},{"en":"In the unlikely event","context":"In the unlikely event of a leak or electrical fault, turn off the main switch beside the fridge and call the building caretaker, Mr Hoang, on 0908 222 410. For anything less urgent, just message me."}],"subtype":"Informal","summary":"Instructions for looking after a flat","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-024","_displayNo":"#024"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Selling a television to a friend","prompt":"You want to sell your television and think a friend may be interested.\n\nWrite to your friend. In your letter:\n- explain why you are selling it\n- describe the television\n- suggest when your friend could see it","answer":"Hi Chris,\n\nI remember you mentioned that you were looking for a television for your new flat, so I thought I'd offer you mine <strong class=\"key-phrase\">before advertising it online</strong>.\n\nI'm only selling it because I'm moving into a furnished apartment next month and there's already a television there. The one I have is a 43-inch Samsung smart TV, about two years old, and it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in excellent working order</strong>. The screen has no scratches, the remote is included, and it supports Netflix, YouTube and wireless screen sharing. I still have the original box and receipt.\n\nI paid £420 for it, but I think £230 would be a fair price, especially as you wouldn't need to arrange delivery. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">There's no pressure at all</strong> if it's not what you want.\n\nYou could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">come round and have a look</strong> on Saturday afternoon or Sunday morning. I can connect it to the internet so that you can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">test the picture, sound and apps properly</strong>.\n\nLet me know what suits you.\n\nAll the best,\nAlex","topic":"Informal","phrases":[{"en":"in excellent working order","context":"The one I have is a 43-inch Samsung smart TV, about two years old, and it's in excellent working order."},{"en":"come round and have a look","context":"You could come round and have a look on Saturday afternoon or Sunday morning. I can connect it to the internet so that you can test the picture, sound and apps properly."},{"en":"before advertising it online","context":"I remember you mentioned that you were looking for a television for your new flat, so I thought I'd offer you mine before advertising it online."},{"en":"There's no pressure at all","context":"I paid £420 for it, but I think £230 would be a fair price, especially as you wouldn't need to arrange delivery. There's no pressure at all if it's not what you want."},{"en":"test the picture, sound and apps properly","context":"You could come round and have a look on Saturday afternoon or Sunday morning. I can connect it to the internet so that you can test the picture, sound and apps properly."}],"subtype":"Informal","summary":"Selling a television to a friend","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-025","_displayNo":"#025"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Planning the annual staff party","prompt":"Your workplace holds an annual party, and you have been asked to help organise the next one.\n\nWrite to your colleagues. In your letter:\n- explain what should be changed from last year\n- mention what did not work well last time\n- suggest a meeting place and time to plan the event","answer":"Hello everyone,\n\nI am writing so that we can start planning this year's staff party before venues become fully booked.\n\nI think we should learn from last year's experience and make the event less formal. Last year's long speeches delayed dinner, while the fixed seating plan made it difficult for colleagues from different departments to mix. The music was also far too loud during the first hour, so conversation was almost impossible.\n\nThis time, I suggest a shorter welcome, a buffet rather than a set meal, and two simple team activities. We should also appoint one person to coordinate the venue and another to look after the schedule on the night, which would help us <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep the programme running smoothly</strong>.\n\nCould we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set aside half an hour</strong> next Wednesday at 4.30 p.m. in Meeting Room 3? I will bring a shortlist of venues and an initial budget. Please send me any dietary requirements or activity ideas before then so that we can discuss them efficiently.\n\nBest regards,\nAlex","topic":"Semi-formal","phrases":[{"en":"keep the programme running smoothly","context":"We should also appoint one person to coordinate the venue and another to look after the schedule on the night, which would help us keep the programme running smoothly."},{"en":"set aside half an hour","context":"Could we set aside half an hour next Wednesday at 4.30 p.m. in Meeting Room 3? I will bring a shortlist of venues and an initial budget. Please send me any dietary requirements or activity ideas before then so that we can discuss them efficiently."}],"subtype":"Semi-formal","summary":"Planning the annual staff party","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-026","_displayNo":"#026"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Request to pay rent late","prompt":"You are having financial difficulties and need to ask your landlord for permission to pay this month's rent late.\n\nWrite to your landlord. In your letter:\n- explain the reason\n- state when you will pay\n- explain how you will avoid the problem in future","answer":"Dear Mr Lewis,\n\nI am writing to ask whether I may pay this month's rent on 15 July rather than on the usual first day of the month.\n\nThe delay is due to a temporary cash-flow problem. My employer changed its payroll provider last week, and several salaries, including mine, will not be processed until 12 July. I have sufficient income to cover the rent, but the funds will not reach my account before the normal payment date.\n\nI will <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make the full payment</strong> of £950 immediately after my salary is deposited and will send you the transfer confirmation that day. I apologise for the inconvenience and appreciate that regular payment is important.\n\nTo prevent a similar delay, I will keep one month's rent in a separate savings account and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set up a standing order</strong> for future payments. This means the rent will be transferred automatically even if my salary date changes again.\n\nI hope this temporary arrangement is acceptable. Please let me know if you need written confirmation from my employer.\n\nYours sincerely,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Semi-formal","phrases":[{"en":"make the full payment","context":"I will make the full payment of £950 immediately after my salary is deposited and will send you the transfer confirmation that day. I apologise for the inconvenience and appreciate that regular payment is important."},{"en":"set up a standing order","context":"To prevent a similar delay, I will keep one month's rent in a separate savings account and set up a standing order for future payments. This means the rent will be transferred automatically even if my salary date changes again."}],"subtype":"Semi-formal","summary":"Request to pay rent late","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-027","_displayNo":"#027"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Requesting extended leave from work","prompt":"You need more than one month of leave from work for personal reasons.\n\nWrite to your manager. In your letter:\n- explain why and how long you need leave\n- describe how you will remain informed\n- suggest how your work can be covered","answer":"Dear Ms Reynolds,\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">I am writing to request</strong> six weeks of unpaid leave from 3 August to 13 September so that I can support my father during his recovery from major surgery.\n\nHe lives alone in another city and will need help with transport, meals and medical appointments for the first month after leaving hospital. My siblings can assist later, but they are unable to cover the initial period.\n\nI am committed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ensuring a smooth handover</strong> during my absence. Before leaving, I will complete the July performance report, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prepare detailed handover notes</strong> and brief Minh on the two client accounts I currently manage. He has already agreed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">act as the main contact</strong>, while Hoa can approve routine expenses.\n\nTo keep up to date, I will check email each weekday and join the Monday team meeting remotely whenever possible. I will also be available by phone for genuinely urgent questions, although I will <strong class=\"key-phrase\">avoid interfering with day-to-day decisions</strong>.\n\nI hope this plan provides adequate cover for the department. I would be grateful for an opportunity to discuss the request this week.\n\nYours sincerely,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Semi-formal","phrases":[{"en":"I am writing to request","context":"I am writing to request six weeks of unpaid leave from 3 August to 13 September so that I can support my father during his recovery from major surgery."},{"en":"ensuring a smooth handover","context":"I am committed to ensuring a smooth handover during my absence."},{"en":"prepare detailed handover notes","context":"Before leaving, I will complete the July performance report, prepare detailed handover notes and brief Minh on the two client accounts I currently manage."},{"en":"act as the main contact","context":"He has already agreed to act as the main contact, while Hoa can approve routine expenses."},{"en":"avoid interfering with day-to-day decisions","context":"To keep up to date, I will check email each weekday and join the Monday team meeting remotely whenever possible. I will also be available by phone for genuinely urgent questions, although I will avoid interfering with day-to-day decisions."}],"subtype":"Semi-formal","summary":"Requesting extended leave from work","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-028","_displayNo":"#028"},{"section":"writing-general","title":"Volunteering on a school trip","prompt":"Your child's school is arranging a three-day trip abroad and has asked parents to volunteer.\n\nWrite to the head teacher. In your letter:\n- explain why you would like to join the trip\n- describe how you could help\n- ask for further practical details","answer":"Dear Mrs Brown,\n\nI am writing to volunteer for the three-day school trip to Singapore next month.\n\nI would be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">happy to assist</strong> because my daughter is travelling abroad without family for the first time, and I also believe parent volunteers can make the trip safer and less demanding for staff. I have previously accompanied two local day trips and am comfortable supervising groups of teenagers.\n\nDuring the visit, I could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take responsibility for</strong> a small group during airport transfers, museum visits and meal breaks. I am trained in first aid and speak conversational Mandarin, which may also be useful if the group needs assistance in public places. In addition, I can help keep attendance records and communicate updates to parents.\n\nBefore confirming, could you please <strong class=\"key-phrase\">clarify the practical arrangements</strong>? I would like to know the travel dates, estimated cost, sleeping arrangements and whether volunteers need a background check or additional insurance.\n\nThank you for considering my offer. I am available to discuss the trip after school any day this week.\n\nYours sincerely,\nAlex Morgan","topic":"Semi-formal","phrases":[{"en":"happy to assist","context":"I would be happy to assist because my daughter is travelling abroad without family for the first time, and I also believe parent volunteers can make the trip safer and less demanding for staff."},{"en":"take responsibility for","context":"During the visit, I could take responsibility for a small group during airport transfers, museum visits and meal breaks."},{"en":"clarify the practical arrangements","context":"Before confirming, could you please clarify the practical arrangements? I would like to know the travel dates, estimated cost, sleeping arrangements and whether volunteers need a background check or additional insurance."}],"subtype":"Semi-formal","summary":"Volunteering on a school trip","visual":null,"id":"writing-general-029","_displayNo":"#029"},{"id":"writing-academic-004","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Cities & Housing","title":"House prices in five cities","prompt":"The diagrams provide some information about the housing prices of five cities around the world from 1990 to 2000. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The charts give information about changes in housing prices in five cities around the world between 1990 and 2000.\n\nOverall, there were significant differences between the housing markets of these five cities during the last decade of the twentieth century. Two of the cities saw a long period of growth, while prices in the other three fell.\n\nSan Francisco and Beijing were the two cities where housing prices <strong class=\"key-phrase\">climbed throughout the ten-year period</strong>. In the first five years, a 2% rise was recorded in San Francisco, and a 4% rise in Beijing. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The increase accelerated</strong> in the following years, with a 3% gain in San Francisco and nearly 6% in Beijing.\n\nBy comparison, the property market in Rome <strong class=\"key-phrase\">was in recession throughout</strong>, with an average drop of 3.5 per cent. Hong Kong and Sydney <strong class=\"key-phrase\">experienced a similar slump</strong> between 1995 and 2000. Housing prices slipped by 6 per cent in Hong Kong, having risen by 5% in the previous five years. The decrease in Sydney was narrower, at only 2 per cent, compared with a 3% increase in the first half of the 1990s.","phrases":[{"en":"experienced a similar slump","context":"Hong Kong and Sydney experienced a similar slump between 1995 and 2000."},{"en":"climbed throughout the ten-year period","context":"San Francisco and Beijing were the two cities where housing prices climbed throughout the ten-year period."},{"en":"The increase accelerated","context":"The increase accelerated in the following years, with a 3% gain in San Francisco and nearly 6% in Beijing."},{"en":"was in recession throughout","context":"By comparison, the property market in Rome was in recession throughout, with an average drop of 3.5 per cent."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-004-1"]},"_displayNo":"#001","summary":"Diagrams provide some information about the housing prices of five cities…"},{"id":"writing-academic-006","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Cities & Housing","title":"Changes in house prices","prompt":"The chart below shows information about changes in average house prices in five different cities between 1990 and 2002 compared with the average house prices in 1989.","answer":"The bar chart compares the cost of an average house in five major cities over a period of 13 years from 1989.\n\nWe can see that house prices fell overall between 1990 and 1995, but the period from 1996 to 2002 was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far more positive for sellers</strong>, with most of the cities seeing rising prices. London <strong class=\"key-phrase\">experienced by far the greatest changes in</strong> house prices over the 13-year period.\n\nOver the 5 years after 1989, the cost of average homes in Tokyo and London dropped by around 7%, while New York house prices went down by 5%. By contrast, prices rose by approximately 2% in both Madrid and Frankfurt.\n\nBetween 1996 and 2002, London house prices <strong class=\"key-phrase\">jumped to around 12% above the 1989 average</strong>. Homebuyers in New York also had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pay significantly more</strong>, with prices rising to 5% above the 1989 average, but homes in Tokyo remained cheaper than they were in 1989. The cost of an average home in Madrid rose by a further 2%, while prices in Frankfurt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remained stable</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"experienced by far the greatest changes in","context":"London experienced by far the greatest changes in house prices over the 13-year period."},{"en":"pay significantly more","context":"Homebuyers in New York also had to pay significantly more, with prices rising to 5% above the 1989 average, but homes in Tokyo remained cheaper than they were in 1989."},{"en":"far more positive for sellers","context":"We can see that house prices fell overall between 1990 and 1995, but the period from 1996 to 2002 was far more positive for sellers, with most of the cities seeing rising prices."},{"en":"jumped to around 12% above the 1989 average","context":"Between 1996 and 2002, London house prices jumped to around 12% above the 1989 average."},{"en":"remained stable","context":"The cost of an average home in Madrid rose by a further 2%, while prices in Frankfurt remained stable."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-006-1"]},"_displayNo":"#002","summary":"Changes in average house prices in five cities from 1990 to 2002 compared with 1989."},{"id":"writing-academic-025","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Crime & Safety","title":"US violent crime","prompt":"The line chart below shows serious violent crime levels from 1978 to 2000 in the US. Serious violent crimes include rape, robbery, aggravated assault and homicide. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The line chart shows how levels of serious violent crime in the United States changed between 1978 and 2000. It gives figures for the total crime level, victimisations reported to the police, crimes recorded by the police and arrests, all measured per thousand people.\n\nOverall, none of the four measures <strong class=\"key-phrase\">was subject to strong fluctuations</strong> over the two decades shown, and the total crime level remained well above the other three throughout. By the end of the century, all of the figures were moving towards lower levels.\n\nIn the first ten years, there were fluctuations in the total crime level, but the overall trend was upward. By about 1989 it had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reached a record high of</strong> around 4.5 offences per thousand Americans, compared with slightly over 3 offences in 1980. Victimisations reported to the police rose and fell at a lower level, between 2 and 2.5 offences, over the same period, while crimes recorded by the police <strong class=\"key-phrase\">showed a steady increase</strong>, reaching their highest point in 1994 at over 1.5 offences per thousand. The arrest rate was the lowest of the four, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">levelling off below</strong> 0.5 offences per 1,000.\n\nAfter this peak, the total violent crime level declined gradually, as did victimisations reported to the police. By 2000, the total stood at 3 offences per thousand Americans, while the figure for victimisations was half that number. Crimes recorded by the police and arrests for violent crime both <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dropped slightly in the last five years</strong>, standing at 1.5 and 0.5 offences respectively in 2000.","phrases":[{"en":"showed a steady increase","context":"Victimisations reported to the police rose and fell at a lower level, between 2 and 2.5 offences, over the same period, while crimes recorded by the police showed a steady increase, reaching their highest point in 1994 at over 1.5 offences per thousand."},{"en":"was subject to strong fluctuations","context":"Overall, none of the four measures was subject to strong fluctuations over the two decades shown, and the total crime level remained well above the other three throughout. By the end of the century, all of the figures were moving towards lower levels."},{"en":"reached a record high of","context":"By about 1989 it had reached a record high of around 4.5 offences per thousand Americans, compared with slightly over 3 offences in 1980."},{"en":"levelling off below","context":"The arrest rate was the lowest of the four, levelling off below 0.5 offences per 1,000."},{"en":"dropped slightly in the last five years","context":"Crimes recorded by the police and arrests for violent crime both dropped slightly in the last five years, standing at 1.5 and 0.5 offences respectively in 2000."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-025-1"]},"_displayNo":"#003","summary":"Line chart below shows serious violent crime levels from 1978 to 2000 in…"},{"id":"writing-academic-061","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Crime & Safety","title":"UK vehicle offences","prompt":"The table below shows motor vehicle offences in England and Wales between 1994 and 2004. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The table shows the numbers of six different types of motor vehicle offence recorded in England and Wales between 1994 and 2004.\n\nOverall, speed limit offences and licence offences were the two main types of motor vehicle offence over this period, and speed limit offences <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stood out because they rose so sharply</strong>.\n\nIn 1994, licence, insurance and record keeping offences were the most common type of offence, and the figure rose to 957 thousand in 2004 after a ten-year period of fluctuations. Speed limit offences initially ranked second in 1994 but became the main type of offence by 2004, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recording a ten-year high of</strong> 2,076 thousand, more than three times the figure ten years earlier. Neglect of traffic signs and directions came third, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">averaging at around</strong> 250 thousand per year.\n\nDangerous, careless or drunken driving was the fourth main type of offence, falling for much of the period from 1994 to 2004 but finally <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hitting an all-time high of</strong> 231 thousand. Two other types, accident offences and the unauthorised taking or theft of motor vehicles, were the least common, also declining gradually.","phrases":[{"en":"averaging at around","context":"Neglect of traffic signs and directions came third, averaging at around 250 thousand per year."},{"en":"stood out because they rose so sharply","context":"Overall, speed limit offences and licence offences were the two main types of motor vehicle offence over this period, and speed limit offences stood out because they rose so sharply."},{"en":"recording a ten-year high of","context":"Speed limit offences initially ranked second in 1994 but became the main type of offence by 2004, recording a ten-year high of 2,076 thousand, more than three times the figure ten years earlier."},{"en":"hitting an all-time high of","context":"Dangerous, careless or drunken driving was the fourth main type of offence, falling for much of the period from 1994 to 2004 but finally hitting an all-time high of 231 thousand."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-061-1"]},"_displayNo":"#004","summary":"Table below shows motor vehicle offences in England and Wales between 1994…"},{"id":"writing-academic-027","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Education","title":"Education and science participation","prompt":"The charts below show the levels of participation in education and science in developing and industrialised countries in 1980 and 1990.","answer":"The three bar charts show average years of schooling, numbers of scientists and technicians, and research and development spending in developing and developed countries. Figures are given for 1980 and 1990.\n\nIt is clear from the charts that the figures for developed countries are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">much higher than those for</strong> developing nations. Also, the charts show an overall increase in participation in education and science from 1980 to 1990.\n\nPeople in developing nations attended school for an average of around 3 years, with only a slight increase in years of schooling from 1980 to 1990. On the other hand, the figure for industrialised countries rose from nearly 9 years of schooling in 1980 to nearly 11 years in 1990.\n\nFrom 1980 to 1990, the number of scientists and technicians in industrialised countries <strong class=\"key-phrase\">almost doubled to</strong> about 70 per 1000 people. Spending on research and development also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saw rapid growth</strong> in these countries, reaching $350 billion in 1990. By contrast, the number of science workers in developing countries <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remained below</strong> 20 per 1000 people, and research spending fell from about $50 billion to only $25 billion.","phrases":[{"en":"much higher than those for","context":"It is clear from the charts that the figures for developed countries are much higher than those for developing nations. Also, the charts show an overall increase in participation in education and science from 1980 to 1990."},{"en":"almost doubled to","context":"From 1980 to 1990, the number of scientists and technicians in industrialised countries almost doubled to about 70 per 1000 people."},{"en":"saw rapid growth","context":"Spending on research and development also saw rapid growth in these countries, reaching $350 billion in 1990."},{"en":"remained below","context":"By contrast, the number of science workers in developing countries remained below 20 per 1000 people, and research spending fell from about $50 billion to only $25 billion."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-027-1"]},"_displayNo":"#005","summary":"Charts below show the levels of participation in education and science in…"},{"id":"writing-academic-028","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Education","title":"UK school spending","prompt":"The three pie charts below show how the changes in annual spending by a particular UK schools in 1981, 1991, and 2001.","answer":"The three pie charts compare how a school in the UK divided its annual budget between different areas of expenditure in three separate years over a 20-year period.\n\nIt is clear that teachers’ salaries <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made up the largest proportion of</strong> the school's spending in all three years (1981, 1991 and 2001). By contrast, insurance was the smallest cost in each year, although its share of the budget grew over the period.\n\nIn 1981, 40% of the school's budget went on teachers’ salaries. This figure rose to 50% in 1991, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fell back by 5%</strong> in 2001. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The proportion of spending on</strong> other workers’ wages <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fell steadily</strong> over the 20-year period, from 28% of the budget in 1981 to only 15% in 2001.\n\nExpenditure on insurance stood at only 2% of the total in 1981, but it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had reached 8% by 2001</strong>. The percentages for resources and for furniture and equipment, meanwhile, fluctuated. The figure for resources was highest in 1991, at 20%, while the proportion of spending on furniture and equipment <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reached its peak</strong> in 2001, at 23%.","phrases":[{"en":"made up the largest proportion of","context":"It is clear that teachers’ salaries made up the largest proportion of the school's spending in all three years (1981, 1991 and 2001). By contrast, insurance was the smallest cost in each year, although its share of the budget grew over the period."},{"en":"The proportion of spending on","context":"The proportion of spending on other workers’ wages fell steadily over the 20-year period, from 28% of the budget in 1981 to only 15% in 2001."},{"en":"fell steadily","context":"The proportion of spending on other workers’ wages fell steadily over the 20-year period, from 28% of the budget in 1981 to only 15% in 2001."},{"en":"reached its peak","context":"The figure for resources was highest in 1991, at 20%, while the proportion of spending on furniture and equipment reached its peak in 2001, at 23%."},{"en":"fell back by 5%","context":"This figure rose to 50% in 1991, but fell back by 5% in 2001."},{"en":"had reached 8% by 2001","context":"Expenditure on insurance stood at only 2% of the total in 1981, but it had reached 8% by 2001."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-028-1"]},"_displayNo":"#006","summary":"Three pie charts below show how the changes in annual spending by a…"},{"id":"writing-academic-030","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Education","title":"Children's TV and academic results","prompt":"This tables show data about the amount of television watched by children in different countries (hours per day) and also the average academic achievement of people in these countries (% of people completing each level). Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The tables give information about the number of hours of television watched each day by children in a range of countries, along with the proportion of people in those countries who complete three levels of education.\n\nIt is clear that both television viewing and educational attainment <strong class=\"key-phrase\">vary widely from country to country</strong>. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The most striking feature is that</strong> the countries whose children watch the most television each day are also those with the highest rates of university completion.\n\nTurning first to television, children in the USA, Canada and the UK watch the most, at 4 hours a day. China and Italy come next with 3 hours, Brazil and India stand at 2 hours each, and Malta and the UAE record only 1 hour per day, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">which is a quarter of the maximum</strong>.\n\nIn terms of academic achievement, 100% of children complete school in Italy, Canada and Malta, and the figures for the other countries are all in the nineties, apart from India at 80%. High school completion <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ranges from</strong> 100% in Malta to figures in the nineties in Italy, Canada and the UAE, and in the eighties in the UK, USA and Brazil. China and India have the lowest rates, at 68% and 60% respectively.\n\nAt university level, the UK has the highest level of completion, at 39%, followed by Canada with 32% and the USA with 29%. Italy, China and Brazil are all in the mid-twenties, while Malta and the UAE stand at 18% and 19%. India <strong class=\"key-phrase\">records the lowest figure of all</strong>, at only 12%.","phrases":[{"en":"ranges from","context":"High school completion ranges from 100% in Malta to figures in the nineties in Italy, Canada and the UAE, and in the eighties in the UK, USA and Brazil."},{"en":"The most striking feature is that","context":"The most striking feature is that the countries whose children watch the most television each day are also those with the highest rates of university completion."},{"en":"vary widely from country to country","context":"It is clear that both television viewing and educational attainment vary widely from country to country."},{"en":"which is a quarter of the maximum","context":"China and Italy come next with 3 hours, Brazil and India stand at 2 hours each, and Malta and the UAE record only 1 hour per day, which is a quarter of the maximum."},{"en":"records the lowest figure of all","context":"India records the lowest figure of all, at only 12%."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-030-1"]},"_displayNo":"#007","summary":"This tables show data about the amount of television watched by children in…"},{"id":"writing-academic-051","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Education","title":"Problems in primary schools","prompt":"The table compares the problems faced by children in two primary schools in 2005 and 2015.","answer":"The table compares two primary schools in terms of the proportions of their pupils who experienced five different educational problems in the years 2005 and 2015.\n\nIt is noticeable that school A had higher proportions of children with all five educational difficulties in both years. However, while school A managed to reduce the incidence of most of the problems between 2005 and 2015, school B <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saw an overall rise</strong> in the percentage of children who were struggling.\n\nIn 2005, 42% of school A's pupils found it difficult to follow instructions, whereas only 6% of pupils in school B experienced this problem. Similarly, between 30 and 40 per cent of children attending school A had problems in the areas of spelling, listening, verbal expression and concentration in lessons, while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the equivalent figures</strong> for school B stood at between 5 and 15 per cent.\n\nIn 2015, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the difference between the two schools was less pronounced</strong>. Notably, the proportion of children who struggled to follow instructions fell by 24% in school A, and this school also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saw falls of</strong> 22%, 15%, 14% and 5% in the figures for children who had problems with concentration, listening, verbal expression and spelling. In school B, however, the proportion of children who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">struggled with</strong> spelling and following instructions doubled, to 10% and 12% respectively, and there was almost no change in the incidence of listening, verbal or concentration problems.","phrases":[{"en":"saw an overall rise","context":"However, while school A managed to reduce the incidence of most of the problems between 2005 and 2015, school B saw an overall rise in the percentage of children who were struggling."},{"en":"the equivalent figures","context":"Similarly, between 30 and 40 per cent of children attending school A had problems in the areas of spelling, listening, verbal expression and concentration in lessons, while the equivalent figures for school B stood at between 5 and 15 per cent."},{"en":"struggled with","context":"In school B, however, the proportion of children who struggled with spelling and following instructions doubled, to 10% and 12% respectively, and there was almost no change in the incidence of listening, verbal or concentration problems."},{"en":"the difference between the two schools was less pronounced","context":"In 2015, the difference between the two schools was less pronounced."},{"en":"saw falls of","context":"Notably, the proportion of children who struggled to follow instructions fell by 24% in school A, and this school also saw falls of 22%, 15%, 14% and 5% in the figures for children who had problems with concentration, listening, verbal expression and spelling."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-051-1"]},"_displayNo":"#008","summary":"Table below gives information about the problems faced by children in two…"},{"id":"writing-academic-002","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Environment & Climate","title":"Kolkata climate","prompt":"The climograph below shows average monthly temperatures and rainfall in the city of Kolkata.","answer":"The chart compares average figures for temperature and precipitation over the course of a calendar year in Kolkata.\n\nIt is noticeable that monthly figures for precipitation in Kolkata <strong class=\"key-phrase\">vary considerably</strong>, whereas monthly temperatures <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remain relatively stable</strong>. Rainfall is highest from July to August, while temperatures are highest in April and May.\n\nBetween the months of January and May, average temperatures in Kolkata rise from their lowest point at around 20°C to a peak of just over 30°C. Average rainfall in the city also rises over the same period, from approximately 20mm of rain in January to 100mm in May.\n\nWhile temperatures <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay roughly the same</strong> for the next four months, the amount of rainfall <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more than doubles</strong> between May and June. Figures for precipitation remain above 250mm from June to September, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">peaking at around 330mm in July</strong>. The final three months of the year <strong class=\"key-phrase\">see a dramatic fall in</strong> precipitation, to a low of about 10mm in December, and a steady drop in temperatures back to the January average.","phrases":[{"en":"stay roughly the same","context":"While temperatures stay roughly the same for the next four months, the amount of rainfall more than doubles between May and June."},{"en":"more than doubles","context":"While temperatures stay roughly the same for the next four months, the amount of rainfall more than doubles between May and June."},{"en":"see a dramatic fall in","context":"The final three months of the year see a dramatic fall in precipitation, to a low of about 10mm in December, and a steady drop in temperatures back to the January average."},{"en":"vary considerably","context":"It is noticeable that monthly figures for precipitation in Kolkata vary considerably, whereas monthly temperatures remain relatively stable. Rainfall is highest from July to August, while temperatures are highest in April and May."},{"en":"remain relatively stable","context":"It is noticeable that monthly figures for precipitation in Kolkata vary considerably, whereas monthly temperatures remain relatively stable. Rainfall is highest from July to August, while temperatures are highest in April and May."},{"en":"peaking at around 330mm in July","context":"Figures for precipitation remain above 250mm from June to September, peaking at around 330mm in July."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-002-1"]},"_displayNo":"#009","summary":"Climograph below shows average monthly temperatures and rainfall in the…"},{"id":"writing-academic-031","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Environment & Climate","title":"European waste management","prompt":"The chart below compares levels of recycling, as well as some less environmentally friendly forms of waste management, in fifteen European countries.","answer":"The bar chart shows the percentage of waste that is recycled, put into landfill or incinerated in various EU countries.\n\nIf we look at the fifteen EU countries as a whole, it is clear that more waste <strong class=\"key-phrase\">goes to landfill sites</strong> than to any other refuse management facility. In fact, while around half of the countries <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put the majority of their waste into landfill sites</strong>, only two countries recycle at least 50% of the waste that they produce.\n\nAustria and Belgium are the most environmentally friendly of the fifteen countries, recycling around 60% and 50% of their waste respectively. Germany and Sweden also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">appear to have effective recycling programmes</strong>, resulting in roughly 40% of waste being processed in this way in each country.\n\nOther EU countries recycle far less. Greece, Ireland and the UK, for example, only recycle around 10% of refuse, and put between 80 and 90 per cent of their waste into landfill. Finally, while most non-recycled waste in the EU area goes to landfill, four countries, namely Luxembourg, Sweden, the Netherlands and Denmark, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">incinerate more waste than they bury</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"goes to landfill sites","context":"If we look at the fifteen EU countries as a whole, it is clear that more waste goes to landfill sites than to any other refuse management facility."},{"en":"put the majority of their waste into landfill sites","context":"In fact, while around half of the countries put the majority of their waste into landfill sites, only two countries recycle at least 50% of the waste that they produce."},{"en":"appear to have effective recycling programmes","context":"Germany and Sweden also appear to have effective recycling programmes, resulting in roughly 40% of waste being processed in this way in each country."},{"en":"incinerate more waste than they bury","context":"Finally, while most non-recycled waste in the EU area goes to landfill, four countries, namely Luxembourg, Sweden, the Netherlands and Denmark, incinerate more waste than they bury."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-031-1"]},"_displayNo":"#010","summary":"Chart below compares levels of recycling, as well as some less…"},{"id":"writing-academic-035","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Environment & Climate","title":"Waste produced by three companies","prompt":"The graph shows the amounts of waste produced by three companies between 2000 and 2015.","answer":"The line graph compares three companies in terms of their waste output between the years 2000 and 2015.\n\nIt is clear that there were significant changes in the amounts of waste produced by all three companies shown on the graph. While companies A and B <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saw waste output fall over the 15-year period</strong>, the amount of waste produced by company C increased considerably.\n\nIn 2000, company A produced 12 tonnes of waste, while companies B and C produced around 8 tonnes and 4 tonnes of waste material respectively. Over the following 5 years, the waste output of companies B and C rose by around 2 tonnes, but the figure for company A fell by approximately 1 tonne.\n\nFrom 2005 to 2015, company A <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cut waste production by roughly 3 tonnes</strong>, and company B reduced its waste by around 7 tonnes. By contrast, company C saw an increase in waste production of approximately 4 tonnes over the same 10-year period. By 2015, company C's waste output <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had risen to 10 tonnes</strong>, while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the respective amounts of waste</strong> from companies A and B had dropped to 8 tonnes and only 3 tonnes.","phrases":[{"en":"saw waste output fall over the 15-year period","context":"It is clear that there were significant changes in the amounts of waste produced by all three companies shown on the graph. While companies A and B saw waste output fall over the 15-year period, the amount of waste produced by company C increased considerably."},{"en":"cut waste production by roughly 3 tonnes","context":"From 2005 to 2015, company A cut waste production by roughly 3 tonnes, and company B reduced its waste by around 7 tonnes."},{"en":"had risen to 10 tonnes","context":"By 2015, company C's waste output had risen to 10 tonnes, while the respective amounts of waste from companies A and B had dropped to 8 tonnes and only 3 tonnes."},{"en":"the respective amounts of waste","context":"By 2015, company C's waste output had risen to 10 tonnes, while the respective amounts of waste from companies A and B had dropped to 8 tonnes and only 3 tonnes."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-035-1"]},"_displayNo":"#011","summary":"Graph below shows the amounts of waste produced by three companies over a…"},{"id":"writing-academic-037","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Environment & Climate","title":"Waste production by country","prompt":"The table below shows the amount of waste production (in millions of tonnes) in six different countries over a twenty-year period.","answer":"The table compares the amounts of waste that were produced in six countries in the years 1980, 1990 and 2000.\n\nIn each of these years, the US produced more waste than Ireland, Japan, Korea, Poland and Portugal combined. It is also noticeable that Korea was the only country that managed to reduce its waste output by the year 2000.\n\nBetween 1980 and 2000, waste production in the US rose from 131 to 192 million tonnes, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rising trends were also seen in</strong> Japan, Poland and Portugal. Japan's waste output increased from 28 to 53 million tonnes, while Poland and Portugal <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saw waste totals increase</strong> from 4 to 6.6 and from 2 to 5 million tonnes respectively.\n\nThe trends for Ireland and Korea were noticeably different from those described above. In Ireland, waste production <strong class=\"key-phrase\">increased more than eightfold</strong>, from only 0.6 million tonnes in 1980 to 5 million tonnes in 2000. Korea, by contrast, cut its waste output by 12 million tonnes between 1990 and 2000. Ireland and Portugal <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remained the smallest producers throughout</strong>, each generating 5 million tonnes by 2000, while the US total was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">almost forty times higher than that figure</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"saw waste totals increase","context":"Japan's waste output increased from 28 to 53 million tonnes, while Poland and Portugal saw waste totals increase from 4 to 6.6 and from 2 to 5 million tonnes respectively."},{"en":"increased more than eightfold","context":"In Ireland, waste production increased more than eightfold, from only 0.6 million tonnes in 1980 to 5 million tonnes in 2000."},{"en":"rising trends were also seen in","context":"Between 1980 and 2000, waste production in the US rose from 131 to 192 million tonnes, and rising trends were also seen in Japan, Poland and Portugal."},{"en":"remained the smallest producers throughout","context":"Ireland and Portugal remained the smallest producers throughout, each generating 5 million tonnes by 2000, while the US total was almost forty times higher than that figure."},{"en":"almost forty times higher than that figure","context":"Ireland and Portugal remained the smallest producers throughout, each generating 5 million tonnes by 2000, while the US total was almost forty times higher than that figure."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-037-1"]},"_displayNo":"#012","summary":"Table below shows the amount of waste production (in millions of tonnes) in…"},{"id":"writing-academic-039","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Environment & Climate","title":"Transport carbon emissions","prompt":"The table below shows carbon dioxide emissions from transport in three European countries in 1994 and 2004.","answer":"The table compares the amount of carbon dioxide produced by transport in Germany, France and the United Kingdom in 1994 and 2004, with the totals broken down by type of transport.\n\nOverall, road transport was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">responsible for the majority of emissions</strong> in all three countries, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remained the biggest source throughout</strong>. Germany was the only one of the three to cut its total emissions, while both the UK and France failed to reduce carbon dioxide emissions from transport between 1994 and 2004.\n\nGermany, which had higher emissions than either the United Kingdom or France, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saw its total decrease slightly</strong> from 47.2 to 46.7 million tonnes. By comparison, France recorded an increase of four million tonnes from 34.7 million in 1994, while the United Kingdom saw a smaller rise of 2.5 million tonnes over the same period.\n\nIn the United Kingdom, road transport produced 32.6 million tonnes of emissions in 2004, 2 million more than ten years earlier, while the other three transport sources <strong class=\"key-phrase\">showed no remarkable growth</strong>. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">A similar pattern was seen</strong> in France, where road transport added 3.6 million tonnes to the total emissions within ten years. Germany, by contrast, was the only country of the three to experience a drop in road transport emissions, and its other transport sources also had a lower emission volume, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with the exception of civil aviation</strong>, where the amount rose to 1.2 million tonnes.","phrases":[{"en":"responsible for the majority of emissions","context":"Overall, road transport was responsible for the majority of emissions in all three countries, and it remained the biggest source throughout."},{"en":"A similar pattern was seen","context":"A similar pattern was seen in France, where road transport added 3.6 million tonnes to the total emissions within ten years."},{"en":"remained the biggest source throughout","context":"Overall, road transport was responsible for the majority of emissions in all three countries, and it remained the biggest source throughout."},{"en":"saw its total decrease slightly","context":"Germany, which had higher emissions than either the United Kingdom or France, saw its total decrease slightly from 47.2 to 46.7 million tonnes."},{"en":"showed no remarkable growth","context":"In the United Kingdom, road transport produced 32.6 million tonnes of emissions in 2004, 2 million more than ten years earlier, while the other three transport sources showed no remarkable growth."},{"en":"with the exception of civil aviation","context":"Germany, by contrast, was the only country of the three to experience a drop in road transport emissions, and its other transport sources also had a lower emission volume, with the exception of civil aviation, where the amount rose to 1.2 million tonnes."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-039-1"]},"_displayNo":"#013","summary":"Table below shows carbon dioxide emissions from transport in three"},{"id":"writing-academic-059","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Environment & Climate","title":"Global greenhouse gas emissions","prompt":"The charts compare the sources of global greenhouse gas emissions in 2002 and emissions from electricity generation.","answer":"The two pie charts compare the shares of global greenhouse gas emissions produced by three groups of countries in 2002 and 2030, while the bar chart shows projected carbon dioxide emissions from different sectors in the same two years.\n\nOverall, developing countries <strong class=\"key-phrase\">will overtake OECD countries</strong> to become the major producer of carbon dioxide emissions by 2030. While industry is responsible for the highest amount of these emissions, the consumer and transportation sectors will experience the greatest increases.\n\nAs can be seen in the two pie charts, developing countries are expected to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">account for a bigger share of emissions</strong> in 2030, 48%, compared to 38% in 2002. In contrast, OECD countries are projected to make up a lower proportion, 43%, 9% down over the same period. Countries with transition economies are estimated to take up the remaining 9% in 2030, 1% less than in 2002.\n\nThe bar chart reveals that carbon dioxide emissions <strong class=\"key-phrase\">will expand to varying degrees</strong> in different sectors by 2030. Industry will continue to be the major producer of carbon dioxide, rising to more than 10 billion tonnes in 2030. Next comes the consumer sector, which is projected to more than double its emissions from less than 4 billion to 8 billion or more tonnes. Transportation, the third largest producer, will see huge growth, to 6 billion tonnes, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">triple the amount in 2002</strong>. By comparison, waste combustion and other sectors <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make up a much lower proportion</strong>, each producing an estimated 1 billion tonnes of carbon dioxide.","phrases":[{"en":"will overtake OECD countries","context":"Overall, developing countries will overtake OECD countries to become the major producer of carbon dioxide emissions by 2030."},{"en":"account for a bigger share of emissions","context":"As can be seen in the two pie charts, developing countries are expected to account for a bigger share of emissions in 2030, 48%, compared to 38% in 2002."},{"en":"will expand to varying degrees","context":"The bar chart reveals that carbon dioxide emissions will expand to varying degrees in different sectors by 2030."},{"en":"triple the amount in 2002","context":"Transportation, the third largest producer, will see huge growth, to 6 billion tonnes, triple the amount in 2002."},{"en":"make up a much lower proportion","context":"By comparison, waste combustion and other sectors make up a much lower proportion, each producing an estimated 1 billion tonnes of carbon dioxide."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-059-1"]},"_displayNo":"#014","summary":"Pie graphs show greenhouse gas emissions worldwide in 2002 and the"},{"id":"writing-academic-026","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Food & Agriculture","title":"Fairtrade coffee and banana sales","prompt":"The tables below give information about sales of Fairtrade-labelled coffee and bananas in 1999 and 2004 in five European countries.","answer":"The tables show the amount of money spent on Fairtrade coffee and bananas in two separate years in the UK, Switzerland, Denmark, Belgium and Sweden.\n\nIt is clear that sales of Fairtrade coffee rose in all five European countries from 1999 to 2004, but sales of Fairtrade bananas only went up in three out of the five countries. Overall, the UK saw by far <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the highest levels of spending</strong> on the two products.\n\nIn 1999, Switzerland had the highest sales of Fairtrade coffee, at €3 million, while revenue from Fairtrade bananas was highest in the UK, at €15 million. By 2004, however, sales of Fairtrade coffee in the UK had risen to €20 million, and this was over three times higher than Switzerland's sales figure for Fairtrade coffee in that year. The year 2004 also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saw dramatic increases in</strong> the money spent on Fairtrade bananas in the UK and Switzerland, with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">revenues rising by €32 million and €4.5 million respectively</strong>.\n\nSales of the two Fairtrade products were far lower in Denmark, Belgium and Sweden. Small increases in sales of Fairtrade coffee can be seen, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">revenue remained at €2 million or below</strong> in all three countries in both years. Finally, it is noticeable that the money spent on Fairtrade bananas actually fell in Belgium and Sweden.","phrases":[{"en":"the highest levels of spending","context":"It is clear that sales of Fairtrade coffee rose in all five European countries from 1999 to 2004, but sales of Fairtrade bananas only went up in three out of the five countries. Overall, the UK saw by far the highest levels of spending on the two products."},{"en":"saw dramatic increases in","context":"The year 2004 also saw dramatic increases in the money spent on Fairtrade bananas in the UK and Switzerland, with revenues rising by €32 million and €4.5 million respectively."},{"en":"revenues rising by €32 million and €4.5 million respectively","context":"The year 2004 also saw dramatic increases in the money spent on Fairtrade bananas in the UK and Switzerland, with revenues rising by €32 million and €4.5 million respectively."},{"en":"revenue remained at €2 million or below","context":"Small increases in sales of Fairtrade coffee can be seen, but revenue remained at €2 million or below in all three countries in both years."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-026-1"]},"_displayNo":"#015","summary":"Tables below give information about sales of Fairtrade*-labelled coffee and…"},{"id":"writing-academic-047","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Food & Agriculture","title":"World food and oil prices","prompt":"The graph below shows changes in global food and oil prices between 2000 and 2011.","answer":"The line graph compares the average price of a barrel of oil with the food price index over a period of 11 years.\n\nIt is clear that average global prices of both oil and food <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rose considerably</strong> between 2000 and 2011. Also, the trends for both commodities were very similar, and so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a strong correlation (93.6%) is suggested</strong>.\n\nIn the year 2000, the average global oil price was close to $25 per barrel, and the food price index stood at just under 90 points. Over the following four years both prices remained relatively stable, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">before rising steadily between 2004 and 2007</strong>. By 2007, the average oil price <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had more than doubled</strong>, to nearly $60 per barrel, and food prices had risen by around 50 points.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">A dramatic increase</strong> in both commodity prices was seen from 2007 to 2008, with oil prices reaching a peak of approximately $130 per barrel and the food price index rising to 220 points. However, by the beginning of 2009 the price of oil had dropped by roughly $90, and the food price index was down by about 80 points. Finally, in 2011, the average oil price rose once again, to nearly $100 per barrel, while the food price index <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reached its peak</strong>, at almost 240 points.","phrases":[{"en":"rose considerably","context":"It is clear that average global prices of both oil and food rose considerably between 2000 and 2011. Also, the trends for both commodities were very similar, and so a strong correlation (93.6%) is suggested."},{"en":"A dramatic increase","context":"A dramatic increase in both commodity prices was seen from 2007 to 2008, with oil prices reaching a peak of approximately $130 per barrel and the food price index rising to 220 points."},{"en":"reached its peak","context":"Finally, in 2011, the average oil price rose once again, to nearly $100 per barrel, while the food price index reached its peak, at almost 240 points."},{"en":"a strong correlation (93.6%) is suggested","context":"It is clear that average global prices of both oil and food rose considerably between 2000 and 2011. Also, the trends for both commodities were very similar, and so a strong correlation (93.6%) is suggested."},{"en":"had more than doubled","context":"By 2007, the average oil price had more than doubled, to nearly $60 per barrel, and food prices had risen by around 50 points."},{"en":"before rising steadily between 2004 and 2007","context":"Over the following four years both prices remained relatively stable, before rising steadily between 2004 and 2007."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-047-1"]},"_displayNo":"#016","summary":"Graph below shows changes in global food and oil prices between 2000 and 2011."},{"id":"writing-academic-048","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Food & Agriculture","title":"US meat consumption","prompt":"The graph below shows trends in US meat and poultry consumption.","answer":"The line graph shows changes in the per capita consumption of beef, pork, broilers and turkey in the United States between 1955 and 2012.\n\nIt is noticeable that beef was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">by far the most popular</strong> of the four types of meat for the majority of the 57-year period. However, a considerable rise can be seen in the consumption of broilers, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with figures eventually surpassing those for</strong> beef.\n\nBetween 1955 and 1976, US beef consumption rose from around 60 to a peak of 90 pounds per person per year. During the same period, consumption of broilers also rose, to nearly 30 pounds per person, while the figures for pork fluctuated between 50 and 40 pounds per person. Turkey was by far the least popular meat, with figures below 10 pounds per capita each year.\n\nBy 2012, the amount of beef consumed by the average American <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had plummeted to around 50 pounds</strong>, but the consumption of broilers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had doubled since the 1970s</strong>, to approximately 55 pounds per capita. By contrast, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">there were no significant changes in the trends for</strong> pork and turkey consumption over the period as a whole.","phrases":[{"en":"with figures eventually surpassing those for","context":"It is noticeable that beef was by far the most popular of the four types of meat for the majority of the 57-year period. However, a considerable rise can be seen in the consumption of broilers, with figures eventually surpassing those for beef."},{"en":"there were no significant changes in the trends for","context":"By contrast, there were no significant changes in the trends for pork and turkey consumption over the period as a whole."},{"en":"by far the most popular","context":"It is noticeable that beef was by far the most popular of the four types of meat for the majority of the 57-year period. However, a considerable rise can be seen in the consumption of broilers, with figures eventually surpassing those for beef."},{"en":"had plummeted to around 50 pounds","context":"By 2012, the amount of beef consumed by the average American had plummeted to around 50 pounds, but the consumption of broilers had doubled since the 1970s, to approximately 55 pounds per capita."},{"en":"had doubled since the 1970s","context":"By 2012, the amount of beef consumed by the average American had plummeted to around 50 pounds, but the consumption of broilers had doubled since the 1970s, to approximately 55 pounds per capita."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-048-1"]},"_displayNo":"#017","summary":"Graph below shows trends in US meat and poultry consumption."},{"id":"writing-academic-057","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Food & Agriculture","title":"Fruit production and fertiliser use","prompt":"The first graph below shows the weight (in tonnes) of fruit produced by a farm during each month in 2013, and also the amount (in tonnes) of fertiliser used. The second graph shows the volume of rainfall in each month (in millimetres). Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The charts give information about the tonnage of fruit produced by a farm in each month of 2013, the amount of fertiliser used, and the volume of rainfall in that year.\n\nOverall, the farm generally used the most fertiliser at times of high rainfall, and by contrast it produced the most fruit when both rainfall and fertiliser use were around their lowest.\n\nFruit production rose steadily from just over 2 tonnes in January to a peak of roughly 4.2 tonnes by August. Output then fell rapidly, declining to around the initial 2 tonne mark in December.\n\nFertiliser use <strong class=\"key-phrase\">followed a largely reversed seasonal pattern</strong>. The greatest amount, just under 1 tonne, was used in April, and this was followed by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a marked decline to zero</strong> in June and July. Fertiliser consumption then increased slightly in the autumn, rising to a total of around 0.7 tonnes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">before dipping to just below 0.5 tonnes</strong> at the end of the year.\n\nRainfall <strong class=\"key-phrase\">appeared to reflect the use of fertiliser</strong>, with an early high of 16mm in February, falling consistently to 4mm in July, August and September. Rainfall then increased steadily to reach 14mm in December.","phrases":[{"en":"followed a largely reversed seasonal pattern","context":"Fertiliser use followed a largely reversed seasonal pattern."},{"en":"a marked decline to zero","context":"The greatest amount, just under 1 tonne, was used in April, and this was followed by a marked decline to zero in June and July."},{"en":"before dipping to just below 0.5 tonnes","context":"Fertiliser consumption then increased slightly in the autumn, rising to a total of around 0.7 tonnes before dipping to just below 0.5 tonnes at the end of the year."},{"en":"appeared to reflect the use of fertiliser","context":"Rainfall appeared to reflect the use of fertiliser, with an early high of 16mm in February, falling consistently to 4mm in July, August and September. Rainfall then increased steadily to reach 14mm in December."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-057-1","writing-academic-057-2"]},"_displayNo":"#018","summary":"First graph below shows the weight (in tonnes) of fruit produced by a farm…"},{"id":"writing-academic-060","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Health","title":"Teen smoking in Britain","prompt":"The table below describes the proportion of smokers in males and females (between the age of 15 and 20) in Britain. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The table shows the proportions of males and females aged between 15 and 20 who were smokers in Britain over a 30-year period.\n\nIt is clear from the table that the 15-to-20-year-old population in Britain had a much higher percentage of smokers by the end of the last century than in the early 1970s.\n\nIn 1970, smoking was not very popular among youngsters aged 15 to 20, with only 10 per cent of males and 5 per cent of females smoking. The following decade saw a dramatic change: the smoking rate among males <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leapt by nearly 13 percentage points</strong> every five years to reach 35% in 1980, compared with slower growth among females, whose rate rose by approximately 8 points every five years to 22% in 1980.\n\nOver the period between 1980 and 1990, smoking remained popular among young males, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accounting for over a third of</strong> the 15-to-20 male population, while young females <strong class=\"key-phrase\">showed a growing interest in smoking</strong>, pushing their smoking rate first to 34% in 1985 and then to 37% in 1990, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">even overtaking their male counterparts</strong>.\n\nOver the final years of the last century, smoking became less common in the 15-20 age group, both among males and among females. In 2000, an estimated 27% to 28% of people in this age group were reported to be smokers, lower than in previous years, though still more than three times higher than 30 years earlier.","phrases":[{"en":"leapt by nearly 13 percentage points","context":"The following decade saw a dramatic change: the smoking rate among males leapt by nearly 13 percentage points every five years to reach 35% in 1980, compared with slower growth among females, whose rate rose by approximately 8 points every five years to 22% in 1980."},{"en":"even overtaking their male counterparts","context":"Over the period between 1980 and 1990, smoking remained popular among young males, accounting for over a third of the 15-to-20 male population, while young females showed a growing interest in smoking, pushing their smoking rate first to 34% in 1985 and then to 37% in 1990, even overtaking their..."},{"en":"accounting for over a third of","context":"Over the period between 1980 and 1990, smoking remained popular among young males, accounting for over a third of the 15-to-20 male population, while young females showed a growing interest in smoking, pushing their smoking rate first to 34% in 1985 and then to 37% in 1990, even overtaking their..."},{"en":"showed a growing interest in smoking","context":"Over the period between 1980 and 1990, smoking remained popular among young males, accounting for over a third of the 15-to-20 male population, while young females showed a growing interest in smoking, pushing their smoking rate first to 34% in 1985 and then to 37% in 1990, even overtaking their..."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-060-1"]},"_displayNo":"#019","summary":"Table below describes the proportion of smokers in males and females…"},{"id":"writing-academic-062","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Health","title":"Emergency department attendance","prompt":"The chart below shows figures for attendances at hospital emergency care departments in Northern Ireland by age group in December 2016 and December 2017.","answer":"The bar chart compares the number of visits made by people in six age categories to hospital emergency wards in Northern Ireland in the December of 2016 and 2017.\n\nIt is clear that elderly people and the youngest children were the most frequent visitors to A&E (accident and emergency) departments. Also, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">there was a slight increase in</strong> the rate of emergency care attendances among all six age groups from December 2016 to December 2017.\n\nLooking first at the older age groups, roughly 65 out of every 1000 people aged 75 and over attended A&E wards in December 2016, and this rose to almost 70 attendances per 1000-population one year later. Interestingly, those aged 65 to 74 were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less than half as likely to require emergency treatment</strong>, with around 30 attendances per 1000-population in both months.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">At the other end of the age scale</strong>, the figures for A&E visits by under-5s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rose marginally</strong> from around 50 to approximately 52 per 1000-population over the one-year period. Children aged 5 to 15 <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made the fewest visits</strong>, at just over 20 per 1000-population in both months. Finally, there was almost no difference in the figures for emergency care attendances among people aged 16 to 44, 45 to 64 and 65 to 74.","phrases":[{"en":"there was a slight increase in","context":"Also, there was a slight increase in the rate of emergency care attendances among all six age groups from December 2016 to December 2017."},{"en":"rose marginally","context":"At the other end of the age scale, the figures for A&E visits by under-5s rose marginally from around 50 to approximately 52 per 1000-population over the one-year period."},{"en":"made the fewest visits","context":"Children aged 5 to 15 made the fewest visits, at just over 20 per 1000-population in both months."},{"en":"less than half as likely to require emergency treatment","context":"Interestingly, those aged 65 to 74 were less than half as likely to require emergency treatment, with around 30 attendances per 1000-population in both months."},{"en":"At the other end of the age scale","context":"At the other end of the age scale, the figures for A&E visits by under-5s rose marginally from around 50 to approximately 52 per 1000-population over the one-year period."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-062-1"]},"_displayNo":"#020","summary":"Chart below shows figures for attendances at hospital emergency care…"},{"id":"writing-academic-064","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Health","title":"UK obesity trends","prompt":"The graphs below summarise the results of a survey carried out by the Department of Health about the overweight population in Britain with projections. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The graphs give information about the numbers of overweight adults and children in Britain, together with projections up to 2010, and about the link between obesity in children and in their parents.\n\nOverall, the UK's obese population will grow in size, including both children and adults. While men are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expected to outnumber women</strong> by 2010, girls will surpass boys, and children with at least one overweight parent are more likely to be obese than those whose parents have a healthy weight.\n\nThe first two graphs show <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a marked rise in obesity</strong> in Britain by 2010. The number of men who have a weight problem is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">estimated to be as high as</strong> 6.8 million in 2010, over 50% higher than in 2003. The increase among women with the same problem tends to be smaller, rising almost 30% from 4.7 million to 6 million.\n\nThe picture is different for children. The survey suggests that overweight girls will see a greater increase during the period 2003-2010, to more than 900 thousand, 100 thousand more than boys with the same problem, although in 2003 there were more boys than girls suffering from obesity, over 700 thousand versus about 690 thousand.\n\nAs shown in the third graph, children in households where both parents are obese are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">twice as likely to be obese as</strong> those in households where one parent is obese (12.5%), and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">five times as likely as</strong> those whose parents have a healthy weight (5%).","phrases":[{"en":"a marked rise in obesity","context":"The first two graphs show a marked rise in obesity in Britain by 2010."},{"en":"estimated to be as high as","context":"The number of men who have a weight problem is estimated to be as high as 6.8 million in 2010, over 50% higher than in 2003."},{"en":"expected to outnumber women","context":"While men are expected to outnumber women by 2010, girls will surpass boys, and children with at least one overweight parent are more likely to be obese than those whose parents have a healthy weight."},{"en":"twice as likely to be obese as","context":"As shown in the third graph, children in households where both parents are obese are twice as likely to be obese as those in households where one parent is obese (12.5%), and five times as likely as those whose parents have a healthy weight (5%)."},{"en":"five times as likely as","context":"As shown in the third graph, children in households where both parents are obese are twice as likely to be obese as those in households where one parent is obese (12.5%), and five times as likely as those whose parents have a healthy weight (5%)."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-064-1","writing-academic-064-2"]},"_displayNo":"#021","summary":"Graphs below summarise the results of a survey carried out by the…"},{"id":"writing-academic-065","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Health","title":"Doctor consultation times","prompt":"The table shows data about the average length of time (in minutes) that people of different ages spend in a consultation with family doctors in a number of countries. Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The table gives information about the typical time that patients spend with their family doctors in a number of countries, broken down by age group.\n\nIt is clear that the most common trend is for consultation time to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">increase consistently with age</strong>, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">there are two exceptions to this pattern</strong>: Japan and Kenya.\n\nThe figure for France <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more than doubles</strong>, from 16 to 38 minutes, between the youngest and the oldest people. Likewise, the USA figure rises from 13 to 31, the UK from 9 to 21, and Italy from 17 to 29. These are all substantial increases, while Chile has a consistent but much smaller rise, from 32 to 39 minutes.\n\nBrazil also shows a rise, although after age 60 there is a decline from 26 minutes to an eventual 20. India has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a more marked rise and fall</strong>, peaking at 21 minutes for people aged 40 to 60 and declining to 11 minutes for those aged 70 and over. In Japan the figures <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fluctuate across the age groups</strong>, between 12 and 16 minutes, while Kenya <strong class=\"key-phrase\">declines steadily</strong> as age increases, from 15 minutes for the youngest to 10 minutes for the oldest people.","phrases":[{"en":"more than doubles","context":"The figure for France more than doubles, from 16 to 38 minutes, between the youngest and the oldest people."},{"en":"a more marked rise and fall","context":"India has a more marked rise and fall, peaking at 21 minutes for people aged 40 to 60 and declining to 11 minutes for those aged 70 and over."},{"en":"declines steadily","context":"In Japan the figures fluctuate across the age groups, between 12 and 16 minutes, while Kenya declines steadily as age increases, from 15 minutes for the youngest to 10 minutes for the oldest people."},{"en":"increase consistently with age","context":"It is clear that the most common trend is for consultation time to increase consistently with age, but there are two exceptions to this pattern: Japan and Kenya."},{"en":"there are two exceptions to this pattern","context":"It is clear that the most common trend is for consultation time to increase consistently with age, but there are two exceptions to this pattern: Japan and Kenya."},{"en":"fluctuate across the age groups","context":"In Japan the figures fluctuate across the age groups, between 12 and 16 minutes, while Kenya declines steadily as age increases, from 15 minutes for the youngest to 10 minutes for the oldest people."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-065-1"]},"_displayNo":"#022","summary":"Table shows data about the average length of time (in minutes) that people…"},{"id":"writing-academic-001","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Maps & Plans","title":"Chorleywood village development","prompt":"Chorleywood is a village near London whose population has increased steadily since the middle of the nineteenth century. The map below shows the development of the village.","answer":"The map shows the growth of a village called Chorleywood between 1868 and 1994.\n\nIt is clear that the village grew as the transport infrastructure was improved. Four periods of development are shown on the map, and each of the populated areas is near to the main roads, the railway or the motorway.\n\nFrom 1868 to 1883, Chorleywood <strong class=\"key-phrase\">covered a small area</strong> next to one of the main roads. Chorleywood Park and Golf Course is now located next to this original village area. The village <strong class=\"key-phrase\">grew along the main road to the south</strong> between 1883 and 1922, and in 1909 a railway line was built crossing this area from west to east. Chorleywood station is in this part of the village.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">The expansion of Chorleywood continued to the east and west</strong> alongside the railway line until 1970. At that time, a motorway was built to the east of the village, and from 1970 to 1994, further development of the village took place <strong class=\"key-phrase\">around motorway intersections</strong> with the railway and one of the main roads.","phrases":[{"en":"covered a small area","context":"From 1868 to 1883, Chorleywood covered a small area next to one of the main roads."},{"en":"grew along the main road to the south","context":"The village grew along the main road to the south between 1883 and 1922, and in 1909 a railway line was built crossing this area from west to east."},{"en":"The expansion of Chorleywood continued to the east and west","context":"The expansion of Chorleywood continued to the east and west alongside the railway line until 1970."},{"en":"around motorway intersections","context":"At that time, a motorway was built to the east of the village, and from 1970 to 1994, further development of the village took place around motorway intersections with the railway and one of the main roads."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-001-1"]},"_displayNo":"#023","summary":"Chorleywood is a village near London whose population has increased…"},{"id":"writing-academic-029","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Maps & Plans","title":"School layout redevelopment","prompt":"The diagrams below show the site of a school in 2004 and the plan for changes to the school site in 2024.","answer":"The two pictures compare the layout of a school as it was in the year 2004 with a proposed site design for the year 2024.\n\nIt is clear that the main change for 2024 involves the addition of a new school building. The school will then be able to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accommodate a considerably larger number of students</strong>.\n\nIn 2004, there were 600 pupils attending the school, and the two school buildings were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">separated by a path</strong> running from the main entrance to the sports field. By 2024, it is expected that there will be 1000 pupils, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a third building will have been constructed</strong>. The plan is also to join the two original buildings together, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">creating a shorter path that links the buildings only</strong>.\n\nAs the third building and a second car park will be built on the site of the original sports field, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a new, smaller sports field will need to be laid</strong>. A new road will also be built from the main entrance to the second car park. Finally, no changes will be made to the main entrance and original car park.","phrases":[{"en":"accommodate a considerably larger number of students","context":"It is clear that the main change for 2024 involves the addition of a new school building. The school will then be able to accommodate a considerably larger number of students."},{"en":"separated by a path","context":"In 2004, there were 600 pupils attending the school, and the two school buildings were separated by a path running from the main entrance to the sports field."},{"en":"a third building will have been constructed","context":"By 2024, it is expected that there will be 1000 pupils, and a third building will have been constructed."},{"en":"creating a shorter path that links the buildings only","context":"The plan is also to join the two original buildings together, creating a shorter path that links the buildings only."},{"en":"a new, smaller sports field will need to be laid","context":"As the third building and a second car park will be built on the site of the original sports field, a new, smaller sports field will need to be laid."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-029-1"]},"_displayNo":"#024","summary":"The site of a school in 2004 and the proposed changes for 2024."},{"id":"writing-academic-050","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Maps & Plans","title":"Garlsdon supermarket sites","prompt":"The map below is of the town of Garlsdon and shows two possible sites for a new supermarket. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The map shows the town of Garlsdon and two possible sites, S1 and S2, for the construction of a new supermarket.\n\nOverall, neither site is appropriate for all the towns, but for customers in Cransdon, Hindon and Garlsdon, the out-of-town site (S1) would probably offer more advantages.\n\nThe first potential location (S1) is outside the town itself, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is sited just off the main road</strong> to the town of Hindon, lying 12 kilometres to the north-west. This site is in the countryside and so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">would be able to accommodate a lot of car parking</strong>. This would make it accessible to shoppers from both Hindon and Garlsdon who could travel by car. Since it is also close to the railway line linking the two towns to Cransdon (25 km to the south-east), <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a potentially large number of shoppers</strong> would also be able to travel by train.\n\nIn contrast, the second potential location (S2) is right in the town centre, which would be good for local residents. Theoretically the store could be accessed by road or rail from the surrounding towns, including Bransdon, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as the central area is a no-traffic zone</strong>, cars would be unable to park and access would be difficult.","phrases":[{"en":"a potentially large number of shoppers","context":"Since it is also close to the railway line linking the two towns to Cransdon (25 km to the south-east), a potentially large number of shoppers would also be able to travel by train."},{"en":"is sited just off the main road","context":"The first potential location (S1) is outside the town itself, and is sited just off the main road to the town of Hindon, lying 12 kilometres to the north-west."},{"en":"would be able to accommodate a lot of car parking","context":"This site is in the countryside and so would be able to accommodate a lot of car parking."},{"en":"as the central area is a no-traffic zone","context":"Theoretically the store could be accessed by road or rail from the surrounding towns, including Bransdon, but as the central area is a no-traffic zone, cars would be unable to park and access would be difficult."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-050-1"]},"_displayNo":"#025","summary":"Map below is of the town of Garlsdon and shows two possible sites for a new…"},{"id":"writing-academic-019","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Money & Spending","title":"Youth spending and life milestones","prompt":"Charts 1 and 2 below show the proportion of income spent on different areas by young people in Canada and Poland. Chart 3 shows the average age of certain life events for these countries, and an estimated global average age. Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The charts show the percentage of youth income spent on various areas in Poland and Canada, and also the average age at which young people in these countries reach three key life events, compared with an estimated global norm.\n\nOverall, young people's expenditure in Canada is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more evenly split</strong> than in Poland, where studies and transport <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take the vast majority of income</strong>. In terms of life events, Poland is closer to the global average than Canada.\n\nLooking first at income, in Canada the two largest areas of expense are leisure and clothing, at 20% and 19% respectively. The other areas (housing, food, transport and studies) are all roughly similar, at between 14% and 16%. In Poland, however, studies <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take almost half the expenditure</strong>, at 43%, followed by transport at 26%. Leisure and clothing are similar at 10% and 11%, and housing and food are the same at 5%.\n\nRegarding life events, in Poland leaving home and buying a car happen at an age <strong class=\"key-phrase\">close to the predicted global norm</strong> of around 26 years, whereas in Canada they happen much earlier, at 20 years or just under. Getting married, by contrast, happens at a similar age in both countries, at around 30 years, which is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">much later than the world average</strong> of just over 20.","phrases":[{"en":"take almost half the expenditure","context":"In Poland, however, studies take almost half the expenditure, at 43%, followed by transport at 26%."},{"en":"more evenly split","context":"Overall, young people's expenditure in Canada is more evenly split than in Poland, where studies and transport take the vast majority of income. In terms of life events, Poland is closer to the global average than Canada."},{"en":"take the vast majority of income","context":"Overall, young people's expenditure in Canada is more evenly split than in Poland, where studies and transport take the vast majority of income. In terms of life events, Poland is closer to the global average than Canada."},{"en":"close to the predicted global norm","context":"Regarding life events, in Poland leaving home and buying a car happen at an age close to the predicted global norm of around 26 years, whereas in Canada they happen much earlier, at 20 years or just under."},{"en":"much later than the world average","context":"Getting married, by contrast, happens at a similar age in both countries, at around 30 years, which is much later than the world average of just over 20."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-019-1","writing-academic-019-2"]},"_displayNo":"#026","summary":"Charts 1 and 2 below show the proportion of income spent on different areas…"},{"id":"writing-academic-021","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Money & Spending","title":"World spending and population","prompt":"The charts below give information about world spending and population.\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The first pie chart compares the shares of four major items in global expenditure, while the second shows how global population is distributed between the continents.\n\nOverall, these four items are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">responsible for three-fifths of the overall spending</strong> around the world, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the most prominent share being that of food</strong>. The largest share of the world's population lives in Asia, the population of which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">outnumbers that of the rest of the world</strong> by 3 to 2.\n\nFood takes almost a quarter of global expenditure, followed by transport, at almost 20%. Housing also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accounts for a considerable proportion</strong>, while the smallest of the four shares goes on clothing, at only 6%. The remaining 40% is spent on a variety of other items.\n\nAccording to the second chart, 3 out of every 5 human beings live in Asia. Europe and the Americas share similar proportions and together are host to roughly one-third of the world's population, while the inhabitants of Africa <strong class=\"key-phrase\">form a mere one-tenth</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"accounts for a considerable proportion","context":"Housing also accounts for a considerable proportion, while the smallest of the four shares goes on clothing, at only 6%."},{"en":"outnumbers that of the rest of the world","context":"The largest share of the world's population lives in Asia, the population of which outnumbers that of the rest of the world by 3 to 2."},{"en":"responsible for three-fifths of the overall spending","context":"Overall, these four items are responsible for three-fifths of the overall spending around the world, the most prominent share being that of food."},{"en":"the most prominent share being that of food","context":"Overall, these four items are responsible for three-fifths of the overall spending around the world, the most prominent share being that of food."},{"en":"form a mere one-tenth","context":"According to the second chart, 3 out of every 5 human beings live in Asia. Europe and the Americas share similar proportions and together are host to roughly one-third of the world's population, while the inhabitants of Africa form a mere one-tenth."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-021-1"]},"_displayNo":"#027","summary":"Charts below give information about world spending and population."},{"id":"writing-academic-053","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Money & Spending","title":"Consumer spending by country","prompt":"The table compares consumer spending on food, clothing and leisure in five countries in 2002.","answer":"The table shows percentages of consumer expenditure for three categories of products and services in five countries in 2002.\n\nIt is clear that the largest proportion of consumer spending in each country <strong class=\"key-phrase\">went on food, drinks and tobacco</strong>. On the other hand, the leisure/education category <strong class=\"key-phrase\">has the lowest percentages in the table</strong>.\n\nOut of the five countries, consumer spending on food, drinks and tobacco was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">noticeably higher</strong> in Turkey, at 32.14%, and Ireland, at nearly 29%. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The proportion of spending on</strong> leisure and education was also highest in Turkey, at 4.35%, while expenditure on clothing and footwear was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">significantly higher in Italy</strong>, at 9%, than in any of the other countries.\n\nIt can be seen that Sweden had the lowest percentages of national consumer expenditure for food/drinks/tobacco and for clothing/footwear, at nearly 16% and just over 5% respectively. Spain had slightly higher figures for these categories, but the lowest figure for leisure/education, at only 1.98%.","phrases":[{"en":"noticeably higher","context":"Out of the five countries, consumer spending on food, drinks and tobacco was noticeably higher in Turkey, at 32.14%, and Ireland, at nearly 29%."},{"en":"The proportion of spending on","context":"The proportion of spending on leisure and education was also highest in Turkey, at 4.35%, while expenditure on clothing and footwear was significantly higher in Italy, at 9%, than in any of the other countries."},{"en":"went on food, drinks and tobacco","context":"It is clear that the largest proportion of consumer spending in each country went on food, drinks and tobacco. On the other hand, the leisure/education category has the lowest percentages in the table."},{"en":"has the lowest percentages in the table","context":"It is clear that the largest proportion of consumer spending in each country went on food, drinks and tobacco. On the other hand, the leisure/education category has the lowest percentages in the table."},{"en":"significantly higher in Italy","context":"The proportion of spending on leisure and education was also highest in Turkey, at 4.35%, while expenditure on clothing and footwear was significantly higher in Italy, at 9%, than in any of the other countries."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-053-1"]},"_displayNo":"#028","summary":"Table below gives information on consumer spending on different items in…"},{"id":"writing-academic-055","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Money & Spending","title":"Australian family poverty","prompt":"The table shows the proportion of different family types living in poverty in Australia in 1999.","answer":"The table gives information about poverty rates among six types of household in Australia in the year 1999.\n\nIt is noticeable that levels of poverty were higher for single people than for couples, and people with children were more likely to be poor than those without. Poverty rates were considerably lower among elderly people.\n\nOverall, 11% of Australians, or 1,837,000 people, were living in poverty in 1999. Aged people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">were the least likely to be poor</strong>, with poverty levels of 6% and 4% for single aged people and aged couples respectively, the lowest rates of all.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Just over one fifth of single parents</strong> were living in poverty, the highest rate of any of the six household types, whereas only 12% of parents living with a partner were classed as poor. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The same pattern can be seen</strong> for people with no children: while 19% of single people in this group were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">living below the poverty line</strong>, the figure for couples was much lower, at only 7%.","phrases":[{"en":"The same pattern can be seen","context":"The same pattern can be seen for people with no children: while 19% of single people in this group were living below the poverty line, the figure for couples was much lower, at only 7%."},{"en":"were the least likely to be poor","context":"Overall, 11% of Australians, or 1,837,000 people, were living in poverty in 1999. Aged people were the least likely to be poor, with poverty levels of 6% and 4% for single aged people and aged couples respectively, the lowest rates of all."},{"en":"Just over one fifth of single parents","context":"Just over one fifth of single parents were living in poverty, the highest rate of any of the six household types, whereas only 12% of parents living with a partner were classed as poor."},{"en":"living below the poverty line","context":"The same pattern can be seen for people with no children: while 19% of single people in this group were living below the poverty line, the figure for couples was much lower, at only 7%."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-055-1"]},"_displayNo":"#029","summary":"Table below shows the proportion of different categories of families living…"},{"id":"writing-academic-058","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Money & Spending","title":"Dubai gold sales","prompt":"The graph below gives information about Dubai gold sales in 2002.\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The diagram given illustrates how gold sales in Dubai fluctuated over a period of 12 months. Overall, gold sales in Dubai <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remained relatively unchanged</strong> in 2002.\n\nIt is observed that in the first month of 2002, gold sales stood at 200 million dirhams and rose slightly to reach about 225 million in February. This was followed by another increase, although much steeper, in March when sales were almost 125 million dirhams higher than February. However, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">this upward trend was suddenly broken</strong> and sales <strong class=\"key-phrase\">plummeted dramatically</strong> over the next 4 months to reach a little over 100 million dirhams in July. August sales showed a significant rise back to January levels as figures nearly doubled, but this was not to last as they dropped again in September to the same level as they were in July. October brought an increase of about 100 million dirhams in sales, after which sales figures <strong class=\"key-phrase\">levelled off</strong> over the last two months of 2002.\n\nTo sum up, sales were at their highest in March while the weakest sales figures could be observed in July and September.","phrases":[{"en":"remained relatively unchanged","context":"The diagram given illustrates how gold sales in Dubai fluctuated over a period of 12 months. Overall, gold sales in Dubai remained relatively unchanged in 2002."},{"en":"plummeted dramatically","context":"However, this upward trend was suddenly broken and sales plummeted dramatically over the next 4 months to reach a little over 100 million dirhams in July."},{"en":"levelled off","context":"October brought an increase of about 100 million dirhams in sales, after which sales figures levelled off over the last two months of 2002."},{"en":"this upward trend was suddenly broken","context":"However, this upward trend was suddenly broken and sales plummeted dramatically over the next 4 months to reach a little over 100 million dirhams in July."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-058-1"]},"_displayNo":"#030","summary":"Graph below gives information about Dubai gold sales in 2002."},{"id":"writing-academic-066","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Money & Spending","title":"Tablet prices and discounts","prompt":"Chart 1 below shows the normal price of a tablet computer in different stores (in US dollars), and the % discount offered by these stores when the products are sold on promotion. Chart 2 shows the percentage increase in volume sales when the discount promotions are happening, and Chart 3 shows funds (in 000 US dollars) spent on TV advertising during these promotions. Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The charts show average prices, promotional discounts, sales uplifts and advertising budgets for seven stores selling tablet computers.\n\nFour of these stores have a normal price of around $300 per item (Slaker, Jones, Scots and Fraser). Two stores (Lewis and Nova) have a much higher price at $460 and $565 respectively, and the lowest price ($150) is seen in Smith. We see <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a similar grouping</strong> in the promotional discounts offered, with the four mid-priced stores offering 10% off, the two higher-priced stores giving 20% (Lewis) and 25% (Nova), and Smith offering only 5% discount.\n\nLewis and Nova also see <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the highest rate of uplift</strong> in sales volumes on promotion, at 21% and 23% respectively, while Slaker, Jones, Scots and Fraser are again similarly grouped with around a 12% increase each. Smith has the smallest sales uplift, at below 5%.\n\nTurning to TV advertising, Slaker, Jones, Scots and Fraser have roughly similar budgets of between $63,000 and $70,000. Lewis and Nova have much lower spends at $18,000 and $21,000, while Smith spends nothing at all.\n\nIn summary, the four mid-priced stores spend the most on advertising and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">achieve similar uplifts with their 10% discounts</strong>. Lewis and Nova spend about one-third of these levels, but achieve <strong class=\"key-phrase\">roughly double the uplift</strong> by offering approximately double the level of discount. Smith, with its lower normal price, is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">barely active on promotions</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"a similar grouping","context":"We see a similar grouping in the promotional discounts offered, with the four mid-priced stores offering 10% off, the two higher-priced stores giving 20% (Lewis) and 25% (Nova), and Smith offering only 5% discount."},{"en":"the highest rate of uplift","context":"Lewis and Nova also see the highest rate of uplift in sales volumes on promotion, at 21% and 23% respectively, while Slaker, Jones, Scots and Fraser are again similarly grouped with around a 12% increase each. Smith has the smallest sales uplift, at below 5%."},{"en":"roughly double the uplift","context":"Lewis and Nova spend about one-third of these levels, but achieve roughly double the uplift by offering approximately double the level of discount."},{"en":"barely active on promotions","context":"Smith, with its lower normal price, is barely active on promotions."},{"en":"achieve similar uplifts with their 10% discounts","context":"In summary, the four mid-priced stores spend the most on advertising and achieve similar uplifts with their 10% discounts."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-066-1","writing-academic-066-2"]},"_displayNo":"#031","summary":"Chart 1 below shows the normal price of a tablet computer in different…"},{"id":"writing-academic-003","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Population & Demographics","title":"Ageing populations","prompt":"The graph below shows the proportion of the population aged 65 and over between 1940 and 2040 in three different countries.","answer":"The line graph compares the percentage of people aged 65 or more in three countries over a period of 100 years.\n\nIt is clear that the proportion of elderly people increases in each country between 1940 and 2040. Japan is expected to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">see the most dramatic changes in</strong> its elderly population.\n\nIn 1940, around 9% of Americans were aged 65 or over, compared to about 7% of Swedish people and 5% of Japanese people. The proportions of elderly people in the USA and Sweden <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rose gradually over the next 50 years</strong>, reaching just under 15% in 1990. By contrast, the figures for Japan <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remained below 5% until the early 2000s</strong>.\n\nLooking into the future, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sudden increase in</strong> the percentage of elderly people is predicted for Japan, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with a jump of</strong> over 15% in just 10 years from 2030 to 2040. By 2040, it is thought that around 27% of the Japanese population will be 65 years old or more, while the figures for Sweden and the USA will be slightly lower, at about 25% and 23% respectively.","phrases":[{"en":"see the most dramatic changes in","context":"It is clear that the proportion of elderly people increases in each country between 1940 and 2040. Japan is expected to see the most dramatic changes in its elderly population."},{"en":"a sudden increase in","context":"Looking into the future, a sudden increase in the percentage of elderly people is predicted for Japan, with a jump of over 15% in just 10 years from 2030 to 2040."},{"en":"with a jump of","context":"Looking into the future, a sudden increase in the percentage of elderly people is predicted for Japan, with a jump of over 15% in just 10 years from 2030 to 2040."},{"en":"rose gradually over the next 50 years","context":"The proportions of elderly people in the USA and Sweden rose gradually over the next 50 years, reaching just under 15% in 1990."},{"en":"remained below 5% until the early 2000s","context":"By contrast, the figures for Japan remained below 5% until the early 2000s."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-003-1"]},"_displayNo":"#032","summary":"Graph below shows the proportion of the population aged 65 and over between…"},{"id":"writing-academic-005","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Population & Demographics","title":"Living alone in England","prompt":"The bar chart below shows the proportions of English men and women of different ages who were living alone in 2011. The pie chart compares the numbers of bedrooms in these one-person households.","answer":"The two charts give information about single-occupant households in England in the year 2011. The bar chart compares figures for occupants' age and gender, and the pie chart shows data about the number of bedrooms in these homes.\n\nOverall, females made up <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a higher proportion of</strong> people living alone than males, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">this difference is particularly noticeable in the older age categories</strong>. We can also see that the most common number of bedrooms in a single-occupant home was two.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">A significant majority of</strong> the people aged 65 or over who were living alone in England in 2011 were female. Women made up around 72% of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">single occupants</strong> aged 75 to 84, and 76% of those aged 85 or over. By contrast, among younger adults the figures for males were higher. For example, in the 35-49 age category, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">men accounted for nearly 65%</strong> of people living alone.\n\nIn the same year, 35.4% of one-person households in England had two bedrooms, while one-bedroom and three-bedroom homes accounted for 28% and 29.8% of the total. Under 7% of single-occupant homes had four or more bedrooms.","phrases":[{"en":"a higher proportion of","context":"Overall, females made up a higher proportion of people living alone than males, and this difference is particularly noticeable in the older age categories. We can also see that the most common number of bedrooms in a single-occupant home was two."},{"en":"single occupants","context":"Women made up around 72% of single occupants aged 75 to 84, and 76% of those aged 85 or over."},{"en":"this difference is particularly noticeable in the older age categories","context":"Overall, females made up a higher proportion of people living alone than males, and this difference is particularly noticeable in the older age categories. We can also see that the most common number of bedrooms in a single-occupant home was two."},{"en":"A significant majority of","context":"A significant majority of the people aged 65 or over who were living alone in England in 2011 were female."},{"en":"men accounted for nearly 65%","context":"For example, in the 35-49 age category, men accounted for nearly 65% of people living alone."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-005-1"]},"_displayNo":"#033","summary":"Bar chart below shows the proportions of English men and women of different…"},{"id":"writing-academic-012","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Population & Demographics","title":"French and Indian age structures","prompt":"The charts below compare the age structure of the population of France and India in 1984.","answer":"The two charts compare the populations of France and India in terms of age distribution by gender in the year 1984.\n\nIt is clear that the population of India was younger than that of France in 1984, with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a noticeably larger proportion of people aged under 20</strong>. France, on the other hand, had a significantly larger percentage of elderly inhabitants.\n\nIn India, close to 14% of people were aged 5 or under, and each five-year age bracket above this contained <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an increasingly smaller proportion of the population</strong>. France's population, by contrast, was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more evenly distributed</strong> across the age ranges, with similar figures (around 7% to 8% of all people) for each five-year cohort between the ages of 0 and 40. Somewhere between 10% and 15% of all French people were aged 70 or older, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the equivalent figure for India was only 2%</strong>.\n\nLooking more closely at gender, there was a noticeably higher proportion of French women than men in every cohort from age 50 upwards. For example, almost 3% of French 70- to 75-year-olds were women, while just under 2% were men. No significant gender differences can be seen on the Indian population chart.","phrases":[{"en":"more evenly distributed","context":"France's population, by contrast, was more evenly distributed across the age ranges, with similar figures (around 7% to 8% of all people) for each five-year cohort between the ages of 0 and 40."},{"en":"a noticeably larger proportion of people aged under 20","context":"It is clear that the population of India was younger than that of France in 1984, with a noticeably larger proportion of people aged under 20. France, on the other hand, had a significantly larger percentage of elderly inhabitants."},{"en":"an increasingly smaller proportion of the population","context":"In India, close to 14% of people were aged 5 or under, and each five-year age bracket above this contained an increasingly smaller proportion of the population."},{"en":"the equivalent figure for India was only 2%","context":"Somewhere between 10% and 15% of all French people were aged 70 or older, but the equivalent figure for India was only 2%."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-012-1"]},"_displayNo":"#034","summary":"Charts below compare the age structure of the population of France and…"},{"id":"writing-academic-013","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Population & Demographics","title":"Yemen and Italy populations","prompt":"The charts show the age structure of the populations of Yemen and Italy in 2000 and projections for 2050.","answer":"The pie charts compare the proportions of people falling into three distinct age groups in Yemen and Italy in two different years.\n\nIt is clear that Italy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had the older population</strong> in the year 2000, and that the same is predicted for the year 2050. The populations of both countries <strong class=\"key-phrase\">are expected to age over the fifty-year period</strong>.\n\nIn the year 2000, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">just over half of</strong> the population of Yemen was aged 14 or under, while most Italians (61.6%) fell into the 15 to 59 age group, and only 14.3% were children under 15 years of age. People aged 60 or over accounted for almost a quarter of the Italian population, but only 3.6% of the inhabitants of Yemen.\n\nBy 2050, the proportion of children under 15 is predicted to drop in both countries, most noticeably in Yemen where the figure is expected to fall by 13.1%. On the other hand, the figures for elderly people are expected to rise, by 2.1% in Yemen and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a massive 18.2% in Italy</strong>. Finally, it is anticipated that the 15 to 59 age group will grow by around 10% in Yemen, but shrink by around 15% in Italy.","phrases":[{"en":"just over half of","context":"In the year 2000, just over half of the population of Yemen was aged 14 or under, while most Italians (61.6%) fell into the 15 to 59 age group, and only 14.3% were children under 15 years of age."},{"en":"had the older population","context":"It is clear that Italy had the older population in the year 2000, and that the same is predicted for the year 2050. The populations of both countries are expected to age over the fifty-year period."},{"en":"are expected to age over the fifty-year period","context":"It is clear that Italy had the older population in the year 2000, and that the same is predicted for the year 2050. The populations of both countries are expected to age over the fifty-year period."},{"en":"a massive 18.2% in Italy","context":"On the other hand, the figures for elderly people are expected to rise, by 2.1% in Yemen and a massive 18.2% in Italy."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-013-1"]},"_displayNo":"#035","summary":"Charts below give information on the ages of the populations of Yemen and…"},{"id":"writing-academic-068","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Population & Demographics","title":"US population demographics","prompt":"Graphs below provide the latest figures about the demographics of the United States, including ethnic groups and the population, based on a census that was carried out and administered by US Census Bureau in 2006. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The table and bar chart describe changes in the size and ethnic composition of the United States population. Overall, the population rose substantially, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the most striking proportional increases</strong> were seen among the Hispanic and Asian communities.\n\nThe total population increased from 200 million in 1966 to 300 million in 2006. White Americans <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remained the largest group</strong>, representing roughly two thirds of the population in 2006, although this group grew more slowly than the population as a whole. The Hispanic population climbed from about 8 million to 44.7 million, while the Asian population <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rose tenfold to 14.3 million</strong>. African Americans, who had been the second-largest group in 1966, became the third-largest by 2006.\n\nThe bar chart also shows that population <strong class=\"key-phrase\">growth accelerated over time</strong>. In the early decades of the twentieth century, the increase varied from around 7.5 million to 20 million per decade. From the middle of the century onwards, the country added at least 20 million people in most ten-year periods, and the rise exceeded 30 million during the 1990s.","phrases":[{"en":"remained the largest group","context":"White Americans remained the largest group, representing roughly two thirds of the population in 2006, although this group grew more slowly than the population as a whole."},{"en":"growth accelerated over time","context":"The bar chart also shows that population growth accelerated over time."},{"en":"the most striking proportional increases","context":"The table and bar chart describe changes in the size and ethnic composition of the United States population. Overall, the population rose substantially, and the most striking proportional increases were seen among the Hispanic and Asian communities."},{"en":"rose tenfold to 14.3 million","context":"The Hispanic population climbed from about 8 million to 44.7 million, while the Asian population rose tenfold to 14.3 million."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-068-1"]},"_displayNo":"#036","summary":"Graphs below provide the latest figures about the demographics of the…"},{"id":"writing-academic-032","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Processes & Cycles","title":"Waste paper recycling","prompt":"The chart below shows the process of waste paper recycling. The flow chart shows how waste paper is recycled.","answer":"The flow chart illustrates the way in which waste paper is recycled. It is clear that there are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">six distinct stages in this process</strong>, from the initial collection of waste paper to the eventual production of usable paper.\n\nAt the first stage in the paper recycling process, waste paper is collected either from paper banks, where members of the public leave their used paper, or directly from businesses. This paper is then sorted by hand and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">separated according to</strong> its grade, with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">any paper that is not suitable for recycling being removed</strong>. Next, the graded paper <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is transported to</strong> a paper mill.\n\nStages four and five of the process both involve cleaning. The paper is cleaned and pulped, and foreign objects such as staples are taken out. Following this, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">all remnants of ink and glue are removed</strong> from the paper at the de-inking stage. Finally, the pulp can be processed in a paper making machine, which makes the end product: usable paper.","phrases":[{"en":"separated according to","context":"This paper is then sorted by hand and separated according to its grade, with any paper that is not suitable for recycling being removed."},{"en":"is transported to","context":"Next, the graded paper is transported to a paper mill."},{"en":"six distinct stages in this process","context":"The flow chart illustrates the way in which waste paper is recycled. It is clear that there are six distinct stages in this process, from the initial collection of waste paper to the eventual production of usable paper."},{"en":"any paper that is not suitable for recycling being removed","context":"This paper is then sorted by hand and separated according to its grade, with any paper that is not suitable for recycling being removed."},{"en":"all remnants of ink and glue are removed","context":"Following this, all remnants of ink and glue are removed from the paper at the de-inking stage."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-032-1"]},"_displayNo":"#037","summary":"Chart below shows the process of waste paper recycling."},{"id":"writing-academic-033","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Processes & Cycles","title":"The water cycle","prompt":"The diagram below shows the water cycle, which is the continuous movement of water on, above and below the surface of the Earth.","answer":"The picture illustrates the way in which water passes from ocean to air to land during the natural process known as the water cycle.\n\nThree main stages are shown on the diagram. Ocean water evaporates, falls as rain, and eventually runs back into the oceans again.\n\nBeginning at the evaporation stage, we can see that 80% of water vapour in the air comes from the oceans. Heat from the sun causes water to evaporate, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">water vapour condenses to form clouds</strong>. At the second stage, labelled ‘precipitation’ on the diagram, water falls as rain or snow.\n\nAt the third stage in the cycle, rainwater <strong class=\"key-phrase\">may take various paths</strong>. Some of it may fall into lakes or return to the oceans via ‘surface runoff’. Otherwise, rainwater may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">filter through the ground</strong>, reaching <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the impervious layer of the earth</strong>. Salt water intrusion is shown to take place just before groundwater passes into the oceans <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to complete the cycle</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"filter through the ground","context":"Otherwise, rainwater may filter through the ground, reaching the impervious layer of the earth."},{"en":"the impervious layer of the earth","context":"Otherwise, rainwater may filter through the ground, reaching the impervious layer of the earth."},{"en":"water vapour condenses to form clouds","context":"Heat from the sun causes water to evaporate, and water vapour condenses to form clouds."},{"en":"may take various paths","context":"At the third stage in the cycle, rainwater may take various paths."},{"en":"to complete the cycle","context":"Salt water intrusion is shown to take place just before groundwater passes into the oceans to complete the cycle."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-033-1"]},"_displayNo":"#038","summary":"Diagram below shows the water cycle, which is the continuous movement of…"},{"id":"writing-academic-038","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Processes & Cycles","title":"Hydroelectricity production","prompt":"The diagram below shows the process of using water to produce electricity.\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The picture explains the process through which water is used to produce electrical power.\n\nOverall, the process <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comprises over ten stages</strong>, turning water from the sea into electricity for a range of different users. It begins with a natural cycle of evaporation and rainfall, and ends with power being distributed from a transformer station.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">The process begins when</strong> seawater is heated by the sun and evaporates to form small clouds in the sky. These clouds then <strong class=\"key-phrase\">merge into a storm cloud</strong>, which rains over the mountain, and the rainwater <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is gathered in the reservoir behind a dam</strong>.\n\nThe following steps involve a pipe which connects the reservoir to a turbine and a pump, and which is controlled using a valve. Once this valve is opened, water flows into the turbine and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rotates it to produce an electrical current</strong>, after which the water is pumped back into the reservoir.\n\nThe electricity produced by the turbine is then transferred to the transformer station through high voltage cables. At the last stage, the electrical power <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is delivered to domestic and industrial consumers</strong>, as well as to educational and medical facilities.","phrases":[{"en":"comprises over ten stages","context":"Overall, the process comprises over ten stages, turning water from the sea into electricity for a range of different users. It begins with a natural cycle of evaporation and rainfall, and ends with power being distributed from a transformer station."},{"en":"The process begins when","context":"The process begins when seawater is heated by the sun and evaporates to form small clouds in the sky. These clouds then merge into a storm cloud, which rains over the mountain, and the rainwater is gathered in the reservoir behind a dam."},{"en":"merge into a storm cloud","context":"The process begins when seawater is heated by the sun and evaporates to form small clouds in the sky. These clouds then merge into a storm cloud, which rains over the mountain, and the rainwater is gathered in the reservoir behind a dam."},{"en":"is gathered in the reservoir behind a dam","context":"The process begins when seawater is heated by the sun and evaporates to form small clouds in the sky. These clouds then merge into a storm cloud, which rains over the mountain, and the rainwater is gathered in the reservoir behind a dam."},{"en":"rotates it to produce an electrical current","context":"Once this valve is opened, water flows into the turbine and rotates it to produce an electrical current, after which the water is pumped back into the reservoir."},{"en":"is delivered to domestic and industrial consumers","context":"The electricity produced by the turbine is then transferred to the transformer station through high voltage cables. At the last stage, the electrical power is delivered to domestic and industrial consumers, as well as to educational and medical facilities."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-038-1"]},"_displayNo":"#039","summary":"Diagram below shows the process of using water to produce electricity."},{"id":"writing-academic-040","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Processes & Cycles","title":"Urban waste sorting","prompt":"Write a description of how urban refuse is assorted for recycling. Use the information in the diagram and write a description of this process.","answer":"The diagram shows the way in which urban refuse is sorted so that it can be recycled.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Collecting and processing recyclables</strong> is presented here as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a continuous loop rather than a one-way process</strong>. There are four main stages: collection from rubbish bins, sorting at a recovery facility, further processing into raw materials, and the return of those materials to households as everyday items.\n\nAt the first stage, recyclables are collected from rubbish bins; the three main types are bottles, made of either plastic or glass, paper, and durables. If this material has already been put into the correct bins, much of the time needed for the second stage is saved. In that second stage, material recovery facilities sort the recyclables more carefully, and some of them are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sent directly to customers for their own use</strong>. Soft drink companies, for example, have their own recycling facilities and can reuse the containers that have been collected, while newspapers and paper towels are sent to paper and pulp mills.\n\nThe rest of the recyclables are cleaned, separated and crushed in the third part of the loop, which is known as the further-processing stage. The recycled materials produced in this way are sold to different businesses: engineering companies <strong class=\"key-phrase\">are regular buyers of aluminium</strong>, while property developers buy glass and plastic for a variety of uses.\n\nAt the final stage, the recycled materials become part of the items that households use every day. Once those items are thrown away, households are expected to put them into the appropriate bins, and this is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">how the recycling loop continues to operate</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"Collecting and processing recyclables","context":"Collecting and processing recyclables is presented here as a continuous loop rather than a one-way process."},{"en":"a continuous loop rather than a one-way process","context":"Collecting and processing recyclables is presented here as a continuous loop rather than a one-way process."},{"en":"sent directly to customers for their own use","context":"In that second stage, material recovery facilities sort the recyclables more carefully, and some of them are sent directly to customers for their own use."},{"en":"are regular buyers of aluminium","context":"The recycled materials produced in this way are sold to different businesses: engineering companies are regular buyers of aluminium, while property developers buy glass and plastic for a variety of uses."},{"en":"how the recycling loop continues to operate","context":"At the final stage, the recycled materials become part of the items that households use every day. Once those items are thrown away, households are expected to put them into the appropriate bins, and this is how the recycling loop continues to operate."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-040-1"]},"_displayNo":"#040","summary":"Write a description of how urban refuse is assorted for recycling."},{"id":"writing-academic-042","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Processes & Cycles","title":"Coffee production","prompt":"The diagram below shows how coffee is produced and prepared for sale in supermarkets and shops.","answer":"The picture illustrates the process of coffee manufacture and preparation for sale on the market.\n\nIt is clear that there are 11 stages in the production of coffee. The process begins with the picking of the beans and ends at the packing stage, and along the way <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the beans pass through heating, cooling, mixing, freezing and drying</strong>.\n\nLooking at the coffee production process in detail, coffee beans must first be picked in the fields. These beans are then dried, roasted and cooled, before being put into a grinding machine which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turns them into ground coffee</strong>.\n\nAt the sixth stage in the process, the ground coffee is mixed with hot water, and the resulting mixture <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is strained</strong>. Next, the mixture is frozen and then passed once again through the grinder. After that, the ground, frozen liquid is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dried in a vacuum so that the water evaporates</strong>, leaving the coffee granules that form the final product. Finally, these granules are packed into coffee jars ready for delivery to shops.","phrases":[{"en":"is strained","context":"At the sixth stage in the process, the ground coffee is mixed with hot water, and the resulting mixture is strained."},{"en":"the beans pass through heating, cooling, mixing, freezing and drying","context":"It is clear that there are 11 stages in the production of coffee. The process begins with the picking of the beans and ends at the packing stage, and along the way the beans pass through heating, cooling, mixing, freezing and drying."},{"en":"turns them into ground coffee","context":"Looking at the coffee production process in detail, coffee beans must first be picked in the fields. These beans are then dried, roasted and cooled, before being put into a grinding machine which turns them into ground coffee."},{"en":"dried in a vacuum so that the water evaporates","context":"After that, the ground, frozen liquid is dried in a vacuum so that the water evaporates, leaving the coffee granules that form the final product."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-042-1"]},"_displayNo":"#041","summary":"Diagram below shows how coffee is produced and prepared for sale in…"},{"id":"writing-academic-043","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Processes & Cycles","title":"Weather forecasting process","prompt":"The diagram below shows how the Australian Bureau of Meteorology collects up-to-the-minute information on the weather in order to produce reliable forecasts","answer":"The figure illustrates the process used by the Australian Bureau of Meteorology to forecast the weather.\n\nThere are four stages in the process, beginning with the collection of information about the weather. This information is then analysed, prepared for presentation, and finally <strong class=\"key-phrase\">broadcast to the public</strong>.\n\nLooking at the first and second stages of the process, there are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">three ways of collecting weather data</strong> and three ways of analysing it. Firstly, incoming information can be received by satellite and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">presented for analysis as a satellite photo</strong>. The same data can also be passed to a radar station and presented on a radar screen or synoptic chart. Secondly, incoming information may be collected directly by radar and analysed on a radar screen or synoptic chart. Finally, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drifting buoys</strong> also receive data which can be shown on a synoptic chart.\n\nAt the third stage of the process, the weather broadcast is prepared on computers. Finally, it is delivered to the public on television, on the radio, or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as a recorded telephone announcement</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"broadcast to the public","context":"There are four stages in the process, beginning with the collection of information about the weather. This information is then analysed, prepared for presentation, and finally broadcast to the public."},{"en":"drifting buoys","context":"Finally, drifting buoys also receive data which can be shown on a synoptic chart."},{"en":"three ways of collecting weather data","context":"Looking at the first and second stages of the process, there are three ways of collecting weather data and three ways of analysing it."},{"en":"presented for analysis as a satellite photo","context":"Firstly, incoming information can be received by satellite and presented for analysis as a satellite photo."},{"en":"as a recorded telephone announcement","context":"At the third stage of the process, the weather broadcast is prepared on computers. Finally, it is delivered to the public on television, on the radio, or as a recorded telephone announcement."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-043-1"]},"_displayNo":"#042","summary":"Diagram below shows how the Australian Bureau of Meteorology collects…"},{"id":"writing-academic-044","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Processes & Cycles","title":"Salmon life cycle","prompt":"The diagram below shows the life cycle of a salmon, from egg to adult fish.","answer":"The diagram illustrates the stages in the life of the salmon, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">from birth to maturity</strong>.\n\nIt is clear that there are six main stages as the salmon develops from egg to mature adult. We can also see that salmon spend time in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">three distinct locations during the cycle</strong>, moving from river to estuary to ocean and then back upstream.\n\nSalmon begin their lives in rivers where the adult fish lay and incubate their eggs. After emerging from eggs, the young salmon spend the next stage of their lives <strong class=\"key-phrase\">being reared in freshwater areas</strong>. Then, at some point in their development, the fish swim downstream to river estuaries where rearing continues.\n\nFollowing the estuary rearing period, the maturing salmon migrate to the ocean, where they eventually become fully grown adults. Finally, the adult fish travel back upstream to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spawning areas</strong> of rivers; here they reproduce and lay their eggs, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the life cycle begins anew</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"from birth to maturity","context":"The diagram illustrates the stages in the life of the salmon, from birth to maturity."},{"en":"spawning areas","context":"Finally, the adult fish travel back upstream to spawning areas of rivers; here they reproduce and lay their eggs, and the life cycle begins anew."},{"en":"three distinct locations during the cycle","context":"It is clear that there are six main stages as the salmon develops from egg to mature adult. We can also see that salmon spend time in three distinct locations during the cycle, moving from river to estuary to ocean and then back upstream."},{"en":"being reared in freshwater areas","context":"After emerging from eggs, the young salmon spend the next stage of their lives being reared in freshwater areas."},{"en":"the life cycle begins anew","context":"Finally, the adult fish travel back upstream to spawning areas of rivers; here they reproduce and lay their eggs, and the life cycle begins anew."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-044-1"]},"_displayNo":"#043","summary":"Diagram below shows the life cycle of a salmon, from egg to adult fish."},{"id":"writing-academic-046","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Processes & Cycles","title":"Silkworm and silk production","prompt":"The diagrams below show the life cycle of the silkworm and the stages in the production of silk","answer":"The diagrams illustrate the stages in the life of a silkworm and the process of producing silk cloth.\n\nThere are four main stages in the life cycle of the silkworm, from eggs to adult moth. The process of silk cloth production involves six steps, from silkworm cocoon to silk material.\n\nAt the first stage in the life cycle of a silkworm, the moth lays its eggs. Around ten days later, silkworm <strong class=\"key-phrase\">larvae hatch from the eggs</strong>; these larvae <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feed on mulberry leaves</strong>. Then, after four to six weeks, the larvae <strong class=\"key-phrase\">become covered in</strong> silk thread, and it takes between three and eight days for a full cocoon to be produced.\n\nFor silk cloth production, a cocoon is first selected and then boiled in water. After boiling, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the silk thread that makes up the cocoon is unwound</strong>, and then several strands of thread <strong class=\"key-phrase\">are twisted together</strong>. At the fifth and sixth stages in the process, the thread can either be dyed and then <strong class=\"key-phrase\">woven into silk fabric</strong>, or it can be woven first and then dyed subsequently.","phrases":[{"en":"become covered in","context":"Then, after four to six weeks, the larvae become covered in silk thread, and it takes between three and eight days for a full cocoon to be produced."},{"en":"are twisted together","context":"After boiling, the silk thread that makes up the cocoon is unwound, and then several strands of thread are twisted together."},{"en":"larvae hatch from the eggs","context":"Around ten days later, silkworm larvae hatch from the eggs; these larvae feed on mulberry leaves."},{"en":"feed on mulberry leaves","context":"Around ten days later, silkworm larvae hatch from the eggs; these larvae feed on mulberry leaves."},{"en":"the silk thread that makes up the cocoon is unwound","context":"After boiling, the silk thread that makes up the cocoon is unwound, and then several strands of thread are twisted together."},{"en":"woven into silk fabric","context":"At the fifth and sixth stages in the process, the thread can either be dyed and then woven into silk fabric, or it can be woven first and then dyed subsequently."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-046-1"]},"_displayNo":"#044","summary":"Diagrams below show the life cycle of the silkworm and the stages in the…"},{"id":"writing-academic-056","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Processes & Cycles","title":"Honey bee life cycle","prompt":"The diagram below shows the life cycle of the honey bee.\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The diagram illustrates the stages in the life of honey bees. All in all, this life cycle is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comprised of six stages</strong> from egg to full maturity, and takes approximately five weeks (34 to 36 days to be exact) to complete.\n\nThe first stage is when the female bee lays up to a couple of oval eggs once every 72 hours. These eggs hatch between nine and ten days later, and immature bees called nymphs emerge; these lack the typical bee stripes.\n\nOver the next 3 weeks the nymphs go through three moulting stages, shedding their skins to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">allow further growth to occur</strong>: the first moulting happens 5 days after the eggs hatch, and the second one takes place a week later. Nine days after that, the nymphs moult for a third time and young adult honey bees emerge, identified by their horizontally striped backs. These take four more days to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mature into larger adult bees</strong> whose backs are marked with bolder and darker stripes, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the cycle starts over again</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"comprised of six stages","context":"The diagram illustrates the stages in the life of honey bees. All in all, this life cycle is comprised of six stages from egg to full maturity, and takes approximately five weeks (34 to 36 days to be exact) to complete."},{"en":"allow further growth to occur","context":"Over the next 3 weeks the nymphs go through three moulting stages, shedding their skins to allow further growth to occur: the first moulting happens 5 days after the eggs hatch, and the second one takes place a week later."},{"en":"mature into larger adult bees","context":"These take four more days to mature into larger adult bees whose backs are marked with bolder and darker stripes, and the cycle starts over again."},{"en":"the cycle starts over again","context":"These take four more days to mature into larger adult bees whose backs are marked with bolder and darker stripes, and the cycle starts over again."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-056-1"]},"_displayNo":"#045","summary":"Diagram below shows the life cycle of the honey bee."},{"id":"writing-academic-008","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Teen internet and gaming time","prompt":"The chart shows the amount of time that 10- to 15-year-olds in the UK spend chatting on the Internet and playing games on consoles on an average school day.","answer":"The bar chart compares the time spent by 10 to 15-year-olds in the UK on two activities, namely chatting online and playing computer games.\n\nOverall, we can see that playing computer games is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">marginally more popular than</strong> chatting on the Internet. However, completely different trends can be seen if we look at the specific figures for boys and girls.\n\nBoys aged between 10 and 15 <strong class=\"key-phrase\">clearly favour playing on games consoles over chatting online</strong>. According to the chart, while 85% of boys play computer games every day, only 55% chat online daily. The majority of boys also play on their consoles for more than one hour each day, and 10% do so for four hours or more.\n\nBy contrast, girls prefer chatting online. Close to 70% of 10 to 15-year-old girls <strong class=\"key-phrase\">engage in online conversation each day</strong>, compared to about 50% of this cohort who play computer games. Of the girls who do play on consoles, most of them play <strong class=\"key-phrase\">for less than an hour</strong>, whereas most girls who chat online do so for more than one hour, and nearly 10% chat for four hours or more.","phrases":[{"en":"marginally more popular than","context":"Overall, we can see that playing computer games is marginally more popular than chatting on the Internet. However, completely different trends can be seen if we look at the specific figures for boys and girls."},{"en":"clearly favour playing on games consoles over chatting online","context":"Boys aged between 10 and 15 clearly favour playing on games consoles over chatting online."},{"en":"engage in online conversation each day","context":"Close to 70% of 10 to 15-year-old girls engage in online conversation each day, compared to about 50% of this cohort who play computer games."},{"en":"for less than an hour","context":"Of the girls who do play on consoles, most of them play for less than an hour, whereas most girls who chat online do so for more than one hour, and nearly 10% chat for four hours or more."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-008-1"]},"_displayNo":"#046","summary":"Chart below shows the amount of"},{"id":"writing-academic-011","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Olympic medal totals","prompt":"The chart below shows the total number of Olympic medals won by twelve different countries.","answer":"The bar chart compares twelve countries in terms of the overall number of medals that they have won at the Olympic Games.\n\nIt is clear that the USA is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">by far the most successful</strong> Olympic medal winning nation. It is also noticeable that the figures for gold, silver and bronze medals won by any particular country <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tend to be fairly similar</strong>.\n\nThe USA has won a total of around 2,300 Olympic medals, including approximately 900 gold medals, 750 silver and 650 bronze. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">In second place on the all-time medals chart</strong> is the Soviet Union, with just over 1,000 medals. Again, the number of gold medals won by this country is slightly higher than the number of silver or bronze medals.\n\nOnly four other countries - the UK, France, Germany and Italy - have won more than 500 Olympic medals, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">all with similar proportions of each medal colour</strong>. Apart from the USA and the Soviet Union, China is the only other country with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a noticeably higher proportion of gold medals</strong> (about 200) compared to silver and bronze (about 100 each).","phrases":[{"en":"by far the most successful","context":"It is clear that the USA is by far the most successful Olympic medal winning nation. It is also noticeable that the figures for gold, silver and bronze medals won by any particular country tend to be fairly similar."},{"en":"tend to be fairly similar","context":"It is clear that the USA is by far the most successful Olympic medal winning nation. It is also noticeable that the figures for gold, silver and bronze medals won by any particular country tend to be fairly similar."},{"en":"a noticeably higher proportion of gold medals","context":"Apart from the USA and the Soviet Union, China is the only other country with a noticeably higher proportion of gold medals (about 200) compared to silver and bronze (about 100 each)."},{"en":"In second place on the all-time medals chart","context":"In second place on the all-time medals chart is the Soviet Union, with just over 1,000 medals."},{"en":"all with similar proportions of each medal colour","context":"Only four other countries - the UK, France, Germany and Italy - have won more than 500 Olympic medals, all with similar proportions of each medal colour."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-011-1"]},"_displayNo":"#047","summary":"Chart below shows the total number of Olympic medals won by twelve…"},{"id":"writing-academic-016","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Children's sports spending","prompt":"The first chart shows British parents' spending on children's sports from 2008 to 2014, while the second shows the number of children participating in three sports.","answer":"The line graphs show the average monthly amount that parents in Britain spent on their children's sporting activities and the number of British children who took part in three different sports from 2008 to 2014.\n\nIt is clear that parents spent more money each year on their children's participation in sports over the six-year period. In terms of the number of children taking part, football was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">significantly more popular than athletics and swimming</strong>.\n\nIn 2008, British parents spent an average of around £20 per month on their children's sporting activities. Parents’ spending on children's sports <strong class=\"key-phrase\">increased gradually over the following six years</strong>, and by 2014 the average monthly amount had risen to just over £30.\n\nLooking at participation numbers, in 2008 approximately 8 million British children played football, while only 2 million children <strong class=\"key-phrase\">were enrolled in swimming clubs</strong> and less than 1 million practised athletics. The figures for football participation remained relatively stable over the following 6 years. By contrast, participation in swimming <strong class=\"key-phrase\">almost doubled, to nearly 4 million children</strong>, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">there was a near fivefold increase in</strong> the number of children doing athletics.","phrases":[{"en":"increased gradually over the following six years","context":"Parents’ spending on children's sports increased gradually over the following six years, and by 2014 the average monthly amount had risen to just over £30."},{"en":"there was a near fivefold increase in","context":"By contrast, participation in swimming almost doubled, to nearly 4 million children, and there was a near fivefold increase in the number of children doing athletics."},{"en":"significantly more popular than athletics and swimming","context":"It is clear that parents spent more money each year on their children's participation in sports over the six-year period. In terms of the number of children taking part, football was significantly more popular than athletics and swimming."},{"en":"were enrolled in swimming clubs","context":"Looking at participation numbers, in 2008 approximately 8 million British children played football, while only 2 million children were enrolled in swimming clubs and less than 1 million practised athletics."},{"en":"almost doubled, to nearly 4 million children","context":"By contrast, participation in swimming almost doubled, to nearly 4 million children, and there was a near fivefold increase in the number of children doing athletics."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-016-1"]},"_displayNo":"#048","summary":"First chart below gives information about the money spent by British…"},{"id":"writing-academic-010","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Technology & Communication","title":"UK telephone calls","prompt":"The chart shows the total number of minutes, in billions, spent on three types of telephone calls in the UK between 1995 and 2002.","answer":"The bar chart compares the amount of time spent by people in the UK on three different types of phone call between 1995 and 2002.\n\nIt is clear that calls made via local, fixed lines <strong class=\"key-phrase\">were the most popular type, in terms of overall usage</strong>, throughout the period shown. The lowest figures on the chart are for mobile calls, but this category also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saw the most dramatic increase in</strong> user minutes.\n\nIn 1995, people in the UK used fixed lines for a total of just over 70 billion minutes for local calls, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">about half of that amount of time</strong> for national or international calls. By contrast, mobile phones were only used for around 4 billion minutes. Over the following four years, the figures for all three types of phone call increased steadily.\n\nBy 1999, the amount of time spent on local calls using landlines had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reached a peak</strong> at 90 billion minutes. Subsequently, the figure for this category fell, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the rise in the other two types of phone call continued</strong>. In 2002, the number of minutes of national / international landline calls passed 60 billion, while the figure for mobiles rose to around 45 billion minutes.","phrases":[{"en":"saw the most dramatic increase in","context":"It is clear that calls made via local, fixed lines were the most popular type, in terms of overall usage, throughout the period shown. The lowest figures on the chart are for mobile calls, but this category also saw the most dramatic increase in user minutes."},{"en":"reached a peak","context":"By 1999, the amount of time spent on local calls using landlines had reached a peak at 90 billion minutes."},{"en":"were the most popular type, in terms of overall usage","context":"It is clear that calls made via local, fixed lines were the most popular type, in terms of overall usage, throughout the period shown. The lowest figures on the chart are for mobile calls, but this category also saw the most dramatic increase in user minutes."},{"en":"about half of that amount of time","context":"In 1995, people in the UK used fixed lines for a total of just over 70 billion minutes for local calls, and about half of that amount of time for national or international calls."},{"en":"the rise in the other two types of phone call continued","context":"Subsequently, the figure for this category fell, but the rise in the other two types of phone call continued."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-010-1"]},"_displayNo":"#049","summary":"Chart below shows the total number of minutes (in billions) of telephone…"},{"id":"writing-academic-041","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Technology & Communication","title":"Digital game sales","prompt":"The bar graph shows the global sales (in billions of dollars) of different types of digital games","answer":"The bar chart compares the turnover in dollars from sales of video games for four different platforms, namely mobile phones, online, consoles and handheld devices, from 2000 to 2006.\n\nIt is clear that sales of games for three out of the four platforms rose each year, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leading to a significant rise in total global turnover</strong> over the 7-year period. Sales figures for handheld games were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at least twice as high as those for any other platform</strong> in almost every year.\n\nIn 2000, worldwide sales of handheld games stood at around $11 billion, while console games earned just under $6 billion. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">No figures are given for</strong> mobile or online games in that year. Over the next 3 years, sales of handheld video games rose by about $4 billion, but the figure for consoles decreased by $2 billion. Mobile phone and online games started to become popular, with sales reaching around $3 billion in 2003.\n\nIn 2006, sales of handheld, online and mobile games <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reached peaks of 17, 9 and 7 billion dollars respectively</strong>. By contrast, turnover from console games <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dropped to its lowest point</strong>, at around $2.5 billion.","phrases":[{"en":"No figures are given for","context":"No figures are given for mobile or online games in that year."},{"en":"dropped to its lowest point","context":"In 2006, sales of handheld, online and mobile games reached peaks of 17, 9 and 7 billion dollars respectively. By contrast, turnover from console games dropped to its lowest point, at around $2.5 billion."},{"en":"leading to a significant rise in total global turnover","context":"It is clear that sales of games for three out of the four platforms rose each year, leading to a significant rise in total global turnover over the 7-year period."},{"en":"at least twice as high as those for any other platform","context":"Sales figures for handheld games were at least twice as high as those for any other platform in almost every year."},{"en":"reached peaks of 17, 9 and 7 billion dollars respectively","context":"In 2006, sales of handheld, online and mobile games reached peaks of 17, 9 and 7 billion dollars respectively. By contrast, turnover from console games dropped to its lowest point, at around $2.5 billion."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-041-1"]},"_displayNo":"#050","summary":"Bar graph shows the global sales (in billions of dollars) of different…"},{"id":"writing-academic-049","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Technology & Communication","title":"US phone expenditure","prompt":"The graph compares US consumers' average annual expenditure on cell-phone and residential phone services between 2001 and 2010.","answer":"The line graph compares the average amount of money spent each year by consumers in the United States on mobile phone services and on residential landline services between 2001 and 2010.\n\nIt is clear that spending on landline phones <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fell steadily</strong> over the 10-year period, while mobile phone expenditure rose quickly. The year 2006 <strong class=\"key-phrase\">marks the point at which expenditure on mobile services overtook</strong> that for residential phone services.\n\nIn 2001, US consumers spent an average of nearly $700 on residential phone services, compared to only around $200 on cell phone services. Over the following five years, average yearly spending on landlines <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dropped by nearly $200</strong>. By contrast, expenditure on mobiles rose by approximately $300.\n\nIn the year 2006, the average American paid out the same amount of money on both types of phone service, spending just over $500 on each. By 2010, expenditure on mobile phones <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had reached around $750</strong>, while the figure for spending on residential services had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fallen to just over half this amount</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"fell steadily","context":"It is clear that spending on landline phones fell steadily over the 10-year period, while mobile phone expenditure rose quickly. The year 2006 marks the point at which expenditure on mobile services overtook that for residential phone services."},{"en":"fallen to just over half this amount","context":"By 2010, expenditure on mobile phones had reached around $750, while the figure for spending on residential services had fallen to just over half this amount."},{"en":"marks the point at which expenditure on mobile services overtook","context":"It is clear that spending on landline phones fell steadily over the 10-year period, while mobile phone expenditure rose quickly. The year 2006 marks the point at which expenditure on mobile services overtook that for residential phone services."},{"en":"dropped by nearly $200","context":"Over the following five years, average yearly spending on landlines dropped by nearly $200."},{"en":"had reached around $750","context":"By 2010, expenditure on mobile phones had reached around $750, while the figure for spending on residential services had fallen to just over half this amount."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-049-1"]},"_displayNo":"#051","summary":"Graph below shows US consumers' average annual expenditures on cell phone…"},{"id":"writing-academic-054","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Technology & Communication","title":"Mobile phone feature use","prompt":"The table below shows the percentage of mobile phones owners using various mobile features.","answer":"The table compares the percentages of people using different functions of their mobile phones between 2006 and 2010.\n\nThroughout the period shown, the main reason why people used their mobile phones was to make calls. However, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">there was a marked increase in</strong> the popularity of other mobile phone features, particularly the Internet search feature.\n\nIn 2006, 100% of mobile phone owners used their phones to make calls, while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the next most popular functions were</strong> text messaging (73%) and taking photos (66%). By contrast, less than 20% of owners played games or music on their phones, and there were no figures for users doing Internet searches or recording video.\n\nOver the following 4 years, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">there was relatively little change in</strong> the figures for the top three mobile phone features. However, the percentage of people using their phones to access the Internet <strong class=\"key-phrase\">jumped to 41% in 2008</strong> and then to 73% in 2010. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">There was also a significant rise in</strong> the use of mobiles to play games and to record video, with figures reaching 41% and 35% respectively in 2010.","phrases":[{"en":"there was a marked increase in","context":"Throughout the period shown, the main reason why people used their mobile phones was to make calls. However, there was a marked increase in the popularity of other mobile phone features, particularly the Internet search feature."},{"en":"there was relatively little change in","context":"Over the following 4 years, there was relatively little change in the figures for the top three mobile phone features."},{"en":"There was also a significant rise in","context":"There was also a significant rise in the use of mobiles to play games and to record video, with figures reaching 41% and 35% respectively in 2010."},{"en":"the next most popular functions were","context":"In 2006, 100% of mobile phone owners used their phones to make calls, while the next most popular functions were text messaging (73%) and taking photos (66%)."},{"en":"jumped to 41% in 2008","context":"However, the percentage of people using their phones to access the Internet jumped to 41% in 2008 and then to 73% in 2010."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-054-1"]},"_displayNo":"#052","summary":"Table below shows the percentage of mobile phones owners using various…"},{"id":"writing-academic-009","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Tourism & Travel","title":"British tourist attractions","prompt":"The chart below shows the results of a survey of people who visited four types of tourist attraction in Britain in the year 1999.","answer":"The pie chart compares figures for visitors to four categories of tourist attraction and to five different theme parks in Britain in 1999.\n\nIt is clear that theme parks and museums/ galleries were the two most popular types of tourist attraction in that year. Blackpool Pleasure Beach <strong class=\"key-phrase\">received by far the highest proportion of visitors</strong> in the theme park sector.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Looking at the information in more detail</strong>, we can see that 38% of the surveyed visitors went to a theme park, and 37% of them went to a museum or gallery. By contrast, historic houses and monuments were visited by only 16% of the sample, while wildlife parks and zoos were the least popular of the four types of tourist attraction, with only 9% of visitors.\n\nIn the theme park sector, almost half of the people surveyed (47%) had been to Blackpool Pleasure Beach. Alton Towers was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the second most popular amusement park</strong>, with 17% of the sample, followed by Pleasureland in Southport, with 16%. Finally, Chessington World of Adventures and Legoland Windsor <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had each welcomed 10% of the surveyed visitors</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"Looking at the information in more detail","context":"Looking at the information in more detail, we can see that 38% of the surveyed visitors went to a theme park, and 37% of them went to a museum or gallery."},{"en":"received by far the highest proportion of visitors","context":"It is clear that theme parks and museums/ galleries were the two most popular types of tourist attraction in that year. Blackpool Pleasure Beach received by far the highest proportion of visitors in the theme park sector."},{"en":"the second most popular amusement park","context":"Alton Towers was the second most popular amusement park, with 17% of the sample, followed by Pleasureland in Southport, with 16%."},{"en":"had each welcomed 10% of the surveyed visitors","context":"Finally, Chessington World of Adventures and Legoland Windsor had each welcomed 10% of the surveyed visitors."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-009-1"]},"_displayNo":"#053","summary":"Chart below shows the results of a survey of people who visited four types…"},{"id":"writing-academic-014","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Tourism & Travel","title":"US travel patterns and problems","prompt":"The charts below show reasons for travel and the main issues for the travelling public in the US in 2009.","answer":"The bar chart and pie chart give information about why US residents travelled and what travel problems they experienced in the year 2009.\n\nIt is clear that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the principal reason why Americans travelled</strong> in 2009 was to commute to and from work. In the same year, the primary concern of Americans, with regard to the trips they made, was the cost of travelling.\n\nLooking more closely at the bar chart, we can see that 49% of the trips made by Americans in 2009 were for the purpose of commuting. By contrast, only 6% of trips were visits to friends or relatives, and one in ten trips were for social or recreation reasons. Shopping <strong class=\"key-phrase\">was cited as the reason for 16% of all travel</strong>, while unspecified ‘personal reasons’ <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accounted for the remaining</strong> 19%.\n\nAccording to the pie chart, price was the key consideration for 36% of American travellers. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Almost one in five people cited safety as their foremost travel concern</strong>, while aggressive driving and highway congestion were the main issues for 17% and 14% of the travelling public. Finally, a total of 14% of those surveyed thought that access to public transport or space for pedestrians were the most important travel issues.","phrases":[{"en":"accounted for the remaining","context":"Shopping was cited as the reason for 16% of all travel, while unspecified ‘personal reasons’ accounted for the remaining 19%."},{"en":"the principal reason why Americans travelled","context":"It is clear that the principal reason why Americans travelled in 2009 was to commute to and from work. In the same year, the primary concern of Americans, with regard to the trips they made, was the cost of travelling."},{"en":"was cited as the reason for 16% of all travel","context":"Shopping was cited as the reason for 16% of all travel, while unspecified ‘personal reasons’ accounted for the remaining 19%."},{"en":"Almost one in five people cited safety as their foremost travel concern","context":"Almost one in five people cited safety as their foremost travel concern, while aggressive driving and highway congestion were the main issues for 17% and 14% of the travelling public."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-014-1"]},"_displayNo":"#054","summary":"Charts below show reasons for travel and the main issues for the travelling…"},{"id":"writing-academic-015","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Tourism & Travel","title":"Parkway Hotel customer service","prompt":"The charts show how visitors rated the Parkway Hotel's customer service in 2005 and 2010.","answer":"The pie charts compare visitors’ responses to a survey about customer service at the Parkway Hotel in 2005 and in 2010.\n\nIt is clear that overall customer satisfaction increased considerably from 2005 to 2010. While most hotel guests rated customer service as satisfactory or poor in 2005, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clear majority described the hotel's service as good or excellent</strong> in 2010.\n\nLooking at the positive responses first, in 2005 only 5% of the hotel's visitors rated its customer service as excellent, but this figure rose to 28% in 2010. There was also a rise in the number of ‘good’ ratings: only 14% of guests described customer service in the hotel as good in 2005, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">almost three times as many people gave this rating</strong> five years later.\n\nWith regard to negative feedback, the proportion of guests who considered the hotel's customer service to be poor fell from 21% in 2005 to only 12% in 2010. Similarly, the proportion of people who thought customer service was very poor <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dropped from 15% to only 4%</strong> over the 5-year period. Finally, a fall in the number of ‘satisfactory’ ratings in 2010 <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reflects the fact that more people gave positive responses</strong> to the survey in that year.","phrases":[{"en":"a clear majority described the hotel's service as good or excellent","context":"It is clear that overall customer satisfaction increased considerably from 2005 to 2010. While most hotel guests rated customer service as satisfactory or poor in 2005, a clear majority described the hotel's service as good or excellent in 2010."},{"en":"almost three times as many people gave this rating","context":"There was also a rise in the number of ‘good’ ratings: only 14% of guests described customer service in the hotel as good in 2005, but almost three times as many people gave this rating five years later."},{"en":"dropped from 15% to only 4%","context":"Similarly, the proportion of people who thought customer service was very poor dropped from 15% to only 4% over the 5-year period."},{"en":"reflects the fact that more people gave positive responses","context":"Finally, a fall in the number of ‘satisfactory’ ratings in 2010 reflects the fact that more people gave positive responses to the survey in that year."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-015-1"]},"_displayNo":"#055","summary":"Charts below show the results of a questionnaire that asked visitors to the…"},{"id":"writing-academic-020","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Tourism & Travel","title":"UK travel and destinations","prompt":"The charts below give information about travel to and from the UK, and about the most popular countries for UK residents to visit.\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The first diagram compares changes in the number of British tourists who travelled abroad with that of foreign visitors to the United Kingdom over the last 20 years of the previous century. The bar chart shows the five countries that were visited most by the British in the last year of that survey.\n\nOverall, tourism both from and to the United Kingdom <strong class=\"key-phrase\">boomed in the 1980s and 90s</strong>, but the number of British people travelling abroad grew far more quickly than the number of visitors coming to the UK. Most British trips abroad were to just five countries.\n\nAccording to the graph, the number of British tourists travelling abroad <strong class=\"key-phrase\">initially stood at</strong> more than 12 million, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">it had grown sixfold by the end of the survey period</strong>. The figure for foreign visitors to the UK was slightly lower than that for British tourists at the beginning of this period, and it reached only about half the British total by the end of the century.\n\nFrance and Spain attracted the largest numbers of British tourists, with about 11 and 9 million visits respectively. Turkey was the least popular of the five destinations, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">receiving only about 30% as many British visitors as France</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"initially stood at","context":"According to the graph, the number of British tourists travelling abroad initially stood at more than 12 million, and it had grown sixfold by the end of the survey period."},{"en":"boomed in the 1980s and 90s","context":"Overall, tourism both from and to the United Kingdom boomed in the 1980s and 90s, but the number of British people travelling abroad grew far more quickly than the number of visitors coming to the UK. Most British trips abroad were to just five countries."},{"en":"it had grown sixfold by the end of the survey period","context":"According to the graph, the number of British tourists travelling abroad initially stood at more than 12 million, and it had grown sixfold by the end of the survey period."},{"en":"receiving only about 30% as many British visitors as France","context":"France and Spain attracted the largest numbers of British tourists, with about 11 and 9 million visits respectively. Turkey was the least popular of the five destinations, receiving only about 30% as many British visitors as France."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-020-1"]},"_displayNo":"#056","summary":"Charts below give information about travel to and from the UK, and about…"},{"id":"writing-academic-017","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Trade & Industry","title":"Oil use and employment","prompt":"Chart A below shows the rate of oil consumption (in millions of barrels) in a small country from 1950 to 2010. Chart B shows the number of people employed in the oil industry in that country, and also the rate of unemployment throughout the country over the same time. Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The two charts show the rate of oil consumption, the number of people employed in the oil industry, and the rate of unemployment in a small country between 1950 and 2010.\n\nOverall, the rise in oil use in this country <strong class=\"key-phrase\">was matched by a roughly similar rate of growth</strong> in the number of people employed in the oil business. At the same time, unemployment in the country as a whole dropped by more than half.\n\nOil consumption fluctuated at around 20 million barrels per year until 1970, but it then rose steadily, reaching 40 million barrels in 2000 and around 50 million by 2010.\n\nTurning to the second chart, the number of people employed in the oil industry <strong class=\"key-phrase\">roughly tripled over the same period</strong>. This figure also fluctuated at around 1,000 until 1970, before climbing consistently, passing 1,800 in 1980 and reaching 3,100 in 2010. National unemployment, on the other hand, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">declined progressively over the entire period</strong>, dropping from 4% in 1950 to 3.1% in 1980, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">finally arriving at 1.6%</strong> by the last year in question.","phrases":[{"en":"was matched by a roughly similar rate of growth","context":"Overall, the rise in oil use in this country was matched by a roughly similar rate of growth in the number of people employed in the oil business. At the same time, unemployment in the country as a whole dropped by more than half."},{"en":"roughly tripled over the same period","context":"Turning to the second chart, the number of people employed in the oil industry roughly tripled over the same period."},{"en":"declined progressively over the entire period","context":"National unemployment, on the other hand, declined progressively over the entire period, dropping from 4% in 1950 to 3.1% in 1980, and finally arriving at 1.6% by the last year in question."},{"en":"finally arriving at 1.6%","context":"National unemployment, on the other hand, declined progressively over the entire period, dropping from 4% in 1950 to 3.1% in 1980, and finally arriving at 1.6% by the last year in question."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-017-1"]},"_displayNo":"#057","summary":"Chart A below shows the rate of oil consumption (in millions of barrels) in…"},{"id":"writing-academic-022","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Trade & Industry","title":"Rotterdam port exports","prompt":"The charts give information about the amount of exports (millions of tonnes) moving through Rotterdam port in Holland to various global destinations in 2002 and 2012; and also the % rates of tax imposed on these exports by the receiving countries. Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The charts show the quantity of exports being transported through Rotterdam over a ten-year period, and the level of taxation imposed on the goods at their destination.\n\nOverall, total export volumes rose slightly over the decade, but the amount going to most individual zones declined. The figures also show a clear connection between increased export volumes and reduced levels of tax.\n\nTotal exports reached 18,600 million tonnes by 2012. Europe – the largest destination – declined from 6,900 million to 6,400 million, and the USA – the second highest – went down by roughly 10% to 3,900 million. China dipped slightly to 2,300 million, and Latin America dropped from 1,800 to 1,500 million, while the ‘other’ zones remained static at 350 million. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">By marked contrast</strong>, volumes to Asia Pacific and Australasia <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more than trebled</strong>, hitting 2,550 and 2,300 million tonnes respectively.\n\nTurning to taxation, we see that tax increased considerably in all the zones that showed a decline in volumes, reaching 2% in the USA, China and Latin America, and rising from 5% to 8% in Europe. The ‘other’ zones <strong class=\"key-phrase\">kept tax stable at 2%</strong>, while the growth zones reduced tax massively, from 5% to 2% in Asia Pacific and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fourfold decrease to 1%</strong> in Australasia.","phrases":[{"en":"By marked contrast","context":"By marked contrast, volumes to Asia Pacific and Australasia more than trebled, hitting 2,550 and 2,300 million tonnes respectively."},{"en":"more than trebled","context":"By marked contrast, volumes to Asia Pacific and Australasia more than trebled, hitting 2,550 and 2,300 million tonnes respectively."},{"en":"kept tax stable at 2%","context":"The ‘other’ zones kept tax stable at 2%, while the growth zones reduced tax massively, from 5% to 2% in Asia Pacific and a fourfold decrease to 1% in Australasia."},{"en":"a fourfold decrease to 1%","context":"The ‘other’ zones kept tax stable at 2%, while the growth zones reduced tax massively, from 5% to 2% in Asia Pacific and a fourfold decrease to 1% in Australasia."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-022-1"]},"_displayNo":"#058","summary":"Charts give information about the amount of exports (millions of tonnes)…"},{"id":"writing-academic-007","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Transport","title":"Transport incidents and injuries","prompt":"The chart below shows numbers of incidents and injuries per 100 million passenger miles travelled (PMT) by transportation type in 2002.","answer":"The bar chart compares the number of incidents and injuries for every 100 million passenger miles travelled on five different types of public transport in 2002.\n\nIt is clear that the most incidents and injuries took place on demand-response vehicles. By contrast, commuter rail services <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recorded by far the lowest figures</strong>.\n\nA total of 225 incidents and 173 injuries, per 100 million passenger miles travelled, took place on demand-response transport services. These figures were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nearly three times as high as</strong> those for the second highest category, bus services. There were 76 incidents and 66 people were injured on buses.\n\nRail services experienced fewer problems. The number of incidents on light rail trains <strong class=\"key-phrase\">equalled the figure recorded for buses</strong>, but there were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">significantly fewer injuries</strong>, at only 39. Heavy rail services saw lower numbers of such events than light rail services, but commuter rail passengers were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">even less likely to experience problems</strong>. In fact, only 20 incidents and 17 injuries occurred on commuter trains.","phrases":[{"en":"nearly three times as high as","context":"These figures were nearly three times as high as those for the second highest category, bus services."},{"en":"significantly fewer injuries","context":"The number of incidents on light rail trains equalled the figure recorded for buses, but there were significantly fewer injuries, at only 39."},{"en":"even less likely to experience problems","context":"Heavy rail services saw lower numbers of such events than light rail services, but commuter rail passengers were even less likely to experience problems."},{"en":"recorded by far the lowest figures","context":"It is clear that the most incidents and injuries took place on demand-response vehicles. By contrast, commuter rail services recorded by far the lowest figures."},{"en":"equalled the figure recorded for buses","context":"The number of incidents on light rail trains equalled the figure recorded for buses, but there were significantly fewer injuries, at only 39."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-007-1"]},"_displayNo":"#059","summary":"Chart below shows numbers of incidents and injuries per 100 million…"},{"id":"writing-academic-018","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Transport","title":"Train passengers and ticket prices","prompt":"The chart below shows the number of passengers arriving at a train station per hour across a day, and also the average price (in US Dollars) of their tickets. Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The chart gives data about passenger volumes reaching a station every two hours during the day, and also the average ticket cost at those times.\n\nPassenger numbers peak three times during the day, and ticket prices <strong class=\"key-phrase\">broadly follow the same pattern</strong>, rising when passenger traffic is high, except during the evening rush hour when they actually fall. Notably, the midday period combines the highest passenger total with the highest average fare.\n\nAt 6am, the number of passengers is 480, rising to 690 by 8am before falling again. There is then a peak of 700 at midday, followed by an afternoon decline to a low of 230 at 4pm. A third spike occurs at 6pm, reaching 670 <strong class=\"key-phrase\">before tailing off</strong>.\n\nTurning to ticket prices, the 8am passenger surge is matched by a high price of $29, followed by a peak of $31 at midday and a decrease to $17 by 4pm. However, the 6pm spike in passenger numbers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is accompanied by a low of $11 in prices</strong>, with a subsequent rise to $22 at 8pm and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a final levelling off</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"broadly follow the same pattern","context":"Passenger numbers peak three times during the day, and ticket prices broadly follow the same pattern, rising when passenger traffic is high, except during the evening rush hour when they actually fall."},{"en":"before tailing off","context":"At 6am, the number of passengers is 480, rising to 690 by 8am before falling again. There is then a peak of 700 at midday, followed by an afternoon decline to a low of 230 at 4pm. A third spike occurs at 6pm, reaching 670 before tailing off."},{"en":"is accompanied by a low of $11 in prices","context":"However, the 6pm spike in passenger numbers is accompanied by a low of $11 in prices, with a subsequent rise to $22 at 8pm and a final levelling off."},{"en":"a final levelling off","context":"However, the 6pm spike in passenger numbers is accompanied by a low of $11 in prices, with a subsequent rise to $22 at 8pm and a final levelling off."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-018-1"]},"_displayNo":"#060","summary":"Chart below shows the number of passengers arriving at a train station per…"},{"id":"writing-academic-023","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Transport","title":"Urban traffic speed and fatalities","prompt":"The charts show (Chart a) the average speed of urban zone traffic (in kilometres per hour or kph) in a number of countries over a fifty year period, including a future estimate; and also (Chart b) the total number of urban zones per country (in thousands). Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The charts show the average speed of city traffic in a number of countries over a fifty-year period, with a forecast for the future, and also the number of urban zones by country.\n\nOverall, the general trend is for the speed to increase and then decrease, although speeds in three of the countries rise over the period as a whole. The data <strong class=\"key-phrase\">does not suggest any correlation</strong> between increasing urban zones and average traffic speeds.\n\nIn France, for example, the average speed increased from 60 kph in 1970 to a peak of 86 kph in 1990, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tailing off to</strong> a forecast 52 kph in 2020. The USA also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">peaked in 1990</strong>, at 57 kph, before declining to 43 kph at the end of the period. The UK and Russia have a similar trend, rising to 56 and 72 kph respectively in 2010 before falling to below the starting level, and Canada shows a decline from 69 kph to 64 kph despite a peak of 73 kph. Chile, however, fluctuates around 51 kph and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remains static over the period</strong>, while Italy, Brazil and Spain, by contrast, show a clear rise, finishing at 58, 57 and 61 kph respectively.\n\nRegarding urban zones, all countries except one <strong class=\"key-phrase\">see predicted increases of roughly four times</strong> by 2020. The exception is Russia, which sees a fall of about half, to 3,200.","phrases":[{"en":"tailing off to","context":"In France, for example, the average speed increased from 60 kph in 1970 to a peak of 86 kph in 1990, tailing off to a forecast 52 kph in 2020."},{"en":"does not suggest any correlation","context":"Overall, the general trend is for the speed to increase and then decrease, although speeds in three of the countries rise over the period as a whole. The data does not suggest any correlation between increasing urban zones and average traffic speeds."},{"en":"peaked in 1990","context":"The USA also peaked in 1990, at 57 kph, before declining to 43 kph at the end of the period."},{"en":"remains static over the period","context":"Chile, however, fluctuates around 51 kph and remains static over the period, while Italy, Brazil and Spain, by contrast, show a clear rise, finishing at 58, 57 and 61 kph respectively."},{"en":"see predicted increases of roughly four times","context":"Regarding urban zones, all countries except one see predicted increases of roughly four times by 2020. The exception is Russia, which sees a fall of about half, to 3,200."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-023-1"]},"_displayNo":"#061","summary":"Charts show (Chart a) the average speed of urban zone traffic…"},{"id":"writing-academic-024","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Transport","title":"Airline crews and passenger capacity","prompt":"The first chart below shows the number of airline crew (pilots, stewards etc) needed to fly a passenger plane between Australia and a number of other countries. The second chart shows the number of passenger complaints per plane journey made on these routes, and average kilogram (kg) weight of luggage (or baggage) carried per passenger. Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The charts give data about four flight destinations from Australia in terms of crew numbers, passenger complaints made and luggage weight per passenger.\n\nOverall, it is apparent that flights to Germany require the largest crews and carry the most luggage per passenger, while flights to New Zealand <strong class=\"key-phrase\">are at the opposite end of the scale on both counts</strong>. However, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the pattern for customer complaints is reversed</strong>, with German flights attracting the fewest complaints and New Zealand flights the most. The Malaysia and Indonesia flights <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lie roughly between these two extremes</strong>.\n\nThe largest crew, of 15 people, is needed for the flight to Germany, followed by Indonesia with 11 and Malaysia with 8, while New Zealand flights need the smallest crew of only 6. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">This pattern is reflected in</strong> the weight of luggage per passenger, with Germany having the heaviest baggage at 38 kg, and Indonesia the second heaviest at 32 kg. Flights to Malaysia carry 30 kg per passenger, and New Zealand has the lightest figure at 15 kg.\n\nTurning to passenger complaints, New Zealand flights receive the most, at 6 per journey, followed by Indonesia and Malaysia with 3 each. Flights to Germany <strong class=\"key-phrase\">produce the fewest</strong>, with only 1 complaint per flight.","phrases":[{"en":"the pattern for customer complaints is reversed","context":"However, the pattern for customer complaints is reversed, with German flights attracting the fewest complaints and New Zealand flights the most."},{"en":"lie roughly between these two extremes","context":"The Malaysia and Indonesia flights lie roughly between these two extremes."},{"en":"are at the opposite end of the scale on both counts","context":"Overall, it is apparent that flights to Germany require the largest crews and carry the most luggage per passenger, while flights to New Zealand are at the opposite end of the scale on both counts."},{"en":"This pattern is reflected in","context":"This pattern is reflected in the weight of luggage per passenger, with Germany having the heaviest baggage at 38 kg, and Indonesia the second heaviest at 32 kg."},{"en":"produce the fewest","context":"Turning to passenger complaints, New Zealand flights receive the most, at 6 per journey, followed by Indonesia and Malaysia with 3 each. Flights to Germany produce the fewest, with only 1 complaint per flight."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-024-1"]},"_displayNo":"#062","summary":"First chart below shows the number of airline crew (pilots, stewards etc)…"},{"id":"writing-academic-052","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Transport","title":"Underground railway systems","prompt":"The table below gives information about the underground railway systems in six cities.","answer":"The table compares the underground rail networks in six major cities in terms of their age, size and the number of people who use them each year.\n\nIt is clear that the three oldest underground systems are larger and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">serve significantly more passengers than</strong> the newer systems.\n\nThe London underground is the oldest system, having opened in 1863. It is also the largest system, with 394 kilometres of route. The second largest system, in Paris, is only about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">half the size of</strong> the London underground, with 199 kilometres of route. However, it serves more people per year. While only third in terms of size, the Tokyo system <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is easily the most used</strong>, with 1,927 million passengers per year.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Of the three newer networks</strong>, the Washington DC underground is the most extensive, with 126 kilometres of route, compared to only 11 kilometres and 28 kilometres for the Kyoto and Los Angeles systems. The Los Angeles network is the newest, having opened in 2001, while the Kyoto network is the smallest and serves only 45 million passengers per year.","phrases":[{"en":"serve significantly more passengers than","context":"It is clear that the three oldest underground systems are larger and serve significantly more passengers than the newer systems."},{"en":"half the size of","context":"The second largest system, in Paris, is only about half the size of the London underground, with 199 kilometres of route."},{"en":"is easily the most used","context":"While only third in terms of size, the Tokyo system is easily the most used, with 1,927 million passengers per year."},{"en":"Of the three newer networks","context":"Of the three newer networks, the Washington DC underground is the most extensive, with 126 kilometres of route, compared to only 11 kilometres and 28 kilometres for the Kyoto and Los Angeles systems."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-052-1"]},"_displayNo":"#063","summary":"Table below gives information about the underground railway systems in six…"},{"id":"writing-academic-067","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Transport","title":"UK commuting trends","prompt":"The graph below shows the average number of UK commuters travelling each day by car, bus or train between 1970 and 2030.","answer":"The line graph compares figures for daily travel by workers in the UK using three different forms of transport over a period of 60 years.\n\nIt is clear that the car is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">by far the most popular means of transport</strong> for UK commuters throughout the period shown. Also, while the numbers of people who use the car and train increase gradually, the number of bus users <strong class=\"key-phrase\">falls steadily</strong>.\n\nIn 1970, around 5 million UK commuters travelled by car on a daily basis, while the bus and train were used by about 4 million and 2 million people respectively. In the year 2000, the number of those driving to work rose to 7 million and the number of commuting rail passengers reached 3 million. However, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">there was a small drop of approximately 0.5 million</strong> in the number of bus users.\n\nBy 2030, the number of people who commute by car is expected to reach almost 9 million, and the number of train users is also predicted to rise, to nearly 5 million. By contrast, buses <strong class=\"key-phrase\">are predicted to become a less popular choice</strong>, with only 3 million daily users.","phrases":[{"en":"falls steadily","context":"It is clear that the car is by far the most popular means of transport for UK commuters throughout the period shown. Also, while the numbers of people who use the car and train increase gradually, the number of bus users falls steadily."},{"en":"by far the most popular means of transport","context":"It is clear that the car is by far the most popular means of transport for UK commuters throughout the period shown. Also, while the numbers of people who use the car and train increase gradually, the number of bus users falls steadily."},{"en":"there was a small drop of approximately 0.5 million","context":"However, there was a small drop of approximately 0.5 million in the number of bus users."},{"en":"are predicted to become a less popular choice","context":"By contrast, buses are predicted to become a less popular choice, with only 3 million daily users."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-067-1"]},"_displayNo":"#064","summary":"Graph below shows the average number of UK commuters travelling each day by…"},{"id":"writing-academic-069","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Transport","title":"Cycling to work in Britain","prompt":"The table shows changes in the number of residents cycling to work in selected areas of the United Kingdom between 2001 and 2011.","answer":"The table compares the numbers of people who cycled to work in twelve areas of the UK in the years 2001 and 2011.\n\nOverall, the number of UK commuters who travelled to work by bicycle <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rose considerably</strong> over the 10-year period. Inner London <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had by far the highest number</strong> of cycling commuters in both years.\n\nIn 2001, well over 43 thousand residents of inner London commuted by bicycle, and this figure rose to more than 106 thousand in 2011, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an increase of 144%</strong>. By contrast, although outer London had the second highest number of cycling commuters in each year, the percentage change, at only 45%, was the lowest of the twelve areas shown in the table.\n\nBrighton and Hove <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recorded the second biggest increase</strong> (109%) in the number of residents cycling to work, but Bristol was the UK's second city in terms of total numbers of cycling commuters, with 8,108 in 2001 and 15,768 in 2011. Figures for the other eight areas <strong class=\"key-phrase\">were below the 10 thousand mark</strong> in both years.","phrases":[{"en":"rose considerably","context":"Overall, the number of UK commuters who travelled to work by bicycle rose considerably over the 10-year period. Inner London had by far the highest number of cycling commuters in both years."},{"en":"had by far the highest number","context":"Overall, the number of UK commuters who travelled to work by bicycle rose considerably over the 10-year period. Inner London had by far the highest number of cycling commuters in both years."},{"en":"an increase of 144%","context":"In 2001, well over 43 thousand residents of inner London commuted by bicycle, and this figure rose to more than 106 thousand in 2011, an increase of 144%."},{"en":"recorded the second biggest increase","context":"Brighton and Hove recorded the second biggest increase (109%) in the number of residents cycling to work, but Bristol was the UK's second city in terms of total numbers of cycling commuters, with 8,108 in 2001 and 15,768 in 2011."},{"en":"were below the 10 thousand mark","context":"Figures for the other eight areas were below the 10 thousand mark in both years."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-069-1"]},"_displayNo":"#065","summary":"Table below shows changes in the numbers of residents cycling to work in"},{"id":"writing-academic-034","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Water & Energy","title":"Global water use","prompt":"The graph and table below give information about water use worldwide and water consumption in two different countries.","answer":"The charts compare the amount of water used for agriculture, industry and homes around the world, and water use in Brazil and the Democratic Republic of Congo.\n\nIt is clear that global water needs <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rose significantly</strong> between 1900 and 2000, and that agriculture <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accounted for the largest proportion of</strong> water used. We can also see that water consumption was considerably higher in Brazil than in the Congo.\n\nIn 1900, around 500km³ of water was used by the agriculture sector worldwide. The figures for industrial and domestic water consumption <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stood at around one fifth of that amount</strong>. By 2000, global water use for agriculture <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had increased to around 3000km³</strong>, industrial water use had risen to just under half that amount, and domestic consumption had reached approximately 500km³.\n\nIn the year 2000, the populations of Brazil and the Congo were 176 million and 5.2 million respectively. Water consumption per person in Brazil, at 359m³, was much higher than that in the Congo, at only 8m³, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">this could be explained by the fact that</strong> Brazil had 265 times more irrigated land.","phrases":[{"en":"rose significantly","context":"It is clear that global water needs rose significantly between 1900 and 2000, and that agriculture accounted for the largest proportion of water used. We can also see that water consumption was considerably higher in Brazil than in the Congo."},{"en":"accounted for the largest proportion of","context":"It is clear that global water needs rose significantly between 1900 and 2000, and that agriculture accounted for the largest proportion of water used. We can also see that water consumption was considerably higher in Brazil than in the Congo."},{"en":"this could be explained by the fact that","context":"Water consumption per person in Brazil, at 359m³, was much higher than that in the Congo, at only 8m³, and this could be explained by the fact that Brazil had 265 times more irrigated land."},{"en":"stood at around one fifth of that amount","context":"The figures for industrial and domestic water consumption stood at around one fifth of that amount."},{"en":"had increased to around 3000km³","context":"By 2000, global water use for agriculture had increased to around 3000km³, industrial water use had risen to just under half that amount, and domestic consumption had reached approximately 500km³."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-034-1"]},"_displayNo":"#066","summary":"Graph and table below give information about water use worldwide and water…"},{"id":"writing-academic-036","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Water & Energy","title":"Water use by sector","prompt":"The pie charts compare water use in San Diego County, California and worldwide.","answer":"The pie charts give information about the water used for residential, industrial and agricultural purposes in San Diego County, California, and the world as a whole.\n\nIt is noticeable that more water is consumed by homes than by industry or agriculture in the two American regions. By contrast, agriculture <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accounts for the vast majority of water used worldwide</strong>.\n\nIn San Diego County and California State, residential water consumption accounts for 60% and 39% of total water usage. By contrast, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a mere 8% of the water used globally</strong> goes to homes. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The opposite trend can be seen</strong> when we look at water consumption for agriculture. This accounts for a massive 69% of global water use, but only 17% and 28% of water usage in San Diego and California respectively.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Such dramatic differences are not seen</strong> when we compare the figures for industrial water use. The same proportion of water (23%) is used by industry in San Diego and worldwide, while the figure for California is 10% higher, at 33%.","phrases":[{"en":"accounts for the vast majority of water used worldwide","context":"It is noticeable that more water is consumed by homes than by industry or agriculture in the two American regions. By contrast, agriculture accounts for the vast majority of water used worldwide."},{"en":"a mere 8% of the water used globally","context":"By contrast, a mere 8% of the water used globally goes to homes."},{"en":"The opposite trend can be seen","context":"The opposite trend can be seen when we look at water consumption for agriculture."},{"en":"Such dramatic differences are not seen","context":"Such dramatic differences are not seen when we compare the figures for industrial water use. The same proportion of water (23%) is used by industry in San Diego and worldwide, while the figure for California is 10% higher, at 33%."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-036-1"]},"_displayNo":"#067","summary":"Pie charts below compare water usage in San Diego, California and"},{"id":"writing-academic-063","section":"writing-academic","topic":"Water & Energy","title":"Hospital electricity use","prompt":"The charts below compare five different countries, showing the electricity used (measured in megawatts or mw) in a typical hospital during one week, and also the number of emergency medical calls made by local residents during the same week. The last chart shows the average time (in minutes) spent on assessing emergency cases by hospital medical staff before the patients are treated. Write a report summarising the information. Select and describe the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","answer":"The three charts compare the amount of electricity used in a typical hospital, the number of emergency calls made by local residents, and the time spent assessing patients before treatment, in five countries during one week.\n\nOverall, it seems that electricity use may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reflect an increase in</strong> emergency calls made, but only in Russia does assessment time increase in the same way.\n\nRegarding electricity, we see that consumption in the French and Japanese hospitals is roughly stable during the week, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fluctuating around 32 and 18 mw respectively</strong>. In Canada and Russia, electricity use <strong class=\"key-phrase\">climbs dramatically</strong> towards the end of the week, going from 26 to 38 mw and from 12 to 24 mw. In Argentina, however, the use actually declines by almost half.\n\nThere is a similar pattern in the number of emergency calls made, which fluctuate in France (around 110 calls) and Japan (around 90). In Canada these calls rise by roughly half, from around 105 on Monday to over 160 on Sunday, with the figure in Russia <strong class=\"key-phrase\">showing a similar growth to 110 by the weekend</strong>. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Argentina is again the exception</strong>, with a decline of about half.\n\nBy contrast, time spent on patient assessment is generally stable during the week in all the countries except Russia, where there is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a marked rise</strong> from Monday's 12 minutes (which is the typical daily rate of each country except Japan) to 28 minutes on Sunday.","phrases":[{"en":"climbs dramatically","context":"In Canada and Russia, electricity use climbs dramatically towards the end of the week, going from 26 to 38 mw and from 12 to 24 mw."},{"en":"a marked rise","context":"By contrast, time spent on patient assessment is generally stable during the week in all the countries except Russia, where there is a marked rise from Monday's 12 minutes (which is the typical daily rate of each country except Japan) to 28 minutes on Sunday."},{"en":"reflect an increase in","context":"Overall, it seems that electricity use may reflect an increase in emergency calls made, but only in Russia does assessment time increase in the same way."},{"en":"fluctuating around 32 and 18 mw respectively","context":"Regarding electricity, we see that consumption in the French and Japanese hospitals is roughly stable during the week, fluctuating around 32 and 18 mw respectively."},{"en":"showing a similar growth to 110 by the weekend","context":"In Canada these calls rise by roughly half, from around 105 on Monday to over 160 on Sunday, with the figure in Russia showing a similar growth to 110 by the weekend."},{"en":"Argentina is again the exception","context":"Argentina is again the exception, with a decline of about half."}],"subtype":"Academic Task 1","visual":{"images":["writing-academic-063-1","writing-academic-063-2"]},"_displayNo":"#068","summary":"Charts below compare five different countries, showing the electricity used…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-001","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Are advertising methods unethical?","prompt":"Some of the methods used in advertising are unethical and unacceptable in today's society. To what extent do you agree with this view?","answer":"Advertising is indispensable to a market economy because it informs consumers and allows firms to compete. Nevertheless, I largely agree that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a significant proportion of</strong> modern advertising is ethically unacceptable, particularly when it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">conceals commercial intent</strong> or targets people who cannot make fully informed choices.\n\nThe first concern is that digital advertising can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">blur the boundary between persuasion and manipulation</strong>. Online platforms collect extensive behavioural data and use it to predict <strong class=\"key-phrase\">when an individual is most receptive to a message</strong>. Consumers may therefore see personalised claims repeatedly without understanding why they have been selected. This practice can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exploit informational asymmetry</strong>: advertisers know far more about the audience than the audience knows about the campaign. The problem is not merely that people are persuaded to buy unnecessary goods, but that their attention and personal data are used without meaningful consent.\n\nA second ethical problem arises when advertising is directed at children, people in financial difficulty or those with health-related anxieties. Such groups may be less able to assess exaggerated promises or long-term consequences. Promotions for high-interest credit, gambling or unproven health products can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disproportionately affect vulnerable consumers</strong>, even when the wording is technically legal. In these cases, commercial freedom should not outweigh <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the duty to prevent foreseeable harm</strong>.\n\nThis does not mean that all advertising is objectionable. Clear product information, verifiable comparisons and creative brand communication can help consumers make better decisions. However, these practices must be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">subject to meaningful oversight</strong>, including transparent data rules, strict protection for minors and penalties for misleading claims.\n\nIn conclusion, advertising remains legitimate, but methods based on covert tracking, manipulation or exploitation are ethically unacceptable. Strong regulation is therefore necessary to preserve both <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consumer autonomy</strong> and public trust.","phrases":[{"en":"a significant proportion of","context":"Nevertheless, I largely agree that a significant proportion of modern advertising is ethically unacceptable, particularly when it conceals commercial intent or targets people who cannot make fully informed choices."},{"en":"blur the boundary between persuasion and manipulation","context":"The first concern is that digital advertising can blur the boundary between persuasion and manipulation."},{"en":"exploit informational asymmetry","context":"This practice can exploit informational asymmetry: advertisers know far more about the audience than the audience knows about the campaign."},{"en":"disproportionately affect vulnerable consumers","context":"Promotions for high-interest credit, gambling or unproven health products can disproportionately affect vulnerable consumers, even when the wording is technically legal."},{"en":"subject to meaningful oversight","context":"However, these practices must be subject to meaningful oversight, including transparent data rules, strict protection for minors and penalties for misleading claims."},{"en":"consumer autonomy","context":"In conclusion, advertising remains legitimate, but methods based on covert tracking, manipulation or exploitation are ethically unacceptable. Strong regulation is therefore necessary to preserve both consumer autonomy and public trust."},{"en":"conceals commercial intent","context":"Nevertheless, I largely agree that a significant proportion of modern advertising is ethically unacceptable, particularly when it conceals commercial intent or targets people who cannot make fully informed choices."},{"en":"when an individual is most receptive to a message","context":"Online platforms collect extensive behavioural data and use it to predict when an individual is most receptive to a message."},{"en":"the duty to prevent foreseeable harm","context":"In these cases, commercial freedom should not outweigh the duty to prevent foreseeable harm."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#001","summary":"Are unethical advertising methods acceptable?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-101","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Does advertising promote quantity over quality?","prompt":"Advertising encourages consumers to buy in quantity rather than in quality. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that advertising persuades people to buy as much as possible rather than to buy well. I largely agree with this view, although the criticism applies to some kinds of advertising far more than to others.\n\nMost advertising is designed to increase the volume of sales, and its methods reflect that aim. Campaigns emphasise price reductions, multi-buy offers and limited-time deals, all of which encourage people to purchase more than they intended. Fashion and technology advertising works differently but towards the same end, by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">presenting last year's perfectly functional clothes or phones as embarrassing</strong>, so that consumers replace items that are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nowhere near worn out</strong>. The result is visible in the quantity of clothing, electronics and food that households throw away every year.\n\nAdvertising also tends to avoid the information that would allow a genuine judgement of quality. Very few advertisements mention <strong class=\"key-phrase\">durability, repairability or running costs</strong>, because these do not encourage frequent repurchase, and attention is directed instead towards image, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">celebrity endorsement</strong> and lifestyle.\n\nI would accept that this is not universal. Advertising for cars, insurance and household appliances often does compare specifications, safety ratings and reliability, because buyers of expensive items research them carefully. Competition in these markets has arguably raised standards, since a manufacturer with a poor reliability record <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot conceal it</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that most advertising promotes consumption rather than careful choice, and that this contributes to considerable waste. The exception is expensive goods, where consumers demand real information and advertisers are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">obliged to provide it</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"nowhere near worn out","context":"Fashion and technology advertising works differently but towards the same end, by presenting last year's perfectly functional clothes or phones as embarrassing, so that consumers replace items that are nowhere near worn out."},{"en":"celebrity endorsement","context":"Very few advertisements mention durability, repairability or running costs, because these do not encourage frequent repurchase, and attention is directed instead towards image, celebrity endorsement and lifestyle."},{"en":"obliged to provide it","context":"In conclusion, I agree that most advertising promotes consumption rather than careful choice, and that this contributes to considerable waste. The exception is expensive goods, where consumers demand real information and advertisers are obliged to provide it."},{"en":"presenting last year's perfectly functional clothes or phones as embarrassing","context":"Fashion and technology advertising works differently but towards the same end, by presenting last year's perfectly functional clothes or phones as embarrassing, so that consumers replace items that are nowhere near worn out."},{"en":"durability, repairability or running costs","context":"Very few advertisements mention durability, repairability or running costs, because these do not encourage frequent repurchase, and attention is directed instead towards image, celebrity endorsement and lifestyle."},{"en":"cannot conceal it","context":"Competition in these markets has arguably raised standards, since a manufacturer with a poor reliability record cannot conceal it."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#002","summary":"Does advertising promote quantity over quality?"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Second-hand clothing among the young","prompt":"In recent years, there has been a rise in the popularity of second-hand clothing amongst the younger generation. Why is this happening? Do you think it's a positive or negative development?","answer":"Second-hand clothing has become increasingly popular among young people. The main reasons are lower prices, environmental concern and the desire to find distinctive items, and social media has made it easier than ever for young people to discover, buy and resell them. I see this as a largely positive development.\n\nUsed clothes are often much cheaper than new products, which matters to students and young workers with limited budgets, and online platforms have made buying and selling simple. Second-hand shops also offer unusual designs that are not available in ordinary chain stores, allowing buyers to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">build a personal style at a lower cost</strong>, and the trade gives charities and small local businesses a useful source of income.\n\nThe environmental benefit is equally important. Producing new clothes uses water, energy and raw materials, while fast fashion encourages people to discard items quickly. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Reusing garments keeps them in circulation for longer</strong> and reduces waste, and although second-hand shopping cannot solve every problem in the fashion industry on its own, it can meaningfully <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce demand for disposable clothing</strong>.\n\nThere are minor concerns about quality, hygiene and misleading online descriptions. These are manageable rather than serious. Clear photographs and descriptions, honest condition ratings and simple washing routines address most of them, and sellers who build a reliable reputation attract repeat buyers. For most shoppers the drawbacks are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a small price to pay for the savings and originality on offer</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the growth of second-hand fashion is positive because it makes clothing more affordable, supports individual style and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">encourages more responsible consumption</strong>.","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","phrases":[{"en":"Reusing garments keeps them in circulation for longer","context":"Reusing garments keeps them in circulation for longer and reduces waste, and although second-hand shopping cannot solve every problem in the fashion industry on its own, it can meaningfully reduce demand for disposable clothing."},{"en":"reduce demand for disposable clothing","context":"Reusing garments keeps them in circulation for longer and reduces waste, and although second-hand shopping cannot solve every problem in the fashion industry on its own, it can meaningfully reduce demand for disposable clothing."},{"en":"encourages more responsible consumption","context":"In conclusion, the growth of second-hand fashion is positive because it makes clothing more affordable, supports individual style and encourages more responsible consumption."},{"en":"build a personal style at a lower cost","context":"Second-hand shops also offer unusual designs that are not available in ordinary chain stores, allowing buyers to build a personal style at a lower cost, and the trade gives charities and small local businesses a useful source of income."},{"en":"a small price to pay for the savings and originality on offer","context":"For most shoppers the drawbacks are a small price to pay for the savings and originality on offer."}],"subtype":"positive","summary":"The rise of second-hand clothing among younger people","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-102","_displayNo":"#003"},{"id":"writing-task-2-104","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Does more advertising do more good than harm?","prompt":"Do the positive effects outweigh negative effects, when advertising gains in popularity?","answer":"Advertising has become almost impossible to avoid, and its growth is more often treated as a problem than as a benefit. In my view, although some of the criticism is deserved, the advantages of widespread advertising <strong class=\"key-phrase\">outweigh its drawbacks</strong>.\n\nThe clearest benefit is information. A consumer who has never heard of a product cannot buy it, and without advertising people would be limited to the few brands sold in their nearest shop. Comparison of prices, features and guarantees is what allows a buyer to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make an informed choice</strong>, and public prices also force sellers to compete rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">quietly charge more than their rivals</strong>. The frequent claim that advertising simply manipulates people is hard to sustain when most new products fail despite large campaigns.\n\nAdvertising also pays for a great deal that the public enjoys without noticing. Commercial television, radio, most news websites and nearly all free online services are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">funded by advertisers rather than by their audiences</strong>, and sport at every level depends on sponsorship. Without that income, either these services disappear or the public pays for them directly. By moving goods quickly, advertising also supports the steady production and employment on which economic development depends.\n\nThe strongest objection is that advertising persuades people to buy things they do not need, and that some of it rests on misleading claims about what a product will do. This is a real cost, but a manageable one. Most countries now regulate advertising directly, requiring evidence for health claims and banning tobacco promotion outright, and audiences are far more sceptical than they were: reviews written by other customers now influence a purchase more than the campaign that preceded it.\n\nIn conclusion, advertising does encourage spending, and it is not the neutral public service its defenders describe. Its growth nevertheless brings better information, sharper competition and the funding of services that audiences would otherwise pay for themselves, while regulation <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deals with its worst excesses</strong>. On balance, the positive effects are the greater.","phrases":[{"en":"outweigh its drawbacks","context":"Advertising has become almost impossible to avoid, and its growth is more often treated as a problem than as a benefit. In my view, although some of the criticism is deserved, the advantages of widespread advertising outweigh its drawbacks."},{"en":"make an informed choice","context":"Comparison of prices, features and guarantees is what allows a buyer to make an informed choice, and public prices also force sellers to compete rather than quietly charge more than their rivals."},{"en":"quietly charge more than their rivals","context":"Comparison of prices, features and guarantees is what allows a buyer to make an informed choice, and public prices also force sellers to compete rather than quietly charge more than their rivals."},{"en":"funded by advertisers rather than by their audiences","context":"Commercial television, radio, most news websites and nearly all free online services are funded by advertisers rather than by their audiences, and sport at every level depends on sponsorship."},{"en":"deals with its worst excesses","context":"Its growth nevertheless brings better information, sharper competition and the funding of services that audiences would otherwise pay for themselves, while regulation deals with its worst excesses."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#004","summary":"Do the positive effects outweigh negative effects, when advertising gains…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-111","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Advertising aimed at children","prompt":"A great deal of advertising is directed at children. Parents argue that children are misled by it, while advertisers claim that it is a useful source of information. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Children are exposed to an enormous amount of advertising, and there is disagreement about whether this is harmful. Parents generally regard it as manipulation, while advertisers describe it as information. In my opinion the parents' concerns are better founded.\n\nAdvertisers do have a case. Children are consumers, and advertisements tell them what exists, what it costs and where it can be bought. Campaigns for milk, dental care, cycle helmets and road safety have all improved children's habits, and older children are far more sceptical than adults assume: most teenagers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recognise exaggeration</strong> and dismiss it.\n\nThe objection, however, is that young children cannot. Below the age of about eight, children do not reliably understand that an advertisement is trying to sell them something, and they treat its claims as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">straightforward statements of fact</strong>. This makes it unreasonable to describe such advertising as information. Much of it also promotes products that are harmful in quantity, particularly sugary drinks, confectionery and fast food, and the rise in childhood obesity has followed it closely. A further problem is the pressure it places on families, since children who are told repeatedly that a toy or a brand of clothing is essential will demand it, and low-income parents are left choosing between <strong class=\"key-phrase\">debt and their child's disappointment</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I accept that advertising can inform older children, but the youngest are simply unable to evaluate what they are being shown. Advertising aimed at the under-tens, and all advertising for unhealthy food, should therefore be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tightly restricted</strong> rather than left to the industry to regulate itself.","phrases":[{"en":"recognise exaggeration","context":"Campaigns for milk, dental care, cycle helmets and road safety have all improved children's habits, and older children are far more sceptical than adults assume: most teenagers recognise exaggeration and dismiss it."},{"en":"straightforward statements of fact","context":"Below the age of about eight, children do not reliably understand that an advertisement is trying to sell them something, and they treat its claims as straightforward statements of fact."},{"en":"debt and their child's disappointment","context":"A further problem is the pressure it places on families, since children who are told repeatedly that a toy or a brand of clothing is essential will demand it, and low-income parents are left choosing between debt and their child's disappointment."},{"en":"tightly restricted","context":"Advertising aimed at the under-tens, and all advertising for unhealthy food, should therefore be tightly restricted rather than left to the industry to regulate itself."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#005","summary":"Advertising aimed at children"},{"id":"writing-task-2-147","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Should we follow fashion?","prompt":"Fashion is difficult to follow, and some people argue that we should not follow it but should instead wear what we like and feel comfortable in. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Some people argue that following fashion is pointless and that individuals should simply wear what they find comfortable and attractive. I largely agree with this view, although fashion serves purposes that its critics tend to overlook.\n\nThe case against following fashion is strong. Trends change deliberately and frequently, precisely so that clothes which are still perfectly wearable <strong class=\"key-phrase\">appear out of date</strong>, and consumers who attempt to keep up spend substantial sums replacing them. The environmental consequence is severe, since the clothing industry is among the largest producers of textile waste and industrial water pollution, and much of what is bought is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worn only a handful of times</strong>. Following trends closely also produces a kind of uniformity that is, ironically, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the very opposite of the individuality it claims to express</strong>.\n\nThere is a social cost as well. Young people in particular judge one another by clothing, and children from poorer families can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">excluded for wearing the wrong brand</strong>, which is one of the main arguments for school uniforms.\n\nNevertheless, clothing is not purely functional. What people wear communicates something about their personality, profession and mood, and taking care over appearance can genuinely improve confidence. Certain occasions, such as interviews and formal events, also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carry expectations that it would be naive to ignore</strong> entirely.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that people should dress according to their own taste and comfort rather than following each new trend. Clothing does communicate meaning, but that is an argument for choosing deliberately rather than for following fashion.","phrases":[{"en":"appear out of date","context":"Trends change deliberately and frequently, precisely so that clothes which are still perfectly wearable appear out of date, and consumers who attempt to keep up spend substantial sums replacing them."},{"en":"worn only a handful of times","context":"The environmental consequence is severe, since the clothing industry is among the largest producers of textile waste and industrial water pollution, and much of what is bought is worn only a handful of times."},{"en":"excluded for wearing the wrong brand","context":"There is a social cost as well. Young people in particular judge one another by clothing, and children from poorer families can be excluded for wearing the wrong brand, which is one of the main arguments for school uniforms."},{"en":"the very opposite of the individuality it claims to express","context":"Following trends closely also produces a kind of uniformity that is, ironically, the very opposite of the individuality it claims to express."},{"en":"carry expectations that it would be naive to ignore","context":"Certain occasions, such as interviews and formal events, also carry expectations that it would be naive to ignore entirely."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#006","summary":"Should we follow fashion?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-148","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Do high sales reflect advertising or real need?","prompt":"The high sales of popular consumer goods reflect the power of advertising rather than the real needs of the society in which they are sold. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that the popularity of consumer goods demonstrates the power of advertising rather than any genuine social need. I largely agree with this view, although advertising cannot create demand entirely on its own.\n\nThe evidence for the argument is easy to find. Many of the products that sell in the greatest quantities <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meet no need that previously existed</strong>. Bottled water is the clearest example, since it is sold at hundreds of times the price of tap water of comparable quality, and its market was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">created almost entirely by marketing</strong>. The annual replacement of phones and clothing follows the same pattern: the previous model still works perfectly, but advertising has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">redefined it as embarrassing</strong>. Enormous sums are involved, and companies would not continue spending them year after year if they did not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">measurably shift behaviour</strong>.\n\nAdvertising is also particularly effective where a product is difficult to judge. With cosmetics, supplements and branded clothing, buyers cannot easily assess quality, so image determines the decision, which is why these industries advertise most heavily.\n\nNevertheless, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the claim can be pushed too far</strong>. Advertising cannot sustain a product that people dislike, and expensive campaigns for genuinely poor products fail regularly. Consumers also buy a great many things, such as washing machines and medicines, simply because they solve real problems, and these would sell with or without promotion.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that advertising rather than necessity explains much of what sells in large quantities. It works, however, by directing existing desires rather than by inventing them from nothing.","phrases":[{"en":"created almost entirely by marketing","context":"Bottled water is the clearest example, since it is sold at hundreds of times the price of tap water of comparable quality, and its market was created almost entirely by marketing."},{"en":"redefined it as embarrassing","context":"The annual replacement of phones and clothing follows the same pattern: the previous model still works perfectly, but advertising has redefined it as embarrassing."},{"en":"meet no need that previously existed","context":"Many of the products that sell in the greatest quantities meet no need that previously existed."},{"en":"measurably shift behaviour","context":"Enormous sums are involved, and companies would not continue spending them year after year if they did not measurably shift behaviour."},{"en":"the claim can be pushed too far","context":"Nevertheless, the claim can be pushed too far."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#007","summary":"Do high sales reflect advertising or real need?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-151","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Does advertising make everyone the same?","prompt":"Some people argue that advertising is discouraging people from being different individuals, and makes people to be or seem to be the same. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Advertising often encourages people to buy the same popular products, but it does not completely remove individuality. I partly agree that advertising promotes conformity, especially through global brands and social media. However, consumers still interpret messages differently and can use products to express personal preferences.\n\nThe pressure to conform is real. Companies repeat images of the ideal home, body or lifestyle <strong class=\"key-phrase\">until these standards appear normal</strong>. Influencers then display the same clothes, phones and beauty products to millions of followers. Young consumers may purchase these items <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mainly to avoid feeling excluded</strong>. In this way, advertising can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turn social approval into a sales tool</strong> and narrow the range of choices that seem acceptable.\n\nNevertheless, people are not passive. Income, age, culture and personal values shape how they respond to a campaign. The same advertisement may attract one person and annoy another. Digital markets also make specialised products easier to find, so individuals can choose independent designers, niche music or goods linked to particular interests. Advertising may influence the options people notice, but it rarely determines every final decision.\n\nPlatforms and regulators can also require clear labels for paid promotion, helping audiences <strong class=\"key-phrase\">separate personal recommendation from commercial persuasion</strong>. It also discourages hidden commercial influence in everyday online content.\n\nIn conclusion, advertising contributes to similar tastes by promoting a limited set of highly visible lifestyles. Yet it cannot erase personal judgement or cultural difference. The best response is not to blame advertising for all conformity, but to improve media literacy and give consumers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a wider and more transparent range of choices</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"turn social approval into a sales tool","context":"In this way, advertising can turn social approval into a sales tool and narrow the range of choices that seem acceptable."},{"en":"a wider and more transparent range of choices","context":"The best response is not to blame advertising for all conformity, but to improve media literacy and give consumers a wider and more transparent range of choices."},{"en":"until these standards appear normal","context":"Companies repeat images of the ideal home, body or lifestyle until these standards appear normal."},{"en":"mainly to avoid feeling excluded","context":"Young consumers may purchase these items mainly to avoid feeling excluded."},{"en":"separate personal recommendation from commercial persuasion","context":"Platforms and regulators can also require clear labels for paid promotion, helping audiences separate personal recommendation from commercial persuasion. It also discourages hidden commercial influence in everyday online content."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#008","summary":"People argue that advertising is discouraging people from being different…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2206","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Is advertising unnecessary?","prompt":"If a product is good and it meets people's needs, people will buy it. So advertising is unnecessary and is no more than a form of entertainment. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Some people claim that a genuinely good product will sell itself, and that advertising is therefore pointless and serves only to amuse us. I disagree with this view, because advertising performs an essential informational role in a competitive market.\n\nThe argument that quality alone is enough ignores a simple problem: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consumers cannot buy what they do not know exists</strong>. Thousands of new products appear every year, and without advertising a small company producing an excellent item would have no way of reaching the public, while established brands would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep their customers by default</strong>. Advertising is what allows a new competitor to challenge a dominant firm, and this competition is what <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drives prices down and quality up</strong>.\n\nAdvertising also carries information that buyers genuinely need, such as price changes, safety warnings, new features and where a product can be obtained. Public information campaigns work in exactly the same way. Government advertising on smoking, road safety, vaccination and energy conservation has changed behaviour <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on a large scale</strong>, and nobody would describe these campaigns as entertainment.\n\nI would accept that some advertising is little more than amusement, and that a proportion of it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">manipulative or misleading</strong>. Advertisements aimed at young children, or those encouraging unhealthy eating and unnecessary debt, deserve criticism and firm regulation. However, the existence of bad advertising is an argument for controlling it, not for concluding that the whole activity is unnecessary.\n\nIn conclusion, I strongly disagree that good products sell themselves. Advertising informs consumers, allows new businesses to compete and supports campaigns that benefit the public, even though it clearly needs to be regulated.","phrases":[{"en":"keep their customers by default","context":"Thousands of new products appear every year, and without advertising a small company producing an excellent item would have no way of reaching the public, while established brands would keep their customers by default."},{"en":"on a large scale","context":"Government advertising on smoking, road safety, vaccination and energy conservation has changed behaviour on a large scale, and nobody would describe these campaigns as entertainment."},{"en":"manipulative or misleading","context":"I would accept that some advertising is little more than amusement, and that a proportion of it is manipulative or misleading."},{"en":"consumers cannot buy what they do not know exists","context":"The argument that quality alone is enough ignores a simple problem: consumers cannot buy what they do not know exists."},{"en":"drives prices down and quality up","context":"Advertising is what allows a new competitor to challenge a dominant firm, and this competition is what drives prices down and quality up."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#009","summary":"Is advertising unnecessary?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2237","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Do people simply copy each other?","prompt":"The tendency of human beings to copy one another is shown in the popularity of fashions in clothes and other consumer goods. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is often claimed that fashion proves how readily people imitate each other. I agree that imitation is a real force behind it, but I think the claim describes only part of what fashion is doing.\n\nThe evidence for copying is hard to deny. Trends spread outwards from a small group and are adopted almost simultaneously by millions who never made an independent judgement about whether a garment suited them, and the same pattern governs phones, cars and kitchens. This behaviour has an obvious explanation: belonging to a group has always been safer than standing outside it, so people read what others wear as a signal of which group they belong to and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dress to match it</strong>. Advertising works precisely because it exploits this.\n\nWhat the claim misses is that imitation is rarely blind. People copy selectively, choosing the styles of groups they wish to join and deliberately avoiding those they do not, which is why teenagers reject their parents' clothing rather than copying the nearest available model. Fashion is therefore a language for saying who one is, and using a shared language is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not the same as having no ideas</strong>.\n\nThere is a further limitation. Much of what looks like imitation is simply what is available, since a shopper can only choose from what shops have decided to stock that season. When a person buys the coat that everyone is wearing, the explanation may be limited choice rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a desire to fit in</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that fashion reveals a genuine tendency to imitate, since the speed at which trends spread cannot be explained any other way. It also reveals people using shared symbols to express difference, which makes fashion a more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deliberate act than the statement suggests</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"dress to match it","context":"This behaviour has an obvious explanation: belonging to a group has always been safer than standing outside it, so people read what others wear as a signal of which group they belong to and dress to match it."},{"en":"not the same as having no ideas","context":"Fashion is therefore a language for saying who one is, and using a shared language is not the same as having no ideas."},{"en":"a desire to fit in","context":"When a person buys the coat that everyone is wearing, the explanation may be limited choice rather than a desire to fit in."},{"en":"deliberate act than the statement suggests","context":"It also reveals people using shared symbols to express difference, which makes fashion a more deliberate act than the statement suggests."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#010","summary":"Whether the popularity of fashion shows a human tendency to imitate"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2242","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Are shoppers really unaffected by adverts?","prompt":"Some people find advertisements amusing or annoying and they are not influenced by them when they shop. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Many shoppers insist that advertising has no effect on them and that they buy purely on price and quality. I disagree, because this belief rests on a mistaken idea of how advertising is designed to work.\n\nThe claim assumes that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">influence means persuasion a person would notice</strong>. In fact most advertising aims at familiarity rather than argument. Repeated exposure <strong class=\"key-phrase\">makes a brand name feel safe</strong>, and a shopper facing two similar products under time pressure reaches for the one that seems familiar without ever forming a conscious opinion about it. This is precisely why companies spend on sponsorship and logos that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make no claim about the product at all</strong>.\n\nThe spending itself is the strongest evidence against the claim. Firms competing hard on cost would not commit billions annually to something that failed, and they measure the return closely enough to know when a campaign works. If consumers were genuinely unmoved, the industry would have collapsed long ago.\n\nI would accept a limited version of the argument. Advertising rarely makes anyone buy something they have no use for, and it is far less effective for large purchases such as a house or a car, where buyers research carefully and compare figures. Its real power lies in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deciding between near-identical options</strong>, which describes most everyday shopping.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree with the statement. Finding an advertisement irritating does not prevent it from working, since it operates through recognition rather than argument, and the people most confident that they are immune are often the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">least likely to notice its effect</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"make no claim about the product at all","context":"This is precisely why companies spend on sponsorship and logos that make no claim about the product at all."},{"en":"deciding between near-identical options","context":"Its real power lies in deciding between near-identical options, which describes most everyday shopping."},{"en":"least likely to notice its effect","context":"Finding an advertisement irritating does not prevent it from working, since it operates through recognition rather than argument, and the people most confident that they are immune are often the least likely to notice its effect."},{"en":"influence means persuasion a person would notice","context":"The claim assumes that influence means persuasion a person would notice."},{"en":"makes a brand name feel safe","context":"Repeated exposure makes a brand name feel safe, and a shopper facing two similar products under time pressure reaches for the one that seems familiar without ever forming a conscious opinion about it."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#011","summary":"Whether people are genuinely uninfluenced by advertising"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2253","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Why advertising persuades less","prompt":"Research indicates that nowadays some consumers are much less influenced by advertising than in the past. What do you think are the reasons for this? Do you think this is a positive or negative development?","answer":"Advertising appears to persuade less effectively than it did a generation ago, despite budgets that have never been larger. The explanation lies in how much more information buyers now have, and in my view the change is positive.\n\nThe main reason is that consumers can check claims. Before a purchase, a shopper can read hundreds of reviews from people who actually own the product, and a manufacturer's assurance that something is durable <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carries little weight against a thousand accounts of it breaking</strong>. Advertising has lost its old advantage of being the only available information. Sheer volume matters too: people who see several thousand advertisements a day stop noticing them, and the growth of ad-blocking and subscription services shows how far audiences will go to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">avoid being marketed to</strong>. Trust has fallen alongside attention, since decades of exaggeration, and the discovery that endorsements are frequently paid for, have taught buyers to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treat advertising as an interested party</strong> rather than as a source of facts.\n\nI regard this as a healthy development. When purchases are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">decided by evidence rather than by the size of a marketing budget</strong>, small firms with genuinely better products can compete against established brands that once won simply by buying more attention. Households also waste less on goods that fail to match their description.\n\nThere is a cost worth noting. Advertising funds newspapers and free services, and as its effectiveness falls, that funding weakens, which has already damaged local journalism in several countries.\n\nIn conclusion, consumers are harder to persuade because they can now verify claims and have learned to distrust the ones they cannot. This is positive overall, since it rewards firms that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compete on quality rather than on volume of advertising</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"avoid being marketed to","context":"Sheer volume matters too: people who see several thousand advertisements a day stop noticing them, and the growth of ad-blocking and subscription services shows how far audiences will go to avoid being marketed to."},{"en":"compete on quality rather than on volume of advertising","context":"In conclusion, consumers are harder to persuade because they can now verify claims and have learned to distrust the ones they cannot. This is positive overall, since it rewards firms that compete on quality rather than on volume of advertising."},{"en":"carries little weight against a thousand accounts of it breaking","context":"Before a purchase, a shopper can read hundreds of reviews from people who actually own the product, and a manufacturer's assurance that something is durable carries little weight against a thousand accounts of it breaking."},{"en":"treat advertising as an interested party","context":"Trust has fallen alongside attention, since decades of exaggeration, and the discovery that endorsements are frequently paid for, have taught buyers to treat advertising as an interested party rather than as a source of facts."},{"en":"decided by evidence rather than by the size of a marketing budget","context":"When purchases are decided by evidence rather than by the size of a marketing budget, small firms with genuinely better products can compete against established brands that once won simply by buying more attention."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#012","summary":"Why consumers are less influenced by advertising than in the past"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2317","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Does age decide how we shop?","prompt":"People's shopping habits depend more on the age group they belong to than on any other factor. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Marketing departments divide customers by age before anything else, and the differences between a teenager and a pensioner in a shop are obvious. I disagree that age is the strongest influence, because income and circumstance explain far more.\n\nAge clearly does shape behaviour. Younger buyers grew up with phones and expect to order at midnight and return items free, while older customers often prefer to handle goods and speak to a person. Each generation also carries the habits of the economy it grew up in: people who remember shortages repair things, and those raised in abundance replace them. Age further decides what is actually needed, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a student and a grandparent are not shopping for the same life</strong>.\n\nIncome is a stronger predictor, however. Two thirty-year-olds, one earning three times the other, will differ far more in what they buy, where and how often than a rich thirty-year-old and a rich sixty-year-old. Money determines whether a person can buy quality that lasts or must <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep replacing the cheapest version</strong>, and whether they can wait for a sale or must buy on credit today. Poverty produces recognisable habits at every age.\n\nOther factors also outrank age. Having a first child changes spending more sharply than any birthday. Where people live decides what is available, and culture, education and values explain why one shopper checks where a product came from and another checks only the price. Age itself is often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">standing in for these things</strong> rather than causing the behaviour.\n\nIn conclusion, age influences shopping in visible ways and is a useful rough guide. Wealth, family stage and location shape what people actually buy more powerfully, so treating age as the main factor <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mistakes a convenient label for a real cause</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"a student and a grandparent are not shopping for the same life","context":"Age further decides what is actually needed, since a student and a grandparent are not shopping for the same life."},{"en":"keep replacing the cheapest version","context":"Money determines whether a person can buy quality that lasts or must keep replacing the cheapest version, and whether they can wait for a sale or must buy on credit today."},{"en":"standing in for these things","context":"Age itself is often standing in for these things rather than causing the behaviour."},{"en":"mistakes a convenient label for a real cause","context":"In conclusion, age influences shopping in visible ways and is a useful rough guide. Wealth, family stage and location shape what people actually buy more powerfully, so treating age as the main factor mistakes a convenient label for a real cause."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#013","summary":"Whether age matters more than other factors in shaping shopping habits"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2379","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"A car, a television and a fridge for everyone","prompt":"It has been suggested that everyone in the world wants to own a car, a television and a fridge. Do the disadvantages of such a development outweigh the advantages?","answer":"Rising incomes in developing regions have brought hundreds of millions of households their first vehicle and their first refrigerator. I believe the disadvantages of universal ownership outweigh the advantages, though the benefits to individual families are genuine and should not be dismissed.\n\nThose benefits are easy to underestimate from a wealthy country. A refrigerator prevents food poisoning and waste, allows a family to buy in quantity and cheaply, and removes the daily journey to market that usually falls to women. A car brings work, hospitals and schools within reach of rural households, and a television carries news, education and language into homes that had neither. For the families concerned these are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improvements in health and opportunity rather than luxuries</strong>.\n\nThe difficulty appears at scale. Extending current levels of ownership to everybody would require materials and energy far beyond what the planet can supply, and cars are the clearest case: cities designed around them consume enormous space, produce most urban air pollution and become slower as ownership rises, so the promised freedom <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disappears in the traffic it creates</strong>. Manufacturing and running billions more appliances would add emissions at a point when they must be falling.\n\nThere is also a social cost. Products are now built to be replaced rather than repaired, so ownership generates a stream of waste, and much of it is exported to poorer countries. Households borrow to buy what they are told they need, and a definition of a good life measured by possessions leaves people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">perpetually short of the next item</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, these goods have improved the lives of families who obtained them, and refusing them to others would be indefensible. Universal ownership on the present model cannot be supplied, so the aim should be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the services these things provide rather than the objects themselves</strong>, through public transport, durable goods and shared infrastructure.","phrases":[{"en":"improvements in health and opportunity rather than luxuries","context":"For the families concerned these are improvements in health and opportunity rather than luxuries."},{"en":"disappears in the traffic it creates","context":"Extending current levels of ownership to everybody would require materials and energy far beyond what the planet can supply, and cars are the clearest case: cities designed around them consume enormous space, produce most urban air pollution and become slower as ownership rises, so the promised f..."},{"en":"perpetually short of the next item","context":"Households borrow to buy what they are told they need, and a definition of a good life measured by possessions leaves people perpetually short of the next item."},{"en":"the services these things provide rather than the objects themselves","context":"Universal ownership on the present model cannot be supplied, so the aim should be the services these things provide rather than the objects themselves, through public transport, durable goods and shared infrastructure."}],"subtype":"Advantages / Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#014","summary":"Whether the drawbacks of universal consumer ownership outweigh the benefits"},{"id":"writing-task-2-002","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Ageing","title":"Living longer, and the problems it brings","prompt":"In the developed world, average life expectancy is increasing. What problems will this cause for individuals and society? Suggest some measures that could be taken to reduce the impact of ageing populations.","answer":"It is true that people in industrialised nations can expect to live longer than ever before. Although there will undoubtedly be some negative consequences of this trend, societies can take steps to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mitigate these potential problems</strong>.\n\nAs people live longer and the populations of developed countries grow older, several related problems can be anticipated. The main issue is that there will obviously be more people of retirement age who will be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">eligible to receive a pension</strong>. The proportion of younger, working adults will be smaller, and governments will therefore receive less money in taxes in relation to the size of the population. In other words, an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ageing population</strong> will mean <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a greater tax burden for working adults</strong>. Further pressures will include a rise in the demand for healthcare, and the fact young adults will increasingly have to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look after their elderly relatives</strong>.\n\nThere are several actions that governments could take to solve the problems described above. Firstly, a simple solution would be to increase the retirement age for working adults, perhaps from 65 to 70. Nowadays, people of this age tend to be healthy enough to continue a productive working life. A second measure would be for governments to encourage immigration in order to increase the number of working adults who pay taxes. Finally, money from national budgets will need to be taken from other areas and spent on vital healthcare, accommodation and transport facilities for the rising numbers of older citizens.\n\nIn conclusion, various measures can be taken to tackle the problems that are certain to arise as the populations of countries grow older.","phrases":[{"en":"mitigate these potential problems","context":"It is true that people in industrialised nations can expect to live longer than ever before. Although there will undoubtedly be some negative consequences of this trend, societies can take steps to mitigate these potential problems."},{"en":"ageing population","context":"In other words, an ageing population will mean a greater tax burden for working adults."},{"en":"eligible to receive a pension","context":"The main issue is that there will obviously be more people of retirement age who will be eligible to receive a pension."},{"en":"a greater tax burden for working adults","context":"In other words, an ageing population will mean a greater tax burden for working adults."},{"en":"look after their elderly relatives","context":"Further pressures will include a rise in the demand for healthcare, and the fact young adults will increasingly have to look after their elderly relatives."}],"subtype":"Causes / Effects / Solutions","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#015","summary":"Ageing populations: problems and responses"},{"id":"writing-task-2-158","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Ageing","title":"An ageing population","prompt":"In many countries, the proportion of older people is increasing steadily. Is this a positive or a negative development for society?","answer":"In most developed countries, and increasingly in developing ones, the proportion of citizens over sixty-five is rising steadily. Although this trend reflects genuine progress, I believe its overall effect on society is negative unless governments respond to it seriously.\n\nThere are reasons to view the change positively. An ageing population is the direct result of better healthcare, safer workplaces and improved nutrition, so it is in one sense <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an achievement rather than a problem</strong>. Older citizens also contribute a great deal that never appears in economic statistics: they care for grandchildren, which allows younger parents to work, and they make up the majority of volunteers in charities, hospitals and community organisations.\n\nThe difficulties, however, are substantial. Pensions and healthcare are funded largely by taxes paid by working adults, so as the number of retired people grows while the workforce shrinks, each worker must <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carry a heavier burden</strong>. Demand for hospital beds, care homes and treatment for chronic illness rises sharply, and these are the most expensive services a state provides. Younger families face a further pressure, since many now support elderly relatives while raising children of their own.\n\nGovernments can reduce these problems by raising the retirement age, encouraging immigration and investing in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">preventive healthcare</strong>, but none of these measures is politically easy and few countries have acted decisively.\n\nIn conclusion, an ageing population is evidence of social success, yet the financial and practical strain it creates is severe. On balance I regard it as a negative development, and one that requires <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far more urgent attention</strong> than it currently receives.","phrases":[{"en":"an achievement rather than a problem","context":"An ageing population is the direct result of better healthcare, safer workplaces and improved nutrition, so it is in one sense an achievement rather than a problem."},{"en":"carry a heavier burden","context":"Pensions and healthcare are funded largely by taxes paid by working adults, so as the number of retired people grows while the workforce shrinks, each worker must carry a heavier burden."},{"en":"preventive healthcare","context":"Governments can reduce these problems by raising the retirement age, encouraging immigration and investing in preventive healthcare, but none of these measures is politically easy and few countries have acted decisively."},{"en":"far more urgent attention","context":"In conclusion, an ageing population is evidence of social success, yet the financial and practical strain it creates is severe. On balance I regard it as a negative development, and one that requires far more urgent attention than it currently receives."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","_displayNo":"#016","summary":"An ageing population"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2297","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Ageing","title":"Is growing old worse than it used to be?","prompt":"Some people say that in the modern world, getting old is entirely bad. Others believe that old people's lives nowadays are much better than in the past. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Old age is described either as a period of neglect in a society that values youth or as the most comfortable it has ever been. My own view is that the second description is closer to the truth in material terms, while the first identifies something real about status.\n\nThe pessimistic account has evidence behind it. Older people once held authority because they carried knowledge nobody else had, and in a world where skills change every few years that authority has gone; an experienced worker is now more likely to be regarded as expensive than as valuable. Families have dispersed, so the daily contact that once made age bearable has been replaced by occasional visits, and loneliness among the elderly is now common enough to appear in health statistics. Pensions are under pressure, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a longer life is not obviously a gift if the money runs out first</strong>.\n\nThe optimistic account rests on comparison. Life expectancy has risen by decades, and those years are healthier: hips are replaced, cataracts removed and heart conditions managed, so people remain active at seventy in ways their grandparents were not at fifty. State pensions and public health care, which barely existed a century ago, mean far fewer older people live in poverty. Technology keeps distant families in contact, and travel, study and second careers are now normal after retirement rather than eccentric.\n\nWeighing these, the material improvement is beyond argument, and few would exchange today's old age for that of a hundred years ago. What has genuinely worsened is position: the elderly are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comfortable but consulted less</strong>, and a society that treats them as a cost rather than a resource wastes them.\n\nIn conclusion, growing old today is healthier, longer and more secure than at any earlier time, so calling it entirely bad is wrong. The loss of respect and daily company is real, and that, rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the condition of the body</strong>, is what most needs repairing.","phrases":[{"en":"a longer life is not obviously a gift if the money runs out first","context":"Pensions are under pressure, and a longer life is not obviously a gift if the money runs out first."},{"en":"comfortable but consulted less","context":"What has genuinely worsened is position: the elderly are comfortable but consulted less, and a society that treats them as a cost rather than a resource wastes them."},{"en":"the condition of the body","context":"In conclusion, growing old today is healthier, longer and more secure than at any earlier time, so calling it entirely bad is wrong. The loss of respect and daily company is real, and that, rather than the condition of the body, is what most needs repairing."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#017","summary":"Whether old age today is entirely bad or better than in the past"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2372","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Ageing","title":"Who should pay for retirement?","prompt":"Some people believe governments should provide financial support for retired people, while others think individuals should save for their own retirement. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Populations are ageing quickly, and the question of who pays for the years after work is now urgent in most developed countries. Both positions contain something necessary, and my own view is that the two have to be combined rather than chosen between.\n\nThe argument for personal saving is partly arithmetic. State pensions are paid by current workers, and as the number of retired people rises relative to workers, that arrangement becomes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a promise the next generation cannot afford to keep</strong>. Private saving also gives individuals control, since money invested over forty years grows substantially, and people who save for themselves can retire when and how they choose. There is a fairness argument too, in that somebody who spent everything they earned has a weaker claim on those who did not.\n\nThe case for state support answers with what happens in practice. Many people cannot save at all: low wages, insecure work and long periods of caring for children or relatives leave nothing spare, and the individuals in that position are precisely those who will need help. Private saving is also risky, because a market collapse in the year before retirement can destroy decades of contributions, and nobody knows how long they will live. A society that leaves the old to their own resources ends up <strong class=\"key-phrase\">paying for them anyway through hospitals and emergency housing</strong>, at greater cost and after more suffering.\n\nThe systems that work best combine the two. A basic state pension guarantees that nobody falls into poverty, while workplace and private schemes, ideally with automatic enrolment, provide the additional income. Enrolling employees by default has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raised saving dramatically without compelling anybody</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, private saving is essential because state pensions alone will become unaffordable. It cannot stand on its own, so a guaranteed public minimum with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compulsory or automatic saving above it</strong> is the arrangement most likely to work.","phrases":[{"en":"a promise the next generation cannot afford to keep","context":"State pensions are paid by current workers, and as the number of retired people rises relative to workers, that arrangement becomes a promise the next generation cannot afford to keep."},{"en":"paying for them anyway through hospitals and emergency housing","context":"A society that leaves the old to their own resources ends up paying for them anyway through hospitals and emergency housing, at greater cost and after more suffering."},{"en":"raised saving dramatically without compelling anybody","context":"Enrolling employees by default has raised saving dramatically without compelling anybody."},{"en":"compulsory or automatic saving above it","context":"In conclusion, private saving is essential because state pensions alone will become unaffordable. It cannot stand on its own, so a guaranteed public minimum with compulsory or automatic saving above it is the arrangement most likely to work."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#018","summary":"Whether the state or the individual should fund old age"},{"id":"writing-task-2-003","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Is protecting wild animals a waste of resources?","prompt":"Wild animals have no place in the 21st century, so protecting them is a waste of resources. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Some people argue that it is pointless to spend money on the protection of wild animals because we humans have no need for them. I completely disagree with this point of view.\n\nIn my opinion, it is absurd to argue that wild animals have no place in the 21st century. I do not believe that planet Earth exists only for the benefit of humans, and there is nothing special about this particular century that means that we suddenly have the right to allow or encourage <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the extinction of any species</strong>. There is also no compelling reason why we should let animals die out. We do not need to exploit or destroy every last square metre of land in order to feed or accommodate the world's population. There is plenty of room for us to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exist side by side</strong> with wild animals, and this should be our aim.\n\nI also disagree with the idea that protecting animals is a waste of resources. It is usually the protection of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">natural habitats</strong> that ensures the survival of wild animals, and most scientists agree that these habitats are also crucial for human survival. For example, rainforests produce oxygen, absorb carbon dioxide and stabilise the Earth's climate. If we destroyed these areas, the costs of managing the resulting changes to our planet would far outweigh the costs of conservation. By protecting wild animals and their habitats, we maintain <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the natural balance of all life on Earth</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, we have no right to decide whether or not wild animals should exist, and I believe that we should do everything we can to protect them.","phrases":[{"en":"the extinction of any species","context":"I do not believe that planet Earth exists only for the benefit of humans, and there is nothing special about this particular century that means that we suddenly have the right to allow or encourage the extinction of any species."},{"en":"exist side by side","context":"There is plenty of room for us to exist side by side with wild animals, and this should be our aim."},{"en":"natural habitats","context":"It is usually the protection of natural habitats that ensures the survival of wild animals, and most scientists agree that these habitats are also crucial for human survival."},{"en":"the natural balance of all life on Earth","context":"By protecting wild animals and their habitats, we maintain the natural balance of all life on Earth."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#019","summary":"Protecting wild animals"},{"id":"writing-task-2-004","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Animal experiments for medicine and product safety","prompt":"Nowadays animal experiments are widely used to develop new medicines and to test the safety of other products. Some people argue that these experiments should be banned because it is morally wrong to cause animals to suffer, while others are in favour of them because of their benefits to humanity. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"It is true that medicines and other products are routinely tested on animals before they are cleared for human use. While I tend towards the viewpoint that animal testing is morally wrong, I would have to support a limited amount of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">animal experimentation</strong> for the development of medicines.\n\nOn the one hand, there are clear ethical arguments against animal experimentation. To use a common example of this practice, laboratory mice may be given an illness so that the effectiveness of a new drug can be measured. Opponents of such research argue that humans have no right to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">subject animals to this kind of trauma</strong>, and that the lives of all creatures should be respected. They believe that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the benefits to humans do not justify the suffering caused</strong>, and that scientists should use alternative methods of research.\n\nOn the other hand, reliable alternatives to animal experimentation may not always be available. Supporters of the use of animals in medical research believe that a certain amount of suffering on the part of mice or rats can be justified if human lives are saved. They argue that opponents of such research might feel differently if a member of their own families needed a medical treatment that had been developed through the use of animal experimentation. Personally, I agree with the banning of animal testing for non-medical products, but I feel that it may be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a necessary evil</strong> where new drugs and medical procedures are concerned.\n\nIn conclusion, it seems to me that it would be wrong to ban testing on animals for vital medical research until equally effective alternatives have been developed.","phrases":[{"en":"animal experimentation","context":"While I tend towards the viewpoint that animal testing is morally wrong, I would have to support a limited amount of animal experimentation for the development of medicines."},{"en":"subject animals to this kind of trauma","context":"Opponents of such research argue that humans have no right to subject animals to this kind of trauma, and that the lives of all creatures should be respected."},{"en":"the benefits to humans do not justify the suffering caused","context":"They believe that the benefits to humans do not justify the suffering caused, and that scientists should use alternative methods of research."},{"en":"a necessary evil","context":"Personally, I agree with the banning of animal testing for non-medical products, but I feel that it may be a necessary evil where new drugs and medical procedures are concerned."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#020","summary":"The ethics of animal experimentation"},{"id":"writing-task-2-005","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Animals in captivity","prompt":"Some people feel that it is always wrong to keep animals in captivity, for instance in zoos. Other people say that there are benefits for the animals and for humans. Discuss both sides of this debate, and give your personal view.","answer":"Zoos continue to be controversial, even now that they have evolved into ‘animal parks’ or similar facilities. Most people love animals, and there are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strong views on both sides</strong>. People opposed to zoos highlight the unnatural way in which animals live in such places. For example, captive animals do not need to hunt or raise offspring, and this means that they are not living as nature intended.\n\nThe anti-zoo lobby also argue that captivity damages animals psychologically, even when enclosures are spacious and close to a natural habitat. Stereotypic behaviour such as repetitive pacing, self-harm or feather-plucking is well documented in captive elephants, big cats and primates, and is rarely seen in the wild. Critics add that zoos contribute little to conservation in practice, since protecting habitat allows far larger, genuinely wild populations to survive than any enclosure can hold.\n\nThose who support zoos reply that, for many species, a zoo enclosure is safer than the wild itself. The Arabian oryx, for instance, was hunted to extinction in its natural range by the 1970s and survives today only because zoos had bred it in captivity; those animals were later reintroduced into protected desert reserves. Modern zoos increasingly operate as one part of a wider conservation system, combining breeding programmes, veterinary research and habitat protection to build up populations of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">endangered species</strong> that would otherwise disappear entirely.\n\nOverall, I feel that zoos provide a valuable service to society and the animal world, as they help to protect many species. This outweighs their possible disadvantages, as long as they are properly staffed and maintained.","phrases":[{"en":"strong views on both sides","context":"Most people love animals, and there are strong views on both sides."},{"en":"endangered species","context":"Modern zoos increasingly operate as one part of a wider conservation system, combining breeding programmes, veterinary research and habitat protection to build up populations of endangered species that would otherwise disappear entirely."}],"subtype":"Discussion","_displayNo":"#021","summary":"People feel that it is always wrong to keep animals in captivity, for…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-006","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Why conservation has failed","prompt":"Far too little has been done to prevent animals and plants from dying out, although people have been aware of the problem for a long time. Why have people failed to improve this situation? What could be done about it?","answer":"Although the loss of plant and animal species has been recognised for decades, remarkably little has been achieved. In my view this failure has two main causes, and there are practical steps that could improve the situation.\n\nThe first reason is economic. The activities that destroy habitats, such as logging, mining, intensive farming and industrial fishing, are extremely profitable and employ millions of people. A government that restricts them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">faces immediate opposition from companies and workers</strong>, whereas the benefits of conservation are distant and difficult to measure. The second reason is that responsibility is shared. Wildlife does not respect borders, so a country that protects a forest gains little if its neighbour clears one, and international agreements are usually voluntary and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rarely enforced</strong>. No individual government therefore feels the loss directly, and there is little pressure from voters, most of whom now live in cities and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never see the decline for themselves</strong>.\n\nThe most effective solution would be to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make destruction expensive</strong>. Taxing timber, palm oil and fish that are produced unsustainably, and refusing to import them, would change commercial behaviour far faster than appeals to conscience. Wealthy countries should also pay poorer ones directly to maintain rainforests, since it is unreasonable to expect a low-income nation to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">forgo income for a global benefit</strong> that everybody else will share. Protected areas need proper funding for rangers and monitoring as well, because reserves that exist only on paper achieve nothing.\n\nIn conclusion, conservation has failed mainly because destruction is profitable and responsibility is divided. Progress therefore depends on financial penalties for those who damage habitats, and on genuine payments to the countries that agree to protect them.","phrases":[{"en":"rarely enforced","context":"Wildlife does not respect borders, so a country that protects a forest gains little if its neighbour clears one, and international agreements are usually voluntary and rarely enforced."},{"en":"forgo income for a global benefit","context":"Wealthy countries should also pay poorer ones directly to maintain rainforests, since it is unreasonable to expect a low-income nation to forgo income for a global benefit that everybody else will share."},{"en":"make destruction expensive","context":"The most effective solution would be to make destruction expensive."},{"en":"faces immediate opposition from companies and workers","context":"A government that restricts them faces immediate opposition from companies and workers, whereas the benefits of conservation are distant and difficult to measure."},{"en":"never see the decline for themselves","context":"No individual government therefore feels the loss directly, and there is little pressure from voters, most of whom now live in cities and never see the decline for themselves."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Solutions","_displayNo":"#022","summary":"Why conservation has failed"},{"id":"writing-task-2-007","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Wildlife protection or helping the poor?","prompt":"Some people believe that money and time should be spent on protecting wild animals, while others argue that this money should be spent on populations living in the poorest areas of the world. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Some people argue that money spent protecting wild animals would be better used helping the world's poorest communities. In my opinion this presents a false choice, since the two aims usually depend on each other.\n\nThe case for prioritising people is emotionally powerful. Millions still lack clean water, basic healthcare and enough food, and it is difficult to justify spending on nature reserves while children die of preventable diseases. Human suffering is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">immediate and measurable</strong>, whereas the benefits of conservation are gradual and often invisible.\n\nHowever, this argument misunderstands where poverty comes from. The poorest communities are usually those most directly dependent on the natural environment: they fish, farm and gather fuel from it. When a forest is cleared or a river polluted, it is these families who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lose their livelihoods first</strong>, so environmental destruction is itself a major cause of poverty rather than a separate concern. Protecting a wetland that supplies fish and controls flooding is therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a form of poverty reduction</strong>.\n\nThere is also a practical point about scale. The sums spent on conservation worldwide are extremely small compared with military budgets, so redirecting them would make very little difference to global poverty. Conservation can also generate income directly, since wildlife tourism in countries such as Kenya and Costa Rica employs large numbers of local people.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that we must choose between wildlife and the poor. The most effective programmes protect habitats while paying the communities that live in them, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">serves both purposes at once</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"immediate and measurable","context":"Human suffering is immediate and measurable, whereas the benefits of conservation are gradual and often invisible."},{"en":"lose their livelihoods first","context":"When a forest is cleared or a river polluted, it is these families who lose their livelihoods first, so environmental destruction is itself a major cause of poverty rather than a separate concern."},{"en":"a form of poverty reduction","context":"Protecting a wetland that supplies fish and controls flooding is therefore a form of poverty reduction."},{"en":"serves both purposes at once","context":"In conclusion, I disagree that we must choose between wildlife and the poor. The most effective programmes protect habitats while paying the communities that live in them, which serves both purposes at once."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#023","summary":"Wildlife protection or helping the poor?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-008","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Animal experimentation","prompt":"Some people strongly oppose animal experimentation, believing that it causes suffering to animals, while others consider it necessary. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Testing on animals divides opinion sharply. Some regard it as unjustifiable cruelty, while others consider it a necessary part of medical progress. In my view it can be defended for medical research, but not for cosmetics.\n\nThe objection is straightforward and serious. Animals used in laboratories experience pain and confinement and, in most cases, are killed afterwards, and they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have no way of consenting</strong>. Critics also question the science, pointing out that a substance which is safe in a mouse may be dangerous in a human, so results <strong class=\"key-phrase\">do not always transfer</strong>. Alternatives now exist, including computer modelling and cultured human tissue, and these are improving rapidly.\n\nThe defence rests on consequences. Almost every major medical advance of the past century, including vaccines, insulin, antibiotics, anaesthetics and cancer treatments, depended at some stage on animal testing, and no country permits a new drug to be given to human volunteers without it. The alternatives, though promising, cannot yet reproduce the reactions of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a complete living system</strong>, so abandoning animal research now would mean either <strong class=\"key-phrase\">halting medical progress</strong> or testing untried compounds directly on people.\n\nThe distinction I would draw concerns purpose. A degree of suffering may be justified when the outcome is a treatment for a fatal illness. It cannot be justified for shampoo or make-up, which is why the European Union has banned cosmetic testing altogether.\n\nIn conclusion, animal experimentation is acceptable for essential medical research under strict regulation, but it should be prohibited wherever <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a non-animal method exists</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"do not always transfer","context":"Critics also question the science, pointing out that a substance which is safe in a mouse may be dangerous in a human, so results do not always transfer."},{"en":"a complete living system","context":"The alternatives, though promising, cannot yet reproduce the reactions of a complete living system, so abandoning animal research now would mean either halting medical progress or testing untried compounds directly on people."},{"en":"a non-animal method exists","context":"In conclusion, animal experimentation is acceptable for essential medical research under strict regulation, but it should be prohibited wherever a non-animal method exists."},{"en":"have no way of consenting","context":"Animals used in laboratories experience pain and confinement and, in most cases, are killed afterwards, and they have no way of consenting."},{"en":"halting medical progress","context":"The alternatives, though promising, cannot yet reproduce the reactions of a complete living system, so abandoning animal research now would mean either halting medical progress or testing untried compounds directly on people."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#024","summary":"Animal experimentation"},{"id":"writing-task-2-103","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"The advantages and disadvantages of keeping pets","prompt":"Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of keeping pets, and give your own opinion.","answer":"Keeping animals in the home is common in most societies, but opinions differ about whether it is a good idea. In my view the benefits usually outweigh the drawbacks, provided the owner is genuinely able to look after the animal.\n\nThe advantages are mainly emotional and social. Pets provide company, which matters enormously to elderly people living alone and to those recovering from illness, and there is good evidence that owning a dog lowers stress and encourages daily exercise. For children, caring for an animal is one of the few opportunities they have to take real responsibility: feeding, cleaning and exercising a pet teaches routine, patience and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an early understanding of illness and death</strong>.\n\nThere are, however, genuine disadvantages. Animals are expensive, since food, vaccinations and veterinary treatment cost a considerable amount over a lifetime, and many owners <strong class=\"key-phrase\">underestimate this</strong>. They also restrict freedom, because a dog cannot be left alone for long and travel becomes complicated. The more serious problem is that pets are frequently acquired <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on impulse</strong> and then abandoned, which is why animal shelters in most cities are full, and certain breeds present a real danger to children if they are poorly trained.\n\nIn my opinion these objections are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">arguments for responsible ownership rather than against pets in general</strong>. Anyone who cannot afford veterinary care, or who is out of the house all day, should not take an animal on.\n\nIn conclusion, pets bring significant benefits to health, companionship and children's development, and the disadvantages arise mainly when people acquire an animal <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without considering the commitment involved</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"underestimate this","context":"Animals are expensive, since food, vaccinations and veterinary treatment cost a considerable amount over a lifetime, and many owners underestimate this."},{"en":"on impulse","context":"The more serious problem is that pets are frequently acquired on impulse and then abandoned, which is why animal shelters in most cities are full, and certain breeds present a real danger to children if they are poorly trained."},{"en":"without considering the commitment involved","context":"In conclusion, pets bring significant benefits to health, companionship and children's development, and the disadvantages arise mainly when people acquire an animal without considering the commitment involved."},{"en":"an early understanding of illness and death","context":"For children, caring for an animal is one of the few opportunities they have to take real responsibility: feeding, cleaning and exercising a pet teaches routine, patience and an early understanding of illness and death."},{"en":"arguments for responsible ownership rather than against pets in general","context":"In my opinion these objections are arguments for responsible ownership rather than against pets in general. Anyone who cannot afford veterinary care, or who is out of the house all day, should not take an animal on."}],"subtype":"Advantages & Disadvantages","_displayNo":"#025","summary":"The advantages and disadvantages of keeping pets"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2209","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Preventing the extinction of species","prompt":"It is a natural process for animal species to become extinct. There is no reason why people should try to prevent this from happening. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes suggested that because species have always died out, there is no point in trying to prevent extinctions today. I strongly disagree with this argument, because the extinctions now taking place are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">neither natural in origin nor natural in speed</strong>.\n\nThe premise itself is misleading. It is true that species disappeared long before humans existed, but the current rate of loss is estimated to be tens or even hundreds of times higher than the natural background rate. This is not the result of gradual evolutionary change; it is caused directly by deforestation, industrial fishing, pollution, hunting and climate change. Describing a process that we ourselves have created as \"natural\" simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">avoids responsibility for it</strong>.\n\nThere are also strong practical reasons to act. Ecosystems depend on the relationships between species, and removing one can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trigger a chain of further collapses</strong>. The decline of bees and other pollinating insects, for example, threatens a large proportion of the world's food crops. A great many medicines were originally derived from wild plants and animals, so every species lost <strong class=\"key-phrase\">may represent a treatment that will now never be found</strong>.\n\nFinally, there is a moral dimension. Humans have acquired the power to destroy other forms of life on a global scale, and with that power comes an obligation. Future generations are entitled to inherit a world that still contains elephants, tigers and coral reefs, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">we have no right to decide otherwise on their behalf</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, extinction caused by human activity is not a natural process, and I believe we have both a practical and a moral duty to prevent it.","phrases":[{"en":"avoids responsibility for it","context":"Describing a process that we ourselves have created as \"natural\" simply avoids responsibility for it."},{"en":"trigger a chain of further collapses","context":"Ecosystems depend on the relationships between species, and removing one can trigger a chain of further collapses."},{"en":"neither natural in origin nor natural in speed","context":"It is sometimes suggested that because species have always died out, there is no point in trying to prevent extinctions today. I strongly disagree with this argument, because the extinctions now taking place are neither natural in origin nor natural in speed."},{"en":"may represent a treatment that will now never be found","context":"A great many medicines were originally derived from wild plants and animals, so every species lost may represent a treatment that will now never be found."},{"en":"we have no right to decide otherwise on their behalf","context":"Future generations are entitled to inherit a world that still contains elephants, tigers and coral reefs, and we have no right to decide otherwise on their behalf."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#026","summary":"Preventing the extinction of species"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2292","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Is losing species the biggest problem?","prompt":"Some people say that the main environmental problem of our time is the loss of particular species of plants and animals. Others say there are more important environmental problems. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Environmental attention is divided between the disappearance of wildlife and problems such as warming, pollution and water shortage. Both sides describe something serious, and my own view is that the argument is less a ranking than a question of what causes what.\n\nThe case for treating species loss as central rests on permanence. A polluted river can be cleaned within a decade, but an extinct animal is gone for ever, and current rates of loss are far above anything in the geological record. Species also perform work that human beings depend on without paying for: pollination of crops, decomposition of waste and the regulation of pests. Losing enough of them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weakens the systems that produce our food</strong>, and since we understand very little about how these connections operate, each loss carries unknown consequences.\n\nThe opposing view argues from immediacy. Air pollution kills millions of people every year, unsafe water kills hundreds of thousands of children, and climate change is already producing failed harvests and displacement. These affect human beings now, and where budgets are limited it is difficult to justify spending on a rare bird rather than on clean water. There is also a practical objection: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a species protected on land that is drying out is not really protected</strong>.\n\nMy own position is that the second group of problems is more urgent while the first is more final, and they are not separate. Warming, pollution and habitat clearance are what is driving species loss, so extinction is largely a symptom, and treating the causes protects wildlife more effectively than any programme aimed at individual animals. That does not make conservation optional, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">some species will disappear before the causes are fixed</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the disappearance of species is the least reversible of our environmental problems. Because it is driven by climate change, pollution and land use, addressing those <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is also the most effective form of conservation</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"weakens the systems that produce our food","context":"Losing enough of them weakens the systems that produce our food, and since we understand very little about how these connections operate, each loss carries unknown consequences."},{"en":"a species protected on land that is drying out is not really protected","context":"There is also a practical objection: a species protected on land that is drying out is not really protected."},{"en":"some species will disappear before the causes are fixed","context":"That does not make conservation optional, since some species will disappear before the causes are fixed."},{"en":"is also the most effective form of conservation","context":"In conclusion, the disappearance of species is the least reversible of our environmental problems. Because it is driven by climate change, pollution and land use, addressing those is also the most effective form of conservation."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#027","summary":"Whether the loss of plants and animals outranks other environmental problems"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2312","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Do we still need animals for food and products?","prompt":"In the modern world, it is no longer necessary to use animals as food or for other products such as medicines and clothing. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Plant-based meat, synthetic fabrics and laboratory-grown materials have made it possible to live comfortably without animal products. I partly agree with the statement, since it is broadly true of food and clothing in wealthy countries, but it does not yet hold for medicine or for much of the world.\n\nFood is where the claim is strongest. Nutritionists accept that a planned vegetarian diet supplies everything the body needs, and the environmental argument is powerful: livestock occupies a vast share of farmland and produces a large share of greenhouse gases, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the same land feeds far more people when it grows crops</strong>. Clothing is similar, because warm and durable alternatives to wool and leather already exist.\n\nMedicine is a different matter. Insulin, vaccines and many cancer treatments were developed using animals, and no regulator anywhere will approve a new drug tested only on cells and computer models. That will change as those methods improve, but it has not changed yet, and pretending otherwise would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cost lives that could have been saved</strong>.\n\nThe claim also assumes conditions that do not exist everywhere. Herders on land too dry for crops have no alternative to their animals, and in many regions meat, milk and eggs remain the cheapest reliable source of protein for children. Substitutes are expensive and often unavailable, so what is a free choice in a rich city is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a matter of survival elsewhere</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, a person in a developed country can reasonably give up animal food and clothing today, and there are strong environmental reasons to do so. Calling animal use unnecessary everywhere ignores both medical reality and the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">communities whose diet depends on livestock</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"the same land feeds far more people when it grows crops","context":"Nutritionists accept that a planned vegetarian diet supplies everything the body needs, and the environmental argument is powerful: livestock occupies a vast share of farmland and produces a large share of greenhouse gases, so the same land feeds far more people when it grows crops."},{"en":"cost lives that could have been saved","context":"That will change as those methods improve, but it has not changed yet, and pretending otherwise would cost lives that could have been saved."},{"en":"a matter of survival elsewhere","context":"Substitutes are expensive and often unavailable, so what is a free choice in a rich city is a matter of survival elsewhere."},{"en":"communities whose diet depends on livestock","context":"Calling animal use unnecessary everywhere ignores both medical reality and the communities whose diet depends on livestock."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#028","summary":"Whether using animals for food, medicine and clothing is still necessary"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2335","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Protect every species or only some?","prompt":"Some people believe that all wild animals should be protected. Others say that only a few wild animals should be protected. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Conservation budgets are small and the number of threatened species is enormous, which forces an uncomfortable question about where the money goes. Both positions here are defensible, and my own view is that protecting habitats resolves most of the disagreement.\n\nThe case for protecting everything rests on how ecosystems actually work. Species are connected in ways that are rarely obvious until something is removed: insects pollinate crops, predators keep grazing animals from stripping grassland, and unremarkable plants hold soil in place. Choosing which creatures matter also assumes a knowledge we do not have, since medicines have repeatedly come from organisms nobody valued. There is a moral argument as well, in that a species which took millions of years to appear should not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disappear for the sake of a short-term convenience</strong>.\n\nSelective protection answers with arithmetic. There is not enough money to save everything, and spreading it thinly saves nothing, so it is better to concentrate on species whose loss would collapse an ecosystem or that can realistically be recovered. Some animals are already beyond saving in the wild, and funds spent on them are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taken from cases where the same money would work</strong>. Conservation also competes with schools and hospitals, which cannot always lose.\n\nMy own position is that the choice is largely a false one, because animals are rarely saved individually. Protecting a forest or a reef preserves thousands of species at once, most of which have never been catalogued, and doing so is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cheaper than any programme aimed at a single animal</strong>. Well-known species still deserve attention, since they attract the public support that funds everything else.\n\nIn conclusion, complete protection is beyond any budget and rigid selection risks losing species we do not yet understand. Concentrating on habitats protects the greatest number for the least money, and that is where the effort belongs.","phrases":[{"en":"disappear for the sake of a short-term convenience","context":"There is a moral argument as well, in that a species which took millions of years to appear should not disappear for the sake of a short-term convenience."},{"en":"taken from cases where the same money would work","context":"Some animals are already beyond saving in the wild, and funds spent on them are taken from cases where the same money would work."},{"en":"cheaper than any programme aimed at a single animal","context":"Protecting a forest or a reef preserves thousands of species at once, most of which have never been catalogued, and doing so is cheaper than any programme aimed at a single animal."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#029","summary":"Whether conservation should cover all wild animals or a selected few"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2363","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Is it too late to save species?","prompt":"It is true that some human activities have negative effects on plant and animal species. Some people say it is too late to address this problem, while others argue that effective measures can still be taken. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Species are disappearing at a rate not seen for millions of years, and opinions divide between resignation and the belief that action still works. Both readings have evidence behind them, and my own view is that the pessimism describes the past while the optimism describes what remains.\n\nThe pessimistic case is not unreasonable. Extinction is permanent, so everything already lost is beyond recovery, and the causes are still accelerating: forests are cleared, oceans warm and habitats fragment faster than protection can be arranged. Climate change adds a difficulty that conservation alone cannot answer, since a protected reserve is useless if the climate inside it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no longer suits the species it was built for</strong>. Political attention is also short, and governments that promise targets routinely miss them.\n\nThe opposing view rests on cases where intervention has worked. Whale populations have recovered since commercial hunting was banned, several birds of prey returned after pesticides were restricted, and protected marine areas refill with fish within a decade. Even severely reduced populations have been rebuilt, which shows that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">decline is not the same thing as inevitability</strong>. Renewable energy and improved farming are reducing the pressure at its source.\n\nMy own position is that the argument confuses two claims. It is genuinely too late to save what has already gone, and too late to prevent all future loss. It is not too late for the vast majority of species still living, and each degree of warming avoided and each forest protected <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changes how much is lost rather than whether anything is</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, some damage is irreversible and pretending otherwise helps nobody. Treating the whole problem as hopeless is a mistake, because the evidence shows recovery wherever pressure is removed, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">giving up guarantees the outcome that is feared</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"no longer suits the species it was built for","context":"Climate change adds a difficulty that conservation alone cannot answer, since a protected reserve is useless if the climate inside it no longer suits the species it was built for."},{"en":"decline is not the same thing as inevitability","context":"Even severely reduced populations have been rebuilt, which shows that decline is not the same thing as inevitability."},{"en":"changes how much is lost rather than whether anything is","context":"It is not too late for the vast majority of species still living, and each degree of warming avoided and each forest protected changes how much is lost rather than whether anything is."},{"en":"giving up guarantees the outcome that is feared","context":"In conclusion, some damage is irreversible and pretending otherwise helps nobody. Treating the whole problem as hopeless is a mistake, because the evidence shows recovery wherever pressure is removed, and giving up guarantees the outcome that is feared."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#030","summary":"Whether the damage to plants and animals can still be reversed"},{"id":"writing-task-2-009","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Arts & Museums","title":"Should the state fund artists?","prompt":"Some people think that governments should give financial support to creative artists such as painters and musicians. Others believe that creative artists should be funded by alternative sources. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People have different views about the funding of creative artists. While some people disagree with the idea of government support for artists, I believe that money for art projects should come from both governments and other sources.\n\nSome art projects definitely require help from the state. In the UK, there are many works of art in public spaces, such as streets or squares in city centres. In Liverpool, for example, there are several new statues and sculptures in the docks area of the city, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">which has been redeveloped recently</strong>. These artworks <strong class=\"key-phrase\">represent culture, heritage and history</strong>. They serve to educate people about the city, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">act as landmarks</strong> or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">talking points for visitors and tourists</strong>. Governments and local councils should pay creative artists to produce this kind of art, because without their funding our cities would be much less interesting and attractive.\n\nOn the other hand, I can understand the arguments against government funding for art. The main reason for this view is that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">governments have more important concerns</strong>. For example, state budgets need to be spent on education, healthcare, infrastructure and security, among other areas. These public services are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">vital for a country to function properly</strong>, whereas the work of creative artists, even in public places, is a luxury. Another reason for this opinion is that artists do a job like any other professional, and they should therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">earn their own money by selling their work</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, there are good reasons why artists should rely on alternative sources of financial support, but in my opinion government help is sometimes necessary.","phrases":[{"en":"act as landmarks","context":"They serve to educate people about the city, and act as landmarks or talking points for visitors and tourists."},{"en":"represent culture, heritage and history","context":"These artworks represent culture, heritage and history."},{"en":"earn their own money by selling their work","context":"Another reason for this opinion is that artists do a job like any other professional, and they should therefore earn their own money by selling their work."},{"en":"which has been redeveloped recently","context":"In Liverpool, for example, there are several new statues and sculptures in the docks area of the city, which has been redeveloped recently."},{"en":"talking points for visitors and tourists","context":"They serve to educate people about the city, and act as landmarks or talking points for visitors and tourists."},{"en":"governments have more important concerns","context":"The main reason for this view is that governments have more important concerns."},{"en":"vital for a country to function properly","context":"These public services are vital for a country to function properly, whereas the work of creative artists, even in public places, is a luxury."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#031","summary":"Public funding for creative artists"},{"id":"writing-task-2-033","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Arts & Museums","title":"Freedom of expression for artists","prompt":"Creative artists should be given freedom to express their ideas through words, pictures, music or films. Some people nevertheless think that the government should restrict artists’ freedom of expression. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Artists need freedom to explore difficult ideas, and governments should not control art simply because it is unpopular or uncomfortable. However, I do not believe that artistic freedom should be completely unlimited. Restrictions are justified when a work clearly encourages violence, hatred or serious harm.\n\nThe strongest argument for freedom is that art helps society examine ideas that ordinary public debate may avoid. A film, novel or painting can question accepted beliefs, expose injustice and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give a voice to people who are rarely heard</strong>: Orwell's Animal Farm and Nineteen Eighty-Four, for example, were banned for decades in the communist states their allegories targeted, precisely because fiction can say plainly what direct political speech cannot risk saying. If artists fear punishment whenever they challenge authority, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">creative work becomes safe but shallow</strong>. Society then loses an important source of critical reflection and cultural progress.\n\nNevertheless, freedom of expression does not remove responsibility. A work that directly incites violence against a group, deliberately exploits children or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">presents criminal behaviour as an instruction manual</strong> can cause real harm. In such cases, a narrow legal limit is reasonable. The key is that restrictions should be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">clearly defined and independently reviewed</strong>. Governments should not ban work merely because it criticises politicians, religion or social customs.\n\nIn practice, many disputes can be handled without banning a work. Age classifications, content warnings and restricted exhibition spaces allow adults to make informed choices while protecting children. This is usually preferable to censorship because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">responds to risk in a proportionate way</strong> and leaves room for serious discussion of controversial ideas.\n\nIn conclusion, artists should enjoy broad freedom because challenging art is valuable to an open society. Yet this freedom should stop where there is a clear and serious risk of harm. A system based on precise laws and independent courts would protect creative freedom without ignoring public safety.","phrases":[{"en":"clearly defined and independently reviewed","context":"The key is that restrictions should be clearly defined and independently reviewed."},{"en":"responds to risk in a proportionate way","context":"This is usually preferable to censorship because it responds to risk in a proportionate way and leaves room for serious discussion of controversial ideas."},{"en":"give a voice to people who are rarely heard","context":"A film, novel or painting can question accepted beliefs, expose injustice and give a voice to people who are rarely heard: Orwell's Animal Farm and Nineteen Eighty-Four, for example, were banned for decades in the communist states their allegories targeted, precisely because fiction can say plain..."},{"en":"creative work becomes safe but shallow","context":"If artists fear punishment whenever they challenge authority, creative work becomes safe but shallow."},{"en":"presents criminal behaviour as an instruction manual","context":"A work that directly incites violence against a group, deliberately exploits children or presents criminal behaviour as an instruction manual can cause real harm."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#032","summary":"Creative artists should be given freedom to express their ideas through…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-041","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Arts & Museums","title":"Public money and the arts","prompt":"Some people argue that governments should spend money on public services and facilities, but not on the arts. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that public money should be reserved for essential services such as healthcare, education and transport, and that the arts should receive none of it. I disagree with this view, because the arts deliver benefits that easily justify a modest share of public spending.\n\nThe argument for concentrating on services is understandable. Hospitals, schools and roads are used by everybody and meet needs that nobody can do without, whereas theatres, galleries and orchestras are attended mainly by a minority. When waiting lists are long and classrooms are overcrowded, subsidising an opera company is difficult to defend.\n\nHowever, this reasoning <strong class=\"key-phrase\">underestimates what the arts contribute</strong>. Museums and galleries are among the largest tourist attractions in most countries, and the visitors they bring support hotels, restaurants and transport, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">generating far more in taxation</strong> than the original subsidy costs. The arts are also an important part of school education, since drama, music and design build creativity and confidence in children who struggle with conventional subjects. Without public funding most of this would disappear, because commercial sponsors <strong class=\"key-phrase\">support only what is already popular</strong>.\n\nThere is a further point about scale. Arts budgets typically represent <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fraction of one per cent</strong> of national spending, so removing them would make no measurable difference to healthcare while destroying institutions that took generations to build.\n\nIn conclusion, essential services must always come first, but I disagree that the arts deserve nothing at all. They <strong class=\"key-phrase\">repay their cost</strong> economically and educationally, and the sums involved are very small.","phrases":[{"en":"generating far more in taxation","context":"Museums and galleries are among the largest tourist attractions in most countries, and the visitors they bring support hotels, restaurants and transport, generating far more in taxation than the original subsidy costs."},{"en":"a fraction of one per cent","context":"There is a further point about scale. Arts budgets typically represent a fraction of one per cent of national spending, so removing them would make no measurable difference to healthcare while destroying institutions that took generations to build."},{"en":"repay their cost","context":"In conclusion, essential services must always come first, but I disagree that the arts deserve nothing at all. They repay their cost economically and educationally, and the sums involved are very small."},{"en":"underestimates what the arts contribute","context":"However, this reasoning underestimates what the arts contribute."},{"en":"support only what is already popular","context":"Without public funding most of this would disappear, because commercial sponsors support only what is already popular."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#033","summary":"Public money and the arts"},{"id":"writing-task-2-137","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Arts & Museums","title":"Museums: to entertain or to educate?","prompt":"Some people think that museums should be enjoyable places to entertain people, while others believe that the purpose of museums is to educate. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People have different views about the role and function of museums. In my opinion, museums can and should be both entertaining and educational.\n\nOn the one hand, it can be argued that the main role of a museum is to entertain. Museums are tourist attractions, and their aim is to exhibit a collection of interesting objects that many people will want to see. The average visitor may become bored if he or she has to read or listen to too much educational content, so museums often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put more of an emphasis on</strong> enjoyment rather than learning. This type of museum is designed to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">visually spectacular</strong>, and may have interactive activities or even games as part of its exhibitions.\n\nOn the other hand, some people argue that museums should focus on education. The aim of any exhibition should be to teach visitors something that they did not previously know. Usually this means that the history behind the museum's exhibits needs to be explained, and this can be done in various ways. Some museums employ professional guides to talk to their visitors, while other museums offer headsets so that visitors can listen to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">detailed commentary about the exhibition</strong>. In this way, museums can play an important role in teaching people about history, culture, science and many other aspects of life.\n\nIn conclusion, it seems to me that a good museum should be able to offer an interesting, enjoyable and educational experience so that people can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have fun and learn something at the same time</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"put more of an emphasis on","context":"The average visitor may become bored if he or she has to read or listen to too much educational content, so museums often put more of an emphasis on enjoyment rather than learning."},{"en":"visually spectacular","context":"This type of museum is designed to be visually spectacular, and may have interactive activities or even games as part of its exhibitions."},{"en":"detailed commentary about the exhibition","context":"Some museums employ professional guides to talk to their visitors, while other museums offer headsets so that visitors can listen to detailed commentary about the exhibition."},{"en":"have fun and learn something at the same time","context":"In conclusion, it seems to me that a good museum should be able to offer an interesting, enjoyable and educational experience so that people can have fun and learn something at the same time."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#034","summary":"Museums: education or entertainment"},{"id":"writing-task-2-138","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Arts & Museums","title":"Why we need music, and whose music matters","prompt":"There are many different types of music in the world today. Why do we need music? Is the traditional music of a country more important than the international music that is heard everywhere nowadays?","answer":"It is true that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a rich variety of musical styles</strong> can be found around the world. Music is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a vital part of</strong> all human cultures for a range of reasons, and I would argue that traditional music is more important than modern, international music.\n\nMusic is something that accompanies all of us throughout our lives. As children, we are taught songs by our parents and teachers as a means of learning language, or simply as a form of enjoyment. Children delight in singing with others, and it would appear that the act of singing in a group creates a connection between participants, regardless of their age. Later in life, people's musical preferences develop, and we come to see our favourite songs as part of our life stories. Music both expresses and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">arouses emotions in a way that words alone cannot</strong>. In short, it is difficult to imagine life without it.\n\nIn my opinion, traditional music should be valued over the international music that has become so popular. International pop music is often catchy and fun, but it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">essentially a commercial product</strong> that is marketed and sold by business people. Traditional music, by contrast, expresses the culture, customs and history of a country. Traditional styles, such as folk songs and ceremonial music, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">connect us to the past</strong> and form part of our cultural identity. It would be a real pity if pop music <strong class=\"key-phrase\">became so predominant that these national styles disappeared</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, music is a necessary part of human existence, and I believe that traditional music should be given more importance than international music.","phrases":[{"en":"a rich variety of musical styles","context":"It is true that a rich variety of musical styles can be found around the world. Music is a vital part of all human cultures for a range of reasons, and I would argue that traditional music is more important than modern, international music."},{"en":"a vital part of","context":"It is true that a rich variety of musical styles can be found around the world. Music is a vital part of all human cultures for a range of reasons, and I would argue that traditional music is more important than modern, international music."},{"en":"connect us to the past","context":"Traditional styles, such as folk songs and ceremonial music, connect us to the past and form part of our cultural identity."},{"en":"arouses emotions in a way that words alone cannot","context":"Music both expresses and arouses emotions in a way that words alone cannot."},{"en":"essentially a commercial product","context":"International pop music is often catchy and fun, but it is essentially a commercial product that is marketed and sold by business people."},{"en":"became so predominant that these national styles disappeared","context":"It would be a real pity if pop music became so predominant that these national styles disappeared."}],"subtype":"Two-part question","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#035","summary":"The value of traditional music"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2240","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Arts & Museums","title":"National or international collections?","prompt":"Museums and art galleries should concentrate on works that show the history and culture of their own country rather than the works of other parts of the world. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Museums must decide how much of their limited space to give to their own country and how much to the wider world. I disagree with the view that they should concentrate on national material, because a collection confined to one country teaches its own history badly.\n\nThe reason is that no national story makes sense in isolation. A country's art, food, religion and language are the product of trade, migration and conquest, so a gallery that shows only local painting cannot explain where its own painters found their techniques. Displaying a local textile beside the imported dyes that coloured it tells visitors far more about their own past than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the object standing alone</strong>.\n\nInternational collections also serve the people least able to travel. A family that will never fly to Athens or Kyoto can stand in front of objects from those places in an afternoon, and for schoolchildren this is often the first encounter with a culture they have only heard described. Removing that would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">narrow the horizons of exactly those visitors</strong> who depend on museums most.\n\nI accept the strongest argument on the other side. Many national collections were assembled during colonial rule, and objects taken without consent should be returned, which will inevitably reduce foreign holdings in some countries. That is an argument about ownership rather than about principle, and museums can continue to show the world through loans, exchanges and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">jointly curated exhibitions</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree with concentrating on national works. A country understands itself better when its own objects are shown beside those that influenced them, and the alternative produces a museum that is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">patriotic but not informative</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"the object standing alone","context":"Displaying a local textile beside the imported dyes that coloured it tells visitors far more about their own past than the object standing alone."},{"en":"narrow the horizons of exactly those visitors","context":"Removing that would narrow the horizons of exactly those visitors who depend on museums most."},{"en":"jointly curated exhibitions","context":"That is an argument about ownership rather than about principle, and museums can continue to show the world through loans, exchanges and jointly curated exhibitions."},{"en":"patriotic but not informative","context":"In conclusion, I disagree with concentrating on national works. A country understands itself better when its own objects are shown beside those that influenced them, and the alternative produces a museum that is patriotic but not informative."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#036","summary":"Whether museums should focus on their own country's history and culture"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2252","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Arts & Museums","title":"Returning historical objects","prompt":"Historical objects in museums all over the world should be returned to their country of origin. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Many of the most famous objects in European museums were removed from countries under colonial rule, and pressure to return them has grown. I largely agree that they should go back, though I would not apply the principle to every object without exception.\n\nThe central argument is about how the objects were obtained. A sculpture taken during a military occupation, from a people who had no power to refuse, was not acquired in any meaningful sense, and continuing to display it treats that history as settled. Many of these objects were also religious or ceremonial rather than decorative, so removing them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stripped them of their purpose</strong> as well as their setting. Returning them acknowledges a wrong in the only way that costs anything.\n\nThere is a practical argument too. Museums hold far more than they can exhibit, and objects that sit in storage for decades in one country would be central exhibits in another, seen by the people whose ancestors made them and studied by scholars who understand the context. Access is currently <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reversed for the communities most concerned</strong>, who must travel abroad to see their own heritage.\n\nI would accept two limits. Where a country genuinely cannot yet protect an object from war or theft, delay is reasonable, provided it is a delay rather than a refusal. Objects bought openly and fairly also stand in a different position from those seized, and treating the two <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as though they were identical</strong> weakens the case.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that historical objects should generally be returned, because ownership acquired by force is not ownership. The argument is strongest for objects taken without consent, and it can be met while keeping the world's collections connected through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long-term loans in both directions</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"stripped them of their purpose","context":"Many of these objects were also religious or ceremonial rather than decorative, so removing them stripped them of their purpose as well as their setting."},{"en":"reversed for the communities most concerned","context":"Access is currently reversed for the communities most concerned, who must travel abroad to see their own heritage."},{"en":"as though they were identical","context":"Objects bought openly and fairly also stand in a different position from those seized, and treating the two as though they were identical weakens the case."},{"en":"long-term loans in both directions","context":"The argument is strongest for objects taken without consent, and it can be met while keeping the world's collections connected through long-term loans in both directions."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#037","summary":"Whether museum objects should be returned to their country of origin"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2266","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Arts & Museums","title":"Public art in towns and cities","prompt":"The government should spend money on putting more works of art such as paintings and statues in towns and cities to make them more attractive. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Public sculpture and murals are often dismissed as decoration that a council cannot justify while other services are cut. I agree that governments should fund such work, provided the money is spent in ways that residents actually value.\n\nThe first argument is that appearance affects behaviour. Neglected streets invite further neglect, whereas a well-maintained square with something worth looking at is used more, and places that are used are safer. Several cities have found that commissioning murals on walls that were repeatedly defaced <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stopped the vandalism altogether</strong>, because a wall that clearly belongs to somebody is treated differently.\n\nPublic art is also the only art that reaches everybody. Entering a gallery requires knowing that one is welcome there, and many people never do, whereas a sculpture in a station is encountered by commuters who would never buy a ticket to see it. For children in areas with no cultural provision, this may be the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">only art they meet outside a screen</strong>. There is an economic return as well, since distinctive public works draw visitors and give a place an identity that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no shopping centre can supply</strong>.\n\nI would attach one condition. Money is wasted when a council commissions an expensive piece that residents dislike and were never consulted about, and such projects damage the case for all the others. Funding should follow consultation, and should include local artists and maintenance rather than a single purchase.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that public money should pay for art in public spaces, because it improves how places are treated and reaches people galleries never do. The spending is justified only where the work is chosen <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with the community rather than for it</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"stopped the vandalism altogether","context":"Several cities have found that commissioning murals on walls that were repeatedly defaced stopped the vandalism altogether, because a wall that clearly belongs to somebody is treated differently."},{"en":"only art they meet outside a screen","context":"For children in areas with no cultural provision, this may be the only art they meet outside a screen."},{"en":"no shopping centre can supply","context":"There is an economic return as well, since distinctive public works draw visitors and give a place an identity that no shopping centre can supply."},{"en":"with the community rather than for it","context":"In conclusion, I agree that public money should pay for art in public spaces, because it improves how places are treated and reaches people galleries never do. The spending is justified only where the work is chosen with the community rather than for it."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#038","summary":"Spending public money on art in towns and cities"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2276","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Arts & Museums","title":"Can anyone create art?","prompt":"Some people say that every human being can create art, while others think that only people born with the ability can create art. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Painting, music and writing are often described either as skills anyone can pick up or as gifts a person is simply born with. I think both descriptions capture something true, but the first is closer to how art actually works.\n\nThe case for natural ability is easy to see in the exceptional. Some children draw accurately or hold a tune before anyone teaches them, and a few adults produce work that decades of training would not explain. There is probably a real difference in how such people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">notice detail and hold ideas in mind</strong>. Anyone who has watched an ordinary student and a gifted one receive the same lesson knows that the results are not equal.\n\nThe wider view is more convincing, though, because it describes the great majority. Drawing is largely a matter of learning to look, and people who claim they cannot draw usually mean that they stopped at eight years old. Skills that appear innate turn out, when examined, to rest on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">years of quiet practice</strong> that nobody saw. Art also covers far more than technical display: cooking, gardening, photographs of a family and songs sung badly at home are all acts of making, and they are open to everyone.\n\nThe distinction matters because of what each belief produces. Treating art as a gift gives most people permission to never try, which is why so many adults say they have no creative ability at all. Treating it as a practice invites them in, and the result is not a nation of masters but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a population that makes things rather than only consuming them</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, exceptional talent is genuine and rare, and it explains the few. Everybody else can still make art of real value through attention and practice, so the belief that creation belongs to the gifted few is both false and discouraging.","phrases":[{"en":"notice detail and hold ideas in mind","context":"There is probably a real difference in how such people notice detail and hold ideas in mind."},{"en":"years of quiet practice","context":"Skills that appear innate turn out, when examined, to rest on years of quiet practice that nobody saw."},{"en":"a population that makes things rather than only consuming them","context":"Treating it as a practice invites them in, and the result is not a nation of masters but a population that makes things rather than only consuming them."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#039","summary":"Whether art is open to everyone or only to the naturally gifted"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2354","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Arts & Museums","title":"Live performance or the screen at home?","prompt":"In today's world, it is no longer necessary to attend live performances such as shows or concerts, because watching them at home on television or a computer is a better option. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"High-quality recordings of concerts and plays are now available for a fraction of the price of a ticket. I disagree that this makes attendance unnecessary, although watching at home is genuinely better in several respects.\n\nThe advantages of the screen are considerable. Cost is the largest: a family can watch an opera for the price of a coffee, while seats would be beyond them entirely. Recordings also remove distance, so people in small towns and those with disabilities or young children gain access to performances they could never reach. The view is frequently better than from a real seat, since cameras show the performer's hands and face, and a recording can be paused, repeated and kept. For learning an instrument or studying a play, that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ability to watch the same moment again</strong> is invaluable.\n\nWhat the screen removes, however, is the reason live performance exists. An audience changes a performance: musicians respond to it, actors adjust their timing to laughter, and no two nights are identical, which is why recordings of the same work differ so much. There is also a physical dimension that no equipment reproduces, since sound in a hall arrives through the body as well as the ears. Watching among strangers who are all <strong class=\"key-phrase\">absorbed in the same thing at the same moment</strong> produces a shared experience that a living room cannot.\n\nAttendance matters for the art form too. Ticket income supports musicians, technicians and venues, and an audience that only ever watches at home <strong class=\"key-phrase\">removes the money that pays for the performances it is watching</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, home viewing has made performance far more accessible and is often the more practical choice. It reproduces the content of an event rather than the event itself, so live attendance <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remains worth the cost and the journey</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"ability to watch the same moment again","context":"For learning an instrument or studying a play, that ability to watch the same moment again is invaluable."},{"en":"absorbed in the same thing at the same moment","context":"Watching among strangers who are all absorbed in the same thing at the same moment produces a shared experience that a living room cannot."},{"en":"removes the money that pays for the performances it is watching","context":"Attendance matters for the art form too. Ticket income supports musicians, technicians and venues, and an audience that only ever watches at home removes the money that pays for the performances it is watching."},{"en":"remains worth the cost and the journey","context":"In conclusion, home viewing has made performance far more accessible and is often the more practical choice. It reproduces the content of an event rather than the event itself, so live attendance remains worth the cost and the journey."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#040","summary":"Whether watching shows at home has made live attendance unnecessary"},{"id":"writing-task-2-010","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Business & Companies","title":"Do businesses have social responsibilities?","prompt":"As well as making money, businesses also have social responsibilities. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Businesses have always aimed to make a profit, but it is becoming increasingly common to hear people talk about the social obligations that companies have. I completely agree with the idea that businesses should do more for society than simply make money.\n\nOn the one hand, I accept that businesses must make money in order to survive in a competitive world. It seems logical that the priority of any company should be to cover its running costs, such as employees’ wages and payments for buildings and utilities. On top of these costs, companies also need to invest in improvements and innovations if they wish to remain successful. If a company is unable to pay its bills or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meet the changing needs of customers</strong>, any concerns about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">social responsibilities become irrelevant</strong>. In other words, a company can only <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make a positive contribution to</strong> society if it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in good financial health</strong>.\n\nOn the other hand, companies should not be run with the sole aim of maximising profit; they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have a wider role to play in</strong> society. One social obligation that owners and managers have is to treat their employees well, rather than exploiting them. For example, they could pay a “living wage” to ensure that workers have a good quality of life. I also like the idea that businesses could use a proportion of their profits to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">support local charities</strong>, environmental projects or education initiatives. Finally, instead of trying to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">minimise their tax payments by using accounting loopholes</strong>, I believe that company bosses should be happy to contribute to society through the tax system.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe that companies should place as much importance on their social responsibilities as they do on their financial objectives.","phrases":[{"en":"meet the changing needs of customers","context":"If a company is unable to pay its bills or meet the changing needs of customers, any concerns about social responsibilities become irrelevant."},{"en":"social responsibilities become irrelevant","context":"If a company is unable to pay its bills or meet the changing needs of customers, any concerns about social responsibilities become irrelevant."},{"en":"make a positive contribution to","context":"In other words, a company can only make a positive contribution to society if it is in good financial health."},{"en":"in good financial health","context":"In other words, a company can only make a positive contribution to society if it is in good financial health."},{"en":"have a wider role to play in","context":"On the other hand, companies should not be run with the sole aim of maximising profit; they have a wider role to play in society."},{"en":"support local charities","context":"I also like the idea that businesses could use a proportion of their profits to support local charities, environmental projects or education initiatives."},{"en":"minimise their tax payments by using accounting loopholes","context":"Finally, instead of trying to minimise their tax payments by using accounting loopholes, I believe that company bosses should be happy to contribute to society through the tax system."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#041","summary":"Corporate social responsibility"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Multinationals in developing countries","prompt":"Multinational companies are becoming increasingly common in developing countries. What are the advantages and disadvantages of this?","answer":"Multinational companies can bring investment, technology and employment to developing countries, but they may also exploit weak regulation. Their overall impact is positive only when governments negotiate fair conditions and enforce labour and environmental standards.\n\nThe main advantage is economic opportunity. A large international firm can create thousands of direct and indirect jobs, train local workers and connect domestic suppliers to global markets. Competition may also improve product quality and introduce new management methods. These effects can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raise productivity across the wider economy</strong>, particularly when knowledge and management practices spread from the foreign firm to local businesses in the same sector.\n\nThe risks are equally real. Some companies move production to countries where wages are low and regulation is weak. Profits may leave the country, while local communities carry the cost of pollution or unsafe work. Governments may also offer excessive tax concessions in the hope of attracting investment.\n\nHost countries can limit the disadvantages through enforceable rules. Foreign firms should meet the same labour, tax and environmental standards as domestic companies, major contracts should include training for local staff, and local suppliers should be given a fair chance to join international production networks rather than being limited to low-paid and insecure work. When regulation is credible, multinational investment can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">build local capability instead of creating dependence</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, multinational companies are neither automatically beneficial nor harmful. Developing countries gain most when foreign investment supports local skills and suppliers rather than operating as an isolated low-cost industry. Strong institutions are needed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turn private investment into public benefit</strong>.","topic":"Business & Companies","phrases":[{"en":"raise productivity across the wider economy","context":"These effects can raise productivity across the wider economy, particularly when knowledge and management practices spread from the foreign firm to local businesses in the same sector."},{"en":"build local capability instead of creating dependence","context":"When regulation is credible, multinational investment can build local capability instead of creating dependence."},{"en":"turn private investment into public benefit","context":"Strong institutions are needed to turn private investment into public benefit."}],"subtype":"advdis","summary":"Multinational companies in developing countries","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-109","_displayNo":"#042"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2371","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Business & Companies","title":"Should big firms provide local facilities?","prompt":"All large companies should provide sports and social facilities for the local community. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Large employers draw workers, traffic and land from the places they operate in, which raises the question of what they should give back. I partly agree that they should fund local facilities, but requiring every large company to build them would be the wrong way to arrange it.\n\nThere is a solid argument for the obligation. Big firms benefit from local infrastructure that the public paid for, and they impose costs through congestion and pressure on housing, so contributing to the community is closer to settling a bill than to charity. They also have the resources: a sports hall costs a large corporation very little and may be beyond a small town entirely. The benefits return to the company as well, since healthier employees take fewer sick days, and firms with a good local reputation <strong class=\"key-phrase\">find it easier to recruit and to obtain planning permission</strong>.\n\nThe objection is that companies are poor providers of public services. Their core purpose is to produce goods and employ people, and facilities built by a firm depend on its profits, so a downturn closes the swimming pool the town has come to rely on. Provision would also be arbitrary, since districts with a large employer would gain while poorer areas with none <strong class=\"key-phrase\">receive nothing at all</strong>. Companies naturally build what suits their own staff rather than what a community needs, and control over local amenities gives a private firm influence that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no elected body has granted it</strong>.\n\nThe better arrangement is financial rather than physical. Companies should pay fair taxes and, where a development imposes real costs, contribute to a fund that the local authority spends according to need. Sponsorship and opening existing facilities to residents are welcome additions.\n\nIn conclusion, large companies owe something to the places that host them, and many give generously. Requiring each to build facilities produces uneven and unreliable provision, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">paying towards services that the community decides on</strong> serves everyone better.","phrases":[{"en":"find it easier to recruit and to obtain planning permission","context":"The benefits return to the company as well, since healthier employees take fewer sick days, and firms with a good local reputation find it easier to recruit and to obtain planning permission."},{"en":"receive nothing at all","context":"Provision would also be arbitrary, since districts with a large employer would gain while poorer areas with none receive nothing at all."},{"en":"no elected body has granted it","context":"Companies naturally build what suits their own staff rather than what a community needs, and control over local amenities gives a private firm influence that no elected body has granted it."},{"en":"paying towards services that the community decides on","context":"In conclusion, large companies owe something to the places that host them, and many give generously. Requiring each to build facilities produces uneven and unreliable provision, so paying towards services that the community decides on serves everyone better."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#043","summary":"Whether large companies ought to build sports and social facilities for the community"},{"id":"writing-task-2-069","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"How much should adults control children?","prompt":"Children are often required to obey the rules set by their parents and teachers. Some people worry that too much control will not prepare them well for adult life. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Children are generally expected to obey their parents and teachers, though some people worry that strict control leaves them unprepared for adult life. In my opinion both discipline and gradually increasing freedom are necessary, and the balance should shift as a child grows older.\n\nThose who defend firm rules make a reasonable case. Young children cannot assess risk, and rules about traffic, strangers and bedtimes protect them from consequences they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot foresee</strong>. Clear boundaries also provide security, since a child who knows what is expected feels safer than one left to guess. Schools cannot function without discipline either, because a disruptive minority can prevent an entire class from learning and teachers cannot negotiate every instruction.\n\nThe opposing concern is equally serious. A young person who has never made a decision has never learned how, and such students often struggle badly at university, where nobody supervises their work. Excessive control also damages the relationship itself: teenagers who are forbidden everything tend to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">conceal what they do rather than obey</strong>, so parents lose the influence they were trying to protect.\n\nIn my view the answer is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a gradual transfer of responsibility</strong>. A ten-year-old needs firm rules; a sixteen-year-old needs to be allowed to choose subjects, manage money and make mistakes that carry a limited cost, while parents intervene only where safety is genuinely at stake.\n\nIn conclusion, obedience matters most in early childhood, but adolescents need practice in deciding for themselves. A family that never relaxes its control has simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">postponed the difficulty</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"cannot foresee","context":"Young children cannot assess risk, and rules about traffic, strangers and bedtimes protect them from consequences they cannot foresee."},{"en":"conceal what they do rather than obey","context":"Excessive control also damages the relationship itself: teenagers who are forbidden everything tend to conceal what they do rather than obey, so parents lose the influence they were trying to protect."},{"en":"a gradual transfer of responsibility","context":"In my view the answer is a gradual transfer of responsibility."},{"en":"postponed the difficulty","context":"In conclusion, obedience matters most in early childhood, but adolescents need practice in deciding for themselves. A family that never relaxes its control has simply postponed the difficulty."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#044","summary":"How much should adults control children?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-092","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Growing up in a city or the countryside","prompt":"Some people believe that a child's character is strongly influenced by whether they grow up in a city or in the countryside. Discuss the effects of both environments and give your own opinion.","answer":"Where a child spends their early years inevitably shapes the kind of adult they become. Cities and the countryside offer very different experiences, and in my view each produces particular strengths rather than one being clearly better.\n\nChildren raised in cities grow up surrounded by variety. They meet people of different nationalities, religions and social backgrounds every day, which tends to make them tolerant and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">quick to adapt</strong>. Museums, libraries, sports clubs and specialised schools are within easy reach, so a child with an unusual interest can pursue it seriously. The disadvantages are equally real: urban children have far less contact with nature, less physical freedom because streets are dangerous, and they often grow up in a competitive atmosphere that produces anxiety.\n\nA rural childhood offers the opposite balance. Children can move around freely and spend much of their time outdoors, which benefits both their health and their understanding of the natural world. Because rural communities are small, children usually know their neighbours and are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">given practical responsibilities early</strong>, and this tends to produce <strong class=\"key-phrase\">independence and a strong sense of belonging</strong>. The cost is limited exposure: fewer facilities, fewer examples of possible careers, and much less contact with people whose lives differ from their own, which can make later adjustment to a city difficult.\n\nIn my opinion neither environment is superior. A rural upbringing tends to build self-reliance, while a city develops adaptability and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">breadth of outlook</strong>, and what matters far more in both cases is the quality of a child's family and schooling.\n\nIn conclusion, place shapes character in different ways, but it is not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the decisive influence</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"independence and a strong sense of belonging","context":"Because rural communities are small, children usually know their neighbours and are given practical responsibilities early, and this tends to produce independence and a strong sense of belonging."},{"en":"breadth of outlook","context":"In my opinion neither environment is superior. A rural upbringing tends to build self-reliance, while a city develops adaptability and breadth of outlook, and what matters far more in both cases is the quality of a child's family and schooling."},{"en":"the decisive influence","context":"In conclusion, place shapes character in different ways, but it is not the decisive influence."},{"en":"quick to adapt","context":"They meet people of different nationalities, religions and social backgrounds every day, which tends to make them tolerant and quick to adapt."},{"en":"given practical responsibilities early","context":"Because rural communities are small, children usually know their neighbours and are given practical responsibilities early, and this tends to produce independence and a strong sense of belonging."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#045","summary":"Growing up in a city or the countryside"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Couples who choose not to have children","prompt":"There is an increasing trend around the world of married couples deciding not to have children. Discuss the advantages and disadvantages for couples who decide to do this.","answer":"More married couples are choosing not to have children. This decision can give adults greater freedom and financial security, but it may also create personal and social disadvantages. On balance, the effects depend on the couple's circumstances and the scale of the trend.\n\nFor individuals, remaining child-free can reduce financial pressure and allow more time for careers, relationships and personal interests. Raising a child requires substantial money, energy and long-term responsibility. Some people also decide that they cannot provide the stable home they would want for a family. In such cases, choosing not to become parents is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a responsible personal decision</strong> rather than a selfish one.\n\nThe disadvantages are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less predictable</strong>. Some couples may later regret the decision or feel isolated in old age, although having children does not guarantee support. At a national level, a very low birth rate can shrink the future workforce and increase the cost of pensions and healthcare. Governments may then need immigration, later retirement or stronger family support policies to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">maintain a balanced population</strong>.\n\nThe scale of the trend also matters. A modest number of child-free couples has little effect on society, but the pattern becomes serious once it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turns into a generational norm</strong>: South Korea's fertility rate, now among the lowest in the world, has already forced schools to close and left some regions with more retirees than working adults. This does not justify pressuring couples into parenthood, but it does mean governments cannot simply hope the trend reverses on its own; the more realistic response is policy that makes parenthood <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a genuine choice rather than a financial penalty</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, couples should be free to decide whether parenthood suits them. The personal advantages can be considerable, but widespread childlessness may create demographic challenges. Policy should make raising children more affordable without pressuring people into parenthood.","topic":"Children & Family","phrases":[{"en":"a responsible personal decision","context":"In such cases, choosing not to become parents is a responsible personal decision rather than a selfish one."},{"en":"less predictable","context":"The disadvantages are less predictable."},{"en":"maintain a balanced population","context":"Governments may then need immigration, later retirement or stronger family support policies to maintain a balanced population."},{"en":"a genuine choice rather than a financial penalty","context":"This does not justify pressuring couples into parenthood, but it does mean governments cannot simply hope the trend reverses on its own; the more realistic response is policy that makes parenthood a genuine choice rather than a financial penalty."},{"en":"turns into a generational norm","context":"A modest number of child-free couples has little effect on society, but the pattern becomes serious once it turns into a generational norm: South Korea's fertility rate, now among the lowest in the world, has already forced schools to close and left some regions with more retirees than working ad..."}],"subtype":"advdis","summary":"Couples who decide not to have children","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-098","_displayNo":"#046"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Children spending less time with their families","prompt":"In modern times, children are spending less time with their families and more time with their friends. Why has this change occurred? Do you think parents should force their children to spend more time at home?","answer":"Many children now spend less time with their families and more time with friends. This change is partly caused by longer school days, digital communication and parents’ working hours. It can support independence, but excessive distance from family may weaken guidance and emotional security.\n\nSeveral things have caused this change. Parents in many families now work longer or less predictable hours, so the house is empty for much of the afternoon, while school, homework and organised activities keep children out until the evening. Smartphones have removed the old limits on contact as well: a teenager can remain inside a group conversation all evening without leaving their bedroom, which was impossible when the telephone stood in the hall. Adolescence is also the stage at which young people naturally look outwards, and time with friends is how they develop <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of identity</strong> outside the home and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">build confidence and social independence</strong>.\n\nHowever, family contact remains essential. Parents usually provide long-term support, set boundaries and notice changes in behaviour that friends may miss. If children spend most of their free time online or away from home, family members may stop discussing school, relationships and personal worries. This can leave young people more exposed to peer pressure and unreliable advice.\n\nParents should respond by protecting regular family time rather than imposing constant control. Shared meals, weekend activities and limits on late-night socialising can maintain connection while respecting a teenager's growing independence. Forced isolation from friends is likely to create resentment, whereas <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reliable routines and open conversation</strong> are more likely to bring children home willingly.\n\nIn conclusion, children now spend more time with friends because household schedules have changed and because constant contact has become effortless, and this is a normal part of growing up. Forcing them to stay at home would be counterproductive; parents do far better to make the household somewhere worth returning to, which is how families <strong class=\"key-phrase\">maintain close relationships as children become more independent</strong>.","topic":"Children & Family","phrases":[{"en":"a sense of identity","context":"Adolescence is also the stage at which young people naturally look outwards, and time with friends is how they develop a sense of identity outside the home and build confidence and social independence."},{"en":"build confidence and social independence","context":"Adolescence is also the stage at which young people naturally look outwards, and time with friends is how they develop a sense of identity outside the home and build confidence and social independence."},{"en":"reliable routines and open conversation","context":"Forced isolation from friends is likely to create resentment, whereas reliable routines and open conversation are more likely to bring children home willingly."},{"en":"maintain close relationships as children become more independent","context":"Forcing them to stay at home would be counterproductive; parents do far better to make the household somewhere worth returning to, which is how families maintain close relationships as children become more independent."}],"subtype":"two-part","summary":"Why children spend less time with their families","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-099","_displayNo":"#047"},{"id":"writing-task-2-143","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Young children and video games","prompt":"Many people today are worried about young children using video games. What problems might these games cause, for children and society as a whole? How could these problems be reduced?","answer":"The almost universal availability of video games among children raises problems that deserve serious attention, alongside some reasonably straightforward ways of managing them. Chief among the risks are the games' content, the amount of time children spend playing, and the physical toll of long, sedentary sessions.\n\nThe most serious concern is the level of violence in many games, which feature soldiers, criminals and killers as central, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">often glamorised, characters</strong>; a child who spends hours controlling such figures can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">come to see aggression as normal</strong>, or even admirable. A related danger is obsessive use, where some children prefer a screen to real friendships, gradually becoming isolated and socially awkward as a result; surveys in several countries have linked more than three hours of daily gaming with poorer concentration at school, suggesting the effect is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">measurable rather than merely anecdotal</strong>. Even moderate but prolonged play takes a physical toll, contributing to eye strain, disrupted sleep and poor posture that can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">difficult to correct once established</strong>.\n\nThese problems point to some fairly direct remedies. Stricter content regulation, with games reviewed by an independent body in the same way that films are classified, would keep the most violent material away from young players. To tackle the problem of obsessive use, schools could offer counselling and practical advice on healthy screen habits, while safety guidance printed on packaging, or built into the games themselves, would keep young players aware of the physical risks of long sessions.\n\nTo sum up, this is a problem that has both social and physical effects, and the solution will require combined action by manufacturers, schools and authorities.","phrases":[{"en":"often glamorised, characters","context":"The most serious concern is the level of violence in many games, which feature soldiers, criminals and killers as central, often glamorised, characters; a child who spends hours controlling such figures can come to see aggression as normal, or even admirable."},{"en":"come to see aggression as normal","context":"The most serious concern is the level of violence in many games, which feature soldiers, criminals and killers as central, often glamorised, characters; a child who spends hours controlling such figures can come to see aggression as normal, or even admirable."},{"en":"measurable rather than merely anecdotal","context":"A related danger is obsessive use, where some children prefer a screen to real friendships, gradually becoming isolated and socially awkward as a result; surveys in several countries have linked more than three hours of daily gaming with poorer concentration at school, suggesting the effect is me..."},{"en":"difficult to correct once established","context":"Even moderate but prolonged play takes a physical toll, contributing to eye strain, disrupted sleep and poor posture that can be difficult to correct once established."}],"subtype":"Causes / Effects","_displayNo":"#048","summary":"People today are worried about young children using video games."},{"id":"writing-task-2-144","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Does growing up poor build resilience?","prompt":"Children who grow up in families short of money are better prepared to deal with the problems of adult life than children brought up by wealthy parents. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes claimed that children who grow up without money handle adult difficulties better than those raised in wealthy families. I partly agree, since financial hardship can teach resilience, but I believe the claim is exaggerated and overlooks the serious disadvantages of poverty.\n\nThere is something in the argument. Children in poorer households usually take on responsibility earlier, whether by working part-time, caring for younger siblings or managing limited resources, and they learn that difficulties must be solved rather than paid away. They may also develop a stronger motivation to succeed, since nothing is guaranteed and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no family safety net exists behind them</strong>. Conversely, a young adult who has never been refused anything may be poorly equipped for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the first serious setback</strong>.\n\nNevertheless, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">this romanticises poverty considerably</strong>. Growing up short of money frequently means poor nutrition, overcrowded housing, weaker schools and parents too exhausted by work to provide attention. Persistent financial stress in childhood is associated with anxiety and lower educational attainment, and these are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">handicaps rather than preparation</strong>. Wealthy families, meanwhile, supply advantages that are genuinely useful in adult life: good health, strong schooling and professional contacts.\n\nIn my view what actually builds resilience is being given responsibility and being allowed to fail, and this depends on parenting rather than on income. A wealthy child who is expected to work for what they receive develops <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exactly the same capacity</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, hardship can produce resourcefulness, but I disagree that poverty is a good preparation for adult life. Its disadvantages generally outweigh whatever character it builds.","phrases":[{"en":"the first serious setback","context":"Conversely, a young adult who has never been refused anything may be poorly equipped for the first serious setback."},{"en":"handicaps rather than preparation","context":"Persistent financial stress in childhood is associated with anxiety and lower educational attainment, and these are handicaps rather than preparation."},{"en":"exactly the same capacity","context":"In my view what actually builds resilience is being given responsibility and being allowed to fail, and this depends on parenting rather than on income. A wealthy child who is expected to work for what they receive develops exactly the same capacity."},{"en":"no family safety net exists behind them","context":"They may also develop a stronger motivation to succeed, since nothing is guaranteed and no family safety net exists behind them."},{"en":"this romanticises poverty considerably","context":"Nevertheless, this romanticises poverty considerably."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#049","summary":"Does growing up poor build resilience?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-149","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Nature or nurture?","prompt":"Opinions differ as to whether nature or nurture plays the more important role in the development of personality. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People disagree about whether personality is determined mainly by inherited characteristics or by upbringing and environment. In my opinion both matter, but the evidence suggests that environment has the greater influence over how a person actually behaves.\n\nThe argument for nature is not weak. Studies of identical twins raised in separate families have repeatedly found striking similarities in temperament, and any parent with more than one child will confirm that babies differ from birth in how anxious, sociable or persistent they are. Nobody chooses to be naturally shy or naturally energetic, and these tendencies <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remain recognisable throughout life</strong>.\n\nNevertheless, inherited temperament <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sets a range rather than a fixed outcome</strong>. A naturally shy child who is encouraged to speak in class and never mocked for hesitating usually becomes a reserved but confident adult; the same child, criticised for shyness, may become genuinely withdrawn. Language, values, ambition and attitudes to work are also learned almost entirely from family and society, which is why children raised in different countries develop such different assumptions about what is normal.\n\nThe most convincing explanation is that the two interact continuously rather than competing. Inherited characteristics influence how a child is treated, since an outgoing baby receives more attention and a difficult one receives more correction, and that treatment then <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reinforces the original tendency</strong>. Separating the two influences is therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">almost impossible in practice</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, personality begins with inherited temperament, but I believe upbringing determines far more of the final result. This matters practically, because it means that good parenting and good schooling can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely change how a child's life turns out</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"remain recognisable throughout life","context":"Nobody chooses to be naturally shy or naturally energetic, and these tendencies remain recognisable throughout life."},{"en":"sets a range rather than a fixed outcome","context":"Nevertheless, inherited temperament sets a range rather than a fixed outcome."},{"en":"genuinely change how a child's life turns out","context":"In conclusion, personality begins with inherited temperament, but I believe upbringing determines far more of the final result. This matters practically, because it means that good parenting and good schooling can genuinely change how a child's life turns out."},{"en":"reinforces the original tendency","context":"Inherited characteristics influence how a child is treated, since an outgoing baby receives more attention and a difficult one receives more correction, and that treatment then reinforces the original tendency."},{"en":"almost impossible in practice","context":"Separating the two influences is therefore almost impossible in practice."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#050","summary":"Nature or nurture?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-154","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Should children learn to compete or to cooperate?","prompt":"Some people think that children should learn to compete, but others think that they should be taught to cooperate with others. What's your opinion?","answer":"Competition can motivate children, while cooperation teaches them to work with others. In my view, schools should develop both qualities, but cooperation deserves greater emphasis because most adult achievements depend on shared effort rather than individual victory.\n\nHealthy competition can encourage pupils to practise, set goals and measure progress. A race, debate or academic challenge may show a child what focused effort can achieve. Problems arise when success is defined only as defeating classmates. Constant ranking can make weaker pupils withdraw, encourage cheating and create the belief that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">another person's progress is a threat</strong>.\n\nCooperative tasks develop a broader set of skills. When pupils plan a presentation or solve a practical problem in a group, they must listen, explain ideas, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">divide responsibilities and manage disagreement</strong>. These abilities are essential in universities and workplaces. Cooperation also allows children with different strengths to contribute, which can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turn individual ability into a stronger collective result</strong>.\n\nTeachers should also assess both the final result and each pupil's contribution. Otherwise, one confident child may control the task while quieter members receive little practice. Clear roles, peer feedback and rotating leadership make cooperation fairer and more educational. It also prevents group work from becoming <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a reward only for confident speakers</strong>.\n\nSchools do not need to remove competition entirely. They can compare teams, reward personal improvement and ensure that every child has a meaningful role. In conclusion, competition is useful when it remains fair and limited, but cooperation should form the foundation of classroom life. It prepares children to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">succeed without treating others as obstacles</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"turn individual ability into a stronger collective result","context":"Cooperation also allows children with different strengths to contribute, which can turn individual ability into a stronger collective result."},{"en":"succeed without treating others as obstacles","context":"It prepares children to succeed without treating others as obstacles."},{"en":"another person's progress is a threat","context":"Constant ranking can make weaker pupils withdraw, encourage cheating and create the belief that another person's progress is a threat."},{"en":"divide responsibilities and manage disagreement","context":"When pupils plan a presentation or solve a practical problem in a group, they must listen, explain ideas, divide responsibilities and manage disagreement."},{"en":"a reward only for confident speakers","context":"It also prevents group work from becoming a reward only for confident speakers."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#051","summary":"People think that children should learn to compete, but others think that…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2199","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Letting children make mistakes","prompt":"Some people believe that children should have the freedom to make mistakes, while others argue that adults should prevent them from doing so. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People disagree about how far adults should intervene when a child is about to do something unwise. Some believe that mistakes are a necessary part of growing up, while others think that adults have a duty to prevent them. In my opinion, both positions are partly right, and the answer depends on how serious the mistake would be.\n\nThose who defend the freedom to make mistakes argue that experience teaches more effectively than instruction. A child who is repeatedly told what to do learns to obey, but a child who fails and then works out why has learned something permanent. Making mistakes also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">builds resilience</strong>. Young people who have never been allowed to fail often struggle at university or in their first job, simply because they have no experience of dealing with disappointment.\n\nOn the other hand, adults have far more knowledge than children and can see consequences that a young person cannot. A ten-year-old cannot reasonably be expected to judge the risk of crossing a busy road, meeting a stranger online or experimenting with drugs. In these situations, allowing a child to \"learn from experience\" is not education but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">negligence</strong>.\n\nIn my view, the sensible approach is to distinguish between mistakes that are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recoverable</strong> and those that are not. Children should be free to choose the wrong subject, spend their pocket money badly or lose a match through poor tactics, because the cost is low and the lesson is valuable. Adults should intervene firmly whenever a decision could damage a child's health, safety or future.\n\nIn conclusion, mistakes are an essential part of learning, but adults must still protect children from errors that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot be undone</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"builds resilience","context":"Making mistakes also builds resilience."},{"en":"negligence","context":"In these situations, allowing a child to \"learn from experience\" is not education but negligence."},{"en":"recoverable","context":"In my view, the sensible approach is to distinguish between mistakes that are recoverable and those that are not."},{"en":"cannot be undone","context":"In conclusion, mistakes are an essential part of learning, but adults must still protect children from errors that cannot be undone."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#052","summary":"Letting children make mistakes"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2231","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Family or the world outside?","prompt":"Some people think that family has the most powerful influence on a child's development, while others believe that other factors such as friends, television and music have a stronger effect. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Children are shaped both by the household they grow up in and by the friends, screens and music that surround them. In my view the family exerts the deeper influence, but its power is concentrated in the early years and declines sharply afterwards.\n\nThe argument for the family rests on timing. Before a child meets a single classmate, parents have already established how language is used at home, whether reading is normal, how conflict is handled and whether adults can be trusted. These patterns are laid down when the brain is most receptive, and they become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the standard against which everything later is measured</strong>. A child who has watched a parent apologise properly carries that model into every relationship, largely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without ever noticing it</strong>.\n\nThe opposing view has force during adolescence. Teenagers spend most of their waking hours among people their own age, and the desire not to be excluded is powerful enough to change how they dress, speak and study. Whether a fifteen-year-old's friends treat schoolwork as worth doing predicts their results more reliably than almost anything a parent says. Music and screens matter here too, because they supply the images of adulthood that young people measure themselves against.\n\nMy own position is that these influences are not equal partners. Parents largely determine which friends a child meets, through where the family lives and which school it chooses, so much of the peer effect is itself <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an indirect family decision</strong>. Children with a secure home also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">resist peer pressure better</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, outside influences dominate the teenage years and should not be dismissed. The family nonetheless matters more, because it builds the foundation on which those later influences <strong class=\"key-phrase\">either take hold or do not</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"without ever noticing it","context":"A child who has watched a parent apologise properly carries that model into every relationship, largely without ever noticing it."},{"en":"an indirect family decision","context":"Parents largely determine which friends a child meets, through where the family lives and which school it chooses, so much of the peer effect is itself an indirect family decision."},{"en":"either take hold or do not","context":"In conclusion, outside influences dominate the teenage years and should not be dismissed. The family nonetheless matters more, because it builds the foundation on which those later influences either take hold or do not."},{"en":"the standard against which everything later is measured","context":"These patterns are laid down when the brain is most receptive, and they become the standard against which everything later is measured."},{"en":"resist peer pressure better","context":"Children with a secure home also resist peer pressure better."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#053","summary":"Family influence compared with influences from outside the home"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2248","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Pressure to succeed","prompt":"Parents are putting a lot of pressure on their children to succeed. What are the reasons for this? Is it a positive or negative development?","answer":"Children in many countries now face expectations that would have been unusual a generation ago, with private tutoring beginning in primary school. The reasons are largely economic, and although the intention is protective, I consider the trend negative.\n\nThe main cause is competition for a shrinking number of secure positions. Where a degree from a particular university determines access to stable work, parents conclude that a child who is not ahead at fifteen has already lost, and this belief spreads: once other families hire tutors, refusing to do so feels like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accepting a disadvantage on a child's behalf</strong>. Smaller families reinforce the pattern, since parents with one child concentrate on that child all the ambition previously spread across four.\n\nSome pressure is undoubtedly useful. Children rarely choose difficult work unprompted, and adults who were expected to try often say later that they are grateful. High expectations communicate belief, and a child who is asked for nothing may reasonably conclude that little is thought of them.\n\nThe harm comes from the intensity now common. Adolescent anxiety and sleep deprivation have risen alongside academic pressure, and children who succeed under it frequently arrive at university unable to work without instruction, having never chosen anything themselves. There is a further cost that is easy to miss: a child pushed towards medicine or law may abandon the subject they were actually good at, so the family <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trades genuine talent for a safer label</strong>. Praise conditional on results also teaches that affection <strong class=\"key-phrase\">has to be earned</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the pressure comes from real competition for secure work, and encouragement is valuable. The current intensity is nonetheless a negative development, because it produces anxious young adults who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">achieve well without knowing what they want</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"accepting a disadvantage on a child's behalf","context":"Where a degree from a particular university determines access to stable work, parents conclude that a child who is not ahead at fifteen has already lost, and this belief spreads: once other families hire tutors, refusing to do so feels like accepting a disadvantage on a child's behalf."},{"en":"trades genuine talent for a safer label","context":"There is a further cost that is easy to miss: a child pushed towards medicine or law may abandon the subject they were actually good at, so the family trades genuine talent for a safer label."},{"en":"has to be earned","context":"Praise conditional on results also teaches that affection has to be earned."},{"en":"achieve well without knowing what they want","context":"In conclusion, the pressure comes from real competition for secure work, and encouragement is valuable. The current intensity is nonetheless a negative development, because it produces anxious young adults who achieve well without knowing what they want."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#054","summary":"Why parents pressure their children to succeed"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2249","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Is television bad for children?","prompt":"Some people think watching TV is bad for children while others think that watching TV has more beneficial effects on children. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Few household habits divide parents as sharply as television. Some regard it as damaging while others see it as a source of learning, and my own view is that the argument is really about quantity and content rather than about the screen itself.\n\nThe case against television rests on what it replaces. Hours spent watching are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hours not spent reading, playing outside or talking to adults</strong>, and language develops through conversation, which a broadcast cannot supply because it never responds to the child. Heavy viewing is associated with poorer sleep and lower fitness, and programmes made for adults expose children to violence and to advertising for food they should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">be eating less of</strong>.\n\nThose who defend television point to what good programmes achieve. Well-designed children's series have measurably improved vocabulary and number skills, particularly among children whose parents read to them little. Documentaries also show things a child could never otherwise see, from the deep ocean to the inside of a volcano, and shared viewing gives a family something to talk about together.\n\nMy position is that both sides describe real effects of different practices. Two hours of unsupervised viewing is not the same activity as thirty minutes of a nature programme watched with a parent who asks questions afterwards. The evidence on harm concerns quantity and unsuitable content, while the evidence on benefit concerns careful selection, so the sensible response is to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">limit the hours and choose the programmes</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, television is neither harmful nor beneficial in itself. What matters is how much a child watches, what they watch and whether an adult watches with them, which makes this a question of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">parental judgement rather than prohibition</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"be eating less of","context":"Heavy viewing is associated with poorer sleep and lower fitness, and programmes made for adults expose children to violence and to advertising for food they should be eating less of."},{"en":"limit the hours and choose the programmes","context":"The evidence on harm concerns quantity and unsuitable content, while the evidence on benefit concerns careful selection, so the sensible response is to limit the hours and choose the programmes."},{"en":"parental judgement rather than prohibition","context":"In conclusion, television is neither harmful nor beneficial in itself. What matters is how much a child watches, what they watch and whether an adult watches with them, which makes this a question of parental judgement rather than prohibition."},{"en":"hours not spent reading, playing outside or talking to adults","context":"Hours spent watching are hours not spent reading, playing outside or talking to adults, and language develops through conversation, which a broadcast cannot supply because it never responds to the child."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#055","summary":"Harmful and beneficial effects of television on children"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2259","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Starting a family later","prompt":"In some countries today, many people are having their first child at an older age. What are the reasons for this trend? Do the advantages outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"In many wealthy countries the average age at which women have a first child has risen by several years within a generation. The causes are mainly economic and educational, and in my view the advantages of the trend slightly outweigh its costs.\n\nThe principal reason is that the years once used for starting a family are now spent in education and early careers. A woman completing a degree and establishing herself professionally is unlikely to be ready for a child before her late twenties, and taking leave at the point when a career is being built carries a lasting penalty in pay. Housing reinforces this: where deposits require years of saving, couples <strong class=\"key-phrase\">postpone children until they have space for them</strong>. Reliable contraception has made this delay a genuine choice rather than an accident.\n\nThe advantages are real. Older parents are typically more financially secure, so a child grows up with fewer of the pressures that money problems create, and parents who have already travelled and worked are less likely to feel that a child <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cost them the life they wanted</strong>. Greater maturity also shows in parenting, and children of older parents perform slightly better at school on average.\n\nThe disadvantages are chiefly biological. Fertility declines with age, so some couples who delay discover that conceiving is difficult and face expensive treatment, and the risk of certain complications rises. Older parents also have less energy for young children and are more likely to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caring for their own parents at the same time</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the trend is driven by longer education and the cost of housing. On balance the benefits are greater, provided couples understand that delaying <strong class=\"key-phrase\">narrows the window rather than closing it later</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"postpone children until they have space for them","context":"Housing reinforces this: where deposits require years of saving, couples postpone children until they have space for them."},{"en":"cost them the life they wanted","context":"Older parents are typically more financially secure, so a child grows up with fewer of the pressures that money problems create, and parents who have already travelled and worked are less likely to feel that a child cost them the life they wanted."},{"en":"caring for their own parents at the same time","context":"Older parents also have less energy for young children and are more likely to be caring for their own parents at the same time."},{"en":"narrows the window rather than closing it later","context":"In conclusion, the trend is driven by longer education and the cost of housing. On balance the benefits are greater, provided couples understand that delaying narrows the window rather than closing it later."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Advantages outweigh disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#056","summary":"Why people are having their first child at an older age"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2261","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"The effects of peer pressure","prompt":"Young people are often influenced in their behaviour and attitudes by others of the same age. This is called peer pressure. Do the disadvantages of peer pressure outweigh the advantages?","answer":"Young people are strongly affected by the expectations of others their own age, and this influence is usually described as a danger. In my view the disadvantages do outweigh the benefits during adolescence, though the effect is not as one-sided as it is often presented.\n\nThe harm is easiest to see in behaviour that carries real risk. Smoking, drinking and dangerous driving almost always begin in a group rather than alone, because a teenager who refuses <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fears exclusion more than the risk itself</strong>, and the part of the brain that weighs consequences is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">still developing at that age</strong>. The pressure also narrows what young people allow themselves to be: a boy who enjoys music or a girl who is good at mathematics may abandon the subject rather than be seen as different, and the cost of that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is paid for years afterwards</strong>.\n\nThe positive side is genuine but weaker. The same mechanism works in the opposite direction when the group values effort: a student whose friends intend to go to university is far more likely to apply, and teams and clubs use exactly this force to build commitment. Peers also teach social skills that adults cannot, since learning to negotiate and apologise happens among equals rather than under adult supervision.\n\nThe imbalance lies in what each side risks. A beneficial influence produces better habits, while a harmful one can produce an addiction, a criminal record or a fatal accident, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the consequences are not symmetrical</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, peer pressure can encourage effort and teaches young people how to live among others. Its drawbacks are nonetheless greater, because the damage it causes is often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">difficult to undo later</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"still developing at that age","context":"Smoking, drinking and dangerous driving almost always begin in a group rather than alone, because a teenager who refuses fears exclusion more than the risk itself, and the part of the brain that weighs consequences is still developing at that age."},{"en":"is paid for years afterwards","context":"The pressure also narrows what young people allow themselves to be: a boy who enjoys music or a girl who is good at mathematics may abandon the subject rather than be seen as different, and the cost of that is paid for years afterwards."},{"en":"difficult to undo later","context":"In conclusion, peer pressure can encourage effort and teaches young people how to live among others. Its drawbacks are nonetheless greater, because the damage it causes is often difficult to undo later."},{"en":"fears exclusion more than the risk itself","context":"Smoking, drinking and dangerous driving almost always begin in a group rather than alone, because a teenager who refuses fears exclusion more than the risk itself, and the part of the brain that weighs consequences is still developing at that age."},{"en":"the consequences are not symmetrical","context":"The imbalance lies in what each side risks. A beneficial influence produces better habits, while a harmful one can produce an addiction, a criminal record or a fatal accident, so the consequences are not symmetrical."}],"subtype":"Advantages & Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#057","summary":"Whether the drawbacks of peer pressure outweigh its benefits"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2275","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Limiting screens and encouraging books","prompt":"Some people say that parents should place restrictions on the hours their children spend watching television and playing computer games, and should encourage them to spend this time reading books instead. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Most children would happily spend every free hour in front of a screen, and many now do. I agree that parents should set limits, though I think the second half of the suggestion, swapping those hours directly for books, works only if it is handled with some care.\n\nLimits are justified because screen time crowds out everything else. A child absorbed in a game is not sleeping, moving, talking to anyone or being bored, and boredom is where children invent things. Games and streaming services are also built to hold attention: episodes play automatically and rewards arrive on a schedule, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stopping requires more willpower than a child has</strong>. Setting an hour is not a punishment but a substitute for self-control the child has not yet developed.\n\nReading is a reasonable thing to steer them towards. It builds vocabulary and concentration in a way no other activity matches, and a child who reads fluently by ten finds every school subject easier. Sitting with a difficult page also teaches patience, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the reward comes slowly</strong> rather than every few seconds.\n\nThe plan fails, however, when reading is imposed as the price of the screen being switched off. Forced reading teaches children that books are a chore, which is the opposite of the intention. What works is making books available and attractive, letting children choose whatever they enjoy, comics included, and having parents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">read visibly themselves</strong>. Nor should the freed hours all go to reading; sport and unstructured play deserve a share.\n\nIn conclusion, parents are right to cap the hours their children spend on screens, since those devices are designed to be hard to put down. Books should be offered generously rather than enforced, because a child who is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made to read stops wanting to</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"stopping requires more willpower than a child has","context":"Games and streaming services are also built to hold attention: episodes play automatically and rewards arrive on a schedule, so stopping requires more willpower than a child has."},{"en":"the reward comes slowly","context":"Sitting with a difficult page also teaches patience, because the reward comes slowly rather than every few seconds."},{"en":"read visibly themselves","context":"What works is making books available and attractive, letting children choose whatever they enjoy, comics included, and having parents read visibly themselves."},{"en":"made to read stops wanting to","context":"In conclusion, parents are right to cap the hours their children spend on screens, since those devices are designed to be hard to put down. Books should be offered generously rather than enforced, because a child who is made to read stops wanting to."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#058","summary":"Whether parents should restrict screen time and push children towards reading"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2289","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"What children watch, or how long?","prompt":"Some people believe that what children watch on television influences their behaviour. Others say that the amount of time spent watching television is what matters. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Parents worried about television face two different warnings: one about the programmes and one about the hours. Both concerns have evidence behind them, and my own view is that duration is the greater problem for most children.\n\nThe argument about content is well established. Young children imitate what they see, and studies going back decades show that those exposed to repeated aggression on screen behave more aggressively soon afterwards, particularly when the violence is rewarded. Content also teaches expectations, since programmes carry assumptions about how families work, what bodies should look like and what wealth means, and a child absorbs these long before they can question them. Advertising works on the same principle, which is why several countries restrict it during children's programmes.\n\nThe argument about time addresses something more basic. An hour spent watching is an hour not spent running, reading, talking or inventing a game, and those activities build language, coordination and social skill during years when the effects are lasting. Heavy viewing is associated with poorer sleep, lower reading ability and weight problems regardless of what was on the screen, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">even excellent programmes displace something the child needs more</strong>. Long passive viewing also trains a child to expect entertainment to arrive without effort.\n\nMy own position is that content matters most for the youngest children and time matters most overall. A four-year-old cannot distinguish a cartoon from instruction, so what is shown is critical, but by school age children understand that television is invented, while the hours <strong class=\"key-phrase\">continue to take the same amount from their day</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, both what children watch and how long they watch shape their behaviour, and parents should attend to each. Limiting the hours is the more useful rule, because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">protects the sleep, activity and conversation</strong> that everything else depends on.","phrases":[{"en":"even excellent programmes displace something the child needs more","context":"Heavy viewing is associated with poorer sleep, lower reading ability and weight problems regardless of what was on the screen, so even excellent programmes displace something the child needs more."},{"en":"continue to take the same amount from their day","context":"A four-year-old cannot distinguish a cartoon from instruction, so what is shown is critical, but by school age children understand that television is invented, while the hours continue to take the same amount from their day."},{"en":"protects the sleep, activity and conversation","context":"In conclusion, both what children watch and how long they watch shape their behaviour, and parents should attend to each. Limiting the hours is the more useful rule, because it protects the sleep, activity and conversation that everything else depends on."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#059","summary":"Whether content or viewing time has the greater effect on children's behaviour"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2342","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Giving children more freedom","prompt":"In many parts of the world, children are given more freedom than in the past. Is this a positive or negative development?","answer":"Children today are consulted about decisions their grandparents would simply have been told about, and physical punishment has largely disappeared. I regard the change as positive overall, though one part of it has gone wrong.\n\nThe gains are considerable. Children who are allowed to express opinions and disagree respectfully grow into adults who can argue, negotiate and resist pressure, which is exactly what modern work and citizenship demand. Freedom to choose subjects, hobbies and friends lets a child discover ability that a strict family would never have noticed. The decline of harsh discipline matters too, since fear teaches obedience while a child is watched and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">teaches nothing about why a rule exists</strong>. Homes are also warmer places, and children who feel able to tell their parents about a problem are far safer than those who cannot.\n\nThere are genuine costs. Freedom without limits leaves children without the structure they need, and constant negotiation over bedtimes and meals exhausts everyone. Some parents have confused respect with equality, so children make decisions they are not equipped to make, and a child who has never been refused anything <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meets adult life without any tolerance for disappointment</strong>.\n\nThe stranger contradiction is that freedom has increased in one direction and collapsed in another. Children today have more say at home but far less liberty outside it: fewer walk to school or play unsupervised than at any point in the past century. That is the loss that matters, because independence is learned by being <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trusted to manage something without an adult present</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the shift towards listening to children and away from harsh discipline is clearly positive. It works only where freedom comes with firm limits, and it remains incomplete while children are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">given a voice indoors and no independence outside</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"teaches nothing about why a rule exists","context":"The decline of harsh discipline matters too, since fear teaches obedience while a child is watched and teaches nothing about why a rule exists."},{"en":"meets adult life without any tolerance for disappointment","context":"Some parents have confused respect with equality, so children make decisions they are not equipped to make, and a child who has never been refused anything meets adult life without any tolerance for disappointment."},{"en":"trusted to manage something without an adult present","context":"That is the loss that matters, because independence is learned by being trusted to manage something without an adult present."},{"en":"given a voice indoors and no independence outside","context":"In conclusion, the shift towards listening to children and away from harsh discipline is clearly positive. It works only where freedom comes with firm limits, and it remains incomplete while children are given a voice indoors and no independence outside."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#060","summary":"Whether the greater freedom children have today is a good thing"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2351","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Families that move abroad for work","prompt":"Nowadays, many parents work abroad and take their families with them. Do the benefits of this trend for families outweigh the drawbacks?","answer":"Skilled workers now move across borders routinely, and many take partners and children with them rather than leaving them behind. On balance I believe the benefits outweigh the drawbacks, largely because the alternative is usually separation.\n\nThe financial gain is the reason such moves are made. Higher salaries abroad lift a family's standard of living immediately and often fund education or property at home. The professional benefit matters too, since experience in a larger market advances a career in a way that years of local work would not.\n\nThe advantages for children may be greater still. They grow up bilingual without effort, attend schools that are frequently better resourced, and learn early that people can live differently without either side being wrong, which is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an understanding that is difficult to teach any other way</strong>. Access to healthcare and safety can also be decisive for a family leaving a country in difficulty.\n\nThe drawbacks are real. Children lose grandparents, cousins and the friendships they had built, and repeated moves leave some with no strong attachment anywhere. Language and cultural gaps can isolate them in the first years, and a gap frequently opens between children who adapt quickly and parents who do not. The accompanying partner often suffers most, giving up a career for a visa that does not permit work, which strains the marriage. Elderly relatives left behind are a permanent worry. These costs are heavy but largely manageable, and they compare with a worse alternative: the parent who works abroad alone. Families divided for years suffer far more, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">children grow up knowing a parent through a screen</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, moving abroad together costs a family its roots and often one partner's career. Higher income, better schooling and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeping the family in one place</strong> outweigh those losses, particularly when the alternative is long separation.","phrases":[{"en":"an understanding that is difficult to teach any other way","context":"They grow up bilingual without effort, attend schools that are frequently better resourced, and learn early that people can live differently without either side being wrong, which is an understanding that is difficult to teach any other way."},{"en":"children grow up knowing a parent through a screen","context":"Families divided for years suffer far more, since children grow up knowing a parent through a screen."},{"en":"keeping the family in one place","context":"In conclusion, moving abroad together costs a family its roots and often one partner's career. Higher income, better schooling and keeping the family in one place outweigh those losses, particularly when the alternative is long separation."}],"subtype":"Advantages / Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#061","summary":"Whether working overseas with the family brings more benefits than drawbacks"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2369","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"The end of the extended family","prompt":"In many nations, people in huge cities live alone or in small family units instead of in large, extended family groups. Is this a positive or negative trend?","answer":"Households in large cities have shrunk from several generations to two people or one. The change brings genuine freedom, but I regard it as negative overall because of what it removes from the young and the old at the same time.\n\nThe advantages are easy to identify. Living apart from an extended family allows people to choose their work, their partner and their beliefs without constant supervision, and it has freed women in particular from obligations that were assumed rather than chosen. Small households reduce friction, since disagreements between generations about money, religion and child-rearing are among the commonest sources of unhappiness. Practically, small units also move easily, which matters where work requires it, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a couple can accept a job in another city without negotiating with a dozen relatives</strong>.\n\nThe losses are heavier than they first appear. Extended families provided childcare, so young parents now pay for what was once shared, and the cost keeps many from having children at all. They also cared for the elderly, and their absence has produced widespread loneliness among older people and a growing bill for residential care that states struggle to meet. Children lose the daily presence of grandparents, from whom they learn a longer view of life. A small household is fragile too, because illness, redundancy or divorce leaves nobody to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">absorb the shock the way a larger group could</strong>.\n\nThere is a wider social effect. Cities of solitary households consume more housing, energy and goods per person, and loneliness has become common enough among young adults to appear in health statistics.\n\nIn conclusion, smaller households have given people freedom that most would not surrender. The trend is nonetheless negative, because it has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">transferred care from families to money</strong>, and many households cannot afford the exchange.","phrases":[{"en":"a couple can accept a job in another city without negotiating with a dozen relatives","context":"Practically, small units also move easily, which matters where work requires it, and a couple can accept a job in another city without negotiating with a dozen relatives."},{"en":"absorb the shock the way a larger group could","context":"A small household is fragile too, because illness, redundancy or divorce leaves nobody to absorb the shock the way a larger group could."},{"en":"transferred care from families to money","context":"In conclusion, smaller households have given people freedom that most would not surrender. The trend is nonetheless negative, because it has transferred care from families to money, and many households cannot afford the exchange."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#062","summary":"Whether living alone or in small units rather than extended families is good"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2377","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Compulsory parenting classes","prompt":"Some people believe that the best way to ensure a happy family life for children is to make mothers and fathers take part in parenting courses every year. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Raising a child is the most demanding task most adults undertake, and almost nobody receives any training for it. I agree that parenting courses help, but I disagree that compulsory annual attendance is the best way to secure a happy family life.\n\nGood courses do work. Parents learn that a two-year-old's tantrums are normal development rather than defiance, which alone prevents a great deal of anger, and they are given alternatives to shouting and hitting that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">actually change a child's behaviour rather than only stopping it</strong>. Programmes for parents of difficult children have measurably reduced conflict, and courses also connect isolated parents with others facing the same week, which is often the greater benefit.\n\nCompulsion is the problem. Attendance is not the same as learning, and adults required to sit in a room every year will do so resentfully while ignoring the content, particularly if they feel their competence is being questioned. The practical obstacles are severe: parents working shifts or two jobs cannot attend, and enforcing it would need childcare, time off and an inspectorate that no country would fund. Annual repetition also treats parenting as a licence to be renewed rather than a skill, and a family in real difficulty needs <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sustained help rather than a yearly session</strong>.\n\nThere is a deeper objection. Most unhappiness in families comes from poverty, overcrowding, insecure work and untreated illness, not from ignorance of technique. A parent working two jobs knows perfectly well that they should read to their child; what they lack is the hour. Courses cannot <strong class=\"key-phrase\">teach a household out of the conditions it lives in</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, parenting education is valuable and should be free, welcoming and widely available, particularly around a first birth. Making it compulsory every year would produce attendance rather than better parenting, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the money would achieve more spent on the pressures families are under</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"actually change a child's behaviour rather than only stopping it","context":"Parents learn that a two-year-old's tantrums are normal development rather than defiance, which alone prevents a great deal of anger, and they are given alternatives to shouting and hitting that actually change a child's behaviour rather than only stopping it."},{"en":"sustained help rather than a yearly session","context":"Annual repetition also treats parenting as a licence to be renewed rather than a skill, and a family in real difficulty needs sustained help rather than a yearly session."},{"en":"teach a household out of the conditions it lives in","context":"Courses cannot teach a household out of the conditions it lives in."},{"en":"the money would achieve more spent on the pressures families are under","context":"Making it compulsory every year would produce attendance rather than better parenting, and the money would achieve more spent on the pressures families are under."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#063","summary":"Whether requiring parents to attend annual parenting courses would help children"},{"id":"writing-task-2-011","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"The difficulties of city life","prompt":"More and more people are migrating to cities in search of a better life, but city life can be extremely difficult. Explain some of the difficulties of living in a city. How can governments make urban life better for everyone?","answer":"Cities are often seen as places of opportunity, but there are also some major drawbacks of living in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a large metropolis</strong>. In my opinion, governments could do much more to improve city life for the average inhabitant.\n\nThe main problem for anyone who hopes to migrate to a large city is that the cost of living is likely to be much higher than it is in a small town or village. Inhabitants of cities have to pay higher prices for housing, transport, and even food. Another issue is that urban areas tend to suffer from social problems such as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">high crime and poverty rates</strong> in comparison with rural areas. Air quality in cities is also often poor, due to pollution from traffic, and the streets and public transport systems are usually overcrowded. As a result, city life can be unhealthy and stressful.\n\nHowever, there are various steps that governments could take to tackle these problems. Firstly, they could invest money in the building of affordable or social housing to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce the cost of living</strong>. Secondly, politicians have the power to ban vehicles from city centres and promote the use of cleaner public transport, which would help to reduce both air pollution and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">traffic congestion</strong>. In London, for example, the introduction of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a congestion charge</strong> for drivers has helped to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">curb the traffic problem</strong>. A third option would be to develop provincial towns and rural areas, by moving industry and jobs to those regions, in order to reduce the pressure on major cities.\n\nIn conclusion, governments could certainly implement a range of measures to enhance the quality of life for all city residents.","phrases":[{"en":"a large metropolis","context":"Cities are often seen as places of opportunity, but there are also some major drawbacks of living in a large metropolis. In my opinion, governments could do much more to improve city life for the average inhabitant."},{"en":"high crime and poverty rates","context":"Another issue is that urban areas tend to suffer from social problems such as high crime and poverty rates in comparison with rural areas."},{"en":"reduce the cost of living","context":"Firstly, they could invest money in the building of affordable or social housing to reduce the cost of living."},{"en":"traffic congestion","context":"Secondly, politicians have the power to ban vehicles from city centres and promote the use of cleaner public transport, which would help to reduce both air pollution and traffic congestion."},{"en":"a congestion charge","context":"In London, for example, the introduction of a congestion charge for drivers has helped to curb the traffic problem."},{"en":"curb the traffic problem","context":"In London, for example, the introduction of a congestion charge for drivers has helped to curb the traffic problem."}],"subtype":"Causes / Effects / Solutions","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#064","summary":"Urban living challenges and solutions"},{"id":"writing-task-2-012","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Moving from the countryside to the city","prompt":"Many countries today are experiencing high levels of migration from rural areas to cities. What are the causes of this trend, and what effects does it have on the existing city dwellers? Give reasons for your answer, and provide ideas and examples from your own experience. You should write at least 250 words.","answer":"Migration from the countryside to cities is certainly a topical issue, as so many nations are seeing this pattern today. There are three main causes, and two key impacts, which I will outline now.\n\nThe main cause is probably the lack of employment available to rural people. Rural industries, ranging from agriculture to food production and animal breeding, have all been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rationalised and made far more efficient</strong> in recent years, meaning that fewer people are needed to staff them. At the same time, the growth of employment opportunities in the cities means that people, especially youngsters, are tempted to move there by higher wages and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the prospect of reliable work</strong>. Finally, many countries still have a weaker infrastructure in rural areas, especially in terms of transport and economic activity. For example, if a country dweller is unable to find public transport to get to work locally, that person will inevitably consider moving to a city where the infrastructure is far better established.\n\nRegarding the effects felt by the urban residents, perhaps the main impact is on accommodation. An increased urban population <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drives up the cost of buying or renting a home</strong>, leading to possible hardship for people who had assumed that prices would remain stable. We can see this clearly in major conurbations such as London, Sao Paolo or Delhi, where property prices are extremely high and continue to rise. The other main effect on the existing city population is the vastly increased competition for job opportunities, especially at a less skilled level. For instance, drivers, shop workers and hospitality staff find themselves competing for work with new arrivals who are often prepared to work for less. This can lead to a reduction in wages and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consequent decline in standard of living</strong>, especially when combined with the increased cost of housing.\n\nTo sum up, the causes of this trend are primarily to do with employment, and the effects are felt in terms of wages and accommodation.","phrases":[{"en":"rationalised and made far more efficient","context":"Rural industries, ranging from agriculture to food production and animal breeding, have all been rationalised and made far more efficient in recent years, meaning that fewer people are needed to staff them."},{"en":"the prospect of reliable work","context":"At the same time, the growth of employment opportunities in the cities means that people, especially youngsters, are tempted to move there by higher wages and the prospect of reliable work."},{"en":"drives up the cost of buying or renting a home","context":"An increased urban population drives up the cost of buying or renting a home, leading to possible hardship for people who had assumed that prices would remain stable."},{"en":"consequent decline in standard of living","context":"This can lead to a reduction in wages and consequent decline in standard of living, especially when combined with the increased cost of housing."}],"subtype":"Causes / Effects","_displayNo":"#065","summary":"Countries today are experiencing high levels of migration from rural areas…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-013","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Overcrowded cities and what to do about them","prompt":"Overpopulation of urban areas has led to numerous problems. Identify one or two serious ones and suggest ways that governments and individuals can tackle these problems.","answer":"Rapid urban population growth can generate economic opportunity, but when housing and infrastructure fail to expand at the same pace, cities become less liveable. The most serious consequences are insecure housing and overloaded transport, both of which require action from governments as well as residents.\n\nA shortage of affordable homes forces low-income households into overcrowded or informal accommodation. Such housing may lack ventilation, sanitation or legal protection, exposing residents to illness and sudden eviction. At the same time, excessive demand for road space and public transport produces long commutes, air pollution and lost productivity. These pressures are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mutually reinforcing</strong>: families pushed to distant suburbs often spend more time and money travelling to work, and this ends up <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deepening existing social inequality</strong>.\n\nGovernments should first increase the supply of well-connected, affordable housing rather than simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">moving poorer residents to the urban fringe</strong>. Planning rules can require new developments to include lower-cost units, while public investment should extend water, schools and clinics alongside housing construction. Cities also need high-capacity public transport <strong class=\"key-phrase\">integrated with land-use planning</strong>. Reliable rail and bus networks allow denser neighbourhoods to function without proportional growth in private car use.\n\nIndividuals cannot solve structural shortages, but they can contribute. Residents can use public transport, share vehicles and support local planning proposals that increase housing density near employment centres. Community organisations can also identify unsafe properties, assist tenants and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hold local authorities accountable for implementation</strong>. These actions are most effective when citizens have access to transparent data and formal channels for participation.\n\nIn conclusion, urban overpopulation becomes damaging when housing and transport systems are neglected. Governments must lead through coordinated investment and planning, while individuals can reduce pressure on infrastructure and demand that public commitments are fulfilled.","phrases":[{"en":"deepening existing social inequality","context":"These pressures are mutually reinforcing: families pushed to distant suburbs often spend more time and money travelling to work, and this ends up deepening existing social inequality."},{"en":"hold local authorities accountable for implementation","context":"Community organisations can also identify unsafe properties, assist tenants and hold local authorities accountable for implementation."},{"en":"mutually reinforcing","context":"These pressures are mutually reinforcing: families pushed to distant suburbs often spend more time and money travelling to work, and this ends up deepening existing social inequality."},{"en":"moving poorer residents to the urban fringe","context":"Governments should first increase the supply of well-connected, affordable housing rather than simply moving poorer residents to the urban fringe."},{"en":"integrated with land-use planning","context":"Cities also need high-capacity public transport integrated with land-use planning."}],"subtype":"Problems and solutions","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#066","summary":"Urban overpopulation: problems and solutions"},{"id":"writing-task-2-037","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Should a building be useful or beautiful?","prompt":"It is more important for a building to serve its purposes than to look beautiful. Architects do not need to worry about whether it is a real work of art. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"A building must first be safe and useful, but this does not mean that appearance is unimportant. I disagree with the idea that architects should ignore beauty. Good architecture combines function, cost and visual quality rather than treating them as separate goals.\n\nPractical requirements are clearly essential. A hospital needs efficient routes for patients and staff, a school needs suitable classrooms, and housing must be safe, affordable and easy to maintain. A beautiful design that creates dark rooms, poor access or excessive construction costs <strong class=\"key-phrase\">has failed its users</strong>. Architects must therefore begin with the needs of the people who use the building.\n\nNevertheless, appearance also affects daily life. Well-designed streets and public buildings can make people feel proud of their neighbourhood and encourage them to care for shared spaces. Natural light, balanced proportions and appropriate materials may also improve comfort without adding unreasonable cost. In addition, distinctive buildings can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strengthen a city's identity</strong> and attract visitors or investment. Beauty is not merely decoration when it contributes to wellbeing and a sense of place.\n\nThe best results come from integrating both aims from the start. Simple, durable materials can be attractive, and an efficient layout can still have character. For this reason, design competitions and planning rules should reward buildings that remain useful for decades, use resources efficiently and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">contribute positively to the public realm</strong>; such standards show that visual quality can be achieved without sacrificing affordability or function.\n\nIn conclusion, function should have priority where safety or affordability is at stake, but architects should also seek <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a practical balance between usefulness and beauty</strong>. A building serves society best when it works well and improves its surroundings.","phrases":[{"en":"a practical balance between usefulness and beauty","context":"In conclusion, function should have priority where safety or affordability is at stake, but architects should also seek a practical balance between usefulness and beauty. A building serves society best when it works well and improves its surroundings."},{"en":"has failed its users","context":"A beautiful design that creates dark rooms, poor access or excessive construction costs has failed its users."},{"en":"strengthen a city's identity","context":"In addition, distinctive buildings can strengthen a city's identity and attract visitors or investment."},{"en":"contribute positively to the public realm","context":"For this reason, design competitions and planning rules should reward buildings that remain useful for decades, use resources efficiently and contribute positively to the public realm; such standards show that visual quality can be achieved without sacrificing affordability or function."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#067","summary":"It is more important for a building to serve its purposes than to look…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-043","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Should new buildings copy traditional styles?","prompt":"Some people believe that new buildings should be built in traditional styles. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this opinion?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that new buildings should be constructed in the traditional style of the country in which they are built. I disagree with this view, because although historic architecture is certainly worth protecting, imitating it indefinitely would leave cities with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no character of their own</strong>.\n\nThe argument for traditional design is understandable. A city whose buildings share a consistent style has a visual identity that visitors recognise immediately, and a modern glass tower inserted into a row of old houses can genuinely spoil a street. Traditional materials such as stone, brick and timber also age well, whereas much of the concrete used in the 1960s has deteriorated badly.\n\nNevertheless, this approach has serious weaknesses. Every style that we now call traditional was modern when it was built, and the districts we admire are valued precisely because each generation <strong class=\"key-phrase\">added something of its own</strong>. A rule requiring imitation would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">freeze a city at an arbitrary date</strong> and produce buildings that are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">neither genuinely old nor honestly new</strong>. There is also a practical objection: traditional construction depends on skills and materials that are now scarce and expensive, so it could never be applied to housing estates, hospitals or schools.\n\nModern buildings can also do things that older ones cannot. Contemporary design allows far better insulation, natural light and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">access for disabled people</strong>, and these matter more to the people who use a building every day than its exterior does.\n\nIn conclusion, historic buildings should certainly be preserved, but I disagree that new ones must copy them. New architecture should respect the scale and materials of its surroundings rather than reproduce the past.","phrases":[{"en":"no character of their own","context":"I disagree with this view, because although historic architecture is certainly worth protecting, imitating it indefinitely would leave cities with no character of their own."},{"en":"added something of its own","context":"Every style that we now call traditional was modern when it was built, and the districts we admire are valued precisely because each generation added something of its own."},{"en":"access for disabled people","context":"Modern buildings can also do things that older ones cannot. Contemporary design allows far better insulation, natural light and access for disabled people, and these matter more to the people who use a building every day than its exterior does."},{"en":"freeze a city at an arbitrary date","context":"A rule requiring imitation would freeze a city at an arbitrary date and produce buildings that are neither genuinely old nor honestly new."},{"en":"neither genuinely old nor honestly new","context":"A rule requiring imitation would freeze a city at an arbitrary date and produce buildings that are neither genuinely old nor honestly new."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#068","summary":"Should new buildings copy traditional styles?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-108","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"The growth of large cities","prompt":"Cities around the world are growing rapidly. What are the causes of this trend, and what consequences might it have?","answer":"Cities in almost every country are expanding rapidly, and in several regions the population of a single urban area now exceeds that of a small nation. This growth has clear causes, and its consequences are both beneficial and damaging.\n\nThe principal cause is economic. Employment is concentrated in cities, wages are higher, and rural work has declined as farming has become mechanised, so millions move in search of a living. Cities also offer services the countryside cannot provide: universities, specialist hospitals and better schools. A further factor is that urban populations grow from within, since migrants are typically young adults who then have families of their own, so growth continues even when new arrivals slow.\n\nSome consequences are positive. Concentrating people makes public transport, water supply and healthcare <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far cheaper to provide per person</strong>, and cities generate the majority of national income and tax revenue. The variety of people living closely together also produces <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the exchange of ideas</strong> on which new industries depend.\n\nThe problems, however, are severe. Housing cannot be built quickly enough, so prices rise and the poorest end up in overcrowded or informal settlements. Roads congest, air quality deteriorates, and demand for water and electricity <strong class=\"key-phrase\">outstrips supply</strong>. Rural areas suffer the opposite difficulty, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">losing the young workers who would have supported them</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, urban growth is driven mainly by the concentration of work and services, and it is unlikely to reverse. It brings genuine efficiency, but without serious investment in housing and public transport the costs will steadily <strong class=\"key-phrase\">outweigh the advantages</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"the exchange of ideas","context":"The variety of people living closely together also produces the exchange of ideas on which new industries depend."},{"en":"outstrips supply","context":"Roads congest, air quality deteriorates, and demand for water and electricity outstrips supply."},{"en":"outweigh the advantages","context":"In conclusion, urban growth is driven mainly by the concentration of work and services, and it is unlikely to reverse. It brings genuine efficiency, but without serious investment in housing and public transport the costs will steadily outweigh the advantages."},{"en":"far cheaper to provide per person","context":"Concentrating people makes public transport, water supply and healthcare far cheaper to provide per person, and cities generate the majority of national income and tax revenue."},{"en":"losing the young workers who would have supported them","context":"Rural areas suffer the opposite difficulty, losing the young workers who would have supported them."}],"subtype":"Causes / Effects","_displayNo":"#069","summary":"The growth of large cities"},{"id":"writing-task-2-155","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Knocking down old buildings","prompt":"People think that old buildings should be knocked down and give way to the new buildings. Do you agree or disagree? How important are old buildings to us?","answer":"Some old buildings should be protected, while others should be replaced when they are unsafe or no longer useful. I therefore disagree with the idea that age alone is a reason either to preserve or demolish a structure. Decisions should be based on historical value, condition and the needs of the surrounding community.\n\nImportant old buildings connect people with the history of a place. A traditional market, theatre or government building may show how earlier generations lived and what materials or techniques they used. Such structures can strengthen local identity, support tourism and provide architects with practical examples of earlier design. Once a unique building is destroyed, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">its cultural value cannot be recreated</strong> by a modern copy.\n\nNevertheless, preservation has limits. Some buildings are structurally dangerous, extremely expensive to maintain or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">poorly suited to modern needs</strong>. Keeping every ageing structure can prevent the construction of housing, schools or transport links in areas where land is scarce. In these cases, demolition may be justified after an independent assessment. Useful parts of the original building, such as a façade or significant interior features, can sometimes be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">incorporated into a new development</strong>.\n\nThe best policy is a clear classification system. Buildings of genuine architectural or historical importance should receive legal protection and realistic maintenance funding. Ordinary structures should be assessed more flexibly, with public consultation and a full comparison of renovation and replacement costs. This creates a balance between heritage and present-day needs.\n\nIn conclusion, old buildings are important when they carry distinctive cultural, historical or design value. Others may reasonably give way to safer and more useful development, provided that the decision is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">based on evidence rather than short-term profit</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"its cultural value cannot be recreated","context":"Once a unique building is destroyed, its cultural value cannot be recreated by a modern copy."},{"en":"poorly suited to modern needs","context":"Some buildings are structurally dangerous, extremely expensive to maintain or poorly suited to modern needs."},{"en":"incorporated into a new development","context":"Useful parts of the original building, such as a façade or significant interior features, can sometimes be incorporated into a new development."},{"en":"based on evidence rather than short-term profit","context":"In conclusion, old buildings are important when they carry distinctive cultural, historical or design value. Others may reasonably give way to safer and more useful development, provided that the decision is based on evidence rather than short-term profit."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#070","summary":"People think that old buildings should be knocked down and give way to the…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2198","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Preserving historic buildings","prompt":"Some people think that certain old buildings that are historically important in cities or the countryside should be preserved. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Some people argue that old buildings of historical significance, whether in urban or rural areas, should be protected rather than demolished. I completely agree with this view, for both cultural and economic reasons.\n\nThe main argument for preservation is that historic buildings are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a physical record of the past</strong>. Written history can be rewritten or forgotten, but a medieval church or a colonial town hall shows later generations exactly how earlier societies lived, built and worshipped. Once such a building is knocked down, that evidence is lost permanently, and no reconstruction can genuinely replace it. Countries that cleared their old quarters during periods of rapid development now find that their cities <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look almost identical to every other modern city</strong> in the world.\n\nPreservation also makes economic sense. Historic architecture attracts enormous numbers of visitors, and tourism supports hotels, restaurants, transport and thousands of local jobs. Cities such as Prague and Kyoto owe much of their income to buildings that could easily have been replaced by offices. Old structures can also be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">adapted rather than replaced</strong>: former factories and warehouses are now used as museums, apartments and workspaces, which is often cheaper and more environmentally friendly than demolition and new construction.\n\nThe obvious objection is cost, since restoration requires specialist skills and materials. However, this expense is small compared with the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">permanent loss of a country's heritage</strong>, and much of it can be recovered through ticket sales and tourist spending.\n\nIn conclusion, I strongly agree that historically important buildings should be preserved, because they connect people with their past and bring lasting economic benefits.","phrases":[{"en":"a physical record of the past","context":"The main argument for preservation is that historic buildings are a physical record of the past."},{"en":"adapted rather than replaced","context":"Old structures can also be adapted rather than replaced: former factories and warehouses are now used as museums, apartments and workspaces, which is often cheaper and more environmentally friendly than demolition and new construction."},{"en":"permanent loss of a country's heritage","context":"The obvious objection is cost, since restoration requires specialist skills and materials. However, this expense is small compared with the permanent loss of a country's heritage, and much of it can be recovered through ticket sales and tourist spending."},{"en":"look almost identical to every other modern city","context":"Countries that cleared their old quarters during periods of rapid development now find that their cities look almost identical to every other modern city in the world."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#071","summary":"Preserving historic buildings"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2203","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Public spaces in towns and cities","prompt":"It is important for all towns and cities to have large public spaces such as squares and parks. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this statement?","answer":"It is often argued that every town and city should provide substantial open areas such as parks and public squares. I completely agree with this view, because such spaces improve residents' health, strengthen communities and make urban environments far more liveable.\n\nThe health benefits are the most obvious. City dwellers spend most of the day indoors and seated, and a nearby park is the cheapest and most accessible place to walk, run, cycle or play sport. Free access matters enormously, since low-income families cannot afford gym memberships. Green space also improves air quality and lowers summer temperatures, which is increasingly important as cities grow hotter, and there is now considerable evidence that regular contact with nature reduces stress and anxiety.\n\nPublic spaces also perform a social function that no private facility can replace. A square or park is one of the very few places where people of different ages, incomes and backgrounds meet <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without having to spend money</strong>. Markets, festivals, concerts and public celebrations all require open ground, and communities that have such places tend to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more cohesive</strong> and less isolated.\n\nThe usual objection is that land in city centres is extremely expensive and could be used for housing or offices. This is a genuine difficulty, but it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">short-sighted</strong>. Property near parks consistently commands higher prices, and cities famous for their open spaces, such as London and New York, have found that these areas <strong class=\"key-phrase\">repay their cost many times over</strong> through tourism, health savings and residential appeal.\n\nIn conclusion, I strongly agree that large public spaces are essential, and planners should protect them even when <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the pressure to build is intense</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"more cohesive","context":"Markets, festivals, concerts and public celebrations all require open ground, and communities that have such places tend to be more cohesive and less isolated."},{"en":"short-sighted","context":"This is a genuine difficulty, but it is short-sighted."},{"en":"repay their cost many times over","context":"Property near parks consistently commands higher prices, and cities famous for their open spaces, such as London and New York, have found that these areas repay their cost many times over through tourism, health savings and residential appeal."},{"en":"without having to spend money","context":"A square or park is one of the very few places where people of different ages, incomes and backgrounds meet without having to spend money."},{"en":"the pressure to build is intense","context":"In conclusion, I strongly agree that large public spaces are essential, and planners should protect them even when the pressure to build is intense."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#072","summary":"Public spaces in towns and cities"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2247","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Building outwards or upwards","prompt":"Some people think that it is best to live in a horizontal city while others think of a vertical city. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"As populations grow, cities must decide whether to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spread outwards in low buildings or upwards in towers</strong>. Both models have committed supporters, and my own view is that the vertical approach is the more responsible one for large cities.\n\nThose who prefer low-rise living point to what happens at ground level. Streets of houses two or three storeys high keep front doors, gardens and pavements close together, so neighbours meet and children can play outside within sight of a window. Such districts are also easier to adapt, since a family can extend a house as it grows. Towers, by contrast, can leave residents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">isolated behind a lift door</strong>, with no space of their own outdoors.\n\nThe argument for building upwards is a matter of arithmetic. Housing the same population across a wider area means longer journeys, more roads, more pipes and more cable, all of which cost money and produce emissions, and it consumes farmland at the edges. Density does the opposite: where enough people live within walking distance, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shops, clinics and a bus route become viable</strong>, and a resident may not need a car at all. Compact cities consistently record lower emissions per person than sprawling ones.\n\nMy view is that the objections to towers describe bad design rather than height itself. Buildings with generous shared gardens, ground-floor shops and well-maintained lifts produce sociable neighbourhoods, whereas towers surrounded by car parks <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fail for reasons that have nothing to do with the storeys</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, low-rise districts are pleasant but cannot house growing populations without consuming the countryside. Building upwards is preferable, provided cities invest in the shared spaces that make density <strong class=\"key-phrase\">liveable rather than merely efficient</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"isolated behind a lift door","context":"Towers, by contrast, can leave residents isolated behind a lift door, with no space of their own outdoors."},{"en":"fail for reasons that have nothing to do with the storeys","context":"Buildings with generous shared gardens, ground-floor shops and well-maintained lifts produce sociable neighbourhoods, whereas towers surrounded by car parks fail for reasons that have nothing to do with the storeys."},{"en":"liveable rather than merely efficient","context":"In conclusion, low-rise districts are pleasant but cannot house growing populations without consuming the countryside. Building upwards is preferable, provided cities invest in the shared spaces that make density liveable rather than merely efficient."},{"en":"spread outwards in low buildings or upwards in towers","context":"As populations grow, cities must decide whether to spread outwards in low buildings or upwards in towers. Both models have committed supporters, and my own view is that the vertical approach is the more responsible one for large cities."},{"en":"shops, clinics and a bus route become viable","context":"Density does the opposite: where enough people live within walking distance, shops, clinics and a bus route become viable, and a resident may not need a car at all."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#073","summary":"Low-rise cities compared with high-rise cities"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2313","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Moving companies out of the cities","prompt":"Traffic and housing problems could be solved by moving large companies, factories and their employees to the countryside. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Congested roads and unaffordable housing are the standard complaints of every large city, and moving employers out of them sounds like a direct cure. I agree that relocation would ease both problems, but I disagree that it would solve them, and it would create new difficulties of its own.\n\nThe logic behind the idea is sound as far as it goes. Traffic and housing pressure are both consequences of too many people needing to be in one place, so removing a large employer removes thousands of daily journeys and thousands of competing tenants at once. Rural areas gain from the same movement, since a factory brings wages, customers for local shops and a reason for young people to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay in the town they grew up in</strong>.\n\nThe plan is weaker than it looks, however. Firms cluster in cities because they need suppliers, specialists, airports and each other, and a company that moves alone loses those advantages and often loses staff, since employees have partners with careers and children in school. Rural roads and railways are also thin, so a relocated workforce <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drives where it used to take a train</strong>, and the traffic reappears in a less efficient form. Housing pressure tends to follow the jobs rather than disappear, which is how expensive commuter towns are created.\n\nMore effective measures work on the city itself: building densely near stations, taxing empty property, improving public transport and allowing people to work from home for part of the week. These <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce the need to travel without dismantling what makes a city productive</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, moving some employers out of crowded cities is a reasonable part of a wider plan, particularly for factories that need space rather than proximity. On its own it would relocate the problems rather than remove them.","phrases":[{"en":"stay in the town they grew up in","context":"Rural areas gain from the same movement, since a factory brings wages, customers for local shops and a reason for young people to stay in the town they grew up in."},{"en":"drives where it used to take a train","context":"Rural roads and railways are also thin, so a relocated workforce drives where it used to take a train, and the traffic reappears in a less efficient form."},{"en":"reduce the need to travel without dismantling what makes a city productive","context":"These reduce the need to travel without dismantling what makes a city productive."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#074","summary":"Whether relocating firms to the countryside would solve traffic and housing problems"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2332","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Trees or houses on urban land?","prompt":"Some people say it is more important to plant trees in the open spaces in towns and cities than to build more housing. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Cities with a housing shortage and a heat problem must decide what their remaining open land is for. I disagree that planting should take priority, because homes meet a need that nothing else can substitute, though the two aims are less opposed than the question suggests.\n\nTrees earn their place. They lower street temperatures by several degrees in summer, which now prevents deaths in cities that were not built for the heat they experience. They absorb rainfall that would otherwise flood drains, filter pollution and reduce noise, and access to greenery has a measurable effect on mental health and recovery from illness. A city without them is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">harder to live in and more expensive to run</strong>.\n\nHousing, however, addresses something more urgent. People priced out of a city do not simply live elsewhere pleasantly; they commute for hours, share overcrowded flats or become homeless, and a shortage pushes rents up for everyone. Overcrowding damages health and children's schooling directly. A person without a home <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gains nothing from the park they cannot afford to live beside</strong>, and where the shortage is severe the claim of housing is stronger.\n\nThe real error is treating the choice as unavoidable. Cities waste enormous quantities of land on car parking, empty offices and derelict industrial sites, and building on those instead protects parks entirely. Density also helps: flats with courtyards and street trees house far more people per hectare than low houses with private gardens, so it is possible to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">add homes and greenery at the same time</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, trees are essential to a liveable city and should never be treated as decoration. Where a genuine housing shortage exists, homes come first, and the answer is to build on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">land that is already wasted</strong> rather than on the parks.","phrases":[{"en":"harder to live in and more expensive to run","context":"A city without them is harder to live in and more expensive to run."},{"en":"gains nothing from the park they cannot afford to live beside","context":"A person without a home gains nothing from the park they cannot afford to live beside, and where the shortage is severe the claim of housing is stronger."},{"en":"add homes and greenery at the same time","context":"Density also helps: flats with courtyards and street trees house far more people per hectare than low houses with private gardens, so it is possible to add homes and greenery at the same time."},{"en":"land that is already wasted","context":"In conclusion, trees are essential to a liveable city and should never be treated as decoration. Where a genuine housing shortage exists, homes come first, and the answer is to build on land that is already wasted rather than on the parks."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#075","summary":"Whether open urban space should be planted rather than built on"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2352","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Turning parks into vegetable gardens","prompt":"In some cities, public parks and open spaces are being turned into gardens where local residents can grow their own fruit and vegetables. Do the advantages of this development outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"Allotments and community plots have reappeared in cities where land is scarce and food is expensive. I believe the advantages outweigh the disadvantages, provided the gardens take only part of the open space available.\n\nThe clearest benefit is what growing food does for the people doing it. Gardening is steady physical activity that continues into old age, and it produces fresh vegetables at a cost that matters to households on low incomes. The mental effect is well documented, since working with plants reduces stress and gives structure to the days of the retired and the unemployed. Community plots also bring neighbours into contact who would otherwise never speak, and shared work <strong class=\"key-phrase\">builds the kind of familiarity that makes a street feel safe</strong>.\n\nThere are wider gains. Children who have grown a carrot understand where food comes from and eat vegetables more willingly. Food grown a few streets away needs no transport or packaging, and gardens absorb rainwater and support insects that a mown lawn does not.\n\nThe objections deserve attention. A garden is not a park: it excludes the football, running and casual sitting that open space allows, and plots are usually allocated to a limited number of households, so land that belonged to everybody now belongs to a few. Gardens also require commitment, and an abandoned plot quickly becomes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">waste ground that nobody wants to look at</strong>. Cities that are short of green space cannot afford to convert much of it. The balance therefore depends on scale. Converting a corner of a large park serves both purposes, while converting the whole of a district's only open space does not.\n\nIn conclusion, community gardens improve health, food access and neighbourly contact at very low cost. They deserve support wherever they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take a share of the open space rather than replacing it</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"builds the kind of familiarity that makes a street feel safe","context":"Community plots also bring neighbours into contact who would otherwise never speak, and shared work builds the kind of familiarity that makes a street feel safe."},{"en":"waste ground that nobody wants to look at","context":"Gardens also require commitment, and an abandoned plot quickly becomes waste ground that nobody wants to look at."},{"en":"take a share of the open space rather than replacing it","context":"In conclusion, community gardens improve health, food access and neighbourly contact at very low cost. They deserve support wherever they take a share of the open space rather than replacing it."}],"subtype":"Advantages / Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#076","summary":"Whether converting urban open space into community gardens is worthwhile"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2359","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Separating homes, shops and offices","prompt":"In many urban areas, planners tend to place shops, schools, offices and homes in separate zones. Do the benefits of this outweigh the drawbacks?","answer":"Twentieth-century planning divided cities into districts with a single purpose, and most new suburbs are still built that way. I believe the drawbacks now outweigh the benefits, though the practice began for reasons that made sense.\n\nSeparation was introduced to protect people from industry. Housing built beside factories meant noise, smoke and danger, and moving homes away from that was a genuine improvement in health. Zoning also allows infrastructure to be planned efficiently, since roads and drainage can be sized for what each district actually does, and it gives residential areas quiet evenings and predictable property values. Shopping centres and business parks benefit from clustering, because customers and suppliers find everything in one place.\n\nThe costs have grown as cities have spread. Separating uses guarantees travel: every trip to work, to school or to buy milk becomes a journey, usually by car, which produces the congestion and pollution that planners then attempt to solve with wider roads. Anyone without a car — the young, the old, the poor — is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stranded in a district that contains nothing they need</strong>. Single-use areas are also lifeless for most of the day, since office districts empty at six and suburbs empty at eight, and streets with nobody on them feel unsafe. Small businesses suffer too, because a corner shop cannot exist where no shops are permitted.\n\nMixed neighbourhoods answer most of this. Where flats sit above shops and offices stand near homes, daily needs are within walking distance, streets stay occupied throughout the day, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">children can reach a school without being driven</strong>. Modern industry is quiet enough for this to be safe.\n\nIn conclusion, zoning improved conditions when cities were polluted by heavy industry, and some separation remains sensible. It now forces unnecessary travel and empties streets, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mixing uses within a neighbourhood</strong> is the better principle for cities being built today.","phrases":[{"en":"stranded in a district that contains nothing they need","context":"Anyone without a car — the young, the old, the poor — is stranded in a district that contains nothing they need."},{"en":"children can reach a school without being driven","context":"Where flats sit above shops and offices stand near homes, daily needs are within walking distance, streets stay occupied throughout the day, and children can reach a school without being driven."},{"en":"mixing uses within a neighbourhood","context":"It now forces unnecessary travel and empties streets, so mixing uses within a neighbourhood is the better principle for cities being built today."}],"subtype":"Advantages / Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#077","summary":"Whether dividing a city into separate zones brings more benefits than drawbacks"},{"id":"writing-task-2-016","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Ex-prisoners talking to teenagers about crime","prompt":"Some people who have been in prison become good citizens later, and it is often argued that these are the best people to talk to teenagers about the dangers of committing a crime. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is true that ex-prisoners can become normal, productive members of society. I completely agree with the idea that allowing such people to speak to teenagers about their experiences is the best way to discourage them from breaking the law.\n\nIn my opinion, teenagers are more likely to accept advice from someone who can speak from experience. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Reformed offenders</strong> can tell young people about how they became involved in crime, the dangers of a criminal lifestyle, and what life in prison is really like. They can also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dispel any ideas</strong> that teenagers may have about criminals leading glamorous lives. While adolescents are often indifferent to the guidance given by older people, I imagine that most of them would be extremely keen to hear the stories of an ex-offender. The vivid and perhaps shocking nature of these stories is likely to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have a powerful impact</strong>.\n\nThe alternatives to using reformed criminals to educate teenagers about crime would be much less effective. One option would be for police officers to visit schools and talk to young people. This could be useful in terms of informing teens about what happens to lawbreakers when they are caught, but young people are often reluctant to take advice from figures of authority. A second option would be for school teachers to speak to their students about crime, but I doubt that students would see teachers as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">credible sources of information</strong> about this topic. Finally, educational films might be informative, but there would be no opportunity for young people to interact and ask questions.\n\nIn conclusion, I fully support the view that people who have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned their lives around</strong> after serving a prison sentence could help to deter teenagers from committing crimes.","phrases":[{"en":"Reformed offenders","context":"Reformed offenders can tell young people about how they became involved in crime, the dangers of a criminal lifestyle, and what life in prison is really like."},{"en":"have a powerful impact","context":"The vivid and perhaps shocking nature of these stories is likely to have a powerful impact."},{"en":"turned their lives around","context":"In conclusion, I fully support the view that people who have turned their lives around after serving a prison sentence could help to deter teenagers from committing crimes."},{"en":"dispel any ideas","context":"They can also dispel any ideas that teenagers may have about criminals leading glamorous lives."},{"en":"credible sources of information","context":"A second option would be for school teachers to speak to their students about crime, but I doubt that students would see teachers as credible sources of information about this topic."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#078","summary":"Ex-prisoners educating teenagers"},{"id":"writing-task-2-017","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Identity theft and its victims","prompt":"In many countries, financial crime involving identity theft is increasing. What are the causes of this trend, and what effect does identity theft have on the victims involved? Give reasons for your answer, and provide ideas and examples from your own experience. You should write at least 250 words.","answer":"There is no doubt that identity fraud is a great concern, especially in western countries where people are most at risk. I think there are two main causes, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a number of damaging effects</strong>.\n\nThe major cause is probably the almost universal use of digital technology to store financial data and undertake financial transactions. It is surprisingly easy for criminals to obtain personal information about a possible victim, and then to use this information to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">impersonate the target</strong>. Because bank accounts and loans can be applied for digitally, without the need for face-to-face contact, the criminals are able to apply for financial products remotely, often before the victim is even aware of the attack. The second key cause is, I believe, the increasing probability of ‘traditional’ crimes (such as armed robbery, mugging or burglary) being detected. When criminals see the widespread use of CCTV, DNA profiling and fingerprinting, they are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less inclined to indulge in such old-fashioned crimes</strong>. Rather, they prefer the lower risk and more profitable use of data-based crimes. The effects on the unfortunate victims can be quite devastating.\n\nFirstly, there is the loss of money from bank accounts or by being connected to a fraudulent loan. In some cases, this can take years for the victim to pay back or resolve with the banks and the authorities. Secondly, there is the damage to their credit rating, which means they may find it impossible to obtain legitimate credit in future. In extreme cases, people's employment prospects can even be damaged as well, as they find themselves <strong class=\"key-phrase\">profiled as a financial risk</strong>.\n\nOverall, the presence of technology and the decline of ‘traditional’ crimes are the key causes behind this trend. The effects on the victims involve long-term financial hardship in many instances.","phrases":[{"en":"a number of damaging effects","context":"There is no doubt that identity fraud is a great concern, especially in western countries where people are most at risk. I think there are two main causes, and a number of damaging effects."},{"en":"impersonate the target","context":"It is surprisingly easy for criminals to obtain personal information about a possible victim, and then to use this information to impersonate the target."},{"en":"less inclined to indulge in such old-fashioned crimes","context":"When criminals see the widespread use of CCTV, DNA profiling and fingerprinting, they are less inclined to indulge in such old-fashioned crimes."},{"en":"profiled as a financial risk","context":"In extreme cases, people's employment prospects can even be damaged as well, as they find themselves profiled as a financial risk."}],"subtype":"Causes / Effects","_displayNo":"#079","summary":"Financial crime involving identity theft is increasing."},{"id":"writing-task-2-018","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Do police patrols reduce crime?","prompt":"Having police officers patrolling the streets is often considered an essential way to reduce crime. How effective do you think police street patrols are? What other ways of reducing crime can you propose? Give reasons for your answer, and provide ideas and examples from your own experience. You should write at least 250 words.","answer":"We are all keen to reduce crime wherever possible, and the role of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">visible policing</strong> is a hotly debated part of this. Personally, I feel that street patrols are highly effective, although there are several <strong class=\"key-phrase\">supplementary methods</strong> too.\n\nRegarding the effectiveness of patrols, evidence suggests that an increase in police on the streets leads directly to a drop in crime, especially crimes of violence and robbery. A good example is the experience of New York when it adopted the ‘zero tolerance’ policy in the 1990s, a high-profile policing tactic which has been extended successfully to many other cities worldwide. When compared to other tactics that have been tried, ranging from citizen patrols to CCTV, there is little doubt that assertive police patrolling is the most effective resource we have.\n\nPatrols work best, however, when the courts and prisons back them up with coordinated action. An offender who is arrested but then <strong class=\"key-phrase\">given a lenient sentence</strong> has little reason to stop, so consistently deterrent sentencing and strict prison regimes matter almost as much as the patrols themselves.\n\nDetection matters just as much as prevention, so police forces also need well-funded detective and forensic departments to ensure that crimes which do occur are actually solved. Ordinary citizens and businesses have a part to play too: something as simple as better locks and outdoor lighting measurably reduces theft and burglary, and a street that looks cared for is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a less attractive target in the first place</strong>.\n\nAll in all, I feel that visible police patrols have been proved to be the most effective way to reduce common crimes. Their effectiveness can be maximised by supporting action from elsewhere in the police and justice system, and by private citizens doing their part as well.","phrases":[{"en":"visible policing","context":"We are all keen to reduce crime wherever possible, and the role of visible policing is a hotly debated part of this. Personally, I feel that street patrols are highly effective, although there are several supplementary methods too."},{"en":"supplementary methods","context":"We are all keen to reduce crime wherever possible, and the role of visible policing is a hotly debated part of this. Personally, I feel that street patrols are highly effective, although there are several supplementary methods too."},{"en":"given a lenient sentence","context":"An offender who is arrested but then given a lenient sentence has little reason to stop, so consistently deterrent sentencing and strict prison regimes matter almost as much as the patrols themselves."},{"en":"a less attractive target in the first place","context":"Ordinary citizens and businesses have a part to play too: something as simple as better locks and outdoor lighting measurably reduces theft and burglary, and a street that looks cared for is a less attractive target in the first place."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#080","summary":"Having police officers patrolling the streets is often considered an…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-019","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Is prison the only effective punishment?","prompt":"“Prison is the only truly effective form of punishment, because it separates criminals from society.” To what extent do you support this view?","answer":"There is no doubt that we need to find effective forms of punishment for criminals, and also find ways to protect citizens from the threat of crime. I personally feel that prison is only one of a range of options we should use, and is not always the most effective.\n\nFirstly, I do not accept that all criminals need to be separated from society. Juvenile and minor offenders can be punished through fines, community service or other <strong class=\"key-phrase\">non-custodial means</strong>. This is cheaper than prison, and prevents the damage to the criminal's family which in itself could generate further social problems.\n\nThere is a second benefit. By keeping these small-scale criminals in society, we can try to rehabilitate them, for example through training or education programmes. This will help to reduce re-offending in future. Besides this, if offenders are believed to be a threat to their fellow citizens, there are ways of containing them such as electronic tagging, curfews or regular reporting to the police. Such methods have been shown to be effective in reducing criminal behaviour and are themselves quite a strong punishment, as they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">restrict an offender's freedom greatly</strong>.\n\nFinally, we should remember that prisons are to some extent ‘<strong class=\"key-phrase\">schools of crime</strong>’ and in many cases prisoners end their jail terms as more capable and determined criminals than when they entered. Of course, it is true that in certain cases we need to both punish criminals and isolate them from other people. I recognise that violent or dangerous people need to be imprisoned – yet I believe this applies to a small minority of instances.\n\nTo conclude, I feel that prison is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a last resort</strong> in cases where there is no alternative. This does not make it the only effective punishment, but rather the most severe among a variety of possibilities.","phrases":[{"en":"non-custodial means","context":"Juvenile and minor offenders can be punished through fines, community service or other non-custodial means."},{"en":"restrict an offender's freedom greatly","context":"Such methods have been shown to be effective in reducing criminal behaviour and are themselves quite a strong punishment, as they restrict an offender's freedom greatly."},{"en":"schools of crime","context":"Finally, we should remember that prisons are to some extent ‘schools of crime’ and in many cases prisoners end their jail terms as more capable and determined criminals than when they entered."},{"en":"a last resort","context":"To conclude, I feel that prison is a last resort in cases where there is no alternative. This does not make it the only effective punishment, but rather the most severe among a variety of possibilities."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#081","summary":"“Prison is the only truly effective form of punishment, because it…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-020","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Is corporal punishment ever acceptable?","prompt":"Although it is generally prohibited, corporal punishment persists in many families. Do you think corporal punishment is an acceptable way to regulate children's behaviour?","answer":"Corporal punishment is still used in some families because it can stop bad behaviour immediately. However, I do not believe that hitting a child is an acceptable form of discipline. It may produce short-term obedience, but it creates serious physical, emotional and educational risks.\n\nThe first problem is that physical punishment can easily <strong class=\"key-phrase\">blur the line between discipline and abuse</strong>. A parent who is angry or tired may use more force than intended, and a child has little power to protect himself or herself. Even when no visible injury occurs, repeated punishment can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cause lasting psychological harm</strong>. Children may become anxious, secretive or resentful, rather than learning why their behaviour was wrong.\n\nA second concern is the lesson that physical punishment teaches. When adults solve conflict by hitting, they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">model aggression as a legitimate response</strong> to frustration. Children may then copy this behaviour with siblings or classmates. Fear can also damage trust between parents and children, making honest communication less likely precisely when young people need guidance.\n\nParents still need effective ways to control unacceptable behaviour. The better approach is to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set clear and consistent boundaries</strong>, explain the consequences of breaking them, and use <strong class=\"key-phrase\">proportionate measures such as loss of privileges</strong> or additional responsibilities. Praise for good behaviour is equally important because it shows children what they should do, not merely what they should avoid.\n\nIn conclusion, corporal punishment is neither necessary nor defensible. Non-violent forms of discipline can correct behaviour while preserving a child's dignity, safety and relationship with the family.","phrases":[{"en":"blur the line between discipline and abuse","context":"The first problem is that physical punishment can easily blur the line between discipline and abuse."},{"en":"cause lasting psychological harm","context":"Even when no visible injury occurs, repeated punishment can cause lasting psychological harm."},{"en":"model aggression as a legitimate response","context":"When adults solve conflict by hitting, they model aggression as a legitimate response to frustration."},{"en":"set clear and consistent boundaries","context":"The better approach is to set clear and consistent boundaries, explain the consequences of breaking them, and use proportionate measures such as loss of privileges or additional responsibilities."},{"en":"proportionate measures such as loss of privileges","context":"The better approach is to set clear and consistent boundaries, explain the consequences of breaking them, and use proportionate measures such as loss of privileges or additional responsibilities."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#082","summary":"Although it is generally prohibited, corporal punishment persists in many…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-021","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"At what age is a child responsible?","prompt":"Do you think that parents should be punished if their five-year-old child commits a crime? From what age should children be held responsible for their own behaviour?","answer":"Parents have a major influence on a young child, but punishing them automatically for every offence committed by a five-year-old would be unfair. At that age, children have only <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a limited understanding of law and consequences</strong>. In my view, parents should be held accountable only when clear neglect or encouragement of wrongdoing can be proved.\n\nA five-year-old normally copies behaviour rather than forming a deliberate criminal intention. Parents are responsible for supervision, basic discipline and the examples they set at home. If a child repeatedly harms others because adults have ignored serious warning signs, allowed access to dangerous objects or actively encouraged aggression, the parents may reasonably face penalties or compulsory support programmes. This approach recognises <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a duty of reasonable supervision</strong> without treating every childhood mistake as parental misconduct.\n\nHowever, responsibility should gradually move from the parent to the child as judgement develops. A single fixed age cannot reflect every case, but the law can use age bands. Children under about ten should usually receive protection, education and family intervention rather than punishment. Older children can be held increasingly responsible when they understand that an act is wrong and can foresee its consequences. For serious offences, courts should consider maturity, intention and personal circumstances instead of relying only on age.\n\nIn conclusion, parents should not be punished simply because their young child commits an offence. Penalties are justified only where there is evidence of serious neglect or deliberate encouragement. Children themselves should assume <strong class=\"key-phrase\">increasing responsibility as their judgement matures</strong>, with rehabilitation remaining the main aim of the youth justice system.","phrases":[{"en":"a limited understanding of law and consequences","context":"At that age, children have only a limited understanding of law and consequences."},{"en":"a duty of reasonable supervision","context":"This approach recognises a duty of reasonable supervision without treating every childhood mistake as parental misconduct."},{"en":"increasing responsibility as their judgement matures","context":"Children themselves should assume increasing responsibility as their judgement matures, with rehabilitation remaining the main aim of the youth justice system."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#083","summary":"Do you think that parents should be punished if their five-year-old child…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-022","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Why crime among young people is rising","prompt":"In recent years, the proportion of crimes committed by young people has risen. What are the causes of this problem, and what solutions can you suggest?","answer":"Youth crime usually has several causes rather than one simple explanation. In my view, the most important factors are unstable family life, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">repeated exposure to violence</strong> and a lack of realistic opportunities. Effective solutions must therefore combine early support with education and employment.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">The breakdown of stable family relationships</strong> can leave young people without consistent supervision or clear boundaries. Parents who work long hours may have little time to notice changes in behaviour, while conflict or abuse at home can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">normalise aggression</strong>. A second factor is repeated exposure to violence through neighbourhood crime, social media or certain forms of entertainment. This does not automatically turn a teenager into an offender, but it can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce sensitivity to harm</strong> and make risky behaviour appear ordinary. Finally, some young people see <strong class=\"key-phrase\">few legal routes to status or income</strong>, especially when they struggle at school and cannot find work.\n\nThe most effective response is early intervention. Schools should identify persistent absence, aggression and learning difficulties before they develop into more serious problems. Counselling and family-support services can help parents establish routines and manage conflict. After-school programmes are also valuable because sport, music and supervised study give teenagers a structured alternative to spending time on the street.\n\nGovernments and employers should also provide <strong class=\"key-phrase\">realistic routes into education and employment</strong>. Paid apprenticeships, vocational courses and entry-level jobs can give young people both practical skills and a reason to plan for the future. Serious offences still require proportionate punishment, but detention alone is unlikely to prevent reoffending unless it includes education and rehabilitation.\n\nIn conclusion, youth crime is driven mainly by weak support, exposure to violence and limited opportunity. Prevention is most successful when families, schools and employers act before criminal behaviour becomes established.","phrases":[{"en":"repeated exposure to violence","context":"In my view, the most important factors are unstable family life, repeated exposure to violence and a lack of realistic opportunities."},{"en":"The breakdown of stable family relationships","context":"The breakdown of stable family relationships can leave young people without consistent supervision or clear boundaries."},{"en":"realistic routes into education and employment","context":"Governments and employers should also provide realistic routes into education and employment."},{"en":"normalise aggression","context":"Parents who work long hours may have little time to notice changes in behaviour, while conflict or abuse at home can normalise aggression."},{"en":"reduce sensitivity to harm","context":"This does not automatically turn a teenager into an offender, but it can reduce sensitivity to harm and make risky behaviour appear ordinary."},{"en":"few legal routes to status or income","context":"Finally, some young people see few legal routes to status or income, especially when they struggle at school and cannot find work."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","_displayNo":"#084","summary":"In recent years, the proportion of the crimes committed by youngsters is on…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-023","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Punishment and road safety","prompt":"The only way to improve road safety is to impose severe punishments for driving offences. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes claimed that harsh penalties for driving offences are the only effective means of making roads safer. I agree that punishment plays an important part, but I disagree that it is the only solution, since several other measures work at least as well.\n\nSevere penalties certainly influence behaviour. Most dangerous driving is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a calculated risk</strong> rather than an accident, and a driver who expects to lose their licence for speeding or drink-driving is far less likely to do either. Countries that introduced heavy fines and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">automatic disqualification for drink-driving</strong> recorded immediate falls in fatalities. It is worth noting, however, that what deters people is the likelihood of being caught rather than the size of the penalty, which is why cameras and roadside testing matter more than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the length of a sentence</strong>.\n\nOther approaches have been equally effective. Road design prevents accidents rather than punishing them, and measures such as roundabouts, separated cycle lanes, lower urban speed limits and crash barriers have reduced deaths substantially. Vehicle technology has contributed as well, since seatbelts, airbags and automatic braking save lives <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regardless of how a driver behaves</strong>. Education plays a part too, particularly for young men, who are involved in a disproportionate share of serious crashes and who often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overestimate their own competence behind the wheel</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that offenders should face real consequences, and that penalties for drink-driving in particular should be severe. I disagree, however, that punishment is the only route to safer roads, since consistent enforcement, safer design and improved vehicles are equally necessary.","phrases":[{"en":"a calculated risk","context":"Most dangerous driving is a calculated risk rather than an accident, and a driver who expects to lose their licence for speeding or drink-driving is far less likely to do either."},{"en":"the length of a sentence","context":"It is worth noting, however, that what deters people is the likelihood of being caught rather than the size of the penalty, which is why cameras and roadside testing matter more than the length of a sentence."},{"en":"regardless of how a driver behaves","context":"Vehicle technology has contributed as well, since seatbelts, airbags and automatic braking save lives regardless of how a driver behaves."},{"en":"automatic disqualification for drink-driving","context":"Countries that introduced heavy fines and automatic disqualification for drink-driving recorded immediate falls in fatalities."},{"en":"overestimate their own competence behind the wheel","context":"Education plays a part too, particularly for young men, who are involved in a disproportionate share of serious crashes and who often overestimate their own competence behind the wheel."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#085","summary":"Punishment and road safety"},{"id":"writing-task-2-024","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Can crime be prevented?","prompt":"Many people are afraid to leave their homes because of crime. Some believe that more can be done to prevent crime, while others think that little can be done. What is your opinion?","answer":"Rising crime has made many people reluctant to leave their homes, and there is disagreement about whether anything can realistically be done about it. In my opinion <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the pessimistic view is mistaken</strong>, because the evidence from countries that have successfully reduced crime is fairly clear.\n\nThe most effective measures address the causes rather than the offence itself. Most crime is committed by young men with poor education, no employment and often an addiction problem, so programmes that keep teenagers in school, provide training and treat addiction reduce offending far more than longer sentences do. Countries that invested in these areas have seen crime fall substantially over the past twenty years, while those relying on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">imprisonment alone</strong> have not.\n\nPractical prevention also works. Better street lighting, secure locks, alarm systems and visible policing all reduce <strong class=\"key-phrase\">opportunistic crime</strong>, which is the majority of it. Vehicle theft has almost disappeared in many countries simply because manufacturers improved the locks and immobilisers, and that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">required no change in anyone's behaviour at all</strong>.\n\nIndividuals can contribute too, although I would be careful here. Taking sensible precautions is reasonable, but the suggestion that victims are negligent, or that people should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">confront criminals themselves</strong>, is both unfair and dangerous. Reporting offences and cooperating with the police is the useful contribution an ordinary citizen can make.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that little can be done about crime. The figures fall reliably when governments tackle poverty and addiction, when opportunities to offend are designed out of everyday life, and when the public is willing to report what it sees.","phrases":[{"en":"imprisonment alone","context":"Countries that invested in these areas have seen crime fall substantially over the past twenty years, while those relying on imprisonment alone have not."},{"en":"opportunistic crime","context":"Better street lighting, secure locks, alarm systems and visible policing all reduce opportunistic crime, which is the majority of it."},{"en":"confront criminals themselves","context":"Taking sensible precautions is reasonable, but the suggestion that victims are negligent, or that people should confront criminals themselves, is both unfair and dangerous."},{"en":"the pessimistic view is mistaken","context":"In my opinion the pessimistic view is mistaken, because the evidence from countries that have successfully reduced crime is fairly clear."},{"en":"required no change in anyone's behaviour at all","context":"Vehicle theft has almost disappeared in many countries simply because manufacturers improved the locks and immobilisers, and that required no change in anyone's behaviour at all."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#086","summary":"Can crime be prevented?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-026","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Should the police carry guns?","prompt":"Police in Britain do not carry guns. Some people believe that unarmed police are unable to protect citizens, but some suggest that arming the police will lead to a surge in violence in society. Discuss those two views.","answer":"Whether police officers should routinely carry guns depends on the level of risk in a particular society. Supporters argue that firearms allow officers to confront dangerous criminals, while opponents fear that routine arming would increase violence. I believe that most officers should remain unarmed, although specially trained units should have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strictly regulated access to firearms</strong>.\n\nThe main argument for arming the police is protection. Officers may face criminals who already possess guns, and an unarmed response can place both the police and the public at risk. The visible possibility of an armed response may also deter some offenders. In areas where gun crime is common, it would be unrealistic to expect officers to deal with every incident without suitable protection.\n\nHowever, routine arming can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raise the risk of fatal mistakes</strong>. Police officers often make decisions in seconds, sometimes in poor light or under intense pressure. A weapon can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">escalate otherwise manageable encounters</strong> and turn a misunderstanding into a death. Widespread firearms may also change the relationship between the police and the public, making ordinary citizens feel that they are dealing with a military force rather than a civil service.\n\nA balanced policy is therefore preferable. Ordinary patrol officers can remain unarmed, while specialist teams receive advanced training and respond to incidents involving firearms or terrorism. Clear rules should govern when weapons may be used, and every shooting should be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">subject to independent investigation</strong>. Officers also need strong training in negotiation, restraint and de-escalation.\n\nIn conclusion, armed police are necessary in exceptional high-risk situations, but routine arming would create avoidable dangers. Limited access, specialist training and strict accountability offer better protection than giving every officer a gun.","phrases":[{"en":"strictly regulated access to firearms","context":"I believe that most officers should remain unarmed, although specially trained units should have strictly regulated access to firearms."},{"en":"raise the risk of fatal mistakes","context":"However, routine arming can raise the risk of fatal mistakes."},{"en":"escalate otherwise manageable encounters","context":"A weapon can escalate otherwise manageable encounters and turn a misunderstanding into a death."},{"en":"subject to independent investigation","context":"Clear rules should govern when weapons may be used, and every shooting should be subject to independent investigation."}],"subtype":"Problems / Solutions","_displayNo":"#087","summary":"Police in Britain do not carry guns."},{"id":"writing-task-2-027","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Are criminals born or made?","prompt":"Some scientists believe that a child's future criminal behaviour can be predicted from their behaviour at a very young age. To what extent is crime a product of human nature, and can children be prevented from growing up to be criminals?","answer":"Research suggesting that difficult behaviour in very young children predicts later offending has led some people to conclude that criminals are born rather than made. In my opinion inherited temperament plays a real but limited part, and the environment in which a child grows up matters far more.\n\nThere is some evidence for the biological argument. Traits such as impulsiveness, aggression and an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">inability to feel guilt</strong> appear early, are partly inherited, and are found much more often among repeat offenders than in the general population. It would be dishonest to pretend that every child begins life with the same disposition.\n\nHowever, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a tendency is not a destiny</strong>. The great majority of impulsive or aggressive three-year-olds never commit a crime, and offending <strong class=\"key-phrase\">correlates far more strongly with</strong> poverty, neglect, family breakdown, poor schooling and drug addiction than with temperament. This is why crime rates differ so sharply between neighbourhoods and between countries, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">differences that genetics cannot possibly explain</strong>.\n\nIt follows that prevention is realistic. Programmes that support struggling parents, identify learning difficulties early, keep teenagers in education and provide activities in deprived areas have repeatedly been shown to reduce later offending, and they cost <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a small fraction of what imprisonment costs</strong>. A child with a difficult temperament who receives consistent guidance usually learns to control it.\n\nIn conclusion, human nature contributes to criminal behaviour but does not determine it. Since circumstances are what turn a difficult child into an offender, I believe most criminality can be prevented by acting early enough.","phrases":[{"en":"inability to feel guilt","context":"Traits such as impulsiveness, aggression and an inability to feel guilt appear early, are partly inherited, and are found much more often among repeat offenders than in the general population."},{"en":"correlates far more strongly with","context":"The great majority of impulsive or aggressive three-year-olds never commit a crime, and offending correlates far more strongly with poverty, neglect, family breakdown, poor schooling and drug addiction than with temperament."},{"en":"a small fraction of what imprisonment costs","context":"Programmes that support struggling parents, identify learning difficulties early, keep teenagers in education and provide activities in deprived areas have repeatedly been shown to reduce later offending, and they cost a small fraction of what imprisonment costs."},{"en":"a tendency is not a destiny","context":"However, a tendency is not a destiny."},{"en":"differences that genetics cannot possibly explain","context":"This is why crime rates differ so sharply between neighbourhoods and between countries, differences that genetics cannot possibly explain."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#088","summary":"Are criminals born or made?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-028","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Security measures in large cities","prompt":"Large cities are introducing increasingly extensive security measures in order to reduce crime. Do the benefits of this outweigh the drawbacks?","answer":"Cities around the world now rely on street cameras, facial recognition, data monitoring and larger police patrols to control crime. In my view these measures bring real benefits, but the drawbacks are serious enough to require strict limits.\n\nThe advantages are straightforward. Surveillance deters offenders, because most crimes are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">committed opportunistically</strong> and the likelihood of being recorded changes that calculation. Cameras also supply the evidence that secures convictions, and they have proved particularly valuable in investigating violent attacks, where witnesses are frightened, absent or simply unreliable. Ordinary residents benefit too: people are far more willing to use public transport or walk home at night in areas they believe are monitored, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeps city centres alive after dark</strong>.\n\nThe problems, however, are significant. Constant monitoring of law-abiding citizens is an intrusion into private life, and the data collected can easily be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">misused</strong>, whether by governments tracking political opponents or by criminals who obtain it through hacking. Facial recognition systems are also known to make more errors with certain ethnic groups, which risks wrongful arrest. There is a further practical objection: much of this technology <strong class=\"key-phrase\">displaces crime</strong> into unmonitored areas rather than preventing it, and the same money might reduce offending more effectively if it were spent on education and drug treatment.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe the benefits are greater, since these measures genuinely reduce crime and allow ordinary people to move around their own city without fear. They are only acceptable, however, where the data is tightly regulated, held for a short period and open to independent inspection.","phrases":[{"en":"keeps city centres alive after dark","context":"Ordinary residents benefit too: people are far more willing to use public transport or walk home at night in areas they believe are monitored, which keeps city centres alive after dark."},{"en":"misused","context":"Constant monitoring of law-abiding citizens is an intrusion into private life, and the data collected can easily be misused, whether by governments tracking political opponents or by criminals who obtain it through hacking."},{"en":"displaces crime","context":"There is a further practical objection: much of this technology displaces crime into unmonitored areas rather than preventing it, and the same money might reduce offending more effectively if it were spent on education and drug treatment."},{"en":"committed opportunistically","context":"Surveillance deters offenders, because most crimes are committed opportunistically and the likelihood of being recorded changes that calculation."}],"subtype":"Benefits & Drawbacks","_displayNo":"#089","summary":"Security measures in large cities"},{"id":"writing-task-2-059","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Prison or rehabilitation?","prompt":"Some people believe that criminals should be sent to prison, while others think they should receive education and job training instead. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"When someone is convicted of a crime, societies must decide whether the response should be imprisonment or rehabilitation through education and training. In my opinion both are necessary, but training should play a much larger role than it currently does.\n\nThe argument for prison is that some offenders are genuinely dangerous. A society has an obligation to protect its citizens, and there is no alternative to detaining people who have committed murder, rape or armed robbery. Imprisonment also deters others and satisfies the public sense that serious wrongdoing must have consequences, without which victims <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lose confidence in the law</strong>.\n\nThe case for education and job training rests on what happens afterwards. Almost all prisoners are eventually released, and the majority reoffend within a few years, largely because they leave with no qualifications, no employment history and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no legitimate way of earning</strong>. Many were poorly educated before they offended, and a significant proportion cannot read fluently. Programmes that teach literacy and a practical trade have been shown to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cut reoffending substantially</strong>, and they cost far less than imprisoning the same individual repeatedly.\n\nIn my view the two are not really alternatives. The sensible approach is to reserve prison for those who present a genuine danger, use community sentences and treatment for minor and drug-related offences, and provide training inside prison rather than leaving inmates idle.\n\nIn conclusion, imprisonment is necessary for serious offenders, but a system that punishes without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">preparing people for release</strong> simply produces the next generation of crime.","phrases":[{"en":"lose confidence in the law","context":"Imprisonment also deters others and satisfies the public sense that serious wrongdoing must have consequences, without which victims lose confidence in the law."},{"en":"no legitimate way of earning","context":"Almost all prisoners are eventually released, and the majority reoffend within a few years, largely because they leave with no qualifications, no employment history and no legitimate way of earning."},{"en":"cut reoffending substantially","context":"Programmes that teach literacy and a practical trade have been shown to cut reoffending substantially, and they cost far less than imprisoning the same individual repeatedly."},{"en":"preparing people for release","context":"In conclusion, imprisonment is necessary for serious offenders, but a system that punishes without preparing people for release simply produces the next generation of crime."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#090","summary":"Prison or rehabilitation?"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Crime is falling, so why do people feel unsafe?","prompt":"In some countries, even though the rates of serious crimes are decreasing, people feel less safe than ever before. What do you think are the causes of this problem and what measures could be taken to solve it?","answer":"People can feel less safe even when serious crime is falling. This apparent contradiction is often caused by intensive news coverage, online rumours and visible signs of disorder. Governments should respond with accurate information as well as effective policing.\n\nModern media reports violent incidents repeatedly because they attract attention. A crime that once appeared in a local newspaper may now circulate nationally through video and social media. As a result, people judge risk by the examples they remember rather than by long-term statistics. This can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">create a distorted sense of danger</strong>, since a single alarming clip can reach millions of people who never encounter the actual location.\n\nA second cause is what people actually see around them. Broken street lighting, graffiti, abandoned buildings, litter and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">low-level antisocial behaviour</strong> all signal that a place is not looked after and that nobody is in charge of it. None of this appears in serious-crime statistics, yet it is what residents encounter every day, and a street that feels neglected is read as a dangerous one long before anything happens there.\n\nThe response should address both causes. Local authorities can repair lighting, clear graffiti quickly and maintain parks and estates, since a well-kept area is judged safer and is in fact easier to police. The same reasoning applies to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">visible community policing</strong>: officers who are known by name in a neighbourhood reassure residents in a way that patrol cars cannot. Police forces should also publish local crime figures in plain language, showing the trend over several years alongside the month's incidents, because accessible data is the surest way to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce the gap between perceived and actual risk</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, falling crime rates do not automatically produce a feeling of safety. Public confidence depends on the condition of neighbourhoods and on information people can trust, and a combination of visible local action and honest communication is the most effective response.","topic":"Crime & Punishment","phrases":[{"en":"create a distorted sense of danger","context":"This can create a distorted sense of danger, since a single alarming clip can reach millions of people who never encounter the actual location."},{"en":"low-level antisocial behaviour","context":"Broken street lighting, graffiti, abandoned buildings, litter and low-level antisocial behaviour all signal that a place is not looked after and that nobody is in charge of it."},{"en":"visible community policing","context":"The same reasoning applies to visible community policing: officers who are known by name in a neighbourhood reassure residents in a way that patrol cars cannot."},{"en":"reduce the gap between perceived and actual risk","context":"Police forces should also publish local crime figures in plain language, showing the trend over several years alongside the month's incidents, because accessible data is the surest way to reduce the gap between perceived and actual risk."}],"subtype":"problem","summary":"Why people feel less safe even as serious crime falls","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-107","_displayNo":"#091"},{"id":"writing-task-2-167","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Cybercrime and what can be done about it","prompt":"Many people today are worried about ‘cybercrime’ such as hacking and identity theft. What problems does ‘cybercrime’ cause, and what solutions can you suggest for ordinary people and businesses to take? Give reasons for your answer, and provide ideas and examples from your own experience. You should write at least 250 words.","answer":"Cybercrime is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a cause of widespread concern</strong> today, as so many people use technology to store and transmit sensitive data. There are a number of problems that people suffer as a result of these crimes, but also some straightforward measures we can take to protect ourselves. Perhaps the worst problem stemming from this is the financial impact. If someone has his or her bank account emptied, or has false credit lines created under their name, they are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">potentially liable for large sums of money</strong>. Even if they can avoid the consequences, their bank or finance company has to cover the losses, which can damage their performance.\n\nThese losses do not stay contained, either: once stolen money enters a criminal network, it can just as easily fund drug trafficking or terrorism as ordinary theft, and by the time it has passed through several accounts <strong class=\"key-phrase\">there is no realistic way to trace where it has gone</strong>. Victims rarely emerge unscathed even when a bank eventually refunds the loss. Months of disputed transactions, replacement documents and the fear of it happening again take a genuine toll on people who did nothing to invite it.\n\nThe most effective response starts with individuals. Strong, unique passwords and the simple habit of shredding old bank statements <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deny criminals the raw material they need</strong> in the first place. Public education matters just as much, since scammers deliberately target the elderly and the very young; campaigns that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raise awareness of the risks</strong> in schools and community centres can close off an easy source of victims before a crime even happens. The greatest responsibility, though, lies with banks and technology firms, whose fraud-detection systems and closer cooperation with the police can stop an attack before a customer notices anything is wrong.\n\nOverall, the problems caused are both financial and social, and the solutions should involve coordinated action by individuals, corporations and the state.","phrases":[{"en":"a cause of widespread concern","context":"Cybercrime is a cause of widespread concern today, as so many people use technology to store and transmit sensitive data."},{"en":"potentially liable for large sums of money","context":"If someone has his or her bank account emptied, or has false credit lines created under their name, they are potentially liable for large sums of money."},{"en":"raise awareness of the risks","context":"Public education matters just as much, since scammers deliberately target the elderly and the very young; campaigns that raise awareness of the risks in schools and community centres can close off an easy source of victims before a crime even happens."},{"en":"there is no realistic way to trace where it has gone","context":"These losses do not stay contained, either: once stolen money enters a criminal network, it can just as easily fund drug trafficking or terrorism as ordinary theft, and by the time it has passed through several accounts there is no realistic way to trace where it has gone."},{"en":"deny criminals the raw material they need","context":"Strong, unique passwords and the simple habit of shredding old bank statements deny criminals the raw material they need in the first place."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","_displayNo":"#092","summary":"People today are worried about ‘cybercrime’ such as hacking and identity…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2205","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Will technology reduce crime?","prompt":"Crime rates are likely to decline due to the advancements in technology, which will help to prevent and solve crimes in an easier way. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is often predicted that advances in technology will make crime easier to prevent and to solve, and that offending will therefore fall. I accept that technology has made certain traditional crimes far more difficult, but I disagree with the wider claim, because the same developments have created entirely new forms of criminal activity.\n\nThere is no doubt that technology assists the police. Street cameras, number-plate recognition and mobile phone records make it much harder to commit a burglary or a robbery without being identified, and DNA analysis has allowed investigators to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">solve cases once considered hopeless</strong>. Banks now use automated systems that detect unusual transactions instantly. Rates of car theft and street crime have indeed fallen sharply in many countries over the past twenty years, and better security technology is a major reason for this.\n\nHowever, criminals adopt new technology just as quickly as the authorities do. Fraud, identity theft and online scams have grown enormously, and in several countries these offences now outnumber all traditional crimes combined. Such crimes are extremely difficult to investigate, because the offender may be in another country, may be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hidden behind encryption</strong>, and may target thousands of victims at once. Overall crime figures have not fallen; <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the crime has simply moved</strong>.\n\nIt is also worth remembering that technology does not address the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">underlying causes of crime</strong>, which are usually poverty, addiction, poor education and inequality. Cameras may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">displace offending</strong> rather than prevent it.\n\nIn conclusion, technology has clearly reduced some categories of crime, but I do not believe it will produce a general decline in crime rates.","phrases":[{"en":"the crime has simply moved","context":"Overall crime figures have not fallen; the crime has simply moved."},{"en":"underlying causes of crime","context":"It is also worth remembering that technology does not address the underlying causes of crime, which are usually poverty, addiction, poor education and inequality. Cameras may displace offending rather than prevent it."},{"en":"displace offending","context":"It is also worth remembering that technology does not address the underlying causes of crime, which are usually poverty, addiction, poor education and inequality. Cameras may displace offending rather than prevent it."},{"en":"hidden behind encryption","context":"Such crimes are extremely difficult to investigate, because the offender may be in another country, may be hidden behind encryption, and may target thousands of victims at once."},{"en":"solve cases once considered hopeless","context":"Street cameras, number-plate recognition and mobile phone records make it much harder to commit a burglary or a robbery without being identified, and DNA analysis has allowed investigators to solve cases once considered hopeless."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#093","summary":"Will technology reduce crime?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2229","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Why released prisoners reoffend","prompt":"Many criminals commit further crimes as soon as they are released from prison. What do you think are the causes of this? What possible solutions can you suggest?","answer":"In many countries around half of those released from prison are convicted again within two years. The causes lie in what happens on release rather than in the sentence itself, and they can be addressed by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treating release as a process rather than an event</strong>.\n\nThe first cause is that prisoners leave with nothing. A person released with a small discharge grant, no address and no job has to solve housing, income and identity documents in the same week, and returning to crime is often the only route they know to money. A criminal record then closes the legitimate routes: employers reject applicants who declare a conviction, so the harder someone tries to find lawful work, the more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">firmly the door is closed</strong>.\n\nA second cause is that prison rarely changes the conditions that produced the offence. Most prisoners entered with addiction, untreated mental illness or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">literacy below the level of an eleven-year-old</strong>, and a sentence spent locked in a cell for twenty hours a day <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaves all of that untouched</strong>. Released into the same streets and the same company, they face exactly the situation that led to conviction.\n\nThe solutions follow. Housing should be arranged before the release date rather than after it, since almost nothing else can be sustained without an address. Treatment for addiction and teaching of basic literacy and a trade should occupy the sentence, because a released prisoner who can read and hold a job has something to lose. Limiting how long employers may consider minor convictions would also remove a needless obstacle.\n\nIn conclusion, reoffending is driven by release without housing, work or treatment. Prisons that prepare people for the outside during the sentence, rather than simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">opening the gate on the final day</strong>, achieve markedly lower rates.","phrases":[{"en":"firmly the door is closed","context":"A criminal record then closes the legitimate routes: employers reject applicants who declare a conviction, so the harder someone tries to find lawful work, the more firmly the door is closed."},{"en":"leaves all of that untouched","context":"Most prisoners entered with addiction, untreated mental illness or literacy below the level of an eleven-year-old, and a sentence spent locked in a cell for twenty hours a day leaves all of that untouched."},{"en":"opening the gate on the final day","context":"In conclusion, reoffending is driven by release without housing, work or treatment. Prisons that prepare people for the outside during the sentence, rather than simply opening the gate on the final day, achieve markedly lower rates."},{"en":"treating release as a process rather than an event","context":"In many countries around half of those released from prison are convicted again within two years. The causes lie in what happens on release rather than in the sentence itself, and they can be addressed by treating release as a process rather than an event."},{"en":"literacy below the level of an eleven-year-old","context":"Most prisoners entered with addiction, untreated mental illness or literacy below the level of an eleven-year-old, and a sentence spent locked in a cell for twenty hours a day leaves all of that untouched."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#094","summary":"Causes of reoffending after release from prison and possible solutions"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2281","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Should young offenders be treated as adults?","prompt":"Some people believe that young people who commit serious crimes should be punished in the same way as adults. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"When a teenager commits a violent crime, the demand that age should make no difference is understandable. I disagree with it, however, because the reasons we punish adults as we do apply only partly to the young.\n\nThe argument for equal treatment rests on the harm done. A victim is no less injured because the attacker was sixteen, and a system that hands down light sentences for serious offences looks unjust to the family involved. There is also a fear that lenient courts will be exploited, with older criminals <strong class=\"key-phrase\">using teenagers to carry out the work</strong>. These points deserve a serious answer rather than dismissal.\n\nThe answer is that responsibility depends on judgement, and judgement is not complete at sixteen. The parts of the brain governing impulse and consequence continue developing into the mid-twenties, which is why young people offend in groups, on impulse and with little thought of what follows. Most also stop: the great majority of teenage offenders commit no crimes as adults, so a long sentence punishes a person who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">would have grown out of it anyway</strong>. Prison makes that worse, since a young man placed among experienced criminals learns from them and leaves with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no qualifications and a permanent record</strong>.\n\nThis is not an argument for doing nothing. Serious crimes should carry serious consequences, including detention where the public must be protected. The difference is that a young offender's sentence should be built around education, treatment and supervision, and courts should retain the power to punish the most extreme cases severely.\n\nIn conclusion, treating children exactly as adults ignores what is known about how they think and how they change. Firm but separate treatment, aimed at stopping the next offence rather than matching the last one, protects the public more effectively.","phrases":[{"en":"using teenagers to carry out the work","context":"There is also a fear that lenient courts will be exploited, with older criminals using teenagers to carry out the work."},{"en":"would have grown out of it anyway","context":"Most also stop: the great majority of teenage offenders commit no crimes as adults, so a long sentence punishes a person who would have grown out of it anyway."},{"en":"no qualifications and a permanent record","context":"Prison makes that worse, since a young man placed among experienced criminals learns from them and leaves with no qualifications and a permanent record."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#095","summary":"Whether young people who commit serious crimes deserve adult punishment"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2353","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Prison or community service?","prompt":"Some people believe that all offenders should be sent to prison, while others argue that there are more effective alternatives for those who commit minor crimes, such as community service. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Prisons in most countries hold more people than they were built for, and a large share of those people are serving short sentences for minor offences. My own view is that imprisonment should be reserved for the dangerous, with community penalties used for the rest.\n\nThe argument for imprisoning everyone rests on consistency and deterrence. If minor offences bring no serious consequence, the law appears optional, and victims of theft or vandalism reasonably feel that a few hours of unpaid work does not answer what was done to them. Prison also removes the offender from the streets, which is the one thing that stops the behaviour <strong class=\"key-phrase\">for as long as the sentence lasts</strong>.\n\nThe case for alternatives is supported by what happens afterwards. Short sentences are long enough to cost a person their job, their housing and often their family, and short enough to teach them nothing, so they leave with fewer reasons to stay honest than when they arrived. Prison also functions as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an education in crime supplied at public expense</strong>, since a first-time offender spends months among experienced ones. Reoffending rates after community sentences are consistently lower, and the cost is a fraction of a prison place.\n\nCommunity penalties also do something imprisonment cannot: they keep the offender working and paying tax, allow treatment for addiction or mental illness where that is the real cause, and let the debt be repaid visibly to the neighbourhood that was harmed. The essential condition is that they are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">supervised strictly enough to be taken seriously</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, prison remains necessary for violent and repeat offenders, and no system can do without it. For minor crimes it is expensive, damaging and largely ineffective, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">penalties that keep people in work and under supervision</strong> protect the public more successfully.","phrases":[{"en":"for as long as the sentence lasts","context":"Prison also removes the offender from the streets, which is the one thing that stops the behaviour for as long as the sentence lasts."},{"en":"an education in crime supplied at public expense","context":"Prison also functions as an education in crime supplied at public expense, since a first-time offender spends months among experienced ones."},{"en":"supervised strictly enough to be taken seriously","context":"The essential condition is that they are supervised strictly enough to be taken seriously."},{"en":"penalties that keep people in work and under supervision","context":"For minor crimes it is expensive, damaging and largely ineffective, so penalties that keep people in work and under supervision protect the public more successfully."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#096","summary":"Whether every offender should be imprisoned or minor crimes handled differently"},{"id":"writing-task-2-029","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Culture & Traditions","title":"Will technology destroy traditional cultures?","prompt":"It is inevitable that traditional cultures will be lost as technology develops. Technology and traditional cultures are incompatible. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this view?","answer":"Some people believe that technological developments lead to the loss of traditional cultures. I partly agree with this assertion; while it may be true in the case of some societies, others seem to be unaffected by technology and the modern world.\n\nOn the one hand, the advances in technology that have driven industrialisation in developed countries have certainly contributed to the disappearance of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">traditional ways of life</strong>. For example, in pre-industrial Britain, generations of families grew up in the same small village communities. These communities had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a strong sense of identity</strong>, due to their shared customs and beliefs. However, developments in transport, communications and manufacturing led to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the dispersal of families</strong> and village communities as people moved to the cities in search of work. Nowadays most British villages are inhabited by commuters, many of whom do not know their closest neighbours.\n\nOn the other hand, in some parts of the world traditional cultures still thrive. There are tribes in the Amazon Rainforest, for example, that have been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completely untouched by the technological developments</strong> of the developed world. These tribal communities continue to hunt and gather food from the forest, and traditional skills are passed on to children by parents and elders. Other traditional cultures, such as farming communities in parts of Africa, are embracing communications technologies. Mobile phones give farmers access to information, from weather predictions to market prices, which helps them to prosper and therefore supports their culture.\n\nIn conclusion, many traditional ways of life have been lost as a result of advances in technology, but other traditional communities have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">survived and even flourished</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"traditional ways of life","context":"On the one hand, the advances in technology that have driven industrialisation in developed countries have certainly contributed to the disappearance of traditional ways of life."},{"en":"the dispersal of families","context":"However, developments in transport, communications and manufacturing led to the dispersal of families and village communities as people moved to the cities in search of work."},{"en":"a strong sense of identity","context":"These communities had a strong sense of identity, due to their shared customs and beliefs."},{"en":"completely untouched by the technological developments","context":"There are tribes in the Amazon Rainforest, for example, that have been completely untouched by the technological developments of the developed world."},{"en":"survived and even flourished","context":"In conclusion, many traditional ways of life have been lost as a result of advances in technology, but other traditional communities have survived and even flourished."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#097","summary":"Technology and traditional cultures"},{"id":"writing-task-2-036","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Culture & Traditions","title":"Does travel build understanding?","prompt":"Some people believe that increased travel between countries helps people to learn about different cultures. Others argue that it increases tension between people from different countries. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"International travel has increased enormously, and opinions differ about its effect on relations between nations. Some argue that it builds understanding, while others believe it creates friction. In my view the outcome depends almost entirely on how people travel.\n\nThere is good reason to think travel promotes understanding. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Prejudice usually survives on ignorance</strong>, and meeting people directly tends to dismantle it: a visitor who is helped by strangers in a country they had been taught to fear finds <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the stereotype difficult to maintain</strong>. Travel also creates practical connections through trade, study and friendship, and countries whose citizens visit each other frequently are historically <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less likely to go to war</strong>.\n\nThe opposite case is not without foundation. Mass tourism can breed resentment when visitors ignore local customs, push up house prices and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treat a community as an attraction rather than a home</strong>, which is why residents of cities such as Barcelona and Venice have protested. Tourists who stay inside resorts and never meet local people learn nothing, and encounters limited to buying and selling can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reinforce prejudice rather than reduce it</strong>.\n\nMy own view is that the difference lies in the depth of contact. A traveller who learns some of the language, eats where local people eat and stays long enough to form relationships almost always returns with more understanding. Someone who spends a week beside a hotel pool does not.\n\nIn conclusion, travel has real potential to reduce hostility, but only where visitors engage seriously with the places they visit.","phrases":[{"en":"the stereotype difficult to maintain","context":"Prejudice usually survives on ignorance, and meeting people directly tends to dismantle it: a visitor who is helped by strangers in a country they had been taught to fear finds the stereotype difficult to maintain."},{"en":"less likely to go to war","context":"Travel also creates practical connections through trade, study and friendship, and countries whose citizens visit each other frequently are historically less likely to go to war."},{"en":"reinforce prejudice rather than reduce it","context":"Tourists who stay inside resorts and never meet local people learn nothing, and encounters limited to buying and selling can reinforce prejudice rather than reduce it."},{"en":"Prejudice usually survives on ignorance","context":"Prejudice usually survives on ignorance, and meeting people directly tends to dismantle it: a visitor who is helped by strangers in a country they had been taught to fear finds the stereotype difficult to maintain."},{"en":"treat a community as an attraction rather than a home","context":"Mass tourism can breed resentment when visitors ignore local customs, push up house prices and treat a community as an attraction rather than a home, which is why residents of cities such as Barcelona and Venice have protested."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#098","summary":"Does travel build understanding?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-039","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Culture & Traditions","title":"Should immigrants adopt the local culture?","prompt":"Some people argue that immigrants should adopt the local culture when they move to a new country. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Moving to another country requires immigrants to understand local customs, but it should not require them to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">abandon their own culture</strong>. I partly agree that newcomers need to adapt. They should respect the law and learn the language, while remaining free to preserve harmless traditions and community ties.\n\nAdaptation is essential for practical reasons. A person who can communicate in the local language is more likely to find work, use public services and build relationships outside an immigrant community. Understanding everyday conventions also prevents avoidable conflict. For example, expectations about punctuality, personal space or workplace communication may differ greatly between countries. Learning these norms helps newcomers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">participate fully in public life</strong>.\n\nHowever, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">integration is not the same as cultural surrender</strong>. Family traditions, food, music and religious practice can give immigrants stability during a difficult transition. Minority organisations may also provide language support, childcare and advice for new arrivals. These communities become harmful only when they isolate members from the wider society or reject basic legal principles. In most cases, they can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">preserve identity while supporting integration</strong>.\n\nThe host country has responsibilities as well. Schools, employers and public institutions should make participation possible and respond firmly to discrimination. Integration works best when newcomers are expected to contribute but are not treated as inferior for having a different background.\n\nIn conclusion, immigrants should adopt the language, laws and civic expectations of their new country. They should not be required to erase their heritage. A successful multicultural society creates <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a shared public culture with room for private difference</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"abandon their own culture","context":"Moving to another country requires immigrants to understand local customs, but it should not require them to abandon their own culture."},{"en":"participate fully in public life","context":"Learning these norms helps newcomers participate fully in public life."},{"en":"preserve identity while supporting integration","context":"In most cases, they can preserve identity while supporting integration."},{"en":"integration is not the same as cultural surrender","context":"However, integration is not the same as cultural surrender."},{"en":"a shared public culture with room for private difference","context":"In conclusion, immigrants should adopt the language, laws and civic expectations of their new country. They should not be required to erase their heritage. A successful multicultural society creates a shared public culture with room for private difference."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#099","summary":"People argue that immigrants should adopt the local culture when"},{"id":"writing-task-2-042","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Culture & Traditions","title":"Tourism and cultural preservation","prompt":"Some people believe that culture will be ruined if it is used to earn tourism revenue, but others consider that tourism is the only way of protecting a culture. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Tourism now provides much of the income that pays for cultural preservation, and opinion is divided over whether this protects a culture or gradually destroys it. In my view it does both, and which effect dominates depends almost entirely on how the tourism is managed.\n\nThe argument that tourism preserves culture is a strong one. Historic buildings, temples and traditional crafts are expensive to maintain, and without visitor income many would simply have been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">left to decay</strong>. Tourism also gives young people a reason to learn traditional skills that would otherwise disappear, because those skills now provide a living. Kyoto and Luang Prabang are both places where the money visitors bring pays directly for conservation work.\n\nThe opposite case is equally clear. When a ceremony that once had religious meaning is performed four times a day for coach parties, something genuine is lost even though the performance continues. Traditional dress and festivals get simplified into whatever photographs well, and local people can end up as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">performers in their own town</strong>. Housing costs also rise until residents move out, leaving a centre that looks authentic but is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">inhabited by nobody who belongs there</strong>.\n\nMy own view is that the distinction lies in control. Where local communities own the businesses and decide what is shown, tourism tends to strengthen a culture. Where outside companies run it and residents are merely employed, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the culture becomes a product</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, tourism can either preserve or damage a culture, and the deciding factor is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">who makes the decisions</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"inhabited by nobody who belongs there","context":"Housing costs also rise until residents move out, leaving a centre that looks authentic but is inhabited by nobody who belongs there."},{"en":"who makes the decisions","context":"In conclusion, tourism can either preserve or damage a culture, and the deciding factor is who makes the decisions."},{"en":"left to decay","context":"Historic buildings, temples and traditional crafts are expensive to maintain, and without visitor income many would simply have been left to decay."},{"en":"performers in their own town","context":"Traditional dress and festivals get simplified into whatever photographs well, and local people can end up as performers in their own town."},{"en":"the culture becomes a product","context":"My own view is that the distinction lies in control. Where local communities own the businesses and decide what is shown, tourism tends to strengthen a culture. Where outside companies run it and residents are merely employed, the culture becomes a product."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#100","summary":"Tourism and cultural preservation"},{"id":"writing-task-2-044","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Culture & Traditions","title":"Traditional dress and national identity","prompt":"People in many countries no longer wear their traditional costume. Some people argue that they will therefore forget their history and traditions. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"In most countries, traditional dress is now worn only at weddings, festivals and official ceremonies. Some people fear that this means a nation's history and customs will gradually be forgotten. I disagree with this view, because culture is preserved by far more than clothing.\n\nIt is easy to see why the change has happened. Traditional garments were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">designed for a way of life that no longer exists</strong>. They are often expensive, difficult to clean and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">impractical for</strong> commuting, office work or factory shifts, whereas modern clothing is cheap, comfortable and suitable for almost any activity. People have therefore abandoned traditional dress for practical reasons, not because they have lost interest in their heritage.\n\nMore importantly, a nation's history is carried by things that are much harder to lose than a style of dress. Language, food, religion, music, festivals, architecture and family customs all <strong class=\"key-phrase\">transmit culture from one generation to the next</strong>, and none of them depends on what people wear to work. Vietnam and Japan are good examples: everyday clothing in both countries is almost entirely Western, yet their languages, cuisines and traditional celebrations remain firmly intact.\n\nI would accept that traditional costume has real value and should not disappear completely. Governments and schools can protect it by teaching children its history, wearing it on national holidays and supporting the craftspeople who still make it. Wearing it occasionally, however, is enough to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep the knowledge alive</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the decline of traditional dress <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reflects practical convenience rather than cultural loss</strong>, and I do not believe it will cause people to forget their history.","phrases":[{"en":"impractical for","context":"They are often expensive, difficult to clean and impractical for commuting, office work or factory shifts, whereas modern clothing is cheap, comfortable and suitable for almost any activity."},{"en":"transmit culture from one generation to the next","context":"Language, food, religion, music, festivals, architecture and family customs all transmit culture from one generation to the next, and none of them depends on what people wear to work."},{"en":"keep the knowledge alive","context":"Wearing it occasionally, however, is enough to keep the knowledge alive."},{"en":"designed for a way of life that no longer exists","context":"Traditional garments were designed for a way of life that no longer exists."},{"en":"reflects practical convenience rather than cultural loss","context":"In conclusion, the decline of traditional dress reflects practical convenience rather than cultural loss, and I do not believe it will cause people to forget their history."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#101","summary":"Traditional dress and national identity"},{"id":"writing-task-2-046","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Culture & Traditions","title":"Does contact between countries erode identity?","prompt":"There is a disagreement on the impact of increased business and culture contact between countries on a country's identity. What is your opinion?","answer":"Closer business and cultural contact between countries can weaken certain traditions, but it does not inevitably destroy national identity. In my view, international contact can strengthen rather than erase national identity when governments and communities protect what is distinctive and allow culture to develop naturally.\n\nThe main risk is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cultural homogenisation</strong>. Global companies sell similar products in many countries, international media promote the same celebrities and younger people often adopt foreign fashions or expressions. Under commercial pressure, small local businesses, minority languages and traditional crafts may struggle to survive. If these trends continue without support, visible differences between societies can gradually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">become less pronounced</strong>.\n\nHowever, contact with other countries can also increase interest in local culture. Tourism creates demand for regional food, music, architecture and festivals. International audiences may give artists and small producers new markets, while digital platforms allow communities to document and share traditions that were previously known only locally. Exposure to other cultures can even <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make people more conscious of their own identity</strong> because comparison shows them what is distinctive about it.\n\nThe outcome therefore depends on policy and public choice. Schools can teach local history and language, governments can support cultural institutions, and planning rules can protect important buildings and neighbourhoods. At the same time, identity should not be treated as something fixed. Cultures have always borrowed ideas from one another, and selective change can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep traditions relevant to younger generations</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, international business and cultural exchange create some pressure towards uniformity, but they also provide resources and audiences for local culture. Countries can preserve distinctive traditions while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">engaging with the world on their own terms</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"cultural homogenisation","context":"The main risk is cultural homogenisation."},{"en":"keep traditions relevant to younger generations","context":"Cultures have always borrowed ideas from one another, and selective change can keep traditions relevant to younger generations."},{"en":"engaging with the world on their own terms","context":"Countries can preserve distinctive traditions while engaging with the world on their own terms."},{"en":"become less pronounced","context":"If these trends continue without support, visible differences between societies can gradually become less pronounced."},{"en":"make people more conscious of their own identity","context":"Exposure to other cultures can even make people more conscious of their own identity because comparison shows them what is distinctive about it."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#102","summary":"There is a disagreement on the impact of increased business and culture…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-100","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Culture & Traditions","title":"Have festivals lost their meaning?","prompt":"Most people have forgotten the meaning behind traditional or religious festivals; during festival periods, people nowadays only want to enjoy themselves. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this opinion?","answer":"Some people argue that we no longer remember <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the original meaning of</strong> festivals, and that most of us treat them as opportunities to have fun. While I agree that enjoyment seems to be the priority during festival times, I do not agree that people have forgotten what these festivals mean.\n\nOn the one hand, religious and traditional festivals have certainly become times for celebration. In the UK, Christmas is a good example of a festival period when people are most concerned with shopping, giving and receiving presents, decorating their homes and enjoying traditional meals with their families. Most people look forward to Christmas as a holiday period, rather than a time to practise religion. Similar behaviour can be seen during non-religious festivals, such as Bonfire Night. People associate this occasion with making fires, watching firework displays, and perhaps going to large events in local parks; in other words, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">enjoyment is people's primary goal</strong>.\n\nHowever, I disagree with the idea that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the underlying meaning of</strong> such festivals has been forgotten. In UK primary schools, children learn in detail about the religious reasons for celebrating Christmas, Easter and a variety of festivals in other religions. For example, in late December, children sing Christmas songs which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have a religious content</strong>, and they may even perform nativity plays telling the story of Jesus’ birth. Families also play a role in passing knowledge of religious festivals’ deeper significance on to the next generation. The same is true for festivals that have a historical background, such as Bonfire Night or Halloween, in the sense that people generally <strong class=\"key-phrase\">learn the stories behind these occasions at an early age</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, although people mainly want to enjoy themselves during festivals, I believe that they are still aware of the reasons for these celebrations.","phrases":[{"en":"the original meaning of","context":"Some people argue that we no longer remember the original meaning of festivals, and that most of us treat them as opportunities to have fun."},{"en":"the underlying meaning of","context":"However, I disagree with the idea that the underlying meaning of such festivals has been forgotten."},{"en":"enjoyment is people's primary goal","context":"People associate this occasion with making fires, watching firework displays, and perhaps going to large events in local parks; in other words, enjoyment is people's primary goal."},{"en":"have a religious content","context":"For example, in late December, children sing Christmas songs which have a religious content, and they may even perform nativity plays telling the story of Jesus’ birth."},{"en":"learn the stories behind these occasions at an early age","context":"The same is true for festivals that have a historical background, such as Bonfire Night or Halloween, in the sense that people generally learn the stories behind these occasions at an early age."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#103","summary":"The meaning of traditional festivals"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2197","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Culture & Traditions","title":"Giving money instead of gifts","prompt":"In more and more countries, people give money on special occasions rather than personally giving gifts. Why might this be the case? Is it a positive or negative development?","answer":"In many countries it has become normal to give money at weddings, birthdays and religious festivals instead of choosing a present. There are two main reasons for this change, and although it has one obvious drawback, I believe it is a largely positive development.\n\nThe main reason is convenience. People today work long hours and often live far from their relatives, so they simply do not have time to visit shops and select something appropriate, whereas sending money through a banking app takes seconds. A second reason is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the fear of choosing badly</strong>. Modern consumers already own a great deal, and tastes in clothes, technology and decoration are highly personal. Almost everyone has received a present that they never used, and giving cash <strong class=\"key-phrase\">avoids this waste entirely</strong>.\n\nI regard the trend as positive, mainly because of what it means for the person receiving the gift. Money allows the recipient to buy exactly what he or she needs, which matters enormously to a young couple furnishing a home or a student paying tuition fees. Nothing is thrown away, and nothing has to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">politely stored in a cupboard</strong>. It is true that a cash transfer can feel impersonal, and that the effort of choosing a present is itself <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sign of affection</strong>. However, this is easily solved by adding a handwritten card or by handing over the money in person.\n\nIn conclusion, people give money because it is quick and reliable, and because they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no longer trust their own judgement</strong>. Despite lacking the personal touch of a carefully chosen present, it is in my view a positive development.","phrases":[{"en":"the fear of choosing badly","context":"A second reason is the fear of choosing badly."},{"en":"avoids this waste entirely","context":"Almost everyone has received a present that they never used, and giving cash avoids this waste entirely."},{"en":"a sign of affection","context":"It is true that a cash transfer can feel impersonal, and that the effort of choosing a present is itself a sign of affection."},{"en":"politely stored in a cupboard","context":"Nothing is thrown away, and nothing has to be politely stored in a cupboard."},{"en":"no longer trust their own judgement","context":"In conclusion, people give money because it is quick and reliable, and because they no longer trust their own judgement. Despite lacking the personal touch of a carefully chosen present, it is in my view a positive development."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#104","summary":"Giving money instead of gifts"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2228","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Culture & Traditions","title":"Cities that look alike","prompt":"In the past, people used to travel abroad to look for many differences from their home country. Nowadays, cities throughout the world are becoming more and more similar. What are the reasons? Do the advantages outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"A traveller arriving in a large city today is likely to find the same coffee chains, the same clothing shops and the same glass towers as at home. This convergence has clear commercial causes, and in my view its disadvantages outweigh its benefits.\n\nThe main reason is the reach of international business. A successful retailer opens in fifty countries with an identical shopfront, because a recognisable brand is precisely what customers are paying for. Construction has followed the same path: the materials, engineering methods and even the architects behind a modern office tower are international, so a new district in Jakarta is designed by much the same process as one in Frankfurt. Local authorities encourage this, since attracting a known company or a famous architect is treated as evidence that a city has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">arrived on the world stage</strong>.\n\nThere are advantages. Familiarity lowers the barrier to travel for people who find unfamiliar places intimidating, and international standards have brought better plumbing, safer buildings and more reliable transport to cities that previously lacked them.\n\nThe losses are heavier, however. Distinctive local architecture, markets and trades are what gave a place its character, and once a historic quarter is replaced by a shopping centre it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot be recreated</strong>. Tourism suffers directly, because visitors travel to see what they cannot see at home, and a city that looks like everywhere else gives them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no particular reason to come</strong>. Local businesses also lose the ground on which they competed.\n\nIn conclusion, cities are converging because commerce and construction now operate globally. On balance the disadvantages are greater, since the practical gains could be achieved without demolishing what made each city <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worth visiting in the first place</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"arrived on the world stage","context":"Local authorities encourage this, since attracting a known company or a famous architect is treated as evidence that a city has arrived on the world stage."},{"en":"cannot be recreated","context":"Distinctive local architecture, markets and trades are what gave a place its character, and once a historic quarter is replaced by a shopping centre it cannot be recreated."},{"en":"no particular reason to come","context":"Tourism suffers directly, because visitors travel to see what they cannot see at home, and a city that looks like everywhere else gives them no particular reason to come."},{"en":"worth visiting in the first place","context":"In conclusion, cities are converging because commerce and construction now operate globally. On balance the disadvantages are greater, since the practical gains could be achieved without demolishing what made each city worth visiting in the first place."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Advantages outweigh disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#105","summary":"Why cities around the world are becoming more similar"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2232","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Culture & Traditions","title":"Is family history worth researching?","prompt":"In many parts of the world, people do research on their family history. Some people believe that finding out about previous generations is a useful thing to do. However, others believe that it is better to focus on present and future generations. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"Tracing ancestors has become a widespread hobby, helped by digitised records and inexpensive testing. Some regard it as valuable self-knowledge while others see it as time better spent on the living, and I believe both positions hold in different circumstances.\n\nThe case for researching the past begins with identity. Knowing that a great-grandmother crossed an ocean alone at nineteen, or that a family survived a famine, gives a person <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of where their own situation sits in a longer story</strong>, which can be steadying during difficult periods. There are practical returns as well. Family records reveal inherited conditions such as heart disease or certain cancers, and a doctor who knows that history can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">screen for it far earlier</strong>. For communities separated by migration or conflict, this research is also the only way of recovering what was lost.\n\nThose who disagree point out that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the past cannot be changed while the present can</strong>. Hours spent in archives are hours not spent with a grandparent who is still alive and whose memories will disappear with them, and there is something uncomfortable about a person who is proud of an ancestor's achievements while having <strong class=\"key-phrase\">contributed nothing of their own</strong>.\n\nMy view is that the objection applies to the extreme case rather than to the activity. Most people research a few evenings a year, which displaces very little, and the results often bring living relatives back into contact rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drawing attention away from them</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, family history is worth pursuing for the medical and personal understanding it offers, provided it does not become a substitute for present relationships. The healthiest approach treats it as a way of understanding the family that is still here.","phrases":[{"en":"screen for it far earlier","context":"Family records reveal inherited conditions such as heart disease or certain cancers, and a doctor who knows that history can screen for it far earlier."},{"en":"contributed nothing of their own","context":"Hours spent in archives are hours not spent with a grandparent who is still alive and whose memories will disappear with them, and there is something uncomfortable about a person who is proud of an ancestor's achievements while having contributed nothing of their own."},{"en":"drawing attention away from them","context":"Most people research a few evenings a year, which displaces very little, and the results often bring living relatives back into contact rather than drawing attention away from them."},{"en":"a sense of where their own situation sits in a longer story","context":"Knowing that a great-grandmother crossed an ocean alone at nineteen, or that a family survived a famine, gives a person a sense of where their own situation sits in a longer story, which can be steadying during difficult periods."},{"en":"the past cannot be changed while the present can","context":"Those who disagree point out that the past cannot be changed while the present can."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#106","summary":"Researching previous generations compared with focusing on the present"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Putting economic growth above everything else","prompt":"In many countries, the government prioritises economic growth above all other concerns. Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of this.","answer":"Economic growth is necessary for jobs, public revenue and better living standards, but it should not be the government's only priority. A country can become richer while its environment, health system and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">social trust deteriorate</strong>. Governments should therefore pursue growth alongside broader measures of progress.\n\nThe benefits of a growing economy are clear. Successful businesses create employment, wages rise and the state collects more tax. This money can fund schools, hospitals and infrastructure. In poorer countries, sustained growth can also reduce extreme poverty and give families access to basic services. For these reasons, governments cannot simply ignore economic performance.\n\nHowever, growth can produce serious costs if it depends on pollution, unsafe work or extreme inequality. Higher national income <strong class=\"key-phrase\">means little to people who cannot afford housing or healthcare</strong>. Governments should evaluate whether growth improves ordinary lives, not only whether production increases. Measures such as life expectancy, educational outcomes, air quality and income distribution provide <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fuller picture of national progress</strong>.\n\nThe central question is how the gains are distributed. When productivity rises across an economy, ordinary households usually see it in wages as well as taxes; but when growth is driven by a handful of highly automated or extractive industries, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">profits can rise sharply while employment and average pay barely move</strong>, as has happened in some oil-exporting and heavily automated economies. Governments therefore need environmental limits and social investment so that higher output <strong class=\"key-phrase\">translates into a broader improvement in living standards</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, economic growth should remain an important objective, but not the objective that overrides every other concern. The best policy is growth that is environmentally sustainable and widely shared, so that higher output translates into better lives.","topic":"Economy & Money","phrases":[{"en":"a fuller picture of national progress","context":"Measures such as life expectancy, educational outcomes, air quality and income distribution provide a fuller picture of national progress."},{"en":"translates into a broader improvement in living standards","context":"Governments therefore need environmental limits and social investment so that higher output translates into a broader improvement in living standards."},{"en":"social trust deteriorate","context":"A country can become richer while its environment, health system and social trust deteriorate."},{"en":"means little to people who cannot afford housing or healthcare","context":"Higher national income means little to people who cannot afford housing or healthcare."},{"en":"profits can rise sharply while employment and average pay barely move","context":"When productivity rises across an economy, ordinary households usually see it in wages as well as taxes; but when growth is driven by a handful of highly automated or extractive industries, profits can rise sharply while employment and average pay barely move, as has happened in some oil-exportin..."}],"subtype":"advdis","summary":"Governments that put economic growth above all other concerns","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-106","_displayNo":"#107"},{"id":"writing-task-2-142","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Economy & Money","title":"Saving money or spending it","prompt":"Some people think that it is best to save money, for example in a bank or savings scheme. Other people feel that money should be spent whenever it is available. Discuss these views and reach an opinion on this debate. Give reasons for your answer, and support your essay with ideas and examples from your own experience. You should write at least 250 words.","answer":"The question of whether to save or spend money is very topical today, as so many people are struggling with financial worries. There are valid arguments on both sides, but I believe that putting money aside whenever possible is the wiser course.\n\nOn the one hand, it is often said that spending money is one way to relax and enjoy life, for instance by shopping or taking holidays, and there is certainly some truth in this. We also have little choice but to spend a large proportion of our salary on day to day expenses such as food, housing and transport, so there is surprisingly little left to save anyway. Spending on products and services also boosts the economy and creates employment, which benefits everyone.\n\nOn the other hand, there are real dangers in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reckless spending</strong>, particularly on impulse purchases financed by credit. Advertising and, more recently, buy-now-pay-later schemes make it very easy to commit future income without noticing, and young people who have never had to manage a budget are especially vulnerable to this.\n\nThe stronger argument for saving, however, is security. Without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a reserve of funds for emergencies</strong>, a single period of unemployment or illness leaves people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">putting themselves at risk of hardship</strong> or even bankruptcy, which is why financial advisers commonly suggest keeping three months of expenses in reserve. Saving also allows people to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accumulate wealth</strong> over a lifetime, so that their children inherit money or property which will increase their security and standard of living.\n\nOverall, I tend to agree that it is wiser to save money wherever possible, for reasons which benefit both individuals today and future generations.","phrases":[{"en":"reckless spending","context":"On the other hand, there are real dangers in reckless spending, particularly on impulse purchases financed by credit."},{"en":"a reserve of funds for emergencies","context":"Without a reserve of funds for emergencies, a single period of unemployment or illness leaves people putting themselves at risk of hardship or even bankruptcy, which is why financial advisers commonly suggest keeping three months of expenses in reserve."},{"en":"putting themselves at risk of hardship","context":"Without a reserve of funds for emergencies, a single period of unemployment or illness leaves people putting themselves at risk of hardship or even bankruptcy, which is why financial advisers commonly suggest keeping three months of expenses in reserve."},{"en":"accumulate wealth","context":"Saving also allows people to accumulate wealth over a lifetime, so that their children inherit money or property which will increase their security and standard of living."}],"subtype":"Discussion","_displayNo":"#108","summary":"People think that it is best to save money, for example in a bank or…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2208","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Economy & Money","title":"Wealth and satisfaction in rich countries","prompt":"Some experts believe that when a country is already rich, any additional increase in economic wealth does not make its citizens any more satisfied. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Some economists argue that once a nation has reached a comfortable standard of living, further increases in national wealth do little to make its people happier. I largely agree with this position, although I would add that how the additional wealth is used matters a great deal.\n\nThe evidence for the argument is fairly strong. Once basic needs such as food, housing, healthcare and education have been met, extra income tends to be spent on goods that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">provide only temporary satisfaction</strong>. People quickly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">become accustomed to</strong> a larger house or a newer car, and then begin comparing themselves with those who have more, so the sense of improvement disappears. Several wealthy countries, including Japan and the United States, have seen national income rise substantially over recent decades with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no corresponding rise in reported life satisfaction</strong>.\n\nThe reason is that the things which make life genuinely satisfying are not usually purchased. Secure relationships, good health, meaningful work, free time and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of belonging</strong> determine well-being far more than income does. Indeed, the pursuit of higher earnings often damages precisely these things, because it demands longer hours and greater stress, leaving people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wealthier but more exhausted and more isolated</strong> than before.\n\nI would qualify this argument in one respect. Additional national wealth can still improve people's lives if it is deliberately directed towards reducing inequality, shortening working hours or improving public healthcare. What fails to increase satisfaction is simply raising average consumption.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that greater economic wealth does little for the citizens of an already prosperous country, unless that wealth is used to improve the conditions in which people actually live.","phrases":[{"en":"become accustomed to","context":"People quickly become accustomed to a larger house or a newer car, and then begin comparing themselves with those who have more, so the sense of improvement disappears."},{"en":"a sense of belonging","context":"Secure relationships, good health, meaningful work, free time and a sense of belonging determine well-being far more than income does."},{"en":"provide only temporary satisfaction","context":"Once basic needs such as food, housing, healthcare and education have been met, extra income tends to be spent on goods that provide only temporary satisfaction."},{"en":"no corresponding rise in reported life satisfaction","context":"Several wealthy countries, including Japan and the United States, have seen national income rise substantially over recent decades with no corresponding rise in reported life satisfaction."},{"en":"wealthier but more exhausted and more isolated","context":"Indeed, the pursuit of higher earnings often damages precisely these things, because it demands longer hours and greater stress, leaving people wealthier but more exhausted and more isolated than before."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#109","summary":"Wealth and satisfaction in rich countries"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2221","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Economy & Money","title":"Are credit cards worth the risk?","prompt":"Nowadays it is easy to apply for and be given a credit card. However, some people experience problems when they are not able to pay their debt back. In your opinion, do the advantages of credit cards outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"Credit cards are now issued almost automatically to anyone in regular employment, and while some borrowers fall into debt they cannot repay, I believe the benefits still outweigh the harm for most users.\n\nThe central advantage is that a card separates when money is needed from when it arrives. A boiler that fails in January cannot wait until payday, and a household without savings would otherwise have to borrow from a lender charging far more. Used this way, a card is a cheap form of short-term insurance rather than a way of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">living beyond one's means</strong>.\n\nCards also offer protection that cash does not. If goods are never delivered or a company collapses, the card provider is jointly liable and the money is returned, whereas cash handed over is simply gone. Paying by card leaves a record too, which makes tracking spending straightforward and gives young adults a credit history, without which renting a flat or obtaining a mortgage becomes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unnecessarily difficult</strong>.\n\nThe danger is genuine and should not be minimised. Interest of twenty per cent or more compounds quickly, and because the minimum repayment is deliberately small, a borrower who pays only that amount can service a debt for years while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">barely reducing the balance</strong>. Lenders have not always been careful about whom they approve, and those with the least income are pushed hardest.\n\nIn conclusion, the advantages outweigh the disadvantages, since a card used sensibly is a convenience and a safeguard. The harm comes not from the card itself but from careless lending and from borrowers who have never been taught how interest works, and both can be addressed by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stricter affordability checks</strong> and basic financial education in schools.","phrases":[{"en":"living beyond one's means","context":"Used this way, a card is a cheap form of short-term insurance rather than a way of living beyond one's means."},{"en":"unnecessarily difficult","context":"Paying by card leaves a record too, which makes tracking spending straightforward and gives young adults a credit history, without which renting a flat or obtaining a mortgage becomes unnecessarily difficult."},{"en":"barely reducing the balance","context":"Interest of twenty per cent or more compounds quickly, and because the minimum repayment is deliberately small, a borrower who pays only that amount can service a debt for years while barely reducing the balance."},{"en":"stricter affordability checks","context":"The harm comes not from the card itself but from careless lending and from borrowers who have never been taught how interest works, and both can be addressed by stricter affordability checks and basic financial education in schools."}],"subtype":"Advantages & Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#110","summary":"The advantages and disadvantages of easy access to credit cards"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2296","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Economy & Money","title":"Development and the values it costs","prompt":"Many countries aim to improve living standards through economic development, but some important social values are lost as a result. Do the advantages of economic development outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"Rapid development transforms what a country can offer its citizens and also changes how they live together. I believe the advantages outweigh the losses, though the losses are real and are felt most by those who did not choose the change.\n\nThe gains are measurable and enormous. Development brings clean water, electricity, vaccination and schooling, and the result is visible in child mortality that falls by more than half within a generation. It offers choice as well: young people can work outside farming, women can earn independently, and families are no longer confined to the village they were born in. Governments also gain the revenue that funds hospitals, pensions and courts, and it is worth remembering that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the values romanticised in poor societies came with early death and rigid hierarchy</strong>.\n\nThe costs are nonetheless genuine. Extended families disperse as work moves to cities, so the care that grandparents and neighbours once provided is replaced by services people must buy. Communities that shared work and celebrations become collections of households with little contact. Time is measured differently, since hospitality and obligation give way to schedules, and a competitive economy encourages people to compare rather than to help. Traditional crafts, dialects and festivals fade because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nobody has the hours to maintain them</strong>.\n\nThe important question is whether the losses are unavoidable, and largely they are not. Some countries have developed while protecting rest days, family time, language and public space, whereas others allowed the market to decide everything. What destroys social values is usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the speed and the absence of any policy</strong> rather than prosperity itself.\n\nIn conclusion, development delivers health, education and freedom that no traditional society provided, and few who have gained them wish to return. The social costs deserve attention, so governments should pursue growth while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deliberately protecting the time and places that hold communities together</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"the values romanticised in poor societies came with early death and rigid hierarchy","context":"Governments also gain the revenue that funds hospitals, pensions and courts, and it is worth remembering that the values romanticised in poor societies came with early death and rigid hierarchy."},{"en":"nobody has the hours to maintain them","context":"Traditional crafts, dialects and festivals fade because nobody has the hours to maintain them."},{"en":"the speed and the absence of any policy","context":"What destroys social values is usually the speed and the absence of any policy rather than prosperity itself."},{"en":"deliberately protecting the time and places that hold communities together","context":"The social costs deserve attention, so governments should pursue growth while deliberately protecting the time and places that hold communities together."}],"subtype":"Advantages / Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#111","summary":"Whether economic development is worth the social values that are lost"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2344","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Economy & Money","title":"Is wealth mainly a chance to help others?","prompt":"Some people think that the most important thing about being rich is that it gives a person the opportunity to help other people. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Wealthy individuals are frequently praised for their donations, and the idea that this is the point of having money is an attractive one. I disagree that it is the most important thing about being rich, although generosity is one of the better uses of a fortune.\n\nThe claim has obvious appeal. Money makes help possible on a scale that goodwill alone cannot reach: a single donor can fund a hospital wing or eliminate a disease from a region, and private foundations have paid for vaccination programmes that governments delayed. Giving also appears to benefit the giver, since people who support others report more satisfaction than those who spend the same amount on themselves, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a purpose beyond accumulation</strong> is what many wealthy people say they were missing.\n\nWhat the statement understates is the first and most personal thing wealth provides, which is security. Money removes the fear that dominates poorer lives: illness does not become catastrophe, a broken car does not cost the job, and children can be educated properly. More than that, it buys time and choice, so a person can leave bad work, refuse bad treatment and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">decide how their own days are spent</strong>. Anyone who has lacked money knows that this, rather than the chance to donate, is what having it means.\n\nThere is also reason for caution about the charitable version. Reliance on private generosity leaves essential services depending on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">whatever a donor happens to find interesting</strong>, and it is no substitute for a fair tax system. Some giving is closer to reputation management than to help.\n\nIn conclusion, wealth genuinely allows people to relieve suffering, and those who use it that way deserve credit. Its greatest significance is nonetheless the freedom and security it gives the person who holds it, and treating charity as the main point <strong class=\"key-phrase\">flatters the rich more than it helps the poor</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"a purpose beyond accumulation","context":"Giving also appears to benefit the giver, since people who support others report more satisfaction than those who spend the same amount on themselves, and a purpose beyond accumulation is what many wealthy people say they were missing."},{"en":"decide how their own days are spent","context":"More than that, it buys time and choice, so a person can leave bad work, refuse bad treatment and decide how their own days are spent."},{"en":"whatever a donor happens to find interesting","context":"Reliance on private generosity leaves essential services depending on whatever a donor happens to find interesting, and it is no substitute for a fair tax system."},{"en":"flatters the rich more than it helps the poor","context":"Its greatest significance is nonetheless the freedom and security it gives the person who holds it, and treating charity as the main point flatters the rich more than it helps the poor."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#112","summary":"Whether the best thing about being rich is the ability to help other people"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2347","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Economy & Money","title":"Growth against the environment","prompt":"Some people believe that economic growth is essential for ending global poverty and hunger, while others argue that it damages the environment. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Growth has lifted more people out of poverty than any other force and has also driven the emissions now changing the climate. Both observations are correct, and my own view is that the argument <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mistakes the amount of growth for the kind of growth</strong>.\n\nThe case for growth is supported by the record. Hundreds of millions of people left extreme poverty within a single generation as economies in Asia expanded, and it was industry and trade rather than aid that did it. Growth also pays for the things that end hunger permanently: clinics, roads, irrigation and schools all require tax revenue, and a government of a poor country <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot redistribute what has not been produced</strong>. Telling such countries to stop developing, having industrialised freely ourselves, is difficult to defend.\n\nThe environmental objection is equally grounded. Rising output has meant rising emissions, cleared forests and exhausted fisheries, and the planet cannot supply every person with the consumption levels of a wealthy country. There is also a fairness point, in that the poorest nations contributed least to the damage and are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suffering the worst of the consequences first</strong>.\n\nThe way out is that growth and emissions are separable. Several developed economies have expanded while their emissions fell, because energy came from wind and solar rather than coal and because services grew faster than heavy industry. Renewable power is now cheaper than fossil fuel in most places, so poorer countries can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">develop without repeating our route</strong> if the finance and technology reach them.\n\nIn conclusion, growth remains the most reliable route out of poverty and should not be abandoned. What has to end is growth measured by consumption of materials, and wealthy nations owe poorer ones the investment that makes cleaner development affordable.","phrases":[{"en":"cannot redistribute what has not been produced","context":"Growth also pays for the things that end hunger permanently: clinics, roads, irrigation and schools all require tax revenue, and a government of a poor country cannot redistribute what has not been produced."},{"en":"suffering the worst of the consequences first","context":"There is also a fairness point, in that the poorest nations contributed least to the damage and are suffering the worst of the consequences first."},{"en":"develop without repeating our route","context":"Renewable power is now cheaper than fossil fuel in most places, so poorer countries can develop without repeating our route if the finance and technology reach them."},{"en":"mistakes the amount of growth for the kind of growth","context":"Growth has lifted more people out of poverty than any other force and has also driven the emissions now changing the climate. Both observations are correct, and my own view is that the argument mistakes the amount of growth for the kind of growth."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#113","summary":"Whether economic growth is the cure for poverty or a threat to the planet"},{"id":"writing-task-2-050","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Study what you like, or what is useful?","prompt":"Some people think that all university students should study whatever they like. Others believe that they should only be allowed to study subjects that will be useful in the future, such as those related to science and technology. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People have different views about how much choice students should have with regard to what they can study at university. While some argue that it would be better for students to be forced into certain key subject areas, I believe that everyone should be able to study the course of their choice.\n\nThere are various reasons why people believe that universities should only offer subjects that will be useful in the future. They may assert that university courses like medicine, engineering and information technology are more likely to be beneficial than certain art degrees. From a personal perspective, it can be argued that these courses <strong class=\"key-phrase\">provide more job opportunities</strong>, career progression, better salaries, and therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an improved quality of life</strong> for students who take them. On the societal level, by forcing people to choose particular university subjects, governments can ensure that any <strong class=\"key-phrase\">knowledge and skill gaps in the economy are covered</strong>. Finally, a focus on technology in higher education could lead to new inventions, economic growth, and greater future prosperity.\n\nIn spite of these arguments, I believe that university students should be free to choose their <strong class=\"key-phrase\">preferred areas of study</strong>. In my opinion, society will benefit more if our students are passionate about what they are learning. Besides, nobody can really predict which areas of knowledge will be most useful to society in the future, and it may be that employers begin to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">value creative thinking skills</strong> above practical or technical skills. If this were the case, perhaps we would need more students of art, history and philosophy than of science or technology.\n\nIn conclusion, although it might seem sensible for universities to focus only on the most useful subjects, I personally prefer the current system in which people have the right to study whatever they like.","phrases":[{"en":"provide more job opportunities","context":"From a personal perspective, it can be argued that these courses provide more job opportunities, career progression, better salaries, and therefore an improved quality of life for students who take them."},{"en":"an improved quality of life","context":"From a personal perspective, it can be argued that these courses provide more job opportunities, career progression, better salaries, and therefore an improved quality of life for students who take them."},{"en":"preferred areas of study","context":"In spite of these arguments, I believe that university students should be free to choose their preferred areas of study."},{"en":"value creative thinking skills","context":"Besides, nobody can really predict which areas of knowledge will be most useful to society in the future, and it may be that employers begin to value creative thinking skills above practical or technical skills."},{"en":"knowledge and skill gaps in the economy are covered","context":"On the societal level, by forcing people to choose particular university subjects, governments can ensure that any knowledge and skill gaps in the economy are covered."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#114","summary":"Freedom to choose university subjects"},{"id":"writing-task-2-051","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Equal numbers of men and women in every subject","prompt":"Universities should accept equal numbers of male and female students in every subject. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"In my opinion, men and women should have the same educational opportunities. However, I do not agree with the idea of accepting equal proportions of each gender in every university subject.\n\nHaving the same number of men and women on all degree courses is simply unrealistic. Student numbers on any course depend on the applications that the institution receives. If a university decided to fill courses with equal numbers of males and females, it would need enough <strong class=\"key-phrase\">applicants of each gender</strong>. In reality, many courses are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more popular with one gender</strong> than the other, and it would not be practical to aim for equal proportions. For example, nursing courses tend to attract more female applicants, and it would be difficult to fill these courses if fifty per cent of the places needed to go to males.\n\nApart from the practical concerns expressed above, I also believe that it would be unfair to base <strong class=\"key-phrase\">admission to university courses</strong> on gender. Universities should continue to select the best candidates for each course according to their qualifications. In this way, both men and women <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have the same opportunities</strong>, and applicants know that they will be successful if they work hard to achieve good grades at school. If a female student is the best candidate for a place on a course, it is surely wrong to reject her in favour of a male student with lower grades or fewer qualifications.\n\nIn conclusion, the selection of university students should be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">based on merit</strong>, and it would be both impractical and unfair to change to a selection procedure based on gender.","phrases":[{"en":"applicants of each gender","context":"If a university decided to fill courses with equal numbers of males and females, it would need enough applicants of each gender."},{"en":"more popular with one gender","context":"In reality, many courses are more popular with one gender than the other, and it would not be practical to aim for equal proportions."},{"en":"admission to university courses","context":"Apart from the practical concerns expressed above, I also believe that it would be unfair to base admission to university courses on gender."},{"en":"have the same opportunities","context":"In this way, both men and women have the same opportunities, and applicants know that they will be successful if they work hard to achieve good grades at school."},{"en":"based on merit","context":"In conclusion, the selection of university students should be based on merit, and it would be both impractical and unfair to change to a selection procedure based on gender."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#115","summary":"Gender quotas in university admissions"},{"id":"writing-task-2-052","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Is homework worth setting?","prompt":"Some people believe that school children should not be given homework by their teachers, whereas others argue that homework plays an important role in the education of children. Discuss both of these views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People's opinions differ as to whether or not school children should be given homework. While there are some strong arguments against the setting of homework, I still believe that it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a necessary aspect of education</strong>.\n\nThere are several reasons why people might argue that homework is an unnecessary burden on children. The strongest is the evidence that it does nothing to improve educational outcomes: Finland sets its school children almost no homework, yet <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consistently outperforms countries where homework is standard practice</strong> in international league tables. Even where the evidence is less clear-cut, the practical case remains: a school day of six or seven hours already <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaves little energy for further study</strong>, and a child who is genuinely tired does not learn much from an extra hour of exercises. The time saved is not wasted either, since play and family time support the kind of brain development that formal lessons cannot provide on their own.\n\nIn spite of the above arguments, I support the view that homework has an important role to play in the schooling of children. The main benefit of homework is that it encourages independent learning and problem solving, as children are challenged to work through tasks alone and at their own pace. In doing so, students must apply the knowledge that they have learned in the classroom. For example, by doing mathematics exercises at home, students <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consolidate their understanding of the concepts</strong> taught by their teacher at school. In my view, it is important for children to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">develop an independent study habit</strong> because this prepares them to work alone as adults.\n\nIn conclusion, homework certainly has its drawbacks, but I believe that the benefits outweigh them in the long term.","phrases":[{"en":"a necessary aspect of education","context":"People's opinions differ as to whether or not school children should be given homework. While there are some strong arguments against the setting of homework, I still believe that it is a necessary aspect of education."},{"en":"consistently outperforms countries where homework is standard practice","context":"The strongest is the evidence that it does nothing to improve educational outcomes: Finland sets its school children almost no homework, yet consistently outperforms countries where homework is standard practice in international league tables."},{"en":"leaves little energy for further study","context":"Even where the evidence is less clear-cut, the practical case remains: a school day of six or seven hours already leaves little energy for further study, and a child who is genuinely tired does not learn much from an extra hour of exercises."},{"en":"consolidate their understanding of the concepts","context":"For example, by doing mathematics exercises at home, students consolidate their understanding of the concepts taught by their teacher at school."},{"en":"develop an independent study habit","context":"In my view, it is important for children to develop an independent study habit because this prepares them to work alone as adults."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#116","summary":"Homework in children's education"},{"id":"writing-task-2-053","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"University or straight into work?","prompt":"Some people believe that studying at university or college is the best route to a successful career, while others believe that it is better to get a job straight after school.","answer":"When they finish school, teenagers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">face the dilemma</strong> of whether to get a job or continue their education. While there are some benefits to getting a job straight after school, I would argue that it is better to go to college or university.\n\nThe option to start work straight after school is attractive for several reasons. Many young people want to start earning money as soon as possible, so that they can become independent and eventually afford their own house or start a family. There is also a genuine career advantage: an eighteen-year-old who joins a retail company or begins a trade apprenticeship as an electrician or plumber can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gain real experience and learn practical skills</strong> on the job. By the time a same-age graduate finally enters the workplace, several years and a considerable debt later, the early starter may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">already have been promoted into a supervisory role</strong>.\n\nOn the other hand, I believe that it is more beneficial for students to continue their studies. Firstly, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">academic qualifications</strong> are required in many professions. For example, it is impossible to become a doctor, teacher or lawyer without having the relevant degree. As a result, university graduates have access to more and better job opportunities, and they tend to earn higher salaries than those with fewer qualifications. Secondly, the job market is becoming <strong class=\"key-phrase\">increasingly competitive</strong>, and sometimes there are hundreds of applicants for one position in a company. Young people who do not have qualifications from a university or college will not be able to compete.\n\nIn conclusion, it seems to me that students are more likely to be successful in their careers if they continue their studies beyond school level.","phrases":[{"en":"face the dilemma","context":"When they finish school, teenagers face the dilemma of whether to get a job or continue their education. While there are some benefits to getting a job straight after school, I would argue that it is better to go to college or university."},{"en":"gain real experience and learn practical skills","context":"There is also a genuine career advantage: an eighteen-year-old who joins a retail company or begins a trade apprenticeship as an electrician or plumber can gain real experience and learn practical skills on the job."},{"en":"academic qualifications","context":"Firstly, academic qualifications are required in many professions."},{"en":"already have been promoted into a supervisory role","context":"By the time a same-age graduate finally enters the workplace, several years and a considerable debt later, the early starter may already have been promoted into a supervisory role."},{"en":"increasingly competitive","context":"Secondly, the job market is becoming increasingly competitive, and sometimes there are hundreds of applicants for one position in a company."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#117","summary":"People believe that studying at university or college is the best route to…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-054","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Private school fees and paying tax for state schools","prompt":"Families who send their children to private schools should not be required to pay taxes that support the state education system. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this statement?","answer":"Some people believe that parents of children who attend private schools should not need to contribute to state schools through taxes. Personally, I completely disagree with this view.\n\nFor a variety of reasons, it would be wrong to reduce taxes for families who pay for private education. Firstly, it would be difficult to calculate the correct amount of tax reduction for these families, and staff would be required to manage this complex process. Secondly, we all pay a certain amount of tax for public services that we may not use. For example, most people are fortunate enough not to have to call the police or fire brigade at any time in their lives, but they would not expect a tax reduction for this. Finally, if wealthy families were given a tax discount for sending their children to private schools, we might have a situation where poorer people pay higher taxes than the rich.\n\nIn my opinion, we should all be happy to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pay our share of the money that supports public schools</strong>. It is beneficial for all members of society to have a high quality education system with equal opportunities for all young people. This will result in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a well-educated workforce</strong>, and in turn a more productive and prosperous nation. Parents of children in private schools may also see the advantages of this in their own lives. For example, a company owner will need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">well qualified and competent staff</strong>, and a well-funded education system can provide such employees.\n\nIn conclusion, I do not believe that any <strong class=\"key-phrase\">financial concessions</strong> should be made for people who choose private education.","phrases":[{"en":"financial concessions","context":"In conclusion, I do not believe that any financial concessions should be made for people who choose private education."},{"en":"pay our share of the money that supports public schools","context":"In my opinion, we should all be happy to pay our share of the money that supports public schools."},{"en":"a well-educated workforce","context":"This will result in a well-educated workforce, and in turn a more productive and prosperous nation."},{"en":"well qualified and competent staff","context":"For example, a company owner will need well qualified and competent staff, and a well-funded education system can provide such employees."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#118","summary":"Private schools and education taxes"},{"id":"writing-task-2-055","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Is a gap year a good idea?","prompt":"Some people believe that a gap year between school and university is a good idea, while others disagree strongly. Consider both sides of this debate and present your own opinion.","answer":"Gap years, in which school leavers delay university for a year to travel or volunteer, have become increasingly common, and opinion is divided about whether they are worth the disruption. In my opinion the benefits usually justify a gap year provided it has a clear purpose, though the arguments against it are real.\n\nThe case against is mainly practical. A year of flights, accommodation and living costs is a real burden that often falls on the family or has to be borrowed against. Travelling for months can also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">blunt academic momentum</strong>, making it harder to return to essay deadlines and exam discipline than for a student who has come straight from school.\n\nSafety is a further concern, particularly for young people travelling independently for the first time. Without the structure of a school trip or a university term, a gap-year traveller is more exposed to medical emergencies, transport accidents and petty crime, often somewhere language barriers make help harder to find.\n\nOn the other hand, supporters of gap years say that the skills learned outweigh the disadvantages. They highlight the independence and assertiveness that a student will acquire, in areas such as time management and interpersonal skills. They also say that these skills lead to greater career success later in life, which cancels out the costs involved. A student who spends six months teaching English or volunteering on a conservation project, for instance, returns with organisational experience and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a self-reliance that few school leavers have</strong>, and this is precisely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what stands out on an application</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, my own feeling is that a year off can indeed be a useful activity, with the experience leading to improved skills that are invaluable in the future. Of course, this is provided that the year is planned carefully and used for something <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely worthwhile</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"genuinely worthwhile","context":"Of course, this is provided that the year is planned carefully and used for something genuinely worthwhile."},{"en":"blunt academic momentum","context":"Travelling for months can also blunt academic momentum, making it harder to return to essay deadlines and exam discipline than for a student who has come straight from school."},{"en":"a self-reliance that few school leavers have","context":"A student who spends six months teaching English or volunteering on a conservation project, for instance, returns with organisational experience and a self-reliance that few school leavers have, and this is precisely what stands out on an application."},{"en":"what stands out on an application","context":"A student who spends six months teaching English or volunteering on a conservation project, for instance, returns with organisational experience and a self-reliance that few school leavers have, and this is precisely what stands out on an application."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#119","summary":"People believe that a gap year between school and university is a good…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-056","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Should sport be compulsory at school?","prompt":"Some people believe that sport is an essential part of school life for children, while others feel it should be purely optional. Discuss these opposing views and give your own opinion.","answer":"The place of sport in the school curriculum is a subject of real disagreement, and there are reasonable arguments on both sides, although in my view the case for making it compulsory is the stronger one. On the one hand, some people feel that sport uses up time which could be better spent on academic subjects such as exam preparation.\n\nCritics also argue that it is unwise to force children into activities they have no interest in. A pupil who dreads every games lesson learns little from it, and some youngsters are simply not sporty and would benefit more from extra study. A third concern is the conduct of professional sport itself: doping and corruption scandals have become common in cycling and athletics, and some parents feel that these set a poor example to young people.\n\nOn the other hand, the clearest benefit of sport is its contribution to children's physical development at the stage when <strong class=\"key-phrase\">habits are formed</strong>. Children who never exercise at school are far less likely to do so as adults, and obesity among young people has risen sharply in countries where physical education has been cut. Poor fitness also affects concentration, so the time given to sport is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">partly repaid in the classroom</strong>.\n\nSupporters of school sport also emphasise the value of team games such as football or hockey, in which children must accept instruction, depend on others and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put a shared aim above their own performance</strong>. A related argument is that a competitive spirit, so important today, is best formed early: learning to lose a match and return to training the following week <strong class=\"key-phrase\">teaches a resilience that examinations alone rarely develop</strong>.\n\nOverall, I believe that sport should be compulsory for school children, because the positive effect on their mental and physical development outweighs the objections. Schools should, however, offer a range wide enough for every child to find something they enjoy, and should teach fair play as firmly as they teach the game itself.","phrases":[{"en":"habits are formed","context":"On the other hand, the clearest benefit of sport is its contribution to children's physical development at the stage when habits are formed."},{"en":"partly repaid in the classroom","context":"Poor fitness also affects concentration, so the time given to sport is partly repaid in the classroom."},{"en":"put a shared aim above their own performance","context":"Supporters of school sport also emphasise the value of team games such as football or hockey, in which children must accept instruction, depend on others and put a shared aim above their own performance."},{"en":"teaches a resilience that examinations alone rarely develop","context":"A related argument is that a competitive spirit, so important today, is best formed early: learning to lose a match and return to training the following week teaches a resilience that examinations alone rarely develop."}],"subtype":"Discussion","_displayNo":"#120","summary":"People believe that sport is an essential part of school life for children, …"},{"id":"writing-task-2-057","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Will computers replace teachers?","prompt":"Computers are now widely used in education, and some people think that teachers will no longer play an important role in the classroom. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"As computers have become central to education, some people predict that teachers will no longer play an important role in the classroom. I disagree with this view, because technology changes what teachers do rather than removing the need for them.\n\nThe argument for the prediction is understandable. A well-designed program can explain a concept as clearly as most teachers, repeat it indefinitely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without impatience</strong>, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">adjust its difficulty to each student</strong>. Online courses give learners in remote areas access to material that would otherwise be unavailable, and software marks tests instantly, which frees a great deal of time.\n\nNevertheless, these advantages address only part of what education involves. Most students, particularly younger ones, do not study consistently without someone who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">notices when they have stopped trying</strong> and asks why. A teacher recognises that a child is struggling because of problems at home rather than because the material is difficult, and no program can make that judgement. Motivation is also personal: pupils work hard for teachers they respect and are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reluctant to disappoint</strong>, and no program can generate that.\n\nTeaching also involves skills that software cannot assess. Discussion, argument and cooperation are learned from other people, and a computer cannot tell a student that their reasoning is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">superficially plausible but wrong</strong>. Experience with remote learning demonstrated this clearly, since results fell sharply and the weakest students suffered most.\n\nIn conclusion, computers are valuable tools that have changed how teachers work, but I disagree that they will make teachers unimportant. The human elements of education have become more important, not less.","phrases":[{"en":"without impatience","context":"A well-designed program can explain a concept as clearly as most teachers, repeat it indefinitely without impatience, and adjust its difficulty to each student."},{"en":"notices when they have stopped trying","context":"Most students, particularly younger ones, do not study consistently without someone who notices when they have stopped trying and asks why."},{"en":"superficially plausible but wrong","context":"Discussion, argument and cooperation are learned from other people, and a computer cannot tell a student that their reasoning is superficially plausible but wrong."},{"en":"adjust its difficulty to each student","context":"A well-designed program can explain a concept as clearly as most teachers, repeat it indefinitely without impatience, and adjust its difficulty to each student."},{"en":"reluctant to disappoint","context":"Motivation is also personal: pupils work hard for teachers they respect and are reluctant to disappoint, and no program can generate that."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#121","summary":"Will computers replace teachers?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-058","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"The advantages and disadvantages of studying abroad","prompt":"Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of studying abroad.","answer":"Increasing numbers of students now complete part of their education in another country. In my view the experience offers substantial benefits, although the difficulties involved are greater than most students expect before they go.\n\nThe clearest advantage is language. Living somewhere the language is spoken constantly produces a fluency that classroom study rarely achieves, because everyday necessities such as shopping, banking and asking directions force students to use it. Cultural understanding develops in the same way: a student who has actually lived in a country learns how people there think and behave, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rather than learning about it second-hand</strong>.\n\nThe second benefit is independence. Students abroad have to manage accommodation, money, cooking and bureaucracy without their families, often in a second language. Most find the first few months genuinely difficult, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the confidence that comes from having coped</strong> is lasting, and employers recognise it immediately. The international friendships formed along the way are valuable too, both personally and later in a career.\n\nThe drawbacks are real, however. Studying abroad is expensive, which restricts it to families who can afford it or students who win scholarships. Loneliness is the more serious problem: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">homesickness affects almost everyone</strong> at some stage, and a student who is struggling has no family nearby and may not yet have formed close friendships. A minority never adjust and return home early.\n\nIn conclusion, studying abroad develops language ability, cultural understanding and self-reliance far more effectively than studying at home ever could. Students should nevertheless <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prepare for a difficult first term</strong> rather than expecting the whole experience to be straightforwardly enjoyable.","phrases":[{"en":"the confidence that comes from having coped","context":"Most find the first few months genuinely difficult, but the confidence that comes from having coped is lasting, and employers recognise it immediately."},{"en":"homesickness affects almost everyone","context":"Loneliness is the more serious problem: homesickness affects almost everyone at some stage, and a student who is struggling has no family nearby and may not yet have formed close friendships."},{"en":"prepare for a difficult first term","context":"Students should nevertheless prepare for a difficult first term rather than expecting the whole experience to be straightforwardly enjoyable."},{"en":"rather than learning about it second-hand","context":"Cultural understanding develops in the same way: a student who has actually lived in a country learns how people there think and behave, rather than learning about it second-hand."}],"subtype":"Advantages & Disadvantages","_displayNo":"#122","summary":"The advantages and disadvantages of studying abroad"},{"id":"writing-task-2-060","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Pushing children to study hard","prompt":"Some people believe that children should be pushed to study hard from a young age. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that children should be pushed to work hard academically from an early age. I agree that high expectations benefit young people, but I believe that excessive pressure does more harm than good.\n\nThere is a strong case for expecting effort. Skills such as reading, mathematics and a foreign language are far easier to acquire when young, and a child who falls behind early rarely catches up. Habits also form early: a student who learns to work steadily, meet deadlines and persist through difficulty acquires something more valuable than the subject content itself. Left entirely to themselves, most children would choose <strong class=\"key-phrase\">immediate entertainment over long-term benefit</strong>, so a degree of adult direction is clearly necessary.\n\nThe difficulty is that pressure has a point beyond which it becomes destructive. In several countries where competition for university places is extreme, rates of anxiety and sleep deprivation among teenagers are among the highest in the world. Excessive pressure also damages learning itself, because a student who studies only to avoid punishment <strong class=\"key-phrase\">memorises material and forgets it immediately</strong>, and often develops <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a lasting dislike of the subject</strong>. Children additionally need time for play, sport and friendship, and these are not luxuries: they are where social skills and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">emotional resilience</strong> develop.\n\nIn my opinion the distinction lies <strong class=\"key-phrase\">between encouragement and coercion</strong>. Parents should set clear expectations and take an interest in progress. They should not threaten, compare a child unfavourably with siblings, or fill every evening with extra classes.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that children should be expected to work hard, but pressure must be kept within limits that protect their health and their interest in learning.","phrases":[{"en":"a lasting dislike of the subject","context":"Excessive pressure also damages learning itself, because a student who studies only to avoid punishment memorises material and forgets it immediately, and often develops a lasting dislike of the subject."},{"en":"emotional resilience","context":"Children additionally need time for play, sport and friendship, and these are not luxuries: they are where social skills and emotional resilience develop."},{"en":"immediate entertainment over long-term benefit","context":"Left entirely to themselves, most children would choose immediate entertainment over long-term benefit, so a degree of adult direction is clearly necessary."},{"en":"memorises material and forgets it immediately","context":"Excessive pressure also damages learning itself, because a student who studies only to avoid punishment memorises material and forgets it immediately, and often develops a lasting dislike of the subject."},{"en":"between encouragement and coercion","context":"In my opinion the distinction lies between encouragement and coercion. Parents should set clear expectations and take an interest in progress. They should not threaten, compare a child unfavourably with siblings, or fill every evening with extra classes."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#123","summary":"Pushing children to study hard"},{"id":"writing-task-2-061","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Teaching international news at school","prompt":"Some people think that international news should be taught as a school subject. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes proposed that international news should be taught as a formal subject in secondary schools. I agree with this suggestion, provided that it is taught as a way of understanding the world rather than as a series of facts to be memorised.\n\nThe main argument in favour is that young people now encounter international events constantly but without any framework for interpreting them. Social media presents war, migration and economic crisis <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in fragments</strong>, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stripped of history and often deliberately distorted</strong>. A student who has been taught how a conflict arose, and who is used to comparing how different countries report the same event, is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">considerably harder to mislead</strong>.\n\nThere is also a practical justification. Economies are now interconnected, and decisions taken abroad affect employment, prices and the environment at home. Young people will vote on questions of trade, immigration and climate policy, and they cannot do so sensibly without some knowledge of the wider world. Employers increasingly value the same awareness, since many graduates work with colleagues in other countries.\n\nThe obvious objection is that the curriculum is already crowded and that news <strong class=\"key-phrase\">becomes outdated immediately</strong>. This is a fair point, and it suggests that the subject should focus on skills rather than events: how to identify a reliable source, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">how to recognise bias</strong>, and how a country's history shapes its behaviour.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that international news deserves a place in secondary education, but it should be taught as media literacy rather than as a list of events.","phrases":[{"en":"in fragments","context":"Social media presents war, migration and economic crisis in fragments, stripped of history and often deliberately distorted."},{"en":"considerably harder to mislead","context":"A student who has been taught how a conflict arose, and who is used to comparing how different countries report the same event, is considerably harder to mislead."},{"en":"becomes outdated immediately","context":"The obvious objection is that the curriculum is already crowded and that news becomes outdated immediately."},{"en":"stripped of history and often deliberately distorted","context":"Social media presents war, migration and economic crisis in fragments, stripped of history and often deliberately distorted."},{"en":"how to recognise bias","context":"This is a fair point, and it suggests that the subject should focus on skills rather than events: how to identify a reliable source, how to recognise bias, and how a country's history shapes its behaviour."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#124","summary":"Teaching international news at school"},{"id":"writing-task-2-062","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Education as a lifelong process","prompt":"In the past, education was a short period of training, but today people treat it as a lifelong process. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is often observed that education is no longer confined to childhood and early adulthood, but continues throughout a person's working life. I agree that this change has taken place, and I believe it is both necessary and beneficial.\n\nThe main reason is that knowledge and technology now change far faster than they once did. A person who trained as an accountant or a mechanic forty years ago could expect the skills learned at twenty to remain useful until retirement. Today, software, regulations and equipment are replaced every few years, and entire occupations disappear. An employee who stops learning becomes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unemployable long before</strong> reaching pension age, so continuous training is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">simply a condition of remaining in work</strong>.\n\nA second reason is that access to learning has become far easier. Online courses, professional qualifications and evening classes allow an adult to study while working, which was rarely possible in the past. Employers increasingly fund this training themselves, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">retraining existing staff</strong> is cheaper than recruiting new ones.\n\nThere are benefits beyond employment. Adults who continue to study tend to be more confident and more adaptable, and there is evidence that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sustained mental activity</strong> helps to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">protect memory in later life</strong>. Many people also return to education for subjects they were unable to pursue when young, which is a genuine improvement in quality of life.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that education has become a lifelong process. This is an unavoidable consequence of rapid technological change, but it also gives adults opportunities that earlier generations never had.","phrases":[{"en":"unemployable long before","context":"An employee who stops learning becomes unemployable long before reaching pension age, so continuous training is simply a condition of remaining in work."},{"en":"retraining existing staff","context":"Employers increasingly fund this training themselves, since retraining existing staff is cheaper than recruiting new ones."},{"en":"protect memory in later life","context":"Adults who continue to study tend to be more confident and more adaptable, and there is evidence that sustained mental activity helps to protect memory in later life."},{"en":"simply a condition of remaining in work","context":"An employee who stops learning becomes unemployable long before reaching pension age, so continuous training is simply a condition of remaining in work."},{"en":"sustained mental activity","context":"Adults who continue to study tend to be more confident and more adaptable, and there is evidence that sustained mental activity helps to protect memory in later life."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#125","summary":"Education as a lifelong process"},{"id":"writing-task-2-063","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Women in male-dominated university subjects","prompt":"Women remain underrepresented in some university subjects. Some people argue that this imbalance can never be eliminated. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Women remain a small minority in subjects such as engineering, physics and computer science, and some people argue that this imbalance can never be removed. I disagree, because the evidence suggests that it results from social influences rather than from any fixed difference in ability.\n\nThose who believe the gap is permanent usually claim that men and women are naturally drawn to different fields. This argument does not survive comparison between countries and periods. Women now outnumber men in medicine, law and veterinary science, all of which were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overwhelmingly male</strong> within living memory, and the proportion of female computing graduates in several Asian countries is far higher than in Western Europe. A preference that varies so widely cannot be biological.\n\nThe real causes are social, and they begin early. Children are given different toys and praised for different achievements, and by secondary school many girls have absorbed the idea that mathematics and technology are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not meant for them</strong>. Science is frequently taught by men, careers advice reflects the same assumptions, and university departments where women are visibly outnumbered can feel unwelcoming, which discourages those who do enrol from continuing.\n\nBecause the causes are social, they can be changed. Countries that have introduced early exposure to coding, recruited female science teachers, publicised women working in these fields and reformed workplace conditions have all seen female enrolment <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rise steadily</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that this imbalance is permanent. It reflects expectations formed in childhood, and expectations can be altered by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deliberate policy</strong> in schools and universities.","phrases":[{"en":"overwhelmingly male","context":"Women now outnumber men in medicine, law and veterinary science, all of which were overwhelmingly male within living memory, and the proportion of female computing graduates in several Asian countries is far higher than in Western Europe."},{"en":"not meant for them","context":"Children are given different toys and praised for different achievements, and by secondary school many girls have absorbed the idea that mathematics and technology are not meant for them."},{"en":"rise steadily","context":"Countries that have introduced early exposure to coding, recruited female science teachers, publicised women working in these fields and reformed workplace conditions have all seen female enrolment rise steadily."},{"en":"deliberate policy","context":"In conclusion, I disagree that this imbalance is permanent. It reflects expectations formed in childhood, and expectations can be altered by deliberate policy in schools and universities."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#126","summary":"Women in male-dominated university subjects"},{"id":"writing-task-2-065","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Are schools still necessary?","prompt":"Some people argue that schools have become unnecessary because children can study at home using the Internet. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Since a great deal of educational material is now freely available online, some people argue that schools are no longer necessary. I strongly disagree, because schools provide far more than the transmission of information.\n\nThe argument for home study has some foundation. Online courses allow a student to progress at their own speed, to repeat difficult explanations and to study subjects their local school does not offer. For children who are seriously ill, live in remote areas or have been badly bullied, this can be genuinely valuable, and in some cases it is the only practical option available.\n\nNevertheless, information is only one part of education. Most children do not study consistently without structure and supervision, and the experience of remote teaching demonstrated this clearly: attainment fell in almost every country, and the children who suffered most were those from poorer homes with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no quiet space and no reliable device</strong>. Removing schools would therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">widen inequality dramatically</strong>.\n\nSchools also teach what cannot be learned alone. Cooperating with people one has not chosen, resolving disagreements, presenting an argument and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accepting correction</strong> are social skills acquired through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">daily practice with others</strong>. A child educated entirely at home may be academically competent but unprepared for a workplace. Schools additionally serve a protective function, since teachers are frequently <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the first adults to notice neglect</strong> or abuse at home.\n\nIn conclusion, online resources are a valuable addition to education, but I disagree that they could replace schools. The social, protective and structural roles that schools perform have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no realistic online equivalent</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"no quiet space and no reliable device","context":"Most children do not study consistently without structure and supervision, and the experience of remote teaching demonstrated this clearly: attainment fell in almost every country, and the children who suffered most were those from poorer homes with no quiet space and no reliable device."},{"en":"daily practice with others","context":"Cooperating with people one has not chosen, resolving disagreements, presenting an argument and accepting correction are social skills acquired through daily practice with others."},{"en":"no realistic online equivalent","context":"In conclusion, online resources are a valuable addition to education, but I disagree that they could replace schools. The social, protective and structural roles that schools perform have no realistic online equivalent."},{"en":"widen inequality dramatically","context":"Removing schools would therefore widen inequality dramatically."},{"en":"accepting correction","context":"Cooperating with people one has not chosen, resolving disagreements, presenting an argument and accepting correction are social skills acquired through daily practice with others."},{"en":"the first adults to notice neglect","context":"Schools additionally serve a protective function, since teachers are frequently the first adults to notice neglect or abuse at home."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#127","summary":"Are schools still necessary?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-066","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Should students evaluate their teachers?","prompt":"Some people believe that high school students should be encouraged to evaluate and criticise their teachers in order to improve the quality of education. Others think this would result in a loss of respect and discipline in the classroom. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Some people believe that allowing secondary students to evaluate their teachers would raise standards, while others fear it would undermine respect and discipline. In my opinion student feedback is valuable, but only if it is collected carefully and used appropriately.\n\nThe argument in favour is that students observe teaching more closely than anyone else. An inspector visits a classroom once every few years, whereas pupils sit through every lesson and know precisely who explains clearly, who marks work promptly and who does not prepare. Without their views, a weak teacher may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">continue unnoticed for decades</strong>. Being consulted also encourages students to take their own learning more seriously, since it treats them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as participants rather than as passive recipients</strong>.\n\nThe objection is equally reasonable. Adolescents may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reward teachers who are entertaining or lenient</strong> and criticise those who set demanding work, so the teachers most likely to raise attainment could be penalised. There is also evidence from universities that such evaluations reflect prejudice about a teacher's gender, age or accent rather than teaching quality. If feedback is public, or affects pay directly, the authority a teacher needs to maintain order may be damaged.\n\nThe sensible approach is therefore a compromise. Feedback should be anonymous, gathered through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">specific questions about clarity and fairness</strong> rather than general popularity, and given to the teacher and head of department as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one source of evidence among several</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I support consulting students, since they possess information nobody else has. It should inform professional judgement, however, rather than function as a public verdict on individual teachers.","phrases":[{"en":"continue unnoticed for decades","context":"Without their views, a weak teacher may continue unnoticed for decades."},{"en":"specific questions about clarity and fairness","context":"Feedback should be anonymous, gathered through specific questions about clarity and fairness rather than general popularity, and given to the teacher and head of department as one source of evidence among several."},{"en":"as participants rather than as passive recipients","context":"Being consulted also encourages students to take their own learning more seriously, since it treats them as participants rather than as passive recipients."},{"en":"reward teachers who are entertaining or lenient","context":"Adolescents may reward teachers who are entertaining or lenient and criticise those who set demanding work, so the teachers most likely to raise attainment could be penalised."},{"en":"one source of evidence among several","context":"Feedback should be anonymous, gathered through specific questions about clarity and fairness rather than general popularity, and given to the teacher and head of department as one source of evidence among several."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#128","summary":"Should students evaluate their teachers?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-067","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Broad study or specialisation?","prompt":"It is argued that university students should study a full range of subjects instead of specialising in one particular area. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that university students should study a wide range of subjects rather than concentrating on a single field. I partly agree: some breadth is valuable, but depth is what a degree exists to provide.\n\nThe case for breadth is reasonable. Employers increasingly want graduates who can do several things, since most jobs now combine tasks that once belonged to separate roles — a marketing position may require statistics, design and writing. Studying different subjects also develops flexible thinking, because each discipline approaches problems differently, and a student who has encountered <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more than one way of reasoning</strong> adapts more easily when their field changes.\n\nNevertheless, a degree spread across many subjects risks producing graduates who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">know a little about everything and are qualified for nothing</strong>. Serious understanding of any discipline takes years, and the value of a graduate lies precisely in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">having gone further into a subject</strong> than a person could manage alone. Nobody would want a doctor or a structural engineer whose training had been broad but shallow. Employers also value the qualification because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">it certifies depth</strong>; a degree guaranteeing only general familiarity would tell them very little.\n\nThe sensible arrangement, in my view, is the one many universities already use: a specialised main subject supported by a smaller number of optional courses outside it. That gives students genuine expertise while ensuring they can write clearly, handle data and understand something of a second field.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that students benefit from studying beyond their own discipline, but this should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">supplement specialisation rather than replace it</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"more than one way of reasoning","context":"Studying different subjects also develops flexible thinking, because each discipline approaches problems differently, and a student who has encountered more than one way of reasoning adapts more easily when their field changes."},{"en":"having gone further into a subject","context":"Serious understanding of any discipline takes years, and the value of a graduate lies precisely in having gone further into a subject than a person could manage alone."},{"en":"supplement specialisation rather than replace it","context":"In conclusion, I agree that students benefit from studying beyond their own discipline, but this should supplement specialisation rather than replace it."},{"en":"know a little about everything and are qualified for nothing","context":"Nevertheless, a degree spread across many subjects risks producing graduates who know a little about everything and are qualified for nothing."},{"en":"it certifies depth","context":"Employers also value the qualification because it certifies depth; a degree guaranteeing only general familiarity would tell them very little."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#129","summary":"Broad study or specialisation?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-068","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"What education should consist of","prompt":"It is generally believed that education is of vital importance to individual development and to the well-being of societies. What should education consist of in order to fulfil both of these functions?","answer":"Education is widely regarded as the foundation of both personal development and national well-being. In my opinion, a system that achieves both must combine practical knowledge with the ability to think independently.\n\nThe first requirement is a solid academic and vocational foundation. Literacy, numeracy and scientific understanding determine what a person can do for the rest of their life, and no other subject <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compensates for weakness</strong> in these. Countries also need engineers, nurses, technicians and electricians, so respected vocational training must exist alongside academic routes rather than being <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treated as an inferior option</strong>. A young person who leaves school with a genuine qualification can support a family, which serves the individual and the state at the same time.\n\nThe second requirement is the ability to reason. Facts are now available instantly, so the more valuable skill is judging which of them are true. Students who are taught to examine evidence, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recognise a weak argument</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">change their minds when the evidence demands it</strong> become much harder to deceive, and a population of this kind is essential to a functioning democracy. This is precisely what <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rote memorisation</strong> fails to produce.\n\nEducation should also teach people to live together. Cooperation, honesty and respect for those who are different are learned through group work and through the way teachers behave, rather than through separate lessons in citizenship.\n\nIn conclusion, education fulfils both its functions when it provides usable skills, trains people to think for themselves, and teaches them to work with others.","phrases":[{"en":"compensates for weakness","context":"Literacy, numeracy and scientific understanding determine what a person can do for the rest of their life, and no other subject compensates for weakness in these."},{"en":"recognise a weak argument","context":"Students who are taught to examine evidence, recognise a weak argument and change their minds when the evidence demands it become much harder to deceive, and a population of this kind is essential to a functioning democracy."},{"en":"rote memorisation","context":"This is precisely what rote memorisation fails to produce."},{"en":"treated as an inferior option","context":"Countries also need engineers, nurses, technicians and electricians, so respected vocational training must exist alongside academic routes rather than being treated as an inferior option."},{"en":"change their minds when the evidence demands it","context":"Students who are taught to examine evidence, recognise a weak argument and change their minds when the evidence demands it become much harder to deceive, and a population of this kind is essential to a functioning democracy."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#130","summary":"What education should consist of"},{"id":"writing-task-2-070","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Should students pay the full cost of a degree?","prompt":"It is widely accepted that people with post-school qualifications earn higher salaries than those who are less educated. University students should therefore pay the full cost of obtaining their degree. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Graduates generally earn more than people who leave school at eighteen, and some argue that students should therefore meet the entire cost of their degrees. I disagree, because although graduates do gain personally, society gains at least as much and should pay a share.\n\nThe argument for full fees is not unreasonable. A degree is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a private investment that raises lifetime earnings</strong> substantially, and it seems unfair to ask a taxpayer who never attended university to fund someone who will eventually earn considerably more. Public budgets are also limited, and money spent on universities cannot be spent on schools or hospitals.\n\nThe difficulty is that education produces benefits far beyond the individual. Doctors, engineers, teachers and nurses are all trained at universities, and no country can function without them, whatever the private earnings of the graduates concerned. Higher education also produces the research on which new industries depend. Since the public receives these benefits, it is reasonable for the public to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">contribute to the cost</strong>.\n\nFull fees would also change who studies. Several degrees of real social value, such as nursing and teaching, are among the worst paid, so students facing enormous debts would avoid <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exactly the professions a country needs most</strong>. Students from low-income families are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the most sensitive to debt</strong>, and they are the ones most likely to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deterred altogether</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that students should bear the full cost. A shared system, in which graduates repay a proportion once they are earning and the state funds the remainder, distributes the burden according to who actually benefits.","phrases":[{"en":"contribute to the cost","context":"Since the public receives these benefits, it is reasonable for the public to contribute to the cost."},{"en":"exactly the professions a country needs most","context":"Several degrees of real social value, such as nursing and teaching, are among the worst paid, so students facing enormous debts would avoid exactly the professions a country needs most."},{"en":"deterred altogether","context":"Students from low-income families are the most sensitive to debt, and they are the ones most likely to be deterred altogether."},{"en":"a private investment that raises lifetime earnings","context":"A degree is a private investment that raises lifetime earnings substantially, and it seems unfair to ask a taxpayer who never attended university to fund someone who will eventually earn considerably more."},{"en":"the most sensitive to debt","context":"Students from low-income families are the most sensitive to debt, and they are the ones most likely to be deterred altogether."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#131","summary":"Should students pay the full cost of a degree?"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Who should teach children to behave well?","prompt":"Some people think that parents should teach their children how to be good members of society. Others, however, believe that school is the best place to learn this. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Children learn social behaviour from both their families and their schools. Parents have the strongest influence in the early years, while schools give children daily opportunities to practise cooperation and responsibility. I therefore believe that both share this duty.\n\nParents shape a child's first ideas about honesty, respect and self-control. Children observe how adults speak to others, keep promises and deal with disagreement. Advice is useful, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">example is more powerful</strong>. A home in which adults act responsibly gives children <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clear moral foundation</strong> before they enter wider society.\n\nSchools then expand this learning. In class, children must wait their turn, follow common rules and work with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">people from different backgrounds</strong>. Teachers can discuss bullying, fairness and civic responsibility, while group projects allow students to put these ideas into practice. Schools are particularly important when children do not receive consistent guidance at home, but they cannot <strong class=\"key-phrase\">replace parental example</strong>.\n\nCooperation between parents and teachers is especially important when a child behaves badly. If the two sides send opposite messages, rules lose their force. Regular communication, clear consequences and shared expectations help adults identify whether a problem begins at home, at school or online. This creates <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a consistent framework for social development</strong>. This shared approach is especially valuable during adolescence, when peer pressure becomes stronger and rules are more likely to be questioned.\n\nIn conclusion, parents should introduce the values that make someone a good member of society, and schools should reinforce them through teaching and shared experience. The most effective approach is close cooperation between home and school, so that children receive <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consistent expectations in different settings</strong>.","topic":"Education","phrases":[{"en":"a clear moral foundation","context":"A home in which adults act responsibly gives children a clear moral foundation before they enter wider society."},{"en":"people from different backgrounds","context":"In class, children must wait their turn, follow common rules and work with people from different backgrounds."},{"en":"replace parental example","context":"Schools are particularly important when children do not receive consistent guidance at home, but they cannot replace parental example."},{"en":"a consistent framework for social development","context":"This creates a consistent framework for social development."},{"en":"consistent expectations in different settings","context":"The most effective approach is close cooperation between home and school, so that children receive consistent expectations in different settings."},{"en":"example is more powerful","context":"Advice is useful, but example is more powerful."}],"subtype":"discuss","summary":"Whether parents or schools should teach children to behave well","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-071","_displayNo":"#132"},{"id":"writing-task-2-072","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Boarding school or day school?","prompt":"Some people argue that it is good for children to go to a boarding school away from their families. Others believe that children should live at home and attend a day school. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Some parents send their children to boarding school, while others believe children should remain at home and attend a local day school. In my opinion a day school is the better choice for most children, although boarding suits particular circumstances.\n\nThe advantages of boarding are real. Pupils live in a structured environment where study time is supervised, and facilities are usually excellent because everything is concentrated on one site. Living away from home also develops independence: children learn to manage their own routines, resolve disagreements and cope <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without a parent solving every problem</strong>, which many find valuable when they later go to university.\n\nNevertheless, the disadvantages are significant for younger children. Family relationships depend on daily contact, and a child who sees their parents only during holidays may never develop the closeness that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">provides security in difficult moments</strong>. Boarding schools are also enclosed communities, and a pupil who is unhappy, homesick or bullied <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot escape at the end of the day</strong>. There is a further concern that such schools are socially narrow, since almost everyone comes from a similar background.\n\nIn my view boarding makes sense where families live abroad, move frequently or are too far from a suitable school, and it works far better for teenagers than for young children. Where a good local school exists, however, there is little reason to send a child away.\n\nIn conclusion, boarding schools build independence, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the emotional cost is high</strong>, and I believe most children benefit more from living with their families.","phrases":[{"en":"without a parent solving every problem","context":"Living away from home also develops independence: children learn to manage their own routines, resolve disagreements and cope without a parent solving every problem, which many find valuable when they later go to university."},{"en":"provides security in difficult moments","context":"Family relationships depend on daily contact, and a child who sees their parents only during holidays may never develop the closeness that provides security in difficult moments."},{"en":"cannot escape at the end of the day","context":"Boarding schools are also enclosed communities, and a pupil who is unhappy, homesick or bullied cannot escape at the end of the day."},{"en":"the emotional cost is high","context":"In conclusion, boarding schools build independence, but the emotional cost is high, and I believe most children benefit more from living with their families."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#133","summary":"Boarding school or day school?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-074","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"A year of work experience before university","prompt":"Some people believe that students who graduate from high school should spend a year gaining work experience before going to university. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes suggested that school leavers should spend a year gaining work experience before beginning a university course. I agree with this proposal, provided that the year is genuinely structured rather than simply a delay.\n\nThe strongest argument concerns the choice of course. Most students select a degree at seventeen, with almost no knowledge of what the corresponding profession actually involves, and a significant proportion later change subject or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">abandon their studies altogether</strong>. A year in a hospital, an office or a laboratory gives <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a far more realistic impression</strong>, and a student who confirms their interest in this way studies with much greater purpose.\n\nWork experience also develops qualities that school does not. Handling money, dealing with difficult customers, arriving punctually and working with colleagues of different ages teach responsibility in a way that examinations cannot. Students who have supported themselves financially also tend to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">manage their time more carefully</strong> at university, having learned <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what an hour of work is actually worth</strong>.\n\nThere are objections worth acknowledging. Some students <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lose academic momentum</strong> and find it hard to return to studying, and a year of unpaid work is easier for wealthy families to arrange than for poor ones, which risks creating a further advantage for those who already have one.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that a year of work experience benefits most school leavers, since it improves their choice of course and their maturity. It should, however, be organised and paid, so that it is available to every student rather than only to those who can afford it.","phrases":[{"en":"abandon their studies altogether","context":"Most students select a degree at seventeen, with almost no knowledge of what the corresponding profession actually involves, and a significant proportion later change subject or abandon their studies altogether."},{"en":"manage their time more carefully","context":"Students who have supported themselves financially also tend to manage their time more carefully at university, having learned what an hour of work is actually worth."},{"en":"a far more realistic impression","context":"A year in a hospital, an office or a laboratory gives a far more realistic impression, and a student who confirms their interest in this way studies with much greater purpose."},{"en":"what an hour of work is actually worth","context":"Students who have supported themselves financially also tend to manage their time more carefully at university, having learned what an hour of work is actually worth."},{"en":"lose academic momentum","context":"Some students lose academic momentum and find it hard to return to studying, and a year of unpaid work is easier for wealthy families to arrange than for poor ones, which risks creating a further advantage for those who already have one."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#134","summary":"A year of work experience before university"},{"id":"writing-task-2-075","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Is distance learning as good as attending university?","prompt":"Many people join distance-learning programmes and study at home, but some people think that this cannot bring the same benefit as attending college or university. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Distance learning has expanded rapidly, and there is disagreement about whether it can match a conventional university education. In my opinion it is genuinely effective for some learners and some subjects, but for most students it does not replace attending in person.\n\nThe weaknesses are well established. Studying alone demands <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a level of self-discipline</strong> that many people, particularly younger students, have not yet developed. Without fixed classes, deadlines and a tutor who notices when work stops arriving, a significant proportion simply fall behind, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completion rates for online courses</strong> are considerably lower than for campus programmes. The experience of remote teaching during the pandemic demonstrated this clearly, and the students who suffered most were those already struggling.\n\nDistance learning also removes a great deal that has nothing to do with the syllabus. Arguing with other students, working in a group, using a laboratory and being able to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ask a lecturer a question immediately</strong> are all part of how understanding develops, and they are difficult to reproduce through a screen. Certain subjects, such as medicine and engineering, cannot be taught this way at all.\n\nThere are, however, real advantages. For working adults, parents and people in remote areas, distance learning is often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the only realistic option</strong>, and a motivated student can achieve a great deal through it. It is also far cheaper, and for someone who simply needs a specific qualification while continuing to work, it is an entirely sensible route.\n\nIn conclusion, distance learning is genuinely valuable for disciplined and self-motivated learners, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">opens doors that would otherwise stay closed</strong>. For most young students, however, I do not believe it delivers the same benefit as attending in person.","phrases":[{"en":"completion rates for online courses","context":"Without fixed classes, deadlines and a tutor who notices when work stops arriving, a significant proportion simply fall behind, and completion rates for online courses are considerably lower than for campus programmes."},{"en":"ask a lecturer a question immediately","context":"Arguing with other students, working in a group, using a laboratory and being able to ask a lecturer a question immediately are all part of how understanding develops, and they are difficult to reproduce through a screen."},{"en":"the only realistic option","context":"For working adults, parents and people in remote areas, distance learning is often the only realistic option, and a motivated student can achieve a great deal through it."},{"en":"a level of self-discipline","context":"Studying alone demands a level of self-discipline that many people, particularly younger students, have not yet developed."},{"en":"opens doors that would otherwise stay closed","context":"In conclusion, distance learning is genuinely valuable for disciplined and self-motivated learners, and it opens doors that would otherwise stay closed. For most young students, however, I do not believe it delivers the same benefit as attending in person."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#135","summary":"Is distance learning as good as attending university?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-076","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Private secondary schools","prompt":"Some people think that students benefit greatly from attending private secondary schools. Others believe that private schools have a negative effect on young people and on society as a whole. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Private secondary schools attract strong opinions. Some believe they give pupils an outstanding education, while others argue that they harm both young people and society. In my view they do benefit their own students, but at a real cost to everyone else.\n\nThe advantages for those who attend are difficult to deny. Private schools have smaller classes, better laboratories and sports facilities, and they can pay more to attract experienced teachers. They also offer activities that state schools cannot afford, such as music tuition, drama and foreign exchanges. Examination results reflect this, and their pupils are consistently more likely to reach the leading universities and the best-paid professions.\n\nThe objections, however, are serious. When wealthy families remove their children from the state system, they also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remove the parents most able to raise money</strong>, question standards and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put pressure on politicians</strong>, and state schools decline as a result. The wider effect is on social mobility, since a child's future comes to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depend on parental income rather than on ability</strong>. In several countries a small number of private schools supply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a disproportionate share</strong> of judges, senior officials and politicians, which means those who make decisions have very little experience of ordinary life.\n\nIn my opinion the solution is not to abolish these schools, which would simply push wealthy families abroad, but to fund state education properly so that the gap in quality closes.\n\nIn conclusion, private schools serve their own pupils well but deepen inequality, and I believe the fairer response is to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raise standards in state schools</strong> rather than accept a divided system.","phrases":[{"en":"put pressure on politicians","context":"When wealthy families remove their children from the state system, they also remove the parents most able to raise money, question standards and put pressure on politicians, and state schools decline as a result."},{"en":"a disproportionate share","context":"In several countries a small number of private schools supply a disproportionate share of judges, senior officials and politicians, which means those who make decisions have very little experience of ordinary life."},{"en":"raise standards in state schools","context":"In conclusion, private schools serve their own pupils well but deepen inequality, and I believe the fairer response is to raise standards in state schools rather than accept a divided system."},{"en":"remove the parents most able to raise money","context":"When wealthy families remove their children from the state system, they also remove the parents most able to raise money, question standards and put pressure on politicians, and state schools decline as a result."},{"en":"depend on parental income rather than on ability","context":"The wider effect is on social mobility, since a child's future comes to depend on parental income rather than on ability."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#136","summary":"Private secondary schools"},{"id":"writing-task-2-077","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Should teachers teach right and wrong?","prompt":"Some people think that teachers should be responsible for teaching students to judge right and wrong and to behave well. Others say that teachers should only teach academic subjects. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People disagree about how wide a teacher's responsibilities should be. Some believe that schools must teach children right from wrong, while others argue that teachers should concentrate on academic subjects alone. In my opinion, moral guidance cannot realistically be separated from teaching.\n\nThose who favour a narrow academic role make a reasonable point. Teachers are trained in mathematics, science or languages, not in ethics, and class sizes are already large. Every hour spent on behaviour is an hour <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not spent on the curriculum</strong>, and examination results are what determine a child's future opportunities. There is also the question of whose values would be taught, since families differ in religion, politics and culture, and many parents would resent a school <strong class=\"key-phrase\">imposing beliefs</strong> they do not share.\n\nThose on the other side reply that children spend a large part of their waking lives at school, and that behaviour is learned there whether teachers intend it or not. A classroom cannot function unless pupils are honest, punctual and respectful, so teachers are obliged to deal with bullying, cheating and rudeness every day. Some families also provide very little guidance at home, and for those children school may be the only place where fair treatment is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consistently modelled</strong>.\n\nMy own view is closer to the second position. Teachers should not deliver lessons in religion or politics, but they should insist on a small number of principles that almost everyone accepts: honesty, respect for others and responsibility for one's own actions. These are taught through example and consistent rules rather than through separate lessons.\n\nIn conclusion, academic instruction must remain the priority, but basic moral standards are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an unavoidable part</strong> of a teacher's work.","phrases":[{"en":"not spent on the curriculum","context":"Every hour spent on behaviour is an hour not spent on the curriculum, and examination results are what determine a child's future opportunities."},{"en":"imposing beliefs","context":"There is also the question of whose values would be taught, since families differ in religion, politics and culture, and many parents would resent a school imposing beliefs they do not share."},{"en":"consistently modelled","context":"Some families also provide very little guidance at home, and for those children school may be the only place where fair treatment is consistently modelled."},{"en":"an unavoidable part","context":"In conclusion, academic instruction must remain the priority, but basic moral standards are an unavoidable part of a teacher's work."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#137","summary":"Should teachers teach right and wrong?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-080","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Should schools drop music and sport?","prompt":"Schools should teach children the academic subjects that are most relevant to their future careers, so other subjects such as music and sport are not important. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that schools should concentrate on the academic subjects that lead directly to employment, and that music and sport are therefore of little value. I disagree with this view, because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">defines the purpose of education far too narrowly</strong>.\n\nThe argument does contain something reasonable. School hours are limited, and literacy, numeracy and scientific understanding determine what a young person can realistically do for the rest of their life. Employers rarely ask whether a candidate played in an orchestra, and a school that produced excellent musicians but weak readers would be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">failing in its central duty</strong>.\n\nHowever, this reasoning misunderstands how the subjects relate to one another. Physical activity improves concentration, sleep and mood, so pupils who exercise regularly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">perform better academically rather than worse</strong>; removing sport would damage the very results it is meant to protect. Music has a similar effect, since learning an instrument demands sustained daily practice and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trains exactly the patience</strong> that difficult academic work requires.\n\nThere is also a serious objection to the premise itself. Nobody can predict which careers will exist in twenty years, and the qualities employers consistently name — creativity, teamwork, discipline and the ability to perform under pressure — are developed at least as much on a sports field or in a rehearsal as in a classroom. For some pupils, music or sport is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the only part of school they enjoy</strong>, and it is often what keeps them attending at all.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that music and sport are unimportant. They support academic achievement and develop qualities that no examination measures.","phrases":[{"en":"failing in its central duty","context":"Employers rarely ask whether a candidate played in an orchestra, and a school that produced excellent musicians but weak readers would be failing in its central duty."},{"en":"trains exactly the patience","context":"Music has a similar effect, since learning an instrument demands sustained daily practice and trains exactly the patience that difficult academic work requires."},{"en":"the only part of school they enjoy","context":"For some pupils, music or sport is the only part of school they enjoy, and it is often what keeps them attending at all."},{"en":"defines the purpose of education far too narrowly","context":"It is sometimes argued that schools should concentrate on the academic subjects that lead directly to employment, and that music and sport are therefore of little value. I disagree with this view, because it defines the purpose of education far too narrowly."},{"en":"perform better academically rather than worse","context":"Physical activity improves concentration, sleep and mood, so pupils who exercise regularly perform better academically rather than worse; removing sport would damage the very results it is meant to protect."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#138","summary":"Should schools drop music and sport?"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Separate schools for boys and girls","prompt":"Some people think that it is better to educate boys and girls in separate schools. Others, however, believe that boys and girls benefit more from attending mixed schools. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Some people believe that boys and girls learn better in separate schools, while others prefer mixed education. Although single-sex schools may reduce certain distractions, I believe mixed schools prepare students more effectively for adult life.\n\nSupporters of separate schools argue that teachers can adapt lessons to different learning patterns and that students may feel less pressure to perform for the opposite sex. In some subjects, girls or boys may also participate more confidently when they are not worried about stereotypes. These schools can therefore create <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a focused academic environment</strong> for some learners.\n\nHowever, the adult world is mixed, and schools should help young people communicate naturally with everyone. In a mixed classroom, students learn to cooperate, challenge stereotypes and judge classmates by ability rather than gender. Problems such as distraction or teasing should be managed through good discipline, not by separating students. Regular contact also helps young people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">develop mutual respect and social confidence</strong>.\n\nIt is also difficult to prove that gender separation itself produces better results. School quality, class size, teacher skill and family support often matter more. A well-run mixed school can address different learning needs through flexible teaching, while avoiding the assumption that all boys or all girls learn in the same way. This is a more evidence-based approach.\n\nIn conclusion, single-sex education can work well in particular cases, but its advantages are not strong enough to justify broad separation. Mixed schools offer both academic learning and realistic social experience, making them better suited to preparing students for study, work and public life.","topic":"Education","phrases":[{"en":"a focused academic environment","context":"These schools can therefore create a focused academic environment for some learners."},{"en":"develop mutual respect and social confidence","context":"Regular contact also helps young people develop mutual respect and social confidence."}],"subtype":"discuss","summary":"Separate schools for boys and girls compared with mixed schools","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-081","_displayNo":"#139"},{"id":"writing-task-2-082","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Employable skills or knowledge for its own sake?","prompt":"Nowadays, some universities teach graduates practical skills that help them find employment, while others believe that the main function of university is to pursue knowledge for its own sake. What is your opinion?","answer":"Universities have two important roles: they develop knowledge and prepare students for adult working life. In my view, these aims are not in conflict. A strong university education should combine academic understanding with practical skills.\n\nKnowledge for its own sake remains valuable. Subjects such as history, mathematics and philosophy train students to analyse evidence, question assumptions and build logical arguments. These abilities are useful far beyond one job. They also allow universities to conduct research that may have no immediate commercial use but can later produce major social benefits. Reducing higher education to short-term job training would therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">narrow the purpose of university</strong>.\n\nAt the same time, graduates need to enter a competitive labour market. Universities should teach students how to communicate clearly, work in teams, solve unfamiliar problems and use relevant technology. Internships, project work and careers advice can help students <strong class=\"key-phrase\">connect theory with practice</strong>. This does not mean that employers should dictate the entire curriculum; rather, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical experience should support serious academic study</strong>.\n\nPractical preparation already works well in fields such as medicine and engineering, where students spend years on hospital wards or in industrial placements <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without losing scientific rigour</strong>. Some countries extend this idea at an institutional level, running applied-science universities alongside research universities so that students who want a profession quickly and those who want an academic career can choose the right kind of course. Expanding this sort of structured choice, rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">diluting every degree equally</strong>, would let more graduates leave with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">skills that transfer between different careers</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, universities should not choose between knowledge and employability. Their best contribution is to give students a deep understanding of a subject while also developing skills that can be transferred to different careers. This balanced approach would produce graduates who are both intellectually independent and work-ready.","phrases":[{"en":"narrow the purpose of university","context":"Reducing higher education to short-term job training would therefore narrow the purpose of university."},{"en":"connect theory with practice","context":"Internships, project work and careers advice can help students connect theory with practice."},{"en":"practical experience should support serious academic study","context":"This does not mean that employers should dictate the entire curriculum; rather, practical experience should support serious academic study."},{"en":"skills that transfer between different careers","context":"Expanding this sort of structured choice, rather than diluting every degree equally, would let more graduates leave with skills that transfer between different careers."},{"en":"without losing scientific rigour","context":"Practical preparation already works well in fields such as medicine and engineering, where students spend years on hospital wards or in industrial placements without losing scientific rigour."},{"en":"diluting every degree equally","context":"Expanding this sort of structured choice, rather than diluting every degree equally, would let more graduates leave with skills that transfer between different careers."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#140","summary":"Some universities offer graduate students skills that assist them to find…"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Is television useful for education?","prompt":"Some people say that television is useful for education, while others say it is useful only for entertainment. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Television can educate and entertain, and these functions often overlap. In my view, its educational value is significant, but most viewers use it mainly for relaxation. The quality of the programme matters more than the medium itself.\n\nEducational television can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make difficult subjects easy to grasp</strong>. A documentary can take a class to the deep ocean or inside a volcano, and film shot in slow motion can show a chemical reaction that no school laboratory could safely run. News programmes also improve public knowledge when they explain an issue carefully rather than simply report the headline. For children and adults with limited access to books or courses, television can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bring useful knowledge into the home</strong> at no extra cost.\n\nAt the same time, entertainment is the main reason many people switch on the television. Drama, sport and comedy provide a simple way to rest after work. This is not necessarily negative, because good entertainment can reflect social problems, introduce new cultures and encourage discussion. The problem begins when viewers watch passively for many hours or when channels <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rely on sensational content to hold attention</strong>.\n\nWhat television does well, though, is not what a book or a classroom does. It can show a process, a place or a period in a way that makes it feel real, but it moves at its own pace and cannot be questioned, so viewers tend to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep the impression and lose the detail</strong>. This is why the strongest programmes are supported by reliable information elsewhere — a website, a booklet or a lesson that follows — which is what allows a broadcast to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">engaging without becoming superficial</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, television is neither purely educational nor purely entertaining, and the two functions are not easily separated. What decides its value is what is broadcast and how it is watched, and the most useful change would be for viewers to choose programmes deliberately rather than leave the set running.","topic":"Education","phrases":[{"en":"bring useful knowledge into the home","context":"For children and adults with limited access to books or courses, television can bring useful knowledge into the home at no extra cost."},{"en":"engaging without becoming superficial","context":"This is why the strongest programmes are supported by reliable information elsewhere — a website, a booklet or a lesson that follows — which is what allows a broadcast to be engaging without becoming superficial."},{"en":"make difficult subjects easy to grasp","context":"Educational television can make difficult subjects easy to grasp."},{"en":"rely on sensational content to hold attention","context":"The problem begins when viewers watch passively for many hours or when channels rely on sensational content to hold attention."},{"en":"keep the impression and lose the detail","context":"It can show a process, a place or a period in a way that makes it feel real, but it moves at its own pace and cannot be questioned, so viewers tend to keep the impression and lose the detail."}],"subtype":"discuss","summary":"Whether television is useful for education or only for entertainment","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-083","_displayNo":"#141"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Music, art and drama against other subjects","prompt":"Some say that music, art and drama are as important as other school subjects, especially at the primary level. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Music, art and drama deserve an important place in primary education. They should not replace literacy, mathematics or science, but they support children's development in ways that academic subjects alone cannot.\n\nCreative subjects help young children <strong class=\"key-phrase\">express ideas before they have the vocabulary</strong> to explain them fully. Drawing, singing and acting also develop concentration, coordination and confidence. A child who performs in a short play must listen to others, remember a sequence and manage nervousness. These are transferable skills that support learning across the curriculum.\n\nThe arts also make school more inclusive. Some pupils who struggle with written tests may show strong visual, musical or practical ability. Giving these children a chance to succeed can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improve motivation and attendance</strong>. In addition, creative activities introduce students to their own culture and to unfamiliar traditions, helping them develop <strong class=\"key-phrase\">curiosity and cultural awareness</strong>.\n\nThere is also a longer-term argument about talent. Creative industries such as design, film and digital media now employ a significant share of the workforce, and ability in these fields often first appears at primary age. A pupil who never draws, performs or composes may simply never discover a talent worth developing, much as a footballer would never be discovered without ever being given a ball. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Spotting this kind of talent early</strong> is arguably as valuable as anything taught in a standard lesson, provided timetables protect enough regular time for these subjects rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treating them as an occasional extra</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, primary schools should treat the arts as a core part of a balanced education, while still protecting enough time for basic academic skills. The aim is not to train every child as an artist, but to use creative work to build confident, observant and cooperative learners.","topic":"Education","phrases":[{"en":"curiosity and cultural awareness","context":"In addition, creative activities introduce students to their own culture and to unfamiliar traditions, helping them develop curiosity and cultural awareness."},{"en":"Spotting this kind of talent early","context":"Spotting this kind of talent early is arguably as valuable as anything taught in a standard lesson, provided timetables protect enough regular time for these subjects rather than treating them as an occasional extra."},{"en":"express ideas before they have the vocabulary","context":"Creative subjects help young children express ideas before they have the vocabulary to explain them fully."},{"en":"improve motivation and attendance","context":"Giving these children a chance to succeed can improve motivation and attendance."},{"en":"treating them as an occasional extra","context":"Spotting this kind of talent early is arguably as valuable as anything taught in a standard lesson, provided timetables protect enough regular time for these subjects rather than treating them as an occasional extra."}],"subtype":"opinion","summary":"Whether music, art and drama matter as much as other school subjects","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-084","_displayNo":"#142"},{"id":"writing-task-2-085","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"University education","prompt":"Some people believe the aim of university education is to help graduates get better jobs. Others believe there are much wider benefits of university education for both individuals and society. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"The purpose of university education is often debated. Some regard it primarily as professional preparation, whereas others emphasise its broader intellectual and social value. Although employability is an essential outcome, I believe universities serve society best when they combine vocational relevance with the development of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">independent judgement</strong>.\n\nThose who prioritise employment have a strong practical case. Higher education requires substantial investment from students, families and governments, so graduates reasonably expect <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a measurable return on that investment</strong>. Degree programmes can provide specialist knowledge, professional accreditation and access to occupations that would otherwise remain closed. In fields such as medicine, engineering and law, universities also protect the public by ensuring that entrants meet rigorous standards. From this perspective, a course that ignores labour-market realities may leave students heavily indebted but poorly equipped for work.\n\nHowever, reducing university to job training would overlook its wider contribution. Students learn to evaluate evidence, challenge assumptions and communicate across cultural or disciplinary boundaries. These capacities help individuals <strong class=\"key-phrase\">navigate complex public and private decisions</strong> long after specific technical knowledge becomes outdated. Universities also generate research, sustain cultural institutions and create spaces in which contested ideas can be examined rather than merely repeated. A society with citizens who can reason critically is better able to resist misinformation and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hold powerful institutions accountable</strong>.\n\nIn my view, the two purposes are not mutually exclusive. Programmes should maintain academic depth while incorporating placements, collaborative projects and transferable skills. This approach would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bridge the gap between scholarship and professional practice</strong> without turning every subject into narrow occupational training.\n\nIn conclusion, better employment is a legitimate aim of university education, but it is not the only one. The most valuable institutions prepare graduates both to earn a living and to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">participate intelligently in civic life</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"independent judgement","context":"Although employability is an essential outcome, I believe universities serve society best when they combine vocational relevance with the development of independent judgement."},{"en":"a measurable return on that investment","context":"Higher education requires substantial investment from students, families and governments, so graduates reasonably expect a measurable return on that investment."},{"en":"navigate complex public and private decisions","context":"These capacities help individuals navigate complex public and private decisions long after specific technical knowledge becomes outdated."},{"en":"bridge the gap between scholarship and professional practice","context":"This approach would bridge the gap between scholarship and professional practice without turning every subject into narrow occupational training."},{"en":"participate intelligently in civic life","context":"In conclusion, better employment is a legitimate aim of university education, but it is not the only one. The most valuable institutions prepare graduates both to earn a living and to participate intelligently in civic life."},{"en":"hold powerful institutions accountable","context":"A society with citizens who can reason critically is better able to resist misinformation and hold powerful institutions accountable."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#143","summary":"The wider aims of university education"},{"id":"writing-task-2-086","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Why school leavers cannot find work","prompt":"Today, more school leavers are unable to find jobs. Discuss the causes of rising unemployment among young adults and suggest any solutions.","answer":"Rising unemployment among school leavers is caused mainly by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a lack of practical experience</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a mismatch between education and labour-market needs</strong>. The problem can be reduced if schools, employers and governments share responsibility for the transition from study to work.\n\nMany young applicants leave school with theoretical knowledge but little evidence that they can use it in a workplace. Employers often prefer experienced candidates because training a beginner takes time and money. At the same time, some students specialise in areas with limited demand, while sectors such as construction, healthcare and technology struggle to recruit people with the right technical skills. Unrealistic expectations can add to the problem when graduates <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reject entry-level opportunities</strong> because the pay, status or location is not ideal.\n\nEducation should respond more closely to actual employment patterns. Schools and colleges can work with local employers to update courses, provide work placements and teach practical skills such as communication, problem-solving and digital literacy. Realistic career guidance is also essential so that students understand salary levels, recruitment requirements and possible training routes before they leave education.\n\nEmployers should create more paid apprenticeships and genuine junior positions. Governments can support this by offering temporary tax incentives for firms that train young workers, while requiring clear standards so that subsidies do not become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a source of cheap labour</strong>. Short vocational programmes can also help unemployed graduates move into sectors with staff shortages.\n\nIn conclusion, youth unemployment is not simply the result of a weak economy. It also reflects limited experience, unsuitable training and poor information. Better coordination between education and employment would give school leavers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a more realistic route into stable work</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"a lack of practical experience","context":"Rising unemployment among school leavers is caused mainly by a lack of practical experience and a mismatch between education and labour-market needs."},{"en":"a more realistic route into stable work","context":"Better coordination between education and employment would give school leavers a more realistic route into stable work."},{"en":"a mismatch between education and labour-market needs","context":"Rising unemployment among school leavers is caused mainly by a lack of practical experience and a mismatch between education and labour-market needs."},{"en":"reject entry-level opportunities","context":"Unrealistic expectations can add to the problem when graduates reject entry-level opportunities because the pay, status or location is not ideal."},{"en":"a source of cheap labour","context":"Governments can support this by offering temporary tax incentives for firms that train young workers, while requiring clear standards so that subsidies do not become a source of cheap labour."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","_displayNo":"#144","summary":"Today, more school leavers are unable to find jobs."},{"id":"writing-task-2-094","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Should libraries buy digital media instead of books?","prompt":"Some people believe that public libraries should spend their money on digital media, such as software, films and online resources, rather than on books. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that public libraries should spend their budgets on digital material rather than on books. I disagree with this view, because the two serve different purposes and a library that abandoned books would lose its central function.\n\nThere are reasonable arguments for digital investment. Many people, particularly younger users, now read on screens, and a single subscription can give an entire community access to thousands of titles and academic journals at once. Libraries also serve those who cannot afford computers or reliable internet at home, so providing machines, software and training addresses <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a genuine form of exclusion</strong>.\n\nHowever, replacing books would be a mistake. Printed books remain the cheapest and most durable way of preserving information: a library copy lasts decades, requires no licence renewal and cannot be withdrawn by a company that has changed its terms. Digital material, by contrast, is usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rented rather than owned</strong>, and libraries have already lost access to titles when publishers altered their agreements. There is also evidence that readers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">retain more from print than from screens</strong>, which matters particularly for children learning to read.\n\nThe stronger argument is that this is a false choice. A well-run library does both, and the more important question is what proportion of the budget each deserves, which depends on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the community the library serves</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, digital resources deserve a place in modern libraries, but I disagree that they should replace books, which remain the more reliable and lasting form of access to knowledge.","phrases":[{"en":"a genuine form of exclusion","context":"Libraries also serve those who cannot afford computers or reliable internet at home, so providing machines, software and training addresses a genuine form of exclusion."},{"en":"rented rather than owned","context":"Digital material, by contrast, is usually rented rather than owned, and libraries have already lost access to titles when publishers altered their agreements."},{"en":"retain more from print than from screens","context":"There is also evidence that readers retain more from print than from screens, which matters particularly for children learning to read."},{"en":"the community the library serves","context":"The stronger argument is that this is a false choice. A well-run library does both, and the more important question is what proportion of the budget each deserves, which depends on the community the library serves."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#145","summary":"Should libraries buy digital media instead of books?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-139","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Universities offering courses online","prompt":"Some universities now offer their courses on the Internet so that people can study online. Is this a positive or negative development?","answer":"It is true that online courses are becoming a common feature of university education. Although there are some drawbacks of Internet-based learning, I would argue that there are far more benefits.\n\nThe main drawback of the trend towards online university courses is that there is less direct interaction. Students may not have the opportunity to engage face-to-face with their teachers, and will instead have to rely on written forms of communication. Similarly, students who study online do not come into direct contact with each other, and this could have a negative impact on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">peer support, discussion and exchange of ideas</strong>. For example, whereas students on traditional courses can attend seminars and even discuss their subjects over coffee after lessons, online learners are restricted to chatting through website forum areas. These learners may also lack the motivation and element of competition that face-to-face group work brings.\n\nDespite the negatives mentioned above, I believe that online university courses are a positive development for various reasons. Firstly, they allow learners to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">study in a flexible way</strong>, meaning that they can work whenever and wherever is convenient, and they can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cover the material at their own pace</strong>. Secondly, the cost of a university education can be greatly reduced, while revenues for institutions may increase as more students can be taught.\n\nFinally, online learning offers open access to anybody who is willing to study, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regardless of age, location, ability and background</strong>. For example, my uncle, who is 65 years old, has recently enrolled on an online MBA course in a different country, which would have been impossible in the days before Internet-based education.\n\nIn conclusion, while I recognise the possible disadvantages of online learning, I consider it to be a positive development overall.","phrases":[{"en":"study in a flexible way","context":"Firstly, they allow learners to study in a flexible way, meaning that they can work whenever and wherever is convenient, and they can cover the material at their own pace."},{"en":"peer support, discussion and exchange of ideas","context":"Similarly, students who study online do not come into direct contact with each other, and this could have a negative impact on peer support, discussion and exchange of ideas."},{"en":"cover the material at their own pace","context":"Firstly, they allow learners to study in a flexible way, meaning that they can work whenever and wherever is convenient, and they can cover the material at their own pace."},{"en":"regardless of age, location, ability and background","context":"Finally, online learning offers open access to anybody who is willing to study, regardless of age, location, ability and background."}],"subtype":"Positive / Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#146","summary":"Online university courses"},{"id":"writing-task-2-152","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Is studying history useful?","prompt":"Some people argue that history is of little or no use to us. Others believe that studying history brings many benefits. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Some people regard the study of history as an interesting but essentially useless subject, while others believe it offers real benefits. In my opinion history is valuable, although the way it is often taught obscures this.\n\nThe case against history is largely practical. School and university time is limited, and many argue that it would be better spent on subjects leading directly to employment, such as mathematics, science and computing. Critics also question its reliability, since accounts are written by the winners and are frequently <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rewritten to suit</strong> whichever government is in power. There is a further objection: circumstances change so completely that the experience of previous centuries offers little guidance for today's problems.\n\nThose who defend the subject reply that history explains the present. Current conflicts over borders, religion and national identity are almost impossible to understand without knowing how they arose, and voters who lack this background are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easily misled</strong> by politicians who invent a convenient past. History also develops a genuinely transferable skill, since students must <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weigh conflicting evidence</strong> and judge which sources to trust, which is exactly what law, journalism and management require.\n\nMy own view is that history is worth studying, but its value depends on the approach. Memorising the dates of battles achieves very little. Examining why a decision was taken, what alternatives existed and what followed teaches judgement.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that history is useless. Properly taught, it explains why societies are as they are and trains people to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">evaluate evidence for themselves</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"rewritten to suit","context":"Critics also question its reliability, since accounts are written by the winners and are frequently rewritten to suit whichever government is in power."},{"en":"easily misled","context":"Current conflicts over borders, religion and national identity are almost impossible to understand without knowing how they arose, and voters who lack this background are easily misled by politicians who invent a convenient past."},{"en":"weigh conflicting evidence","context":"History also develops a genuinely transferable skill, since students must weigh conflicting evidence and judge which sources to trust, which is exactly what law, journalism and management require."},{"en":"evaluate evidence for themselves","context":"In conclusion, I disagree that history is useless. Properly taught, it explains why societies are as they are and trains people to evaluate evidence for themselves."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#147","summary":"Is studying history useful?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2195","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Study time versus physical activity","prompt":"Parents should encourage children to spend less time studying and more time doing physical activities. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that children would benefit from doing more exercise and less schoolwork. I agree that many children are far too inactive, but in my view the time for sport should come from their leisure hours rather than from their studies.\n\nThere are clear reasons why children need more physical activity. Rates of childhood obesity have risen sharply in most developed countries, and doctors link this trend to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">increasingly sedentary lifestyles</strong>. Regular exercise also improves concentration and mood, which means that an active child is likely to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">learn more efficiently, not less</strong>. A child who plays football for an hour after school will probably return to homework <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in a far better state of mind</strong> than one who has spent the afternoon in front of a screen.\n\nHowever, I do not believe that study time is the right thing to sacrifice. School hours are already limited, and the skills that children acquire in class, such as literacy, numeracy and scientific reasoning, determine their opportunities for the rest of their lives. If parents reduce study time, weaker students in particular will fall behind, and this is difficult to correct later. The real problem is not homework but the number of hours that young people spend on phones, computers and television. Cutting an hour of screen time each day would free enough time for sport <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at no academic cost</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I strongly support the idea that children should be more physically active, but I disagree that this must involve studying less. Parents should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">target passive entertainment instead</strong>, and treat exercise as a normal part of a balanced daily routine.","phrases":[{"en":"increasingly sedentary lifestyles","context":"Rates of childhood obesity have risen sharply in most developed countries, and doctors link this trend to increasingly sedentary lifestyles."},{"en":"at no academic cost","context":"Cutting an hour of screen time each day would free enough time for sport at no academic cost."},{"en":"learn more efficiently, not less","context":"Regular exercise also improves concentration and mood, which means that an active child is likely to learn more efficiently, not less."},{"en":"in a far better state of mind","context":"A child who plays football for an hour after school will probably return to homework in a far better state of mind than one who has spent the afternoon in front of a screen."},{"en":"target passive entertainment instead","context":"Parents should target passive entertainment instead, and treat exercise as a normal part of a balanced daily routine."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#148","summary":"Study time versus physical activity"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2196","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Free university education for all","prompt":"Some people believe that everyone has a right to have access to university education and the governments should make it free for all students no matter what financial background they have. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Some people believe that a university place is a basic right, and that governments should therefore fund it for every student regardless of income. While I agree that money should never prevent a talented student from studying, I do not think that free education for all is the best use of public funds.\n\nThe argument for free tuition is a strong one. When students have to pay high fees, those from poorer families are either excluded altogether or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">forced into debt that takes decades to repay</strong>. This <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wastes talent</strong>, because a bright child from a low-income household may be far more capable than a wealthy one who can simply afford the fees. A country that educates its most able citizens also benefits economically, since graduates pay higher taxes and fill skilled positions in medicine, engineering and teaching.\n\nHowever, universal free education creates serious problems. Government budgets are limited, and money spent on universities cannot be spent on hospitals, schools or transport. It is also worth remembering that a degree brings <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a substantial private reward</strong>: graduates typically earn far more over a lifetime than those who leave school at eighteen. It seems unfair to ask a factory worker who never attended university to pay, through taxation, for the education of someone who will eventually earn twice as much.\n\nIn conclusion, access to university should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depend on ability rather than wealth</strong>, but rather than making courses free for everyone, governments should target their support at students who genuinely cannot afford to pay, through full scholarships and living grants.","phrases":[{"en":"wastes talent","context":"This wastes talent, because a bright child from a low-income household may be far more capable than a wealthy one who can simply afford the fees."},{"en":"a substantial private reward","context":"It is also worth remembering that a degree brings a substantial private reward: graduates typically earn far more over a lifetime than those who leave school at eighteen."},{"en":"forced into debt that takes decades to repay","context":"When students have to pay high fees, those from poorer families are either excluded altogether or forced into debt that takes decades to repay."},{"en":"depend on ability rather than wealth","context":"In conclusion, access to university should depend on ability rather than wealth, but rather than making courses free for everyone, governments should target their support at students who genuinely cannot afford to pay, through full scholarships and living grants."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#149","summary":"Free university education for all"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2213","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Team sports and cooperation","prompt":"The best way to teach children to cooperate is through team sports at school. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is often claimed that school team sports are the most effective method of teaching children to work together. I agree that sport teaches cooperation extremely well, but I do not accept that it is the best method for every child.\n\nThe strengths of team sport are clear. In a game of football or basketball, cooperation is not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an abstract idea to be explained in a classroom</strong>; it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">immediately and visibly necessary</strong>. A player who refuses to pass will lose, and the consequences are obvious to everyone within seconds. Children also learn to accept different roles, to trust others to do their part, to support a teammate who has made a mistake and to deal with defeat as a group. These lessons are memorable precisely because they are physical and emotional rather than theoretical.\n\nHowever, sport suits some children far better than others. A child who is unathletic, physically small or simply uninterested may spend years being chosen last and blamed for defeats, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the lesson learned is exclusion rather than cooperation</strong>. Team sports can also encourage the wrong attitudes, since aggressive competition and pressure to win sometimes produce selfish behaviour instead.\n\nIn my opinion, schools should therefore use a range of methods. Group projects in science, drama productions, orchestras, debating teams and school newspapers all require pupils to plan together, divide responsibilities and depend on one another, and they engage children whose talents are not physical.\n\nIn conclusion, team sports are a valuable way of teaching cooperation, but they work best <strong class=\"key-phrase\">alongside other collaborative activities</strong> rather than as the only approach.","phrases":[{"en":"immediately and visibly necessary","context":"In a game of football or basketball, cooperation is not an abstract idea to be explained in a classroom; it is immediately and visibly necessary."},{"en":"alongside other collaborative activities","context":"In conclusion, team sports are a valuable way of teaching cooperation, but they work best alongside other collaborative activities rather than as the only approach."},{"en":"an abstract idea to be explained in a classroom","context":"In a game of football or basketball, cooperation is not an abstract idea to be explained in a classroom; it is immediately and visibly necessary."},{"en":"the lesson learned is exclusion rather than cooperation","context":"A child who is unathletic, physically small or simply uninterested may spend years being chosen last and blamed for defeats, and the lesson learned is exclusion rather than cooperation."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#150","summary":"Team sports and cooperation"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2220","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Are libraries still necessary?","prompt":"Students today can access information online, so libraries are no longer necessary. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Since almost every text a student needs can now be opened on a laptop, some people conclude that library buildings have outlived their purpose. I disagree, because this argument mistakes a library for a store of books when its real value lies elsewhere.\n\nThe first thing a library provides is a place to work. Many students have nowhere at home that is quiet, warm and free of interruption, particularly those sharing a room or living with young children, and a desk in a reading room is the only environment in which sustained study is possible for them. Removing that would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disadvantage precisely the students</strong> who already have the least support.\n\nLibraries also solve the problem the internet creates. Searching online produces thousands of results of wildly varying reliability, and a first-year student rarely knows which to trust. Librarians teach people how to judge a source, how to search a specialist database and how to reference properly, and universities pay for subscriptions to journals that are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">locked behind expensive paywalls</strong> and cannot simply be found through a search engine.\n\nI accept that the traditional function of lending printed books has genuinely declined, and that libraries which do nothing else look increasingly redundant. The response, though, has been to change rather than to close: many now offer computers, study rooms, language help and community classes, and their visitor numbers have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">risen rather than fallen</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I strongly disagree that libraries are unnecessary. Online access has removed one of their functions while making the others more valuable, because a student who can reach everything needs help more than ever in deciding what is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worth reading at all</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"disadvantage precisely the students","context":"Removing that would disadvantage precisely the students who already have the least support."},{"en":"locked behind expensive paywalls","context":"Librarians teach people how to judge a source, how to search a specialist database and how to reference properly, and universities pay for subscriptions to journals that are locked behind expensive paywalls and cannot simply be found through a search engine."},{"en":"risen rather than fallen","context":"The response, though, has been to change rather than to close: many now offer computers, study rooms, language help and community classes, and their visitor numbers have risen rather than fallen."},{"en":"worth reading at all","context":"Online access has removed one of their functions while making the others more valuable, because a student who can reach everything needs help more than ever in deciding what is worth reading at all."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#151","summary":"Whether online access has made libraries unnecessary"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2223","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Why university study feels harder","prompt":"Many students find it is harder to study at university or college compared to when they were at school. Why is this? What are some possible solutions for this problem?","answer":"Many students who did well at school struggle badly in their first year of higher education. The difficulty is usually caused by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the sudden removal of structure</strong> rather than by the subject matter itself, and it can be reduced by teaching students how to work independently.\n\nThe main cause is that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">supervision disappears overnight</strong>. At school a timetable fills every hour, homework is set and checked weekly, and a teacher notices immediately when a pupil falls behind. At university a student may have twelve contact hours and one deadline in three months, which means nobody will point out that no work has been done until it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far too late to recover</strong>. Reading also changes in kind: instead of a textbook chapter, students are given a list of papers and expected to decide for themselves what matters.\n\nLiving arrangements make this worse. Most first-year students are managing money, cooking and a new social life for the first time, often several hundred miles from anyone who knows them, and the energy this consumes is energy not spent on study. Those who feel isolated tend to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">withdraw rather than ask for help</strong>.\n\nThe solutions follow from the causes. Universities should break long assignments into smaller submitted stages during the first year, so that a struggling student is identified in week four rather than week twenty. Explicit teaching of academic skills — how to read a paper, plan an essay and reference sources — should be part of the timetable rather than left to chance. Assigning each new student a named tutor and a second-year mentor also gives them somebody obvious to approach.\n\nIn conclusion, the difficulty comes from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">independence arriving all at once</strong>, and the remedy is to introduce it gradually while making support easy to reach.","phrases":[{"en":"far too late to recover","context":"At university a student may have twelve contact hours and one deadline in three months, which means nobody will point out that no work has been done until it is far too late to recover."},{"en":"withdraw rather than ask for help","context":"Those who feel isolated tend to withdraw rather than ask for help."},{"en":"the sudden removal of structure","context":"The difficulty is usually caused by the sudden removal of structure rather than by the subject matter itself, and it can be reduced by teaching students how to work independently."},{"en":"supervision disappears overnight","context":"The main cause is that supervision disappears overnight."},{"en":"independence arriving all at once","context":"In conclusion, the difficulty comes from independence arriving all at once, and the remedy is to introduce it gradually while making support easy to reach."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#152","summary":"Why studying at university is harder than at school"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2234","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"When should school begin?","prompt":"Some people think that children should start school at a very early age, but others believe that children should not go to school until they are older. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"School starting ages vary widely, from four in Britain to seven in much of Scandinavia. The disagreement is genuine, and my own view is that early attendance is valuable provided that what happens in the classroom suits the age of the children in it.\n\nThose in favour of an early start point to the pace of early development. Vocabulary and social behaviour develop fastest before the age of six, and a structured setting exposes children to more language, more books and more negotiation with other children than many homes can provide. The effect is largest for children from disadvantaged families, for whom nursery and early school close a gap that otherwise <strong class=\"key-phrase\">only widens with time</strong>. Early attendance also allows parents to return to work.\n\nThe opposing argument is that formal instruction too early does more harm than good. A four-year-old asked to sit still and form letters may simply learn that school is unpleasant, and countries that begin reading instruction at seven catch up within a few years while reporting fewer children who dislike school. Long days away from home at that age can also be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely tiring for a small child</strong>.\n\nMy position is that this debate confuses starting age with teaching method. The evidence against early schooling concerns formal lessons and testing, not attendance itself, and countries with the best results send children early but spend those first years on play, talk and stories rather than on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worksheets and assessment</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, an early start benefits children, particularly the least advantaged, provided the early years are built around play and language. The mistake is not sending a four-year-old to school but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treating a four-year-old like a pupil of ten</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"only widens with time","context":"The effect is largest for children from disadvantaged families, for whom nursery and early school close a gap that otherwise only widens with time."},{"en":"genuinely tiring for a small child","context":"Long days away from home at that age can also be genuinely tiring for a small child."},{"en":"worksheets and assessment","context":"The evidence against early schooling concerns formal lessons and testing, not attendance itself, and countries with the best results send children early but spend those first years on play, talk and stories rather than on worksheets and assessment."},{"en":"treating a four-year-old like a pupil of ten","context":"In conclusion, an early start benefits children, particularly the least advantaged, provided the early years are built around play and language. The mistake is not sending a four-year-old to school but treating a four-year-old like a pupil of ten."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#153","summary":"Starting school at a very early age compared with starting later"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2236","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Studying in a group or alone","prompt":"Some people think that it is more effective for students to study in a group while others believe that it is better for them to study alone. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Students differ over whether learning is better done together or in private, and both methods have measurable strengths. My own view is that they suit different stages of learning and that the sensible approach is to combine them.\n\nThe argument for group study rests on explanation. A student who has to describe a concept aloud discovers immediately which parts they do not actually understand, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gaps that survive silent reading do not survive a question from someone else</strong>. Groups also expose students to methods they would never have found alone: in a mathematics problem there may be three routes to an answer, and hearing the other two deepens understanding of all of them. A fixed meeting time is useful in itself, because it makes the work <strong class=\"key-phrase\">harder to postpone</strong>.\n\nStudying alone has different advantages. Difficult material requires sustained concentration, and a group inevitably interrupts, drifts into conversation or moves at a pace that suits nobody exactly. Working alone also lets a student spend three hours on the one topic that is defeating them rather than the ten minutes a group would allow, and it removes the temptation to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rely on a stronger classmate</strong> instead of struggling through.\n\nIn my view the two are complementary rather than competing. The most effective pattern is to learn the material alone first, so that everyone arrives with something to contribute, and then to meet in order to test each other and argue about the difficult points. A group whose members have done no preparation simply wastes an afternoon.\n\nIn conclusion, both methods work, but for different purposes. Independent study builds the knowledge and group work <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exposes what is missing from it</strong>, which is why serious students use both.","phrases":[{"en":"harder to postpone","context":"A fixed meeting time is useful in itself, because it makes the work harder to postpone."},{"en":"rely on a stronger classmate","context":"Working alone also lets a student spend three hours on the one topic that is defeating them rather than the ten minutes a group would allow, and it removes the temptation to rely on a stronger classmate instead of struggling through."},{"en":"exposes what is missing from it","context":"In conclusion, both methods work, but for different purposes. Independent study builds the knowledge and group work exposes what is missing from it, which is why serious students use both."},{"en":"gaps that survive silent reading do not survive a question from someone else","context":"A student who has to describe a concept aloud discovers immediately which parts they do not actually understand, since gaps that survive silent reading do not survive a question from someone else."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#154","summary":"Group study compared with studying alone"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2238","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Should children be encouraged to watch television?","prompt":"Children can learn effectively by watching television. Therefore, they should be encouraged to watch television regularly at home and at school. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Television can undoubtedly teach children things that a classroom cannot show them. I agree with the first half of this statement but disagree with the conclusion drawn from it, because the value of television depends entirely on what is watched and for how long.\n\nThe learning argument is sound within limits. A documentary can take a child to the deep ocean or inside an erupting volcano, and a chemistry programme can show a reaction that no school laboratory could safely run. Well-made children's programmes have measurably improved vocabulary and number skills, particularly among children whose parents read to them little, so for those families television <strong class=\"key-phrase\">closes a gap rather than creating one</strong>.\n\nThe recommendation that follows does not hold, however. Almost none of what children actually watch is educational, and encouraging regular viewing in practice means encouraging entertainment. Television is also passive in a way that matters: a child watching an experiment learns less than a child performing one, because understanding is built by doing something and getting it wrong. Hours in front of a screen additionally <strong class=\"key-phrase\">displace reading, play and conversation</strong>, which are the activities that develop language fastest.\n\nThe school proposal is weaker still. Class time is scarce, and a teacher who can question, correct and adapt to a confused pupil does something no broadcast can. Television belongs in a lesson as an occasional illustration, not as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a substitute for teaching</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, children do learn from good programmes, and those programmes deserve support. The conclusion that viewing should be encouraged regularly is wrong, because it treats television as uniformly educational when the value lies in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a narrow band of what is broadcast</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"closes a gap rather than creating one","context":"Well-made children's programmes have measurably improved vocabulary and number skills, particularly among children whose parents read to them little, so for those families television closes a gap rather than creating one."},{"en":"a substitute for teaching","context":"The school proposal is weaker still. Class time is scarce, and a teacher who can question, correct and adapt to a confused pupil does something no broadcast can. Television belongs in a lesson as an occasional illustration, not as a substitute for teaching."},{"en":"a narrow band of what is broadcast","context":"The conclusion that viewing should be encouraged regularly is wrong, because it treats television as uniformly educational when the value lies in a narrow band of what is broadcast."},{"en":"displace reading, play and conversation","context":"Hours in front of a screen additionally displace reading, play and conversation, which are the activities that develop language fastest."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#155","summary":"Whether children learn effectively from watching television"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2241","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Who should take children to school?","prompt":"Some people say it is the government's responsibility to transport children to school, while others believe parents should transport their children to school. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"Getting children to school is a daily problem for millions of families, and there is disagreement over whether the state or the family should solve it. In my view the responsibility belongs primarily to government, though not in every circumstance.\n\nThe argument for public provision follows from compulsory education. If a country requires a child to attend school, it has effectively created the journey and cannot reasonably leave families to manage it alone. The burden otherwise falls hardest where it can least be carried: a single parent working an early shift, or a family without a car in a village with no bus, may find that attendance depends on transport they cannot arrange. A school bus also removes hundreds of individual car journeys, which reduces congestion outside school gates and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cuts emissions in residential streets</strong>.\n\nThose who favour parental responsibility argue that raising a child includes getting them to school, and that public transport is expensive to run and rarely used efficiently. There is a further point in their favour: the walk or drive is often the only unhurried time a parent and child spend together in a working day, and handing it to a bus <strong class=\"key-phrase\">removes something worth keeping</strong>.\n\nMy position is that the answer depends on distance. Where a school is within walking distance, families should manage the journey themselves, and councils should make that safe with crossings and pavements. Where children live several miles away or roads are dangerous, transport is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a condition of attendance</strong> and the state should provide it.\n\nIn conclusion, parents can reasonably be expected to handle short local journeys, but a government that makes schooling compulsory must ensure that no child is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">kept away by the distance</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"cuts emissions in residential streets","context":"A school bus also removes hundreds of individual car journeys, which reduces congestion outside school gates and cuts emissions in residential streets."},{"en":"removes something worth keeping","context":"There is a further point in their favour: the walk or drive is often the only unhurried time a parent and child spend together in a working day, and handing it to a bus removes something worth keeping."},{"en":"a condition of attendance","context":"Where children live several miles away or roads are dangerous, transport is a condition of attendance and the state should provide it."},{"en":"kept away by the distance","context":"In conclusion, parents can reasonably be expected to handle short local journeys, but a government that makes schooling compulsory must ensure that no child is kept away by the distance."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#156","summary":"Government responsibility for school transport compared with parents'"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2245","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Should students only study?","prompt":"Full-time university students spend a lot of time studying. Some say they should do other activities too. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"University students are often advised to concentrate entirely on their degree, since that is what they are paying for. I strongly agree that they should also do other things, because the value of a degree is limited by what a graduate can do besides recall a syllabus.\n\nThe practical reason is that employers rarely choose between candidates on marks alone. When fifty applicants hold the same qualification, the decision turns on evidence that someone has organised a society, held a part-time job or captained a team, because these show that a person can meet a deadline that nobody set for them. A student with excellent results and nothing else has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no way of demonstrating that</strong>.\n\nThere is also a direct effect on the studying itself. Concentration is a finite resource, and a student reading for ten hours absorbs far less in the last three than in the first three. Exercise, sport and time with friends restore attention, which is why students who train regularly often report that they achieve more in fewer hours. Isolation works the other way: the students who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">withdraw into their books</strong> are the ones most likely to become depressed and abandon the course.\n\nI would add one qualification. Activities crowd out study when they become excessive, and a student working thirty hours a week in a bar to pay rent is not developing anything. The argument is for balance rather than for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">filling every spare hour</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree strongly. Other activities improve employability and protect the concentration that studying depends on, so a student who does nothing but read is not even <strong class=\"key-phrase\">maximising their academic results</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"no way of demonstrating that","context":"A student with excellent results and nothing else has no way of demonstrating that."},{"en":"withdraw into their books","context":"Isolation works the other way: the students who withdraw into their books are the ones most likely to become depressed and abandon the course."},{"en":"filling every spare hour","context":"I would add one qualification. Activities crowd out study when they become excessive, and a student working thirty hours a week in a bar to pay rent is not developing anything. The argument is for balance rather than for filling every spare hour."},{"en":"maximising their academic results","context":"In conclusion, I agree strongly. Other activities improve employability and protect the concentration that studying depends on, so a student who does nothing but read is not even maximising their academic results."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#157","summary":"Whether full-time students should spend time on other activities"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2254","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Does class size matter in language learning?","prompt":"Some people argue that small classes for language students are beneficial, whereas others say numbers do not matter. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"Language schools often advertise small classes as their main advantage, while others insist that the number of students is far less important than how they are taught. In my view size matters more in language teaching than in most other subjects.\n\nThe argument for small groups rests on speaking time. A language is learned by producing it, and in a class of thirty each student may speak for under a minute in an hour, whereas in a group of eight the same lesson gives them ten times as much practice. Correction also becomes possible: a teacher with eight students can hear individual errors and address them, while a teacher with thirty can only <strong class=\"key-phrase\">correct the class as a whole</strong>. Smaller groups are less intimidating too, and learners who are afraid of appearing foolish in front of a large audience often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay silent for an entire term</strong>.\n\nThose who say numbers do not matter point to method. A skilled teacher can divide a large class into pairs so that half the room is speaking at once, and a poor teacher with six students may still lecture at them. Motivation matters more than either: a determined learner who practises daily will progress in any class, while a reluctant one will not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improve in a group of four</strong>.\n\nMy own position accepts this second point without accepting its conclusion. Method and motivation are decisive, but a large class limits what even a good teacher can do, because speaking time is divided by the number of people present.\n\nIn conclusion, teaching quality matters most, yet class size sets a ceiling on it. Small groups are worth paying for in language learning specifically, because the subject <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot be learned by listening</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"correct the class as a whole","context":"Correction also becomes possible: a teacher with eight students can hear individual errors and address them, while a teacher with thirty can only correct the class as a whole."},{"en":"stay silent for an entire term","context":"Smaller groups are less intimidating too, and learners who are afraid of appearing foolish in front of a large audience often stay silent for an entire term."},{"en":"improve in a group of four","context":"Motivation matters more than either: a determined learner who practises daily will progress in any class, while a reluctant one will not improve in a group of four."},{"en":"cannot be learned by listening","context":"In conclusion, teaching quality matters most, yet class size sets a ceiling on it. Small groups are worth paying for in language learning specifically, because the subject cannot be learned by listening."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#158","summary":"Whether small classes benefit language students"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2264","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Education or leisure for young people?","prompt":"Too much emphasis is placed on education for young people. Some people believe that governments should spend more money on leisure activities for the youth instead. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that societies concentrate too heavily on schooling and should divert money towards sport, music and youth centres. I disagree with the proposition as stated, though I accept that leisure provision is undervalued and deserves more support than it receives.\n\nEducation cannot reasonably be described as over-emphasised. It is the single strongest predictor of income, health and life expectancy, and a young person who leaves school without qualifications faces a permanent disadvantage that no amount of leisure provision will repair. In economic terms, schools produce the skilled workers a country depends on, and reducing that spending to fund recreation would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trade a long-term asset for a short-term one</strong>.\n\nI would nonetheless agree that leisure has been cut too far in many places. Youth clubs, sports pitches and music tuition have disappeared from exactly the neighbourhoods where families cannot buy private alternatives, and the results are visible: children with nowhere to go after school are more likely to drift into crime, and areas that closed youth services have seen <strong class=\"key-phrase\">antisocial behaviour rise measurably</strong>. Such activities also teach persistence and teamwork, which schools deliver less directly.\n\nThe two are complementary rather than competing. Children who exercise concentrate better, and those who feel that something outside lessons belongs to them attend school more reliably, so leisure spending <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improves educational results rather than diluting them</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that too much emphasis falls on education, since qualifications determine life chances in a way recreation does not. Governments should still restore funding for youth activities, and should do so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as an addition rather than a replacement</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"trade a long-term asset for a short-term one","context":"In economic terms, schools produce the skilled workers a country depends on, and reducing that spending to fund recreation would trade a long-term asset for a short-term one."},{"en":"antisocial behaviour rise measurably","context":"Youth clubs, sports pitches and music tuition have disappeared from exactly the neighbourhoods where families cannot buy private alternatives, and the results are visible: children with nowhere to go after school are more likely to drift into crime, and areas that closed youth services have seen..."},{"en":"improves educational results rather than diluting them","context":"Children who exercise concentrate better, and those who feel that something outside lessons belongs to them attend school more reliably, so leisure spending improves educational results rather than diluting them."},{"en":"as an addition rather than a replacement","context":"Governments should still restore funding for youth activities, and should do so as an addition rather than a replacement."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#159","summary":"Spending on leisure activities for young people instead of education"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2277","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Adults who cannot read or write","prompt":"Despite better access to education, many adults today still cannot read or write. In what ways are they disadvantaged? What can governments do to help them?","answer":"Schooling has spread widely over the past century, yet a substantial number of adults still cannot read a form or write a sentence. Their difficulties reach well beyond employment, and governments can address them with measures that are neither complicated nor expensive.\n\nThe most obvious disadvantage is economic. Almost every job above manual labour now requires reading instructions, filling in records or using a screen, so illiterate adults are pushed into insecure and poorly paid work, and are the first to be dismissed when it disappears. They also lose money quietly, because they cannot check a contract, a payslip or a loan agreement and must <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trust whoever is holding the paper</strong>.\n\nThe social cost is heavier and less visible. An adult who cannot read struggles to follow a prescription, understand a diagnosis or vote on the basis of anything written, which narrows both health and citizenship. Many arrange their lives around hiding the problem, claiming they have forgotten their glasses or asking a relative to deal with letters, and that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">daily effort to conceal it</strong> produces real shame. Their children suffer too, since nobody reads with them at home.\n\nGovernments can act in three ways. Free classes must be built for adults rather than borrowed from schools: in the evening, near workplaces, in small groups, and taught with the documents learners actually face, such as bank forms and medical letters. Second, employers should be paid to release staff for a few hours a week, which removes the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">choice between wages and lessons</strong>. Third, public information should be offered in spoken and pictorial form, so that people are not excluded while they learn.\n\nIn conclusion, illiteracy limits earnings, health and confidence rather than reading alone. Practical adult classes, workplace support and accessible public information would reduce that disadvantage substantially.","phrases":[{"en":"trust whoever is holding the paper","context":"They also lose money quietly, because they cannot check a contract, a payslip or a loan agreement and must trust whoever is holding the paper."},{"en":"daily effort to conceal it","context":"Many arrange their lives around hiding the problem, claiming they have forgotten their glasses or asking a relative to deal with letters, and that daily effort to conceal it produces real shame."},{"en":"choice between wages and lessons","context":"Second, employers should be paid to release staff for a few hours a week, which removes the choice between wages and lessons."}],"subtype":"Problem / Solution","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#160","summary":"The disadvantages faced by illiterate adults and how governments can help"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2279","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"What is school actually for?","prompt":"Some people say that the main purpose of school is to turn children into good citizens and workers, rather than to benefit them as individuals. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"States pay for education out of taxes, so it is natural to ask what they are buying. I partly agree that schools exist to produce capable citizens and workers, but I think the statement sets up a division that does not hold.\n\nThe social purpose is undeniable. No government funds twelve years of teaching out of pure generosity. Schools deliver the literacy and numeracy an economy needs, and they also teach queuing, punctuality, sharing and disagreeing without violence, which is where <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the habits of living together</strong> are actually learned. A country whose schools failed at this would find both its workplaces and its politics unmanageable.\n\nThe individual purpose is equally real, however. A child who learns to read gains a private life of books that has nothing to do with any employer. Music, sport and history repay nobody in productivity, yet they are what many adults look back on. Education also lets a child <strong class=\"key-phrase\">discover what they are good at</strong>, which is the difference between a chosen life and an assigned one.\n\nWhat makes the statement misleading is that these aims mostly produce each other. A confident, curious adult is a better employee than an obedient one, and a person who has been encouraged to think for themselves makes a better citizen than someone trained only to comply. The two purposes only separate at the extremes, where a system <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drills children for examinations</strong> and turns out graduates who can pass tests but cannot think.\n\nIn conclusion, schools do serve society, and there is nothing shameful in saying so. Yet they serve it best by developing individuals rather than by shaping them for a role, so the choice the statement offers is a false one.","phrases":[{"en":"the habits of living together","context":"Schools deliver the literacy and numeracy an economy needs, and they also teach queuing, punctuality, sharing and disagreeing without violence, which is where the habits of living together are actually learned."},{"en":"discover what they are good at","context":"Education also lets a child discover what they are good at, which is the difference between a chosen life and an assigned one."},{"en":"drills children for examinations","context":"The two purposes only separate at the extremes, where a system drills children for examinations and turns out graduates who can pass tests but cannot think."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#161","summary":"Whether schools exist to produce good citizens and workers or to benefit individuals"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2286","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Teaching yourself or learning from a teacher","prompt":"Some people think adults should learn practical skills by themselves, while others believe that learning from a teacher in a classroom is better. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"An adult who wants to cook, code or rewire a lamp can now choose between a video and a course. Both routes work, and my own view is that the choice should depend on the consequences of getting it wrong.\n\nTeaching yourself has clear advantages. It costs almost nothing, fits around work and family, and lets the learner move at their own pace, repeating a difficult step and skipping what they already know. Online material is often of high quality and shows the task from angles a classroom cannot. The independence also has value in itself, since somebody who has learned one skill alone <strong class=\"key-phrase\">knows how to teach themselves the next one</strong>.\n\nA teacher offers something different. The first is correction: a self-taught learner cannot see their own mistakes, and a bad technique practised for months becomes very hard to remove, whereas a teacher notices it within minutes. The second is sequence, because an experienced instructor knows which step must come before another, while a beginner choosing videos <strong class=\"key-phrase\">assembles knowledge with gaps in the middle</strong>. A class also supplies commitment through a fixed time and other people, which is why most people who begin online courses never finish them.\n\nThe distinction that matters is risk. Cooking, drawing or basic gardening can be learned alone, because a mistake costs a meal. Electrical work, driving, using power tools and anything involving other people's safety should be taught, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the first serious error may be the last one</strong>. Professional qualifications also require supervised practice for the same reason.\n\nIn conclusion, independent learning is cheap, flexible and increasingly effective, and adults should use it freely for skills where errors are harmless. Where safety or a recognised qualification is involved, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an experienced teacher who corrects the fault early</strong> is worth the cost.","phrases":[{"en":"knows how to teach themselves the next one","context":"The independence also has value in itself, since somebody who has learned one skill alone knows how to teach themselves the next one."},{"en":"assembles knowledge with gaps in the middle","context":"The second is sequence, because an experienced instructor knows which step must come before another, while a beginner choosing videos assembles knowledge with gaps in the middle."},{"en":"the first serious error may be the last one","context":"Electrical work, driving, using power tools and anything involving other people's safety should be taught, since the first serious error may be the last one."},{"en":"an experienced teacher who corrects the fault early","context":"Where safety or a recognised qualification is involved, an experienced teacher who corrects the fault early is worth the cost."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#162","summary":"Whether adults should learn practical skills alone or in a classroom"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2299","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Educated for society or for yourself?","prompt":"Some people say that the purpose of education is to prepare individuals to be useful to society. Others say its purpose is to help individuals achieve their personal ambitions. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Education consumes a large share of public money and a large share of a person's life, so what it is for is a fair question. Both answers describe something real, and my own view is that they are the same aim seen from different sides.\n\nThe social argument is the reason education is funded publicly. Societies need doctors, engineers, teachers and farmers, and a country that produced none of them would not survive whatever its citizens had studied. Education also transmits the shared knowledge, language and rules that hold a community together, and taxpayers who pay for a medical degree <strong class=\"key-phrase\">may reasonably expect a doctor at the end of it</strong>. Where shortages are severe, directing students towards needed fields is defensible.\n\nThe individual argument answers that people are not instruments. A person spends their own years studying, and a system that assigns them to whatever the economy requires ignores what they might become. Education also does things with no social return at all: it lets someone understand music, argue about history or read for pleasure, and those belong to the person rather than to the state. Ambition is also the strongest motivation available, since students working towards something they chose <strong class=\"key-phrase\">learn far more than those completing an allocation</strong>.\n\nThe division dissolves on examination. People who pursue their own interests generally become useful, because they work harder at what absorbs them, and most valuable contributions were made by people following curiosity rather than instruction. In the other direction, useful work is satisfying, so a nurse or an engineer usually finds personal meaning in exactly the work society needed. Conflict arises only when a system <strong class=\"key-phrase\">forces everybody down a single route</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, education must supply the skills a society depends on, and it must also let individuals become what they are capable of. These aims support each other, and the failures occur where <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of them is pursued as if the other did not matter</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"may reasonably expect a doctor at the end of it","context":"Education also transmits the shared knowledge, language and rules that hold a community together, and taxpayers who pay for a medical degree may reasonably expect a doctor at the end of it."},{"en":"learn far more than those completing an allocation","context":"Ambition is also the strongest motivation available, since students working towards something they chose learn far more than those completing an allocation."},{"en":"forces everybody down a single route","context":"Conflict arises only when a system forces everybody down a single route."},{"en":"one of them is pursued as if the other did not matter","context":"In conclusion, education must supply the skills a society depends on, and it must also let individuals become what they are capable of. These aims support each other, and the failures occur where one of them is pursued as if the other did not matter."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#163","summary":"Whether education should serve society or individual ambition"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2306","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Too few students studying science","prompt":"It is observed that in many countries not enough students are choosing to study science subjects at university. What are the causes, and what will be the effects on society?","answer":"Applications to physics, chemistry and engineering have fallen in many developed countries even as demand for those skills has risen. The causes lie in school experience and in the returns students expect, and the consequences reach further than a shortage of laboratory staff.\n\nSchool is where most of the decision is made. Science taught as facts to be memorised, with little laboratory work and often by teachers trained in another subject, convinces capable students that it is dull and difficult. The subjects are also marked more severely than many alternatives, so students protecting their grades <strong class=\"key-phrase\">choose the safer route to the same university place</strong>. A shortage of specialist teachers makes this self-reinforcing, since fewer science graduates means fewer science teachers.\n\nExpectations about work matter too. A science degree is demanding and often leads to research posts that are insecure and modestly paid, while finance, law and business offer higher salaries for less mathematics. Students also see very few scientists in public life and have little idea what the career actually involves, and girls in particular <strong class=\"key-phrase\">receive the impression early that the field is not for them</strong>.\n\nThe effects on society are serious. Countries without enough engineers and scientists cannot build the energy systems, medical capacity and digital infrastructure their economies depend on, and they end up importing both the technology and the specialists, which is expensive and unreliable. Innovation slows, since new industries form where the expertise already exists. There is also a wider cost to public life: a population with little scientific understanding <strong class=\"key-phrase\">struggles to judge claims about health, climate or risk</strong>, and decisions on those subjects then follow whoever argues most loudly.\n\nIn conclusion, students avoid science because it is taught poorly, graded harshly and rewarded modestly. The result is a country that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depends on expertise it has not produced</strong> and a public less able to evaluate the questions that matter most.","phrases":[{"en":"choose the safer route to the same university place","context":"The subjects are also marked more severely than many alternatives, so students protecting their grades choose the safer route to the same university place."},{"en":"receive the impression early that the field is not for them","context":"Students also see very few scientists in public life and have little idea what the career actually involves, and girls in particular receive the impression early that the field is not for them."},{"en":"struggles to judge claims about health, climate or risk","context":"There is also a wider cost to public life: a population with little scientific understanding struggles to judge claims about health, climate or risk, and decisions on those subjects then follow whoever argues most loudly."},{"en":"depends on expertise it has not produced","context":"In conclusion, students avoid science because it is taught poorly, graded harshly and rewarded modestly. The result is a country that depends on expertise it has not produced and a public less able to evaluate the questions that matter most."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Effects","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#164","summary":"Why fewer students choose science at university and the effects on society"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2314","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Should governments fund science above other subjects?","prompt":"Some people think the government should invest more money in teaching science than in other subjects in order for a country to develop and progress. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Science and engineering are visibly linked to prosperity, so governments under pressure often favour them when budgets are set. I partly agree that science deserves generous funding, but I disagree that progress comes from teaching it at the expense of everything else.\n\nThe argument for priority is practical. Laboratories, equipment and qualified teachers cost far more than a history classroom, so equal funding is not equal provision. Countries also face problems that only technical training can address, from clean energy to disease, and firms in most economies report shortages of engineers and data specialists rather than of graduates in general. Investing here <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shortens the distance between a classroom and a working economy</strong>.\n\nThe case against overcorrection is just as strong. A scientific advance becomes useful only when other skills carry it: a vaccine needs communicators to explain it and lawyers to license it, and an invention that nobody trusts is not adopted. Many of the most valuable industries in developed economies, from design to entertainment, rest on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">training that no science budget would cover</strong>. History, languages and ethics also produce the judgement a technological society needs, and a country full of engineers who cannot argue or write <strong class=\"key-phrase\">builds things without being able to decide whether it should</strong>.\n\nThere is a practical objection too. Children pushed into science because that is where the money went perform poorly and leave the field, so the policy fails on its own terms. Progress depends on people working where they are able.\n\nIn conclusion, science teaching is expensive and important, and it is reasonable for governments to fund it well. Treating other subjects as secondary would weaken exactly the skills that turn scientific work into national development.","phrases":[{"en":"shortens the distance between a classroom and a working economy","context":"Investing here shortens the distance between a classroom and a working economy."},{"en":"training that no science budget would cover","context":"Many of the most valuable industries in developed economies, from design to entertainment, rest on training that no science budget would cover."},{"en":"builds things without being able to decide whether it should","context":"History, languages and ethics also produce the judgement a technological society needs, and a country full of engineers who cannot argue or write builds things without being able to decide whether it should."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#165","summary":"Whether teaching science deserves more public money than other subjects"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2320","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Why girls choose arts and boys choose science","prompt":"In schools and universities, girls tend to choose arts subjects while boys prefer science. What are the reasons for this trend, and do you think it should be changed?","answer":"Physics and engineering lecture halls remain largely male, while literature and languages are largely female. The reasons have little to do with ability, and the pattern should be changed because of what it costs both sexes.\n\nThe influences begin early. Children are handed different toys and different praise: construction sets and puzzles for boys, drawing and stories for girls, and by the age of seven many children already describe science as something boys do. Teachers, without intending it, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ask boys the harder questions in maths</strong> and reward girls for neatness. Textbooks and news coverage supply almost no female engineers, so girls have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nobody obvious to imagine themselves as</strong>. By the time subjects are chosen at sixteen, the decision feels personal but has been shaped for a decade.\n\nTwo further factors matter at that point. Girls who are equally good at both often choose the arts because they expect the sciences to be an uncomfortable environment, and reports of harassment in male-dominated fields confirm this. Boys, meanwhile, avoid languages and literature because those subjects are treated as unmasculine, which is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a loss that receives far less attention</strong>.\n\nThe pattern deserves to be changed for three reasons. It wastes talent a country needs, since shortages of engineers exist alongside capable girls who were steered away. It also limits earnings, because technical work pays more, so the divide quietly widens the pay gap. Products designed by uniform teams fail predictably: crash tests built around male bodies made cars less safe for women.\n\nIn conclusion, the split arises from expectations set in childhood rather than from aptitude. Changing it through early exposure, visible role models and better teaching practice would benefit both the economy and the individuals currently choosing by habit.","phrases":[{"en":"ask boys the harder questions in maths","context":"Teachers, without intending it, ask boys the harder questions in maths and reward girls for neatness."},{"en":"nobody obvious to imagine themselves as","context":"Textbooks and news coverage supply almost no female engineers, so girls have nobody obvious to imagine themselves as."},{"en":"a loss that receives far less attention","context":"Boys, meanwhile, avoid languages and literature because those subjects are treated as unmasculine, which is a loss that receives far less attention."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#166","summary":"The reasons behind subject choice by gender and whether it should change"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2325","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Why children cannot concentrate at school","prompt":"Nowadays, children find it difficult to concentrate or pay attention in school. What are the causes of this problem? What solutions can be suggested?","answer":"Teachers across very different countries report the same change: pupils lose focus within minutes. The causes lie mostly outside the classroom, and the remedies have to begin there as well.\n\nSleep is the first and most underestimated cause. Children go to bed with phones, and lessons that start early meet pupils who are several hours short of what they need, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">attention fails before the day begins</strong>. The second cause is the entertainment they are used to. Short videos deliver a new stimulus every few seconds, and a mind trained on that pace finds a twenty-minute explanation intolerably slow. Notifications add constant interruption even when the device is silent.\n\nDiet and movement matter too. Sugary breakfasts produce a rise and then a collapse in energy, and children who once walked to school and played at breaks now sit for most of the day, with break times cut to protect examination results. There are also causes closer to home: worry about money or conflict between parents occupies a child's attention completely, and large classes make it easy to drift unnoticed.\n\nPractical solutions exist. Schools can start later for teenagers, which has raised attendance and results wherever it has been tried, and can require phones to be handed in rather than merely silenced. Lessons can be broken into shorter segments with movement between them, and break times and physical education restored. Families can set a fixed hour when devices leave the bedroom, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the strongest single measure is protecting sleep</strong>. Teachers also need small enough groups to notice the child who has quietly stopped listening.\n\nIn conclusion, poor concentration comes from lost sleep, fragmented entertainment and long hours of sitting rather than from any change in children themselves. Later starts, phone-free classrooms and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more movement in the school day</strong> would address it directly.","phrases":[{"en":"attention fails before the day begins","context":"Children go to bed with phones, and lessons that start early meet pupils who are several hours short of what they need, so attention fails before the day begins."},{"en":"the strongest single measure is protecting sleep","context":"Families can set a fixed hour when devices leave the bedroom, since the strongest single measure is protecting sleep."},{"en":"more movement in the school day","context":"In conclusion, poor concentration comes from lost sleep, fragmented entertainment and long hours of sitting rather than from any change in children themselves. Later starts, phone-free classrooms and more movement in the school day would address it directly."}],"subtype":"Problem / Solution","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#167","summary":"The causes of falling concentration in class and possible remedies"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2326","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"University places for the many","prompt":"It is neither possible nor useful for a country to provide university places for a high proportion of young people. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Half of all school leavers now enter higher education in many countries, and critics argue that this is both unaffordable and pointless. I partly agree: mass expansion has been achieved, so it is clearly possible, but its usefulness depends entirely on how it is done.\n\nThe claim that it cannot be done is the weaker half. Several countries already educate more than half their young adults, and the cost, while large, is smaller than the tax revenue graduates return over a working life. What is genuinely difficult is expanding without diluting: hurried growth produces overcrowded lectures, unqualified teaching and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">degrees that no employer treats as evidence of anything</strong>.\n\nThe usefulness argument carries more weight. Many graduates now work in jobs that never required a degree, which suggests the qualification is being used to sort applicants rather than to prepare them. Students pay years of fees and lost earnings for that sorting, and countries with strong technical training produce skilled workers faster and at lower cost. There is a real shortage of electricians and nurses alongside a surplus of graduates in crowded fields, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the money has been spent on the wrong training</strong>.\n\nThe conclusion I draw is not that fewer people should be educated, but that education after school should be broader than a single model. Apprenticeships, technical colleges and short professional qualifications deserve the funding and the respect currently reserved for universities, and school leavers need honest information about where each route leads. A country also benefits from graduates beyond their wages, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an educated population argues better and is harder to mislead</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, providing university places for many young people is possible and has already happened. It is useful only where the teaching is genuine and the alternatives are properly funded, and pushing everyone through the same route <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wastes both money and talent</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"degrees that no employer treats as evidence of anything","context":"What is genuinely difficult is expanding without diluting: hurried growth produces overcrowded lectures, unqualified teaching and degrees that no employer treats as evidence of anything."},{"en":"the money has been spent on the wrong training","context":"There is a real shortage of electricians and nurses alongside a surplus of graduates in crowded fields, so the money has been spent on the wrong training."},{"en":"an educated population argues better and is harder to mislead","context":"A country also benefits from graduates beyond their wages, because an educated population argues better and is harder to mislead."},{"en":"wastes both money and talent","context":"It is useful only where the teaching is genuine and the alternatives are properly funded, and pushing everyone through the same route wastes both money and talent."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#168","summary":"Whether it is possible or useful to send a high proportion of young people to university"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2334","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Teachers or parents: who shapes a child more?","prompt":"Once students go to school, teachers have a greater influence on their intellectual and social development than parents. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"School occupies six hours of a child's day for a dozen years, so the claim that teachers take over from parents is not unreasonable. I disagree with it, though the balance shifts more than many parents realise.\n\nTeachers clearly hold advantages in the intellectual half of the argument. They are trained to explain, they know the order in which ideas should be introduced, and they can identify a difficulty that a parent would miss entirely. A gifted teacher can turn a child towards a subject for life, and for children whose homes contain few books and no help with study, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">school is the only place learning happens at all</strong>. Socially, too, the classroom supplies what a family cannot: a crowd of equals, with the negotiating, queuing and losing that go with it.\n\nParents nonetheless remain the stronger influence for structural reasons. They are there before school, after it, and long after it has ended, and their effect is cumulative rather than annual. They decide the things that determine whether teaching works: sleep, meals, whether homework is possible, whether reading happens at home, and whether school is spoken about with respect. Children also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">copy behaviour rather than instruction</strong>, and the model they see daily is at home. Values around honesty, money and how people are treated are absorbed there, and a teacher who contradicts them rarely wins.\n\nThe two are not really in competition. Teachers work with what parents send them, and the pupils who advance fastest almost always have both, which is why parental involvement predicts results more reliably than the choice of school.\n\nIn conclusion, teachers have a greater influence on academic knowledge and on how a child behaves among equals. Parents remain more powerful overall, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">their influence is constant, early and long</strong>, and the best outcomes come from the two working in the same direction.","phrases":[{"en":"school is the only place learning happens at all","context":"A gifted teacher can turn a child towards a subject for life, and for children whose homes contain few books and no help with study, school is the only place learning happens at all."},{"en":"copy behaviour rather than instruction","context":"Children also copy behaviour rather than instruction, and the model they see daily is at home."},{"en":"their influence is constant, early and long","context":"Parents remain more powerful overall, because their influence is constant, early and long, and the best outcomes come from the two working in the same direction."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#169","summary":"Whether teachers influence children more than parents once school begins"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2337","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Vocational training or university?","prompt":"Nations should spend more money on skills and vocational training for practical work rather than on university education. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Countries with plenty of graduates and no electricians are beginning to question where their education budgets go. I largely agree that vocational training deserves a greater share, though not at the cost of weakening universities.\n\nThe practical argument is strong. Economies report persistent shortages of plumbers, welders, nurses and technicians, and those shortages hold up housing, healthcare and energy projects directly. Training for such work is also cheaper and faster: an apprenticeship takes two or three years, costs a fraction of a degree and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leads to a job that exists in the town where the student lives</strong>. Graduates in oversupplied fields, meanwhile, often end up in work that never required their qualification, having paid heavily for it.\n\nThere is a fairness argument too. Countries that fund universities generously and technical colleges poorly are effectively subsidising the route taken by wealthier families, while the students who choose practical training receive less support for work the country urgently needs. Nations with strong apprenticeship systems show what the alternative looks like: skilled trades are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">respected rather than treated as a fallback</strong>, and youth unemployment is markedly lower.\n\nThe argument has limits, however. Universities produce the doctors, engineers and researchers no apprenticeship can, and they generate the knowledge on which new industries are built. Practical training also dates quickly, so a technician educated narrowly may find the equipment obsolete within a decade, whereas broader study makes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">retraining later far easier</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, shifting money towards vocational education is justified by real shortages, lower costs and fairness to students who do not attend university. The aim should be parity between the two routes rather than the replacement of one by the other, since a country needs both.","phrases":[{"en":"leads to a job that exists in the town where the student lives","context":"Training for such work is also cheaper and faster: an apprenticeship takes two or three years, costs a fraction of a degree and leads to a job that exists in the town where the student lives."},{"en":"respected rather than treated as a fallback","context":"Nations with strong apprenticeship systems show what the alternative looks like: skilled trades are respected rather than treated as a fallback, and youth unemployment is markedly lower."},{"en":"retraining later far easier","context":"Practical training also dates quickly, so a technician educated narrowly may find the equipment obsolete within a decade, whereas broader study makes retraining later far easier."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#170","summary":"Whether public money should favour practical skills training over university education"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2374","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"School until eighteen for everyone?","prompt":"Some people think everyone should stay at school until the age of eighteen. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Several countries have raised the leaving age to eighteen, and others are considering it. I agree that everybody should be in education or training until that age, but not that everybody should remain in a school classroom.\n\nThe case for staying is strong. Jobs available to a sixteen-year-old today are few, poorly paid and unstable, and those who leave early earn less throughout their lives and are the first to lose work in a downturn. Two additional years also cover the period when many young people mature enough to take study seriously, and leaving at sixteen closes doors that are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">very difficult to reopen once earning has begun</strong>. There is a social argument as well, since early leavers are more likely to be unemployed or in trouble at eighteen than those who stayed.\n\nThe objection is that compulsion produces resentment. A student who has disliked school for a decade will not improve by being kept in the same building for two more years, and a disruptive class harms everybody in it. Some sixteen-year-olds genuinely learn better through work, and an apprenticeship at that age can lead somewhere a reluctant sixth form will not. Families depending on a young person's wage also feel the cost, and forcing attendance without support <strong class=\"key-phrase\">simply moves the problem into a register</strong>.\n\nThe way to resolve this is to broaden what counts. Requiring education or training until eighteen, and including apprenticeships, technical colleges and part-time study alongside work, keeps young people learning without insisting they all learn in the same way. Countries that have done this report better outcomes than those that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raised the age and offered only one route</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, leaving education at sixteen limits a person's earnings and choices for life, so a requirement until eighteen is justified. It succeeds only if the requirement is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">training of some kind rather than school itself</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"very difficult to reopen once earning has begun","context":"Two additional years also cover the period when many young people mature enough to take study seriously, and leaving at sixteen closes doors that are very difficult to reopen once earning has begun."},{"en":"simply moves the problem into a register","context":"Families depending on a young person's wage also feel the cost, and forcing attendance without support simply moves the problem into a register."},{"en":"raised the age and offered only one route","context":"Countries that have done this report better outcomes than those that raised the age and offered only one route."},{"en":"training of some kind rather than school itself","context":"In conclusion, leaving education at sixteen limits a person's earnings and choices for life, so a requirement until eighteen is justified. It succeeds only if the requirement is training of some kind rather than school itself."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#171","summary":"Whether all young people should remain in school until they are eighteen"},{"id":"writing-task-2-088","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"How humans damage the environment","prompt":"Explain some of the ways in which humans are damaging the environment. What can governments do to address these problems? What can individual people do?","answer":"Humans are responsible for a variety of environmental problems, but we can also take steps to reduce the damage that we are causing to the planet. Both governments and individuals need to act, since neither group can solve pollution and waste on its own.\n\nTwo of the biggest threats to the environment are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">air pollution and waste</strong>. Gas emissions from factories and exhaust fumes from vehicles lead to global warming, which may have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a devastating effect on</strong> the planet in the future. As the human population increases, we are also producing ever greater quantities of waste, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">contaminates the earth and pollutes rivers and oceans</strong>.\n\nGovernments could certainly make more effort to reduce air pollution. They could introduce laws to limit emissions from factories or to force companies to use renewable energy from solar, wind or water power. They could also impose ‘green taxes’ on drivers and airline companies. In this way, people would be encouraged to use public transport and to take fewer flights abroad, therefore reducing emissions.\n\nIndividuals should also take responsibility for the impact they have on the environment. They can insulate their homes and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">switch off appliances that are not in use</strong>, choose products with less packaging, and recycle as much as possible. Most supermarkets now provide reusable bags for shoppers as well as ‘banks’ for recycling glass, plastic and paper in their car parks. By reusing and recycling, we can help to reduce waste.\n\nIn conclusion, both national governments and individuals must <strong class=\"key-phrase\">play their part in looking after the environment</strong> for future generations.","phrases":[{"en":"air pollution and waste","context":"Two of the biggest threats to the environment are air pollution and waste."},{"en":"a devastating effect on","context":"Gas emissions from factories and exhaust fumes from vehicles lead to global warming, which may have a devastating effect on the planet in the future."},{"en":"contaminates the earth and pollutes rivers and oceans","context":"As the human population increases, we are also producing ever greater quantities of waste, which contaminates the earth and pollutes rivers and oceans."},{"en":"switch off appliances that are not in use","context":"They can insulate their homes and switch off appliances that are not in use, choose products with less packaging, and recycle as much as possible."},{"en":"play their part in looking after the environment","context":"In conclusion, both national governments and individuals must play their part in looking after the environment for future generations."}],"subtype":"Causes / Effects / Solutions","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#172","summary":"Environmental damage: shared responsibility"},{"id":"writing-task-2-089","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Can the environment only be protected internationally?","prompt":"Environmental problems are serious in many countries. It means that the only possible way to protect the environment is at an international level. To what extent do you agree or disagree on this hypothesis?","answer":"Globally, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">environmental degradation</strong> has been recognised as one of the main challenges that people have to overcome in the 21st century. The rapid deterioration of the environment is believed to impose a severe threat on people's survival in the years ahead, if not solved effectively. In addressing this issue, countries worldwide should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">join forces and make a concerted effort</strong>, instead of working individually for inconsistent targets.\n\nThe clearest reason for international cooperation is that environmental harm <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rarely stays inside one border</strong>. Rivers and ocean currents connect countries whether governments intend this or not, so pollution created in one place <strong class=\"key-phrase\">quickly becomes another country's problem</strong>. Pesticide run-off from farms beside a shared river, for instance, can poison fishing grounds hundreds of kilometres downstream in a neighbouring state, and carbon released by factories in one country contributes to flooding and drought in others that had no part in producing it. Because no government controls an entire river, ocean or atmosphere alone, protecting shared natural resources is only realistic if neighbouring states act together.\n\nA second benefit is the transfer of money and technology that no country can generate alone. Cleaning a polluted river, replacing coal-fired power stations or protecting a forest all require funding and expertise that many governments simply do not have; <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the absence of</strong> outside funding, a government struggling with debt will rarely choose conservation over factories or farmland, however much it might prefer to. This is why funds financed mainly by wealthier, industrialised nations, which produced most of the world's historical emissions, matter: they give <strong class=\"key-phrase\">support from the global community</strong> to countries that would otherwise face <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a straight choice between economic survival and protecting their environment</strong>.\n\nTo summarise, cooperation between countries is inevitable in this global campaign, not only because the ecosystem is a unified mechanism but also because international engagement can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make optimal use of</strong> <strong class=\"key-phrase\">financial and human resources</strong>. By collaborating with each other, countries can protect the global environment while using the fewest possible resources.","phrases":[{"en":"environmental degradation","context":"Globally, environmental degradation has been recognised as one of the main challenges that people have to overcome in the 21st century."},{"en":"in the absence of","context":"Cleaning a polluted river, replacing coal-fired power stations or protecting a forest all require funding and expertise that many governments simply do not have; in the absence of outside funding, a government struggling with debt will rarely choose conservation over factories or farmland, howeve..."},{"en":"support from the global community","context":"This is why funds financed mainly by wealthier, industrialised nations, which produced most of the world's historical emissions, matter: they give support from the global community to countries that would otherwise face a straight choice between economic survival and protecting their environment."},{"en":"make optimal use of","context":"To summarise, cooperation between countries is inevitable in this global campaign, not only because the ecosystem is a unified mechanism but also because international engagement can make optimal use of financial and human resources."},{"en":"financial and human resources","context":"To summarise, cooperation between countries is inevitable in this global campaign, not only because the ecosystem is a unified mechanism but also because international engagement can make optimal use of financial and human resources."},{"en":"rarely stays inside one border","context":"The clearest reason for international cooperation is that environmental harm rarely stays inside one border."},{"en":"join forces and make a concerted effort","context":"In addressing this issue, countries worldwide should join forces and make a concerted effort, instead of working individually for inconsistent targets."},{"en":"quickly becomes another country's problem","context":"Rivers and ocean currents connect countries whether governments intend this or not, so pollution created in one place quickly becomes another country's problem."},{"en":"a straight choice between economic survival and protecting their environment","context":"This is why funds financed mainly by wealthier, industrialised nations, which produced most of the world's historical emissions, matter: they give support from the global community to countries that would otherwise face a straight choice between economic survival and protecting their environment."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#173","summary":"Environmental problems are serious in many countries."},{"id":"writing-task-2-090","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Is environmental damage the price of a better standard of living?","prompt":"Some industrialised countries have serious environmental problems. The damage to the environment is an inevitable result when a country tends to improve the standard of living. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Economic development can place heavy pressure on the environment, but serious damage is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not an unavoidable price of a higher standard of living</strong>. I disagree with the view that pollution and resource loss must accompany progress. The outcome depends on the energy, transport and industrial policies a country chooses.\n\nRapid growth often increases electricity use, construction, traffic and consumption. If this expansion depends on coal, inefficient factories and weak regulation, air and water quality will deteriorate. Poorer governments may also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prioritise immediate jobs and tax revenue</strong> over long-term environmental protection. These pressures explain why industrialisation has caused severe pollution in many countries.\n\nHowever, development also gives societies the money and technology needed to solve these problems. Cleaner power, efficient public transport and strict emissions standards can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">allow incomes to rise while pollution falls</strong>. Governments can price carbon, protect forests and require companies to pay for the damage they cause. Investment in insulation and renewable energy may even reduce household costs, so environmental policy can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">support rather than obstruct prosperity</strong>.\n\nPublic reporting is equally important: when emissions and health effects are measured openly, voters and investors can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reward cleaner choices</strong> and challenge industries that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">transfer their costs to society</strong>. This also makes environmental performance easier to compare across sectors and over time.\n\nIn conclusion, environmental harm is a risk of badly managed growth, not an inevitable result of improving living standards. Countries should measure progress through health, resource security and quality of life as well as income. With firm regulation and clean investment, they can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raise living standards without repeating past mistakes</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"support rather than obstruct prosperity","context":"Investment in insulation and renewable energy may even reduce household costs, so environmental policy can support rather than obstruct prosperity."},{"en":"raise living standards without repeating past mistakes","context":"With firm regulation and clean investment, they can raise living standards without repeating past mistakes."},{"en":"prioritise immediate jobs and tax revenue","context":"Poorer governments may also prioritise immediate jobs and tax revenue over long-term environmental protection."},{"en":"transfer their costs to society","context":"Public reporting is equally important: when emissions and health effects are measured openly, voters and investors can reward cleaner choices and challenge industries that transfer their costs to society."},{"en":"not an unavoidable price of a higher standard of living","context":"Economic development can place heavy pressure on the environment, but serious damage is not an unavoidable price of a higher standard of living."},{"en":"allow incomes to rise while pollution falls","context":"Cleaner power, efficient public transport and strict emissions standards can allow incomes to rise while pollution falls."},{"en":"reward cleaner choices","context":"Public reporting is equally important: when emissions and health effects are measured openly, voters and investors can reward cleaner choices and challenge industries that transfer their costs to society."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#174","summary":"Industrialised countries have serious environmental problems."},{"id":"writing-task-2-091","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Raising fuel prices to protect the environment","prompt":"It is said that the best way to solve the world's environmental problems is to increase the price of fuel. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It has been suggested that raising the price of fuel is the most effective way to tackle the world's environmental problems. I agree that higher fuel prices would reduce emissions, but I disagree that this measure alone could solve problems of such variety and scale.\n\nThe argument for it is straightforward. Burning petrol, diesel and coal is the largest single source of the greenhouse gases that cause climate change, and price is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the most reliable influence on behaviour</strong>. When fuel becomes expensive, people drive less, use public transport, buy smaller cars and insulate their homes, while manufacturers redesign products to use less energy. Countries with high fuel taxes, such as those in northern Europe, consistently record lower emissions per person than countries where fuel is cheap.\n\nThere are, however, two serious objections. The first is fairness. Fuel costs represent a much larger share of a poor family's income than of a wealthy one's, and a rural worker with no bus service <strong class=\"key-phrase\">has no realistic alternative</strong> to driving. A price rise would therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">punish those least able to change their behaviour</strong>, unless the money raised were returned to them.\n\nThe second is that fuel is only one part of the problem. Deforestation, intensive agriculture, plastic waste, overfishing and habitat loss would continue regardless of what petrol costs, and these require regulation and international agreement rather than pricing.\n\nIn conclusion, higher fuel prices are a useful and probably necessary tool, and they should be introduced gradually so that households can adjust. I disagree, however, that they represent <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the best or only solution</strong> to problems of this range and complexity.","phrases":[{"en":"has no realistic alternative","context":"Fuel costs represent a much larger share of a poor family's income than of a wealthy one's, and a rural worker with no bus service has no realistic alternative to driving."},{"en":"the best or only solution","context":"In conclusion, higher fuel prices are a useful and probably necessary tool, and they should be introduced gradually so that households can adjust. I disagree, however, that they represent the best or only solution to problems of this range and complexity."},{"en":"the most reliable influence on behaviour","context":"Burning petrol, diesel and coal is the largest single source of the greenhouse gases that cause climate change, and price is the most reliable influence on behaviour."},{"en":"punish those least able to change their behaviour","context":"A price rise would therefore punish those least able to change their behaviour, unless the money raised were returned to them."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#175","summary":"Raising fuel prices to protect the environment"},{"id":"writing-task-2-093","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Should air travel be restricted?","prompt":"Some people believe that air travel should be restricted because it causes serious pollution and will use up the world's resources. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"The environmental impact of air travel has attracted growing attention as more people use planes for work and leisure. Some therefore argue that restrictions should be imposed on flying. There are valid concerns behind this view, although a complete restriction would be neither practical nor necessary.\n\nThe first concern is that aircraft are responsible for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a significant share of</strong> air pollution. Planes consume large quantities of fuel while taking off, landing and taxiing, and they produce emissions at high altitude. Some might suggest that air travel is the only suitable mode of transport for long-distance journeys. However, trains can often provide an effective alternative on shorter routes while using less energy and causing less pollution.\n\nAircraft also create noise pollution. Although the precise impact of noise on daily life is difficult to measure, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">persistent exposure can increase anxiety and annoyance</strong>. For people living near airports or beneath flight paths, this noise can become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a serious and continuing disturbance</strong>.\n\nNevertheless, these problems do not mean that air travel should be heavily restricted. A more balanced response is to make aviation cleaner and more efficient. For example, airlines can replace older engines with cleaner models, improve flight planning and use fuller aircraft so that more passengers are carried with the same amount of fuel. Governments can also support high-speed rail where it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a realistic substitute</strong>.\n\nIn light of these considerations, the environmental damage caused by aviation deserves serious attention. However, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">targeted technological improvements</strong> and sensible transport policies are preferable to broad restrictions on air travel.","phrases":[{"en":"a significant share of","context":"The first concern is that aircraft are responsible for a significant share of air pollution."},{"en":"persistent exposure can increase anxiety and annoyance","context":"Although the precise impact of noise on daily life is difficult to measure, persistent exposure can increase anxiety and annoyance."},{"en":"a serious and continuing disturbance","context":"For people living near airports or beneath flight paths, this noise can become a serious and continuing disturbance."},{"en":"a realistic substitute","context":"Governments can also support high-speed rail where it is a realistic substitute."},{"en":"targeted technological improvements","context":"In light of these considerations, the environmental damage caused by aviation deserves serious attention. However, targeted technological improvements and sensible transport policies are preferable to broad restrictions on air travel."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#176","summary":"People believe that air travel should be restricted because it causes…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-095","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Water shortages","prompt":"Water resources around the world are becoming increasingly scarce. What are the possible reasons for this, and what solutions can you suggest?","answer":"Fresh water is becoming scarce in many regions, and several countries now face serious shortages. The causes are both natural and human, and addressing them requires changes in agriculture, infrastructure and pricing.\n\nAgriculture is the largest single cause, accounting for roughly seventy per cent of all fresh water used worldwide. Much of it is wasted through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">flood irrigation</strong>, in which most of the water evaporates or drains away before reaching the crop, and farmers frequently grow thirsty crops such as rice and cotton in dry regions because subsidies make it profitable. Population growth and urbanisation add further pressure, since cities concentrate demand and often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lose a third of their supply through leaking pipes</strong>. Climate change compounds all of this by making rainfall less predictable.\n\nThe most effective solution is to use water more efficiently in farming. Drip irrigation delivers water directly to the roots and can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">halve consumption</strong>, and choosing crops suited to the local climate would save more still. Repairing urban water systems is equally important and often cheaper than building new reservoirs.\n\nPricing is the most difficult but most powerful measure. Water is heavily subsidised in most countries, so there is little incentive to conserve it. Charging industry and large-scale agriculture a realistic price, while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">guaranteeing a free basic allowance</strong> to households, would reduce waste without harming the poor.\n\nIn conclusion, water shortages are caused mainly by inefficient irrigation, urban growth and a changing climate. Efficient technology, repaired infrastructure and sensible pricing offer the most realistic response.","phrases":[{"en":"flood irrigation","context":"Much of it is wasted through flood irrigation, in which most of the water evaporates or drains away before reaching the crop, and farmers frequently grow thirsty crops such as rice and cotton in dry regions because subsidies make it profitable."},{"en":"lose a third of their supply through leaking pipes","context":"Population growth and urbanisation add further pressure, since cities concentrate demand and often lose a third of their supply through leaking pipes."},{"en":"halve consumption","context":"Drip irrigation delivers water directly to the roots and can halve consumption, and choosing crops suited to the local climate would save more still."},{"en":"guaranteeing a free basic allowance","context":"Charging industry and large-scale agriculture a realistic price, while guaranteeing a free basic allowance to households, would reduce waste without harming the poor."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","_displayNo":"#177","summary":"Water shortages"},{"id":"writing-task-2-153","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"The throw-away society","prompt":"Many people believe that modern society has become a throw-away society, filled with plastic bags and rubbish. What are the causes of this, and what solutions can you suggest?","answer":"It is widely observed that modern societies discard an enormous quantity of packaging, plastic and barely used goods. This problem has clear causes, and it can only be solved by changing the economics of production rather than by appealing to consumers.\n\nThe principal cause is that manufacturing has become extremely cheap. Mass production and low-cost labour mean that a new appliance or garment often costs less than repairing the old one, and repair skills have become correspondingly rare. Manufacturers reinforce this deliberately: products are designed with sealed cases and non-replaceable batteries, and new models are released annually to make functioning items <strong class=\"key-phrase\">appear outdated</strong>. A second cause is packaging, since retailers wrap goods in plastic to protect them in transit and to display them attractively, and almost none of this material is reused.\n\nThe most effective solution would be to make producers responsible for what they sell. If manufacturers were legally obliged to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">collect and recycle their own products</strong>, they would immediately design them to last longer and to be dismantled easily. A second measure is to tax packaging and single-use plastics, since charging for plastic bags has reduced their use dramatically wherever it has been tried. Finally, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a legal right to repair</strong>, requiring spare parts and manuals to be available at reasonable cost, would extend the life of expensive items considerably.\n\nIn conclusion, the throw-away society exists because discarding is cheaper than maintaining. Reversing that calculation through regulation and taxation is far more likely to succeed than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">asking individuals to consume less</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"appear outdated","context":"Manufacturers reinforce this deliberately: products are designed with sealed cases and non-replaceable batteries, and new models are released annually to make functioning items appear outdated."},{"en":"collect and recycle their own products","context":"If manufacturers were legally obliged to collect and recycle their own products, they would immediately design them to last longer and to be dismantled easily."},{"en":"a legal right to repair","context":"Finally, a legal right to repair, requiring spare parts and manuals to be available at reasonable cost, would extend the life of expensive items considerably."},{"en":"asking individuals to consume less","context":"In conclusion, the throw-away society exists because discarding is cheaper than maintaining. Reversing that calculation through regulation and taxation is far more likely to succeed than asking individuals to consume less."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","_displayNo":"#178","summary":"The throw-away society"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2222","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Who should control fresh water?","prompt":"Some people think that people should be given the right to use fresh water as they like. Others believe governments should strictly control the use of fresh water. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Fresh water is both a basic necessity and a limited resource, and there is disagreement over whether households should use it freely or whether governments should regulate consumption. I believe some control is unavoidable, though it must be designed carefully.\n\nThe argument for free use rests on the idea that water is a right rather than a commodity. People need it to drink, cook and stay clean, and rationing it risks harming the poorest households first, since a family that cannot afford a metered supply may cut back on washing before anything else. There is also a fairness objection: domestic users account for a small fraction of consumption, so restricting them while agriculture and industry continue as before <strong class=\"key-phrase\">places the burden on the wrong people</strong>.\n\nThe case for control is simply that demand can exceed supply. Aquifers in northern India and the American west are being <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pumped faster than rainfall replaces them</strong>, and once a water table falls the damage takes decades to reverse. Where no limits exist, water tends to be used on whatever is most profitable rather than most necessary, which is why arid regions end up <strong class=\"key-phrase\">growing thirsty crops for export</strong>.\n\nMy view is that governments must regulate, but should aim at the largest users rather than at families. Charging industry and irrigation the real cost of water, while guaranteeing every household an affordable basic allowance, cuts consumption without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">threatening anybody's health</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, unrestricted use is not sustainable in regions where water is scarce, and the alternative is not rationing showers but pricing large-scale use properly. Handled this way, control protects the resource while leaving ordinary people with what they actually need.","phrases":[{"en":"places the burden on the wrong people","context":"There is also a fairness objection: domestic users account for a small fraction of consumption, so restricting them while agriculture and industry continue as before places the burden on the wrong people."},{"en":"growing thirsty crops for export","context":"Where no limits exist, water tends to be used on whatever is most profitable rather than most necessary, which is why arid regions end up growing thirsty crops for export."},{"en":"threatening anybody's health","context":"Charging industry and irrigation the real cost of water, while guaranteeing every household an affordable basic allowance, cuts consumption without threatening anybody's health."},{"en":"pumped faster than rainfall replaces them","context":"Aquifers in northern India and the American west are being pumped faster than rainfall replaces them, and once a water table falls the damage takes decades to reverse."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#179","summary":"Free use of fresh water compared with strict government control"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2243","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Where should recycling be taught?","prompt":"Some people think that children should be taught at school to recycle materials and avoid waste. Other people believe that children should be taught this at home. Discuss both opinions and give your own opinion.","answer":"Environmental habits form early, which raises the question of whether schools or parents should be responsible for teaching them. Both have a part to play, and my own view is that the two do different jobs and neither can be dropped.\n\nThe argument for schools is that they reach every child and can explain the reasoning. A parent can say that a bottle goes in a particular bin, but a teacher can show what happens to plastic that reaches the sea and why some materials can be reprocessed while others cannot, and a child who understands the reason will apply it to situations nobody has covered. Schools also reach children whose families recycle nothing, which is where teaching <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changes the most behaviour</strong>. Pupils frequently take the habit home and press their parents to adopt it.\n\nThe case for the home rests on repetition. Recycling is a daily routine rather than a body of knowledge, and it is learned by sorting rubbish several times a week from the age of three. Children also copy what adults actually do rather than what they are told, so a lesson at school carries little weight in a household where everything <strong class=\"key-phrase\">goes into the same bin</strong>.\n\nIn my view the objection to each is answered by the other. Schools supply the understanding but only a few hours of it; homes supply the daily practice but not always the reason, and sometimes not the example. A child who receives both learns a habit that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">survives leaving home</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, this is not a choice between two options. Schools should explain why waste matters and homes should make sorting normal, since knowledge without routine is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">forgotten within a term</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"changes the most behaviour","context":"Schools also reach children whose families recycle nothing, which is where teaching changes the most behaviour."},{"en":"goes into the same bin","context":"Children also copy what adults actually do rather than what they are told, so a lesson at school carries little weight in a household where everything goes into the same bin."},{"en":"survives leaving home","context":"A child who receives both learns a habit that survives leaving home."},{"en":"forgotten within a term","context":"In conclusion, this is not a choice between two options. Schools should explain why waste matters and homes should make sorting normal, since knowledge without routine is forgotten within a term."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#180","summary":"Teaching children about recycling at school or at home"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2251","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Food and drink in plastic","prompt":"In many countries today, more and more food and drink is sold in plastic containers. Do you think the advantages of this trend outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"Almost everything in a modern supermarket arrives wrapped in plastic. The material solves genuine problems, but in my view its disadvantages now outweigh its benefits.\n\nThe advantages are worth stating fairly. Plastic is light, cheap and airtight, so a sealed package keeps meat and salad edible for days longer than an open one, which reduces the amount of food thrown away. Because it weighs so little, transporting goods in plastic burns far less fuel than transporting them in glass, and sealed containers protect food from contamination between the factory and the shelf. In countries without reliable refrigeration, this <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prevents a great deal of illness</strong>.\n\nThe difficulty is what happens afterwards. Food packaging is used for minutes and lasts for centuries, and most of it is not recycled: it is thin, often made of mixed layers that cannot be separated, and contaminated by the food it held. What is collected is frequently exported to countries with weaker regulation, where it is burned or dumped. The result is visible on coastlines and invisible in the food chain, since plastic breaks into fragments that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">enter fish and eventually people</strong>.\n\nThe cost also falls in the wrong place. Manufacturers gain the savings while councils and taxpayers pay for collection and disposal, so there is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no pressure on producers to use less</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, plastic packaging genuinely reduces food waste and illness, and simply banning it would create new problems. Its disadvantages nonetheless outweigh those gains, because the damage is permanent while the benefit lasts a few days, and this will only change when producers are made responsible for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what happens to the packaging they create</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"prevents a great deal of illness","context":"In countries without reliable refrigeration, this prevents a great deal of illness."},{"en":"enter fish and eventually people","context":"The result is visible on coastlines and invisible in the food chain, since plastic breaks into fragments that enter fish and eventually people."},{"en":"no pressure on producers to use less","context":"The cost also falls in the wrong place. Manufacturers gain the savings while councils and taxpayers pay for collection and disposal, so there is no pressure on producers to use less."},{"en":"what happens to the packaging they create","context":"Its disadvantages nonetheless outweigh those gains, because the damage is permanent while the benefit lasts a few days, and this will only change when producers are made responsible for what happens to the packaging they create."}],"subtype":"Advantages outweigh disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#181","summary":"The rise of plastic packaging for food and drink"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2255","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Flights or cars?","prompt":"Long distance flight uses more fuel than cars and brings pollution to the environment. We should discourage non-essential flights rather than limit the use of cars. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Aviation and road transport both damage the atmosphere, and it is argued that policy should target unnecessary flying rather than driving. I partly agree, because flying is harder to make clean, but I do not accept that cars should therefore be left alone.\n\nThe case for concentrating on flights is strong in one respect. A single return flight between Europe and Asia produces more carbon per passenger than a year of typical local driving, and the emissions are released high in the atmosphere, where their warming effect is greater. Aviation fuel is also untaxed in most countries, so tickets are artificially cheap, and the technology to replace kerosene on long routes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">does not yet exist at scale</strong>. Discouraging the least necessary journeys is therefore one of the few tools available.\n\nHowever, treating this as an alternative to reducing car use misreads the arithmetic. Road transport produces several times more emissions in total than aviation, simply because so many more people drive so much more often, and a small reduction in a very large number outweighs a large reduction in a small one. Flying is also concentrated among wealthier travellers, so a policy aimed only at flights <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaves most of the problem untouched</strong> while appearing decisive.\n\nCars are additionally the easier problem to solve. Electric vehicles, better buses and cycle lanes already work, whereas no equivalent exists for a long-haul aircraft, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">progress is available now</strong> on the road.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that unnecessary flights deserve discouragement, since aviation is difficult to decarbonise and currently undertaxed. Presenting this as a reason to spare cars is mistaken, because driving causes the larger share of the damage and is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the part we already know how to fix</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"does not yet exist at scale","context":"Aviation fuel is also untaxed in most countries, so tickets are artificially cheap, and the technology to replace kerosene on long routes does not yet exist at scale."},{"en":"leaves most of the problem untouched","context":"Flying is also concentrated among wealthier travellers, so a policy aimed only at flights leaves most of the problem untouched while appearing decisive."},{"en":"progress is available now","context":"Cars are additionally the easier problem to solve. Electric vehicles, better buses and cycle lanes already work, whereas no equivalent exists for a long-haul aircraft, so progress is available now on the road."},{"en":"the part we already know how to fix","context":"Presenting this as a reason to spare cars is mistaken, because driving causes the larger share of the damage and is the part we already know how to fix."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#182","summary":"Discouraging non-essential flights rather than limiting car use"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2260","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Drilling in untouched places","prompt":"The increased world demand for oil and gas has made it necessary to locate these sources in remote and untouched natural areas. Do you think the advantages outweigh the disadvantages of damaging these natural areas?","answer":"As accessible reserves are exhausted, energy companies have moved into the Arctic, deep oceans and rainforest. I do not believe the advantages outweigh the damage, although the arguments in favour deserve to be taken seriously.\n\nThe case for extraction rests on need. Modern economies still run on oil and gas, and a country that cannot secure supply faces rising prices that fall hardest on poorer households, who spend the largest share of their income on heating and transport. Domestic production also reduces dependence on unstable exporters, and for developing countries a discovery can fund schools and hospitals that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">would otherwise be unaffordable</strong>.\n\nThe damage, however, is of a different kind from the benefit. These regions are remote precisely because they are difficult to reach, which means that any spill is almost impossible to contain: oil in Arctic ice degrades over decades rather than years, and there is no equipment capable of cleaning it. Extraction also brings roads, pipelines and settlements into ecosystems that had none, so the disturbance <strong class=\"key-phrase\">extends far beyond the drilling site</strong>. Indigenous communities lose the land their way of life depends on.\n\nThe decisive objection is that this is investment in the wrong direction. A new field takes decades to repay its cost, which commits a country to burning fossil fuels long after it has promised to stop, and the same capital spent on renewable generation would deliver energy security <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without any of the damage</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the disadvantages outweigh the advantages. The energy obtained is real but temporary, whereas the harm to fragile environments is effectively permanent, and the money involved would buy more security if it were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">directed at replacing the demand instead</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"would otherwise be unaffordable","context":"Domestic production also reduces dependence on unstable exporters, and for developing countries a discovery can fund schools and hospitals that would otherwise be unaffordable."},{"en":"extends far beyond the drilling site","context":"Extraction also brings roads, pipelines and settlements into ecosystems that had none, so the disturbance extends far beyond the drilling site."},{"en":"without any of the damage","context":"A new field takes decades to repay its cost, which commits a country to burning fossil fuels long after it has promised to stop, and the same capital spent on renewable generation would deliver energy security without any of the damage."},{"en":"directed at replacing the demand instead","context":"The energy obtained is real but temporary, whereas the harm to fragile environments is effectively permanent, and the money involved would buy more security if it were directed at replacing the demand instead."}],"subtype":"Advantages outweigh disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#183","summary":"Extracting oil and gas from remote natural areas"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2269","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"The cost of making more goods","prompt":"The increase in the production of consumer goods results in damage to the natural environment. What are the causes of this? What can be done to solve this problem?","answer":"Factories now produce more clothing, electronics and furniture than at any point in history, and the environmental cost is visible in emissions, landfill and polluted rivers. The causes lie in how goods are designed and priced, and the solutions must change both.\n\nThe first cause is that products are built to be replaced. Clothing is made cheaply enough to be worn a few times, and appliances are designed so that a failed component cannot be replaced without buying the whole machine, which means each purchase must be manufactured, shipped and eventually discarded. Marketing reinforces this by presenting last year's model as embarrassing rather than adequate, so goods are thrown away while still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">perfectly capable of working</strong>.\n\nThe second cause is that prices do not reflect damage. A manufacturer pays for materials and labour but not for the river it pollutes or the carbon it releases, so the cheapest production method is usually the dirtiest and the cost is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carried by the public instead</strong>.\n\nThe solutions follow directly. Making producers legally responsible for their products at the end of life gives them a financial reason to design goods that can be repaired and dismantled, and this has already reduced electronic waste where it has been applied. Pricing pollution through carbon taxes removes the advantage currently enjoyed by dirty production. Requiring spare parts to be available for a fixed number of years would also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make repair a realistic option</strong> again.\n\nIn conclusion, the damage arises because goods are designed for a short life and their true cost is never charged. It can be reduced by making manufacturers responsible for what they produce and by ensuring that the price of an item <strong class=\"key-phrase\">includes the harm it causes</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"perfectly capable of working","context":"Marketing reinforces this by presenting last year's model as embarrassing rather than adequate, so goods are thrown away while still perfectly capable of working."},{"en":"carried by the public instead","context":"A manufacturer pays for materials and labour but not for the river it pollutes or the carbon it releases, so the cheapest production method is usually the dirtiest and the cost is carried by the public instead."},{"en":"make repair a realistic option","context":"Requiring spare parts to be available for a fixed number of years would also make repair a realistic option again."},{"en":"includes the harm it causes","context":"It can be reduced by making manufacturers responsible for what they produce and by ensuring that the price of an item includes the harm it causes."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#184","summary":"Environmental damage caused by rising production of consumer goods"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2283","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Why so little gets recycled","prompt":"In many countries, there is not enough recycling of waste materials such as paper, glass and cans. What are the reasons for this, and what can be done to solve the problem?","answer":"Most households claim to support recycling, yet the proportion of material actually recovered remains low in many countries. The causes are practical and economic rather than a lack of goodwill, and the remedies have to work on both.\n\nConfusion is the first obstacle. Rules differ between districts and change without explanation, packaging is rarely labelled clearly, and a resident who cannot tell whether a container is acceptable <strong class=\"key-phrase\">puts it in the ordinary bin to be safe</strong>. Convenience is the second: where recycling requires washing items, separating them into four containers and carrying them to a distant point, participation falls sharply, particularly among people in flats with no space for the bins.\n\nThe economics matter more than most people realise. Recycled material must compete with new material, and while oil is cheap new plastic is cheaper than recovered plastic, so collected waste sometimes has no buyer. Products are also designed without recovery in mind: mixed plastics, glued laminates and coated cartons <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot be separated at any reasonable cost</strong>. Meanwhile landfill and incineration remain the cheap option for local authorities in many places.\n\nEffective measures exist. Deposit schemes on bottles and cans, where a small sum is refunded on return, achieve recovery rates above ninety per cent wherever they operate, because they attach a price to the object. Simple, standardised rules across a country and clear symbols on packaging remove the confusion, and frequent collection from the doorstep removes the effort. The strongest measure is to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make producers responsible for the cost of disposal</strong>, which pushes them to redesign packaging rather than leaving councils to deal with it. Charging by the weight of unsorted rubbish also works, provided low-income households are protected.\n\nIn conclusion, recycling rates are low because the system is confusing, inconvenient and often uneconomic. Deposit schemes, uniform rules and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">charging producers for what they create</strong> would change all three.","phrases":[{"en":"puts it in the ordinary bin to be safe","context":"Rules differ between districts and change without explanation, packaging is rarely labelled clearly, and a resident who cannot tell whether a container is acceptable puts it in the ordinary bin to be safe."},{"en":"cannot be separated at any reasonable cost","context":"Products are also designed without recovery in mind: mixed plastics, glued laminates and coated cartons cannot be separated at any reasonable cost."},{"en":"make producers responsible for the cost of disposal","context":"The strongest measure is to make producers responsible for the cost of disposal, which pushes them to redesign packaging rather than leaving councils to deal with it."},{"en":"charging producers for what they create","context":"In conclusion, recycling rates are low because the system is confusing, inconvenient and often uneconomic. Deposit schemes, uniform rules and charging producers for what they create would change all three."}],"subtype":"Problem / Solution","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#185","summary":"The reasons for low recycling rates and how they could be raised"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2290","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Prevent climate change or adapt to it?","prompt":"Some people say that instead of preventing climate change we should find a way to live with it. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Adaptation has moved from a fringe idea to a central part of climate policy, and some now argue it should replace the attempt to cut emissions. I disagree with that substitution, although adaptation is necessary and currently underfunded.\n\nThe argument for adapting has a factual basis. A degree of warming is already unavoidable, because emissions released decades ago continue to act, so sea defences, drought-resistant crops and heat-proofed buildings are needed whatever happens next. Adaptation also has the advantage that a country benefits directly from its own spending, unlike emission cuts, whose benefit is shared with everyone including those who do nothing. Given how slowly international agreements progress, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">protecting yourself is at least within your own control</strong>.\n\nThe case against treating it as an alternative is decisive, however. Adaptation has limits that prevention does not: a wall can be built against a metre of sea level rise but not against ten, and no engineering protects a region whose rainfall has stopped. Costs also rise faster than the temperature, so each further degree makes adaptation less affordable, and the ability to pay is unequal, meaning a wealthy country builds defences while poorer ones <strong class=\"key-phrase\">absorb losses they did not cause</strong>. Some consequences cannot be adapted to at all, since a species that cannot move or a glacier feeding a river system <strong class=\"key-phrase\">does not respond to engineering</strong>.\n\nThe two are also not competing for the same money in the way the argument suggests. Preventing further warming reduces how much adaptation will eventually be required, so spending on emissions <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lowers the future adaptation bill</strong> rather than diverting funds from it.\n\nIn conclusion, adapting to changes already under way is unavoidable and deserves far more investment than it receives. Abandoning prevention would raise the eventual cost beyond anything adaptation could meet, so both must be pursued together.","phrases":[{"en":"protecting yourself is at least within your own control","context":"Given how slowly international agreements progress, protecting yourself is at least within your own control."},{"en":"absorb losses they did not cause","context":"Costs also rise faster than the temperature, so each further degree makes adaptation less affordable, and the ability to pay is unequal, meaning a wealthy country builds defences while poorer ones absorb losses they did not cause."},{"en":"does not respond to engineering","context":"Some consequences cannot be adapted to at all, since a species that cannot move or a glacier feeding a river system does not respond to engineering."},{"en":"lowers the future adaptation bill","context":"Preventing further warming reduces how much adaptation will eventually be required, so spending on emissions lowers the future adaptation bill rather than diverting funds from it."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#186","summary":"Whether we should adapt to a changing climate rather than try to stop it"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2302","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Climate change and business","prompt":"Some people think that climate change could have a negative effect on business, while others think it could create more business opportunities. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Companies now treat climate change as a financial question rather than an environmental one, and their conclusions differ sharply. Both readings are accurate for particular industries, and my own view is that the losses will be larger and will fall on those least able to absorb them.\n\nThe case for damage is straightforward. Floods, storms and heat destroy factories, crops and transport links, and insurers in several regions have stopped covering the risk altogether, which makes some businesses impossible to run. Supply chains are disrupted by drought and low rivers, and agriculture, tourism and fishing face conditions their whole business model assumed would not change. Regulation adds a second cost, since carbon pricing and emissions rules make <strong class=\"key-phrase\">existing methods more expensive to continue</strong>.\n\nThe opportunity argument is equally grounded. Any problem of this size creates markets: renewable energy, insulation, batteries, electric transport and efficient farming are among the fastest-growing industries anywhere, and companies that moved early have profited substantially. Adaptation is a market of its own, covering flood defences, cooling, drought-resistant seed and insurance products, and firms that reduce energy use <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cut their costs while reducing emissions</strong>.\n\nThe two views can be reconciled by asking who gains. The opportunities are concentrated in wealthy countries with capital, technology and stable institutions, while the damage falls hardest on poorer regions whose economies depend on agriculture and which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot borrow to rebuild</strong>. Even where new industries grow, they arrive in different places from the ones that decline.\n\nIn conclusion, climate change will destroy some businesses and create others, and the sectors benefiting are already visible. The destruction will be greater in total and unevenly distributed, so treating the situation as an opportunity <strong class=\"key-phrase\">describes the view from the countries least affected</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"existing methods more expensive to continue","context":"Regulation adds a second cost, since carbon pricing and emissions rules make existing methods more expensive to continue."},{"en":"cut their costs while reducing emissions","context":"Adaptation is a market of its own, covering flood defences, cooling, drought-resistant seed and insurance products, and firms that reduce energy use cut their costs while reducing emissions."},{"en":"cannot borrow to rebuild","context":"The opportunities are concentrated in wealthy countries with capital, technology and stable institutions, while the damage falls hardest on poorer regions whose economies depend on agriculture and which cannot borrow to rebuild."},{"en":"describes the view from the countries least affected","context":"The destruction will be greater in total and unevenly distributed, so treating the situation as an opportunity describes the view from the countries least affected."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#187","summary":"Whether a changing climate threatens business or creates opportunities"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2315","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Politicians or individuals on the environment?","prompt":"Environmental protection is the responsibility of politicians, not individuals, because individuals can do too little. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Faced with a problem the size of climate change, most people conclude that their own recycling counts for nothing. I largely agree that governments carry the decisive responsibility, but the conclusion that individuals therefore have none is mistaken.\n\nGovernments hold the tools that matter. Only a state can price carbon, set building standards, rewrite planning law or shut down a coal plant, and a single one of those decisions outweighs the lifetime efforts of thousands of careful households. Governments also fix the conditions under which individuals choose: a person cannot cycle where there is no safe route, and cannot avoid a car where nothing else runs. Blaming consumers is often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a convenient way of moving attention away from policy</strong>, which is why some of the loudest campaigns about personal footprints were funded by the industries responsible.\n\nIndividual behaviour still matters for three reasons. It adds up, since household energy, food and transport account for a large share of national emissions, and no policy reaches those choices directly. It also creates the market that policy needs, because electric cars and plant-based food became cheap only after enough people bought the expensive early versions. Most importantly, individuals are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">voters, employees and shareholders</strong> as well as consumers, and the political will to pass difficult laws only exists where the public has already <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shifted its own expectations</strong>.\n\nThe two are therefore linked rather than opposed. Governments act when the public demands it, and the public demands what it has begun to practise.\n\nIn conclusion, politicians must lead, because the decisions with real weight are theirs alone. Individuals who conclude that nothing they do counts remove the pressure that makes such leadership possible.","phrases":[{"en":"a convenient way of moving attention away from policy","context":"Blaming consumers is often a convenient way of moving attention away from policy, which is why some of the loudest campaigns about personal footprints were funded by the industries responsible."},{"en":"voters, employees and shareholders","context":"Most importantly, individuals are voters, employees and shareholders as well as consumers, and the political will to pass difficult laws only exists where the public has already shifted its own expectations."},{"en":"shifted its own expectations","context":"Most importantly, individuals are voters, employees and shareholders as well as consumers, and the political will to pass difficult laws only exists where the public has already shifted its own expectations."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#188","summary":"Whether environmental protection is the job of politicians rather than individuals"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2338","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Who should reduce packaging?","prompt":"Some people feel that manufacturers and supermarkets have the responsibility to reduce the amount of packaging on goods. Others argue that customers should avoid buying goods with a lot of packaging. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Household bins fill mostly with wrapping rather than with anything that was bought. Responsibility for that can be placed on the companies producing it or on the shoppers accepting it, and although both hold some, the weight falls clearly on the producers.\n\nThe argument for consumer responsibility is straightforward. Companies supply what sells, so a widespread refusal to buy heavily wrapped goods would change shelves quickly, as the reaction to plastic straws showed. Shoppers can also choose loose vegetables, refill containers and larger sizes, and since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the purchase is the moment the waste is created</strong>, that is where it can be prevented.\n\nThe case against relying on shoppers is stronger. Choice is largely an illusion in a supermarket, because a customer who wants unwrapped rice may find every brand in plastic, and avoiding packaging then means going without the product. Poorer households in particular cannot pay the premium that unpackaged goods usually carry. Consumers also cannot judge what they are buying, since materials are unlabelled and much of what appears recyclable is not, so the choice <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depends on information the shopper does not have</strong>. Placing the duty on individuals is convenient for producers precisely because it moves attention away from the decisions that matter.\n\nManufacturers and retailers hold the real levers. They decide the material, the size and whether a product needs a tray inside a box inside a wrapper, and a single change made centrally removes millions of items at once. They also profit from packaging, which sells the product and extends its life on the shelf, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the cost of disposal falls on somebody who never chose it</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, shoppers should use the influence they have, and their preferences do shift markets. Real reduction requires producers to redesign packaging and governments to make them pay for what they create, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">only the producer can change what is on the shelf</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"the purchase is the moment the waste is created","context":"Shoppers can also choose loose vegetables, refill containers and larger sizes, and since the purchase is the moment the waste is created, that is where it can be prevented."},{"en":"depends on information the shopper does not have","context":"Consumers also cannot judge what they are buying, since materials are unlabelled and much of what appears recyclable is not, so the choice depends on information the shopper does not have."},{"en":"the cost of disposal falls on somebody who never chose it","context":"They also profit from packaging, which sells the product and extends its life on the shelf, so the cost of disposal falls on somebody who never chose it."},{"en":"only the producer can change what is on the shelf","context":"Real reduction requires producers to redesign packaging and governments to make them pay for what they create, because only the producer can change what is on the shelf."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#189","summary":"Whether producers or shoppers are responsible for cutting packaging waste"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2341","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Pollution from transport: whose job?","prompt":"Research has shown that the transport of goods and people is a main source of pollution. Some people believe governments should deal with this, while others believe it is the responsibility of individuals. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Moving people and freight now produces a substantial share of global emissions, and the argument about who should fix it repeats every time fuel prices rise. Both parties have a role, but the decisive one belongs to government.\n\nThe case for individual responsibility is that the journeys are ours. Nobody forces a person to drive a large car to a shop half a mile away, to fly for a weekend break or to order goods separately for next-day delivery. Millions of such decisions add up, and if enough people cycled, combined their trips or flew less, emissions would fall without any law being passed. Consumer preference also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">moves companies faster than legislation does</strong>, as the demand for electric cars has shown.\n\nThe limits of that view appear quickly, however. Individual choice depends on what exists: a family cannot take a train where the line was closed, and cannot cycle where the road is dangerous. Freight, which produces much of the pollution, is not chosen by consumers at all. There is also a fairness problem, because the person who gives up a car pays a real cost while everybody else enjoys the cleaner air, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">acting alone is a sacrifice rather than a solution</strong>.\n\nGovernments hold the tools that change the underlying conditions: fuel and emissions standards, investment in railways and cycle routes, planning rules that stop cities sprawling, and taxes that make polluting transport reflect its true cost. One decision on vehicle standards achieves what millions of careful drivers could not.\n\nIn conclusion, individuals should travel more thoughtfully, and public opinion is what makes difficult policies possible. Responsibility rests mainly with governments, because only they can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">build the alternatives that individual choices depend on</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"moves companies faster than legislation does","context":"Consumer preference also moves companies faster than legislation does, as the demand for electric cars has shown."},{"en":"acting alone is a sacrifice rather than a solution","context":"There is also a fairness problem, because the person who gives up a car pays a real cost while everybody else enjoys the cleaner air, so acting alone is a sacrifice rather than a solution."},{"en":"build the alternatives that individual choices depend on","context":"In conclusion, individuals should travel more thoughtfully, and public opinion is what makes difficult policies possible. Responsibility rests mainly with governments, because only they can build the alternatives that individual choices depend on."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#190","summary":"Whether governments or individuals should tackle pollution from moving goods and people"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2348","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Acting together on fossil fuels","prompt":"The international community must take immediate action to ensure that all nations reduce their consumption of fossil fuels such as gas and oil. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Coal, oil and gas still supply most of the world's energy, and the emissions from burning them are the principal cause of a warming climate. I agree that immediate international action is necessary, but the word \"all\" needs qualifying if the effort is to succeed.\n\nThe argument for urgency rests on how the atmosphere works. Carbon dioxide remains for centuries, so today's emissions determine conditions long after the power stations have closed, and delay is not neutral: each year of high output makes the eventual reduction steeper and more expensive. The damage is already visible in heatwaves, failed harvests and flooding, and the cost of acting later <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rises faster than the cost of acting now</strong>.\n\nCooperation is essential because the problem ignores borders. A country that reduces its emissions alone gains a fraction of the benefit and carries the whole cost, while its industries move to wherever rules are loosest. Only an international agreement removes that incentive, and only shared finance and technology make the transition possible for countries that cannot pay for it. Agreements also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give companies the certainty they need to invest</strong> in something other than oil.\n\nThe qualification matters, however. Demanding identical cuts from every nation would be neither fair nor effective, since wealthy countries produced most of the accumulated emissions and can afford to move fastest, while a poor country using fossil fuel to supply electricity for the first time is in a different position. Insisting otherwise <strong class=\"key-phrase\">guarantees that no agreement is signed at all</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, immediate and coordinated action on fossil fuels is justified, because the cost of waiting grows and no country can solve this alone. Reductions have to be shared according to wealth and past emissions, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an unfair agreement will not be kept</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"rises faster than the cost of acting now","context":"The damage is already visible in heatwaves, failed harvests and flooding, and the cost of acting later rises faster than the cost of acting now."},{"en":"give companies the certainty they need to invest","context":"Agreements also give companies the certainty they need to invest in something other than oil."},{"en":"guarantees that no agreement is signed at all","context":"Insisting otherwise guarantees that no agreement is signed at all."},{"en":"an unfair agreement will not be kept","context":"Reductions have to be shared according to wealth and past emissions, since an unfair agreement will not be kept."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#191","summary":"Whether the world must act immediately to cut fossil fuel use"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2355","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"The shift to wind and solar power","prompt":"While fossil fuels are still the main source of energy for most countries, others have begun to rely on renewable options such as wind and solar power. Is this a positive or negative trend?","answer":"Wind and solar installations have grown from a curiosity to the cheapest new source of electricity in barely two decades. I regard the trend as clearly positive, although the transition brings difficulties that deserve honest treatment.\n\nThe environmental gain is the obvious one. Generating power without burning anything removes both the carbon dioxide driving climate change and the particles that cause respiratory disease in cities, and the health benefit alone justifies the change in many countries. There is an economic argument as well: sunlight and wind are free once the equipment is built, so electricity costs stop rising and falling with the price of imported fuel. That also matters politically, since a country generating its own power <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot be pressured by whoever supplies its gas</strong>. Employment follows the investment, and renewable projects now support more workers than coal in several economies.\n\nThe problems are practical rather than fundamental. Wind and sunshine vary, so a grid depending on them needs storage and connections across regions, and building both takes time and money. Manufacturing turbines and batteries requires mining, which carries its own environmental and human cost, and the panels will eventually need recycling. Communities built around coal also lose their industry, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a transition that abandons those towns</strong> deservedly loses public support.\n\nNone of this outweighs the alternative. Fossil fuels carry far larger costs that are simply paid elsewhere, in hospital admissions and in damage from floods and heat, and their price is set by markets no importing country controls.\n\nIn conclusion, moving to wind and solar power lowers emissions, stabilises energy prices and reduces dependence on imports. It is a positive trend provided governments invest in storage and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">plan properly for the workers it displaces</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"cannot be pressured by whoever supplies its gas","context":"That also matters politically, since a country generating its own power cannot be pressured by whoever supplies its gas."},{"en":"a transition that abandons those towns","context":"Communities built around coal also lose their industry, and a transition that abandons those towns deservedly loses public support."},{"en":"plan properly for the workers it displaces","context":"In conclusion, moving to wind and solar power lowers emissions, stabilises energy prices and reduces dependence on imports. It is a positive trend provided governments invest in storage and plan properly for the workers it displaces."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#192","summary":"Whether the move from fossil fuels to renewable energy is a positive trend"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2358","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"A global body for the environment?","prompt":"Some people believe that tackling environmental problems should be managed by a global organisation instead of by national governments individually. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Pollution and climate change ignore borders, which makes managing them through separate national policies look inefficient. I partly agree that a global body is needed, but it should coordinate and enforce rather than replace national governments.\n\nThe argument for central management is strong where the problem is genuinely shared. Emissions released anywhere affect everyone, so a country acting alone bears the full cost and receives a fraction of the benefit, which is why so many governments promise action and deliver little. A global authority could set binding limits, prevent industries from relocating to wherever rules are weakest, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stop each country waiting for the others to move first</strong>. Oceans and the atmosphere belong to nobody in particular, so only an international body can defend them, and problems such as overfishing show what happens when nothing does.\n\nThe objection is that such an organisation would have no way of acting. Environmental measures require changing taxes, planning law, farming and energy supply, all of which depend on domestic institutions that no international body possesses. Countries also differ enormously in what they can afford and in what caused the damage, so uniform rules would be unjust as well as unworkable. Any authority strong enough to overrule governments would also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lack the accountability that elections provide</strong>, and states would simply refuse to join.\n\nThe workable arrangement is division of labour. An international body should set targets, verify emissions independently, distribute finance to poorer countries and impose consequences for breaking agreements, while national governments choose how to meet the targets in their own conditions. That is roughly what existing agreements attempt, and their weakness is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the absence of any penalty for failure</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, environmental problems are international and require an international institution with real enforcement powers. Delivery must remain national, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">only governments can change what happens on the ground</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"stop each country waiting for the others to move first","context":"A global authority could set binding limits, prevent industries from relocating to wherever rules are weakest, and stop each country waiting for the others to move first."},{"en":"lack the accountability that elections provide","context":"Any authority strong enough to overrule governments would also lack the accountability that elections provide, and states would simply refuse to join."},{"en":"the absence of any penalty for failure","context":"That is roughly what existing agreements attempt, and their weakness is the absence of any penalty for failure."},{"en":"only governments can change what happens on the ground","context":"In conclusion, environmental problems are international and require an international institution with real enforcement powers. Delivery must remain national, because only governments can change what happens on the ground."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#193","summary":"Whether one international organisation should handle environmental problems"},{"id":"writing-task-2-030","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Equality","title":"Laws requiring equal numbers of men and women","prompt":"‘We should introduce laws to make businesses and state services employ equal numbers of male and female workers in every department or area of the company.’ How far do you support this idea? Give reasons for your answer, and support your essay with ideas and examples from your own experience. You should write at least 250 words.","answer":"The issue of equality for men and women at work is still widely debated, despite the extensive legislation that now exists in many countries. Personally, I do not feel that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">imposing a numerical target</strong> for gender is a sensible idea, and I will explain why.\n\nFirstly, we have to consider whether equal numbers of men and women really wish to do every job which is available. For example, few women wish to be airline pilots, and only a minority of school teachers are male. If we impose a law on airlines or schools, where will they find the people to fill these posts willingly?\n\nThere is also the huge issue of the existing workforce. Would it be right to dismiss a worker because he or she is of a certain gender, in order to create a statistically perfect balance? The effects would be disastrous for many hard-working people.\n\nFinally, I believe we should find ways to encourage equality of opportunity through support and guidance, rather than top-down legislation. For instance, improved child care facilities and subsidies would probably encourage more women into parts of the workforce which may currently be difficult to enter. I do understand the opposing view, which is that legislation is the quickest and most effective way to achieve gender equality.\n\nNevertheless, I do not believe that a fairer workplace can be created by a change imposed overnight, at the cost of the jobs and prospects of people who are already doing the work. Overall, equality of opportunity is a goal worth pursuing, but it is reached by removing the obstacles that keep women out of certain careers, not by fixing the numbers in advance.","phrases":[{"en":"imposing a numerical target","context":"The issue of equality for men and women at work is still widely debated, despite the extensive legislation that now exists in many countries. Personally, I do not feel that imposing a numerical target for gender is a sensible idea, and I will explain why."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#194","summary":"‘We should introduce laws to make businesses and state services employ…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-096","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Equality","title":"Should governments cap the highest salaries?","prompt":"In many countries, a small number of people earn extremely high salaries. Some people believe that this is good for the country, but others think that governments should not allow salaries above a certain level. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People have different views about whether governments should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">introduce a maximum wage</strong>. While in some ways it may seem reasonable to allow people to earn as much as companies are willing to pay, I personally believe that employee remuneration should be capped at a certain level.\n\nThere are various reasons why it might be considered beneficial to allow people to be paid extremely high salaries. If companies offer excellent pay packages, they can attract the most talented people in their fields to work for them. For example, technology companies like Google are able to employ the best programmers because of the huge sums that they are willing to pay. These well-paid employees are also likely to be highly motivated to work hard and therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drive their businesses successfully</strong>. In theory, this should result in a thriving economy and increased tax revenues, which means that paying high salaries benefits everyone.\n\nHowever, I agree with those who argue that there should be a maximum wage. By introducing a limit on earnings, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the pay-gap between bosses and employees</strong> can be reduced. Currently, the difference between normal and top salaries is huge, and this can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">demotivate workers who feel that the situation is unfair</strong>. With lower executive salaries, it might become feasible to introduce higher minimum wages, and everybody would be better off. One possible consequence of greater equality could be that poverty and crime rates fall because the general population will experience an improved standard of living.\n\nIn conclusion, it seems to me that it would be better, on balance, for governments to set a limit on the wages of the highest earners in society.","phrases":[{"en":"introduce a maximum wage","context":"People have different views about whether governments should introduce a maximum wage."},{"en":"drive their businesses successfully","context":"These well-paid employees are also likely to be highly motivated to work hard and therefore drive their businesses successfully."},{"en":"the pay-gap between bosses and employees","context":"By introducing a limit on earnings, the pay-gap between bosses and employees can be reduced."},{"en":"demotivate workers who feel that the situation is unfair","context":"Currently, the difference between normal and top salaries is huge, and this can demotivate workers who feel that the situation is unfair."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#195","summary":"Capping extremely high salaries"},{"id":"writing-task-2-097","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Equality","title":"Equality and personal achievement","prompt":"In recent years, there has been growing interest in the relationship between equality and personal achievement. Some people believe that individuals can achieve more in egalitarian societies. Others believe that high levels of personal achievement are possible only if individuals are free to succeed or fail according to their individual merits. What is your view of the relationship between equality and personal success?","answer":"In my opinion, an egalitarian society is one in which everyone has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the same rights and the same opportunities</strong>. I completely agree that people can achieve more in this kind of society.\n\nEducation is an important factor with regard to personal success in life. I believe that all children should have access to free schooling, and higher education should be either free or affordable for all those who choose to pursue a university degree. Consider two students of equal ability: one whose parents can pay for tutoring, textbooks and a debt-free degree, and another whose family cannot afford any of this. In a society without free schooling or affordable higher education, it is the first student, not the more talented one, who would be better prepared for the job market. This kind of inequality would ensure the success of some but harm the prospects of others, for reasons that have nothing to do with ability.\n\nI would argue that equal rights and opportunities are not in conflict with people's freedom to succeed or fail. In other words, equality does not mean that people lose their motivation to succeed, or that they are not allowed to fail. On the contrary, I believe that most people would feel more motivated to work hard and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reach their potential</strong> if they thought that they lived in a fair society. Those who did not make the same effort would know that they had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wasted their opportunity</strong>. Inequality, on the other hand, would be more likely to demotivate people because they would know that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the odds of success were stacked in favour of those from privileged backgrounds</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, it seems to me that there is a positive relationship between equality and personal success.","phrases":[{"en":"the same rights and the same opportunities","context":"In my opinion, an egalitarian society is one in which everyone has the same rights and the same opportunities. I completely agree that people can achieve more in this kind of society."},{"en":"reach their potential","context":"On the contrary, I believe that most people would feel more motivated to work hard and reach their potential if they thought that they lived in a fair society."},{"en":"wasted their opportunity","context":"Those who did not make the same effort would know that they had wasted their opportunity."},{"en":"the odds of success were stacked in favour of those from privileged backgrounds","context":"Inequality, on the other hand, would be more likely to demotivate people because they would know that the odds of success were stacked in favour of those from privileged backgrounds."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#196","summary":"Equality and personal achievement"},{"id":"writing-task-2-159","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Equality","title":"Would women leaders make the world more peaceful?","prompt":"Throughout the history, male leaders often made the society more violent and conflicting. If women governed the world, the world would be more peaceful. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"The claim that female leaders would automatically make the world more peaceful is attractive but too simple. Gender may influence personal experience and priorities, yet <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gender alone is a poor predictor of political behaviour</strong>. Decisions about war and peace are shaped more strongly by institutions, national interests and the circumstances a leader faces.\n\nSupporters of the claim argue that women are generally more cooperative and less aggressive than men. They may also bring greater attention to welfare, family security and the human cost of conflict. Broader representation can improve debate because a government that includes people with different experiences is less likely to rely on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a narrow set of assumptions</strong>. For this reason, increasing the number of women in leadership is valuable in its own right.\n\nHowever, a head of government does not act solely according to personality. Leaders operate under <strong class=\"key-phrase\">institutional constraints</strong>, military advice, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">electoral incentives</strong> and international pressure. A female leader may still authorise decisive action when national security is threatened, just as a male leader may choose negotiation and restraint. History contains examples of both patterns. It is therefore unsafe to treat <strong class=\"key-phrase\">peacefulness as a fixed female characteristic</strong> or violence as a fixed male one.\n\nThe better way to reduce conflict is to strengthen accountable institutions. Transparent decision-making, effective diplomacy, independent media and legislative oversight make it harder for any leader to start an unnecessary war. Diverse leadership can support these safeguards, but it cannot replace them.\n\nIn conclusion, the world would benefit from more women in positions of power because representation should be fair and decision-making should include wider perspectives. Nevertheless, peace depends primarily on political systems and incentives, not on the sex of the individual leader.","phrases":[{"en":"gender alone is a poor predictor of political behaviour","context":"Gender may influence personal experience and priorities, yet gender alone is a poor predictor of political behaviour."},{"en":"institutional constraints","context":"Leaders operate under institutional constraints, military advice, electoral incentives and international pressure."},{"en":"electoral incentives","context":"Leaders operate under institutional constraints, military advice, electoral incentives and international pressure."},{"en":"a narrow set of assumptions","context":"Broader representation can improve debate because a government that includes people with different experiences is less likely to rely on a narrow set of assumptions."},{"en":"peacefulness as a fixed female characteristic","context":"It is therefore unsafe to treat peacefulness as a fixed female characteristic or violence as a fixed male one."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#197","summary":"Throughout the history, male leaders often made the society more violent…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-163","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Equality","title":"Women in the army","prompt":"In many countries, women join the army as men do. However, some people argue that the army solely needs males. What is your opinion?","answer":"In the 21st century, women have entered nearly every walk of life. However, the army is still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a male-dominated area</strong> in much of the world. A great many people hold biases against women's enrolment in the army. In my view, women can contribute to the army just as men do, even though they face obstacles that men do not.\n\nThe first argument for recruiting women is that they now have far greater access to education. In comparison with the past, when recruitment concentrated on strength and fitness, armies today select heavily on technical and academic ability. Hand-to-hand fighting <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accounts for only a small part of modern warfare</strong>, which is dominated by tanks, armoured vehicles, missiles and aircraft, most of them computerised. In other words, women can perform military tasks as their male counterparts do, provided that they are well-educated and well-trained.\n\nSecondly, women bring qualities that modern military organisations actually need. Much of service life consists of intelligence analysis, logistics, medical support and communications, roles that reward patience and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meticulous attention to detail</strong> rather than physical force. Mixed units are also more effective in conflicts fought among civilians: in Afghanistan, armies deployed female engagement teams because male soldiers could not speak to local women without causing serious offence.\n\nThese strengths do not remove the obstacles that women in uniform still face. The first is social attitudes. Many women have to cope with pressure from their families and friends, because soldiering is still widely seen as a dangerous occupation that is suitable only for men. The second is discrimination and harassment, which remains common in most armies and discourages capable recruits from staying long enough to reach senior rank.\n\nIn conclusion, the presence of women in the army should be accepted and encouraged. Modern soldiering rewards the education and precision that women bring to it, and the prejudice they still meet is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an argument for reforming military culture</strong>, not for excluding them.","phrases":[{"en":"a male-dominated area","context":"However, the army is still a male-dominated area in much of the world."},{"en":"accounts for only a small part of modern warfare","context":"Hand-to-hand fighting accounts for only a small part of modern warfare, which is dominated by tanks, armoured vehicles, missiles and aircraft, most of them computerised."},{"en":"meticulous attention to detail","context":"Much of service life consists of intelligence analysis, logistics, medical support and communications, roles that reward patience and meticulous attention to detail rather than physical force."},{"en":"an argument for reforming military culture","context":"Modern soldiering rewards the education and precision that women bring to it, and the prejudice they still meet is an argument for reforming military culture, not for excluding them."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#198","summary":"Women join the army as men do."},{"id":"writing-task-2-2370","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Equality","title":"Would a smaller income gap make us happier?","prompt":"Many believe that the best way to ensure a happier society is to reduce the difference in income between the rich and the poor. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Countries with similar levels of wealth report very different levels of satisfaction, and the distribution of income is one of the clearest things separating them. I largely agree that narrowing the gap raises happiness, though calling it the best single measure claims too much.\n\nThe evidence for the connection is substantial. Beyond the point where basic needs are met, additional income adds little to wellbeing, but relative position continues to matter, because people judge their lives by comparison with those around them. Wide gaps therefore produce dissatisfaction at every level except the top. More equal societies also record better health, lower crime and higher social trust, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">people are more willing to contribute where they feel treated fairly</strong>. Practically, money moved from the wealthy to the poor buys more happiness, as the same sum means far more to a household struggling with rent.\n\nThere are limits to the argument, however. Equality achieved by making everyone poorer helps nobody, and countries that suppressed differences entirely produced neither prosperity nor contentment. What people object to is usually unfairness rather than difference: a gap earned through effort is accepted, while one inherited or extracted is resented. Other factors also weigh heavily, since employment, health, safety and the quality of relationships shape happiness at least as much as the income distribution does.\n\nThe most effective approach is therefore indirect. Rather than redistributing income alone, governments raise satisfaction by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">providing services that everyone uses regardless of income</strong> — schools, health care, transport — which reduces the practical consequences of the gap while allowing differences in earnings to remain.\n\nIn conclusion, reducing the distance between rich and poor genuinely improves health, trust and satisfaction, so the belief is well founded. It works best when it comes from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fair opportunity and strong public services</strong> rather than from levelling incomes for its own sake.","phrases":[{"en":"people are more willing to contribute where they feel treated fairly","context":"More equal societies also record better health, lower crime and higher social trust, since people are more willing to contribute where they feel treated fairly."},{"en":"providing services that everyone uses regardless of income","context":"Rather than redistributing income alone, governments raise satisfaction by providing services that everyone uses regardless of income — schools, health care, transport — which reduces the practical consequences of the gap while allowing differences in earnings to remain."},{"en":"fair opportunity and strong public services","context":"It works best when it comes from fair opportunity and strong public services rather than from levelling incomes for its own sake."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#199","summary":"Whether reducing the gap between rich and poor is the route to a happier society"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Does fame bring more benefits than problems?","prompt":"Being a celebrity, such as a famous film star or sports personality, brings problems as well as benefits. Do you think that being a celebrity brings more benefits or more problems?","answer":"Celebrity brings wealth, influence and unusual opportunities, but it also removes much of a person's privacy. In my view, the benefits can be substantial, yet fame is only positive when the individual has strong support and realistic boundaries.\n\nA successful actor or athlete may earn a high income, travel widely and work on projects that would otherwise be impossible. Public recognition can also give celebrities a platform to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">support charities or raise awareness</strong> of important issues. In career terms, fame can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">open doors that talent alone may not</strong>.\n\nThe disadvantages are more personal. Famous people are photographed, judged and discussed by strangers, and ordinary mistakes can become public scandals. Their families may also face unwanted attention. Constant pressure to maintain an image can damage mental health, while it becomes difficult to know whether new relationships are genuine. High earnings do not automatically <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compensate for</strong> <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the loss of a private life</strong>.\n\nWhat makes the difference is whether the fame rests on the work or on the person. An actor or athlete is judged mainly by performance and can withdraw between films or seasons, so their private life remains largely their own. Someone who is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">famous for being famous</strong> must keep supplying personal material in order to stay visible, which is why reality television and social media have produced so many people who cannot step back without disappearing altogether.\n\nIn conclusion, being a celebrity offers exceptional rewards but also serious costs. I would not describe it as clearly beneficial or harmful. The experience depends on whether the person can control access to their life, manage public pressure and maintain relationships outside the entertainment or sports industry.","topic":"Famous People","phrases":[{"en":"support charities or raise awareness","context":"Public recognition can also give celebrities a platform to support charities or raise awareness of important issues."},{"en":"open doors that talent alone may not","context":"In career terms, fame can open doors that talent alone may not."},{"en":"compensate for","context":"High earnings do not automatically compensate for the loss of a private life."},{"en":"the loss of a private life","context":"High earnings do not automatically compensate for the loss of a private life."},{"en":"famous for being famous","context":"Someone who is famous for being famous must keep supplying personal material in order to stay visible, which is why reality television and social media have produced so many people who cannot step back without disappearing altogether."}],"subtype":"two-part","summary":"Whether being a celebrity brings more benefits than problems","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-123","_displayNo":"#200"},{"id":"writing-task-2-134","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Famous People","title":"Famous for glamour rather than achievement","prompt":"Nowadays celebrities are more famous for their glamour and wealth than for their achievements, and this sets a bad example to young people. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this statement?","answer":"It is true that some celebrities are known for their glamorous lifestyles rather than for the work they do. While I agree that these celebrities set a bad example for children, I believe that other famous people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">act as positive role models</strong>.\n\nOn the one hand, many people do achieve fame without really working for it. They may have inherited money from parents, married a famous or wealthy person, or they may have appeared in gossip magazines or on a reality TV programme. A good example would be Paris Hilton, who is rich and famous for the wrong reasons. She spends her time attending parties and nightclubs, and her behaviour promotes the idea that appearance, glamour and media profile are more important than hard work and good character. The message to young people is that success can be achieved easily, and that school work is not necessary.\n\nOn the other hand, there are at least as many celebrities whose accomplishments make them excellent role models for young people. Actors, musicians and sports stars become famous idols because they have worked hard and applied themselves to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">develop real skills and abilities</strong>. They demonstrate great effort, determination and ambition, which is required for someone who wants to be truly successful in their chosen field. An example is the actor and martial artist Jackie Chan, who has become world famous through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">years of practice and hard work</strong>. This kind of self-made celebrity can inspire children to develop their talents through application and perseverance.\n\nIn conclusion, it seems to me that the influence of celebrities on young people can be positive as well as negative.","phrases":[{"en":"act as positive role models","context":"It is true that some celebrities are known for their glamorous lifestyles rather than for the work they do. While I agree that these celebrities set a bad example for children, I believe that other famous people act as positive role models."},{"en":"develop real skills and abilities","context":"Actors, musicians and sports stars become famous idols because they have worked hard and applied themselves to develop real skills and abilities."},{"en":"years of practice and hard work","context":"An example is the actor and martial artist Jackie Chan, who has become world famous through years of practice and hard work."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#201","summary":"Celebrity influence on young people"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2207","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Famous People","title":"Celebrities and media intrusion","prompt":"In some countries, celebrities complain about the way the media publicizes their private lives. Some people say that they should accept it as part of their fame. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Public figures frequently complain that newspapers and websites report on their private lives, while others reply that this is simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the price of being famous</strong>. I disagree with the second view, because fame does not remove a person's basic right to privacy.\n\nThose who defend media intrusion argue that celebrities have chosen public life and benefit enormously from it. Actors and musicians rely on publicity to sell films and concert tickets, and many of them actively invite attention by discussing their relationships in interviews or posting details of their homes online. On this argument, it is inconsistent to welcome coverage when it is flattering and object to it when it is not.\n\nI find this reasoning unconvincing. Accepting a job in the public eye is not the same as consenting to be photographed inside one's own house or followed while taking children to school. A footballer is paid to play football and an actor to act; neither has agreed to surrender family life. The consequences are also serious, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">constant surveillance</strong> has contributed to depression and breakdown among performers, and the children of famous people have made no such choice at all.\n\nThere is, however, an important exception. Where a politician or a public official behaves dishonestly, and where that behaviour affects how they carry out their duties, reporting it is legitimate journalism rather than intrusion. The proper test is whether a story <strong class=\"key-phrase\">serves the public interest</strong>, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not whether the public happens to be interested</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that celebrities must simply accept media intrusion. Private life should be reported only where there is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a genuine public interest</strong> in doing so.","phrases":[{"en":"serves the public interest","context":"The proper test is whether a story serves the public interest, not whether the public happens to be interested."},{"en":"a genuine public interest","context":"In conclusion, I disagree that celebrities must simply accept media intrusion. Private life should be reported only where there is a genuine public interest in doing so."},{"en":"the price of being famous","context":"Public figures frequently complain that newspapers and websites report on their private lives, while others reply that this is simply the price of being famous. I disagree with the second view, because fame does not remove a person's basic right to privacy."},{"en":"constant surveillance","context":"The consequences are also serious, since constant surveillance has contributed to depression and breakdown among performers, and the children of famous people have made no such choice at all."},{"en":"not whether the public happens to be interested","context":"The proper test is whether a story serves the public interest, not whether the public happens to be interested."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#202","summary":"Celebrities and media intrusion"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2364","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Famous People","title":"Pop stars instead of national heroes","prompt":"Many young people today know more about international pop and film stars than about famous people in the history of their own country. Why is this? What can be done to increase young people's interest in their national history?","answer":"A teenager who can name a dozen foreign performers may struggle to identify the figures on their own currency. The reasons lie in how information now reaches them, and the remedy is to make history as vivid as the alternative rather than to make it compulsory.\n\nExposure is the main cause. Entertainment is delivered continuously through phones, and the industry producing it is global, well funded and designed to hold attention, so a young person encounters the same faces hundreds of times a week. Celebrities are also present, changing and easy to follow, while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a historical figure never posts anything new</strong>. English-language culture dominates the platforms most young people use, and that shifts attention outwards by default.\n\nTeaching plays a part too. History in many schools is delivered as dates and treaties to be memorised for an examination, which is the surest way to make an interesting subject dull. National figures are frequently presented as faultless statues, and adolescents distrust anything presented that way. Families have also stopped passing stories down as they once did, since grandparents live further away.\n\nSeveral measures would help. History taught as argument rather than fact — why a decision was made, what the alternatives were, who suffered — engages students who would never memorise a list. Local history works particularly well, because a child who learns what happened in their own street finds the subject <strong class=\"key-phrase\">attached to somewhere they already know</strong>. Museums, restored sites and school visits do more than any textbook. Beyond the classroom, well-made national drama and documentary reach young audiences on the platforms they actually use, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">competing for attention rather than demanding it</strong> is the only realistic approach.\n\nIn conclusion, foreign celebrities dominate because they are marketed constantly while history is taught as examination material. Livelier teaching, local focus and good national storytelling would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give the past a chance to be interesting</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"a historical figure never posts anything new","context":"Celebrities are also present, changing and easy to follow, while a historical figure never posts anything new."},{"en":"attached to somewhere they already know","context":"Local history works particularly well, because a child who learns what happened in their own street finds the subject attached to somewhere they already know."},{"en":"competing for attention rather than demanding it","context":"Beyond the classroom, well-made national drama and documentary reach young audiences on the platforms they actually use, and competing for attention rather than demanding it is the only realistic approach."},{"en":"give the past a chance to be interesting","context":"In conclusion, foreign celebrities dominate because they are marketed constantly while history is taught as examination material. Livelier teaching, local focus and good national storytelling would give the past a chance to be interesting."}],"subtype":"Problem / Solution","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#203","summary":"Why young people know foreign celebrities better than their own history"},{"id":"writing-task-2-048","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Traditional meals replaced by fast food","prompt":"In many countries, traditional home-cooked meals are being replaced by fast food. Why is this happening, and what effects does it have?","answer":"In many countries, meals that were once prepared at home are increasingly bought ready-made from restaurants and takeaways. This change has several practical causes, and in my view its effects are largely negative.\n\nThe principal cause is a shortage of time. In most households every adult now works, often with a long commute, and traditional cooking is slow: preparing a Vietnamese or Italian family meal from raw ingredients can take well over an hour, whereas fast food arrives in minutes. Cost reinforces this. Because international chains <strong class=\"key-phrase\">buy on an enormous scale</strong>, a burger is frequently cheaper than the fresh vegetables, meat and spices required to cook properly, which matters most to low-income families. Advertising is a further factor, since it has made these brands familiar and fashionable among young people.\n\nThe effects on health are the most serious. Fast food is typically <strong class=\"key-phrase\">high in salt, sugar and saturated fat and low in fibre</strong>, and rates of obesity, type 2 diabetes and heart disease have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">risen sharply</strong> in every country where it has become common. There is also a cultural cost. Traditional dishes are passed on by being cooked and shared, so a generation that never learns to prepare them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">will not pass them on</strong> either. The family meal itself is disappearing, and with it one of the few daily occasions when a household sits down together.\n\nIn conclusion, fast food has spread because it is quick, cheap and heavily promoted, and the consequences for public health and for culinary traditions are worrying.","phrases":[{"en":"risen sharply","context":"Fast food is typically high in salt, sugar and saturated fat and low in fibre, and rates of obesity, type 2 diabetes and heart disease have risen sharply in every country where it has become common."},{"en":"will not pass them on","context":"Traditional dishes are passed on by being cooked and shared, so a generation that never learns to prepare them will not pass them on either."},{"en":"buy on an enormous scale","context":"Because international chains buy on an enormous scale, a burger is frequently cheaper than the fresh vegetables, meat and spices required to cook properly, which matters most to low-income families."},{"en":"high in salt, sugar and saturated fat and low in fibre","context":"Fast food is typically high in salt, sugar and saturated fat and low in fibre, and rates of obesity, type 2 diabetes and heart disease have risen sharply in every country where it has become common."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Effects","_displayNo":"#204","summary":"Traditional meals replaced by fast food"},{"id":"writing-task-2-124","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"The hidden cost of cheap food","prompt":"Food can be produced much more cheaply today because of improved fertilisers and better machinery. However, some of the methods used to achieve this may be dangerous to human health and may have negative effects on local communities. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Modern farming has made food dramatically cheaper through chemical fertilisers, pesticides and mechanised production. I agree that these methods carry real risks, both to human health and to the communities in which the food is grown.\n\nThe health concerns are well documented. Pesticide residues remain on fruit and vegetables, and agricultural workers who handle these chemicals directly suffer measurably higher rates of certain cancers and respiratory illness. The routine use of antibiotics in intensive livestock farming is a further danger, since it accelerates the development of resistant bacteria that ordinary medicines can no longer treat. Cheap food is also, on average, less nutritious, because varieties are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">selected for yield and durability in transport</strong> rather than for taste or vitamin content, and much of it is heavily processed before it reaches the shops.\n\nThe effects on local communities are equally serious. Mechanisation means that a farm which once employed fifty people may now need five, and the villages that depended on that work have declined. Chemical fertilisers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">run off into rivers</strong>, damaging fisheries on which other families rely, and repeated intensive cropping <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exhausts the soil</strong>, leaving land <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less productive for the next generation</strong>.\n\nThis does not mean the methods should be abandoned, since they feed a population that traditional farming could not support. It means they need proper regulation: strict limits on chemicals, restrictions on antibiotics and support for farmers who protect their land.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that cheap food carries hidden costs in health and in rural livelihoods. These costs are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rarely reflected in the price</strong>, which is precisely why consumers have so little reason to demand better methods.","phrases":[{"en":"run off into rivers","context":"Chemical fertilisers run off into rivers, damaging fisheries on which other families rely, and repeated intensive cropping exhausts the soil, leaving land less productive for the next generation."},{"en":"exhausts the soil","context":"Chemical fertilisers run off into rivers, damaging fisheries on which other families rely, and repeated intensive cropping exhausts the soil, leaving land less productive for the next generation."},{"en":"rarely reflected in the price","context":"In conclusion, I agree that cheap food carries hidden costs in health and in rural livelihoods. These costs are rarely reflected in the price, which is precisely why consumers have so little reason to demand better methods."},{"en":"selected for yield and durability in transport","context":"Cheap food is also, on average, less nutritious, because varieties are selected for yield and durability in transport rather than for taste or vitamin content, and much of it is heavily processed before it reaches the shops."},{"en":"less productive for the next generation","context":"Chemical fertilisers run off into rivers, damaging fisheries on which other families rely, and repeated intensive cropping exhausts the soil, leaving land less productive for the next generation."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#205","summary":"The hidden cost of cheap food"},{"id":"writing-task-2-127","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Is killing animals for food cruel?","prompt":"Some people think that killing animals for food is cruel, while others claim that animals are a necessary part of the human diet. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Some people regard the killing of animals for food as fundamentally cruel, while others consider meat a necessary part of the human diet. In my view the objection to cruelty is justified, but it applies to how animals are raised and killed rather than to eating meat itself.\n\nThose who object have a serious case. Most meat now comes from intensive farms where animals spend their lives in confined spaces, unable to move naturally, and are transported long distances before slaughter. Animals of this kind <strong class=\"key-phrase\">demonstrably feel pain and fear</strong>, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">inflicting that on a large scale</strong> for the sake of a cheaper product is difficult to defend. Critics also point out that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the necessity argument is weak in wealthy countries</strong>, where <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a varied plant-based diet</strong> is readily available.\n\nThe opposing view rests on biology and culture. Humans have eaten meat throughout their evolution, and it remains the most reliable source of iron, vitamin B12 and complete protein, which matters particularly for children and for people in regions where alternatives are scarce. Livestock also supports the livelihoods of hundreds of millions of small farmers.\n\nMy own position is that killing an animal for food is not in itself wrong, but causing unnecessary suffering is. The practical conclusion is that intensive farming should be reformed rather than that meat should be abandoned.\n\nIn conclusion, I do not accept that eating meat is cruel by definition, but I do believe that the way most meat is currently produced <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot be morally justified</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"demonstrably feel pain and fear","context":"Animals of this kind demonstrably feel pain and fear, and inflicting that on a large scale for the sake of a cheaper product is difficult to defend."},{"en":"a varied plant-based diet","context":"Critics also point out that the necessity argument is weak in wealthy countries, where a varied plant-based diet is readily available."},{"en":"cannot be morally justified","context":"In conclusion, I do not accept that eating meat is cruel by definition, but I do believe that the way most meat is currently produced cannot be morally justified."},{"en":"the necessity argument is weak in wealthy countries","context":"Critics also point out that the necessity argument is weak in wealthy countries, where a varied plant-based diet is readily available."},{"en":"inflicting that on a large scale","context":"Animals of this kind demonstrably feel pain and fear, and inflicting that on a large scale for the sake of a cheaper product is difficult to defend."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#206","summary":"Is killing animals for food cruel?"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Is a vegetarian diet worth it?","prompt":"In some countries, it is becoming increasingly common for people to adopt a vegetarian diet. Do the advantages of this outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"A vegetarian diet can improve health and reduce environmental pressure, but it requires careful planning. In my view, the advantages generally outweigh the disadvantages for people who have access to a varied diet and reliable nutritional advice.\n\nPlant-based meals are often rich in fibre, fruit and vegetables, and they can contain less saturated fat than diets based heavily on processed meat. Reducing meat consumption may therefore lower the risk of certain chronic diseases. It also decreases demand for land, water and animal feed, which can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce the environmental cost of food production</strong> at a time when agriculture is a major contributor to greenhouse gas emissions.\n\nThe main disadvantage is the possibility of nutritional deficiency. People who remove all animal products need suitable sources of protein, iron, vitamin B12 and other nutrients. This can be difficult for children, pregnant women or low-income households if specialist foods are expensive, and cultural preferences or limited restaurant choices may also make the diet inconvenient. These risks are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">manageable rather than decisive</strong>: clear nutritional advice, fortified foods, and restaurants and schools offering affordable, clearly labelled meals can make balanced vegetarian eating <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical rather than restrictive</strong>. The strongest case for vegetarianism is therefore based on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a balanced diet rather than the simple removal of meat</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, vegetarianism offers important health and environmental benefits, but it should not be adopted carelessly. Public advice should focus on balanced meals rather than simply telling people to avoid meat. With sensible planning, a vegetarian diet can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">both healthy and sustainable</strong>.","topic":"Food & Diet","phrases":[{"en":"reduce the environmental cost of food production","context":"It also decreases demand for land, water and animal feed, which can reduce the environmental cost of food production at a time when agriculture is a major contributor to greenhouse gas emissions."},{"en":"a balanced diet rather than the simple removal of meat","context":"The strongest case for vegetarianism is therefore based on a balanced diet rather than the simple removal of meat."},{"en":"both healthy and sustainable","context":"With sensible planning, a vegetarian diet can be both healthy and sustainable."},{"en":"manageable rather than decisive","context":"These risks are manageable rather than decisive: clear nutritional advice, fortified foods, and restaurants and schools offering affordable, clearly labelled meals can make balanced vegetarian eating practical rather than restrictive."},{"en":"practical rather than restrictive","context":"These risks are manageable rather than decisive: clear nutritional advice, fortified foods, and restaurants and schools offering affordable, clearly labelled meals can make balanced vegetarian eating practical rather than restrictive."}],"subtype":"outweigh","summary":"Whether the advantages of a vegetarian diet outweigh the disadvantages","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-129","_displayNo":"#207"},{"id":"writing-task-2-181","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Flying fruit and vegetables around the world","prompt":"Aircraft are increasingly used to transport fruit and vegetables to countries where these products are unavailable or out of season. Do the advantages of this development outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"Flying fruit and vegetables across long distances gives consumers more choice and provides farmers with access to valuable export markets. However, routine air freight also creates high emissions and may encourage wasteful consumption. In my view, the advantages outweigh the disadvantages only for limited products and circumstances, not as a general food policy.\n\nThe main benefit is a more reliable food supply. Countries with short growing seasons can obtain fresh produce throughout the year, and unusual weather in one region <strong class=\"key-phrase\">does not have to cause an immediate shortage</strong>. Export income can also support farmers in countries whose climate allows them to grow high-value crops efficiently. For consumers, greater variety may improve diet, particularly where certain fruit or vegetables cannot be produced locally.\n\nThe environmental cost is substantial. Aircraft emit far more carbon per tonne of cargo than ships or trains, while refrigeration, packaging and rapid road delivery add further energy use. Air freight can also create <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the misleading impression that every product should be available in every season</strong>. This weakens demand for local crops and may increase food waste when delicate imports spoil before sale. These costs are difficult to justify for ordinary products that could be grown locally or transported more slowly.\n\nA sensible policy would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reserve air transport for urgent shortages</strong>, highly perishable goods or small quantities with clear nutritional or economic value. Retailers should publish transport information and favour sea or rail freight whenever possible. In conclusion, imported produce can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improve resilience and consumer choice</strong>, but unrestricted air freight has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an environmental cost that cannot be ignored</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"improve resilience and consumer choice","context":"In conclusion, imported produce can improve resilience and consumer choice, but unrestricted air freight has an environmental cost that cannot be ignored."},{"en":"an environmental cost that cannot be ignored","context":"In conclusion, imported produce can improve resilience and consumer choice, but unrestricted air freight has an environmental cost that cannot be ignored."},{"en":"does not have to cause an immediate shortage","context":"Countries with short growing seasons can obtain fresh produce throughout the year, and unusual weather in one region does not have to cause an immediate shortage."},{"en":"the misleading impression that every product should be available in every season","context":"Air freight can also create the misleading impression that every product should be available in every season."},{"en":"reserve air transport for urgent shortages","context":"A sensible policy would reserve air transport for urgent shortages, highly perishable goods or small quantities with clear nutritional or economic value."}],"subtype":"Discussion","_displayNo":"#208","summary":"Aircraft have been increasingly used to transport fruit and vegetables to…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2200","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Should everyone be a vegetarian?","prompt":"Some people think everyone should be a vegetarian, because we do not need to eat meat to have a healthy diet. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes claimed that people should give up meat altogether, on the grounds that a healthy diet does not require it. While I accept that most of us eat far more meat than we need, I disagree with the idea that everyone should become a vegetarian.\n\nThere are certainly good arguments for eating less meat. Livestock farming uses enormous quantities of land, water and grain, and produces a significant share of global greenhouse gas emissions. There is also strong medical evidence linking high consumption of red and processed meat with heart disease and certain cancers. A diet based mainly on vegetables, grains and pulses is cheaper, and it is perfectly possible to obtain enough protein from beans, nuts and dairy products.\n\nNevertheless, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a universal rule would be neither practical nor fair</strong>. Meat and fish remain the most reliable sources of iron and vitamin B12, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deficiencies are common</strong> among people who remove them without careful planning. This is a particular risk for children, pregnant women and families in poorer countries, where the range of alternative foods is limited. Food is also central to culture, and asking every community to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">abandon dishes that have been prepared for centuries</strong> would be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an unreasonable interference</strong> in private life.\n\nIn my opinion, the sensible response is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">moderation rather than prohibition</strong>. Governments should educate the public about the health and environmental costs of excessive meat consumption, and make plant-based options widely available, while leaving the final decision to the individual.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that we should all eat less meat, but I disagree that vegetarianism should be required of everyone.","phrases":[{"en":"deficiencies are common","context":"Meat and fish remain the most reliable sources of iron and vitamin B12, and deficiencies are common among people who remove them without careful planning."},{"en":"an unreasonable interference","context":"Food is also central to culture, and asking every community to abandon dishes that have been prepared for centuries would be an unreasonable interference in private life."},{"en":"moderation rather than prohibition","context":"In my opinion, the sensible response is moderation rather than prohibition."},{"en":"a universal rule would be neither practical nor fair","context":"Nevertheless, a universal rule would be neither practical nor fair."},{"en":"abandon dishes that have been prepared for centuries","context":"Food is also central to culture, and asking every community to abandon dishes that have been prepared for centuries would be an unreasonable interference in private life."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#209","summary":"Should everyone be a vegetarian?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2305","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Fast food replacing traditional meals","prompt":"In many countries, traditional foods are being replaced by international fast foods, and this is having a negative effect on families and societies. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Fast-food chains now appear in almost every country, and the meals they sell are displacing dishes that had been cooked for generations. I largely agree that the effect is negative, though the harm is more social than culinary and the causes deserve some sympathy.\n\nThe clearest damage is to health. Fast food is engineered to be cheap and appealing, which in practice means high in sugar, salt and fat and low in fibre, and countries that adopted it rapidly have seen obesity and diabetes follow within a decade. Traditional diets, whatever their origin, were built around vegetables, grains and small quantities of meat, and replacing them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changes what a whole population eats within a generation</strong>.\n\nThe family effect is subtler and possibly more important. A cooked meal requires shopping, preparation and a time when everybody sits down, and that shared hour is where much of the conversation between parents and children actually takes place. Food bought quickly and eaten separately in front of screens removes it. Cooking knowledge also passes from one generation to the next only by being used, so a household that stops cooking <strong class=\"key-phrase\">loses recipes that no book records</strong>. Socially, uniform food weakens the small distinctions that make places feel different, and money spent in international chains leaves the local economy rather than reaching nearby farmers and family restaurants.\n\nThe trend is not simple ignorance, however. Fast food is genuinely cheaper for a household counting every coin, and quick for parents working long hours, so blaming families for choosing it ignores what pushed them. The remedies are practical: teaching cooking in schools, restricting advertising to children, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">making fresh ingredients cheaper than processed meals</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the replacement of traditional food harms health, reduces family contact and drains money from local producers. The response should address the cost and time pressures behind it rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treating it as a failure of willpower</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"changes what a whole population eats within a generation","context":"Traditional diets, whatever their origin, were built around vegetables, grains and small quantities of meat, and replacing them changes what a whole population eats within a generation."},{"en":"loses recipes that no book records","context":"Cooking knowledge also passes from one generation to the next only by being used, so a household that stops cooking loses recipes that no book records."},{"en":"making fresh ingredients cheaper than processed meals","context":"The remedies are practical: teaching cooking in schools, restricting advertising to children, and making fresh ingredients cheaper than processed meals."},{"en":"treating it as a failure of willpower","context":"In conclusion, the replacement of traditional food harms health, reduces family contact and drains money from local producers. The response should address the cost and time pressures behind it rather than treating it as a failure of willpower."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#210","summary":"Whether the spread of international fast food harms families and societies"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2308","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Should countries grow all their own food?","prompt":"Countries should try to produce all the food for their population and import as little food as possible. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Recent shortages and rising shipping costs have revived the idea that every country should feed itself. I agree that nations should protect a core level of domestic production, but I disagree with the stronger claim that imports should be minimised.\n\nThe case for growing your own food is a case about risk. A country that buys most of its grain abroad is exposed to any war, export ban or bad harvest elsewhere, and food is the one commodity a government cannot allow to run short. Domestic farming also keeps rural communities alive, protects the knowledge and land needed to expand production in a crisis, and cuts the emissions of moving food across oceans. These are serious arguments, and no state should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depend entirely on somebody else's fields</strong>.\n\nTotal self-sufficiency, though, is either impossible or ruinous. Climate and soil decide what will grow: coffee, bananas and rice cannot be produced in northern Europe, and Gulf states have neither the water nor the land. Forcing production against nature means heated glasshouses, heavy irrigation and chemical inputs, so the environmental cost usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exceeds the cost of shipping</strong>. Diets would narrow, prices would rise, and the poorest households would feel it first.\n\nThere is also a hidden danger in relying on one country's harvest alone. A single drought or plant disease then affects everything at once, whereas buying from several regions <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spreads the risk across different climates</strong>. Trade is not only cheaper; it is often safer.\n\nIn conclusion, every country should retain enough farming to survive a disruption and to keep its rural economy working. Aiming to grow everything would raise prices, damage the environment and, by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">concentrating the risk at home</strong>, make the food supply less secure rather than more.","phrases":[{"en":"depend entirely on somebody else's fields","context":"These are serious arguments, and no state should depend entirely on somebody else's fields."},{"en":"exceeds the cost of shipping","context":"Forcing production against nature means heated glasshouses, heavy irrigation and chemical inputs, so the environmental cost usually exceeds the cost of shipping."},{"en":"spreads the risk across different climates","context":"A single drought or plant disease then affects everything at once, whereas buying from several regions spreads the risk across different climates."},{"en":"concentrating the risk at home","context":"Aiming to grow everything would raise prices, damage the environment and, by concentrating the risk at home, make the food supply less secure rather than more."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#211","summary":"Whether nations should aim for food self-sufficiency and minimal imports"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2329","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Why restaurants and shops throw food away","prompt":"In many countries, people throw away a lot of food from restaurants and shops. Why do people waste food in this way? What can be done to reduce the amount of food thrown away?","answer":"A large share of everything grown is discarded before anyone eats it, and much of that loss happens in kitchens and supermarkets rather than in homes. The causes are commercial rather than careless, and the remedies have to change the commercial logic.\n\nShops waste food because empty shelves cost more than wasted stock. A customer who finds no bread may shop elsewhere permanently, so managers order generously and discard the surplus, and that surplus is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cheaper than the risk of disappointing a buyer</strong>. Cosmetic standards add to it: fruit of the wrong shape is rejected before it reaches a shelf. Date labels create more waste still, since \"best before\" describes quality rather than safety, yet perfectly good food is thrown out on the day it is reached.\n\nRestaurants waste for related reasons. Menus must be available in full until closing, portions have grown far beyond what most people finish, and buffets guarantee that whatever remains is discarded. Kitchens also work at speed, so trimmings that a home cook would use become rubbish.\n\nSeveral measures work. France requires large supermarkets to donate unsold food rather than destroy it, and similar laws elsewhere have raised donations sharply. Clearer labelling, with safety dates separated from quality dates, prevents an enormous amount of unnecessary disposal. Shops can price food down through the day instead of discarding it at closing, sell irregular produce cheaply, and use ordering systems that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">predict demand instead of guessing at it</strong>. Restaurants can offer two portion sizes and make taking leftovers home normal rather than embarrassing.\n\nIn conclusion, commercial food waste arises because throwing food away is currently cheaper and safer for a business than running short. Donation laws, honest date labels and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prices that fall as the day ends</strong> would reverse that calculation and cut the losses substantially.","phrases":[{"en":"cheaper than the risk of disappointing a buyer","context":"A customer who finds no bread may shop elsewhere permanently, so managers order generously and discard the surplus, and that surplus is cheaper than the risk of disappointing a buyer."},{"en":"predict demand instead of guessing at it","context":"Shops can price food down through the day instead of discarding it at closing, sell irregular produce cheaply, and use ordering systems that predict demand instead of guessing at it."},{"en":"prices that fall as the day ends","context":"Donation laws, honest date labels and prices that fall as the day ends would reverse that calculation and cut the losses substantially."}],"subtype":"Problem / Solution","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#212","summary":"The causes of commercial food waste and how it can be reduced"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2333","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Buying imported food instead of local","prompt":"In many countries, more and more people choose to buy imported food rather than food produced locally. Why do people buy imported food? What could be done to encourage people to buy local food?","answer":"Supermarket shelves now carry fruit from three continents in the middle of winter, and shoppers reach for it readily. The reasons are mostly price and habit, and reversing the trend requires making local food easier to buy rather than lecturing people about buying it.\n\nCost is the first explanation. Large-scale farms abroad, cheaper labour and government subsidies mean imported apples or rice often undercut the same product grown nearby, and for a household on a tight budget that decides the matter. Availability is the second: imports allow strawberries in December and food that simply cannot be grown in the local climate. Quality and consistency play a part too, since importers grade produce carefully while local supply varies with the season. There is also a matter of status, because in many countries foreign brands <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carry an assumption of higher quality</strong> whether or not it is deserved.\n\nSeveral measures would change this. The most effective is price: supporting local growers, and taxing the environmental cost of long-distance transport, would narrow the gap that drives the decision. Distribution matters as much, because farmers often cannot meet supermarket contracts, so investment in cold storage, processing and cooperatives would let them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">supply the shelves rather than only the roadside</strong>.\n\nDemand can be built as well. Clear country-of-origin labelling lets people choose deliberately, and public institutions such as schools and hospitals can be required to buy locally, which creates guaranteed orders. Markets, cookery programmes and seasonal campaigns help by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">making local produce feel like the better option rather than the patriotic one</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, people buy imported food because it is cheaper, always available and often better presented. Encouraging local purchases means closing the price gap, strengthening the supply chain and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">labelling clearly enough for the choice to be real</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"carry an assumption of higher quality","context":"There is also a matter of status, because in many countries foreign brands carry an assumption of higher quality whether or not it is deserved."},{"en":"supply the shelves rather than only the roadside","context":"Distribution matters as much, because farmers often cannot meet supermarket contracts, so investment in cold storage, processing and cooperatives would let them supply the shelves rather than only the roadside."},{"en":"making local produce feel like the better option rather than the patriotic one","context":"Markets, cookery programmes and seasonal campaigns help by making local produce feel like the better option rather than the patriotic one."},{"en":"labelling clearly enough for the choice to be real","context":"In conclusion, people buy imported food because it is cheaper, always available and often better presented. Encouraging local purchases means closing the price gap, strengthening the supply chain and labelling clearly enough for the choice to be real."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Solutions","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#213","summary":"Why shoppers choose imported food and how local produce could be encouraged"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2340","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Who is responsible for what we eat?","prompt":"Some people think the government must ensure that citizens have a healthy diet, while others think this is an individual's own duty. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Obesity and diabetes now cost health systems more than most diseases, which raises the question of whose problem eating badly actually is. Both sides have a point, and my own view is that governments should shape the choices while leaving the choosing to individuals.\n\nThe argument for personal responsibility is a moral one. Adults are entitled to decide what they eat, and a state that dictates diet has crossed into an area where it does not belong. People also know the basic facts already, so the failure is one of will rather than knowledge, and taxes on food fall hardest on the poor while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaving the wealthy free to eat as they please</strong>.\n\nThe case for government action is more practical. Individual choice is heavily conditioned: processed food is cheap, filling and advertised constantly, while fresh food is expensive and takes time that people working two jobs do not have. Children cannot choose at all. Diet is also not a private matter once the consequences arrive, since the treatment of preventable illness is paid for by everyone. Governments already regulate food safety without controversy, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nobody argues that adults should judge bacteria for themselves</strong>.\n\nThe measures that work support this view. Cutting sugar in drinks through a levy reduced consumption without banning anything, and clearer labelling, limits on advertising to children and proper school meals all change behaviour at scale. These <strong class=\"key-phrase\">adjust the setting rather than the person</strong>, which is both more effective and less intrusive than instructing people what to eat.\n\nIn conclusion, individuals must make their own decisions, and no government should decide what appears on a plate. Governments are nonetheless responsible for the prices, labels and advertising that surround those decisions, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a free choice between bad options is not really free</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"leaving the wealthy free to eat as they please","context":"People also know the basic facts already, so the failure is one of will rather than knowledge, and taxes on food fall hardest on the poor while leaving the wealthy free to eat as they please."},{"en":"nobody argues that adults should judge bacteria for themselves","context":"Governments already regulate food safety without controversy, and nobody argues that adults should judge bacteria for themselves."},{"en":"adjust the setting rather than the person","context":"These adjust the setting rather than the person, which is both more effective and less intrusive than instructing people what to eat."},{"en":"a free choice between bad options is not really free","context":"Governments are nonetheless responsible for the prices, labels and advertising that surround those decisions, because a free choice between bad options is not really free."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#214","summary":"Whether diet is a government duty or a personal one"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2343","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Feeding a growing world","prompt":"Demand for food is increasing worldwide. What is the cause of this? What measures can the international community and governments take to meet this demand?","answer":"The world will need substantially more food within a generation, and the pressure is already visible in prices. The causes are population and prosperity together, and meeting the demand depends less on growing more than on losing less.\n\nPopulation is the obvious cause, since several billion more people will need feeding this century, most of them in regions that already struggle to produce enough. The less obvious cause is rising income. As households become wealthier they eat more meat and dairy, and livestock consumes many times more grain than it returns as food, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a richer world needs far more crops per person</strong>. Two further pressures make this harder: farmland is being lost to cities and erosion, and climate change is reducing yields in exactly the regions where demand is growing fastest.\n\nGovernments can act on supply first. Investment in irrigation, storage and rural roads raises output quickly in poorer countries, where much of the harvest is currently lost between the field and the market, and better seed and advice can close the gap between what smallholders grow and what the same land could produce. Research into drought-resistant crops deserves public funding rather than being left to a few companies.\n\nDemand can be managed as well. Reducing waste is the cheapest measure available, since roughly a third of food produced is never eaten, and shifting diets slightly away from meat frees enormous quantities of grain. Internationally, keeping trade open matters, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">export bans turn a local shortage into a global one</strong>, and emergency reserves prevent panic buying from driving prices beyond the reach of poor households.\n\nIn conclusion, demand is rising because there are more people and because they are eating richer diets. Investment in farming in developing regions, serious action on waste and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">open trade with proper reserves</strong> would meet it without clearing more land.","phrases":[{"en":"a richer world needs far more crops per person","context":"As households become wealthier they eat more meat and dairy, and livestock consumes many times more grain than it returns as food, so a richer world needs far more crops per person."},{"en":"export bans turn a local shortage into a global one","context":"Internationally, keeping trade open matters, because export bans turn a local shortage into a global one, and emergency reserves prevent panic buying from driving prices beyond the reach of poor households."},{"en":"open trade with proper reserves","context":"In conclusion, demand is rising because there are more people and because they are eating richer diets. Investment in farming in developing regions, serious action on waste and open trade with proper reserves would meet it without clearing more land."}],"subtype":"Problem / Solution","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#215","summary":"Why global food demand is rising and how governments can meet it"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2360","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Cheaper vegetables or costlier junk food?","prompt":"Some argue that subsidising fruit and vegetables is a good way for governments to make healthy food cheaper, while others maintain that taxing junk food would work better. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Diet-related illness now consumes a large share of health budgets, and price is one of the few levers a government can pull. Both approaches have evidence behind them, and my own view is that they work properly only together.\n\nSubsidy has the advantage of pushing rather than punishing. Fresh produce is expensive relative to processed food, particularly for households buying calories on a fixed budget, so lowering its price removes a real barrier rather than adding a burden. Schemes that give low-income families vouchers for fruit and vegetables have raised consumption measurably. Politically it is far easier to introduce, since nobody objects to cheaper food, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">helps the poorest households rather than charging them</strong>. It also supports growers.\n\nTaxation has stronger evidence behind it. Levies on sugary drinks have reduced sugar consumption in every country that has tried them, partly because buyers switch and partly because manufacturers reformulate products to avoid the charge, which changes what is on the shelf for everyone. Tax also raises revenue instead of costing it, and that money can fund the subsidy. The principle is familiar from tobacco: where a product imposes costs on the health service, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the price should carry some of that cost</strong>.\n\nThe objections are equally clear. Subsidy alone is expensive and does not stop anybody buying what they were buying already. Taxation alone falls hardest on the poor, who spend more of their income on food and have fewest alternatives, and it looks like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">punishing people for being unable to afford anything better</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, taxing unhealthy products changes both purchases and recipes, while subsidies make the healthy option genuinely affordable. Used separately each has a serious weakness, so the sensible policy is to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tax the harm and spend the revenue on the alternative</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"helps the poorest households rather than charging them","context":"Politically it is far easier to introduce, since nobody objects to cheaper food, and it helps the poorest households rather than charging them."},{"en":"the price should carry some of that cost","context":"The principle is familiar from tobacco: where a product imposes costs on the health service, the price should carry some of that cost."},{"en":"punishing people for being unable to afford anything better","context":"Taxation alone falls hardest on the poor, who spend more of their income on food and have fewest alternatives, and it looks like punishing people for being unable to afford anything better."},{"en":"tax the harm and spend the revenue on the alternative","context":"Used separately each has a serious weakness, so the sensible policy is to tax the harm and spend the revenue on the alternative."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#216","summary":"Whether subsidising healthy food or taxing unhealthy food works better"},{"id":"writing-task-2-114","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Global Issues","title":"Helping our own country or the whole world?","prompt":"We cannot help everyone in the world that needs help, so we should only be concerned with our own communities and countries. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this statement?","answer":"Some people believe that we should not help people in other countries as long as there are problems in our own society. I disagree with this view because I believe that we should try to help as many people as possible.\n\nOn the one hand, I accept that it is important to help our neighbours and fellow citizens. In most communities there are people who are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">impoverished or disadvantaged</strong> in some way. It is possible to find homeless people, for example, in even the wealthiest of cities, and for those who are concerned about this problem, there are usually opportunities to volunteer time or give money to support these people. In the UK, people can help in a variety of ways, from donating clothing to serving free food in a soup kitchen. As <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the problems are on our doorstep</strong>, and there are obvious ways to help, I can understand why some people feel that we should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prioritise local charity</strong>.\n\nAt the same time, I believe that we have an obligation to help those who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">live beyond our national borders</strong>. In some countries the problems that people face are much more serious than those in our own communities, and it is often even easier to help. For example, when children are dying from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">curable diseases</strong> in African countries, governments and individuals in richer countries can save lives simply by paying for vaccines that already exist. A small donation to an international charity might have a much greater impact than helping in our local area.\n\nIn conclusion, it is true that we cannot help everyone, but in my opinion national boundaries should not stop us from helping those who are in need.","phrases":[{"en":"impoverished or disadvantaged","context":"In most communities there are people who are impoverished or disadvantaged in some way."},{"en":"prioritise local charity","context":"As the problems are on our doorstep, and there are obvious ways to help, I can understand why some people feel that we should prioritise local charity."},{"en":"live beyond our national borders","context":"At the same time, I believe that we have an obligation to help those who live beyond our national borders."},{"en":"curable diseases","context":"For example, when children are dying from curable diseases in African countries, governments and individuals in richer countries can save lives simply by paying for vaccines that already exist."},{"en":"the problems are on our doorstep","context":"As the problems are on our doorstep, and there are obvious ways to help, I can understand why some people feel that we should prioritise local charity."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#217","summary":"Helping locally or helping people abroad"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2257","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Global Issues","title":"Do countries need each other more than ever?","prompt":"As we are facing more and more problems which affect the whole planet, good relationships between different countries are becoming more important than ever before. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"The largest problems now facing humanity do not respect borders, which has led to the claim that cooperation between states matters more than at any previous point in history. I agree with this, because the specific threats we face cannot be solved by any country acting alone.\n\nThe clearest case is the atmosphere. Emissions released in one country warm the whole planet, so a state that reduces its own output while others expand theirs achieves nothing except its own economic disadvantage. This is why agreements matter more than national policies: cooperation is not a moral preference here but the only arrangement in which any effort <strong class=\"key-phrase\">produces a result at all</strong>.\n\nDisease works the same way. A virus that appears in one city reaches every continent within weeks, and the speed of detection depends entirely on whether governments share information honestly and quickly. Countries that concealed early outbreaks damaged themselves as much as their neighbours, and vaccine development during the last pandemic depended on laboratories in several countries <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exchanging results within days</strong>.\n\nThe main counter-argument is that cooperation often fails when interests genuinely conflict, and that agreements are ignored the moment they become expensive. This is a fair description of how international politics frequently works, but it argues for building stronger institutions rather than for abandoning the attempt, since the alternative <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaves every country worse off</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that good relations between countries have never mattered more. Climate change, pandemics and the regulation of new technologies are problems whose causes and effects cross borders, and no government, however powerful, can protect its own citizens from them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">by acting within its own territory</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"produces a result at all","context":"This is why agreements matter more than national policies: cooperation is not a moral preference here but the only arrangement in which any effort produces a result at all."},{"en":"exchanging results within days","context":"Countries that concealed early outbreaks damaged themselves as much as their neighbours, and vaccine development during the last pandemic depended on laboratories in several countries exchanging results within days."},{"en":"leaves every country worse off","context":"This is a fair description of how international politics frequently works, but it argues for building stronger institutions rather than for abandoning the attempt, since the alternative leaves every country worse off."},{"en":"by acting within its own territory","context":"Climate change, pandemics and the regulation of new technologies are problems whose causes and effects cross borders, and no government, however powerful, can protect its own citizens from them by acting within its own territory."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#218","summary":"The growing importance of good relations between countries"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2268","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Global Issues","title":"What is international cooperation for?","prompt":"Some people think the main benefit of international cooperation is in the protection of the environment, while others think that the main benefits are in world business. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"Countries cooperate for many reasons, and there is disagreement over whether the greatest return comes from protecting the environment or from trade. Both produce real gains, but in my view the environmental case is the stronger one.\n\nThe commercial argument is easy to demonstrate. Agreements that lower tariffs and standardise regulations have allowed goods to move cheaply, which has raised living standards and lifted hundreds of millions out of poverty in Asia within a generation. Shared technical standards matter just as much: aviation, shipping and telecommunications function only because states agreed on common rules, and a phone bought in one country works in another because of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">decisions taken jointly decades ago</strong>.\n\nThe environmental argument rests on a different logic. Trade produces benefits that a country could partly achieve alone through domestic reform, whereas no country can protect its own atmosphere or oceans by itself. When states agreed to phase out the chemicals destroying the ozone layer, the hole began to close; no individual government could have produced that outcome, however determined. This is the defining feature of environmental cooperation: it is not one route to a benefit but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the only route there is</strong>.\n\nThe two are also connected rather than separate. Trade agreements now increasingly carry environmental conditions, and the flow of clean technology between countries depends on the same commercial arrangements that move everything else, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">progress in one supports the other</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, cooperation on business has raised incomes on a scale that should not be understated. Environmental cooperation matters more, because a failure to reach agreement there produces damage that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no amount of trade can compensate for</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"decisions taken jointly decades ago","context":"Shared technical standards matter just as much: aviation, shipping and telecommunications function only because states agreed on common rules, and a phone bought in one country works in another because of decisions taken jointly decades ago."},{"en":"the only route there is","context":"This is the defining feature of environmental cooperation: it is not one route to a benefit but the only route there is."},{"en":"progress in one supports the other","context":"Trade agreements now increasingly carry environmental conditions, and the flow of clean technology between countries depends on the same commercial arrangements that move everything else, so progress in one supports the other."},{"en":"no amount of trade can compensate for","context":"In conclusion, cooperation on business has raised incomes on a scale that should not be understated. Environmental cooperation matters more, because a failure to reach agreement there produces damage that no amount of trade can compensate for."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#219","summary":"The main benefits of international cooperation"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2361","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Global Issues","title":"Can any country solve these problems alone?","prompt":"Most of the urgent problems facing the world today can only be solved with international cooperation. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Climate change, pandemics and financial instability are frequently described as problems no government can handle alone. I largely agree, though the claim needs care, since cooperation is essential for some problems and irrelevant to others.\n\nThe strongest evidence comes from problems that cross borders by nature. A virus reaches every continent within weeks, so a country that vaccinates its own population while the disease circulates elsewhere buys only a delay. Emissions behave the same way, and a nation that eliminated its own would still face rising seas. Tax avoidance, organised crime and the regulation of powerful technology companies all fail for the same reason: firms simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">move to whichever country asks the fewest questions</strong>. In each case acting alone means bearing the cost while others enjoy the benefit, and that is precisely the situation only an agreement can fix. There is a further argument about capacity. Poorer countries often understand their problems perfectly well and lack the money and expertise to act, so international finance and shared research make the difference between a plan and a result.\n\nThe claim can be overstated, however. Many urgent problems are domestic: housing shortages, failing schools, corruption and inequality are created and solved within a country's own institutions, and blaming international conditions can become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a convenient excuse for doing nothing at home</strong>. Cooperation also has a poor record where interests genuinely conflict, since agreements without penalties are frequently signed and ignored.\n\nThe honest position is that cooperation is necessary but not sufficient. It works when countries see a shared benefit and when <strong class=\"key-phrase\">obligations are checked rather than promised</strong>, and it collapses when either is missing.\n\nIn conclusion, the largest threats of this century cross borders and cannot be solved by any government acting alone. Much of the practical work remains national, so the useful formula is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">international agreement with domestic delivery</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"move to whichever country asks the fewest questions","context":"Tax avoidance, organised crime and the regulation of powerful technology companies all fail for the same reason: firms simply move to whichever country asks the fewest questions."},{"en":"a convenient excuse for doing nothing at home","context":"Many urgent problems are domestic: housing shortages, failing schools, corruption and inequality are created and solved within a country's own institutions, and blaming international conditions can become a convenient excuse for doing nothing at home."},{"en":"obligations are checked rather than promised","context":"The honest position is that cooperation is necessary but not sufficient. It works when countries see a shared benefit and when obligations are checked rather than promised, and it collapses when either is missing."},{"en":"international agreement with domestic delivery","context":"In conclusion, the largest threats of this century cross borders and cannot be solved by any government acting alone. Much of the practical work remains national, so the useful formula is international agreement with domestic delivery."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#220","summary":"Whether the world's urgent problems require international cooperation"},{"id":"writing-task-2-073","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Government & Politics","title":"Public money for theatres and stadiums","prompt":"Some people argue that governments should spend money only on medical care and education, and not on theatres or sports stadiums. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that public funds should be restricted to hospitals and schools, and that theatres and sports stadiums should receive nothing. I disagree, because facilities of this kind deliver benefits that healthcare and education cannot provide on their own.\n\nThe strongest argument concerns public health. Obesity, diabetes and heart disease are now among the costliest problems that health services face, and regular exercise is the cheapest way of reducing them. Money spent on swimming pools, athletics tracks and sports halls therefore returns to the health budget <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the form of illnesses that never develop</strong>. Treating a patient after a heart attack costs far more than providing the facilities that might have prevented it, so this spending is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not an alternative to healthcare but part of it</strong>.\n\nCultural venues work in a similar way, though less directly. Theatres, concert halls and galleries give people somewhere to go and something to belong to, which matters particularly in small towns where young people otherwise have very little. They also attract visitors, and the money those visitors spend supports local businesses and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">returns to the state in taxation</strong>.\n\nI would accept that priorities matter. When hospitals are short of staff, a city does not need a spectacular new stadium, and such projects should be modest and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely open to the public</strong> rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">built for prestige</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, medical care and education must come first, but I disagree that sport and culture deserve no public money, since both reduce costs elsewhere and improve daily life.","phrases":[{"en":"in the form of illnesses that never develop","context":"Money spent on swimming pools, athletics tracks and sports halls therefore returns to the health budget in the form of illnesses that never develop."},{"en":"returns to the state in taxation","context":"They also attract visitors, and the money those visitors spend supports local businesses and returns to the state in taxation."},{"en":"genuinely open to the public","context":"I would accept that priorities matter. When hospitals are short of staff, a city does not need a spectacular new stadium, and such projects should be modest and genuinely open to the public rather than built for prestige."},{"en":"not an alternative to healthcare but part of it","context":"Treating a patient after a heart attack costs far more than providing the facilities that might have prevented it, so this spending is not an alternative to healthcare but part of it."},{"en":"built for prestige","context":"I would accept that priorities matter. When hospitals are short of staff, a city does not need a spectacular new stadium, and such projects should be modest and genuinely open to the public rather than built for prestige."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#221","summary":"Public money for theatres and stadiums"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Why young people do not vote","prompt":"In some countries, younger people are neglecting their right to vote. What problems does this cause and what are some of the possible solutions?","answer":"When young people do not vote, governments receive <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a less accurate picture of the population's priorities</strong>. This can produce policies that favour older voters and weaken confidence in democracy. The best solutions are practical civic education and easier access to voting.\n\nLow youth turnout creates a representation problem. Politicians naturally pay more attention to groups that vote regularly, so issues such as housing costs, entry-level jobs and education may receive less priority. Young adults may then feel ignored, which further reduces participation and creates <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a cycle of political disengagement</strong>.\n\nSeveral measures could improve the situation. Schools should teach how elections and public budgets affect everyday life, rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">presenting politics as a list of institutions</strong>. Registration should be simple, polling information should be written in plain language, and early or postal voting should be widely available so that work or study commitments are not a barrier. Parties also need to select younger candidates and discuss policies in language that is direct and relevant, rather than assuming that young voters are simply uninterested in politics. Youth councils, school mock elections and public debates can also show that political participation <strong class=\"key-phrase\">produces visible results in local communities</strong>, which helps make the case for taking part before people reach voting age.\n\nIn conclusion, neglecting the vote reduces the influence of young citizens and makes public policy less balanced. Governments cannot force genuine interest, but they can remove unnecessary barriers and help young people see that voting is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a practical way to protect their interests</strong>.","topic":"Government & Politics","phrases":[{"en":"a cycle of political disengagement","context":"Young adults may then feel ignored, which further reduces participation and creates a cycle of political disengagement."},{"en":"a practical way to protect their interests","context":"Governments cannot force genuine interest, but they can remove unnecessary barriers and help young people see that voting is a practical way to protect their interests."},{"en":"a less accurate picture of the population's priorities","context":"When young people do not vote, governments receive a less accurate picture of the population's priorities."},{"en":"presenting politics as a list of institutions","context":"Schools should teach how elections and public budgets affect everyday life, rather than presenting politics as a list of institutions."},{"en":"produces visible results in local communities","context":"Youth councils, school mock elections and public debates can also show that political participation produces visible results in local communities, which helps make the case for taking part before people reach voting age."}],"subtype":"problem","summary":"Why younger people are not using their right to vote","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-113","_displayNo":"#222"},{"id":"writing-task-2-115","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Government & Politics","title":"Is economic progress the most important goal?","prompt":"Many governments think that economic progress is their most important goal. Some people, however, think that other types of progress are equally important for a country. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People have different views about how governments should measure their countries’ progress. While economic progress is of course essential, I agree with those who believe that other measures of progress are just as important.\n\nThere are three key reasons why economic growth is seen as a fundamental goal for countries. Firstly, a healthy economy results in job creation, a high level of employment, and better salaries for all citizens. Secondly, economic progress ensures that more money is available for governments to spend on infrastructure and public services. For example, a government with higher revenues can invest in the country's transport network, its education system and its hospitals. Finally, a strong economy can help <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a country's standing on the global stage</strong>, in terms of its political influence and trading power.\n\nHowever, I would argue that various other forms of progress are just as significant as the economic factors mentioned above. In particular, we should consider the area of social justice, human rights, equality and democracy itself. For example, the treatment of minority groups is often seen as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a reflection of the moral standards</strong> and level of development of a society. Perhaps another key consideration when judging the progress of a modern country should be how well that country protects the natural environment, and whether it is moving towards <strong class=\"key-phrase\">environmental sustainability</strong>. Alternatively, the success of a nation could be measured by looking at the health, well-being and happiness of its residents.\n\nIn conclusion, the economy is obviously <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a key marker of a country's success</strong>, but social, environmental and health criteria are equally significant.","phrases":[{"en":"environmental sustainability","context":"Perhaps another key consideration when judging the progress of a modern country should be how well that country protects the natural environment, and whether it is moving towards environmental sustainability."},{"en":"a country's standing on the global stage","context":"Finally, a strong economy can help a country's standing on the global stage, in terms of its political influence and trading power."},{"en":"a reflection of the moral standards","context":"For example, the treatment of minority groups is often seen as a reflection of the moral standards and level of development of a society."},{"en":"a key marker of a country's success","context":"In conclusion, the economy is obviously a key marker of a country's success, but social, environmental and health criteria are equally significant."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#223","summary":"Economic growth versus broader progress"},{"id":"writing-task-2-116","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Government & Politics","title":"Is it better to save than to spend?","prompt":"It is better to save money than to spend it. How far do you agree with this statement? Is saving more important than spending in today's world? Give reasons for your answer, and provide ideas and examples from your own experience. You should write at least 250 words.","answer":"With the world in such economic turmoil, many of us face difficult choices in managing our money. Personally, I feel that saving is preferable to high spending, and I will explain why.\n\nThe clearest reason to save is that the future is unpredictable. Saving is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sensible precaution</strong> against unemployment, illness or a sudden repair bill, any of which can appear with almost no warning. A household with funds in reserve can absorb a shock like this without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">borrowing at a punishing rate of interest</strong>; one without any savings is often pushed onto a credit card, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a temporary setback turns into years of debt</strong>.\n\nThis habit of setting money aside also builds a discipline that spending does not. Someone who transfers a fixed share of each pay cheque, even ten per cent, into a separate account is forced to plan ahead rather than spend whatever is left at the end of the month. Treating that transfer as a fixed bill rather than an optional extra is usually enough, over several years, to turn an ordinary salary into a house deposit or a comfortable retirement fund.\n\nThe 2008 financial crisis illustrates what happens when this discipline disappears on a larger scale: banks, governments and households had all borrowed heavily against future income, and when that borrowing could no longer be rolled over, the resulting crash cost millions of people their jobs and homes. Some argue that life is short and money should be enjoyed now rather than hoarded, which is fair advice for everyday spending. Treating saving as optional, however, assumes that good times will continue indefinitely, an assumption the last two decades have repeatedly shown to be unwise.\n\nTo conclude, I believe that saving is the wiser course, not just for financial but also for personal and political reasons. Sensible saving is an invaluable skill and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a buffer against uncertainty</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"a sensible precaution","context":"Saving is a sensible precaution against unemployment, illness or a sudden repair bill, any of which can appear with almost no warning."},{"en":"borrowing at a punishing rate of interest","context":"A household with funds in reserve can absorb a shock like this without borrowing at a punishing rate of interest; one without any savings is often pushed onto a credit card, and a temporary setback turns into years of debt."},{"en":"a temporary setback turns into years of debt","context":"A household with funds in reserve can absorb a shock like this without borrowing at a punishing rate of interest; one without any savings is often pushed onto a credit card, and a temporary setback turns into years of debt."},{"en":"a buffer against uncertainty","context":"To conclude, I believe that saving is the wiser course, not just for financial but also for personal and political reasons. Sensible saving is an invaluable skill and a buffer against uncertainty."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#224","summary":"It is better to save money than to spend it."},{"id":"writing-task-2-117","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Government & Politics","title":"Is international aid a moral obligation?","prompt":"The advocates of international aid believe that countries have a moral obligation to help each other, while the opponents consider it unnecessary, because money is misspent by the governments that receive it. Discuss these two points of view and give your opinion.","answer":"Supporters of international aid argue that wealthy countries have a moral duty to assist poorer ones, while critics point to corruption and waste. Both concerns are valid. In my view, aid remains necessary, but it should be targeted, transparent and designed to build local capacity rather than permanent dependence.\n\nThe strongest case for aid arises during emergencies. After war, drought or an earthquake, outside funding can provide food, medicine, clean water and temporary shelter far more quickly than a damaged government can. Longer-term support can also fund vaccination, teacher training and basic infrastructure. These investments save lives and may prevent instability from spreading across borders.\n\nCritics are right that money can be diverted by officials, spent on unsuitable projects or tied to the donor's commercial interests. Large transfers made without local oversight may weaken accountability and encourage governments to rely on external finance. The answer, however, is not to abandon assistance. Donors should publish contracts, audit outcomes, work with reliable local organisations and release funds in stages. Wherever possible, aid should transfer skills and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strengthen institutions that can continue the work</strong>.\n\nAid can also affect a government's own incentives. When a large share of the budget arrives as foreign transfers, ministers face less pressure to build effective domestic tax collection, since public spending no longer depends entirely on what citizens are willing to pay. Aid that is tied to measurable progress on revenue collection, rather than simply replacing it, is more likely to leave a government financially self-reliant once the programme ends.\n\nIn conclusion, international aid is justified both morally and practically, but its quality matters more than the headline amount. Carefully monitored programmes that respond to local priorities can deliver lasting benefits instead of short-term publicity.","phrases":[{"en":"strengthen institutions that can continue the work","context":"Wherever possible, aid should transfer skills and strengthen institutions that can continue the work."}],"subtype":"Discussion","_displayNo":"#225","summary":"Advocates of international aid believe that countries have a moral…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-118","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Government & Politics","title":"Can only government solve housing shortages?","prompt":"Only government action can solve housing shortages in big cities. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes claimed that only government action can solve the housing shortages affecting large cities. I disagree, because although the state has an essential role, the problem cannot be solved without private construction as well.\n\nGovernments do have powers that nobody else possesses. They control planning permission, which is usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the real constraint on building</strong>, and they can release public land, invest in the transport that makes outer districts viable, and enforce safety standards. They are also the only body that will house the people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the market ignores</strong>: the disabled, the elderly and families on very low incomes will never be profitable to build for, and without public housing they simply go without.\n\nHowever, the scale of the shortage is beyond what any state budget can fund alone. Major cities need hundreds of thousands of new homes, and building them requires private capital, private construction firms and the speed that competition produces. Countries that relied entirely on state provision generally ended up with too few homes, built slowly and to a uniform design that suited nobody in particular. Restricting the market also tends to reduce supply further, since developers move their investment elsewhere.\n\nThe effective approach combines the two. Government sets the rules, provides land and builds for those the market will not serve, while developers build the remainder under conditions requiring <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a proportion of affordable units</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, government action is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">necessary but not sufficient</strong>. Solving housing shortages requires the state and the private sector to do <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the parts each does better</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"the market ignores","context":"They are also the only body that will house the people the market ignores: the disabled, the elderly and families on very low incomes will never be profitable to build for, and without public housing they simply go without."},{"en":"a proportion of affordable units","context":"The effective approach combines the two. Government sets the rules, provides land and builds for those the market will not serve, while developers build the remainder under conditions requiring a proportion of affordable units."},{"en":"the parts each does better","context":"In conclusion, government action is necessary but not sufficient. Solving housing shortages requires the state and the private sector to do the parts each does better."},{"en":"the real constraint on building","context":"They control planning permission, which is usually the real constraint on building, and they can release public land, invest in the transport that makes outer districts viable, and enforce safety standards."},{"en":"necessary but not sufficient","context":"In conclusion, government action is necessary but not sufficient. Solving housing shortages requires the state and the private sector to do the parts each does better."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#226","summary":"Can only government solve housing shortages?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-119","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Government & Politics","title":"Should people pay taxes?","prompt":"Some people believe that individuals should keep all the money they earn and should not pay taxes to the state. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Some people argue that individuals should keep everything they earn and pay nothing to the state. I strongly disagree with this view, because almost everything that allows a modern society to function depends on taxation.\n\nThe most obvious objection is that the services people rely on cannot be produced individually. Roads, police, courts, street lighting, clean water and national defence are used by everyone, and it is impossible to supply them only to those who choose to pay. Without compulsory taxation, each person would have an incentive to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">let others fund them</strong>, and the services would simply cease to exist. Even the business owner who resents paying tax depends on educated employees, functioning roads and a legal system that enforces contracts, none of which the private market would supply on its own.\n\nTaxation also protects people against risks they cannot predict. Serious illness, disability, unemployment and old age can affect anyone, and very few individuals could meet the cost of major surgery or decades of retirement from savings alone. A tax-funded system <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spreads that risk</strong> across the whole population, which is why countries without one see families <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ruined by a single accident</strong>.\n\nI would accept that taxes must be reasonable and spent efficiently, and that waste and corruption justify criticism. That, however, is an argument for better government rather than for abolishing taxation altogether.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree entirely with this proposal. Taxation is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the price of living in an organised society</strong>, and a country that abandoned it would quickly find that almost everyone, including the wealthy, was considerably worse off.","phrases":[{"en":"let others fund them","context":"Without compulsory taxation, each person would have an incentive to let others fund them, and the services would simply cease to exist."},{"en":"spreads that risk","context":"A tax-funded system spreads that risk across the whole population, which is why countries without one see families ruined by a single accident."},{"en":"the price of living in an organised society","context":"In conclusion, I disagree entirely with this proposal. Taxation is the price of living in an organised society, and a country that abandoned it would quickly find that almost everyone, including the wealthy, was considerably worse off."},{"en":"ruined by a single accident","context":"A tax-funded system spreads that risk across the whole population, which is why countries without one see families ruined by a single accident."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#227","summary":"Should people pay taxes?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2244","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Government & Politics","title":"Who should pay for schools and hospitals?","prompt":"Some people think that governments should pay for education and health care, while others believe that it is not their responsibility. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Whether the state should fund schools and hospitals is one of the oldest disagreements in politics. I believe governments should pay for both, although the argument for private provision alongside them is stronger than it is usually allowed to be.\n\nThe case for public funding is that these two services <strong class=\"key-phrase\">determine what a person can become</strong>. A child whose family cannot afford school fees loses opportunities permanently, and an untreated illness can end a working life, so leaving both to private purchase means that ability counts for less than the income a person happens to be born into. There is also an efficiency argument that is often overlooked: an educated, healthy population is what produces skilled workers and taxpayers, so the spending <strong class=\"key-phrase\">returns to the treasury over time</strong> rather than disappearing.\n\nThose who disagree point to cost and quality. Health spending rises faster than any government's income as populations age, and state monopolies face no competition, which can leave patients waiting months for treatment they would receive privately in a week. Taxing everyone to fund a service that many choose not to use is, on this view, difficult to justify.\n\nMy own position accepts the criticism without accepting the conclusion. Waiting lists are an argument for funding and managing public services properly, not for withdrawing them, and countries that rely on private provision spend more per person while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">covering fewer of their citizens</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, governments should fund education and health care, because both determine life chances and both repay the investment. Private alternatives can reasonably exist for those who want them, provided they operate <strong class=\"key-phrase\">alongside a public system rather than instead of one</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"returns to the treasury over time","context":"There is also an efficiency argument that is often overlooked: an educated, healthy population is what produces skilled workers and taxpayers, so the spending returns to the treasury over time rather than disappearing."},{"en":"covering fewer of their citizens","context":"Waiting lists are an argument for funding and managing public services properly, not for withdrawing them, and countries that rely on private provision spend more per person while covering fewer of their citizens."},{"en":"alongside a public system rather than instead of one","context":"Private alternatives can reasonably exist for those who want them, provided they operate alongside a public system rather than instead of one."},{"en":"determine what a person can become","context":"The case for public funding is that these two services determine what a person can become."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#228","summary":"Government responsibility for education and health care"},{"id":"writing-task-2-112","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Health","title":"The causes of stress and how to manage it","prompt":"Stress is increasingly common in modern life. What are the main causes of stress, and how can people cope with it?","answer":"Stress has become one of the most common health complaints in modern societies. It has several identifiable causes, and there are practical measures that individuals and employers can take to manage it.\n\nThe most frequent cause is work. Employees face heavier workloads, less job security and, since the arrival of smartphones, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an expectation of constant availability</strong>, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the working day never clearly ends</strong>. Financial pressure is closely related, as housing costs in most cities now consume a large share of income and many families have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">little margin for an unexpected bill</strong>. A third cause is social: people move away from the communities they grew up in, and those without close friends or relatives nearby have nobody to share difficulties with. Comparison on social media adds to this, since users measure their own lives against carefully edited versions of everyone else's.\n\nSeveral responses are effective. At the individual level, regular physical exercise is among the best-evidenced treatments for mild anxiety, and adequate sleep matters more than most people assume, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exhaustion makes ordinary problems feel unmanageable</strong>. Setting a fixed time to stop checking messages restores the boundary that technology removed.\n\nEmployers and governments carry greater responsibility, however, because the causes are largely structural. Realistic workloads, predictable hours, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a right to disconnect</strong> outside working time and accessible mental health services address the source rather than the symptoms.\n\nIn conclusion, stress arises mainly from work pressure, financial insecurity and social isolation, and lasting improvement depends on changing how work itself is organised.","phrases":[{"en":"the working day never clearly ends","context":"Employees face heavier workloads, less job security and, since the arrival of smartphones, an expectation of constant availability, so the working day never clearly ends."},{"en":"little margin for an unexpected bill","context":"Financial pressure is closely related, as housing costs in most cities now consume a large share of income and many families have little margin for an unexpected bill."},{"en":"a right to disconnect","context":"Realistic workloads, predictable hours, a right to disconnect outside working time and accessible mental health services address the source rather than the symptoms."},{"en":"an expectation of constant availability","context":"Employees face heavier workloads, less job security and, since the arrival of smartphones, an expectation of constant availability, so the working day never clearly ends."},{"en":"exhaustion makes ordinary problems feel unmanageable","context":"At the individual level, regular physical exercise is among the best-evidenced treatments for mild anxiety, and adequate sleep matters more than most people assume, since exhaustion makes ordinary problems feel unmanageable."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","_displayNo":"#229","summary":"The causes of stress and how to manage it"},{"id":"writing-task-2-121","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Health","title":"Health and the way we live now","prompt":"In many countries today, there are concerns about the unhealthy lifestyles that people lead. What health issues are linked to modern lifestyles? What answers to these problems can you suggest? Give reasons for your answer, and support your essay with ideas and examples from your own experience. You should write at least 250 words.","answer":"Most people agree that modern lifestyles can be unhealthy, both physically and mentally. I believe there are three main problems, but also some practical steps we can take. Perhaps the largest problem is the issue of obesity, which is growing drastically in most Western countries. With people working at desks and using transport rather than walking, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weight gain seems inevitable</strong>, and being overweight causes various medical issues including heart trouble and strokes.\n\nAnother major problem linked to lifestyle is poor diet, with people who are short of time eating junk food rather than proper nutrients, and resultant health problems range from obesity to high cholesterol and even organ failure. Finally, we must consider the issue of mental health. Modern lifestyles are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">highly pressurised</strong>, with people expected to work at a fast pace for extended periods, and little scope for family life or relaxation, leading to enormous stress and anxiety.\n\nTurning to possible solutions, the biggest step would be to improve the level of education regarding health issues connected to lifestyle, especially obesity. The government, media and schools should work together to raise awareness of the dangers and to promote sports and other positive practices. A further step would be to use legislation to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">curb the consumption of unhealthy foods</strong>, for example by imposing higher sales taxes or by restricting availability in retail outlets. To deal with the psychological impacts, we should also encourage employers to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">adopt more flexible working practices</strong>, allowing workers to lead a fuller private life with less stress.\n\nOverall, the key problems of obesity and stress can be addressed through a combination of awareness, targeted legislation and better employment practices.","phrases":[{"en":"weight gain seems inevitable","context":"With people working at desks and using transport rather than walking, weight gain seems inevitable, and being overweight causes various medical issues including heart trouble and strokes."},{"en":"highly pressurised","context":"Modern lifestyles are highly pressurised, with people expected to work at a fast pace for extended periods, and little scope for family life or relaxation, leading to enormous stress and anxiety."},{"en":"curb the consumption of unhealthy foods","context":"A further step would be to use legislation to curb the consumption of unhealthy foods, for example by imposing higher sales taxes or by restricting availability in retail outlets."},{"en":"adopt more flexible working practices","context":"To deal with the psychological impacts, we should also encourage employers to adopt more flexible working practices, allowing workers to lead a fuller private life with less stress."}],"subtype":"Problems / Solutions","_displayNo":"#230","summary":"Today, there are concerns about the unhealthy lifestyles that people lead."},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"More sports facilities, or other measures?","prompt":"Some people say that the best way to improve public health is by increasing the number of sports facilities. Others, however, say that this would have little effect on public health and that other measures are required. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Building more sports facilities may encourage some people to exercise, but it is not the best single way to improve public health. I agree with those who argue that broader measures are required because health is shaped by diet, education, income and the local environment.\n\nAdditional gyms, parks and swimming pools can make exercise more convenient. This is particularly useful in neighbourhoods where safe outdoor space is limited. However, facilities have little effect if they are expensive, badly located or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">used mainly by people who are already active</strong>. Access alone does not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">change deeply established habits</strong>.\n\nA more effective strategy would combine affordable exercise with prevention. Schools should teach practical nutrition, cities should provide safe walking and cycling routes, and governments should limit misleading marketing of unhealthy products to children. Basic healthcare should also identify problems such as high blood pressure before they become severe. These policies reach people who may never choose to join a sports centre.\n\nOne reason facilities receive so much funding is political rather than medical. A new swimming pool gives a minister something to open and photograph; a falling blood-pressure average across the population never gets one. Health budgets are too often decided by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">which project looks most impressive</strong>, not by which saves the most lives, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prevention usually loses that contest</strong> despite being the more cost-effective choice.\n\nIn conclusion, sports facilities are a useful part of public-health policy, but they cannot address the full range of causes behind poor health. The greatest improvement will come from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">making healthy choices easy and affordable</strong> in everyday life.","topic":"Health","phrases":[{"en":"change deeply established habits","context":"Access alone does not change deeply established habits."},{"en":"making healthy choices easy and affordable","context":"In conclusion, sports facilities are a useful part of public-health policy, but they cannot address the full range of causes behind poor health. The greatest improvement will come from making healthy choices easy and affordable in everyday life."},{"en":"which project looks most impressive","context":"Health budgets are too often decided by which project looks most impressive, not by which saves the most lives, and prevention usually loses that contest despite being the more cost-effective choice."},{"en":"used mainly by people who are already active","context":"However, facilities have little effect if they are expensive, badly located or used mainly by people who are already active."},{"en":"prevention usually loses that contest","context":"Health budgets are too often decided by which project looks most impressive, not by which saves the most lives, and prevention usually loses that contest despite being the more cost-effective choice."}],"subtype":"discuss","summary":"Sports facilities compared with other ways of improving public health","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-125","_displayNo":"#231"},{"id":"writing-task-2-130","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Health","title":"Why more children are overweight","prompt":"The percentage of overweight children in western society has increased by almost 20% in the last ten years. Discuss the causes and effects of this disturbing trend.","answer":"The sharp rise in childhood obesity is not the result of a single poor choice. It reflects changes in the food environment, family routines and children's daily activity, and its consequences extend well beyond appearance or short-term fitness.\n\nOne major cause is the widespread availability of inexpensive, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">energy-dense food</strong>. Highly processed meals and snacks are convenient for time-pressed households, while sophisticated marketing makes them especially attractive to children. Portion sizes have also increased, meaning that excessive calorie intake can become normal before a child understands nutrition. At the same time, many neighbourhoods and schools provide fewer opportunities for safe, unstructured physical activity. Screen-based entertainment has therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">displaced a substantial amount of active play</strong>, particularly where parents are reluctant to let children play outdoors unsupervised.\n\nThe immediate effects include reduced stamina, sleep problems and a higher likelihood of social stigma. More seriously, obesity during childhood can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">establish patterns that persist into adulthood</strong>. Children who become accustomed to sugary food and sedentary routines may find those habits difficult to reverse later. This increases the risk of type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease and joint problems at a younger age than previous generations experienced.\n\nThere are also psychological and social consequences. Overweight children may face bullying or exclusion, which can damage confidence and encourage further withdrawal from sport. Families and health systems then carry a growing financial burden as preventable conditions require long-term treatment. The trend therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">creates a cycle of physical and emotional disadvantage</strong> rather than an isolated medical issue.\n\nIn conclusion, childhood obesity is driven by unhealthy food environments and declining everyday activity. Its effects are cumulative, affecting health, self-esteem and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">public expenditure</strong>. Because the causes are structural as well as personal, the problem should be addressed through coordinated action by families, schools, food producers and governments.","phrases":[{"en":"displaced a substantial amount of active play","context":"Screen-based entertainment has therefore displaced a substantial amount of active play, particularly where parents are reluctant to let children play outdoors unsupervised."},{"en":"establish patterns that persist into adulthood","context":"More seriously, obesity during childhood can establish patterns that persist into adulthood."},{"en":"creates a cycle of physical and emotional disadvantage","context":"The trend therefore creates a cycle of physical and emotional disadvantage rather than an isolated medical issue."},{"en":"public expenditure","context":"Its effects are cumulative, affecting health, self-esteem and public expenditure."},{"en":"energy-dense food","context":"One major cause is the widespread availability of inexpensive, energy-dense food."}],"subtype":"Causes and effects","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#232","summary":"Why childhood obesity is increasing"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2219","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Health","title":"Preventing illness through policy","prompt":"Some people think that governments should focus on reducing environmental pollution and housing problems to help people to prevent illness and disease. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that the best way to improve public health is not to build more hospitals but to clean the air and fix people's homes. I largely agree with this, because the causes of much modern illness lie outside the health service altogether.\n\nThe evidence on air quality is difficult to dismiss. Fine particles from traffic and coal burning penetrate deep into the lungs, and cities with heavy pollution record markedly higher rates of asthma, heart disease and stroke. A government that lowers emissions therefore prevents thousands of admissions that would otherwise have to be treated, which is both cheaper and kinder than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treating the consequences afterwards</strong>.\n\nHousing works in the same way. Damp and cold homes are directly linked to chest infections and childhood asthma, while overcrowding spreads infectious disease and undermines the sleep and study that children need. Insulating an old housing estate is unglamorous work, but it removes a cause of illness permanently rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">managing it year after year</strong>.\n\nI would qualify my agreement in one respect. Prevention cannot replace treatment, because clean air does nothing for a person who has already developed cancer or been injured in an accident, and a government that diverted its entire health budget into housing would leave those patients with nowhere to go. The sensible position is that prevention deserves a far larger share of spending than it usually receives, not that it should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take the place of medical care</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that reducing pollution and improving housing should be central to health policy, since both remove causes of disease rather than symptoms. A country that acts on them will need fewer hospital beds in twenty years, but it will still need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the beds it has today</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"treating the consequences afterwards","context":"A government that lowers emissions therefore prevents thousands of admissions that would otherwise have to be treated, which is both cheaper and kinder than treating the consequences afterwards."},{"en":"managing it year after year","context":"Insulating an old housing estate is unglamorous work, but it removes a cause of illness permanently rather than managing it year after year."},{"en":"take the place of medical care","context":"The sensible position is that prevention deserves a far larger share of spending than it usually receives, not that it should take the place of medical care."},{"en":"the beds it has today","context":"A country that acts on them will need fewer hospital beds in twenty years, but it will still need the beds it has today."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#233","summary":"Tackling pollution and housing in order to prevent illness"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2273","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Health","title":"Should medical care be run for profit?","prompt":"Some people think that good health is very important to every person, so medical services should not be run by profit-making companies. Do the advantages of private health care outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"Health is unlike other goods, because somebody who cannot afford treatment is not simply going without a luxury. Private providers do bring speed and investment that state systems often lack, but the disadvantages of running medicine for profit outweigh those gains.\n\nThe advantages are real. Private hospitals compete for patients, so they have a strong reason to shorten waiting times, buy modern equipment and treat people courteously. They also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relieve pressure on the public system</strong>, since every patient who pays for a knee operation frees a state bed for somebody who cannot. Where taxes no longer cover rising costs, private money keeps hospitals building and researching rather than closing wards.\n\nThe central difficulty is that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">profit and health point in different directions</strong>. A company earns most from patients who are wealthy and only mildly ill, and least from the elderly, the chronically sick and the poor, who are precisely the people who need care most. The result is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treatment shaped by ability to pay</strong> rather than by medical need. It also encourages unnecessary procedures, because a clinic paid for each scan has an obvious reason to order one.\n\nThere is a further cost to the country as a whole. When wealthier families leave the public system, they stop caring how well it works, and the political pressure to fund it fades. Experienced doctors follow the money as well, so the hospitals that serve the majority lose their best staff. What forms is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a two-tier service that is very hard to reverse</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, private medicine delivers efficiency and additional funding, and no country should dismiss those benefits. They are outweighed, though, by a system that allocates care according to wealth and slowly weakens the public provision on which most citizens depend.","phrases":[{"en":"relieve pressure on the public system","context":"They also relieve pressure on the public system, since every patient who pays for a knee operation frees a state bed for somebody who cannot."},{"en":"profit and health point in different directions","context":"The central difficulty is that profit and health point in different directions."},{"en":"treatment shaped by ability to pay","context":"The result is treatment shaped by ability to pay rather than by medical need."},{"en":"a two-tier service that is very hard to reverse","context":"What forms is a two-tier service that is very hard to reverse."}],"subtype":"Advantages / Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#234","summary":"Whether the advantages of private health care outweigh the disadvantages"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2278","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Health","title":"Healthier in the countryside or the city?","prompt":"Many people believe that it is easier to have a healthy lifestyle in the countryside. Others believe that there are health benefits to living in cities. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"The countryside is usually pictured as clean air and fresh vegetables, and the city as noise, traffic and takeaway food. Both settings carry genuine advantages, and I believe the city has the stronger claim once medical care is taken into account.\n\nRural living clearly helps in some respects. The air is cleaner, which matters enormously for children with asthma and for anyone with a weak heart. Food is often grown nearby and eaten in season. Physical work and long walks build activity into the day rather than requiring a gym, and quieter surroundings and darker nights produce better sleep, which affects almost every measure of health.\n\nCities answer with different strengths. Hospitals, specialists and emergency services are concentrated there, and for a stroke or a difficult birth the distance to a surgeon decides the outcome. Urban residents also have more choice: a supermarket carries fresh food all year, and pools, parks and clubs make exercise easy to sustain. Walking to a station or a shop means many city dwellers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">move more than they realise</strong>, while rural life increasingly means driving everywhere. Loneliness, which harms health as much as smoking does, is easier to escape where people are close together.\n\nMy own view is that the countryside offers the better environment and the city the better safety net, and the safety net wins. Clean air cannot compensate for an ambulance that takes fifty minutes, and rural areas now suffer from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shortages of doctors and few services for the old</strong>. The healthiest arrangement is a city with parks, cycle routes and controlled traffic, since it keeps medical access while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">borrowing what the countryside does well</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, each setting protects health in its own way. Given the weight of medical care and social contact, city living is on balance the healthier option, provided that pollution and inactivity are actively managed.","phrases":[{"en":"move more than they realise","context":"Walking to a station or a shop means many city dwellers move more than they realise, while rural life increasingly means driving everywhere."},{"en":"shortages of doctors and few services for the old","context":"Clean air cannot compensate for an ambulance that takes fifty minutes, and rural areas now suffer from shortages of doctors and few services for the old."},{"en":"borrowing what the countryside does well","context":"The healthiest arrangement is a city with parks, cycle routes and controlled traffic, since it keeps medical access while borrowing what the countryside does well."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#235","summary":"Whether rural or urban living is better for health"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2304","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Health","title":"Prevention or treatment?","prompt":"It is more important to spend public money on promoting a healthy lifestyle in order to prevent illness than on treating people who are already ill. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Health budgets everywhere are growing faster than the economies that fund them, which makes the balance between prevention and treatment a practical question rather than a philosophical one. I largely agree that prevention deserves a greater share, though it cannot come at the expense of care for those already ill.\n\nThe argument for prevention is mainly arithmetic. A large proportion of the conditions filling hospitals — type 2 diabetes, heart disease, several cancers, lung disease — is driven by smoking, diet, inactivity and alcohol, and preventing a case costs a fraction of treating one for decades. Measures such as vaccination, clean water, tobacco control and sugar levies have produced the largest gains in life expectancy in history, far exceeding anything achieved by treatment. Prevention also delivers something treatment cannot, since a person who never develops a disease <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeps the years rather than surviving them</strong>.\n\nThe opposing consideration is that treatment cannot be reduced. People are ill now, and a government that redirected money from hospitals to campaigns would be abandoning them; illness also arrives through accident, infection and inheritance, none of which any lifestyle prevents. There is a further difficulty in that prevention pays off over decades while ministers are judged over years, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the savings appear long after the spending</strong>.\n\nThe strongest objection concerns what prevention means. Advice and campaigns achieve very little on their own and tend to reach the people already healthy, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">widens the gap between rich and poor rather than closing it</strong>. What works is structural: taxes, regulation of advertising, safe places to walk and cycle, decent housing and school meals.\n\nIn conclusion, prevention protects more lives per pound than treatment and should receive a larger and protected share of health spending. Treatment must be funded fully alongside it, and prevention must mean <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changing conditions rather than issuing advice</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"keeps the years rather than surviving them","context":"Prevention also delivers something treatment cannot, since a person who never develops a disease keeps the years rather than surviving them."},{"en":"the savings appear long after the spending","context":"There is a further difficulty in that prevention pays off over decades while ministers are judged over years, so the savings appear long after the spending."},{"en":"widens the gap between rich and poor rather than closing it","context":"Advice and campaigns achieve very little on their own and tend to reach the people already healthy, which widens the gap between rich and poor rather than closing it."},{"en":"changing conditions rather than issuing advice","context":"In conclusion, prevention protects more lives per pound than treatment and should receive a larger and protected share of health spending. Treatment must be funded fully alongside it, and prevention must mean changing conditions rather than issuing advice."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#236","summary":"Whether public money is better spent preventing illness than treating it"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2323","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Health","title":"The rise of cosmetic surgery","prompt":"An increasing number of people are choosing to have cosmetic surgery in order to improve their appearance. Why are more people choosing to change the way they look? Do you think this is a positive or negative trend?","answer":"Procedures that were once rare and expensive are now advertised beside dental work. Several forces explain the increase, and although the freedom involved is genuine, I regard the trend as largely negative.\n\nThe first reason is exposure. People used to compare themselves with those they met; now they compare themselves with edited images seen hundreds of times a day, and filters have made everyone familiar with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a version of their own face that does not exist</strong>. Video calls have added the strange experience of watching yourself while talking. The second reason is access: treatments have become cheaper, quicker and less invasive, and clinics offer payment by instalment, so surgery has moved from a decision to a purchase. The third is that appearance carries measurable rewards in hiring and dating, which makes the spending feel rational.\n\nSome outcomes are clearly positive. Reconstruction after burns, accidents or illness restores lives, and an adult who has been troubled by a single feature for decades may gain real confidence. Choosing what to do with your own body is also a reasonable freedom.\n\nThe wider effects are harder to defend. Surgery treats a feeling of inadequacy by changing the body, and the feeling frequently <strong class=\"key-phrase\">moves to the next feature instead of disappearing</strong>. Every procedure narrows the range of faces that look normal, which increases the pressure on everyone else, and the age of patients keeps falling. Regulation lags behind demand, so unqualified practitioners operate freely, and complications from cheap treatment abroad now occupy public hospitals.\n\nIn conclusion, cosmetic surgery has spread because ideal images are unavoidable and treatment is easy to buy. It restores some people and helps a few, but it mostly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">solves a problem created by the images themselves</strong>, and for that reason the trend is a negative one.","phrases":[{"en":"a version of their own face that does not exist","context":"People used to compare themselves with those they met; now they compare themselves with edited images seen hundreds of times a day, and filters have made everyone familiar with a version of their own face that does not exist."},{"en":"moves to the next feature instead of disappearing","context":"Surgery treats a feeling of inadequacy by changing the body, and the feeling frequently moves to the next feature instead of disappearing."},{"en":"solves a problem created by the images themselves","context":"It restores some people and helps a few, but it mostly solves a problem created by the images themselves, and for that reason the trend is a negative one."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#237","summary":"Why more people alter their appearance surgically and whether this is positive"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2324","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Health","title":"Looking up symptoms instead of seeing a doctor","prompt":"Nowadays, many people use the Internet to get medical advice instead of going to see a doctor. Why is this? Do you think it is a positive or negative development?","answer":"Most people now search their symptoms before deciding whether to book an appointment. The reasons are largely practical, and the development is positive if patients use the Internet to prepare for a consultation rather than to replace one.\n\nThe main cause is difficulty of access. Waiting weeks for an appointment, or paying for one, makes a free answer in ten seconds extremely attractive, and shift workers and parents of small children often cannot attend during opening hours at all. Embarrassment plays a part too, since people will type a question about a rash or a mental health symptom that they would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hesitate to say aloud to a stranger</strong>. There is also a rise in the wish to understand rather than simply obey, and patients arrive wanting to discuss options instead of receiving instructions.\n\nThe benefits are real. Reliable health sites explain conditions clearly and repeatedly, which no appointment allows, and a well-informed patient asks better questions and follows treatment more faithfully. Online guidance also prevents unnecessary visits for minor complaints and, in remote areas, is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the only medical information available at all</strong>. Support communities help people with rare conditions find others in the same position.\n\nThe dangers are equally clear. Search results are not sorted by accuracy, and commercial sites sell remedies while pretending to inform. Serious illness is missed when a symptom is explained away, and the opposite error is common too: ordinary tiredness read as something grave produces <strong class=\"key-phrase\">anxiety out of all proportion to the evidence</strong>. Nothing online can examine a patient, which is where diagnosis actually happens.\n\nIn conclusion, people search because care is slow, costly or awkward to obtain, and good information genuinely helps them. The development is positive only where the Internet <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prepares the patient rather than replaces the examination</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"hesitate to say aloud to a stranger","context":"Embarrassment plays a part too, since people will type a question about a rash or a mental health symptom that they would hesitate to say aloud to a stranger."},{"en":"the only medical information available at all","context":"Online guidance also prevents unnecessary visits for minor complaints and, in remote areas, is the only medical information available at all."},{"en":"anxiety out of all proportion to the evidence","context":"Serious illness is missed when a symptom is explained away, and the opposite error is common too: ordinary tiredness read as something grave produces anxiety out of all proportion to the evidence."},{"en":"prepares the patient rather than replaces the examination","context":"In conclusion, people search because care is slow, costly or awkward to obtain, and good information genuinely helps them. The development is positive only where the Internet prepares the patient rather than replaces the examination."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#238","summary":"Why people seek medical advice online and whether this is a good development"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2373","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Health","title":"Why older patients ignore exercise advice","prompt":"Many doctors recommend that older people should take regular exercise, but most patients do not follow this advice. What are the reasons for this? What can be done to solve this problem?","answer":"Doctors know that movement does more for an ageing body than most medication, yet the advice is rarely acted on. The reasons are practical and psychological rather than a lack of willingness, and the remedies have to remove the obstacles rather than repeat the instruction.\n\nFear is the first barrier. Older patients worry about falling, about pain and about making an existing condition worse, and told simply to exercise they have no idea what is safe, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">doing nothing feels like the cautious choice</strong>. Vague advice makes this worse: an instruction to be more active carries no starting point, no amount and no way of knowing whether it is working.\n\nCircumstances add to it. Many older people live with arthritis or breathlessness that makes the first weeks genuinely unpleasant, and the benefits arrive months later while the discomfort arrives immediately. Facilities are often unsuitable, since gyms are designed for the young and can be intimidating, and getting to one may be impossible without a car. Cost matters on a pension. There is also a belief, still common, that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">slowing down is simply what age requires</strong>, and those who exercise alone find little to sustain the habit.\n\nThe solutions follow directly. Doctors should prescribe activity as precisely as medicine, naming the type, the amount and the progression, and refer patients to supervised classes rather than leaving them to arrange it. Programmes designed for older bodies — chair exercises, walking groups, swimming — work far better than general facilities, and free or subsidised places in community centres remove the cost. Group classes matter most, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the company is what brings people back</strong>, and families and local councils can help by making pavements, benches and safe routes available.\n\nIn conclusion, older patients ignore the advice because it is vague, frightening and hard to act on. Specific prescriptions, supervised group classes and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">facilities built for their age rather than borrowed from the young</strong> would change that.","phrases":[{"en":"doing nothing feels like the cautious choice","context":"Older patients worry about falling, about pain and about making an existing condition worse, and told simply to exercise they have no idea what is safe, so doing nothing feels like the cautious choice."},{"en":"slowing down is simply what age requires","context":"There is also a belief, still common, that slowing down is simply what age requires, and those who exercise alone find little to sustain the habit."},{"en":"the company is what brings people back","context":"Group classes matter most, because the company is what brings people back, and families and local councils can help by making pavements, benches and safe routes available."},{"en":"facilities built for their age rather than borrowed from the young","context":"In conclusion, older patients ignore the advice because it is vague, frightening and hard to act on. Specific prescriptions, supervised group classes and facilities built for their age rather than borrowed from the young would change that."}],"subtype":"Problem / Solution","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#239","summary":"Why older people do not follow advice to exercise and what would help"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"A house or an apartment?","prompt":"Some people prefer to live in a house, while others feel that there are more advantages to living in an apartment. Are there more advantages than disadvantages of living in a house compared with living in an apartment?","answer":"Both houses and apartments can provide a comfortable home, but they suit different priorities. In my view, a house offers more space and privacy, while an apartment is usually more convenient and affordable. For many urban residents, the advantages of apartment living are greater.\n\nA house is attractive because it gives residents more control over their property. Families may have extra bedrooms, a garden and space for children or pets. Noise from neighbours is also less likely to be a problem. These benefits make a house especially suitable for larger families who value privacy and long-term stability.\n\nHowever, houses often cost more to buy, heat and maintain. They may also be located far from city centres, which increases commuting time. Apartments generally require less maintenance and are often close to jobs, shops and public transport. Modern buildings may also provide security, parking and shared facilities. For a single person or a small family, this can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce both cost and daily inconvenience</strong>.\n\nA further consideration is how long people expect to stay in one place. Young adults change jobs and cities frequently, and an apartment can be let or sold far more quickly, which makes it a sensible choice at the start of a career. A house, by contrast, is a commitment of ten or twenty years, and buyers who overestimate how settled they are may find that it is not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the most economical choice over the long term</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, houses provide space and independence, but apartments offer practical advantages that are particularly important in crowded cities. The better choice depends on family size and income, although apartment living is likely to be more suitable for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a larger proportion of</strong> urban residents because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">makes efficient use of limited space</strong>.","topic":"Housing","phrases":[{"en":"reduce both cost and daily inconvenience","context":"For a single person or a small family, this can reduce both cost and daily inconvenience."},{"en":"the most economical choice over the long term","context":"A house, by contrast, is a commitment of ten or twenty years, and buyers who overestimate how settled they are may find that it is not the most economical choice over the long term."},{"en":"a larger proportion of","context":"The better choice depends on family size and income, although apartment living is likely to be more suitable for a larger proportion of urban residents because it makes efficient use of limited space."},{"en":"makes efficient use of limited space","context":"The better choice depends on family size and income, although apartment living is likely to be more suitable for a larger proportion of urban residents because it makes efficient use of limited space."}],"subtype":"two-part","summary":"Living in a house compared with living in an apartment","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-014","_displayNo":"#240"},{"id":"writing-task-2-132","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Housing","title":"More people living alone","prompt":"In some countries, many more people are choosing to live alone nowadays than in the past. Do you think this is a positive or negative development?","answer":"In recent years it has become far more normal for people to live alone, particularly in large cities in the developed world. In my opinion, this trend brings genuine benefits at a personal and economic level, but it also carries a real social and financial cost.\n\nThe rise in one-person households can be seen as positive for both personal and economic reasons. On an individual level, people who live alone tend to become more independent and self-reliant than those who share a home with family, since there is no one else to cook, clean, pay the bills or manage the budget for them. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">From an economic perspective</strong>, the same trend creates <strong class=\"key-phrase\">greater demand for housing</strong>, which benefits the construction industry, estate agents and the many businesses that rely on people furnishing a home of their own.\n\nHowever, the personal and economic arguments above can be viewed from the opposite angle. Rather than feeling more independent, people who live alone may instead experience loneliness, isolation and worry, since they miss out on the emotional support and daily conversation that family or flatmates would normally provide. They must also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bear the weight of every household bill</strong> and decision without anyone to share it.\n\nThe same reversal applies to the economic argument: greater demand for housing tends to push up property prices and rents, so while the construction industry and estate agents may gain, the wider population, including people who live alone, ends up facing higher housing costs than before.\n\nIn conclusion, living alone offers genuine personal and economic benefits, but these come paired with a real emotional and financial cost. Whether the trend is ultimately positive probably depends on whether it is freely chosen: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">independence that people actively want</strong> looks very different from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an isolation they were simply unable to avoid</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"From an economic perspective","context":"From an economic perspective, the same trend creates greater demand for housing, which benefits the construction industry, estate agents and the many businesses that rely on people furnishing a home of their own."},{"en":"greater demand for housing","context":"From an economic perspective, the same trend creates greater demand for housing, which benefits the construction industry, estate agents and the many businesses that rely on people furnishing a home of their own."},{"en":"bear the weight of every household bill","context":"They must also bear the weight of every household bill and decision without anyone to share it."},{"en":"independence that people actively want","context":"Whether the trend is ultimately positive probably depends on whether it is freely chosen: independence that people actively want looks very different from an isolation they were simply unable to avoid."},{"en":"an isolation they were simply unable to avoid","context":"Whether the trend is ultimately positive probably depends on whether it is freely chosen: independence that people actively want looks very different from an isolation they were simply unable to avoid."}],"subtype":"Positive / Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#241","summary":"The rise of one-person households"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2246","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Housing","title":"Should housing be provided free?","prompt":"Housing is a basic necessity for everyone. Therefore, the government should provide free housing for people who cannot afford to pay for it. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Shelter is as fundamental as food and medical care, and it is argued that governments should therefore house those who cannot house themselves. I largely agree, though I believe subsidised housing is a better instrument than housing provided at no cost at all.\n\nThe strongest argument is that homelessness is more expensive than housing. A person sleeping outside uses emergency services repeatedly, is admitted to hospital far more often and cannot hold a job without an address, and several cities that simply gave permanent housing to long-term rough sleepers found that the total public cost <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fell rather than rose</strong>. Housing is also the condition for everything else: children in temporary accommodation change schools repeatedly and fall behind, so the cost of not acting <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is paid by the next generation</strong>.\n\nThere is a market argument as well. In cities where private rents have risen far faster than wages, nurses, cleaners and bus drivers cannot afford to live where they work, and a city that cannot house the people who run it will eventually fail to function.\n\nMy reservation concerns the word free. Housing that costs nothing is expensive to maintain, tends to be built cheaply and can trap tenants, since taking a job may mean losing the home. Systems that work best charge rent capped as a proportion of income, so that a tenant contributes what they can and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeps the gain from earning more</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that governments must house people who cannot house themselves, because the alternative costs more and damages children permanently. The provision should be secure and affordable rather than free, so that it supports people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without discouraging work</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"fell rather than rose","context":"A person sleeping outside uses emergency services repeatedly, is admitted to hospital far more often and cannot hold a job without an address, and several cities that simply gave permanent housing to long-term rough sleepers found that the total public cost fell rather than rose."},{"en":"is paid by the next generation","context":"Housing is also the condition for everything else: children in temporary accommodation change schools repeatedly and fall behind, so the cost of not acting is paid by the next generation."},{"en":"keeps the gain from earning more","context":"Systems that work best charge rent capped as a proportion of income, so that a tenant contributes what they can and keeps the gain from earning more."},{"en":"without discouraging work","context":"The provision should be secure and affordable rather than free, so that it supports people without discouraging work."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#242","summary":"Government provision of free housing for those who cannot afford it"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2362","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Housing","title":"Homes without gardens","prompt":"With a growing population in cities, more and more people live in homes with small outdoor areas or none at all. Is this a positive or negative trend?","answer":"As cities grow upwards, the private garden is disappearing from ordinary housing. I regard the trend as negative for the people living in these homes, although the density that causes it brings real advantages to the city as a whole.\n\nThe case in its favour is worth setting out. Building flats rather than houses allows far more people to live close to work, shops and public transport, which shortens journeys, reduces car use and protects farmland from being covered. Small homes are also cheaper to heat and to maintain, and a household without a garden gains hours each month that would have gone on mowing and repairs. For many young workers, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a small flat in the centre beats a house they cannot reach</strong>.\n\nThe losses are nonetheless serious. A private outdoor space is where small children play unsupervised, where families eat in summer and where people grow things, and none of these transfers easily to a public park several streets away. Access to greenery affects sleep, stress and recovery from illness, and the difference shows in health statistics between districts. Children in homes without gardens are less physically active, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">going outside becomes an expedition rather than a decision</strong>. The pandemic demonstrated the cost sharply, when families confined to small flats suffered far more than those with a step outside.\n\nThere is also a fairness problem. Gardens have not vanished; they have become expensive, so outdoor space is quietly turning into <strong class=\"key-phrase\">something only wealthier households can buy</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, dense housing serves the city by shortening journeys and preserving land, and it is not the density that causes the harm. The loss of any private outdoor space damages health and childhood, so cities should require balconies, courtyards and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">parks within a few minutes of every door</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"a small flat in the centre beats a house they cannot reach","context":"For many young workers, a small flat in the centre beats a house they cannot reach."},{"en":"going outside becomes an expedition rather than a decision","context":"Children in homes without gardens are less physically active, because going outside becomes an expedition rather than a decision."},{"en":"something only wealthier households can buy","context":"There is also a fairness problem. Gardens have not vanished; they have become expensive, so outdoor space is quietly turning into something only wealthier households can buy."},{"en":"parks within a few minutes of every door","context":"The loss of any private outdoor space damages health and childhood, so cities should require balconies, courtyards and parks within a few minutes of every door."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#243","summary":"Whether living in homes with little or no outdoor space is a good trend"},{"id":"writing-task-2-031","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Language & Communication","title":"English as a global language","prompt":"The advantages of the spread of English as a global language will continue\nto outweigh its disadvantages. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"English has become the default language of science, international business and diplomacy, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an increasing number of</strong> countries now teach it from primary school. Opinion is divided about whether this trend helps or harms the world. In my view the advantages outweigh the disadvantages, chiefly because a shared language lets people who would otherwise have no way to communicate trade and collaborate directly.\n\nThe practical benefits are considerable. A conference of scientists or engineers from a dozen countries can function efficiently only because there is one common working language, and multinational companies rely on English to move essential information between offices in different countries without the delay and expense of translation. Researchers who read English fluently also reach new findings years before a translation would ever appear.\n\nThe clearest cost is to smaller languages, not major national ones. Where a minority language coexists with a national or global one, it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">language proficiency</strong> in the dominant tongue, not the local one, that helps a career, so parents increasingly speak the dominant language at home instead, even though nothing forces the choice. UNESCO estimates that a language disappears roughly every two weeks, and most of those still spoken are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on the verge of extinction</strong> because English and a handful of other dominant languages have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made them commercially irrelevant</strong>.\n\nThere is a related worry about cultural diversity: because so much of the world’s news, film and academic publishing appears in English first, audiences everywhere absorb <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a disproportionate share of content</strong> shaped by British and American assumptions about which stories are worth telling and how they should be told. This is a genuine loss, and countries are right to keep investing in media and education in their own languages rather than treating English content as an adequate substitute. On balance, however, I still think the trade is worthwhile: the ability of people who share no other language to trade, research and simply talk to one another outweighs the cultural cost, provided that cost is actively managed rather than ignored.","phrases":[{"en":"an increasing number of","context":"English has become the default language of science, international business and diplomacy, and an increasing number of countries now teach it from primary school."},{"en":"language proficiency","context":"Where a minority language coexists with a national or global one, it is language proficiency in the dominant tongue, not the local one, that helps a career, so parents increasingly speak the dominant language at home instead, even though nothing forces the choice."},{"en":"on the verge of extinction","context":"UNESCO estimates that a language disappears roughly every two weeks, and most of those still spoken are on the verge of extinction because English and a handful of other dominant languages have made them commercially irrelevant."},{"en":"made them commercially irrelevant","context":"UNESCO estimates that a language disappears roughly every two weeks, and most of those still spoken are on the verge of extinction because English and a handful of other dominant languages have made them commercially irrelevant."},{"en":"a disproportionate share of content","context":"There is a related worry about cultural diversity: because so much of the world’s news, film and academic publishing appears in English first, audiences everywhere absorb a disproportionate share of content shaped by British and American assumptions about which stories are worth telling and how t..."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#244","summary":"Advantages of the spread of English as a global language will continue to…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-040","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Language & Communication","title":"Learning a language means learning a culture","prompt":"Some people argue that learning a second language necessarily involves learning about the culture of the countries where it is spoken. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Some people believe that it is impossible to learn a foreign language properly without also studying the culture of the countries in which it is spoken. I largely agree with this view, although the amount of cultural knowledge required depends on why the language is being learned.\n\nLanguage and culture are difficult to separate because meaning depends on context. Idioms, humour, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">levels of politeness and forms of address</strong> all reflect how a society thinks, and a learner who ignores them will produce sentences that are grammatically correct but socially wrong. A student who translates directly from Vietnamese into English, for example, may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sound abrupt or excessively formal</strong> without realising it. Cultural knowledge also aids memory, since vocabulary met in films, songs and real situations is retained far better than words <strong class=\"key-phrase\">memorised from a list</strong>.\n\nCulture also supplies the motivation that long-term study requires. Most people who reach a high level have developed a genuine interest in the literature, music or history of the language they are learning, and this is what carries them through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the difficult intermediate stage</strong>.\n\nI would qualify this in one respect. English is now used mainly between people who are not native speakers, so an engineer writing technical reports needs clarity and accuracy far more than familiarity with British or American customs. For such learners, cultural study is useful rather than essential.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that culture is an important part of language learning for anyone who wants to communicate naturally, but it matters much less for those who need a language purely as a professional tool.","phrases":[{"en":"memorised from a list","context":"Cultural knowledge also aids memory, since vocabulary met in films, songs and real situations is retained far better than words memorised from a list."},{"en":"the difficult intermediate stage","context":"Most people who reach a high level have developed a genuine interest in the literature, music or history of the language they are learning, and this is what carries them through the difficult intermediate stage."},{"en":"levels of politeness and forms of address","context":"Idioms, humour, levels of politeness and forms of address all reflect how a society thinks, and a learner who ignores them will produce sentences that are grammatically correct but socially wrong."},{"en":"sound abrupt or excessively formal","context":"A student who translates directly from Vietnamese into English, for example, may sound abrupt or excessively formal without realising it."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#245","summary":"Learning a language means learning a culture"},{"id":"writing-task-2-133","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Language & Communication","title":"Should public money save dying languages?","prompt":"Several languages are in danger of extinction because they are spoken by very small numbers of people. Some people say that governments should spend public money on saving these languages, while others believe that would be a waste of money. Discuss both these views and give your opinion.","answer":"It is true that some minority languages may disappear in the near future. Although it can be argued that governments could save money by allowing this to happen, I believe that these languages should be protected and preserved.\n\nThere are several reasons why saving minority languages could be seen as a waste of money. Teaching a language to more people than currently speak it requires building facilities, training teachers and running <strong class=\"key-phrase\">years of sustained marketing</strong>, and that money might arguably serve the public better if it were spent on hospitals, schools or roads instead, especially when the language in question has only a few thousand remaining speakers. Running a country in a single language would also be simpler and cheaper, since ministries would no longer need to translate official documents, court proceedings and public signage for each minority group separately.\n\nDespite the above arguments, I believe that governments should try to preserve languages that are less widely spoken. A language is much more than simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a means of communication</strong>; it has a vital connection with the cultural identity of the people who speak it. If a language disappears, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a whole way of life will disappear with it</strong>, and we will lose the rich cultural diversity that makes societies more interesting. By spending money to protect minority languages, governments can also preserve traditions, customs and behaviours that are part of a country's history.\n\nIn conclusion, it may save money in the short term if we allow minority languages to disappear, but in the long term this would have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an extremely negative impact on</strong> our cultural heritage.","phrases":[{"en":"a means of communication","context":"A language is much more than simply a means of communication; it has a vital connection with the cultural identity of the people who speak it."},{"en":"an extremely negative impact on","context":"In conclusion, it may save money in the short term if we allow minority languages to disappear, but in the long term this would have an extremely negative impact on our cultural heritage."},{"en":"years of sustained marketing","context":"Teaching a language to more people than currently speak it requires building facilities, training teachers and running years of sustained marketing, and that money might arguably serve the public better if it were spent on hospitals, schools or roads instead, especially when the language in quest..."},{"en":"a whole way of life will disappear with it","context":"If a language disappears, a whole way of life will disappear with it, and we will lose the rich cultural diversity that makes societies more interesting."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#246","summary":"Protecting endangered languages"},{"id":"writing-task-2-174","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Language & Communication","title":"Will the skill of writing disappear?","prompt":"As the use of mobile phones and computers increases, fewer people write letters. Some people believe that the skill of writing will disappear completely. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"As mobile phones and computers have replaced handwritten letters, some people predict that the ability to write will eventually disappear. I disagree with this view, because technology has changed how we write rather than whether we write at all.\n\nThere is no doubt that certain forms of writing are dying out. Personal letters are now rare, handwriting is deteriorating because most people use a keyboard, and features such as voice messages and predictive text allow users to communicate with very little effort. Schools in several countries have already reduced the time spent teaching cursive script, and some have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">removed it from the curriculum altogether</strong>.\n\nNevertheless, most people now write far more than any previous generation did. A typical office worker produces dozens of emails, reports and messages every day, and social media consists almost entirely of written text. The medium has changed, but the underlying skill of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">organising an idea and expressing it clearly</strong> is required far more often than it was when letters were posted once a month.\n\nThat skill also remains essential where it matters most. Contracts, medical records, academic papers, journalism and job applications all depend on precise writing, and employers regularly identify it as the ability they find <strong class=\"key-phrase\">most lacking in graduates</strong>. Voice technology cannot replace it, because a spoken sentence and a written argument are structured quite differently.\n\nIn conclusion, handwriting and traditional letters are certainly in decline, and that particular loss is worth regretting. I do not believe, however, that writing itself will disappear. If anything, digital communication has made the ability to write clearly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more valuable than before</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"organising an idea and expressing it clearly","context":"The medium has changed, but the underlying skill of organising an idea and expressing it clearly is required far more often than it was when letters were posted once a month."},{"en":"most lacking in graduates","context":"Contracts, medical records, academic papers, journalism and job applications all depend on precise writing, and employers regularly identify it as the ability they find most lacking in graduates."},{"en":"more valuable than before","context":"If anything, digital communication has made the ability to write clearly more valuable than before."},{"en":"removed it from the curriculum altogether","context":"Schools in several countries have already reduced the time spent teaching cursive script, and some have removed it from the curriculum altogether."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#247","summary":"Will the skill of writing disappear?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2287","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Language & Communication","title":"Must every child learn a foreign language?","prompt":"Because some children do not seem to have a natural ability to learn another language, schools should not force those children to study a foreign language. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Every class contains a few students who make almost no progress in a second language despite years of lessons. I disagree that they should be excused, because the argument rests on a false view of how languages are learned.\n\nThe case for excusing them is not absurd. Time spent failing at a subject is time taken from one where the same student might succeed, and repeated failure damages confidence in a way that spreads to other lessons. Where a child struggles with literacy in their first language, adding a second can reasonably be delayed.\n\nThe reasoning nevertheless fails at its foundation. Almost every human being acquires a language successfully as a child, which shows the capacity is general rather than exceptional. What differs is not ability but the method and the amount of exposure: classroom teaching of grammar for three hours a week suits some learners and defeats many others, and a student written off as untalented frequently makes rapid progress once immersed in the language or taught differently. Judging a twelve-year-old to have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no aptitude for something they have barely practised</strong> is a prediction dressed as a diagnosis.\n\nThe consequences of excusing them would also be unequal. Decisions of that kind fall disproportionately on children from poorer families, whose difficulty is usually a lack of exposure rather than of capacity, and removing them from language study <strong class=\"key-phrase\">closes doors they do not yet know exist</strong>, since languages affect university options and employment abroad. The wider benefits would be lost too: learning a second language improves understanding of one's own, and it teaches that other ways of describing the world are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">different rather than wrong</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, no child should be released from language study on the basis of apparent talent. Where progress is poor the sensible response is to change the teaching, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the failure usually belongs to the method</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"no aptitude for something they have barely practised","context":"Judging a twelve-year-old to have no aptitude for something they have barely practised is a prediction dressed as a diagnosis."},{"en":"closes doors they do not yet know exist","context":"Decisions of that kind fall disproportionately on children from poorer families, whose difficulty is usually a lack of exposure rather than of capacity, and removing them from language study closes doors they do not yet know exist, since languages affect university options and employment abroad."},{"en":"different rather than wrong","context":"The wider benefits would be lost too: learning a second language improves understanding of one's own, and it teaches that other ways of describing the world are different rather than wrong."},{"en":"the failure usually belongs to the method","context":"In conclusion, no child should be released from language study on the basis of apparent talent. Where progress is poor the sensible response is to change the teaching, since the failure usually belongs to the method."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#248","summary":"Whether children without aptitude should be excused from language study"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2350","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Language & Communication","title":"Are screens damaging literacy?","prompt":"There is a view that the widespread use of computers and mobile phones has contributed to a decline in reading and writing abilities among young people. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Teachers and employers regularly complain that young people cannot write a paragraph or read a long text. I partly agree that devices are involved, but the problem concerns stamina and register rather than literacy itself.\n\nYoung people today probably read and write more words than any earlier generation, since messaging, posts and comments are all text. What has changed is the shape of it. Screens train the eye to scan for the useful line and move on, which works for a notice and fails for an argument, so students struggle with anything requiring <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sustained attention on a single page</strong>. Writing has shifted in the same direction: quick exchanges reward brevity and speed, and formal structure is used so rarely that it is never practised. Predictive text and automatic correction remove the effort that once fixed spelling in the memory, and now <strong class=\"key-phrase\">errors are corrected without ever being noticed</strong>.\n\nThere are reasons for scepticism about the decline, however. Complaints about the writing of the young are centuries old. Measured literacy rates have risen, not fallen, and today's students write for real readers rather than only for teachers, which usually improves clarity. Adapting language to the situation is a skill in itself, and a student who writes casually to friends and formally in an examination is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">demonstrating range rather than ignorance</strong>.\n\nThe honest conclusion is that devices have changed the balance of practice. Reading many short fragments is not the same exercise as reading a chapter, and schools now have to teach deliberately what used to develop by itself: long reading, planning, and revising a draft.\n\nIn conclusion, phones and computers have not destroyed literacy, and young people use written language constantly. They have weakened concentration and formal writing, so the remedy is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practice at length rather than restrictions on technology</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"sustained attention on a single page","context":"Screens train the eye to scan for the useful line and move on, which works for a notice and fails for an argument, so students struggle with anything requiring sustained attention on a single page."},{"en":"errors are corrected without ever being noticed","context":"Predictive text and automatic correction remove the effort that once fixed spelling in the memory, and now errors are corrected without ever being noticed."},{"en":"demonstrating range rather than ignorance","context":"Adapting language to the situation is a skill in itself, and a student who writes casually to friends and formally in an examination is demonstrating range rather than ignorance."},{"en":"practice at length rather than restrictions on technology","context":"In conclusion, phones and computers have not destroyed literacy, and young people use written language constantly. They have weakened concentration and formal writing, so the remedy is practice at length rather than restrictions on technology."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#249","summary":"Whether computers and phones have weakened young people's reading and writing"},{"id":"writing-task-2-025","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Reporting crime in detail","prompt":"Some people suggest that there should be restrictions on detailed descriptions of crimes in newspapers and on television. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Some people believe that newspapers and television should not be allowed to describe crimes in detail. I largely agree, particularly where violent offences are concerned, although a complete restriction would be neither possible nor desirable.\n\nThe main argument for limits is that detailed reporting can encourage further offending. Descriptions of exactly how a fraud was carried out, or how a security system was defeated, amount to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">instructions that others can follow</strong>. There is also evidence of imitation in cases of extreme violence: research on mass shootings has repeatedly found that heavy coverage naming the attacker is followed by an increase in similar attacks, which is why several countries now advise the media not to publish the offender's name.\n\nA second concern is the effect on the public. Graphic reporting distresses victims and their families, who are forced to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relive the event</strong> in public, and it distorts the audience's sense of risk. Crime is reported far more often than it occurs, which is why people in many countries believe offending is rising when official figures show the opposite, and this fear <strong class=\"key-phrase\">falls most heavily on the elderly</strong>.\n\nHowever, restriction has limits. The press must be free to report what happens, since concealing crime would allow the authorities to hide their own failures, and the public has a right to know about genuine dangers in their area.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that gratuitous detail should be restricted, but reporting itself must remain free. The sensible test is whether a detail <strong class=\"key-phrase\">informs the public or merely entertains it</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"instructions that others can follow","context":"Descriptions of exactly how a fraud was carried out, or how a security system was defeated, amount to instructions that others can follow."},{"en":"relive the event","context":"Graphic reporting distresses victims and their families, who are forced to relive the event in public, and it distorts the audience's sense of risk."},{"en":"falls most heavily on the elderly","context":"Crime is reported far more often than it occurs, which is why people in many countries believe offending is rising when official figures show the opposite, and this fear falls most heavily on the elderly."},{"en":"informs the public or merely entertains it","context":"In conclusion, I agree that gratuitous detail should be restricted, but reporting itself must remain free. The sensible test is whether a detail informs the public or merely entertains it."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#250","summary":"Reporting crime in detail"},{"id":"writing-task-2-045","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Is the influence of news media negative?","prompt":"Some people think that the news media has a great influence on people's lives today and that this is a negative development. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"There is no doubt that the news media shapes the way people see the world, and some argue that this influence has become harmful. I partly agree: the way news is now produced and consumed has created real problems, although the underlying activity remains valuable.\n\nThe negative effects are easy to identify. Because news organisations compete for attention, they emphasise conflict, crime and disaster, which leaves audiences with a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">distorted impression</strong> of how dangerous their society actually is. Surveys regularly show that people believe crime is rising when official figures show it falling. Online news has made this worse, since algorithms show each user the material they already agree with, and readers gradually come to regard those who disagree as dishonest rather than merely mistaken. The speed of publication also means that unverified claims <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spread widely before they can be corrected</strong>.\n\nNevertheless, the media performs a function that nothing else can replace. Investigative journalism has exposed corruption, unsafe products and abuses of power that would otherwise have remained hidden, and citizens cannot vote sensibly without reliable information about what their government is doing. Coverage of famines and natural disasters has also produced humanitarian assistance <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on an enormous scale</strong>.\n\nThe problem, therefore, is not influence itself but the commercial model that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rewards outrage</strong>. Stronger rules on accuracy, together with greater public funding for independent broadcasters who do not depend on advertising revenue, would go a long way towards addressing it.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that current trends in news reporting are damaging, but the media's central role in informing the public remains essential.","phrases":[{"en":"distorted impression","context":"Because news organisations compete for attention, they emphasise conflict, crime and disaster, which leaves audiences with a distorted impression of how dangerous their society actually is."},{"en":"spread widely before they can be corrected","context":"The speed of publication also means that unverified claims spread widely before they can be corrected."},{"en":"on an enormous scale","context":"Coverage of famines and natural disasters has also produced humanitarian assistance on an enormous scale."},{"en":"rewards outrage","context":"The problem, therefore, is not influence itself but the commercial model that rewards outrage."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#251","summary":"Is the influence of news media negative?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-049","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Global media and cultural diversity","prompt":"When international media (including movies, fashion shows, advertisements and other TV programmes) convey the same messages to the global audience, people argue that the expansion of international media has negative impacts on cultural diversity. What is your opinion?","answer":"International media gives people access to films, music and ideas from around the world. Although this can make popular culture more similar across countries, I do not think it must destroy cultural diversity. The outcome depends mainly on how local communities respond.\n\nThere is a genuine risk of cultural uniformity. Large media companies have the money to promote the same films, celebrities and consumer trends in many markets. Local artists may struggle to compete, and young audiences may start to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">see foreign lifestyles as more modern or attractive than their own</strong>. Over time, this can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weaken local traditions</strong> and reduce demand for regional languages, music and crafts.\n\nHowever, international media can also widen cultural understanding. Streaming platforms allow small producers to reach audiences far beyond their home countries, while viewers can discover stories that were previously unavailable to them. Governments and schools can support this positive effect by funding local content, teaching cultural history and ensuring that national broadcasters include regional voices. In this way, societies can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">engage with global culture</strong> without abandoning their identity.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Cultural influence is also rarely one-way</strong>. South Korean pop music, for example, borrows the production techniques and marketing methods of the American entertainment industry, yet its lyrics, choreography and star system remain distinctly Korean, and the genre has pulled millions of new listeners towards Korean language and fashion rather than away from them. Absorbing outside methods does not have to mean losing what is local: a culture confident enough to blend in this way can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compete on more equal terms</strong> with the media companies that once threatened to drown it out.\n\nIn conclusion, global media can place pressure on cultural diversity, but cultural loss is not inevitable. If countries invest in local creative industries and people remain interested in their own heritage, international media can become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a channel for exchange rather than a force for uniformity</strong>. The aim should be to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">preserve local identity while remaining open</strong> to outside influence.","phrases":[{"en":"weaken local traditions","context":"Over time, this can weaken local traditions and reduce demand for regional languages, music and crafts."},{"en":"engage with global culture","context":"In this way, societies can engage with global culture without abandoning their identity."},{"en":"compete on more equal terms","context":"Absorbing outside methods does not have to mean losing what is local: a culture confident enough to blend in this way can compete on more equal terms with the media companies that once threatened to drown it out."},{"en":"preserve local identity while remaining open","context":"The aim should be to preserve local identity while remaining open to outside influence."},{"en":"see foreign lifestyles as more modern or attractive than their own","context":"Local artists may struggle to compete, and young audiences may start to see foreign lifestyles as more modern or attractive than their own."},{"en":"Cultural influence is also rarely one-way","context":"Cultural influence is also rarely one-way."},{"en":"a channel for exchange rather than a force for uniformity","context":"If countries invest in local creative industries and people remain interested in their own heritage, international media can become a channel for exchange rather than a force for uniformity."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#252","summary":"When international media including movies, fashion shows, advertisements…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-135","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Why people prefer foreign films","prompt":"Many people prefer to watch foreign films rather than locally produced films. Why could this be? Should governments give more financial support to local film industries?","answer":"It is true that foreign films are more popular in many countries than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">domestically produced films</strong>. There are several reasons for this, and in my view governments should respond by subsidising the local film industry rather than leaving it to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compete unaided</strong>.\n\nOne reason is simply budget. The established film industries of a few countries have huge sums available for staging action sequences, generating special effects and filming in spectacular locations, and Hollywood blockbusters such as Avatar or the James Bond films show what that money can buy: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">their global appeal is undeniable</strong>.\n\nAnother reason why these big-budget films are so successful is that they often star the most famous actors and actresses, and they are made by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the most accomplished producers and directors</strong>. The poor quality, low-budget filmmaking in many countries <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suffers in comparison</strong>.\n\nIn my view, governments should support local film industries financially. In every country, there may be talented amateur film-makers who just need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">be given the opportunity to</strong> prove themselves. To compete with big-budget productions from overseas, these people need money to pay for film crews, actors and a host of other costs related to producing high-quality films. If governments did help with these costs, they would see an increase in employment in the film industry, income from film sales, and perhaps even <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a rise in tourist numbers</strong>. New Zealand, for example, has seen an increase in tourism related to the 'Lord of the Rings' films, which were partly funded by government subsidies.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe that increased financial support could help to raise the quality of locally made films and allow them to compete with the foreign productions that currently dominate the market.","phrases":[{"en":"domestically produced films","context":"It is true that foreign films are more popular in many countries than domestically produced films. There are several reasons for this, and in my view governments should respond by subsidising the local film industry rather than leaving it to compete unaided."},{"en":"be given the opportunity to","context":"In every country, there may be talented amateur film-makers who just need to be given the opportunity to prove themselves."},{"en":"compete unaided","context":"It is true that foreign films are more popular in many countries than domestically produced films. There are several reasons for this, and in my view governments should respond by subsidising the local film industry rather than leaving it to compete unaided."},{"en":"suffers in comparison","context":"The poor quality, low-budget filmmaking in many countries suffers in comparison."},{"en":"a rise in tourist numbers","context":"If governments did help with these costs, they would see an increase in employment in the film industry, income from film sales, and perhaps even a rise in tourist numbers."},{"en":"their global appeal is undeniable","context":"The established film industries of a few countries have huge sums available for staging action sequences, generating special effects and filming in spectacular locations, and Hollywood blockbusters such as Avatar or the James Bond films show what that money can buy: their global appeal is undenia..."},{"en":"the most accomplished producers and directors","context":"Another reason why these big-budget films are so successful is that they often star the most famous actors and actresses, and they are made by the most accomplished producers and directors."}],"subtype":"Causes / Effects / Solutions","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#253","summary":"Foreign films and local film funding"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Should politicians' private lives be reported?","prompt":"Details of politicians' private lives should not be published in newspapers. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Politicians should not lose every right to privacy simply because they hold public office. Newspapers should publish private information only when it is clearly relevant to a politician's duties, honesty or use of public resources.\n\nThere is a legitimate public interest in some personal matters. Undeclared financial relationships, conflicts of interest or behaviour that directly contradicts a public claim may affect trust and decision-making. Investigative reporting is therefore essential when private conduct reveals corruption, abuse of power or serious hypocrisy. In these cases, publication can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hold powerful people accountable</strong>.\n\nHowever, details about family relationships, health or ordinary leisure activities are often published mainly to attract attention. Such stories can harm relatives who did not choose a public role and may discourage capable people from entering politics. Curiosity is not the same as public interest. Editors should be able to explain how a private fact affects the politician's work before releasing it.\n\nThe real difficulty is who applies that test. Editors compete for sales, and leaving the decision entirely to them has repeatedly failed: the British phone-hacking scandal revealed journalists intercepting private voicemail messages for stories with no bearing on anyone's public duty. An independent regulator able to impose meaningful penalties, rather than a brief apology printed weeks later, would give the standard some force.\n\nIn conclusion, the press needs freedom to investigate those in power, but that freedom should be guided by relevance and proportionality. Private information should be published when it exposes conduct that affects public duty, not merely because it is sensational. This standard would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">protect both accountability and personal dignity</strong>.","topic":"News & Media","phrases":[{"en":"hold powerful people accountable","context":"In these cases, publication can hold powerful people accountable."},{"en":"protect both accountability and personal dignity","context":"This standard would protect both accountability and personal dignity."}],"subtype":"opinion","summary":"Whether politicians' private lives should be reported","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-136","_displayNo":"#254"},{"id":"writing-task-2-156","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Is radio out of date?","prompt":"Some people think that radio has become out of date and that there is no longer any need to listen to it for entertainment or news. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"With television, streaming services and podcasts widely available, some people believe that radio has become obsolete. I disagree, because radio possesses practical advantages that no other medium has replaced.\n\nThe argument that radio is outdated is easy to understand. Audiences for traditional stations have fallen, particularly among young people, who choose their own music rather than accept a presenter's selection. Television provides images that radio cannot, and on-demand services allow listeners to hear exactly what they want at any time instead of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">waiting for a scheduled programme</strong>. Advertising revenue has moved online accordingly, and a number of long-established stations have closed.\n\nHowever, radio survives because it is the only medium that can be used while doing something else. Drivers, cooks, factory workers and runners cannot look at a screen, and for them audio is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not an inferior option but the only possible one</strong>, which is why listening remains high during commuting hours. Radio is also extremely cheap to produce and receive, requires almost no bandwidth and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">works during power cuts and natural disasters</strong>, when television and internet services fail. In many rural regions it remains the main source of news.\n\nRadio has also adapted rather than declined. Podcasts are essentially radio distributed differently, and their audience has grown enormously, which suggests that demand for spoken audio is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">increasing rather than disappearing</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that radio is out of date. Its audience has changed and its distribution has moved online, but the underlying medium remains uniquely convenient, cheap and reliable.","phrases":[{"en":"waiting for a scheduled programme","context":"Television provides images that radio cannot, and on-demand services allow listeners to hear exactly what they want at any time instead of waiting for a scheduled programme."},{"en":"works during power cuts and natural disasters","context":"Radio is also extremely cheap to produce and receive, requires almost no bandwidth and works during power cuts and natural disasters, when television and internet services fail."},{"en":"increasing rather than disappearing","context":"Radio has also adapted rather than declined. Podcasts are essentially radio distributed differently, and their audience has grown enormously, which suggests that demand for spoken audio is increasing rather than disappearing."},{"en":"not an inferior option but the only possible one","context":"Drivers, cooks, factory workers and runners cannot look at a screen, and for them audio is not an inferior option but the only possible one, which is why listening remains high during commuting hours."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#255","summary":"Is radio out of date?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-161","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Why people distrust journalists","prompt":"We obtain most of our knowledge of the world from the news, yet many people no longer trust journalists. Why do people hold this view, and what qualities should a good journalist have?","answer":"Public confidence in journalism has fallen sharply in many countries. In my view there are two clear reasons for this, and they point directly to the qualities that a good journalist needs.\n\nThe first reason is commercial pressure. News organisations now earn money from the number of people who click on a story, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rewards dramatic headlines rather than careful reporting</strong>. Articles are published before they have been checked, minor events are exaggerated, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">corrections appear quietly several days later</strong>. The second reason is bias. Most large outlets are owned by wealthy individuals or corporations with political interests, and readers have noticed that coverage of the same event can differ enormously from one newspaper to another. Social media has made this worse by allowing anyone to publish, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuine reporting now competes with rumour</strong>.\n\nThe qualities of a good journalist follow directly from these problems. The most important is accuracy: every claim should be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">verified through independent sources</strong> before publication, however attractive the story. The second is independence, meaning <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a willingness to print something that will displease an owner</strong>, an advertiser or a government. A good reporter must also be fair to the people they write about, giving them an opportunity to respond before an accusation appears in print. Courage matters too, since investigating corruption or organised crime carries real personal risk, and journalists in several countries have been imprisoned or killed for such work. Finally, a journalist must be able to explain complicated subjects such as economics or medicine clearly, without distorting them.\n\nIn conclusion, distrust of journalists is largely the result of commercial pressure and political bias. Restoring public confidence requires reporters who are accurate, independent, courageous and clear.","phrases":[{"en":"genuine reporting now competes with rumour","context":"Social media has made this worse by allowing anyone to publish, so genuine reporting now competes with rumour."},{"en":"verified through independent sources","context":"The most important is accuracy: every claim should be verified through independent sources before publication, however attractive the story."},{"en":"rewards dramatic headlines rather than careful reporting","context":"News organisations now earn money from the number of people who click on a story, which rewards dramatic headlines rather than careful reporting."},{"en":"corrections appear quietly several days later","context":"Articles are published before they have been checked, minor events are exaggerated, and corrections appear quietly several days later."},{"en":"a willingness to print something that will displease an owner","context":"The second is independence, meaning a willingness to print something that will displease an owner, an advertiser or a government."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Solutions","_displayNo":"#256","summary":"Why people distrust journalists"},{"id":"writing-task-2-169","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Will newspapers survive online news?","prompt":"Although more and more people read news on the Internet, newspapers will remain the most important source of news. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"The Internet is beginning to rival newspapers as the best place to find information about what is happening in the world. I believe that this trend will continue, and the Internet will soon be just as important as the traditional press.\n\nOn the one hand, newspapers will probably remain <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a vital source of information</strong> even in the Internet age. Not everyone wants to, or is able to, go online instead: older people and those in rural areas may have limited internet access, while plenty of readers with a perfectly good connection <strong class=\"key-phrase\">still prefer newsprint out of habit</strong>. Newspapers also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carry a level of trust that many websites lack</strong>, since their stories pass through professional journalists and editors before publication, and for many readers there remains something satisfying about holding an actual paper rather than staring at a screen.\n\nHowever, the Internet is likely to become just as popular as newspapers, largely because it delivers news faster and follows readers everywhere, updating on a phone the moment a story breaks rather than the next morning. Online news is also more interactive: readers can share articles, argue about them in comment sections, and post their own updates on platforms such as Twitter and YouTube, so that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the audience becomes part of the reporting</strong> rather than a passive recipient of it. Producing news digitally also avoids the paper, ink and delivery lorries that a print run requires, which makes it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the less wasteful option environmentally</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree with the view that newspapers will continue to be the main source of news, because I believe that the Internet will soon be equally important.","phrases":[{"en":"a vital source of information","context":"On the one hand, newspapers will probably remain a vital source of information even in the Internet age."},{"en":"still prefer newsprint out of habit","context":"Not everyone wants to, or is able to, go online instead: older people and those in rural areas may have limited internet access, while plenty of readers with a perfectly good connection still prefer newsprint out of habit."},{"en":"carry a level of trust that many websites lack","context":"Newspapers also carry a level of trust that many websites lack, since their stories pass through professional journalists and editors before publication, and for many readers there remains something satisfying about holding an actual paper rather than staring at a screen."},{"en":"the audience becomes part of the reporting","context":"Online news is also more interactive: readers can share articles, argue about them in comment sections, and post their own updates on platforms such as Twitter and YouTube, so that the audience becomes part of the reporting rather than a passive recipient of it."},{"en":"the less wasteful option environmentally","context":"Producing news digitally also avoids the paper, ink and delivery lorries that a print run requires, which makes it the less wasteful option environmentally."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#257","summary":"Although more and more people read news on the Internet, newspapers will…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2204","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Newspapers versus other media","prompt":"Some people think that newspapers are the best way to learn about news. However, others believe that they can learn news more effectively through other media. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Although newspapers were once the main source of information about current events, most people now follow the news online, on television or through social media. I would argue that other media are faster and more convenient, but that newspapers still provide the most reliable and thorough coverage.\n\nThe advantages of newspapers concern depth and accuracy. Articles are researched over days or weeks, checked by editors and written by journalists who specialise in a particular field, so a newspaper report on an election or a court case is likely to be far more detailed and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more carefully verified than anything else available</strong>. Reading a paper also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exposes people to stories they would never have chosen themselves</strong>, which gives a broader picture of what is happening in the world.\n\nOther forms of media, however, have obvious strengths. Television and news websites can report an earthquake or a political announcement within minutes, whereas a newspaper reader <strong class=\"key-phrase\">must wait until the following morning</strong>. Online sources are free, can be reached anywhere on a phone, and offer video, audio and live updates that print cannot match. Social media also allows ordinary people to report events directly from places where journalists are not present.\n\nIn my opinion the two are best used together. I rely on my phone for immediate headlines, but I turn to established newspapers, whether printed or online, when I want to understand why something has happened. The main danger of social media is that it spreads <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unverified claims</strong> extremely quickly, and professional journalism remains <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the only effective check on this</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, other media are better for speed and access, but newspapers continue to offer the most trustworthy analysis.","phrases":[{"en":"unverified claims","context":"The main danger of social media is that it spreads unverified claims extremely quickly, and professional journalism remains the only effective check on this."},{"en":"exposes people to stories they would never have chosen themselves","context":"Reading a paper also exposes people to stories they would never have chosen themselves, which gives a broader picture of what is happening in the world."},{"en":"more carefully verified than anything else available","context":"Articles are researched over days or weeks, checked by editors and written by journalists who specialise in a particular field, so a newspaper report on an election or a court case is likely to be far more detailed and more carefully verified than anything else available."},{"en":"the only effective check on this","context":"The main danger of social media is that it spreads unverified claims extremely quickly, and professional journalism remains the only effective check on this."},{"en":"must wait until the following morning","context":"Television and news websites can report an earthquake or a political announcement within minutes, whereas a newspaper reader must wait until the following morning."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#258","summary":"Newspapers versus other media"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2272","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Should violent films and games be banned?","prompt":"Films and computer games containing violence are popular. Some people say they have a negative effect on society and should be banned. Others say they are just harmless relaxation. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Violent films and games sell in enormous numbers, and opinion divides between those who blame them for real aggression and those who regard them as ordinary entertainment. My own position falls between the two, though closer to the second.\n\nThose who want a ban argue that repeated exposure changes attitudes. Watching violence presented as exciting and consequence-free may make aggression seem a normal response to conflict, and this concern is sharpest for children, whose sense of what is real is still forming. Games are held to be worse than films because the player performs the violence rather than watching it, and short-term studies do find <strong class=\"key-phrase\">measurably increased hostility</strong> immediately after play.\n\nThe opposing view points to what the wider evidence shows. Violent games have spread to almost every country over three decades while violent crime among young people has generally fallen, which is difficult to reconcile with a strong causal effect. Fiction has always dealt with violence, from Greek tragedy onwards, and audiences have consistently proved able to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">distinguish a story from an instruction</strong>. There is also a strong argument about freedom, since banning material because a small minority may respond badly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">restricts everyone for the sake of a few</strong>.\n\nMy view is that a ban is both disproportionate and ineffective. The evidence supports a small short-term effect rather than a cause of serious violence, and prohibition simply moves material to unregulated channels where nobody checks who is watching.\n\nIn conclusion, these concerns should not be dismissed, particularly regarding young children. Age ratings enforced properly, together with parents knowing what their children play, address the real risk without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">removing the choice from adults</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"measurably increased hostility","context":"Games are held to be worse than films because the player performs the violence rather than watching it, and short-term studies do find measurably increased hostility immediately after play."},{"en":"distinguish a story from an instruction","context":"Fiction has always dealt with violence, from Greek tragedy onwards, and audiences have consistently proved able to distinguish a story from an instruction."},{"en":"restricts everyone for the sake of a few","context":"There is also a strong argument about freedom, since banning material because a small minority may respond badly restricts everyone for the sake of a few."},{"en":"removing the choice from adults","context":"In conclusion, these concerns should not be dismissed, particularly regarding young children. Age ratings enforced properly, together with parents knowing what their children play, address the real risk without removing the choice from adults."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#259","summary":"Whether violent films and computer games should be banned"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2301","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Is following the news a waste of time?","prompt":"Some people think the news has no connection to people's lives, so reading newspapers and watching television news is a waste of time. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"A growing number of people have stopped following the news, saying that it neither affects nor improves their lives. I disagree with that position, although the complaint behind it identifies something wrong with how news is currently produced.\n\nThe claim fails because the connection is usually indirect rather than absent. Decisions reported in the news — interest rates, tax changes, planning laws, public health rules — reach households within months, and someone who ignored them would be surprised by a mortgage payment or a school closure they could have anticipated. News also makes voting meaningful, since a citizen who knows nothing about a government's record cannot judge it, and reporting is one of the few pressures that keeps officials and companies honest. There is a further point about understanding other people: a person who never encounters events beyond their own street <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mistakes their own experience for how the world works</strong>.\n\nThe complaint nevertheless has a foundation. Much of what is broadcast genuinely is irrelevant: celebrity stories, political arguments about nothing, and distant events reported without any explanation of why they matter. Coverage is also relentlessly negative and repetitive, and consuming it continuously produces anxiety without producing knowledge, which is why some readers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel worse informed the more they watch</strong>.\n\nThe sensible response is to change the habit rather than abandon it. Reading a serious source once a day, choosing analysis over rolling coverage, and following the subjects that actually affect one's work and family delivers the benefit without the exhaustion. It is the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">continuous drip of alerts</strong> that wastes time, not information itself.\n\nIn conclusion, the news connects private lives to decisions made elsewhere, and ignoring it leaves people unprepared and easier to mislead. Much daily coverage is genuinely worthless, so the answer is to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">read less and choose better</strong> rather than to stop.","phrases":[{"en":"mistakes their own experience for how the world works","context":"There is a further point about understanding other people: a person who never encounters events beyond their own street mistakes their own experience for how the world works."},{"en":"feel worse informed the more they watch","context":"Coverage is also relentlessly negative and repetitive, and consuming it continuously produces anxiety without producing knowledge, which is why some readers feel worse informed the more they watch."},{"en":"continuous drip of alerts","context":"It is the continuous drip of alerts that wastes time, not information itself."},{"en":"read less and choose better","context":"In conclusion, the news connects private lives to decisions made elsewhere, and ignoring it leaves people unprepared and easier to mislead. Much daily coverage is genuinely worthless, so the answer is to read less and choose better rather than to stop."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#260","summary":"Whether news is disconnected from ordinary life and not worth following"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2331","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Why the news is mostly bad","prompt":"News plays an important part in most people's lives. Why is news so important to people? Why is so much news dedicated to bad news? Should the news focus on good news instead?","answer":"Most people check the news before they have finished breakfast. It occupies that position because it tells us what has changed in a world we cannot see for ourselves, and it leans towards disaster for reasons that are partly commercial and partly human.\n\nNews matters because almost everything that affects a life is decided somewhere else. Interest rates, elections, war and epidemics all reach ordinary households, and knowing about them early allows people to act rather than react. It also makes citizenship possible, since a voter with no information is choosing blindly, and reporting is one of the few forces that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">makes powerful people uncomfortable</strong>. Beyond that, news gives a scattered population a shared set of events to discuss.\n\nBad news dominates for two reasons. The first is in us: human attention evolved to notice threats, so a report of danger is read while a report of steady improvement is scrolled past. The second is commercial, because outlets are paid by attention, and alarming headlines earn more of it. Bad events are also easier to report, since a bridge collapsing is a moment while a bridge standing safely for forty years is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a process without a date</strong>.\n\nReplacing this with good news would be a mistake, however. Reporting exists to warn and to hold power to account, and a diet of pleasant stories would leave the public unaware of what needs fixing. The honest correction is proportion rather than positivity: alongside each crisis, coverage should explain what is being done, how common the danger actually is, and what has improved, so that readers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">understand scale as well as drama</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, news is important because it connects private lives to distant decisions, and it favours bad news because both our attention and the market reward it. The answer is more context and better proportion, not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a cheerful substitute for reporting</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"makes powerful people uncomfortable","context":"It also makes citizenship possible, since a voter with no information is choosing blindly, and reporting is one of the few forces that makes powerful people uncomfortable."},{"en":"a process without a date","context":"Bad events are also easier to report, since a bridge collapsing is a moment while a bridge standing safely for forty years is a process without a date."},{"en":"understand scale as well as drama","context":"The honest correction is proportion rather than positivity: alongside each crisis, coverage should explain what is being done, how common the danger actually is, and what has improved, so that readers understand scale as well as drama."},{"en":"a cheerful substitute for reporting","context":"In conclusion, news is important because it connects private lives to distant decisions, and it favours bad news because both our attention and the market reward it. The answer is more context and better proportion, not a cheerful substitute for reporting."}],"subtype":"Two-Part Question","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#261","summary":"Why news matters, why it favours bad news, and whether it should change"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2349","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Imported films or home-made ones?","prompt":"Some people believe that showing imported films and television programmes is beneficial for a country's culture, while others argue that countries should produce their own. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Streaming services have made foreign films available everywhere at once, and small national film industries are struggling against them. Both sides of this argument identify something real, and my own view is that the two are not alternatives.\n\nImported films bring genuine benefits. They show audiences how other people live, which reduces the suspicion that distance produces, and they carry ideas that a closed culture would take much longer to encounter. Viewers gain access to work of a technical quality that few countries can afford to match, and languages are learned painlessly from subtitles. Domestic film-makers also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">learn their craft by watching the best of it</strong>, which is why national industries usually improve after exposure rather than before it.\n\nThe opposing case is about balance rather than contact. When most of what a country watches is made elsewhere, children absorb foreign assumptions about family, success and beauty while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">their own surroundings appear nowhere on screen</strong>, and local stories go untold because the audience has been trained to expect a different style. There is an economic argument too: money spent on imported programming leaves the country, and writers, actors and technicians lose the work that would keep the industry alive. Small languages suffer most, since a film in one is expensive to make and easy to skip.\n\nThe evidence suggests protection and openness can coexist. Countries that fund domestic production and require broadcasters to carry a share of local content have produced films that then export successfully, so support built the industry rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sheltering it from competition</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, imported films widen a culture and raise standards, and no country should shut them out. National production needs deliberate funding as well, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a country that never sees itself on screen loses the habit of telling its own stories</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"learn their craft by watching the best of it","context":"Domestic film-makers also learn their craft by watching the best of it, which is why national industries usually improve after exposure rather than before it."},{"en":"their own surroundings appear nowhere on screen","context":"When most of what a country watches is made elsewhere, children absorb foreign assumptions about family, success and beauty while their own surroundings appear nowhere on screen, and local stories go untold because the audience has been trained to expect a different style."},{"en":"sheltering it from competition","context":"Countries that fund domestic production and require broadcasters to carry a share of local content have produced films that then export successfully, so support built the industry rather than sheltering it from competition."},{"en":"a country that never sees itself on screen loses the habit of telling its own stories","context":"In conclusion, imported films widen a culture and raise standards, and no country should shut them out. National production needs deliberate funding as well, because a country that never sees itself on screen loses the habit of telling its own stories."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#262","summary":"Whether foreign films benefit a culture or countries should make their own"},{"id":"writing-task-2-015","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Compulsory unpaid work for teenagers","prompt":"Some people think that all teenagers should be required to do unpaid work in their free time to help the local community. They believe this would benefit both the individual teenager and society as a whole. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Many young people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work on a volunteer basis</strong>, and this can only be beneficial for both the individual and society as a whole and encourage <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of shared civic responsibility</strong>. However, I do not agree that we should therefore force all teenagers to do unpaid work.\n\nMost young people are already under enough pressure with their studies, without being given the added responsibility of working in their spare time. School is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">just as demanding as a full-time job</strong>, and teachers expect their students to do <strong class=\"key-phrase\">homework and exam revision</strong> on top of attending lessons every day. When young people do have some free time, we should encourage them to enjoy it with their friends or to spend it doing sports and other leisure activities. They have many years of work ahead of them when they finish their studies.\n\nAt the same time, I do not believe that society has anything to gain from obliging young people to do unpaid work. In fact, I would argue that it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">goes against the values of</strong> a free and fair society to force a group of people to do something against their will. Doing this can only <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lead to resentment amongst young people</strong>, who would feel that they were being used, and parents, who would not want to be told how to raise their children. Currently, nobody is forced to volunteer, and this is surely the best system.\n\nIn conclusion, teenagers may choose to work for free and help others, but in my opinion we should not make this compulsory.","phrases":[{"en":"work on a volunteer basis","context":"Many young people work on a volunteer basis, and this can only be beneficial for both the individual and society as a whole and encourage a sense of shared civic responsibility."},{"en":"homework and exam revision","context":"School is just as demanding as a full-time job, and teachers expect their students to do homework and exam revision on top of attending lessons every day."},{"en":"a sense of shared civic responsibility","context":"Many young people work on a volunteer basis, and this can only be beneficial for both the individual and society as a whole and encourage a sense of shared civic responsibility."},{"en":"just as demanding as a full-time job","context":"School is just as demanding as a full-time job, and teachers expect their students to do homework and exam revision on top of attending lessons every day."},{"en":"goes against the values of","context":"In fact, I would argue that it goes against the values of a free and fair society to force a group of people to do something against their will."},{"en":"lead to resentment amongst young people","context":"Doing this can only lead to resentment amongst young people, who would feel that they were being used, and parents, who would not want to be told how to raise their children."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#263","summary":"Compulsory community work for teenagers"},{"id":"writing-task-2-047","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Military service or community service?","prompt":"What are the benefits of requiring young people to serve in the army? Does participation in community work qualify as an alternative?","answer":"Some countries still require young people to complete a period of military service. In my opinion this brings certain genuine benefits, but community service would achieve most of the same aims without the drawbacks.\n\nThe advantages of military training are not difficult to identify. Recruits become considerably fitter, and they learn discipline, punctuality and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">how to function under pressure</strong>, which many young people have never previously been required to do. There is a social benefit as well: conscription mixes young men from different regions and backgrounds who would otherwise never meet, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strengthens national cohesion</strong> in a way little else does. Employers frequently <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regard former conscripts as reliable, punctual and well organised</strong>, and in some countries the experience is treated almost as a qualification in itself.\n\nThe objections, however, are serious. Military training is designed to prepare people for combat, and not everyone is suited to that environment; a significant number find the stress genuinely damaging. It also removes a year or two from a young person's education or career <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at exactly the point when those years matter most</strong>.\n\nCommunity service seems to me a better instrument for the same purpose. Working in hospitals, care homes, conservation projects or disaster relief teaches responsibility and teamwork just as effectively, and it exposes young people to parts of society they would otherwise never see. It also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">produces something of immediate value</strong>, whereas peacetime military training <strong class=\"key-phrase\">produces a readiness that is rarely used</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, military service genuinely does develop discipline and physical fitness, and I would not dismiss it. Community service, however, delivers comparable benefits at a far lower personal cost, and I would prefer it as the general requirement.","phrases":[{"en":"strengthens national cohesion","context":"There is a social benefit as well: conscription mixes young men from different regions and backgrounds who would otherwise never meet, which strengthens national cohesion in a way little else does."},{"en":"at exactly the point when those years matter most","context":"It also removes a year or two from a young person's education or career at exactly the point when those years matter most."},{"en":"produces something of immediate value","context":"It also produces something of immediate value, whereas peacetime military training produces a readiness that is rarely used."},{"en":"how to function under pressure","context":"Recruits become considerably fitter, and they learn discipline, punctuality and how to function under pressure, which many young people have never previously been required to do."},{"en":"regard former conscripts as reliable, punctual and well organised","context":"Employers frequently regard former conscripts as reliable, punctual and well organised, and in some countries the experience is treated almost as a qualification in itself."},{"en":"produces a readiness that is rarely used","context":"It also produces something of immediate value, whereas peacetime military training produces a readiness that is rarely used."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#264","summary":"Military service or community service?"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"The teenage years or adult life?","prompt":"Some people think that the teenage years are the happiest time of most people's lives. Others think that adult life brings more happiness, in spite of greater responsibility. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Some people remember adolescence as the happiest period of life, while others prefer adulthood. I believe happiness depends more on personal circumstances than age, although adulthood often offers greater freedom and control.\n\nTeenagers may enjoy fewer financial responsibilities and more time with friends. School holidays, new experiences and strong social bonds can make this stage exciting. However, adolescence also involves academic pressure, insecurity and limited independence. Young people are often expected to make important choices before they fully understand themselves.\n\nAdults face work, bills and family duties, but they usually have more control over where they live, whom they meet and how they spend their time. They can build a career, form stable relationships and make decisions based on experience. For many people, this <strong class=\"key-phrase\">greater sense of agency</strong> outweighs the heavier responsibilities.\n\nIn my own view, the decisive difference is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not the amount of pressure but the ability to escape it</strong>. A teenager who dislikes school or lives in a difficult household has almost no way out and must simply endure the situation for years. An unhappy adult can resign, move to another city or end a relationship, and even when those choices are painful they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remove the sense of being trapped</strong>. A feeling of control is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of the strongest predictors of life satisfaction</strong>, and it is largely unavailable before adulthood.\n\nIn conclusion, there is no universal happiest age. A supportive family, good health, meaningful relationships and a sense of purpose matter more than whether someone is a teenager or an adult. Nevertheless, I would choose adulthood because independence makes it easier to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shape life according to personal priorities</strong>.","topic":"Society","phrases":[{"en":"greater sense of agency","context":"For many people, this greater sense of agency outweighs the heavier responsibilities."},{"en":"shape life according to personal priorities","context":"Nevertheless, I would choose adulthood because independence makes it easier to shape life according to personal priorities."},{"en":"not the amount of pressure but the ability to escape it","context":"In my own view, the decisive difference is not the amount of pressure but the ability to escape it."},{"en":"remove the sense of being trapped","context":"An unhappy adult can resign, move to another city or end a relationship, and even when those choices are painful they remove the sense of being trapped."},{"en":"one of the strongest predictors of life satisfaction","context":"A feeling of control is one of the strongest predictors of life satisfaction, and it is largely unavailable before adulthood."}],"subtype":"discuss","summary":"Whether the teenage years or adult life bring more happiness","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-110","_displayNo":"#265"},{"id":"writing-task-2-120","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"What happiness is, and how to reach it","prompt":"Happiness is considered very important in life. Why is it difficult to define? What factors are important in achieving happiness?","answer":"It is no doubt true that the majority of people would like to be happy in their lives. While <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the personal nature of happiness</strong> makes it difficult to describe, there do seem to be some common needs that we all share with regard to experiencing or achieving happiness.\n\nHappiness is difficult to define because it means something different to each individual person. Nobody can fully understand or experience another person's feelings, and we all have our own particular passions from which we take pleasure. Some people, for example, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">derive a sense of satisfaction from</strong> earning money or achieving success, whereas for others, health and family are much more important. At the same time, a range of other feelings, from excitement to peacefulness, may be associated with the idea of happiness, and the same person may therefore feel happy in a variety of different ways.\n\nAlthough it seems almost impossible to give <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a precise definition of happiness</strong>, most people would agree that there are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">some basic preconditions to achieving it</strong>. Firstly, it is hard for a person to be happy if he or she does not have a safe place to live and enough food to eat. Our basic survival needs must surely be met before we can lead a pleasant life. Secondly, the greatest joy in life is usually found in shared experiences with family and friends, and it is rare to find a person who is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">content to live in complete isolation</strong>. Other key factors could be individual freedom and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of purpose in life</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, happiness is difficult to define because it is particular to each individual, but I believe that our basic needs for shelter, food and company need to be fulfilled before we can experience it.","phrases":[{"en":"the personal nature of happiness","context":"While the personal nature of happiness makes it difficult to describe, there do seem to be some common needs that we all share with regard to experiencing or achieving happiness."},{"en":"a precise definition of happiness","context":"Although it seems almost impossible to give a precise definition of happiness, most people would agree that there are some basic preconditions to achieving it."},{"en":"a sense of purpose in life","context":"Other key factors could be individual freedom and a sense of purpose in life."},{"en":"derive a sense of satisfaction from","context":"Some people, for example, derive a sense of satisfaction from earning money or achieving success, whereas for others, health and family are much more important."},{"en":"some basic preconditions to achieving it","context":"Although it seems almost impossible to give a precise definition of happiness, most people would agree that there are some basic preconditions to achieving it."},{"en":"content to live in complete isolation","context":"Secondly, the greatest joy in life is usually found in shared experiences with family and friends, and it is rare to find a person who is content to live in complete isolation."}],"subtype":"Two-part question","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#266","summary":"Defining and achieving happiness"},{"id":"writing-task-2-140","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"More dependent on each other, or more independent?","prompt":"Some people think that in the modern world we are more dependent on each other, while others think that people have become more independent. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People have different views about whether we are more or less dependent on others nowadays. In my view, modern life forces us to be more independent than people were in the past.\n\nOne reason people give for growing interdependence is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the cost of living</strong>, which has risen so sharply that young adults increasingly rely on their parents for help when buying a house; property prices are now so high that many people cannot <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pay a deposit and a mortgage</strong> without that support. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Rising ambition compounds the pressure</strong>: as families want a better quality of life, both parents typically need to work full-time, which in turn makes them dependent on grandparents and paid babysitters for childcare that a single wage earner's household would once have managed alone.\n\nHowever, I would agree with those who believe that people are more independent these days. In most countries, families are becoming <strong class=\"key-phrase\">smaller and more dispersed</strong>, which means that people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot count on relatives as much as they used to</strong>. We also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have more freedom to</strong> travel and live far away from our home towns. For example, many students choose to study abroad instead of going to their local university, and this experience makes them more independent as they learn to live alone. Another factor in this growing independence is technology, which allows us to work alone and from any part of the world.\n\nIn conclusion, while there are some reasons to believe that people now depend on each other more, my own view is that we are more independent than ever.","phrases":[{"en":"the cost of living","context":"One reason people give for growing interdependence is the cost of living, which has risen so sharply that young adults increasingly rely on their parents for help when buying a house; property prices are now so high that many people cannot pay a deposit and a mortgage without that support."},{"en":"pay a deposit and a mortgage","context":"One reason people give for growing interdependence is the cost of living, which has risen so sharply that young adults increasingly rely on their parents for help when buying a house; property prices are now so high that many people cannot pay a deposit and a mortgage without that support."},{"en":"have more freedom to","context":"We also have more freedom to travel and live far away from our home towns."},{"en":"cannot count on relatives as much as they used to","context":"In most countries, families are becoming smaller and more dispersed, which means that people cannot count on relatives as much as they used to."},{"en":"smaller and more dispersed","context":"In most countries, families are becoming smaller and more dispersed, which means that people cannot count on relatives as much as they used to."},{"en":"Rising ambition compounds the pressure","context":"Rising ambition compounds the pressure: as families want a better quality of life, both parents typically need to work full-time, which in turn makes them dependent on grandparents and paid babysitters for childcare that a single wage earner's household would once have managed alone."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#267","summary":"Modern independence and interdependence"},{"id":"writing-task-2-141","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Older generations and their ideas about how to live","prompt":"The older generations tend to have very traditional ideas about how people should live, think and behave. However, some people believe that these ideas are not helpful in preparing younger generations for modern life. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this view?","answer":"It is true that many older people believe in traditional values that often seem incompatible with the needs of younger people. While I agree that some traditional ideas are outdated, I believe that others are still useful and should not be forgotten.\n\nOn the one hand, many of the ideas that elderly people have about life are becoming less relevant for younger people. In the past, for example, people were advised to learn a profession and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">find a secure job</strong> for life, but today's workers expect much more variety and diversity from their careers. At the same time, the “rules’ around relationships <strong class=\"key-phrase\">are being eroded</strong> as young adults <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make their own choices</strong> about who and when to marry. But perhaps <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the greatest disparity between the generations</strong> can be seen in their attitudes towards gender roles. The traditional roles of men and women, as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">breadwinners and housewives</strong>, are no longer accepted as necessary or appropriate by most younger people.\n\nOn the other hand, some traditional views and values are certainly applicable to the modern world. For example, older generations attach great importance to working hard, doing one's best, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taking pride in one's work</strong>, and these behaviours can surely benefit young people as they enter today's competitive job market. Other characteristics that are perhaps seen as traditional are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">politeness and good manners</strong>. In our globalised world, young adults can expect to come into contact with people from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a huge variety of backgrounds</strong>, and it is more important than ever to treat others with respect. Finally, I believe that young people would lead happier lives if they had a more “old-fashioned’ sense of community and neighbourliness.\n\nIn conclusion, although the views of older people may sometimes seem unhelpful in today's world, we should not dismiss all traditional ideas as irrelevant.","phrases":[{"en":"find a secure job","context":"In the past, for example, people were advised to learn a profession and find a secure job for life, but today's workers expect much more variety and diversity from their careers."},{"en":"make their own choices","context":"At the same time, the “rules’ around relationships are being eroded as young adults make their own choices about who and when to marry."},{"en":"breadwinners and housewives","context":"The traditional roles of men and women, as breadwinners and housewives, are no longer accepted as necessary or appropriate by most younger people."},{"en":"politeness and good manners","context":"Other characteristics that are perhaps seen as traditional are politeness and good manners."},{"en":"a huge variety of backgrounds","context":"In our globalised world, young adults can expect to come into contact with people from a huge variety of backgrounds, and it is more important than ever to treat others with respect."},{"en":"are being eroded","context":"At the same time, the “rules’ around relationships are being eroded as young adults make their own choices about who and when to marry."},{"en":"the greatest disparity between the generations","context":"But perhaps the greatest disparity between the generations can be seen in their attitudes towards gender roles."},{"en":"taking pride in one's work","context":"For example, older generations attach great importance to working hard, doing one's best, and taking pride in one's work, and these behaviours can surely benefit young people as they enter today's competitive job market."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#268","summary":"Traditional values in modern life"},{"id":"writing-task-2-145","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Why people define happiness differently","prompt":"Discuss the reasons why people define happiness differently and how to\nobtain happiness?","answer":"People define happiness differently because they have different needs, values and life circumstances. For one person, happiness may mean professional success; for another, it may come from family life, health or personal freedom. In my view, people are most likely to become happy when their goals <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reflect their own values rather than social pressure</strong>.\n\nAge and circumstance strongly influence what matters to an individual. A student may value friendship and opportunity, while a parent may place greater importance on security and time with children. Income also changes priorities. Someone struggling to pay for food or housing will naturally connect happiness with financial stability, whereas a wealthy person may seek meaning, relationships or creative achievement. These differences explain why there is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no single formula for happiness</strong>.\n\nPersonality is equally important. Some people enjoy competition and public recognition, while others prefer a quiet and predictable life. Culture also shapes expectations about marriage, work and personal independence. Problems arise when people pursue goals mainly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">because those goals appear impressive to others</strong>. A high salary or prestigious title may bring little satisfaction if the daily work conflicts with a person's interests or health.\n\nA practical route to happiness is to identify a small number of realistic priorities and invest time in them consistently. Close relationships, useful work, adequate rest and physical health provide <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a stronger foundation than constant comparison with other people</strong>. It is also helpful to accept that happiness is not a permanent emotional state. A good life can include difficulty while still offering <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a stable sense of purpose and contentment</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, happiness varies because people differ in both circumstances and values. The best way to pursue it is to set realistic goals, protect important relationships and judge progress by personally meaningful standards.","phrases":[{"en":"no single formula for happiness","context":"These differences explain why there is no single formula for happiness."},{"en":"a stable sense of purpose and contentment","context":"A good life can include difficulty while still offering a stable sense of purpose and contentment."},{"en":"reflect their own values rather than social pressure","context":"In my view, people are most likely to become happy when their goals reflect their own values rather than social pressure."},{"en":"because those goals appear impressive to others","context":"Problems arise when people pursue goals mainly because those goals appear impressive to others."},{"en":"a stronger foundation than constant comparison with other people","context":"Close relationships, useful work, adequate rest and physical health provide a stronger foundation than constant comparison with other people."}],"subtype":"Causes / Effects","_displayNo":"#269","summary":"Discuss the reasons why people define happiness differently and how…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-157","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Is it ever right to lie?","prompt":"Some people think that telling the truth is not always essential. It is necessary to tell lies sometimes. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Honesty is essential for trust, but I do not believe that every fact must be stated in every situation. Small lies can sometimes be justified when they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prevent unnecessary harm</strong> and do not deceive someone about an important decision.\n\nThe main reason to value truth is that relationships and institutions depend on reliable information. People need accurate facts when they make choices about health, money, work or family matters. A person who lies for personal gain may damage others and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">eventually lose credibility</strong>. For this reason, honesty should remain <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the normal rule rather than the exception</strong>.\n\nEven so, complete openness can occasionally be cruel or pointless. A gentle compliment may protect someone's feelings, and a surprise party requires temporary secrecy. In more serious cases, a doctor might choose careful language when giving difficult news, although the patient should still receive the information needed to make decisions. The important test is whether the lie is intended to protect another person or merely to protect the liar. A harmless social lie should not be treated in the same way as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deliberate deception for personal benefit</strong>.\n\nThere is also a risk in allowing any exception at all: the reasoning used to justify a kind lie can just as easily <strong class=\"key-phrase\">excuse a self-interested one</strong>. A manager who quietly delays bad news to avoid alarming staff is using the same logic as someone hiding a gambling debt from their family. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The exception must therefore stay narrow</strong>, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">judged by its effect on the other person</strong> rather than the liar's comfort.\n\nIn conclusion, telling the truth is normally the best policy because it creates trust and accountability. Nevertheless, limited exceptions are reasonable when the issue is minor, the intention is kind and no important right is affected. This approach <strong class=\"key-phrase\">balances honesty with compassion</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"the normal rule rather than the exception","context":"For this reason, honesty should remain the normal rule rather than the exception."},{"en":"deliberate deception for personal benefit","context":"A harmless social lie should not be treated in the same way as deliberate deception for personal benefit."},{"en":"balances honesty with compassion","context":"This approach balances honesty with compassion."},{"en":"excuse a self-interested one","context":"There is also a risk in allowing any exception at all: the reasoning used to justify a kind lie can just as easily excuse a self-interested one."},{"en":"judged by its effect on the other person","context":"The exception must therefore stay narrow, judged by its effect on the other person rather than the liar's comfort."},{"en":"prevent unnecessary harm","context":"Honesty is essential for trust, but I do not believe that every fact must be stated in every situation. Small lies can sometimes be justified when they prevent unnecessary harm and do not deceive someone about an important decision."},{"en":"eventually lose credibility","context":"A person who lies for personal gain may damage others and eventually lose credibility."},{"en":"The exception must therefore stay narrow","context":"The exception must therefore stay narrow, judged by its effect on the other person rather than the liar's comfort."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#270","summary":"People think that telling the truth is not always essential."},{"id":"writing-task-2-160","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Greed, selfishness and going back to old values","prompt":"Today, people are getting greedy and selfish. Some people argue that we should go back to the old days, and show respect for the family and community, so that we can make the world a better place to live in. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is often said that people today are more selfish than previous generations, and that the remedy is to return to older habits of respect for family and community. I largely agree. Individual ambition has brought real benefits, but the weakening of family and neighbourhood ties has left many people materially better off and noticeably more alone.\n\nThe clearest effect is on family life. When every member of a household pursues a separate career and separate interests, the family becomes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a set of overlapping schedules rather than a shared life</strong>, and the practical exchange it once depended on breaks down: grandparents no longer help with childcare, and adult children are less willing to look after elderly parents. The greater loss is harder to see, because families supply emotional support <strong class=\"key-phrase\">that no paid service can replace</strong>, and people who face illness, redundancy or bereavement without it recover far more slowly.\n\nCommunity life has weakened in the same way. Neighbours who have never spoken will not notice that an elderly resident has not opened her curtains for two days, and volunteering, local sports clubs and residents' associations all depend on people who are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">willing to give time for no personal return</strong>. Selfishness has a commercial form too: a company that raises short-term profit by selling unnecessary products or quietly reducing quality <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treats its customers as a resource rather than as neighbours</strong>, and once that attitude becomes normal, the trust between strangers that makes a city workable begins to disappear.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that greater respect for family and community would improve modern life, although this does not require returning to the past. Keeping in regular contact with relatives, giving a few hours to a local organisation and knowing the people in one's own street cost very little individually, yet together they rebuild <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of community</strong> and the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">social cohesion</strong> that competitive, individualistic societies steadily wear away.","phrases":[{"en":"a sense of community","context":"Keeping in regular contact with relatives, giving a few hours to a local organisation and knowing the people in one's own street cost very little individually, yet together they rebuild a sense of community and the social cohesion that competitive, individualistic societies steadily wear away."},{"en":"social cohesion","context":"Keeping in regular contact with relatives, giving a few hours to a local organisation and knowing the people in one's own street cost very little individually, yet together they rebuild a sense of community and the social cohesion that competitive, individualistic societies steadily wear away."},{"en":"a set of overlapping schedules rather than a shared life","context":"When every member of a household pursues a separate career and separate interests, the family becomes a set of overlapping schedules rather than a shared life, and the practical exchange it once depended on breaks down: grandparents no longer help with childcare, and adult children are less willi..."},{"en":"that no paid service can replace","context":"The greater loss is harder to see, because families supply emotional support that no paid service can replace, and people who face illness, redundancy or bereavement without it recover far more slowly."},{"en":"willing to give time for no personal return","context":"Neighbours who have never spoken will not notice that an elderly resident has not opened her curtains for two days, and volunteering, local sports clubs and residents' associations all depend on people who are willing to give time for no personal return."},{"en":"treats its customers as a resource rather than as neighbours","context":"Selfishness has a commercial form too: a company that raises short-term profit by selling unnecessary products or quietly reducing quality treats its customers as a resource rather than as neighbours, and once that attitude becomes normal, the trust between strangers that makes a city workable be..."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#271","summary":"Today, people are getting greedy and selfish."},{"id":"writing-task-2-2217","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Why people move home more often","prompt":"People used to live in the same city throughout their lives, but now they change where they live several times. What is the cause? Do you think it is a positive or negative trend?","answer":"A generation ago it was normal to be born, work and die in the same town. Today many people move several times, and I regard this as a broadly positive change despite the strain it places on communities.\n\nThe main cause is the way work has changed. Employment is no longer tied to a single local industry, and careers now advance by moving between firms rather than climbing within one, so a graduate may follow openings to three cities in a decade. Housing costs reinforce this: when young families are priced out of the city where they work, they relocate to a cheaper region and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accept a longer commute</strong>. Cheap transport and video calling have also made distance less painful, because a move no longer means losing contact with parents and old friends.\n\nThe clearest benefit is that people can go where their skills are actually wanted. A nurse or an engineer who is unemployed in one region can be in demand in another, and allowing that movement raises both individual incomes and national productivity. Moving also broadens people. Someone who has lived in three different places tends to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hold their assumptions more loosely</strong>, having seen more than one way of organising ordinary life.\n\nThe cost falls on the places left behind and on the movers themselves. Towns that lose their young people are left with an ageing population and closing schools, while those who move repeatedly may find that they know their neighbours only slightly and have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no one to call on</strong> in a crisis.\n\nIn conclusion, mobility is driven mainly by where work and affordable housing are, and on balance it is positive, because the freedom to move is what allows people to build a life that suits them rather than one <strong class=\"key-phrase\">decided by birthplace</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"accept a longer commute","context":"Housing costs reinforce this: when young families are priced out of the city where they work, they relocate to a cheaper region and accept a longer commute."},{"en":"hold their assumptions more loosely","context":"Someone who has lived in three different places tends to hold their assumptions more loosely, having seen more than one way of organising ordinary life."},{"en":"no one to call on","context":"Towns that lose their young people are left with an ageing population and closing schools, while those who move repeatedly may find that they know their neighbours only slightly and have no one to call on in a crisis."},{"en":"decided by birthplace","context":"In conclusion, mobility is driven mainly by where work and affordable housing are, and on balance it is positive, because the freedom to move is what allows people to build a life that suits them rather than one decided by birthplace."}],"subtype":"Causes / Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#272","summary":"People changing where they live several times in a lifetime"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2226","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"The demand for instant results","prompt":"Nowadays more and more people want to get things done instantly, whether that is a service, information or a task. Why is this? Do you think this is a positive or negative development?","answer":"People now expect a delivery within hours, an answer within seconds and a reply to a message almost immediately. This expectation has been created by technology itself, and although speed brings genuine benefits, I regard the trend as negative overall.\n\nThe main cause is that a small number of companies have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made instant service normal and everything else feel broken</strong>. Once a search engine returns results in a fraction of a second and a film starts playing the moment it is selected, a two-day wait for a bank transfer <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feels like an error rather than a process</strong>. Competition then spreads the standard, because a retailer offering delivery in three days simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">loses customers to a faster rival</strong>. Working life pushes in the same direction: employees are reachable at all hours, so a message sent in the evening is expected to be answered in the evening.\n\nThe advantages are real. Instant access to information saves an enormous amount of ordinary time, and services that once required a morning in a queue now take a minute, which matters most to people who cannot easily take time off work.\n\nMy concern is with what speed displaces. Work that is genuinely valuable — writing, research, learning a skill, recovering from illness — cannot be accelerated, and a population trained to expect immediate results becomes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unwilling to persist</strong> when something takes months. Decisions made quickly are also made badly, and the pressure to respond at once leaves people permanently on call and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">measurably more anxious</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the expectation of instant results comes from technology setting a standard that then spreads to everything else. I consider it a negative development, because the things that most improve a life are precisely the ones that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot be hurried</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"loses customers to a faster rival","context":"Competition then spreads the standard, because a retailer offering delivery in three days simply loses customers to a faster rival."},{"en":"unwilling to persist","context":"Work that is genuinely valuable — writing, research, learning a skill, recovering from illness — cannot be accelerated, and a population trained to expect immediate results becomes unwilling to persist when something takes months."},{"en":"cannot be hurried","context":"In conclusion, the expectation of instant results comes from technology setting a standard that then spreads to everything else. I consider it a negative development, because the things that most improve a life are precisely the ones that cannot be hurried."},{"en":"made instant service normal and everything else feel broken","context":"The main cause is that a small number of companies have made instant service normal and everything else feel broken."},{"en":"feels like an error rather than a process","context":"Once a search engine returns results in a fraction of a second and a film starts playing the moment it is selected, a two-day wait for a bank transfer feels like an error rather than a process."},{"en":"measurably more anxious","context":"Decisions made quickly are also made badly, and the pressure to respond at once leaves people permanently on call and measurably more anxious."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#273","summary":"Why people increasingly expect things to be done instantly"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2262","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"The gap between generations","prompt":"Nowadays in many countries young people do not communicate with older people as much as they used to. Why is this happening? How could this situation be improved?","answer":"Contact between generations has weakened in many societies, and older people now report far fewer conversations with anyone under thirty. The causes are structural rather than personal, and they can be reduced by rebuilding the places where the two groups used to meet.\n\nThe main cause is that families no longer live together. Where three generations once shared a house or a street, young adults now move to cities for work while parents and grandparents remain behind, so contact becomes an occasional visit rather than a daily habit. Housing costs push in the same direction, since few young families can afford a home large enough for an older relative.\n\nA second cause is that daily life has separated by age. Schools, workplaces and retirement housing each contain a single generation, and much social contact has moved onto platforms that older people use least, so a young person may pass an entire week <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without speaking to anyone over fifty</strong>. Both sides then find conversation harder, and each assumes the other <strong class=\"key-phrase\">has nothing in common with them</strong>.\n\nThe remedies follow from the causes. Schools can arrange regular visits in which older residents talk about local history or listen to children read, which gives both a reason to meet rather than a duty. Housing policy matters as well: developments that place family homes and retirement flats around a shared garden produce contact <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as a by-product of ordinary life</strong>. Volunteering schemes that pair students with older neighbours for shopping or technology help work in the same way.\n\nIn conclusion, the distance between generations has grown because families have dispersed and daily life is now organised by age. It narrows whenever the two groups are given <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a shared task rather than an appointment</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"without speaking to anyone over fifty","context":"Schools, workplaces and retirement housing each contain a single generation, and much social contact has moved onto platforms that older people use least, so a young person may pass an entire week without speaking to anyone over fifty."},{"en":"has nothing in common with them","context":"Both sides then find conversation harder, and each assumes the other has nothing in common with them."},{"en":"as a by-product of ordinary life","context":"Housing policy matters as well: developments that place family homes and retirement flats around a shared garden produce contact as a by-product of ordinary life."},{"en":"a shared task rather than an appointment","context":"In conclusion, the distance between generations has grown because families have dispersed and daily life is now organised by age. It narrows whenever the two groups are given a shared task rather than an appointment."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#274","summary":"Why young people communicate less with older people"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2265","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Is competitiveness a good quality?","prompt":"In many societies, competitiveness is regarded as an important personal quality. How does this affect individuals? Do you think this is a beneficial or harmful trend?","answer":"Many cultures treat the desire to outperform others as a virtue and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reward it from primary school onwards</strong>. The effects on individuals run in both directions, and I regard the trend as harmful once competition becomes the main measure of a person.\n\nThe positive effects are worth acknowledging. Competition supplies motivation that is difficult to generate any other way, since most people work harder when their performance is visible and compared. It also produces useful information: an athlete or a researcher discovers where they actually stand, which allows them to improve rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">assume they are already good enough</strong>. Societies that reward achievement rather than family connections also allow talented people to rise from unpromising beginnings.\n\nThe harmful effects appear when <strong class=\"key-phrase\">competitiveness becomes an identity</strong>. A person who measures their worth by ranking is anxious whenever they are not winning, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">since there is always somebody ahead</strong>, the anxiety is permanent. This shows in the mental health of students in the most competitive education systems, where <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rates of exhaustion and depression are highest</strong>. Highly competitive individuals also cooperate poorly, because sharing an idea with a colleague means giving away an advantage, and most valuable work now depends on precisely that sharing.\n\nThere is a further cost. Competition rewards what can be measured, so people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">abandon work that matters but cannot be scored</strong>, and choose safe targets they are confident of hitting rather than difficult problems where they might <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fail in front of others</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, competitiveness raises effort and identifies talent, and a society without it would stagnate. The current emphasis is nonetheless harmful, because treating comparison as the measure of a person produces anxious individuals who cannot work together and who avoid the risks that real progress requires.","phrases":[{"en":"assume they are already good enough","context":"It also produces useful information: an athlete or a researcher discovers where they actually stand, which allows them to improve rather than assume they are already good enough."},{"en":"rates of exhaustion and depression are highest","context":"This shows in the mental health of students in the most competitive education systems, where rates of exhaustion and depression are highest."},{"en":"fail in front of others","context":"There is a further cost. Competition rewards what can be measured, so people abandon work that matters but cannot be scored, and choose safe targets they are confident of hitting rather than difficult problems where they might fail in front of others."},{"en":"reward it from primary school onwards","context":"Many cultures treat the desire to outperform others as a virtue and reward it from primary school onwards. The effects on individuals run in both directions, and I regard the trend as harmful once competition becomes the main measure of a person."},{"en":"competitiveness becomes an identity","context":"The harmful effects appear when competitiveness becomes an identity."},{"en":"since there is always somebody ahead","context":"A person who measures their worth by ranking is anxious whenever they are not winning, and since there is always somebody ahead, the anxiety is permanent."},{"en":"abandon work that matters but cannot be scored","context":"There is a further cost. Competition rewards what can be measured, so people abandon work that matters but cannot be scored, and choose safe targets they are confident of hitting rather than difficult problems where they might fail in front of others."}],"subtype":"Effects / Beneficial or Harmful","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#275","summary":"How valuing competitiveness affects individuals"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2267","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Is life better than it used to be?","prompt":"People living in the 21st century have a better quality of life than people living in previous centuries. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes claimed that modern life, for all its conveniences, is more stressful and less satisfying than life in earlier times. I disagree with this view, because the improvements in the conditions that determine whether a life goes well have been extraordinary.\n\nThe most important change is that people survive. Global life expectancy has roughly doubled since 1900, and the fall in child mortality means that most parents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no longer expect to bury a child</strong>, which was a normal experience for previous generations. Diseases that once killed routinely are now prevented by vaccination, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">injuries that were fatal are treated in an afternoon</strong>. Any comparison that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overlooks this outweighs everything else</strong>.\n\nMaterial and social conditions have improved alongside. The share of the world living in extreme poverty has fallen sharply within a single lifetime, literacy is close to universal in most countries, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work that once destroyed bodies by forty</strong> is now done by machines. Groups who were legally excluded for centuries — women, minorities, people with disabilities — have rights that would have been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unimaginable to their great-grandparents</strong>.\n\nThe honest objection is that some things have deteriorated. Anxiety and loneliness are widespread, communities are looser than they were, and previous centuries did not face the possibility of environmental collapse. These are serious and should not be dismissed.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree strongly with any suggestion that earlier centuries offered a better life. Modern societies have real problems of isolation and environmental risk, and solving them matters, but they are the difficulties of people who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expect to live into their eighties</strong> — a position almost nobody in history would have refused.","phrases":[{"en":"overlooks this outweighs everything else","context":"Any comparison that overlooks this outweighs everything else."},{"en":"unimaginable to their great-grandparents","context":"Groups who were legally excluded for centuries — women, minorities, people with disabilities — have rights that would have been unimaginable to their great-grandparents."},{"en":"expect to live into their eighties","context":"Modern societies have real problems of isolation and environmental risk, and solving them matters, but they are the difficulties of people who expect to live into their eighties — a position almost nobody in history would have refused."},{"en":"no longer expect to bury a child","context":"Global life expectancy has roughly doubled since 1900, and the fall in child mortality means that most parents no longer expect to bury a child, which was a normal experience for previous generations."},{"en":"injuries that were fatal are treated in an afternoon","context":"Diseases that once killed routinely are now prevented by vaccination, and injuries that were fatal are treated in an afternoon."},{"en":"work that once destroyed bodies by forty","context":"The share of the world living in extreme poverty has fallen sharply within a single lifetime, literacy is close to universal in most countries, and work that once destroyed bodies by forty is now done by machines."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#276","summary":"Quality of life today compared with previous centuries"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2307","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"What actually produces success?","prompt":"Some people believe that success is the result of hard work and determination. Others think that there are more important factors, such as money and appearance. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Whether success is earned or inherited is one of the oldest arguments about how societies work, and both answers can be supported with plenty of examples. My own view is that effort decides what a person does with an opportunity, while money and appearance largely decide which opportunities arrive.\n\nThe case for effort is supported by what any career actually requires. Skill in medicine, music or business is built through years of practice that nobody enjoys, and people who begin with every advantage still fail without it. Determination also explains recovery, since almost every successful career contains a serious setback, and the difference between those who continue and those who stop is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">willingness to start again after a failure</strong>. Believing that effort matters is itself useful, because people who think outcomes are fixed stop trying.\n\nThe opposing view is uncomfortable but well evidenced. Wealth buys tutoring, unpaid internships, useful contacts and the ability to take risks without ruin, so two equally able children finish in very different places. Appearance also has measurable effects on hiring, promotion and pay, and connections open doors that no application will. Anyone who insists that success is purely earned has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">usually not looked closely at where they began</strong>.\n\nThe two combine rather than compete. Advantages of money and appearance move a person nearer the front of the queue, but they do not complete the work, and privileged people who do nothing are visible in every field. Equally, effort without opportunity is frequently wasted, which is why talented people in poor countries or poor districts <strong class=\"key-phrase\">achieve less than mediocre people elsewhere</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, hard work is necessary and explains why some people succeed while others with the same advantages do not. Money, contacts and appearance decide how far that work carries, so a fair society is one that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduces how much the starting point matters</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"willingness to start again after a failure","context":"Determination also explains recovery, since almost every successful career contains a serious setback, and the difference between those who continue and those who stop is willingness to start again after a failure."},{"en":"usually not looked closely at where they began","context":"Anyone who insists that success is purely earned has usually not looked closely at where they began."},{"en":"achieve less than mediocre people elsewhere","context":"Equally, effort without opportunity is frequently wasted, which is why talented people in poor countries or poor districts achieve less than mediocre people elsewhere."},{"en":"reduces how much the starting point matters","context":"In conclusion, hard work is necessary and explains why some people succeed while others with the same advantages do not. Money, contacts and appearance decide how far that work carries, so a fair society is one that reduces how much the starting point matters."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#277","summary":"Whether success comes from effort or from money and appearance"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2311","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Is ambition a good quality?","prompt":"Some people think that having ambition is a good quality. Is it important to be ambitious? Is it a positive or negative characteristic?","answer":"Ambition is praised in job interviews and warned against in old proverbs. It is genuinely important for anyone who wants to achieve something demanding, and it is a positive quality up to the point where the goal begins to matter more than the person pursuing it.\n\nAmbition matters because difficult things take years and offer little reward along the way. A surgeon studies for a decade, a novelist writes badly before writing well, and neither continues on enjoyment alone. What carries a person through the dull middle of any long project is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a picture of where the effort leads</strong>. Ambition also raises the target: people tend to achieve roughly what they aim at, so those who aim low finish low, and quiet ability without direction usually goes nowhere in particular.\n\nIt is a positive characteristic for the same reason. Ambitious individuals learn faster, because they seek out criticism instead of avoiding it, and societies depend on them, since medicine, engineering and the arts advance through people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unwilling to accept the current limit</strong>.\n\nThe quality turns harmful when it detaches from any purpose beyond status. A person who wants promotion rather than the work will take credit, hide mistakes and treat colleagues as obstacles. Ambition of that kind also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">makes satisfaction permanently unavailable</strong>, since each achievement resets the target, and the years spent chasing it are usually taken from health and family. Its worst form is measuring yourself entirely against other people.\n\nIn conclusion, ambition is important and largely positive, because sustained effort needs a destination. It becomes damaging when the goal is standing rather than substance, so the useful version is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ambition attached to the work itself</strong> rather than to the recognition it brings.","phrases":[{"en":"a picture of where the effort leads","context":"What carries a person through the dull middle of any long project is a picture of where the effort leads."},{"en":"unwilling to accept the current limit","context":"Ambitious individuals learn faster, because they seek out criticism instead of avoiding it, and societies depend on them, since medicine, engineering and the arts advance through people unwilling to accept the current limit."},{"en":"makes satisfaction permanently unavailable","context":"Ambition of that kind also makes satisfaction permanently unavailable, since each achievement resets the target, and the years spent chasing it are usually taken from health and family."},{"en":"ambition attached to the work itself","context":"It becomes damaging when the goal is standing rather than substance, so the useful version is ambition attached to the work itself rather than to the recognition it brings."}],"subtype":"Two-Part Question","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#278","summary":"How much ambition matters for success and whether it is a positive trait"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2316","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Film or documents for learning about the past?","prompt":"It is better to learn about the way people lived in the past through films and video records than through written documents. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Documentaries and archive footage now reach far more people than any history book, and they clearly teach something a page cannot. I disagree that they are the better source overall, though the two work best together.\n\nFilm has genuine advantages. It shows what writing can only describe: how a street sounded, how quickly people walked, what a kitchen actually contained. Details of daily life that a diarist never bothered to record survive in the background of a recording, and viewers remember them because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">images stay in the mind longer than sentences</strong>. Film also reaches those who would never open a scholarly volume, and recorded interviews preserve the voices of people who left nothing written at all.\n\nIts weaknesses are equally clear. Recording covers barely a century, so anything earlier must be reconstructed by actors, and a costume drama tells us mainly what its own decade believed. Film is also shaped by whoever holds the camera: it selects, edits and adds music, and the result feels like evidence while being an argument. Written sources are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easier to check against one another</strong>, since a reader can compare accounts, weigh a letter against an official record and see where the writer had reason to lie.\n\nDocuments also carry what film cannot. Wills, tax records and court papers reveal what ordinary people owned and quarrelled over, and letters show what they thought, which no camera can film. History depends on that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">slow comparison of unglamorous material</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, film brings the past close and should be used widely, particularly for the recent period it genuinely covers. Written records remain the stronger foundation, because they reach further back and can be tested, and the sensible approach is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to use film for texture and documents for evidence</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"images stay in the mind longer than sentences","context":"Details of daily life that a diarist never bothered to record survive in the background of a recording, and viewers remember them because images stay in the mind longer than sentences."},{"en":"easier to check against one another","context":"Written sources are easier to check against one another, since a reader can compare accounts, weigh a letter against an official record and see where the writer had reason to lie."},{"en":"slow comparison of unglamorous material","context":"Documents also carry what film cannot. Wills, tax records and court papers reveal what ordinary people owned and quarrelled over, and letters show what they thought, which no camera can film. History depends on that slow comparison of unglamorous material."},{"en":"to use film for texture and documents for evidence","context":"Written records remain the stronger foundation, because they reach further back and can be tested, and the sensible approach is to use film for texture and documents for evidence."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#279","summary":"Whether films and recordings teach history better than written documents"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2319","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"A growing population of children","prompt":"In some countries, the number of children aged 15 and younger is increasing dramatically. What are the current and future effects of this growing population?","answer":"In parts of Africa and South Asia, children now make up close to half the population. The immediate effects fall on services and family budgets, while the longer-term consequences depend almost entirely on what those children receive today.\n\nThe present effects are felt first in schools and clinics. Classrooms fill faster than they can be built, so teaching quality falls even where enrolment rises, and paediatric wards and vaccination programmes come under similar strain. Households feel it too: a family with six children spends everything it earns on food and school fees, which leaves nothing to save or invest, and older girls are often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">kept at home to look after the younger ones</strong>. Governments face the same squeeze at national level, since money spent on expanding basic services cannot go into roads or industry.\n\nThe future effects divide sharply. If these children are educated and healthy, the country receives what economists call a demographic dividend: a large working population supporting comparatively few dependents, which is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the condition behind almost every rapid rise in national income</strong>. Several Asian economies grew precisely this way.\n\nIf the investment is not made, the same numbers become a burden. Millions of young adults will reach working age without qualifications and without jobs waiting for them, and large groups of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unemployed young men in crowded cities</strong> are associated with unrest and with heavy migration. The population will also age eventually, so a country that fails to build wealth while its workers are numerous will meet that stage poor.\n\nIn conclusion, a rapidly growing child population strains schools, health services and family finances now. Whether it later becomes an advantage or a crisis is decided by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what is invested in those children before they grow up</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"kept at home to look after the younger ones","context":"Households feel it too: a family with six children spends everything it earns on food and school fees, which leaves nothing to save or invest, and older girls are often kept at home to look after the younger ones."},{"en":"the condition behind almost every rapid rise in national income","context":"If these children are educated and healthy, the country receives what economists call a demographic dividend: a large working population supporting comparatively few dependents, which is the condition behind almost every rapid rise in national income."},{"en":"unemployed young men in crowded cities","context":"Millions of young adults will reach working age without qualifications and without jobs waiting for them, and large groups of unemployed young men in crowded cities are associated with unrest and with heavy migration."},{"en":"what is invested in those children before they grow up","context":"In conclusion, a rapidly growing child population strains schools, health services and family finances now. Whether it later becomes an advantage or a crisis is decided by what is invested in those children before they grow up."}],"subtype":"Effects","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#280","summary":"The present and future effects of a rapidly rising number of children"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2366","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"What would make a society perfect?","prompt":"People have always imagined living in a perfect society, but there is no clear agreement on what that would look like. What do you think is the most important factor in creating a perfect society? How can this ideal be achieved?","answer":"Descriptions of the ideal society differ enormously, but most of them assume something they rarely state. In my view the essential factor is trust, and it is built through institutions that treat people equally rather than through any grand design.\n\nTrust deserves first place because everything else depends on it. Where people expect honesty, contracts are cheap to make, taxes are paid without heavy enforcement, strangers help each other and neighbours leave their children to play outside. Where trust is absent, the same society needs guards, lawyers, locks and bribes, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wealth is spent protecting people from each other</strong>. Countries that score highly on trust also report the highest satisfaction, regardless of climate or size, which suggests it matters more than prosperity alone.\n\nTrust rests on two conditions. The first is that rules apply to everybody, since nothing destroys confidence faster than watching the powerful escape consequences that fall on ordinary people. The second is that inequality remains within limits people regard as fair, because a society divided into groups that share no schools, hospitals or streets <strong class=\"key-phrase\">loses the experience of having anything in common</strong>.\n\nAchieving this is slow work rather than a single reform. Courts and police must be independent enough to convict the influential, and corruption must be prosecuted visibly. Public services that everyone uses — schools, transport, health care — keep different groups in contact, and taxation must be seen to be collected fairly from those best able to pay. Education plays a part by teaching children to disagree without treating opponents as enemies.\n\nIn conclusion, no society will be perfect, and the attempt to design one has usually ended badly. A society where people can reasonably trust each other and the rules comes closest, and it is built by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">enforcing fairness consistently over a long period</strong> rather than by any single change.","phrases":[{"en":"wealth is spent protecting people from each other","context":"Where trust is absent, the same society needs guards, lawyers, locks and bribes, and wealth is spent protecting people from each other."},{"en":"loses the experience of having anything in common","context":"The second is that inequality remains within limits people regard as fair, because a society divided into groups that share no schools, hospitals or streets loses the experience of having anything in common."},{"en":"enforcing fairness consistently over a long period","context":"A society where people can reasonably trust each other and the rules comes closest, and it is built by enforcing fairness consistently over a long period rather than by any single change."}],"subtype":"Two-Part Question","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#281","summary":"The most important element of an ideal society and how to move towards it"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2368","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Why people are rarely at home","prompt":"Today, many people spend less and less time at home. What are the reasons for this? What are the effects on individuals and on society?","answer":"Homes in many cities now stand empty for most of the day. The reasons are largely economic, and the effects reach both individual health and the character of neighbourhoods.\n\nWork explains most of it. Housing costs have risen faster than wages, so households increasingly need two incomes and often more than one job each, and the same cost pushes families to live far from where they work, adding hours of commuting to an already long day. In many countries staying late is also expected regardless of output. Beyond work, life has moved outwards: shopping, eating, exercising and socialising all happen in commercial spaces rather than at home, and children's activities are organised and located elsewhere. Small flats contribute as well, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a home too cramped to relax in becomes somewhere to sleep</strong>.\n\nThe effects on individuals are mostly negative. Long days away shorten sleep and leave little time for cooking, which shows in diet and weight. Family relationships suffer in a quieter way, since parents and children pass each other rather than eat together, and much of what children learn from adults happens in unhurried time that no longer exists. Eating and entertaining outside the home is also expensive, so households <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spend more precisely because they are at home less</strong>.\n\nSociety feels it too. Neighbourhoods where nobody is present during the day lose their informal supervision, so streets feel less safe and elderly residents go unchecked. Local shops close for lack of daytime customers, and community activity depends on volunteers who no longer have the hours. There is one encouraging countercurrent, since working from home has begun to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">return some daytime life to residential streets</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, people are absent from home mainly because of long hours and long journeys forced by the cost of housing. The consequences are worse health, thinner family life and neighbourhoods that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">empty out for most of the day</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"a home too cramped to relax in becomes somewhere to sleep","context":"Small flats contribute as well, since a home too cramped to relax in becomes somewhere to sleep."},{"en":"spend more precisely because they are at home less","context":"Eating and entertaining outside the home is also expensive, so households spend more precisely because they are at home less."},{"en":"return some daytime life to residential streets","context":"There is one encouraging countercurrent, since working from home has begun to return some daytime life to residential streets."},{"en":"empty out for most of the day","context":"In conclusion, people are absent from home mainly because of long hours and long journeys forced by the cost of housing. The consequences are worse health, thinner family life and neighbourhoods that empty out for most of the day."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Effects","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#282","summary":"The reasons people spend less time at home and the effects of this"},{"id":"writing-task-2-034","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"The advantages and disadvantages of sport","prompt":"Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of playing sport and taking regular physical exercise, and give your own opinion.","answer":"Playing sport and taking regular exercise are widely encouraged, though both carry certain costs. In my view the advantages are considerably greater than the disadvantages.\n\nThe benefits to health are the clearest. Regular physical activity reduces the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes, stroke and several cancers, and it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of the most effective treatments available for anxiety and mild depression</strong>. It also protects people in later life, since those who have exercised consistently <strong class=\"key-phrase\">retain mobility and independence</strong> far longer than those who have not.\n\nSport also develops qualities that transfer to other areas of life. Training for a competition teaches discipline and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the acceptance that improvement is gradual</strong>, while team sports require cooperation and the ability to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lose without resentment</strong>. For children in particular, a club provides <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a structured environment and adult supervision outside school hours</strong>, and it is one of the few settings where they meet people from different backgrounds on equal terms.\n\nThere are genuine drawbacks. Injuries are common, and contact sports such as rugby and boxing carry a risk of lasting damage, including to the brain. Serious training also demands time and money that a family may not have, and excessive competition can produce eating disorders or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an unhealthy obsession with results</strong>, especially among young athletes pushed too hard.\n\nIn conclusion, sport carries real risks of injury and, at a competitive level, considerable psychological pressure. For the great majority of people, however, these drawbacks are far outweighed by the benefits to physical health, mental well-being and social development, and they can be reduced further by sensible coaching.","phrases":[{"en":"retain mobility and independence","context":"It also protects people in later life, since those who have exercised consistently retain mobility and independence far longer than those who have not."},{"en":"lose without resentment","context":"Training for a competition teaches discipline and the acceptance that improvement is gradual, while team sports require cooperation and the ability to lose without resentment."},{"en":"one of the most effective treatments available for anxiety and mild depression","context":"Regular physical activity reduces the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes, stroke and several cancers, and it is one of the most effective treatments available for anxiety and mild depression."},{"en":"the acceptance that improvement is gradual","context":"Training for a competition teaches discipline and the acceptance that improvement is gradual, while team sports require cooperation and the ability to lose without resentment."},{"en":"a structured environment and adult supervision outside school hours","context":"For children in particular, a club provides a structured environment and adult supervision outside school hours, and it is one of the few settings where they meet people from different backgrounds on equal terms."},{"en":"an unhealthy obsession with results","context":"Serious training also demands time and money that a family may not have, and excessive competition can produce eating disorders or an unhealthy obsession with results, especially among young athletes pushed too hard."}],"subtype":"Advantages & Disadvantages","_displayNo":"#283","summary":"The advantages and disadvantages of sport"},{"id":"writing-task-2-105","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"The future of major sporting events","prompt":"International sporting events such as the Olympic Games are becoming increasingly expensive to host. Some people believe that they will gradually lose their importance. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"International sporting events such as the Olympic Games now cost host cities billions of dollars, and some people predict that they will gradually lose their importance. I disagree with this view, although I accept that the way such events are organised will have to change.\n\nThere are certainly reasons to be pessimistic. Recent Games have left several host cities with enormous debts and stadiums that stand empty afterwards, and a number of cities have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">withdrawn their bids after residents voted against them</strong>. Doping scandals and disputes over political influence have also damaged public confidence. Meanwhile, audiences can now watch elite sport of every kind at any time, so a single event <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no longer feels as rare as it once did</strong>.\n\nNevertheless, the underlying appeal remains extremely strong. The Olympic Games attract billions of viewers because they offer something no ordinary competition can: the world's best athletes, in dozens of sports, competing under a national flag every four years. Broadcasters and sponsors <strong class=\"key-phrase\">continue to pay record sums for the rights</strong>, which is the clearest evidence that interest has not declined. These events also give minority sports such as fencing and rowing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">their only global audience</strong>.\n\nWhat is likely to change is the model rather than the event itself. Future competitions will probably use existing venues, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">be shared between several cities</strong> and be organised on a far more modest budget, as recent hosts have already begun to do.\n\nIn conclusion, I do not believe that major international sporting events will fade away. Their financial structure will have to become more realistic, but their appeal to audiences <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shows no sign of weakening</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"their only global audience","context":"These events also give minority sports such as fencing and rowing their only global audience."},{"en":"shows no sign of weakening","context":"In conclusion, I do not believe that major international sporting events will fade away. Their financial structure will have to become more realistic, but their appeal to audiences shows no sign of weakening."},{"en":"withdrawn their bids after residents voted against them","context":"Recent Games have left several host cities with enormous debts and stadiums that stand empty afterwards, and a number of cities have withdrawn their bids after residents voted against them."},{"en":"no longer feels as rare as it once did","context":"Meanwhile, audiences can now watch elite sport of every kind at any time, so a single event no longer feels as rare as it once did."},{"en":"continue to pay record sums for the rights","context":"Broadcasters and sponsors continue to pay record sums for the rights, which is the clearest evidence that interest has not declined."},{"en":"be shared between several cities","context":"What is likely to change is the model rather than the event itself. Future competitions will probably use existing venues, be shared between several cities and be organised on a far more modest budget, as recent hosts have already begun to do."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#284","summary":"The future of major sporting events"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Why athletes use banned substances","prompt":"In many professional sports, there is an increase in the number of athletes using banned substances to improve their performance. What are the causes of the phenomenon and what are some of the possible solutions?","answer":"The use of banned performance-enhancing drugs is increasing in some professional sports. The main causes are intense financial pressure, weak detection and a culture in which winning is valued above long-term health. Solutions must address both athletes and the organisations around them.\n\nElite competitors may believe that a small physical advantage can determine contracts, sponsorship and selection. If they suspect that rivals are already using drugs, they may feel forced to do the same. Coaches, doctors or managers can also encourage abuse when their own careers depend on results. This creates <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a powerful incentive to take hidden risks</strong>.\n\nSports bodies should use independent testing, share information internationally and impose penalties on support staff as well as athletes. Testing should be frequent and unpredictable, with samples stored for later analysis as technology improves. At the same time, athletes need confidential medical advice and clear education about long-term harm. Whistle-blowers should receive protection so that organised doping systems are easier to expose.\n\nThe financial pressure that drives athletes to dope in the first place also needs to be addressed directly. Sponsors and broadcasters could link the size of their contracts to a federation's testing record rather than only to results, giving the sport itself a commercial reason to catch cheats rather than protect its stars. Funding for retired or injured athletes, whose careers can otherwise end overnight, would also reduce the desperation that pushes some competitors toward a short-lived advantage.\n\nIn conclusion, punishment alone will not solve the problem. Sport must reduce the pressure that rewards cheating and increase the chance that abuse is detected. A combination of independent oversight, education and protection for honest athletes can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">restore trust in competition</strong>.","topic":"Sport & Leisure","phrases":[{"en":"a powerful incentive to take hidden risks","context":"This creates a powerful incentive to take hidden risks."},{"en":"restore trust in competition","context":"A combination of independent oversight, education and protection for honest athletes can restore trust in competition."}],"subtype":"problem","summary":"Why athletes use banned substances, and what can be done","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-122","_displayNo":"#285"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Natural talent against training","prompt":"It is generally believed that some people are born with certain talents, for instance for sport or music, and others are not. However, it is sometimes claimed that any child can be taught to become a good sports person or musician. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Natural ability can give people an early advantage in music or sport, but high performance is rarely the result of talent alone. I believe most people can reach a strong level through good teaching and sustained practice, although not everyone will become world-class.\n\nSome physical and mental characteristics are partly inherited. Height may help a basketball player, while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sensitive ear can make musical training easier</strong>. Children also differ in coordination, memory and speed of learning. These differences explain why two beginners may progress at different rates under the same conditions.\n\nNevertheless, talent has limited value without disciplined practice. Skilled performers repeat difficult tasks, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">receive detailed feedback and gradually correct weaknesses</strong>. Access to coaching, equipment and family support is often more important than people realise. A moderately talented child with excellent training may therefore outperform a gifted child who lacks opportunity or motivation. Practice can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turn potential into reliable performance</strong>.\n\nThe two views also interact in ways that are easy to overlook. A child judged to have no ear for music is offered fewer lessons and less encouragement, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the original judgement quietly produces the result it predicted</strong>. Youth sport shows the same effect: players born in the months just after the annual selection cut-off are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">heavily over-represented in professional academies</strong>, simply because they were bigger and stronger at the age of ten. Giving every child a long period of proper coaching therefore prevents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">early labels from limiting later development</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, people are not born with identical abilities, but talent should not be treated as a fixed limit. Schools and coaches should give learners time, expert guidance and realistic goals. This approach recognises natural differences while preserving the belief that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ability can be developed</strong>.","topic":"Sport & Leisure","phrases":[{"en":"turn potential into reliable performance","context":"Practice can turn potential into reliable performance."},{"en":"early labels from limiting later development","context":"Giving every child a long period of proper coaching therefore prevents early labels from limiting later development."},{"en":"ability can be developed","context":"This approach recognises natural differences while preserving the belief that ability can be developed."},{"en":"a sensitive ear can make musical training easier","context":"Height may help a basketball player, while a sensitive ear can make musical training easier."},{"en":"receive detailed feedback and gradually correct weaknesses","context":"Skilled performers repeat difficult tasks, receive detailed feedback and gradually correct weaknesses."},{"en":"the original judgement quietly produces the result it predicted","context":"A child judged to have no ear for music is offered fewer lessons and less encouragement, so the original judgement quietly produces the result it predicted."},{"en":"heavily over-represented in professional academies","context":"Youth sport shows the same effect: players born in the months just after the annual selection cut-off are heavily over-represented in professional academies, simply because they were bigger and stronger at the age of ten."}],"subtype":"discuss","summary":"Natural talent compared with training and practice","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-126","_displayNo":"#286"},{"id":"writing-task-2-131","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Do hobbies have to be difficult to be enjoyable?","prompt":"Some people believe that hobbies need to be difficult to be enjoyable. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Some hobbies are relatively easy, while others present more of a challenge. Personally, I believe that both types of hobby can be fun, and I therefore disagree with the statement that hobbies need to be difficult in order to be enjoyable.\n\nOn the one hand, many people enjoy easy hobbies. One example of an activity that is easy for most people is swimming. This hobby requires very little equipment, it is simple to learn, and it is inexpensive. I remember learning to swim at my local swimming pool when I was a child, and it never felt like a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">demanding or challenging experience</strong>. Another hobby that I find easy and fun is photography. In my opinion, anyone can take interesting pictures without knowing too much about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the technicalities of operating a camera</strong>. Despite being straightforward, taking photos is a satisfying activity.\n\nOn the other hand, difficult hobbies can sometimes be more exciting. If an activity is more challenging, we might feel <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a greater sense of satisfaction</strong> when we manage to do it successfully. For example, film editing is a hobby that requires <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a high level of knowledge and expertise</strong>. In my case, it took me around two years before I became competent at this activity, but now I enjoy it much more than I did when I started. I believe that many hobbies give us more pleasure when we reach a higher level of performance because the results are better and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the feeling of achievement</strong> is greater.\n\nIn conclusion, simple hobbies can be fun and relaxing, but difficult hobbies can be equally pleasurable for different reasons.","phrases":[{"en":"demanding or challenging experience","context":"I remember learning to swim at my local swimming pool when I was a child, and it never felt like a demanding or challenging experience."},{"en":"the technicalities of operating a camera","context":"In my opinion, anyone can take interesting pictures without knowing too much about the technicalities of operating a camera."},{"en":"a greater sense of satisfaction","context":"If an activity is more challenging, we might feel a greater sense of satisfaction when we manage to do it successfully."},{"en":"a high level of knowledge and expertise","context":"For example, film editing is a hobby that requires a high level of knowledge and expertise."},{"en":"the feeling of achievement","context":"I believe that many hobbies give us more pleasure when we reach a higher level of performance because the results are better and the feeling of achievement is greater."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#287","summary":"Do difficult hobbies bring more enjoyment?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-166","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Do video games do more harm than good?","prompt":"Some people regard video games as harmless fun, or even as a useful educational tool. Others, however, believe that videos games are having an adverse effect on the people who play them. In your opinion, do the drawbacks of video games outweigh the benefits?","answer":"Many people, and children in particular, enjoy playing computer games. While I accept that these games can sometimes have a positive effect on the user, I believe that they are more likely to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have a harmful impact</strong>.\n\nOn the one hand, video games can be both entertaining and educational. Users, or gamers, are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">transported into virtual worlds</strong> which are often more exciting and engaging than real-life pastimes. From an educational perspective, these games encourage imagination and creativity, as well as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">concentration, logical thinking and problem solving</strong>, all of which are useful skills outside the gaming context. Computer simulation games have also been shown to improve users’ motor skills and help to prepare them for real-world tasks, such as flying a plane.\n\nHowever, I would argue that these benefits are outweighed by the drawbacks. Gaming can be highly addictive because users are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">constantly given scores, new targets and frequent rewards</strong> to keep them playing. Many children now spend hours each day trying to progress through the levels of a game or to get a higher score than their friends. This type of addiction can have effects ranging from lack of sleep to problems at school, when <strong class=\"key-phrase\">homework is sacrificed for a few more hours</strong> on the computer or console. The rise in obesity in recent years has also been linked in part to the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sedentary lifestyle</strong> and lack of exercise that often accompany gaming addiction.\n\nIn conclusion, it seems to me that the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">potential dangers</strong> of video games are more significant than the possible benefits.","phrases":[{"en":"have a harmful impact","context":"Many people, and children in particular, enjoy playing computer games. While I accept that these games can sometimes have a positive effect on the user, I believe that they are more likely to have a harmful impact."},{"en":"sedentary lifestyle","context":"The rise in obesity in recent years has also been linked in part to the sedentary lifestyle and lack of exercise that often accompany gaming addiction."},{"en":"potential dangers","context":"In conclusion, it seems to me that the potential dangers of video games are more significant than the possible benefits."},{"en":"transported into virtual worlds","context":"Users, or gamers, are transported into virtual worlds which are often more exciting and engaging than real-life pastimes."},{"en":"concentration, logical thinking and problem solving","context":"From an educational perspective, these games encourage imagination and creativity, as well as concentration, logical thinking and problem solving, all of which are useful skills outside the gaming context."},{"en":"constantly given scores, new targets and frequent rewards","context":"Gaming can be highly addictive because users are constantly given scores, new targets and frequent rewards to keep them playing."},{"en":"homework is sacrificed for a few more hours","context":"This type of addiction can have effects ranging from lack of sleep to problems at school, when homework is sacrificed for a few more hours on the computer or console."}],"subtype":"Advantages outweigh disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#288","summary":"The benefits and harms of video games"},{"id":"writing-task-2-189","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Funding national athletes","prompt":"Some people believe that national sports teams and individual athletes who represent their country should be funded by the government. Others think they should be funded by private businesses and sponsorship. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"When athletes compete for their country, the cost of training and supporting them can be met either by the state or by private sponsors. In my opinion a combination works best, but public funding should remain the foundation.\n\nThe argument for private money is a practical one. Governments have limited budgets, and every pound spent on elite sport is a pound not spent on hospitals or schools. Companies, by contrast, are willing to pay large sums because international competition brings them valuable publicity, and sports such as football and motor racing survive comfortably without any public support at all. Sponsorship also rewards success quickly, since backers move their money towards athletes who perform.\n\nThe weakness of this model is that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sponsorship follows popularity rather than merit</strong>. A commercially attractive sport with a large television audience will attract more money than it needs, while archery, wrestling or the Paralympic disciplines may attract almost none. Talented competitors in minority sports would then be forced to abandon their careers, and a country's representation would effectively be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">decided by advertisers</strong>. Sponsors can also withdraw suddenly, or exert influence over which events an athlete enters.\n\nFor this reason I believe the state should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">guarantee a basic level of support</strong>, distributed according to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sporting potential rather than commercial appeal</strong>. Private money can then supplement it, particularly for athletes who already earn well.\n\nIn conclusion, sponsorship is a useful addition, but only public funding can ensure that athletes are selected on ability and that less popular sports continue to exist.","phrases":[{"en":"decided by advertisers","context":"Talented competitors in minority sports would then be forced to abandon their careers, and a country's representation would effectively be decided by advertisers."},{"en":"sporting potential rather than commercial appeal","context":"For this reason I believe the state should guarantee a basic level of support, distributed according to sporting potential rather than commercial appeal. Private money can then supplement it, particularly for athletes who already earn well."},{"en":"guarantee a basic level of support","context":"For this reason I believe the state should guarantee a basic level of support, distributed according to sporting potential rather than commercial appeal. Private money can then supplement it, particularly for athletes who already earn well."},{"en":"sponsorship follows popularity rather than merit","context":"The weakness of this model is that sponsorship follows popularity rather than merit."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#289","summary":"Funding national athletes"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2211","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Should extreme sports be banned?","prompt":"Some people believe that extreme sports like skydiving and skiing are too dangerous and should therefore be prohibited. Do you agree or disagree with this belief?","answer":"Activities such as skydiving, skiing and rock climbing carry a real risk of serious injury, and some people believe they should be banned for that reason. I disagree, because adults are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">entitled to take calculated risks with their own lives</strong>.\n\nThe principal argument against prohibition is one of personal freedom. In a free society, individuals are allowed to make choices that may harm themselves, and we do not ban smoking, alcohol or motorcycling, even though these kill far more people every year than extreme sports do. Participants are usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">well informed about the dangers</strong>, undergo training and use specialised safety equipment. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">A ban would therefore be inconsistent</strong>, and it would also be very hard to enforce, since people would simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practise without supervision</strong>, which is considerably more dangerous.\n\nThere are positive reasons to allow such sports as well. They demand high levels of fitness, discipline and concentration, and many participants describe <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of achievement</strong> that ordinary exercise does not provide. These sports also support a large industry of instructors, guides, equipment manufacturers and mountain resorts, on which many rural communities depend economically.\n\nI would accept that the risk should not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fall on other people</strong>. Rescue operations are expensive and endanger the emergency services, so it is entirely reasonable to require participants to hold insurance, to use licensed instructors and to obey safety rules, and to close areas when conditions are genuinely hazardous.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that extreme sports should be prohibited. Regulation, training and compulsory insurance are a far more sensible response than a ban on activities that adults freely choose.","phrases":[{"en":"well informed about the dangers","context":"Participants are usually well informed about the dangers, undergo training and use specialised safety equipment."},{"en":"a sense of achievement","context":"They demand high levels of fitness, discipline and concentration, and many participants describe a sense of achievement that ordinary exercise does not provide."},{"en":"fall on other people","context":"I would accept that the risk should not fall on other people."},{"en":"entitled to take calculated risks with their own lives","context":"Activities such as skydiving, skiing and rock climbing carry a real risk of serious injury, and some people believe they should be banned for that reason. I disagree, because adults are entitled to take calculated risks with their own lives."},{"en":"A ban would therefore be inconsistent","context":"A ban would therefore be inconsistent, and it would also be very hard to enforce, since people would simply practise without supervision, which is considerably more dangerous."},{"en":"practise without supervision","context":"A ban would therefore be inconsistent, and it would also be very hard to enforce, since people would simply practise without supervision, which is considerably more dangerous."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#290","summary":"Should extreme sports be banned?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2212","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Elite training centres or public sports facilities","prompt":"Some countries achieve international success by building specialised facilities to train top athletes instead of providing sports facilities that everyone can use. Is it a positive or negative development?","answer":"Some governments concentrate their sports budgets on specialised training centres for a small number of elite competitors, rather than on facilities that the general public can use. In my opinion this is a negative development, although it does bring certain benefits.\n\nThe advantages are not difficult to identify. Success at the Olympic Games or a World Cup <strong class=\"key-phrase\">generates national pride and international recognition</strong>, and it can encourage young people to take up a sport when they see a compatriot winning. Elite athletes also require specialist coaching, sports science and equipment that ordinary facilities cannot possibly provide, so without dedicated centres a country will not compete <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at the highest level</strong>.\n\nHowever, the costs of this approach are considerable. Elite programmes are extremely expensive and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">benefit a very small number of people</strong>, whereas the same money spent on local swimming pools, running tracks and sports halls would improve the health of an entire population. This matters because obesity, diabetes and heart disease are now among the largest <strong class=\"key-phrase\">burdens on public health budgets</strong>, and regular exercise is the cheapest way of reducing them. Spending public money on a handful of professional athletes while ordinary citizens have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nowhere affordable to exercise</strong> is difficult to justify.\n\nThere is also a flaw in the argument that elite success inspires participation. Studies following major sporting events have generally found little lasting increase in the number of people exercising, largely because those who are inspired discover that there is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no accessible pool or pitch nearby</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I regard this as a negative trend. Governments should fund facilities that serve the whole community, and support elite athletes from a much smaller share of the budget.","phrases":[{"en":"at the highest level","context":"Elite athletes also require specialist coaching, sports science and equipment that ordinary facilities cannot possibly provide, so without dedicated centres a country will not compete at the highest level."},{"en":"burdens on public health budgets","context":"This matters because obesity, diabetes and heart disease are now among the largest burdens on public health budgets, and regular exercise is the cheapest way of reducing them."},{"en":"generates national pride and international recognition","context":"Success at the Olympic Games or a World Cup generates national pride and international recognition, and it can encourage young people to take up a sport when they see a compatriot winning."},{"en":"benefit a very small number of people","context":"Elite programmes are extremely expensive and benefit a very small number of people, whereas the same money spent on local swimming pools, running tracks and sports halls would improve the health of an entire population."},{"en":"nowhere affordable to exercise","context":"Spending public money on a handful of professional athletes while ordinary citizens have nowhere affordable to exercise is difficult to justify."},{"en":"no accessible pool or pitch nearby","context":"Studies following major sporting events have generally found little lasting increase in the number of people exercising, largely because those who are inspired discover that there is no accessible pool or pitch nearby."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#291","summary":"Elite training centres or public sports facilities"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2224","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Hosting international sporting events","prompt":"Some people think hosting major international sporting events brings a lot of advantages to a country, while others believe there are more disadvantages. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Countries compete fiercely for the right to stage events such as the Olympic Games or the World Cup, yet many host cities are still paying for them a decade later. I believe such events can be worthwhile, but only for countries that already need the infrastructure they require.\n\nSupporters point to benefits beyond the sport. Hosting forces a government to complete transport and housing projects that might otherwise be delayed for years, as Barcelona did in 1992 when the Olympics were used to reconnect the city to its own coastline. The weeks of global coverage also work as advertising that no tourist board could afford, and success by home athletes tends to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raise participation in sport</strong> among children who watch it.\n\nCritics answer that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the costs are consistently understated</strong>. Bids are approved on the basis of optimistic budgets, and the final figure is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">routinely several times the original estimate</strong>, with the difference met from taxation. The stadiums themselves are the clearest evidence: purpose-built venues in cities without a team large enough to fill them stand empty afterwards while still costing money to maintain. Poorer residents may also be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">moved out of areas earmarked for development</strong>.\n\nMy own view is that the decisive question is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what the country would have built anyway</strong>. Where a host uses the deadline to deliver a metro line or housing that residents will use for fifty years, the expense is justified. Where it builds arenas that serve no purpose once the visitors leave, the money would have been better spent elsewhere.\n\nIn conclusion, hosting is neither automatically beneficial nor automatically wasteful. It repays a country that plans for the years after the event, and punishes one that plans only for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the fortnight itself</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"raise participation in sport","context":"The weeks of global coverage also work as advertising that no tourist board could afford, and success by home athletes tends to raise participation in sport among children who watch it."},{"en":"the fortnight itself","context":"In conclusion, hosting is neither automatically beneficial nor automatically wasteful. It repays a country that plans for the years after the event, and punishes one that plans only for the fortnight itself."},{"en":"the costs are consistently understated","context":"Critics answer that the costs are consistently understated."},{"en":"routinely several times the original estimate","context":"Bids are approved on the basis of optimistic budgets, and the final figure is routinely several times the original estimate, with the difference met from taxation."},{"en":"moved out of areas earmarked for development","context":"Poorer residents may also be moved out of areas earmarked for development."},{"en":"what the country would have built anyway","context":"My own view is that the decisive question is what the country would have built anyway."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#292","summary":"The advantages and disadvantages of hosting major sporting events"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2225","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"School sport or professional performance?","prompt":"Government money should be used to support children in school for sports rather than to support professional sports and arts that perform for the general public. To what extent do you agree?","answer":"Public budgets for sport and culture are limited, and it is sometimes argued that all of the money should go to school children rather than to professionals who perform for an audience. I agree that school provision deserves priority, but not that professional funding should be withdrawn entirely.\n\nThe case for schools is strongest on grounds of reach. Money spent on a stadium benefits the few thousand who attend, whereas money spent on school pitches, equipment and coaching reaches every child in the country, including those whose families could never pay for a club membership. The health argument reinforces this. Habits formed before the age of twelve tend to last, so a child who learns to swim or enjoys running is far less likely to become an inactive adult, and the saving to health services from that single change is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">difficult to overstate</strong>.\n\nSchool sport also does work that professional sport cannot. Losing a match and returning to training the following week teaches persistence, and a mixed-ability team teaches children to depend on people who are not like them. These lessons come from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taking part rather than watching</strong>.\n\nI would not go as far as the statement, however. Professional sport and the arts are what give children something to aim at, and a national orchestra or athletics squad also supports a wider network of teachers, coaches and technicians whose skills eventually reach schools. Removing that funding would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weaken the base as well as the top</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I largely agree that children in school should come first, because participation delivers more health and social value per pound than performance does. The right approach is to shift the balance decisively towards schools while keeping enough professional support to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give young people something to aspire to</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"difficult to overstate","context":"Habits formed before the age of twelve tend to last, so a child who learns to swim or enjoys running is far less likely to become an inactive adult, and the saving to health services from that single change is difficult to overstate."},{"en":"taking part rather than watching","context":"These lessons come from taking part rather than watching."},{"en":"weaken the base as well as the top","context":"Removing that funding would weaken the base as well as the top."},{"en":"give young people something to aspire to","context":"The right approach is to shift the balance decisively towards schools while keeping enough professional support to give young people something to aspire to."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#293","summary":"Public money for school sport rather than professional sport and the arts"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2294","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Sport as a business","prompt":"Nowadays, sport is a big business, with high earnings for professional sportspeople and the companies involved. Is this a positive or negative development?","answer":"Television, sponsorship and merchandising have turned professional sport into an industry worth hundreds of billions. I regard the development as broadly positive for the standard of sport, though it has damaged the relationship between clubs and the people who support them.\n\nThe money has clearly improved what spectators watch. Athletes can train full-time with proper coaching, nutrition and medical care, which is why records fall in almost every discipline, and injuries that once ended careers are now treated successfully. Commercial income has also opened sport to people who could not previously afford to pursue it, since a talented child from a poor family can now be supported through an academy, and women's competitions have grown rapidly on the same funding after decades of neglect. Broadcasting reaches audiences that would never have seen these events.\n\nThe costs fall on the sport itself. Wealth concentrates, so the same few clubs win repeatedly and competitions become predictable, and smaller clubs that formed the base of the game struggle or disappear. Fixtures are arranged for broadcasters rather than for supporters, with matches moved to inconvenient times and competitions restructured to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suit the audiences that pay the most</strong>. Ticket prices have risen beyond what many lifelong supporters can pay, and a club that was a local institution can be sold to owners with no connection to the place.\n\nThere are consequences for the athletes too. Pressure to perform and to appear constantly on television produces injuries, exhaustion and a measurable amount of doping, and young players are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">signed and discarded before they are twenty</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, commercial money has raised standards, widened access and made sport visible to millions, which is a genuine gain. It has also made competition less equal and pushed supporters aside, so the development is positive only where <strong class=\"key-phrase\">income is shared and clubs remain answerable to their communities</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"suit the audiences that pay the most","context":"Fixtures are arranged for broadcasters rather than for supporters, with matches moved to inconvenient times and competitions restructured to suit the audiences that pay the most."},{"en":"signed and discarded before they are twenty","context":"There are consequences for the athletes too. Pressure to perform and to appear constantly on television produces injuries, exhaustion and a measurable amount of doping, and young players are signed and discarded before they are twenty."},{"en":"income is shared and clubs remain answerable to their communities","context":"It has also made competition less equal and pushed supporters aside, so the development is positive only where income is shared and clubs remain answerable to their communities."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#294","summary":"Whether the huge earnings in professional sport are a good development"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2303","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Competition and young athletes","prompt":"Some people believe that sports competitions are a source of emotional stress for young people, and that young people should therefore not take part in them. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Children in competitive sport are sometimes visibly distressed by defeat, and the pressure placed on them by adults can be severe. I agree that the stress is real, but I disagree that removing competition is the answer.\n\nThe concern is legitimate. Young athletes in demanding programmes train for hours daily, and anxiety, injury and eating disorders occur at measurable rates. Much of the pressure comes from adults: parents who shout from the touchline and coaches whose position depends on results teach a child that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">their worth depends on the last result</strong>. Children develop at different rates, so early selection frequently rejects those who would have been better a few years later, and many able young people abandon sport entirely because of a bad experience before they were fourteen.\n\nRemoving competition would nonetheless cost more than it saved. Competing teaches how to lose, and a child who has never lost anything meets the first serious failure in adulthood with no experience of recovering from it. Competition also supplies the reason to practise, since improvement is dull without a test of whether it worked, and children themselves invent competitions in every playground, which suggests the instinct is not imposed. Sport is also one of the few areas where a young person can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">earn something entirely on their own effort</strong>.\n\nThe distinction that matters is between competition and the conditions surrounding it. What harms children is not the match but adult behaviour: excessive selection, year-round training, and treating a child's performance as a family investment. Age-appropriate leagues, limits on training hours, coaches trained in child development and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">parents kept quiet on the touchline</strong> address the problem directly.\n\nIn conclusion, competitive sport can cause genuine stress, and the concern deserves attention rather than dismissal. Banning young people from competing would remove resilience, motivation and enjoyment, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reforming how adults run it</strong> is the better response.","phrases":[{"en":"their worth depends on the last result","context":"Much of the pressure comes from adults: parents who shout from the touchline and coaches whose position depends on results teach a child that their worth depends on the last result."},{"en":"earn something entirely on their own effort","context":"Sport is also one of the few areas where a young person can earn something entirely on their own effort."},{"en":"parents kept quiet on the touchline","context":"Age-appropriate leagues, limits on training hours, coaches trained in child development and parents kept quiet on the touchline address the problem directly."},{"en":"reforming how adults run it","context":"In conclusion, competitive sport can cause genuine stress, and the concern deserves attention rather than dismissal. Banning young people from competing would remove resilience, motivation and enjoyment, so reforming how adults run it is the better response."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#295","summary":"Whether young people should be kept out of sports competitions"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2328","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Games on handheld devices","prompt":"Entertainment such as electronic games on handheld devices is harmful to individuals and to the society they live in. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Phones have put an endless supply of games into every pocket, and the criticism that follows them is severe. I partly agree, because the harm lies less in the games themselves than in how these particular ones are built.\n\nThe defence is reasonable. Games demand attention, planning and quick judgement, and studies of regular players show improvements in spatial reasoning and reaction time. They are a cheap and harmless way to fill a commute, and multiplayer games sustain friendships across distance, which matters to people who are isolated. Treating them as worthless while accepting television as normal is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a double standard rather than an argument</strong>.\n\nThe concerns are still legitimate. Handheld games are designed by companies paid for attention, so they use daily rewards, streaks and near-misses borrowed directly from gambling. The purpose is to make stopping difficult, and for a minority it succeeds completely: schoolwork, sleep and exercise disappear. Many now include purchases dressed as chance, which introduces children to gambling before they can recognise it. The social cost is subtler, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hours absorbed in a screen are hours taken from somewhere else</strong>, most often from sleep, movement and the company of other people.\n\nThe distinction that matters is between playing and being kept playing. A game with an ending is entertainment; a game engineered never to finish is a product designed against its user. This points to remedies rather than bans: regulating purchases aimed at children, requiring companies to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disclose how the rewards are timed</strong>, and teaching families to set limits.\n\nIn conclusion, portable games are not harmful in themselves, and they give millions of people pleasure and connection. The commercial design behind many of them is harmful, and that is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what regulation should be aimed at</strong> rather than the games as a whole.","phrases":[{"en":"a double standard rather than an argument","context":"Treating them as worthless while accepting television as normal is a double standard rather than an argument."},{"en":"hours absorbed in a screen are hours taken from somewhere else","context":"The social cost is subtler, since hours absorbed in a screen are hours taken from somewhere else, most often from sleep, movement and the company of other people."},{"en":"disclose how the rewards are timed","context":"This points to remedies rather than bans: regulating purchases aimed at children, requiring companies to disclose how the rewards are timed, and teaching families to set limits."},{"en":"what regulation should be aimed at","context":"In conclusion, portable games are not harmful in themselves, and they give millions of people pleasure and connection. The commercial design behind many of them is harmful, and that is what regulation should be aimed at rather than the games as a whole."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#296","summary":"Whether portable electronic games harm individuals and society"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2336","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Traditional games against modern ones","prompt":"Some people think traditional games are better than modern games in helping children develop their abilities. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Skipping ropes and hide-and-seek have been replaced in most childhoods by consoles and phones. I partly agree that the older games develop a child more completely, but the advantage lies in how they are played rather than in their age.\n\nTraditional games teach through the conditions they impose. Children playing outside must agree the rules themselves, settle disputes without a referee and include whoever turns up, which is where negotiation and fairness are actually learned. The games involve running, climbing and balancing, so physical development happens without anyone calling it exercise. Simple equipment also forces invention, since a stick becomes several different things, and boredom between games <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pushes children into making something up</strong>.\n\nModern games are stronger than critics allow, however. Complex titles require planning, resource management and rapid decisions under pressure, and children who play together online must coordinate with people they cannot see, which is a genuine social skill. Games are also the entry point through which many children discover coding and design, and they include those whose disability or geography rules out the playground.\n\nThe real difference is the amount of freedom involved. A traditional game is shaped by the children playing it; a commercial game is shaped by designers who decide the goals, the rewards and when the next one arrives. That is why the modern version is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easier to enjoy and harder to stop</strong>, and why it develops fewer of the abilities that come from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">having to organise the fun yourself</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, traditional games do more for physical health, social negotiation and imagination, so the preference for them is justified. Modern games train useful skills of their own, and the sensible aim is a childhood containing both rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nostalgia for one of them</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"pushes children into making something up","context":"Simple equipment also forces invention, since a stick becomes several different things, and boredom between games pushes children into making something up."},{"en":"easier to enjoy and harder to stop","context":"That is why the modern version is easier to enjoy and harder to stop, and why it develops fewer of the abilities that come from having to organise the fun yourself."},{"en":"having to organise the fun yourself","context":"That is why the modern version is easier to enjoy and harder to stop, and why it develops fewer of the abilities that come from having to organise the fun yourself."},{"en":"nostalgia for one of them","context":"Modern games train useful skills of their own, and the sensible aim is a childhood containing both rather than nostalgia for one of them."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#297","summary":"Whether traditional games develop children's abilities better than modern games"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2339","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Team sports or individual sports?","prompt":"Some people think it is more beneficial to take part in team sports such as football, while others think individual sports such as swimming are better. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Football and swimming both keep a person fit, yet they teach quite different things. Each has a serious claim, and my own view is that the better choice depends on what a particular person needs rather than on the sports themselves.\n\nTeam sports offer lessons that cannot be learned alone. A player must read what teammates are doing, accept a role that may not be glamorous and keep going when a match is lost through somebody else's mistake, which is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">closer to how most work and family life actually feels</strong>. The social pull is also practical: turning up matters because other people depend on it, so team players tend to keep exercising when individuals give up. For children who are shy or new to an area, a team supplies friendships that would otherwise take years.\n\nIndividual sports have their own strengths. Progress depends entirely on the person, so improvement is visible and honestly earned, and that produces a durable kind of confidence. There is no substitute to hide behind and no captain to blame, which builds self-discipline. They are also more flexible, since a swimmer or runner trains at any hour without organising twenty other people, and they can be continued into old age long after <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the body has stopped tolerating collisions</strong>. Children who dislike competition or find crowds difficult often thrive here after failing in a team.\n\nThe choice therefore depends on the individual. A child who avoids other people benefits from a team; one who is constantly compared with others benefits from a sport where <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the only opponent is a previous time</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, team sports develop cooperation and keep people participating, while individual sports build discipline and last a lifetime. The greatest benefit comes from matching the sport to the person, and ideally from experiencing both.","phrases":[{"en":"closer to how most work and family life actually feels","context":"A player must read what teammates are doing, accept a role that may not be glamorous and keep going when a match is lost through somebody else's mistake, which is closer to how most work and family life actually feels."},{"en":"the body has stopped tolerating collisions","context":"They are also more flexible, since a swimmer or runner trains at any hour without organising twenty other people, and they can be continued into old age long after the body has stopped tolerating collisions."},{"en":"the only opponent is a previous time","context":"The choice therefore depends on the individual. A child who avoids other people benefits from a team; one who is constantly compared with others benefits from a sport where the only opponent is a previous time."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#298","summary":"Whether taking part in team sports is more beneficial than individual sports"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2346","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Games instead of sport","prompt":"Nowadays, many children spend a great deal of time playing computer games and less time taking part in sport. Why is this the case? Is this a beneficial or harmful trend?","answer":"Children who once spent afternoons on a pitch now spend them on a console. Several practical changes explain the shift, and I regard it as harmful, chiefly because of what the body loses.\n\nAvailability is the first cause. A game is always ready, requires no equipment, no travel and no other players who happen to be free, whereas sport demands a pitch, a coach and a fixed time. Cities have made this worse by building over the open ground children used to play on, and parents who fear traffic and strangers now keep children indoors. Cost matters too, since organised sport carries fees that many families cannot meet, while a game already installed is free. The games themselves are also more compelling than they were. They are designed by companies whose income depends on time spent, so progress, rewards and friends inside the game make stopping difficult, and a child who loses at football feels it publicly while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a game lets you try again immediately and privately</strong>.\n\nThere are benefits worth acknowledging: strategy games train planning, online play maintains friendships, and children who dislike competitive sport are no longer excluded from everything.\n\nThe costs are heavier. Physical activity in childhood determines bone strength, heart health and weight for the rest of life, and none of it can be recovered later. Sport also teaches losing, cooperation and the value of practice in a way that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a screen cannot reproduce because nothing physical is at stake</strong>. Sitting for hours affects sleep and posture, and inactive children usually become inactive adults, which is why obesity and diabetes now appear at ages that were once unheard of.\n\nIn conclusion, games have grown because they are cheap, always available and skilfully designed, while sport has become harder to access. The trend is harmful, and the remedy lies in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">free local facilities and safe streets</strong> rather than in criticising children.","phrases":[{"en":"a game lets you try again immediately and privately","context":"They are designed by companies whose income depends on time spent, so progress, rewards and friends inside the game make stopping difficult, and a child who loses at football feels it publicly while a game lets you try again immediately and privately."},{"en":"a screen cannot reproduce because nothing physical is at stake","context":"Sport also teaches losing, cooperation and the value of practice in a way that a screen cannot reproduce because nothing physical is at stake."},{"en":"free local facilities and safe streets","context":"In conclusion, games have grown because they are cheap, always available and skilfully designed, while sport has become harder to access. The trend is harmful, and the remedy lies in free local facilities and safe streets rather than in criticising children."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Beneficial or Harmful","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#299","summary":"Why children play more computer games and less sport, and whether this is harmful"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2365","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Companies sponsoring sport","prompt":"Some businesses now use sports sponsorship as a form of advertising. While some believe this benefits the sports industry, others argue that it brings drawbacks. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Company names appear on shirts, stadiums and competitions at every level of sport. The money involved has transformed what athletes and clubs can do, and my own view is that the benefits are real but need firmer limits than most countries impose.\n\nThe argument for sponsorship is largely financial. Professional sport is expensive, and ticket sales alone cannot pay for stadiums, coaching and medical care. Sponsorship allows athletes in minority sports to train full-time rather than fitting practice around a job, and it funds the broadcasting that lets millions watch without paying. Money also reaches the bottom of the pyramid, since sponsors support youth academies and local clubs, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a child's first team often exists because a local firm pays for the kit</strong>. Women's sport in particular has grown on sponsorship after decades of neglect.\n\nThe drawbacks concern control and content. A sport dependent on commercial money begins to shape itself around it: matches are scheduled for foreign television audiences, breaks are inserted for advertising, and competitions are restructured to favour the clubs that attract sponsors, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">widens the gap between rich clubs and everybody else</strong>. Sponsors can also withdraw suddenly, leaving clubs that planned around the income in serious trouble. The most serious objection is the nature of some sponsors, since gambling, alcohol and fast-food brands buy visibility with young audiences precisely because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">association with sport makes an unhealthy product look wholesome</strong>.\n\nThe reasonable position is therefore to keep the money and regulate the source. Restricting gambling and alcohol advertising in sport, and requiring a share of income to be passed down to grassroots clubs, addresses the main harms without removing the funding.\n\nIn conclusion, sponsorship pays for much of what makes modern sport possible and reaches far beyond elite competition. It distorts fixtures and promotes harmful products, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rules on who may sponsor and how the money is shared</strong> are what the industry now needs.","phrases":[{"en":"a child's first team often exists because a local firm pays for the kit","context":"Money also reaches the bottom of the pyramid, since sponsors support youth academies and local clubs, and a child's first team often exists because a local firm pays for the kit."},{"en":"widens the gap between rich clubs and everybody else","context":"A sport dependent on commercial money begins to shape itself around it: matches are scheduled for foreign television audiences, breaks are inserted for advertising, and competitions are restructured to favour the clubs that attract sponsors, which widens the gap between rich clubs and everybody e..."},{"en":"association with sport makes an unhealthy product look wholesome","context":"The most serious objection is the nature of some sponsors, since gambling, alcohol and fast-food brands buy visibility with young audiences precisely because association with sport makes an unhealthy product look wholesome."},{"en":"rules on who may sponsor and how the money is shared","context":"In conclusion, sponsorship pays for much of what makes modern sport possible and reaches far beyond elite competition. It distorts fixtures and promotes harmful products, so rules on who may sponsor and how the money is shared are what the industry now needs."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#300","summary":"Whether corporate sponsorship helps or harms the sports industry"},{"id":"writing-task-2-038","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Space research or living standards on Earth?","prompt":"Millions of dollars are spent on space research every year. Some people argue that the money should be spent on improving living standards on Earth. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Space research requires enormous public investment, while many people still lack adequate housing, healthcare and education. Although these social needs deserve priority, I do not agree that governments should abandon space programmes. A limited and carefully directed budget can produce direct practical benefits on Earth and support valuable long-term research.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">The strongest argument against space spending is opportunity cost</strong>. Money used for a distant mission cannot be spent on hospitals, schools or clean water. In poorer countries, these services can save lives immediately, whereas the benefits of space exploration may seem uncertain or remote. Governments should therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">avoid prestige projects</strong> that consume large sums without a clear scientific or public purpose.\n\nHowever, space research is not simply an expensive adventure. Satellite technology <strong class=\"key-phrase\">supports weather forecasting, navigation, communications and disaster management</strong>. It helps farmers monitor crops, allows emergency services to track storms and gives scientists reliable evidence about climate change. Research carried out for space missions has also contributed to advances in materials, computing and medicine. These benefits are shared far beyond the space industry.\n\nSpace science is also a long-term investment. Satellites have recorded polar ice and sea temperatures for more than forty years, and a continuous record of that kind <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot be created once a crisis has already begun</strong>; the expertise and international cooperation behind such programmes take decades to build. Missions should still be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reviewed independently</strong>, so that governments can show what each one delivers when it competes with pressing social needs.\n\nIn conclusion, basic living standards should receive the largest share of public money, but this does not make space research wasteful. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">A balanced funding model</strong> can meet urgent needs while preserving a programme that delivers practical technology and scientific knowledge.","phrases":[{"en":"A balanced funding model","context":"A balanced funding model can meet urgent needs while preserving a programme that delivers practical technology and scientific knowledge."},{"en":"The strongest argument against space spending is opportunity cost","context":"The strongest argument against space spending is opportunity cost."},{"en":"avoid prestige projects","context":"Governments should therefore avoid prestige projects that consume large sums without a clear scientific or public purpose."},{"en":"supports weather forecasting, navigation, communications and disaster management","context":"Satellite technology supports weather forecasting, navigation, communications and disaster management."},{"en":"cannot be created once a crisis has already begun","context":"Satellites have recorded polar ice and sea temperatures for more than forty years, and a continuous record of that kind cannot be created once a crisis has already begun; the expertise and international cooperation behind such programmes take decades to build."},{"en":"reviewed independently","context":"Missions should still be reviewed independently, so that governments can show what each one delivers when it competes with pressing social needs."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#301","summary":"Millions of dollars are spent on space research every year."},{"id":"writing-task-2-128","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"The problems of mobile phones","prompt":"There are social, medical and technical problems associated with the use of mobile phones. What form do these problems take? Do the problems of using mobile phones outweigh the benefits?","answer":"Mobile phones create difficulties that are social, medical and technical in nature. In my opinion these problems are real but manageable, and they do not outweigh the considerable benefits that the technology provides.\n\nThe social problems are the most visible. People frequently attend to their phones rather than to those physically present, which weakens conversation within families, and constant availability means that work now <strong class=\"key-phrase\">intrudes into evenings and holidays</strong>. Children are exposed to bullying that follows them home, and online harassment is far harder to escape than the playground variety ever was.\n\nMedical concerns are more limited than often claimed. There is no reliable evidence that phone radiation causes cancer, but the effects on sleep are well established, since using a screen before bed <strong class=\"key-phrase\">delays the onset of sleep</strong>. Phone use while driving is a proven cause of fatal accidents, and repetitive strain in the hands and neck is now widespread. Technical problems include the loss of personal data through hacking, the theft of devices, and the environmental cost of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">manufacturing and discarding</strong> hundreds of millions of handsets.\n\nAgainst these must be set benefits that were unimaginable a generation ago. A phone is a telephone, camera, map, bank, library and translator combined. It allows a farmer to check market prices, a traveller to navigate an unfamiliar city and anyone to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">summon emergency help immediately</strong>. In poorer countries, mobile banking has given millions their first access to financial services.\n\nIn conclusion, mobile phones create genuine social, medical and technical difficulties, but these arise mainly from how the devices are used. The advantages remain substantially greater.","phrases":[{"en":"intrudes into evenings and holidays","context":"People frequently attend to their phones rather than to those physically present, which weakens conversation within families, and constant availability means that work now intrudes into evenings and holidays."},{"en":"delays the onset of sleep","context":"There is no reliable evidence that phone radiation causes cancer, but the effects on sleep are well established, since using a screen before bed delays the onset of sleep."},{"en":"manufacturing and discarding","context":"Technical problems include the loss of personal data through hacking, the theft of devices, and the environmental cost of manufacturing and discarding hundreds of millions of handsets."},{"en":"summon emergency help immediately","context":"It allows a farmer to check market prices, a traveller to navigate an unfamiliar city and anyone to summon emergency help immediately."}],"subtype":"Benefits & Drawbacks","_displayNo":"#302","summary":"The problems of mobile phones"},{"id":"writing-task-2-146","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Older inventions against recent ones","prompt":"Earlier technological development brought more changes to the life of ordinary people than recent technological development do. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Earlier inventions such as electricity, sanitation, the railway and the motor car transformed ordinary life on a remarkable scale. Nevertheless, I do not agree that recent technology has produced fewer changes. Digital systems have altered communication, work, commerce and access to knowledge in ways that are equally far-reaching.\n\nThe case for earlier technology is strong because it solved basic physical problems. Electricity extended productive hours, clean water reduced disease, and modern transport allowed people and goods to move quickly. These developments <strong class=\"key-phrase\">created the foundations of urban life</strong> and raised life expectancy. Their effect was immediate and visible across entire populations.\n\nRecent technology, however, has changed how people organise almost every daily activity. A smartphone now serves as a bank, map, camera, library and communication device. Remote work and online education allow services to cross national borders, while digital medical records and data analysis improve diagnosis. These changes may appear less dramatic than the arrival of electricity, but they have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">restructured the way information and decisions move through society</strong>.\n\nThe impact of recent innovation is also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">still unfolding</strong>, particularly in artificial intelligence, biotechnology and clean energy. Their long-term consequences may eventually be as visible as those of the railway or the electric grid.\n\nThe two periods are also connected: modern digital tools depend on earlier infrastructure. It is therefore unhelpful to rank one era as universally more important. In conclusion, earlier inventions met fundamental physical needs, while recent innovations have transformed coordination and knowledge. Both have brought <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changes of comparable depth</strong> in different areas of life.","phrases":[{"en":"created the foundations of urban life","context":"These developments created the foundations of urban life and raised life expectancy."},{"en":"restructured the way information and decisions move through society","context":"These changes may appear less dramatic than the arrival of electricity, but they have restructured the way information and decisions move through society."},{"en":"changes of comparable depth","context":"Both have brought changes of comparable depth in different areas of life."},{"en":"still unfolding","context":"The impact of recent innovation is also still unfolding, particularly in artificial intelligence, biotechnology and clean energy. Their long-term consequences may eventually be as visible as those of the railway or the electric grid."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#303","summary":"Earlier technological development brought more changes to the life of…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-150","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Optimism about what science will do this century","prompt":"Many people are optimistic about the 21st century and expect scientists to make positive changes to the world. To what extent do you agree or disagree with their optimism? What changes would you like to see in the new century?","answer":"I am cautiously optimistic about the twenty-first century. Science has the potential to improve health, energy and living standards, but progress will only be positive if governments use new knowledge responsibly. The changes I would most like to see concern clean energy and equal access to healthcare.\n\nThere are good reasons for optimism. Medical research has already made many diseases easier to diagnose and treat, while digital technology allows knowledge to spread quickly. Further advances in vaccines, early screening and personalised treatment could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">extend healthy life expectancy</strong>. Science can also make farming more efficient and help cities use water and energy more carefully.\n\nHowever, scientific progress can create new risks. Automation may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remove jobs faster than workers can retrain</strong>, and poorly regulated data systems can threaten privacy. Environmental technology will also have limited value if countries continue to depend on fossil fuels. I would therefore like governments to invest heavily in renewable energy, public transport and energy-efficient housing. These measures would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce emissions at source</strong> rather than simply deal with their consequences.\n\nA second priority should be affordable healthcare. Breakthroughs are of little value if only wealthy people can use them, and the gap is already visible: new cancer drugs and gene therapies are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">priced far beyond what most health systems can afford</strong>, and during the recent pandemic richer countries <strong class=\"key-phrase\">secured vaccine supplies many months before poorer ones</strong>. Public funding, international cooperation and fair pricing are needed so that essential treatment is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">available regardless of income</strong>.\n\nScientific progress alone will not guarantee a better century. New medicines, clean energy and more efficient farming can improve millions of lives, but access and regulation determine who benefits. I would particularly like to see research focused on affordable disease prevention and low-carbon infrastructure. These priorities could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turn technical progress into widely shared human progress</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"extend healthy life expectancy","context":"Further advances in vaccines, early screening and personalised treatment could extend healthy life expectancy."},{"en":"reduce emissions at source","context":"These measures would reduce emissions at source rather than simply deal with their consequences."},{"en":"available regardless of income","context":"Public funding, international cooperation and fair pricing are needed so that essential treatment is available regardless of income."},{"en":"turn technical progress into widely shared human progress","context":"These priorities could turn technical progress into widely shared human progress."},{"en":"remove jobs faster than workers can retrain","context":"Automation may remove jobs faster than workers can retrain, and poorly regulated data systems can threaten privacy."},{"en":"priced far beyond what most health systems can afford","context":"Breakthroughs are of little value if only wealthy people can use them, and the gap is already visible: new cancer drugs and gene therapies are priced far beyond what most health systems can afford, and during the recent pandemic richer countries secured vaccine supplies many months before poorer..."},{"en":"secured vaccine supplies many months before poorer ones","context":"Breakthroughs are of little value if only wealthy people can use them, and the gap is already visible: new cancer drugs and gene therapies are priced far beyond what most health systems can afford, and during the recent pandemic richer countries secured vaccine supplies many months before poorer..."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#304","summary":"People are optimistic about the 21st century and expect scientists to make…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-168","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"What the arts tell us that science cannot","prompt":"The advance of science and technology has a significant impact on people, but the role of artists is also important, such as musicians, painters and writers. What can the arts tell us about life that science cannot?","answer":"Science explains how the natural world works, while the arts explore what human experience feels like. Both are important, but they answer different questions. Art can reveal emotion, identity and moral conflict in ways that scientific measurement alone cannot.\n\nA scientific study can describe the biological effects of grief, but a novel, painting or piece of music can help an audience understand the personal reality of loss. Art gives form to experiences that are difficult to measure, including love, fear, memory and belonging. Because people interpret a work differently, it can also encourage them to examine their own assumptions. In this sense, the arts provide <strong class=\"key-phrase\">insight into the inner life of other people</strong>.\n\nThe arts also preserve the character of a society. Stories, songs and images record how communities understand family, authority, religion and historical change. Science may explain the materials used in an ancient object, but art and literature help us consider what that object meant to the people who made it. This cultural record allows later generations to see the world through a different set of values.\n\nFinally, art can explore moral questions before society reaches a clear answer. Films about artificial intelligence, genetic engineering or war do not provide technical solutions, but they can show the human consequences of a decision. This imaginative function is valuable because facts do not automatically tell us what we ought to do.\n\nIn conclusion, science offers reliable explanations and practical discoveries, whereas the arts deepen our understanding of emotion, culture and moral choice. They do not compete with science; they provide <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a complementary way of understanding life</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"insight into the inner life of other people","context":"In this sense, the arts provide insight into the inner life of other people."},{"en":"a complementary way of understanding life","context":"In conclusion, science offers reliable explanations and practical discoveries, whereas the arts deepen our understanding of emotion, culture and moral choice. They do not compete with science; they provide a complementary way of understanding life."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#305","summary":"Advance of science and technology has a significant impact on people, but…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-170","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Computers and children's development","prompt":"Some people believe that computers do not help children learn more effectively, and that using them has a negative effect on children's physical and mental development. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes claimed that computers do not improve children's learning and that they damage physical and mental development. I disagree with the first part of this statement, although I accept that excessive screen use causes genuine harm.\n\nThe educational benefits are difficult to dismiss. Software can adapt to a child's level, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">providing extra practice where it is needed</strong>, and immediate feedback helps learners <strong class=\"key-phrase\">correct mistakes before they become habits</strong>. Simulations allow students to observe chemical reactions or historical reconstructions that no classroom could provide, and children with dyslexia or impaired sight can use tools that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make written material accessible</strong> for the first time.\n\nThe concerns about development, however, deserve to be taken seriously. Children who spend many hours seated exercise less, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rates of obesity and poor posture have risen accordingly</strong>. Screen use before bed <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disrupts sleep</strong>, which affects concentration the following day. There is also reasonable evidence linking heavy social media use among adolescents, particularly girls, with anxiety and low self-esteem.\n\nThe important distinction is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">between purposeful and unlimited use</strong>. A child using educational software for an hour is in an entirely different situation from one scrolling through videos for five hours, and research on this subject is frequently criticised for confusing the two.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that computers fail to help children learn, since the evidence points clearly in the opposite direction. The real problem is the quantity and the type of screen time, and the sensible response is to regulate both rather than to avoid technology altogether.","phrases":[{"en":"make written material accessible","context":"Simulations allow students to observe chemical reactions or historical reconstructions that no classroom could provide, and children with dyslexia or impaired sight can use tools that make written material accessible for the first time."},{"en":"disrupts sleep","context":"Screen use before bed disrupts sleep, which affects concentration the following day."},{"en":"providing extra practice where it is needed","context":"Software can adapt to a child's level, providing extra practice where it is needed, and immediate feedback helps learners correct mistakes before they become habits."},{"en":"correct mistakes before they become habits","context":"Software can adapt to a child's level, providing extra practice where it is needed, and immediate feedback helps learners correct mistakes before they become habits."},{"en":"rates of obesity and poor posture have risen accordingly","context":"Children who spend many hours seated exercise less, and rates of obesity and poor posture have risen accordingly."},{"en":"between purposeful and unlimited use","context":"The important distinction is between purposeful and unlimited use."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#306","summary":"Computers and children's development"},{"id":"writing-task-2-171","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Has technology given us more leisure time?","prompt":"Modern technology has given people more leisure time than in the past. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is often claimed that machines and digital devices have freed people from work and given them more time to relax. I largely disagree with this view, because although technology has removed a great deal of physical labour, it has also created new demands that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consume the time it saves</strong>.\n\nThere is no doubt that technology has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">eliminated many hours of drudgery</strong>. Washing machines, dishwashers and vacuum cleaners have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduced housework from a full-time occupation to a few hours a week</strong>, which has been particularly important for women. In the workplace, computers perform in seconds calculations that once took clerks days, and online banking and shopping have removed the need for countless journeys. Measured purely in tasks completed per hour, we are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far more productive than our grandparents were</strong>.\n\nThe difficulty is that the time saved has not become free time. Employers have simply raised their expectations, so employees now produce far more within the same working day. Smartphones have also destroyed <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the boundary between work and home</strong>, since emails arrive in the evening and at weekends and most people feel obliged to answer them. Much of what remains is absorbed by the devices themselves, because several hours a day now go to social media and streaming, which is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">distraction rather than genuine rest</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, technology has certainly made individual tasks quicker, but I disagree that it has given us more leisure. The hours it saves are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">immediately filled by heavier workloads</strong> and by the constant demands of the technology itself.","phrases":[{"en":"consume the time it saves","context":"I largely disagree with this view, because although technology has removed a great deal of physical labour, it has also created new demands that consume the time it saves."},{"en":"distraction rather than genuine rest","context":"Much of what remains is absorbed by the devices themselves, because several hours a day now go to social media and streaming, which is distraction rather than genuine rest."},{"en":"the boundary between work and home","context":"Smartphones have also destroyed the boundary between work and home, since emails arrive in the evening and at weekends and most people feel obliged to answer them."},{"en":"eliminated many hours of drudgery","context":"There is no doubt that technology has eliminated many hours of drudgery."},{"en":"reduced housework from a full-time occupation to a few hours a week","context":"Washing machines, dishwashers and vacuum cleaners have reduced housework from a full-time occupation to a few hours a week, which has been particularly important for women."},{"en":"far more productive than our grandparents were","context":"Measured purely in tasks completed per hour, we are far more productive than our grandparents were."},{"en":"immediately filled by heavier workloads","context":"In conclusion, technology has certainly made individual tasks quicker, but I disagree that it has given us more leisure. The hours it saves are immediately filled by heavier workloads and by the constant demands of the technology itself."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#307","summary":"Has technology given us more leisure time?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-173","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"The fast pace of modern life","prompt":"It is said that the fast pace of everyday life, resulting from the rapid development of telecommunications and travel, has negative effects on individuals, nations and the world as a whole. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is often claimed that modern communications and cheap travel have made life faster, and that the consequences for individuals, nations and the planet are harmful. I partly agree: the pressures on individuals and the environment are real, but the picture is more mixed than this argument suggests.\n\nThe effects on individuals are the most convincing part of the case. Email and mobile phones have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">removed the boundary between work and rest</strong>, so employees now answer messages in the evening and on holiday, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rates of stress-related illness have risen accordingly</strong>. Constant availability also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fragments attention</strong>, since people who are interrupted every few minutes find sustained concentration increasingly difficult.\n\nAt a global level the environmental argument is equally strong. Air travel is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of the fastest-growing sources of carbon emissions</strong>, and the expectation of rapid delivery has produced <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an enormous volume of freight and packaging</strong> that did not exist a generation ago.\n\nHowever, the same technologies have brought <strong class=\"key-phrase\">benefits that critics tend to overlook</strong>. Doctors can consult specialists on another continent within minutes, families separated by migration can see one another daily, and countries that were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">economically isolated</strong> now export to global markets. Emergency response to earthquakes and epidemics also depends entirely on the speed of modern communication, and thousands of lives are saved each year as a direct result.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that the pace of modern life damages individual well-being and the environment, and that both deserve serious attention. I do not accept, however, that the effects are simply negative, since the same developments have transformed healthcare, family contact and economic opportunity for the better.","phrases":[{"en":"fragments attention","context":"Constant availability also fragments attention, since people who are interrupted every few minutes find sustained concentration increasingly difficult."},{"en":"an enormous volume of freight and packaging","context":"Air travel is one of the fastest-growing sources of carbon emissions, and the expectation of rapid delivery has produced an enormous volume of freight and packaging that did not exist a generation ago."},{"en":"economically isolated","context":"Doctors can consult specialists on another continent within minutes, families separated by migration can see one another daily, and countries that were economically isolated now export to global markets."},{"en":"removed the boundary between work and rest","context":"Email and mobile phones have removed the boundary between work and rest, so employees now answer messages in the evening and on holiday, and rates of stress-related illness have risen accordingly."},{"en":"rates of stress-related illness have risen accordingly","context":"Email and mobile phones have removed the boundary between work and rest, so employees now answer messages in the evening and on holiday, and rates of stress-related illness have risen accordingly."},{"en":"one of the fastest-growing sources of carbon emissions","context":"Air travel is one of the fastest-growing sources of carbon emissions, and the expectation of rapid delivery has produced an enormous volume of freight and packaging that did not exist a generation ago."},{"en":"benefits that critics tend to overlook","context":"However, the same technologies have brought benefits that critics tend to overlook."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#308","summary":"The fast pace of modern life"},{"id":"writing-task-2-175","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Has technology made our leisure less creative?","prompt":"The leisure industry is growing rapidly. The active role of modern technology in people's leisure lives nowadays has made them less creative. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Technology now occupies a large part of leisure time, leading some people to believe that it has reduced creativity. I disagree with this generalisation. Passive screen use can certainly become repetitive, but modern technology also gives ordinary people powerful tools to create, learn and share original work.\n\nThe negative argument is understandable. Recommendation systems often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">encourage users to watch similar videos for hours</strong>, and many games provide fixed goals rather than genuine freedom. When entertainment becomes automatic, people may spend less time reading, making things or inventing their own activities. This is mainly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a problem of habit and design</strong>, not proof that all digital leisure is uncreative.\n\nIn contrast, affordable software allows people to edit films, compose music, draw, write and build games at home. Online courses teach techniques that were once available only through specialist schools, while creative communities provide feedback and collaboration. Even gaming can involve strategy, storytelling and the design of new worlds. Technology therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lowers the barrier between consuming and creating</strong>.\n\nEducation can improve the balance by teaching people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">how digital platforms compete for attention</strong>. When users understand recommendation systems and set limits on passive viewing, they have more time for demanding activities such as writing, design or music production. Schools and families can reinforce this habit by valuing finished creative projects, not simply hours spent online.\n\nIn conclusion, modern leisure can become passive when people use technology without purpose. However, the same devices can greatly expand creative opportunity. The important issue is whether users <strong class=\"key-phrase\">merely scroll through content</strong> or choose to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">use digital tools actively and deliberately</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"lowers the barrier between consuming and creating","context":"Technology therefore lowers the barrier between consuming and creating."},{"en":"use digital tools actively and deliberately","context":"The important issue is whether users merely scroll through content or choose to use digital tools actively and deliberately."},{"en":"encourage users to watch similar videos for hours","context":"Recommendation systems often encourage users to watch similar videos for hours, and many games provide fixed goals rather than genuine freedom."},{"en":"a problem of habit and design","context":"This is mainly a problem of habit and design, not proof that all digital leisure is uncreative."},{"en":"how digital platforms compete for attention","context":"Education can improve the balance by teaching people how digital platforms compete for attention."},{"en":"merely scroll through content","context":"The important issue is whether users merely scroll through content or choose to use digital tools actively and deliberately."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#309","summary":"Leisure industry is growing rapidly."},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"How technology has changed our relationships","prompt":"Nowadays the way many people interact with each other has changed because of technology. In what ways has technology affected the types of relationships people make? Is this a positive or negative development?","answer":"Technology has changed both the number and nature of people's relationships. It allows individuals to maintain contact across distance and to meet others with similar interests, but it can also make communication more superficial. Overall, the effect is positive when digital contact supports rather than replaces real interaction.\n\nMessaging, video calls and social networks make long-distance relationships easier to maintain. Families separated by work or migration can speak regularly, and professional teams can cooperate across countries. Online communities also help people with rare interests or health conditions find support. Technology has therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">removed many barriers created by distance</strong>.\n\nThe weakness is that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">convenience can reduce depth</strong>. Short messages often replace longer conversations, and people divide their attention between a screen and the person sitting in front of them. Social media also encourages comparison, since users measure their ordinary days against <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the edited version of life that their friends choose to post</strong>. These problems, though, come less from the technology itself than from habits of use.\n\nTechnology has also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expanded weak connections far more than close ones</strong>. Keeping in touch with former classmates or professional networks now costs almost nothing, whereas a close friendship is built by listening to someone at length when they are in difficulty, and no platform can do that on a user's behalf. It is quite possible to have several hundred online contacts and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nobody to telephone at midnight</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, technology has widened the range of people we can reach while making it easier for that contact to remain shallow. On balance I regard the change as positive, provided that people set <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deliberate boundaries around online communication</strong> and use digital tools to arrange meetings and share real experiences rather than as a substitute for time spent together.","topic":"Technology & Science","phrases":[{"en":"removed many barriers created by distance","context":"Technology has therefore removed many barriers created by distance."},{"en":"deliberate boundaries around online communication","context":"On balance I regard the change as positive, provided that people set deliberate boundaries around online communication and use digital tools to arrange meetings and share real experiences rather than as a substitute for time spent together."},{"en":"convenience can reduce depth","context":"The weakness is that convenience can reduce depth."},{"en":"the edited version of life that their friends choose to post","context":"Social media also encourages comparison, since users measure their ordinary days against the edited version of life that their friends choose to post."},{"en":"expanded weak connections far more than close ones","context":"Technology has also expanded weak connections far more than close ones."},{"en":"nobody to telephone at midnight","context":"It is quite possible to have several hundred online contacts and nobody to telephone at midnight."}],"subtype":"positive","summary":"How technology has affected the relationships people make","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-176","_displayNo":"#310"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Giving companies your data in exchange for software","prompt":"Many people are now opting to provide technology companies with their personal data in exchange for access to software. Do the advantages of this outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"Many people exchange personal data for access to useful software. This arrangement offers convenience and lower prices, but it also creates serious privacy risks. In my view, the advantages outweigh the disadvantages only when users receive clear information and genuine control.\n\nData can improve a service. Navigation apps use location information to suggest routes, while streaming platforms recommend content based on previous choices. Advertising revenue also allows many tools to remain free. For users with limited budgets, this can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expand access to valuable digital services</strong>.\n\nThe problem is that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consent is often weak</strong>. Privacy policies are long, data may be shared with third parties and a breach can expose sensitive information. Users may also be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">profiled in ways that affect prices, employment or political messaging</strong> without their knowledge. Companies should collect only what is necessary, explain the purpose in plain language and offer a paid or less personalised alternative.\n\nRelying on companies to police themselves rarely works, since collecting more data is what makes many of these businesses more valuable and better at selling advertising. A firm that voluntarily limits what it collects earns less than a rival that does not, so left alone <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the incentive runs the wrong way</strong>. Independent enforcement, through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">audits and real fines for breaches</strong>, is needed to make <strong class=\"key-phrase\">data minimisation</strong> something companies must do, not merely something they might choose to do.\n\nIn conclusion, sharing some data can be a reasonable exchange, but only under strict conditions. Strong regulation, limited collection and easy deletion are needed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give users meaningful control over their information</strong>. Without these safeguards, the convenience of free software does not justify the loss of privacy.","topic":"Technology & Science","phrases":[{"en":"expand access to valuable digital services","context":"For users with limited budgets, this can expand access to valuable digital services."},{"en":"data minimisation","context":"Independent enforcement, through audits and real fines for breaches, is needed to make data minimisation something companies must do, not merely something they might choose to do."},{"en":"give users meaningful control over their information","context":"Strong regulation, limited collection and easy deletion are needed to give users meaningful control over their information."},{"en":"consent is often weak","context":"The problem is that consent is often weak."},{"en":"profiled in ways that affect prices, employment or political messaging","context":"Users may also be profiled in ways that affect prices, employment or political messaging without their knowledge."},{"en":"the incentive runs the wrong way","context":"A firm that voluntarily limits what it collects earns less than a rival that does not, so left alone the incentive runs the wrong way."},{"en":"audits and real fines for breaches","context":"Independent enforcement, through audits and real fines for breaches, is needed to make data minimisation something companies must do, not merely something they might choose to do."}],"subtype":"outweigh","summary":"Giving personal data to technology companies in exchange for software","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-177","_displayNo":"#311"},{"id":"writing-task-2-182","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Did the Moon landing matter?","prompt":"The Moon landing is often described as one giant leap for mankind. Yet some people argue that this achievement made very little difference to ordinary people's daily lives. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"The first Moon landing is often described as one of humanity's greatest achievements, yet some argue that it changed very little for ordinary people. In my opinion both claims contain some truth, but the achievement was more significant than its critics allow.\n\nThe sceptical position is easy to understand. The programme cost an enormous sum at a time when the United States faced poverty and racial conflict at home, and it was driven largely by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">competition with the Soviet Union</strong> rather than by science. No permanent base was established, nobody returned for half a century, and the daily life of an average family was not visibly altered by the fact that twelve men had walked on the Moon.\n\nThose who defend it point to consequences that were not obvious at the time. The programme required equipment that did not yet exist, and the effort to create it produced the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">miniaturised computing</strong> on which every modern device depends, along with advances in materials, medical monitoring and satellites. Satellite technology in particular now underpins weather forecasting, navigation and global communication, and few people realise how directly it descends from that programme.\n\nThere is also a less measurable effect. The landing demonstrated what a coordinated national effort could achieve, and photographs of the Earth taken from the Moon are widely credited with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strengthening the environmental movement</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the Moon landing did not transform daily life directly, and its motives were undeniably political. Its technological and cultural consequences, however, were substantial enough to justify calling it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a genuine leap forward</strong> rather than an expensive gesture.","phrases":[{"en":"competition with the Soviet Union","context":"The programme cost an enormous sum at a time when the United States faced poverty and racial conflict at home, and it was driven largely by competition with the Soviet Union rather than by science."},{"en":"miniaturised computing","context":"The programme required equipment that did not yet exist, and the effort to create it produced the miniaturised computing on which every modern device depends, along with advances in materials, medical monitoring and satellites."},{"en":"strengthening the environmental movement","context":"There is also a less measurable effect. The landing demonstrated what a coordinated national effort could achieve, and photographs of the Earth taken from the Moon are widely credited with strengthening the environmental movement."},{"en":"a genuine leap forward","context":"Its technological and cultural consequences, however, were substantial enough to justify calling it a genuine leap forward rather than an expensive gesture."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#312","summary":"Did the Moon landing matter?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-190","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Doing business without meeting face to face","prompt":"Nowadays people can carry out tasks such as shopping, banking and even business transactions without meeting each other face to face. What are the effects of this on individuals and on society?","answer":"Shopping, banking and even substantial business negotiations can now be completed without the parties ever meeting. This change has significant effects on individuals and on society, and in my view they are both positive and negative.\n\nFor individuals, the most obvious effect is convenience. Tasks that once required <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a morning of queuing</strong> now take a few minutes, which particularly benefits elderly and disabled people, those with young children and anyone living far from a town centre. Online comparison also gives consumers far more information about prices than they ever had before. Against this, people who conduct all their business remotely lose <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the incidental social contact</strong> that a trip to the bank or market provided, and for isolated individuals these brief exchanges may have been their only conversation of the day. Remote transactions also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expose people to fraud</strong> in ways that face-to-face dealing did not.\n\nThe social consequences are equally mixed. Businesses can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">operate with fewer premises and staff</strong>, which reduces costs and prices, and firms in small countries can now sell worldwide. However, the closure of local branches and shops has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">emptied many high streets</strong>, and the employees concerned are often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">older workers who find retraining difficult</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the removal of face-to-face contact has made daily life considerably more efficient and has opened markets that were previously closed. The cost falls on social contact, on local employment and on those who cannot use the technology, and governments should ensure that these groups are not simply left behind.","phrases":[{"en":"the incidental social contact","context":"Against this, people who conduct all their business remotely lose the incidental social contact that a trip to the bank or market provided, and for isolated individuals these brief exchanges may have been their only conversation of the day."},{"en":"emptied many high streets","context":"However, the closure of local branches and shops has emptied many high streets, and the employees concerned are often older workers who find retraining difficult."},{"en":"a morning of queuing","context":"Tasks that once required a morning of queuing now take a few minutes, which particularly benefits elderly and disabled people, those with young children and anyone living far from a town centre."},{"en":"expose people to fraud","context":"Remote transactions also expose people to fraud in ways that face-to-face dealing did not."},{"en":"operate with fewer premises and staff","context":"Businesses can operate with fewer premises and staff, which reduces costs and prices, and firms in small countries can now sell worldwide."},{"en":"older workers who find retraining difficult","context":"However, the closure of local branches and shops has emptied many high streets, and the employees concerned are often older workers who find retraining difficult."}],"subtype":"Causes / Effects","_displayNo":"#313","summary":"Doing business without meeting face to face"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2201","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Is space exploration worth the money?","prompt":"Some people think that space exploration is a waste of time and money, while others believe that it is important for humankind. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Space programmes cost billions of dollars every year, and opinion is divided over whether this money is well spent. Some regard space exploration as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an expensive distraction</strong>, while others see it as essential to humanity's future. I belong firmly to the second group.\n\nThe case against space exploration is easy to understand. The problems that governments face on Earth are urgent and enormous: poverty, disease, climate change and inadequate schools and hospitals. Critics argue that the sums involved in sending probes to distant planets would produce far greater returns if they were spent on vaccination programmes or clean water supplies. There is also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no guarantee of any benefit at all</strong>, since missions frequently fail and many discoveries interest only scientists.\n\nSupporters reply that the practical benefits are already substantial. Satellite technology, which came directly from the space programme, now <strong class=\"key-phrase\">underpins</strong> weather forecasting, global communications, navigation and the monitoring of deforestation and melting ice. Medical scanners, water purification systems and lightweight materials all <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have their origins in</strong> space research. In the longer term, understanding other planets may be the only way to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">protect the Earth from asteroid impacts</strong> or to secure resources once our own are exhausted.\n\nMy own view is that space exploration is a reasonable investment. The amounts involved are relatively small, since most space agencies receive <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fraction of what governments spend on defence</strong>, so cancelling them would not solve poverty. Meanwhile the scientific returns have repeatedly proved <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far larger than anyone predicted</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, although the money could certainly be spent elsewhere, I believe space exploration is genuinely important for humankind.","phrases":[{"en":"underpins","context":"Satellite technology, which came directly from the space programme, now underpins weather forecasting, global communications, navigation and the monitoring of deforestation and melting ice."},{"en":"have their origins in","context":"Medical scanners, water purification systems and lightweight materials all have their origins in space research."},{"en":"far larger than anyone predicted","context":"Meanwhile the scientific returns have repeatedly proved far larger than anyone predicted."},{"en":"an expensive distraction","context":"Some regard space exploration as an expensive distraction, while others see it as essential to humanity's future."},{"en":"no guarantee of any benefit at all","context":"There is also no guarantee of any benefit at all, since missions frequently fail and many discoveries interest only scientists."},{"en":"a fraction of what governments spend on defence","context":"The amounts involved are relatively small, since most space agencies receive a fraction of what governments spend on defence, so cancelling them would not solve poverty."},{"en":"protect the Earth from asteroid impacts","context":"In the longer term, understanding other planets may be the only way to protect the Earth from asteroid impacts or to secure resources once our own are exhausted."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#314","summary":"Is space exploration worth the money?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2215","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Are robots essential or dangerous?","prompt":"Some people think that robots are very important to humans' future development, while others think that they are dangerous and have negative effects on society. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"Robots are already common in factories, hospitals and warehouses, and opinion is divided over whether they represent progress or a threat. In my view the technology itself is clearly valuable, and the real danger lies in how societies choose to manage its effects.\n\nThose who see robots as essential point to work that machines simply do better than people. A machine <strong class=\"key-phrase\">does not tire, lose concentration or need to breathe</strong>, so it can weld the same joint ten thousand times without variation, or inspect a damaged nuclear reactor where a human being would receive a fatal dose of radiation. Surgical robots allow instruments to move within a millimetre of a target, which shortens recovery times and reduces the risk of complications. Work of this kind is either beyond human capability or dangerous enough that we should welcome a machine taking it on.\n\nThe opposing argument is that automation <strong class=\"key-phrase\">destroys livelihoods faster than new ones appear</strong>. When a distribution centre installs robotic pickers, the jobs that disappear are exactly the ones that gave school leavers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a first foothold in employment</strong>, and a warehouse worker of fifty cannot easily <strong class=\"key-phrase\">retrain as a software engineer</strong>. There is also a legitimate worry about who owns the machines, because the gains from automation flow to the small number of firms that can afford it.\n\nI find the second concern serious but misdirected. Every objection above describes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a failure of policy rather than a fault in the technology</strong>, and countries that fund retraining and tax productivity gains have absorbed automation without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mass displacement</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, robots will be central to future development because they extend what human beings can safely do. Whether that development benefits everyone depends on governments preparing for the transition rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaving workers to adjust alone</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"retrain as a software engineer","context":"When a distribution centre installs robotic pickers, the jobs that disappear are exactly the ones that gave school leavers a first foothold in employment, and a warehouse worker of fifty cannot easily retrain as a software engineer."},{"en":"mass displacement","context":"I find the second concern serious but misdirected. Every objection above describes a failure of policy rather than a fault in the technology, and countries that fund retraining and tax productivity gains have absorbed automation without mass displacement."},{"en":"leaving workers to adjust alone","context":"In conclusion, robots will be central to future development because they extend what human beings can safely do. Whether that development benefits everyone depends on governments preparing for the transition rather than leaving workers to adjust alone."},{"en":"does not tire, lose concentration or need to breathe","context":"A machine does not tire, lose concentration or need to breathe, so it can weld the same joint ten thousand times without variation, or inspect a damaged nuclear reactor where a human being would receive a fatal dose of radiation."},{"en":"destroys livelihoods faster than new ones appear","context":"The opposing argument is that automation destroys livelihoods faster than new ones appear."},{"en":"a first foothold in employment","context":"When a distribution centre installs robotic pickers, the jobs that disappear are exactly the ones that gave school leavers a first foothold in employment, and a warehouse worker of fifty cannot easily retrain as a software engineer."},{"en":"a failure of policy rather than a fault in the technology","context":"I find the second concern serious but misdirected. Every objection above describes a failure of policy rather than a fault in the technology, and countries that fund retraining and tax productivity gains have absorbed automation without mass displacement."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#315","summary":"Robots and humanity's future development"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2218","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Storing knowledge online","prompt":"In the past, knowledge was stored in books. Today, people store knowledge on the internet. Do the advantages of this outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"Human knowledge was once held in printed volumes on library shelves; it now sits on servers and is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">retrieved in seconds</strong>. In my view the advantages of this shift are decisive, although it has introduced problems that printed books never had.\n\nThe most important gain is access. A medical paper that would once have existed in a few hundred university libraries can now be read by a doctor in a rural clinic with a phone, and a student who cannot afford textbooks can study from free encyclopaedias and lecture recordings. Digital storage also allows knowledge to be corrected quickly. When a scientific finding is overturned, an online reference is updated within days, whereas a printed edition <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carries the error for years</strong> until the next reprint.\n\nSearchability matters just as much. Finding one statistic in a printed collection could take an afternoon; the same search now takes a moment, which frees researchers to spend their time thinking rather than hunting for material.\n\nThe drawbacks are real, however. Anyone can publish online, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accurate work sits beside deliberate falsehood</strong> with no obvious difference in appearance, and readers who have never been taught to check a source are easily misled. Digital records are also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less permanent than they look</strong>. Websites are taken down, formats become unreadable and whole archives can be lost when a company closes, while a book printed two centuries ago <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remains legible on the shelf</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the benefits clearly outweigh the costs, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">open access to reliable information</strong> changes far more lives than any library system ever could. The disadvantages are nonetheless worth taking seriously, and they argue for teaching people to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">evaluate what they read</strong> and for funding proper digital archives rather than for returning to print.","phrases":[{"en":"carries the error for years","context":"When a scientific finding is overturned, an online reference is updated within days, whereas a printed edition carries the error for years until the next reprint."},{"en":"remains legible on the shelf","context":"Websites are taken down, formats become unreadable and whole archives can be lost when a company closes, while a book printed two centuries ago remains legible on the shelf."},{"en":"evaluate what they read","context":"The disadvantages are nonetheless worth taking seriously, and they argue for teaching people to evaluate what they read and for funding proper digital archives rather than for returning to print."},{"en":"retrieved in seconds","context":"Human knowledge was once held in printed volumes on library shelves; it now sits on servers and is retrieved in seconds. In my view the advantages of this shift are decisive, although it has introduced problems that printed books never had."},{"en":"accurate work sits beside deliberate falsehood","context":"Anyone can publish online, so accurate work sits beside deliberate falsehood with no obvious difference in appearance, and readers who have never been taught to check a source are easily misled."},{"en":"less permanent than they look","context":"Digital records are also less permanent than they look."},{"en":"open access to reliable information","context":"In conclusion, the benefits clearly outweigh the costs, because open access to reliable information changes far more lives than any library system ever could."}],"subtype":"Advantages & Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#316","summary":"Knowledge stored in books compared with on the internet"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2233","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Who benefits from communications technology?","prompt":"Some groups of people have benefited from modern communications technology but some people do not benefit at all. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Mobile networks and the internet are often described as universal, yet their advantages have reached some groups far more completely than others. I agree that the benefits are unevenly distributed, although the claim that certain people gain nothing at all goes too far.\n\nThe clearest winners are those who already had education and money. A professional who can work remotely, bank online and study from anywhere <strong class=\"key-phrase\">converts the technology directly into higher income and greater freedom</strong>. Businesses gain in the same way, since a small firm can now reach customers abroad without an office or a sales force, and a farmer with a phone can check prices before selling rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accepting whatever a trader offers</strong>.\n\nOthers are largely excluded. Elderly people who never used a computer at work face services that assume everyone can navigate an application, and as banks and government offices close their counters, those who cannot go online <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lose access to things they previously managed unaided</strong>. Households too poor for a connection are similarly cut off, and this now damages children directly, since homework and job applications both <strong class=\"key-phrase\">assume a device at home</strong>.\n\nI would qualify the statement in one respect. Even people who use no technology themselves <strong class=\"key-phrase\">benefit from it indirectly</strong>, because ambulances are dispatched faster, medicines are distributed more reliably and the goods in local shops arrive because of systems they never see. The gain is real but invisible to them.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that the benefits fall unequally, and that being unable to get online is now <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a serious disadvantage rather than a preference</strong>. The remedy is not to slow the technology but to keep services reachable by other means and to treat a connection as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a basic utility rather than a luxury</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"accepting whatever a trader offers","context":"Businesses gain in the same way, since a small firm can now reach customers abroad without an office or a sales force, and a farmer with a phone can check prices before selling rather than accepting whatever a trader offers."},{"en":"assume a device at home","context":"Households too poor for a connection are similarly cut off, and this now damages children directly, since homework and job applications both assume a device at home."},{"en":"a basic utility rather than a luxury","context":"The remedy is not to slow the technology but to keep services reachable by other means and to treat a connection as a basic utility rather than a luxury."},{"en":"converts the technology directly into higher income and greater freedom","context":"A professional who can work remotely, bank online and study from anywhere converts the technology directly into higher income and greater freedom."},{"en":"lose access to things they previously managed unaided","context":"Elderly people who never used a computer at work face services that assume everyone can navigate an application, and as banks and government offices close their counters, those who cannot go online lose access to things they previously managed unaided."},{"en":"benefit from it indirectly","context":"Even people who use no technology themselves benefit from it indirectly, because ambulances are dispatched faster, medicines are distributed more reliably and the goods in local shops arrive because of systems they never see."},{"en":"a serious disadvantage rather than a preference","context":"In conclusion, I agree that the benefits fall unequally, and that being unable to get online is now a serious disadvantage rather than a preference."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#317","summary":"Uneven benefits from modern communications technology"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2239","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Should we contact other worlds?","prompt":"Some scientists think that there are intelligent life forms on other planets and that a message should be sent to contact them. Other scientists think that it is a bad idea and would be dangerous. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"Astronomers have identified thousands of planets capable of supporting life, which has revived an old argument about whether humanity should announce its presence. Both sides make serious points, and I favour the cautious position for reasons that are practical rather than fearful.\n\nThose in favour argue that contact would be the most significant discovery in history. Confirming that we are not alone would transform biology, and any civilisation capable of receiving a signal would almost certainly be older than ours, so what could be learned about energy, medicine or survival is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">impossible to estimate in advance</strong>. There is also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an argument from consistency</strong>: we already listen for signals, and a universe in which everyone listens and nobody transmits <strong class=\"key-phrase\">produces silence by design</strong>.\n\nThe opposing view rests on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the asymmetry between us and anyone who replies</strong>. A civilisation able to cross interstellar distances would be so far ahead of us that we would have no way of protecting ourselves if its intentions were not friendly, and human history offers little comfort about what happens when a technologically advanced society meets a weaker one. Critics also object that a handful of researchers are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">speaking on behalf of the entire planet</strong> <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without any mandate to do so</strong>.\n\nMy own view rests on that last point. The risk cannot be estimated, since we have no evidence at all about who might be listening, and a decision whose consequences fall on everyone should not be taken by a small group.\n\nIn conclusion, the scientific case for contact is genuine, and I would not oppose transmission permanently. It should follow an international agreement rather than an individual initiative, because this is one experiment that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot be stopped once it has begun</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"impossible to estimate in advance","context":"Confirming that we are not alone would transform biology, and any civilisation capable of receiving a signal would almost certainly be older than ours, so what could be learned about energy, medicine or survival is impossible to estimate in advance."},{"en":"speaking on behalf of the entire planet","context":"Critics also object that a handful of researchers are speaking on behalf of the entire planet without any mandate to do so."},{"en":"cannot be stopped once it has begun","context":"It should follow an international agreement rather than an individual initiative, because this is one experiment that cannot be stopped once it has begun."},{"en":"an argument from consistency","context":"There is also an argument from consistency: we already listen for signals, and a universe in which everyone listens and nobody transmits produces silence by design."},{"en":"produces silence by design","context":"There is also an argument from consistency: we already listen for signals, and a universe in which everyone listens and nobody transmits produces silence by design."},{"en":"the asymmetry between us and anyone who replies","context":"The opposing view rests on the asymmetry between us and anyone who replies."},{"en":"without any mandate to do so","context":"Critics also object that a handful of researchers are speaking on behalf of the entire planet without any mandate to do so."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#318","summary":"Whether messages should be sent to intelligent life on other planets"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2250","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Are we losing face-to-face skills?","prompt":"Today more and more people are using mobile phones and computers. As a result, people are losing the ability to communicate face to face. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is often claimed that constant messaging is eroding our capacity to talk to each other in person. I partly agree: certain skills are genuinely weakening, but the broader claim that the ability is being lost overstates what the evidence shows.\n\nThere is something in the argument. Conversation in person requires reading a face, tolerating a pause and responding without time to think, and these are skills that improve only with practice. A teenager who conducts most of their social life through a screen gets less of that practice, and it shows in situations such as interviews, where young candidates increasingly report that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">speaking to a stranger feels unnatural</strong>. Devices also interrupt the conversations that do happen, since a phone placed on a table measurably reduces how much either person discloses.\n\nThe stronger objection is that most digital communication replaces no conversation at all. A message to a friend in another country substitutes for a letter or for silence, not for a meeting, and video calls have allowed families separated by migration to see each other in a way that was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">impossible a generation ago</strong>. Technology has also given a voice to people who find speaking difficult, including those with severe anxiety or autism, for whom writing is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not a lesser form of contact</strong>.\n\nMy own conclusion is that the skill is being redistributed rather than lost. People still talk at length to those they care about; what has declined is casual conversation with strangers, because the situations that once forced it have disappeared.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that certain conversational habits are weakening and that phones interrupt time spent together. The ability itself is not disappearing, and the remedy is to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">protect occasions for talking</strong> rather than to reject the technology.","phrases":[{"en":"speaking to a stranger feels unnatural","context":"A teenager who conducts most of their social life through a screen gets less of that practice, and it shows in situations such as interviews, where young candidates increasingly report that speaking to a stranger feels unnatural."},{"en":"impossible a generation ago","context":"A message to a friend in another country substitutes for a letter or for silence, not for a meeting, and video calls have allowed families separated by migration to see each other in a way that was impossible a generation ago."},{"en":"not a lesser form of contact","context":"Technology has also given a voice to people who find speaking difficult, including those with severe anxiety or autism, for whom writing is not a lesser form of contact."},{"en":"protect occasions for talking","context":"In conclusion, I agree that certain conversational habits are weakening and that phones interrupt time spent together. The ability itself is not disappearing, and the remedy is to protect occasions for talking rather than to reject the technology."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#319","summary":"Whether phones and computers are eroding face-to-face communication"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2258","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Do printed books still matter?","prompt":"Some think that printed books are not necessary in the digital era, because all writing can be stored electronically. Others think printed books still play an important role. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"Now that any text can be stored and read on a device, some people regard printed books as an expensive habit. Others argue that the physical object still does something a file cannot, and I find myself closer to the second position.\n\nThe argument against print is largely practical. A single device holds thousands of titles, weighs less than one hardback and can be carried anywhere, which matters greatly to students and to anyone living in a small flat. Digital text is also cheaper to produce and distribute, so a reader in a country with few bookshops can obtain a title immediately, and adjustable type makes reading possible for people with poor eyesight. Printing consumes paper, ink and transport, none of which a download <strong class=\"key-phrase\">requires at any stage</strong>.\n\nThose who defend print point to how reading actually works. Studies of comprehension consistently find that readers remember more from a printed page, partly because the physical position of text helps memory and partly because a book does not interrupt with notifications. A printed book is also permanent in a way that a licensed file is not, since a publisher can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">withdraw a digital edition from devices that have already paid for it</strong>, which has happened more than once.\n\nMy own view is that the two suit different purposes rather than competing. Reference material and casual reading belong on a screen, whereas anything that must be read closely or kept for decades is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">better held in print</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, digital storage has genuine advantages of cost and portability, and it has made reading possible for people who lacked access. Printed books remain necessary nonetheless, because they support deeper reading and because ownership of a physical copy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot be revoked</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"requires at any stage","context":"Printing consumes paper, ink and transport, none of which a download requires at any stage."},{"en":"better held in print","context":"My own view is that the two suit different purposes rather than competing. Reference material and casual reading belong on a screen, whereas anything that must be read closely or kept for decades is better held in print."},{"en":"cannot be revoked","context":"Printed books remain necessary nonetheless, because they support deeper reading and because ownership of a physical copy cannot be revoked."},{"en":"withdraw a digital edition from devices that have already paid for it","context":"A printed book is also permanent in a way that a licensed file is not, since a publisher can withdraw a digital edition from devices that have already paid for it, which has happened more than once."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#320","summary":"The role of printed books in the digital era"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2270","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Has technology made life complicated?","prompt":"Some people think the developments of technology make people's lives more complex, so we should make life simpler without using technology. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Modern life certainly feels busier than it once did, and some conclude that we should simplify it by using less technology. I agree that technology has added complexity, but I disagree entirely with the remedy proposed.\n\nThe observation itself is fair. Tasks that were once single actions now involve accounts, passwords and updates, and a person may manage a dozen applications simply to pay bills and arrange a journey. Technology has also removed the boundaries that used to structure a day, since messages arrive at any hour and work follows people home, so many feel that they are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never entirely off duty</strong>. The pace of change adds to this, because skills learned once now have to be relearned repeatedly.\n\nThe proposed solution nonetheless collapses on inspection. Almost everything that makes contemporary life bearable rests on the technology being rejected: medicine that treats illnesses which were once fatal, the systems that put food in shops, and the networks that let families separated by migration speak daily. Abandoning technology would not produce a simpler life but a shorter and harder one, and the people who romanticise simplicity rarely propose <strong class=\"key-phrase\">giving up antibiotics or clean water</strong>.\n\nThe complexity is also better explained by how technology is used than by its existence. A phone interrupts because notifications are switched on, and work reaches people in the evening because employers expect it to, so both can be changed <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without discarding the device</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that technology has made daily life more complicated and more difficult to switch off from. The answer is to set limits on how it is used rather than to reject it, since removing it would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take the benefits along with the problems</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"never entirely off duty","context":"Technology has also removed the boundaries that used to structure a day, since messages arrive at any hour and work follows people home, so many feel that they are never entirely off duty."},{"en":"giving up antibiotics or clean water","context":"Abandoning technology would not produce a simpler life but a shorter and harder one, and the people who romanticise simplicity rarely propose giving up antibiotics or clean water."},{"en":"without discarding the device","context":"A phone interrupts because notifications are switched on, and work reaches people in the evening because employers expect it to, so both can be changed without discarding the device."},{"en":"take the benefits along with the problems","context":"In conclusion, I agree that technology has made daily life more complicated and more difficult to switch off from. The answer is to set limits on how it is used rather than to reject it, since removing it would take the benefits along with the problems."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#321","summary":"Whether life should be simplified by using less technology"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2293","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Who should control research?","prompt":"Many people believe that scientific research should be carried out and controlled by governments rather than by private companies. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"An increasing share of the world's research is now funded by companies rather than states. I partly agree that governments should control it, but the answer depends on the kind of research rather than on a general principle.\n\nThe argument for public control is strongest where the incentives of business point the wrong way. A company must produce a return, so it works on problems with a paying market: treatments for wealthy patients rather than diseases of the poor, and products rather than the basic knowledge those products rest on. Firms also own their results, so findings are protected by patents instead of shared, and research that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">threatens the value of an existing product</strong> may never be published at all. Public funding is what supports work with no obvious application, which is where the largest discoveries have historically come from.\n\nPrivate research nonetheless does things governments do badly. Turning a discovery into something people can actually use requires enormous investment and a tolerance for failure that no minister enjoys explaining, and companies are far quicker at development, manufacture and distribution. They also respond to demand faster than a committee allocating grants, and public research has weaknesses of its own, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">political priorities can shape findings as easily as commercial ones</strong>.\n\nThe arrangement that works is division rather than control. Governments should fund basic science, medicine for conditions with no market, and anything affecting public safety, while regulating private research strictly: registering trials in advance, requiring publication of negative results, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">preventing a company from suppressing what it does not like</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, government funding is essential for research that markets will not pay for and for knowledge that should belong to everyone. Private research delivers the practical results, so the sensible policy is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">public money for the questions and firm rules for the answers</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"threatens the value of an existing product","context":"Firms also own their results, so findings are protected by patents instead of shared, and research that threatens the value of an existing product may never be published at all."},{"en":"political priorities can shape findings as easily as commercial ones","context":"They also respond to demand faster than a committee allocating grants, and public research has weaknesses of its own, since political priorities can shape findings as easily as commercial ones."},{"en":"preventing a company from suppressing what it does not like","context":"Governments should fund basic science, medicine for conditions with no market, and anything affecting public safety, while regulating private research strictly: registering trials in advance, requiring publication of negative results, and preventing a company from suppressing what it does not like."},{"en":"public money for the questions and firm rules for the answers","context":"Private research delivers the practical results, so the sensible policy is public money for the questions and firm rules for the answers."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#322","summary":"Whether governments rather than private firms should run scientific research"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2295","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Holidays in space","prompt":"Space travel has been possible for some time, and some people believe that space tourism could be developed in the future. Do you think this is a positive or negative development?","answer":"Several companies now sell brief flights beyond the atmosphere, and longer trips are promised within a decade. I regard the development as negative at present, though the reasons are practical rather than a hostility to space exploration.\n\nThere is a reasonable case in its favour. Paying passengers fund the engineering that reduces the cost of reaching orbit, and much of what makes satellites and scientific missions cheaper has been developed by companies whose income comes partly from tourism. The industry creates skilled employment and encourages young people into science, and the experience itself appears to change how travellers regard the planet, since almost every astronaut describes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">seeing the earth without borders</strong> as the strongest impression of the journey.\n\nThe objections are stronger. Each launch releases a large quantity of carbon and particles directly into the upper atmosphere, where the effect on the climate is worse than at ground level, and this is done for a journey lasting minutes. It is difficult to justify at a time when ordinary people are being asked to fly less. The cost is the second objection: tickets are priced beyond almost everyone, so the resources go towards <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an experience available only to the very wealthy</strong> while problems on the ground remain unfunded.\n\nThere are further difficulties. Orbit is already crowded with debris, and increasing traffic raises the risk of collisions that could damage the satellites weather forecasting and communication depend on. Safety regulation has not kept pace with the industry either, and a serious accident involving paying passengers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">would set the whole field back many years</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, space tourism advances useful technology and inspires people, and those benefits are real. Its environmental cost, extreme exclusivity and the pressure it places on orbit make it, for now, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a luxury the moment cannot justify</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"seeing the earth without borders","context":"The industry creates skilled employment and encourages young people into science, and the experience itself appears to change how travellers regard the planet, since almost every astronaut describes seeing the earth without borders as the strongest impression of the journey."},{"en":"an experience available only to the very wealthy","context":"The cost is the second objection: tickets are priced beyond almost everyone, so the resources go towards an experience available only to the very wealthy while problems on the ground remain unfunded."},{"en":"would set the whole field back many years","context":"Safety regulation has not kept pace with the industry either, and a serious accident involving paying passengers would set the whole field back many years."},{"en":"a luxury the moment cannot justify","context":"In conclusion, space tourism advances useful technology and inspires people, and those benefits are real. Its environmental cost, extreme exclusivity and the pressure it places on orbit make it, for now, a luxury the moment cannot justify."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#323","summary":"Whether the development of space tourism would be a good thing"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2310","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Is the Internet the greatest invention?","prompt":"There have been many inventions in human history, such as the wheel. Some people think the most important one is the Internet. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"The Internet has reshaped work, learning and friendship within a single generation, which makes the claim that it leads all inventions tempting. I disagree, because a handful of older inventions did something the Internet has not: they made human life possible at its present scale.\n\nThe case for the Internet is strong on reach. It has given ordinary people access to almost all recorded knowledge, allowed a farmer to check a market price and a student in a village to attend a lecture, and it carries the science, banking and logistics that modern economies run on. It also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">removed distance as a barrier to working together</strong>, which is why research now moves faster than at any earlier point.\n\nThe comparison still fails. Writing, agriculture, the printing press, antibiotics and electricity each altered what a human being could be. Farming allowed settlement and therefore cities; writing allowed one generation to speak to the next; antibiotics turned a scratch from a probable death into an inconvenience. Without electricity there is no Internet at all, and an invention that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depends on an earlier one</strong> can hardly outrank it.\n\nThere is a further difference in kind. Older inventions expanded what was available to humanity; the Internet mostly changes the speed and cost of moving what already exists. That is an enormous gain, but the world managed without it thirty years ago, whereas it could not manage a month without electricity or clean water. Its harms, from surveillance to the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">collapse of shared and reliable information</strong>, also complicate any simple verdict.\n\nIn conclusion, the Internet is arguably the most significant invention of recent decades and has changed daily life profoundly. Measured against inventions that made settlement, memory and survival itself possible, it belongs high on the list rather than at the top of it.","phrases":[{"en":"removed distance as a barrier to working together","context":"It also removed distance as a barrier to working together, which is why research now moves faster than at any earlier point."},{"en":"depends on an earlier one","context":"Without electricity there is no Internet at all, and an invention that depends on an earlier one can hardly outrank it."},{"en":"collapse of shared and reliable information","context":"Its harms, from surveillance to the collapse of shared and reliable information, also complicate any simple verdict."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#324","summary":"Whether the Internet is the most important invention in human history"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2322","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"How technology changed children's free time","prompt":"New technologies have changed the way children spend their free time. Do the advantages of this trend outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"A generation ago children filled their afternoons with bicycles and board games; now most of that time belongs to screens. The change brings real gains, but I believe the disadvantages currently outweigh them.\n\nThe advantages should not be dismissed. A child with a tablet has access to explanations, languages and skills that no local library could supply, and many learn coding, drawing or editing entirely on their own. Games develop planning and quick decision-making, and online play keeps friendships alive across distance, which matters greatly to children who are isolated, disabled or living far from anyone their own age. Technology has also given quiet children <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a way of finding others who share their interests</strong>.\n\nThe costs, though, are physical, social and psychological at once. Time on a screen is time not spent moving, and childhood obesity and poor posture have risen alongside it. Sleep suffers, because devices are used late and delay it. Social skills develop through unsupervised play, where children argue, negotiate and repair things themselves, and a generation with less of that experience is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less practised at handling ordinary friction</strong>. Social media adds a further layer, since children now compare themselves with an endless supply of edited lives, and rates of anxiety among teenagers have climbed accordingly.\n\nThe deepest problem is design. These products are built by companies whose income depends on attention, so they are engineered to be difficult to stop using. Adults struggle against them, and expecting a ten-year-old to succeed is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an unreasonable demand disguised as a lesson in self-control</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, technology has widened what children can learn and how they stay in touch, and no sensible parent would remove it entirely. The harm to sleep, activity and confidence is presently larger, so the answer lies in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">limits set by adults rather than by the child</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"a way of finding others who share their interests","context":"Technology has also given quiet children a way of finding others who share their interests."},{"en":"less practised at handling ordinary friction","context":"Social skills develop through unsupervised play, where children argue, negotiate and repair things themselves, and a generation with less of that experience is less practised at handling ordinary friction."},{"en":"an unreasonable demand disguised as a lesson in self-control","context":"Adults struggle against them, and expecting a ten-year-old to succeed is an unreasonable demand disguised as a lesson in self-control."},{"en":"limits set by adults rather than by the child","context":"The harm to sleep, activity and confidence is presently larger, so the answer lies in limits set by adults rather than by the child."}],"subtype":"Advantages / Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#325","summary":"Whether the effect of new technology on children's leisure is mainly good"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2327","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Should research concentrate on health?","prompt":"Some people think scientific research should focus on solving world health problems. Others think there are more important issues. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Research budgets are finite, so deciding what scientists work on is a real choice rather than an abstract one. Health has an obvious claim on that money, but I believe climate and energy now have a stronger one.\n\nThe argument for health is immediate and human. Millions still die each year from malaria, tuberculosis and diarrhoeal disease, all of which are treatable, and the recent pandemic showed how quickly an unsolved medical problem can halt the world. Health research also pays for itself, since a healthy population works, learns and raises children, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a country cannot develop while its workers are sick</strong>. Ageing populations add further urgency, because dementia and cancer will otherwise consume an ever larger share of national spending.\n\nThe case for other priorities rests on sequence. Climate change is the clearest example: rising temperatures spread disease, ruin harvests and displace populations, so failing to address it creates health crises faster than medicine can cure them. Water, food security and clean energy work the same way, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the cause sits upstream of the symptom</strong>. There is also a practical point that many of the greatest medical advances, from antibiotics to imaging, came from research that was not aimed at any disease, which suggests that directing all science at health would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">close off the discoveries that make health possible</strong>.\n\nMy own view is that the division is artificial and that the sensible order runs from causes to consequences. Money should go first to problems that will generate many others if ignored, which today means energy and climate, while health research continues at a level that keeps existing progress moving.\n\nIn conclusion, world health deserves substantial and permanent funding, and no country should reduce it. Research aimed at <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the conditions that produce ill health</strong> protects more lives than treatment alone, so it deserves the first claim on new money.","phrases":[{"en":"a country cannot develop while its workers are sick","context":"Health research also pays for itself, since a healthy population works, learns and raises children, and a country cannot develop while its workers are sick."},{"en":"the cause sits upstream of the symptom","context":"Water, food security and clean energy work the same way, since the cause sits upstream of the symptom."},{"en":"close off the discoveries that make health possible","context":"There is also a practical point that many of the greatest medical advances, from antibiotics to imaging, came from research that was not aimed at any disease, which suggests that directing all science at health would close off the discoveries that make health possible."},{"en":"the conditions that produce ill health","context":"In conclusion, world health deserves substantial and permanent funding, and no country should reduce it. Research aimed at the conditions that produce ill health protects more lives than treatment alone, so it deserves the first claim on new money."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#326","summary":"Whether scientific research should focus on world health problems above other issues"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2330","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Putting personal details online","prompt":"Many people put their personal and private information, such as their address, phone number and card details, online. Is this a positive or negative development?","answer":"Buying anything, booking anything or applying for anything now requires handing over details that were once kept private. The convenience is genuine, but I regard the development as negative because of how little control people retain afterwards.\n\nThe benefits explain why it happened. Stored details make a purchase take seconds rather than minutes, and an address held by a delivery company allows goods to arrive the next day. Verified identity also makes services safer in some respects, since banks can detect unusual activity and platforms can trace fraudulent sellers. For people who cannot easily travel, being able to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">arrange an entire life from a screen</strong> is not a small gain.\n\nThe dangers are structural rather than occasional. Once information is given, its owner cannot see where it goes: it is copied, sold to advertisers and stored on systems the customer never chose. Major breaches occur every year, and a leaked address, date of birth and card number are enough for identity theft, which victims spend months undoing. The damage is also permanent in a way physical theft is not, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a stolen password can be changed but a date of birth cannot</strong>.\n\nThere is a quieter cost as well. Details collected for one purpose are combined to build profiles that predict income, health and political leaning, and those profiles decide what prices people see and what jobs they are shown. Most users agreed to none of this in any meaningful sense, since consent means <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accepting terms nobody has time to read</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, sharing personal information online buys real convenience and some safety. It is on balance negative, because the risks fall on the individual while the benefits and the data <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accumulate with the companies collecting them</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"arrange an entire life from a screen","context":"For people who cannot easily travel, being able to arrange an entire life from a screen is not a small gain."},{"en":"a stolen password can be changed but a date of birth cannot","context":"The damage is also permanent in a way physical theft is not, because a stolen password can be changed but a date of birth cannot."},{"en":"accepting terms nobody has time to read","context":"Most users agreed to none of this in any meaningful sense, since consent means accepting terms nobody has time to read."},{"en":"accumulate with the companies collecting them","context":"In conclusion, sharing personal information online buys real convenience and some safety. It is on balance negative, because the risks fall on the individual while the benefits and the data accumulate with the companies collecting them."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#327","summary":"Whether sharing addresses, phone numbers and card details online is a good development"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2356","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Getting older people online","prompt":"Mobile phones and the Internet offer many benefits for elderly people, yet they remain the group that uses these technologies the least. In what ways can these tools help older adults? What can be done to encourage them to use modern technology?","answer":"Older people have most to gain from technology that removes distance and effort, yet they are the least likely to use it. The benefits reach independence and health rather than convenience alone, and closing the gap requires teaching rather than persuasion.\n\nThe first benefit is contact. Video calls allow a grandparent to see grandchildren who live in another country, and messaging keeps friendships alive when walking to a friend's house is no longer easy, which matters because loneliness in old age raises the risk of illness as sharply as smoking does. The second is health: appointments, prescriptions and consultations can be handled from home, and monitors can alert a family if something is wrong, so an older person <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stays in their own house for years longer</strong>. Practical independence follows, since banking, shopping and paying bills online spare a difficult journey, and access to news, courses and games gives structure to days that can otherwise be empty.\n\nThe reasons for low use explain what would help. Many older adults are not uninterested but afraid of making an irreversible mistake, and fear of fraud is entirely rational given how often they are targeted. Devices are designed for younger hands and eyes, with small text and constant updates that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">move the buttons they had just learned to find</strong>.\n\nEncouragement therefore has to be practical. Free classes in libraries and community centres, taught slowly and repeated, work far better than a leaflet, and older volunteers teaching their peers removes the embarrassment of asking. Manufacturers can offer simplified settings by default, and banks and health services should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep a telephone alternative open</strong> so that nobody is forced online before they are ready.\n\nIn conclusion, technology can keep older people connected, healthy and independent. Patient teaching, simpler devices and honest advice about fraud would do more than any campaign urging them to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catch up with everybody else</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"stays in their own house for years longer","context":"The second is health: appointments, prescriptions and consultations can be handled from home, and monitors can alert a family if something is wrong, so an older person stays in their own house for years longer."},{"en":"move the buttons they had just learned to find","context":"Devices are designed for younger hands and eyes, with small text and constant updates that move the buttons they had just learned to find."},{"en":"keep a telephone alternative open","context":"Manufacturers can offer simplified settings by default, and banks and health services should keep a telephone alternative open so that nobody is forced online before they are ready."},{"en":"catch up with everybody else","context":"In conclusion, technology can keep older people connected, healthy and independent. Patient teaching, simpler devices and honest advice about fraud would do more than any campaign urging them to catch up with everybody else."}],"subtype":"Two-Part Question","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#328","summary":"How technology helps the elderly and how they can be encouraged to use it"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2367","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Should scientists work across borders?","prompt":"Some people say that scientists around the world should work together on scientific research, but others worry that this may cause problems. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Research teams now routinely span several countries, and the results appear faster than any national effort could produce them. The worries about this are not imaginary, but I believe the benefits are far greater and the risks can be managed by restricting a narrow category of work.\n\nThe case for collaboration is practical. Modern science is expensive: particle accelerators, telescopes and genome projects cost more than most countries could justify alone, and sharing the bill makes them possible at all. Combining specialists also accelerates results, which the development of vaccines within a year demonstrated clearly, and problems such as climate, antibiotic resistance and pandemics <strong class=\"key-phrase\">do not stop at any border that a research budget respects</strong>. Poorer countries gain access to equipment and training they could not otherwise obtain, and results checked by teams in several places are more reliable, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">independent groups catch each other's mistakes</strong>.\n\nThe concerns deserve serious answers. Some research has obvious military application, and openly shared work on viruses, weapons or artificial intelligence could be misused. Countries also compete economically, and a government that funded a discovery may reasonably object to a foreign firm profiting from it. Collaboration can be unequal too, with scientists in developing nations collecting data while institutions elsewhere take the credit, and there is a genuine risk that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">safety rules are avoided by moving the work somewhere permissive</strong>.\n\nThe answer is to sort research by risk rather than to close borders. The overwhelming majority — medicine, climate science, materials, agriculture — should be shared as widely as possible, while a small class of dual-use work is governed by common international standards and proper security.\n\nIn conclusion, scientific cooperation produces faster, cheaper and more trustworthy results, and the largest problems cannot be solved without it. The dangers are real but confined, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regulating the exceptions is wiser than restricting the whole</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"do not stop at any border that a research budget respects","context":"Combining specialists also accelerates results, which the development of vaccines within a year demonstrated clearly, and problems such as climate, antibiotic resistance and pandemics do not stop at any border that a research budget respects."},{"en":"independent groups catch each other's mistakes","context":"Poorer countries gain access to equipment and training they could not otherwise obtain, and results checked by teams in several places are more reliable, since independent groups catch each other's mistakes."},{"en":"safety rules are avoided by moving the work somewhere permissive","context":"Collaboration can be unequal too, with scientists in developing nations collecting data while institutions elsewhere take the credit, and there is a genuine risk that safety rules are avoided by moving the work somewhere permissive."},{"en":"regulating the exceptions is wiser than restricting the whole","context":"In conclusion, scientific cooperation produces faster, cheaper and more trustworthy results, and the largest problems cannot be solved without it. The dangers are real but confined, so regulating the exceptions is wiser than restricting the whole."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#329","summary":"Whether international scientific collaboration is worth its risks"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2376","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Is much research a waste of money?","prompt":"Some people say that a lot of the scientific research conducted today is a waste of time and money. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Newspapers regularly report studies that appear trivial, and the sums involved are public money. I disagree with the complaint in general, though it identifies genuine problems in how research is currently organised.\n\nThe main reason for disagreeing is that useful research cannot be identified in advance. Work on bacterial defence mechanisms, undertaken out of curiosity, produced the gene-editing tools now used against inherited disease, and the mathematics behind encryption was developed by people who expected no application at all. Judging a project by whether its title sounds practical would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have prevented most of the discoveries we now depend on</strong>. Failure is also part of the process, since an experiment that rules out an explanation saves everyone else from repeating it, even though nothing is announced.\n\nThe criticism nonetheless has substance. A great deal of research is never read, because academic careers depend on the number of papers published rather than on their value, which encourages small, safe studies split into as many articles as possible. Results that fail to confirm a hypothesis often go unpublished, so the same ground <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is covered repeatedly by teams unaware of each other</strong>. There is also a serious problem with reliability, since many findings in some fields cannot be reproduced, and money spent on work nobody can confirm is genuinely wasted.\n\nThese are arguments for reform rather than for cutting research. Funding longer projects, rewarding replication, requiring that all results be published and judging scientists on quality rather than quantity would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remove most of the waste without touching the discovery</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the value of research cannot be predicted, and work that looks pointless has repeatedly turned out to matter enormously. The waste lies in the incentives placed on researchers, so the answer is to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">change how science is funded and judged</strong> rather than to spend less on it.","phrases":[{"en":"have prevented most of the discoveries we now depend on","context":"Judging a project by whether its title sounds practical would have prevented most of the discoveries we now depend on."},{"en":"is covered repeatedly by teams unaware of each other","context":"Results that fail to confirm a hypothesis often go unpublished, so the same ground is covered repeatedly by teams unaware of each other."},{"en":"remove most of the waste without touching the discovery","context":"Funding longer projects, rewarding replication, requiring that all results be published and judging scientists on quality rather than quantity would remove most of the waste without touching the discovery."},{"en":"change how science is funded and judged","context":"The waste lies in the incentives placed on researchers, so the answer is to change how science is funded and judged rather than to spend less on it."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#330","summary":"Whether a large share of current scientific research is time and money wasted"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Why people travel more, and what they gain","prompt":"Today people are travelling more than ever before. Why is this the case? What are the benefits of travelling for the traveller?","answer":"People travel more than in the past because journeys have become cheaper, faster and easier to arrange. Higher incomes and flexible working patterns have also made travel possible for a wider group of people. Overall, this trend brings substantial personal and economic benefits.\n\nLow-cost airlines, online booking and improved transport networks have reduced both the price and difficulty of travel. Social media also exposes people to destinations that they might never have considered. In addition, many people now study, work or have relatives abroad, so international travel has become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">part of ordinary life</strong> rather than a rare luxury.\n\nTravel can broaden people's understanding by placing them in unfamiliar environments. Visitors learn how others live, communicate and solve everyday problems. Tourism also creates employment in transport, accommodation and local services. When managed well, it can provide income for rural areas and help fund the protection of historic sites and natural attractions.\n\nHowever, these benefits depend on responsible behaviour. Overcrowding, pollution and rising housing costs can harm local communities. Governments should therefore control development, improve public transport and ensure that tourism revenue reaches residents. With sensible planning, travel can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">support cultural exchange and local prosperity</strong> without overwhelming destinations.\n\nTravel also builds a kind of practical confidence that a familiar routine at home rarely offers. Even a short trip forces travellers to solve unfamiliar problems alone, such as reading a foreign menu or rearranging a delayed connection, and most people manage far better than they expected. At the same time, employers and universities increasingly value international experience, treating it as evidence of independence. A well-planned trip can therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">combine personal enjoyment with practical learning</strong>, rather than serving only as a break from routine.","topic":"Tourism & Travel","phrases":[{"en":"part of ordinary life","context":"In addition, many people now study, work or have relatives abroad, so international travel has become part of ordinary life rather than a rare luxury."},{"en":"support cultural exchange and local prosperity","context":"With sensible planning, travel can support cultural exchange and local prosperity without overwhelming destinations."},{"en":"combine personal enjoyment with practical learning","context":"A well-planned trip can therefore combine personal enjoyment with practical learning, rather than serving only as a break from routine."}],"subtype":"two-part","summary":"Why people travel more, and what travellers gain from it","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-087","_displayNo":"#331"},{"id":"writing-task-2-178","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Tourism & Travel","title":"Should tourists pay more than local visitors?","prompt":"Foreign visitors should pay more than local visitors for cultural and historical attractions. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this opinion?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that tourists from overseas should be charged more than local residents to visit important sites and monuments. I completely disagree with this idea.\n\nThe argument in favour of higher prices for foreign tourists would be that cultural or historical attractions often depend on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">state subsidies</strong> to keep them going, which means that the resident population already pays money to these sites through the tax system. However, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">I believe this to be a very shortsighted view</strong>. Foreign tourists contribute to the economy of the host country with the money they spend on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a wide range of goods and services</strong>, including food, souvenirs, accommodation and travel. The governments and inhabitants of every country should be happy to subsidise important tourist sites and encourage people from the rest of the world to visit them.\n\nIf travellers realised that they would have to pay more to visit historical and cultural attractions in a particular nation, they would perhaps decide not to go to that country on holiday. To take the UK as an example, the tourism industry and many related jobs rely on visitors coming to the country to see places like Windsor Castle or Saint Paul's Cathedral. These two sites charge the same price <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regardless of nationality</strong>, and this helps to promote the nation's cultural heritage. If overseas tourists stopped coming due to higher prices, there would be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a risk of insufficient funding for the maintenance</strong> of these important buildings.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe that every effort should be made to attract tourists from overseas, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">it would be counter productive</strong> to make them pay more than local residents.","phrases":[{"en":"state subsidies","context":"The argument in favour of higher prices for foreign tourists would be that cultural or historical attractions often depend on state subsidies to keep them going, which means that the resident population already pays money to these sites through the tax system."},{"en":"a wide range of goods and services","context":"Foreign tourists contribute to the economy of the host country with the money they spend on a wide range of goods and services, including food, souvenirs, accommodation and travel."},{"en":"I believe this to be a very shortsighted view","context":"However, I believe this to be a very shortsighted view."},{"en":"regardless of nationality","context":"These two sites charge the same price regardless of nationality, and this helps to promote the nation's cultural heritage."},{"en":"a risk of insufficient funding for the maintenance","context":"If overseas tourists stopped coming due to higher prices, there would be a risk of insufficient funding for the maintenance of these important buildings."},{"en":"it would be counter productive","context":"In conclusion, I believe that every effort should be made to attract tourists from overseas, and it would be counter productive to make them pay more than local residents."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#332","summary":"Higher entrance fees for foreign tourists"},{"id":"writing-task-2-179","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Tourism & Travel","title":"Is tourism always a force for good?","prompt":"'Tourism is always a force for good which enables people of different countries to understand each other.' To what extent do you agree with this idea?","answer":"The role of tourism in the world today is widely debated, with some people claiming that it is indeed <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a force for international understanding</strong>. However, I personally do not entirely accept this, and I will explain why in this essay.\n\nFirstly, we must consider the damage that visitors do to the places they travel to see. When large numbers of western tourists reach relatively untouched regions, the roads, hotels and flights built to accommodate them bring waste, traffic and pressure on scarce water. We see this in the litter left on the trails around Machu Picchu in Peru, and in the erosion and disturbance to wildlife caused by safari vehicles in East Africa.\n\nThere are also numerous examples of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tension between tourists and local cultures</strong>, ranging from drunken British tourists in Europe to American ‘spring breakers’ in Canadian or Latin American resorts. In these cases, tourism actually leads to resentment and distrust between nationalities, not better understanding.\n\nA further reason to be wary of this idea is the economic aspect. It is sometimes said that tourism improves international relations because it encourages the flow of money from wealthy to less developed countries. In reality, however, the wealth generated usually stays in the hands of private companies or local officials, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rarely drips down into the population</strong>. Many of us have visited less developed nations as tourists, and have witnessed at first hand the poverty that exists <strong class=\"key-phrase\">outside the confines of the resort hotels</strong>. We leave with the impression that our stay has contributed nothing to the lives of those we have observed.\n\nThis is not to say that tourism is entirely bad, of course. There are benefits in terms of improving local infrastructure such as roads and airports. But to say that it is 'always a force for good' is to ignore the various problems it causes.","phrases":[{"en":"a force for international understanding","context":"The role of tourism in the world today is widely debated, with some people claiming that it is indeed a force for international understanding. However, I personally do not entirely accept this, and I will explain why in this essay."},{"en":"tension between tourists and local cultures","context":"There are also numerous examples of tension between tourists and local cultures, ranging from drunken British tourists in Europe to American ‘spring breakers’ in Canadian or Latin American resorts."},{"en":"rarely drips down into the population","context":"In reality, however, the wealth generated usually stays in the hands of private companies or local officials, and rarely drips down into the population."},{"en":"outside the confines of the resort hotels","context":"Many of us have visited less developed nations as tourists, and have witnessed at first hand the poverty that exists outside the confines of the resort hotels."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#333","summary":"‘Tourism is always a force for good which enables people of different…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-180","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Punishing drivers, or other routes to road safety","prompt":"Some people think that strict punishments for driving offences are the key to reducing traffic accidents. Others, however, believe that other measures would be more effective in improving road safety. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People have differing views with regard to the question of how to make our roads safer. In my view, both punishments and a range of other measures can be used together to promote better driving habits. On the one hand, strict punishments can certainly help to encourage people to drive more safely. Penalties for dangerous drivers can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">act as a deterrent</strong>, meaning that people avoid <strong class=\"key-phrase\">repeating the same offence</strong>. There are various types of driving penalty, such as small fines, licence suspension, driver awareness courses, and even prison sentences. The aim of these punishments is to show dangerous drivers that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">their actions have negative consequences</strong>. As a result, we would hope that drivers become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more disciplined and alert</strong>, and that they follow the rules more carefully.\n\nSeveral other approaches, however, do not rely on punishment at all. Education is probably the most important: a driver who has never been taught to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">anticipate danger</strong> will not necessarily drive more carefully simply because a fine is possible, so schools and extended driving tests could teach hazard awareness <strong class=\"key-phrase\">before bad habits form</strong>, rather than punishing them once the damage is done. Road design can reinforce the same goal. Speed bumps, road bends and warning signs physically slow traffic down in places where drivers might otherwise take risks, and speed cameras extend that effect to stretches of road where no officer is watching.\n\nA different kind of solution is to reduce the number of car journeys that take place at all. When governments and local councils invest in reliable, affordable public transport, they give people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a genuine alternative to driving</strong>, and a road with fewer cars on it is safer for everyone, regardless of how carefully any individual driver behaves.\n\nIn conclusion, while punishment plays a useful role in deterring dangerous drivers, I believe it works best alongside education, safer road design and investment in transport that reduces the need to drive in the first place.","phrases":[{"en":"act as a deterrent","context":"Penalties for dangerous drivers can act as a deterrent, meaning that people avoid repeating the same offence."},{"en":"repeating the same offence","context":"Penalties for dangerous drivers can act as a deterrent, meaning that people avoid repeating the same offence."},{"en":"their actions have negative consequences","context":"The aim of these punishments is to show dangerous drivers that their actions have negative consequences."},{"en":"more disciplined and alert","context":"As a result, we would hope that drivers become more disciplined and alert, and that they follow the rules more carefully."},{"en":"anticipate danger","context":"Education is probably the most important: a driver who has never been taught to anticipate danger will not necessarily drive more carefully simply because a fine is possible, so schools and extended driving tests could teach hazard awareness before bad habits form, rather than punishing them once..."},{"en":"before bad habits form","context":"Education is probably the most important: a driver who has never been taught to anticipate danger will not necessarily drive more carefully simply because a fine is possible, so schools and extended driving tests could teach hazard awareness before bad habits form, rather than punishing them once..."},{"en":"a genuine alternative to driving","context":"When governments and local councils invest in reliable, affordable public transport, they give people a genuine alternative to driving, and a road with fewer cars on it is safer for everyone, regardless of how carefully any individual driver behaves."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#334","summary":"Road safety: punishment or prevention"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2227","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Raising the legal driving age","prompt":"Some people think that the best way to improve road safety is to increase the minimum legal age for driving a car or motorbike. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Young drivers are involved in a disproportionate share of serious accidents, and one proposed remedy is simply to make them wait longer for a licence. I agree that raising the age would save lives, but I do not accept that it is the best measure available.\n\nThere is a sound reason why age matters. The part of the brain that weighs risk against reward continues developing into the mid-twenties, which is why a seventeen-year-old is more likely to overtake on a blind bend or drive at speed with friends in the car. Raising the minimum age by two years would remove the least experienced and most impulsive drivers from the road at the point when they are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">most likely to misjudge a situation</strong>, and the accident figures would fall accordingly.\n\nThe objection is that age is a crude substitute for what actually causes crashes, which is inexperience. A twenty-year-old in their first month of driving is little safer than an eighteen-year-old in their first month, so delaying the licence <strong class=\"key-phrase\">postpones the risk rather than removing it</strong>. There is also a cost that falls unevenly: in rural areas without buses, losing two years of independent travel can mean losing access to work and education.\n\nMeasures aimed directly at inexperience appear to work better. Graduated licensing, which restricts night driving and the number of young passengers during the first year, has cut fatalities substantially where it has been introduced, and it does so without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shutting anyone out of employment</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I partly agree, since a higher age would reduce deaths. It is nonetheless a blunt instrument, and a graduated licence combined with stricter testing addresses the same problem while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">imposing a far smaller cost</strong> on young people who need to travel.","phrases":[{"en":"most likely to misjudge a situation","context":"Raising the minimum age by two years would remove the least experienced and most impulsive drivers from the road at the point when they are most likely to misjudge a situation, and the accident figures would fall accordingly."},{"en":"postpones the risk rather than removing it","context":"A twenty-year-old in their first month of driving is little safer than an eighteen-year-old in their first month, so delaying the licence postpones the risk rather than removing it."},{"en":"shutting anyone out of employment","context":"Graduated licensing, which restricts night driving and the number of young passengers during the first year, has cut fatalities substantially where it has been introduced, and it does so without shutting anyone out of employment."},{"en":"imposing a far smaller cost","context":"In conclusion, I partly agree, since a higher age would reduce deaths. It is nonetheless a blunt instrument, and a graduated licence combined with stricter testing addresses the same problem while imposing a far smaller cost on young people who need to travel."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#335","summary":"Increasing the minimum driving age to improve road safety"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2256","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Cars or bicycles in cities?","prompt":"In some cities, people are choosing cars instead of bicycles, while in other cities riding bikes is replacing cars. Why is this the case? Which development do you think is better?","answer":"Cities are moving in opposite directions on transport: some are filling with cars while others are steadily returning to the bicycle. The difference is explained mainly by income and by planning decisions, and I believe the shift towards cycling is clearly the better development.\n\nThe move towards cars usually happens in rapidly developing economies. As incomes rise, a car becomes affordable and is treated as evidence of success, while cycling is associated with not being able to afford anything else. Distance reinforces this, since cities that grow outwards create journeys too long to cycle, and where roads have no protected lanes, riding among heavy traffic is genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">too dangerous to be attractive</strong>.\n\nThe return to bicycles is largely a matter of deliberate policy. Cities such as Amsterdam and Copenhagen did not simply discover a cultural preference; they built separated lanes, secure parking and traffic rules that favour cyclists, and usage followed. Congestion helps, because in a dense city a bicycle is often faster door to door than a car, and awareness of air quality has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changed how residents vote</strong>.\n\nCycling is the better direction for reasons that go beyond emissions. A city that moves people by bicycle needs far less space for each traveller, which frees streets for housing, trees and shops rather than parking. The health effect is also substantial, since a commute that includes exercise reduces heart disease measurably, and quieter streets are safer for children. Cars deliver comfort to the driver while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">imposing noise and danger on everyone else</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the two trends reflect rising incomes on one side and investment in infrastructure on the other. Cycling is preferable, because it produces a city that is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">healthier and more pleasant to live in</strong> rather than merely easier to drive through.","phrases":[{"en":"too dangerous to be attractive","context":"Distance reinforces this, since cities that grow outwards create journeys too long to cycle, and where roads have no protected lanes, riding among heavy traffic is genuinely too dangerous to be attractive."},{"en":"changed how residents vote","context":"Congestion helps, because in a dense city a bicycle is often faster door to door than a car, and awareness of air quality has changed how residents vote."},{"en":"imposing noise and danger on everyone else","context":"Cars deliver comfort to the driver while imposing noise and danger on everyone else."},{"en":"healthier and more pleasant to live in","context":"In conclusion, the two trends reflect rising incomes on one side and investment in infrastructure on the other. Cycling is preferable, because it produces a city that is healthier and more pleasant to live in rather than merely easier to drive through."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#336","summary":"Why some cities are shifting to cars and others to bicycles"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2263","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Rails or roads?","prompt":"Some people believe the government should spend money on building train and subway lines to reduce traffic congestion. Others think that building more and wider roads is the better solution. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Governments facing congestion must choose between investing in rail and expanding the road network. Both approaches have been tried extensively, and the evidence points strongly towards rail in large cities.\n\nThose who favour roads argue from flexibility and cost. A road serves cars, buses, lorries and emergency vehicles alike, and it can be built in stages wherever demand appears, whereas a metro line takes a decade and enormous capital before carrying a single passenger. Roads also reach places rail cannot justify, and for freight and for residents of low-density areas there is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no practical alternative to driving</strong>.\n\nThe argument for rail rests on capacity. A single metro line can move as many people per hour as a dozen lanes of traffic, on a fraction of the land, which matters most in exactly the dense areas where congestion is worst. There is also a well-documented problem with the road solution: added capacity fills up. When a motorway is widened, journeys that were previously avoided become attractive, traffic grows to fill the new lanes, and within a few years the delay <strong class=\"key-phrase\">returns to where it started</strong>. Rail does not generate congestion in the same way.\n\nMy own view is that this asymmetry decides the question for cities. Widening roads treats the symptom and reliably recreates the problem, while a rail line removes cars permanently by offering a faster option, and it does so without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">demolishing the streets it is meant to serve</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, roads remain necessary for freight and for areas too thinly populated for rail. In congested cities, however, investment should go to trains and metros, because road building has repeatedly been shown to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">create the traffic it was intended to relieve</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"no practical alternative to driving","context":"Roads also reach places rail cannot justify, and for freight and for residents of low-density areas there is no practical alternative to driving."},{"en":"returns to where it started","context":"When a motorway is widened, journeys that were previously avoided become attractive, traffic grows to fill the new lanes, and within a few years the delay returns to where it started."},{"en":"demolishing the streets it is meant to serve","context":"Widening roads treats the symptom and reliably recreates the problem, while a rail line removes cars permanently by offering a faster option, and it does so without demolishing the streets it is meant to serve."},{"en":"create the traffic it was intended to relieve","context":"In congested cities, however, investment should go to trains and metros, because road building has repeatedly been shown to create the traffic it was intended to relieve."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#337","summary":"Building rail lines compared with building more roads"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2285","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"A driving test every year","prompt":"The best way to make road transport safer is to require drivers to take a driving test every year. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Most drivers pass one examination in their late teens and are never assessed again, which is a strange arrangement for an activity that kills over a million people a year. I agree that periodic checks would help, but annual testing for everybody would be an expensive way to address the wrong problem.\n\nThe reasoning behind re-testing is sound. Skills decay and bad habits accumulate, so a driver of thirty years' experience may have forgotten rules that have since changed and acquired several dangerous routines. Eyesight and reaction times also decline gradually, and the person concerned is usually the last to notice. A test would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">correct faults before they cause an accident</strong> rather than after.\n\nThe objections are practical and substantial. Testing every licence holder each year would require an enormous administration, and the cost would fall on drivers, which penalises those who need a car to work. It is also poorly targeted, since most accidents are not caused by an inability to drive: speeding, alcohol, tiredness and phone use cause the majority, and all of them are committed by people who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">would pass any test on the day of the examination</strong>. A driver on their best behaviour for forty minutes proves very little about the other days.\n\nMeasures with stronger evidence behind them exist. Enforcement of speed limits by camera, serious penalties for drink-driving and phone use, graduated licences that restrict new drivers, safer road design and vehicle technology such as automatic braking have all <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduced deaths wherever they were introduced</strong>. Periodic checks still have a place, particularly eyesight tests and assessments for older drivers, but every few years rather than annually.\n\nIn conclusion, drivers should be re-checked from time to time, since skills and eyesight change. Annual testing would cost far more than it saved, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">enforcement, road design and vehicle safety</strong> remain the better routes to safer roads.","phrases":[{"en":"correct faults before they cause an accident","context":"A test would correct faults before they cause an accident rather than after."},{"en":"would pass any test on the day of the examination","context":"It is also poorly targeted, since most accidents are not caused by an inability to drive: speeding, alcohol, tiredness and phone use cause the majority, and all of them are committed by people who would pass any test on the day of the examination."},{"en":"reduced deaths wherever they were introduced","context":"Enforcement of speed limits by camera, serious penalties for drink-driving and phone use, graduated licences that restrict new drivers, safer road design and vehicle technology such as automatic braking have all reduced deaths wherever they were introduced."},{"en":"enforcement, road design and vehicle safety","context":"In conclusion, drivers should be re-checked from time to time, since skills and eyesight change. Annual testing would cost far more than it saved, so enforcement, road design and vehicle safety remain the better routes to safer roads."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#338","summary":"Whether annual re-testing of drivers is the best way to improve road safety"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2288","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Should people be encouraged into cities?","prompt":"The best way to solve traffic and transport problems is to encourage people to live in cities rather than in suburbs or the countryside. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Suburban living generates far more travel per household than city living does, which makes concentrating the population look like an obvious remedy. I partly agree, since density genuinely reduces the need to travel, but calling it the best solution overstates what it can achieve.\n\nThe transport case for density is strong. A city dweller can walk to a shop, cycle to work or take a train that runs every few minutes, because enough passengers exist to justify the service, whereas a suburb of detached houses cannot support a bus route at all. Journeys are also shorter: the same trip that takes twenty minutes by car in a spread-out area takes five on foot in a dense one. Fewer vehicles means fewer emissions and accidents, and the land saved from roads and car parks can be used for housing.\n\nThe proposal has serious limits, however. People live in suburbs largely because city housing is expensive and cramped, so encouraging a move without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">building affordable family homes in the centre</strong> simply pushes prices higher and moves the problem. Cities also become congested in their own way, and a district that gains residents without gaining transport capacity <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exchanges long journeys for crowded ones</strong>. Not everyone can move in any case, since farming, tourism and much industry are located where they are, and emptying rural areas has consequences of its own.\n\nMore effective measures work on the journeys rather than the addresses. Building homes near stations, allowing shops and offices within residential districts, improving rural bus services and letting people work remotely for part of the week all <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shorten travel without requiring anybody to relocate</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, denser cities do reduce transport demand and deserve encouragement through planning and housing policy. Density alone will not solve the problem, so it belongs among several measures rather than ahead of them.","phrases":[{"en":"building affordable family homes in the centre","context":"People live in suburbs largely because city housing is expensive and cramped, so encouraging a move without building affordable family homes in the centre simply pushes prices higher and moves the problem."},{"en":"exchanges long journeys for crowded ones","context":"Cities also become congested in their own way, and a district that gains residents without gaining transport capacity exchanges long journeys for crowded ones."},{"en":"shorten travel without requiring anybody to relocate","context":"Building homes near stations, allowing shops and offices within residential districts, improving rural bus services and letting people work remotely for part of the week all shorten travel without requiring anybody to relocate."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#339","summary":"Whether concentrating the population in cities is the way to fix transport problems"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2291","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Why fares keep rising","prompt":"Nowadays, public transport prices are constantly increasing. Why is this happening? How can this problem be solved?","answer":"Bus and rail fares in many countries have risen faster than wages for years, which is a strange outcome for a service governments say they want people to use. The causes lie in how the service is funded, and the remedies must change that rather than simply capping the price.\n\nThe underlying cost is genuinely rising. Fuel, electricity and insurance have all become more expensive, staff wages have to keep pace with the labour market, and ageing trains, track and buses need replacement, which is paid for over years through fares. Falling passenger numbers make this worse, because a service with fewer travellers still costs the same to run, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the remaining passengers carry a larger share of a fixed bill</strong>. Where operators are private and hold a monopoly on a route, there is little pressure to keep prices down. Above all, governments have shifted the burden from taxation to the traveller, and each reduction in subsidy appears directly on the ticket.\n\nSeveral remedies are available. The most direct is to restore public funding, on the reasoning that transport produces benefits — less congestion, cleaner air, access to work — that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">passengers pay for but everybody enjoys</strong>. Money can also be raised from the beneficiaries: congestion charges, parking levies and taxes on the land whose value a new station creates. Simpler, cheaper fares tend to be self-financing, because they increase ridership, and countries that introduced flat monthly tickets saw both use and revenue rise.\n\nEfficiency matters too. Contracts open to competition, shared ticketing across operators and long-term investment plans reduce cost, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stop-and-start funding makes every project more expensive</strong>. Targeted discounts protect students, the elderly and low-paid workers without subsidising everyone.\n\nIn conclusion, fares rise because costs are increasing while public support is falling. Restoring subsidy, raising money from drivers and land value, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">simplifying fares to attract more passengers</strong> would keep transport affordable.","phrases":[{"en":"the remaining passengers carry a larger share of a fixed bill","context":"Falling passenger numbers make this worse, because a service with fewer travellers still costs the same to run, so the remaining passengers carry a larger share of a fixed bill."},{"en":"passengers pay for but everybody enjoys","context":"The most direct is to restore public funding, on the reasoning that transport produces benefits — less congestion, cleaner air, access to work — that passengers pay for but everybody enjoys."},{"en":"stop-and-start funding makes every project more expensive","context":"Contracts open to competition, shared ticketing across operators and long-term investment plans reduce cost, since stop-and-start funding makes every project more expensive."},{"en":"simplifying fares to attract more passengers","context":"In conclusion, fares rise because costs are increasing while public support is falling. Restoring subsidy, raising money from drivers and land value, and simplifying fares to attract more passengers would keep transport affordable."}],"subtype":"Problem / Solution","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#340","summary":"The reasons behind rising public transport fares and how to control them"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2298","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Living by car","prompt":"Many people depend on their cars for everyday domestic, social and working needs. However, unlimited use of cars causes a number of problems. What are some of these problems? What can be done to reduce car use?","answer":"In many countries the car has moved from a convenience to a requirement, and the costs of that arrangement have become difficult to ignore. The problems are environmental, spatial and social at once, and reducing dependence means changing what cities look like rather than lecturing drivers.\n\nPollution is the most familiar problem. Vehicles are a major source of the carbon dioxide driving climate change and of the fine particles that cause respiratory and heart disease in cities. Road deaths are the second, since traffic remains one of the leading causes of death among young people. Space is a less obvious cost: roads, junctions and parking consume a vast share of urban land that could hold housing or parks, and each additional lane fills with new traffic within a few years. There are social effects as well, because streets designed for vehicles are unpleasant to walk on, so children are driven rather than allowed to travel alone, and those without a car — the young, the old, the poor — <strong class=\"key-phrase\">find that the city has been built around a machine they do not own</strong>.\n\nReducing use requires making the alternatives genuinely better. Frequent, reliable public transport matters most, along with protected cycle lanes, since most people will not cycle beside heavy traffic. Planning is equally important: mixing homes, shops and schools within a district <strong class=\"key-phrase\">removes the journey rather than shortening it</strong>. Where driving remains attractive, congestion charges and parking prices work, provided the revenue improves the alternatives. Working from home for part of the week has already cut commuting substantially, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">electric vehicles help with pollution but not with space or safety</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, heavy car use damages health, consumes land and excludes those without a vehicle. Cutting it depends on building cities where <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the shortest and easiest journey is not by car</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"find that the city has been built around a machine they do not own","context":"There are social effects as well, because streets designed for vehicles are unpleasant to walk on, so children are driven rather than allowed to travel alone, and those without a car — the young, the old, the poor — find that the city has been built around a machine they do not own."},{"en":"removes the journey rather than shortening it","context":"Planning is equally important: mixing homes, shops and schools within a district removes the journey rather than shortening it."},{"en":"electric vehicles help with pollution but not with space or safety","context":"Working from home for part of the week has already cut commuting substantially, and electric vehicles help with pollution but not with space or safety."},{"en":"the shortest and easiest journey is not by car","context":"In conclusion, heavy car use damages health, consumes land and excludes those without a vehicle. Cutting it depends on building cities where the shortest and easiest journey is not by car."}],"subtype":"Problem / Solution","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#341","summary":"The problems caused by heavy car dependence and how to reduce it"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2300","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Days without cars in the centre","prompt":"Some cities have vehicle-free days when private cars, trucks and motorcycles are banned from the city centre, and people are encouraged to use public transport. Do the advantages outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"A number of cities now close their centres to private vehicles on selected days, and the results are usually striking. I believe the advantages outweigh the disadvantages, mainly because of what such days demonstrate rather than what they achieve directly.\n\nThe immediate benefits are easy to measure. Air quality improves sharply within hours, and the reduction in noise changes the character of a street entirely. Roads become safe enough for children to cross alone and for cyclists who would not normally ride, and shops and cafes spread onto pavements that cars usually occupy. Public transport carries more passengers, which shows operators where extra capacity is needed.\n\nThe greater value is what residents learn. Most people cannot imagine their city without traffic until they experience it, and a single day changes that: streets that had been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treated as space for moving vehicles</strong> are revealed as places where people would gather. Cities that later introduced permanent pedestrian zones frequently began with occasional closures, because the public opposition faded once the alternative had been seen.\n\nThe disadvantages are real but manageable. Traders fear losing customers, though evidence from pedestrianised streets suggests footfall usually rises. People with disabilities, and those who live outside the reach of public transport, are genuinely inconvenienced, and deliveries, taxis and emergency vehicles need arrangements. Traffic can also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">push itself into the surrounding streets</strong> rather than disappear, which shifts the pollution to residential areas. These objections point to how such days should be organised rather than against them: exemptions for disabled drivers, deliveries scheduled outside the closure, extra services on the day and enough notice for everybody to plan.\n\nIn conclusion, vehicle-free days deliver cleaner air and safer streets immediately, and they let a city <strong class=\"key-phrase\">see what a permanent change would feel like</strong>. With exemptions for those who need them, the benefits clearly outweigh the disruption.","phrases":[{"en":"treated as space for moving vehicles","context":"Most people cannot imagine their city without traffic until they experience it, and a single day changes that: streets that had been treated as space for moving vehicles are revealed as places where people would gather."},{"en":"push itself into the surrounding streets","context":"Traffic can also push itself into the surrounding streets rather than disappear, which shifts the pollution to residential areas."},{"en":"see what a permanent change would feel like","context":"In conclusion, vehicle-free days deliver cleaner air and safer streets immediately, and they let a city see what a permanent change would feel like. With exemptions for those who need them, the benefits clearly outweigh the disruption."}],"subtype":"Advantages / Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#342","summary":"Whether banning private vehicles from city centres on certain days is worthwhile"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2318","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Charging drivers to enter the city","prompt":"In some countries, the government has tried to reduce traffic by imposing a congestion charge during rush hour. Do you think this development is positive or negative?","answer":"Several cities now charge drivers for entering the centre at busy times, and the policy is unpopular with motorists everywhere it appears. I regard it as a positive development, provided the money raised is spent on the alternatives.\n\nThe main reason is that it works. Cities that have introduced such charges report immediate falls in traffic and faster journeys, because a price makes people ask whether a particular trip is necessary. Some travel earlier, some share a car, and many switch to the train, and the roads clear enough for buses, ambulances and delivery vehicles to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">move at a usable speed again</strong>. Cleaner air and fewer accidents follow directly.\n\nThere is a fairness argument as well. Driving into a crowded centre imposes costs on everybody else through delay, noise and pollution, and the driver pays none of them. A charge simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">returns those costs to the person who creates them</strong>, which is the ordinary principle applied to every other kind of pollution.\n\nThe objections deserve an answer. A flat charge takes a larger share of a low income than a high one, and it can push shoppers towards out-of-town centres. Both problems are manageable rather than fatal: exemptions for disabled drivers and residents, discounts for low earners, and the visible reinvestment of revenue in buses and cycle routes turn the charge from a tax into an exchange. Where governments have collected the money and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">left the alternatives unimproved</strong>, resentment has been justified.\n\nIn conclusion, congestion charging reduces traffic quickly and asks drivers to pay for a cost they impose on others. It is a positive measure as long as the revenue is returned to public transport and the poorest travellers are protected.","phrases":[{"en":"move at a usable speed again","context":"Some travel earlier, some share a car, and many switch to the train, and the roads clear enough for buses, ambulances and delivery vehicles to move at a usable speed again."},{"en":"returns those costs to the person who creates them","context":"A charge simply returns those costs to the person who creates them, which is the ordinary principle applied to every other kind of pollution."},{"en":"left the alternatives unimproved","context":"Where governments have collected the money and left the alternatives unimproved, resentment has been justified."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#343","summary":"Whether congestion charging during rush hour is a positive development"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2357","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Should cyclists pass a test?","prompt":"Some experts propose that cyclists should be required to pass a test before they are allowed to use public roads. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"As cycling has grown in cities, so have complaints about riders who ignore signals and ride on pavements. I disagree with compulsory testing, because it would prevent far more cycling than it would prevent danger, though training itself is valuable.\n\nThe argument for a test is reasonable in principle. Roads are shared, and a cyclist who does not understand junctions or signalling endangers pedestrians and themselves. Drivers must prove competence before being allowed out, and consistency suggests the same should apply to anyone using the same space. A test would also ensure that riders know the rules they are accused of breaking.\n\nThe objection is one of proportion. A bicycle weighs a few kilograms and travels at fifteen miles an hour; a car weighs well over a tonne and can travel at seventy. Testing exists because of the harm a vehicle can do to others, and cyclists <strong class=\"key-phrase\">injure almost nobody except themselves</strong>. Applying the same procedure to both ignores that difference entirely.\n\nThe practical consequences would be worse than the problem. A test costs money and time, and would stop exactly the people cycling should be encouraging: children, low-income workers and older riders. Cities gain enormously from more cyclists through cleaner air, less congestion and better public health, and any measure that reduces the number <strong class=\"key-phrase\">costs more in illness than it saves in accidents</strong>. Enforcement would also be impossible without licensing every bicycle, which no country has managed cheaply. What does work is free voluntary training, taught in schools, along with cycle lanes separated from traffic, since most conflict arises where riders and vehicles are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">forced to compete for the same narrow strip of road</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, cyclists should certainly know the rules, and training deserves public funding. A compulsory test would deter cycling without making roads meaningfully safer, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">better infrastructure is the more sensible investment</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"injure almost nobody except themselves","context":"Testing exists because of the harm a vehicle can do to others, and cyclists injure almost nobody except themselves."},{"en":"costs more in illness than it saves in accidents","context":"Cities gain enormously from more cyclists through cleaner air, less congestion and better public health, and any measure that reduces the number costs more in illness than it saves in accidents."},{"en":"forced to compete for the same narrow strip of road","context":"What does work is free voluntary training, taught in schools, along with cycle lanes separated from traffic, since most conflict arises where riders and vehicles are forced to compete for the same narrow strip of road."},{"en":"better infrastructure is the more sensible investment","context":"In conclusion, cyclists should certainly know the rules, and training deserves public funding. A compulsory test would deter cycling without making roads meaningfully safer, so better infrastructure is the more sensible investment."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#344","summary":"Whether cyclists ought to be tested before using public roads"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2378","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Free public transport around the clock","prompt":"Some people believe that the best way to reduce traffic congestion is for governments to provide free public transport 24 hours a day, seven days a week. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Removing fares entirely is an attractive proposal, and several cities have tried it. I agree that public transport should be cheap and frequent, but I disagree that making it free is the best way to reduce congestion.\n\nThe argument in its favour is straightforward. Price deters use, so removing it should move people out of cars, and free travel helps low-income households immediately, since transport is one of their largest costs. Buses would move faster without fare collection, and running services through the night would serve shift workers who currently have no alternative to driving. Cleaner air and fewer accidents would follow any real shift away from cars.\n\nThe evidence, however, suggests fares are not what keeps drivers in their cars. Cities that abolished them found that most new passengers had previously walked or cycled rather than driven, so congestion changed very little. What actually deters drivers is a service that is slow, infrequent or unreliable, and a driver <strong class=\"key-phrase\">choosing comfort and speed will not switch because the alternative is free</strong>. Free travel also removes the revenue that pays for the service, so unless the government replaces it in full, the result is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">crowded vehicles on routes that are gradually cut</strong> — the opposite of what was intended.\n\nMeasures that work address the road rather than the ticket. Congestion charging, higher parking prices, bus lanes and dedicated cycle routes make driving less attractive while making the alternative faster, and the revenue funds the improvement. Cheap fares belong in this package, especially for the young, the old and the poor, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">frequency and reliability matter more than price</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, free public transport is a generous policy with real social benefits and deserves consideration for that reason. As a cure for congestion it is ineffective, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drivers respond to time rather than to fares</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"choosing comfort and speed will not switch because the alternative is free","context":"What actually deters drivers is a service that is slow, infrequent or unreliable, and a driver choosing comfort and speed will not switch because the alternative is free."},{"en":"crowded vehicles on routes that are gradually cut","context":"Free travel also removes the revenue that pays for the service, so unless the government replaces it in full, the result is crowded vehicles on routes that are gradually cut — the opposite of what was intended."},{"en":"frequency and reliability matter more than price","context":"Cheap fares belong in this package, especially for the young, the old and the poor, but frequency and reliability matter more than price."},{"en":"drivers respond to time rather than to fares","context":"In conclusion, free public transport is a generous policy with real social benefits and deserves consideration for that reason. As a cure for congestion it is ineffective, because drivers respond to time rather than to fares."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#345","summary":"Whether free public transport at all hours would solve congestion"},{"id":"writing-task-2-032","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"What makes a job satisfying?","prompt":"As most people spend a major part of adult life at the place of work, job satisfaction is an important part of individual well-being. What are the factors that contribute to job satisfaction? Are all the expectations for job satisfaction realistic?","answer":"Job satisfaction matters because adults spend a large part of their lives at work. It depends on several practical and psychological factors, but it is unrealistic to expect any job to provide constant happiness.\n\nAt the most basic level, employees need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fair pay and reasonable job security</strong>. A salary should cover normal living costs, and workers should not feel that their position could disappear without warning. Working conditions also matter: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">manageable hours, respectful treatment and competent leadership</strong> can have a direct effect on morale. Beyond these basic needs, many people want a sense of purpose. They are more satisfied when they understand how their work contributes to a useful result and when their effort is recognised.\n\nAnother important factor is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">scope for professional growth</strong>. Employees are likely to remain motivated if they can learn new skills, take on greater responsibility and see <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clear route to promotion</strong>. Relationships with colleagues are equally significant because a supportive team can make demanding work more enjoyable. In contrast, poor communication or persistent conflict can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make even a well-paid job difficult to tolerate</strong>.\n\nNevertheless, some expectations are unrealistic. No occupation can be interesting every day, and every workplace includes routine tasks, pressure and occasional disagreement. It is also unreasonable to expect an employer to provide personal fulfilment in every area of life. A healthy work-life balance usually depends on choices outside work as well as conditions inside it.\n\nIn conclusion, job satisfaction is built on fair treatment, meaningful work, development and supportive relationships. These are reasonable expectations, but employees should also accept that work will never be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">free from boredom or stress</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"fair pay and reasonable job security","context":"At the most basic level, employees need fair pay and reasonable job security."},{"en":"scope for professional growth","context":"Another important factor is scope for professional growth."},{"en":"manageable hours, respectful treatment and competent leadership","context":"Working conditions also matter: manageable hours, respectful treatment and competent leadership can have a direct effect on morale."},{"en":"a clear route to promotion","context":"Employees are likely to remain motivated if they can learn new skills, take on greater responsibility and see a clear route to promotion."},{"en":"make even a well-paid job difficult to tolerate","context":"In contrast, poor communication or persistent conflict can make even a well-paid job difficult to tolerate."},{"en":"free from boredom or stress","context":"In conclusion, job satisfaction is built on fair treatment, meaningful work, development and supportive relationships. These are reasonable expectations, but employees should also accept that work will never be free from boredom or stress."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#346","summary":"As most people spend a major part of adult life at the place of work, job…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-064","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Sending new doctors and teachers to rural areas","prompt":"Good facilities can be found in cities but not in the countryside. Some people therefore argue that newly qualified doctors and teachers should be required to work in rural areas. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Rural areas in most countries have far fewer hospitals and schools than cities, and it is sometimes proposed that newly qualified doctors and teachers should be required to work there. I agree that rural communities need these professionals urgently, but I disagree that compulsion is the right way to achieve it.\n\nThe need is undeniable. A village without a doctor means that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treatable conditions become serious</strong>, and a school without qualified teachers condemns a generation of children to limited opportunities. Newly qualified professionals also gain genuinely from rural work: a doctor in a small clinic sees a far wider range of conditions and must make decisions independently, which develops competence more quickly than a junior post in a large hospital.\n\nCompulsion, however, tends to fail. Professionals forced into rural posts usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">count the days until they can leave</strong>, and unwilling staff provide poor care and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depart the moment their term ends</strong>, so the community faces <strong class=\"key-phrase\">constant turnover instead of continuity</strong>. Skilled people may also emigrate rather than accept the requirement, which makes the shortage worse.\n\nThe better approach is to make rural work genuinely attractive. Higher salaries, subsidised housing, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cancellation of student loans</strong>, guaranteed promotion afterwards and decent equipment have all been shown to work. Recruiting students from rural areas is particularly effective, since they already have family and friends outside the cities and are far more likely to return home and stay.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that rural communities deserve properly qualified doctors and teachers, and that the current imbalance is unacceptable. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Incentives, however, will retain such people where obligation will not</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"constant turnover instead of continuity","context":"Professionals forced into rural posts usually count the days until they can leave, and unwilling staff provide poor care and depart the moment their term ends, so the community faces constant turnover instead of continuity."},{"en":"cancellation of student loans","context":"Higher salaries, subsidised housing, cancellation of student loans, guaranteed promotion afterwards and decent equipment have all been shown to work."},{"en":"treatable conditions become serious","context":"A village without a doctor means that treatable conditions become serious, and a school without qualified teachers condemns a generation of children to limited opportunities."},{"en":"count the days until they can leave","context":"Professionals forced into rural posts usually count the days until they can leave, and unwilling staff provide poor care and depart the moment their term ends, so the community faces constant turnover instead of continuity."},{"en":"depart the moment their term ends","context":"Professionals forced into rural posts usually count the days until they can leave, and unwilling staff provide poor care and depart the moment their term ends, so the community faces constant turnover instead of continuity."},{"en":"Incentives, however, will retain such people where obligation will not","context":"In conclusion, I agree that rural communities deserve properly qualified doctors and teachers, and that the current imbalance is unacceptable. Incentives, however, will retain such people where obligation will not."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#347","summary":"Sending new doctors and teachers to rural areas"},{"id":"writing-task-2-079","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"What really produces success?","prompt":"Some people who have been successful in life do not attribute their success to the theoretical knowledge they learned at university. In your opinion, what are the main factors that contribute to a person's achievement?","answer":"Many successful people say that they owe little to the theoretical knowledge they acquired at university. In my opinion academic study contributes more than they admit, but personal qualities and opportunity explain the greater part of achievement.\n\nThere is some truth in the sceptical view. University courses often teach models that are simplified or outdated, and much of the detail is forgotten within a few years. Several of the most successful entrepreneurs of the past generation left university without finishing, which suggests that formal qualifications are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not essential in every field</strong>.\n\nNevertheless, the value of higher education is easy to underestimate because it is indirect. A degree teaches people to structure an argument, evaluate evidence and work independently to a deadline, and these habits remain long after the content has faded. Certain professions are also impossible without it: nobody would accept a surgeon or a structural engineer who had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">learned informally</strong>.\n\nThe factors that matter most, however, are personal. Persistence appears repeatedly in accounts of successful careers, since most achievement follows a period of failure that others would not have tolerated. The ability to work with people is equally important, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">almost nothing significant is accomplished alone</strong>. Opportunity should not be ignored either: family wealth, contacts and country of birth determine what is realistically available, and much of what is called merit is partly luck.\n\nIn conclusion, university provides useful habits of thought rather than directly usable knowledge, which is why graduates often underestimate it. Persistence, cooperation and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">circumstance</strong> still account for a far larger share of what people achieve.","phrases":[{"en":"not essential in every field","context":"Several of the most successful entrepreneurs of the past generation left university without finishing, which suggests that formal qualifications are not essential in every field."},{"en":"learned informally","context":"Certain professions are also impossible without it: nobody would accept a surgeon or a structural engineer who had learned informally."},{"en":"almost nothing significant is accomplished alone","context":"The ability to work with people is equally important, because almost nothing significant is accomplished alone."},{"en":"circumstance","context":"In conclusion, university provides useful habits of thought rather than directly usable knowledge, which is why graduates often underestimate it. Persistence, cooperation and circumstance still account for a far larger share of what people achieve."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#348","summary":"What really produces success?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-162","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Is a compulsory retirement age out of date?","prompt":"As populations age, some countries are considering abolishing compulsory retirement ages. Do you think a fixed retirement age is out of date, or should it be maintained?","answer":"As people live longer and remain healthy for longer, several countries are debating whether a fixed retirement age still makes sense. In my opinion compulsory retirement is out of date and should be replaced by more flexible arrangements.\n\nThe case for keeping a fixed age is not without merit. It allows employers to plan, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">opens positions for younger staff</strong> who would otherwise wait years for promotion. In physically demanding or safety-critical occupations, such as construction, surgery or flying aircraft, there is also a reasonable argument for setting a limit rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">assessing each individual</strong>.\n\nNevertheless, the principle is difficult to defend. Forcing a competent employee to stop working on a particular birthday is a form of age discrimination, since it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">judges a person by their date of birth rather than by their performance</strong>. It is also wasteful, because experienced staff take decades of knowledge with them and many are dismissed while still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at the height of their abilities</strong>. From a national perspective it is unsustainable, as a shrinking workforce cannot support a rapidly growing number of pensioners.\n\nThe sensible alternative is to remove the fixed age and rely instead on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regular assessment of health and competence</strong>, combined with the option of reduced hours. Those who wish to leave at sixty should be free to do so, provided their pension allows it, and those who wish to continue should be able to.\n\nIn conclusion, a compulsory retirement age belongs to an era of shorter lives and larger young populations, and I believe it should now give way to individual choice.","phrases":[{"en":"assessing each individual","context":"In physically demanding or safety-critical occupations, such as construction, surgery or flying aircraft, there is also a reasonable argument for setting a limit rather than assessing each individual."},{"en":"opens positions for younger staff","context":"It allows employers to plan, and it opens positions for younger staff who would otherwise wait years for promotion."},{"en":"judges a person by their date of birth rather than by their performance","context":"Forcing a competent employee to stop working on a particular birthday is a form of age discrimination, since it judges a person by their date of birth rather than by their performance."},{"en":"at the height of their abilities","context":"It is also wasteful, because experienced staff take decades of knowledge with them and many are dismissed while still at the height of their abilities."},{"en":"regular assessment of health and competence","context":"The sensible alternative is to remove the fixed age and rely instead on regular assessment of health and competence, combined with the option of reduced hours."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#349","summary":"Is a compulsory retirement age out of date?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-172","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Working from home: who benefits?","prompt":"Many employees may work at home with modern technology. Some people claim that it benefits only workers, but not employers. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"The spread of technologies such as the internet, video calls and instant messaging has made it far easier for employees to work from home. Some people claim that this arrangement benefits only the worker, while employers gain nothing in return. In my opinion, employers can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take full advantage of</strong> home working as well.\n\nHome working lets employers recruit far beyond people who can commute to a single office. Parents with young children, carers and people with disabilities are often highly capable but cannot accept a role that demands a long daily journey; once that barrier is removed, a company can hire the best person for the job rather than simply the closest one. This wider pool gives businesses access to skills and experience they would otherwise miss entirely.\n\nHome working can also raise motivation. Employees who control their own schedule can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strike a balance between</strong> work and family life, and many report feeling more trusted and less anxious without a manager watching over their shoulder throughout the day. Motivated staff of this kind tend to produce better work and stay with a company for longer, which reduces the cost of recruitment.\n\nHome working is not without difficulties for employers, however. Managers cannot see directly whether staff are meeting deadlines, so performance has to be judged by results rather than by hours spent at a desk, which some managers find hard to trust. Team spirit can also suffer when colleagues rarely meet in person, and on-the-job training is far harder to deliver remotely than in a shared office, where a new employee can simply watch a colleague and ask questions.\n\nIn conclusion, employers as well as employees stand to gain from home working, through access to a wider range of talent and a more motivated workforce. To capture these benefits fully, however, companies need clear ways of measuring performance and a deliberate effort to keep teams connected.","phrases":[{"en":"take full advantage of","context":"In my opinion, employers can take full advantage of home working as well."},{"en":"strike a balance between","context":"Employees who control their own schedule can strike a balance between work and family life, and many report feeling more trusted and less anxious without a manager watching over their shoulder throughout the day."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#350","summary":"Employees may work at home with modern technology."},{"id":"writing-task-2-183","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"One career for life, or several?","prompt":"Many people decide on a career path early in their lives and keep to it. This, they argue, leads to a more satisfying working life. To what extent do you agree with this view? What other things can people do in order to have a satisfying working life?","answer":"It is true that some people know from an early age what career they want to pursue, and they are happy to spend the rest of their lives in the same profession. While I accept that this may suit many people, I believe that others enjoy changing careers or seeking job satisfaction in different ways.\n\nOn the one hand, having <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a defined career path</strong> can certainly lead to a satisfying working life. Many people decide as young children what they want to do as adults, and it gives them a great sense of satisfaction to work towards their goals and gradually achieve them. For example, many children dream of becoming doctors, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to realise this ambition</strong> they need to gain the relevant qualifications and undertake years of training. In my experience, very few people who have qualified as doctors choose to change career because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">they find their work so rewarding</strong>, and because they have invested so much time and effort to reach their goal.\n\nOn the other hand, people find happiness in their working lives in different ways, and no single path suits everyone. Someone who has never had one particular ambition can still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thrive by trying a variety of professions</strong>, since starting out on a completely new career path can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a reinvigorating experience</strong> rather than a setback. For others, work is simply a means of earning money, and as long as their salary allows them to enjoy life outside work, changing jobs whenever a better offer appears causes them no distress. What ultimately determines satisfaction, for many people, is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less the job title than the conditions surrounding it</strong>: a positive working atmosphere, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">enthusiastic colleagues</strong>, and an inspirational boss can make working life far more rewarding than the career itself, whatever profession a person happens to be in.\n\nIn conclusion, it can certainly be satisfying to pursue a particular career for the whole of one's life, but this is by no means the only route to fulfilment.","phrases":[{"en":"a defined career path","context":"On the one hand, having a defined career path can certainly lead to a satisfying working life."},{"en":"a reinvigorating experience","context":"Someone who has never had one particular ambition can still thrive by trying a variety of professions, since starting out on a completely new career path can be a reinvigorating experience rather than a setback."},{"en":"enthusiastic colleagues","context":"What ultimately determines satisfaction, for many people, is less the job title than the conditions surrounding it: a positive working atmosphere, enthusiastic colleagues, and an inspirational boss can make working life far more rewarding than the career itself, whatever profession a person happe..."},{"en":"to realise this ambition","context":"For example, many children dream of becoming doctors, but to realise this ambition they need to gain the relevant qualifications and undertake years of training."},{"en":"they find their work so rewarding","context":"In my experience, very few people who have qualified as doctors choose to change career because they find their work so rewarding, and because they have invested so much time and effort to reach their goal."},{"en":"thrive by trying a variety of professions","context":"Someone who has never had one particular ambition can still thrive by trying a variety of professions, since starting out on a completely new career path can be a reinvigorating experience rather than a setback."},{"en":"less the job title than the conditions surrounding it","context":"What ultimately determines satisfaction, for many people, is less the job title than the conditions surrounding it: a positive working atmosphere, enthusiastic colleagues, and an inspirational boss can make working life far more rewarding than the career itself, whatever profession a person happe..."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#351","summary":"People decide on a career path early in their lives and keep to it."},{"id":"writing-task-2-184","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Is salary the most important thing about a job?","prompt":"When choosing a job, the salary is the most important consideration. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Many people choose their jobs based on the size of the salary offered. Personally, I disagree with the idea that money is the key consideration when deciding on a career, because I believe that other factors are equally important.\n\nOn the one hand, I agree that money is necessary in order for people to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meet their basic needs</strong>. For example, we all need money to pay for housing, food, bills, health care, and education. Most people consider it a priority to at least earn a salary that allows them to cover these needs and have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a reasonable quality of life</strong>. If people chose their jobs based on enjoyment or other non-financial factors, they might find it difficult to support themselves. Artists and musicians, for instance, are known for choosing a career path that they love, but that does not always provide them with enough money to live comfortably and raise a family.\n\nNevertheless, I believe that other considerations are just as important as what we earn in our jobs. Firstly, personal relationships and the atmosphere in a workplace are extremely important when choosing a job. Having a good manager or friendly colleagues, for example, can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make a huge difference to</strong> workers’ levels of happiness and general quality of life. Secondly, many people's feelings of job satisfaction come from their professional achievements, the skills they learn, and the position they reach, rather than the money they earn. Finally, some people choose a career because they want to help others and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">contribute something positive to</strong> society.\n\nIn conclusion, while salaries certainly affect people's choice of profession, I do not believe that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">money outweighs all other motivators</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"contribute something positive to","context":"Finally, some people choose a career because they want to help others and contribute something positive to society."},{"en":"meet their basic needs","context":"On the one hand, I agree that money is necessary in order for people to meet their basic needs."},{"en":"a reasonable quality of life","context":"Most people consider it a priority to at least earn a salary that allows them to cover these needs and have a reasonable quality of life."},{"en":"make a huge difference to","context":"Having a good manager or friendly colleagues, for example, can make a huge difference to workers’ levels of happiness and general quality of life."},{"en":"money outweighs all other motivators","context":"In conclusion, while salaries certainly affect people's choice of profession, I do not believe that money outweighs all other motivators."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#352","summary":"Salary versus other job factors"},{"id":"writing-task-2-185","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Balancing work and personal life","prompt":"Many people today find it difficult to balance the demands of their work and personal life. What are the causes of this situation, and what can individuals and employers do to reduce the problem?","answer":"Finding a compromise between one's job and one's private life is probably harder than ever these days, especially if one has a family. There are several reasons for this, but also a number of measures we can take. Possibly the major factor is the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">increased workload</strong> that many people have to deal with. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The predominance of the service sector</strong> means that most people work in office-related jobs, and they find that employers expect them to stay longer or be available outside traditional office hours. On top of this, the rise of communication tools – ranging from smart phones to laptops and teleconferencing equipment – <strong class=\"key-phrase\">has accelerated the trend</strong>, because people are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">always contactable</strong>.\n\nThe economic recession has also made the labour market increasingly competitive, and so workers are under pressure to cooperate in this process, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fearing loss of promotion or even redundancy</strong> if they resist. However, there are various steps that can be taken to lessen the problem.\n\nIndividuals can protect some of this time themselves, for instance by agreeing clear hours with their employer during which they are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely free of work and uncontactable</strong>. Employers, for their part, could allow more flexible working hours, so that staff can step away during the day for important family events rather than requesting a full day off. Coaching in time management and stress control would also help, since many employees have never been taught how to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">structure a heavy workload rather than simply enduring it</strong>.\n\nIn summary, we can say that technology and economics are creating this situation, but also that employees and companies together can reduce the impact.","phrases":[{"en":"increased workload","context":"Possibly the major factor is the increased workload that many people have to deal with."},{"en":"always contactable","context":"On top of this, the rise of communication tools – ranging from smart phones to laptops and teleconferencing equipment – has accelerated the trend, because people are always contactable."},{"en":"The predominance of the service sector","context":"The predominance of the service sector means that most people work in office-related jobs, and they find that employers expect them to stay longer or be available outside traditional office hours."},{"en":"has accelerated the trend","context":"On top of this, the rise of communication tools – ranging from smart phones to laptops and teleconferencing equipment – has accelerated the trend, because people are always contactable."},{"en":"fearing loss of promotion or even redundancy","context":"The economic recession has also made the labour market increasingly competitive, and so workers are under pressure to cooperate in this process, fearing loss of promotion or even redundancy if they resist."},{"en":"genuinely free of work and uncontactable","context":"Individuals can protect some of this time themselves, for instance by agreeing clear hours with their employer during which they are genuinely free of work and uncontactable."},{"en":"structure a heavy workload rather than simply enduring it","context":"Coaching in time management and stress control would also help, since many employees have never been taught how to structure a heavy workload rather than simply enduring it."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","_displayNo":"#353","summary":"People today find it difficult to balance the demands of their work and…"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Uniforms at work","prompt":"Some companies have uniforms for their staff which must be worn at all times. Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of this.","answer":"Staff uniforms can strengthen a company's identity and make employees easy to recognise, but they may also reduce comfort and personal choice. Their value depends on the type of work and the quality of the uniform.\n\nThe main advantage is clarity. In hospitals, hotels or transport services, customers can quickly identify the person who can help them. A uniform can also create a professional appearance and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce pressure on employees to buy different work clothes</strong>. In hazardous jobs, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">standard clothing may provide essential protection</strong> and support consistent safety standards, since visible, well-fitted equipment is easier to inspect and enforce.\n\nThe disadvantages arise when a uniform is uncomfortable, unsuitable for the weather or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">designed without consulting staff</strong>. Employees may feel that the company ignores individual needs, especially in relation to religion, disability or body shape. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The cost of cleaning and replacement</strong> can also be unfair if workers must pay it themselves.\n\nResponsibility for the uniform therefore matters. Employers should provide enough sets, pay for specialist cleaning when required, offer versions suitable for different climates, religions and physical needs, and review the design when duties or staff needs change rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaving it fixed for reasons of tradition alone</strong>. A policy is more acceptable when staff are consulted and the rules have a clear purpose. This can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">protect professional standards without creating unnecessary resentment</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, uniforms are useful where identification, hygiene or safety matters. They are less necessary in roles with little public contact. A sensible policy should balance company image with comfort, dignity and practical needs.","topic":"Work & Employment","phrases":[{"en":"standard clothing may provide essential protection","context":"In hazardous jobs, standard clothing may provide essential protection and support consistent safety standards, since visible, well-fitted equipment is easier to inspect and enforce."},{"en":"protect professional standards without creating unnecessary resentment","context":"This can protect professional standards without creating unnecessary resentment."},{"en":"reduce pressure on employees to buy different work clothes","context":"A uniform can also create a professional appearance and reduce pressure on employees to buy different work clothes."},{"en":"designed without consulting staff","context":"The disadvantages arise when a uniform is uncomfortable, unsuitable for the weather or designed without consulting staff."},{"en":"The cost of cleaning and replacement","context":"The cost of cleaning and replacement can also be unfair if workers must pay it themselves."},{"en":"leaving it fixed for reasons of tradition alone","context":"Employers should provide enough sets, pay for specialist cleaning when required, offer versions suitable for different climates, religions and physical needs, and review the design when duties or staff needs change rather than leaving it fixed for reasons of tradition alone."}],"subtype":"advdis","summary":"The advantages and disadvantages of staff uniforms","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-186","_displayNo":"#354"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Should companies reserve senior posts for women?","prompt":"Most high-level positions in companies are filled by men even though the workforce in many developed countries is more than 50 per cent female. Companies should be required to allocate a certain percentage of these positions to women. To what extent do you agree?","answer":"Men still occupy most senior positions in many companies, even where women form a large part of the workforce. Some people propose fixed quotas for women in leadership. I support temporary targets and transparent selection, but not automatic promotion based only on gender.\n\nThe case for intervention is strong. Senior appointments often depend on informal networks, previous opportunities and assumptions about who looks like a leader. Women may also receive fewer high-profile assignments or face career penalties for caring responsibilities. If companies simply wait for the imbalance to correct itself without setting any measurable goal, progress can remain extremely slow, sometimes across an entire career. Clear targets can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">force organisations to examine hidden barriers</strong> that a purely voluntary approach allows them to overlook indefinitely.\n\nRigid quotas, however, carry a cost of their own. When a fixed percentage is imposed, colleagues may assume that a woman was appointed to meet the figure rather than on her record, which weakens the authority she needs to do the job well. It is more effective to change the process that produces the shortlist: insisting that every list for a senior post contains qualified women, publishing promotion and pay data, and making sure that talented staff are given the high-profile projects from which future directors are chosen. Norway's forty per cent board quota, for example, quickly raised the number of women on boards but left the executive ranks below them almost unchanged, which shows that a headline figure is a poor guide to whether a company <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reflects genuine equality of opportunity</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, companies should take active steps to correct unequal opportunity, but leadership decisions must remain evidence-based. Time-limited targets combined with transparent recruitment can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improve representation without lowering standards</strong>.","topic":"Work & Employment","phrases":[{"en":"force organisations to examine hidden barriers","context":"Clear targets can force organisations to examine hidden barriers that a purely voluntary approach allows them to overlook indefinitely."},{"en":"reflects genuine equality of opportunity","context":"Norway's forty per cent board quota, for example, quickly raised the number of women on boards but left the executive ranks below them almost unchanged, which shows that a headline figure is a poor guide to whether a company reflects genuine equality of opportunity."},{"en":"improve representation without lowering standards","context":"In conclusion, companies should take active steps to correct unequal opportunity, but leadership decisions must remain evidence-based. Time-limited targets combined with transparent recruitment can improve representation without lowering standards."}],"subtype":"opinion","summary":"Requiring companies to reserve senior positions for women","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-187","_displayNo":"#355"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Competing with younger people for the same jobs","prompt":"Nowadays more and more people have to compete with young people for the same jobs. What problems does this cause? What are some possible solutions?","answer":"Older workers increasingly compete with younger applicants for the same jobs. This can create longer unemployment, resentment and a loss of confidence for both groups. The best response is not to favour one age group, but to improve training and make recruitment more transparent.\n\nYoung applicants may struggle to gain their first experience because employers prefer candidates who can work immediately. Older applicants, meanwhile, may face assumptions that their skills are outdated or that they will be expensive to employ. When both groups are rejected repeatedly, the result can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long-term exclusion from the labour market</strong>.\n\nGovernments should fund practical retraining linked to real vacancies, while companies should describe the skills required rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">asking for a narrow age profile</strong>. Paid internships can help younger people enter a field, and short conversion courses can help experienced workers update digital or technical skills. Mixed-age teams should also be encouraged because they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">combine recent training with practical judgement</strong>.\n\nThere is also a direct financial case for retaining experienced staff rather than replacing them wholesale. Recruiting and training a new employee typically costs several months of salary before that person becomes fully productive, so losing a worker's accumulated knowledge of clients, systems and established professional networks is expensive to replace, not merely inconvenient. A structured handover, in which an employee nearing retirement mentors a newer colleague for several months rather than leaving on a fixed date, protects this knowledge while still opening a genuine route into the profession for someone younger. Public employment services could support such arrangements directly, by funding the mentoring period itself rather than offering generic courses with little labour-market value.\n\nIn conclusion, age competition becomes harmful when employers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rely on stereotypes instead of evidence</strong>. Skills-based recruitment, accessible training and flexible career routes can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give both generations a fair chance</strong> and reduce unnecessary conflict.","topic":"Work & Employment","phrases":[{"en":"give both generations a fair chance","context":"In conclusion, age competition becomes harmful when employers rely on stereotypes instead of evidence. Skills-based recruitment, accessible training and flexible career routes can give both generations a fair chance and reduce unnecessary conflict."},{"en":"long-term exclusion from the labour market","context":"When both groups are rejected repeatedly, the result can be long-term exclusion from the labour market."},{"en":"asking for a narrow age profile","context":"Governments should fund practical retraining linked to real vacancies, while companies should describe the skills required rather than asking for a narrow age profile."},{"en":"combine recent training with practical judgement","context":"Mixed-age teams should also be encouraged because they combine recent training with practical judgement."},{"en":"rely on stereotypes instead of evidence","context":"In conclusion, age competition becomes harmful when employers rely on stereotypes instead of evidence. Skills-based recruitment, accessible training and flexible career routes can give both generations a fair chance and reduce unnecessary conflict."}],"subtype":"problem","summary":"Competition between older and younger people for the same jobs","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-188","_displayNo":"#356"},{"id":"writing-task-2-191","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Unpaid community work for young adults","prompt":"People tend to believe that all young adults should undertake a period of unpaid work for the good of the community. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Unpaid community work can give young adults useful experience and strengthen local services. However, I disagree with making it compulsory for everyone. Schools and governments should encourage participation and remove practical barriers, but the final choice should remain voluntary.\n\nVolunteering can be highly beneficial. Young people may learn to communicate with strangers, organise tasks and work as part of a team. A student who helps at a food bank or supports an environmental project also gains a clearer understanding of problems outside the classroom. These experiences can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">build practical skills and social awareness</strong> at the same time.\n\nThe difficulty with compulsory service is that unpaid work may affect young people unequally. Some students have caring duties, paid jobs or health problems, so a fixed requirement could place a heavier burden on them. Forced participation may also reduce motivation and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lead organisations to receive unwilling workers</strong>. A better system would offer several forms of service, flexible hours and help with travel costs. This would make volunteering <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accessible rather than punitive</strong>.\n\nCommunity organisations also need quality rather than reluctant attendance. Volunteers should receive a clear role, basic training and feedback so that their time produces a real benefit. Schools can recognise service through certificates or course credit without making it a legal duty. This approach <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rewards meaningful participation rather than mere compliance</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, community work should be strongly promoted but not imposed as a universal obligation. Young adults are more likely to contribute seriously when they understand the purpose of the work and can choose a suitable role. Voluntary programmes with good support would create <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lasting commitment to the community</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"build practical skills and social awareness","context":"These experiences can build practical skills and social awareness at the same time."},{"en":"accessible rather than punitive","context":"This would make volunteering accessible rather than punitive."},{"en":"rewards meaningful participation rather than mere compliance","context":"This approach rewards meaningful participation rather than mere compliance."},{"en":"lasting commitment to the community","context":"Voluntary programmes with good support would create lasting commitment to the community."},{"en":"lead organisations to receive unwilling workers","context":"Forced participation may also reduce motivation and lead organisations to receive unwilling workers."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#357","summary":"People tend to believe that all young adults should undertake a period of…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-192","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Should working hours be restricted?","prompt":"People tend to work longer hours nowadays. Working long hours has a negative effect on workers, their families and society, so working hours should be restricted. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"In many countries employees now work longer hours than they did a generation ago. I agree that this trend damages workers, families and society, and that legal limits on working time are justified.\n\nThe effects on the individual are well established. Employees who regularly work beyond about fifty hours a week show higher rates of heart disease, depression and stroke, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exhaustion makes accidents far more likely</strong> in medicine, transport and construction. There is also strong evidence that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">productivity per hour falls sharply</strong> once people are tired, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the extra hours frequently produce very little additional work</strong>.\n\nFamilies suffer in a less measurable but equally serious way. A parent who leaves before their children wake and returns after they are asleep cannot provide guidance or attention, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relationships between partners deteriorate</strong> when neither has any time left. Society loses too, because people who work constantly do not volunteer, coach a team or care for elderly relatives, and it is precisely these activities that hold communities together.\n\nThe usual objection is that limits reduce freedom and damage competitiveness. In practice, countries with strictly regulated hours, such as Germany and the Netherlands, are among the most productive in the world, which suggests <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the fear is misplaced</strong>. Individuals cannot solve this alone either, since an employee who refuses overtime while colleagues accept it risks their position.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that working hours should be restricted. The health, family and social costs are substantial, and the evidence indicates that shorter hours <strong class=\"key-phrase\">need not reduce output</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"productivity per hour falls sharply","context":"There is also strong evidence that productivity per hour falls sharply once people are tired, so the extra hours frequently produce very little additional work."},{"en":"need not reduce output","context":"In conclusion, I agree that working hours should be restricted. The health, family and social costs are substantial, and the evidence indicates that shorter hours need not reduce output."},{"en":"exhaustion makes accidents far more likely","context":"Employees who regularly work beyond about fifty hours a week show higher rates of heart disease, depression and stroke, and exhaustion makes accidents far more likely in medicine, transport and construction."},{"en":"the extra hours frequently produce very little additional work","context":"There is also strong evidence that productivity per hour falls sharply once people are tired, so the extra hours frequently produce very little additional work."},{"en":"relationships between partners deteriorate","context":"A parent who leaves before their children wake and returns after they are asleep cannot provide guidance or attention, and relationships between partners deteriorate when neither has any time left."},{"en":"the fear is misplaced","context":"In practice, countries with strictly regulated hours, such as Germany and the Netherlands, are among the most productive in the world, which suggests the fear is misplaced."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#358","summary":"Should working hours be restricted?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-193","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Is changing jobs periodically a good idea?","prompt":"Some people think that changing jobs periodically is good for a person's career. What is your opinion?","answer":"Some people believe that moving to a new employer every few years is a sensible career strategy. I largely agree, provided that the moves are purposeful rather than merely habitual.\n\nThere are clear reasons why periodic change benefits an individual. Pay rises are now usually larger when someone joins a new organisation than when they stay, because internal increases are constrained by salary structures that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">external offers are not</strong>. Changing employers also broadens experience: a person who has worked in three companies has seen three ways of solving the same problems, whereas someone who has stayed in one may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mistake local habit for the only method</strong>. Moving is also often the only way to escape a role where promotion is blocked indefinitely by somebody who has no intention of leaving.\n\nThere are limits, however. Every move costs several months of reduced productivity while a person learns new systems and colleagues, and some professions reward the accumulated depth that only comes from staying. A history of very short appointments also makes recruiters cautious, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">training someone who leaves within a year</strong> is expensive.\n\nFor employers the trend is uncomfortable but not entirely negative. Constant turnover damages the knowledge held within a team and makes long projects harder to staff, yet new arrivals bring methods from elsewhere, and organisations that never recruit from outside tend to stagnate quietly.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that changing jobs periodically is generally good for a career, as long as each move <strong class=\"key-phrase\">adds something rather than repeating the last one</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"external offers are not","context":"Pay rises are now usually larger when someone joins a new organisation than when they stay, because internal increases are constrained by salary structures that external offers are not."},{"en":"mistake local habit for the only method","context":"Changing employers also broadens experience: a person who has worked in three companies has seen three ways of solving the same problems, whereas someone who has stayed in one may mistake local habit for the only method."},{"en":"training someone who leaves within a year","context":"A history of very short appointments also makes recruiters cautious, since training someone who leaves within a year is expensive."},{"en":"adds something rather than repeating the last one","context":"In conclusion, I agree that changing jobs periodically is generally good for a career, as long as each move adds something rather than repeating the last one."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#359","summary":"Is changing jobs periodically a good idea?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-194","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Fathers at home while mothers work","prompt":"These days more fathers stay at home and take care of their children while mothers go out to work. What could be the reasons for this? Do you think it is a positive or a negative development?","answer":"It is true that men are increasingly likely to take on the role of househusband, while more women than ever are the breadwinners in their families. There could be several reasons for this, and I consider it to be a very positive trend.\n\nIn recent years, parents have had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">adapt to various changes</strong> in our societies. Equal rights movements have made great progress, and it is no longer remarkable for a woman to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">out-earn her partner</strong> after years spent gaining the same qualifications and workplace experience that were once reserved for men. It has also become socially acceptable for a man to stay at home and raise his children, rather than being seen as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">failing to provide for his family</strong>. At the same time, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the rising cost of living</strong>, especially childcare fees in large cities, means that once a nursery place costs almost as much as one partner earns, it often makes more financial sense for that parent, not automatically the mother, to stay at home instead. Couples therefore increasingly decide who works and who cares for the children <strong class=\"key-phrase\">based on income and preference rather than on tradition</strong>.\n\nIn my view, the changes described above should be seen as progress. We should be happy to live in a society in which men and women have equal opportunities, and in which women are not put under pressure to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sacrifice their careers</strong>. Equally, it seems only fair that men should be free to leave their jobs to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">assume childcare responsibilities</strong> if this is what they wish to do. Couples should be left to make their own decisions about which parental role each partner takes, according to their particular circumstances and needs.\n\nIn conclusion, the changing roles of men and women in the family are a result of wider changes in society, and I believe that these developments are desirable.","phrases":[{"en":"adapt to various changes","context":"In recent years, parents have had to adapt to various changes in our societies."},{"en":"the rising cost of living","context":"At the same time, the rising cost of living, especially childcare fees in large cities, means that once a nursery place costs almost as much as one partner earns, it often makes more financial sense for that parent, not automatically the mother, to stay at home instead."},{"en":"assume childcare responsibilities","context":"Equally, it seems only fair that men should be free to leave their jobs to assume childcare responsibilities if this is what they wish to do."},{"en":"out-earn her partner","context":"Equal rights movements have made great progress, and it is no longer remarkable for a woman to out-earn her partner after years spent gaining the same qualifications and workplace experience that were once reserved for men."},{"en":"failing to provide for his family","context":"It has also become socially acceptable for a man to stay at home and raise his children, rather than being seen as failing to provide for his family."},{"en":"based on income and preference rather than on tradition","context":"Couples therefore increasingly decide who works and who cares for the children based on income and preference rather than on tradition."},{"en":"sacrifice their careers","context":"We should be happy to live in a society in which men and women have equal opportunities, and in which women are not put under pressure to sacrifice their careers."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#360","summary":"These days more fathers stay at home and take care of their children while…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2202","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Working in old age","prompt":"Some people think that it is a good idea for people to continue working in their old age. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"In many countries people are living longer and healthier lives, and it is increasingly common for them to keep working beyond the traditional retirement age. I largely agree that this is a positive choice, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">provided that it remains a choice rather than a necessity</strong>.\n\nContinued employment brings clear benefits to the individual. Work provides structure, purpose and daily contact with other people, and the loss of these things often explains why some retired people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">become bored, isolated or depressed</strong>. There is also evidence that staying mentally and socially active helps to delay cognitive decline. The financial argument is equally important, because pensions in most countries are modest, and a few extra years of earnings can make <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the difference between comfort and hardship</strong>.\n\nSociety gains as well. Older employees possess <strong class=\"key-phrase\">experience and judgement that cannot be taught quickly</strong>, and they are usually the people who train younger colleagues. As birth rates fall, many economies simply cannot afford to lose millions of skilled workers at sixty-five, since a shrinking workforce has to support a growing number of pensioners.\n\nThere are, however, important conditions. Some jobs are physically demanding, and it would be unreasonable to expect a builder or a nurse to continue into their seventies. Older employees may also need reduced hours or a change of role. Most importantly, people who have worked all their lives should not be forced to continue simply because their pension is too small to live on.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that working in old age is a good idea for those who are able and willing, but it must be supported by flexible conditions and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an adequate pension system</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"provided that it remains a choice rather than a necessity","context":"I largely agree that this is a positive choice, provided that it remains a choice rather than a necessity."},{"en":"become bored, isolated or depressed","context":"Work provides structure, purpose and daily contact with other people, and the loss of these things often explains why some retired people become bored, isolated or depressed."},{"en":"experience and judgement that cannot be taught quickly","context":"Older employees possess experience and judgement that cannot be taught quickly, and they are usually the people who train younger colleagues."},{"en":"the difference between comfort and hardship","context":"The financial argument is equally important, because pensions in most countries are modest, and a few extra years of earnings can make the difference between comfort and hardship."},{"en":"an adequate pension system","context":"In conclusion, I agree that working in old age is a good idea for those who are able and willing, but it must be supported by flexible conditions and an adequate pension system."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#361","summary":"Working in old age"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2210","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Young people changing careers","prompt":"A growing number of young people are changing their jobs and career paths. What are the reasons behind this phenomenon? Do the benefits of this trend outweigh the drawbacks?","answer":"It is increasingly common for young workers to move between employers, and even between professions, rather than remaining in a single career. This trend has two main causes, and in my view its advantages outweigh its disadvantages.\n\nThe first reason is economic. Permanent contracts have become rarer, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">whole industries can disappear within a decade</strong>, and pay rises are now often achieved by moving to a competitor rather than by waiting for promotion, so young people have little incentive to stay. The second reason is a change in expectations. Unlike their parents, most graduates today are looking for work that is interesting and personally meaningful, and they are willing to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">retrain</strong> when a job fails to provide this. Online courses and professional qualifications have made such transitions far easier than they were a generation ago.\n\nThe obvious drawback is instability. Workers who change roles frequently may lose pension contributions and other benefits, and some employers still treat a varied employment history as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">evidence of unreliability</strong>. Companies suffer too, since recruiting and training staff is expensive and constant <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turnover</strong> damages the knowledge held within a team.\n\nNevertheless, I believe the benefits are greater. Employees who have worked in several organisations acquire a wider range of skills and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">adapt more easily when technology reshapes their industry</strong>, which is exactly the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">flexibility a modern economy requires</strong>. They are also far more likely to find work that genuinely suits them, and people who enjoy their jobs are more productive and healthier than those who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay in unsuitable positions out of fear</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, job mobility is driven by economic insecurity and by rising expectations, and its advantages clearly outweigh its drawbacks.","phrases":[{"en":"retrain","context":"Unlike their parents, most graduates today are looking for work that is interesting and personally meaningful, and they are willing to retrain when a job fails to provide this."},{"en":"turnover","context":"Companies suffer too, since recruiting and training staff is expensive and constant turnover damages the knowledge held within a team."},{"en":"flexibility a modern economy requires","context":"Employees who have worked in several organisations acquire a wider range of skills and adapt more easily when technology reshapes their industry, which is exactly the flexibility a modern economy requires."},{"en":"whole industries can disappear within a decade","context":"Permanent contracts have become rarer, whole industries can disappear within a decade, and pay rises are now often achieved by moving to a competitor rather than by waiting for promotion, so young people have little incentive to stay."},{"en":"evidence of unreliability","context":"Workers who change roles frequently may lose pension contributions and other benefits, and some employers still treat a varied employment history as evidence of unreliability."},{"en":"adapt more easily when technology reshapes their industry","context":"Employees who have worked in several organisations acquire a wider range of skills and adapt more easily when technology reshapes their industry, which is exactly the flexibility a modern economy requires."},{"en":"stay in unsuitable positions out of fear","context":"They are also far more likely to find work that genuinely suits them, and people who enjoy their jobs are more productive and healthier than those who stay in unsuitable positions out of fear."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Benefits & Drawbacks","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#362","summary":"Young people changing careers"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2214","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"One career or several?","prompt":"Some people think that people who choose a job early and keep doing it are more likely to get a satisfying career life than those who frequently change jobs. Do you agree or disagree with this belief?","answer":"Some people believe that those who commit to a single occupation early in life end up more satisfied than those who move between jobs. I disagree with this view, because satisfaction depends on how well the work suits the individual rather than on how long it has been done.\n\nThere is some force in the argument for stability. A person who remains in one field <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accumulates deep expertise</strong>, builds a professional reputation and usually reaches a senior position with a good salary and a secure pension. Surgeons, lawyers and academics all require many years of continuous experience, and the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sense of mastery</strong> this produces is a genuine source of satisfaction. Constant movement, by contrast, can leave someone <strong class=\"key-phrase\">permanently a beginner</strong>.\n\nNevertheless, this argument <strong class=\"key-phrase\">assumes that the original choice was a good one</strong>. Most people select a career at seventeen or eighteen, with almost no experience of the working world, and it would be surprising if they always chose correctly. Someone who discovers that a profession does not suit them, but who stays out of habit or fear, is likely to spend decades in quiet frustration. Changing direction is precisely what allows such a person to find work that matches their abilities and interests.\n\nThere is also a practical point. Industries now change so quickly that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lifelong loyalty</strong> may not be rewarded. Workers with varied experience adapt more easily when their sector declines, whereas those with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a single narrow specialism</strong> can find themselves <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unemployable in middle age</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that staying in one job produces a more satisfying career. What matters is finding work that genuinely suits the person, whether that takes one decision or several.","phrases":[{"en":"sense of mastery","context":"Surgeons, lawyers and academics all require many years of continuous experience, and the sense of mastery this produces is a genuine source of satisfaction."},{"en":"a single narrow specialism","context":"Workers with varied experience adapt more easily when their sector declines, whereas those with a single narrow specialism can find themselves unemployable in middle age."},{"en":"lifelong loyalty","context":"Industries now change so quickly that lifelong loyalty may not be rewarded."},{"en":"accumulates deep expertise","context":"A person who remains in one field accumulates deep expertise, builds a professional reputation and usually reaches a senior position with a good salary and a secure pension."},{"en":"permanently a beginner","context":"Constant movement, by contrast, can leave someone permanently a beginner."},{"en":"assumes that the original choice was a good one","context":"Nevertheless, this argument assumes that the original choice was a good one."},{"en":"unemployable in middle age","context":"Workers with varied experience adapt more easily when their sector declines, whereas those with a single narrow specialism can find themselves unemployable in middle age."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#363","summary":"One career or several?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2216","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Should social life come from work?","prompt":"Some people think a job not only provides income but also social life. Others think it is better to develop social life with people you do not work with. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"Most adults spend more waking hours with colleagues than with anyone else, which raises the question of whether friendships should grow out of work or be kept separate from it. Both arrangements have merit, although I believe a social life built entirely around the office carries real risks.\n\nThe case for workplace friendship is practical. Colleagues already share a routine, a location and a set of problems, so conversation starts easily in a way it rarely does among strangers. People who enjoy the company around them also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cooperate more readily, ask for help sooner and stay in a job longer</strong>, which is why many employers deliberately fund social events. For someone who has moved to a new city for work, the office may be the only place where <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meeting people happens naturally</strong>.\n\nThe opposing view is that mixing the two makes both harder. A disagreement over a project becomes personal, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a promotion turns a friend into a manager</strong>, and confiding in a colleague about money or family can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">quietly damage a professional reputation</strong>. There is a further problem: if every friend is a coworker, redundancy removes an income and a social circle at the same time, which is precisely when support matters most.\n\nMy own position is that friendships at work are worth having but should not be the whole of one's social life. Interests pursued outside the office — a sport, a language class, volunteering — put a person among people who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have no stake in their career</strong>, and those relationships tend to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more honest for that reason</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, colleagues can become genuine friends and there is no reason to avoid it. The mistake is to rely on work for everything, because a life with only one source of company is a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fragile arrangement</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"meeting people happens naturally","context":"For someone who has moved to a new city for work, the office may be the only place where meeting people happens naturally."},{"en":"have no stake in their career","context":"Interests pursued outside the office — a sport, a language class, volunteering — put a person among people who have no stake in their career, and those relationships tend to be more honest for that reason."},{"en":"fragile arrangement","context":"In conclusion, colleagues can become genuine friends and there is no reason to avoid it. The mistake is to rely on work for everything, because a life with only one source of company is a fragile arrangement."},{"en":"cooperate more readily, ask for help sooner and stay in a job longer","context":"People who enjoy the company around them also cooperate more readily, ask for help sooner and stay in a job longer, which is why many employers deliberately fund social events."},{"en":"a promotion turns a friend into a manager","context":"A disagreement over a project becomes personal, a promotion turns a friend into a manager, and confiding in a colleague about money or family can quietly damage a professional reputation."},{"en":"quietly damage a professional reputation","context":"A disagreement over a project becomes personal, a promotion turns a friend into a manager, and confiding in a colleague about money or family can quietly damage a professional reputation."},{"en":"more honest for that reason","context":"Interests pursued outside the office — a sport, a language class, volunteering — put a person among people who have no stake in their career, and those relationships tend to be more honest for that reason."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#364","summary":"Whether a job should also provide a social life"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2230","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Do social skills matter as much as qualifications?","prompt":"Most employers nowadays put increasing emphasis on social skills. Some people believe that social skills are important in addition to good qualifications for job success. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Job advertisements now routinely ask for communication and teamwork alongside technical qualifications. I agree that social skills have become genuinely important, though I would argue they matter for reasons that are often stated badly.\n\nThe practical reason is that almost no work is done alone any more. A software project, a hospital ward and a building site all depend on people handing information to each other accurately, and an engineer who cannot explain a risk to a manager is dangerous regardless of how good the calculation was. Most failures in organisations are failures of communication rather than of expertise, which is why employers have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">started screening for it deliberately</strong>.\n\nThe second reason is that technical knowledge dates quickly. A programming language learned at university may be obsolete within a decade, so employers increasingly hire for the ability to learn from colleagues, take criticism and work through disagreement, on the assumption that specific skills can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taught on the job</strong>.\n\nThere is a limit to this argument, however. Social confidence is not a substitute for competence, and organisations that promote the most persuasive rather than the most capable make expensive mistakes. There is also a fairness problem: interviews reward a particular manner of speaking, which tends to favour candidates from comfortable backgrounds and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">penalise those who were never coached</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that social skills matter alongside qualifications, because modern work is collaborative and knowledge dates faster than the ability to cooperate. They should nonetheless be judged as a genuine skill that can be taught and assessed, rather than as an impression formed in twenty minutes, so that employers reward <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what a candidate can actually do</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"started screening for it deliberately","context":"Most failures in organisations are failures of communication rather than of expertise, which is why employers have started screening for it deliberately."},{"en":"taught on the job","context":"A programming language learned at university may be obsolete within a decade, so employers increasingly hire for the ability to learn from colleagues, take criticism and work through disagreement, on the assumption that specific skills can be taught on the job."},{"en":"penalise those who were never coached","context":"There is also a fairness problem: interviews reward a particular manner of speaking, which tends to favour candidates from comfortable backgrounds and penalise those who were never coached."},{"en":"what a candidate can actually do","context":"They should nonetheless be judged as a genuine skill that can be taught and assessed, rather than as an impression formed in twenty minutes, so that employers reward what a candidate can actually do."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#365","summary":"The growing emphasis employers place on social skills"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2235","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Laws that limit working hours","prompt":"Some countries have introduced laws to limit working hours for employees. Why are these laws introduced? Do you think they are a positive or negative development?","answer":"Several countries now cap the working week by law, usually at around forty-eight hours. These laws exist because individual employees cannot protect themselves, and I regard them as a clearly positive development.\n\nThe first reason for legislating is health. Research consistently links long hours to heart disease, depression and accidents, and the risk rises sharply beyond about fifty-five hours a week. Because the resulting illness is treated at public expense, governments have a direct financial interest in preventing it rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">paying for the consequences later</strong>. Tired workers are also dangerous to others, which is why drivers and surgeons were regulated first.\n\nThe second reason is that employees have little real choice. Where refusing overtime means losing a promotion or a contract, agreement is not genuine consent, and one worker accepting longer hours forces colleagues to match them. A legal ceiling removes that pressure by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">applying to everyone equally</strong>, which no individual negotiation can achieve.\n\nThe main objection is that such laws reduce output and restrict freedom. In practice the evidence points the other way: productivity per hour falls steeply once people are tired, so countries with shorter statutory weeks, such as Germany and the Netherlands, produce more per hour than countries with longer ones. The freedom argument also carries less weight than it appears to, since the law caps compulsory hours rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">forbidding anyone from working</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, these laws are introduced to protect health and to stop competition between employees driving hours upwards. They are positive, because they prevent a race that harms workers without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">producing more in the end</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"paying for the consequences later","context":"Because the resulting illness is treated at public expense, governments have a direct financial interest in preventing it rather than paying for the consequences later."},{"en":"applying to everyone equally","context":"A legal ceiling removes that pressure by applying to everyone equally, which no individual negotiation can achieve."},{"en":"forbidding anyone from working","context":"The freedom argument also carries less weight than it appears to, since the law caps compulsory hours rather than forbidding anyone from working."},{"en":"producing more in the end","context":"In conclusion, these laws are introduced to protect health and to stop competition between employees driving hours upwards. They are positive, because they prevent a race that harms workers without producing more in the end."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#366","summary":"Why laws limiting working hours are introduced"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2271","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Banning age discrimination in hiring","prompt":"In some countries, it is illegal for companies to reject job applicants because of their age. Is this a positive or negative development?","answer":"Several countries now prohibit employers from turning down candidates on grounds of age. I regard this as a positive development, both for the individuals protected and for the economies that adopt it.\n\nThe strongest argument is fairness. Age says almost nothing about whether a particular person can do a particular job, and rejecting a candidate at fifty-five because of an assumption about energy or adaptability is a judgement about a category rather than about the applicant. The consequences are severe, because older workers who lose a job take far longer to find another and many leave the workforce permanently, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not by choice but by exhaustion</strong>.\n\nThere is also a practical case that will matter more as populations age. Countries with falling birth rates cannot afford to lose experienced workers a decade before they wish to retire, since the pensions and health care of an ageing society are paid for by those still in work. Older employees additionally bring judgement built over decades and lower turnover, and mixed-age teams consistently outperform uniform ones because they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">approach problems from different starting points</strong>.\n\nThe objection is that such laws are difficult to enforce, since an employer can always give another reason for a rejection, and that they may make firms reluctant to hire anyone they might struggle to dismiss. This has some force, but the same was said of laws on sex and disability discrimination, which changed behaviour substantially even though <strong class=\"key-phrase\">individual cases remained hard to prove</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, this is a positive development. It protects people at the point in life when unemployment does the most lasting damage, and it keeps skills in an economy that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">will need them for longer</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"not by choice but by exhaustion","context":"The consequences are severe, because older workers who lose a job take far longer to find another and many leave the workforce permanently, not by choice but by exhaustion."},{"en":"approach problems from different starting points","context":"Older employees additionally bring judgement built over decades and lower turnover, and mixed-age teams consistently outperform uniform ones because they approach problems from different starting points."},{"en":"individual cases remained hard to prove","context":"This has some force, but the same was said of laws on sex and disability discrimination, which changed behaviour substantially even though individual cases remained hard to prove."},{"en":"will need them for longer","context":"In conclusion, this is a positive development. It protects people at the point in life when unemployment does the most lasting damage, and it keeps skills in an economy that will need them for longer."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#367","summary":"Laws making it illegal to reject applicants because of age"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2274","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Is work the most important thing in life?","prompt":"Some people think that work is the most important thing in people's life. Without the success of a career, life becomes meaningless. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Work takes up most of our waking hours and shapes how we see ourselves, so its importance is easy to overstate. A career matters a great deal, but I disagree that a life without professional success is empty.\n\nWork deserves much of the weight placed on it. A job supplies income, and poverty limits almost everything else a person might want to do. It also provides structure, colleagues and a sense of being useful, which is why unemployment damages health even when benefits cover the bills. People who are good at something and paid for it usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take genuine pride in the work itself</strong>, and that pride is no small part of a life.\n\nThe claim collapses once success is treated as the source of meaning. Careers end. Illness, redundancy and retirement remove the job and leave the person, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">anyone whose identity sat entirely inside a job title</strong> is left with nothing at that point. Judging a life by promotion also produces a strange verdict: a nurse who never rises above the ward, a parent who raises three children well and a volunteer who runs a local club for thirty years would all count as failures, which nobody actually believes.\n\nMeaning comes from a wider set of things: being loved, being trusted, learning, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaving something behind that outlasts you</strong>. Work can carry some of that, and so can family, friendship and the ordinary care people give one another. Societies that rank employment above everything else tend to produce <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long hours and lonely people</strong>, which is evidence against the belief rather than for it.\n\nIn conclusion, work is central to a good life because it provides money, purpose and self-respect. It is not the whole of it, and treating career success as the measure of a person leaves most lives, including successful ones, badly misjudged.","phrases":[{"en":"take genuine pride in the work itself","context":"People who are good at something and paid for it usually take genuine pride in the work itself, and that pride is no small part of a life."},{"en":"leaving something behind that outlasts you","context":"Meaning comes from a wider set of things: being loved, being trusted, learning, and leaving something behind that outlasts you."},{"en":"long hours and lonely people","context":"Societies that rank employment above everything else tend to produce long hours and lonely people, which is evidence against the belief rather than for it."},{"en":"anyone whose identity sat entirely inside a job title","context":"Illness, redundancy and retirement remove the job and leave the person, and anyone whose identity sat entirely inside a job title is left with nothing at that point."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#368","summary":"Whether life is meaningless without a successful career"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2280","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Unemployment or a job you dislike?","prompt":"It is better for people to be unemployed than to be in a job they do not enjoy. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Advice to leave any job that makes you unhappy is now common, particularly among younger workers. I disagree with it as a general rule, although there are situations where walking away is clearly the right decision.\n\nUnemployment is far more damaging than the phrase suggests. It removes income first, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">financial fear quickly reaches the family</strong>, the housing and the ability to plan anything. It also removes structure, colleagues and the sense of contributing, which is why long spells out of work are linked to depression far more strongly than a disliked job is. Skills fade, and employers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treat a long gap with suspicion</strong>, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">each month out makes the next job harder to obtain</strong>.\n\nA job that is merely unenjoyable still supplies a good deal. It pays for the parts of life that do give pleasure, and most people find their satisfaction outside work in any case. It teaches things the worker did not expect to learn, and it is a far better position from which to look for something better, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">searching from inside employment</strong> <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carries neither desperation nor a gap in the record</strong>.\n\nThere are limits to this. Work that damages health, involves abuse or requires dishonesty should be left, and where savings or family support exist, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a short deliberate break to retrain</strong> can be a sound investment rather than an escape. The distinction is between leaving towards something and leaving away from something.\n\nIn conclusion, quitting is justified when a job harms a person or when a real plan is waiting. In the ordinary case of boredom or mild dislike, staying while looking is the wiser course, since unemployment usually costs more than the dissatisfaction it was meant to cure.","phrases":[{"en":"treat a long gap with suspicion","context":"Skills fade, and employers treat a long gap with suspicion, so each month out makes the next job harder to obtain."},{"en":"searching from inside employment","context":"It teaches things the worker did not expect to learn, and it is a far better position from which to look for something better, because searching from inside employment carries neither desperation nor a gap in the record."},{"en":"financial fear quickly reaches the family","context":"It removes income first, and financial fear quickly reaches the family, the housing and the ability to plan anything."},{"en":"each month out makes the next job harder to obtain","context":"Skills fade, and employers treat a long gap with suspicion, so each month out makes the next job harder to obtain."},{"en":"carries neither desperation nor a gap in the record","context":"It teaches things the worker did not expect to learn, and it is a far better position from which to look for something better, because searching from inside employment carries neither desperation nor a gap in the record."},{"en":"a short deliberate break to retrain","context":"Work that damages health, involves abuse or requires dishonesty should be left, and where savings or family support exist, a short deliberate break to retrain can be a sound investment rather than an escape."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#369","summary":"Whether it is better to be unemployed than to work in a job you do not enjoy"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2282","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Time off work for new fathers","prompt":"All fathers should be entitled to time off work when their children are born. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Paid leave for mothers is now standard almost everywhere, while fathers in many countries receive a few days or nothing. I agree strongly that fathers should be entitled to leave, because the benefits reach the mother, the child and the workplace at once.\n\nThe most immediate reason concerns the mother. Recovery from birth takes weeks, and a woman managing that alone with a newborn is at real risk of exhaustion and depression. A partner at home shares the night feeds, the cooking and the visits, and rates of postnatal depression are measurably lower where fathers take leave. The relationship between the parents also benefits, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the load is shared instead of quietly resented</strong>.\n\nThe child gains as well. Fathers who care for an infant in the first weeks are more involved for years afterwards, and children with engaged fathers do better at school and show fewer behavioural difficulties. Caring for a baby is a skill learned by doing it, and a father excluded at the start <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never acquires the confidence to take over later</strong>. There is a strong equality argument too. Where only mothers take leave, employers come to regard young women as the risky hire, which is a substantial part of the pay gap. Leave taken by both parents removes that calculation and lets women return to work sooner.\n\nThe objection is cost, particularly for small firms losing a worker for weeks. That is a real difficulty, but it is met by funding the leave through the state rather than the employer, as several countries already do, and the cost is small against the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">savings in maternal illness and lost female earnings</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, paternity leave improves the mother's health, the child's development and equality at work. It should be a paid entitlement rather than a favour, and ideally <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reserved for the father so that it is actually taken</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"the load is shared instead of quietly resented","context":"The relationship between the parents also benefits, since the load is shared instead of quietly resented."},{"en":"never acquires the confidence to take over later","context":"Caring for a baby is a skill learned by doing it, and a father excluded at the start never acquires the confidence to take over later."},{"en":"savings in maternal illness and lost female earnings","context":"That is a real difficulty, but it is met by funding the leave through the state rather than the employer, as several countries already do, and the cost is small against the savings in maternal illness and lost female earnings."},{"en":"reserved for the father so that it is actually taken","context":"In conclusion, paternity leave improves the mother's health, the child's development and equality at work. It should be a paid entitlement rather than a favour, and ideally reserved for the father so that it is actually taken."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#370","summary":"Whether all fathers should be entitled to leave when a child is born"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2284","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Should managers earn far more?","prompt":"Some people think it is a good thing for senior managers to have much higher salaries than the other workers in a company. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Chief executives in large firms now earn many times the salary of their average employee, and the multiple has grown steadily for decades. I agree that senior managers should be paid more, but not by the margins that have become normal.\n\nThere are respectable reasons for a substantial gap. Senior decisions carry consequences that ordinary roles do not: a single judgement about a market or an acquisition can determine whether thousands keep their jobs. The work also demands experience that takes decades to acquire, and the responsibility is personal, since a chief executive is dismissed when results fail while most employees are not. Competition is real too, because able managers are scarce and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a firm that underpays them loses them to a competitor</strong>. A visible reward for reaching the top also encourages people to take on difficult roles.\n\nThe size of the current gap is much harder to defend. Executive pay has multiplied while wages below have barely moved, and no comparable change in the difficulty of the work explains it. The pay is often set by committees of other executives, so it reflects <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comparison with peers rather than performance</strong>, and it rises after failure as reliably as after success. Very large gaps also damage the organisation, since employees who see enormous rewards at the top and refused pay claims below stop believing that effort is fairly recognised, and companies with extreme differentials show higher staff turnover.\n\nThere is a wider effect, in that pay at the top has become a principal driver of inequality, and money paid to a handful of individuals is money <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not spent on wages, training or investment</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, senior managers carry heavier responsibility and should be paid accordingly. The present multiples reflect weak governance rather than value, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">transparent limits linked to average pay</strong> would be a reasonable correction.","phrases":[{"en":"a firm that underpays them loses them to a competitor","context":"Competition is real too, because able managers are scarce and a firm that underpays them loses them to a competitor."},{"en":"comparison with peers rather than performance","context":"The pay is often set by committees of other executives, so it reflects comparison with peers rather than performance, and it rises after failure as reliably as after success."},{"en":"not spent on wages, training or investment","context":"There is a wider effect, in that pay at the top has become a principal driver of inequality, and money paid to a handful of individuals is money not spent on wages, training or investment."},{"en":"transparent limits linked to average pay","context":"In conclusion, senior managers carry heavier responsibility and should be paid accordingly. The present multiples reflect weak governance rather than value, so transparent limits linked to average pay would be a reasonable correction."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#371","summary":"Whether large pay gaps between senior managers and other staff are justified"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2309","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Should employers care how staff dress?","prompt":"Some people believe that employers should not be concerned about the way their employees dress, but should be concerned about the quality of their work. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Offices that once required suits now accept jeans, and many employers say they judge output alone. I largely agree with that position, though appearance still carries weight in a limited set of jobs.\n\nOutput is the honest measure. A programmer writes the same code in a hooded top as in a jacket, and an accountant's figures do not improve with a tie. Judging staff by clothing is also unreliable in a way that quietly damages a company, because it favours those who can afford expensive clothes and those whose background matches the manager's, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">capable people are overlooked for reasons unrelated to ability</strong>. Relaxed rules bring practical gains too: staff are more comfortable, save money, and firms that dropped their dress codes have found recruitment easier rather than standards lower.\n\nThere are exceptions, and they are not trivial. Where employees meet clients, patients or the public, dress is part of the service: a surgeon in a stained shirt or a lawyer in beachwear damages the trust a client needs to feel. Uniforms also serve identification and safety, which is why steel-capped boots and high-visibility jackets are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">requirements rather than preferences</strong>. In these cases the employer is protecting the customer, not imposing taste.\n\nThe sensible position is therefore a narrow standard rather than none. A company can reasonably ask for clean, safe clothing that suits the setting, and should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">explain the reason behind any rule it sets</strong>. What it should not do is police style, since a preference for formality is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a matter of habit, not of performance</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, quality of work is the proper measure and employers should concentrate on it. Dress codes are justified only where safety or a client relationship depends on them, and beyond that they achieve very little.","phrases":[{"en":"capable people are overlooked for reasons unrelated to ability","context":"Judging staff by clothing is also unreliable in a way that quietly damages a company, because it favours those who can afford expensive clothes and those whose background matches the manager's, so capable people are overlooked for reasons unrelated to ability."},{"en":"requirements rather than preferences","context":"Uniforms also serve identification and safety, which is why steel-capped boots and high-visibility jackets are requirements rather than preferences."},{"en":"explain the reason behind any rule it sets","context":"A company can reasonably ask for clean, safe clothing that suits the setting, and should explain the reason behind any rule it sets."},{"en":"a matter of habit, not of performance","context":"What it should not do is police style, since a preference for formality is a matter of habit, not of performance."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#372","summary":"Whether appearance at work matters or only the quality of the work"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2321","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Would younger leaders be better?","prompt":"Leaders and directors in organisations are normally older people. Some people think that having a younger leader would be better. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Boardrooms and cabinets are dominated by people in their fifties and sixties, and the suggestion that younger leaders would do better is heard more often each year. I partly agree: age is a poor qualification in itself, but replacing it with youth would repeat the same mistake.\n\nThe case for younger leaders is strongest where the world is changing quickly. Someone who grew up with the technology their company sells understands its customers without translation, and younger leaders tend to be less attached to methods that once worked. They also have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no reputation to protect from a change of direction</strong>, which makes difficult decisions easier, and they will personally live with the long-term consequences of choices about climate, debt and pensions, so they weigh them differently.\n\nExperience nonetheless earns its position. Judgement is largely a store of remembered failures, and a leader who has managed a recession, a scandal or a lost contract recognises the early signs. Much of leadership is also negotiation, and knowing which people to call and how far they can be pushed is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">knowledge that cannot be acquired quickly</strong>. Confidence without that store produces expensive errors, as several young founders have demonstrated.\n\nThe real objection to the present arrangement is not that leaders are old but that promotion often follows length of service rather than ability, so capable people wait for their turn while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weak leaders remain in place until retirement</strong>. The answer is to select on evidence of judgement and to build teams of mixed ages, where the young supply energy and current knowledge and the experienced supply pattern recognition.\n\nIn conclusion, younger leaders would improve many organisations, particularly fast-moving ones. Youth is not a qualification either, and the sensible reform is to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stop treating age as a proxy for competence</strong> in either direction.","phrases":[{"en":"no reputation to protect from a change of direction","context":"They also have no reputation to protect from a change of direction, which makes difficult decisions easier, and they will personally live with the long-term consequences of choices about climate, debt and pensions, so they weigh them differently."},{"en":"knowledge that cannot be acquired quickly","context":"Much of leadership is also negotiation, and knowing which people to call and how far they can be pushed is knowledge that cannot be acquired quickly."},{"en":"weak leaders remain in place until retirement","context":"The real objection to the present arrangement is not that leaders are old but that promotion often follows length of service rather than ability, so capable people wait for their turn while weak leaders remain in place until retirement."},{"en":"stop treating age as a proxy for competence","context":"In conclusion, younger leaders would improve many organisations, particularly fast-moving ones. Youth is not a qualification either, and the sensible reform is to stop treating age as a proxy for competence in either direction."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#373","summary":"Whether organisations would benefit from younger people in charge"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2345","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Why some people work harder","prompt":"In both education and employment, some people work harder than others. Why do some people work harder than others? Is working hard always a good thing?","answer":"In any classroom or office, effort varies far more than ability does. The reasons lie in circumstance and motivation rather than character, and hard work is valuable only while it remains attached to a purpose.\n\nNecessity is the first explanation. A student whose family has sacrificed to pay the fees, or an employee supporting relatives, works from a pressure that a comfortable colleague simply does not feel. Interest is the second and stronger one: people who find the subject absorbing put in hours that do not feel like effort, while those in the wrong course or job <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spend most of their energy on getting started</strong>. Upbringing matters too, since children raised where persistence was expected treat difficulty as normal rather than as a signal to stop.\n\nThere is also a question of belief. People work hard when they expect the effort to change something, which is why students who think ability is fixed give up quickly, and why employees in workplaces where promotion follows connections rather than results <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stop trying for reasons that are entirely rational</strong>.\n\nHard work is not always good, however. Beyond a certain point the returns fall sharply, and long hours produce exhaustion, poor judgement and errors that undo the extra time. Effort can also be misdirected, since somebody working twelve hours on the wrong task achieves less than a colleague who thinks first. The heaviest cost is personal, because health, family and friendship are what usually pay for the additional hours, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nobody recovers that time later</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, people work hard because of financial pressure, genuine interest, upbringing and a belief that effort matters. Hard work is admirable when it is aimed at something worthwhile and bounded by rest, and harmful once it becomes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a habit that no longer serves any goal</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"spend most of their energy on getting started","context":"Interest is the second and stronger one: people who find the subject absorbing put in hours that do not feel like effort, while those in the wrong course or job spend most of their energy on getting started."},{"en":"stop trying for reasons that are entirely rational","context":"People work hard when they expect the effort to change something, which is why students who think ability is fixed give up quickly, and why employees in workplaces where promotion follows connections rather than results stop trying for reasons that are entirely rational."},{"en":"nobody recovers that time later","context":"The heaviest cost is personal, because health, family and friendship are what usually pay for the additional hours, and nobody recovers that time later."},{"en":"a habit that no longer serves any goal","context":"Hard work is admirable when it is aimed at something worthwhile and bounded by rest, and harmful once it becomes a habit that no longer serves any goal."}],"subtype":"Two-Part Question","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#374","summary":"The reasons behind differences in effort and whether hard work is always good"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2375","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Follow your interest or choose a safe job?","prompt":"Some people believe that young people should be free to pursue the jobs they want, while others think they should choose jobs that are more practical for their future. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Few family arguments are as common as the one between a young person's ambition and a parent's sense of what is sensible. Both sides are arguing for the same person's welfare, and my own view is that the disagreement is usually about timing rather than principle.\n\nThe argument for free choice rests on how work actually goes. Careers last forty years, and nobody sustains that in a field they find dull, so people pushed into safe professions frequently leave them exhausted in their thirties. Interest also produces skill, because someone absorbed in a subject practises far beyond what is required, and the difference between adequate and excellent is usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the hours nobody asked them to put in</strong>. Fields that once looked frivolous — games, sport, design — now employ millions, so the definition of a practical job keeps changing.\n\nThe practical view answers with what happens to those who fail. Some fields accept a tiny fraction of those who train for them, and the rest arrive at thirty with debt, no relevant experience and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a qualification that does not transfer</strong>. Parents also know that financial security shapes everything else, and that a stable income makes it possible to keep an interest alive outside work. In countries without a safety net, this is not timidity but arithmetic.\n\nThe sensible position combines them. Young people should choose their direction, since a life spent in work chosen by somebody else rarely goes well, but they should choose with honest information about earnings and competition, and should keep <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a qualification that can be used elsewhere</strong>. Testing an ambition seriously for a few years is very different from pursuing it indefinitely.\n\nIn conclusion, forcing young people into practical work produces capable but resentful adults, while ignoring the odds produces avoidable hardship. The answer is freedom exercised with realism and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a route back if the first attempt fails</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"the hours nobody asked them to put in","context":"Interest also produces skill, because someone absorbed in a subject practises far beyond what is required, and the difference between adequate and excellent is usually the hours nobody asked them to put in."},{"en":"a qualification that does not transfer","context":"Some fields accept a tiny fraction of those who train for them, and the rest arrive at thirty with debt, no relevant experience and a qualification that does not transfer."},{"en":"a qualification that can be used elsewhere","context":"Young people should choose their direction, since a life spent in work chosen by somebody else rarely goes well, but they should choose with honest information about earnings and competition, and should keep a qualification that can be used elsewhere."},{"en":"a route back if the first attempt fails","context":"In conclusion, forcing young people into practical work produces capable but resentful adults, while ignoring the odds produces avoidable hardship. The answer is freedom exercised with realism and a route back if the first attempt fails."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#375","summary":"Whether young people should pursue the work they want or the practical option"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2380","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Should doctors earn more than entertainers?","prompt":"Professional workers like doctors, nurses and teachers make a greater contribution to society, so they should be paid more than sports and entertainment personalities. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"A nurse earns in a year what a leading footballer earns in a morning, and most people find that ordering of rewards uncomfortable. I agree that essential professions are underpaid, but I disagree that the two groups can be compared in the way the statement assumes.\n\nThe case for paying professionals more is strong on its own terms. Doctors, nurses and teachers do work that a society cannot function without, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">they train for years to do it</strong>. Their pay has fallen behind living costs in many countries, which is why hospitals and schools now <strong class=\"key-phrase\">struggle to fill posts</strong>, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a shortage of nurses costs far more than a pay rise would</strong>. There is also a moral objection to a scale of rewards in which entertainment is valued above care, since children learn from it what a society thinks is worth doing.\n\nThe comparison nevertheless misunderstands where the money comes from. A teacher is paid from taxation, and salaries are limited by what a country can raise and by how many teachers it employs. A footballer is paid by a club that sells tickets, shirts and broadcasting rights to millions of willing customers, so the salary reflects <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what an audience chooses to spend</strong> rather than any judgement about value. Nobody decided that entertainers should earn more; <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the difference emerges from scarcity</strong>, since thousands can be trained as accountants while very few can fill a stadium.\n\nThe practical conclusion is that professional pay should be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raised on its own merits rather than by capping entertainers</strong>. Reducing an athlete's income would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">transfer nothing to a hospital</strong>, and would simply move the talent abroad.\n\nIn conclusion, doctors, nurses and teachers contribute more to society than performers and deserve considerably better pay. The two salaries are set by entirely different mechanisms, so the argument for raising one is not an argument for lowering the other.","phrases":[{"en":"a shortage of nurses costs far more than a pay rise would","context":"Their pay has fallen behind living costs in many countries, which is why hospitals and schools now struggle to fill posts, and a shortage of nurses costs far more than a pay rise would."},{"en":"what an audience chooses to spend","context":"A footballer is paid by a club that sells tickets, shirts and broadcasting rights to millions of willing customers, so the salary reflects what an audience chooses to spend rather than any judgement about value."},{"en":"transfer nothing to a hospital","context":"The practical conclusion is that professional pay should be raised on its own merits rather than by capping entertainers. Reducing an athlete's income would transfer nothing to a hospital, and would simply move the talent abroad."},{"en":"they train for years to do it","context":"Doctors, nurses and teachers do work that a society cannot function without, and they train for years to do it."},{"en":"struggle to fill posts","context":"Their pay has fallen behind living costs in many countries, which is why hospitals and schools now struggle to fill posts, and a shortage of nurses costs far more than a pay rise would."},{"en":"the difference emerges from scarcity","context":"Nobody decided that entertainers should earn more; the difference emerges from scarcity, since thousands can be trained as accountants while very few can fill a stadium."},{"en":"raised on its own merits rather than by capping entertainers","context":"The practical conclusion is that professional pay should be raised on its own merits rather than by capping entertainers. Reducing an athlete's income would transfer nothing to a hospital, and would simply move the talent abroad."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#376","summary":"Whether essential professions deserve higher pay than sports and entertainment stars"},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Your favourite animal, then and now","prompt":"What is your favourite animal and why?\nDo you like the same animal you liked when you were younger?\nWhat can you learn about people from the animals they like?\nWhich animals are popular as pets in your country?","answer":"Q: What is your favourite animal and why?\nA: Dogs are my favourite because they're responsive and emotionally expressive. A well-trained dog can become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a genuine companion</strong>, rather than simply a pet.\n\nQ: Do you like the same animal you liked when you were younger?\nA: Yes. I liked dogs as a child because my grandparents had one, and that's probably why I still love them today. I still admire how easily dogs <strong class=\"key-phrase\">build trust with people</strong>.\n\nQ: What can you learn about people from the animals they like?\nA: You might get a small clue about someone's lifestyle, but I wouldn't read too much into it. An energetic person may prefer dogs, while someone who values quiet may choose cats, but personality is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far more complex</strong> than a pet preference.\n\nQ: Which animals are popular as pets in your country?\nA: Dogs and cats are the most common pets, while fish and small birds are popular in apartments. The choice often depends on space, cost and how much time the owner can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">devote to daily care</strong>.","topic":"Animals","phrases":[{"en":"a genuine companion","context":"Dogs are my favourite because they're responsive and emotionally expressive. A well-trained dog can become a genuine companion, rather than simply a pet."},{"en":"build trust with people","context":"Yes. I liked dogs as a child because my grandparents had one, and that's probably why I still love them today. I still admire how easily dogs build trust with people."},{"en":"far more complex","context":"You might get a small clue about someone's lifestyle, but I wouldn't read too much into it. An energetic person may prefer dogs, while someone who values quiet may choose cats, but personality is far more complex than a pet preference."},{"en":"devote to daily care","context":"Dogs and cats are the most common pets, while fish and small birds are popular in apartments. The choice often depends on space, cost and how much time the owner can devote to daily care."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Animal","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-001","_displayNo":"#001","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-001-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-001-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-001-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-001-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Dogs are my favourite animals because they react to people and show their feelings clearly. If a dog is well trained, it can become a close friend, not just a pet.","phrases":[{"en":"a close friend","meaning":"Someone you know well and care about."}],"question":"What is your favourite animal and why?","html":"Dogs are my favourite animals because they react to people and show their feelings clearly. If a dog is well trained, it can become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a close friend</strong>, not just a pet."},{"text":"Yes, I still like dogs. My grandparents had one when I was a child, so I grew up liking them. I also like how easily dogs learn to trust people.","phrases":[{"en":"grew up","meaning":"Spent your childhood and became older."}],"question":"Do you like the same animal you liked when you were younger?","html":"Yes, I still like dogs. My grandparents had one when I was a child, so I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">grew up</strong> liking them. I also like how easily dogs learn to trust people."},{"text":"You may learn a little about someone's lifestyle. Active people might like dogs, while quieter people may prefer cats. However, you cannot judge someone's whole personality from their favourite animal.","phrases":[{"en":"judge someone","meaning":"Form an opinion about a person."}],"question":"What can you learn about people from the animals they like?","html":"You may learn a little about someone&#x27;s lifestyle. Active people might like dogs, while quieter people may prefer cats. However, you cannot <strong class=\"key-phrase\">judge someone</strong>&#x27;s whole personality from their favourite animal."},{"text":"Dogs and cats are the most popular pets. People in flats also keep fish and small birds. Their choice depends on their space, budget and the time they have to look after a pet.","phrases":[{"en":"look after","meaning":"Take care of someone or something."}],"question":"Which animals are popular as pets in your country?","html":"Dogs and cats are the most popular pets. People in flats also keep fish and small birds. Their choice depends on their space, budget and the time they have to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look after</strong> a pet."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-001-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-001-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-001-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-001-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Art lessons, drawing and galleries","prompt":"Did you enjoy doing art lessons when you were a child?\nDo you ever draw or paint pictures now?\nWhen was the last time you went to an art gallery or exhibition?\nWhat kind of pictures do you like having in your home?","answer":"Q: Did you enjoy doing art lessons when you were a child?\nA: No I didn't. I was never very good at drawing or painting, and I found art lessons quite slow and boring. I preferred <strong class=\"key-phrase\">language and science lessons</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you ever draw or paint pictures now?\nA: No, I don't draw or paint these days, because there's never any need for me to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">create art at work</strong>, and I still don't enjoy art as a hobby.\n\nQ: When was the last time you went to an art gallery or exhibition?\nA: I honestly can't remember the last time I went to any kind of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">art exhibition</strong>; I'm <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not much of</strong> an art fan, and I tend to go to music concerts, the cinema or the theatre instead of art shows.\n\nQ: What kind of pictures do you like having in your home?\nA: I don't have any paintings or artistic images in my home, but I do have photos of family members and some nice photos of landscapes on the walls to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">brighten the place up</strong>.","topic":"Art","phrases":[{"en":"language and science lessons","context":"No I didn't. I was never very good at drawing or painting, and I found art lessons quite slow and boring. I preferred language and science lessons."},{"en":"create art at work","context":"No, I don't draw or paint these days, because there's never any need for me to create art at work, and I still don't enjoy art as a hobby."},{"en":"art exhibition","context":"I honestly can't remember the last time I went to any kind of art exhibition; I'm not much of an art fan, and I tend to go to music concerts, the cinema or the theatre instead of art shows."},{"en":"not much of","context":"I honestly can't remember the last time I went to any kind of art exhibition; I'm not much of an art fan, and I tend to go to music concerts, the cinema or the theatre instead of art shows."},{"en":"brighten the place up","context":"I don't have any paintings or artistic images in my home, but I do have photos of family members and some nice photos of landscapes on the walls to brighten the place up."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Art","id":"speaking-part-1-002","_displayNo":"#002","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-002-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-002-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-002-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-002-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"No, I didn't really enjoy them. I wasn't good at drawing or painting, and the lessons felt slow and boring. I liked language and science classes more.","phrases":[{"en":"wasn't good at","meaning":"Did not have much skill in something."}],"question":"Did you enjoy doing art lessons when you were a child?","html":"No, I didn&#x27;t really enjoy them. I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wasn&#x27;t good at</strong> drawing or painting, and the lessons felt slow and boring. I liked language and science classes more."},{"text":"No, I don't draw or paint now. I don't need to do it for my job, and it is still not a hobby that I enjoy.","phrases":[{"en":"for my job","meaning":"As part of the work I do."}],"question":"Do you ever draw or paint pictures now?","html":"No, I don&#x27;t draw or paint now. I don&#x27;t need to do it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">for my job</strong>, and it is still not a hobby that I enjoy."},{"text":"I can't remember my last visit because I'm not very interested in art. I usually choose a concert, a film or a play instead of an art exhibition.","phrases":[{"en":"instead of","meaning":"In place of something else."}],"question":"When was the last time you went to an art gallery or exhibition?","html":"I can&#x27;t remember my last visit because I&#x27;m not very interested in art. I usually choose a concert, a film or a play <strong class=\"key-phrase\">instead of</strong> an art exhibition."},{"text":"I don't have paintings at home, but I do have family photos and pictures of beautiful places. They make the rooms look brighter and more welcoming.","phrases":[{"en":"more welcoming","meaning":"Making people feel more comfortable and happy to be there."}],"question":"What kind of pictures do you like having in your home?","html":"I don&#x27;t have paintings at home, but I do have family photos and pictures of beautiful places. They make the rooms look brighter and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more welcoming</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-002-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-002-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-002-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-002-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"How bicycles are used in your country","prompt":"How are bicycles used in your country?\nHow popular are bicycles in your country?\nAre they used more now than in the past and why?\nWhat do bicycles mean to you?","answer":"Q: How are bicycles used in your country?\nA: Bicycles are mainly used for short journeys, exercise and leisure. In large cities they're less common for commuting because motorbikes dominate the roads, although public bicycle schemes are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gradually gaining ground</strong>.\n\nQ: How popular are bicycles in your country?\nA: They're moderately popular, particularly among students and people who cycle for fitness. But the lack of protected lanes prevents cycling from becoming <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a mainstream form of transport</strong>.\n\nQ: Are they used more now than in the past and why?\nA: Recreational cycling is more visible than it was a decade ago, partly because people are more health-conscious. Everyday commuting hasn't grown as quickly, since traffic safety remains <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a major concern</strong>.\n\nQ: What do bicycles mean to you?\nA: To me, a bicycle represents independence and uncomplicated exercise. It lets me travel <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at my own pace</strong>, avoid short car journeys and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">clear my head</strong> at the same time.","topic":"Bicycles","phrases":[{"en":"gradually gaining ground","context":"Bicycles are mainly used for short journeys, exercise and leisure. In large cities they're less common for commuting because motorbikes dominate the roads, although public bicycle schemes are gradually gaining ground."},{"en":"a mainstream form of transport","context":"They're moderately popular, particularly among students and people who cycle for fitness. But the lack of protected lanes prevents cycling from becoming a mainstream form of transport."},{"en":"a major concern","context":"Recreational cycling is more visible than it was a decade ago, partly because people are more health-conscious. Everyday commuting hasn't grown as quickly, since traffic safety remains a major concern."},{"en":"at my own pace","context":"To me, a bicycle represents independence and uncomplicated exercise. It lets me travel at my own pace, avoid short car journeys and clear my head at the same time."},{"en":"clear my head","context":"To me, a bicycle represents independence and uncomplicated exercise. It lets me travel at my own pace, avoid short car journeys and clear my head at the same time."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Bicycle","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-003","_displayNo":"#003","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-003-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-003-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-003-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-003-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"People mainly use bicycles for short trips, exercise and fun. Fewer people cycle to work in big cities because there are so many motorbikes, although public bicycle services are becoming more common.","phrases":[{"en":"short trips","meaning":"Journeys that do not take much time or cover much distance."}],"question":"How are bicycles used in your country?","html":"People mainly use bicycles for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">short trips</strong>, exercise and fun. Fewer people cycle to work in big cities because there are so many motorbikes, although public bicycle services are becoming more common."},{"text":"They are fairly popular with students and people who want to exercise. However, there aren't enough safe cycle lanes, so many people do not use bicycles as their main way of travelling.","phrases":[{"en":"cycle lanes","meaning":"Parts of a road intended for people riding bicycles."}],"question":"How popular are bicycles in your country?","html":"They are fairly popular with students and people who want to exercise. However, there aren&#x27;t enough safe <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cycle lanes</strong>, so many people do not use bicycles as their main way of travelling."},{"text":"More people seem to cycle for exercise than ten years ago because they care more about their health. Cycling to work has not increased as much because people still worry about road safety.","phrases":[{"en":"road safety","meaning":"Keeping people safe when they use roads."}],"question":"Are they used more now than in the past and why?","html":"More people seem to cycle for exercise than ten years ago because they care more about their health. Cycling to work has not increased as much because people still worry about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">road safety</strong>."},{"text":"A bicycle gives me freedom and an easy way to exercise. I can choose my own speed, avoid using a car for short trips and relax my mind while I ride.","phrases":[{"en":"my own speed","meaning":"A speed that I choose and find comfortable."}],"question":"What do bicycles mean to you?","html":"A bicycle gives me freedom and an easy way to exercise. I can choose <strong class=\"key-phrase\">my own speed</strong>, avoid using a car for short trips and relax my mind while I ride."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-003-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-003-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-003-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-003-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Reading in English and where you read","prompt":"What kinds of things do you enjoy reading in English?\nAre you someone who can read anywhere, or do you have a favourite place?\nDo you need peace and quiet to read, or can you concentrate in noisy places?\nHow did you first learn to read?","answer":"Q: What kinds of things do you enjoy reading in English?\nA: I mostly read non-fiction in English — psychology, technology, that kind of thing, and books on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">personal development</strong>. They're great for my vocabulary, and they give me ideas I can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">apply in real life</strong>.\n\nQ: Are you someone who can read anywhere, or do you have a favourite place?\nA: I can read almost anywhere, but I concentrate best at a quiet desk by a window. That's what helps me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay fully absorbed</strong>, without checking my phone every few minutes.\n\nQ: Do you need peace and quiet to read, or can you concentrate in noisy places?\nA: If it's something difficult, I need a fairly quiet environment. I can skim an article in a cafe, but serious reading needs enough silence for me to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">process the ideas properly</strong>.\n\nQ: How did you first learn to read?\nA: I first learned by sounding out short words with my parents, then reading picture books at school. Once I found books I genuinely enjoyed, reading didn't feel like homework any more — it just became <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a habit of my own</strong>.","topic":"Books","phrases":[{"en":"personal development","context":"I mostly read non-fiction in English — psychology, technology, that kind of thing, and books on personal development. They're great for my vocabulary, and they give me ideas I can apply in real life."},{"en":"apply in real life","context":"I mostly read non-fiction in English — psychology, technology, that kind of thing, and books on personal development. They're great for my vocabulary, and they give me ideas I can apply in real life."},{"en":"stay fully absorbed","context":"I can read almost anywhere, but I concentrate best at a quiet desk by a window. That's what helps me stay fully absorbed, without checking my phone every few minutes."},{"en":"process the ideas properly","context":"If it's something difficult, I need a fairly quiet environment. I can skim an article in a cafe, but serious reading needs enough silence for me to process the ideas properly."},{"en":"a habit of my own","context":"I first learned by sounding out short words with my parents, then reading picture books at school. Once I found books I genuinely enjoyed, reading didn't feel like homework any more — it just became a habit of my own."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Book","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-004","_displayNo":"#004","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-004-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-004-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-004-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-004-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I mainly read books about real subjects, such as psychology, technology and improving myself. They help me learn new English words and give me useful ideas for everyday life.","phrases":[{"en":"everyday life","meaning":"The normal things people do each day."}],"question":"What kinds of things do you enjoy reading in English?","html":"I mainly read books about real subjects, such as psychology, technology and improving myself. They help me learn new English words and give me useful ideas for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">everyday life</strong>."},{"text":"I can read in most places, but my favourite spot is a quiet desk near a window. I can focus there without looking at my phone all the time.","phrases":[{"en":"focus","meaning":"Give your attention to one thing."}],"question":"Are you someone who can read anywhere, or do you have a favourite place?","html":"I can read in most places, but my favourite spot is a quiet desk near a window. I can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">focus</strong> there without looking at my phone all the time."},{"text":"I need quiet when I read something difficult. I can read a short article quickly in a cafe, but I need less noise to understand more serious ideas.","phrases":[{"en":"less noise","meaning":"A smaller amount of unwanted sound."}],"question":"Do you need peace and quiet to read, or can you concentrate in noisy places?","html":"I need quiet when I read something difficult. I can read a short article quickly in a cafe, but I need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less noise</strong> to understand more serious ideas."},{"text":"My parents first helped me read short words by saying their sounds. Later, I read picture books at school. When I found books I liked, I started reading because I wanted to.","phrases":[{"en":"picture books","meaning":"Books that use pictures to help tell a story."}],"question":"How did you first learn to read?","html":"My parents first helped me read short words by saying their sounds. Later, I read <strong class=\"key-phrase\">picture books</strong> at school. When I found books I liked, I started reading because I wanted to."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-004-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-004-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-004-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-004-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"An interesting building, and preserving old ones","prompt":"Can you tell me about an interesting building in your hometown?\nWhy should we preserve historical buildings?\nHow have architecture and building materials changed in your country?\nWhat type of building do you prefer to live in and why?","answer":"Q: Can you tell me about an interesting building in your hometown?\nA: The Hanoi Opera House is one of the most striking buildings in my city. Its French-inspired façade and central location give it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a distinctive character</strong> that modern glass towers often lack.\n\nQ: Why should we preserve historical buildings?\nA: Historical buildings preserve evidence of how a city developed and give residents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of continuity</strong>. Once they're demolished, the knowledge and atmosphere they carry can't be recreated convincingly.\n\nQ: How have architecture and building materials changed in your country?\nA: New buildings use more steel, glass and prefabricated materials, while older homes relied heavily on brick and timber. Designs have also become more compact and energy-efficient to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meet urban needs</strong>.\n\nQ: What type of building do you prefer to live in and why?\nA: I'd prefer a medium-sized apartment with natural light, good soundproofing and access to public transport. I don't need luxury, but I value a home that's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easy to maintain</strong> and comfortable throughout the year.","topic":"Buildings","phrases":[{"en":"a distinctive character","context":"The Hanoi Opera House is one of the most striking buildings in my city. Its French-inspired façade and central location give it a distinctive character that modern glass towers often lack."},{"en":"a sense of continuity","context":"Historical buildings preserve evidence of how a city developed and give residents a sense of continuity. Once they're demolished, the knowledge and atmosphere they carry can't be recreated convincingly."},{"en":"meet urban needs","context":"New buildings use more steel, glass and prefabricated materials, while older homes relied heavily on brick and timber. Designs have also become more compact and energy-efficient to meet urban needs."},{"en":"easy to maintain","context":"I'd prefer a medium-sized apartment with natural light, good soundproofing and access to public transport. I don't need luxury, but I value a home that's easy to maintain and comfortable throughout the year."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Building","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-005","_displayNo":"#005","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-005-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-005-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-005-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-005-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"The Hanoi Opera House is an interesting building in my city. It has a French-style front and stands in the city centre. It looks quite different from modern glass buildings.","phrases":[{"en":"city centre","meaning":"The main central area of a city."}],"question":"Can you tell me about an interesting building in your hometown?","html":"The Hanoi Opera House is an interesting building in my city. It has a French-style front and stands in the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">city centre</strong>. It looks quite different from modern glass buildings."},{"text":"Old buildings show us how a city has changed and help people feel connected to its past. If we knock them down, it is difficult to bring back their history and special feeling.","phrases":[{"en":"knock them down","meaning":"Destroy buildings so they no longer stand."}],"question":"Why should we preserve historical buildings?","html":"Old buildings show us how a city has changed and help people feel connected to its past. If we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">knock them down</strong>, it is difficult to bring back their history and special feeling."},{"text":"Modern buildings use more steel, glass and ready-made parts, while older houses used mainly bricks and wood. New designs also save space and energy because cities have different needs now.","phrases":[{"en":"save space","meaning":"Use less room or make better use of it."}],"question":"How have architecture and building materials changed in your country?","html":"Modern buildings use more steel, glass and ready-made parts, while older houses used mainly bricks and wood. New designs also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">save space</strong> and energy because cities have different needs now."},{"text":"I'd like a medium-sized flat with plenty of daylight, little noise and public transport nearby. It doesn't need to be expensive, but it should be comfortable and easy to keep in good condition.","phrases":[{"en":"in good condition","meaning":"Working well and not damaged."}],"question":"What type of building do you prefer to live in and why?","html":"I&#x27;d like a medium-sized flat with plenty of daylight, little noise and public transport nearby. It doesn&#x27;t need to be expensive, but it should be comfortable and easy to keep <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in good condition</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-005-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-005-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-005-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-005-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Chocolate: what you eat and when","prompt":"Do you like chocolate?\nWhat type of chocolate do you prefer?\nWhen do you usually eat chocolate?\nDo people give chocolate as a present in your country?","answer":"Q: Do you like chocolate?\nA: Yes, I love chocolate. I like the taste, the texture and the feeling that you get when you eat it. And it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">goes really well with</strong> my favourite drink, coffee.\n\nQ: What type of chocolate do you prefer?\nA: I prefer <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dark chocolate</strong> to milk chocolate, because it has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a stronger taste</strong>. I also like chocolate when it's in a cake, and I love drinking hot chocolate too.\n\nQ: When do you usually eat chocolate?\nA: I try not to eat too much, but I probably eat or drink something that contains chocolate <strong class=\"key-phrase\">every couple of days</strong>.\n\nQ: Do people give chocolate as a present in your country?\nA: Yes, it's very common to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give boxes of individual chocolates</strong> as a gift. You can give chocolates on any special occasion really: birthdays, Valentine's Day, Easter, Christmas, and so on.","topic":"Chocolate","phrases":[{"en":"goes really well with","context":"Yes, I love chocolate. I like the taste, the texture and the feeling that you get when you eat it. And it goes really well with my favourite drink, coffee."},{"en":"dark chocolate","context":"I prefer dark chocolate to milk chocolate, because it has a stronger taste. I also like chocolate when it's in a cake, and I love drinking hot chocolate too."},{"en":"a stronger taste","context":"I prefer dark chocolate to milk chocolate, because it has a stronger taste. I also like chocolate when it's in a cake, and I love drinking hot chocolate too."},{"en":"every couple of days","context":"I try not to eat too much, but I probably eat or drink something that contains chocolate every couple of days."},{"en":"give boxes of individual chocolates","context":"Yes, it's very common to give boxes of individual chocolates as a gift. You can give chocolates on any special occasion really: birthdays, Valentine's Day, Easter, Christmas, and so on."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Chocolate","id":"speaking-part-1-006","_displayNo":"#006","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-006-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-006-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-006-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-006-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, I really like chocolate. I enjoy its taste and how it feels when I eat it. It also tastes good with coffee, which is my favourite drink.","phrases":[{"en":"tastes good with","meaning":"Has a pleasant flavour when eaten or drunk with something else."}],"question":"Do you like chocolate?","html":"Yes, I really like chocolate. I enjoy its taste and how it feels when I eat it. It also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tastes good with</strong> coffee, which is my favourite drink."},{"text":"I prefer dark chocolate because its flavour is stronger than milk chocolate. I also enjoy chocolate cake and hot chocolate, so I like it in different forms.","phrases":[{"en":"in different forms","meaning":"In several different types or ways."}],"question":"What type of chocolate do you prefer?","html":"I prefer dark chocolate because its flavour is stronger than milk chocolate. I also enjoy chocolate cake and hot chocolate, so I like it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in different forms</strong>."},{"text":"I try to limit how much chocolate I have. Still, I usually eat or drink something with chocolate in it about once every two days.","phrases":[{"en":"limit","meaning":"Control something so there is not too much of it."}],"question":"When do you usually eat chocolate?","html":"I try to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">limit</strong> how much chocolate I have. Still, I usually eat or drink something with chocolate in it about once every two days."},{"text":"Yes, boxes of chocolates are common gifts in my country. People give them for birthdays and celebrations such as Valentine's Day, Easter or Christmas.","phrases":[{"en":"common gifts","meaning":"Presents that people often give."}],"question":"Do people give chocolate as a present in your country?","html":"Yes, boxes of chocolates are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">common gifts</strong> in my country. People give them for birthdays and celebrations such as Valentine&#x27;s Day, Easter or Christmas."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-006-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-006-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-006-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-006-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Clothes for different situations","prompt":"Are clothes and clothing fashions important to you?\nWhat different clothes do you wear for different situations?\nDo you wear different styles of clothes now compared to 10 years ago?\nDo you think the clothes we wear say something about who we are?","answer":"Q: Are clothes and clothing fashions important to you?\nA: No, clothes and fashion aren't really important to me. I tend to wear clothes that are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comfortable and practical</strong> rather than fashionable.\n\nQ: What different clothes do you wear for different situations?\nA: Well, I have to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dress quite formally</strong> for work, so I wear a shirt and trousers. At home I prefer to wear jeans and a T-shirt, and on special occasions I might wear a suit.\n\nQ: Do you wear different styles of clothes now compared to 10 years ago?\nA: No, not really, because I don't follow fashion. I've dressed <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in a similar way</strong> for the last 10 years.\n\nQ: Do you think the clothes we wear say something about who we are?\nA: Yes, they probably do. Some people are really careful about what they wear because they want to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">be seen as</strong> stylish. Other people wear clothes that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">show wealth or status</strong>, such as clothes by famous designers.","topic":"Clothes","phrases":[{"en":"comfortable and practical","context":"No, clothes and fashion aren't really important to me. I tend to wear clothes that are comfortable and practical rather than fashionable."},{"en":"dress quite formally","context":"Well, I have to dress quite formally for work, so I wear a shirt and trousers. At home I prefer to wear jeans and a T-shirt, and on special occasions I might wear a suit."},{"en":"in a similar way","context":"No, not really, because I don't follow fashion. I've dressed in a similar way for the last 10 years."},{"en":"be seen as","context":"Yes, they probably do. Some people are really careful about what they wear because they want to be seen as stylish. Other people wear clothes that show wealth or status, such as clothes by famous designers."},{"en":"show wealth or status","context":"Yes, they probably do. Some people are really careful about what they wear because they want to be seen as stylish. Other people wear clothes that show wealth or status, such as clothes by famous designers."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Clothes","id":"speaking-part-1-008","_displayNo":"#007","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-008-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-008-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-008-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-008-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Not really. I care more about feeling comfortable than looking fashionable. I usually choose clothes that are easy to wear and suitable for what I need to do.","phrases":[{"en":"suitable for","meaning":"Right or useful for a particular situation."}],"question":"Are clothes and clothing fashions important to you?","html":"Not really. I care more about feeling comfortable than looking fashionable. I usually choose clothes that are easy to wear and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suitable for</strong> what I need to do."},{"text":"I wear a shirt and trousers at work because I need to look formal. At home, I prefer jeans and a T-shirt. For a special event, I might wear a suit.","phrases":[{"en":"a special event","meaning":"An important occasion that is different from an ordinary day."}],"question":"What different clothes do you wear for different situations?","html":"I wear a shirt and trousers at work because I need to look formal. At home, I prefer jeans and a T-shirt. For <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a special event</strong>, I might wear a suit."},{"text":"No, my style has stayed much the same. I don't follow fashion closely, so I have worn similar kinds of clothes for about ten years.","phrases":[{"en":"stayed much the same","meaning":"Changed very little."}],"question":"Do you wear different styles of clothes now compared to 10 years ago?","html":"No, my style has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stayed much the same</strong>. I don&#x27;t follow fashion closely, so I have worn similar kinds of clothes for about ten years."},{"text":"Yes, clothes can tell us something about people. Some choose them to look fashionable, while others buy famous brands to show that they have money or a high social position.","phrases":[{"en":"famous brands","meaning":"Product names that many people recognise."}],"question":"Do you think the clothes we wear say something about who we are?","html":"Yes, clothes can tell us something about people. Some choose them to look fashionable, while others buy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">famous brands</strong> to show that they have money or a high social position."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-008-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-008-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-008-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-008-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Clothes you dislike, and how your style has changed","prompt":"How important are clothes and fashion to you?\nWhat kind of clothes do you dislike and why?\nHow different are the clothes you wear now from those you wore 10 years ago?\nWhat do you think the clothes we wear say about us?","answer":"Q: How important are clothes and fashion to you?\nA: Clothes matter to me mainly in terms of comfort and appropriateness. I like to look presentable, but I don't follow every trend, because style is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a matter of personal choice</strong>.\n\nQ: What kind of clothes do you dislike and why?\nA: I dislike clothes that are overly tight, heavily branded or difficult to care for. Even if something looks fashionable, I won't buy it unless it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comfortable enough for daily wear</strong>.\n\nQ: How different are the clothes you wear now from those you wore 10 years ago?\nA: My clothes are simpler and more practical now. Ten years ago I experimented with brighter colours, but these days I prefer neutral items that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work in several settings</strong>.\n\nQ: What do you think the clothes we wear say about us?\nA: Clothing can suggest someone's profession, taste or attitude to formality, but it's not a complete picture of their character. People sometimes dress to meet expectations rather than to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">express their real personality</strong>.","topic":"Clothes","phrases":[{"en":"a matter of personal choice","context":"Clothes matter to me mainly in terms of comfort and appropriateness. I like to look presentable, but I don't follow every trend, because style is a matter of personal choice."},{"en":"comfortable enough for daily wear","context":"I dislike clothes that are overly tight, heavily branded or difficult to care for. Even if something looks fashionable, I won't buy it unless it's comfortable enough for daily wear."},{"en":"work in several settings","context":"My clothes are simpler and more practical now. Ten years ago I experimented with brighter colours, but these days I prefer neutral items that work in several settings."},{"en":"express their real personality","context":"Clothing can suggest someone's profession, taste or attitude to formality, but it's not a complete picture of their character. People sometimes dress to meet expectations rather than to express their real personality."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Clothes","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-009","_displayNo":"#008","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-009-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-009-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-009-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-009-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Clothes matter because I want to feel comfortable and dress properly for each situation. I like looking neat, but I don't follow every new style. People should choose what suits them.","phrases":[{"en":"what suits them","meaning":"What is right for their needs, taste or appearance."}],"question":"How important are clothes and fashion to you?","html":"Clothes matter because I want to feel comfortable and dress properly for each situation. I like looking neat, but I don&#x27;t follow every new style. People should choose <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what suits them</strong>."},{"text":"I don't like very tight clothes, large brand names or clothes that are hard to wash. Even if they look fashionable, I won't buy them unless they feel comfortable for daily use.","phrases":[{"en":"daily use","meaning":"Regular use in normal everyday situations."}],"question":"What kind of clothes do you dislike and why?","html":"I don&#x27;t like very tight clothes, large brand names or clothes that are hard to wash. Even if they look fashionable, I won&#x27;t buy them unless they feel comfortable for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">daily use</strong>."},{"text":"My clothes are simpler now. Ten years ago, I tried brighter colours, but these days I prefer plain colours that I can wear in different places and situations.","phrases":[{"en":"these days","meaning":"During the present period of your life."}],"question":"How different are the clothes you wear now from those you wore 10 years ago?","html":"My clothes are simpler now. Ten years ago, I tried brighter colours, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">these days</strong> I prefer plain colours that I can wear in different places and situations."},{"text":"Clothes may suggest someone's job or taste, but they do not tell us everything about their character. Sometimes people dress a certain way because others expect them to.","phrases":[{"en":"a certain way","meaning":"A particular manner or style."}],"question":"What do you think the clothes we wear say about us?","html":"Clothes may suggest someone&#x27;s job or taste, but they do not tell us everything about their character. Sometimes people dress <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a certain way</strong> because others expect them to."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-009-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-009-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-009-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-009-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"What people in your country wear","prompt":"What types of clothes do you like to wear?\nWhat kind of clothes do people in your country usually wear?\nHow important is fashion to you?\nWhat kind of clothes do you dislike?","answer":"Q: What types of clothes do you like to wear?\nA: I usually wear simple shirts, trousers and comfortable shoes. I prefer well-made basics because they're easy to combine and don't <strong class=\"key-phrase\">go out of style quickly</strong>.\n\nQ: What kind of clothes do people in your country usually wear?\nA: Most people wear casual Western-style clothes in daily life, while traditional dress is reserved for weddings, festivals and formal occasions. Office workers tend to choose smart clothes that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strike a balance between</strong> comfort and professionalism.\n\nQ: How important is fashion to you?\nA: Fashion doesn't play a big part in my life. I want to look neat and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">appropriate for the occasion</strong>, but comfort, durability and fit matter more to me than following every seasonal trend.\n\nQ: What kind of clothes do you dislike?\nA: I avoid clothes that are uncomfortable, covered in large logos or impractical for the weather. I prefer an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">understated appearance</strong> and clothes I can wear again and again.","topic":"Clothes","phrases":[{"en":"go out of style quickly","context":"I usually wear simple shirts, trousers and comfortable shoes. I prefer well-made basics because they're easy to combine and don't go out of style quickly."},{"en":"strike a balance between","context":"Most people wear casual Western-style clothes in daily life, while traditional dress is reserved for weddings, festivals and formal occasions. Office workers tend to choose smart clothes that strike a balance between comfort and professionalism."},{"en":"appropriate for the occasion","context":"Fashion doesn't play a big part in my life. I want to look neat and appropriate for the occasion, but comfort, durability and fit matter more to me than following every seasonal trend."},{"en":"understated appearance","context":"I avoid clothes that are uncomfortable, covered in large logos or impractical for the weather. I prefer an understated appearance and clothes I can wear again and again."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Clothes","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-010","_displayNo":"#009","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-010-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-010-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-010-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-010-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I like simple shirts, trousers and comfortable shoes. I choose good-quality basic clothes because they are easy to wear together and stay useful even when fashion changes.","phrases":[{"en":"good-quality","meaning":"Made well and likely to last."}],"question":"What types of clothes do you like to wear?","html":"I like simple shirts, trousers and comfortable shoes. I choose <strong class=\"key-phrase\">good-quality</strong> basic clothes because they are easy to wear together and stay useful even when fashion changes."},{"text":"People usually wear casual Western-style clothes every day. Traditional clothes are more common at weddings and festivals. Office workers often choose clothes that look smart but still feel comfortable.","phrases":[{"en":"look smart","meaning":"Look neat and properly dressed."}],"question":"What kind of clothes do people in your country usually wear?","html":"People usually wear casual Western-style clothes every day. Traditional clothes are more common at weddings and festivals. Office workers often choose clothes that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look smart</strong> but still feel comfortable."},{"text":"Fashion isn't very important to me. I want to look tidy and dress well for the occasion, but I care more about comfort, fit and how long clothes last.","phrases":[{"en":"for the occasion","meaning":"In a way that suits a particular event."}],"question":"How important is fashion to you?","html":"Fashion isn&#x27;t very important to me. I want to look tidy and dress well <strong class=\"key-phrase\">for the occasion</strong>, but I care more about comfort, fit and how long clothes last."},{"text":"I dislike uncomfortable clothes, big logos and clothes that do not suit the weather. I prefer a simple look and things that I can wear many times.","phrases":[{"en":"suit the weather","meaning":"Be appropriate for how hot, cold or wet it is."}],"question":"What kind of clothes do you dislike?","html":"I dislike uncomfortable clothes, big logos and clothes that do not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suit the weather</strong>. I prefer a simple look and things that I can wear many times."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-010-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-010-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-010-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-010-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Collecting things as a hobby","prompt":"Have you ever collected anything as a hobby?\nWhat kinds of thing do people often like collecting?\nWhy do you think people collect certain objects?\nIs there anything that you would like to collect in the future?","answer":"Q: Have you ever collected anything as a hobby?\nA: Yes. When I was younger I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">used to collect</strong> keyrings. I remember that my favourite was a Lego man keyring that someone bought me as a present.\n\nQ: What kinds of thing do people often like collecting?\nA: Some people collect cheap items like stamps, keyrings or toys. Others collect <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expensive antiques</strong>, watches, jewellery or even cars.\n\nQ: Why do you think people collect certain objects?\nA: Maybe they like the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">constant search</strong> for a new item, or they want to remember past experiences. Others might like the feeling of organising and presenting what they own.\n\nQ: Is there anything that you would like to collect in the future?\nA: Yes. If I could afford it, I'd love to have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a collection of guitars</strong> in different colours, shapes and sizes!","topic":"Collecting","phrases":[{"en":"used to collect","context":"Yes. When I was younger I used to collect keyrings. I remember that my favourite was a Lego man keyring that someone bought me as a present."},{"en":"expensive antiques","context":"Some people collect cheap items like stamps, keyrings or toys. Others collect expensive antiques, watches, jewellery or even cars."},{"en":"constant search","context":"Maybe they like the constant search for a new item, or they want to remember past experiences. Others might like the feeling of organising and presenting what they own."},{"en":"a collection of guitars","context":"Yes. If I could afford it, I'd love to have a collection of guitars in different colours, shapes and sizes!"}],"subtype":"","summary":"Collecting","id":"speaking-part-1-011","_displayNo":"#010","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-011-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-011-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-011-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-011-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, I collected keyrings when I was younger. My favourite was a little Lego man that someone gave me. I remember liking it more than the others in my collection.","phrases":[{"en":"my collection","meaning":"The group of similar objects I have gathered."}],"question":"Have you ever collected anything as a hobby?","html":"Yes, I collected keyrings when I was younger. My favourite was a little Lego man that someone gave me. I remember liking it more than the others in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">my collection</strong>."},{"text":"People collect many things, from inexpensive stamps, keyrings and toys to valuable old objects. Some also collect watches, jewellery or even cars if they have enough money.","phrases":[{"en":"valuable","meaning":"Worth a lot of money or important to someone."}],"question":"What kinds of thing do people often like collecting?","html":"People collect many things, from inexpensive stamps, keyrings and toys to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">valuable</strong> old objects. Some also collect watches, jewellery or even cars if they have enough money."},{"text":"Some people enjoy looking for the next object in their collection. Others collect things to remember experiences, or because they like arranging their objects and showing them to people.","phrases":[{"en":"showing them to people","meaning":"Letting other people see them."}],"question":"Why do you think people collect certain objects?","html":"Some people enjoy looking for the next object in their collection. Others collect things to remember experiences, or because they like arranging their objects and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">showing them to people</strong>."},{"text":"Yes, I'd like to collect guitars if I had enough money. It would be lovely to own several guitars with different colours, shapes and sizes.","phrases":[{"en":"if I had enough money","meaning":"On the condition that I could afford it."}],"question":"Is there anything that you would like to collect in the future?","html":"Yes, I&#x27;d like to collect guitars <strong class=\"key-phrase\">if I had enough money</strong>. It would be lovely to own several guitars with different colours, shapes and sizes."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-011-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-011-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-011-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-011-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Colours you like and how they affect mood","prompt":"What colours do you like?\nDo colours affect your mood?\nDo you wear bright clothes?","answer":"Q: What colours do you like?\nA: I'm particularly fond of blue because it feels calm and clean. It also works well in clothing and interior design without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drawing too much attention</strong>.\n\nQ: Do colours affect your mood?\nA: Yes, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to some extent</strong>. Soft, natural colours help me relax, but extremely bright colours can feel tiring when they cover a large space. The effect is subtle but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">noticeable over time</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you wear bright clothes?\nA: Only occasionally. I may wear one bright item, but I usually combine it with neutral colours so that the overall look remains <strong class=\"key-phrase\">simple and balanced</strong>.","topic":"Colours","phrases":[{"en":"drawing too much attention","context":"I'm particularly fond of blue because it feels calm and clean. It also works well in clothing and interior design without drawing too much attention."},{"en":"to some extent","context":"Yes, to some extent. Soft, natural colours help me relax, but extremely bright colours can feel tiring when they cover a large space. The effect is subtle but noticeable over time."},{"en":"noticeable over time","context":"Yes, to some extent. Soft, natural colours help me relax, but extremely bright colours can feel tiring when they cover a large space. The effect is subtle but noticeable over time."},{"en":"simple and balanced","context":"Only occasionally. I may wear one bright item, but I usually combine it with neutral colours so that the overall look remains simple and balanced."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Colours","id":"speaking-part-1-012","_displayNo":"#011","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-012-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-012-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-012-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I especially like blue because it makes me think of calm, clean places. It also looks good in clothes and rooms without being too bright or noticeable.","phrases":[{"en":"looks good","meaning":"Has an attractive appearance."}],"question":"What colours do you like?","html":"I especially like blue because it makes me think of calm, clean places. It also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">looks good</strong> in clothes and rooms without being too bright or noticeable."},{"text":"Yes, a little. Soft, natural colours help me feel relaxed, but a large area of very bright colour can make me tired. The effect is small, but I notice it over time.","phrases":[{"en":"over time","meaning":"Gradually, as time passes."}],"question":"Do colours affect your mood?","html":"Yes, a little. Soft, natural colours help me feel relaxed, but a large area of very bright colour can make me tired. The effect is small, but I notice it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">over time</strong>."},{"text":"Sometimes, but not very often. I might wear one bright piece of clothing with plain colours. That way, my clothes still look simple and go well together.","phrases":[{"en":"go well together","meaning":"Look or work nicely when combined."}],"question":"Do you wear bright clothes?","html":"Sometimes, but not very often. I might wear one bright piece of clothing with plain colours. That way, my clothes still look simple and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">go well together</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-012-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-012-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-012-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Your favourite colour, then and now","prompt":"What is your favourite colour and why?\nDo you like the same colours now as you did when you were younger and why?\nWhat can you learn about people from the colours they like?\nDo colours have special meaning in your culture?","answer":"Q: What is your favourite colour and why?\nA: Navy blue is my favourite colour because it looks calm and professional, and it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easy to combine with</strong> other colours. I also associate it with the sea and open space.\n\nQ: Do you like the same colours now as you did when you were younger and why?\nA: Not entirely. As a child I preferred bright red and orange, but I now go for blue, grey and dark green. My taste has become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more understated</strong> as I've grown older.\n\nQ: What can you learn about people from the colours they like?\nA: Colour preferences might give a small clue about whether someone likes bold or quieter styles, but I wouldn't read too much into it. Culture, fashion and practical needs can influence choices just as much as personality, so it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not a reliable test</strong>.\n\nQ: Do colours have special meaning in your culture?\nA: Yes, definitely. Red's often linked to luck and celebrations, while white can be linked to mourning, so a colour's meaning really can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depend on the culture</strong>.","topic":"Colours","phrases":[{"en":"easy to combine with","context":"Navy blue is my favourite colour because it looks calm and professional, and it's easy to combine with other colours. I also associate it with the sea and open space."},{"en":"more understated","context":"Not entirely. As a child I preferred bright red and orange, but I now go for blue, grey and dark green. My taste has become more understated as I've grown older."},{"en":"not a reliable test","context":"Colour preferences might give a small clue about whether someone likes bold or quieter styles, but I wouldn't read too much into it. Culture, fashion and practical needs can influence choices just as much as personality, so it's not a reliable test."},{"en":"depend on the culture","context":"Yes, definitely. Red's often linked to luck and celebrations, while white can be linked to mourning, so a colour's meaning really can depend on the culture."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Colours","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-013","_displayNo":"#012","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-013-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-013-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-013-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-013-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"My favourite colour is navy blue because it looks calm and smart. It matches many other colours, and it also makes me think of the sea and wide, open places.","phrases":[{"en":"matches","meaning":"Looks good together with something else."}],"question":"What is your favourite colour and why?","html":"My favourite colour is navy blue because it looks calm and smart. It <strong class=\"key-phrase\">matches</strong> many other colours, and it also makes me think of the sea and wide, open places."},{"text":"Not exactly. I liked bright red and orange as a child. Now I prefer blue, grey and dark green because I enjoy quieter, less noticeable colours.","phrases":[{"en":"less noticeable","meaning":"Less easy to see or attract attention."}],"question":"Do you like the same colours now as you did when you were younger and why?","html":"Not exactly. I liked bright red and orange as a child. Now I prefer blue, grey and dark green because I enjoy quieter, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less noticeable</strong> colours."},{"text":"Colours may show whether someone likes a bright or quiet style, but they do not tell us much about personality. Culture, fashion and practical reasons also affect what people choose.","phrases":[{"en":"practical reasons","meaning":"Reasons connected with usefulness or everyday needs."}],"question":"What can you learn about people from the colours they like?","html":"Colours may show whether someone likes a bright or quiet style, but they do not tell us much about personality. Culture, fashion and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical reasons</strong> also affect what people choose."},{"text":"Yes. In my culture, red is often used for luck and celebrations, while white can be connected with death and sadness. The same colour can mean different things in different cultures.","phrases":[{"en":"connected with","meaning":"Related to something."}],"question":"Do colours have special meaning in your culture?","html":"Yes. In my culture, red is often used for luck and celebrations, while white can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">connected with</strong> death and sadness. The same colour can mean different things in different cultures."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-013-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-013-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-013-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-013-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Keeping in touch: phone, email and letters","prompt":"How do you usually keep in touch with members of your family?\nDo you prefer to speak to people by phone or by writing emails?\nDo you ever write letters by hand?\nIs there anything you dislike about mobile phones?","answer":"Q: How do you usually keep in touch with members of your family?\nA: I mostly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep in touch with</strong> my family by phone, and I've started using the Internet to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make video calls</strong>. I also try to visit members of my family in person as often as I can.\n\nQ: Do you prefer to speak to people by phone or by writing emails?\nA: It depends on the situation. I use email and text messages to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">communicate with friends</strong>, but my parents prefer it if I phone them.\n\nQ: Do you ever write letters by hand?\nA: No, I don't <strong class=\"key-phrase\">write letters by hand</strong> because it's so much easier to send an email that will arrive immediately.\n\nQ: Is there anything you dislike about mobile phones?\nA: Well, I really don't like it when someone's phone <strong class=\"key-phrase\">goes off</strong> in the cinema or during a lesson, and I'm not keen on overhearing other people's conversations in public either.","topic":"Communication","phrases":[{"en":"keep in touch with","context":"How do you usually keep in touch with members of your family?"},{"en":"make video calls","context":"I mostly keep in touch with my family by phone, and I've started using the Internet to make video calls. I also try to visit members of my family in person as often as I can."},{"en":"communicate with friends","context":"It depends on the situation. I use email and text messages to communicate with friends, but my parents prefer it if I phone them."},{"en":"write letters by hand","context":"Do you ever write letters by hand?"},{"en":"goes off","context":"Well, I really don't like it when someone's phone goes off in the cinema or during a lesson, and I'm not keen on overhearing other people's conversations in public either."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Communication","id":"speaking-part-1-014","_displayNo":"#013","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-014-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-014-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-014-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-014-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I usually speak to my family on the phone, and I have also started making video calls. I try to visit them whenever I can because meeting in person is important too.","phrases":[{"en":"in person","meaning":"Being physically together rather than using a phone or computer."}],"question":"How do you usually keep in touch with members of your family?","html":"I usually speak to my family on the phone, and I have also started making video calls. I try to visit them whenever I can because meeting <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in person</strong> is important too."},{"text":"It depends on who I'm speaking to. I often email or text my friends, but my parents prefer a phone call, so I usually call them.","phrases":[{"en":"depends on","meaning":"Is decided or affected by something."}],"question":"Do you prefer to speak to people by phone or by writing emails?","html":"It <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depends on</strong> who I&#x27;m speaking to. I often email or text my friends, but my parents prefer a phone call, so I usually call them."},{"text":"No, I don't usually write letters by hand. Sending an email is much easier, and the other person receives it straight away, so I find it more useful.","phrases":[{"en":"straight away","meaning":"Immediately, without waiting."}],"question":"Do you ever write letters by hand?","html":"No, I don&#x27;t usually write letters by hand. Sending an email is much easier, and the other person receives it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">straight away</strong>, so I find it more useful."},{"text":"I dislike phones ringing during films or lessons because they disturb other people. I also don't enjoy hearing someone's private conversation when they talk loudly on their phone in public.","phrases":[{"en":"disturb","meaning":"Interrupt someone or stop them feeling comfortable or focused."}],"question":"Is there anything you dislike about mobile phones?","html":"I dislike phones ringing during films or lessons because they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disturb</strong> other people. I also don&#x27;t enjoy hearing someone&#x27;s private conversation when they talk loudly on their phone in public."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-014-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-014-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-014-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-014-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Using computers and their effect on health","prompt":"When did you first use a computer?\nDo you think computers are bad for your health?\nWhich computer software do you use most?\nWhat did you mainly use your first computer for?","answer":"Q: When did you first use a computer?\nA: I first used a desktop computer at primary school. We learned basic typing and simple drawing programs, and I remember being fascinated by how quickly the machine <strong class=\"key-phrase\">responded to instructions</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you think computers are bad for your health?\nA: They can be harmful if people sit for hours with poor posture or stare at the screen without breaks. The problem usually isn't the computer itself, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the way it's used</strong>.\n\nQ: Which computer software do you use most?\nA: I use web browsers, word-processing software and spreadsheets most often. They support almost every part of my work, from lesson preparation to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">organising information efficiently</strong>.\n\nQ: What did you mainly use your first computer for?\nA: My first computer was mainly for school assignments, basic research and educational games. Over time it became an essential tool for communication and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">independent learning</strong>.","topic":"Computers","phrases":[{"en":"responded to instructions","context":"I first used a desktop computer at primary school. We learned basic typing and simple drawing programs, and I remember being fascinated by how quickly the machine responded to instructions."},{"en":"the way it's used","context":"They can be harmful if people sit for hours with poor posture or stare at the screen without breaks. The problem usually isn't the computer itself, but the way it's used."},{"en":"organising information efficiently","context":"I use web browsers, word-processing software and spreadsheets most often. They support almost every part of my work, from lesson preparation to organising information efficiently."},{"en":"independent learning","context":"My first computer was mainly for school assignments, basic research and educational games. Over time it became an essential tool for communication and independent learning."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Computer","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-015","_displayNo":"#014","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-015-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-015-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-015-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-015-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I first used a desktop computer at primary school. We practised typing and used simple drawing programs. I was amazed that it could do things so quickly when we gave it instructions.","phrases":[{"en":"amazed","meaning":"Very surprised by something impressive."}],"question":"When did you first use a computer?","html":"I first used a desktop computer at primary school. We practised typing and used simple drawing programs. I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">amazed</strong> that it could do things so quickly when we gave it instructions."},{"text":"They can be bad for your health if you sit badly or use them for hours without a break. I think the main issue is how we use them.","phrases":[{"en":"without a break","meaning":"Continuously, without stopping to rest."}],"question":"Do you think computers are bad for your health?","html":"They can be bad for your health if you sit badly or use them for hours <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without a break</strong>. I think the main issue is how we use them."},{"text":"I mainly use internet browsers, programs for writing documents and spreadsheets. They help with most of my work, including preparing lessons and keeping information organised.","phrases":[{"en":"keeping information organised","meaning":"Arranging facts or records so they are easy to use."}],"question":"Which computer software do you use most?","html":"I mainly use internet browsers, programs for writing documents and spreadsheets. They help with most of my work, including preparing lessons and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeping information organised</strong>."},{"text":"I mainly used it for homework, finding information and educational games. Later, it became important for talking to people and learning new things on my own.","phrases":[{"en":"on my own","meaning":"Without help from other people."}],"question":"What did you mainly use your first computer for?","html":"I mainly used it for homework, finding information and educational games. Later, it became important for talking to people and learning new things <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on my own</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-015-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-015-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-015-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-015-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Cooking at home and learning to cook","prompt":"Do you like cooking?\nWho did the cooking in your family when you were a child?\nDo you think that it's important to know how to cook well?\nDo you think that children should be taught cookery at school?","answer":"Q: Do you like cooking?\nA: Sometimes. I like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">preparing a special meal</strong> for family or friends who visit, but everyday cooking's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a bit of a chore</strong> — it has to be done, but it's not really fun.\n\nQ: Who did the cooking in your family when you were a child?\nA: My mother almost always did the cooking when I was young. I don't think she trusted my father to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make a nice meal</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you think that it's important to know how to cook well?\nA: I'm not sure whether it's important to cook well, but I do think that everyone should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">know the basics</strong>. It definitely isn't healthy to rely on pre-prepared meals or fast food.\n\nQ: Do you think that children should be taught cookery at school?\nA: Yes, that's probably a good idea. If children learned to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cook from scratch</strong> — even just a few basic, healthy meals — that would definitely be useful for later in life.","topic":"Cooking","phrases":[{"en":"preparing a special meal","context":"Sometimes. I like preparing a special meal for family or friends who visit, but everyday cooking's a bit of a chore — it has to be done, but it's not really fun."},{"en":"a bit of a chore","context":"Sometimes. I like preparing a special meal for family or friends who visit, but everyday cooking's a bit of a chore — it has to be done, but it's not really fun."},{"en":"make a nice meal","context":"My mother almost always did the cooking when I was young. I don't think she trusted my father to make a nice meal."},{"en":"know the basics","context":"I'm not sure whether it's important to cook well, but I do think that everyone should know the basics. It definitely isn't healthy to rely on pre-prepared meals or fast food."},{"en":"cook from scratch","context":"Yes, that's probably a good idea. If children learned to cook from scratch — even just a few basic, healthy meals — that would definitely be useful for later in life."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Cooking","id":"speaking-part-1-016","_displayNo":"#015","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-016-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-016-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-016-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-016-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Sometimes. I enjoy making a special meal when family or friends visit. However, cooking every day feels more like a job I have to do than something fun.","phrases":[{"en":"have to do","meaning":"Need to do because it is necessary."}],"question":"Do you like cooking?","html":"Sometimes. I enjoy making a special meal when family or friends visit. However, cooking every day feels more like a job I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have to do</strong> than something fun."},{"text":"My mother did most of the cooking when I was a child. I don't think she believed my father could prepare a good meal, so she usually did it herself.","phrases":[{"en":"most of","meaning":"The largest part of something."}],"question":"Who did the cooking in your family when you were a child?","html":"My mother did <strong class=\"key-phrase\">most of</strong> the cooking when I was a child. I don&#x27;t think she believed my father could prepare a good meal, so she usually did it herself."},{"text":"I think everyone should be able to cook a few simple meals, even if they are not excellent cooks. Eating ready-made meals or fast food all the time is not healthy.","phrases":[{"en":"ready-made meals","meaning":"Meals prepared in advance that usually only need heating."}],"question":"Do you think that it's important to know how to cook well?","html":"I think everyone should be able to cook a few simple meals, even if they are not excellent cooks. Eating <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ready-made meals</strong> or fast food all the time is not healthy."},{"text":"Yes, I think that would be useful. Children could learn to make a few simple, healthy meals using basic ingredients. Those skills would help them when they are older.","phrases":[{"en":"basic ingredients","meaning":"The main foods used to prepare a dish."}],"question":"Do you think that children should be taught cookery at school?","html":"Yes, I think that would be useful. Children could learn to make a few simple, healthy meals using <strong class=\"key-phrase\">basic ingredients</strong>. Those skills would help them when they are older."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-016-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-016-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-016-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-016-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"A typical weekday and a typical weekend","prompt":"What would you like to change in your daily routine?\nAre all your days the same?\nTell me about a typical weekday and a typical weekend.\nWhat is the balance of work/ study and free time in your normal days?","answer":"Q: What would you like to change in your daily routine?\nA: I'd like to spend less time looking at screens in the evening. Reading or going for a short walk instead would help me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wind down properly</strong> before bed.\n\nQ: Are all your days the same?\nA: Weekdays follow a fairly stable pattern, but weekends are much more flexible. I still keep a few fixed habits, such as exercise and meal times, because they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give my day structure</strong>.\n\nQ: Tell me about a typical weekday and a typical weekend.\nA: On weekdays I work, exercise and prepare for the following day. At weekends I sleep a little later, meet friends or handle personal errands, so the pace is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far less demanding</strong>.\n\nQ: What is the balance of work/ study and free time in your normal days?\nA: Work takes up most of my weekdays, but I protect time for exercise, meals and family. The balance isn't perfect, but I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make a conscious effort</strong> to maintain <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sustainable routine</strong>.","topic":"Daily Routine","phrases":[{"en":"wind down properly","context":"I'd like to spend less time looking at screens in the evening. Reading or going for a short walk instead would help me wind down properly before bed."},{"en":"give my day structure","context":"Weekdays follow a fairly stable pattern, but weekends are much more flexible. I still keep a few fixed habits, such as exercise and meal times, because they give my day structure."},{"en":"far less demanding","context":"On weekdays I work, exercise and prepare for the following day. At weekends I sleep a little later, meet friends or handle personal errands, so the pace is far less demanding."},{"en":"make a conscious effort","context":"Work takes up most of my weekdays, but I protect time for exercise, meals and family. The balance isn't perfect, but I make a conscious effort to maintain a sustainable routine."},{"en":"a sustainable routine","context":"Work takes up most of my weekdays, but I protect time for exercise, meals and family. The balance isn't perfect, but I make a conscious effort to maintain a sustainable routine."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Daily Routine","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-017","_displayNo":"#016","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-017-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-017-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-017-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-017-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I'd like to use screens less in the evening. I could read or take a short walk instead, which would help me relax before going to bed.","phrases":[{"en":"relax","meaning":"Become calmer and less worried or tense."}],"question":"What would you like to change in your daily routine?","html":"I&#x27;d like to use screens less in the evening. I could read or take a short walk instead, which would help me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relax</strong> before going to bed."},{"text":"No. My weekdays are quite similar, but I have more freedom at weekends. I still keep regular exercise and meal times because they help me organise my day.","phrases":[{"en":"regular","meaning":"Happening at similar times or in a usual pattern."}],"question":"Are all your days the same?","html":"No. My weekdays are quite similar, but I have more freedom at weekends. I still keep <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regular</strong> exercise and meal times because they help me organise my day."},{"text":"On weekdays, I work, exercise and get ready for the next day. At weekends, I get up a little later, meet friends or do personal tasks, so life feels less busy.","phrases":[{"en":"get ready","meaning":"Prepare yourself for something."}],"question":"Tell me about a typical weekday and a typical weekend.","html":"On weekdays, I work, exercise and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get ready</strong> for the next day. At weekends, I get up a little later, meet friends or do personal tasks, so life feels less busy."},{"text":"Work fills most of my weekdays, but I still make time for exercise, meals and family. The balance could be better, although I try to keep a routine I can manage.","phrases":[{"en":"make time for","meaning":"Arrange to have enough time to do something."}],"question":"What is the balance of work/ study and free time in your normal days?","html":"Work fills most of my weekdays, but I still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make time for</strong> exercise, meals and family. The balance could be better, although I try to keep a routine I can manage."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-017-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-017-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-017-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-017-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Dancing, and whether traditional dance will last","prompt":"Do you like dancing?\nHas anyone ever taught you to dance?\nDo you think that traditional dancing will be popular in the future?","answer":"Q: Do you like dancing?\nA: No, I don't like dancing. I'm not a big music fan, and dancing just makes me feel <strong class=\"key-phrase\">uncomfortable and self-conscious</strong>.\n\nQ: Has anyone ever taught you to dance?\nA: No, I've never been interested in learning to dance, so I've never <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taken any lessons</strong> or asked anyone to show me how to do it.\n\nQ: Do you think that traditional dancing will be popular in the future?\nA: I'm afraid I have no idea, because I don't <strong class=\"key-phrase\">follow what's happening</strong> in the dancing world! I suppose it will always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">be popular with</strong> some people.","topic":"Dancing","phrases":[{"en":"uncomfortable and self-conscious","context":"No, I don't like dancing. I'm not a big music fan, and dancing just makes me feel uncomfortable and self-conscious."},{"en":"taken any lessons","context":"No, I've never been interested in learning to dance, so I've never taken any lessons or asked anyone to show me how to do it."},{"en":"follow what's happening","context":"I'm afraid I have no idea, because I don't follow what's happening in the dancing world! I suppose it will always be popular with some people."},{"en":"be popular with","context":"I'm afraid I have no idea, because I don't follow what's happening in the dancing world! I suppose it will always be popular with some people."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Dancing","id":"speaking-part-1-018","_displayNo":"#017","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-018-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-018-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-018-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"No, I don't really enjoy dancing. I'm not very interested in music, and I feel uncomfortable when I dance because I worry about how I look.","phrases":[{"en":"worry about","meaning":"Feel nervous or concerned about something."}],"question":"Do you like dancing?","html":"No, I don&#x27;t really enjoy dancing. I&#x27;m not very interested in music, and I feel uncomfortable when I dance because I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worry about</strong> how I look."},{"text":"No, nobody has taught me. I've never really wanted to learn, so I haven't taken classes or asked anyone to teach me any dance steps.","phrases":[{"en":"dance steps","meaning":"The movements people make when dancing."}],"question":"Has anyone ever taught you to dance?","html":"No, nobody has taught me. I&#x27;ve never really wanted to learn, so I haven&#x27;t taken classes or asked anyone to teach me any <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dance steps</strong>."},{"text":"I'm not sure because I don't know much about dance. However, I think some people will continue to enjoy traditional dancing, even if it isn't popular with everyone.","phrases":[{"en":"continue to","meaning":"Keep doing something."}],"question":"Do you think that traditional dancing will be popular in the future?","html":"I&#x27;m not sure because I don&#x27;t know much about dance. However, I think some people will <strong class=\"key-phrase\">continue to</strong> enjoy traditional dancing, even if it isn&#x27;t popular with everyone."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-018-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-018-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-018-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Learning to dance and traditional dance","prompt":"Do you enjoy dancing and why?\nHas anyone ever taught you to dance?\nTell me about a traditional dance in your country.\nDo you think that traditional dancing will be popular in the future?","answer":"Q: Do you enjoy dancing and why?\nA: I enjoy dancing at social events, although I wouldn't call myself skilled. It's enjoyable because the atmosphere is relaxed and people are more concerned with having fun than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">performing perfectly</strong>.\n\nQ: Has anyone ever taught you to dance?\nA: No one has taught me formally. I've picked up a few basic steps by watching friends and copying them, so my dancing is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">entirely informal</strong>.\n\nQ: Tell me about a traditional dance in your country.\nA: Vietnam has several traditional dances linked to festivals and regional cultures. They often use coordinated movements, colourful costumes and music to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tell a cultural story</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you think that traditional dancing will be popular in the future?\nA: I think it'll survive, especially if schools and cultural groups present it in an engaging way. It may not become an everyday hobby, but it remains <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worth preserving</strong>.","topic":"Dancing","phrases":[{"en":"performing perfectly","context":"I enjoy dancing at social events, although I wouldn't call myself skilled. It's enjoyable because the atmosphere is relaxed and people are more concerned with having fun than performing perfectly."},{"en":"entirely informal","context":"No one has taught me formally. I've picked up a few basic steps by watching friends and copying them, so my dancing is entirely informal."},{"en":"tell a cultural story","context":"Vietnam has several traditional dances linked to festivals and regional cultures. They often use coordinated movements, colourful costumes and music to tell a cultural story."},{"en":"worth preserving","context":"I think it'll survive, especially if schools and cultural groups present it in an engaging way. It may not become an everyday hobby, but it remains worth preserving."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Dancing","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-019","_displayNo":"#018","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-019-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-019-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-019-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-019-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, I enjoy dancing at social events, even though I'm not very good at it. People are usually relaxed and want to have fun rather than dance perfectly.","phrases":[{"en":"have fun","meaning":"Enjoy yourself."}],"question":"Do you enjoy dancing and why?","html":"Yes, I enjoy dancing at social events, even though I&#x27;m not very good at it. People are usually relaxed and want to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have fun</strong> rather than dance perfectly."},{"text":"I've never had proper dance lessons. I have learned a few easy steps by watching my friends and copying them, so I only dance in a casual way.","phrases":[{"en":"copying them","meaning":"Doing the same actions as other people."}],"question":"Has anyone ever taught you to dance?","html":"I&#x27;ve never had proper dance lessons. I have learned a few easy steps by watching my friends and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">copying them</strong>, so I only dance in a casual way."},{"text":"There are several traditional dances in Vietnam, often performed during festivals. Dancers move together, wear colourful clothes and use music and movement to tell stories about local culture.","phrases":[{"en":"local culture","meaning":"The traditions and way of life of people in a particular area."}],"question":"Tell me about a traditional dance in your country.","html":"There are several traditional dances in Vietnam, often performed during festivals. Dancers move together, wear colourful clothes and use music and movement to tell stories about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">local culture</strong>."},{"text":"Yes, I think people will keep doing it, especially if schools and cultural groups make it interesting. It may not become a common hobby, but it is worth keeping.","phrases":[{"en":"worth keeping","meaning":"Important or useful enough to preserve."}],"question":"Do you think that traditional dancing will be popular in the future?","html":"Yes, I think people will keep doing it, especially if schools and cultural groups make it interesting. It may not become a common hobby, but it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worth keeping</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-019-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-019-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-019-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-019-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Remembering dreams and what they teach","prompt":"Do you often remember your dreams?\nCan dreams teach us anything?\nDo you think dreams can come true?","answer":"Q: Do you often remember your dreams?\nA: I remember dreams only occasionally, usually when I wake up during the night. Most details disappear within minutes unless the dream is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unusually vivid</strong> or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">emotionally intense</strong>.\n\nQ: Can dreams teach us anything?\nA: They may reveal what has been occupying our attention, but I'd be cautious about treating them as predictions. At most, they can provide <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a useful prompt for reflection</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you think dreams can come true?\nA: Not in a supernatural sense. Occasionally a dream resembles a later event, but that's probably coincidence combined with selective memory rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">evidence of prediction</strong>.","topic":"Dreams","phrases":[{"en":"unusually vivid","context":"I remember dreams only occasionally, usually when I wake up during the night. Most details disappear within minutes unless the dream is unusually vivid or emotionally intense."},{"en":"emotionally intense","context":"I remember dreams only occasionally, usually when I wake up during the night. Most details disappear within minutes unless the dream is unusually vivid or emotionally intense."},{"en":"a useful prompt for reflection","context":"They may reveal what has been occupying our attention, but I'd be cautious about treating them as predictions. At most, they can provide a useful prompt for reflection."},{"en":"evidence of prediction","context":"Not in a supernatural sense. Occasionally a dream resembles a later event, but that's probably coincidence combined with selective memory rather than evidence of prediction."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Dreams","id":"speaking-part-1-020","_displayNo":"#019","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-020-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-020-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-020-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Only sometimes, usually if I wake up in the night. I forget most of the details quickly, unless the dream feels very clear or makes me feel something strongly.","phrases":[{"en":"the details","meaning":"The smaller pieces of information about something."}],"question":"Do you often remember your dreams?","html":"Only sometimes, usually if I wake up in the night. I forget most of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the details</strong> quickly, unless the dream feels very clear or makes me feel something strongly."},{"text":"Dreams may show what we have been thinking about. I wouldn't use them to predict the future, but they can encourage us to think about our feelings and experiences.","phrases":[{"en":"predict the future","meaning":"Say what will happen later."}],"question":"Can dreams teach us anything?","html":"Dreams may show what we have been thinking about. I wouldn&#x27;t use them to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">predict the future</strong>, but they can encourage us to think about our feelings and experiences."},{"text":"I don't think dreams can tell the future. A dream may sometimes be similar to a later event, but that is probably chance. We may simply remember the dreams that seem to match.","phrases":[{"en":"chance","meaning":"Happening without being planned or caused on purpose."}],"question":"Do you think dreams can come true?","html":"I don&#x27;t think dreams can tell the future. A dream may sometimes be similar to a later event, but that is probably <strong class=\"key-phrase\">chance</strong>. We may simply remember the dreams that seem to match."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-020-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-020-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-020-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"How often you dream, and nightmares","prompt":"How often do you have dreams?\nWhat do you dream about?\nWhy do some people have nightmares?\nIs dreaming important to our well-being and health? Why?","answer":"Q: How often do you have dreams?\nA: I probably dream every night, but I remember only one or two dreams a week. I usually recall them when they're <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unusually vivid</strong> or when I wake up before the dream has faded.\n\nQ: What do you dream about?\nA: My dreams often combine ordinary places with unrealistic events, such as being late for an exam in a building I've never seen. They seem to reflect fragments of recent experiences rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a hidden message</strong>.\n\nQ: Why do some people have nightmares?\nA: Nightmares can be triggered by stress, disturbed sleep or frightening experiences. When the brain is processing strong emotions, it may create threatening situations that feel <strong class=\"key-phrase\">surprisingly real</strong>.\n\nQ: Is dreaming important to our well-being and health? Why?\nA: Good-quality sleep is essential, but dreaming itself is still difficult to interpret. It may help the brain <strong class=\"key-phrase\">process memories and emotions</strong>, although its precise role remains <strong class=\"key-phrase\">open to interpretation</strong>.","topic":"Dreams","phrases":[{"en":"unusually vivid","context":"I probably dream every night, but I remember only one or two dreams a week. I usually recall them when they're unusually vivid or when I wake up before the dream has faded."},{"en":"a hidden message","context":"My dreams often combine ordinary places with unrealistic events, such as being late for an exam in a building I've never seen. They seem to reflect fragments of recent experiences rather than a hidden message."},{"en":"surprisingly real","context":"Nightmares can be triggered by stress, disturbed sleep or frightening experiences. When the brain is processing strong emotions, it may create threatening situations that feel surprisingly real."},{"en":"process memories and emotions","context":"Good-quality sleep is essential, but dreaming itself is still difficult to interpret. It may help the brain process memories and emotions, although its precise role remains open to interpretation."},{"en":"open to interpretation","context":"Good-quality sleep is essential, but dreaming itself is still difficult to interpret. It may help the brain process memories and emotions, although its precise role remains open to interpretation."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Dreams","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-021","_displayNo":"#020","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-021-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-021-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-021-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-021-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I probably dream every night, but I only remember one or two dreams each week. I usually remember them if they feel very clear or I wake up soon afterwards.","phrases":[{"en":"afterwards","meaning":"After something has happened."}],"question":"How often do you have dreams?","html":"I probably dream every night, but I only remember one or two dreams each week. I usually remember them if they feel very clear or I wake up soon <strong class=\"key-phrase\">afterwards</strong>."},{"text":"My dreams often mix normal places with strange events, like being late for an exam in an unfamiliar building. I think they come from recent experiences rather than secret messages.","phrases":[{"en":"unfamiliar","meaning":"Not known or recognised by you."}],"question":"What do you dream about?","html":"My dreams often mix normal places with strange events, like being late for an exam in an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unfamiliar</strong> building. I think they come from recent experiences rather than secret messages."},{"text":"People may have bad dreams when they feel stressed, sleep badly or have frightening experiences. Strong feelings can appear in dreams as scary situations that seem real.","phrases":[{"en":"stressed","meaning":"Feeling worried or under too much pressure."}],"question":"Why do some people have nightmares?","html":"People may have bad dreams when they feel <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stressed</strong>, sleep badly or have frightening experiences. Strong feelings can appear in dreams as scary situations that seem real."},{"text":"Good sleep is important for health, but we still do not fully understand dreaming. Dreams may help us deal with memories and feelings, although their exact purpose is not clear.","phrases":[{"en":"deal with","meaning":"Handle or respond to something."}],"question":"Is dreaming important to our well-being and health? Why?","html":"Good sleep is important for health, but we still do not fully understand dreaming. Dreams may help us <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deal with</strong> memories and feelings, although their exact purpose is not clear."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-021-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-021-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-021-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-021-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Writing by hand or on a computer","prompt":"At work or in your studies, do you often write things?\nHow often do you send emails?\nDo you prefer to write by hand or on a computer?\nDo you think computers might one day replace handwriting?","answer":"Q: At work or in your studies, do you often write things?\nA: Yes, I write a lot of emails <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as part of my job</strong>. I work for a company that has offices in several countries, so we use email to communicate.\n\nQ: How often do you send emails?\nA: I probably write between 5 and 10 work emails every day, and I send emails to friends or family <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a few times a week</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you prefer to write by hand or on a computer?\nA: It depends on what I'm doing. I prefer the computer for most things because it's faster and you can save or copy things, but I still like making notes or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">writing ideas down</strong> by hand.\n\nQ: Do you think computers might one day replace handwriting?\nA: No, I think we'll always write by hand as well. I think it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an important skill</strong>, and schools will continue to teach children to write by hand before they learn to type.","topic":"Emails & Handwriting","phrases":[{"en":"as part of my job","context":"Yes, I write a lot of emails as part of my job. I work for a company that has offices in several countries, so we use email to communicate."},{"en":"a few times a week","context":"I probably write between 5 and 10 work emails every day, and I send emails to friends or family a few times a week."},{"en":"writing ideas down","context":"It depends on what I'm doing. I prefer the computer for most things because it's faster and you can save or copy things, but I still like making notes or writing ideas down by hand."},{"en":"an important skill","context":"No, I think we'll always write by hand as well. I think it's an important skill, and schools will continue to teach children to write by hand before they learn to type."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Emails And Handwriting","id":"speaking-part-1-022","_displayNo":"#021","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-022-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-022-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-022-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-022-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, I write many emails for work. My company has offices in different countries, so email is an important way for us to exchange information and stay in contact.","phrases":[{"en":"stay in contact","meaning":"Continue communicating with someone."}],"question":"At work or in your studies, do you often write things?","html":"Yes, I write many emails for work. My company has offices in different countries, so email is an important way for us to exchange information and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay in contact</strong>."},{"text":"I write about five to ten emails for work each day. I also email friends or family a few times a week, although work makes up most of my email use.","phrases":[{"en":"a few times a week","meaning":"On several occasions during a week."}],"question":"How often do you send emails?","html":"I write about five to ten emails for work each day. I also email friends or family <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a few times a week</strong>, although work makes up most of my email use."},{"text":"I usually prefer a computer because typing is faster and I can save or copy my work easily. However, I still like using a pen for short notes and ideas.","phrases":[{"en":"short notes","meaning":"Brief pieces of writing used to remember information."}],"question":"Do you prefer to write by hand or on a computer?","html":"I usually prefer a computer because typing is faster and I can save or copy my work easily. However, I still like using a pen for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">short notes</strong> and ideas."},{"text":"No, I think people will still need handwriting. It is a useful skill, and schools will probably continue teaching children to write with a pen before they learn typing.","phrases":[{"en":"handwriting","meaning":"Writing done by hand with a pen or pencil."}],"question":"Do you think computers might one day replace handwriting?","html":"No, I think people will still need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">handwriting</strong>. It is a useful skill, and schools will probably continue teaching children to write with a pen before they learn typing."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-022-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-022-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-022-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-022-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Describing your family and time together","prompt":"Can you describe your family?\nDo you have a large or a small family?\nHow much time do you spend with your family?\nWhat do you like to do together as a family?","answer":"Q: Can you describe your family?\nA: I come from a small, close family consisting of my parents, one sibling and me. We've got different personalities, but we support one another and maintain a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">close-knit family</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you have a large or a small family?\nA: My immediate family's fairly small. That makes it easier for us to stay informed about one another's lives and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">resolve disagreements</strong> before they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">become serious problems</strong>.\n\nQ: How much time do you spend with your family?\nA: I see my family regularly, especially in the evenings and at weekends. Even when everyone's busy, we try to share at least one meal and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catch up with</strong> each other.\n\nQ: What do you like to do together as a family?\nA: We usually share a meal, take short trips or simply talk about what's happened during the week. None of those activities is unusual, but they give us <strong class=\"key-phrase\">uninterrupted time together</strong>.","topic":"Family","phrases":[{"en":"resolve disagreements","context":"My immediate family's fairly small. That makes it easier for us to stay informed about one another's lives and resolve disagreements before they become serious problems."},{"en":"become serious problems","context":"My immediate family's fairly small. That makes it easier for us to stay informed about one another's lives and resolve disagreements before they become serious problems."},{"en":"catch up with","context":"I see my family regularly, especially in the evenings and at weekends. Even when everyone's busy, we try to share at least one meal and catch up with each other."},{"en":"uninterrupted time together","context":"We usually share a meal, take short trips or simply talk about what's happened during the week. None of those activities is unusual, but they give us uninterrupted time together."},{"en":"close-knit family","context":"I come from a small, close family consisting of my parents, one sibling and me. We've got different personalities, but we support one another and maintain a close-knit family."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Family","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-023","_displayNo":"#022","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-023-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-023-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-023-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-023-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"There are four people in my family: my parents, my sibling and me. We have different personalities, but we are close and always try to support each other.","phrases":[{"en":"support each other","meaning":"Help one another when needed."}],"question":"Can you describe your family?","html":"There are four people in my family: my parents, my sibling and me. We have different personalities, but we are close and always try to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">support each other</strong>."},{"text":"My immediate family is quite small. We know what is happening in each other's lives, and it is usually easy to talk through problems before they become serious.","phrases":[{"en":"immediate family","meaning":"Your closest family members, such as parents and siblings."}],"question":"Do you have a large or a small family?","html":"My <strong class=\"key-phrase\">immediate family</strong> is quite small. We know what is happening in each other&#x27;s lives, and it is usually easy to talk through problems before they become serious."},{"text":"I spend time with my family regularly, mostly in the evenings and at weekends. Even when we are busy, we try to eat together and talk about our day.","phrases":[{"en":"regularly","meaning":"Often, or following a repeated pattern."}],"question":"How much time do you spend with your family?","html":"I spend time with my family <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regularly</strong>, mostly in the evenings and at weekends. Even when we are busy, we try to eat together and talk about our day."},{"text":"We enjoy eating together, taking short trips and talking about our week. These are simple activities, but they give us time to enjoy each other's company without interruptions.","phrases":[{"en":"each other's company","meaning":"Being together and spending time with one another."}],"question":"What do you like to do together as a family?","html":"We enjoy eating together, taking short trips and talking about our week. These are simple activities, but they give us time to enjoy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">each other&#x27;s company</strong> without interruptions."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-023-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-023-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-023-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-023-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Who you live with and what you do together","prompt":"Who do you live with?\nDo you spend much time with your family?\nIs your family small or quite large?\nWhat do you do when you are all together?","answer":"Q: Who do you live with?\nA: I live with close family members in Hanoi. Sharing a home requires compromise, but it also provides <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical support</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of belonging</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you spend much time with your family?\nA: I spend a reasonable amount of time with my family, mainly over dinner and at weekends. We're all busy, so those regular conversations help us <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay closely connected</strong>.\n\nQ: Is your family small or quite large?\nA: My family is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relatively small</strong>, which means decisions are easier to coordinate and everyone knows what's happening in one another's lives.\n\nQ: What do you do when you are all together?\nA: We usually eat together, talk about work and occasionally <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take a short trip</strong>. The activity itself isn't important; what matters is having uninterrupted time to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strengthen our relationship</strong>.","topic":"Family","phrases":[{"en":"practical support","context":"I live with close family members in Hanoi. Sharing a home requires compromise, but it also provides practical support and a sense of belonging."},{"en":"a sense of belonging","context":"I live with close family members in Hanoi. Sharing a home requires compromise, but it also provides practical support and a sense of belonging."},{"en":"stay closely connected","context":"I spend a reasonable amount of time with my family, mainly over dinner and at weekends. We're all busy, so those regular conversations help us stay closely connected."},{"en":"relatively small","context":"My family is relatively small, which means decisions are easier to coordinate and everyone knows what's happening in one another's lives."},{"en":"take a short trip","context":"We usually eat together, talk about work and occasionally take a short trip. The activity itself isn't important; what matters is having uninterrupted time to strengthen our relationship."},{"en":"strengthen our relationship","context":"We usually eat together, talk about work and occasionally take a short trip. The activity itself isn't important; what matters is having uninterrupted time to strengthen our relationship."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Family","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-024","_displayNo":"#023","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-024-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-024-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-024-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-024-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I live with close family members in Hanoi. We sometimes need to accept each other's wishes, but living together means we can help one another and feel part of a family.","phrases":[{"en":"part of a family","meaning":"Connected to and included in a family group."}],"question":"Who do you live with?","html":"I live with close family members in Hanoi. We sometimes need to accept each other&#x27;s wishes, but living together means we can help one another and feel <strong class=\"key-phrase\">part of a family</strong>."},{"text":"Yes, I spend a fair amount of time with them, especially at dinner and at weekends. We all have busy lives, so these regular chats help us stay close.","phrases":[{"en":"stay close","meaning":"Keep a strong and caring relationship."}],"question":"Do you spend much time with your family?","html":"Yes, I spend a fair amount of time with them, especially at dinner and at weekends. We all have busy lives, so these regular chats help us <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay close</strong>."},{"text":"My family is quite small. It is easier to make plans together because there are fewer people, and we usually know what everyone else is doing.","phrases":[{"en":"make plans","meaning":"Decide what you will do in the future."}],"question":"Is your family small or quite large?","html":"My family is quite small. It is easier to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make plans</strong> together because there are fewer people, and we usually know what everyone else is doing."},{"text":"We usually have meals, talk about work and sometimes go on short trips. What we do matters less than having time together without distractions, which helps us become closer.","phrases":[{"en":"distractions","meaning":"Things that take your attention away from what you are doing."}],"question":"What do you do when you are all together?","html":"We usually have meals, talk about work and sometimes go on short trips. What we do matters less than having time together without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">distractions</strong>, which helps us become closer."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-024-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-024-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-024-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-024-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"The main festivals in your country","prompt":"Can you describe one of the main festivals celebrated in your country?\nWhat can you tell me about the most important festival in your country?\nWhat special food and activities are connected with this festival?\nWhat do you most enjoy about it?","answer":"Q: Can you describe one of the main festivals celebrated in your country?\nA: Tet, the Lunar New Year, is the most important festival in Vietnam. Families reunite, clean and decorate their homes, and prepare for a fresh start, so the celebration <strong class=\"key-phrase\">brings people together</strong>.\n\nQ: What can you tell me about the most important festival in your country?\nA: Tet matters because it marks a new beginning and gives everyone a proper break from work. Beyond the celebrations, it's a time to honour ancestors and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">renew family connections</strong>.\n\nQ: What special food and activities are connected with this festival?\nA: Traditional foods include traditional sticky-rice cakes, candied fruit and various family dishes. People also visit relatives, give lucky money to children and pay respect to ancestors, which helps <strong class=\"key-phrase\">preserve local identity</strong>.\n\nQ: What do you most enjoy about it?\nA: I enjoy the slower pace and the chance to spend uninterrupted time with family. The atmosphere's festive, but the most valuable part is the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sense of renewal</strong>.","topic":"Festivals","phrases":[{"en":"brings people together","context":"Tet, the Lunar New Year, is the most important festival in Vietnam. Families reunite, clean and decorate their homes, and prepare for a fresh start, so the celebration brings people together."},{"en":"renew family connections","context":"Tet matters because it marks a new beginning and gives everyone a proper break from work. Beyond the celebrations, it's a time to honour ancestors and renew family connections."},{"en":"preserve local identity","context":"Traditional foods include traditional sticky-rice cakes, candied fruit and various family dishes. People also visit relatives, give lucky money to children and pay respect to ancestors, which helps preserve local identity."},{"en":"sense of renewal","context":"I enjoy the slower pace and the chance to spend uninterrupted time with family. The atmosphere's festive, but the most valuable part is the sense of renewal."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Festival","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-025","_displayNo":"#024","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-025-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-025-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-025-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-025-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Tet is the Lunar New Year and Vietnam's most important festival. Families come together, clean and decorate their homes, and get ready for a new year. It is a time for being together.","phrases":[{"en":"come together","meaning":"Meet and spend time as a group."}],"question":"Can you describe one of the main festivals celebrated in your country?","html":"Tet is the Lunar New Year and Vietnam&#x27;s most important festival. Families <strong class=\"key-phrase\">come together</strong>, clean and decorate their homes, and get ready for a new year. It is a time for being together."},{"text":"Tet marks the start of a new year and gives people a break from work. It is also a time to remember family members who have died and reconnect with relatives.","phrases":[{"en":"reconnect with","meaning":"Become close to or communicate with someone again."}],"question":"What can you tell me about the most important festival in your country?","html":"Tet marks the start of a new year and gives people a break from work. It is also a time to remember family members who have died and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reconnect with</strong> relatives."},{"text":"People eat sticky-rice cakes, sweet preserved fruit and special family dishes. They visit relatives, give children lucky money and remember their ancestors. These customs help keep Vietnamese traditions alive.","phrases":[{"en":"ancestors","meaning":"Family members from generations before your parents or grandparents."}],"question":"What special food and activities are connected with this festival?","html":"People eat sticky-rice cakes, sweet preserved fruit and special family dishes. They visit relatives, give children lucky money and remember their <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ancestors</strong>. These customs help keep Vietnamese traditions alive."},{"text":"I most enjoy having a slower, quieter few days with my family. The celebrations are fun, but I especially like the feeling of starting again with fresh energy.","phrases":[{"en":"starting again","meaning":"Making a new beginning."}],"question":"What do you most enjoy about it?","html":"I most enjoy having a slower, quieter few days with my family. The celebrations are fun, but I especially like the feeling of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">starting again</strong> with fresh energy."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-025-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-025-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-025-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-025-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Film stars' pay, violence and censorship","prompt":"Are film stars paid too much money, in your opinion?\nDo you think there is too much violence in modern movies?\nWhat do you think about film censorship, and is it necessary?\nWhat was the last film you saw? Did you enjoy it?","answer":"Q: Are film stars paid too much money, in your opinion?\nA: A few global stars are paid extraordinary sums, but their names can determine whether a film attracts investment and audiences. I think the salaries seem excessive, although that's more about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">commercial value rather than social value</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you think there is too much violence in modern movies?\nA: Some films rely too heavily on violence because it creates immediate excitement. I don't think violence is a problem in itself, but repeated graphic scenes can become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a substitute for good writing</strong>.\n\nQ: What do you think about film censorship, and is it necessary?\nA: I think some classification is necessary to protect children and warn viewers, but blanket censorship is risky. Adults should generally be free to choose what they watch, provided there's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">clear age guidance</strong>.\n\nQ: What was the last film you saw? Did you enjoy it?\nA: The last film I watched was a thoughtful drama about a family dealing with change. I enjoyed it because the characters felt believable and the story avoided <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an overly predictable ending</strong>.","topic":"Films","phrases":[{"en":"commercial value rather than social value","context":"A few global stars are paid extraordinary sums, but their names can determine whether a film attracts investment and audiences. I think the salaries seem excessive, although that's more about commercial value rather than social value."},{"en":"a substitute for good writing","context":"Some films rely too heavily on violence because it creates immediate excitement. I don't think violence is a problem in itself, but repeated graphic scenes can become a substitute for good writing."},{"en":"clear age guidance","context":"I think some classification is necessary to protect children and warn viewers, but blanket censorship is risky. Adults should generally be free to choose what they watch, provided there's clear age guidance."},{"en":"an overly predictable ending","context":"The last film I watched was a thoughtful drama about a family dealing with change. I enjoyed it because the characters felt believable and the story avoided an overly predictable ending."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Films","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-026","_displayNo":"#025","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-026-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-026-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-026-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-026-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Some famous actors earn far more than seems reasonable to me. However, their names help attract money and audiences to films. Their pay reflects their business value more than their wider contribution to society.","phrases":[{"en":"attract","meaning":"Make people interested in coming or taking part."}],"question":"Are film stars paid too much money, in your opinion?","html":"Some famous actors earn far more than seems reasonable to me. However, their names help <strong class=\"key-phrase\">attract</strong> money and audiences to films. Their pay reflects their business value more than their wider contribution to society."},{"text":"Yes, some films use violence too often to make things exciting. Violence can be part of a story, but too many detailed, violent scenes cannot make up for a weak story.","phrases":[{"en":"make up for","meaning":"Balance or improve a bad situation by adding something else."}],"question":"Do you think there is too much violence in modern movies?","html":"Yes, some films use violence too often to make things exciting. Violence can be part of a story, but too many detailed, violent scenes cannot <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make up for</strong> a weak story."},{"text":"I think films need age ratings so parents can protect children and viewers know what to expect. However, adults should usually choose for themselves rather than have whole films banned.","phrases":[{"en":"age ratings","meaning":"Labels showing which age groups a film is suitable for."}],"question":"What do you think about film censorship, and is it necessary?","html":"I think films need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">age ratings</strong> so parents can protect children and viewers know what to expect. However, adults should usually choose for themselves rather than have whole films banned."},{"text":"The last film I saw was a drama about a family going through changes. I enjoyed it because the people seemed real and I could not easily guess how it would end.","phrases":[{"en":"going through","meaning":"Experiencing a situation or period of change."}],"question":"What was the last film you saw? Did you enjoy it?","html":"The last film I saw was a drama about a family <strong class=\"key-phrase\">going through</strong> changes. I enjoyed it because the people seemed real and I could not easily guess how it would end."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-026-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-026-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-026-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-026-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Watching films alone, with friends or at the cinema","prompt":"Do you enjoy watching movies?\nDo you prefer to see a movie alone or with your friends?\nDo you prefer watching films at the cinema or at home?\nWhat is your most/least favourite type of movie?","answer":"Q: Do you enjoy watching movies?\nA: Yes, especially well-written dramas and documentaries. A good film allows me to relax while seeing a situation from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a different point of view</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you prefer to see a movie alone or with your friends?\nA: I usually watch films with friends because discussing them afterwards adds to the experience. For serious or complex films, however, I sometimes prefer watching alone so I can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pay close attention to</strong> the details.\n\nQ: Do you prefer watching films at the cinema or at home?\nA: I prefer the cinema for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">visually impressive</strong> films, but home's more convenient for ordinary viewing. The cinema offers immersion, while home allows me to pause and watch <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at my own pace</strong>.\n\nQ: What is your most/least favourite type of movie?\nA: My favourite genre is realistic drama, and I'm least interested in formulaic horror films. I prefer stories with convincing characters and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">emotional depth</strong>.","topic":"Films","phrases":[{"en":"a different point of view","context":"Yes, especially well-written dramas and documentaries. A good film allows me to relax while seeing a situation from a different point of view."},{"en":"pay close attention to","context":"I usually watch films with friends because discussing them afterwards adds to the experience. For serious or complex films, however, I sometimes prefer watching alone so I can pay close attention to the details."},{"en":"visually impressive","context":"I prefer the cinema for visually impressive films, but home's more convenient for ordinary viewing. The cinema offers immersion, while home allows me to pause and watch at my own pace."},{"en":"at my own pace","context":"I prefer the cinema for visually impressive films, but home's more convenient for ordinary viewing. The cinema offers immersion, while home allows me to pause and watch at my own pace."},{"en":"emotional depth","context":"My favourite genre is realistic drama, and I'm least interested in formulaic horror films. I prefer stories with convincing characters and emotional depth."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Films","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-027","_displayNo":"#026","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-027-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-027-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-027-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-027-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, especially dramas with good stories and documentaries. Watching a film helps me relax, and it can also help me understand a situation from someone else's point of view.","phrases":[{"en":"point of view","meaning":"The way a person thinks about or understands something."}],"question":"Do you enjoy watching movies?","html":"Yes, especially dramas with good stories and documentaries. Watching a film helps me relax, and it can also help me understand a situation from someone else&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">point of view</strong>."},{"text":"I usually watch with friends because I enjoy talking about the film afterwards. However, I sometimes watch complicated or serious films alone so I can concentrate on the details.","phrases":[{"en":"concentrate on","meaning":"Give your full attention to something."}],"question":"Do you prefer to see a movie alone or with your friends?","html":"I usually watch with friends because I enjoy talking about the film afterwards. However, I sometimes watch complicated or serious films alone so I can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">concentrate on</strong> the details."},{"text":"For films with impressive pictures, I prefer the cinema. For other films, home is easier because I can pause or choose my own speed. Both have advantages, depending on the film.","phrases":[{"en":"pause","meaning":"Stop something for a short time before continuing."}],"question":"Do you prefer watching films at the cinema or at home?","html":"For films with impressive pictures, I prefer the cinema. For other films, home is easier because I can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pause</strong> or choose my own speed. Both have advantages, depending on the film."},{"text":"I like realistic dramas most because I enjoy believable people and meaningful feelings. I am least interested in horror films that repeat the same ideas and have predictable stories.","phrases":[{"en":"predictable","meaning":"Easy to guess before it happens."}],"question":"What is your most/least favourite type of movie?","html":"I like realistic dramas most because I enjoy believable people and meaningful feelings. I am least interested in horror films that repeat the same ideas and have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">predictable</strong> stories."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-027-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-027-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-027-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-027-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Cooking at home and family meals","prompt":"Do you like cooking?\nWho does the cooking in your home?\nDo you think family meals are important?\nWhat kind of food do you like?","answer":"Q: Do you like cooking?\nA: Yes, although I cook simple meals rather than elaborate dishes. I enjoy being able to control the ingredients and adjust flavours <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to my own taste</strong>.\n\nQ: Who does the cooking in your home?\nA: Cooking is shared in my home, depending on who's got time. That arrangement is practical and prevents one person from carrying <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the entire responsibility</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you think family meals are important?\nA: Yes, because they create a regular opportunity for people to talk without distractions. Even a short meal can help family members <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay connected</strong>.\n\nQ: What kind of food do you like?\nA: I like fresh Vietnamese food, particularly dishes with vegetables, herbs and light broths. I'm less keen on food that's excessively oily because it leaves me feeling <strong class=\"key-phrase\">heavy and tired</strong>.","topic":"Food","phrases":[{"en":"to my own taste","context":"Yes, although I cook simple meals rather than elaborate dishes. I enjoy being able to control the ingredients and adjust flavours to my own taste."},{"en":"the entire responsibility","context":"Cooking is shared in my home, depending on who's got time. That arrangement is practical and prevents one person from carrying the entire responsibility."},{"en":"stay connected","context":"Yes, because they create a regular opportunity for people to talk without distractions. Even a short meal can help family members stay connected."},{"en":"heavy and tired","context":"I like fresh Vietnamese food, particularly dishes with vegetables, herbs and light broths. I'm less keen on food that's excessively oily because it leaves me feeling heavy and tired."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Food","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-028","_displayNo":"#027","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-028-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-028-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-028-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-028-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, but I mainly make simple meals. I enjoy choosing the ingredients myself and changing the flavour until the food tastes the way I like it.","phrases":[{"en":"ingredients","meaning":"The foods used to make a dish."}],"question":"Do you like cooking?","html":"Yes, but I mainly make simple meals. I enjoy choosing the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ingredients</strong> myself and changing the flavour until the food tastes the way I like it."},{"text":"We share the cooking, depending on who has time. This works well because one person does not have to prepare all the meals on their own.","phrases":[{"en":"share the cooking","meaning":"Divide the work of preparing meals between people."}],"question":"Who does the cooking in your home?","html":"We <strong class=\"key-phrase\">share the cooking</strong>, depending on who has time. This works well because one person does not have to prepare all the meals on their own."},{"text":"Yes, family meals are important because they give us a regular chance to talk. Even a quick meal together can help us stay close if we give each other our attention.","phrases":[{"en":"a regular chance","meaning":"An opportunity that happens often or repeatedly."}],"question":"Do you think family meals are important?","html":"Yes, family meals are important because they give us <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a regular chance</strong> to talk. Even a quick meal together can help us stay close if we give each other our attention."},{"text":"I enjoy fresh Vietnamese dishes, especially vegetables, herbs and light soups. I don't like food with too much oil because it can make me feel full in an uncomfortable way and tired.","phrases":[{"en":"herbs","meaning":"Plants or leaves used to add flavour to food."}],"question":"What kind of food do you like?","html":"I enjoy fresh Vietnamese dishes, especially vegetables, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">herbs</strong> and light soups. I don&#x27;t like food with too much oil because it can make me feel full in an uncomfortable way and tired."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-028-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-028-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-028-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-028-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Favourite foods and eating out","prompt":"What are your most/ least favourite foods?\nIf you invited a friend over for a meal, what would you serve?\nDo you prefer eating at home or in a restaurant and why?\nWhat special dish from your country would you recommend to a visitor?","answer":"Q: What are your most/ least favourite foods?\nA: I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">particularly enjoy</strong> fresh Vietnamese dishes with herbs, vegetables and light broths. I'm least fond of food that's extremely greasy or sweet because it leaves me feeling <strong class=\"key-phrase\">heavy and tired</strong>.\n\nQ: If you invited a friend over for a meal, what would you serve?\nA: I'd probably serve grilled chicken, rice, vegetables and a light soup. It's easy to prepare for several people and can be adapted to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">different dietary preferences</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you prefer eating at home or in a restaurant and why?\nA: I prefer eating at home on ordinary days because it's healthier and less expensive. Restaurants are enjoyable for special occasions, but home cooking gives me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more control over ingredients</strong>.\n\nQ: What special dish from your country would you recommend to a visitor?\nA: I'd recommend Vietnamese noodle soup because it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">widely available</strong> yet <strong class=\"key-phrase\">closely associated with</strong> Vietnamese cuisine. Its clear broth, herbs and rice noodles create <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a balanced combination of flavours</strong>.","topic":"Food","phrases":[{"en":"particularly enjoy","context":"I particularly enjoy fresh Vietnamese dishes with herbs, vegetables and light broths. I'm least fond of food that's extremely greasy or sweet because it leaves me feeling heavy and tired."},{"en":"heavy and tired","context":"I particularly enjoy fresh Vietnamese dishes with herbs, vegetables and light broths. I'm least fond of food that's extremely greasy or sweet because it leaves me feeling heavy and tired."},{"en":"different dietary preferences","context":"I'd probably serve grilled chicken, rice, vegetables and a light soup. It's easy to prepare for several people and can be adapted to different dietary preferences."},{"en":"more control over ingredients","context":"I prefer eating at home on ordinary days because it's healthier and less expensive. Restaurants are enjoyable for special occasions, but home cooking gives me more control over ingredients."},{"en":"widely available","context":"I'd recommend Vietnamese noodle soup because it's widely available yet closely associated with Vietnamese cuisine. Its clear broth, herbs and rice noodles create a balanced combination of flavours."},{"en":"closely associated with","context":"I'd recommend Vietnamese noodle soup because it's widely available yet closely associated with Vietnamese cuisine. Its clear broth, herbs and rice noodles create a balanced combination of flavours."},{"en":"a balanced combination of flavours","context":"I'd recommend Vietnamese noodle soup because it's widely available yet closely associated with Vietnamese cuisine. Its clear broth, herbs and rice noodles create a balanced combination of flavours."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Food","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-029","_displayNo":"#028","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-029-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-029-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-029-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-029-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I like fresh Vietnamese food with vegetables, herbs and light soup. I dislike very oily or very sweet food because it makes me feel uncomfortable and tired afterwards.","phrases":[{"en":"oily","meaning":"Containing a lot of oil or fat."}],"question":"What are your most/ least favourite foods?","html":"I like fresh Vietnamese food with vegetables, herbs and light soup. I dislike very <strong class=\"key-phrase\">oily</strong> or very sweet food because it makes me feel uncomfortable and tired afterwards."},{"text":"I'd make grilled chicken with rice, vegetables and a light soup. It is easy to prepare for a group, and I can change parts of the meal to suit what people eat.","phrases":[{"en":"suit","meaning":"Be right for someone's needs or preferences."}],"question":"If you invited a friend over for a meal, what would you serve?","html":"I&#x27;d make grilled chicken with rice, vegetables and a light soup. It is easy to prepare for a group, and I can change parts of the meal to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suit</strong> what people eat."},{"text":"I prefer eating at home most days because it costs less and I can choose healthier ingredients. I still enjoy restaurants for special events, but I like having control over my everyday meals.","phrases":[{"en":"having control over","meaning":"Being able to decide how something is done."}],"question":"Do you prefer eating at home or in a restaurant and why?","html":"I prefer eating at home most days because it costs less and I can choose healthier ingredients. I still enjoy restaurants for special events, but I like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">having control over</strong> my everyday meals."},{"text":"I'd suggest Vietnamese noodle soup. Visitors can find it easily, and it is an important part of our food culture. The clear soup, herbs and rice noodles taste good together.","phrases":[{"en":"food culture","meaning":"The foods and eating traditions connected with a group of people."}],"question":"What special dish from your country would you recommend to a visitor?","html":"I&#x27;d suggest Vietnamese noodle soup. Visitors can find it easily, and it is an important part of our <strong class=\"key-phrase\">food culture</strong>. The clear soup, herbs and rice noodles taste good together."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-029-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-029-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-029-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-029-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Time at home and what you do with free time","prompt":"How much time do you spend at home?\nDo you prefer to spend your free time at home or outside the house?\nWhat do you do when you have some free time and you're at home?\nWould you like to have more spare time to spend at home in the future?","answer":"Q: How much time do you spend at home?\nA: Obviously I sleep at home, but I don't <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spend very much time at home</strong> during the day, because I'm usually busy with work.\n\nQ: Do you prefer to spend your free time at home or outside the house?\nA: During the day I prefer to be out of the house, so I like visiting family or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meeting up with friends</strong>. But I do like relaxing at home in the evenings.\n\nQ: What do you do when you have some free time and you're at home?\nA: I usually sit and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">watch some TV</strong> or a film, or I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">listen to some music</strong>. I also like sitting outside if the weather is nice.\n\nQ: Would you like to have more spare time to spend at home in the future?\nA: No, not really. I get bored if I'm at home for too long. In the future, I imagine I'll still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prefer to go out</strong> and do things rather than stay indoors.","topic":"Free Time","phrases":[{"en":"spend very much time at home","context":"Obviously I sleep at home, but I don't spend very much time at home during the day, because I'm usually busy with work."},{"en":"meeting up with friends","context":"During the day I prefer to be out of the house, so I like visiting family or meeting up with friends. But I do like relaxing at home in the evenings."},{"en":"watch some TV","context":"I usually sit and watch some TV or a film, or I listen to some music. I also like sitting outside if the weather is nice."},{"en":"listen to some music","context":"I usually sit and watch some TV or a film, or I listen to some music. I also like sitting outside if the weather is nice."},{"en":"prefer to go out","context":"No, not really. I get bored if I'm at home for too long. In the future, I imagine I'll still prefer to go out and do things rather than stay indoors."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Free Time","id":"speaking-part-1-030","_displayNo":"#029","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-030-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-030-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-030-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-030-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I sleep at home, of course, but I'm usually out working during the day. Because of that, I don't spend very much of my daytime at home.","phrases":[{"en":"during the day","meaning":"In the hours between morning and evening."}],"question":"How much time do you spend at home?","html":"I sleep at home, of course, but I&#x27;m usually out working <strong class=\"key-phrase\">during the day</strong>. Because of that, I don&#x27;t spend very much of my daytime at home."},{"text":"I like going out during the day to visit family or meet friends. In the evening, though, I enjoy staying at home and relaxing after a busy day.","phrases":[{"en":"meet friends","meaning":"Spend time with people you know and like."}],"question":"Do you prefer to spend your free time at home or outside the house?","html":"I like going out during the day to visit family or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meet friends</strong>. In the evening, though, I enjoy staying at home and relaxing after a busy day."},{"text":"I usually watch television or a film, or listen to music. If the weather is pleasant, I also enjoy sitting outside and taking a break.","phrases":[{"en":"taking a break","meaning":"Stopping an activity for a short rest."}],"question":"What do you do when you have some free time and you're at home?","html":"I usually watch television or a film, or listen to music. If the weather is pleasant, I also enjoy sitting outside and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taking a break</strong>."},{"text":"Not really. I become bored if I stay home too long. I think I will still enjoy going out and doing things rather than spending more time indoors.","phrases":[{"en":"indoors","meaning":"Inside a building."}],"question":"Would you like to have more spare time to spend at home in the future?","html":"Not really. I become bored if I stay home too long. I think I will still enjoy going out and doing things rather than spending more time <strong class=\"key-phrase\">indoors</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-030-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-030-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-030-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-030-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"The age of your friends and when you see them","prompt":"Are your friends mostly your age or different ages?\nDo you usually see your friends during the week or at weekends?\nThe last time you saw your friends, what did you do together?\nIn what ways are your friends important to you?","answer":"Q: Are your friends mostly your age or different ages?\nA: Most of my friends are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">about the same age</strong> as me because we met at school or university. I've got one or two friends who are older or younger that I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">met through work</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you usually see your friends during the week or at weekends?\nA: I tend to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meet up with my friends</strong> at weekends because everyone's too busy during the week.\n\nQ: The last time you saw your friends, what did you do together?\nA: It was one of my friends' birthday last weekend. Six of us went <strong class=\"key-phrase\">out for a meal</strong> to celebrate.\n\nQ: In what ways are your friends important to you?\nA: I think it's important to have friends that you can talk to and share experiences with. My friends <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make me laugh</strong>, but I know I can also rely on them whenever I need help or support.","topic":"Friends","phrases":[{"en":"about the same age","context":"Most of my friends are about the same age as me because we met at school or university. I've got one or two friends who are older or younger that I met through work."},{"en":"met through work","context":"Most of my friends are about the same age as me because we met at school or university. I've got one or two friends who are older or younger that I met through work."},{"en":"meet up with my friends","context":"I tend to meet up with my friends at weekends because everyone's too busy during the week."},{"en":"out for a meal","context":"It was one of my friends' birthday last weekend. Six of us went out for a meal to celebrate."},{"en":"make me laugh","context":"I think it's important to have friends that you can talk to and share experiences with. My friends make me laugh, but I know I can also rely on them whenever I need help or support."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Friends","id":"speaking-part-1-031","_displayNo":"#030","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-031-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-031-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-031-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-031-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Most are around my age because we met at school or university. I also have one or two older or younger friends whom I met through work.","phrases":[{"en":"around my age","meaning":"About as old as I am."}],"question":"Are your friends mostly your age or different ages?","html":"Most are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">around my age</strong> because we met at school or university. I also have one or two older or younger friends whom I met through work."},{"text":"I usually see my friends at weekends. We are all busy with work or other things during the week, so it is easier to find time then.","phrases":[{"en":"at weekends","meaning":"On Saturdays and Sundays."}],"question":"Do you usually see your friends during the week or at weekends?","html":"I usually see my friends <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at weekends</strong>. We are all busy with work or other things during the week, so it is easier to find time then."},{"text":"We went out for dinner last weekend because it was a friend's birthday. There were six of us, and we had a meal together to celebrate.","phrases":[{"en":"celebrate","meaning":"Do something enjoyable to mark a special occasion."}],"question":"The last time you saw your friends, what did you do together?","html":"We went out for dinner last weekend because it was a friend&#x27;s birthday. There were six of us, and we had a meal together to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">celebrate</strong>."},{"text":"My friends give me people to talk to and share experiences with. They make me laugh, and I know they will help me when I need support.","phrases":[{"en":"support","meaning":"Help or encouragement when you need it."}],"question":"In what ways are your friends important to you?","html":"My friends give me people to talk to and share experiences with. They make me laugh, and I know they will help me when I need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">support</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-031-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-031-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-031-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-031-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"One close friend or a group of friends","prompt":"Do you prefer to have one particular friend or a group of friends?\nWhat do you like doing most with your friends?\nDo you think it's important to keep in touch with childhood friends?\nWhat makes a good friend?","answer":"Q: Do you prefer to have one particular friend or a group of friends?\nA: I prefer a small group of close friends rather than one exclusive friendship. Different friends bring different perspectives, while the group still gives me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a reliable support network</strong>.\n\nQ: What do you like doing most with your friends?\nA: We usually have coffee, exercise or take short trips. The activity itself isn't the main point; I mainly enjoy having time to talk openly and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">share everyday experiences</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you think it's important to keep in touch with childhood friends?\nA: It's valuable when the friendship remains healthy, because childhood friends understand your background. But people change, so staying in touch should feel natural rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a social obligation</strong>.\n\nQ: What makes a good friend?\nA: A good friend is trustworthy, honest and able to listen without immediately judging. Mutual respect matters more than constant agreement because it creates <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a genuine connection</strong>.","topic":"Friends","phrases":[{"en":"a reliable support network","context":"I prefer a small group of close friends rather than one exclusive friendship. Different friends bring different perspectives, while the group still gives me a reliable support network."},{"en":"share everyday experiences","context":"We usually have coffee, exercise or take short trips. The activity itself isn't the main point; I mainly enjoy having time to talk openly and share everyday experiences."},{"en":"a social obligation","context":"It's valuable when the friendship remains healthy, because childhood friends understand your background. But people change, so staying in touch should feel natural rather than a social obligation."},{"en":"a genuine connection","context":"A good friend is trustworthy, honest and able to listen without immediately judging. Mutual respect matters more than constant agreement because it creates a genuine connection."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Friends","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-032","_displayNo":"#031","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-032-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-032-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-032-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-032-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I prefer a small group of close friends. Each person has different ideas, and I can learn from them. At the same time, I have several people I can depend on.","phrases":[{"en":"depend on","meaning":"Trust someone to help or do what you need."}],"question":"Do you prefer to have one particular friend or a group of friends?","html":"I prefer a small group of close friends. Each person has different ideas, and I can learn from them. At the same time, I have several people I can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depend on</strong>."},{"text":"We usually have coffee, exercise or go on short trips. I mainly enjoy having time to talk honestly and share things from our daily lives, whatever activity we choose.","phrases":[{"en":"talk honestly","meaning":"Say what you really think or feel."}],"question":"What do you like doing most with your friends?","html":"We usually have coffee, exercise or go on short trips. I mainly enjoy having time to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">talk honestly</strong> and share things from our daily lives, whatever activity we choose."},{"text":"Yes, if the friendship is still good for both people. Childhood friends know a lot about your past. However, people change, so staying friends should be a choice rather than a duty.","phrases":[{"en":"a duty","meaning":"Something you are expected or required to do."}],"question":"Do you think it's important to keep in touch with childhood friends?","html":"Yes, if the friendship is still good for both people. Childhood friends know a lot about your past. However, people change, so staying friends should be a choice rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a duty</strong>."},{"text":"A good friend is honest, reliable and willing to listen. You don't have to agree on everything, but you should respect one another and feel comfortable talking openly.","phrases":[{"en":"reliable","meaning":"Someone you can trust to do what they say."}],"question":"What makes a good friend?","html":"A good friend is honest, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reliable</strong> and willing to listen. You don&#x27;t have to agree on everything, but you should respect one another and feel comfortable talking openly."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-032-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-032-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-032-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-032-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Popular games and how people learn them","prompt":"What games are popular in your country?\nDo you play any games?\nHow do people learn to play games in your country?\nDo you think it's important for people to play games?","answer":"Q: What games are popular in your country?\nA: Well, if we count sports as games, then football's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">by far the most popular game</strong> in my country, but we also love board games like chess.\n\nQ: Do you play any games?\nA: Not regularly these days. I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">play the occasional game of</strong> tennis with my brother-in-law, and I recently played a bowling game on the Nintendo Wii with my nephew. I wasn't very good at it.\n\nQ: How do people learn to play games in your country?\nA: I think children mostly teach each other how to play games, and that's how I learned chess too. A friend at primary school <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taught me the rules</strong> when we were young.\n\nQ: Do you think it's important for people to play games?\nA: Yes, it's really important, especially for children. I think research has shown that play can improve <strong class=\"key-phrase\">brain development</strong>, intelligence, creativity, and the ability to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cooperate with others</strong>.","topic":"Games","phrases":[{"en":"by far the most popular game","context":"Well, if we count sports as games, then football's by far the most popular game in my country, but we also love board games like chess."},{"en":"play the occasional game of","context":"Not regularly these days. I play the occasional game of tennis with my brother-in-law, and I recently played a bowling game on the Nintendo Wii with my nephew. I wasn't very good at it."},{"en":"taught me the rules","context":"I think children mostly teach each other how to play games, and that's how I learned chess too. A friend at primary school taught me the rules when we were young."},{"en":"brain development","context":"Yes, it's really important, especially for children. I think research has shown that play can improve brain development, intelligence, creativity, and the ability to cooperate with others."},{"en":"cooperate with others","context":"Yes, it's really important, especially for children. I think research has shown that play can improve brain development, intelligence, creativity, and the ability to cooperate with others."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Games","id":"speaking-part-1-033","_displayNo":"#032","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-033-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-033-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-033-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-033-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Football is the most popular if we include sports. People also enjoy board games such as chess, so there are both active games and quieter ones to choose from.","phrases":[{"en":"board games","meaning":"Games played on a board, often using small pieces."}],"question":"What games are popular in your country?","html":"Football is the most popular if we include sports. People also enjoy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">board games</strong> such as chess, so there are both active games and quieter ones to choose from."},{"text":"Not very often now. I sometimes play tennis with my brother-in-law. Recently, I also tried a bowling game on the Nintendo Wii with my nephew, but I wasn't very good.","phrases":[{"en":"Not very often","meaning":"Only sometimes, rather than regularly."}],"question":"Do you play any games?","html":"<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Not very often</strong> now. I sometimes play tennis with my brother-in-law. Recently, I also tried a bowling game on the Nintendo Wii with my nephew, but I wasn&#x27;t very good."},{"text":"Children often learn games from other children. That is how I learned chess: a friend at primary school explained the rules and showed me how to play.","phrases":[{"en":"the rules","meaning":"The instructions that tell you how a game works."}],"question":"How do people learn to play games in your country?","html":"Children often learn games from other children. That is how I learned chess: a friend at primary school explained <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the rules</strong> and showed me how to play."},{"text":"Yes, especially for children. Playing games can help them think, use their imagination and learn to work with other people. So games can be useful as well as enjoyable.","phrases":[{"en":"imagination","meaning":"The ability to form new ideas or pictures in your mind."}],"question":"Do you think it's important for people to play games?","html":"Yes, especially for children. Playing games can help them think, use their <strong class=\"key-phrase\">imagination</strong> and learn to work with other people. So games can be useful as well as enjoyable."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-033-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-033-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-033-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-033-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Games children and adults play","prompt":"Do you enjoy playing games?\nWhat kind of games do children play in your country?\nDo you think adults should play games?\nWhat kinds of games were popular when you were younger?","answer":"Q: Do you enjoy playing games?\nA: Yes, particularly strategy and word games. They're entertaining but also require concentration, so they provide <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a healthy mental challenge</strong>.\n\nQ: What kind of games do children play in your country?\nA: Children play mobile games, football, hide-and-seek and a variety of classroom games. Digital games are more visible now, but physical games remain important for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">social and motor skills</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you think adults should play games?\nA: Certainly, provided gaming doesn't replace work, exercise or relationships. Games can reduce stress and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep the mind active</strong>, so they're not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">only for children</strong>.\n\nQ: What kinds of games were popular when you were younger?\nA: When I was younger, outdoor games and simple board games were common. Children had fewer devices, so entertainment depended more on imagination and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">face-to-face interaction</strong>.","topic":"Games","phrases":[{"en":"a healthy mental challenge","context":"Yes, particularly strategy and word games. They're entertaining but also require concentration, so they provide a healthy mental challenge."},{"en":"social and motor skills","context":"Children play mobile games, football, hide-and-seek and a variety of classroom games. Digital games are more visible now, but physical games remain important for social and motor skills."},{"en":"keep the mind active","context":"Certainly, provided gaming doesn't replace work, exercise or relationships. Games can reduce stress and keep the mind active, so they're not only for children."},{"en":"only for children","context":"Certainly, provided gaming doesn't replace work, exercise or relationships. Games can reduce stress and keep the mind active, so they're not only for children."},{"en":"face-to-face interaction","context":"When I was younger, outdoor games and simple board games were common. Children had fewer devices, so entertainment depended more on imagination and face-to-face interaction."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Games","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-034","_displayNo":"#033","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-034-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-034-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-034-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-034-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, I especially enjoy strategy games and word games. They are fun, but I also have to think carefully and pay attention, which gives my mind some exercise.","phrases":[{"en":"pay attention","meaning":"Listen, watch or think carefully about something."}],"question":"Do you enjoy playing games?","html":"Yes, I especially enjoy strategy games and word games. They are fun, but I also have to think carefully and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pay attention</strong>, which gives my mind some exercise."},{"text":"Children play phone games, football, hide-and-seek and games at school. Phone games are common now, but active games still help children learn to move well and play with others.","phrases":[{"en":"active games","meaning":"Games that involve moving your body."}],"question":"What kind of games do children play in your country?","html":"Children play phone games, football, hide-and-seek and games at school. Phone games are common now, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">active games</strong> still help children learn to move well and play with others."},{"text":"Yes, as long as games leave enough time for work, exercise and relationships. They can help adults relax and keep their minds busy, so they are not just for children.","phrases":[{"en":"as long as","meaning":"On the condition that something is true."}],"question":"Do you think adults should play games?","html":"Yes, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as long as</strong> games leave enough time for work, exercise and relationships. They can help adults relax and keep their minds busy, so they are not just for children."},{"text":"Outdoor games and simple board games were popular when I was younger. We had fewer electronic devices, so we used our imagination more and played with other children in person.","phrases":[{"en":"electronic devices","meaning":"Equipment such as phones, tablets and computers."}],"question":"What kinds of games were popular when you were younger?","html":"Outdoor games and simple board games were popular when I was younger. We had fewer <strong class=\"key-phrase\">electronic devices</strong>, so we used our imagination more and played with other children in person."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-034-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-034-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-034-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-034-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Your health and health concerns in your country","prompt":"Is your health important to you?\nWhat do you do to improve your health?\nWhat is a major health concern in your country?\nWhat can be done about it?","answer":"Q: Is your health important to you?\nA: Yes, because health affects energy, concentration and independence. It's easy to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take it for granted</strong> until a problem appears, so prevention is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a worthwhile investment</strong>.\n\nQ: What do you do to improve your health?\nA: I exercise several times a week, sleep consistently and try to eat mostly unprocessed food. None of these habits is dramatic, but together they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make a noticeable difference</strong>.\n\nQ: What is a major health concern in your country?\nA: I'd say lifestyle-related conditions such as obesity, diabetes and stress are growing concerns. They develop gradually, which means public education and early screening should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">play a central role</strong>.\n\nQ: What can be done about it?\nA: Governments can improve preventive care, safe public spaces and nutrition education, while individuals need realistic habits they can maintain. In the end, I think it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a shared responsibility</strong>.","topic":"Health & Fitness","phrases":[{"en":"take it for granted","context":"Yes, because health affects energy, concentration and independence. It's easy to take it for granted until a problem appears, so prevention is a worthwhile investment."},{"en":"a worthwhile investment","context":"Yes, because health affects energy, concentration and independence. It's easy to take it for granted until a problem appears, so prevention is a worthwhile investment."},{"en":"make a noticeable difference","context":"I exercise several times a week, sleep consistently and try to eat mostly unprocessed food. None of these habits is dramatic, but together they make a noticeable difference."},{"en":"play a central role","context":"I'd say lifestyle-related conditions such as obesity, diabetes and stress are growing concerns. They develop gradually, which means public education and early screening should play a central role."},{"en":"a shared responsibility","context":"Governments can improve preventive care, safe public spaces and nutrition education, while individuals need realistic habits they can maintain. In the end, I think it's a shared responsibility."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Health & Fitness","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-035","_displayNo":"#034","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-035-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-035-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-035-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-035-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, very important. Good health helps me feel energetic, focus and do things without help. I think it is better to look after it before problems appear.","phrases":[{"en":"energetic","meaning":"Having plenty of energy to do things."}],"question":"Is your health important to you?","html":"Yes, very important. Good health helps me feel <strong class=\"key-phrase\">energetic</strong>, focus and do things without help. I think it is better to look after it before problems appear."},{"text":"I exercise several times a week, keep regular sleeping habits and try to eat mostly fresh, simple food. These small habits work together to help me feel healthier.","phrases":[{"en":"sleeping habits","meaning":"Your usual patterns of going to sleep and resting."}],"question":"What do you do to improve your health?","html":"I exercise several times a week, keep regular <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sleeping habits</strong> and try to eat mostly fresh, simple food. These small habits work together to help me feel healthier."},{"text":"I think obesity, diabetes and stress are important concerns linked to people's lifestyles. These problems can develop slowly, so people need clear information and health checks before things get worse.","phrases":[{"en":"health checks","meaning":"Examinations to find out whether someone has a health problem."}],"question":"What is a major health concern in your country?","html":"I think obesity, diabetes and stress are important concerns linked to people&#x27;s lifestyles. These problems can develop slowly, so people need clear information and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">health checks</strong> before things get worse."},{"text":"Governments can provide better health services, safe places to exercise and advice about food. People also need healthy habits they can keep. I think both sides have a part to play.","phrases":[{"en":"a part to play","meaning":"A role or responsibility in something."}],"question":"What can be done about it?","html":"Governments can provide better health services, safe places to exercise and advice about food. People also need healthy habits they can keep. I think both sides have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a part to play</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-035-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-035-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-035-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-035-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"The exercise you do and why it matters","prompt":"What kind of exercise do you do regularly?\nWhat kind of exercise do you enjoy least?\nWhy is exercise good for health?\nWhat kind of exercise would you suggest to someone who is very unfit?","answer":"Q: What kind of exercise do you do regularly?\nA: I do strength training and brisk walking several times a week. The combination improves fitness without requiring specialised facilities and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fits naturally into my routine</strong>.\n\nQ: What kind of exercise do you enjoy least?\nA: I enjoy long-distance running least because repetitive impact can be uncomfortable. I prefer exercise that combines strength, mobility and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">steady progress</strong>.\n\nQ: Why is exercise good for health?\nA: Exercise strengthens the heart and muscles, improves sleep and helps regulate stress. It's also good for long-term independence, which is often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more important than appearance</strong>.\n\nQ: What kind of exercise would you suggest to someone who is very unfit?\nA: I'd suggest daily walking and simple body-weight exercises. They're low-cost, easy to adjust and allow an unfit person to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">build confidence gradually</strong>.","topic":"Health & Fitness","phrases":[{"en":"fits naturally into my routine","context":"I do strength training and brisk walking several times a week. The combination improves fitness without requiring specialised facilities and fits naturally into my routine."},{"en":"steady progress","context":"I enjoy long-distance running least because repetitive impact can be uncomfortable. I prefer exercise that combines strength, mobility and steady progress."},{"en":"more important than appearance","context":"Exercise strengthens the heart and muscles, improves sleep and helps regulate stress. It's also good for long-term independence, which is often more important than appearance."},{"en":"build confidence gradually","context":"I'd suggest daily walking and simple body-weight exercises. They're low-cost, easy to adjust and allow an unfit person to build confidence gradually."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Health & Fitness","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-036","_displayNo":"#035","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-036-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-036-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-036-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-036-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I lift weights and walk quickly several times a week. Together, these activities help me stay fit, and I can fit them into my normal schedule without needing special facilities for every session.","phrases":[{"en":"fit them into","meaning":"Find time for them in a schedule."}],"question":"What kind of exercise do you do regularly?","html":"I lift weights and walk quickly several times a week. Together, these activities help me stay fit, and I can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fit them into</strong> my normal schedule without needing special facilities for every session."},{"text":"I like long-distance running least because the repeated movement can feel uncomfortable. I prefer activities that build strength, help me move better and let me improve gradually.","phrases":[{"en":"gradually","meaning":"Slowly, through small changes over time."}],"question":"What kind of exercise do you enjoy least?","html":"I like long-distance running least because the repeated movement can feel uncomfortable. I prefer activities that build strength, help me move better and let me improve <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gradually</strong>."},{"text":"Exercise helps the heart and muscles, improves sleep and makes stress easier to manage. It can also help us keep doing everyday things as we get older, which matters more than appearance.","phrases":[{"en":"appearance","meaning":"The way someone or something looks."}],"question":"Why is exercise good for health?","html":"Exercise helps the heart and muscles, improves sleep and makes stress easier to manage. It can also help us keep doing everyday things as we get older, which matters more than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">appearance</strong>."},{"text":"I'd suggest walking every day and doing simple exercises using their own body weight. These are cheap and can be made easier or harder, helping the person improve and feel more confident.","phrases":[{"en":"body weight","meaning":"The weight of your own body used as resistance in exercise."}],"question":"What kind of exercise would you suggest to someone who is very unfit?","html":"I&#x27;d suggest walking every day and doing simple exercises using their own <strong class=\"key-phrase\">body weight</strong>. These are cheap and can be made easier or harder, helping the person improve and feel more confident."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-036-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-036-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-036-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-036-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Your hobbies and how you spend spare time","prompt":"Do you have any hobbies?\nWhat are some of your hobbies?\nWhat do you usually do in your spare time?\nDo you prefer to stay alone or with your friends?","answer":"Q: Do you have any hobbies?\nA: Yes, I've got a few. I enjoy reading, I do some strength training at the gym, and I'm learning Spanish at the moment too. Having a few different interests gives me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a healthy outlet</strong> outside work.\n\nQ: What are some of your hobbies?\nA: Reading's probably my favourite — I try to read a little most evenings. Strength training gives me something to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work towards</strong>, and learning a language keeps my brain active in a completely different way.\n\nQ: What do you usually do in your spare time?\nA: I usually read, exercise or meet up with a friend for coffee. I try to spend my free time doing something worthwhile rather than just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">scrolling without purpose</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you prefer to stay alone or with your friends?\nA: A bit of both, really. Reading and training on my own help me focus, but I also make time for friends so I don't end up too isolated — I suppose I just try to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strike a balance</strong>.","topic":"Hobbies","phrases":[{"en":"a healthy outlet","context":"Yes, I've got a few. I enjoy reading, I do some strength training at the gym, and I'm learning Spanish at the moment too. Having a few different interests gives me a healthy outlet outside work."},{"en":"work towards","context":"Reading's probably my favourite — I try to read a little most evenings. Strength training gives me something to work towards, and learning a language keeps my brain active in a completely different way."},{"en":"scrolling without purpose","context":"I usually read, exercise or meet up with a friend for coffee. I try to spend my free time doing something worthwhile rather than just scrolling without purpose."},{"en":"strike a balance","context":"A bit of both, really. Reading and training on my own help me focus, but I also make time for friends so I don't end up too isolated — I suppose I just try to strike a balance."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Hobbies","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-037","_displayNo":"#036","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-037-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-037-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-037-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-037-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, I enjoy reading, lifting weights at the gym and learning Spanish. Having different interests gives me enjoyable things to do and helps me relax when I'm not working.","phrases":[{"en":"interests","meaning":"Activities or subjects that you enjoy learning about or doing."}],"question":"Do you have any hobbies?","html":"Yes, I enjoy reading, lifting weights at the gym and learning Spanish. Having different <strong class=\"key-phrase\">interests</strong> gives me enjoyable things to do and helps me relax when I&#x27;m not working."},{"text":"Reading is probably my favourite, and I read most evenings. I also enjoy getting stronger at the gym, while learning a language gives my mind a different kind of activity.","phrases":[{"en":"getting stronger","meaning":"Becoming able to use more physical strength."}],"question":"What are some of your hobbies?","html":"Reading is probably my favourite, and I read most evenings. I also enjoy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">getting stronger</strong> at the gym, while learning a language gives my mind a different kind of activity."},{"text":"I usually read, exercise or have coffee with a friend. I like using my free time for something useful or enjoyable instead of looking through my phone without a reason.","phrases":[{"en":"free time","meaning":"Time when you do not have to work or study."}],"question":"What do you usually do in your spare time?","html":"I usually read, exercise or have coffee with a friend. I like using my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">free time</strong> for something useful or enjoyable instead of looking through my phone without a reason."},{"text":"I enjoy both. Reading and exercising alone help me focus, but I also spend time with friends. I try to have enough quiet time without becoming too separated from other people.","phrases":[{"en":"separated from","meaning":"Away from or not closely connected with someone."}],"question":"Do you prefer to stay alone or with your friends?","html":"I enjoy both. Reading and exercising alone help me focus, but I also spend time with friends. I try to have enough quiet time without becoming too <strong class=\"key-phrase\">separated from</strong> other people."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-037-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-037-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-037-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-037-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"A spare-time activity and how long you have done it","prompt":"What do you do in your spare time?\nHow long have you been interested in this activity?\nDo you do this alone or with other people?\nHow much time do you spend doing this activity?","answer":"Q: What do you do in your spare time?\nA: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">In my spare time</strong> I usually train at the gym, read or go for a quiet walk. I prefer activities that leave me feeling refreshed rather than just filling the time.\n\nQ: How long have you been interested in this activity?\nA: I've been strength training for several years now. I started just to get fit, but tracking small improvements is what's made it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">personally rewarding</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you do this alone or with other people?\nA: I do some hobbies alone because being on my own helps me concentrate, but I also exercise or go out with friends. That combination lets me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strike a healthy balance</strong> between independence and social contact.\n\nQ: How much time do you spend doing this activity?\nA: I spend roughly four or five hours a week on it. That's enough to make progress without letting the hobby <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take over my schedule</strong>.","topic":"Hobbies","phrases":[{"en":"In my spare time","context":"In my spare time I usually train at the gym, read or go for a quiet walk. I prefer activities that leave me feeling refreshed rather than just filling the time."},{"en":"personally rewarding","context":"I've been strength training for several years now. I started just to get fit, but tracking small improvements is what's made it personally rewarding."},{"en":"strike a healthy balance","context":"I do some hobbies alone because being on my own helps me concentrate, but I also exercise or go out with friends. That combination lets me strike a healthy balance between independence and social contact."},{"en":"take over my schedule","context":"I spend roughly four or five hours a week on it. That's enough to make progress without letting the hobby take over my schedule."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Hobbies","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-038","_displayNo":"#037","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-038-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-038-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-038-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-038-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I usually go to the gym, read or take a quiet walk. I choose things that help me feel rested and ready for the next part of my day.","phrases":[{"en":"rested","meaning":"Feeling less tired after relaxing or sleeping."}],"question":"What do you do in your spare time?","html":"I usually go to the gym, read or take a quiet walk. I choose things that help me feel <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rested</strong> and ready for the next part of my day."},{"text":"I've been lifting weights for several years. At first, I just wanted to get fit. Now I enjoy seeing small improvements, which makes the activity feel worthwhile.","phrases":[{"en":"worthwhile","meaning":"Useful or enjoyable enough to deserve your time."}],"question":"How long have you been interested in this activity?","html":"I&#x27;ve been lifting weights for several years. At first, I just wanted to get fit. Now I enjoy seeing small improvements, which makes the activity feel <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worthwhile</strong>."},{"text":"I do some hobbies alone because I can concentrate better. I also exercise or go out with friends, so I get both time to myself and time with other people.","phrases":[{"en":"time to myself","meaning":"Time spent alone doing what I choose."}],"question":"Do you do this alone or with other people?","html":"I do some hobbies alone because I can concentrate better. I also exercise or go out with friends, so I get both <strong class=\"key-phrase\">time to myself</strong> and time with other people."},{"text":"I spend about four or five hours a week on it. That gives me enough practice to improve while still leaving time for my other responsibilities.","phrases":[{"en":"responsibilities","meaning":"Things you need to do or take care of."}],"question":"How much time do you spend doing this activity?","html":"I spend about four or five hours a week on it. That gives me enough practice to improve while still leaving time for my other <strong class=\"key-phrase\">responsibilities</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-038-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-038-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-038-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-038-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Where you live and what you like about it","prompt":"How long have you lived there?\nWhat do you like about living there?\nWhat sort of accommodation would you most like to live in?","answer":"Q: How long have you lived there?\nA: I've lived there for about five years. It's the first house that I've owned; before that I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">renting an apartment</strong>.\n\nQ: What do you like about living there?\nA: I like living there because I think the house <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reflects my personality</strong>: I decorated it myself, I chose all the furniture, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">everything is where I want it to be</strong>.\n\nQ: What sort of accommodation would you most like to live in?\nA: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">If I could</strong>, I'd buy a bigger house near a beach or in the mountains, preferably somewhere <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with a warm climate</strong>.","topic":"Home & Accommodation","phrases":[{"en":"renting an apartment","context":"I've lived there for about five years. It's the first house that I've owned; before that I was renting an apartment."},{"en":"reflects my personality","context":"I like living there because I think the house reflects my personality: I decorated it myself, I chose all the furniture, and everything is where I want it to be."},{"en":"everything is where I want it to be","context":"I like living there because I think the house reflects my personality: I decorated it myself, I chose all the furniture, and everything is where I want it to be."},{"en":"If I could","context":"If I could, I'd buy a bigger house near a beach or in the mountains, preferably somewhere with a warm climate."},{"en":"with a warm climate","context":"If I could, I'd buy a bigger house near a beach or in the mountains, preferably somewhere with a warm climate."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Home & Accommodation","id":"speaking-part-1-039","_displayNo":"#038","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-039-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-039-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-039-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I've lived there for about five years. It is the first house I have owned. Before moving there, I lived in a flat that I rented.","phrases":[{"en":"rented","meaning":"Paid to use a home owned by someone else."}],"question":"How long have you lived there?","html":"I&#x27;ve lived there for about five years. It is the first house I have owned. Before moving there, I lived in a flat that I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rented</strong>."},{"text":"I like it because it feels like my own place. I decorated it and chose the furniture myself, so everything looks the way I want it to.","phrases":[{"en":"decorated","meaning":"Chose colours and other things to make a room look attractive."}],"question":"What do you like about living there?","html":"I like it because it feels like my own place. I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">decorated</strong> it and chose the furniture myself, so everything looks the way I want it to."},{"text":"I'd love a bigger house near the beach or in the mountains if I could buy one. Ideally, it would be somewhere warm because I prefer that kind of weather.","phrases":[{"en":"Ideally","meaning":"In the best situation you can imagine."}],"question":"What sort of accommodation would you most like to live in?","html":"I&#x27;d love a bigger house near the beach or in the mountains if I could buy one. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Ideally</strong>, it would be somewhere warm because I prefer that kind of weather."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-039-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-039-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-039-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Your favourite room and what you would change","prompt":"Do you live in a house or an apartment?\nWhich is your favourite room in your home?\nWould you change anything about your home?\nWould you like to move to a different home in the future?","answer":"Q: Do you live in a house or an apartment?\nA: I live in a semi-detached house with three bedrooms in a suburb of Manchester. I've lived there for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">just over a year</strong>.\n\nQ: Which is your favourite room in your home?\nA: I don't really have a favourite room. But <strong class=\"key-phrase\">if I had to choose</strong>, I'd say the living room because that's where I go to sit and relax.\n\nQ: Would you change anything about your home?\nA: Yes, I've <strong class=\"key-phrase\">been meaning to</strong> redecorate it since I moved in last year. It needs a new kitchen, so that's what I'd change first.\n\nQ: Would you like to move to a different home in the future?\nA: Yes, I'd definitely move again <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at some point</strong> if I could afford to. I'd like to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">live in the countryside</strong>, or maybe in a different city or country.","topic":"Home & Accommodation","phrases":[{"en":"just over a year","context":"I live in a semi-detached house with three bedrooms in a suburb of Manchester. I've lived there for just over a year."},{"en":"if I had to choose","context":"I don't really have a favourite room. But if I had to choose, I'd say the living room because that's where I go to sit and relax."},{"en":"been meaning to","context":"Yes, I've been meaning to redecorate it since I moved in last year. It needs a new kitchen, so that's what I'd change first."},{"en":"at some point","context":"Yes, I'd definitely move again at some point if I could afford to. I'd like to live in the countryside, or maybe in a different city or country."},{"en":"live in the countryside","context":"Yes, I'd definitely move again at some point if I could afford to. I'd like to live in the countryside, or maybe in a different city or country."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Home & Accommodation","id":"speaking-part-1-040","_displayNo":"#039","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-040-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-040-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-040-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-040-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I live in a three-bedroom house outside central Manchester. It is joined to one other house, and I have lived there for a little over a year.","phrases":[{"en":"a little over","meaning":"Slightly more than a particular amount."}],"question":"Do you live in a house or an apartment?","html":"I live in a three-bedroom house outside central Manchester. It is joined to one other house, and I have lived there for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a little over</strong> a year."},{"text":"I don't have a strong favourite, but I would probably choose the living room. It is the place where I sit down and relax, so I enjoy spending time there.","phrases":[{"en":"living room","meaning":"The room at home used for sitting and relaxing."}],"question":"Which is your favourite room in your home?","html":"I don&#x27;t have a strong favourite, but I would probably choose the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">living room</strong>. It is the place where I sit down and relax, so I enjoy spending time there."},{"text":"Yes, I want to decorate the house again. I have planned to do that since moving in last year. The kitchen needs replacing, so I would start there.","phrases":[{"en":"replacing","meaning":"Removing something and putting a new one in its place."}],"question":"Would you change anything about your home?","html":"Yes, I want to decorate the house again. I have planned to do that since moving in last year. The kitchen needs <strong class=\"key-phrase\">replacing</strong>, so I would start there."},{"text":"Yes, if I had enough money. I'd like to try living in the countryside, or perhaps another city or country, at some time in the future.","phrases":[{"en":"countryside","meaning":"Areas outside towns and cities, with fields or natural land."}],"question":"Would you like to move to a different home in the future?","html":"Yes, if I had enough money. I&#x27;d like to try living in the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">countryside</strong>, or perhaps another city or country, at some time in the future."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-040-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-040-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-040-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-040-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"House or apartment, and changes you would make","prompt":"Do you live in a house or an apartment?\nWhich room do you like most in your house?\nIs there anything about your house that you would like to change?","answer":"Q: Do you live in a house or an apartment?\nA: I live in a semi-detached house with three bedrooms and a small garden, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comes in handy</strong> when the weather's nice.\n\nQ: Which room do you like most in your house?\nA: My favourite room is the kitchen because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">looks out onto</strong> the back garden, and it's on the side of the house that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catches the afternoon sun</strong>.\n\nQ: Is there anything about your house that you would like to change?\nA: Well, it would be nice if it were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a detached house</strong> so that we could have windows on the wall that we currently share with our neighbours.","topic":"Home & Accommodation","phrases":[{"en":"comes in handy","context":"I live in a semi-detached house with three bedrooms and a small garden, which comes in handy when the weather's nice."},{"en":"looks out onto","context":"My favourite room is the kitchen because it looks out onto the back garden, and it's on the side of the house that catches the afternoon sun."},{"en":"catches the afternoon sun","context":"My favourite room is the kitchen because it looks out onto the back garden, and it's on the side of the house that catches the afternoon sun."},{"en":"a detached house","context":"Well, it would be nice if it were a detached house so that we could have windows on the wall that we currently share with our neighbours."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Home & Accommodation","id":"speaking-part-1-041","_displayNo":"#040","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-041-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-041-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-041-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I live in a house that shares one wall with another house. It has three bedrooms and a small garden, which is useful when the weather is pleasant.","phrases":[{"en":"shares one wall","meaning":"Has a wall that also forms part of a neighbouring building."}],"question":"Do you live in a house or an apartment?","html":"I live in a house that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shares one wall</strong> with another house. It has three bedrooms and a small garden, which is useful when the weather is pleasant."},{"text":"My favourite room is the kitchen. I can see the back garden from there, and the room gets sunlight in the afternoon, which makes it a pleasant place.","phrases":[{"en":"sunlight","meaning":"Natural light from the sun."}],"question":"Which room do you like most in your house?","html":"My favourite room is the kitchen. I can see the back garden from there, and the room gets <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sunlight</strong> in the afternoon, which makes it a pleasant place."},{"text":"I'd like the house to be separate from our neighbours' home. Then we could put windows in the wall we currently share, which would let in more light.","phrases":[{"en":"separate from","meaning":"Not joined or attached to something else."}],"question":"Is there anything about your house that you would like to change?","html":"I&#x27;d like the house to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">separate from</strong> our neighbours&#x27; home. Then we could put windows in the wall we currently share, which would let in more light."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-041-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-041-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-041-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"The town you come from","prompt":"Which town or city do you come from?\nWhat's the best thing about living there?\nIs there anything you don't like?\nWhich places would you recommend to a tourist?","answer":"Q: Which town or city do you come from?\nA: I come from Hanoi, a large city in northern Vietnam. It combines historic neighbourhoods with rapidly developing business districts, so it feels both traditional and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">constantly in motion</strong>.\n\nQ: What's the best thing about living there?\nA: I think the best thing is the variety — there are parks, cafes, cultural sites and excellent food, all within a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relatively small</strong> area. I also like that daily services are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">within easy reach</strong>.\n\nQ: Is there anything you don't like?\nA: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Traffic congestion</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">air pollution</strong> are the main drawbacks. At peak times, even a short journey can be exhausting, so the city needs a more reliable <strong class=\"key-phrase\">public transport network</strong>.\n\nQ: Which places would you recommend to a tourist?\nA: I'd recommend the Old Quarter, Hoan Kiem Lake and the Temple of Literature. Together they give visitors <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a balanced picture</strong> of the city's history and everyday life.","topic":"Hometown","phrases":[{"en":"constantly in motion","context":"I come from Hanoi, a large city in northern Vietnam. It combines historic neighbourhoods with rapidly developing business districts, so it feels both traditional and constantly in motion."},{"en":"relatively small","context":"I think the best thing is the variety — there are parks, cafes, cultural sites and excellent food, all within a relatively small area. I also like that daily services are within easy reach."},{"en":"within easy reach","context":"I think the best thing is the variety — there are parks, cafes, cultural sites and excellent food, all within a relatively small area. I also like that daily services are within easy reach."},{"en":"Traffic congestion","context":"Traffic congestion and air pollution are the main drawbacks. At peak times, even a short journey can be exhausting, so the city needs a more reliable public transport network."},{"en":"air pollution","context":"Traffic congestion and air pollution are the main drawbacks. At peak times, even a short journey can be exhausting, so the city needs a more reliable public transport network."},{"en":"public transport network","context":"Traffic congestion and air pollution are the main drawbacks. At peak times, even a short journey can be exhausting, so the city needs a more reliable public transport network."},{"en":"a balanced picture","context":"I'd recommend the Old Quarter, Hoan Kiem Lake and the Temple of Literature. Together they give visitors a balanced picture of the city's history and everyday life."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"City","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-007","_displayNo":"#041","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-007-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-007-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-007-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-007-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I'm from Hanoi, a large city in the north of Vietnam. It has old neighbourhoods as well as new business areas, so it feels traditional but is also changing quickly.","phrases":[{"en":"as well as","meaning":"In addition to something else."}],"question":"Which town or city do you come from?","html":"I&#x27;m from Hanoi, a large city in the north of Vietnam. It has old neighbourhoods <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as well as</strong> new business areas, so it feels traditional but is also changing quickly."},{"text":"The best thing is the choice of things to do. There are parks, cafes, interesting cultural places and great food close together. Shops and other daily services are also easy to reach.","phrases":[{"en":"easy to reach","meaning":"Not difficult to get to."}],"question":"What's the best thing about living there?","html":"The best thing is the choice of things to do. There are parks, cafes, interesting cultural places and great food close together. Shops and other daily services are also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easy to reach</strong>."},{"text":"I dislike the heavy traffic and dirty air. Travelling can be tiring during busy hours, even for a short distance. I think the city needs more dependable public transport.","phrases":[{"en":"heavy traffic","meaning":"A large number of vehicles on the roads."}],"question":"Is there anything you don't like?","html":"I dislike the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">heavy traffic</strong> and dirty air. Travelling can be tiring during busy hours, even for a short distance. I think the city needs more dependable public transport."},{"text":"I'd suggest the Old Quarter, Hoan Kiem Lake and the Temple of Literature. These places help visitors learn about Hanoi's history and see what daily life in the city is like.","phrases":[{"en":"daily life","meaning":"The usual activities and experiences of each day."}],"question":"Which places would you recommend to a tourist?","html":"I&#x27;d suggest the Old Quarter, Hoan Kiem Lake and the Temple of Literature. These places help visitors learn about Hanoi&#x27;s history and see what <strong class=\"key-phrase\">daily life</strong> in the city is like."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-007-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-007-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-007-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-007-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Your hometown and whether you would move","prompt":"Where are you from?\nDo you like your hometown?\nWould you prefer to live somewhere else?\nDo you think your hometown is a good place for young people?","answer":"Q: Where are you from?\nA: I'm from Manchester, which is a city in the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">north west of England</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you like your hometown?\nA: Yes, I like living in Manchester because it's where most of my friends live, and because there are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">plenty of things to do</strong> there. The only thing I don't like is the weather.\n\nQ: Would you prefer to live somewhere else?\nA: For the moment I'm happy living here, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at some point</strong> I'd like to live in a country with a warmer climate, and I'd like to live near a beach!\n\nQ: Do you think your hometown is a good place for young people?\nA: Yes, Manchester has lots of things for young people, such as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sports facilities</strong>, music and cinemas.","topic":"Hometown","phrases":[{"en":"north west of England","context":"I'm from Manchester, which is a city in the north west of England."},{"en":"plenty of things to do","context":"Yes, I like living in Manchester because it's where most of my friends live, and because there are plenty of things to do there. The only thing I don't like is the weather."},{"en":"at some point","context":"For the moment I'm happy living here, but at some point I'd like to live in a country with a warmer climate, and I'd like to live near a beach!"},{"en":"sports facilities","context":"Yes, Manchester has lots of things for young people, such as sports facilities, music and cinemas."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Hometown","id":"speaking-part-1-042","_displayNo":"#042","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-042-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-042-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-042-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-042-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I'm from Manchester, a city in the north-west of England. That is my hometown, and it is the place I think of when someone asks where I'm from.","phrases":[{"en":"hometown","meaning":"The town or city you come from."}],"question":"Where are you from?","html":"I&#x27;m from Manchester, a city in the north-west of England. That is my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hometown</strong>, and it is the place I think of when someone asks where I&#x27;m from."},{"text":"Yes, I enjoy living there because most of my friends are nearby and there are many things to do. The weather is the only part I don't really like.","phrases":[{"en":"nearby","meaning":"Not far away."}],"question":"Do you like your hometown?","html":"Yes, I enjoy living there because most of my friends are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nearby</strong> and there are many things to do. The weather is the only part I don&#x27;t really like."},{"text":"I'm happy here for now, but I'd like to live somewhere warmer in the future. A place near a beach would be especially nice because I like that idea.","phrases":[{"en":"for now","meaning":"At the present time, even if things may change later."}],"question":"Would you prefer to live somewhere else?","html":"I&#x27;m happy here <strong class=\"key-phrase\">for now</strong>, but I&#x27;d like to live somewhere warmer in the future. A place near a beach would be especially nice because I like that idea."},{"text":"Yes, I think it is. Manchester has places to play sport, enjoy music and watch films. Young people have plenty of different activities to choose from.","phrases":[{"en":"plenty of","meaning":"A large or more than enough amount of something."}],"question":"Do you think your hometown is a good place for young people?","html":"Yes, I think it is. Manchester has places to play sport, enjoy music and watch films. Young people have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">plenty of</strong> different activities to choose from."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-042-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-042-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-042-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-042-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Where you were born and how it has changed","prompt":"Describe a place where you were born?\nTell me about the main types of buildings there?\nHow have buildings in your hometown changed since you were a child?\nCan you describe your hometown?","answer":"Q: Describe a place where you were born?\nA: I was born in Hanoi, a historic city in northern Vietnam. It's densely populated, but the lakes, old streets and mature trees give a lot of areas <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a distinctive atmosphere</strong>.\n\nQ: Tell me about the main types of buildings there?\nA: The city's got a real mix — traditional houses, French colonial buildings, apartment blocks and modern offices. It's more a case of different stages of development than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a single architectural style</strong>.\n\nQ: How have buildings in your hometown changed since you were a child?\nA: There are a lot more high-rise apartments and commercial buildings now. The city's gained some modern facilities, but some neighbourhoods have lost part of their <strong class=\"key-phrase\">original character</strong>.\n\nQ: Can you describe your hometown?\nA: Hanoi's lively, culturally rich and sometimes a bit chaotic. It's got great food and services, but the traffic and pollution <strong class=\"key-phrase\">place considerable pressure on</strong> daily life.","topic":"Hometown","phrases":[{"en":"a distinctive atmosphere","context":"I was born in Hanoi, a historic city in northern Vietnam. It's densely populated, but the lakes, old streets and mature trees give a lot of areas a distinctive atmosphere."},{"en":"a single architectural style","context":"The city's got a real mix — traditional houses, French colonial buildings, apartment blocks and modern offices. It's more a case of different stages of development than a single architectural style."},{"en":"original character","context":"There are a lot more high-rise apartments and commercial buildings now. The city's gained some modern facilities, but some neighbourhoods have lost part of their original character."},{"en":"place considerable pressure on","context":"Hanoi's lively, culturally rich and sometimes a bit chaotic. It's got great food and services, but the traffic and pollution place considerable pressure on daily life."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Hometown","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-043","_displayNo":"#043","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-043-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-043-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-043-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-043-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I was born in Hanoi, an old city in northern Vietnam. A lot of people live there, but its lakes, old streets and large trees make many areas feel special.","phrases":[{"en":"northern","meaning":"In the north of a country or region."}],"question":"Describe a place where you were born?","html":"I was born in Hanoi, an old city in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">northern</strong> Vietnam. A lot of people live there, but its lakes, old streets and large trees make many areas feel special."},{"text":"There are traditional houses, French-style old buildings, blocks of flats and modern offices. The city has developed at different times, so there is a mixture of building styles.","phrases":[{"en":"a mixture of","meaning":"Several different things together."}],"question":"Tell me about the main types of buildings there?","html":"There are traditional houses, French-style old buildings, blocks of flats and modern offices. The city has developed at different times, so there is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a mixture of</strong> building styles."},{"text":"There are more tall flats and business buildings now. The city has better modern services, but some older neighbourhoods no longer have quite the same look and feeling.","phrases":[{"en":"neighbourhoods","meaning":"Areas of a town where people live."}],"question":"How have buildings in your hometown changed since you were a child?","html":"There are more tall flats and business buildings now. The city has better modern services, but some older <strong class=\"key-phrase\">neighbourhoods</strong> no longer have quite the same look and feeling."},{"text":"Hanoi is busy and full of culture, with good food and useful services. However, it can also feel crowded and confusing, and traffic and pollution make everyday life harder.","phrases":[{"en":"pollution","meaning":"Harmful substances or dirty air in the environment."}],"question":"Can you describe your hometown?","html":"Hanoi is busy and full of culture, with good food and useful services. However, it can also feel crowded and confusing, and traffic and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pollution</strong> make everyday life harder."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-043-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-043-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-043-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-043-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Your hometown and its well-known places","prompt":"Where do you come from?\nWhere do you live?\nCan you tell me something about your hometown?\nCan you name some well-known scenic places in your city?","answer":"Q: Where do you come from?\nA: I come from Hanoi, Vietnam's capital. It's a really energetic city with a long history, a strong food culture, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a pace of development</strong> that's visible almost everywhere you look.\n\nQ: Where do you live?\nA: I live in Hanoi, in an urban district with easy access to shops and public services. It's busy, but it's convenient enough to make daily errands <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relatively straightforward</strong>.\n\nQ: Can you tell me something about your hometown?\nA: My hometown's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a city of contrasts</strong>: historic streets sit right next to modern districts, and quiet lakes are surrounded by really busy roads. That mixture is what gives Hanoi its character, I think.\n\nQ: Can you name some well-known scenic places in your city?\nA: Hoan Kiem Lake, the Old Quarter and West Lake are probably the best-known spots. They each show a different side of the city and they're <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easy for visitors to explore</strong>.","topic":"Hometown","phrases":[{"en":"a pace of development","context":"I come from Hanoi, Vietnam's capital. It's a really energetic city with a long history, a strong food culture, and a pace of development that's visible almost everywhere you look."},{"en":"relatively straightforward","context":"I live in Hanoi, in an urban district with easy access to shops and public services. It's busy, but it's convenient enough to make daily errands relatively straightforward."},{"en":"a city of contrasts","context":"My hometown's a city of contrasts: historic streets sit right next to modern districts, and quiet lakes are surrounded by really busy roads. That mixture is what gives Hanoi its character, I think."},{"en":"easy for visitors to explore","context":"Hoan Kiem Lake, the Old Quarter and West Lake are probably the best-known spots. They each show a different side of the city and they're easy for visitors to explore."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Hometown","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-044","_displayNo":"#044","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-044-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-044-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-044-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-044-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I'm from Hanoi, the capital of Vietnam. It is a lively city with a long history and great food. Many parts of it are changing quickly as new buildings and businesses appear.","phrases":[{"en":"lively","meaning":"Full of energy and activity."}],"question":"Where do you come from?","html":"I&#x27;m from Hanoi, the capital of Vietnam. It is a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lively</strong> city with a long history and great food. Many parts of it are changing quickly as new buildings and businesses appear."},{"text":"I live in a busy district of Hanoi. Shops and public services are close by, so although it can be crowded, doing everyday tasks is fairly easy.","phrases":[{"en":"close by","meaning":"Near where you are."}],"question":"Where do you live?","html":"I live in a busy district of Hanoi. Shops and public services are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">close by</strong>, so although it can be crowded, doing everyday tasks is fairly easy."},{"text":"Hanoi has both old streets and modern areas. There are peaceful lakes beside busy roads, too. I think these different sides of the city are what make it interesting.","phrases":[{"en":"peaceful","meaning":"Quiet and calm."}],"question":"Can you tell me something about your hometown?","html":"Hanoi has both old streets and modern areas. There are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">peaceful</strong> lakes beside busy roads, too. I think these different sides of the city are what make it interesting."},{"text":"Hoan Kiem Lake, the Old Quarter and West Lake are well-known places. They are easy for visitors to explore, and each gives a different impression of Hanoi.","phrases":[{"en":"explore","meaning":"Visit a place and learn about it by looking around."}],"question":"Can you name some well-known scenic places in your city?","html":"Hoan Kiem Lake, the Old Quarter and West Lake are well-known places. They are easy for visitors to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">explore</strong>, and each gives a different impression of Hanoi."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-044-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-044-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-044-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-044-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"How you use the internet and its advantages","prompt":"Do you use the internet much during the day?\nWhich website have you visited recently?\nWhat are some advantages of the internet?\nDo people in your country use the internet a lot?","answer":"Q: Do you use the internet much during the day?\nA: Yes, I'm on it throughout the day really — for work, communication and research. I try to use it deliberately though, because constant notifications can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fragment my attention</strong>.\n\nQ: Which website have you visited recently?\nA: I recently visited an educational website to compare language-learning resources. I liked the clear structure, and the fact that the information was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">supported by practical examples</strong>.\n\nQ: What are some advantages of the internet?\nA: The internet gives people rapid <strong class=\"key-phrase\">access to information</strong>, services and communication. I'd say its greatest advantage is that it can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lower the barrier to entry</strong> for education and business.\n\nQ: Do people in your country use the internet a lot?\nA: Yes, definitely, especially in the cities, where people rely on it for banking, shopping, entertainment and work. It's pretty widespread now, although digital skills and good-quality access are still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unevenly distributed</strong>.","topic":"Internet","phrases":[{"en":"fragment my attention","context":"Yes, I'm on it throughout the day really — for work, communication and research. I try to use it deliberately though, because constant notifications can fragment my attention."},{"en":"supported by practical examples","context":"I recently visited an educational website to compare language-learning resources. I liked the clear structure, and the fact that the information was supported by practical examples."},{"en":"access to information","context":"The internet gives people rapid access to information, services and communication. I'd say its greatest advantage is that it can lower the barrier to entry for education and business."},{"en":"lower the barrier to entry","context":"The internet gives people rapid access to information, services and communication. I'd say its greatest advantage is that it can lower the barrier to entry for education and business."},{"en":"unevenly distributed","context":"Yes, definitely, especially in the cities, where people rely on it for banking, shopping, entertainment and work. It's pretty widespread now, although digital skills and good-quality access are still unevenly distributed."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Internet","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-045","_displayNo":"#045","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-045-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-045-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-045-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-045-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, I use it throughout the day for work, messages and research. I try to have a clear purpose because too many alerts can make it difficult to focus.","phrases":[{"en":"a clear purpose","meaning":"A definite reason for doing something."}],"question":"Do you use the internet much during the day?","html":"Yes, I use it throughout the day for work, messages and research. I try to have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clear purpose</strong> because too many alerts can make it difficult to focus."},{"text":"I recently used an education website to compare materials for learning languages. It was easy to follow, and the examples helped me understand how I could use the information.","phrases":[{"en":"easy to follow","meaning":"Simple to understand or use."}],"question":"Which website have you visited recently?","html":"I recently used an education website to compare materials for learning languages. It was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easy to follow</strong>, and the examples helped me understand how I could use the information."},{"text":"It helps people find information, use services and communicate quickly. One big benefit is that it makes education and starting a business more accessible to people who had fewer chances before.","phrases":[{"en":"accessible","meaning":"Easy for people to get or use."}],"question":"What are some advantages of the internet?","html":"It helps people find information, use services and communicate quickly. One big benefit is that it makes education and starting a business more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accessible</strong> to people who had fewer chances before."},{"text":"Yes, especially in cities. People use it for shopping, banking, work and entertainment. However, not everyone has the same level of internet skills or a good connection.","phrases":[{"en":"connection","meaning":"The link that allows a device to use the internet."}],"question":"Do people in your country use the internet a lot?","html":"Yes, especially in cities. People use it for shopping, banking, work and entertainment. However, not everyone has the same level of internet skills or a good <strong class=\"key-phrase\">connection</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-045-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-045-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-045-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-045-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"What makes you laugh and why it matters","prompt":"What kinds of thing make you laugh?\nDo you like making other people laugh?\nDo you think it's important for people to laugh?\nIs laughing the same as feeling happy?","answer":"Q: What kinds of thing make you laugh?\nA: I laugh most when I'm with friends talking about funny things that have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">happened to</strong> us. I also like watching comedians and comedy films.\n\nQ: Do you like making other people laugh?\nA: Yes, it's a nice feeling when you can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make someone laugh</strong> because you can see that you've made them happy. It's great to share a funny moment with someone.\n\nQ: Do you think it's important for people to laugh?\nA: Yes, of course. It's important to see the funny side of things; humour helps us not to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take life too seriously</strong>. I think that laughter is good for our health.\n\nQ: Is laughing the same as feeling happy?\nA: It's not exactly the same because you can be happy without laughing, and sometimes we laugh when something bad has happened; <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of humour</strong> can help us to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cope with difficult situations</strong>.","topic":"Laughing","phrases":[{"en":"happened to","context":"I laugh most when I'm with friends talking about funny things that have happened to us. I also like watching comedians and comedy films."},{"en":"make someone laugh","context":"Yes, it's a nice feeling when you can make someone laugh because you can see that you've made them happy. It's great to share a funny moment with someone."},{"en":"take life too seriously","context":"Yes, of course. It's important to see the funny side of things; humour helps us not to take life too seriously. I think that laughter is good for our health."},{"en":"a sense of humour","context":"It's not exactly the same because you can be happy without laughing, and sometimes we laugh when something bad has happened; a sense of humour can help us to cope with difficult situations."},{"en":"cope with difficult situations","context":"It's not exactly the same because you can be happy without laughing, and sometimes we laugh when something bad has happened; a sense of humour can help us to cope with difficult situations."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Laughing","id":"speaking-part-1-047","_displayNo":"#046","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-047-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-047-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-047-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-047-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Funny stories with friends make me laugh the most, especially when we talk about things that happened to us. I also enjoy watching comedians and funny films.","phrases":[{"en":"comedians","meaning":"People whose job is to make an audience laugh."}],"question":"What kinds of thing make you laugh?","html":"Funny stories with friends make me laugh the most, especially when we talk about things that happened to us. I also enjoy watching <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comedians</strong> and funny films."},{"text":"Yes, I enjoy it because it feels good to make someone happy. Sharing a joke or a funny moment can be a nice way to connect with another person.","phrases":[{"en":"connect with","meaning":"Build a friendly or understanding relationship with someone."}],"question":"Do you like making other people laugh?","html":"Yes, I enjoy it because it feels good to make someone happy. Sharing a joke or a funny moment can be a nice way to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">connect with</strong> another person."},{"text":"Yes, laughter helps us enjoy life and not worry about everything too much. Being able to find something funny can help us relax, and I think that is good for us.","phrases":[{"en":"relax","meaning":"Feel calmer and less worried."}],"question":"Do you think it's important for people to laugh?","html":"Yes, laughter helps us enjoy life and not worry about everything too much. Being able to find something funny can help us <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relax</strong>, and I think that is good for us."},{"text":"Not exactly. You can feel happy without laughing, and people sometimes laugh during difficult times. Humour can help us deal with problems even when we are not feeling very happy.","phrases":[{"en":"Humour","meaning":"The ability to find things funny or make people laugh."}],"question":"Is laughing the same as feeling happy?","html":"Not exactly. You can feel happy without laughing, and people sometimes laugh during difficult times. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Humour</strong> can help us deal with problems even when we are not feeling very happy."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-047-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-047-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-047-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-047-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Using maps and asking for directions","prompt":"Do you ever use maps?\nWhen do people usually need to use a map?\nDo you prefer electronic or paper maps?\nDo you ever ask people for directions instead of using a map?","answer":"Q: Do you ever use maps?\nA: Yes. Whenever I go somewhere new, I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">plan my journey</strong> with the help of a map.\n\nQ: When do people usually need to use a map?\nA: I imagine that some people use a map every day if they travel to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">different places for work</strong>. Others might only use a map when they're on holiday.\n\nQ: Do you prefer electronic or paper maps?\nA: I still prefer paper maps for a long journey; I like being able to open the map out on a table and see the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">full journey ahead</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you ever ask people for directions instead of using a map?\nA: Only if I'm really lost. Whenever I ask for directions, I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">find it difficult to</strong> remember what the person said. So I prefer to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">find my own way</strong>.","topic":"Maps","phrases":[{"en":"plan my journey","context":"Yes. Whenever I go somewhere new, I plan my journey with the help of a map."},{"en":"different places for work","context":"I imagine that some people use a map every day if they travel to different places for work. Others might only use a map when they're on holiday."},{"en":"full journey ahead","context":"I still prefer paper maps for a long journey; I like being able to open the map out on a table and see the full journey ahead."},{"en":"find it difficult to","context":"Only if I'm really lost. Whenever I ask for directions, I find it difficult to remember what the person said. So I prefer to find my own way."},{"en":"find my own way","context":"Only if I'm really lost. Whenever I ask for directions, I find it difficult to remember what the person said. So I prefer to find my own way."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Maps","id":"speaking-part-1-048","_displayNo":"#047","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-048-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-048-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-048-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-048-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, I use maps when I visit a place I don't know. Looking at a map before I leave helps me plan how to get there.","phrases":[{"en":"plan","meaning":"Decide in advance how to do something."}],"question":"Do you ever use maps?","html":"Yes, I use maps when I visit a place I don&#x27;t know. Looking at a map before I leave helps me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">plan</strong> how to get there."},{"text":"Some people need maps regularly because their work takes them to different places. Others mainly use them on holiday when they are somewhere new and need help finding the way.","phrases":[{"en":"finding the way","meaning":"Working out how to reach a place."}],"question":"When do people usually need to use a map?","html":"Some people need maps regularly because their work takes them to different places. Others mainly use them on holiday when they are somewhere new and need help <strong class=\"key-phrase\">finding the way</strong>."},{"text":"For a long trip, I prefer a paper map. I can spread it out on a table and look at the whole route at once, which I find useful.","phrases":[{"en":"route","meaning":"The way you follow to travel from one place to another."}],"question":"Do you prefer electronic or paper maps?","html":"For a long trip, I prefer a paper map. I can spread it out on a table and look at the whole <strong class=\"key-phrase\">route</strong> at once, which I find useful."},{"text":"Only when I'm really lost. I often forget spoken directions, so I usually prefer using a map and working out the route by myself.","phrases":[{"en":"lost","meaning":"Not knowing where you are or how to get somewhere."}],"question":"Do you ever ask people for directions instead of using a map?","html":"Only when I&#x27;m really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lost</strong>. I often forget spoken directions, so I usually prefer using a map and working out the route by myself."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-048-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-048-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-048-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-048-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Studying mathematics and whether everyone needs it","prompt":"At what age did you start studying mathematics?\nDo you like mathematics?\nIs it necessary for everyone to learn mathematics?\nDo you prefer to use a calculator when doing mathematics?","answer":"Q: At what age did you start studying mathematics?\nA: I can't exactly remember, but it was probably in my first year at primary school when I was 5 years old. I probably learned <strong class=\"key-phrase\">simple addition</strong> at that age.\n\nQ: Do you like mathematics?\nA: I don't dislike maths, but I wouldn't say that I've ever really liked the subject either. I'm happy to do <strong class=\"key-phrase\">simple calculations</strong>, but my brain can't cope with the complex stuff!\n\nQ: Is it necessary for everyone to learn mathematics?\nA: Yes, I think it is. We all need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a basic grounding in maths</strong> so that we can do daily tasks, like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">managing our money</strong>, working out bills, and so on.\n\nQ: Do you prefer to use a calculator when doing mathematics?\nA: It depends. I quite like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exercising my brain</strong> with anything that's easy enough, but I use the calculator on my phone for anything tricky.","topic":"Mathematics","phrases":[{"en":"simple addition","context":"I can't exactly remember, but it was probably in my first year at primary school when I was 5 years old. I probably learned simple addition at that age."},{"en":"simple calculations","context":"I don't dislike maths, but I wouldn't say that I've ever really liked the subject either. I'm happy to do simple calculations, but my brain can't cope with the complex stuff!"},{"en":"a basic grounding in maths","context":"Yes, I think it is. We all need a basic grounding in maths so that we can do daily tasks, like managing our money, working out bills, and so on."},{"en":"managing our money","context":"Yes, I think it is. We all need a basic grounding in maths so that we can do daily tasks, like managing our money, working out bills, and so on."},{"en":"exercising my brain","context":"It depends. I quite like exercising my brain with anything that's easy enough, but I use the calculator on my phone for anything tricky."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Mathematics","id":"speaking-part-1-049","_displayNo":"#048","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-049-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-049-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-049-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-049-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I don't remember exactly, but I probably started at primary school when I was five. At that age, I think we learned simple things such as adding numbers together.","phrases":[{"en":"adding","meaning":"Putting numbers together to find a total."}],"question":"At what age did you start studying mathematics?","html":"I don&#x27;t remember exactly, but I probably started at primary school when I was five. At that age, I think we learned simple things such as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">adding</strong> numbers together."},{"text":"I don't mind basic maths, but it has never been my favourite subject. I can do simple calculations, while more difficult maths is hard for me to understand.","phrases":[{"en":"calculations","meaning":"Ways of using numbers to find an answer."}],"question":"Do you like mathematics?","html":"I don&#x27;t mind basic maths, but it has never been my favourite subject. I can do simple <strong class=\"key-phrase\">calculations</strong>, while more difficult maths is hard for me to understand."},{"text":"Yes, everyone needs some basic maths. We use it in ordinary life, for example when we manage money, check a bill or work out how much something costs.","phrases":[{"en":"work out","meaning":"Find an answer by thinking or calculating."}],"question":"Is it necessary for everyone to learn mathematics?","html":"Yes, everyone needs some basic maths. We use it in ordinary life, for example when we manage money, check a bill or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work out</strong> how much something costs."},{"text":"For easy sums, I like doing them in my head because it gives my brain some practice. If a calculation is more difficult, I use the calculator on my phone.","phrases":[{"en":"in my head","meaning":"By thinking, without writing or using a device."}],"question":"Do you prefer to use a calculator when doing mathematics?","html":"For easy sums, I like doing them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in my head</strong> because it gives my brain some practice. If a calculation is more difficult, I use the calculator on my phone."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-049-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-049-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-049-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-049-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Museums you have visited and would like to visit","prompt":"Which museums have you visited recently?\nWhat did you enjoy seeing there?\nWhich famous museum would you like to visit?\nWhich collections would you particularly like to see there?","answer":"Q: Which museums have you visited recently?\nA: I recently visited the Vietnam Museum of Ethnology in Hanoi. It shows the cultures of different ethnic groups through objects, photos and reconstructed houses, so the whole visit's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">highly informative</strong>.\n\nQ: What did you enjoy seeing there?\nA: I particularly enjoyed the traditional houses and everyday objects. They showed how climate, materials and local customs shape the way communities <strong class=\"key-phrase\">organise daily life</strong>.\n\nQ: Which famous museum would you like to visit?\nA: I'd love to visit the British Museum because its collection covers such an enormous span of history. Seeing the original objects would give me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a more tangible connection</strong> with the past.\n\nQ: Which collections would you particularly like to see there?\nA: I'd be most interested in the ancient civilisations and the history of writing. Those collections show how ideas travelled and how societies <strong class=\"key-phrase\">influenced one another</strong>.","topic":"Museums","phrases":[{"en":"highly informative","context":"I recently visited the Vietnam Museum of Ethnology in Hanoi. It shows the cultures of different ethnic groups through objects, photos and reconstructed houses, so the whole visit's highly informative."},{"en":"organise daily life","context":"I particularly enjoyed the traditional houses and everyday objects. They showed how climate, materials and local customs shape the way communities organise daily life."},{"en":"a more tangible connection","context":"I'd love to visit the British Museum because its collection covers such an enormous span of history. Seeing the original objects would give me a more tangible connection with the past."},{"en":"influenced one another","context":"I'd be most interested in the ancient civilisations and the history of writing. Those collections show how ideas travelled and how societies influenced one another."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Museum","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-050","_displayNo":"#049","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-050-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-050-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-050-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-050-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I recently visited the Vietnam Museum of Ethnology in Hanoi. It has objects, photos and examples of traditional houses from different ethnic groups, so I learned a lot during the visit.","phrases":[{"en":"ethnic groups","meaning":"Groups of people who share a cultural background or traditions."}],"question":"Which museums have you visited recently?","html":"I recently visited the Vietnam Museum of Ethnology in Hanoi. It has objects, photos and examples of traditional houses from different <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ethnic groups</strong>, so I learned a lot during the visit."},{"text":"I liked the traditional houses and everyday objects most. They helped me see how weather, local materials and traditions affect the way different groups of people live.","phrases":[{"en":"local materials","meaning":"Things available in an area that people can use to make objects or buildings."}],"question":"What did you enjoy seeing there?","html":"I liked the traditional houses and everyday objects most. They helped me see how weather, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">local materials</strong> and traditions affect the way different groups of people live."},{"text":"I'd love to visit the British Museum because it has objects from so many periods of history. Seeing the real things would help me feel closer to the past.","phrases":[{"en":"periods of history","meaning":"Different times or ages in the past."}],"question":"Which famous museum would you like to visit?","html":"I&#x27;d love to visit the British Museum because it has objects from so many <strong class=\"key-phrase\">periods of history</strong>. Seeing the real things would help me feel closer to the past."},{"text":"I'd especially like to see objects from ancient societies and the history of writing. They can show how people shared ideas and learned from other societies.","phrases":[{"en":"ancient","meaning":"Belonging to a time very far in the past."}],"question":"Which collections would you particularly like to see there?","html":"I&#x27;d especially like to see objects from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ancient</strong> societies and the history of writing. They can show how people shared ideas and learned from other societies."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-050-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-050-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-050-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-050-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"The music you listen to and going to concerts","prompt":"Do you like music?\nWhat kind of music did you listen to when you were young?\nWhen was the last time you went to a concert?\nWould you like to participate in a live music show?","answer":"Q: Do you like music?\nA: No, I don't really like music, and I don't listen to it. It's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not something that I've ever been interested in</strong>.\n\nQ: What kind of music did you listen to when you were young?\nA: I can't remember listening to much music as a child either. I suppose I must have heard some traditional music at festivals or parties, but I didn't <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take much notice of</strong> it.\n\nQ: When was the last time you went to a concert?\nA: I've never been to a music concert. Maybe I should try going to one; perhaps it would be interesting to see an orchestra playing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">classical music</strong>.\n\nQ: Would you like to participate in a live music show?\nA: No, definitely not. I don't <strong class=\"key-phrase\">play a musical instrument</strong> and I don't sing, so I can't imagine that I'd ever find myself performing to an audience.","topic":"Music","phrases":[{"en":"not something that I've ever been interested in","context":"No, I don't really like music, and I don't listen to it. It's not something that I've ever been interested in."},{"en":"take much notice of","context":"I can't remember listening to much music as a child either. I suppose I must have heard some traditional music at festivals or parties, but I didn't take much notice of it."},{"en":"classical music","context":"I've never been to a music concert. Maybe I should try going to one; perhaps it would be interesting to see an orchestra playing classical music."},{"en":"play a musical instrument","context":"No, definitely not. I don't play a musical instrument and I don't sing, so I can't imagine that I'd ever find myself performing to an audience."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Music","id":"speaking-part-1-051","_displayNo":"#050","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-051-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-051-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-051-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-051-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"No, I'm not really interested in music. I don't usually listen to it, and it has never been an important hobby or part of my life.","phrases":[{"en":"interested in","meaning":"Wanting to know about or spend time doing something."}],"question":"Do you like music?","html":"No, I&#x27;m not really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">interested in</strong> music. I don&#x27;t usually listen to it, and it has never been an important hobby or part of my life."},{"text":"I don't remember listening to much music as a child. I probably heard traditional music at celebrations and parties, but I didn't pay much attention to it.","phrases":[{"en":"pay much attention","meaning":"Notice or think about something carefully."}],"question":"What kind of music did you listen to when you were young?","html":"I don&#x27;t remember listening to much music as a child. I probably heard traditional music at celebrations and parties, but I didn&#x27;t <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pay much attention</strong> to it."},{"text":"I've never been to a music concert. I might try one in the future, though. Watching an orchestra play classical music could be an interesting experience.","phrases":[{"en":"orchestra","meaning":"A large group of musicians who play instruments together."}],"question":"When was the last time you went to a concert?","html":"I&#x27;ve never been to a music concert. I might try one in the future, though. Watching an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">orchestra</strong> play classical music could be an interesting experience."},{"text":"No, I wouldn't want to perform. I don't sing or play an instrument, so I would not feel comfortable getting on a stage in front of other people.","phrases":[{"en":"perform","meaning":"Sing, act or play music for an audience."}],"question":"Would you like to participate in a live music show?","html":"No, I wouldn&#x27;t want to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">perform</strong>. I don&#x27;t sing or play an instrument, so I would not feel comfortable getting on a stage in front of other people."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-051-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-051-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-051-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-051-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Listening habits and how your taste has changed","prompt":"How often do you listen to music?\nHas the music you like changed since you were younger?\nDo you prefer listening to music alone or with other people?\nDo you listen to music while you are working or studying?","answer":"Q: How often do you listen to music?\nA: Pretty much daily, though it is usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on in the background</strong> rather than something I sit down for. The commute is when I actually pay attention to it.\n\nQ: Has the music you like changed since you were younger?\nA: Quite a lot, yes. As a teenager I mostly listened to whatever my friends were into, and I have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drifted towards</strong> quieter things since then — acoustic stuff, a bit of jazz. I think my taste got narrower but deeper.\n\nQ: Do you prefer listening to music alone or with other people?\nA: Alone, definitely. Music is quite a private thing for me, and when other people are around I end up <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worrying about whether they like it</strong> instead of listening properly.\n\nQ: Do you listen to music while you are working or studying?\nA: Only if the work is routine. Anything that needs concentration and lyrics <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get in the way</strong>, so I either put on something instrumental or work in silence.","topic":"Music","phrases":[{"en":"on in the background","context":"Pretty much daily, though it is usually on in the background rather than something I sit down for. The commute is when I actually pay attention to it."},{"en":"drifted towards","context":"Quite a lot, yes. As a teenager I mostly listened to whatever my friends were into, and I have drifted towards quieter things since then — acoustic stuff, a bit of jazz. I think my taste got narrower but deeper."},{"en":"worrying about whether they like it","context":"Alone, definitely. Music is quite a private thing for me, and when other people are around I end up worrying about whether they like it instead of listening properly."},{"en":"get in the way","context":"Only if the work is routine. Anything that needs concentration and lyrics get in the way, so I either put on something instrumental or work in silence."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Music","id":"speaking-part-1-052","_displayNo":"#051","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-052-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-052-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-052-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-052-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I listen almost every day, usually while doing something else. I pay more attention to the music when I travel to and from work, because that is time I can really listen.","phrases":[{"en":"to and from work","meaning":"Going to your workplace and returning home."}],"question":"How often do you listen to music?","html":"I listen almost every day, usually while doing something else. I pay more attention to the music when I travel <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to and from work</strong>, because that is time I can really listen."},{"text":"Yes, quite a lot. As a teenager, I listened to the music my friends liked. Now I prefer quieter music, such as acoustic songs and some jazz, and know those styles better.","phrases":[{"en":"acoustic","meaning":"Using instruments whose sound is not produced electronically."}],"question":"Has the music you like changed since you were younger?","html":"Yes, quite a lot. As a teenager, I listened to the music my friends liked. Now I prefer quieter music, such as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">acoustic</strong> songs and some jazz, and know those styles better."},{"text":"I prefer listening alone. Music feels quite personal to me, and with other people I worry about whether they enjoy my choice instead of simply enjoying it myself.","phrases":[{"en":"personal","meaning":"Connected with your own feelings, taste or private life."}],"question":"Do you prefer listening to music alone or with other people?","html":"I prefer listening alone. Music feels quite <strong class=\"key-phrase\">personal</strong> to me, and with other people I worry about whether they enjoy my choice instead of simply enjoying it myself."},{"text":"Only when the task is simple and familiar. Words in songs can distract me when I need to think, so I choose music without singing or work with no music at all.","phrases":[{"en":"distract me","meaning":"Take my attention away from what I am doing."}],"question":"Do you listen to music while you are working or studying?","html":"Only when the task is simple and familiar. Words in songs can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">distract me</strong> when I need to think, so I choose music without singing or work with no music at all."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-052-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-052-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-052-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-052-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Instruments you enjoy and learning at school","prompt":"Which instruments do you like listening to most and why?\nHave you ever learned to play a musical instrument at school and why?\nDo you think children should learn to play a musical instrument at school?\nHow easy would it be to learn to play an instrument without a teacher?","answer":"Q: Which instruments do you like listening to most and why?\nA: I enjoy the piano and acoustic guitar most. Both can sound intimate or powerful, and they work across loads of musical styles without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overwhelming the melody</strong>.\n\nQ: Have you ever learned to play a musical instrument at school and why?\nA: I had a few basic music lessons at school but never really got good at it. Even so, they taught me rhythm and showed me how much <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consistent practice</strong> a musician needs.\n\nQ: Do you think children should learn to play a musical instrument at school?\nA: Yes, at least at a basic level. It's great for their listening, coordination and discipline, and it gives them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a creative form of expression</strong> too.\n\nQ: How easy would it be to learn to play an instrument without a teacher?\nA: It's possible with videos and apps, but a teacher can correct your technique before bad habits set in. Self-study works best when you've already got <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clear practice plan</strong>.","topic":"Music","phrases":[{"en":"overwhelming the melody","context":"I enjoy the piano and acoustic guitar most. Both can sound intimate or powerful, and they work across loads of musical styles without overwhelming the melody."},{"en":"consistent practice","context":"I had a few basic music lessons at school but never really got good at it. Even so, they taught me rhythm and showed me how much consistent practice a musician needs."},{"en":"a creative form of expression","context":"Yes, at least at a basic level. It's great for their listening, coordination and discipline, and it gives them a creative form of expression too."},{"en":"a clear practice plan","context":"It's possible with videos and apps, but a teacher can correct your technique before bad habits set in. Self-study works best when you've already got a clear practice plan."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Music","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-053","_displayNo":"#052","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-053-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-053-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-053-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-053-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I most enjoy piano and acoustic guitar. They can sound gentle or strong and fit many styles of music, while still allowing me to hear the tune clearly.","phrases":[{"en":"tune","meaning":"The main series of notes in a piece of music."}],"question":"Which instruments do you like listening to most and why?","html":"I most enjoy piano and acoustic guitar. They can sound gentle or strong and fit many styles of music, while still allowing me to hear the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tune</strong> clearly."},{"text":"I had a few simple music lessons at school, but I never became very good. Still, I learned about rhythm and realised that musicians need to practise regularly.","phrases":[{"en":"rhythm","meaning":"The pattern of beats in music."}],"question":"Have you ever learned to play a musical instrument at school and why?","html":"I had a few simple music lessons at school, but I never became very good. Still, I learned about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rhythm</strong> and realised that musicians need to practise regularly."},{"text":"Yes, I think they should learn the basics. Playing an instrument helps children listen carefully, control their movements and practise regularly. It also gives them a way to be creative.","phrases":[{"en":"creative","meaning":"Able to use imagination to make or express something."}],"question":"Do you think children should learn to play a musical instrument at school?","html":"Yes, I think they should learn the basics. Playing an instrument helps children listen carefully, control their movements and practise regularly. It also gives them a way to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">creative</strong>."},{"text":"You can learn from videos and apps, but it may be harder without a teacher to correct mistakes. Learning alone works better if you have a clear plan for practice.","phrases":[{"en":"correct mistakes","meaning":"Show what is wrong and help make it right."}],"question":"How easy would it be to learn to play an instrument without a teacher?","html":"You can learn from videos and apps, but it may be harder without a teacher to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">correct mistakes</strong>. Learning alone works better if you have a clear plan for practice."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-053-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-053-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-053-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-053-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"How you were named and what names mean","prompt":"How did your parents choose your name?\nDoes your name have any special meaning?\nIs your name common or unusual in your country?\nIf you could change your name, would you?","answer":"Q: How did your parents choose your name?\nA: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">To be honest</strong>, I'm not sure how they chose it; I've never asked them. I suppose it was just a name that they both liked, and maybe they thought it suited me.\n\nQ: Does your name have any special meaning?\nA: It might do, but I don't know what that meaning is; I've never <strong class=\"key-phrase\">looked it up</strong>. Maybe I'll google it later today; you've <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made me curious to find out</strong>!\n\nQ: Is your name common or unusual in your country?\nA: Yes, it's quite a common name. I remember that there were two other people with the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">same name as me</strong> in my class at school.\n\nQ: If you could change your name, would you?\nA: No, I definitely wouldn't. My name is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">part of my identity</strong> now, and it would feel strange to suddenly change it. I don't think my friends and family would like that either.","topic":"Names","phrases":[{"en":"To be honest","context":"To be honest, I'm not sure how they chose it; I've never asked them. I suppose it was just a name that they both liked, and maybe they thought it suited me."},{"en":"looked it up","context":"It might do, but I don't know what that meaning is; I've never looked it up. Maybe I'll google it later today; you've made me curious to find out!"},{"en":"made me curious to find out","context":"It might do, but I don't know what that meaning is; I've never looked it up. Maybe I'll google it later today; you've made me curious to find out!"},{"en":"same name as me","context":"Yes, it's quite a common name. I remember that there were two other people with the same name as me in my class at school."},{"en":"part of my identity","context":"No, I definitely wouldn't. My name is part of my identity now, and it would feel strange to suddenly change it. I don't think my friends and family would like that either."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Names","id":"speaking-part-1-054","_displayNo":"#053","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-054-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-054-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-054-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-054-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I'm not sure because I've never asked my parents. I imagine they chose a name that they both liked and thought would be right for me.","phrases":[{"en":"imagine","meaning":"Think something is probably true without knowing for certain."}],"question":"How did your parents choose your name?","html":"I&#x27;m not sure because I&#x27;ve never asked my parents. I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">imagine</strong> they chose a name that they both liked and thought would be right for me."},{"text":"It might have a special meaning, but I haven't checked. I may search for it online later because this question has made me want to find out.","phrases":[{"en":"find out","meaning":"Discover or learn some information."}],"question":"Does your name have any special meaning?","html":"It might have a special meaning, but I haven&#x27;t checked. I may search for it online later because this question has made me want to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">find out</strong>."},{"text":"It is quite common. When I was at school, there were two other people in my class with the same name, so it wasn't unusual there.","phrases":[{"en":"common","meaning":"Happening often or found in many people or places."}],"question":"Is your name common or unusual in your country?","html":"It is quite <strong class=\"key-phrase\">common</strong>. When I was at school, there were two other people in my class with the same name, so it wasn&#x27;t unusual there."},{"text":"No, I wouldn't change it. My name feels like part of who I am, so a different one would seem strange. I don't think my family or friends would like it either.","phrases":[{"en":"part of who I am","meaning":"Something closely connected with my identity."}],"question":"If you could change your name, would you?","html":"No, I wouldn&#x27;t change it. My name feels like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">part of who I am</strong>, so a different one would seem strange. I don&#x27;t think my family or friends would like it either."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-054-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-054-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-054-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-054-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Knowing your neighbours, and problems with them","prompt":"How well do you know the people who live next door to you?\nHow often do you see them?\nWhat kinds of problem do people sometimes have with their neighbours?\nHow do you think neighbours can help each other?","answer":"Q: How well do you know the people who live next door to you?\nA: I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">know my neighbours quite well</strong>. They're really nice people, and I always stop to talk whenever I see them.\n\nQ: How often do you see them?\nA: I see my neighbours at least <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a few times a week</strong>. We usually see each other when we're leaving for work in the morning or coming home in the evening.\n\nQ: What kinds of problem do people sometimes have with their neighbours?\nA: I think the most common problem is probably noise. It's difficult to live next to people who have a dog that barks at night, or who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">play loud music</strong>.\n\nQ: How do you think neighbours can help each other?\nA: Neighbours can do little things like watching your house if you go on holiday or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taking a delivery</strong> if something arrives by post when you're not at home. I think <strong class=\"key-phrase\">these little favours</strong> are a good way for neighbours to help each other.","topic":"Neighbours","phrases":[{"en":"know my neighbours quite well","context":"I know my neighbours quite well. They're really nice people, and I always stop to talk whenever I see them."},{"en":"a few times a week","context":"I see my neighbours at least a few times a week. We usually see each other when we're leaving for work in the morning or coming home in the evening."},{"en":"play loud music","context":"I think the most common problem is probably noise. It's difficult to live next to people who have a dog that barks at night, or who play loud music."},{"en":"taking a delivery","context":"Neighbours can do little things like watching your house if you go on holiday or taking a delivery if something arrives by post when you're not at home. I think these little favours are a good way for neighbours to help each other."},{"en":"these little favours","context":"Neighbours can do little things like watching your house if you go on holiday or taking a delivery if something arrives by post when you're not at home. I think these little favours are a good way for neighbours to help each other."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Neighbours","id":"speaking-part-1-055","_displayNo":"#054","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-055-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-055-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-055-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-055-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I know them quite well. They are friendly, and when I see them, I usually stop for a short conversation. We have a good relationship.","phrases":[{"en":"a good relationship","meaning":"A friendly connection in which people get along well."}],"question":"How well do you know the people who live next door to you?","html":"I know them quite well. They are friendly, and when I see them, I usually stop for a short conversation. We have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a good relationship</strong>."},{"text":"I see them a few times a week, often when we leave for work in the morning or return in the evening. Those are the times we tend to meet outside.","phrases":[{"en":"tend to","meaning":"Usually or often do something."}],"question":"How often do you see them?","html":"I see them a few times a week, often when we leave for work in the morning or return in the evening. Those are the times we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tend to</strong> meet outside."},{"text":"Noise is probably the most common problem. A dog barking at night or someone playing loud music can make it hard for the people next door to rest.","phrases":[{"en":"next door","meaning":"In the home immediately beside yours."}],"question":"What kinds of problem do people sometimes have with their neighbours?","html":"Noise is probably the most common problem. A dog barking at night or someone playing loud music can make it hard for the people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">next door</strong> to rest."},{"text":"They can check your home while you are away or accept a parcel when you are out. Small helpful actions like these make living near each other easier.","phrases":[{"en":"a parcel","meaning":"A package sent or delivered to someone."}],"question":"How do you think neighbours can help each other?","html":"They can check your home while you are away or accept <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a parcel</strong> when you are out. Small helpful actions like these make living near each other easier."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-055-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-055-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-055-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-055-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Where you get news and how reporting differs","prompt":"Where do you normally get news from?\nHow does news reporting in your country differ from reporting abroad?\nTell me about an interesting news item you have read or heard recently.\nAre you interested in the news, and what kind of news do you follow?","answer":"Q: Where do you normally get news from?\nA: I mainly get my news from a few reputable websites, and I've also signed up to some specialist newsletters. If a story's really important, I'll compare more than one source, because headlines alone don't always give you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the full picture</strong>.\n\nQ: How does news reporting in your country differ from reporting abroad?\nA: I'd say our media focuses more on local politics and social issues, whereas foreign news often looks at the same story from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a completely different angle</strong>. Comparing the two actually helps me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spot any bias</strong>.\n\nQ: Tell me about an interesting news item you have read or heard recently.\nA: I recently read about a new piece of medical technology that lets doctors detect diseases much earlier. What interested me most was how it showed research can have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a direct practical benefit</strong> for everyday people.\n\nQ: Are you interested in the news, and what kind of news do you follow?\nA: Yes, I am, especially anything to do with science, education or major world events. I don't try to follow every single update though, because constant exposure just creates noise rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reliable understanding</strong>.","topic":"News","phrases":[{"en":"the full picture","context":"I mainly get my news from a few reputable websites, and I've also signed up to some specialist newsletters. If a story's really important, I'll compare more than one source, because headlines alone don't always give you the full picture."},{"en":"a completely different angle","context":"I'd say our media focuses more on local politics and social issues, whereas foreign news often looks at the same story from a completely different angle. Comparing the two actually helps me spot any bias."},{"en":"spot any bias","context":"I'd say our media focuses more on local politics and social issues, whereas foreign news often looks at the same story from a completely different angle. Comparing the two actually helps me spot any bias."},{"en":"a direct practical benefit","context":"I recently read about a new piece of medical technology that lets doctors detect diseases much earlier. What interested me most was how it showed research can have a direct practical benefit for everyday people."},{"en":"reliable understanding","context":"Yes, I am, especially anything to do with science, education or major world events. I don't try to follow every single update though, because constant exposure just creates noise rather than reliable understanding."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"News","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-056","_displayNo":"#055","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-056-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-056-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-056-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-056-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I use a few news websites I trust and receive some newsletters about subjects I follow. For an important story, I check several sources because a headline does not explain everything.","phrases":[{"en":"sources","meaning":"Places or people that provide information."}],"question":"Where do you normally get news from?","html":"I use a few news websites I trust and receive some newsletters about subjects I follow. For an important story, I check several <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sources</strong> because a headline does not explain everything."},{"text":"News here often pays more attention to local politics and social problems. Reports from other countries may explain the same event differently. Reading both helps me notice when a report favours one side.","phrases":[{"en":"favours one side","meaning":"Supports one view more strongly than another."}],"question":"How does news reporting in your country differ from reporting abroad?","html":"News here often pays more attention to local politics and social problems. Reports from other countries may explain the same event differently. Reading both helps me notice when a report <strong class=\"key-phrase\">favours one side</strong>."},{"text":"I recently read about medical technology that helps doctors find diseases earlier. I found it interesting because it showed how research can lead to something useful in ordinary people's lives.","phrases":[{"en":"lead to","meaning":"Cause or make something possible."}],"question":"Tell me about an interesting news item you have read or heard recently.","html":"I recently read about medical technology that helps doctors find diseases earlier. I found it interesting because it showed how research can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lead to</strong> something useful in ordinary people&#x27;s lives."},{"text":"Yes, I follow science, education and important world events. I don't read every update, though, because too much news can make it harder to understand what is really important.","phrases":[{"en":"update","meaning":"New information about something that is changing."}],"question":"Are you interested in the news, and what kind of news do you follow?","html":"Yes, I follow science, education and important world events. I don&#x27;t read every <strong class=\"key-phrase\">update</strong>, though, because too much news can make it harder to understand what is really important."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-056-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-056-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-056-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-056-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Special numbers and remembering them","prompt":"Do you have a favourite number or a number that is special to you?\nDo any numbers have special importance in your country?\nAre you good at remembering numbers?\nWhat numbers do people often have to memorise?","answer":"Q: Do you have a favourite number or a number that is special to you?\nA: My favourite number is seven because it's the date of my birthday, and I've always had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a soft spot for it</strong>. I wouldn't call it genuinely lucky, but I do feel a strong attachment to it.\n\nQ: Do any numbers have special importance in your country?\nA: Yes. The number 7, for example, is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">supposed to be a special number</strong> in my country. It appears in so many places, like the seven days of the week, the seven main notes in music, and the seven ages of man that Shakespeare described.\n\nQ: Are you good at remembering numbers?\nA: No, I'm not very good at remembering numbers. I don't think it's necessary to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">learn them by heart</strong>; I just save them on my phone.\n\nQ: What numbers do people often have to memorise?\nA: The most important number to remember <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on a daily basis</strong> is the PIN number for your bank card. Without this you can't pay for anything or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get cash out</strong>.","topic":"Numbers","phrases":[{"en":"a soft spot for it","context":"My favourite number is seven because it's the date of my birthday, and I've always had a soft spot for it. I wouldn't call it genuinely lucky, but I do feel a strong attachment to it."},{"en":"supposed to be a special number","context":"Yes. The number 7, for example, is supposed to be a special number in my country. It appears in so many places, like the seven days of the week, the seven main notes in music, and the seven ages of man that Shakespeare described."},{"en":"learn them by heart","context":"No, I'm not very good at remembering numbers. I don't think it's necessary to learn them by heart; I just save them on my phone."},{"en":"on a daily basis","context":"The most important number to remember on a daily basis is the PIN number for your bank card. Without this you can't pay for anything or get cash out."},{"en":"get cash out","context":"The most important number to remember on a daily basis is the PIN number for your bank card. Without this you can't pay for anything or get cash out."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Numbers","id":"speaking-part-1-057","_displayNo":"#056","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-057-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-057-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-057-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-057-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Seven is special to me because my birthday is on the seventh. I don't really believe it brings good luck, but I like it because of that personal connection.","phrases":[{"en":"personal connection","meaning":"A link with your own life or experiences."}],"question":"Do you have a favourite number or a number that is special to you?","html":"Seven is special to me because my birthday is on the seventh. I don&#x27;t really believe it brings good luck, but I like it because of that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">personal connection</strong>."},{"text":"Yes, seven is seen as special, for example. There are seven days in a week and seven main musical notes. Shakespeare also described human life as having seven stages.","phrases":[{"en":"stages","meaning":"Different parts of a process or period of development."}],"question":"Do any numbers have special importance in your country?","html":"Yes, seven is seen as special, for example. There are seven days in a week and seven main musical notes. Shakespeare also described human life as having seven <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stages</strong>."},{"text":"No, I find numbers difficult to remember. I usually save them on my phone rather than try to remember every one, which is more practical for me.","phrases":[{"en":"practical","meaning":"Useful and suitable for everyday needs."}],"question":"Are you good at remembering numbers?","html":"No, I find numbers difficult to remember. I usually save them on my phone rather than try to remember every one, which is more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical</strong> for me."},{"text":"A bank card PIN is an important number to remember. People often need it to pay by card or take money from a cash machine, so forgetting it can cause problems.","phrases":[{"en":"PIN","meaning":"A secret number used to confirm that you can use a card or account."}],"question":"What numbers do people often have to memorise?","html":"A bank card <strong class=\"key-phrase\">PIN</strong> is an important number to remember. People often need it to pay by card or take money from a cash machine, so forgetting it can cause problems."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-057-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-057-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-057-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-057-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Visiting parks and why towns need them","prompt":"Do you like parks?\nHow often do you visit parks?\nWhy are parks an important part of many towns and cities?\nWhat do you think could be done to make parks better?","answer":"Q: Do you like parks?\nA: Yes, I like parks because they're great places to relax or go for a walk. I think every city needs some green space where people can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">escape from the crowds</strong>.\n\nQ: How often do you visit parks?\nA: There's a park very close to where I live, so I probably go there <strong class=\"key-phrase\">once or twice a week</strong> if the weather's nice.\n\nQ: Why are parks an important part of many towns and cities?\nA: Parks are like an oasis where people get away from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the hustle and bustle</strong> of city life. They are where people go to have lunch, to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take some exercise</strong>, or to forget about work for a while.\n\nQ: What do you think could be done to make parks better?\nA: Even well-designed parks have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">room for improvement</strong>. Cities could add safer paths, accessible toilets, shaded seating and better maintenance, while protecting quiet areas from excessive commercial activity.","topic":"Parks","phrases":[{"en":"once or twice a week","context":"There's a park very close to where I live, so I probably go there once or twice a week if the weather's nice."},{"en":"the hustle and bustle","context":"Parks are like an oasis where people get away from the hustle and bustle of city life. They are where people go to have lunch, to take some exercise, or to forget about work for a while."},{"en":"take some exercise","context":"Parks are like an oasis where people get away from the hustle and bustle of city life. They are where people go to have lunch, to take some exercise, or to forget about work for a while."},{"en":"room for improvement","context":"Even well-designed parks have room for improvement. Cities could add safer paths, accessible toilets, shaded seating and better maintenance, while protecting quiet areas from excessive commercial activity."},{"en":"escape from the crowds","context":"Yes, I like parks because they're great places to relax or go for a walk. I think every city needs some green space where people can escape from the crowds."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Parks","id":"speaking-part-1-058","_displayNo":"#057","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-058-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-058-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-058-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-058-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, I like them because I can relax or walk there. Busy cities need green places where people can take a break from crowds and noise.","phrases":[{"en":"green places","meaning":"Areas with grass, trees or other plants."}],"question":"Do you like parks?","html":"Yes, I like them because I can relax or walk there. Busy cities need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">green places</strong> where people can take a break from crowds and noise."},{"text":"There is a park near my home, so I usually visit once or twice a week when the weather is good. Being close makes it easy to go regularly.","phrases":[{"en":"once or twice a week","meaning":"One or two times during a week."}],"question":"How often do you visit parks?","html":"There is a park near my home, so I usually visit <strong class=\"key-phrase\">once or twice a week</strong> when the weather is good. Being close makes it easy to go regularly."},{"text":"Parks give people a quieter place away from busy streets. People can eat lunch, exercise or stop thinking about work for a while, so they make city life more pleasant.","phrases":[{"en":"for a while","meaning":"For a short period of time."}],"question":"Why are parks an important part of many towns and cities?","html":"Parks give people a quieter place away from busy streets. People can eat lunch, exercise or stop thinking about work <strong class=\"key-phrase\">for a while</strong>, so they make city life more pleasant."},{"text":"Cities could improve paths, add shaded seats and toilets that everyone can use, and look after parks better. Quiet areas should also be protected from too many shops or other business activities.","phrases":[{"en":"shaded","meaning":"Protected from direct sunlight."}],"question":"What do you think could be done to make parks better?","html":"Cities could improve paths, add <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shaded</strong> seats and toilets that everyone can use, and look after parks better. Quiet areas should also be protected from too many shops or other business activities."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-058-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-058-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-058-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-058-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Patience, politeness and rude behaviour","prompt":"Are you a patient person?\nIs it important to be polite?\nWhat do you do when others are rude?","answer":"Q: Are you a patient person?\nA: I'd say I'm fairly patient, especially when something's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">out of someone's control</strong>. But I do get a bit frustrated when the same avoidable mistake keeps happening. I try to pause for a second before reacting, so I don't let frustration <strong class=\"key-phrase\">control my response</strong>.\n\nQ: Is it important to be polite?\nA: Yes, definitely, because it stops unnecessary arguments and shows respect. Being polite doesn't mean you have to agree with everyone, though; it just means you can disagree without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">attacking the person</strong>.\n\nQ: What do you do when others are rude?\nA: I try to stay calm and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set a clear boundary</strong>, without matching their tone. If it carries on, I'll just end the conversation rather than letting things <strong class=\"key-phrase\">escalate further</strong>.","topic":"Patience & Politeness","phrases":[{"en":"out of someone's control","context":"I'd say I'm fairly patient, especially when something's out of someone's control. But I do get a bit frustrated when the same avoidable mistake keeps happening. I try to pause for a second before reacting, so I don't let frustration control my response."},{"en":"control my response","context":"I'd say I'm fairly patient, especially when something's out of someone's control. But I do get a bit frustrated when the same avoidable mistake keeps happening. I try to pause for a second before reacting, so I don't let frustration control my response."},{"en":"attacking the person","context":"Yes, definitely, because it stops unnecessary arguments and shows respect. Being polite doesn't mean you have to agree with everyone, though; it just means you can disagree without attacking the person."},{"en":"set a clear boundary","context":"I try to stay calm and set a clear boundary, without matching their tone. If it carries on, I'll just end the conversation rather than letting things escalate further."},{"en":"escalate further","context":"I try to stay calm and set a clear boundary, without matching their tone. If it carries on, I'll just end the conversation rather than letting things escalate further."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Patience & Politeness","id":"speaking-part-1-059","_displayNo":"#058","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-059-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-059-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-059-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I'm usually patient when someone cannot control the situation. Repeated mistakes can annoy me, though. I try to stop and think before responding so I don't speak only out of frustration.","phrases":[{"en":"frustration","meaning":"The annoyed feeling of being unable to do or change something."}],"question":"Are you a patient person?","html":"I&#x27;m usually patient when someone cannot control the situation. Repeated mistakes can annoy me, though. I try to stop and think before responding so I don&#x27;t speak only out of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">frustration</strong>."},{"text":"Yes, being polite shows respect and helps prevent arguments. You can still disagree with someone, but you should explain your opinion without being hurtful or insulting them.","phrases":[{"en":"insulting","meaning":"Speaking to someone in a rude or offensive way."}],"question":"Is it important to be polite?","html":"Yes, being polite shows respect and helps prevent arguments. You can still disagree with someone, but you should explain your opinion without being hurtful or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">insulting</strong> them."},{"text":"I try to stay calm and explain what behaviour I will not accept. If the person continues being rude, I end the conversation before it becomes a bigger argument.","phrases":[{"en":"stay calm","meaning":"Keep your feelings under control and avoid becoming angry."}],"question":"What do you do when others are rude?","html":"I try to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay calm</strong> and explain what behaviour I will not accept. If the person continues being rude, I end the conversation before it becomes a bigger argument."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-059-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-059-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-059-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"The photos you take and what you do with them","prompt":"What type of photos do you like taking?\nWhat do you do with photos you take?\nWhen you visit other places, do you take photos or buy postcards?\nDo you like people taking photos of you?","answer":"Q: What type of photos do you like taking?\nA: I rarely take photographs because I prefer to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay present in the moment</strong>. I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make an exception for</strong> family occasions or unusual places that I genuinely want to remember.\n\nQ: What do you do with photos you take?\nA: Most photos remain on my phone or in cloud storage. I occasionally create an album after a trip, but I admit that I don't <strong class=\"key-phrase\">organise them systematically</strong>.\n\nQ: When you visit other places, do you take photos or buy postcards?\nA: I don't really do either. I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leave it to my friends</strong> or other family members to take photographs; I'm usually with someone who likes taking photos and can share them with me later.\n\nQ: Do you like people taking photos of you?\nA: No, I prefer not to be in photos. I always feel awkward when someone asks me to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">smile for the camera</strong>, and I don't think I'm very photogenic.","topic":"Photography","phrases":[{"en":"stay present in the moment","context":"I rarely take photographs because I prefer to stay present in the moment. I make an exception for family occasions or unusual places that I genuinely want to remember."},{"en":"make an exception for","context":"I rarely take photographs because I prefer to stay present in the moment. I make an exception for family occasions or unusual places that I genuinely want to remember."},{"en":"organise them systematically","context":"Most photos remain on my phone or in cloud storage. I occasionally create an album after a trip, but I admit that I don't organise them systematically."},{"en":"smile for the camera","context":"No, I prefer not to be in photos. I always feel awkward when someone asks me to smile for the camera, and I don't think I'm very photogenic."},{"en":"leave it to my friends","context":"I don't really do either. I leave it to my friends or other family members to take photographs; I'm usually with someone who likes taking photos and can share them with me later."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Photography","id":"speaking-part-1-060","_displayNo":"#059","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-060-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-060-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-060-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-060-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I don't take many photos because I prefer enjoying what is happening. I mainly take them at family events or unusual places that I especially want to remember.","phrases":[{"en":"family events","meaning":"Occasions when family members meet or celebrate together."}],"question":"What type of photos do you like taking?","html":"I don&#x27;t take many photos because I prefer enjoying what is happening. I mainly take them at <strong class=\"key-phrase\">family events</strong> or unusual places that I especially want to remember."},{"text":"Most stay on my phone or in online storage. Sometimes I make an album after a trip, but I don't have a very organised system for keeping all my photos.","phrases":[{"en":"an album","meaning":"A collection of photographs arranged together."}],"question":"What do you do with photos you take?","html":"Most stay on my phone or in online storage. Sometimes I make <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an album</strong> after a trip, but I don&#x27;t have a very organised system for keeping all my photos."},{"text":"I usually do neither. I often travel with friends or family who enjoy taking pictures, so I let them take the photos and share them with me later.","phrases":[{"en":"share them with me","meaning":"Let me see or have copies of them."}],"question":"When you visit other places, do you take photos or buy postcards?","html":"I usually do neither. I often travel with friends or family who enjoy taking pictures, so I let them take the photos and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">share them with me</strong> later."},{"text":"No, I don't really enjoy it. I feel uncomfortable when someone asks me to smile for a photo, and I don't think I usually look good in pictures.","phrases":[{"en":"uncomfortable","meaning":"Not relaxed or at ease."}],"question":"Do you like people taking photos of you?","html":"No, I don&#x27;t really enjoy it. I feel <strong class=\"key-phrase\">uncomfortable</strong> when someone asks me to smile for a photo, and I don&#x27;t think I usually look good in pictures."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-060-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-060-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-060-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-060-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Whether photography is growing, and whether cameras lie","prompt":"Is photography becoming more popular these days, in your experience?\nDo you agree with the saying “The camera never lies”?\nWould you say photography is an art?","answer":"Q: Is photography becoming more popular these days, in your experience?\nA: Yes, I'd say it definitely is. Smartphones have made photography available to almost everyone, so people now document everyday moments as well as special occasions. But with so many images around, an individual photo can easily <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel less significant</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you agree with the saying “The camera never lies”?\nA: No, I don't, really. A photo does capture something real, but the framing, timing, editing and captions can completely change the message it sends. So photos are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">powerful evidence</strong>, but they're still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">open to interpretation</strong>.\n\nQ: Would you say photography is an art?\nA: Yes, I think it can be, when the photographer's using light, composition and timing to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get across an idea</strong> or a feeling. It's not just about technical accuracy; artistic photography needs <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a deliberate point of view</strong>.","topic":"Photography","phrases":[{"en":"feel less significant","context":"Yes, I'd say it definitely is. Smartphones have made photography available to almost everyone, so people now document everyday moments as well as special occasions. But with so many images around, an individual photo can easily feel less significant."},{"en":"powerful evidence","context":"No, I don't, really. A photo does capture something real, but the framing, timing, editing and captions can completely change the message it sends. So photos are powerful evidence, but they're still open to interpretation."},{"en":"open to interpretation","context":"No, I don't, really. A photo does capture something real, but the framing, timing, editing and captions can completely change the message it sends. So photos are powerful evidence, but they're still open to interpretation."},{"en":"get across an idea","context":"Yes, I think it can be, when the photographer's using light, composition and timing to get across an idea or a feeling. It's not just about technical accuracy; artistic photography needs a deliberate point of view."},{"en":"a deliberate point of view","context":"Yes, I think it can be, when the photographer's using light, composition and timing to get across an idea or a feeling. It's not just about technical accuracy; artistic photography needs a deliberate point of view."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Photography","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-061","_displayNo":"#060","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-061-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-061-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-061-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, smartphones make it easy for almost everyone to take photos. People now photograph ordinary moments as well as special events. However, because there are so many pictures, each one may feel less important.","phrases":[{"en":"ordinary moments","meaning":"Normal everyday experiences, rather than special events."}],"question":"Is photography becoming more popular these days, in your experience?","html":"Yes, smartphones make it easy for almost everyone to take photos. People now photograph <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ordinary moments</strong> as well as special events. However, because there are so many pictures, each one may feel less important."},{"text":"No, not completely. A photograph shows something real, but the choice of what to include, editing and the words added to it can change its meaning. We still need to think about the context.","phrases":[{"en":"context","meaning":"The background or situation that helps explain something."}],"question":"Do you agree with the saying “The camera never lies”?","html":"No, not completely. A photograph shows something real, but the choice of what to include, editing and the words added to it can change its meaning. We still need to think about the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">context</strong>."},{"text":"Yes, photography can be art. A photographer can use light, timing and the way things are arranged to express an idea or feeling. It involves more than just taking a clear picture.","phrases":[{"en":"express","meaning":"Show or communicate a feeling or idea."}],"question":"Would you say photography is an art?","html":"Yes, photography can be art. A photographer can use light, timing and the way things are arranged to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">express</strong> an idea or feeling. It involves more than just taking a clear picture."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-061-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-061-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-061-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Rain and how it affects people","prompt":"Do you like rain?\nDo you think that rain affects people's mood?\nDoes it often rain where you live?\nHave you ever been caught in the rain without a coat or umbrella?","answer":"Q: Do you like rain?\nA: No, not at all. It rains far too often in Manchester, where I live, and I'm <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fed up of it</strong>; I wish we had more sun!\n\nQ: Do you think that rain affects people's mood?\nA: Yes, definitely. I think <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rainy days and grey skies</strong> can be a bit sad and depressing. Where I live, people are much more cheerful when the sun comes out.\n\nQ: Does it often rain where you live?\nA: Yes, unfortunately it does. As I said, Manchester is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">known for being a rainy place</strong>; you can't trust the weather here, because it can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cloud over</strong> and start raining at any moment.\n\nQ: Have you ever been caught in the rain without a coat or umbrella?\nA: Yes, many times. Just last week I went out without an umbrella and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">got soaked</strong>; I did have a coat on, but it didn't really help.","topic":"Rain","phrases":[{"en":"fed up of it","context":"No, not at all. It rains far too often in Manchester, where I live, and I'm fed up of it; I wish we had more sun!"},{"en":"rainy days and grey skies","context":"Yes, definitely. I think rainy days and grey skies can be a bit sad and depressing. Where I live, people are much more cheerful when the sun comes out."},{"en":"known for being a rainy place","context":"Yes, unfortunately it does. As I said, Manchester is known for being a rainy place; you can't trust the weather here, because it can cloud over and start raining at any moment."},{"en":"cloud over","context":"Yes, unfortunately it does. As I said, Manchester is known for being a rainy place; you can't trust the weather here, because it can cloud over and start raining at any moment."},{"en":"got soaked","context":"Yes, many times. Just last week I went out without an umbrella and got soaked; I did have a coat on, but it didn't really help."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Rain","id":"speaking-part-1-062","_displayNo":"#061","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-062-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-062-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-062-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-062-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"No, I don't like it much. It rains very often in Manchester, where I live, and I'm tired of the wet weather. I'd really like more sunshine.","phrases":[{"en":"tired of","meaning":"Annoyed because something has happened too often."}],"question":"Do you like rain?","html":"No, I don&#x27;t like it much. It rains very often in Manchester, where I live, and I&#x27;m <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tired of</strong> the wet weather. I&#x27;d really like more sunshine."},{"text":"Yes, rainy days and grey skies can make people feel a little sad. People where I live seem much happier when the sun appears, so I think weather can affect mood.","phrases":[{"en":"mood","meaning":"The way someone feels at a particular time."}],"question":"Do you think that rain affects people's mood?","html":"Yes, rainy days and grey skies can make people feel a little sad. People where I live seem much happier when the sun appears, so I think weather can affect <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mood</strong>."},{"text":"Yes, it rains a lot in Manchester. The weather changes quickly, so even if it looks fine at first, clouds and rain can arrive quite suddenly.","phrases":[{"en":"suddenly","meaning":"Quickly and without much warning."}],"question":"Does it often rain where you live?","html":"Yes, it rains a lot in Manchester. The weather changes quickly, so even if it looks fine at first, clouds and rain can arrive quite <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suddenly</strong>."},{"text":"Yes, many times. Last week, I went out without an umbrella and became completely wet. I was wearing a coat, but it did not keep the rain out very well.","phrases":[{"en":"keep the rain out","meaning":"Stop rain from getting inside or reaching you."}],"question":"Have you ever been caught in the rain without a coat or umbrella?","html":"Yes, many times. Last week, I went out without an umbrella and became completely wet. I was wearing a coat, but it did not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep the rain out</strong> very well."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-062-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-062-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-062-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-062-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Reading for leisure and childhood books","prompt":"Do you like reading?\nDo you usually read for leisure or for work purposes?\nWhat was your favourite book or story when you were a child?\nDo you think it's important that children read regularly?","answer":"Q: Do you like reading?\nA: Yes, I like reading a lot. I read <strong class=\"key-phrase\">all sorts of things</strong>, including novels, newspapers, magazines, and online articles.\n\nQ: Do you usually read for leisure or for work purposes?\nA: Well, I've got to do a certain amount of reading <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as part of my job</strong>, but I probably read more for enjoyment. I pick up a newspaper most days, and I usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have a book on the go</strong>.\n\nQ: What was your favourite book or story when you were a child?\nA: The first book I remember loving was Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone. The magical setting immediately captured my imagination, and I read it so quickly that I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">could hardly put it down</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you think it's important that children read regularly?\nA: Definitely. I think reading's possibly the most important skill that children learn. The ability to read <strong class=\"key-phrase\">opens the door to</strong> all aspects of education.","topic":"Reading","phrases":[{"en":"all sorts of things","context":"Yes, I like reading a lot. I read all sorts of things, including novels, newspapers, magazines, and online articles."},{"en":"as part of my job","context":"Well, I've got to do a certain amount of reading as part of my job, but I probably read more for enjoyment. I pick up a newspaper most days, and I usually have a book on the go."},{"en":"have a book on the go","context":"Well, I've got to do a certain amount of reading as part of my job, but I probably read more for enjoyment. I pick up a newspaper most days, and I usually have a book on the go."},{"en":"could hardly put it down","context":"The first book I remember loving was Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone. The magical setting immediately captured my imagination, and I read it so quickly that I could hardly put it down."},{"en":"opens the door to","context":"Definitely. I think reading's possibly the most important skill that children learn. The ability to read opens the door to all aspects of education."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Reading","id":"speaking-part-1-063","_displayNo":"#062","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-063-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-063-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-063-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-063-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, I enjoy reading many different things. I read novels, newspapers, magazines and articles online, so it is a hobby that gives me plenty of choice.","phrases":[{"en":"novels","meaning":"Books that tell made-up stories."}],"question":"Do you like reading?","html":"Yes, I enjoy reading many different things. I read <strong class=\"key-phrase\">novels</strong>, newspapers, magazines and articles online, so it is a hobby that gives me plenty of choice."},{"text":"I read for both, but probably more for pleasure. I need to read for my job, but I also read a newspaper most days and usually have a book that I'm reading.","phrases":[{"en":"for pleasure","meaning":"Because you enjoy it, rather than because you have to."}],"question":"Do you usually read for leisure or for work purposes?","html":"I read for both, but probably more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">for pleasure</strong>. I need to read for my job, but I also read a newspaper most days and usually have a book that I&#x27;m reading."},{"text":"My favourite was Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone. I loved the magical world and wanted to know what happened next, so I read it very quickly.","phrases":[{"en":"magical","meaning":"Involving powers or events that cannot happen in the normal world."}],"question":"What was your favourite book or story when you were a child?","html":"My favourite was Harry Potter and the Philosopher&#x27;s Stone. I loved the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">magical</strong> world and wanted to know what happened next, so I read it very quickly."},{"text":"Yes, regular reading is very important. When children can read well, they can learn about many subjects more easily. It helps them with almost every part of their education.","phrases":[{"en":"education","meaning":"The process of learning, especially at school or college."}],"question":"Do you think it's important that children read regularly?","html":"Yes, regular reading is very important. When children can read well, they can learn about many subjects more easily. It helps them with almost every part of their <strong class=\"key-phrase\">education</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-063-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-063-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-063-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-063-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Robots at home and in childhood cartoons","prompt":"What do you think of robots?\nDid you watch any cartoons related to robots in your childhood?\nDo you like the idea of robots helping you at home or driving your car?","answer":"Q: What do you think of robots?\nA: I think robots are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clever idea</strong>. I'd love to have a robot that could speak to me and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">do things for me</strong>!\n\nQ: Did you watch any cartoons related to robots in your childhood?\nA: I can't remember <strong class=\"key-phrase\">watching cartoons with robots</strong>, but I remember that I liked the robots in films like Star Wars.\n\nQ: Do you like the idea of robots helping you at home or driving your car?\nA: Yes, definitely. It would be great to have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a robot helper</strong> at home, and I like the idea of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">driverless cars</strong> - I think driving would be safer if cars were controlled by robots.","topic":"Robots","phrases":[{"en":"a clever idea","context":"I think robots are a clever idea. I'd love to have a robot that could speak to me and do things for me!"},{"en":"do things for me","context":"I think robots are a clever idea. I'd love to have a robot that could speak to me and do things for me!"},{"en":"watching cartoons with robots","context":"I can't remember watching cartoons with robots, but I remember that I liked the robots in films like Star Wars."},{"en":"a robot helper","context":"Yes, definitely. It would be great to have a robot helper at home, and I like the idea of driverless cars - I think driving would be safer if cars were controlled by robots."},{"en":"driverless cars","context":"Yes, definitely. It would be great to have a robot helper at home, and I like the idea of driverless cars - I think driving would be safer if cars were controlled by robots."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Robots","id":"speaking-part-1-064","_displayNo":"#063","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-064-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-064-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-064-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I think robots are a very clever invention. I'd enjoy having one that could talk to me and help with tasks, because it would make daily life easier.","phrases":[{"en":"invention","meaning":"Something new that someone has designed or made."}],"question":"What do you think of robots?","html":"I think robots are a very clever <strong class=\"key-phrase\">invention</strong>. I&#x27;d enjoy having one that could talk to me and help with tasks, because it would make daily life easier."},{"text":"I don't remember any cartoons about robots, but I liked the robots in films such as Star Wars. Those are the ones I remember from childhood.","phrases":[{"en":"childhood","meaning":"The period of life when you were a child."}],"question":"Did you watch any cartoons related to robots in your childhood?","html":"I don&#x27;t remember any cartoons about robots, but I liked the robots in films such as Star Wars. Those are the ones I remember from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">childhood</strong>."},{"text":"Yes, I'd like a robot to help around the house. I also like the idea of cars that drive themselves, and I think they could make driving safer if the technology works well.","phrases":[{"en":"drive themselves","meaning":"Move and control the journey without a person driving."}],"question":"Do you like the idea of robots helping you at home or driving your car?","html":"Yes, I&#x27;d like a robot to help around the house. I also like the idea of cars that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drive themselves</strong>, and I think they could make driving safer if the technology works well."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-064-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-064-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-064-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Living near the sea and how it makes you feel","prompt":"Do you live near the sea?\nHow do you feel when you are near the sea?\nWhat do you like and dislike about the sea?\nWould you like to live on an island and why?","answer":"Q: Do you live near the sea?\nA: I don't live right by the sea, so going to the coast still feels like a bit of a treat. It takes a while to get there, but that's what makes each trip <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a refreshing change</strong>.\n\nQ: How do you feel when you are near the sea?\nA: I usually feel a lot calmer near the sea, because the open horizon and the sound of the waves just clear my head. It's one of the few places where I can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">switch off completely</strong>.\n\nQ: What do you like and dislike about the sea?\nA: I like the fresh air and the views, but I'm not so keen on crowded beaches or plastic waste. The coast's always nicer when tourism doesn't end up <strong class=\"key-phrase\">damaging the environment</strong>.\n\nQ: Would you like to live on an island and why?\nA: I'd enjoy living on an island for a short while, but probably not permanently. Isolation might be peaceful, but limited healthcare, transport and job options would make everyday life <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less convenient</strong>.","topic":"Sea","phrases":[{"en":"a refreshing change","context":"I don't live right by the sea, so going to the coast still feels like a bit of a treat. It takes a while to get there, but that's what makes each trip a refreshing change."},{"en":"switch off completely","context":"I usually feel a lot calmer near the sea, because the open horizon and the sound of the waves just clear my head. It's one of the few places where I can switch off completely."},{"en":"damaging the environment","context":"I like the fresh air and the views, but I'm not so keen on crowded beaches or plastic waste. The coast's always nicer when tourism doesn't end up damaging the environment."},{"en":"less convenient","context":"I'd enjoy living on an island for a short while, but probably not permanently. Isolation might be peaceful, but limited healthcare, transport and job options would make everyday life less convenient."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Sea","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-065","_displayNo":"#064","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-065-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-065-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-065-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-065-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"No, I don't live close to the sea. It takes some time to get there, so visiting the coast feels special and gives me a pleasant change from normal life.","phrases":[{"en":"the coast","meaning":"The land beside the sea."}],"question":"Do you live near the sea?","html":"No, I don&#x27;t live close to the sea. It takes some time to get there, so visiting <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the coast</strong> feels special and gives me a pleasant change from normal life."},{"text":"I feel much calmer there. Seeing the open sea and hearing the waves help me stop worrying and relax. It is a place where I can really take a mental break.","phrases":[{"en":"waves","meaning":"The moving lines of water that rise and fall on the sea."}],"question":"How do you feel when you are near the sea?","html":"I feel much calmer there. Seeing the open sea and hearing the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">waves</strong> help me stop worrying and relax. It is a place where I can really take a mental break."},{"text":"I enjoy the fresh air and beautiful views. However, I don't like busy beaches or plastic rubbish. The sea is more enjoyable when visitors look after the environment.","phrases":[{"en":"environment","meaning":"The natural world around us, including land, water and air."}],"question":"What do you like and dislike about the sea?","html":"I enjoy the fresh air and beautiful views. However, I don&#x27;t like busy beaches or plastic rubbish. The sea is more enjoyable when visitors look after the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">environment</strong>."},{"text":"I'd like to try it for a short time, but not forever. An island might be peaceful, but fewer hospitals, jobs and transport options could make normal life difficult.","phrases":[{"en":"options","meaning":"Different choices that are available."}],"question":"Would you like to live on an island and why?","html":"I&#x27;d like to try it for a short time, but not forever. An island might be peaceful, but fewer hospitals, jobs and transport <strong class=\"key-phrase\">options</strong> could make normal life difficult."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-065-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-065-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-065-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-065-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Buying shoes: comfort or style","prompt":"Do you like to buy expensive shoes?\nWhen you buy shoes, do you prefer convenience or style?\nWhy do you think some people buy many pairs of shoes?\nHave you ever bought shoes online?","answer":"Q: Do you like to buy expensive shoes?\nA: No. I tend to buy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">average-priced shoes</strong> because I'm more interested in comfort than expensive brands.\n\nQ: When you buy shoes, do you prefer convenience or style?\nA: I mainly look for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comfort and convenience</strong>. But obviously I consider the style too, because I do choose shoes that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">match the clothes I wear</strong>.\n\nQ: Why do you think some people buy many pairs of shoes?\nA: I suppose they either like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">following the latest fashions</strong>, or they just enjoy having a different pair for every occasion.\n\nQ: Have you ever bought shoes online?\nA: No, I haven't. I always like to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">try shoes on</strong> before I buy them, to make sure that they fit properly and feel comfortable.","topic":"Shoes","phrases":[{"en":"comfort and convenience","context":"I mainly look for comfort and convenience. But obviously I consider the style too, because I do choose shoes that match the clothes I wear."},{"en":"match the clothes I wear","context":"I mainly look for comfort and convenience. But obviously I consider the style too, because I do choose shoes that match the clothes I wear."},{"en":"following the latest fashions","context":"I suppose they either like following the latest fashions, or they just enjoy having a different pair for every occasion."},{"en":"try shoes on","context":"No, I haven't. I always like to try shoes on before I buy them, to make sure that they fit properly and feel comfortable."},{"en":"average-priced shoes","context":"No. I tend to buy average-priced shoes because I'm more interested in comfort than expensive brands."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Shoes","id":"speaking-part-1-066","_displayNo":"#065","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-066-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-066-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-066-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-066-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"No, I usually buy shoes at a reasonable price. I care more about how comfortable they feel than whether they come from an expensive or famous brand.","phrases":[{"en":"reasonable price","meaning":"A cost that seems fair and not too high."}],"question":"Do you like to buy expensive shoes?","html":"No, I usually buy shoes at a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reasonable price</strong>. I care more about how comfortable they feel than whether they come from an expensive or famous brand."},{"text":"Comfort and ease of use matter most to me. I still think about appearance, though, because I want the shoes to look good with the clothes I usually wear.","phrases":[{"en":"appearance","meaning":"The way something looks."}],"question":"When you buy shoes, do you prefer convenience or style?","html":"Comfort and ease of use matter most to me. I still think about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">appearance</strong>, though, because I want the shoes to look good with the clothes I usually wear."},{"text":"Some people enjoy following new fashions. Others like having different shoes for work, parties and other situations, so they buy several pairs to suit different occasions.","phrases":[{"en":"occasions","meaning":"Particular events or situations."}],"question":"Why do you think some people buy many pairs of shoes?","html":"Some people enjoy following new fashions. Others like having different shoes for work, parties and other situations, so they buy several pairs to suit different <strong class=\"key-phrase\">occasions</strong>."},{"text":"No, I haven't. I prefer to put shoes on in a shop before buying them. That way, I can check that they are the right size and feel comfortable.","phrases":[{"en":"the right size","meaning":"A size that fits properly."}],"question":"Have you ever bought shoes online?","html":"No, I haven&#x27;t. I prefer to put shoes on in a shop before buying them. That way, I can check that they are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the right size</strong> and feel comfortable."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-066-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-066-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-066-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-066-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Who shops in your household and what you enjoy buying","prompt":"Who does most of the shopping in your household?\nWhat kind of shopping do you like doing?\nIs shopping a popular activity in your country?\nWhat types of shops do teenagers like best in your country?","answer":"Q: Who does most of the shopping in your household?\nA: There isn't one person who does most of the shopping. I'd say it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a shared responsibility</strong> because we tend to go shopping together as a family.\n\nQ: What kind of shopping do you like doing?\nA: I quite like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shopping for presents</strong> for people's birthdays or for Christmas. My favourite type of shop would have to be a bookshop.\n\nQ: Is shopping a popular activity in your country?\nA: Yes, it's very popular. Saturday is the busiest shopping day, and lots of people treat shopping as a kind of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leisure activity</strong>, rather than something practical.\n\nQ: What types of shops do teenagers like best in your country?\nA: I'm <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not an expert on</strong> teenage behaviour, but I guess they like buying clothes, music, gadgets, that kind of thing.","topic":"Shopping","phrases":[{"en":"a shared responsibility","context":"There isn't one person who does most of the shopping. I'd say it's a shared responsibility because we tend to go shopping together as a family."},{"en":"shopping for presents","context":"I quite like shopping for presents for people's birthdays or for Christmas. My favourite type of shop would have to be a bookshop."},{"en":"leisure activity","context":"Yes, it's very popular. Saturday is the busiest shopping day, and lots of people treat shopping as a kind of leisure activity, rather than something practical."},{"en":"not an expert on","context":"I'm not an expert on teenage behaviour, but I guess they like buying clothes, music, gadgets, that kind of thing."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Shopping","id":"speaking-part-1-067","_displayNo":"#066","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-067-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-067-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-067-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-067-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"We share the shopping rather than leaving it to one person. We often go together as a family, so everyone takes part and no one does most of it alone.","phrases":[{"en":"takes part","meaning":"Joins in an activity."}],"question":"Who does most of the shopping in your household?","html":"We share the shopping rather than leaving it to one person. We often go together as a family, so everyone <strong class=\"key-phrase\">takes part</strong> and no one does most of it alone."},{"text":"I enjoy buying presents for birthdays or Christmas. My favourite shops are bookshops because I like looking at books, so that is the shopping I enjoy most.","phrases":[{"en":"presents","meaning":"Things given to someone as gifts."}],"question":"What kind of shopping do you like doing?","html":"I enjoy buying <strong class=\"key-phrase\">presents</strong> for birthdays or Christmas. My favourite shops are bookshops because I like looking at books, so that is the shopping I enjoy most."},{"text":"Yes, shopping is very popular. Saturdays are especially busy, and many people go shopping for enjoyment rather than just to buy things they need.","phrases":[{"en":"for enjoyment","meaning":"To have fun or feel pleased."}],"question":"Is shopping a popular activity in your country?","html":"Yes, shopping is very popular. Saturdays are especially busy, and many people go shopping <strong class=\"key-phrase\">for enjoyment</strong> rather than just to buy things they need."},{"text":"I'm not completely sure, but I think teenagers enjoy shops selling clothes, music and electronic gadgets. Those seem like things many young people are interested in buying.","phrases":[{"en":"gadgets","meaning":"Small useful electronic devices."}],"question":"What types of shops do teenagers like best in your country?","html":"I&#x27;m not completely sure, but I think teenagers enjoy shops selling clothes, music and electronic <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gadgets</strong>. Those seem like things many young people are interested in buying."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-067-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-067-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-067-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-067-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Brands, and whether they matter","prompt":"Are you loyal to any brands?\nDo you want other people to know you buy a particular brand?\nDo you buy any products without considering the brand name?\nHow do you feel about going shopping?","answer":"Q: Are you loyal to any brands?\nA: I'm only loyal to a brand once it's proved <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consistently reliable</strong>. I usually compare quality, after-sales service and price first, because I care more about how well something works than the brand name.\n\nQ: Do you want other people to know you buy a particular brand?\nA: Not really. I just want products that work well, and I don't really see brand names as a way to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">show off</strong>. That kind of shopping can easily become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more about image than value</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you buy any products without considering the brand name?\nA: Yes, definitely, especially with things like basic food, stationery and household goods. For simple stuff like that, I'll compare ingredients, durability or price rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relying on brand recognition</strong>.\n\nQ: How do you feel about going shopping?\nA: I don't really see shopping as entertainment, more of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a practical task</strong>. I enjoy it when I know exactly what I need, but crowded shops and too many choices can make it needlessly time-consuming.","topic":"Shopping","phrases":[{"en":"consistently reliable","context":"I'm only loyal to a brand once it's proved consistently reliable. I usually compare quality, after-sales service and price first, because I care more about how well something works than the brand name."},{"en":"show off","context":"Not really. I just want products that work well, and I don't really see brand names as a way to show off. That kind of shopping can easily become more about image than value."},{"en":"more about image than value","context":"Not really. I just want products that work well, and I don't really see brand names as a way to show off. That kind of shopping can easily become more about image than value."},{"en":"relying on brand recognition","context":"Yes, definitely, especially with things like basic food, stationery and household goods. For simple stuff like that, I'll compare ingredients, durability or price rather than relying on brand recognition."},{"en":"a practical task","context":"I don't really see shopping as entertainment, more of a practical task. I enjoy it when I know exactly what I need, but crowded shops and too many choices can make it needlessly time-consuming."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Shopping","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-068","_displayNo":"#067","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-068-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-068-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-068-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-068-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I keep buying a brand only after its products have worked well for me. I compare quality, price and help after purchase, because those matter more than the name.","phrases":[{"en":"after purchase","meaning":"After you have bought something."}],"question":"Are you loyal to any brands?","html":"I keep buying a brand only after its products have worked well for me. I compare quality, price and help <strong class=\"key-phrase\">after purchase</strong>, because those matter more than the name."},{"text":"No, I don't need people to notice the brands I buy. I mainly want products that work properly. Buying something just to impress others matters less to me than getting good value.","phrases":[{"en":"good value","meaning":"Worth the money you pay for it."}],"question":"Do you want other people to know you buy a particular brand?","html":"No, I don&#x27;t need people to notice the brands I buy. I mainly want products that work properly. Buying something just to impress others matters less to me than getting <strong class=\"key-phrase\">good value</strong>."},{"text":"Yes, with basic food, pens, paper and things for the home. I usually check what they contain, how long they last and their price rather than choosing a familiar name.","phrases":[{"en":"familiar","meaning":"Well known to you or easy to recognise."}],"question":"Do you buy any products without considering the brand name?","html":"Yes, with basic food, pens, paper and things for the home. I usually check what they contain, how long they last and their price rather than choosing a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">familiar</strong> name."},{"text":"I see shopping mainly as a task, not entertainment. I enjoy it when I know what I need, but crowds and too much choice can make it take longer than necessary.","phrases":[{"en":"necessary","meaning":"Needed for a particular purpose."}],"question":"How do you feel about going shopping?","html":"I see shopping mainly as a task, not entertainment. I enjoy it when I know what I need, but crowds and too much choice can make it take longer than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">necessary</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-068-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-068-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-068-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-068-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Shopping online and what you dislike about shopping","prompt":"Do you like shopping and why?\nDo you like shopping on the internet?\nWhat don't you like about shopping?\nWhat time do you do the shopping and why?","answer":"Q: Do you like shopping and why?\nA: I don't really see shopping as a hobby, but I enjoy it when I've got <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clear purpose</strong> and enough time to compare things properly. I find <strong class=\"key-phrase\">aimless shopping</strong> tiring rather than relaxing.\n\nQ: Do you like shopping on the internet?\nA: Yes, for everyday products that are easy to compare online. It saves time and gives me more choice, although I still go to actual shops for clothes or anything that needs <strong class=\"key-phrase\">careful inspection</strong>.\n\nQ: What don't you like about shopping?\nA: I'm not keen on misleading discounts, pushy sales staff or long queues. I think a good shop should let you compare things properly and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make an informed decision</strong>, without all the pressure.\n\nQ: What time do you do the shopping and why?\nA: I usually go shopping early in the morning or on weekday evenings, when it's quieter. Avoiding the busy times just makes the whole thing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far more efficient</strong>.","topic":"Shopping","phrases":[{"en":"a clear purpose","context":"I don't really see shopping as a hobby, but I enjoy it when I've got a clear purpose and enough time to compare things properly. I find aimless shopping tiring rather than relaxing."},{"en":"aimless shopping","context":"I don't really see shopping as a hobby, but I enjoy it when I've got a clear purpose and enough time to compare things properly. I find aimless shopping tiring rather than relaxing."},{"en":"careful inspection","context":"Yes, for everyday products that are easy to compare online. It saves time and gives me more choice, although I still go to actual shops for clothes or anything that needs careful inspection."},{"en":"make an informed decision","context":"I'm not keen on misleading discounts, pushy sales staff or long queues. I think a good shop should let you compare things properly and make an informed decision, without all the pressure."},{"en":"far more efficient","context":"I usually go shopping early in the morning or on weekday evenings, when it's quieter. Avoiding the busy times just makes the whole thing far more efficient."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Shopping","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-069","_displayNo":"#068","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-069-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-069-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-069-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-069-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I don't treat it as a hobby. It is more enjoyable when I know what I need and have time to compare products. Walking around shops without a purpose makes me tired.","phrases":[{"en":"compare products","meaning":"Look at different items to see how they are similar or different."}],"question":"Do you like shopping and why?","html":"I don&#x27;t treat it as a hobby. It is more enjoyable when I know what I need and have time to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compare products</strong>. Walking around shops without a purpose makes me tired."},{"text":"Yes, I buy everyday products online when they are easy to compare. It saves time and offers more choice. For clothes or things I need to examine closely, I still visit shops.","phrases":[{"en":"examine closely","meaning":"Look at something carefully to check its details."}],"question":"Do you like shopping on the internet?","html":"Yes, I buy everyday products online when they are easy to compare. It saves time and offers more choice. For clothes or things I need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">examine closely</strong>, I still visit shops."},{"text":"I dislike discounts that are not really good deals, salespeople who pressure me and long lines. I want time and clear information to choose what is right for me.","phrases":[{"en":"pressure me","meaning":"Try strongly to make me do something."}],"question":"What don't you like about shopping?","html":"I dislike discounts that are not really good deals, salespeople who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pressure me</strong> and long lines. I want time and clear information to choose what is right for me."},{"text":"I usually shop early in the morning or on weekday evenings because the shops are quieter. It is easier to find what I need and finish quickly then.","phrases":[{"en":"weekday evenings","meaning":"The evenings from Monday to Friday."}],"question":"What time do you do the shopping and why?","html":"I usually shop early in the morning or on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weekday evenings</strong> because the shops are quieter. It is easier to find what I need and finish quickly then."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-069-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-069-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-069-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-069-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"The sports you do and whether you prefer watching","prompt":"What sports or physical activities do you regularly do?\nWhich sport or game would you like to be good at?\nDo you prefer watching or playing sports?\nDo you think children should be encouraged to do more sport?","answer":"Q: What sports or physical activities do you regularly do?\nA: Unfortunately I don't have time to do any sports or physical activities because of my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work commitments</strong>. I'd like to find more time for regular exercise.\n\nQ: Which sport or game would you like to be good at?\nA: I would like to be good at tennis because it combines stamina, timing and precise control. Being able to place the ball accurately <strong class=\"key-phrase\">under pressure</strong> would be extremely satisfying.\n\nQ: Do you prefer watching or playing sports?\nA: I don't watch much sport on TV and I'm <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not a big sports fan</strong>, so I think I would probably enjoy doing sports more than watching them.\n\nQ: Do you think children should be encouraged to do more sport?\nA: Yes. Sport helps children develop <strong class=\"key-phrase\">teamwork and resilience</strong>, and it gives them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a constructive way to use their energy</strong>. The emphasis should be on participation and improvement rather than winning at all costs.","topic":"Sports","phrases":[{"en":"work commitments","context":"Unfortunately I don't have time to do any sports or physical activities because of my work commitments. I'd like to find more time for regular exercise."},{"en":"under pressure","context":"I would like to be good at tennis because it combines stamina, timing and precise control. Being able to place the ball accurately under pressure would be extremely satisfying."},{"en":"not a big sports fan","context":"I don't watch much sport on TV and I'm not a big sports fan, so I think I would probably enjoy doing sports more than watching them."},{"en":"teamwork and resilience","context":"Yes. Sport helps children develop teamwork and resilience, and it gives them a constructive way to use their energy. The emphasis should be on participation and improvement rather than winning at all costs."},{"en":"a constructive way to use their energy","context":"Yes. Sport helps children develop teamwork and resilience, and it gives them a constructive way to use their energy. The emphasis should be on participation and improvement rather than winning at all costs."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Sports","id":"speaking-part-1-070","_displayNo":"#069","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-070-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-070-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-070-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-070-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I don't currently do regular sport because work takes up so much time. I'd like to change that and find more time for exercise in my weekly routine.","phrases":[{"en":"takes up","meaning":"Uses an amount of time or space."}],"question":"What sports or physical activities do you regularly do?","html":"I don&#x27;t currently do regular sport because work <strong class=\"key-phrase\">takes up</strong> so much time. I&#x27;d like to change that and find more time for exercise in my weekly routine."},{"text":"I'd like to be good at tennis. You need energy, good timing and control of the ball. I think being able to hit accurately during a difficult game would feel very satisfying.","phrases":[{"en":"accurately","meaning":"Correctly and in the exact place intended."}],"question":"Which sport or game would you like to be good at?","html":"I&#x27;d like to be good at tennis. You need energy, good timing and control of the ball. I think being able to hit <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accurately</strong> during a difficult game would feel very satisfying."},{"text":"I would probably prefer playing. I don't watch much sport on television and I'm not a big fan, but taking part seems more enjoyable than watching other people.","phrases":[{"en":"taking part","meaning":"Joining in and doing an activity yourself."}],"question":"Do you prefer watching or playing sports?","html":"I would probably prefer playing. I don&#x27;t watch much sport on television and I&#x27;m not a big fan, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taking part</strong> seems more enjoyable than watching other people."},{"text":"Yes, sport helps children learn to work together and keep trying when things are difficult. It also uses their energy well. Enjoying it and improving should matter more than always winning.","phrases":[{"en":"keep trying","meaning":"Continue making an effort, even when something is difficult."}],"question":"Do you think children should be encouraged to do more sport?","html":"Yes, sport helps children learn to work together and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep trying</strong> when things are difficult. It also uses their energy well. Enjoying it and improving should matter more than always winning."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-070-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-070-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-070-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-070-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Watching and taking part in sport","prompt":"Do you like sports?\nDo you enjoy watching sports?\nDo you enjoy participating in any sports?\nWhat is your favourite sports activity?","answer":"Q: Do you like sports?\nA: Yes. I enjoy strength training, swimming and occasionally badminton. I prefer activities where progress can be measured and technique improves through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consistent practice</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you enjoy watching sports?\nA: I watch major events occasionally, but I'd rather participate than sit through an entire season. Watching is most enjoyable when I understand the strategy and have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a reason to care about the result</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you enjoy participating in any sports?\nA: Yes, particularly in individual exercise and casual games with friends. Participation improves my mood and gives me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clear physical challenge</strong>.\n\nQ: What is your favourite sports activity?\nA: Strength training's my favourite because it supports health, posture and long-term mobility. I also like the fact that progress is gradual and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easy to track</strong>.","topic":"Sports","phrases":[{"en":"consistent practice","context":"Yes. I enjoy strength training, swimming and occasionally badminton. I prefer activities where progress can be measured and technique improves through consistent practice."},{"en":"a reason to care about the result","context":"I watch major events occasionally, but I'd rather participate than sit through an entire season. Watching is most enjoyable when I understand the strategy and have a reason to care about the result."},{"en":"a clear physical challenge","context":"Yes, particularly in individual exercise and casual games with friends. Participation improves my mood and gives me a clear physical challenge."},{"en":"easy to track","context":"Strength training's my favourite because it supports health, posture and long-term mobility. I also like the fact that progress is gradual and easy to track."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Sports","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-071","_displayNo":"#070","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-071-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-071-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-071-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-071-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, I enjoy lifting weights, swimming and sometimes playing badminton. I like activities where regular practice helps me improve and where I can see the progress I make.","phrases":[{"en":"progress","meaning":"Improvement or movement towards a goal."}],"question":"Do you like sports?","html":"Yes, I enjoy lifting weights, swimming and sometimes playing badminton. I like activities where regular practice helps me improve and where I can see the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">progress</strong> I make."},{"text":"I sometimes watch important events, but I prefer doing sport myself. Watching is more interesting when I understand how the game works and care about who wins.","phrases":[{"en":"care about","meaning":"Feel interested in or concerned about something."}],"question":"Do you enjoy watching sports?","html":"I sometimes watch important events, but I prefer doing sport myself. Watching is more interesting when I understand how the game works and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">care about</strong> who wins."},{"text":"Yes, especially exercise I can do by myself and relaxed games with friends. Being active makes me feel happier and gives me a physical goal to work towards.","phrases":[{"en":"work towards","meaning":"Make an effort to reach a goal."}],"question":"Do you enjoy participating in any sports?","html":"Yes, especially exercise I can do by myself and relaxed games with friends. Being active makes me feel happier and gives me a physical goal to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work towards</strong>."},{"text":"Lifting weights is my favourite. It helps me stay healthy, stand better and keep moving well as I get older. I also enjoy seeing small improvements over time.","phrases":[{"en":"over time","meaning":"Gradually, as time passes."}],"question":"What is your favourite sports activity?","html":"Lifting weights is my favourite. It helps me stay healthy, stand better and keep moving well as I get older. I also enjoy seeing small improvements <strong class=\"key-phrase\">over time</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-071-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-071-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-071-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-071-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Sports played in your country and local facilities","prompt":"What sports do you like?\nWhat sports are played in your country?\nWhat are the sporting facilities like in your hometown?\nWhat is your favourite sport?","answer":"Q: What sports do you like?\nA: I like badminton and swimming because they're both <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accessible and technically demanding</strong>. I also do strength training, although I see that more as structured exercise than a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">competitive sport</strong>.\n\nQ: What sports are played in your country?\nA: Football, badminton, volleyball and running are all common. Football attracts the largest audiences, while badminton's easy to play in schools, parks and community spaces, making it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">open to almost anyone</strong>.\n\nQ: What are the sporting facilities like in your hometown?\nA: Facilities have improved, with more private gyms and sports centres than before. Public spaces are still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unevenly distributed</strong>, so affordable access remains <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a practical concern</strong>.\n\nQ: What is your favourite sport?\nA: Badminton's probably my favourite sport because it's fast, social and easy to play without expensive equipment. It also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rewards timing and technique</strong> rather than physical size alone.","topic":"Sports","phrases":[{"en":"accessible and technically demanding","context":"I like badminton and swimming because they're both accessible and technically demanding. I also do strength training, although I see that more as structured exercise than a competitive sport."},{"en":"competitive sport","context":"I like badminton and swimming because they're both accessible and technically demanding. I also do strength training, although I see that more as structured exercise than a competitive sport."},{"en":"open to almost anyone","context":"Football, badminton, volleyball and running are all common. Football attracts the largest audiences, while badminton's easy to play in schools, parks and community spaces, making it open to almost anyone."},{"en":"unevenly distributed","context":"Facilities have improved, with more private gyms and sports centres than before. Public spaces are still unevenly distributed, so affordable access remains a practical concern."},{"en":"a practical concern","context":"Facilities have improved, with more private gyms and sports centres than before. Public spaces are still unevenly distributed, so affordable access remains a practical concern."},{"en":"rewards timing and technique","context":"Badminton's probably my favourite sport because it's fast, social and easy to play without expensive equipment. It also rewards timing and technique rather than physical size alone."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Sports","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-072","_displayNo":"#071","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-072-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-072-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-072-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-072-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I like badminton and swimming because many people can try them, but learning to do them well takes skill. I also lift weights, although I think of that more as exercise.","phrases":[{"en":"takes skill","meaning":"Requires ability developed through practice."}],"question":"What sports do you like?","html":"I like badminton and swimming because many people can try them, but learning to do them well <strong class=\"key-phrase\">takes skill</strong>. I also lift weights, although I think of that more as exercise."},{"text":"Football, badminton, volleyball and running are common. Football has the most spectators, while badminton is easy to play in schools, parks and local spaces, so many people can join in.","phrases":[{"en":"spectators","meaning":"People who watch a sport or event."}],"question":"What sports are played in your country?","html":"Football, badminton, volleyball and running are common. Football has the most <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spectators</strong>, while badminton is easy to play in schools, parks and local spaces, so many people can join in."},{"text":"There are more gyms and sports centres than before, so facilities have improved. However, public places to exercise are not equally available everywhere, and some options are still too expensive for people.","phrases":[{"en":"facilities","meaning":"Places and equipment provided for a particular activity."}],"question":"What are the sporting facilities like in your hometown?","html":"There are more gyms and sports centres than before, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">facilities</strong> have improved. However, public places to exercise are not equally available everywhere, and some options are still too expensive for people."},{"text":"Badminton is probably my favourite. It is fast and fun to play with others, and you don't need costly equipment. Good timing and skill matter, not just being big or strong.","phrases":[{"en":"equipment","meaning":"The things needed to do a particular activity."}],"question":"What is your favourite sport?","html":"Badminton is probably my favourite. It is fast and fun to play with others, and you don&#x27;t need costly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">equipment</strong>. Good timing and skill matter, not just being big or strong."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-072-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-072-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-072-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-072-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Studying alone or with others, and where","prompt":"Do you like studying?\nDo you prefer to study alone or with others?\nWhere do you prefer to go when you need to study?\nHow do you feel if someone disturbs you when you are studying?","answer":"Q: Do you like studying?\nA: Yes, I like studying because it's the best way to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gain a deeper insight into</strong> any subject. I like reading, learning from others, and trying to understand difficult concepts.\n\nQ: Do you prefer to study alone or with others?\nA: I prefer to study alone because I need to be able to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">concentrate fully</strong>. Other people are a distraction.\n\nQ: Where do you prefer to go when you need to study?\nA: I like to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">find a quiet corner</strong> in my local library; being in an old building and surrounded by books seems to help me to focus on my work.\n\nQ: How do you feel if someone disturbs you when you are studying?\nA: It frustrates me because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">breaks my concentration</strong>. I prefer to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">avoid distractions</strong> by going somewhere where nobody will interrupt me.","topic":"Study & Education","phrases":[{"en":"gain a deeper insight into","context":"Yes, I like studying because it's the best way to gain a deeper insight into any subject. I like reading, learning from others, and trying to understand difficult concepts."},{"en":"concentrate fully","context":"I prefer to study alone because I need to be able to concentrate fully. Other people are a distraction."},{"en":"find a quiet corner","context":"I like to find a quiet corner in my local library; being in an old building and surrounded by books seems to help me to focus on my work."},{"en":"breaks my concentration","context":"It frustrates me because it breaks my concentration. I prefer to avoid distractions by going somewhere where nobody will interrupt me."},{"en":"avoid distractions","context":"It frustrates me because it breaks my concentration. I prefer to avoid distractions by going somewhere where nobody will interrupt me."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Study & Education","id":"speaking-part-1-073","_displayNo":"#072","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-073-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-073-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-073-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-073-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, I enjoy learning because it helps me understand a subject more fully. I like reading, hearing other people's ideas and trying to make sense of difficult topics.","phrases":[{"en":"make sense of","meaning":"Understand something that seems difficult or confusing."}],"question":"Do you like studying?","html":"Yes, I enjoy learning because it helps me understand a subject more fully. I like reading, hearing other people&#x27;s ideas and trying to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make sense of</strong> difficult topics."},{"text":"I prefer studying alone because I can give the work all my attention. Having other people around can distract me, so I usually learn better on my own.","phrases":[{"en":"on my own","meaning":"Alone, without other people."}],"question":"Do you prefer to study alone or with others?","html":"I prefer studying alone because I can give the work all my attention. Having other people around can distract me, so I usually learn better <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on my own</strong>."},{"text":"I prefer a quiet corner in my local library. The old building and all the books around me create a setting where I find it easier to focus.","phrases":[{"en":"setting","meaning":"The place and surroundings where something happens."}],"question":"Where do you prefer to go when you need to study?","html":"I prefer a quiet corner in my local library. The old building and all the books around me create a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">setting</strong> where I find it easier to focus."},{"text":"I feel annoyed because it makes me lose focus. That is why I try to study somewhere quiet where people are unlikely to interrupt me.","phrases":[{"en":"interrupt","meaning":"Stop someone while they are doing something."}],"question":"How do you feel if someone disturbs you when you are studying?","html":"I feel annoyed because it makes me lose focus. That is why I try to study somewhere quiet where people are unlikely to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">interrupt</strong> me."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-073-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-073-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-073-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-073-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Your time at school and the subjects you liked","prompt":"Do you enjoy your time at school?\nWould you recommend your school to others?\nWhat subject did you find most interesting when you were at school?\nApart from classes, what else did you enjoy at school?","answer":"Q: Do you enjoy your time at school?\nA: Overall, yes. School gave me close friendships and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a strong academic foundation</strong>, although the exam pressure wasn't always easy to handle. Looking back, it was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a valuable learning experience</strong>.\n\nQ: Would you recommend your school to others?\nA: I'd recommend it, mainly because the teachers were committed and the environment was disciplined without being hostile. No school's perfect, but it offered <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a supportive environment</strong>.\n\nQ: What subject did you find most interesting when you were at school?\nA: English was the most interesting subject for me because it opened access to books, films and people from other countries. It felt like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a practical skill</strong> I'd keep using, not just something for exams.\n\nQ: Apart from classes, what else did you enjoy at school?\nA: I enjoyed clubs, school trips and informal time with classmates. Those activities developed confidence and teamwork in ways that ordinary lessons couldn't <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fully replicate</strong>.","topic":"Study & Education","phrases":[{"en":"a strong academic foundation","context":"Overall, yes. School gave me close friendships and a strong academic foundation, although the exam pressure wasn't always easy to handle. Looking back, it was a valuable learning experience."},{"en":"a valuable learning experience","context":"Overall, yes. School gave me close friendships and a strong academic foundation, although the exam pressure wasn't always easy to handle. Looking back, it was a valuable learning experience."},{"en":"a supportive environment","context":"I'd recommend it, mainly because the teachers were committed and the environment was disciplined without being hostile. No school's perfect, but it offered a supportive environment."},{"en":"fully replicate","context":"I enjoyed clubs, school trips and informal time with classmates. Those activities developed confidence and teamwork in ways that ordinary lessons couldn't fully replicate."},{"en":"a practical skill","context":"English was the most interesting subject for me because it opened access to books, films and people from other countries. It felt like a practical skill I'd keep using, not just something for exams."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Study & Education","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-074","_displayNo":"#073","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-074-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-074-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-074-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-074-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Overall, I enjoyed school. I made close friends and learned useful knowledge and skills. Exams were stressful sometimes, but looking back, I think school was a valuable experience.","phrases":[{"en":"looking back","meaning":"Thinking about something that happened in the past."}],"question":"Do you enjoy your time at school?","html":"Overall, I enjoyed school. I made close friends and learned useful knowledge and skills. Exams were stressful sometimes, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">looking back</strong>, I think school was a valuable experience."},{"text":"Yes, mainly because the teachers worked hard and supported us. The school had clear rules, but it wasn't an unfriendly place. It had problems like any school, but I would still recommend it.","phrases":[{"en":"recommend","meaning":"Suggest that something is a good choice."}],"question":"Would you recommend your school to others?","html":"Yes, mainly because the teachers worked hard and supported us. The school had clear rules, but it wasn&#x27;t an unfriendly place. It had problems like any school, but I would still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recommend</strong> it."},{"text":"English interested me most. It helped me understand books and films and communicate with people from other countries. I could see how I would use it outside school as well as in exams.","phrases":[{"en":"communicate","meaning":"Share information, ideas or feelings with other people."}],"question":"What subject did you find most interesting when you were at school?","html":"English interested me most. It helped me understand books and films and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">communicate</strong> with people from other countries. I could see how I would use it outside school as well as in exams."},{"text":"I enjoyed clubs, trips and spending time with classmates outside lessons. These experiences helped me become more confident and learn to work with others in a different way.","phrases":[{"en":"classmates","meaning":"Other students in the same class as you."}],"question":"Apart from classes, what else did you enjoy at school?","html":"I enjoyed clubs, trips and spending time with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">classmates</strong> outside lessons. These experiences helped me become more confident and learn to work with others in a different way."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-074-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-074-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-074-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-074-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"What you studied and your favourite subject","prompt":"What have you studied?\nWhat was your major?\nWhat kind of school did you attend?\nWhat was your favourite subject?","answer":"Q: What have you studied?\nA: I've studied English language and teaching. The course combined linguistics, classroom methods and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical training</strong>, so it gave me both theory and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical experience</strong>.\n\nQ: What was your major?\nA: My major was English education. I'd always been interested in language, and I wanted a career where communication would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">play a central role</strong>.\n\nQ: What kind of school did you attend?\nA: I attended a state school with a fairly traditional structure — classes were teacher-led, but that didn't stop good teachers from encouraging questions and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">critical thinking</strong>.\n\nQ: What was your favourite subject?\nA: English was my favourite subject because progress was practical and visible. Each new skill gave me access to more books, conversations and professional opportunities <strong class=\"key-phrase\">well beyond the classroom</strong>.","topic":"Study & Education","phrases":[{"en":"practical training","context":"I've studied English language and teaching. The course combined linguistics, classroom methods and practical training, so it gave me both theory and practical experience."},{"en":"practical experience","context":"I've studied English language and teaching. The course combined linguistics, classroom methods and practical training, so it gave me both theory and practical experience."},{"en":"play a central role","context":"My major was English education. I'd always been interested in language, and I wanted a career where communication would play a central role."},{"en":"critical thinking","context":"I attended a state school with a fairly traditional structure — classes were teacher-led, but that didn't stop good teachers from encouraging questions and critical thinking."},{"en":"well beyond the classroom","context":"English was my favourite subject because progress was practical and visible. Each new skill gave me access to more books, conversations and professional opportunities well beyond the classroom."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Study & Education","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-075","_displayNo":"#074","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-075-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-075-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-075-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-075-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I studied English and teaching. My course included how language works, ways to teach a class and practical training, so I learned ideas and had chances to use them.","phrases":[{"en":"practical training","meaning":"Learning by practising the skills needed for real work."}],"question":"What have you studied?","html":"I studied English and teaching. My course included how language works, ways to teach a class and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical training</strong>, so I learned ideas and had chances to use them."},{"text":"I majored in English education. I had always liked languages and wanted a job where speaking with people and explaining ideas would be important parts of my work.","phrases":[{"en":"majored in","meaning":"Studied a subject as the main part of a university course."}],"question":"What was your major?","html":"I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">majored in</strong> English education. I had always liked languages and wanted a job where speaking with people and explaining ideas would be important parts of my work."},{"text":"I went to a state school. Lessons were usually led by the teacher, but good teachers still encouraged us to ask questions and think carefully about ideas.","phrases":[{"en":"state school","meaning":"A school funded and run through the government."}],"question":"What kind of school did you attend?","html":"I went to a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">state school</strong>. Lessons were usually led by the teacher, but good teachers still encouraged us to ask questions and think carefully about ideas."},{"text":"English was my favourite because I could use each new skill in real life. As I improved, I could read more, have more conversations and see more possibilities for future work.","phrases":[{"en":"possibilities","meaning":"Things that may happen or choices that may become available."}],"question":"What was your favourite subject?","html":"English was my favourite because I could use each new skill in real life. As I improved, I could read more, have more conversations and see more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">possibilities</strong> for future work."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-075-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-075-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-075-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-075-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"How often you call and who you talk to","prompt":"How often do you make telephone calls?\nWho do you spend most time talking to on the telephone?\nWhen do you think you'll next make a telephone call?\nDo you sometimes prefer to send a text message instead of telephoning?","answer":"Q: How often do you make telephone calls?\nA: I probably make three or four phone calls every day, usually just to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make arrangements with</strong> work colleagues or to speak to family and friends.\n\nQ: Who do you spend most time talking to on the telephone?\nA: I think it would be one of my closest work colleagues. We often call each other rather than meeting <strong class=\"key-phrase\">face-to-face</strong>.\n\nQ: When do you think you'll next make a telephone call?\nA: My family are expecting me to phone them as soon as I finish this exam. They'll want to know <strong class=\"key-phrase\">how it went</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you sometimes prefer to send a text message instead of telephoning?\nA: Yes. For a simple arrangement, a text message is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more efficient than a phone call</strong> because both people can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deal with it in their own time</strong>. I still call when it's urgent or needs a proper discussion.","topic":"Telephoning","phrases":[{"en":"make arrangements with","context":"I probably make three or four phone calls every day, usually just to make arrangements with work colleagues or to speak to family and friends."},{"en":"face-to-face","context":"I think it would be one of my closest work colleagues. We often call each other rather than meeting face-to-face."},{"en":"how it went","context":"My family are expecting me to phone them as soon as I finish this exam. They'll want to know how it went."},{"en":"more efficient than a phone call","context":"Yes. For a simple arrangement, a text message is more efficient than a phone call because both people can deal with it in their own time. I still call when it's urgent or needs a proper discussion."},{"en":"deal with it in their own time","context":"Yes. For a simple arrangement, a text message is more efficient than a phone call because both people can deal with it in their own time. I still call when it's urgent or needs a proper discussion."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Telephoning","id":"speaking-part-1-076","_displayNo":"#075","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-076-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-076-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-076-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-076-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I make about three or four calls a day. Most are to arrange things with people at work or to speak to family and friends, so they are usually part of everyday life.","phrases":[{"en":"arrange things","meaning":"Make plans or organise what needs to happen."}],"question":"How often do you make telephone calls?","html":"I make about three or four calls a day. Most are to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">arrange things</strong> with people at work or to speak to family and friends, so they are usually part of everyday life."},{"text":"Probably one of my closest colleagues. We often phone each other instead of meeting in person, so I spend more time on the phone with that person than with most others.","phrases":[{"en":"colleagues","meaning":"People you work with."}],"question":"Who do you spend most time talking to on the telephone?","html":"Probably one of my closest <strong class=\"key-phrase\">colleagues</strong>. We often phone each other instead of meeting in person, so I spend more time on the phone with that person than with most others."},{"text":"I'll probably call my family as soon as this exam finishes. They are expecting to hear from me because they want to know how it went.","phrases":[{"en":"expecting","meaning":"Thinking or waiting for something to happen."}],"question":"When do you think you'll next make a telephone call?","html":"I&#x27;ll probably call my family as soon as this exam finishes. They are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expecting</strong> to hear from me because they want to know how it went."},{"text":"Yes, for simple plans I prefer texting because each person can reply when they are free. I still phone if something is urgent or we need to discuss it properly.","phrases":[{"en":"urgent","meaning":"Needing attention or action very soon."}],"question":"Do you sometimes prefer to send a text message instead of telephoning?","html":"Yes, for simple plans I prefer texting because each person can reply when they are free. I still phone if something is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">urgent</strong> or we need to discuss it properly."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-076-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-076-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-076-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-076-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"How much television you watch, and your favourite programme","prompt":"Do you watch a lot of TV?\nTell me about television in your country?\nWhat is your favourite TV programme?\nIs TV more important to you now than in the past?","answer":"Q: Do you watch a lot of TV?\nA: No, not much. I'd rather watch selected programmes online than follow a fixed television schedule, because that way I can choose content and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">avoid passive viewing</strong>.\n\nQ: Tell me about television in your country?\nA: Television's still got a broad audience for news, sport and major entertainment programmes, but younger viewers increasingly stream content, so broadcasters are adapting to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changing viewing habits</strong>.\n\nQ: What is your favourite TV programme?\nA: I enjoy well-produced documentaries about science and society. They've got strong visuals and reliable explanations, and they often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put complex issues into perspective</strong>.\n\nQ: Is TV more important to you now than in the past?\nA: It's less important to me now because I can access information and entertainment on demand. Television's become just one option among many rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the centre of home entertainment</strong>.","topic":"Television","phrases":[{"en":"avoid passive viewing","context":"No, not much. I'd rather watch selected programmes online than follow a fixed television schedule, because that way I can choose content and avoid passive viewing."},{"en":"changing viewing habits","context":"Television's still got a broad audience for news, sport and major entertainment programmes, but younger viewers increasingly stream content, so broadcasters are adapting to changing viewing habits."},{"en":"put complex issues into perspective","context":"I enjoy well-produced documentaries about science and society. They've got strong visuals and reliable explanations, and they often put complex issues into perspective."},{"en":"the centre of home entertainment","context":"It's less important to me now because I can access information and entertainment on demand. Television's become just one option among many rather than the centre of home entertainment."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Television","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-077","_displayNo":"#076","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-077-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-077-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-077-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-077-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"No, I don't watch much. I prefer choosing programmes online and watching when I want, rather than following a TV schedule or watching whatever happens to be on.","phrases":[{"en":"schedule","meaning":"A plan showing when programmes or activities happen."}],"question":"Do you watch a lot of TV?","html":"No, I don&#x27;t watch much. I prefer choosing programmes online and watching when I want, rather than following a TV <strong class=\"key-phrase\">schedule</strong> or watching whatever happens to be on."},{"text":"Many people still watch TV for news, sport and big entertainment shows. Younger people often choose programmes online, so television companies are changing how they provide their content.","phrases":[{"en":"content","meaning":"Programmes, videos or other material that people watch or read."}],"question":"Tell me about television in your country?","html":"Many people still watch TV for news, sport and big entertainment shows. Younger people often choose programmes online, so television companies are changing how they provide their <strong class=\"key-phrase\">content</strong>."},{"text":"I especially enjoy documentaries about science and society. Good pictures and clear, trustworthy explanations help me understand complicated subjects and see why they matter.","phrases":[{"en":"trustworthy","meaning":"Able to be believed or relied on."}],"question":"What is your favourite TV programme?","html":"I especially enjoy documentaries about science and society. Good pictures and clear, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trustworthy</strong> explanations help me understand complicated subjects and see why they matter."},{"text":"No, it is less important now. I can find information and entertainment online whenever I want. Television is still useful, but it is only one of several choices at home.","phrases":[{"en":"whenever I want","meaning":"At any time I choose."}],"question":"Is TV more important to you now than in the past?","html":"No, it is less important now. I can find information and entertainment online <strong class=\"key-phrase\">whenever I want</strong>. Television is still useful, but it is only one of several choices at home."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-077-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-077-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-077-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-077-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"The toys you played with as a child","prompt":"Did you play with toys when you were a child?\nWhat kinds of toys did you like?\nIn your country, do boys and girls play with the same types of toys?\nDo you think that toys help children to learn?","answer":"Q: Did you play with toys when you were a child?\nA: Yes, of course. I loved <strong class=\"key-phrase\">playing with toys</strong> when I was a child; I think all children do.\n\nQ: What kinds of toys did you like?\nA: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">As far as I remember</strong>, I mostly played with toy cars, planes and action figures from films or cartoons. I also liked building things with Lego.\n\nQ: In your country, do boys and girls play with the same types of toys?\nA: Not really. I think boys like the kinds of toys that I mentioned before, whereas girls play with dolls. My niece, for example, doesn't like toy cars; she prefers dressing dolls in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">different outfits</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you think that toys help children to learn?\nA: Yes, I do. All toys encourage children to use their <strong class=\"key-phrase\">imagination and creativity</strong>. Even with simple toys, children imagine situations and invent games and rules.","topic":"Toys","phrases":[{"en":"playing with toys","context":"Yes, of course. I loved playing with toys when I was a child; I think all children do."},{"en":"As far as I remember","context":"As far as I remember, I mostly played with toy cars, planes and action figures from films or cartoons. I also liked building things with Lego."},{"en":"different outfits","context":"Not really. I think boys like the kinds of toys that I mentioned before, whereas girls play with dolls. My niece, for example, doesn't like toy cars; she prefers dressing dolls in different outfits."},{"en":"imagination and creativity","context":"Yes, I do. All toys encourage children to use their imagination and creativity. Even with simple toys, children imagine situations and invent games and rules."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Toys","id":"speaking-part-1-078","_displayNo":"#077","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-078-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-078-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-078-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-078-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, I loved playing with toys as a child. They were an enjoyable part of my childhood, and I think many other children feel the same way.","phrases":[{"en":"childhood","meaning":"The time in your life when you were a child."}],"question":"Did you play with toys when you were a child?","html":"Yes, I loved playing with toys as a child. They were an enjoyable part of my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">childhood</strong>, and I think many other children feel the same way."},{"text":"I remember playing mostly with toy cars, planes and figures from films or cartoons. I also liked Lego because I could use the pieces to build different things.","phrases":[{"en":"figures","meaning":"Small models shaped like people or characters."}],"question":"What kinds of toys did you like?","html":"I remember playing mostly with toy cars, planes and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">figures</strong> from films or cartoons. I also liked Lego because I could use the pieces to build different things."},{"text":"Not always. Many boys choose cars or action figures, while some girls prefer dolls. For example, my niece likes putting different clothes on dolls rather than playing with toy cars, although every child is different.","phrases":[{"en":"action figures","meaning":"Small toy models of characters from films, games or stories."}],"question":"In your country, do boys and girls play with the same types of toys?","html":"Not always. Many boys choose cars or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">action figures</strong>, while some girls prefer dolls. For example, my niece likes putting different clothes on dolls rather than playing with toy cars, although every child is different."},{"text":"Yes, toys can help children use their imagination and make new things. Even simple toys let them create situations and invent their own games and rules.","phrases":[{"en":"invent","meaning":"Think of or create something new."}],"question":"Do you think that toys help children to learn?","html":"Yes, toys can help children use their imagination and make new things. Even simple toys let them create situations and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">invent</strong> their own games and rules."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-078-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-078-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-078-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-078-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Public transport where you live","prompt":"Do you use public transport?\nWhich type of public transport do you prefer?\nHow good is the public transport where you live?\nDo you think people should use public transport more?","answer":"Q: Do you use public transport?\nA: These days I mostly drive, and the main reason is that my usual journeys are much faster by car. For example, the half-hour drive that I do twice a week would take me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">three times as long</strong> by train and bus.\n\nQ: Which type of public transport do you prefer?\nA: I definitely prefer the train. I like being able to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stroll up and down</strong>, and on some trains I can book a seat with a table and a socket, so that I can do some work.\n\nQ: How good is the public transport where you live?\nA: It's quite good. There's a train station not far from my house, and it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a direct line</strong> straight into the city centre. There are lots of bus routes too, and there's even a tram network in the centre of Manchester.\n\nQ: Do you think people should use public transport more?\nA: Yes, as long as it's convenient for them. Public transport is obviously more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">environmentally friendly</strong> than private cars, so we could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce air pollution</strong> by using it more.","topic":"Transport","phrases":[{"en":"stroll up and down","context":"I definitely prefer the train. I like being able to stroll up and down, and on some trains I can book a seat with a table and a socket, so that I can do some work."},{"en":"a direct line","context":"It's quite good. There's a train station not far from my house, and it's a direct line straight into the city centre. There are lots of bus routes too, and there's even a tram network in the centre of Manchester."},{"en":"environmentally friendly","context":"Yes, as long as it's convenient for them. Public transport is obviously more environmentally friendly than private cars, so we could reduce air pollution by using it more."},{"en":"reduce air pollution","context":"Yes, as long as it's convenient for them. Public transport is obviously more environmentally friendly than private cars, so we could reduce air pollution by using it more."},{"en":"three times as long","context":"These days I mostly drive, and the main reason is that my usual journeys are much faster by car. For example, the half-hour drive that I do twice a week would take me three times as long by train and bus."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Transport","id":"speaking-part-1-079","_displayNo":"#078","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-079-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-079-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-079-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-079-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I mainly drive now because it is faster for my usual trips. A journey I make twice a week takes half an hour by car but about three times longer by train and bus.","phrases":[{"en":"usual trips","meaning":"The journeys you normally make."}],"question":"Do you use public transport?","html":"I mainly drive now because it is faster for my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">usual trips</strong>. A journey I make twice a week takes half an hour by car but about three times longer by train and bus."},{"text":"I prefer trains because I can get up and walk around. On some, I can reserve a seat with a table and a power socket, which lets me work during the journey.","phrases":[{"en":"reserve a seat","meaning":"Arrange in advance to have a particular place to sit."}],"question":"Which type of public transport do you prefer?","html":"I prefer trains because I can get up and walk around. On some, I can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reserve a seat</strong> with a table and a power socket, which lets me work during the journey."},{"text":"It is quite good. A nearby station has trains straight to the city centre, and there are many bus routes. Central Manchester also has trams, so there are several ways to travel.","phrases":[{"en":"trams","meaning":"Passenger vehicles that travel on rails along city streets."}],"question":"How good is the public transport where you live?","html":"It is quite good. A nearby station has trains straight to the city centre, and there are many bus routes. Central Manchester also has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trams</strong>, so there are several ways to travel."},{"text":"Yes, if it is convenient for their journeys. Using buses and trains instead of separate cars can reduce pollution, so I think it is a good choice when people can manage it.","phrases":[{"en":"convenient","meaning":"Easy and suitable for your needs."}],"question":"Do you think people should use public transport more?","html":"Yes, if it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">convenient</strong> for their journeys. Using buses and trains instead of separate cars can reduce pollution, so I think it is a good choice when people can manage it."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-079-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-079-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-079-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-079-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"The transport system and traffic in your hometown","prompt":"Can you describe the transport system in your hometown?\nWhat is the most common means of transport in your country?\nDo traffic jams often occur in your city?\nHow can we solve this problem?","answer":"Q: Can you describe the transport system in your hometown?\nA: Hanoi relies heavily on motorbikes, buses, cars and an expanding metro system. The network's improving, but transfers between different modes still need to become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more seamless</strong>.\n\nQ: What is the most common means of transport in your country?\nA: Motorbikes are still the most common personal vehicle because they're flexible in dense streets. Buses and the metro are increasingly important for people who want to avoid <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the cost of driving</strong>.\n\nQ: Do traffic jams often occur in your city?\nA: Yes, especially during morning and evening peaks. Rapid urban growth and high private-vehicle use have placed <strong class=\"key-phrase\">considerable pressure</strong> on roads that weren't built to handle this many vehicles.\n\nQ: How can we solve this problem?\nA: The city needs frequent public transport, safe walking routes and better traffic management. Restrictions on private vehicles won't work unless people have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a reliable alternative</strong>.","topic":"Transport","phrases":[{"en":"more seamless","context":"Hanoi relies heavily on motorbikes, buses, cars and an expanding metro system. The network's improving, but transfers between different modes still need to become more seamless."},{"en":"the cost of driving","context":"Motorbikes are still the most common personal vehicle because they're flexible in dense streets. Buses and the metro are increasingly important for people who want to avoid the cost of driving."},{"en":"considerable pressure","context":"Yes, especially during morning and evening peaks. Rapid urban growth and high private-vehicle use have placed considerable pressure on roads that weren't built to handle this many vehicles."},{"en":"a reliable alternative","context":"The city needs frequent public transport, safe walking routes and better traffic management. Restrictions on private vehicles won't work unless people have a reliable alternative."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Transport","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-080","_displayNo":"#079","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-080-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-080-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-080-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-080-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"People in Hanoi use motorbikes, buses and cars, and the metro is growing. Transport is improving, but changing from one type to another still needs to become easier.","phrases":[{"en":"metro","meaning":"A city train system, often partly underground or above street level."}],"question":"Can you describe the transport system in your hometown?","html":"People in Hanoi use motorbikes, buses and cars, and the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">metro</strong> is growing. Transport is improving, but changing from one type to another still needs to become easier."},{"text":"Motorbikes are still very common because they are easy to use in crowded streets. Buses and the metro are becoming important too, especially for people who want to spend less on travel.","phrases":[{"en":"crowded streets","meaning":"Roads with many people or vehicles using them."}],"question":"What is the most common means of transport in your country?","html":"Motorbikes are still very common because they are easy to use in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">crowded streets</strong>. Buses and the metro are becoming important too, especially for people who want to spend less on travel."},{"text":"Yes, especially when people travel to work and home again. The city has grown quickly and there are many private vehicles, while the roads do not have enough space for them all.","phrases":[{"en":"private vehicles","meaning":"Cars or motorbikes used by individuals rather than shared public services."}],"question":"Do traffic jams often occur in your city?","html":"Yes, especially when people travel to work and home again. The city has grown quickly and there are many <strong class=\"key-phrase\">private vehicles</strong>, while the roads do not have enough space for them all."},{"text":"We need buses and trains that run often, safe places to walk and better control of traffic. Before limiting private vehicles, the city should give people other dependable ways to travel.","phrases":[{"en":"dependable","meaning":"Able to be trusted to work well when needed."}],"question":"How can we solve this problem?","html":"We need buses and trains that run often, safe places to walk and better control of traffic. Before limiting private vehicles, the city should give people other <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dependable</strong> ways to travel."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-080-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-080-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-080-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-080-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Holidays: travelling or staying at home","prompt":"Do you like holidays?\nDo you prefer to travel or stay at home during holiday periods?\nWhat do most people do during their holidays in your country?\nWhen do you prefer to take your holidays?","answer":"Q: Do you like holidays?\nA: Yes, I love holidays because I get the chance to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take a break</strong> from work, to relax and unwind, and to spend time with my family.\n\nQ: Do you prefer to travel or stay at home during holiday periods?\nA: If I can I prefer to travel. It's nice to get away from everything and to have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a change of scenery</strong>. Also, I like visiting new places and doing some sightseeing.\n\nQ: What do most people do during their holidays in your country?\nA: During the summer especially, I think most British people go somewhere hot and sunny, to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">escape from the British weather</strong>!\n\nQ: When do you prefer to take your holidays?\nA: I like taking my holidays just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">outside the main summer season</strong>, which is July and August. If you go on holiday in May, June or September, everything is cheaper and places aren't so crowded with tourists.","topic":"Travel & Holidays","phrases":[{"en":"take a break","context":"Yes, I love holidays because I get the chance to take a break from work, to relax and unwind, and to spend time with my family."},{"en":"a change of scenery","context":"If I can I prefer to travel. It's nice to get away from everything and to have a change of scenery. Also, I like visiting new places and doing some sightseeing."},{"en":"escape from the British weather","context":"During the summer especially, I think most British people go somewhere hot and sunny, to escape from the British weather!"},{"en":"outside the main summer season","context":"I like taking my holidays just outside the main summer season, which is July and August. If you go on holiday in May, June or September, everything is cheaper and places aren't so crowded with tourists."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Travel & Holidays","id":"speaking-part-1-081","_displayNo":"#080","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-081-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-081-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-081-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-081-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, I love holidays. They give me time away from work to relax and spend time with my family, which helps me feel refreshed before returning to my routine.","phrases":[{"en":"refreshed","meaning":"Feeling rested and ready to do things again."}],"question":"Do you like holidays?","html":"Yes, I love holidays. They give me time away from work to relax and spend time with my family, which helps me feel <strong class=\"key-phrase\">refreshed</strong> before returning to my routine."},{"text":"I prefer travelling if I can. Visiting new places gives me a break from my usual surroundings, and I enjoy looking around and seeing interesting sights.","phrases":[{"en":"surroundings","meaning":"The places and things around you."}],"question":"Do you prefer to travel or stay at home during holiday periods?","html":"I prefer travelling if I can. Visiting new places gives me a break from my usual <strong class=\"key-phrase\">surroundings</strong>, and I enjoy looking around and seeing interesting sights."},{"text":"I think many British people like going somewhere warm and sunny, especially in summer. They enjoy taking a break from the weather at home during their holidays.","phrases":[{"en":"taking a break from","meaning":"Spending some time away from something you usually experience."}],"question":"What do most people do during their holidays in your country?","html":"I think many British people like going somewhere warm and sunny, especially in summer. They enjoy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taking a break from</strong> the weather at home during their holidays."},{"text":"I prefer May, June or September, just before or after the busiest summer months. Prices are usually lower and there are fewer tourists, so the trip feels more relaxed.","phrases":[{"en":"tourists","meaning":"People visiting a place for pleasure."}],"question":"When do you prefer to take your holidays?","html":"I prefer May, June or September, just before or after the busiest summer months. Prices are usually lower and there are fewer <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tourists</strong>, so the trip feels more relaxed."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-081-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-081-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-081-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-081-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"How you travel and what you do while travelling","prompt":"What form of transport do you prefer to use?\nHow much time do you spend travelling on a normal day?\nWhat do you do while you are travelling?\nDo you ever have problems with transport?","answer":"Q: What form of transport do you prefer to use?\nA: I prefer to travel by car because it means that I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have my own space</strong>. Also, the buses and trains in my city are usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">packed out</strong>; my car's much more comfortable.\n\nQ: How much time do you spend travelling on a normal day?\nA: I probably spend about an hour and a half travelling to and from work. I travel during <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the rush hour</strong>, so there's quite a lot of traffic.\n\nQ: What do you do while you are travelling?\nA: I usually listen to the radio or a CD. In the morning I like to listen to the news to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">find out what is happening</strong> in the world.\n\nQ: Do you ever have problems with transport?\nA: Yes, as I said, I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get stuck in traffic</strong> on my way to and from work. I used to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get annoyed by</strong> traffic jams, but now I'm used to them.","topic":"Travel & Holidays","phrases":[{"en":"have my own space","context":"I prefer to travel by car because it means that I have my own space. Also, the buses and trains in my city are usually packed out; my car's much more comfortable."},{"en":"packed out","context":"I prefer to travel by car because it means that I have my own space. Also, the buses and trains in my city are usually packed out; my car's much more comfortable."},{"en":"find out what is happening","context":"I usually listen to the radio or a CD. In the morning I like to listen to the news to find out what is happening in the world."},{"en":"get stuck in traffic","context":"Yes, as I said, I get stuck in traffic on my way to and from work. I used to get annoyed by traffic jams, but now I'm used to them."},{"en":"get annoyed by","context":"Yes, as I said, I get stuck in traffic on my way to and from work. I used to get annoyed by traffic jams, but now I'm used to them."},{"en":"the rush hour","context":"I probably spend about an hour and a half travelling to and from work. I travel during the rush hour, so there's quite a lot of traffic."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Travel & Holidays","id":"speaking-part-1-082","_displayNo":"#081","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-082-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-082-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-082-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-082-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I prefer travelling by car because I have my own space and feel more comfortable. The buses and trains in my city are often very crowded, which makes them less pleasant for me.","phrases":[{"en":"my own space","meaning":"An area where I can feel comfortable without being crowded by others."}],"question":"What form of transport do you prefer to use?","html":"I prefer travelling by car because I have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">my own space</strong> and feel more comfortable. The buses and trains in my city are often very crowded, which makes them less pleasant for me."},{"text":"I spend about an hour and a half going to work and coming home each day. I travel at busy times, so traffic adds quite a lot to the journey.","phrases":[{"en":"busy times","meaning":"Periods when many people are doing the same activity or travelling."}],"question":"How much time do you spend travelling on a normal day?","html":"I spend about an hour and a half going to work and coming home each day. I travel at <strong class=\"key-phrase\">busy times</strong>, so traffic adds quite a lot to the journey."},{"text":"I usually listen to the radio or a CD while travelling. In the morning, I like hearing the news so I know what is happening in the world.","phrases":[{"en":"the news","meaning":"Reports about recent events."}],"question":"What do you do while you are travelling?","html":"I usually listen to the radio or a CD while travelling. In the morning, I like hearing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the news</strong> so I know what is happening in the world."},{"text":"Yes, I often get delayed by traffic on my way to and from work. It used to annoy me a lot, but it happens so often that I'm used to it now.","phrases":[{"en":"used to it","meaning":"Familiar with something so it no longer feels unusual or surprising."}],"question":"Do you ever have problems with transport?","html":"Yes, I often get delayed by traffic on my way to and from work. It used to annoy me a lot, but it happens so often that I&#x27;m <strong class=\"key-phrase\">used to it</strong> now."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-082-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-082-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-082-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-082-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Travelling alone or with friends","prompt":"Do you like travelling? Why?\nDo you like to travel alone or with your friends?\nWhich other places have you travelled to?\nWhat do you usually do when you first arrive in a new place?","answer":"Q: Do you like travelling? Why?\nA: Yes, because it's a break from my usual routine and it exposes me to different ways of living. Even a short trip can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">broaden my horizons</strong> if I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pay attention to</strong> the place rather than simply taking photographs.\n\nQ: Do you like to travel alone or with your friends?\nA: I prefer travelling with one or two people I get on with well. It's safer and more enjoyable that way, and a small group still leaves room for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">personal flexibility</strong>.\n\nQ: Which other places have you travelled to?\nA: I've travelled to several places in Vietnam, including Hue, Da Nang and Ho Chi Minh City. Each trip was different, but Hue was especially memorable because of its historic sites and slower <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pace of life</strong>.\n\nQ: What do you usually do when you first arrive in a new place?\nA: I usually check the neighbourhood on foot, learn the basic transport routes and identify a few practical places nearby — it doesn't take long, and it helps me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel at ease</strong> for the rest of the trip.","topic":"Travel & Holidays","phrases":[{"en":"broaden my horizons","context":"Yes, because it's a break from my usual routine and it exposes me to different ways of living. Even a short trip can broaden my horizons if I pay attention to the place rather than simply taking photographs."},{"en":"pay attention to","context":"Yes, because it's a break from my usual routine and it exposes me to different ways of living. Even a short trip can broaden my horizons if I pay attention to the place rather than simply taking photographs."},{"en":"personal flexibility","context":"I prefer travelling with one or two people I get on with well. It's safer and more enjoyable that way, and a small group still leaves room for personal flexibility."},{"en":"pace of life","context":"I've travelled to several places in Vietnam, including Hue, Da Nang and Ho Chi Minh City. Each trip was different, but Hue was especially memorable because of its historic sites and slower pace of life."},{"en":"feel at ease","context":"I usually check the neighbourhood on foot, learn the basic transport routes and identify a few practical places nearby — it doesn't take long, and it helps me feel at ease for the rest of the trip."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Travel & Holidays","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-083","_displayNo":"#082","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-083-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-083-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-083-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-083-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, travelling gives me a break from normal life and helps me see how other people live. Even a short trip can teach me something if I pay attention to the place.","phrases":[{"en":"pay attention to","meaning":"Notice and think carefully about something."}],"question":"Do you like travelling? Why?","html":"Yes, travelling gives me a break from normal life and helps me see how other people live. Even a short trip can teach me something if I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pay attention to</strong> the place."},{"text":"I prefer travelling with one or two friends I get along with. It feels safer and more enjoyable, while a small group still lets us change our plans fairly easily.","phrases":[{"en":"get along with","meaning":"Have a friendly and comfortable relationship with someone."}],"question":"Do you like to travel alone or with your friends?","html":"I prefer travelling with one or two friends I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get along with</strong>. It feels safer and more enjoyable, while a small group still lets us change our plans fairly easily."},{"text":"I've visited several places in Vietnam, including Hue, Da Nang and Ho Chi Minh City. Hue stands out because of its historic places and more relaxed way of life.","phrases":[{"en":"stands out","meaning":"Is especially noticeable or memorable compared with other things."}],"question":"Which other places have you travelled to?","html":"I&#x27;ve visited several places in Vietnam, including Hue, Da Nang and Ho Chi Minh City. Hue <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stands out</strong> because of its historic places and more relaxed way of life."},{"text":"I usually walk around the area, find out how the transport works and look for useful places nearby. Doing that helps me feel comfortable for the rest of my stay.","phrases":[{"en":"find out","meaning":"Learn information you did not know before."}],"question":"What do you usually do when you first arrive in a new place?","html":"I usually walk around the area, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">find out</strong> how the transport works and look for useful places nearby. Doing that helps me feel comfortable for the rest of my stay."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-083-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-083-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-083-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-083-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"The benefits of visiting other countries","prompt":"What are the benefits of visiting different countries?\nWhat are the negative effects of tourism?\nWhat do you need to do before you go abroad on holiday?\nWhat type of problems can people have on holiday?","answer":"Q: What are the benefits of visiting different countries?\nA: Visiting other countries can challenge assumptions, improve communication and provide first-hand knowledge you just can't get from books. It's especially valuable when travellers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">engage with local life</strong> rather than staying in a tourist bubble.\n\nQ: What are the negative effects of tourism?\nA: Uncontrolled tourism can raise housing costs, overcrowd public spaces and damage natural sites. The problem isn't tourism itself — it's growth that ignores <strong class=\"key-phrase\">local carrying capacity</strong>.\n\nQ: What do you need to do before you go abroad on holiday?\nA: I check entry rules, insurance, transport, accommodation and basic local customs. Careful preparation means I don't get caught out, while still leaving enough freedom for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spontaneous experiences</strong>.\n\nQ: What type of problems can people have on holiday?\nA: Common holiday problems include delayed transport, illness, lost documents and unexpected costs, but they're much easier to manage with travel insurance, digital copies of documents and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a realistic backup plan</strong>.","topic":"Travel & Holidays","phrases":[{"en":"engage with local life","context":"Visiting other countries can challenge assumptions, improve communication and provide first-hand knowledge you just can't get from books. It's especially valuable when travellers engage with local life rather than staying in a tourist bubble."},{"en":"local carrying capacity","context":"Uncontrolled tourism can raise housing costs, overcrowd public spaces and damage natural sites. The problem isn't tourism itself — it's growth that ignores local carrying capacity."},{"en":"spontaneous experiences","context":"I check entry rules, insurance, transport, accommodation and basic local customs. Careful preparation means I don't get caught out, while still leaving enough freedom for spontaneous experiences."},{"en":"a realistic backup plan","context":"Common holiday problems include delayed transport, illness, lost documents and unexpected costs, but they're much easier to manage with travel insurance, digital copies of documents and a realistic backup plan."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Travel & Holidays","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-084","_displayNo":"#083","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-084-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-084-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-084-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-084-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Travelling abroad can help us question our ideas, communicate better and learn through real experiences. It is especially useful when we spend time with local people and see how they live.","phrases":[{"en":"abroad","meaning":"In or to another country."}],"question":"What are the benefits of visiting different countries?","html":"Travelling <strong class=\"key-phrase\">abroad</strong> can help us question our ideas, communicate better and learn through real experiences. It is especially useful when we spend time with local people and see how they live."},{"text":"Too many tourists can make housing more expensive, crowd public places and damage nature. Tourism becomes a problem when visitor numbers grow beyond what an area can comfortably support.","phrases":[{"en":"beyond","meaning":"Further or more than a particular limit."}],"question":"What are the negative effects of tourism?","html":"Too many tourists can make housing more expensive, crowd public places and damage nature. Tourism becomes a problem when visitor numbers grow <strong class=\"key-phrase\">beyond</strong> what an area can comfortably support."},{"text":"I check the rules for entering the country, insurance, travel, a place to stay and local customs. Planning helps me avoid problems, but I still leave time for unexpected activities.","phrases":[{"en":"customs","meaning":"The usual traditions and ways of behaving in a place."}],"question":"What do you need to do before you go abroad on holiday?","html":"I check the rules for entering the country, insurance, travel, a place to stay and local <strong class=\"key-phrase\">customs</strong>. Planning helps me avoid problems, but I still leave time for unexpected activities."},{"text":"People may face travel delays, illness, lost passports or costs they did not expect. Insurance, copies of documents and a sensible second plan can make those problems easier to handle.","phrases":[{"en":"delays","meaning":"Situations in which something happens later than planned."}],"question":"What type of problems can people have on holiday?","html":"People may face travel <strong class=\"key-phrase\">delays</strong>, illness, lost passports or costs they did not expect. Insurance, copies of documents and a sensible second plan can make those problems easier to handle."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-084-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-084-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-084-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-084-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Trees in your hometown and why they matter","prompt":"Are there many trees in your hometown?\nWhat are the benefits of planting more trees?\nDo cities have enough trees?","answer":"Q: Are there many trees in your hometown?\nA: Some older parts of my hometown have mature trees, but the newer districts often don't have as many. It's pretty uneven, so how much greenery you get depends a lot on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">where people live</strong>.\n\nQ: What are the benefits of planting more trees?\nA: Trees reduce heat, absorb some pollution, manage rainwater and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make streets more pleasant</strong>. They're good for urban wildlife too, and overall they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improve quality of life</strong>.\n\nQ: Do cities have enough trees?\nA: Many cities don't have enough healthy, well-maintained trees. Planting campaigns help, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long-term care</strong> and protection during construction are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">equally important</strong>.","topic":"Trees","phrases":[{"en":"where people live","context":"Some older parts of my hometown have mature trees, but the newer districts often don't have as many. It's pretty uneven, so how much greenery you get depends a lot on where people live."},{"en":"make streets more pleasant","context":"Trees reduce heat, absorb some pollution, manage rainwater and make streets more pleasant. They're good for urban wildlife too, and overall they improve quality of life."},{"en":"improve quality of life","context":"Trees reduce heat, absorb some pollution, manage rainwater and make streets more pleasant. They're good for urban wildlife too, and overall they improve quality of life."},{"en":"long-term care","context":"Many cities don't have enough healthy, well-maintained trees. Planting campaigns help, but long-term care and protection during construction are equally important."},{"en":"equally important","context":"Many cities don't have enough healthy, well-maintained trees. Planting campaigns help, but long-term care and protection during construction are equally important."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Trees","id":"speaking-part-1-085","_displayNo":"#084","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-085-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-085-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-085-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"There are many large, older trees in some parts of my hometown. Newer areas often have fewer, so the amount of green space depends on which neighbourhood you are in.","phrases":[{"en":"green space","meaning":"An area with trees, grass or other plants."}],"question":"Are there many trees in your hometown?","html":"There are many large, older trees in some parts of my hometown. Newer areas often have fewer, so the amount of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">green space</strong> depends on which neighbourhood you are in."},{"text":"Trees provide shade, make places cooler, take in some pollution and help with rainwater. They also support wildlife and make streets nicer, which helps people enjoy living in the city.","phrases":[{"en":"wildlife","meaning":"Animals and other living things that live naturally in an area."}],"question":"What are the benefits of planting more trees?","html":"Trees provide shade, make places cooler, take in some pollution and help with rainwater. They also support <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wildlife</strong> and make streets nicer, which helps people enjoy living in the city."},{"text":"I don't think many cities have enough healthy trees. Planting new ones helps, but cities also need to care for them over time and protect them when building work happens nearby.","phrases":[{"en":"protect","meaning":"Keep something safe from harm or damage."}],"question":"Do cities have enough trees?","html":"I don&#x27;t think many cities have enough healthy trees. Planting new ones helps, but cities also need to care for them over time and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">protect</strong> them when building work happens nearby."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-085-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-085-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-085-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Volunteering, and whether schools should encourage it","prompt":"Have you ever done volunteer work?\nWhy do people volunteer?\nShould schools encourage volunteering?","answer":"Q: Have you ever done volunteer work?\nA: Yes, I've helped with small educational and community activities. The work was modest, but it showed me that regular <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical support</strong> can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make a meaningful contribution</strong>.\n\nQ: Why do people volunteer?\nA: People volunteer to support a cause, gain experience or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel connected to their community</strong>. Usually it's a combination of empathy and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of purpose</strong> that motivates people most.\n\nQ: Should schools encourage volunteering?\nA: Yes, provided the work is age-appropriate and genuinely useful. Volunteering can develop responsibility and empathy, but it shouldn't become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a box-ticking exercise</strong>.","topic":"Volunteer Work","phrases":[{"en":"practical support","context":"Yes, I've helped with small educational and community activities. The work was modest, but it showed me that regular practical support can make a meaningful contribution."},{"en":"make a meaningful contribution","context":"Yes, I've helped with small educational and community activities. The work was modest, but it showed me that regular practical support can make a meaningful contribution."},{"en":"feel connected to their community","context":"People volunteer to support a cause, gain experience or feel connected to their community. Usually it's a combination of empathy and a sense of purpose that motivates people most."},{"en":"a sense of purpose","context":"People volunteer to support a cause, gain experience or feel connected to their community. Usually it's a combination of empathy and a sense of purpose that motivates people most."},{"en":"a box-ticking exercise","context":"Yes, provided the work is age-appropriate and genuinely useful. Volunteering can develop responsibility and empathy, but it shouldn't become a box-ticking exercise."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Volunteer Work","id":"speaking-part-1-086","_displayNo":"#085","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-086-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-086-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-086-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, I've helped with small activities related to education and the local community. They were not big projects, but they showed me how regular, practical help can make a difference.","phrases":[{"en":"make a difference","meaning":"Have a useful or positive effect."}],"question":"Have you ever done volunteer work?","html":"Yes, I&#x27;ve helped with small activities related to education and the local community. They were not big projects, but they showed me how regular, practical help can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make a difference</strong>."},{"text":"People volunteer because they want to help, gain experience or feel part of their community. Often, they care about others and want to do something that has a purpose.","phrases":[{"en":"gain experience","meaning":"Learn and develop skills by doing something."}],"question":"Why do people volunteer?","html":"People volunteer because they want to help, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gain experience</strong> or feel part of their community. Often, they care about others and want to do something that has a purpose."},{"text":"Yes, if the tasks suit the children's age and provide real help. Volunteering can teach responsibility and understanding, but it should mean more than simply completing a requirement.","phrases":[{"en":"responsibility","meaning":"Being willing to do what you should and accept the results of your actions."}],"question":"Should schools encourage volunteering?","html":"Yes, if the tasks suit the children&#x27;s age and provide real help. Volunteering can teach <strong class=\"key-phrase\">responsibility</strong> and understanding, but it should mean more than simply completing a requirement."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-086-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-086-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-086-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Walking in the city and in the countryside","prompt":"Do you like walking?\nDo you think walking is important?\nDo you think walking in the countryside is better than walking in the city?\nWhat could be done to improve the experience of walking in cities?","answer":"Q: Do you like walking?\nA: I'm <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not one for</strong> <strong class=\"key-phrase\">going on really long walks</strong> in the countryside, but I don't mind walking when I'm in a town or city, for example when I'm sightseeing in a new place.\n\nQ: Do you think walking is important?\nA: Yes, I think it's important to be active, and walking is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the most basic physical activity</strong> that we can do.\n\nQ: Do you think walking in the countryside is better than walking in the city?\nA: Personally, no. As I said before, I like walking in the city, especially if it's somewhere with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a lot of history</strong> like London or Paris.\n\nQ: What could be done to improve the experience of walking in cities?\nA: I suppose the main issues are space and safety. Pavements need to be wide enough to accommodate lots of pedestrians, and we need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">safe places to cross the street</strong>.","topic":"Walking","phrases":[{"en":"not one for","context":"I'm not one for going on really long walks in the countryside, but I don't mind walking when I'm in a town or city, for example when I'm sightseeing in a new place."},{"en":"going on really long walks","context":"I'm not one for going on really long walks in the countryside, but I don't mind walking when I'm in a town or city, for example when I'm sightseeing in a new place."},{"en":"the most basic physical activity","context":"Yes, I think it's important to be active, and walking is the most basic physical activity that we can do."},{"en":"a lot of history","context":"Personally, no. As I said before, I like walking in the city, especially if it's somewhere with a lot of history like London or Paris."},{"en":"safe places to cross the street","context":"I suppose the main issues are space and safety. Pavements need to be wide enough to accommodate lots of pedestrians, and we need safe places to cross the street."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Walking","id":"speaking-part-1-087","_displayNo":"#086","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-087-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-087-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-087-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-087-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I don't really enjoy very long country walks. However, I like walking around towns and cities, especially when I'm visiting a new place and looking at the sights.","phrases":[{"en":"the sights","meaning":"Interesting or famous places that visitors go to see."}],"question":"Do you like walking?","html":"I don&#x27;t really enjoy very long country walks. However, I like walking around towns and cities, especially when I&#x27;m visiting a new place and looking at <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the sights</strong>."},{"text":"Yes, walking is a simple way to keep moving and stay active. It is one of the easiest forms of exercise to include in everyday life.","phrases":[{"en":"stay active","meaning":"Keep moving your body regularly."}],"question":"Do you think walking is important?","html":"Yes, walking is a simple way to keep moving and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay active</strong>. It is one of the easiest forms of exercise to include in everyday life."},{"text":"Not for me. I prefer walking in cities, especially places with interesting history, such as London or Paris. There is more that I personally enjoy looking at there.","phrases":[{"en":"personally","meaning":"In my own opinion or experience."}],"question":"Do you think walking in the countryside is better than walking in the city?","html":"Not for me. I prefer walking in cities, especially places with interesting history, such as London or Paris. There is more that I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">personally</strong> enjoy looking at there."},{"text":"Cities should give people more space and make walking safer. Wider pavements would help when streets are busy, and we need safe places to cross the road.","phrases":[{"en":"pavements","meaning":"Paths at the side of a road for people to walk on."}],"question":"What could be done to improve the experience of walking in cities?","html":"Cities should give people more space and make walking safer. Wider <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pavements</strong> would help when streets are busy, and we need safe places to cross the road."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-087-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-087-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-087-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-087-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Your name and its meaning","prompt":"Could you tell me your full name please?\nWhat is your name?\nDoes your name have any special meaning?\nWhy do many people change their names?","answer":"Q: Could you tell me your full name please?\nA: My full name is Nguyen Nhat Dien, although people usually call me Dien. It's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a straightforward name</strong>, so I rarely need to explain or shorten it.\n\nQ: What is your name?\nA: My name's Nguyen Nhat Dien, but almost everyone just calls me Dien — it's the name I answer to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without even thinking</strong>.\n\nQ: Does your name have any special meaning?\nA: It's got a positive meaning in Vietnamese, and it was chosen by my family. I value it mainly because it connects me with them and gives me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of identity</strong>.\n\nQ: Why do many people change their names?\nA: People may change their names because of marriage, migration, personal identity or professional reasons. A new name can make communication easier, although changing it isn't always simple — it usually involves <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a great deal of paperwork</strong>.","topic":"Warm-Up","phrases":[{"en":"a straightforward name","context":"My full name is Nguyen Nhat Dien, although people usually call me Dien. It's a straightforward name, so I rarely need to explain or shorten it."},{"en":"without even thinking","context":"My name's Nguyen Nhat Dien, but almost everyone just calls me Dien — it's the name I answer to without even thinking."},{"en":"a sense of identity","context":"It's got a positive meaning in Vietnamese, and it was chosen by my family. I value it mainly because it connects me with them and gives me a sense of identity."},{"en":"a great deal of paperwork","context":"People may change their names because of marriage, migration, personal identity or professional reasons. A new name can make communication easier, although changing it isn't always simple — it usually involves a great deal of paperwork."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Warm-Up","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-088","_displayNo":"#087","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-088-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-088-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-088-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-088-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"My full name is Nguyen Nhat Dien, but most people call me Dien. It is quite easy to use, so I rarely have to explain it or make it shorter.","phrases":[{"en":"full name","meaning":"Your complete name, including all its parts."}],"question":"Could you tell me your full name please?","html":"My <strong class=\"key-phrase\">full name</strong> is Nguyen Nhat Dien, but most people call me Dien. It is quite easy to use, so I rarely have to explain it or make it shorter."},{"text":"My name is Nguyen Nhat Dien. Most people just call me Dien, so that is the name I'm most used to hearing in everyday conversations.","phrases":[{"en":"used to hearing","meaning":"Familiar with hearing something often."}],"question":"What is your name?","html":"My name is Nguyen Nhat Dien. Most people just call me Dien, so that is the name I&#x27;m most <strong class=\"key-phrase\">used to hearing</strong> in everyday conversations."},{"text":"Yes, it has a positive meaning in Vietnamese. My family chose it, and it is important to me because it reminds me of them and feels like part of who I am.","phrases":[{"en":"positive","meaning":"Expressing something good or hopeful."}],"question":"Does your name have any special meaning?","html":"Yes, it has a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">positive</strong> meaning in Vietnamese. My family chose it, and it is important to me because it reminds me of them and feels like part of who I am."},{"text":"People change names for marriage, moving countries, personal reasons or work. A different name can be easier to use, but making the change official often means completing many forms.","phrases":[{"en":"official","meaning":"Formally accepted or recorded by the relevant authorities."}],"question":"Why do many people change their names?","html":"People change names for marriage, moving countries, personal reasons or work. A different name can be easier to use, but making the change <strong class=\"key-phrase\">official</strong> often means completing many forms."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-088-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-088-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-088-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-088-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"The weather where you live and your favourite season","prompt":"What's the weather like in your country?\nDoes the weather affect your mood?\nWhat's your favourite season of the year?\nWhat do you usually do in winter?","answer":"Q: What's the weather like in your country?\nA: Vietnam has a varied climate, but Hanoi has hot, humid summers and cooler, drier winters. The transition seasons can be pleasant, although the weather's becoming <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less predictable</strong>.\n\nQ: Does the weather affect your mood?\nA: Yes, slightly. Bright, mild weather gives me more energy, while long periods of humidity can make daily tasks feel tiring. I try not to let it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">determine my whole day</strong>.\n\nQ: What's your favourite season of the year?\nA: Autumn is my favourite because the temperature is comfortable and the air is usually clearer. It's the easiest season for walking, working and spending time <strong class=\"key-phrase\">outdoors comfortably</strong>.\n\nQ: What do you usually do in winter?\nA: In winter I exercise indoors, drink hot tea and spend more time reading. I also enjoy walking on dry, cool days because the city feels <strong class=\"key-phrase\">quieter and calmer</strong>.","topic":"Weather","phrases":[{"en":"less predictable","context":"Vietnam has a varied climate, but Hanoi has hot, humid summers and cooler, drier winters. The transition seasons can be pleasant, although the weather's becoming less predictable."},{"en":"determine my whole day","context":"Yes, slightly. Bright, mild weather gives me more energy, while long periods of humidity can make daily tasks feel tiring. I try not to let it determine my whole day."},{"en":"outdoors comfortably","context":"Autumn is my favourite because the temperature is comfortable and the air is usually clearer. It's the easiest season for walking, working and spending time outdoors comfortably."},{"en":"quieter and calmer","context":"In winter I exercise indoors, drink hot tea and spend more time reading. I also enjoy walking on dry, cool days because the city feels quieter and calmer."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Weather","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-089","_displayNo":"#088","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-089-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-089-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-089-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-089-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"The weather varies across Vietnam. Hanoi has hot, wet summers and cooler, drier winters. Spring and autumn can be pleasant, although it is becoming harder to know what weather to expect.","phrases":[{"en":"varies","meaning":"Is different in different places or situations."}],"question":"What's the weather like in your country?","html":"The weather <strong class=\"key-phrase\">varies</strong> across Vietnam. Hanoi has hot, wet summers and cooler, drier winters. Spring and autumn can be pleasant, although it is becoming harder to know what weather to expect."},{"text":"Yes, a little. Sunny weather that is not too hot gives me more energy, while long periods of damp heat make me tired. I still try not to let it control my day.","phrases":[{"en":"damp heat","meaning":"Warm conditions with a lot of moisture in the air."}],"question":"Does the weather affect your mood?","html":"Yes, a little. Sunny weather that is not too hot gives me more energy, while long periods of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">damp heat</strong> make me tired. I still try not to let it control my day."},{"text":"Autumn is my favourite because it is neither too hot nor too cold, and the air often feels clearer. I find it comfortable for walking, working and spending time outside.","phrases":[{"en":"neither too hot nor too cold","meaning":"Having a comfortable temperature between the two extremes."}],"question":"What's your favourite season of the year?","html":"Autumn is my favourite because it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">neither too hot nor too cold</strong>, and the air often feels clearer. I find it comfortable for walking, working and spending time outside."},{"text":"I exercise inside, drink hot tea and read more in winter. I also enjoy walking when it is cool and dry because the city seems quieter and more peaceful.","phrases":[{"en":"peaceful","meaning":"Calm and quiet."}],"question":"What do you usually do in winter?","html":"I exercise inside, drink hot tea and read more in winter. I also enjoy walking when it is cool and dry because the city seems quieter and more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">peaceful</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-089-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-089-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-089-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-089-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Your job and your plans for your career","prompt":"Can you tell me about the job you do?\nWhat do you like or dislike about it?\nWhat plans do you have for your career in the future?\nHow serious is unemployment in your country?","answer":"Q: Can you tell me about the job you do?\nA: I work as an English teacher. My job involves planning lessons, explaining tricky points, and giving feedback that helps students <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make steady progress</strong>.\n\nQ: What do you like or dislike about it?\nA: I like seeing students become more confident and independent. The hard part's the amount of preparation and marking, especially when a few deadlines <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fall in the same week</strong>.\n\nQ: What plans do you have for your career in the future?\nA: I'd like to develop my teaching skills further and get better at using technology in my classes. Longer term, I'd love to create learning materials that are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">both practical and rigorous</strong>.\n\nQ: How serious is unemployment in your country?\nA: It varies a lot by sector, but young graduates often struggle to find work that matches what they've trained for. It's not just about the number of jobs available — it's also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the mismatch of skills</strong>.","topic":"Work","phrases":[{"en":"make steady progress","context":"I work as an English teacher. My job involves planning lessons, explaining tricky points, and giving feedback that helps students make steady progress."},{"en":"fall in the same week","context":"I like seeing students become more confident and independent. The hard part's the amount of preparation and marking, especially when a few deadlines fall in the same week."},{"en":"both practical and rigorous","context":"I'd like to develop my teaching skills further and get better at using technology in my classes. Longer term, I'd love to create learning materials that are both practical and rigorous."},{"en":"the mismatch of skills","context":"It varies a lot by sector, but young graduates often struggle to find work that matches what they've trained for. It's not just about the number of jobs available — it's also the mismatch of skills."}],"subtype":"Part 1","summary":"Job","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-1-046","_displayNo":"#089","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-046-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-046-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-046-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-046-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I'm an English teacher. I plan lessons, explain difficult language and give students advice on their work. My main aim is to help them improve step by step.","phrases":[{"en":"step by step","meaning":"Gradually, through a series of small stages."}],"question":"Can you tell me about the job you do?","html":"I&#x27;m an English teacher. I plan lessons, explain difficult language and give students advice on their work. My main aim is to help them improve <strong class=\"key-phrase\">step by step</strong>."},{"text":"I like seeing students become more confident and able to learn by themselves. However, preparing lessons and checking work takes a lot of time, especially when several tasks are due together.","phrases":[{"en":"due","meaning":"Needing to be finished or handed in by a particular time."}],"question":"What do you like or dislike about it?","html":"I like seeing students become more confident and able to learn by themselves. However, preparing lessons and checking work takes a lot of time, especially when several tasks are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">due</strong> together."},{"text":"I want to become a better teacher and use technology more effectively in class. Later, I'd like to make learning materials that are useful, clear and carefully checked.","phrases":[{"en":"effectively","meaning":"In a way that produces the result you want."}],"question":"What plans do you have for your career in the future?","html":"I want to become a better teacher and use technology more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">effectively</strong> in class. Later, I&#x27;d like to make learning materials that are useful, clear and carefully checked."},{"text":"It depends on the type of work. Many young graduates find it hard to get jobs related to their studies. Sometimes jobs exist, but applicants do not have the skills employers need.","phrases":[{"en":"graduates","meaning":"People who have completed a university course."}],"question":"How serious is unemployment in your country?","html":"It depends on the type of work. Many young <strong class=\"key-phrase\">graduates</strong> find it hard to get jobs related to their studies. Sometimes jobs exist, but applicants do not have the skills employers need."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-046-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-046-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-046-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-046-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-1","title":"Your job, and working alone or with others","prompt":"Do you work or are you a student?\nDo you enjoy your job?\nWhat's the best thing about your job?\nDo you prefer working alone or with others?","answer":"Q: Do you work or are you a student?\nA: I'm <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a qualified doctor</strong>, but at the moment I'm studying English so that I can work in this country.\n\nQ: Do you enjoy your job?\nA: Yes. I've wanted to become a doctor since childhood, and I still find the work meaningful because it allows me to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">help people recover</strong> and make difficult periods more manageable.\n\nQ: What's the best thing about your job?\nA: The best thing about my job is that I can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make a difference</strong> in people's lives. It's a great feeling when patients and their families feel reassured just because we took the time to explain things clearly.\n\nQ: Do you prefer working alone or with others?\nA: In a hospital you have to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work as part of a team</strong>. I've got used to working with others, and I think you can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">achieve a lot more as a team</strong> than you would alone.","topic":"Work","phrases":[{"en":"a qualified doctor","context":"I'm a qualified doctor, but at the moment I'm studying English so that I can work in this country."},{"en":"help people recover","context":"Yes. I've wanted to become a doctor since childhood, and I still find the work meaningful because it allows me to help people recover and make difficult periods more manageable."},{"en":"make a difference","context":"The best thing about my job is that I can make a difference in people's lives. It's a great feeling when patients and their families feel reassured just because we took the time to explain things clearly."},{"en":"work as part of a team","context":"In a hospital you have to work as part of a team. I've got used to working with others, and I think you can achieve a lot more as a team than you would alone."},{"en":"achieve a lot more as a team","context":"In a hospital you have to work as part of a team. I've got used to working with others, and I think you can achieve a lot more as a team than you would alone."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Work","id":"speaking-part-1-090","_displayNo":"#090","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-090-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-090-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-090-q03.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-1-090-q04.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I'm a trained doctor, but I'm currently studying English. I want to improve my English so I can work as a doctor in this country.","phrases":[{"en":"trained","meaning":"Having learned the skills and knowledge needed for a job."}],"question":"Do you work or are you a student?","html":"I&#x27;m a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trained</strong> doctor, but I&#x27;m currently studying English. I want to improve my English so I can work as a doctor in this country."},{"text":"Yes, I do. I've wanted to be a doctor since I was a child. Helping people get better and supporting them during difficult times still makes the work feel important to me.","phrases":[{"en":"get better","meaning":"Recover or become healthier."}],"question":"Do you enjoy your job?","html":"Yes, I do. I&#x27;ve wanted to be a doctor since I was a child. Helping people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get better</strong> and supporting them during difficult times still makes the work feel important to me."},{"text":"The best part is helping patients and their families. Sometimes, explaining a situation clearly makes them feel less worried. It is rewarding to know that my work can improve their lives.","phrases":[{"en":"rewarding","meaning":"Giving you a feeling of satisfaction because you have done something useful."}],"question":"What's the best thing about your job?","html":"The best part is helping patients and their families. Sometimes, explaining a situation clearly makes them feel less worried. It is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rewarding</strong> to know that my work can improve their lives."},{"text":"I work with others because hospital work depends on a team. I'm used to it now, and I think we can do more together than one person could achieve alone.","phrases":[{"en":"achieve","meaning":"Succeed in doing something you have worked towards."}],"question":"Do you prefer working alone or with others?","html":"I work with others because hospital work depends on a team. I&#x27;m used to it now, and I think we can do more together than one person could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">achieve</strong> alone."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-090-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-090-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-090-q03.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-1/speaking-part-1-090-q04.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"My Quiet Documentary Ritual","prompt":"Describe an activity you enjoy doing in your free time.\nYou should say:\n- what the activity is\n- when and how often you do it\n- how you became interested in it\n- and explain why you enjoy it.","answer":"One activity I genuinely enjoy in my free time is watching short documentaries, especially ones about science, design and unusual places.\n\nI usually do it on quiet weekend mornings, when I have no urgent plans. I make coffee, put my phone on silent and watch one or two episodes on my laptop. It is a simple routine, but it gives me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clean break from</strong> work and social media.\n\nI became interested in documentaries <strong class=\"key-phrase\">almost by accident</strong>. A few years ago, a friend sent me a ten-minute film about how suspension bridges are built. I expected it to be dry, but the explanation was so clear and visual that I ended up watching several more videos. Since then, I have gradually moved from engineering to nature, history and psychology.\n\nWhat I like most is that the activity is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">both relaxing and useful</strong>. I can switch off without feeling that I am wasting time, because I nearly always learn one memorable fact or see a familiar subject <strong class=\"key-phrase\">from a different angle</strong>. It also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gives me plenty to talk about</strong> with other people. For me, that combination of curiosity and relaxation is exactly what good free time should provide.","topic":"Core Story · Activity","phrases":[{"en":"a clean break from","context":"I usually do it on quiet weekend mornings, when I have no urgent plans. I make coffee, put my phone on silent and watch one or two episodes on my laptop. It is a simple routine, but it gives me a clean break from work and social media."},{"en":"almost by accident","context":"I became interested in documentaries almost by accident."},{"en":"from a different angle","context":"I can switch off without feeling that I am wasting time, because I nearly always learn one memorable fact or see a familiar subject from a different angle."},{"en":"both relaxing and useful","context":"What I like most is that the activity is both relaxing and useful."},{"en":"gives me plenty to talk about","context":"It also gives me plenty to talk about with other people."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"My Quiet Documentary Ritual","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-01","_displayNo":"CORE 01","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":1,"outline":{"topic":"My Quiet Documentary Ritual","intro":"free-time ritual • short documentaries • science, design, unusual places","body":["quiet weekend mornings • coffee • phone on silent • 1–2 episodes","started with a suspension-bridge video • expanded to nature, history, psychology","relaxing + informative • memorable facts • fresh perspectives • conversation material"],"conclusion":"curiosity + relaxation • meaningful downtime"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-01.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"I enjoy watching short documentaries in my free time, especially about science, design and unusual places. I usually watch them on quiet weekend mornings. I make coffee, put my phone on silent and watch one or two episodes on my laptop. It gives me a proper break from work and social media.\n\nMy interest began when a friend sent me a ten-minute video about building suspension bridges. I expected it to be boring, but the pictures and explanations were so clear that I watched several more. Later, I started exploring nature, history and psychology as well.\n\nI like this activity because I can relax and learn at the same time. I nearly always remember an interesting fact or discover a different way of looking at something. The programmes also give me ideas to discuss with other people. It is a simple hobby, but it makes my free time feel useful and enjoyable.","html":"I enjoy watching short documentaries in my free time, especially about science, design and unusual places. I usually watch them on quiet weekend mornings. I make coffee, put my phone on silent and watch one or two episodes on my laptop. It gives me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a proper break</strong> from work and social media.\n\nMy interest began when a friend sent me a ten-minute video about building suspension bridges. I expected it to be boring, but the pictures and explanations were so clear that I watched several more. Later, I started exploring nature, history and psychology as well.\n\nI like this activity because I can relax and learn <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at the same time</strong>. I nearly always remember an interesting fact or discover a different way of looking at something. The programmes also give me ideas to discuss with other people. It is a simple hobby, but it makes my free time feel useful and enjoyable.","phrases":[{"en":"a proper break","meaning":"Enough time away from an activity to rest properly."},{"en":"at the same time","meaning":"Together or during the same period."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-01.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Finishing a Professional Certificate While Working","prompt":"Describe an achievement you are proud of.\nYou should say:\n- what the achievement was\n- when and where it happened\n- what you did to achieve it\n- and explain why you are proud of it.","answer":"An achievement I am <strong class=\"key-phrase\">particularly proud of</strong> is completing a demanding professional certificate while working full-time.\n\nThe course was designed to take about a year, and it covered project planning, data analysis and presentation skills. I decided to finish it in eight months, not because I wanted to rush, but because I had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clear reason for doing it</strong>: I wanted to qualify for more challenging work.\n\nThe difficult part was managing my energy. I studied for about an hour before work three days a week and used part of Sunday for longer assignments. I also broke every module into <strong class=\"key-phrase\">small weekly targets</strong>, which stopped the workload from becoming overwhelming. There were a few evenings when I was tempted to skip everything, but keeping a visible progress tracker helped me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay consistent</strong>.\n\nI am proud of the result because it was not based on talent or luck. It came from discipline, planning and doing fairly ordinary things repeatedly. More importantly, the experience changed how I approach big goals. I no longer wait until I feel highly motivated; I create a system and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">let consistency do most of the work</strong>.","topic":"Core Story · Achievement","phrases":[{"en":"particularly proud of","context":"An achievement I am particularly proud of is completing a demanding professional certificate while working full-time."},{"en":"a clear reason for doing it","context":"I decided to finish it in eight months, not because I wanted to rush, but because I had a clear reason for doing it: I wanted to qualify for more challenging work."},{"en":"small weekly targets","context":"I also broke every module into small weekly targets, which stopped the workload from becoming overwhelming."},{"en":"stay consistent","context":"There were a few evenings when I was tempted to skip everything, but keeping a visible progress tracker helped me stay consistent."},{"en":"let consistency do most of the work","context":"I no longer wait until I feel highly motivated; I create a system and let consistency do most of the work."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"Finishing a Professional Certificate While Working","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-02","_displayNo":"CORE 02","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":2,"outline":{"topic":"Finishing a Professional Certificate While Working","intro":"proud achievement • professional certificate • full-time work","body":["12-month course • project planning, data analysis, presentation skills • finished in 8 months","study before work 3×/week • Sunday assignments • small weekly targets","energy management • visible progress tracker • consistency over motivation"],"conclusion":"discipline + systems • confidence with long-term goals"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-02.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An achievement I'm proud of is finishing a professional certificate while working full-time. The course included project planning, data analysis and presentation skills. It normally took about a year, but I planned to complete it in eight months because I wanted to qualify for more challenging work.\n\nFinding enough energy was the hardest part. I studied for an hour before work three mornings a week and did longer assignments on Sundays. I divided each part of the course into small weekly goals, so it did not feel impossible. Keeping a record of my progress helped when I wanted to skip a session.\n\nI'm proud because I succeeded through regular effort rather than luck. The experience also changed how I deal with large goals. Instead of waiting to feel excited about studying, I make a clear plan and follow it. It showed me that ordinary actions, repeated consistently, can produce an important result.","html":"An achievement I'm proud of is finishing a professional certificate while working full-time. The course included project planning, data analysis and presentation skills. It normally took about a year, but I planned to complete it in eight months because I wanted to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">qualify for</strong> more challenging work.\n\nFinding enough energy was the hardest part. I studied for an hour before work three mornings a week and did longer assignments on Sundays. I divided each part of the course into small <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weekly goals</strong>, so it did not feel impossible. Keeping a record of my progress helped when I wanted to skip a session.\n\nI'm proud because I succeeded through regular effort rather than luck. The experience also changed how I deal with large goals. Instead of waiting to feel excited about studying, I make a clear plan and follow it. It showed me that ordinary actions, repeated consistently, can produce an important result.","phrases":[{"en":"qualify for","meaning":"Have the skills or official requirements needed for something."},{"en":"weekly goals","meaning":"Things you aim to complete within each week."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-02.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Starting Over in a New City","prompt":"Describe a difficult challenge you faced.\nYou should say:\n- what the challenge was\n- why it was difficult\n- what you did to deal with it\n- and explain what you learned from it.","answer":"One difficult challenge I faced was moving to a large city where I knew almost nobody and had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">build a routine from scratch</strong>.\n\nThe move itself was exciting, but the first few weeks were surprisingly difficult. I had to find affordable accommodation, learn the transport system and adjust to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a much faster pace of life</strong>. The hardest part, however, was the lack of familiar people. Even simple problems felt bigger because I had nobody nearby to ask for help.\n\nI dealt with it by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">making the situation more manageable</strong>. I learned one neighbourhood at a time, joined a weekend sports group and made a point of speaking to colleagues instead of going straight home after work. I also kept a basic weekly routine for shopping, exercise and meal preparation, which gave the new place <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of structure</strong>.\n\nAfter a couple of months, the city stopped feeling intimidating and started to feel like home. The main lesson I learned was that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">confidence often comes after action</strong>, not before it. I did not suddenly become fearless; I simply became more capable because I kept solving small problems every day.","topic":"Core Story · Challenge","phrases":[{"en":"build a routine from scratch","context":"One difficult challenge I faced was moving to a large city where I knew almost nobody and had to build a routine from scratch."},{"en":"a much faster pace of life","context":"I had to find affordable accommodation, learn the transport system and adjust to a much faster pace of life."},{"en":"making the situation more manageable","context":"I dealt with it by making the situation more manageable."},{"en":"a sense of structure","context":"I also kept a basic weekly routine for shopping, exercise and meal preparation, which gave the new place a sense of structure."},{"en":"confidence often comes after action","context":"The main lesson I learned was that confidence often comes after action, not before it."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"Starting Over in a New City","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-03","_displayNo":"CORE 03","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":3,"outline":{"topic":"Starting Over in a New City","intro":"major challenge • unfamiliar big city • rebuild daily life from scratch","body":["housing • transport • faster pace • very few local contacts","one neighbourhood at a time • weekend sports group • talk to colleagues","weekly routine • shopping, exercise, meal prep • small daily wins"],"conclusion":"confidence follows action • adaptability through repetition"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-03.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A difficult challenge was moving to a large city where I knew almost nobody. I was excited, but the first few weeks were harder than I expected. I needed an affordable place to live, had to understand public transport and was not used to the busy lifestyle.\n\nThe biggest problem was having no familiar people nearby. Even small difficulties felt serious when I had nobody to ask. I dealt with this by learning one neighbourhood at a time and joining a weekend sports group. I also talked more to colleagues instead of always going straight home. A regular routine for shopping, exercise and cooking helped me feel organised.\n\nAfter a couple of months, the city began to feel like home. I did not suddenly stop being nervous. I simply became more confident as I solved everyday problems. The experience taught me that sometimes you need to take action before confidence develops.","html":"A difficult challenge was moving to a large city where I knew almost nobody. I was excited, but the first few weeks were harder than I expected. I needed an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">affordable</strong> place to live, had to understand public transport and was not used to the busy lifestyle.\n\nThe biggest problem was having no familiar people nearby. Even small difficulties felt serious when I had nobody to ask. I dealt with this by learning one neighbourhood at a time and joining a weekend sports group. I also talked more to colleagues instead of always going straight home. A regular routine for shopping, exercise and cooking helped me feel organised.\n\nAfter a couple of months, the city began to feel like home. I did not suddenly stop being nervous. I simply became more confident as I solved everyday problems. The experience taught me that sometimes you need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take action</strong> before confidence develops.","phrases":[{"en":"affordable","meaning":"Not too expensive for your budget."},{"en":"take action","meaning":"Do something practical to improve or change a situation."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-03.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Choosing Growth Over a Comfortable Option","prompt":"Describe an important decision you have made.\nYou should say:\n- what the decision was\n- when you made it\n- why you made it\n- and explain how it may affect your future.","answer":"One important decision I made was turning down a comfortable role and choosing a more demanding position that offered much better <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long-term growth</strong>.\n\nThe safer option paid reasonably well and involved work I already knew how to do. The other role required me to learn new software, speak more in meetings and take responsibility for projects with visible results. At first, the uncertainty made the decision difficult because there was a real chance that I would struggle.\n\nI finally chose the second option after asking myself a simple question: which decision would I respect more in five years? That <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changed my perspective</strong>. I realised I was mainly attracted to the first job because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">felt familiar</strong>, not because it matched the person I wanted to become.\n\nThe transition was uncomfortable for several months, but it forced me to develop skills I would probably have avoided otherwise. I became more confident, more organised and much better at <strong class=\"key-phrase\">communicating under pressure</strong>. The decision taught me that a good choice is not always the one that removes uncertainty; sometimes it is the one that gives you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the strongest reason to grow</strong>.","topic":"Core Story · Decision","phrases":[{"en":"long-term growth","context":"One important decision I made was turning down a comfortable role and choosing a more demanding position that offered much better long-term growth."},{"en":"changed my perspective","context":"That changed my perspective."},{"en":"felt familiar","context":"I realised I was mainly attracted to the first job because it felt familiar, not because it matched the person I wanted to become."},{"en":"communicating under pressure","context":"I became more confident, more organised and much better at communicating under pressure."},{"en":"the strongest reason to grow","context":"The decision taught me that a good choice is not always the one that removes uncertainty; sometimes it is the one that gives you the strongest reason to grow."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"Choosing Growth Over a Comfortable Option","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-04","_displayNo":"CORE 04","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":4,"outline":{"topic":"Choosing Growth Over a Comfortable Option","intro":"major decision • comfortable job vs demanding growth role","body":["safe option • familiar work • reasonable pay","growth option • new software • meetings • visible project responsibility","five-year perspective • choose future growth over familiarity"],"conclusion":"short-term discomfort • stronger skills • growth over certainty"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-04.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An important decision I made was choosing a difficult new job instead of a comfortable, familiar one. The safer role paid fairly well and involved work I already understood. The other job required new software, more speaking in meetings and responsibility for projects.\n\nI worried that I might struggle. Finally, I asked myself which choice I would feel better about in five years. I realised I liked the comfortable option mainly because it felt safe. The harder role offered more chance to become the person I wanted to be, so I chose it.\n\nThe first few months were not easy, but I developed skills I might otherwise have avoided. I became more organised and more confident about explaining ideas under pressure. I am glad I made the decision. It taught me that a good choice does not always remove worry; sometimes it gives you a strong reason to learn and improve.","html":"An important decision I made was choosing a difficult new job instead of a comfortable, familiar one. The safer role paid fairly well and involved work I already understood. The other job required new software, more speaking in meetings and responsibility for projects.\n\nI worried that I might struggle. Finally, I asked myself which choice I would feel better about in five years. I realised I liked the comfortable option mainly because it felt safe. The harder role offered more chance to become the person I wanted to be, so I chose it.\n\nThe first few months were not easy, but I developed skills I might <strong class=\"key-phrase\">otherwise</strong> have avoided. I became more organised and more confident about explaining ideas <strong class=\"key-phrase\">under pressure</strong>. I am glad I made the decision. It taught me that a good choice does not always remove worry; sometimes it gives you a strong reason to learn and improve.","phrases":[{"en":"under pressure","meaning":"While dealing with demanding or stressful conditions."},{"en":"otherwise","meaning":"If the situation had been different."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-04.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A Power Cut in a Mountain Cabin","prompt":"Describe an unusual event you experienced.\nYou should say:\n- when and where it happened\n- what happened\n- how you reacted\n- and explain why the event was memorable.","answer":"One unusual event I remember clearly was a sudden power cut during a weekend stay in a small mountain cabin.\n\nA few friends and I had rented the cabin because we wanted a quiet break from the city. Late in the evening, a storm arrived and the electricity disappeared <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without warning</strong>. At first we assumed it would return in a few minutes, but the entire valley went dark.\n\nWhat made the experience memorable was how quickly the atmosphere changed. Without lights, television or reliable phone signal, we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had to improvise</strong>. We found two candles, used a camping stove to make hot drinks and sat near the window listening to the rain. Someone started telling ridiculous childhood stories, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">before long</strong> we were laughing far more than we had when all our devices were working.\n\nThe power came back around midnight, but by then nobody rushed to turn anything on. The event reminded me how <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dependent modern life is on</strong> electricity, yet it also showed me that losing convenience can occasionally create a better kind of evening. It was inconvenient, slightly eerie and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unexpectedly enjoyable</strong> at the same time.","topic":"Core Story · Event","phrases":[{"en":"without warning","context":"A few friends and I had rented the cabin because we wanted a quiet break from the city. Late in the evening, a storm arrived and the electricity disappeared without warning. At first we assumed it would return in a few minutes, but the entire valley went dark."},{"en":"had to improvise","context":"Without lights, television or reliable phone signal, we had to improvise."},{"en":"before long","context":"Someone started telling ridiculous childhood stories, and before long we were laughing far more than we had when all our devices were working."},{"en":"dependent modern life is on","context":"The event reminded me how dependent modern life is on electricity, yet it also showed me that losing convenience can occasionally create a better kind of evening."},{"en":"unexpectedly enjoyable","context":"It was inconvenient, slightly eerie and unexpectedly enjoyable at the same time."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"A Power Cut in a Mountain Cabin","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-05","_displayNo":"CORE 05","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":5,"outline":{"topic":"A Power Cut in a Mountain Cabin","intro":"unusual event • storm • sudden power cut • mountain cabin","body":["weekend escape • entire valley dark • no TV • weak phone signal","candles • camping stove • hot drinks • childhood stories","devices off • more conversation • unexpectedly warm atmosphere"],"conclusion":"inconvenient but enjoyable • dependence on electricity • unexpected connection"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-05.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An unusual event happened during a weekend with friends in a small mountain cabin. We had rented it for a peaceful break from the city. Late one evening, a storm arrived and the electricity suddenly went off. We expected a short interruption, but the whole valley stayed dark.\n\nWithout lights, television or a reliable phone signal, we had to find other things to do. We found two candles and made hot drinks using a camping stove. Then we sat near the window listening to the rain. Someone began telling funny childhood stories, and soon we were laughing together.\n\nThe power returned around midnight, but nobody hurried to use their devices. Although the darkness had felt a little frightening, it had also created a lovely evening. I remember it because it showed how much we rely on electricity, but also how enjoyable time together can be without our normal entertainment.","html":"An unusual event happened during a weekend with friends in a small mountain cabin. We had rented it for a peaceful break from the city. Late one evening, a storm arrived and the electricity suddenly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">went off</strong>. We expected a short interruption, but the whole valley stayed dark.\n\nWithout lights, television or a reliable phone signal, we had to find other things to do. We found two candles and made hot drinks using a camping stove. Then we sat near the window listening to the rain. Someone began telling funny childhood stories, and soon we were laughing together.\n\nThe power returned around midnight, but nobody hurried to use their devices. Although the darkness had felt a little frightening, it had also created a lovely evening. I remember it because it showed how much we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rely on</strong> electricity, but also how enjoyable time together can be without our normal entertainment.","phrases":[{"en":"went off","meaning":"Stopped working or supplying power."},{"en":"rely on","meaning":"Need or depend on something."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-05.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A Bowl of Homemade Mushroom Risotto","prompt":"Describe a food you really enjoy.\nYou should say:\n- what the food is\n- what it contains\n- when you usually eat it\n- and explain why you like it so much.","answer":"One food I really enjoy is mushroom risotto, a simple Italian rice dish that is rich, savoury and surprisingly comforting.\n\nThe basic ingredients are rice, mushrooms, stock, onion and a little cheese. What makes it special is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the method rather than the ingredient list</strong>. The stock is added gradually while the rice is stirred, so the final texture becomes creamy without needing a heavy sauce.\n\nI first learned to make it from a cooking video when I wanted a meal that felt more interesting than my usual quick dinner. My first attempt was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far from perfect</strong> because I added too much liquid at once, but after a few tries I learned to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">judge the texture properly</strong>.\n\nI usually make it on a cool evening when I have enough time to cook without rushing. I like it because it is filling but not complicated, and the smell of mushrooms and toasted rice makes the whole kitchen feel warm. It is also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easy to adapt</strong>: I can add spinach, peas or roasted vegetables depending on what I have. For me, it is the kind of food that proves <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a modest list of ingredients</strong> can still produce something memorable.","topic":"Core Story · Food","phrases":[{"en":"the method rather than the ingredient list","context":"What makes it special is the method rather than the ingredient list."},{"en":"far from perfect","context":"My first attempt was far from perfect because I added too much liquid at once, but after a few tries I learned to judge the texture properly."},{"en":"judge the texture properly","context":"My first attempt was far from perfect because I added too much liquid at once, but after a few tries I learned to judge the texture properly."},{"en":"easy to adapt","context":"It is also easy to adapt: I can add spinach, peas or roasted vegetables depending on what I have."},{"en":"a modest list of ingredients","context":"For me, it is the kind of food that proves a modest list of ingredients can still produce something memorable."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"A Bowl of Homemade Mushroom Risotto","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-06","_displayNo":"CORE 06","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":6,"outline":{"topic":"A Bowl of Homemade Mushroom Risotto","intro":"favourite food • homemade mushroom risotto • Italian comfort food","body":["rice + mushrooms + stock + onion + cheese","gradual stock • steady stirring • creamy texture","first attempt imperfect • learned timing and texture • easy to adapt"],"conclusion":"simple ingredients • warm, filling, memorable"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-06.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"One food I enjoy is mushroom risotto, an Italian rice dish. It uses rice, mushrooms, stock, onion and a little cheese. You add the liquid slowly and keep stirring, which makes the rice soft and creamy without a heavy sauce.\n\nI learned to cook it from a video because I wanted something different from my usual quick meals. My first attempt was not very successful. I added too much liquid at once, but after practising a few times, I understood the right texture better.\n\nI usually make it on a cool evening when I have time to cook slowly. The smell of mushrooms and rice makes the kitchen feel warm and welcoming. It is filling and easy to change: I can add spinach, peas or roasted vegetables. I like it because a few simple ingredients can make a meal that feels special without being complicated or expensive.","html":"One food I enjoy is mushroom risotto, an Italian rice dish. It uses rice, mushrooms, stock, onion and a little cheese. You add the liquid slowly and keep stirring, which makes the rice soft and creamy without a heavy sauce.\n\nI learned to cook it from a video because I wanted something different from my usual quick meals. My first attempt was not very successful. I added too much liquid <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at once</strong>, but after practising a few times, I understood the right texture better.\n\nI usually make it on a cool evening when I have time to cook slowly. The smell of mushrooms and rice makes the kitchen feel warm and welcoming. It is filling and easy to change: I can add spinach, peas or roasted vegetables. I like it because a few simple <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ingredients</strong> can make a meal that feels special without being complicated or expensive.","phrases":[{"en":"at once","meaning":"All together or at the same moment."},{"en":"ingredients","meaning":"Foods used to make a dish."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-06.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A Fountain Pen from a Former Teacher","prompt":"Describe a meaningful gift you received.\nYou should say:\n- what the gift was\n- who gave it to you\n- when you received it\n- and explain why it was meaningful.","answer":"One of the most meaningful gifts I have received was a simple fountain pen from a former teacher.\n\nI received it at the end of a short course that had been quite important to me. The pen itself was not expensive or decorative. It had a dark metal body, a fine nib and a small case. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">What made it special</strong> was the note that came with it, which said that clear thinking usually begins with writing things down.\n\nAt the time, I was trying to make a difficult career decision and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">my thoughts were all over the place</strong>. I started using the pen to keep a small notebook of questions, plans and ideas. That habit helped me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">separate what I actually wanted</strong> from what I thought other people expected me to do.\n\nI still keep the pen on my desk. I do not use it every day, but whenever I have a complicated problem, I tend to reach for it. The gift is meaningful because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">represents much more than the object itself</strong>. It reminds me of useful advice given <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at exactly the right moment</strong>, and of the value of slowing down long enough to think clearly.","topic":"Core Story · Gift","phrases":[{"en":"what made it special","context":"What made it special was the note that came with it, which said that clear thinking usually begins with writing things down."},{"en":"my thoughts were all over the place","context":"At the time, I was trying to make a difficult career decision and my thoughts were all over the place."},{"en":"separate what I actually wanted","context":"That habit helped me separate what I actually wanted from what I thought other people expected me to do."},{"en":"represents much more than the object itself","context":"The gift is meaningful because it represents much more than the object itself."},{"en":"at exactly the right moment","context":"It reminds me of useful advice given at exactly the right moment, and of the value of slowing down long enough to think clearly."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"A Fountain Pen from a Former Teacher","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-07","_displayNo":"CORE 07","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":7,"outline":{"topic":"A Fountain Pen from a Former Teacher","intro":"meaningful gift • fountain pen • former teacher","body":["end of an important course • dark metal pen • handwritten note","career uncertainty • notebook for questions, plans and ideas","writing clarified priorities • still used for difficult decisions"],"conclusion":"timely advice • slow down • think clearly"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-07.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A special gift I received was a fountain pen from a former teacher. It was given to me at the end of an important short course. The pen had a dark metal body, a fine tip and a small case. It was simple rather than expensive.\n\nThe teacher included a note saying that writing things down helps us think clearly. At the time, I was facing a difficult career decision and felt confused. I began using the pen in a notebook to list my questions, plans and ideas. This helped me see the difference between my own wishes and other people's expectations.\n\nI still keep it on my desk, although I do not use it every day. When a problem is complicated, writing with it helps me slow down. The gift matters because it reminds me of advice I received when I needed it, not because of its price.","html":"A special gift I received was a fountain pen from a former teacher. It was given to me at the end of an important short course. The pen had a dark metal body, a fine tip and a small case. It was simple rather than expensive.\n\nThe teacher included a note saying that writing things down helps us think clearly. At the time, I was facing a difficult career decision and felt confused. I began using the pen in a notebook to list my questions, plans and ideas. This helped me see the difference between my own wishes and other people's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expectations</strong>.\n\nI still keep it on my desk, although I do not use it every day. When a problem is complicated, writing with it helps me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">slow down</strong>. The gift matters because it reminds me of advice I received when I needed it, not because of its price.","phrases":[{"en":"expectations","meaning":"Ideas about what someone thinks you should do."},{"en":"slow down","meaning":"Take more time and stop rushing."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-07.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A Small Mechanical Watch","prompt":"Describe an object that is important to you.\nYou should say:\n- what the object is\n- what it looks like\n- how you got it\n- and explain why it is important to you.","answer":"An object that is important to me is a small mechanical watch that I bought from a second-hand market several years ago.\n\nIt is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">quite understated</strong>: a plain silver case, a cream-coloured face and a dark leather strap. Unlike a digital watch, it has no notifications, apps or smart features. It simply tells the time, and it needs to be wound by hand every couple of days.\n\nI originally bought it because I liked the design, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">over time</strong> it became meaningful for a different reason. Winding it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a tiny ritual</strong> that makes me notice time rather than constantly check it. When I wear it, I am less tempted to pull out my phone every few minutes and get distracted by messages.\n\nI also like the fact that the mechanism is visible through the back. You can see dozens of small parts working together <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with remarkable precision</strong>. It is a useful reminder that something does not have to be new or complicated to be valuable. In a world full of disposable technology, this old watch feels calm, durable and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">refreshingly simple</strong>.","topic":"Core Story · Object","phrases":[{"en":"quite understated","context":"It is quite understated: a plain silver case, a cream-coloured face and a dark leather strap. Unlike a digital watch, it has no notifications, apps or smart features. It simply tells the time, and it needs to be wound by hand every couple of days."},{"en":"over time","context":"I originally bought it because I liked the design, but over time it became meaningful for a different reason."},{"en":"a tiny ritual","context":"Winding it is a tiny ritual that makes me notice time rather than constantly check it."},{"en":"with remarkable precision","context":"You can see dozens of small parts working together with remarkable precision."},{"en":"refreshingly simple","context":"In a world full of disposable technology, this old watch feels calm, durable and refreshingly simple."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"A Small Mechanical Watch","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-08","_displayNo":"CORE 08","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":8,"outline":{"topic":"A Small Mechanical Watch","intro":"important object • second-hand mechanical watch","body":["silver case • cream face • leather strap • hand-wound","bought for design • became an anti-distraction ritual","visible mechanism • precision • durable, non-disposable object"],"conclusion":"simple object • calmer relationship with time"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-08.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An important object I own is a small mechanical watch from a second-hand market. I bought it several years ago because I liked its simple design. It has a silver case, a cream face and a dark leather strap. There are no apps or messages; it only tells the time.\n\nI need to wind it by hand every couple of days. That small task has become an enjoyable habit. Wearing the watch also means I check my phone less often, so I am not pulled into messages whenever I want to know the time.\n\nAnother detail I like is the clear back. I can see many tiny parts moving together. It reminds me that something can be old and still work beautifully. With so much technology designed to be replaced, this watch feels different. I value its practical use, its strong construction and the calm, simple feeling it gives me.","html":"An important object I own is a small mechanical watch from a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">second-hand</strong> market. I bought it several years ago because I liked its simple design. It has a silver case, a cream face and a dark leather strap. There are no apps or messages; it only tells the time.\n\nI need to wind it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">by hand</strong> every couple of days. That small task has become an enjoyable habit. Wearing the watch also means I check my phone less often, so I am not pulled into messages whenever I want to know the time.\n\nAnother detail I like is the clear back. I can see many tiny parts moving together. It reminds me that something can be old and still work beautifully. With so much technology designed to be replaced, this watch feels different. I value its practical use, its strong construction and the calm, simple feeling it gives me.","phrases":[{"en":"second-hand","meaning":"Previously owned by someone else."},{"en":"by hand","meaning":"Using your hands rather than an automatic machine."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-08.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A Teacher Who Made Difficult Ideas Feel Simple","prompt":"Describe a person you admire.\nYou should say:\n- who the person is\n- what this person is like\n- what this person did\n- and explain why you admire this person.","answer":"A person I admire is a former science teacher who had an unusual ability to make difficult ideas feel simple <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without making them superficial</strong>.\n\nWhat I remember most was the way he explained things. Instead of starting with definitions, he usually began with an everyday question. For example, he might ask why a metal spoon feels colder than a wooden one even when both are in the same room. Once everyone was curious, he introduced the scientific principle behind it.\n\nHe was also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remarkably patient</strong>. If somebody gave a wrong answer, he never made them feel embarrassed. He treated mistakes as useful evidence of how a person was thinking, and then guided the class towards a better explanation. That created an atmosphere where people were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">willing to speak up</strong>.\n\nI admire him because he understood that expertise is not just knowing a lot; it is being able to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">communicate knowledge clearly</strong> and respectfully. His teaching style influenced the way I explain things to other people today. Whenever I need to present a complex idea, I try to begin with something concrete, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remove unnecessary jargon</strong> and focus on the logic that actually matters.","topic":"Core Story · Person","phrases":[{"en":"without making them superficial","context":"A person I admire is a former science teacher who had an unusual ability to make difficult ideas feel simple without making them superficial."},{"en":"remarkably patient","context":"He was also remarkably patient."},{"en":"willing to speak up","context":"That created an atmosphere where people were willing to speak up."},{"en":"communicate knowledge clearly","context":"I admire him because he understood that expertise is not just knowing a lot; it is being able to communicate knowledge clearly and respectfully."},{"en":"remove unnecessary jargon","context":"Whenever I need to present a complex idea, I try to begin with something concrete, remove unnecessary jargon and focus on the logic that actually matters."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"A Teacher Who Made Difficult Ideas Feel Simple","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-09","_displayNo":"CORE 09","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":9,"outline":{"topic":"A Teacher Who Made Difficult Ideas Feel Simple","intro":"admired person • former science teacher • clear explanations","body":["everyday questions before theory • curiosity first","patient with mistakes • no embarrassment • guided reasoning","expertise + communication • concrete examples • minimal jargon"],"conclusion":"influenced how I explain complex ideas"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-09.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A person I admire is a former science teacher. He could explain difficult ideas clearly without leaving out the important meaning. Rather than beginning with a definition, he asked an everyday question, such as why a metal spoon feels colder than a wooden one in the same room.\n\nOnce we were interested, he explained the science. He was also patient when someone answered incorrectly. Instead of embarrassing them, he used the mistake to understand their thinking and helped them find a better explanation. That made us more willing to speak in class.\n\nI admire him because knowing a subject and explaining it well are different skills. He could do both while treating people with respect. His approach still influences me. When I explain a difficult idea, I try to start with something familiar, use clear language and show the steps. He taught me that good communication helps other people understand, rather than simply showing how much you know.","html":"A person I admire is a former science teacher. He could explain difficult ideas clearly without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaving out</strong> the important meaning. Rather than beginning with a definition, he asked an everyday question, such as why a metal spoon feels colder than a wooden one in the same room.\n\nOnce we were interested, he explained the science. He was also patient when someone answered incorrectly. Instead of embarrassing them, he used the mistake to understand their thinking and helped them find a better explanation. That made us more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">willing to</strong> speak in class.\n\nI admire him because knowing a subject and explaining it well are different skills. He could do both while treating people with respect. His approach still influences me. When I explain a difficult idea, I try to start with something familiar, use clear language and show the steps. He taught me that good communication helps other people understand, rather than simply showing how much you know.","phrases":[{"en":"leaving out","meaning":"Not including something."},{"en":"willing to","meaning":"Ready or happy to do something."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-09.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Lisbon and Its Waterfront","prompt":"Describe a place you would like to visit again.\nYou should say:\n- where the place is\n- when you first visited it\n- what you did there\n- and explain why you want to return.","answer":"A place I would genuinely like to visit again is Lisbon, especially the older neighbourhoods and the waterfront along the Tagus River.\n\nI visited the city on a short trip and expected to spend most of my time seeing famous landmarks. In reality, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the best part was simply walking</strong>. The streets rise and fall sharply, old trams move through narrow corners, and small viewpoints suddenly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">open onto</strong> rooftops and the river.\n\nOne afternoon I walked down towards the waterfront just before sunset. The light reflected off the pale buildings and the river, and the whole area felt lively <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without being chaotic</strong>. I bought a coffee, sat outside and watched commuters, tourists and local families pass by. Nothing dramatic happened, but the scene had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a strong sense of place</strong>.\n\nI want to return because I felt I only saw the surface of the city. Next time I would stay longer, explore areas <strong class=\"key-phrase\">away from the main tourist routes</strong> and take more time with local food and museums. Lisbon is memorable to me because it combines history, everyday life and beautiful public spaces in a very natural way.","topic":"Core Story · Place","phrases":[{"en":"the best part was simply walking","context":"In reality, the best part was simply walking."},{"en":"open onto","context":"The streets rise and fall sharply, old trams move through narrow corners, and small viewpoints suddenly open onto rooftops and the river."},{"en":"without being chaotic","context":"The light reflected off the pale buildings and the river, and the whole area felt lively without being chaotic."},{"en":"a strong sense of place","context":"Nothing dramatic happened, but the scene had a strong sense of place."},{"en":"away from the main tourist routes","context":"Next time I would stay longer, explore areas away from the main tourist routes and take more time with local food and museums."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"Lisbon and Its Waterfront","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-10","_displayNo":"CORE 10","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":10,"outline":{"topic":"Lisbon and Its Waterfront","intro":"place to revisit • Lisbon • old neighbourhoods + Tagus waterfront","body":["hilly streets • old trams • viewpoints • rooftops","sunset waterfront walk • coffee • locals + tourists • lively, not chaotic","next visit • slower pace • local food • museums • quieter areas"],"conclusion":"history + everyday life + beautiful public spaces"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-10.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"I'd like to visit Lisbon again, especially its old neighbourhoods and the area beside the Tagus River. I first went on a short trip and thought the famous sights would be the best part. Instead, I most enjoyed simply walking around.\n\nThe streets were steep, old trams passed through narrow corners, and viewpoints opened onto rooftops and water. One afternoon, I reached the river just before sunset. Light shone on the pale buildings, and the area felt busy but not stressful. I bought a coffee, sat outside and watched local families, workers and tourists pass by.\n\nI want to return because the trip was too short to understand the city fully. Next time, I would explore quieter areas, try more local food and spend longer in museums. Lisbon stays in my memory because its history and ordinary daily life sit naturally together. Even doing very little there felt interesting and enjoyable.","html":"I'd like to visit Lisbon again, especially its old neighbourhoods and the area beside the Tagus River. I first went on a short trip and thought the famous sights would be the best part. Instead, I most enjoyed simply walking around.\n\nThe streets were steep, old trams passed through narrow corners, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">viewpoints</strong> opened onto rooftops and water. One afternoon, I reached the river just before sunset. Light shone on the pale buildings, and the area felt busy but not stressful. I bought a coffee, sat outside and watched local families, workers and tourists <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pass by</strong>.\n\nI want to return because the trip was too short to understand the city fully. Next time, I would explore quieter areas, try more local food and spend longer in museums. Lisbon stays in my memory because its history and ordinary daily life sit naturally together. Even doing very little there felt interesting and enjoyable.","phrases":[{"en":"viewpoints","meaning":"Places offering a clear or attractive view."},{"en":"pass by","meaning":"Go past a place or person."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-10.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Learning to Lift Weights Safely","prompt":"Describe a skill you learned.\nYou should say:\n- what the skill is\n- when and how you learned it\n- why it was difficult or useful\n- and explain how it has changed you.","answer":"A useful skill I learned is how to perform basic weight-training exercises safely and with good technique.\n\nWhen I first started going to a gym, I assumed progress was mainly about lifting heavier weights. I soon realised that poor technique could make an exercise less effective and, more importantly, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">increase the risk of injury</strong>. That made me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take the skill more seriously</strong>.\n\nI learned by watching qualified coaches, recording a few of my own sets and comparing what I was doing with reliable demonstrations. I focused on basic movements such as squats, rows and presses, and I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deliberately used lighter weights</strong> until the movement felt controlled. The most difficult part was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">resisting the temptation</strong> to add weight too quickly.\n\nThis skill has changed the way I train because I now pay much more attention to consistency, range of motion and recovery. It has also taught me a broader lesson: doing something properly is often more valuable than doing it impressively. Good technique is not exciting, but it lets you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make progress for years</strong> rather than for a few reckless weeks.","topic":"Core Story · Skill","phrases":[{"en":"increase the risk of injury","context":"I soon realised that poor technique could make an exercise less effective and, more importantly, increase the risk of injury."},{"en":"take the skill more seriously","context":"That made me take the skill more seriously."},{"en":"deliberately used lighter weights","context":"I focused on basic movements such as squats, rows and presses, and I deliberately used lighter weights until the movement felt controlled."},{"en":"resisting the temptation","context":"The most difficult part was resisting the temptation to add weight too quickly."},{"en":"make progress for years","context":"Good technique is not exciting, but it lets you make progress for years rather than for a few reckless weeks."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"Learning to Lift Weights Safely","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-11","_displayNo":"CORE 11","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":11,"outline":{"topic":"Learning to Lift Weights Safely","intro":"useful skill • safe weight training • good technique","body":["early mistake: heavier = better • injury risk","qualified coaches • self-recording • reliable demonstrations","basic lifts • lighter loads • controlled range of motion • resist ego lifting"],"conclusion":"quality over impressiveness • sustainable progress"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-11.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A useful skill I learned is lifting weights with safe, controlled technique. When I first went to the gym, I thought improvement mainly meant using heavier weights. I soon realised that moving badly could make an exercise less useful and increase the chance of injury.\n\nI learned from qualified coaches, reliable demonstrations and recordings of my own training. I focused on basic exercises such as squats, rows and presses. I kept the weights light until I could control each movement. The hardest part was resisting the urge to increase the weight too soon.\n\nNow I pay more attention to regular practice, proper movement and enough recovery. This has changed the way I train. I have also learned a lesson that applies outside the gym: doing something correctly matters more than making it look impressive. Good technique may not attract much attention, but it helps me keep improving over a longer period.","html":"A useful skill I learned is lifting weights with safe, controlled technique. When I first went to the gym, I thought improvement mainly meant using heavier weights. I soon realised that moving badly could make an exercise less useful and increase the chance of injury.\n\nI learned from qualified coaches, reliable demonstrations and recordings of my own training. I focused on basic exercises such as squats, rows and presses. I kept the weights light until I could control each movement. The hardest part was resisting <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the urge</strong> to increase the weight too soon.\n\nNow I pay more attention to regular practice, proper movement and enough <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recovery</strong>. This has changed the way I train. I have also learned a lesson that applies outside the gym: doing something correctly matters more than making it look impressive. Good technique may not attract much attention, but it helps me keep improving over a longer period.","phrases":[{"en":"the urge","meaning":"A strong wish to do something."},{"en":"recovery","meaning":"Time or activity that helps you rest and become ready again."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-11.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An Overnight Train Through the Alps","prompt":"Describe a memorable journey or trip.\nYou should say:\n- where and when you travelled\n- who you travelled with\n- what happened during the journey\n- and explain why it was memorable.","answer":"One of the most memorable journeys I have taken was an overnight train trip through the Alps.\n\nI chose the train instead of flying because I wanted the journey itself to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">part of the experience</strong>. We left a busy city in the evening, and for the first hour the view was fairly ordinary. Later, the buildings disappeared and the landscape became darker and much more dramatic.\n\nI did not sleep particularly well, but that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be</strong> a good thing. Just after sunrise, I looked out of the window and saw steep mountains, small villages and patches of snow above green valleys. The scenery <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changed every few minutes</strong>, and because the train was quiet, it felt almost like watching a film with no soundtrack.\n\nWhat made the journey memorable was the contrast between how slowly it felt and how much distance we actually covered. Air travel is faster, but it often reduces a trip to airports and waiting. The train gave me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clearer sense of</strong> moving through a landscape. I arrived tired, but I also felt that the holiday had already begun <strong class=\"key-phrase\">before I reached the destination</strong>.","topic":"Core Story · Trip","phrases":[{"en":"part of the experience","context":"I chose the train instead of flying because I wanted the journey itself to be part of the experience."},{"en":"turned out to be","context":"I did not sleep particularly well, but that turned out to be a good thing."},{"en":"changed every few minutes","context":"The scenery changed every few minutes, and because the train was quiet, it felt almost like watching a film with no soundtrack."},{"en":"a clearer sense of","context":"The train gave me a clearer sense of moving through a landscape."},{"en":"before I reached the destination","context":"I arrived tired, but I also felt that the holiday had already begun before I reached the destination."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"An Overnight Train Through the Alps","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-12","_displayNo":"CORE 12","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":12,"outline":{"topic":"An Overnight Train Through the Alps","intro":"memorable journey • overnight train • the Alps","body":["train instead of flying • journey as part of the experience","night travel • little sleep • sunrise reveal","mountains • villages • snow • quiet carriage • constantly changing scenery"],"conclusion":"slower travel • real sense of distance • holiday begins en route"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-12.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A journey I remember clearly was an overnight train trip through the Alps. I chose the train because I wanted travelling itself to be part of the holiday. We left a busy city in the evening. At first, the view was ordinary, but later the buildings disappeared and the landscape became darker.\n\nI did not sleep very well. However, this meant I was awake just after sunrise, when we passed mountains, villages and green valleys with snow above them. The view changed every few minutes. Inside the quiet train, it almost felt like watching a film without music.\n\nThe journey seemed peaceful even though we covered a long distance. Flying would have been faster, but much of that experience would have involved airports and waiting. On the train, I could actually watch the places changing. I arrived tired, but I already felt I had experienced something special before reaching my destination.","html":"A journey I remember clearly was an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overnight</strong> train trip through the Alps. I chose the train because I wanted travelling itself to be part of the holiday. We left a busy city in the evening. At first, the view was ordinary, but later the buildings disappeared and the landscape became darker.\n\nI did not sleep very well. However, this meant I was awake just after sunrise, when we passed mountains, villages and green valleys with snow above them. The view changed every few minutes. Inside the quiet train, it almost felt like watching a film without music.\n\nThe journey seemed peaceful even though we covered a long distance. Flying would have been faster, but much of that experience would have involved airports and waiting. On the train, I could actually watch the places changing. I arrived tired, but I already felt I had experienced something special before reaching my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">destination</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"overnight","meaning":"During the night, often continuing until morning."},{"en":"destination","meaning":"The place you are travelling to."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-12.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"The Sagrada Familia","prompt":"Describe a building that you would like to visit.\nYou should say:\n- what the building is\n- where it is located\n- what you know about it\n- and explain why you would like to visit it.","answer":"A building I would like to visit is the Sagrada Familia in Barcelona, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of the most distinctive</strong> churches in the world.\n\nI first became interested in it after seeing photographs of the interior. From the outside, the building is extremely detailed and almost looks as if it has grown rather than been constructed. Inside, tall columns branch out like trees and coloured light enters through large stained-glass windows.\n\nWhat fascinates me is the combination of engineering, art and symbolism. The architect, Antoni Gaudi, used forms <strong class=\"key-phrase\">inspired by nature</strong>, but the building is not simply decorative. Many of those shapes also help support the structure. I find that idea appealing because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">beauty and function</strong> are working together rather than competing with each other.\n\nI would like to see it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in person</strong> because photographs can show the details but not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the scale or atmosphere</strong>. I would probably spend most of my visit looking at how the light changes across the interior. For me, it is the kind of building that can interest someone even if they know very little about architecture.","topic":"Core Story · Building","phrases":[{"en":"one of the most distinctive","context":"A building I would like to visit is the Sagrada Familia in Barcelona, one of the most distinctive churches in the world."},{"en":"inspired by nature","context":"The architect, Antoni Gaudi, used forms inspired by nature, but the building is not simply decorative."},{"en":"beauty and function","context":"I find that idea appealing because beauty and function are working together rather than competing with each other."},{"en":"in person","context":"I would like to see it in person because photographs can show the details but not the scale or atmosphere."},{"en":"the scale or atmosphere","context":"I would like to see it in person because photographs can show the details but not the scale or atmosphere."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"The Sagrada Familia","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-13","_displayNo":"CORE 13","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":13,"outline":{"topic":"The Sagrada Familia","intro":"building to visit • Sagrada Familia • Barcelona","body":["organic exterior • tree-like columns • stained glass","Gaudí • nature-inspired forms • engineering + art + symbolism","want to experience scale • atmosphere • changing interior light"],"conclusion":"beauty + function • memorable even without architecture expertise"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-13.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A building I'd like to visit is the Sagrada Familia in Barcelona. I became interested in it after seeing photographs of the inside. Outside, it has so many unusual details that it almost looks grown rather than built. Inside, tall columns look like tree branches and coloured windows fill the space with light.\n\nThe architect, Antoni Gaudi, took ideas from nature. I find it interesting that these shapes do more than look attractive. Some also help support the building. The combination of beauty and practical design is what interests me most.\n\nI would like to see it myself because pictures cannot fully show its size or atmosphere. I would probably spend a long time watching how the light changes inside. I do not think someone needs expert knowledge of architecture to enjoy this place. It seems like a building that can make almost anyone curious about how it was designed and built.","html":"A building I'd like to visit is the Sagrada Familia in Barcelona. I became interested in it after seeing photographs of the inside. Outside, it has so many unusual details that it almost looks grown rather than built. Inside, tall <strong class=\"key-phrase\">columns</strong> look like tree branches and coloured windows fill the space with light.\n\nThe architect, Antoni Gaudi, took ideas from nature. I find it interesting that these shapes do more than look attractive. Some also help support the building. The combination of beauty and practical design is what interests me most.\n\nI would like to see it myself because pictures cannot fully show its size or atmosphere. I would probably spend a long time watching how the light changes inside. I do not think someone needs <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expert knowledge</strong> of architecture to enjoy this place. It seems like a building that can make almost anyone curious about how it was designed and built.","phrases":[{"en":"columns","meaning":"Tall upright structures that support a roof or building."},{"en":"expert knowledge","meaning":"A deep understanding of a particular subject."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-13.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"The Little Prince","prompt":"Describe a book that you have read and enjoyed.\nYou should say:\n- what the book was\n- when and where you read it\n- what it was about\n- and explain why you enjoyed reading it.","answer":"A book I have read and genuinely enjoyed is The Little Prince by Antoine de Saint-Exupery.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">On the surface</strong>, it is a short story about a young prince who travels from one tiny planet to another and meets a series of strange adults. The language is simple and the illustrations make it look like a children's book, which is partly why I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">underestimated it at first</strong>.\n\nWhat makes the book interesting is that many of the conversations are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">really about adult behaviour</strong>. It touches on loneliness, friendship, responsibility and the way people can become obsessed with status or numbers. The ideas are not presented as long arguments; they appear in small scenes that are easy to remember.\n\nI enjoyed it because the book <strong class=\"key-phrase\">works on two levels</strong>. A child can follow the story, while an adult can recognise the criticism underneath it. I also like books that can be reread without feeling repetitive, and this is one of them. Different lines stand out depending on what is happening in your own life. It is short, imaginative and much <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more thoughtful than its simple appearance suggests</strong>.","topic":"Core Story · Book","phrases":[{"en":"on the surface","context":"On the surface, it is a short story about a young prince who travels from one tiny planet to another and meets a series of strange adults."},{"en":"underestimated it at first","context":"The language is simple and the illustrations make it look like a children's book, which is partly why I underestimated it at first."},{"en":"really about adult behaviour","context":"What makes the book interesting is that many of the conversations are really about adult behaviour."},{"en":"works on two levels","context":"I enjoyed it because the book works on two levels."},{"en":"more thoughtful than its simple appearance suggests","context":"It is short, imaginative and much more thoughtful than its simple appearance suggests."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"The Little Prince","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-14","_displayNo":"CORE 14","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":14,"outline":{"topic":"The Little Prince","intro":"book enjoyed • The Little Prince • Antoine de Saint-Exupéry","body":["young prince • tiny planets • strange adults • simple style","themes: loneliness • friendship • responsibility • status • numbers","works for children + adults • memorable scenes • strong reread value"],"conclusion":"short + imaginative + much deeper than it looks"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-14.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A book I enjoyed is The Little Prince by Antoine de Saint-Exupery. It follows a young prince travelling between tiny planets and meeting unusual adults. At first, I thought it was mainly for children because it is short, has simple language and includes pictures.\n\nHowever, the conversations explore ideas that adults recognise too. They are about friendship, loneliness, responsibility and becoming too interested in status or numbers. Instead of giving long explanations, the writer puts these ideas into short, memorable scenes.\n\nI like the book because different readers can enjoy it in different ways. A child can follow the adventure, while an adult may think about the criticism of adult behaviour. I also enjoy reading it again because different lines feel important at different times in my life. Although it looks simple, it gives me a lot to consider. It is imaginative, easy to follow and more thoughtful than I expected.","html":"A book I enjoyed is The Little Prince by Antoine de Saint-Exupery. It follows a young prince travelling between tiny planets and meeting unusual adults. At first, I thought it was mainly for children because it is short, has simple language and includes pictures.\n\nHowever, the conversations explore ideas that adults recognise too. They are about friendship, loneliness, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">responsibility</strong> and becoming too interested in status or numbers. Instead of giving long explanations, the writer puts these ideas into short, memorable scenes.\n\nI like the book because different readers can enjoy it in different ways. A child can follow the adventure, while an adult may think about the criticism of adult behaviour. I also enjoy reading it again because different lines feel important at different times in my life. Although it looks simple, it gives me a lot to consider. It is imaginative, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easy to follow</strong> and more thoughtful than I expected.","phrases":[{"en":"easy to follow","meaning":"Not difficult to understand."},{"en":"responsibility","meaning":"A duty to care for something or act properly."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-14.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"David Attenborough","prompt":"Describe a famous person you admire.\nYou should say:\n- who this person is\n- how you know about him\n- what he has achieved\n- and explain why you admire him.","answer":"A famous person I admire is David Attenborough, the British broadcaster and natural historian known for presenting wildlife documentaries.\n\nI first became familiar with his work through nature programmes. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">What immediately stood out</strong> was his voice and the way he explained complex behaviour without sounding dramatic or technical. He could describe a tiny insect or a huge ecosystem with the same level of curiosity.\n\nHis career is impressive because it has lasted for decades, but longevity is not the main reason I admire him. What matters more is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the effect of his work</strong>. He has helped millions of people see wildlife as something <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worth understanding and protecting</strong>, rather than as background scenery. In recent years, he has also spoken clearly about climate change and biodiversity loss.\n\nI admire him because he <strong class=\"key-phrase\">combines expertise with communication</strong>. He rarely makes himself the centre of the story; the subject remains more important than the presenter. That is a quality I respect in any expert. Being knowledgeable is valuable, but being able to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make other people curious</strong> is a much rarer skill.","topic":"Core Story · Famous Person","phrases":[{"en":"what immediately stood out","context":"What immediately stood out was his voice and the way he explained complex behaviour without sounding dramatic or technical."},{"en":"the effect of his work","context":"What matters more is the effect of his work."},{"en":"worth understanding and protecting","context":"He has helped millions of people see wildlife as something worth understanding and protecting, rather than as background scenery."},{"en":"combines expertise with communication","context":"I admire him because he combines expertise with communication."},{"en":"make other people curious","context":"Being knowledgeable is valuable, but being able to make other people curious is a much rarer skill."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"David Attenborough","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-15","_displayNo":"CORE 15","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":15,"outline":{"topic":"David Attenborough","intro":"famous person admired • David Attenborough • broadcaster + natural historian","body":["nature documentaries • calm voice • clear explanations","decades-long career • wildlife awareness • conservation","climate + biodiversity advocacy • subject more important than presenter"],"conclusion":"expertise + communication + ability to create curiosity"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-15.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A famous person I admire is David Attenborough, the British presenter known for nature documentaries. I first learned about him through his wildlife programmes. I noticed his calm voice and the clear way he explained animal behaviour without using too much difficult language.\n\nHis career has lasted many years, but that is not the main reason I respect him. His work has helped people become interested in wildlife and understand why it needs protection. He can make a tiny insect seem as interesting as a large animal. More recently, he has also discussed climate change and the loss of species.\n\nWhat I admire most is his ability to share knowledge. He usually keeps attention on the subject rather than himself. I think that is an important quality in an expert. Knowing a great deal is useful, but helping other people become curious can have an even wider effect. His programmes show how powerful good explanations can be.","html":"A famous person I admire is David Attenborough, the British presenter known for nature documentaries. I first learned about him through his wildlife programmes. I noticed his calm voice and the clear way he explained animal behaviour without using too much difficult language.\n\nHis career has lasted many years, but that is not the main reason I respect him. His work has helped people become interested in wildlife and understand why it needs <strong class=\"key-phrase\">protection</strong>. He can make a tiny insect seem as interesting as a large animal. More recently, he has also discussed climate change and the loss of species.\n\nWhat I admire most is his ability to share knowledge. He usually keeps attention on the subject rather than himself. I think that is an important quality in an expert. Knowing a great deal is useful, but helping other people become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">curious</strong> can have an even wider effect. His programmes show how powerful good explanations can be.","phrases":[{"en":"protection","meaning":"Action taken to keep something safe from harm."},{"en":"curious","meaning":"Interested in learning or discovering more."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-15.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A Wooden Construction Set","prompt":"Describe a toy you enjoyed playing with when you were a child.\nYou should say:\n- what the toy was\n- who gave it to you\n- what it looked like\n- and explain why you remember it.","answer":"A childhood toy I remember particularly well was a wooden construction set made up of small blocks, wheels and connecting pieces.\n\nIt was not electronic and it did not come with a fixed set of instructions. I could build a car one day, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take it apart</strong> and turn the same pieces into a bridge or a strange-looking machine the next day. Some of my creations were completely impractical, but that was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">part of the fun</strong>.\n\nI received the set as a birthday present when I was in primary school. For several months, it was the first thing I reached for after finishing my homework. I often tried to build something I had seen outside, such as a crane or a bus, and then changed the design when the pieces did not fit.\n\nI remember it because it kept me interested for much longer than toys with only one function. It encouraged <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trial and error</strong> and gave me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a small sense of achievement</strong> whenever a design finally worked. Looking back, I think it taught me that creativity is often less about having unlimited options and more about finding new uses for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">limited resources</strong>.","topic":"Core Story · Toy","phrases":[{"en":"take it apart","context":"I could build a car one day, take it apart and turn the same pieces into a bridge or a strange-looking machine the next day."},{"en":"part of the fun","context":"Some of my creations were completely impractical, but that was part of the fun."},{"en":"trial and error","context":"It encouraged trial and error and gave me a small sense of achievement whenever a design finally worked."},{"en":"a small sense of achievement","context":"It encouraged trial and error and gave me a small sense of achievement whenever a design finally worked."},{"en":"limited resources","context":"Looking back, I think it taught me that creativity is often less about having unlimited options and more about finding new uses for limited resources."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"A Wooden Construction Set","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-16","_displayNo":"CORE 16","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":16,"outline":{"topic":"A Wooden Construction Set","intro":"childhood toy • wooden construction set • blocks, wheels, connectors","body":["no electronics • no fixed instructions • endless possible builds","birthday gift • cranes, buses, bridges • redesign when pieces fail","trial and error • creativity within constraints • small achievements"],"conclusion":"long-lasting play • problem-solving + imagination"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-16.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A childhood toy I remember well was a wooden building set. It contained small blocks, wheels and pieces that connected together. I received it for a birthday while I was at primary school. There were no electronic parts or fixed instructions, so I could decide what to make.\n\nI often played with it after homework. One day, I built a car; another day, the same pieces became a bridge or a strange machine. I also tried copying things I had seen outside, such as buses or cranes. If the pieces did not fit, I changed my plan and tried again.\n\nThe set kept me interested much longer than toys with only one purpose. It felt satisfying when a design finally worked. Looking back, it taught me to experiment and solve small problems. I did not need unlimited materials to be creative. I just needed to find different ways of using the pieces I already had.","html":"A childhood toy I remember well was a wooden building set. It contained small blocks, wheels and pieces that connected together. I received it for a birthday while I was at primary school. There were no electronic parts or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fixed instructions</strong>, so I could decide what to make.\n\nI often played with it after homework. One day, I built a car; another day, the same pieces became a bridge or a strange machine. I also tried copying things I had seen outside, such as buses or cranes. If the pieces did not fit, I changed my plan and tried again.\n\nThe set kept me interested much longer than toys with only one purpose. It felt satisfying when a design finally worked. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Looking back</strong>, it taught me to experiment and solve small problems. I did not need unlimited materials to be creative. I just needed to find different ways of using the pieces I already had.","phrases":[{"en":"fixed instructions","meaning":"Directions that tell you exactly what to do."},{"en":"Looking back","meaning":"Thinking about an experience in the past."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-16.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Excellent Help After a Cancelled Flight","prompt":"Describe a good service you received from a company or organisation.\nYou should say:\n- what the service was\n- when and where you received it\n- why you used the service\n- and explain why you thought it was good.","answer":"One of the best examples of customer service I have experienced happened at a hotel after my evening flight was cancelled.\n\nBy the time I reached the hotel, it was late and the lobby was full of other <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stranded travellers</strong>. I expected a long wait and a fairly mechanical check-in. Instead, the receptionist organised the queue quickly, explained the available rooms and gave clear information about breakfast and transport back to the airport.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">What impressed me most</strong> was how calmly the staff handled people who were tired and frustrated. One family had lost access to their luggage, so the hotel provided basic toiletries and arranged a larger room <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without making the situation complicated</strong>. When I mentioned that I had an early replacement flight, the receptionist also booked a taxi for me before I even asked.\n\nThe service was good because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">solved practical problems</strong> while also reducing stress. Nobody used exaggerated language or made promises they could not keep; they simply noticed what people needed and acted efficiently. That experience changed my idea of good service. It is not about luxury. It is about making a difficult situation <strong class=\"key-phrase\">noticeably easier</strong>.","topic":"Core Story · Service","phrases":[{"en":"stranded travellers","context":"By the time I reached the hotel, it was late and the lobby was full of other stranded travellers."},{"en":"what impressed me most","context":"What impressed me most was how calmly the staff handled people who were tired and frustrated."},{"en":"without making the situation complicated","context":"One family had lost access to their luggage, so the hotel provided basic toiletries and arranged a larger room without making the situation complicated."},{"en":"solved practical problems","context":"The service was good because it solved practical problems while also reducing stress."},{"en":"noticeably easier","context":"It is about making a difficult situation noticeably easier."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"Excellent Help After a Cancelled Flight","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-17","_displayNo":"CORE 17","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":17,"outline":{"topic":"Excellent Help After a Cancelled Flight","intro":"excellent service • hotel • cancelled evening flight","body":["late arrival • stranded travellers • expected chaos","fast queue • clear room, breakfast and transport information","toiletries + larger room for a family • taxi arranged proactively"],"conclusion":"practical help + calm staff • good service reduces stress"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-17.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"I received excellent service at a hotel after my evening flight was cancelled. I arrived late, tired and worried. Many other passengers were waiting in the lobby, so I expected a slow check-in. Instead, the receptionist organised the queue and explained the rooms, breakfast and transport clearly.\n\nThe staff stayed calm even when guests were upset. One family could not get their luggage, so the hotel gave them basic washing supplies and arranged a larger room. When I mentioned my early replacement flight, the receptionist booked a taxi before I needed to ask.\n\nWhat impressed me was the way they noticed practical problems and solved them without making things complicated. They did not make unrealistic promises or use fancy language. Their actions simply made us feel less stressed. This changed my idea of good service. It does not have to mean luxury; sometimes it means helping someone through a difficult situation calmly and efficiently.","html":"I received excellent service at a hotel after my evening flight was cancelled. I arrived late, tired and worried. Many other passengers were waiting in the lobby, so I expected a slow check-in. Instead, the receptionist organised the queue and explained the rooms, breakfast and transport clearly.\n\nThe staff stayed calm even when guests were upset. One family could not get their luggage, so the hotel gave them basic washing supplies and arranged a larger room. When I mentioned my early <strong class=\"key-phrase\">replacement flight</strong>, the receptionist booked a taxi before I needed to ask.\n\nWhat impressed me was the way they noticed practical problems and solved them without making things complicated. They did not make <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unrealistic promises</strong> or use fancy language. Their actions simply made us feel less stressed. This changed my idea of good service. It does not have to mean luxury; sometimes it means helping someone through a difficult situation calmly and efficiently.","phrases":[{"en":"replacement flight","meaning":"Another flight provided because the original one cannot be taken."},{"en":"unrealistic promises","meaning":"Claims about future help that cannot reasonably be kept."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-17.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Why I Like Dogs","prompt":"Describe an animal that you like.\nYou should say:\n- what the animal is\n- what you know about it\n- whether you have ever kept one\n- and explain why you like it.","answer":"An animal I have gradually come to appreciate is the dog, mainly because of how social, observant and adaptable dogs can be.\n\nI was not especially interested in animals when I was younger, so my opinion changed quite slowly. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The turning point came when</strong> a close friend adopted a rescue dog. At first the dog was nervous around strangers, but after a few visits it began to recognise me and wait near the door when I arrived.\n\nWhat I find interesting is how well dogs <strong class=\"key-phrase\">read human behaviour</strong>. They notice tone of voice, routine and body language, and many of them seem to know when somebody is excited or upset. Of course, that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">varies from one animal to another</strong>, but the level of communication can be remarkable even without words.\n\nI also respect the practical roles dogs can perform, from search and rescue to guiding people with visual impairments. That combination of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">companionship and usefulness</strong> is unusual. I do not currently keep a dog because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">requires time and responsibility</strong>, but if my lifestyle becomes more suitable in the future, I would seriously consider adopting one.","topic":"Core Story · Animal","phrases":[{"en":"the turning point came when","context":"The turning point came when a close friend adopted a rescue dog."},{"en":"read human behaviour","context":"What I find interesting is how well dogs read human behaviour."},{"en":"varies from one animal to another","context":"Of course, that varies from one animal to another, but the level of communication can be remarkable even without words."},{"en":"companionship and usefulness","context":"That combination of companionship and usefulness is unusual."},{"en":"requires time and responsibility","context":"I do not currently keep a dog because it requires time and responsibility, but if my lifestyle becomes more suitable in the future, I would seriously consider adopting one."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"Why I Like Dogs","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-18","_displayNo":"CORE 18","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":18,"outline":{"topic":"Why I Like Dogs","intro":"animal I like • dogs • social, observant, adaptable","body":["interest grew through a friend's rescue dog","read tone, routines and body language • trust develops gradually","companionship + practical roles • search and rescue • guide dogs"],"conclusion":"would adopt when lifestyle allows • responsibility matters"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-18.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An animal I have learned to appreciate is the dog. I was not very interested in animals when I was younger. My opinion began to change when a close friend adopted a rescue dog. At first, it was nervous around strangers, but after several visits it recognised me and waited near the door.\n\nI was impressed by how well dogs notice human behaviour. They respond to voice, daily habits and body language, and some seem to understand when people feel happy or upset. Of course, every dog is different, but this communication without words is interesting.\n\nDogs can also do important work, such as helping rescue teams or guiding people who cannot see well. I like that they can be both useful and good company. I do not currently own one because it needs time and regular care. If my lifestyle becomes suitable, though, I would seriously consider adopting a dog in the future.","html":"An animal I have learned to appreciate is the dog. I was not very interested in animals when I was younger. My opinion began to change when a close friend adopted a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rescue dog</strong>. At first, it was nervous around strangers, but after several visits it recognised me and waited near the door.\n\nI was impressed by how well dogs notice human behaviour. They respond to voice, daily habits and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">body language</strong>, and some seem to understand when people feel happy or upset. Of course, every dog is different, but this communication without words is interesting.\n\nDogs can also do important work, such as helping rescue teams or guiding people who cannot see well. I like that they can be both useful and good company. I do not currently own one because it needs time and regular care. If my lifestyle becomes suitable, though, I would seriously consider adopting a dog in the future.","phrases":[{"en":"rescue dog","meaning":"A dog taken into care after being abandoned or needing help."},{"en":"body language","meaning":"Feelings or messages shown through movement and posture."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-18.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A Phone-Free Morning Walk","prompt":"Describe a positive habit that you have developed.\nYou should say:\n- what the habit is\n- when you started it\n- how often you do it\n- and explain how it has improved your life.","answer":"A positive habit I have developed is taking a short morning walk without looking at my phone.\n\nThe habit started when I noticed that I was checking messages almost immediately after waking up. Nothing urgent was happening, but the habit made the day feel busy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">before it had properly begun</strong>. I decided to leave my phone in my pocket and walk for about half an hour instead.\n\nAt first, it felt strangely unproductive because I was used to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">filling every spare minute</strong> with information. After a week or two, however, I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">started to value the quiet</strong>. I use the walk to think through the day, notice what is happening around me and sometimes solve small problems that felt confusing the night before.\n\nThe biggest benefit is not physical, although walking obviously helps with that. It is the mental transition between sleep and work. I begin the day with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clearer head</strong> and I am less likely to react immediately to every notification. The habit costs nothing, requires no special equipment and is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easy to maintain</strong>, which is probably why it has lasted.","topic":"Core Story · Habit","phrases":[{"en":"before it had properly begun","context":"Nothing urgent was happening, but the habit made the day feel busy before it had properly begun."},{"en":"filling every spare minute","context":"At first, it felt strangely unproductive because I was used to filling every spare minute with information."},{"en":"started to value the quiet","context":"After a week or two, however, I started to value the quiet."},{"en":"a clearer head","context":"I begin the day with a clearer head and I am less likely to react immediately to every notification."},{"en":"easy to maintain","context":"The habit costs nothing, requires no special equipment and is easy to maintain, which is probably why it has lasted."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"A Phone-Free Morning Walk","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-19","_displayNo":"CORE 19","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":19,"outline":{"topic":"A Phone-Free Morning Walk","intro":"positive habit • phone-free morning walk • about 30 minutes","body":["started after checking messages immediately on waking","phone stays in pocket • quiet thinking • notice surroundings","clearer head • fewer reactive notifications • easy and free to maintain"],"conclusion":"mental transition from sleep to work • sustainable routine"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-19.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A positive habit I have developed is taking a morning walk without using my phone. I started because I noticed I was checking messages almost as soon as I woke up. Nothing was urgent, but I already felt busy before the day had really started.\n\nI decided to walk for about half an hour with my phone left in my pocket. At first, it felt strange not to fill the time with information. After a week or two, I began enjoying the quiet. I could think about my plans, notice my surroundings and sometimes find answers to small problems.\n\nThe main benefit is a clearer mind before work. I do not feel as much need to react to every notification immediately. Walking also gives me some exercise, but the mental break matters most. This habit costs nothing and needs no special equipment. Because it is easy to fit into daily life, I have managed to keep doing it.","html":"A positive habit I have developed is taking a morning walk without using my phone. I started because I noticed I was checking messages almost as soon as I woke up. Nothing was urgent, but I already felt busy before the day had really started.\n\nI decided to walk for about half an hour with my phone left in my pocket. At first, it felt strange not to fill the time with information. After a week or two, I began enjoying the quiet. I could think about my plans, notice my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">surroundings</strong> and sometimes find answers to small problems.\n\nThe main benefit is a clearer mind before work. I do not feel as much need to react to every <strong class=\"key-phrase\">notification</strong> immediately. Walking also gives me some exercise, but the mental break matters most. This habit costs nothing and needs no special equipment. Because it is easy to fit into daily life, I have managed to keep doing it.","phrases":[{"en":"surroundings","meaning":"The places and things around you."},{"en":"notification","meaning":"A message or alert sent by a device or app."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-19.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An AI Language Assistant","prompt":"Describe a piece of technology that you find useful.\nYou should say:\n- what it is\n- when you started using it\n- what you use it for\n- and explain why you find it useful.","answer":"A piece of technology I find extremely useful is an AI language assistant that I use for writing, speaking practice and quick research.\n\nI started using this kind of tool when generative AI became widely available. At first, I treated it like a faster search box, but I soon realised that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">its real value was interaction</strong>. I could ask a vague question, challenge the answer, request a simpler explanation and then test my understanding in the same conversation.\n\nFor language learning, I use it to generate speaking questions, analyse awkward sentences and suggest <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more natural alternatives</strong>. I also ask it to compare two versions of a paragraph and explain why one is clearer. That is more useful than simply receiving a corrected sentence because it helps me understand <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the underlying pattern</strong>.\n\nI find the technology useful because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduces the friction</strong> between having a question and exploring it properly. However, I do not treat every answer as automatically correct. I still check important facts and make my own decisions. Used that way, AI is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not a replacement for thinking</strong>; it is a tool that can make deliberate practice much faster and more focused.","topic":"Core Story · Technology","phrases":[{"en":"its real value was interaction","context":"At first, I treated it like a faster search box, but I soon realised that its real value was interaction."},{"en":"more natural alternatives","context":"For language learning, I use it to generate speaking questions, analyse awkward sentences and suggest more natural alternatives."},{"en":"the underlying pattern","context":"That is more useful than simply receiving a corrected sentence because it helps me understand the underlying pattern."},{"en":"reduces the friction","context":"I find the technology useful because it reduces the friction between having a question and exploring it properly."},{"en":"not a replacement for thinking","context":"Used that way, AI is not a replacement for thinking; it is a tool that can make deliberate practice much faster and more focused."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"An AI Language Assistant","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-20","_displayNo":"CORE 20","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":20,"outline":{"topic":"An AI Language Assistant","intro":"useful technology • AI language assistant","body":["started with generative AI • search tool → interactive learning","speaking questions • sentence analysis • natural alternatives • paragraph comparison","fast feedback • understand patterns • verify important facts"],"conclusion":"thinking partner, not replacement • faster deliberate practice"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-20.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A useful piece of technology is an AI language assistant. I use it for writing, speaking practice and finding explanations. I started when these tools became widely available. At first, I treated it like a faster search engine, but later I found the conversation itself more useful.\n\nI can ask a question, request a simpler answer and check whether I have understood. For English, I ask for speaking questions, explanations of awkward sentences and more natural expressions. Comparing two paragraphs also helps me see why one is clearer, rather than only receiving a correction.\n\nI like it because I can explore a question straight away. However, I do not assume every answer is right. I still check important facts and make my own decisions. Used carefully, it makes practice quicker and more focused. For me, its best purpose is helping me think and learn more clearly, not doing all the thinking for me.","html":"A useful piece of technology is an AI language assistant. I use it for writing, speaking practice and finding explanations. I started when these tools became widely available. At first, I treated it like a faster search engine, but later I found the conversation itself more useful.\n\nI can ask a question, request a simpler answer and check whether I have understood. For English, I ask for speaking questions, explanations of awkward sentences and more natural expressions. Comparing two paragraphs also helps me see why one is clearer, rather than only receiving a correction.\n\nI like it because I can explore a question <strong class=\"key-phrase\">straight away</strong>. However, I do not assume every answer is right. I still check important facts and make my own decisions. Used carefully, it makes practice quicker and more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">focused</strong>. For me, its best purpose is helping me think and learn more clearly, not doing all the thinking for me.","phrases":[{"en":"straight away","meaning":"Immediately, without waiting."},{"en":"focused","meaning":"Giving clear attention to a particular purpose."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-20.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A car journey you went on","prompt":"Describe a car journey you went on.\nYou should say:\n- Where did you go;\n- What did you do there;\n- Who you went there with;\n- Why you wanted to go there for a car journey;\n- and how you felt about it.","answer":"I'd like to talk about a car journey I took with my best friend about three years ago, just after I'd graduated from university. It was the middle of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">summer vacation</strong>, and we decided on the spur of the moment to drive down to Nha Trang, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a coastal city</strong> on the south-central coast of Vietnam. Because it's so far from my hometown, we knew we were facing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a long drive</strong>, so we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set out</strong> really early in the morning and chose to go by car rather than by motorbike, just to be safe.\n\nAfter about five hours on the road, we finally arrived. The beaches there were stunning - fine white sand and clear, warm water, exactly what we'd been picturing the whole drive. We kept ourselves busy for the next few days: eating out most nights, swimming whenever the weather allowed, and just wandering around the city. We ended up staying with my uncle's family for three nights, and we lent a hand with a few jobs around their house before setting off home on the last day.\n\nHonestly, I'd been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">under the impression that</strong> the trip might turn out a bit dull, since it was just the two of us and I didn't know many people down there. But it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be</strong> one of the best trips I've had - we came away knowing so much more about country life and how differently people live outside the city. If I get the chance again, I'd jump at doing another trip like that.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"summer vacation","context":"It was the middle of summer vacation, and we decided on the spur of the moment to drive down to Nha Trang, a coastal city on the south-central coast of Vietnam."},{"en":"a coastal city","context":"It was the middle of summer vacation, and we decided on the spur of the moment to drive down to Nha Trang, a coastal city on the south-central coast of Vietnam."},{"en":"set out","context":"Because it's so far from my hometown, we knew we were facing a long drive, so we set out really early in the morning and chose to go by car rather than by motorbike, just to be safe."},{"en":"a long drive","context":"Because it's so far from my hometown, we knew we were facing a long drive, so we set out really early in the morning and chose to go by car rather than by motorbike, just to be safe."},{"en":"under the impression that","context":"Honestly, I'd been under the impression that the trip might turn out a bit dull, since it was just the two of us and I didn't know many people down there."},{"en":"turned out to be","context":"But it turned out to be one of the best trips I've had - we came away knowing so much more about country life and how differently people live outside the city."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a car journey you went on","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-112","_displayNo":"#001","outline":{"topic":"Nha Trang road trip","intro":"story car journey took part in","body":["middle summer vacation best friend decided go trip Nha Trang coastal","amazed see beautiful beaches w/ fine clean sand well clear ocean","stayed three nights w/ uncle's family helped w/ regular tasks before","However turned out amazing one helped learn countryside way life live"],"conclusion":"opportunities future love make trips like that"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-112.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A car journey I remember was a trip to Nha Trang with my best friend about three years ago. We had just graduated from university and decided to travel during the summer holiday. We left early because it was a long way from home.\n\nThe drive took about five hours. When we arrived, we were pleased to see clean beaches, white sand and clear water. We stayed with my uncle's family for three nights. During the day, we went swimming, explored the city and tried local food. We also helped my uncle's family with a few jobs around the house before returning home.\n\nBefore leaving, I had worried that travelling with just one friend might be boring. In fact, the trip was much better than I expected. We had time to talk properly and learn about a different way of life outside our usual surroundings. Going by car also made the journey itself part of the holiday. I would happily take another trip like it if we both had enough free time.","html":"A car journey I remember was a trip to Nha Trang with my best friend about three years ago. We had just graduated from university and decided to travel during the summer holiday. We left early because it was a long way from home.\n\nThe drive took about five hours. When we arrived, we were pleased to see clean beaches, white sand and clear water. We stayed with my uncle's family for three nights. During the day, we went swimming, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">explored the city</strong> and tried local food. We also helped my uncle's family with a few jobs around the house before returning home.\n\nBefore leaving, I had worried that travelling with just one friend might be boring. In fact, the trip was much better than I expected. We had time to talk properly and learn about a different way of life outside our <strong class=\"key-phrase\">usual surroundings</strong>. Going by car also made the journey itself part of the holiday. I would happily take another trip like it if we both had enough free time.","phrases":[{"en":"explored the city","meaning":"Travelled around the city to discover different places."},{"en":"usual surroundings","meaning":"The places and conditions that someone normally experiences."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-112.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A future aim","prompt":"Describe a future aim.\nYou should say:\n- what the aim is\n- when you hope to achieve it\n- what you need to do to reach your goal\n- and why this goal is important to you.","answer":"I'm going to describe a future aim of mine, which is to write a book. I'd love to write a novel - something fictional, but probably <strong class=\"key-phrase\">be influenced by</strong> a lot of my own experiences, ideas and opinions. The trouble is, I honestly have no idea where I'd start or what it would even be about, so realistically I don't think I'll achieve this until much later in life.\n\nIt might end up being something I take up as a hobby once I retire, or maybe I'll suddenly get inspired and start much sooner than that - right now it's really just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a vague objective</strong> in the back of my mind. I imagine it must be incredibly hard to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">find the motivation to write a novel</strong>, so more than anything, I'd need time, commitment and enough passion to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep working until</strong> I actually finished it.\n\nI'd also need to set myself a target, like writing for a fixed amount of time every day, and of course I'd need a decent idea for a story in the first place! What draws me to this goal is that I've always wondered how people manage to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bring a story to life</strong> out of nothing, and whether I'd be capable of doing the same. I've always loved reading, so I'd really like to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">follow in the footsteps of</strong> my favourite authors one day.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"be influenced by","context":"I'd love to write a novel - something fictional, but probably be influenced by a lot of my own experiences, ideas and opinions."},{"en":"a vague objective","context":"It might end up being something I take up as a hobby once I retire, or maybe I'll suddenly get inspired and start much sooner than that - right now it's really just a vague objective in the back of my mind."},{"en":"find the motivation to write a novel","context":"I imagine it must be incredibly hard to find the motivation to write a novel, so more than anything, I'd need time, commitment and enough passion to keep working until I actually finished it."},{"en":"keep working until","context":"I imagine it must be incredibly hard to find the motivation to write a novel, so more than anything, I'd need time, commitment and enough passion to keep working until I actually finished it."},{"en":"bring a story to life","context":"What draws me to this goal is that I've always wondered how people manage to bring a story to life out of nothing, and whether I'd be capable of doing the same."},{"en":"follow in the footsteps of","context":"I've always loved reading, so I'd really like to follow in the footsteps of my favourite authors one day."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a future aim","id":"speaking-part-2-192","_displayNo":"#002","outline":{"topic":"write a book","intro":"going describe future aim write book","body":["Unfortunately no idea start novel cannot imagine achieving aim until later","Maybe something hobby retire suddenly inspired start writing sooner","think extremely difficult find motivation write novel anything need time commitment"],"conclusion":"always enjoyed reading like follow footsteps favourite authors"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-192.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A future aim of mine is to write a book, probably a novel. I enjoy reading and have often wondered whether I could create a story that other people would want to follow. I imagine using some of my experiences and ideas while inventing the characters and events.\n\nAt the moment, I do not have a clear plot, so this is a long-term ambition rather than an immediate plan. Perhaps I would begin after retirement, although a good idea might encourage me to start sooner. I know writing a complete novel would require much more than an exciting first chapter.\n\nTo achieve it, I would need a routine, such as writing for a fixed time every day. I would also need patience to continue when the story became difficult or my motivation fell. What attracts me is the challenge of making something from my imagination. My favourite authors have given me many enjoyable hours, and I would like to try creating a similar experience for someone else, even if the book never became widely known.","html":"A future aim of mine is to write a book, probably a novel. I enjoy reading and have often wondered whether I could create a story that other people would want to follow. I imagine using some of my experiences and ideas while inventing the characters and events.\n\nAt the moment, I do not have a clear <strong class=\"key-phrase\">plot</strong>, so this is a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long-term ambition</strong> rather than an immediate plan. Perhaps I would begin after retirement, although a good idea might encourage me to start sooner. I know writing a complete novel would require much more than an exciting first chapter.\n\nTo achieve it, I would need a routine, such as writing for a fixed time every day. I would also need patience to continue when the story became difficult or my motivation fell. What attracts me is the challenge of making something from my imagination. My favourite authors have given me many enjoyable hours, and I would like to try creating a similar experience for someone else, even if the book never became widely known.","phrases":[{"en":"long-term ambition","meaning":"Something important that someone hopes to achieve in the future."},{"en":"plot","meaning":"The main events and structure of a story."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-192.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A holiday or vacation you have been on","prompt":"Describe a holiday or vacation you have been on\nYou should say:\nwhen you had it\nwhere you went\nwhat you did\nand explain how you felt about it","answer":"A few months ago, I went to the south of Vietnam on a family holiday. We went to the lovely coastal city of Nha Trang, which is also a really famous <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tourist destination</strong>. We decided to fly there since it's nearly 1,200 kilometres from Hanoi, and going by car or train would've taken forever.\n\nI was so excited about the trip that I'd packed everything - clothes, camera, all the bits and pieces - <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a week in advance</strong>. The flight only took about an hour, and luckily it left right on time. What I remember most is the view from the window as we took off: the whole city looked incredible from above, and we landed just as this amazing sunset was starting.\n\nOur time in Nha Trang was brilliant. We tried out so many things we'd <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never done before</strong>, like parasailing, surfing, and diving to see the coral reef. The beaches there are honestly some of the best in Vietnam - deep blue water, pearly white sand - and the food was incredible too. But if I had to pick the best moment, it'd be the night we bought fresh seafood from a local market and grilled it ourselves right on the beach. Sitting by the fire with that smoky, freshly barbecued fish smell drifting around us on a breezy night - I don't think I'll ever forget it.\n\nThat trip really was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the holiday of a lifetime</strong> for us - so much fun, and so much incredible scenery. We'd honestly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">go back in a heartbeat</strong> if we ever got the chance.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"tourist destination","context":"We went to the lovely coastal city of Nha Trang, which is also a really famous tourist destination."},{"en":"a week in advance","context":"I was so excited about the trip that I'd packed everything - clothes, camera, all the bits and pieces - a week in advance."},{"en":"never done before","context":"We tried out so many things we'd never done before, like parasailing, surfing, and diving to see the coral reef."},{"en":"the holiday of a lifetime","context":"That trip really was the holiday of a lifetime for us - so much fun, and so much incredible scenery. We'd honestly go back in a heartbeat if we ever got the chance."},{"en":"go back in a heartbeat","context":"That trip really was the holiday of a lifetime for us - so much fun, and so much incredible scenery. We'd honestly go back in a heartbeat if we ever got the chance."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a holiday or vacation you have been on","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-113","_displayNo":"#003","outline":{"topic":"Nha Trang holiday","intro":"few months ago went South Vietnam vacation w/ family","body":["decided go plane nearly 1200 kilometres Hanoi take long time car","trip began w/ incredible view airplane window remember overwhelmed beauty city","think memorable moment trip definitely bought fresh ingredients local market grilled","Sitting fire breezy night w/ delicious smell freshly barbequed fish amazing"],"conclusion":"definitely enjoyed definitely go again chance"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-113.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A holiday I enjoyed was a family trip to Nha Trang a few months ago. We travelled from Hanoi by plane because the distance was almost twelve hundred kilometres. I was so excited that I packed my clothes and camera a week early.\n\nThe flight left on time, and I enjoyed looking down at the city through the window. We arrived as the sun was setting. During the holiday, we tried activities such as surfing, parasailing and diving near coral. I especially liked the clear blue water and white sandy beaches.\n\nMy favourite memory was an evening when we bought fresh seafood at a local market and grilled it on the beach. We sat together near the fire while a cool breeze came from the sea. The food tasted wonderful, but being with my family made the moment special. The trip gave us new experiences and a proper break from our normal routines. We returned home with many photographs and happy memories, and I would love to go back if we had the opportunity.","html":"A holiday I enjoyed was a family trip to Nha Trang a few months ago. We travelled from Hanoi by plane because the distance was almost twelve hundred kilometres. I was so excited that I packed my clothes and camera a week early.\n\nThe flight left on time, and I enjoyed looking down at the city through the window. We arrived as the sun was setting. During the holiday, we tried activities such as surfing, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">parasailing</strong> and diving near coral. I especially liked the clear blue water and white sandy beaches.\n\nMy favourite memory was an evening when we bought fresh seafood at a local market and grilled it on the beach. We sat together near the fire while a cool breeze came from the sea. The food tasted wonderful, but being with my family made the moment special. The trip gave us new experiences and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a proper break</strong> from our normal routines. We returned home with many photographs and happy memories, and I would love to go back if we had the opportunity.","phrases":[{"en":"parasailing","meaning":"Being pulled above the water in a parachute attached to a boat."},{"en":"a proper break","meaning":"Enough time away from normal duties to relax well."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-113.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A holiday you would like to take in the future","prompt":"Describe a holiday you would like to take in the future\nYou should say:\n- When and where you would like to go\n- Who you would like to go with\n- What you would like to do on this holiday\n- Why you would like to take the holiday","answer":"I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have itchy feet</strong>, so there's basically always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a detailed plan</strong> for the next family holiday sitting in the back of my mind. This time, I'm waiting for my kids' <strong class=\"key-phrase\">summer vacation</strong> so the whole family can go away together. I'm a family person at heart, and summer just isn't complete without suntans and beach photos, so it has to be somewhere on the coast - and Vinh Hy Bay is my top choice. A friend of mine raved about it after her own trip there; apparently it's known for its stunning scenery and cheap, fresh seafood.\n\nI've wanted to go for ages but could never find the time, thanks to my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tight working schedule</strong>. This summer, though, we're finally making it happen. It's only about a four-hour drive from our city, so we'll take our time getting there, stopping wherever looks interesting along the way rather than rushing. One thing I love about Vinh Hy is that it's still pretty untouched - it's not overrun with tourists, so the food stays reasonably priced and the beaches stay quiet.\n\nUnlike other beaches that are packed with water sports like canoeing, scuba diving and jet-skiing, there isn't really much to do in Vinh Hy besides sunbathing and eating seafood - but honestly, that suits us fine, since this trip is really just about being together as a family. In case the kids get restless, I'm planning to bring some games, like UNO, and a few sandcastle-building tools to keep them busy on the beach.\n\nI'm honestly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in serious need of</strong> this holiday. I want to escape the stress and pressure of city life for a while with my spouse and kids, and I think it's also a nice way to reward the children after a hard year of studying.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"have itchy feet","context":"I have itchy feet, so there's basically always a detailed plan for the next family holiday sitting in the back of my mind."},{"en":"a detailed plan","context":"I have itchy feet, so there's basically always a detailed plan for the next family holiday sitting in the back of my mind."},{"en":"summer vacation","context":"This time, I'm waiting for my kids' summer vacation so the whole family can go away together."},{"en":"tight working schedule","context":"I've wanted to go for ages but could never find the time, thanks to my tight working schedule."},{"en":"in serious need of","context":"I'm honestly in serious need of this holiday. I want to escape the stress and pressure of city life for a while with my spouse and kids, and I think it's also a nice way to reward the children after a hard year of studying."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a holiday you would like to take in the future","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-114","_displayNo":"#004","outline":{"topic":"family trip to Vinh Hy Bay","intro":"itchy feet always detailed plan next holiday trip mind","body":["time going wait children's summer vacation take whole family trip together","heard place long time ago friend highly recommended place after trip","Unlike beaches offer variety water sports like canoeing scuba-diving waterskiing skydiving","dire need vacation want escape stressful workload pressure city life w/"],"conclusion":"think good idea reward children w/ trip after studying hard"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-114.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A holiday I would like to take is a family trip to Vinh Hy Bay during my children's summer break. A friend recommended it because of its beautiful scenery, quiet beaches and fresh seafood.\n\nIt is about four hours from our city by car. We would drive slowly and stop at interesting places along the way instead of rushing. I have wanted to visit for a long time, but my busy work schedule has made it difficult. This summer seems like a good opportunity for us to go together.\n\nWe would mainly relax on the beach and enjoy meals as a family. I would bring UNO cards and tools for making sandcastles so the children had something to do. We do not need a holiday full of organised activities. The main purpose is to spend time together away from the pressure of city life. I also think it would be a nice reward for the children after a year of studying. I hope we come home feeling rested and closer to one another.","html":"A holiday I would like to take is a family trip to Vinh Hy Bay during my children's summer break. A friend recommended it because of its beautiful scenery, quiet beaches and fresh seafood.\n\nIt is about four hours from our city by car. We would drive slowly and stop at interesting places along the way instead of rushing. I have wanted to visit for a long time, but my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">busy work schedule</strong> has made it difficult. This summer seems like a good opportunity for us to go together.\n\nWe would mainly relax on the beach and enjoy meals as a family. I would bring UNO cards and tools for making sandcastles so the children had something to do. We do not need a holiday full of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">organised activities</strong>. The main purpose is to spend time together away from the pressure of city life. I also think it would be a nice reward for the children after a year of studying. I hope we come home feeling rested and closer to one another.","phrases":[{"en":"busy work schedule","meaning":"A working timetable with many tasks and little spare time."},{"en":"organised activities","meaning":"Planned events or things arranged for people to do."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-114.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A leisure activity near or on the sea","prompt":"Describe a leisure activity near or on the sea\nYou should say:\nWhat it is\nWhat you should prepare\nWhy you have to prepare\nAnd explain how you feel about it.","answer":"I'd like to talk about one of my favourite things to do at the beach - swimming. I live in Hai Phong, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a coastal city</strong>, and the nearest beach is only about fifteen minutes from my house, so in summer my friends and I often head down there to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">swim in the cool water</strong> and escape the heat.\n\nSwimming's actually a pretty easy sport to pick up. I learned when I was about eight, and it only took me a week or so to get the hang of a few strokes - breaststroke, backstroke, that sort of thing. Before I get in the water, I always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">warm up and stretch</strong> properly, just to avoid pulling a muscle, and then it's straight in.\n\nI love swimming at the beach for a few reasons, really. Unlike sports like diving or windsurfing, which need all sorts of expensive gear, swimming doesn't cost a thing. It's also brilliant for your health - it gives you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an all-over body workout</strong> since you're using almost every muscle, and it builds up your stamina, strength and cardiovascular fitness at the same time. On top of that, it's just a great way to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unwind and de-stress</strong>, and I get to spend that time with my mates too.\n\nSummer's coming up again, so I'm already planning a trip to the beach soon. I'm thinking of heading to Nha Trang this time, since it's supposed to be one of the most spectacular beaches in the world.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"a coastal city","context":"I live in Hai Phong, a coastal city, and the nearest beach is only about fifteen minutes from my house, so in summer my friends and I often head down there to swim in the cool water and escape the heat."},{"en":"swim in the cool water","context":"I live in Hai Phong, a coastal city, and the nearest beach is only about fifteen minutes from my house, so in summer my friends and I often head down there to swim in the cool water and escape the heat."},{"en":"warm up and stretch","context":"Before I get in the water, I always warm up and stretch properly, just to avoid pulling a muscle, and then it's straight in."},{"en":"an all-over body workout","context":"It's also brilliant for your health - it gives you an all-over body workout since you're using almost every muscle, and it builds up your stamina, strength and cardiovascular fitness at the same time."},{"en":"unwind and de-stress","context":"On top of that, it's just a great way to unwind and de-stress, and I get to spend that time with my mates too."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a leisure activity near or on the sea","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-115","_displayNo":"#005","outline":{"topic":"swimming","intro":"one favourite activities beach swimming","body":["live Hai Phong coastal city w/ golden beaches nearest seashore fifteen","learned swim 8 y/o took week get familiar w/ number swimming","unlike water sports diving windsurfing require costly equipment swimming costs nothing","Lastly going swimming good means recreation allows unwind alleviate stress great"],"conclusion":"intend go Nha Trang beach it's considered one magnificent beaches"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-115.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An activity I enjoy by the sea is swimming. I live in Hai Phong, and the nearest beach is about fifteen minutes from my house. In summer, my friends and I often go there to cool down and spend time together.\n\nI learned to swim when I was about eight. After some practice, I could use a few different strokes, including breaststroke and backstroke. Before entering the water, I usually do some gentle warm-up exercises. I enjoy the feeling of moving through the sea rather than staying on the hot sand.\n\nOne reason I like swimming is that it does not require much equipment compared with sports such as diving or windsurfing. It also gives my body exercise and helps me relax. Being with friends makes the experience more enjoyable because we can talk before and after swimming. I am thinking about visiting Nha Trang when I have another chance to travel. For me, a beach holiday feels incomplete without some time in the water, provided the conditions are suitable for swimming.","html":"An activity I enjoy by the sea is swimming. I live in Hai Phong, and the nearest beach is about fifteen minutes from my house. In summer, my friends and I often go there to cool down and spend time together.\n\nI learned to swim when I was about eight. After some practice, I could use a few different <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strokes</strong>, including breaststroke and backstroke. Before entering the water, I usually do some gentle warm-up exercises. I enjoy the feeling of moving through the sea rather than staying on the hot sand.\n\nOne reason I like swimming is that it does not require much equipment compared with sports such as diving or windsurfing. It also gives my body exercise and helps me relax. Being with friends makes the experience more enjoyable because we can talk before and after swimming. I am thinking about visiting Nha Trang when I have another chance to travel. For me, a beach holiday feels incomplete without some time in the water, provided the conditions are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suitable for swimming</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"strokes","meaning":"Different ways of moving the arms and legs while swimming."},{"en":"suitable for swimming","meaning":"Safe and appropriate for people to swim in."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-115.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A lesson that you enjoyed","prompt":"Describe a lesson that you enjoyed\nYou should say:\nWhen it was\nWhat it was about\nWhat happened And\nexplain why you liked it","answer":"I'd like to talk about a really enjoyable lesson I had back at university, in one of my English classes.\n\nOur regular teacher was off sick one day, so the school sent in a substitute, a guy called Thomas Hall, who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">delivered an amazing lecture</strong> about how he thought we should all be learning languages by watching films rather than textbooks. For two hours, he explained how films can help you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">immerse yourself in a language</strong> - you pick up vocabulary and grammar naturally just by following the dialogue, and he reckoned that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the long run</strong>, that does more for your overall level than any textbook could.\n\nAt first, we were all a bit sceptical, to be honest, but after watching a short clip and trying a quick test on it, we were pretty much convinced - learning through films wasn't nearly as hard as we'd assumed, and it was something we could actually use to improve at school.\n\nWhat I liked most was that this method genuinely worked for me. I'd always found traditional study a bit demotivating, and my results never really reflected the effort I put in. But once I started watching my favourite films in English instead, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">things clicked</strong>, and my vocabulary grew fast, and I got a lot more confident writing longer essays. I still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put it into practice</strong> now, actually, whenever I've got a new film to watch.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"delivered an amazing lecture","context":"Our regular teacher was off sick one day, so the school sent in a substitute, a guy called Thomas Hall, who delivered an amazing lecture about how he thought we should all be learning languages by watching films rather than textbooks."},{"en":"immerse yourself in a language","context":"For two hours, he explained how films can help you immerse yourself in a language - you pick up vocabulary and grammar naturally just by following the dialogue, and he reckoned that in the long run, that does more for your overall level than any textbook could."},{"en":"in the long run","context":"For two hours, he explained how films can help you immerse yourself in a language - you pick up vocabulary and grammar naturally just by following the dialogue, and he reckoned that in the long run, that does more for your overall level than any textbook could."},{"en":"things clicked","context":"But once I started watching my favourite films in English instead, things clicked, and my vocabulary grew fast, and I got a lot more confident writing longer essays."},{"en":"put it into practice","context":"I still put it into practice now, actually, whenever I've got a new film to watch."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a lesson that you enjoyed","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-116","_displayNo":"#006","outline":{"topic":"English class w/ Thomas Hall","intro":"pleasant experience English class uni","body":["substitute teacher Thomas Hall delivered amazing lecture learn languages via film","1st sceptical ideas introduced however after watching short video practice doing","enjoyed lesson bc find method particularly effective me demotivated by old study methods","once started watching favourite films English things clicked vocab grew fast"],"conclusion":"still put it into practice now whenever there's a new film to watch"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-116.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A lesson I enjoyed was an English class at university. Our normal teacher was ill, so a substitute teacher called Thomas Hall came instead. He spent the lesson explaining how films could help us learn a language.\n\nHe said that following conversations in a film could introduce vocabulary and grammar in a natural context. At first, my classmates and I were doubtful because we were used to studying with textbooks. Then we watched a short clip and answered some questions. The activity was easier and more useful than I had expected.\n\nWhat I liked most was discovering a method that suited my interests. Traditional study sometimes made me lose motivation, but I already enjoyed watching films. After that lesson, I began choosing English versions and paying attention to useful expressions. Over time, I noticed that I understood more words and felt more confident when writing. I still use films alongside other study methods. The lesson was memorable because it changed something I already enjoyed into an extra opportunity to practise English.","html":"A lesson I enjoyed was an English class at university. Our normal teacher was ill, so a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">substitute teacher</strong> called Thomas Hall came instead. He spent the lesson explaining how films could help us learn a language.\n\nHe said that following conversations in a film could introduce vocabulary and grammar in a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">natural context</strong>. At first, my classmates and I were doubtful because we were used to studying with textbooks. Then we watched a short clip and answered some questions. The activity was easier and more useful than I had expected.\n\nWhat I liked most was discovering a method that suited my interests. Traditional study sometimes made me lose motivation, but I already enjoyed watching films. After that lesson, I began choosing English versions and paying attention to useful expressions. Over time, I noticed that I understood more words and felt more confident when writing. I still use films alongside other study methods. The lesson was memorable because it changed something I already enjoyed into an extra opportunity to practise English.","phrases":[{"en":"substitute teacher","meaning":"A teacher who temporarily takes the place of another teacher."},{"en":"natural context","meaning":"A real or realistic situation that helps explain how language is used."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-116.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A long journey you travelled by car","prompt":"Describe a long journey you travelled by car\nYou should say:\nwhat it was\nwhen and where it happened\nwho was involved\nAnd explain if you enjoyed it or not.","answer":"I'll tell you about a long car journey I took last week, driving up from my hometown, Hue, all the way to Hanoi with three close friends.\n\nWe'd planned to leave really early to miss the worst of the traffic, so we all met at my place at 5 a.m. I'm not exactly a morning person, but somehow I managed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get up that early</strong> and be ready on time! We took it in turns driving, and since the weather was gorgeous that day, we drove most of the way with the windows down, blasting music and just chatting. There's something about the fresh air and the quiet countryside rolling past that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made me feel relaxed but energetic</strong> at the same time.\n\nWe didn't actually reach Hanoi until around half six in the evening, and instead of heading straight to where we were staying, we drove a couple of laps around the lake as the sun went down and the streets started to quieten. Then we parked up and grabbed coffee at this little cafe we always go to, just to unwind after being in the car all day.\n\nWhat made the trip so good, honestly, wasn't really Hanoi itself - it was the drive there. Normally I'd just fly or take the train and miss all of it, but going by car meant we could stop wherever we felt like, whether that was a roadside food stall or just some random viewpoint that looked nice. It really let me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hit the open road</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leave my worries behind</strong> for a day, and being stuck in a car together for that long meant we actually talked properly instead of just messaging each other - <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catching up properly</strong> for the first time in ages.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"get up that early","context":"I'm not exactly a morning person, but somehow I managed to get up that early and be ready on time!"},{"en":"made me feel relaxed but energetic","context":"There's something about the fresh air and the quiet countryside rolling past that made me feel relaxed but energetic at the same time."},{"en":"hit the open road","context":"It really let me hit the open road and leave my worries behind for a day, and being stuck in a car together for that long meant we actually talked properly instead of just messaging each other - catching up properly for the first time in ages."},{"en":"leave my worries behind","context":"It really let me hit the open road and leave my worries behind for a day, and being stuck in a car together for that long meant we actually talked properly instead of just messaging each other - catching up properly for the first time in ages."},{"en":"catching up properly","context":"It really let me hit the open road and leave my worries behind for a day, and being stuck in a car together for that long meant we actually talked properly instead of just messaging each other - catching up properly for the first time in ages."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a long journey you travelled by car","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-117","_displayNo":"#007","outline":{"topic":"Hue → Hanoi road trip","intro":"travelled w/ three close friends agreed meet house 5 a.m","body":["like describe long exciting car journey took last week hometown Hue","After driving day arrived Hanoi late around 6 30 p.m. continued","great chance see country different angle road trip w/ mates","car trip helped clear mind bc hit open road leave worries"],"conclusion":"great opportunity friends enjoy other's company exchange stories along way"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-117.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A long car journey I took was from Hue, my hometown, to Hanoi last week. I travelled with three close friends. We met at my home at five in the morning because we wanted to leave before the roads became too busy.\n\nWe shared the driving and enjoyed good weather for most of the journey. We listened to music, talked and watched the countryside pass. Going by car meant we could stop for food or a view whenever we wanted. That freedom made the long distance feel less tiring.\n\nWe arrived in Hanoi at about half past six in the evening. Before going to our accommodation, we spent some time near the lake and stopped at a familiar cafe. Looking back, the journey was as enjoyable as the destination. Usually, I would fly or take a train and miss the smaller places along the way. Being together in the car also gave us a chance to catch up properly. It felt good to put everyday worries aside and spend a whole day with friends.","html":"A long car journey I took was from Hue, my hometown, to Hanoi last week. I travelled with three close friends. We met at my home at five in the morning because we wanted to leave before the roads became too busy.\n\nWe shared the driving and enjoyed good weather for most of the journey. We listened to music, talked and watched the countryside pass. Going by car meant we could stop for food or a view whenever we wanted. That freedom made the long distance feel less tiring.\n\nWe arrived in Hanoi at about half past six in the evening. Before going to our accommodation, we spent some time near the lake and stopped at a familiar cafe. Looking back, the journey was as enjoyable as the destination. Usually, I would fly or take a train and miss the smaller places along the way. Being together in the car also gave us a chance to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catch up properly</strong>. It felt good to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put everyday worries aside</strong> and spend a whole day with friends.","phrases":[{"en":"catch up properly","meaning":"Spend enough time sharing news after not talking much for a while."},{"en":"put everyday worries aside","meaning":"Stop thinking about ordinary problems for a period of time."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-117.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A long walk you had","prompt":"Describe a long walk you had\nYou should say:\nwhere you went to\nwhen you went\nwho you went with\nAnd explain how you felt about this walk.","answer":"I'd like to talk about a long walk I took recently, around Hoan Kiem Lake. I actually do this walk quite often, usually late in the evening and always on my own, whenever I'm feeling <strong class=\"key-phrase\">under pressure</strong> from work or school.\n\nThat night, the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fresh air and tranquil atmosphere</strong> around the lake <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made me feel relaxed</strong> but still quite energetic, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">helped me clear my mind</strong> and get ready for whatever the next day would bring. There's also a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">visible police presence</strong> around that whole area, so I felt completely safe and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">didn't have to worry about being pickpocketed</strong> or anything like that - I could just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">focus on enjoying the walk</strong>.\n\nThere's a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long, spacious pavement</strong> all the way around the lake where people go jogging or walking, so after I'd walked for a while, I just sat down on a bench and took in the atmosphere for a bit - mostly just staring up at the stars, trying to let my mind switch off and unwind.\n\nThe reason I went for that walk in the first place was because I'd just had a huge argument with my best friend - he'd started questioning things about my life that really weren't his business, and it left me feeling pretty awful, to the point where I even started questioning our whole friendship. Everything just felt a bit surreal that evening, so I went out looking for something that would calm me down.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"under pressure","context":"I'd like to talk about a long walk I took recently, around Hoan Kiem Lake. I actually do this walk quite often, usually late in the evening and always on my own, whenever I'm feeling under pressure from work or school."},{"en":"fresh air and tranquil atmosphere","context":"That night, the fresh air and tranquil atmosphere around the lake made me feel relaxed but still quite energetic, and it helped me clear my mind and get ready for whatever the next day would bring."},{"en":"made me feel relaxed","context":"That night, the fresh air and tranquil atmosphere around the lake made me feel relaxed but still quite energetic, and it helped me clear my mind and get ready for whatever the next day would bring."},{"en":"helped me clear my mind","context":"That night, the fresh air and tranquil atmosphere around the lake made me feel relaxed but still quite energetic, and it helped me clear my mind and get ready for whatever the next day would bring."},{"en":"visible police presence","context":"There's also a visible police presence around that whole area, so I felt completely safe and didn't have to worry about being pickpocketed or anything like that - I could just focus on enjoying the walk."},{"en":"didn't have to worry about being pickpocketed","context":"There's also a visible police presence around that whole area, so I felt completely safe and didn't have to worry about being pickpocketed or anything like that - I could just focus on enjoying the walk."},{"en":"focus on enjoying the walk","context":"There's also a visible police presence around that whole area, so I felt completely safe and didn't have to worry about being pickpocketed or anything like that - I could just focus on enjoying the walk."},{"en":"long, spacious pavement","context":"There's a long, spacious pavement all the way around the lake where people go jogging or walking, so after I'd walked for a while, I just sat down on a bench and took in the atmosphere for a bit - mostly just staring up at the stars, trying to let my mind switch off and unwind."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a long walk you had","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-118","_displayNo":"#008","outline":{"topic":"walk around Hoan Kiem Lake","intro":"long walk took recently","body":["night fresh air tranquil atmosphere around lake made feel relaxed energetic","visible police presence around area made feel safe not worry pickpocketed","Around lake long spacious pavement people usually go jogging walking after"],"conclusion":"Things started feeling bizarre went looking something calm mind"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-118.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A long walk I remember was around Hoan Kiem Lake one evening. I often walk there alone when work or study makes me feel stressed, but that particular night I had also argued with my best friend.\n\nHe had questioned personal decisions that I felt were not his business. The conversation upset me enough to make me think about our friendship. Rather than stay at home feeling angry, I went out for some fresh air.\n\nThe lake was peaceful, with a wide pavement and space to walk without rushing. There were police nearby, which helped me feel comfortable in the area. After walking for a while, I sat on a bench and looked at the stars. I did not solve every problem that evening, but the quiet surroundings helped me calm down and think more clearly. By the time I returned home, I felt better prepared for the next day. The experience reminded me that a simple walk can create useful distance between a difficult conversation and my reaction to it.","html":"A long walk I remember was around Hoan Kiem Lake one evening. I often walk there alone when work or study makes me feel stressed, but that particular night I had also argued with my best friend.\n\nHe had questioned personal decisions that I felt were not his business. The conversation upset me enough to make me think about our friendship. Rather than stay at home feeling angry, I went out for some fresh air.\n\nThe lake was peaceful, with a wide pavement and space to walk without rushing. There were police nearby, which helped me feel comfortable in the area. After walking for a while, I sat on a bench and looked at the stars. I did not solve every problem that evening, but the quiet surroundings helped me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">calm down</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">think more clearly</strong>. By the time I returned home, I felt better prepared for the next day. The experience reminded me that a simple walk can create useful distance between a difficult conversation and my reaction to it.","phrases":[{"en":"calm down","meaning":"Become less angry, worried or excited."},{"en":"think more clearly","meaning":"Understand a situation with a calmer and more organised mind."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-118.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A new skill you would like to learn","prompt":"Describe a new skill you would like to learn\nYou should say:\nWhat it is\nWho you want to learn it from\nHow you are going to learn it\nAnd explain why you want to learn it","answer":"Today I'd like to talk about a skill I've <strong class=\"key-phrase\">always desired to learn</strong>, which is speaking German.\n\nA couple of years ago I went on a fantastic trip to Europe, and I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deeply impressed by</strong> the beauty and style of Berlin. While I was there, I spent ages <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wandering around</strong> different shopping malls trying to find my dad his favourite black Nike coat, and I finally tracked one down in a place called Kaufland. The only problem was, when I got home, I realised I'd bought the women's size by mistake! That's actually the moment I decided to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">try my hand at</strong> learning German - I figured if I understood the language a bit better, I wouldn't make silly mistakes like that again.\n\nGerman appeals to me for a few reasons. For one thing, it's an official language in Germany, Austria and Switzerland, so it'd genuinely be useful across quite a few countries. It <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bears a close resemblance to</strong> English too - they're both Germanic languages, so a lot of the vocabulary feels oddly familiar, which should make it easier to pick up. And if I get good enough, it could even open the door to studying in Germany one day.\n\nI also just think learning another language is genuinely useful, whether that's for travelling or just feeling more confident generally. As for how I'd actually go about it, I'd probably start with an app to build up the basics, then look for a proper tutor or an evening class once I've got the fundamentals down. So sometime soon, once work and study calm down a bit, I'd love to finally give it a go.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"always desired to learn","context":"Today I'd like to talk about a skill I've always desired to learn, which is speaking German."},{"en":"deeply impressed by","context":"A couple of years ago I went on a fantastic trip to Europe, and I was deeply impressed by the beauty and style of Berlin."},{"en":"wandering around","context":"While I was there, I spent ages wandering around different shopping malls trying to find my dad his favourite black Nike coat, and I finally tracked one down in a place called Kaufland."},{"en":"try my hand at","context":"That's actually the moment I decided to try my hand at learning German - I figured if I understood the language a bit better, I wouldn't make silly mistakes like that again."},{"en":"bears a close resemblance to","context":"It bears a close resemblance to English too - they're both Germanic languages, so a lot of the vocabulary feels oddly familiar, which should make it easier to pick up."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a new skill you would like to learn","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-119","_displayNo":"#009","outline":{"topic":"learn German","intro":"skill always desired learn speaking German","body":["couple years ago fantastic trip Europe deeply impressed beauty uniqueness modern","1st reason makes German attractive language learn pretty 2nd common language","secondly German bears close resemblance English shares pretty same alphabet think","think learning new language handy travelling make decisive bring multitudes cognitive"],"conclusion":"sometime near future handle work study load love give learning"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-119.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A skill I would like to learn is speaking German. I became interested after travelling in Europe a couple of years ago. I especially liked Berlin, but one shopping mistake made me realise the value of understanding the language.\n\nI wanted to buy my father a black Nike coat. After searching several shops, I found one at Kaufland. Unfortunately, when I got home, I discovered that I had bought a women's size. I thought better German might have helped me understand the information before paying.\n\nThe language would also be useful beyond shopping. It is spoken in several European countries, and learning it could make travel easier. If I became good enough, I might consider studying in Germany. I would probably start with an app to learn basic words and then join a class or find a tutor. Some vocabulary seems similar to English, although I know the grammar would take effort. I want to learn because it could turn an enjoyable travel experience into a longer-term personal and educational opportunity.","html":"A skill I would like to learn is speaking German. I became interested after travelling in Europe a couple of years ago. I especially liked Berlin, but one shopping mistake made me realise the value of understanding the language.\n\nI wanted to buy my father a black Nike coat. After searching several shops, I found one at Kaufland. Unfortunately, when I got home, I discovered that I had bought a women's size. I thought better German might have helped me understand the information before paying.\n\nThe language would also be useful beyond shopping. It is spoken in several European countries, and learning it could make travel easier. If I became good enough, I might consider studying in Germany. I would probably start with an app to learn basic words and then join a class or find a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tutor</strong>. Some vocabulary seems similar to English, although I know the grammar would take effort. I want to learn because it could turn an enjoyable travel experience into a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">longer-term</strong> personal and educational opportunity.","phrases":[{"en":"tutor","meaning":"A teacher who gives lessons to one person or a small group."},{"en":"longer-term","meaning":"Continuing or being useful over a longer period of time."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-119.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A plan","prompt":"Describe a plan\nYou should say:\nWhat the plan is/was\nWhen you thought of the plan\nWho is involved in the plan\nAnd say how you did/think you will achieve the plan","answer":"I just turned eighteen this year, so I've been thinking it's about time I planned something a bit special. There are loads of things I could do, but the plan I'm most excited about right now is taking a trip to Singapore.\n\nI came up with the idea right after finishing my university entrance exams back in June, and three of my closest friends are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keen to come along</strong> too. After twelve years of nonstop studying, we all felt that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taking a break is really essential</strong>. We settled on Singapore because it's such a clean, modern city, and it's also just a short flight from Vietnam.\n\nThe tricky part is that we're technically only just adults, so it might take a bit of convincing to get our parents on board. First, we've decided to book an organised tour rather than travel independently, since that'll <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put our parents' minds at ease</strong> - even if it does mean a bit of a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lack of spontaneous activities</strong> along the way. Price matters too: if the tour's too expensive, I doubt our parents will agree to it at all, so finding something affordable is a must.\n\nOn top of that, we'll need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">book our plane tickets in advance</strong>, both to get a decent price and to make sure everything's properly sorted before we go. Once we're actually there, I think we'll just try to be sensible tourists - listen to the tour guide, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay out of trouble</strong>, that sort of thing. So that's the plan, really - our reward for twelve years of hard work.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"keen to come along","context":"I came up with the idea right after finishing my university entrance exams back in June, and three of my closest friends are keen to come along too."},{"en":"taking a break is really essential","context":"After twelve years of nonstop studying, we all felt that taking a break is really essential."},{"en":"put our parents' minds at ease","context":"First, we've decided to book an organised tour rather than travel independently, since that'll put our parents' minds at ease - even if it does mean a bit of a lack of spontaneous activities along the way."},{"en":"lack of spontaneous activities","context":"First, we've decided to book an organised tour rather than travel independently, since that'll put our parents' minds at ease - even if it does mean a bit of a lack of spontaneous activities along the way."},{"en":"book our plane tickets in advance","context":"On top of that, we'll need to book our plane tickets in advance, both to get a decent price and to make sure everything's properly sorted before we go."},{"en":"stay out of trouble","context":"Once we're actually there, I think we'll just try to be sensible tourists - listen to the tour guide, stay out of trouble, that sort of thing."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a plan","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-195","_displayNo":"#010","outline":{"topic":"Singapore trip w/ friends","intro":"things one plans find intriguing go travelling Singapore","body":["came up w/ plan after finished uni entrance exam June three","think decided Singapore bc it's modern city w/ clean environment not","it's too expensive afraid parents not let go either choosing affordable","reach Singapore think polite tourists listen carefully tour guide avoid doing"],"conclusion":"that's great plan achieve much-needed vacation"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-195.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A plan I am excited about is travelling to Singapore with three close friends. I turned eighteen this year, and the idea came after we finished our university entrance exams in June. We felt we deserved a break after many years of school.\n\nWe chose Singapore because it seems clean, modern and convenient to reach from Vietnam. However, we still need our parents to feel comfortable with the idea. To make the trip easier to approve, we are looking for an organised tour instead of arranging every part ourselves.\n\nPrice is important, so we need an affordable package and reasonably priced flights. Booking early should also give us time to prepare properly. Once there, we would follow the guide and behave responsibly rather than treating the holiday as complete freedom from every rule. I know a tour may offer less flexibility, but it seems a sensible choice for our first trip as young adults. I hope the plan works because it would be both a reward for studying and a chance to share a new experience with friends.","html":"A plan I am excited about is travelling to Singapore with three close friends. I turned eighteen this year, and the idea came after we finished our university entrance exams in June. We felt we deserved a break after many years of school.\n\nWe chose Singapore because it seems clean, modern and convenient to reach from Vietnam. However, we still need our parents to feel comfortable with the idea. To make the trip easier to approve, we are looking for an organised tour instead of arranging every part ourselves.\n\nPrice is important, so we need an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">affordable package</strong> and reasonably priced flights. Booking early should also give us time to prepare properly. Once there, we would follow the guide and behave responsibly rather than treating the holiday as complete freedom from every rule. I know a tour may offer less <strong class=\"key-phrase\">flexibility</strong>, but it seems a sensible choice for our first trip as young adults. I hope the plan works because it would be both a reward for studying and a chance to share a new experience with friends.","phrases":[{"en":"affordable package","meaning":"A group of travel services sold together at a price someone can manage."},{"en":"flexibility","meaning":"The freedom to change plans or choose different options."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-195.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A project or a piece of work you did together with someone","prompt":"Describe a project or a piece of work you did together with someone\nYou should say:\n- What it was\n- What you have done\n- Why you have chosen this person(s) to work with\nAnd explain what you did to finish the project.","answer":"I'm going to talk about a project I did with my younger sister that I've always been quite proud of - organising my mum's fiftieth birthday party last year.\n\nTo be honest, I'm not usually much of a team player, and most group projects I've been part of end in some kind of disaster, so when my sister, Khanh, first suggested throwing mum a surprise party, I couldn't have cared less. But Khanh was so determined that she basically forced me to help - and to be fair, she's always been far more organised about that kind of thing than I am. I hesitated at first, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on second thoughts</strong>, I was curious to see mum's reaction the moment she'd <strong class=\"key-phrase\">be taken by surprise</strong>, so I agreed to get involved.\n\nAt first, I wanted to take the easy way out and just buy mum a gift, but Khanh wasn't having it - she told me to help her make a video of our memories together instead, and I couldn't really argue.\n\nWhen mum's birthday came around, she had no idea what was going on, since we'd kept it secret. The moment she walked in, she was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taken aback by</strong> a note telling her to go straight to the kitchen - we'd turned off every light, so she had to feel her way there in the dark. Once we played the video and her favourite song, she stood still for a second, then burst into tears of joy.\n\nIn the end, the whole thing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be</strong> a real success, and on top of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of accomplishment</strong>, I felt genuinely thankful to see mum that happy for the first time in ages.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"on second thoughts","context":"I hesitated at first, but on second thoughts, I was curious to see mum's reaction the moment she'd be taken by surprise, so I agreed to get involved."},{"en":"be taken by surprise","context":"I hesitated at first, but on second thoughts, I was curious to see mum's reaction the moment she'd be taken by surprise, so I agreed to get involved."},{"en":"taken aback by","context":"The moment she walked in, she was taken aback by a note telling her to go straight to the kitchen - we'd turned off every light, so she had to feel her way there in the dark."},{"en":"turned out to be","context":"In the end, the whole thing turned out to be a real success, and on top of a sense of accomplishment, I felt genuinely thankful to see mum that happy for the first time in ages."},{"en":"a sense of accomplishment","context":"In the end, the whole thing turned out to be a real success, and on top of a sense of accomplishment, I felt genuinely thankful to see mum that happy for the first time in ages."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a project or a piece of work you did together with someone","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-120","_displayNo":"#011","outline":{"topic":"mum's 50th birthday project","intro":"project w/ younger sister always taken pride in","body":["That's couldn't care less little girl brought up idea throwing surprise","hesitate course 2nd thoughts curious mother's reaction moment taken surprise","rather told make mother video full memories together couldn't anything follow","lights house turned off carefully make way there"],"conclusion":"After plan turned out success besides sense accomplishment felt thankful"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-120.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A project I completed with someone was organising my mother's fiftieth birthday last year. I worked with my younger sister, Khanh. She suggested a surprise party and persuaded me to help, although I was not very enthusiastic at first.\n\nMy first idea was simply to buy a present. However, Khanh wanted us to make a video using family memories. She was more organised than me, so she helped plan the tasks while we collected material and prepared the surprise. We kept everything secret from our mother.\n\nOn her birthday, we left a note directing her to the kitchen and turned off the lights. When she arrived, we played the video with her favourite song. She stood still for a moment and then began crying with happiness. Seeing her reaction made all the preparation worthwhile. I felt proud of what my sister and I had achieved together. The project showed me that working with another person can produce something more meaningful than taking the quickest and easiest option alone.","html":"A project I completed with someone was organising my mother's fiftieth birthday last year. I worked with my younger sister, Khanh. She suggested a surprise party and persuaded me to help, although I was not very enthusiastic at first.\n\nMy first idea was simply to buy a present. However, Khanh wanted us to make a video using family memories. She was more organised than me, so she helped plan the tasks while we collected material and prepared the surprise. We kept everything secret from our mother.\n\nOn her birthday, we left a note directing her to the kitchen and turned off the lights. When she arrived, we played the video with her favourite song. She stood still for a moment and then began crying with happiness. Seeing her reaction <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made all the preparation worthwhile</strong>. I felt proud of what my sister and I had achieved together. The project showed me that working with another person can produce something more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meaningful</strong> than taking the quickest and easiest option alone.","phrases":[{"en":"made all the preparation worthwhile","meaning":"Made the result good enough to justify the time and effort spent."},{"en":"meaningful","meaning":"Having personal importance or a special purpose."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-120.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A short trip that was special to you","prompt":"Describe a short trip that was special to you\nYou should say:\nwhen it was\nwhere you went\nwho you went with\nAnd explain why it was special.","answer":"I'd like to describe a short bike ride I went on last Saturday with three friends, around Hoan Kiem Lake - or Sword Lake, as it's also known - right in the middle of Hanoi.\n\nWe met at the Ly Thai To statue at half past five, while it was still pretty dark, and cycled a full loop of the lake a few times before the sun properly came up. What I remember most is how quiet and misty it was - there were already a couple of photographers set up, taking pictures of women in ao dai by the water, which I thought was such a lovely scene to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stumble across</strong> that early. Around six, we stopped at a street stall for banh mi and just sat on the kerb eating breakfast while the city slowly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">woke up around us</strong>.\n\nI really enjoyed that ride for quite a few reasons. Mainly, it let me see a side of Hanoi I never usually notice - no traffic, no noise, just the lake and a few other <strong class=\"key-phrase\">early risers</strong> out jogging or doing tai chi. It was also a nice bit of exercise <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without it feeling like a chore</strong>, since we were chatting the whole way round instead of really thinking about the effort. And once the streets did start filling up with traffic around seven, we cycled home a completely different way, just to see a few streets none of us had really paid attention to before.\n\nIt wasn't a long trip by any means, maybe two hours all together, but it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuck with me</strong> as one of my favourite mornings this year - sometimes the simplest plans end up being the ones you remember best.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"stumble across","context":"What I remember most is how quiet and misty it was - there were already a couple of photographers set up, taking pictures of women in ao dai by the water, which I thought was such a lovely scene to stumble across that early."},{"en":"woke up around us","context":"Around six, we stopped at a street stall for banh mi and just sat on the kerb eating breakfast while the city slowly woke up around us."},{"en":"early risers","context":"Mainly, it let me see a side of Hanoi I never usually notice - no traffic, no noise, just the lake and a few other early risers out jogging or doing tai chi."},{"en":"without it feeling like a chore","context":"It was also a nice bit of exercise without it feeling like a chore, since we were chatting the whole way round instead of really thinking about the effort."},{"en":"stuck with me","context":"It wasn't a long trip by any means, maybe two hours all together, but it's stuck with me as one of my favourite mornings this year - sometimes the simplest plans end up being the ones you remember best."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a short trip that was special to you","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-121","_displayNo":"#012","outline":{"topic":"cycle around Sword Lake","intro":"short bike ride last Saturday w/ three friends around Hoan Kiem/Sword Lake","body":["met Ly Thai To statue 5:30am still dark, looped lake before sunrise","misty quiet, photographers taking ao dai photos by water, stopped for banh mi on kerb","loved seeing Hanoi w/o traffic/noise, easy exercise while chatting, not a chore","cycled home different route once streets got busy, saw streets never noticed before"],"conclusion":"short trip ~2hrs but stuck with me as favourite morning this year"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-121.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A short trip that was special to me was a bike ride around Hoan Kiem Lake last Saturday. I went with three friends, and we met at the Ly Thai To statue at half past five in the morning.\n\nIt was still dark and a little misty when we started. We cycled around the lake several times while the city was quiet. Some photographers were taking pictures of women wearing ao dai by the water. Around six, we stopped at a street stall for banh mi and ate breakfast together.\n\nI liked the trip because it showed me a calmer side of Hanoi. There was little traffic, and we could enjoy the view while chatting. The exercise did not feel like hard work because we were having fun. As the streets became busier around seven, we cycled home by a different route and noticed places we usually ignored. The whole outing lasted only about two hours, but it became one of my favourite mornings of the year. It reminded me that a memorable trip does not have to be long or expensive.","html":"A short trip that was special to me was a bike ride around Hoan Kiem Lake last Saturday. I went with three friends, and we met at the Ly Thai To statue at half past five in the morning.\n\nIt was still dark and a little misty when we started. We cycled around the lake several times while the city was quiet. Some photographers were taking pictures of women wearing ao dai by the water. Around six, we stopped at a street stall for banh mi and ate breakfast together.\n\nI liked the trip because it showed me a calmer side of Hanoi. There was little traffic, and we could enjoy the view while chatting. The exercise did not feel like hard work because we were having fun. As the streets became busier around seven, we cycled home by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a different route</strong> and noticed places we usually ignored. The whole <strong class=\"key-phrase\">outing</strong> lasted only about two hours, but it became one of my favourite mornings of the year. It reminded me that a memorable trip does not have to be long or expensive.","phrases":[{"en":"outing","meaning":"A short journey or visit made for enjoyment."},{"en":"a different route","meaning":"Another way of travelling between places."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-121.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A special trip in the near future","prompt":"Describe a special trip in the near future\nYou should say:\nWhen it will possibly be\nWhere you would go\nWhat preparation you need for the trip\nAnd explain why this would be a special trip","answer":"Travelling is honestly what I enjoy most in life, and I've already visited most of the well-known places in Vietnam. But nearly all of those trips have been with friends, so this time I really want to do something different and take my parents on a special trip to Thien Cam Beach.\n\nWe're planning to go next June. There's no school for me at that time of year, so it should be pretty easy to organise. My parents are just as excited as I am - they're <strong class=\"key-phrase\">really looking forward to it</strong>.\n\nThien Cam is actually the largest beach in Vietnam and a really popular <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tourist destination</strong>, famous for its huge waves. It's in the central part of the country, so it's not too far from where we live. Once we get there, besides just relaxing on the beach, we'll probably visit a few other attractions nearby, and I imagine we'll <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pick up some souvenirs</strong> for relatives along the way too.\n\nAs for preparation, we're planning to go by train so we can actually enjoy the scenery on the way rather than just flying over it. We'll obviously need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">book a hotel in advance</strong>, and pack the usual things - swimsuits, sunscreen, summer clothes - plus some basic medicine, just in case anyone gets sick.\n\nThe main reason this trip means so much to me is that my parents will be there, and it'll be their first time visiting Thien Cam. Having them with me will make it genuinely special, because it's been over seven years since we last <strong class=\"key-phrase\">took a holiday together</strong> as a family.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"really looking forward to it","context":"We're planning to go next June. There's no school for me at that time of year, so it should be pretty easy to organise. My parents are just as excited as I am - they're really looking forward to it."},{"en":"tourist destination","context":"Thien Cam is actually the largest beach in Vietnam and a really popular tourist destination, famous for its huge waves."},{"en":"pick up some souvenirs","context":"Once we get there, besides just relaxing on the beach, we'll probably visit a few other attractions nearby, and I imagine we'll pick up some souvenirs for relatives along the way too."},{"en":"book a hotel in advance","context":"We'll obviously need to book a hotel in advance, and pack the usual things - swimsuits, sunscreen, summer clothes - plus some basic medicine, just in case anyone gets sick."},{"en":"took a holiday together","context":"Having them with me will make it genuinely special, because it's been over seven years since we last took a holiday together as a family."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a special trip in the near future","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-122","_displayNo":"#013","outline":{"topic":"Thien Cam Beach trip w/ parents","intro":"Travelling enjoy life now visited famous places Vietnam","body":["However trips always w/ friends now want something different take parents","Thien Cam largest beach Vietnam popular tourist destination famous big waves","arrive aside enjoying beach probably visit tourist attractions around area guess"],"conclusion":"parents w/ make special bc it's 7y took holiday together"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-122.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A special trip I am planning is a visit to Thien Cam Beach with my parents next June. I have travelled to many places with friends, but I have not taken a family holiday with my parents for over seven years.\n\nJune is convenient because I will not have school. We plan to travel by train so we can enjoy the scenery on the way. Before leaving, we will book a hotel and pack summer clothes, swimsuits, sunscreen and the usual things we need for a short holiday.\n\nAt the beach, I would like us to relax, enjoy the sea view and visit a few nearby attractions. We may also buy small souvenirs for relatives. What makes the trip special is not simply the destination. It will be my parents' first visit to Thien Cam, and I want to share that experience with them. We are all looking forward to having time together without our normal responsibilities. I hope the holiday gives us new memories and encourages us to travel as a family more often.","html":"A special trip I am planning is a visit to Thien Cam Beach with my parents next June. I have travelled to many places with friends, but I have not taken a family holiday with my parents for over seven years.\n\nJune is convenient because I will not have school. We plan to travel by train so we can enjoy the scenery on the way. Before leaving, we will book a hotel and pack summer clothes, swimsuits, sunscreen and the usual things we need for a short holiday.\n\nAt the beach, I would like us to relax, enjoy the sea view and visit a few nearby attractions. We may also buy small <strong class=\"key-phrase\">souvenirs</strong> for relatives. What makes the trip special is not simply the destination. It will be my parents' first visit to Thien Cam, and I want to share that experience with them. We are all looking forward to having time together without our <strong class=\"key-phrase\">normal responsibilities</strong>. I hope the holiday gives us new memories and encourages us to travel as a family more often.","phrases":[{"en":"souvenirs","meaning":"Objects bought to remind someone of a place or trip."},{"en":"normal responsibilities","meaning":"The usual duties a person has at work, school or home."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-122.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A team project that you worked on","prompt":"Describe a team project that you worked on.\nYou should say:\n- what it was\n- what you did","answer":"I'd like to talk about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a team project</strong> that I was involved in during my final term at business school. There were four of us on the team, and our task was to work with a local company to research a new market, in a European country, for one of their products or services. Our objective was to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">produce a report and give a presentation</strong>.\n\nThe first thing we did was split into two pairs. We'd been assigned a company that made a range of bicycle accessories, so two of us spent time getting to know the company itself, while the other two researched the market and the competitors in our target country, which was Germany. In the end, I think it was a successful project, because we managed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">identify a possible gap in the market</strong> in Germany for one of the company's products.\n\nOur group presentation went really well too. Up until that point, the course had been all about business theory, so it was quite a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">learning experience</strong> to actually work with a real company. I felt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a real sense of accomplishment</strong> when we handed in the report and gave our presentation, and I think we were all pretty proud of what we'd done.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"a team project","context":"I'd like to talk about a team project that I was involved in during my final term at business school."},{"en":"produce a report and give a presentation","context":"Our objective was to produce a report and give a presentation."},{"en":"identify a possible gap in the market","context":"In the end, I think it was a successful project, because we managed to identify a possible gap in the market in Germany for one of the company's products."},{"en":"learning experience","context":"Up until that point, the course had been all about business theory, so it was quite a learning experience to actually work with a real company."},{"en":"a real sense of accomplishment","context":"I felt a real sense of accomplishment when we handed in the report and gave our presentation, and I think we were all pretty proud of what we'd done."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a team project that you worked on","id":"speaking-part-2-123","_displayNo":"#014","outline":{"topic":"business team project","intro":"team project involved during final term business school","body":["four team task work w/ local company research new market European","assigned company produced range bicycle accessories two spent time getting know","end think successful project bc managed identify possible gap market Germany","Until point course business theory learning experience work w/ real company"],"conclusion":"felt real sense accomplishment handed report delivered presentation think proud"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-123.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A team project I worked on was during my final term at business school. There were four students in our group. We had to help a local company research a possible new market in Germany and then write a report and give a presentation.\n\nThe company made accessories for bicycles. We divided into two pairs to use our time effectively. One pair learned about the business and its products, while the other researched German customers and competing companies. We then compared our findings and discussed possible opportunities.\n\nThe project went well because we identified a product that might meet a need in that market. Our presentation was also successful, and we felt proud when we handed in the report. Before this task, most of our course had focused on business theory. Working with a real company made those ideas easier to understand. I learned that clear roles and regular communication are important in a group. It was satisfying to produce something useful together rather than simply complete another classroom exercise.","html":"A team project I worked on was during my final term at business school. There were four students in our group. We had to help a local company research a possible new market in Germany and then write a report and give a presentation.\n\nThe company made <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accessories</strong> for bicycles. We divided into two pairs to use our time effectively. One pair learned about the business and its products, while the other researched German customers and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">competing companies</strong>. We then compared our findings and discussed possible opportunities.\n\nThe project went well because we identified a product that might meet a need in that market. Our presentation was also successful, and we felt proud when we handed in the report. Before this task, most of our course had focused on business theory. Working with a real company made those ideas easier to understand. I learned that clear roles and regular communication are important in a group. It was satisfying to produce something useful together rather than simply complete another classroom exercise.","phrases":[{"en":"accessories","meaning":"Extra items that are used with a main product."},{"en":"competing companies","meaning":"Businesses that try to sell similar things to the same customers."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-123.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time you travelled by public transport","prompt":"Describe a time you travelled by public transport.\nYou should say:\n- what type of public transport you used\n- where you travelled from and to\n- what you did during the journey\n- and explain how you felt about travelling in this way","answer":"I'm going to describe <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a train journey</strong> that I took last year between Manchester and London. I've made the same journey loads of times, but I'll never forget this particular one, because something happened that I really wasn't expecting. It was a Sunday morning and I was due in London that afternoon. There was no traffic at all as I drove into Manchester, and I was expecting a nice, peaceful trip on a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">half-empty train</strong>.\n\nBut my first surprise came when I tried to print my prepaid ticket at one of the machines: there wasn't a reserved seat number on it, which meant I'd have to find an available seat myself. I was still puzzling over this when I heard loud voices and singing coming from the main station concourse. Suddenly I realised what was going on - I was about to be on a train full of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">football fans</strong> travelling to a big match! Luckily, I managed to find a seat before the train completely filled up.\n\nHowever, the noisy fans made sure I couldn't concentrate on any work - I couldn't even read the newspaper without getting interrupted by laughter, singing and football stories. In the end, I just sat back with a cup of coffee and watched the excited fans instead. All in all, it wasn't a bad journey; I love travelling by train, and at least I had a comfortable seat so I could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sit back and relax</strong> for a couple of hours. I'd have preferred a quieter trip, but the fans were all <strong class=\"key-phrase\">friendly and good-natured</strong>, so I can't really complain.\n\nEven so, I made sure I avoided them on the way home later that day!","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"a train journey","context":"I'm going to describe a train journey that I took last year between Manchester and London."},{"en":"half-empty train","context":"There was no traffic at all as I drove into Manchester, and I was expecting a nice, peaceful trip on a half-empty train."},{"en":"football fans","context":"Suddenly I realised what was going on - I was about to be on a train full of football fans travelling to a big match!"},{"en":"sit back and relax","context":"All in all, it wasn't a bad journey; I love travelling by train, and at least I had a comfortable seat so I could sit back and relax for a couple of hours."},{"en":"friendly and good-natured","context":"I'd have preferred a quieter trip, but the fans were all friendly and good-natured, so I can't really complain."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time you travelled by public transport","id":"speaking-part-2-124","_displayNo":"#015","outline":{"topic":"Manchester → London train","intro":"going describe train journey took last year between Manchester London","body":["made same journey times never forget particular time bc something happened","1st surprise came tried print prepaid ticket one ticket machines not","However noisy fans made sure couldn't concentrate work couldn't read newspaper","preferred quieter trip football fans friendly good-natured cannot complain"],"conclusion":"made sure avoided way home later day"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-124.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A memorable public transport journey was a train trip from Manchester to London last year. It was a Sunday morning, and I expected a quiet journey. I hoped to do some work because I thought the train would be fairly empty.\n\nMy first surprise was discovering that my ticket did not include a reserved seat. Then I heard singing in the station and realised many football supporters were travelling to a match. Luckily, I found a seat before the train filled up.\n\nThe fans talked, laughed and sang throughout the journey. I could not concentrate on work or even read my newspaper properly. Eventually, I gave up, bought coffee and watched what was happening around me. Although it was much noisier than expected, the people were friendly and in a good mood. I still had a comfortable seat, so I could relax for a couple of hours. I would have preferred more peace, but it was not a bad experience. However, I did choose a quieter option for my journey home.","html":"A memorable public transport journey was a train trip from Manchester to London last year. It was a Sunday morning, and I expected a quiet journey. I hoped to do some work because I thought the train would be fairly empty.\n\nMy first surprise was discovering that my ticket did not include a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reserved seat</strong>. Then I heard singing in the station and realised many football supporters were travelling to a match. Luckily, I found a seat before the train filled up.\n\nThe fans talked, laughed and sang throughout the journey. I could not concentrate on work or even read my newspaper properly. Eventually, I gave up, bought coffee and watched what was happening around me. Although it was much noisier than expected, the people were friendly and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in a good mood</strong>. I still had a comfortable seat, so I could relax for a couple of hours. I would have preferred more peace, but it was not a bad experience. However, I did choose a quieter option for my journey home.","phrases":[{"en":"reserved seat","meaning":"A seat kept for a particular passenger in advance."},{"en":"in a good mood","meaning":"Feeling happy and pleasant."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-124.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A trip you took by bike","prompt":"Describe a trip you took by bike\nYou should say:\nWhen and where the trip was\nWho you went with\nWhy you went by bike\nAnd explain how you felt about it.","answer":"I'll tell you about a two-day cycling trip I took along the Mekong Delta with my cousin, back in February this year.\n\nWe flew down to Can Tho and hired bikes for two days, cycling through small villages, past fruit orchards and along the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">narrow paths beside the canals</strong> that the delta is famous for.\n\nWe chose to go by bike specifically because a lot of that area simply isn't accessible by car - the paths between villages are far too narrow, and a lot of local life happens right beside the water, so cycling let us <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get close to things</strong> a bus or taxi never could. It was also just more practical: the distances between villages are short, so a bike was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the perfect speed for actually seeing things</strong> rather than rushing past them.\n\nCycling that slowly meant we could stop whenever something <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caught our eye</strong> - a floating market, someone weaving mats by the roadside, kids swimming in the canal. We ended up talking to a fruit farmer who insisted on giving us free dragon fruit, which became <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the highlight of the whole trip</strong>.\n\nBy the end of the second day my legs were aching and I was completely sunburnt, but I felt like I'd actually experienced the delta rather than just looked at it through a window. Honestly, I don't think I'd have noticed half of what we saw if we'd taken any other form of transport.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"narrow paths beside the canals","context":"We flew down to Can Tho and hired bikes for two days, cycling through small villages, past fruit orchards and along the narrow paths beside the canals that the delta is famous for."},{"en":"get close to things","context":"We chose to go by bike specifically because a lot of that area simply isn't accessible by car - the paths between villages are far too narrow, and a lot of local life happens right beside the water, so cycling let us get close to things a bus or taxi never could."},{"en":"the perfect speed for actually seeing things","context":"It was also just more practical: the distances between villages are short, so a bike was the perfect speed for actually seeing things rather than rushing past them."},{"en":"caught our eye","context":"Cycling that slowly meant we could stop whenever something caught our eye - a floating market, someone weaving mats by the roadside, kids swimming in the canal."},{"en":"the highlight of the whole trip","context":"We ended up talking to a fruit farmer who insisted on giving us free dragon fruit, which became the highlight of the whole trip."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a trip you took by bike","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-125","_displayNo":"#016","outline":{"topic":"Mekong Delta cycling","intro":"2-day cycling trip along Mekong Delta w/ cousin, February this year","body":["flew to Can Tho, hired bikes, cycled through villages, orchards, canal paths","chose bike bc area not accessible by car, paths too narrow","cycling slow enough to stop for floating market, mat weaver, farmer gave free dragon fruit","legs aching + sunburnt by day 2 but felt like really experienced the delta"],"conclusion":"wouldn't have noticed half of it on any other form of transport"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-125.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A bike trip I enjoyed was a two-day ride in the Mekong Delta with my cousin in February. We flew to Can Tho and hired bicycles so we could explore small villages and paths beside the canals.\n\nWe chose bikes because many of those paths were too narrow for cars. Distances between villages were also short, so cycling allowed us to move at a comfortable speed and notice more. We passed fruit gardens, people making mats and children swimming near their homes.\n\nBecause we could stop whenever we wanted, we had several interesting conversations. One fruit farmer gave us dragon fruit without asking for payment. His friendliness became my favourite part of the trip. By the end, my legs ached and I had spent too much time in the sun, but I felt pleased with the experience. Travelling slowly helped me understand local life better than watching from a bus window. I would choose a bicycle again for a place like this, where the small details are an important part of the visit.","html":"A bike trip I enjoyed was a two-day ride in the Mekong Delta with my cousin in February. We flew to Can Tho and hired bicycles so we could explore small villages and paths beside the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">canals</strong>.\n\nWe chose bikes because many of those paths were too narrow for cars. Distances between villages were also short, so cycling allowed us to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">move at a comfortable speed</strong> and notice more. We passed fruit gardens, people making mats and children swimming near their homes.\n\nBecause we could stop whenever we wanted, we had several interesting conversations. One fruit farmer gave us dragon fruit without asking for payment. His friendliness became my favourite part of the trip. By the end, my legs ached and I had spent too much time in the sun, but I felt pleased with the experience. Travelling slowly helped me understand local life better than watching from a bus window. I would choose a bicycle again for a place like this, where the small details are an important part of the visit.","phrases":[{"en":"canals","meaning":"Narrow waterways made or used for boats, drainage or water supply."},{"en":"move at a comfortable speed","meaning":"Travel at a pace that feels easy and manageable."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-125.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An activity you do to keep fit","prompt":"Describe an activity you do to keep fit.\nYou should say:\n- What you do.\n- When and where you usually do it.\n- How you do it.\n- Why it can keep you fit.","answer":"Today I'd like to talk about jogging - my favourite activity for staying healthy. It's basically running at a slow, comfortable pace, and it really helps to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">build up your physical fitness</strong> over time.\n\nI much prefer jogging in open spaces, like parks or along the lakeside, because you get fresh air and you can watch other people out doing their own thing - running, aerobics, badminton, whatever. I usually go in the mornings with a close friend of mine, most days, for about thirty minutes to an hour, just to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep fit</strong>.\n\nThere are a few reasons it works so well for me. For one, it keeps me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in decent shape</strong> without much effort, and it's done wonders for my immune system too - I hardly get ill compared to a few years ago. It's also refreshingly low-effort compared to other sports; I can just amble along and take in whatever's around me instead of concentrating hard the whole time. But honestly, the biggest thing is that it lets me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ease my mind</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">switch off</strong> from my usual routine for a bit.\n\nThese days I try not to skip a session unless the weather's really against me, and I can already notice the difference it's made, both in how I look and in how I deal with stress generally. It's become such a normal part of my day now that I honestly can't imagine giving it up.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"build up your physical fitness","context":"Today I'd like to talk about jogging - my favourite activity for staying healthy. It's basically running at a slow, comfortable pace, and it really helps to build up your physical fitness over time."},{"en":"keep fit","context":"I usually go in the mornings with a close friend of mine, most days, for about thirty minutes to an hour, just to keep fit."},{"en":"in decent shape","context":"For one, it keeps me in decent shape without much effort, and it's done wonders for my immune system too - I hardly get ill compared to a few years ago."},{"en":"ease my mind","context":"But honestly, the biggest thing is that it lets me ease my mind and switch off from my usual routine for a bit."},{"en":"switch off","context":"But honestly, the biggest thing is that it lets me ease my mind and switch off from my usual routine for a bit."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an activity you do to keep fit","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-126","_displayNo":"#017","outline":{"topic":"jogging","intro":"jogging favourite activity usually stay healthy","body":["prefer go jogging open spaces like parks lakesides bc enjoy fresh","normally go jogging morning w/ close friend daily basis 30 minutes","activity good way gain attractive appearance keep good shape beneficial health","Unlike forms exercise jogging easy allows observe people things pass by"],"conclusion":"2nd important reason doing activity allows ease mind get away"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-126.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An activity I do to keep fit is jogging. I usually go in the morning with a close friend and spend about thirty minutes to an hour running at a comfortable pace. We prefer parks or paths beside a lake.\n\nI like exercising outdoors because there is fresh air and plenty to see. Other people are walking, doing aerobics or playing games, so the surroundings feel lively. Jogging also needs little organisation. I do not have to book a court or collect a large amount of equipment.\n\nThe biggest benefit for me is the effect on my mood. Running gives me time away from work and daily worries. Afterwards, I feel more relaxed and ready to begin the day. I have also noticed improvements in my fitness since making it a regular habit. I try not to miss a session unless the weather is particularly bad. It no longer feels like an extra task I must force myself to do. It has become a normal and enjoyable part of my routine.","html":"An activity I do to keep fit is jogging. I usually go in the morning with a close friend and spend about thirty minutes to an hour running at a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comfortable pace</strong>. We prefer parks or paths beside a lake.\n\nI like exercising outdoors because there is fresh air and plenty to see. Other people are walking, doing aerobics or playing games, so the surroundings feel lively. Jogging also needs little organisation. I do not have to book a court or collect a large amount of equipment.\n\nThe biggest benefit for me is the effect on my mood. Running gives me time away from work and daily worries. Afterwards, I feel more relaxed and ready to begin the day. I have also noticed improvements in my fitness since making it a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regular habit</strong>. I try not to miss a session unless the weather is particularly bad. It no longer feels like an extra task I must force myself to do. It has become a normal and enjoyable part of my routine.","phrases":[{"en":"comfortable pace","meaning":"A speed that someone can manage without too much difficulty."},{"en":"regular habit","meaning":"Something a person does repeatedly as part of normal life."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-126.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An art and craft activity you did at school","prompt":"Describe an art and craft activity you did at school\nYou should say:\nWhat it was\nWhat you did for this activity\nWho you did it with\nAnd explain how you felt about this activity.","answer":"I'd like to talk about a craft activity I did back in primary school - origami. I probably don't need to explain much, since it's already so popular all over the world; it's basically the art of folding paper into a particular shape or animal.\n\nIt was all the rage at my old school, because it takes real skill and patience to fold a decent-looking figure - it felt like a proper challenge for us as kids. I got swept up in the hype too, so I asked my desk mate to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">show me the ropes</strong>. He was easily one of the best in our class at it. He decided to teach me the simplest figure first, the crane, and made it look effortless. He told me to just follow the steps exactly and I'd end up with a paper crane.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Easier said than done</strong>, though - mine came out <strong class=\"key-phrase\">misshapen beyond recognition</strong>. I genuinely had no idea what I was doing wrong. Even when he patiently showed me a second time, it barely improved, and eventually he just gave up on me. I was pretty disappointed in myself, to be honest.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">In retrospect</strong>, I think I was just too careless and in too much of a rush - I wanted to get it right straightaway, but origami really isn't about speed, it's about patience and precision. That's probably why I admire origami artists so much now. I might give it another go someday, but next time I'll <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take my time</strong> and actually relax into it.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"show me the ropes","context":"I got swept up in the hype too, so I asked my desk mate to show me the ropes."},{"en":"Easier said than done","context":"Easier said than done, though - mine came out misshapen beyond recognition."},{"en":"misshapen beyond recognition","context":"Easier said than done, though - mine came out misshapen beyond recognition."},{"en":"In retrospect","context":"In retrospect, I think I was just too careless and in too much of a rush - I wanted to get it right straightaway, but origami really isn't about speed, it's about patience and precision."},{"en":"take my time","context":"I might give it another go someday, but next time I'll take my time and actually relax into it."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an art and craft activity you did at school","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-127","_displayNo":"#018","outline":{"topic":"origami","intro":"craft activity elementary school - origami","body":["rage old school bc skilful patient make beautiful paper figure","told follow exact steps create paper crane","Easier said done bc end crane misshapen beyond recognition"],"conclusion":"Maybe future try origami again time take time relax more"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-127.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A craft activity I tried at primary school was origami, the art of making shapes by folding paper. Many children in my class enjoyed it, and I wanted to learn because their paper animals looked impressive.\n\nI asked my desk mate to teach me. He was good at it and chose a paper crane as our first project. He showed each fold and told me to follow the steps carefully. However, my paper quickly became uneven, and the finished shape did not look like a bird. He explained it again patiently, but my second attempt was not much better.\n\nI felt disappointed at the time. Looking back, I think I was rushing because I wanted a good result immediately. Origami requires careful, accurate folds, not speed. The experience helped me understand why skilled paper artists deserve respect. A simple-looking shape can take a lot of patience. I would like to try again one day, but next time I would work more slowly and enjoy learning rather than expecting to succeed at once.","html":"A craft activity I tried at primary school was origami, the art of making shapes by folding paper. Many children in my class enjoyed it, and I wanted to learn because their paper animals looked impressive.\n\nI asked my desk mate to teach me. He was good at it and chose a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">paper crane</strong> as our first project. He showed each fold and told me to follow the steps carefully. However, my paper quickly became uneven, and the finished shape did not look like a bird. He explained it again patiently, but my second attempt was not much better.\n\nI felt disappointed at the time. Looking back, I think I was rushing because I wanted a good result immediately. Origami requires careful, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accurate folds</strong>, not speed. The experience helped me understand why skilled paper artists deserve respect. A simple-looking shape can take a lot of patience. I would like to try again one day, but next time I would work more slowly and enjoy learning rather than expecting to succeed at once.","phrases":[{"en":"paper crane","meaning":"A folded-paper model of a long-legged bird."},{"en":"accurate folds","meaning":"Bends in paper made in exactly the right places."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-127.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An indoor game that you enjoyed as a child","prompt":"Describe an indoor game that you enjoyed as a child.\nYou should say:\n- what the game was\n- where you played it\n- who played this game with you\n- and why you liked it","answer":"As a child I enjoyed <strong class=\"key-phrase\">playing chess</strong>. I think chess is probably the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">best known board game</strong> in the world. It's a game for two players, and the aim is to defeat the other player by taking his or her pieces and eventually trapping his King. This final move is called checkmate.\n\nI remember that it was a classmate of mine at primary school who first taught me to play chess. He had a small, portable chess set, and once I knew how each piece moved, we started playing at break and lunch times; we played in our classroom or outside on the school playground. Later my parents bought me my own chess set as a birthday present so that I could play at home. I taught my younger brother to play, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at some point</strong> I joined the school chess club.\n\nThere I had the chance to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hone my skills</strong> against some of the older pupils, and in my final year of primary school I made it onto the school team. There were five of us on the team, and we competed against children from other primary schools in the same town. I liked playing chess because I enjoyed the challenge of thinking ahead and trying to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">outwit my opponent</strong>. I was probably seven or eight years old when I started playing, and it seemed like a very mysterious and intellectual game at that time.\n\nAlso, although I loved winning, chess taught me to learn from my losses and to congratulate the person who had beaten me.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"playing chess","context":"As a child I enjoyed playing chess."},{"en":"best known board game","context":"I think chess is probably the best known board game in the world."},{"en":"at some point","context":"I taught my younger brother to play, and at some point I joined the school chess club."},{"en":"hone my skills","context":"There I had the chance to hone my skills against some of the older pupils, and in my final year of primary school I made it onto the school team."},{"en":"outwit my opponent","context":"I liked playing chess because I enjoyed the challenge of thinking ahead and trying to outwit my opponent."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an indoor game that you enjoyed as a child","id":"speaking-part-2-129","_displayNo":"#019","outline":{"topic":"chess","intro":"child enjoyed playing chess","body":["It's game two players aim defeat player taking pieces eventually trapping","small portable chess set once knew piece moved started playing break","chance hone skills against older pupils final year primary school made","probably seven eight years old started playing seemed like mysterious intellectual"],"conclusion":"loved winning chess taught learn losses congratulate person beaten me"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-129.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An indoor game I enjoyed as a child was chess. A primary school classmate taught me when I was about seven or eight. He had a small chess set, and we played during breaks once I understood how the pieces moved.\n\nChess is played by two people. Each player tries to protect their king while putting the other king into a position where it cannot escape. Later, my parents bought me my own set for my birthday. I taught my younger brother, and I also joined the school chess club.\n\nPractising against older pupils helped me improve. In my final primary year, I joined the five-person school team and competed against other schools in our town. I liked the challenge of thinking ahead instead of choosing the first move that looked good. Winning felt exciting, but losing was useful too because I could see my mistakes. Chess taught me to be patient and to congratulate an opponent who played better. It was enjoyable, but it also helped me develop a more careful way of thinking.","html":"An indoor game I enjoyed as a child was chess. A primary school classmate taught me when I was about seven or eight. He had a small chess set, and we played during breaks once I understood how the pieces moved.\n\nChess is played by two people. Each player tries to protect their king while putting the other king into a position where it cannot escape. Later, my parents bought me my own set for my birthday. I taught my younger brother, and I also joined the school chess club.\n\nPractising against older pupils helped me improve. In my final primary year, I joined the five-person school team and competed against other schools in our town. I liked the challenge of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thinking ahead</strong> instead of choosing the first move that looked good. Winning felt exciting, but losing was useful too because I could see my mistakes. Chess taught me to be patient and to congratulate an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">opponent</strong> who played better. It was enjoyable, but it also helped me develop a more careful way of thinking.","phrases":[{"en":"thinking ahead","meaning":"Considering what may happen next before deciding what to do."},{"en":"opponent","meaning":"The person you are playing or competing against."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-129.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting lesson that you attended","prompt":"Describe an interesting lesson that you attended.\nYou should say:\n- where you attended this lesson\n- what it was about\n- and why you found it interesting","answer":"I'm going to talk about an interesting science lesson I attended at secondary school, when I was fourteen or fifteen. It was quite a while ago now, so I can't remember every detail, but the lesson was about respiration - we learned how the lungs work, how we breathe, and how oxygen <strong class=\"key-phrase\">passes into the blood</strong>. The teacher also talked to us about the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">effects of smoking</strong> on the lungs.\n\nI found the lesson interesting because my science teacher, Dr Smith, always introduced new topics with a video. We watched a short film about how respiration works, and I found that a lot easier to follow than a textbook - it had diagrams showing the whole breathing process step by step. Later on, we saw real photos of healthy lungs next to lungs that had been damaged by smoking; they'd actually turned black. I think that image is probably the reason this lesson <strong class=\"key-phrase\">has stuck with me</strong> all these years.\n\nWhat really made the lesson work, though, wasn't just the images. Dr Smith always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stopped the video halfway through</strong> and asked us to predict what would happen next, so we were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thinking rather than simply watching</strong>. He even brought in a set of model lungs made out of plastic bags and straws, and got a few of us up to demonstrate breathing in front of the class.\n\nI've forgotten pretty much everything else from that year, but I could still explain how respiration works today, which says a lot about how much more effective that approach was than just reading a chapter and answering questions.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"passes into the blood","context":"It was quite a while ago now, so I can't remember every detail, but the lesson was about respiration - we learned how the lungs work, how we breathe, and how oxygen passes into the blood."},{"en":"effects of smoking","context":"The teacher also talked to us about the effects of smoking on the lungs."},{"en":"has stuck with me","context":"I think that image is probably the reason this lesson has stuck with me all these years."},{"en":"stopped the video halfway through","context":"Dr Smith always stopped the video halfway through and asked us to predict what would happen next, so we were thinking rather than simply watching."},{"en":"thinking rather than simply watching","context":"Dr Smith always stopped the video halfway through and asked us to predict what would happen next, so we were thinking rather than simply watching."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting lesson that you attended","id":"speaking-part-2-130","_displayNo":"#020","outline":{"topic":"science lesson","intro":"long time ago can't remember detail lesson respiration","body":["I'm going talk interesting science lesson attended secondary school 14 15","found lesson interesting bc science teacher Dr. Smith always introduced new","watched short film respiration works found easier understand science textbook","Later lesson saw real photos healthy lungs lungs damaged result smoking","Dr Smith stopped video halfway asked predict what happen next","brought model lungs plastic bags straws let us demonstrate","forgotten everything else that yr but could still explain respiration today"],"conclusion":"forgotten everything else that yr but could still explain respiration today"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-130.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An interesting lesson I attended was a science class at secondary school when I was about fourteen or fifteen. Our teacher, Dr Smith, explained respiration: how we breathe and how oxygen reaches the blood.\n\nHe began with a short video showing the process through moving diagrams. I found this easier to understand than reading a textbook alone. He stopped the video at different points and asked us to predict what would happen next. This kept us involved instead of simply watching the screen.\n\nHe also brought model lungs made with plastic bags and straws. Some students demonstrated breathing in front of the class. Later, we saw pictures comparing healthy lungs with lungs damaged by smoking. Those images stayed in my memory. What made the lesson effective was the combination of explanation, pictures and practical activity. I have forgotten many details from that school year, but I can still remember the main ideas from this class. It showed me that taking part and asking questions can make learning much more memorable.","html":"An interesting lesson I attended was a science class at secondary school when I was about fourteen or fifteen. Our teacher, Dr Smith, explained <strong class=\"key-phrase\">respiration</strong>: how we breathe and how oxygen reaches the blood.\n\nHe began with a short video showing the process through moving diagrams. I found this easier to understand than reading a textbook alone. He stopped the video at different points and asked us to predict what would happen next. This kept us involved instead of simply watching the screen.\n\nHe also brought model lungs made with plastic bags and straws. Some students demonstrated breathing in front of the class. Later, we saw pictures comparing healthy lungs with lungs damaged by smoking. Those images stayed in my memory. What made the lesson effective was the combination of explanation, pictures and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical activity</strong>. I have forgotten many details from that school year, but I can still remember the main ideas from this class. It showed me that taking part and asking questions can make learning much more memorable.","phrases":[{"en":"respiration","meaning":"The process of breathing and using oxygen in the body."},{"en":"practical activity","meaning":"A task where people learn by doing something themselves."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-130.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"One activity stopped by the weather","prompt":"Describe one activity stopped by the weather\nYou should say:\nWhen and where it happened\nWho was involved\nWhat the activity was\nAnd what you did after the activity was stopped","answer":"Anyone who's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a big fan of tennis</strong> will know the name Novak Djokovic, and today I want to tell you about the day I skipped school to watch a match between him and Dominic Thiem at the 2017 French Open - a match that, in the end, never actually happened.\n\nI've been hooked on tennis since I first discovered it. My dad and I always turn into total couch potatoes whenever a big tournament's on - Novak's always been my favourite, while dad's more of a Roger Federer man. I love watching him play regardless of the result, and I've always admired his sportsmanship and the way he moves around the court, so powerfully but elegantly.\n\nThat particular day was the quarter-final, and Novak needed to beat Thiem to reach the semis. I was desperate to watch it live, especially since he'd lost the French Open final twice in a row before that. Unfortunately, I had a mid-term test scheduled for exactly that time, which meant there was no way to watch it properly. After a fair bit of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wrestling with my conscience</strong>, I ended up lying to my dad so I could stay home and watch instead of sitting the test.\n\nAs it happened, though, the weather in Paris that day <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be</strong> so bad that the match got cancelled entirely. Rather than the final I'd been hoping for, I just watched old highlights of Novak's matches instead, feeling sorry for myself. I felt incredibly guilty once the truth came out, and I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">got into big trouble</strong> with my dad for skipping the test for nothing - though I did sit a makeup exam a few days later.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"a big fan of tennis","context":"Anyone who's a big fan of tennis will know the name Novak Djokovic, and today I want to tell you about the day I skipped school to watch a match between him and Dominic Thiem at the 2017 French Open - a match that, in the end, never actually happened."},{"en":"wrestling with my conscience","context":"After a fair bit of wrestling with my conscience, I ended up lying to my dad so I could stay home and watch instead of sitting the test."},{"en":"turned out to be","context":"As it happened, though, the weather in Paris that day turned out to be so bad that the match got cancelled entirely."},{"en":"got into big trouble","context":"I felt incredibly guilty once the truth came out, and I got into big trouble with my dad for skipping the test for nothing - though I did sit a makeup exam a few days later."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"one activity stopped by the weather","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-131","_displayNo":"#021","outline":{"topic":"Djokovic match cancelled by rain","intro":"Anyone big fan tennis definitely knows name tennis legend Novak","body":["today going talk story skipped school watch big game not happen","always love watching tennis match w/ favourite player whether wins loses","particular day quarterfinals Novak defeat Thiem go through semi-final excited watch","after wrestling w/ conscience decided tell lie father stay home watch"],"conclusion":"felt guilty found out game cancelled got big trouble father"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-131.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An activity stopped by the weather was watching a tennis match between Novak Djokovic and Dominic Thiem at the French Open in 2017. Djokovic was my favourite player, and I was very excited to watch the match live.\n\nUnfortunately, I had a mid-term test at the same time. Instead of making a sensible decision, I lied to my father so I could stay home. I knew it was wrong, but I let my interest in tennis become more important than the test.\n\nThen the weather in Paris became so bad that the match did not go ahead as scheduled. I ended up watching old highlights instead of the game I had planned my day around. When my father discovered the truth, he was angry and disappointed. I felt guilty, especially because I had missed the test for nothing. I later took a replacement exam. The experience is memorable because the weather ruined my plan, but it also taught me to take my responsibilities seriously rather than letting excitement decide everything.","html":"An activity stopped by the weather was watching a tennis match between Novak Djokovic and Dominic Thiem at the French Open in 2017. Djokovic was my favourite player, and I was very excited to watch the match <strong class=\"key-phrase\">live</strong>.\n\nUnfortunately, I had a mid-term test at the same time. Instead of making a sensible decision, I lied to my father so I could stay home. I knew it was wrong, but I let my interest in tennis become more important than the test.\n\nThen the weather in Paris became so bad that the match did not go ahead as scheduled. I ended up watching old highlights instead of the game I had planned my day around. When my father discovered the truth, he was angry and disappointed. I felt guilty, especially because I had missed the test for nothing. I later took a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">replacement exam</strong>. The experience is memorable because the weather ruined my plan, but it also taught me to take my responsibilities seriously rather than letting excitement decide everything.","phrases":[{"en":"live","meaning":"At the same time as an event is actually happening."},{"en":"replacement exam","meaning":"Another test arranged for someone who missed the original one."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-131.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Something you do to keep healthy","prompt":"Describe something you do to keep healthy.\nYou should say:\n- What the activity is\n- When you do it\n- How often you do it\nAnd explain why it is a good way to look after your health.","answer":"I'd like to talk about something I'm <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keen on doing</strong> in my free time to stay healthy - jogging.\n\nI <strong class=\"key-phrase\">took up this habit</strong> about two years ago, back when I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">up to my ears with study</strong> for my graduation exams and feeling pretty <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stressed out</strong>. I remember thinking I needed something energetic to burn off the stress, so I decided to have a go at jogging.\n\nIt was tough to begin with, since I'd been living a pretty <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sedentary lifestyle</strong> up to that point - just sitting in front of my laptop all day doing assignments. After a few days of aching muscles, though, I got used to it, and these days I try to get out most evenings, five or six times a week.\n\nHonestly, it's helped me a lot, both physically and mentally. I lost my belly and got a lot leaner thanks to it, and I'm pretty happy with how I look now - I try to keep it that way. It also just helps me unwind and get some fresh air; every time I go, I come back feeling like my head's been cleared out a bit. I'm planning to keep it up long-term, and maybe branch out into other sports eventually too.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"keen on doing","context":"I'd like to talk about something I'm keen on doing in my free time to stay healthy - jogging."},{"en":"took up this habit","context":"I took up this habit about two years ago, back when I was up to my ears with study for my graduation exams and feeling pretty stressed out. I remember thinking I needed something energetic to burn off the stress, so I decided to have a go at jogging."},{"en":"up to my ears with study","context":"I took up this habit about two years ago, back when I was up to my ears with study for my graduation exams and feeling pretty stressed out. I remember thinking I needed something energetic to burn off the stress, so I decided to have a go at jogging."},{"en":"stressed out","context":"I took up this habit about two years ago, back when I was up to my ears with study for my graduation exams and feeling pretty stressed out. I remember thinking I needed something energetic to burn off the stress, so I decided to have a go at jogging."},{"en":"sedentary lifestyle","context":"It was tough to begin with, since I'd been living a pretty sedentary lifestyle up to that point - just sitting in front of my laptop all day doing assignments."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"something you do to keep healthy","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-132","_displayNo":"#022","outline":{"topic":"jogging","intro":"something keen doing leisure time stay healthy jogging","body":["time up ears w/ study prepare graduation examination little bit stressed","after few days suffering muscle aches got used jogging kept doing","say sport helped lot strengthening physical health well mental health","future going keep doing stay healthy maybe try sports too"],"conclusion":"That's"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-132.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Something I do to stay healthy is jogging. I started about two years ago while preparing for my graduation exams. I was spending most of the day at my laptop, and the pressure of studying made me feel stressed.\n\nI decided to try running because I needed an active break. At first, it was difficult. My muscles ached, and I was not used to exercising regularly. However, after a few days I began to feel more comfortable. Now I try to go out five or six evenings a week.\n\nI have noticed both physical and mental benefits. I feel fitter, and I am happier with my appearance. More importantly, time outside helps me stop thinking about work and study for a while. I usually return home with a clearer mind. I plan to continue rather than treating jogging as a short-term way to change my body. It has become a habit I enjoy, and I may try other sports later. Starting slowly helped me discover an activity that fits well into my daily life.","html":"Something I do to stay healthy is jogging. I started about two years ago while preparing for my graduation exams. I was spending most of the day at my laptop, and the pressure of studying made me feel stressed.\n\nI decided to try running because I needed an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">active break</strong>. At first, it was difficult. My muscles ached, and I was not used to exercising regularly. However, after a few days I began to feel more comfortable. Now I try to go out five or six evenings a week.\n\nI have noticed both physical and mental benefits. I feel fitter, and I am happier with my appearance. More importantly, time outside helps me stop thinking about work and study for a while. I usually return home with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clearer mind</strong>. I plan to continue rather than treating jogging as a short-term way to change my body. It has become a habit I enjoy, and I may try other sports later. Starting slowly helped me discover an activity that fits well into my daily life.","phrases":[{"en":"active break","meaning":"Time away from work or study spent moving or exercising."},{"en":"a clearer mind","meaning":"A calmer state in which it is easier to think."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-132.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Something you enjoy doing with an old person in your family","prompt":"Describe something you enjoy doing with an old person in your family\nYou should say:\n- What it is\n- How often you do it\n- Who you do it with\n- Why you enjoy doing it with this person","answer":"I'd like to tell you about something I really enjoy doing with my 80-year-old grandmother - watching an Indian soap opera together. The one we're currently <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hooked on</strong> is called Eight-Year-Old Bride, and it's on Today TV every evening.\n\nWe first <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stumbled upon</strong> it about four months ago, after catching the very first episode almost by accident. We were both completely drawn in by one of the characters, a girl called Anandi, and we haven't missed a single episode since. The show follows Anandi's life after she's forced into marriage at just eight years old, and everything she goes through afterwards.\n\nIt airs every evening at eight, so I always try to arrange my schedule around it so I can watch with my grandma. While we watch, she often points out little details in the story that I'd probably miss completely on my own - she's surprisingly sharp when it comes to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">picking up on things</strong> like that.\n\nHonestly, I've learned a lot from both the show and from her, and I think the biggest lesson has to be that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">getting married too young</strong> can honestly ruin a person's whole life. But more than the story itself, it's really just become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">our little routine</strong> together, and I think that's what I'd miss most if it ever ended.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"hooked on","context":"I'd like to tell you about something I really enjoy doing with my 80-year-old grandmother - watching an Indian soap opera together. The one we're currently hooked on is called Eight-Year-Old Bride, and it's on Today TV every evening."},{"en":"stumbled upon","context":"We first stumbled upon it about four months ago, after catching the very first episode almost by accident."},{"en":"picking up on things","context":"While we watch, she often points out little details in the story that I'd probably miss completely on my own - she's surprisingly sharp when it comes to picking up on things like that."},{"en":"getting married too young","context":"Honestly, I've learned a lot from both the show and from her, and I think the biggest lesson has to be that getting married too young can honestly ruin a person's whole life."},{"en":"our little routine","context":"But more than the story itself, it's really just become our little routine together, and I think that's what I'd miss most if it ever ended."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"something you enjoy doing with an old person in your family","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-133","_displayNo":"#023","outline":{"topic":"watch 8-Year-Old Bride w/ grandma","intro":"now going tell something activity particularly keen doing w/ 80-year-old","body":["1st time grandma knew TV series 4 mos ago after seeing","series mainly tells story girl named Anandi live miserable life forced","mentioned series shown evening 8 pm always try best arrange work","During time watch tells lot things meaningful details episode hardly realise"],"conclusion":"fact learned lot wonderful lessons series grandmother important lesson Getting"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-133.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Something I enjoy doing with an older family member is watching television with my eighty-year-old grandmother. At the moment, we follow an Indian drama called Eight-Year-Old Bride on Today TV.\n\nWe discovered it about four months ago after seeing the first episode by chance. The story follows a girl called Anandi who is forced into marriage when she is only eight. We became interested in her life and have continued watching. The programme begins at eight each evening, so I try to finish other tasks before then.\n\nMy grandmother often notices small details that I miss. We discuss the characters' choices and what might happen next. The story has also encouraged serious conversations about the problems caused by marrying too young. However, the most important part for me is our shared routine. It gives us a regular reason to sit together and talk. If the programme ended, I would miss that time more than the story itself. I like an activity that allows two different generations to enjoy something and learn from each other's opinions.","html":"Something I enjoy doing with an older family member is watching television with my eighty-year-old grandmother. At the moment, we follow an Indian drama called Eight-Year-Old Bride on Today TV.\n\nWe discovered it about four months ago after seeing the first episode by chance. The story follows a girl called Anandi who is forced into marriage when she is only eight. We became interested in her life and have continued watching. The programme begins at eight each evening, so I try to finish other tasks before then.\n\nMy grandmother often notices small details that I miss. We discuss the characters' choices and what might happen next. The story has also encouraged serious conversations about the problems caused by marrying too young. However, the most important part for me is our <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shared routine</strong>. It gives us a regular reason to sit together and talk. If the programme ended, I would miss that time more than the story itself. I like an activity that allows two different <strong class=\"key-phrase\">generations</strong> to enjoy something and learn from each other's opinions.","phrases":[{"en":"shared routine","meaning":"An activity that people regularly do together."},{"en":"generations","meaning":"Groups of people born around the same period, such as grandparents and grandchildren."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-133.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"What you would do if you had a day off","prompt":"Describe what you would do if you had a day off.\nYou should say:\n- What would you do?\n- Who would you do it with?\n- Why would you like to do it?","answer":"I'd like to talk about what I'd do if I had a day off. I haven't been able to rest properly in ages, so I'm <strong class=\"key-phrase\">desperately longing for</strong> a day just to completely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recharge my energy</strong>.\n\nIf I had a day like that, I'd want to spend it entirely on my own, to be honest. The first thing I'd do is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turn off my phone</strong>, so nobody could reach me and no work could creep in. I wouldn't even ring my best friend - I'd just want to completely switch off and laze around at home. There's so much I want to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catch up on</strong>, like reading and a couple of shows I've fallen behind on. I'd probably spend the whole morning binge-watching the latest season of Master Chef.\n\nIn the afternoon, I'd probably <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take a walk</strong> in the park near my new place. I moved in a few months back but still haven't actually been, so it'd be nice to finally check it out while I've got the time.\n\nThere's no big reason I'd rather stay close to home - I just really need some time to myself. Between work and being sociable all the time, I get worn out, so I'd want a day to forget about all of that completely. I reckon it'd help me get my energy back and be a lot more productive afterwards.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"desperately longing for","context":"I'd like to talk about what I'd do if I had a day off. I haven't been able to rest properly in ages, so I'm desperately longing for a day just to completely recharge my energy."},{"en":"recharge my energy","context":"I'd like to talk about what I'd do if I had a day off. I haven't been able to rest properly in ages, so I'm desperately longing for a day just to completely recharge my energy."},{"en":"turn off my phone","context":"The first thing I'd do is turn off my phone, so nobody could reach me and no work could creep in."},{"en":"catch up on","context":"There's so much I want to catch up on, like reading and a couple of shows I've fallen behind on."},{"en":"take a walk","context":"In the afternoon, I'd probably take a walk in the park near my new place. I moved in a few months back but still haven't actually been, so it'd be nice to finally check it out while I've got the time."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"what you would do if you had a day off","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-134","_displayNo":"#024","outline":{"topic":"quiet day at home","intro":"Actually not able rest properly lately desperately longing day off","body":["wouldn't bother call bestie want totally shut myself off everything laze","Let's see things want catch up like reading book favourite TV","honest moved new apartment few months ago never chance visit park","Too socialising working tires out want forget while"],"conclusion":"That's end answer"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-134.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"If I had a day off, I would spend most of it quietly at home by myself. Recently, work and social plans have left me tired, so I would like time without a timetable or anyone expecting something from me.\n\nI would first turn off my phone to avoid work messages. During the morning, I would read and watch some programmes I have not had time to finish, especially Master Chef. I would not worry about being productive every minute. The point would be to rest properly.\n\nIn the afternoon, I would take a walk in the park near my new home. I moved a few months ago but have not explored it yet. Staying close to home would make the day simple and avoid the effort of organising travel. Some people prefer an exciting outing on a free day, but I mainly need quiet time. I think a relaxed day like this would help me recover my energy and return to work feeling more focused rather than more exhausted.","html":"If I had a day off, I would spend most of it quietly at home by myself. Recently, work and social plans have left me tired, so I would like time without a timetable or anyone expecting something from me.\n\nI would first turn off my phone to avoid work messages. During the morning, I would read and watch some programmes I have not had time to finish, especially Master Chef. I would not worry about being <strong class=\"key-phrase\">productive</strong> every minute. The point would be to rest properly.\n\nIn the afternoon, I would take a walk in the park near my new home. I moved a few months ago but have not explored it yet. Staying close to home would make the day simple and avoid the effort of organising travel. Some people prefer an exciting outing on a free day, but I mainly need quiet time. I think a relaxed day like this would help me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recover my energy</strong> and return to work feeling more focused rather than more exhausted.","phrases":[{"en":"productive","meaning":"Achieving useful work or results."},{"en":"recover my energy","meaning":"Become rested and feel able to do things again."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-134.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"What you would do if you were given a day off","prompt":"Describe what you would do if you were given a day off\nYou should say:\nwhat you would like to do\nwho you would like to be with\nwhere you would like to do it\nAnd explain how you would feel at the end of the day.","answer":"I'm going to talk about something I'd love to do if I had a day off - spend the whole day out doing various outdoor activities, just to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relieve some stress</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recharge my batteries</strong>. I've already got a few ideas in mind for when I get the chance, like hiking or cycling somewhere mountainous, or visiting a few historical sites.\n\nIf I had a day off, I'd want to spend it with my best friend, Lan. She loves travelling and exploring new places just as much as I do, and she actually knows basic first aid and what to do if we ever got lost, so she'd be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the perfect partner to go with</strong>.\n\nI'd probably head to Ninh Binh, since it's actually my hometown. I grew up there, but I still haven't explored the whole province - it's huge, and there's a lot I've never seen. With so many attractions around, it'd let Lan and me enjoy some <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fresh atmosphere</strong>, go hiking, and visit places like the Stone Church and Hoa Lu Palace.\n\nI've been dealing with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a heavy workload</strong> at work recently, so I could really use a break like that. I imagine that by the end of the day, tired as I'd probably be after all that hiking, I'd feel completely recharged and just a lot happier - way more than any lazy day at home could manage.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"relieve some stress","context":"I'm going to talk about something I'd love to do if I had a day off - spend the whole day out doing various outdoor activities, just to relieve some stress and recharge my batteries."},{"en":"recharge my batteries","context":"I'm going to talk about something I'd love to do if I had a day off - spend the whole day out doing various outdoor activities, just to relieve some stress and recharge my batteries."},{"en":"the perfect partner to go with","context":"If I had a day off, I'd want to spend it with my best friend, Lan. She loves travelling and exploring new places just as much as I do, and she actually knows basic first aid and what to do if we ever got lost, so she'd be the perfect partner to go with."},{"en":"fresh atmosphere","context":"With so many attractions around, it'd let Lan and me enjoy some fresh atmosphere, go hiking, and visit places like the Stone Church and Hoa Lu Palace."},{"en":"a heavy workload","context":"I've been dealing with a heavy workload at work recently, so I could really use a break like that."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"what you would do if you were given a day off","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-135","_displayNo":"#025","outline":{"topic":"outdoor day off","intro":"going talk activity love day off spend entire day participating","body":["making plans number things like get chance hiking cycling mountainous area","loves travelling discovering new places well knows basic 1st aid survive","Ninh Binh province hometown like go free day born brought up","numerous tourist attractions Ninh Binh enable friend enjoy fresh atmosphere go"],"conclusion":"suffering heavy workload job recently need day off allow time"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-135.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"If I were given a day off, I would spend it outdoors with my best friend Lan. We both enjoy travelling and discovering unfamiliar places, so I would choose a short trip rather than staying at home.\n\nI would probably go to Ninh Binh, my hometown. Although I grew up there, I have not explored the whole province. We could cycle or walk through the countryside and visit places such as the Stone Church and Hoa Lu Palace. Lan would be a good companion because she enjoys these activities and knows basic first aid.\n\nI would plan enough to avoid wasting time, but leave space to stop whenever we saw something interesting. Recently, my workload has been heavy, and I have spent too many hours indoors. A day of movement and fresh air would help me take my mind off those responsibilities. I would probably feel physically tired by the evening, but mentally refreshed. More than anything, I would enjoy having a whole day to share an experience with a close friend.","html":"If I were given a day off, I would spend it outdoors with my best friend Lan. We both enjoy travelling and discovering unfamiliar places, so I would choose a short trip rather than staying at home.\n\nI would probably go to Ninh Binh, my hometown. Although I grew up there, I have not explored the whole province. We could cycle or walk through the countryside and visit places such as the Stone Church and Hoa Lu Palace. Lan would be a good <strong class=\"key-phrase\">companion</strong> because she enjoys these activities and knows basic first aid.\n\nI would plan enough to avoid wasting time, but leave space to stop whenever we saw something interesting. Recently, my workload has been heavy, and I have spent too many hours indoors. A day of movement and fresh air would help me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take my mind off</strong> those responsibilities. I would probably feel physically tired by the evening, but mentally refreshed. More than anything, I would enjoy having a whole day to share an experience with a close friend.","phrases":[{"en":"companion","meaning":"Someone who goes somewhere or spends time with you."},{"en":"take my mind off","meaning":"Help me stop thinking about something worrying or tiring."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-135.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A dinner you enjoyed with your friends","prompt":"Describe a dinner you enjoyed with your friends.\nYou should say:\nwhen and where you had the dinner\nwhat you had\nwho you went with\nand explain why you enjoyed the dinner.","answer":"I've been to plenty of dinners and parties over the years, but there's one that really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stands out for me</strong>. It was a barbecue on the rooftop of my friend Lan's apartment building, back in early spring, and it was actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a farewell party</strong> for my best friend Mai, because she was leaving to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">study abroad</strong> in Australia the following week.\n\nThere were about eight of us altogether, all close friends from university. We'd been planning the evening for weeks, and on the day itself we met at Lan's place, went shopping together for the meat and drinks, and spent a couple of hours setting up the rooftop terrace. Mai is a brilliant cook, so she took charge of the marinade, and honestly, I don't think I've ever had grilled pork that tasted so good.\n\nAlongside the meat we had grilled corn, salad, and far too many drinks. The dessert is what I remember best, though. Someone had ordered a red velvet cake from a bakery near Hoan Kiem Lake, with a thick layer of cream cheese frosting, and it was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worth every penny</strong> — I actually went back for a second slice, which I never normally do.\n\nBut what made that evening so good wasn't really the food, it was the company. We stayed up on that rooftop until it got properly dark, grilling, chatting, and telling stories about things we'd done together over the years. At one point the conversation <strong class=\"key-phrase\">got a bit emotional</strong> when we talked about Mai leaving, but mostly we just laughed. It might be years before all of us are in the same place again, and that's exactly why that dinner has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuck in my memory</strong>.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"stands out for me","context":"I've been to plenty of dinners and parties over the years, but there's one that really stands out for me."},{"en":"a farewell party","context":"It was a barbecue on the rooftop of my friend Lan's apartment building, back in early spring, and it was actually a farewell party for my best friend Mai, because she was leaving to study abroad in Australia the following week."},{"en":"study abroad","context":"It was a barbecue on the rooftop of my friend Lan's apartment building, back in early spring, and it was actually a farewell party for my best friend Mai, because she was leaving to study abroad in Australia the following week."},{"en":"worth every penny","context":"Someone had ordered a red velvet cake from a bakery near Hoan Kiem Lake, with a thick layer of cream cheese frosting, and it was worth every penny — I actually went back for a second slice, which I never normally do."},{"en":"got a bit emotional","context":"At one point the conversation got a bit emotional when we talked about Mai leaving, but mostly we just laughed."},{"en":"stuck in my memory","context":"It might be years before all of us are in the same place again, and that's exactly why that dinner has stuck in my memory."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a dinner you enjoyed with your friends","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-136","_displayNo":"#026","outline":{"topic":"rooftop BBQ farewell party for Mai","intro":"barbecue rooftop friend Lan's building early spring farewell party best friend Mai study abroad Australia","body":["eight of us all close uni friends planned evening weeks shopped together set up rooftop terrace","Mai brilliant cook took charge marinade grilled pork salty sweet charred just right","grilled corn salad drinks beer red velvet cake bakery near Hoan Kiem Lake worth every penny"],"conclusion":"what made evening good wasn't food it was company stayed up chatting bit emotional about Mai leaving stuck in memory"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-136.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A dinner I enjoyed with friends was a rooftop barbecue at Lan's apartment building in early spring. It was a farewell meal for my best friend Mai, who was leaving to study in Australia the following week.\n\nAbout eight university friends came. We bought meat and drinks together and spent the afternoon preparing the rooftop. Mai was the best cook, so she prepared the meat with seasoning. We also had grilled corn and salad. For dessert, someone brought a red velvet cake from a bakery near Hoan Kiem Lake, and I enjoyed it enough to have a second slice.\n\nThe food was excellent, but the company made the evening special. We stayed until after dark, telling stories and laughing about our university years. At times, we became emotional when we spoke about Mai leaving. We knew it might be a long time before everyone could meet again. I remember the meal because it gave us time to appreciate our friendship before our lives moved in different directions. It felt like a warm and happy way to say goodbye.","html":"A dinner I enjoyed with friends was a rooftop barbecue at Lan's apartment building in early spring. It was a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">farewell meal</strong> for my best friend Mai, who was leaving to study in Australia the following week.\n\nAbout eight university friends came. We bought meat and drinks together and spent the afternoon preparing the rooftop. Mai was the best cook, so she prepared the meat with seasoning. We also had grilled corn and salad. For dessert, someone brought a red velvet cake from a bakery near Hoan Kiem Lake, and I enjoyed it enough to have a second slice.\n\nThe food was excellent, but the company made the evening special. We stayed until after dark, telling stories and laughing about our university years. At times, we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">became emotional</strong> when we spoke about Mai leaving. We knew it might be a long time before everyone could meet again. I remember the meal because it gave us time to appreciate our friendship before our lives moved in different directions. It felt like a warm and happy way to say goodbye.","phrases":[{"en":"farewell meal","meaning":"A meal held to say goodbye to someone who is leaving."},{"en":"became emotional","meaning":"Started to feel strong feelings, such as sadness or happiness."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-136.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A family celebration that you remember","prompt":"Describe a family celebration that you remember.\nYou should say:\n- what you were celebrating\n- who was present\n- what you and your family did to make the celebration special\n- and why you enjoyed the occasion.","answer":"I'm going to describe my sister's wedding day, which took place a few years ago in the small town where I grew up. For my sister, Trang, it was the biggest and most important day of her life. There were around a hundred guests at the marriage ceremony itself, which was held in the old church on the edge of town where our parents got married too.\n\nEven more people turned up for the reception afterwards. Most of my family were there, of course, along with the groom's family and a good number of the couple's friends and colleagues from work. To make the day feel special, we all <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pitched in</strong> — it wasn't just left to my parents to organise everything.\n\nMy mother made sure the church and the reception hall were beautifully decorated; there were white lilies on every table, and fairy lights strung along the ceiling that made the whole place glow once it got dark. We'd all <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dressed for the occasion</strong>, obviously, and there were bridesmaids, a live band, and a wedding cake that took my sister nearly an hour to choose. I enjoyed the day mainly because it was wonderful to see my sister looking so happy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on her big day</strong>.\n\nThe ceremony itself was moving — there wasn't <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a dry eye in the church</strong> when my sister walked in — and we all had a fantastic time at the reception afterwards, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dancing until well past midnight</strong>. It's rare for me to see my whole family and all our old friends together in one place, so that's probably what <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made the day so memorable</strong> for me.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"dressed for the occasion","context":"We'd all dressed for the occasion, obviously, and there were bridesmaids, a live band, and a wedding cake that took my sister nearly an hour to choose."},{"en":"on her big day","context":"I enjoyed the day mainly because it was wonderful to see my sister looking so happy on her big day."},{"en":"made the day so memorable","context":"It's rare for me to see my whole family and all our old friends together in one place, so that's probably what made the day so memorable for me."},{"en":"pitched in","context":"To make the day feel special, we all pitched in — it wasn't just left to my parents to organise everything."},{"en":"a dry eye in the church","context":"The ceremony itself was moving — there wasn't a dry eye in the church when my sister walked in — and we all had a fantastic time at the reception afterwards, dancing until well past midnight."},{"en":"dancing until well past midnight","context":"The ceremony itself was moving — there wasn't a dry eye in the church when my sister walked in — and we all had a fantastic time at the reception afterwards, dancing until well past midnight."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a family celebration that you remember","id":"speaking-part-2-137","_displayNo":"#027","outline":{"topic":"sister's wedding","intro":"sister biggest important day life","body":["going describe sister's wedding day took place few years ago town","members family well groom's family collection bride groom's friends colleagues","mother made sure church reception venue nicely decorated - flowers everywhere","enjoyed occasion bc great see sister happy big day"],"conclusion":"It's rare see family friends together one place that's probably"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-137.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A family celebration I remember is my sister Trang's wedding a few years ago. It took place in the small town where we grew up. The ceremony was in the old church where our parents had also married.\n\nAbout a hundred people attended the ceremony, and even more joined the reception afterwards. Both families helped organise the day. My mother arranged decorations, including white lilies on the tables and small lights across the ceiling. There was a live band and a wedding cake, and everyone dressed carefully for the occasion.\n\nI enjoyed seeing my sister so happy. The ceremony was emotional, especially when she entered the church. Afterwards, we celebrated by eating, talking and dancing until after midnight. What made the event particularly memorable was having so many relatives and old friends in one place. That does not happen often now that people live and work in different areas. The wedding was important for my sister, but it also gave the whole family a chance to reconnect and share a joyful day together.","html":"A family celebration I remember is my sister Trang's wedding a few years ago. It took place in the small town where we grew up. The ceremony was in the old church where our parents had also married.\n\nAbout a hundred people attended the ceremony, and even more joined the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reception</strong> afterwards. Both families helped organise the day. My mother arranged decorations, including white lilies on the tables and small lights across the ceiling. There was a live band and a wedding cake, and everyone dressed carefully for the occasion.\n\nI enjoyed seeing my sister so happy. The ceremony was emotional, especially when she entered the church. Afterwards, we celebrated by eating, talking and dancing until after midnight. What made the event particularly memorable was having so many relatives and old friends in one place. That does not happen often now that people live and work in different areas. The wedding was important for my sister, but it also gave the whole family a chance to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reconnect</strong> and share a joyful day together.","phrases":[{"en":"reception","meaning":"A celebration held after a wedding ceremony."},{"en":"reconnect","meaning":"Become close again after spending time apart."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-137.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A festival that is important in your country","prompt":"Describe a festival that is important in your country\nYou should say:\n- when the festival occurs\n- what you did during it\n- what you like or dislike about it\n- and explain why this festival is important","answer":"I'd like to describe Tet, the Lunar New Year, which is the most important traditional festival in Vietnam. It usually falls in late January or February and marks both the beginning of a new lunar year and a rare period when families can spend several uninterrupted days together.\n\nBefore Tet, my family cleans the house, prepares food and buys flowers. On New Year's Eve, we have dinner together, and during the following days we visit relatives, give children <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lucky money</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pay respect to our ancestors</strong>. What I enjoy most is the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sense of renewal</strong>: unfinished disagreements are often put aside, and people begin the year with more generous intentions.\n\nThe only aspect I dislike is the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">commercial pressure</strong>. Travel becomes expensive, shops are crowded and some families feel obliged to spend more than they can afford. Even so, Tet remains deeply important because it preserves <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cultural identity</strong> and renews family bonds. For many people who live far from home, it's the one occasion when returning to their family takes priority over work.\n\nI should probably add what the atmosphere is actually like, because that's the part outsiders find hardest to picture. In the week beforehand <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the whole country seems to be in motion</strong> — stations and coach terminals are packed, and every market is selling peach blossom and kumquat trees. Then on the first morning of the new year the streets go completely silent, because everyone is indoors with their family. That contrast between <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the frantic preparation and the sudden stillness</strong> is what I associate most strongly with Tet, and it's something I've never experienced with any other holiday.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"sense of renewal","context":"What I enjoy most is the sense of renewal: unfinished disagreements are often put aside, and people begin the year with more generous intentions."},{"en":"commercial pressure","context":"The only aspect I dislike is the commercial pressure."},{"en":"cultural identity","context":"Even so, Tet remains deeply important because it preserves cultural identity and renews family bonds."},{"en":"the whole country seems to be in motion","context":"In the week beforehand the whole country seems to be in motion — stations and coach terminals are packed, and every market is selling peach blossom and kumquat trees."},{"en":"the frantic preparation and the sudden stillness","context":"That contrast between the frantic preparation and the sudden stillness is what I associate most strongly with Tet, and it's something I've never experienced with any other holiday."},{"en":"lucky money","context":"On New Year's Eve, we have dinner together, and during the following days we visit relatives, give children lucky money and pay respect to our ancestors."},{"en":"pay respect to our ancestors","context":"On New Year's Eve, we have dinner together, and during the following days we visit relatives, give children lucky money and pay respect to our ancestors."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a festival that is important in your country","id":"speaking-part-2-138","_displayNo":"#028","outline":{"topic":"Tet","intro":"most important traditional festival Vietnam late Jan/Feb rare time family together","body":["before Tet clean house prepare food buy flowers NYE dinner visit relatives lucky money pay respect ancestors sense of renewal","dislike commercial pressure travel expensive shops crowded but preserves cultural identity renews family bonds priority over work","atmosphere week before whole country in motion stations packed markets selling peach blossom kumquat trees"],"conclusion":"1st morning streets completely silent everyone indoors family contrast frantic prep vs sudden stillness never experienced any other holiday"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-138.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An important festival in Vietnam is Tet, the Lunar New Year. It usually takes place in late January or February. For my family, it is a chance to spend several days together rather than fitting visits around work.\n\nBefore Tet, we clean the house, prepare food and buy flowers. On New Year's Eve, we have a family dinner. During the following days, we visit relatives, remember our ancestors and give children lucky money. I especially like the feeling of a fresh start, when people try to put old disagreements aside.\n\nThe atmosphere changes dramatically. Before the holiday, markets and stations are crowded. On the first morning, the streets become much quieter because many people are at home with their families. I enjoy that contrast. There are disadvantages, including expensive travel and pressure to spend money on gifts and food. However, Tet remains important because it helps people maintain family connections and traditions. For those living far away, returning home often becomes the most important plan of the year.","html":"An important festival in Vietnam is Tet, the Lunar New Year. It usually takes place in late January or February. For my family, it is a chance to spend several days together rather than fitting visits around work.\n\nBefore Tet, we clean the house, prepare food and buy flowers. On New Year's Eve, we have a family dinner. During the following days, we visit relatives, remember our ancestors and give children lucky money. I especially like the feeling of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fresh start</strong>, when people try to put old disagreements aside.\n\nThe atmosphere changes dramatically. Before the holiday, markets and stations are crowded. On the first morning, the streets become much quieter because many people are at home with their families. I enjoy that contrast. There are disadvantages, including expensive travel and pressure to spend money on gifts and food. However, Tet remains important because it helps people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">maintain family connections</strong> and traditions. For those living far away, returning home often becomes the most important plan of the year.","phrases":[{"en":"a fresh start","meaning":"A chance to begin again in a more positive way."},{"en":"maintain family connections","meaning":"Keep close relationships with relatives over time."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-138.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A happy family event that you remember well","prompt":"Describe a happy family event that you remember well\nYou should say:\nwhat the event was\nwhen and where it happened\nWhat you saw or did\nand explain why you remember this event so well.","answer":"This is a lovely topic for me, because one of my most memorable childhood family events happened when I was about ten, one weekend at my parents' house — the celebration we held to welcome my nephew into the world. My eldest sister <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gave birth to</strong> a healthy baby boy, and he was the very first grandchild on either side of the family, so you can imagine how excited everyone was.\n\nAfter my father saw the baby for the first time, he announced that we simply had to celebrate properly, so the whole family <strong class=\"key-phrase\">got to work straight away</strong>. My parents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set out</strong> to invite all the relatives and do the shopping, while my grandmother and my sister spent the whole morning in the kitchen cooking enough food for what felt like the entire street.\n\nI was so excited that I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">barely left the baby's side</strong> all afternoon. I kept wanting to hold him and play with him, but he was obviously far too small for any of the toys I'd bought, so I mostly just sat there staring at his tiny face, which didn't bother me at all — I could have watched him sleep for hours.\n\nIt turned into a day full of noise, laughter and general chaos in the best possible way. Aunts, uncles and neighbours kept arriving with gifts, and by the evening our living room was completely packed. What I remember most is how one small baby managed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pull the whole family together</strong>, and that's why this event has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stayed with me ever since</strong>.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"gave birth to","context":"My eldest sister gave birth to a healthy baby boy, and he was the very first grandchild on either side of the family, so you can imagine how excited everyone was."},{"en":"set out","context":"My parents set out to invite all the relatives and do the shopping, while my grandmother and my sister spent the whole morning in the kitchen cooking enough food for what felt like the entire street."},{"en":"got to work straight away","context":"After my father saw the baby for the first time, he announced that we simply had to celebrate properly, so the whole family got to work straight away."},{"en":"barely left the baby's side","context":"I was so excited that I barely left the baby's side all afternoon."},{"en":"pull the whole family together","context":"What I remember most is how one small baby managed to pull the whole family together, and that's why this event has stayed with me ever since."},{"en":"stayed with me ever since","context":"What I remember most is how one small baby managed to pull the whole family together, and that's why this event has stayed with me ever since."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a happy family event that you remember well","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-139","_displayNo":"#029","outline":{"topic":"nephew's birth celebration","intro":"lovely topic memorable childhood family event welcomed nephew eldest sister gave birth healthy boy first grandchild","body":["father saw baby 1st time announced celebrate properly whole family got to work straight away","parents set out invite relatives shopping grandmother sister cooked whole morning","so excited barely left baby's side wanted hold play too small for toys stared at his face"],"conclusion":"day full noise laughter chaos aunts uncles neighbours gifts one small baby pulled family together stayed with me ever since"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-139.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A happy family event I remember happened when I was about ten. My eldest sister had given birth to a healthy baby boy, and our family held a celebration at my parents' home. He was the first grandchild on both sides, so everyone was excited.\n\nAfter seeing him, my father decided we should invite the relatives. My parents organised the shopping and invitations, while my grandmother and sister prepared plenty of food. Aunts, uncles and neighbours arrived with presents, and by the evening our living room was full.\n\nI spent much of the afternoon beside the baby. I wanted to hold him and show him toys, although he was far too small to play. I mostly watched him sleep and noticed how tiny his face and hands were. The day was noisy and busy, but in a happy way. What I remember most is how one new family member brought everyone together. It was one of the first times I understood why adults felt so emotional about a baby's arrival.","html":"A happy family event I remember happened when I was about ten. My eldest sister had given birth to a healthy baby boy, and our family held a celebration at my parents' home. He was the first grandchild on both sides, so everyone was excited.\n\nAfter seeing him, my father decided we should invite the relatives. My parents organised the shopping and invitations, while my grandmother and sister prepared plenty of food. Aunts, uncles and neighbours arrived with presents, and by the evening our living room was full.\n\nI spent much of the afternoon beside the baby. I wanted to hold him and show him toys, although he was far too small to play. I mostly watched him sleep and noticed how tiny his face and hands were. The day was noisy and busy, but in a happy way. What I remember most is how one new family member <strong class=\"key-phrase\">brought everyone together</strong>. It was one of the first times I understood why adults felt so emotional about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a baby's arrival</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"brought everyone together","meaning":"Gave people a reason to meet and share an experience."},{"en":"a baby's arrival","meaning":"The birth of a baby and its joining the family."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-139.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A recent happy event","prompt":"Describe a recent happy event\nYou should say:\nwhat it was\nwhen and where it happened\nwho is involved\nAnd explain why it was happy.","answer":"I'd like to talk about my brother's wedding, which I went to a couple of months ago — it's probably <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of the happiest events of my whole life</strong>, and I still remember the day pretty clearly. My brother, who's my only sibling, decided to get married last summer.\n\nSince his wife is Catholic, the wedding ended up being a mix of Catholic and traditional Vietnamese customs. Because we were on the groom's side, my family and I had to get up really early to go with my brother to the bride's house, where he formally <strong class=\"key-phrase\">asked her parents for permission</strong> to take their daughter into our family. Once that was done, everyone headed off to a fancy restaurant for the ceremony itself.\n\nI remember how beautifully the room was decorated, with strings of sparkling lights and purple ribbons everywhere, which happens to be my sister-in-law's favourite colour. During the ceremony we all watched the two of them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exchanging vows</strong>, and I have to admit it was genuinely moving — it was the first wedding I'd ever really been a proper part of, rather than just a guest, so it felt like a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">once-in-a-lifetime experience</strong> for me too.\n\nWhat struck me most was how obviously <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completely in love</strong> they were, just from the way they looked at each other while they were speaking. The whole day <strong class=\"key-phrase\">went smoothly from start to finish</strong>, and by the time the reception ended, I think every single guest had had a brilliant time.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"one of the happiest events of my whole life","context":"I'd like to talk about my brother's wedding, which I went to a couple of months ago — it's probably one of the happiest events of my whole life, and I still remember the day pretty clearly. My brother, who's my only sibling, decided to get married last summer."},{"en":"asked her parents for permission","context":"Because we were on the groom's side, my family and I had to get up really early to go with my brother to the bride's house, where he formally asked her parents for permission to take their daughter into our family."},{"en":"exchanging vows","context":"During the ceremony we all watched the two of them exchanging vows, and I have to admit it was genuinely moving — it was the first wedding I'd ever really been a proper part of, rather than just a guest, so it felt like a once-in-a-lifetime experience for me too."},{"en":"once-in-a-lifetime experience","context":"During the ceremony we all watched the two of them exchanging vows, and I have to admit it was genuinely moving — it was the first wedding I'd ever really been a proper part of, rather than just a guest, so it felt like a once-in-a-lifetime experience for me too."},{"en":"completely in love","context":"What struck me most was how obviously completely in love they were, just from the way they looked at each other while they were speaking."},{"en":"went smoothly from start to finish","context":"The whole day went smoothly from start to finish, and by the time the reception ended, I think every single guest had had a brilliant time."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a recent happy event","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-140","_displayNo":"#030","outline":{"topic":"brother's wedding","intro":"brother's wedding couple months ago one happiest events whole life brother only sibling married last summer","body":["wife Catholic mix Catholic Vietnamese customs groom's side up early bride's house asked permission","headed fancy restaurant ceremony room decorated sparkling lights purple ribbons sister-in-law's favourite colour","watched exchanging vows genuinely moving 1st wedding properly part of once-in-a-lifetime experience"],"conclusion":"struck by how completely in love they were whole day went smoothly from start to finish guests brilliant time"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-140.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A recent happy event was my brother's wedding a couple of months ago. He is my only sibling, so the occasion was especially important to me. His wife is Catholic, and the day included both Catholic and traditional Vietnamese customs.\n\nWe got up early and accompanied him to the bride's house. There, he formally asked her parents' permission before the families continued to a restaurant for the celebration. The room had sparkling lights and purple ribbons, my sister-in-law's favourite colour.\n\nDuring the ceremony, the couple exchanged promises, and I was moved by how happy they looked together. It was the first wedding where I had been closely involved instead of attending only as a guest. That made every part feel more personal. The whole day went smoothly, and people enjoyed the meal and reception. I returned home tired but very happy. I remember it not just as a beautiful wedding, but as the moment our family welcomed someone new and saw my brother begin an important new stage of life.","html":"A recent happy event was my brother's wedding a couple of months ago. He is my only <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sibling</strong>, so the occasion was especially important to me. His wife is Catholic, and the day included both Catholic and traditional Vietnamese customs.\n\nWe got up early and accompanied him to the bride's house. There, he formally asked her parents' permission before the families continued to a restaurant for the celebration. The room had sparkling lights and purple ribbons, my sister-in-law's favourite colour.\n\nDuring the ceremony, the couple exchanged promises, and I was moved by how happy they looked together. It was the first wedding where I had been closely involved instead of attending only as a guest. That made every part feel more personal. The whole day <strong class=\"key-phrase\">went smoothly</strong>, and people enjoyed the meal and reception. I returned home tired but very happy. I remember it not just as a beautiful wedding, but as the moment our family welcomed someone new and saw my brother begin an important new stage of life.","phrases":[{"en":"sibling","meaning":"A brother or sister."},{"en":"went smoothly","meaning":"Happened successfully without serious problems."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-140.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A special day out (A day out which does not cost too much)","prompt":"Describe a special day out (A day out which does not cost too much)\nYou should say:\n- Where did you go on that day.\n- What did you do.\n- Who did you go out with.\n- Why was it special to you.","answer":"A day out that didn't cost much but that I really enjoyed was an afternoon in the park with an old friend from high school. We hadn't seen each other for almost a year, and since we were both on our semester break, we decided it was finally time to meet up and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catch up over dinner</strong>.\n\nWe'd planned to meet at the park first and then decide where to go afterwards, but my friend turned up pretty late, so we just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ended up staying there</strong>. We grabbed two takeaway milk teas and some kebabs from a small <strong class=\"key-phrase\">street vendor</strong> nearby and found a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comfortable corner</strong> of the park to sit in. We spent ages just watching people walk past and chatting about whatever came into our heads. It had been raining on and off all week, so we were half expecting to get rained out, but it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be</strong> a lovely, mild evening.\n\nI think what made the day special wasn't really the plan — because we didn't stick to one — it was that even after waiting around and not going anywhere fancy, we still had far more fun than I'd expected. We caught up on everything going on in our lives and talked about our plans for the future. It was a nice feeling to realise that even though we can't meet up often, we can still fall straight back into easy conversation over milk tea and kebabs. It <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made me realise that</strong> sometimes all you really need is good company to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make something memorable</strong>.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"catch up over dinner","context":"We hadn't seen each other for almost a year, and since we were both on our semester break, we decided it was finally time to meet up and catch up over dinner."},{"en":"ended up staying there","context":"We'd planned to meet at the park first and then decide where to go afterwards, but my friend turned up pretty late, so we just ended up staying there."},{"en":"street vendor","context":"We grabbed two takeaway milk teas and some kebabs from a small street vendor nearby and found a comfortable corner of the park to sit in."},{"en":"comfortable corner","context":"We grabbed two takeaway milk teas and some kebabs from a small street vendor nearby and found a comfortable corner of the park to sit in."},{"en":"turned out to be","context":"It had been raining on and off all week, so we were half expecting to get rained out, but it turned out to be a lovely, mild evening."},{"en":"make something memorable","context":"It made me realise that sometimes all you really need is good company to make something memorable."},{"en":"made me realise that","context":"It made me realise that sometimes all you really need is good company to make something memorable."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a special day out (A day out which does not cost too much)","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-141","_displayNo":"#031","outline":{"topic":"cheap park day w/ friend","intro":"good friend high school not seen almost year","body":["actually planned meet park decide go later friend got pretty late","got two takeout milk tea glasses kebabs small street vendor found","guess makes day special fact despite wait friend show up not","nice feeling know couldn't meet talk frequently bond easily milk tea"],"conclusion":"made realise sometimes need good company good conversations make something"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-141.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A special day out that cost little was an afternoon in a park with an old high school friend. We had not seen each other for almost a year and were both on a semester break. We planned to meet before going somewhere for dinner.\n\nMy friend arrived late, so we decided to stay in the park instead. We bought two milk teas and some kebabs from a nearby street seller, then found a comfortable place to sit. Although it had rained earlier that week, the evening turned out mild and pleasant.\n\nWe talked for hours about work, study and our future plans. We also watched people passing and laughed about old memories. Nothing expensive or carefully organised happened, but I enjoyed the evening more than I expected. It was reassuring to discover that our friendship still felt easy after so much time apart. The day reminded me that good company matters more than a fancy location. Sometimes a simple drink and enough time to talk are all that is needed for a memorable outing.","html":"A special day out that cost little was an afternoon in a park with an old high school friend. We had not seen each other for almost a year and were both on a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">semester break</strong>. We planned to meet before going somewhere for dinner.\n\nMy friend arrived late, so we decided to stay in the park instead. We bought two milk teas and some kebabs from a nearby street seller, then found a comfortable place to sit. Although it had rained earlier that week, the evening turned out mild and pleasant.\n\nWe talked for hours about work, study and our future plans. We also watched people passing and laughed about old memories. Nothing expensive or carefully organised happened, but I enjoyed the evening more than I expected. It was reassuring to discover that our friendship still felt easy after so much time apart. The day reminded me that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">good company</strong> matters more than a fancy location. Sometimes a simple drink and enough time to talk are all that is needed for a memorable outing.","phrases":[{"en":"semester break","meaning":"A holiday between periods of study at a school or university."},{"en":"good company","meaning":"People whose presence makes an activity enjoyable."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-141.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An enjoyable day out in the countryside that you had","prompt":"Describe an enjoyable day out in the countryside that you had.\nYou should say:\n- where you went on this day out\n- who you were with\n- what you did\n- and explain why you enjoyed the day","answer":"I'm going to describe a recent trip to a lovely place called the Tissington Trail. The Tissington Trail is a path or track that follows the route of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a former railway line</strong> through beautiful countryside in the county of Derbyshire, here in the UK. The old railway line was taken up many years ago, and it was transformed into a recreational trail that's ideal for walkers, cyclists and horse riders.\n\nI went to the Tissington Trail because we had some family members, my brother and sister-in-law and their daughter, staying with us over the weekend. They'd come up to Manchester from London, and they wanted my wife and me to take them somewhere they could breathe in some <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fresh countryside air</strong> and get away from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the hustle and bustle</strong> of the city. To reach the trail we had to park in Tissington itself, which is a charming, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">picturesque village</strong>.\n\nWe hired some bicycles and set off along the trail. It was an easy ride because the trail is mostly flat, with just a few gentle dips and inclines, and we were able to enjoy the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stunning scenery</strong> — woodland, a dramatic limestone ravine, and grassy meadows covered with colourful wild flowers. The part of the day I enjoyed most was cycling through a historic railway tunnel.\n\nThe tunnel has been fitted with a sound installation that plays eerie steam train noises as you pass through; we all loved the idea that the sounds <strong class=\"key-phrase\">transport you back in time</strong>, like you're really on an old steam train with its whistles and clunking machinery. It was a really nice day out, and we all enjoyed relaxing in the village tearoom afterwards.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"a former railway line","context":"The Tissington Trail is a path or track that follows the route of a former railway line through beautiful countryside in the county of Derbyshire, here in the UK."},{"en":"the hustle and bustle","context":"They'd come up to Manchester from London, and they wanted my wife and me to take them somewhere they could breathe in some fresh countryside air and get away from the hustle and bustle of the city."},{"en":"fresh countryside air","context":"They'd come up to Manchester from London, and they wanted my wife and me to take them somewhere they could breathe in some fresh countryside air and get away from the hustle and bustle of the city."},{"en":"picturesque village","context":"To reach the trail we had to park in Tissington itself, which is a charming, picturesque village."},{"en":"stunning scenery","context":"It was an easy ride because the trail is mostly flat, with just a few gentle dips and inclines, and we were able to enjoy the stunning scenery — woodland, a dramatic limestone ravine, and grassy meadows covered with colourful wild flowers."},{"en":"transport you back in time","context":"The tunnel has been fitted with a sound installation that plays eerie steam train noises as you pass through; we all loved the idea that the sounds transport you back in time, like you're really on an old steam train with its whistles and clunking machinery."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an enjoyable day out in the countryside that you had","id":"speaking-part-2-142","_displayNo":"#032","outline":{"topic":"Tissington Trail trip","intro":"going describe recent trip lovely place called Tissington Trail","body":["Tissington Trail path track follows route former railway line through beautiful","come up Manchester London wanted wife take somewhere breathe fresh countryside","easy ride bc trail mostly flat w/ few gentle dips inclines"],"conclusion":"nice day out enjoyed relaxing village tearoom end bike ride"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-142.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An enjoyable countryside trip I took was to the Tissington Trail in Derbyshire. It follows an old railway route that is now used by walkers, cyclists and horse riders. I went with my wife, my brother, his wife and their daughter during a weekend visit.\n\nOur relatives had come from London and wanted a break from city life. We parked in the village of Tissington, hired bicycles and followed the trail. It was mostly flat, with gentle slopes, so the ride was comfortable. We passed trees, open fields, wildflowers and a deep rocky valley.\n\nMy favourite part was riding through an old railway tunnel. Recorded steam-train sounds played as we went through, making it easy to imagine the route's past. Everyone enjoyed that unusual detail. Afterwards, we relaxed in a village tearoom. The day was memorable because it combined gentle exercise, beautiful views and family time without feeling rushed. I liked seeing how an old railway could become a place that people of different ages can continue to enjoy.","html":"An enjoyable countryside trip I took was to the Tissington Trail in Derbyshire. It follows an old railway route that is now used by walkers, cyclists and horse riders. I went with my wife, my brother, his wife and their daughter during a weekend visit.\n\nOur relatives had come from London and wanted a break from city life. We parked in the village of Tissington, hired bicycles and followed the trail. It was mostly flat, with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gentle slopes</strong>, so the ride was comfortable. We passed trees, open fields, wildflowers and a deep rocky valley.\n\nMy favourite part was riding through an old railway tunnel. Recorded steam-train sounds played as we went through, making it easy to imagine the route's past. Everyone enjoyed that unusual detail. Afterwards, we relaxed in a village <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tearoom</strong>. The day was memorable because it combined gentle exercise, beautiful views and family time without feeling rushed. I liked seeing how an old railway could become a place that people of different ages can continue to enjoy.","phrases":[{"en":"gentle slopes","meaning":"Areas of ground that rise or fall gradually rather than steeply."},{"en":"tearoom","meaning":"A small place serving tea, cakes and light food."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-142.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An important event that you celebrated","prompt":"Describe an important event that you celebrated\nYou should say:\n- What it was\n- Where it happened\n- Why it was important\n- How people felt about it","answer":"I'd like to talk about the time I organised a birthday party for my younger sister, Linh. It was back in 2015, when she passed the university entrance exam <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with flying colours</strong> and got offers from three of the top universities in Ho Chi Minh City, which honestly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">came as a surprise</strong> to the whole family — we knew she was clever, but not that clever.\n\nSo, to mark her <strong class=\"key-phrase\">resounding success</strong> as well as her turning eighteen, I threw her a birthday party just a few days after the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">acceptance letters</strong> arrived. It wasn't a huge event, more of a family dinner at home that included just my parents and a handful of her closest friends, but I still wanted to make it one of the most memorable nights of her life.\n\nI spent the whole afternoon in the kitchen cooking several of her favourite dishes, from bun cha to pho, and I also booked a small live band to play some of her favourite songs, which made the atmosphere feel far cosier than a normal family dinner. When the cake came out, everyone sang the birthday song together while she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">blew out the candles</strong>, and we all congratulated her and wished her a brilliant few years at university.\n\nAfterwards, Linh told me it was the best birthday she'd ever had, and I could tell my parents were genuinely moved too — it definitely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">brought us closer together</strong> as a family. That's why, even now, I think of that evening as one of the happiest our family has shared.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"came as a surprise","context":"It was back in 2015, when she passed the university entrance exam with flying colours and got offers from three of the top universities in Ho Chi Minh City, which honestly came as a surprise to the whole family — we knew she was clever, but not that clever."},{"en":"resounding success","context":"So, to mark her resounding success as well as her turning eighteen, I threw her a birthday party just a few days after the acceptance letters arrived."},{"en":"acceptance letters","context":"So, to mark her resounding success as well as her turning eighteen, I threw her a birthday party just a few days after the acceptance letters arrived."},{"en":"with flying colours","context":"It was back in 2015, when she passed the university entrance exam with flying colours and got offers from three of the top universities in Ho Chi Minh City, which honestly came as a surprise to the whole family — we knew she was clever, but not that clever."},{"en":"blew out the candles","context":"When the cake came out, everyone sang the birthday song together while she blew out the candles, and we all congratulated her and wished her a brilliant few years at university."},{"en":"brought us closer together","context":"Afterwards, Linh told me it was the best birthday she'd ever had, and I could tell my parents were genuinely moved too — it definitely brought us closer together as a family."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an important event that you celebrated","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-143","_displayNo":"#033","outline":{"topic":"sister's 18th birthday party","intro":"2015 sister Linh passed uni entrance exam flying colours 3 top HCMC unis surprise whole family","body":["mark resounding success turning 18 threw party few days after acceptance letters family dinner at home","cooked favourite dishes bun cha pho booked small live band played favourite songs cosy atmosphere","cake came out sang birthday song blew out candles congratulated wished brilliant uni years"],"conclusion":"Linh said best birthday ever parents genuinely moved brought us closer together happiest family evening"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-143.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An important event I celebrated was my younger sister Linh's eighteenth birthday in 2015. It happened just after she passed her university entrance exam and received offers from three leading universities in Ho Chi Minh City.\n\nI wanted to celebrate both achievements, so I arranged a small dinner at home with our parents and a few of her closest friends. During the afternoon, I cooked some of her favourite dishes, including bun cha and pho. I also arranged for a small band to play songs she liked, which made the evening feel different from a normal family meal.\n\nWhen we brought out the cake, everyone sang and congratulated her. She blew out the candles, and we wished her well for university. Later, Linh said it was the best birthday she had ever had. That made me feel very pleased with the effort. I think the celebration brought our family closer together. It showed her that we were proud of her work and excited about the new opportunities ahead of her.","html":"An important event I celebrated was my younger sister Linh's eighteenth birthday in 2015. It happened just after she passed her <strong class=\"key-phrase\">university entrance exam</strong> and received offers from three leading universities in Ho Chi Minh City.\n\nI wanted to celebrate both achievements, so I arranged a small dinner at home with our parents and a few of her closest friends. During the afternoon, I cooked some of her favourite dishes, including bun cha and pho. I also arranged for a small band to play songs she liked, which made the evening feel different from a normal family meal.\n\nWhen we brought out the cake, everyone sang and congratulated her. She blew out the candles, and we wished her well for university. Later, Linh said it was the best birthday she had ever had. That made me feel very pleased with the effort. I think the celebration <strong class=\"key-phrase\">brought our family closer together</strong>. It showed her that we were proud of her work and excited about the new opportunities ahead of her.","phrases":[{"en":"university entrance exam","meaning":"A test used to decide whether someone can enter university."},{"en":"brought our family closer together","meaning":"Strengthened the relationships between people in our family."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-143.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting conversation you had with someone","prompt":"Describe an interesting conversation you had with someone\nYou should say:\n- who the person was\n- where the conversation took place\n- what you talked about\n- and explain why you found the conversation interesting.","answer":"I'm going to talk about an interesting conversation I had a couple of weeks ago in a music shop. I was walking along one of the main shopping streets in the city centre, just browsing on my way home from work, when a big window full of musical instruments <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caught my eye</strong>. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Out of curiosity</strong>, I went in to have a look around, and I ended up chatting to a shop assistant on the second floor, in the section <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dedicated to</strong> acoustic guitars.\n\nI hadn't planned on talking to anyone, but the assistant came over in a really friendly way and asked if I had any questions. I told him I hadn't played guitar in years, but that I was curious about the differences between all the acoustic guitars on display. He <strong class=\"key-phrase\">talked me through</strong> the different makes and models, whether they were factory-made or handmade, the types of wood and varnish used, how they each affected the sound, and of course the price range.\n\nI found the whole conversation fascinating because the assistant was so knowledgeable — it was obvious he genuinely had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a passion for guitars</strong>. He didn't seem to mind spending ages talking to me either, even though I'd <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made it pretty clear</strong> I wasn't planning to buy anything. He even picked up three or four of the guitars, one after another, and played them so I could hear the differences in their sound for myself, which I thought was a genuinely generous thing for him to do.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"caught my eye","context":"I was walking along one of the main shopping streets in the city centre, just browsing on my way home from work, when a big window full of musical instruments caught my eye."},{"en":"Out of curiosity","context":"Out of curiosity, I went in to have a look around, and I ended up chatting to a shop assistant on the second floor, in the section dedicated to acoustic guitars."},{"en":"dedicated to","context":"Out of curiosity, I went in to have a look around, and I ended up chatting to a shop assistant on the second floor, in the section dedicated to acoustic guitars."},{"en":"talked me through","context":"He talked me through the different makes and models, whether they were factory-made or handmade, the types of wood and varnish used, how they each affected the sound, and of course the price range."},{"en":"a passion for guitars","context":"I found the whole conversation fascinating because the assistant was so knowledgeable — it was obvious he genuinely had a passion for guitars."},{"en":"made it pretty clear","context":"He didn't seem to mind spending ages talking to me either, even though I'd made it pretty clear I wasn't planning to buy anything."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting conversation you had with someone you","id":"speaking-part-2-144","_displayNo":"#034","outline":{"topic":"conversation in a music shop","intro":"interesting conversation couple weeks ago music shop","body":["walking along one main shopping streets city centre large window displaying","person ended up speaking shop assistant 2nd floor area shop dedicated","talked different makes models whether factory hand made woods varnishes used","obvious passion guitar not mind talking made clear not intend buy"],"conclusion":"picked up played three four instruments demonstrate differences sound"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-144.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An interesting conversation I had was with a music shop assistant a couple of weeks ago. I was walking home from work when a window full of instruments attracted me. I went inside and reached the acoustic guitar section upstairs.\n\nThe assistant asked if I needed help. I explained that I had not played guitar for years but was curious about the different models. He described how materials, construction and price affected the instruments. He also explained the difference between handmade and factory-made guitars.\n\nWhat made the conversation special was his enthusiasm. He clearly enjoyed the subject and did not hurry me, even after I said I was not planning to buy anything. He played several guitars so I could hear how their sounds differed. This helped me understand much more than reading price labels would have done. I left feeling that I had learned something useful from someone who cared about his work. It was an unexpected reminder of how enjoyable an ordinary conversation can be when another person is willing to share their knowledge.","html":"An interesting conversation I had was with a music shop assistant a couple of weeks ago. I was walking home from work when a window full of instruments attracted me. I went inside and reached the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">acoustic guitar</strong> section upstairs.\n\nThe assistant asked if I needed help. I explained that I had not played guitar for years but was curious about the different models. He described how materials, construction and price affected the instruments. He also explained the difference between handmade and factory-made guitars.\n\nWhat made the conversation special was his <strong class=\"key-phrase\">enthusiasm</strong>. He clearly enjoyed the subject and did not hurry me, even after I said I was not planning to buy anything. He played several guitars so I could hear how their sounds differed. This helped me understand much more than reading price labels would have done. I left feeling that I had learned something useful from someone who cared about his work. It was an unexpected reminder of how enjoyable an ordinary conversation can be when another person is willing to share their knowledge.","phrases":[{"en":"acoustic guitar","meaning":"A guitar that produces sound mainly through its body rather than electronic amplification."},{"en":"enthusiasm","meaning":"A strong feeling of interest and enjoyment."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-144.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting event","prompt":"Describe an interesting event\nYou should say:\nWhat the event was\nHow you knew about this event\nWhy it was so interesting\nAnd explain your feelings towards this event.","answer":"The event I want to talk about is my best friend Huong's wedding, which happened three weeks ago and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">left a deep impression on me</strong>. She'd called me about a month before to tell me she was finally <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tying the knot</strong>, and honestly, I'd been waiting for that call for years, since I know both her and her husband really well. There was no question I'd <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take part in</strong> the day, whatever else I had on.\n\nWhat made it such a wonderful occasion was partly the setting — the room was decorated with fairy lights, candles and roses everywhere, which gave it a really cosy, romantic feel — and partly how modern the whole ceremony was. I'd only ever been to fairly traditional weddings before, so it felt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">refreshingly different</strong>.\n\nThe moment that really got me, though, was when the groom stood up and sang a song to the bride, with all of us joining in and a few people getting up to dance. He'd also put together a video of himself talking about why he loved her, and I'm not ashamed to say I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">welled up a bit</strong> watching it.\n\nOn top of all that, the wedding gave me a chance to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catch up with old friends</strong> I hadn't seen in ages. We spent a good chunk of the evening taking photos with each other and, of course, with the newly-weds.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"left a deep impression on me","context":"The event I want to talk about is my best friend Huong's wedding, which happened three weeks ago and left a deep impression on me."},{"en":"tying the knot","context":"She'd called me about a month before to tell me she was finally tying the knot, and honestly, I'd been waiting for that call for years, since I know both her and her husband really well."},{"en":"take part in","context":"There was no question I'd take part in the day, whatever else I had on."},{"en":"refreshingly different","context":"I'd only ever been to fairly traditional weddings before, so it felt refreshingly different."},{"en":"welled up a bit","context":"He'd also put together a video of himself talking about why he loved her, and I'm not ashamed to say I welled up a bit watching it."},{"en":"catch up with old friends","context":"On top of all that, the wedding gave me a chance to catch up with old friends I hadn't seen in ages. We spent a good chunk of the evening taking photos with each other and, of course, with the newly-weds."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting event","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-145","_displayNo":"#035","outline":{"topic":"best friend Huong's wedding","intro":"best friend Huong's wedding 3 weeks ago left deep impression called month before tying knot","body":["know bride groom well no question take part in day whatever else on","wonderful occasion setting fairy lights candles roses cosy romantic modern ceremony refreshingly different","groom stood up sang song bride guests joined in danced video himself welled up bit watching"],"conclusion":"chance to catch up with old friends not seen ages spent evening taking photos newly-weds"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-145.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An interesting event I attended was my best friend Huong's wedding three weeks ago. She had phoned about a month earlier to tell me the news. I knew both her and her future husband well, so I was excited to be there.\n\nThe room was decorated with small lights, candles and roses. It felt warm and romantic. The ceremony was more modern than the weddings I had attended before, which made it especially interesting. My favourite moment was when the groom sang to Huong. Guests joined in, and some people began dancing.\n\nHe had also prepared a video explaining why he loved her. I found it touching and became a little tearful. Later, I met old friends I had not seen for a long time, and we took many photographs together and with the couple. I enjoyed the event because it combined a close friend's happiness with the chance to reconnect with others. It was not just a formal ceremony. The personal songs and video helped us understand the couple's relationship and share their joy.","html":"An interesting event I attended was my best friend Huong's wedding three weeks ago. She had phoned about a month earlier to tell me the news. I knew both her and her future husband well, so I was excited to be there.\n\nThe room was decorated with small lights, candles and roses. It felt warm and romantic. The ceremony was more modern than the weddings I had attended before, which made it especially interesting. My favourite moment was when the groom sang to Huong. Guests joined in, and some people began dancing.\n\nHe had also prepared a video explaining why he loved her. I found it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">touching</strong> and became a little tearful. Later, I met old friends I had not seen for a long time, and we took many photographs together and with the couple. I enjoyed the event because it combined a close friend's happiness with the chance to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reconnect with others</strong>. It was not just a formal ceremony. The personal songs and video helped us understand the couple's relationship and share their joy.","phrases":[{"en":"touching","meaning":"Making someone feel moved by kindness, love or emotion."},{"en":"reconnect with others","meaning":"Renew relationships with people after time apart."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-145.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting event in your school","prompt":"Describe an interesting event in your school\nYou should say:\n- What the event was.\n- When the event was.\n- Why it was interesting.\n- How you felt about it.","answer":"An interesting event at my school was the annual sports day when I was about thirteen. It took place on the school field on a bright Saturday morning in early summer, and included running races, long jump, high jump, shot put and a tug-of-war competition between the different year groups.\n\nWhat made the day enjoyable was the atmosphere. Teachers and parents stood around the field and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cheered us on</strong>, while each class had its own banner. The music, announcements and friendly rivalry created <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a real sense of occasion</strong>. I entered the 100-metre race, the long jump and shot put. I'd been nervous before the first event, but once the competition began, I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pushed myself harder than usual</strong>.\n\nI eventually won the long jump with a personal best distance, and came second in the other two events. My class finished as the overall runner-up, so we weren't exactly thrilled about it, but we were still proud of our result. The head teacher presented the trophies at the end of the afternoon, and everyone stayed behind to applaud the winners, even though most of us were exhausted by then.\n\nI remember the event because it wasn't just an ordinary school day, it was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a memorable shared experience</strong>. It showed me that sport can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bring people together</strong> and give quieter students a chance to contribute. I also felt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">proud of contributing to my class</strong>, not just of winning an individual medal.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"cheered us on","context":"Teachers and parents stood around the field and cheered us on, while each class had its own banner."},{"en":"a real sense of occasion","context":"The music, announcements and friendly rivalry created a real sense of occasion."},{"en":"pushed myself harder than usual","context":"I'd been nervous before the first event, but once the competition began, I pushed myself harder than usual."},{"en":"a memorable shared experience","context":"I remember the event because it wasn't just an ordinary school day, it was a memorable shared experience."},{"en":"bring people together","context":"It showed me that sport can bring people together and give quieter students a chance to contribute."},{"en":"proud of contributing to my class","context":"I also felt proud of contributing to my class, not just of winning an individual medal."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting event in your school","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-146","_displayNo":"#036","outline":{"topic":"school sports day","intro":"took place school field included running races long jump high","body":["Teachers parents stood around field cheered class own banner","nervous before 1st event once competition began pushed myself harder usual","class finished overall runner-up slightly disappointed not win proud result","remember event bc memorable shared experience rather ordinary school day"],"conclusion":"felt proud contributing class not winning individual medal"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-146.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An interesting school event was our sports day when I was about thirteen. It took place on the school field on a sunny Saturday in early summer. There were running races, jumping events, shot put and a tug-of-war competition.\n\nI entered the hundred-metre race, long jump and shot put. Before the first event, I was nervous, but hearing teachers, parents and classmates cheering helped me try harder. Each class had a banner, and the music and announcements made the day feel special.\n\nI won the long jump with my best distance so far and came second in my other two events. Our class finished second overall. We would have liked to win, but we were still proud. At the end, the head teacher gave out trophies, and everyone applauded despite being tired. I remember the day because success felt shared. My results helped my class, and other students contributed in their own ways. It showed me that school sport can build confidence and bring people together, not simply reward the fastest or strongest person.","html":"An interesting school event was our sports day when I was about thirteen. It took place on the school field on a sunny Saturday in early summer. There were running races, jumping events, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shot put</strong> and a tug-of-war competition.\n\nI entered the hundred-metre race, long jump and shot put. Before the first event, I was nervous, but hearing teachers, parents and classmates cheering helped me try harder. Each class had a banner, and the music and announcements made the day feel special.\n\nI won the long jump with my best distance so far and came second in my other two events. Our class finished <strong class=\"key-phrase\">second overall</strong>. We would have liked to win, but we were still proud. At the end, the head teacher gave out trophies, and everyone applauded despite being tired. I remember the day because success felt shared. My results helped my class, and other students contributed in their own ways. It showed me that school sport can build confidence and bring people together, not simply reward the fastest or strongest person.","phrases":[{"en":"shot put","meaning":"A sport in which someone throws a heavy metal ball as far as possible."},{"en":"second overall","meaning":"In second place after all the results are combined."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-146.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting talk or speech you heard","prompt":"Describe an interesting talk or speech you heard\nYou should say:\nWho gave the talk/speech\nWhere and when you heard it\nWhat it was about\nAnd explain why it was interesting?","answer":"I'm going to talk about a talk I came across on TV about two months ago called The Power of Vulnerability. It's a TED talk given by Brene Brown, an American researcher and author who's spent years studying <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a range of topics</strong>, including vulnerability, courage, shame and empathy.\n\nI stumbled across it completely by accident. I'd had a pretty rough day at work and was just flicking through channels at home when I landed on it, and within about two minutes I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completely hooked</strong>. What really got to me was the way she talked about how we all try to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">numb difficult feelings</strong> — and how, when you do that, you end up numbing the good feelings too, like joy and gratitude, not just the bad ones.\n\nThat idea properly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuck with me</strong>, because it described exactly what I'd been doing for years without realising it — avoiding anything that made me feel exposed, whether that was admitting I was wrong about something or telling a friend I actually needed help. Since watching it, I've genuinely tried to be more open, even in small ways, like being honest when I'm struggling instead of just saying I'm fine.\n\nI found the talk so interesting because it wasn't full of vague advice — she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">backed everything up with</strong> years of her own research, but she also told really personal, sometimes embarrassing stories about herself, which made the whole thing feel <strong class=\"key-phrase\">honest rather than preachy</strong>. It's the kind of talk I still think about whenever I'm having a bad day.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"a range of topics","context":"It's a TED talk given by Brene Brown, an American researcher and author who's spent years studying a range of topics, including vulnerability, courage, shame and empathy."},{"en":"completely hooked","context":"I'd had a pretty rough day at work and was just flicking through channels at home when I landed on it, and within about two minutes I was completely hooked."},{"en":"numb difficult feelings","context":"What really got to me was the way she talked about how we all try to numb difficult feelings — and how, when you do that, you end up numbing the good feelings too, like joy and gratitude, not just the bad ones."},{"en":"stuck with me","context":"That idea properly stuck with me, because it described exactly what I'd been doing for years without realising it — avoiding anything that made me feel exposed, whether that was admitting I was wrong about something or telling a friend I actually needed help."},{"en":"backed everything up with","context":"I found the talk so interesting because it wasn't full of vague advice — she backed everything up with years of her own research, but she also told really personal, sometimes embarrassing stories about herself, which made the whole thing feel honest rather than preachy."},{"en":"honest rather than preachy","context":"I found the talk so interesting because it wasn't full of vague advice — she backed everything up with years of her own research, but she also told really personal, sometimes embarrassing stories about herself, which made the whole thing feel honest rather than preachy."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting talk or speech you heard","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-147","_displayNo":"#037","outline":{"topic":"Brene Brown 'Power of Vulnerability' TED talk","intro":"talk on TV 2 months ago Power of Vulnerability TED talk Brene Brown American researcher author range of topics","body":["stumbled across by accident rough day work flicking channels landed on it completely hooked","talked about how we numb difficult feelings numbing good feelings too not just bad ones","idea stuck with me described what I'd been doing avoiding feeling exposed since tried be more open"],"conclusion":"interesting bc backed up with research personal stories honest rather than preachy still think about it on bad days"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-147.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An interesting talk I heard was The Power of Vulnerability by Brene Brown. She is an American researcher and writer. I came across the talk on television about two months ago after a difficult day at work.\n\nOne idea caught my attention: when we try to avoid painful feelings completely, we may also make it harder to experience happiness and gratitude. She connected this with being willing to admit mistakes, ask for help and allow other people to see our worries.\n\nI recognised some of my own behaviour in her examples. I often said I was fine even when I was struggling because I did not want to seem weak. Since watching, I have tried to be more honest in small conversations with friends. I liked the talk because she combined research with personal stories, including experiences that were embarrassing for her. This made the message easier to understand and believe. I still think about it when I need support. It reminded me that being open about a problem can be a form of courage rather than a failure.","html":"An interesting talk I heard was The Power of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Vulnerability</strong> by Brene Brown. She is an American researcher and writer. I came across the talk on television about two months ago after a difficult day at work.\n\nOne idea caught my attention: when we try to avoid painful feelings completely, we may also make it harder to experience happiness and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gratitude</strong>. She connected this with being willing to admit mistakes, ask for help and allow other people to see our worries.\n\nI recognised some of my own behaviour in her examples. I often said I was fine even when I was struggling because I did not want to seem weak. Since watching, I have tried to be more honest in small conversations with friends. I liked the talk because she combined research with personal stories, including experiences that were embarrassing for her. This made the message easier to understand and believe. I still think about it when I need support. It reminded me that being open about a problem can be a form of courage rather than a failure.","phrases":[{"en":"Vulnerability","meaning":"Being open about feelings or difficulties even when this makes you feel exposed."},{"en":"gratitude","meaning":"A feeling of being thankful."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-147.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An outdoor meal or picnic that you had","prompt":"Describe an outdoor meal or picnic that you had\nYou should say:\n- Where it took place\n- What was the occasion\n- What you ate and drank there\nAnd explain whether you enjoyed it or not","answer":"I'll talk about a picnic four of us had by West Lake in Hanoi back in early autumn last year. There wasn't really a special occasion behind it — we're all old university friends who'd just started full-time jobs, and once term ended we hardly saw each other, so one of us suggested we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set aside a whole Sunday</strong> just to sit by the lake and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catch up properly</strong>.\n\nWe didn't buy anything ready-made. Each of us brought something we'd cooked ourselves — I made a big batch of fried rice with sausage, one friend brought spring rolls, another turned up with a huge bag of fresh fruit, and someone else made this incredibly sweet iced lotus tea in a flask. We spread an old picnic mat out on the grass near the water and laid everything out in the middle.\n\nWe ended up staying there for almost the whole afternoon, eating, playing cards, and occasionally getting up to walk along the path by the lake. At one point a stray dog wandered over and practically adopted us for an hour, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had all of us in stitches</strong>. Barely anyone checked their phone, which almost never happens these days.\n\nI really did enjoy it, probably more than any restaurant meal I've had this year. It <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wasn't fancy or expensive</strong>, but it reminded me how easy it is to have a genuinely good time as long as you're <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with the right people</strong>. We've already agreed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make it a regular thing</strong> before the weather turns cold.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"set aside a whole Sunday","context":"There wasn't really a special occasion behind it — we're all old university friends who'd just started full-time jobs, and once term ended we hardly saw each other, so one of us suggested we set aside a whole Sunday just to sit by the lake and catch up properly."},{"en":"catch up properly","context":"There wasn't really a special occasion behind it — we're all old university friends who'd just started full-time jobs, and once term ended we hardly saw each other, so one of us suggested we set aside a whole Sunday just to sit by the lake and catch up properly."},{"en":"had all of us in stitches","context":"At one point a stray dog wandered over and practically adopted us for an hour, which had all of us in stitches."},{"en":"wasn't fancy or expensive","context":"It wasn't fancy or expensive, but it reminded me how easy it is to have a genuinely good time as long as you're with the right people."},{"en":"with the right people","context":"It wasn't fancy or expensive, but it reminded me how easy it is to have a genuinely good time as long as you're with the right people."},{"en":"make it a regular thing","context":"We've already agreed to make it a regular thing before the weather turns cold."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an outdoor meal or picnic that you had","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-149","_displayNo":"#038","outline":{"topic":"picnic by West Lake with university friends","intro":"picnic four of us West Lake Hanoi early autumn last yr no special occasion just old uni friends new jobs","body":["set aside whole Sunday catch up properly everyone brought own homemade food fried rice spring rolls fruit lotus tea","spread picnic mat by water stayed almost whole afternoon eating playing cards walking","stray dog wandered over had us in stitches barely anyone checked phone"],"conclusion":"enjoyed more than any restaurant meal this year wasn't fancy or expensive right people agreed make it regular thing"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-149.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An outdoor meal I enjoyed was a picnic beside West Lake in Hanoi last autumn. There were four of us, all university friends who had recently started full-time jobs. We were seeing less of each other, so we chose a Sunday to catch up.\n\nEach person brought something homemade. I prepared fried rice with sausage, while the others brought spring rolls, fruit and iced lotus tea. We spread a mat on the grass near the water and shared everything. We stayed for most of the afternoon, eating, playing cards and taking short walks along the lake.\n\nAt one point, a stray dog came over and stayed near us for almost an hour. We found this unexpectedly funny. Most of the time, nobody checked their phone. I enjoyed the picnic more than many restaurant meals because we had enough time to talk without rushing. It cost little, but it helped us feel close again. We agreed to repeat it before the weather became cold. The afternoon showed me that good food and good company do not need an expensive setting.","html":"An outdoor meal I enjoyed was a picnic beside West Lake in Hanoi last autumn. There were four of us, all university friends who had recently started full-time jobs. We were seeing less of each other, so we chose a Sunday to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catch up</strong>.\n\nEach person brought something homemade. I prepared fried rice with sausage, while the others brought spring rolls, fruit and iced lotus tea. We spread a mat on the grass near the water and shared everything. We stayed for most of the afternoon, eating, playing cards and taking short walks along the lake.\n\nAt one point, a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stray dog</strong> came over and stayed near us for almost an hour. We found this unexpectedly funny. Most of the time, nobody checked their phone. I enjoyed the picnic more than many restaurant meals because we had enough time to talk without rushing. It cost little, but it helped us feel close again. We agreed to repeat it before the weather became cold. The afternoon showed me that good food and good company do not need an expensive setting.","phrases":[{"en":"catch up","meaning":"Share news after not seeing someone for a while."},{"en":"stray dog","meaning":"A dog without an owner looking after it in that place."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-149.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An unforgettable dinner","prompt":"Describe an unforgettable dinner\nYou should say:\n- When was the dinner.\n- Where was the dinner.\n- Who did you dine with.\n- Why is it unforgettable.","answer":"I'd like to talk about an evening meal I'll never forget: dinner at my boyfriend's parents' house last summer, the first time I met them properly. I was excited but pretty nervous — we'd been at the same high school, so I'd met his mum before, just never as his girlfriend. I picked a weekday, the tenth, because that's the date we started going out, and his parents would both be home by six.\n\nAt first we were going to eat out, but then I decided a home-cooked meal would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leave a good impression</strong> on his parents. That <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be</strong> a fairly reckless decision, because I genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">couldn't cook to save my life</strong>. After hours of googling recipes and watching cooking videos, I settled on an olive salad and roast chicken, figuring they'd be hard to mess up. While I panicked in the kitchen, my boyfriend was outside setting up the table and fairy lights on the veranda to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">create a warm atmosphere</strong>.\n\nJust as we'd finished, his parents arrived home. We sat down around a small round table, and the first few minutes were pretty awkward because I didn't know what to say. Halfway through the meal, though, his mum complimented me on the food and started telling embarrassing stories about my boyfriend as a kid, which completely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">eased my nerves</strong>. We ended up talking for hours, like old friends.\n\nI'll admit the food itself wasn't a great success — a bit too salty, and nothing like the photos I'd copied — but none of that mattered, because we had such a great time talking. That evening really helped <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strengthen our relationship</strong>, mostly because it made me feel like I was already part of his family.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"leave a good impression","context":"At first we were going to eat out, but then I decided a home-cooked meal would leave a good impression on his parents."},{"en":"turned out to be","context":"That turned out to be a fairly reckless decision, because I genuinely couldn't cook to save my life."},{"en":"couldn't cook to save my life","context":"That turned out to be a fairly reckless decision, because I genuinely couldn't cook to save my life."},{"en":"create a warm atmosphere","context":"While I panicked in the kitchen, my boyfriend was outside setting up the table and fairy lights on the veranda to create a warm atmosphere."},{"en":"eased my nerves","context":"Halfway through the meal, though, his mum complimented me on the food and started telling embarrassing stories about my boyfriend as a kid, which completely eased my nerves."},{"en":"strengthen our relationship","context":"That evening really helped strengthen our relationship, mostly because it made me feel like I was already part of his family."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an unforgettable dinner","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-150","_displayNo":"#039","outline":{"topic":"dinner at boyfriend's parents' house","intro":"evening meal never forget dinner boyfriend's parents' house last summer 1st time met them properly nervous","body":["classmates same high school met mum before never as girlfriend picked 10th of month date started dating","decided home-cooked meal leave good impression turned out reckless couldn't cook googled recipes olive salad roast chicken","boyfriend set up table fairy lights veranda warm atmosphere parents arrived awkward at first","halfway through meal mum complimented food embarrassing childhood stories eased my nerves talked for hours"],"conclusion":"food not great success but had great time talking helped strengthen relationship felt part of family"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-150.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An unforgettable dinner was at my boyfriend's parents' home last summer. It was the first time I met them properly as his girlfriend, so I felt excited and nervous. We chose the tenth because that was the date our relationship had begun.\n\nI decided to prepare a meal instead of eating out, hoping to make a good impression. Unfortunately, I was not a confident cook. After searching online, I chose roast chicken and an olive salad. My boyfriend arranged the table and small lights on the veranda while I worked in the kitchen.\n\nWhen his parents arrived, the conversation was awkward at first. Then his mother praised the food and began telling funny stories about his childhood. I relaxed, and we ended up talking for hours. The meal itself was a little too salty and looked different from the recipe photos, but nobody seemed to mind. What mattered was getting to know each other. By the end of the evening, I felt welcome in the family, which made the experience much more important than the quality of my cooking.","html":"An unforgettable dinner was at my boyfriend's parents' home last summer. It was the first time I met them properly as his girlfriend, so I felt excited and nervous. We chose the tenth because that was the date our relationship had begun.\n\nI decided to prepare a meal instead of eating out, hoping to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make a good impression</strong>. Unfortunately, I was not a confident cook. After searching online, I chose roast chicken and an olive salad. My boyfriend arranged the table and small lights on the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">veranda</strong> while I worked in the kitchen.\n\nWhen his parents arrived, the conversation was awkward at first. Then his mother praised the food and began telling funny stories about his childhood. I relaxed, and we ended up talking for hours. The meal itself was a little too salty and looked different from the recipe photos, but nobody seemed to mind. What mattered was getting to know each other. By the end of the evening, I felt welcome in the family, which made the experience much more important than the quality of my cooking.","phrases":[{"en":"make a good impression","meaning":"Help someone form a positive opinion of you."},{"en":"veranda","meaning":"A covered outdoor area along the side of a house."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-150.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An unusual dinner that you had","prompt":"Describe an unusual dinner that you had\nYou should say:\n- When you had this dinner\n- Who you had it with\n- What dishes you had\n- And explain why you think this dinner was unusual","answer":"My family usually has dinner at home with simple, traditional dishes, so the one time we went somewhere really different stands out. It was about three years ago, right after my final exams. I'd been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tied up in revision</strong> for weeks, and when I passed with flying colours, my dad decided to take the whole family out to a French restaurant downtown, partly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to ease my mind</strong> and partly to celebrate.\n\nWhat made it unusual was that we'd never eaten anywhere that fancy before — my mum and I were honestly a bit stunned when we walked in. I remember starting with a rich mushroom soup, then having steak in a red wine sauce, about as far from our usual home cooking as you could get. The restaurant's in the centre of Ho Chi Minh City, in a busy area, and it's normally packed every night. The food wasn't cheap at all; it cost my dad <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a small fortune</strong>, which made the evening feel even more special. He's not the kind of person who earns huge amounts of money, so the fact he was still willing to spend that much on us, as a reward for my results, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meant a lot to me</strong>.\n\nThe meal helped me properly unwind after months of stress — I remember just sitting there, completely relaxed <strong class=\"key-phrase\">for the first time in ages</strong>. It was also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a good chance to reconnect</strong>, since the three of us had barely seen each other in weeks.\n\nIf I ever have the money, I'd love to take my parents back to a place like that, maybe try lobster or salmon this time. For now, that one dinner is still the meal I think of whenever anyone asks about a special family occasion.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"tied up in revision","context":"I'd been tied up in revision for weeks, and when I passed with flying colours, my dad decided to take the whole family out to a French restaurant downtown, partly to ease my mind and partly to celebrate."},{"en":"to ease my mind","context":"I'd been tied up in revision for weeks, and when I passed with flying colours, my dad decided to take the whole family out to a French restaurant downtown, partly to ease my mind and partly to celebrate."},{"en":"a small fortune","context":"The food wasn't cheap at all; it cost my dad a small fortune, which made the evening feel even more special."},{"en":"meant a lot to me","context":"He's not the kind of person who earns huge amounts of money, so the fact he was still willing to spend that much on us, as a reward for my results, meant a lot to me."},{"en":"for the first time in ages","context":"The meal helped me properly unwind after months of stress — I remember just sitting there, completely relaxed for the first time in ages. It was also a good chance to reconnect, since the three of us had barely seen each other in weeks."},{"en":"a good chance to reconnect","context":"The meal helped me properly unwind after months of stress — I remember just sitting there, completely relaxed for the first time in ages. It was also a good chance to reconnect, since the three of us had barely seen each other in weeks."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an unusual dinner that you had","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-151","_displayNo":"#040","outline":{"topic":"family dinner at French restaurant","intro":"family usually eats simple traditional food at home one time went French restaurant 3 yrs ago after final exams","body":["tied up in revision weeks passed flying colours dad took family out to ease my mind celebrate","never eaten anywhere so fancy restaurant downtown HCMC packed every night cost small fortune","dad not rich still willing spend that much reward meant a lot properly unwind for 1st time in ages"],"conclusion":"good chance to reconnect busy w/ work school future want take parents back try lobster salmon"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-151.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An unusual dinner I had was at a French restaurant in central Ho Chi Minh City about three years ago. My family usually ate simple Vietnamese meals at home, so this was a different experience. My father arranged it to celebrate my good final exam results.\n\nThe restaurant felt much more formal than anywhere we normally went. I remember having mushroom soup followed by steak with a red wine sauce. My mother and I were surprised by the surroundings, and the food was very different from our usual dishes.\n\nThe meal was expensive for my father, which made his decision especially meaningful. He did not earn a great deal, but he wanted to reward my effort and give us a special evening together. After weeks of revision, I finally felt relaxed. We also had time to talk properly after being busy for so long. I would like to take my parents somewhere similar when I can afford it. For now, that dinner remains a happy memory of both a personal achievement and my father's generosity.","html":"An unusual dinner I had was at a French restaurant in central Ho Chi Minh City about three years ago. My family usually ate simple Vietnamese meals at home, so this was a different experience. My father arranged it to celebrate my good final exam results.\n\nThe restaurant felt much more formal than anywhere we normally went. I remember having mushroom soup followed by steak with a red wine sauce. My mother and I were surprised by the surroundings, and the food was very different from our usual dishes.\n\nThe meal was expensive for my father, which made his decision especially meaningful. He did not earn a great deal, but he wanted to reward my effort and give us a special evening together. After weeks of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">revision</strong>, I finally felt relaxed. We also had time to talk properly after being busy for so long. I would like to take my parents somewhere similar when I can afford it. For now, that dinner remains a happy memory of both a personal achievement and my father's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">generosity</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"revision","meaning":"Studying material again before an exam."},{"en":"generosity","meaning":"Willingness to give time, money or help to others."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-151.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A historical era you are interested in","prompt":"Describe a historical era you are interested in\nYou should say:\n- When this era was.\n- How you learned about it.\n- What it was famous for.\n- Why you are interested in this era.","answer":"If I could travel back in time, I'd choose to go to the Hong Bang period, around 2000 BC, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">right at the very beginning</strong> of my country's history. Back then this place wasn't even called Vietnam — it was ruled by the Hung Kings and known as Van Lang instead.\n\nI first learned about this era in history and literature classes at school, and what fascinates me is all the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">legends and myths</strong> that grew out of it. Van Lang was a small country, but it was invaded again and again by enemies from the north, and some of those wars are said to have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dragged on for decades</strong>. Nobody really knows how such a small population with such simple weapons survived those wars, so over the centuries people told stories to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">honour the bravery of their ancestors</strong> instead.\n\nThere's the legend of a three-year-old boy who grew into a giant overnight and rode off to fight the invaders, the myth of a magic crossbow that could fire thousands of arrows in one shot, and the tragic story of a princess who trusted the wrong man and betrayed her own father. I know they're not literally true, but stories like that still shaped how I feel about my country — I grew up <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely proud of it</strong>.\n\nWhat's interesting is that quite a few <strong class=\"key-phrase\">modern Vietnamese cultural practices</strong> actually date back to this era, like making sticky rice cakes and setting out five-fruit trays at Tet. In a way, that period is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the cradle of</strong> our culture. I'm honestly curious about where our traditions came from, and I think going back would give me a much clearer picture of where my country's identity really started.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"right at the very beginning","context":"If I could travel back in time, I'd choose to go to the Hong Bang period, around 2000 BC, right at the very beginning of my country's history. Back then this place wasn't even called Vietnam — it was ruled by the Hung Kings and known as Van Lang instead."},{"en":"legends and myths","context":"I first learned about this era in history and literature classes at school, and what fascinates me is all the legends and myths that grew out of it."},{"en":"dragged on for decades","context":"Van Lang was a small country, but it was invaded again and again by enemies from the north, and some of those wars are said to have dragged on for decades."},{"en":"honour the bravery of their ancestors","context":"Nobody really knows how such a small population with such simple weapons survived those wars, so over the centuries people told stories to honour the bravery of their ancestors instead."},{"en":"genuinely proud of it","context":"I know they're not literally true, but stories like that still shaped how I feel about my country — I grew up genuinely proud of it."},{"en":"modern Vietnamese cultural practices","context":"What's interesting is that quite a few modern Vietnamese cultural practices actually date back to this era, like making sticky rice cakes and setting out five-fruit trays at Tet."},{"en":"the cradle of","context":"In a way, that period is the cradle of our culture."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a historical era you are interested in","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-193","_displayNo":"#041","outline":{"topic":"Hong Bang period","intro":"travel back in time Hong Bang period ~2000 BC beginning of country's history ruled Hung Kings named Van Lang","body":["learned era history literature class school fascinated by legends myths small country invaded repeatedly north dragged on decades","no one knows how survived wars w/ simple weapons stories told to honour bravery of ancestors","legend of 3yo boy giant overnight magic crossbow princess betraying father not literally true but shaped pride in country"],"conclusion":"modern Vietnamese cultural practices date back era sticky rice cakes 5-fruit trays Tet cradle of culture curious about origins"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-193.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A historical period I am interested in is the Hong Bang era, associated with the Hung Kings and the early state of Van Lang. I first learned about it through school history and literature lessons.\n\nWhat attracted me most was the collection of legends connected with Vietnam's early history. Stories about a child growing into a giant hero, a magical weapon and royal families made the past feel vivid. I understand that legends are not the same as reliable historical records, but they show how later generations imagined courage, loyalty and the origins of their country.\n\nI am also curious about the beginnings of traditions that people still practise, including those connected with Tet and special foods. I would like to learn more about which customs can be traced to early communities and how they changed over time. The period interests me because it raises questions about identity rather than only dates and rulers. Understanding it better could help me see how stories about the past continue to influence the way people feel about their country today.","html":"A historical period I am interested in is the Hong Bang era, associated with the Hung Kings and the early state of Van Lang. I first learned about it through school history and literature lessons.\n\nWhat attracted me most was the collection of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">legends</strong> connected with Vietnam's early history. Stories about a child growing into a giant hero, a magical weapon and royal families made the past feel vivid. I understand that legends are not the same as reliable <strong class=\"key-phrase\">historical records</strong>, but they show how later generations imagined courage, loyalty and the origins of their country.\n\nI am also curious about the beginnings of traditions that people still practise, including those connected with Tet and special foods. I would like to learn more about which customs can be traced to early communities and how they changed over time. The period interests me because it raises questions about identity rather than only dates and rulers. Understanding it better could help me see how stories about the past continue to influence the way people feel about their country today.","phrases":[{"en":"legends","meaning":"Traditional stories that may include imagined events or characters."},{"en":"historical records","meaning":"Written or other evidence about events in the past."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-193.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A book that you enjoyed reading because it made you think deeply","prompt":"Describe a book that you enjoyed reading because it made you think deeply\nYou should say:\n- what this book was\n- why you decided to read it\n- what reading this book made you think about\n- and explain why you enjoyed reading this book.","answer":"I'd like to talk about a book that really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made me think</strong>, and that's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had a positive effect on</strong> my life. It's called Atomic Habits, by James Clear, and it's basically about the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compounding effect</strong> that small decisions and tiny changes can have on someone's life if you stick with them for long enough.\n\nI decided to read it after I saw an interview with James Clear on a podcast about a year and a half ago. He was talking about the science of tiny habits and telling stories about how something as small as making your bed every morning can eventually change your whole mindset. I found the whole idea so fascinating that I ordered the book that same evening.\n\nWhile I was reading it, I couldn't help comparing the ideas to my own life, and to be honest it wasn't always comfortable. I realised I wasn't anywhere near as productive as I thought I was, and that most of my bad habits, like checking my phone the second I wake up, were happening <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on autopilot</strong> without me even noticing. The book gave me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clear framework for understanding</strong> why habits form and how to break the ones that aren't serving me, and I've been trying to apply it ever since, starting with small things like laying out my running shoes the night before.\n\nI thoroughly enjoyed reading Atomic Habits because it wasn't just the author's opinions dressed up as advice; everything was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">backed by research</strong> and real examples. I love books that change the way I think or give me something practical I can actually use, and this one definitely did that.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"made me think","context":"I'd like to talk about a book that really made me think, and that's had a positive effect on my life."},{"en":"had a positive effect on","context":"I'd like to talk about a book that really made me think, and that's had a positive effect on my life."},{"en":"compounding effect","context":"It's called Atomic Habits, by James Clear, and it's basically about the compounding effect that small decisions and tiny changes can have on someone's life if you stick with them for long enough."},{"en":"on autopilot","context":"I realised I wasn't anywhere near as productive as I thought I was, and that most of my bad habits, like checking my phone the second I wake up, were happening on autopilot without me even noticing."},{"en":"a clear framework for understanding","context":"The book gave me a clear framework for understanding why habits form and how to break the ones that aren't serving me, and I've been trying to apply it ever since, starting with small things like laying out my running shoes the night before."},{"en":"backed by research","context":"I thoroughly enjoyed reading Atomic Habits because it wasn't just the author's opinions dressed up as advice; everything was backed by research and real examples."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a book that you enjoyed reading because it made you think deeply","id":"speaking-part-2-063","_displayNo":"#042","outline":{"topic":"Atomic Habits","intro":"book made me think, positive effect on life, James Clear, compounding effect of small habits","body":["decided to read after seeing James Clear podcast interview, science of tiny habits","reading forced me to compare ideas to my own life, bad habits happening on autopilot","book gave a clear framework for understanding how habits form and how to break them","enjoyed it because ideas were backed by research, not just personal opinion"],"conclusion":"love books that change the way I think or give me practical ideas to use"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-063.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A book that made me think is Atomic Habits by James Clear. It explains how small actions can lead to important changes if we repeat them over time. I read it after hearing the author in a podcast interview about a year and a half ago.\n\nHis ideas interested me enough to order the book that evening. While reading, I began comparing his examples with my own life. I realised that I often checked my phone as soon as I woke up without making a conscious decision. The book helped me understand how habits form and how to make helpful actions easier.\n\nFor example, I now put my running shoes out the night before I plan to exercise. It is a small change, but it makes starting easier in the morning. I enjoyed the practical examples because I could use them immediately. The book did not make my life perfect, but it encouraged me to pay attention to everyday choices. It showed me that improvement does not always require one dramatic decision.","html":"A book that made me think is Atomic Habits by James Clear. It explains how small actions can lead to important changes if we repeat them over time. I read it after hearing the author in a podcast interview about a year and a half ago.\n\nHis ideas interested me enough to order the book that evening. While reading, I began comparing his examples with my own life. I realised that I often checked my phone as soon as I woke up without making <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a conscious decision</strong>. The book helped me understand how habits form and how to make helpful actions easier.\n\nFor example, I now put my running shoes out the night before I plan to exercise. It is a small change, but it makes starting easier in the morning. I enjoyed the practical examples because I could use them immediately. The book did not make my life perfect, but it encouraged me to pay attention to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">everyday choices</strong>. It showed me that improvement does not always require one dramatic decision.","phrases":[{"en":"a conscious decision","meaning":"A choice you think about and make deliberately."},{"en":"everyday choices","meaning":"Small decisions made during ordinary daily life."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-063.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A book that you would like to read again","prompt":"Describe a book that you would like to read again\nYou should say:\n- What the book was about\n- Why you read it the first place\n- What you learned from this book\nAnd explain why you would like to read it again.","answer":"The book I'd love to read again is Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban — it's the third one in J.K. Rowling's series, and it's the one I keep going back to out of all seven. It's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fantasy novel</strong>, but this particular book reads almost like a mystery: an escaped prisoner called Sirius Black is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on the loose</strong>, and everyone believes he's hunting Harry down, so the whole school is on edge.\n\nI picked it up in the first place because my cousin forced it into my hands one summer when I was bored and complaining there was nothing to do. She'd already read the whole series twice and told me I'd regret not starting it, so eventually I gave in. I finished it in about four days.\n\nWhat <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuck with me</strong> from that book is how wrong first impressions can be. Without spoiling it for anyone who hasn't read it, there's a character everyone assumes is dangerous and one everyone trusts completely, and by the end those assumptions get <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned upside down</strong>. It taught me not to judge people too quickly, and that's genuinely shaped how I get to know people even now.\n\nThe reason I'd pick it up again rather than any other book on my shelf is that it's just so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cleverly plotted</strong> — there's a time-travel element near the end that only really makes sense once you know how the story ends, so on a reread you notice clues you missed the first time. It's also become a bit of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a comfort read</strong> for me; whenever I'm stressed with exams, going back to Hogwarts for a few chapters helps me switch off completely.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"a fantasy novel","context":"It's a fantasy novel, but this particular book reads almost like a mystery: an escaped prisoner called Sirius Black is on the loose, and everyone believes he's hunting Harry down, so the whole school is on edge."},{"en":"on the loose","context":"It's a fantasy novel, but this particular book reads almost like a mystery: an escaped prisoner called Sirius Black is on the loose, and everyone believes he's hunting Harry down, so the whole school is on edge."},{"en":"stuck with me","context":"What stuck with me from that book is how wrong first impressions can be."},{"en":"turned upside down","context":"Without spoiling it for anyone who hasn't read it, there's a character everyone assumes is dangerous and one everyone trusts completely, and by the end those assumptions get turned upside down."},{"en":"cleverly plotted","context":"The reason I'd pick it up again rather than any other book on my shelf is that it's just so cleverly plotted — there's a time-travel element near the end that only really makes sense once you know how the story ends, so on a reread you notice clues you missed the first time."},{"en":"a comfort read","context":"It's also become a bit of a comfort read for me; whenever I'm stressed with exams, going back to Hogwarts for a few chapters helps me switch off completely."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a book that you would like to read again","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-064","_displayNo":"#043","outline":{"topic":"Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban","intro":"book I'd reread, 3rd in the HP series, reads almost like a mystery, Sirius Black on the loose","body":["cousin pushed me to read it one bored summer, gave in, finished it in about four days","what stuck with me: how wrong first impressions can be, trust/danger assumptions turned upside down","cleverly plotted, time-travel element only makes full sense once you know the ending","become a comfort read, helps me switch off completely when I'm stressed with exams"],"conclusion":"would pick it up again before any other book on my shelf"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-064.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A book I would like to read again is Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban by J.K. Rowling. It is the third book in the series. The story involves an escaped prisoner, Sirius Black, who people believe is looking for Harry.\n\nMy cousin gave it to me one summer when I was bored. She loved the series and encouraged me to try it. Once I started, I became interested quickly and finished it in about four days.\n\nWhat I remember most is how the story changes our opinion of certain characters. Someone who seems dangerous may be different from what we expect. This reminded me not to judge people too quickly. I want to reread it because knowing the ending would help me notice clues I missed, especially in the part involving time travel. I also find the familiar world of Hogwarts comforting when exams are stressful. It is both an entertaining mystery and a book that helps me relax, which makes it worth returning to.","html":"A book I would like to read again is Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban by J.K. Rowling. It is the third book in the series. The story involves an escaped prisoner, Sirius Black, who people believe is looking for Harry.\n\nMy cousin gave it to me one summer when I was bored. She loved the series and encouraged me to try it. Once I started, I became interested quickly and finished it in about four days.\n\nWhat I remember most is how the story changes our opinion of certain characters. Someone who seems dangerous may be different from what we expect. This reminded me not to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">judge people too quickly</strong>. I want to reread it because knowing the ending would help me notice <strong class=\"key-phrase\">clues</strong> I missed, especially in the part involving time travel. I also find the familiar world of Hogwarts comforting when exams are stressful. It is both an entertaining mystery and a book that helps me relax, which makes it worth returning to.","phrases":[{"en":"judge people too quickly","meaning":"Form an opinion about people before knowing enough about them."},{"en":"clues","meaning":"Details that help someone understand a mystery or solve a problem."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-064.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A book you liked to read in your childhood","prompt":"Describe a book you liked to read in your childhood\nYou should say:\nwho wrote this book\nwhat it is about\nwhen you read it\nand explain why you liked it.","answer":"Well, I'd like to tell you about the book I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">was really fond of</strong> when I was little — Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone. It's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fantasy novel</strong> by the British author J.K. Rowling, and she's become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">really popular</strong> <strong class=\"key-phrase\">all over the world</strong> thanks to this one series alone.\n\nThe story follows the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">school life of a young wizard</strong>, Harry Potter, and how he ends up best friends with Ron and Hermione after a pretty rocky start on the train to Hogwarts. There's also a villain lurking in the background for most of the book, Lord Voldemort, though you don't actually find out how dangerous he really is until near the very end, when he turns out to have been hiding in plain sight the whole time.\n\nI started reading it when I was about eight. At that age I was still working hard to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improve my reading skills</strong>, and Harry Potter was a thick book for a kid my size to carry around, so it honestly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">took me ages</strong> to get through the whole thing, though I remember feeling proud every time I finished another chapter.\n\nI didn't understand every detail back then, but I loved the friendship between Harry, Ron and Hermione, and I think it actually helped me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel more confident</strong> about making friends at my own school — some of whom I still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep in touch with</strong> today. That's the main reason this book still means so much to me, even all these years later.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"was really fond of","context":"Well, I'd like to tell you about the book I was really fond of when I was little — Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone."},{"en":"a fantasy novel","context":"It's a fantasy novel by the British author J.K."},{"en":"really popular","context":"Rowling, and she's become really popular all over the world thanks to this one series alone."},{"en":"all over the world","context":"Rowling, and she's become really popular all over the world thanks to this one series alone."},{"en":"school life of a young wizard","context":"The story follows the school life of a young wizard, Harry Potter, and how he ends up best friends with Ron and Hermione after a pretty rocky start on the train to Hogwarts."},{"en":"improve my reading skills","context":"At that age I was still working hard to improve my reading skills, and Harry Potter was a thick book for a kid my size to carry around, so it honestly took me ages to get through the whole thing, though I remember feeling proud every time I finished another chapter."},{"en":"took me ages","context":"At that age I was still working hard to improve my reading skills, and Harry Potter was a thick book for a kid my size to carry around, so it honestly took me ages to get through the whole thing, though I remember feeling proud every time I finished another chapter."},{"en":"feel more confident","context":"I didn't understand every detail back then, but I loved the friendship between Harry, Ron and Hermione, and I think it actually helped me feel more confident about making friends at my own school — some of whom I still keep in touch with today."},{"en":"keep in touch with","context":"I didn't understand every detail back then, but I loved the friendship between Harry, Ron and Hermione, and I think it actually helped me feel more confident about making friends at my own school — some of whom I still keep in touch with today."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a book you liked to read in your childhood","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-065","_displayNo":"#044","outline":{"topic":"Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone","intro":"book I was really fond of as a kid, Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone, fantasy novel, J.K. Rowling","body":["story follows school life of a young wizard, friendship w/ Ron and Hermione after a rocky start","villain Voldemort lurking in the background, danger not clear until near the end","started reading around age 8, still improving reading skills, thick book took ages to finish","didn't understand every detail but loved the friendship, helped me feel more confident making friends"],"conclusion":"still keep in touch with some of those school friends today, main reason the book means so much"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-065.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A book I enjoyed as a child was Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone by J.K. Rowling. It tells the story of a young boy who discovers that he is a wizard and goes to a magical school called Hogwarts.\n\nAt school, Harry becomes friends with Ron and Hermione. Together, they face problems and learn more about the dangerous Lord Voldemort. I liked the magic, but the friendship between the three children interested me just as much.\n\nI started reading the book when I was about eight. It was much longer than the books I usually read, so finishing it took a long time. However, I felt proud whenever I completed another chapter. I did not understand every detail, but I wanted to find out what would happen next. The story also made me feel more confident about making friends at my own school. Some of those friendships have continued until today. That combination of an exciting story and happy childhood memories is why the book still matters to me.","html":"A book I enjoyed as a child was Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone by J.K. Rowling. It tells the story of a young boy who discovers that he is a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wizard</strong> and goes to a magical school called Hogwarts.\n\nAt school, Harry becomes friends with Ron and Hermione. Together, they face problems and learn more about the dangerous Lord Voldemort. I liked the magic, but the friendship between the three children interested me just as much.\n\nI started reading the book when I was about eight. It was much longer than the books I usually read, so finishing it took a long time. However, I felt proud whenever I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completed another chapter</strong>. I did not understand every detail, but I wanted to find out what would happen next. The story also made me feel more confident about making friends at my own school. Some of those friendships have continued until today. That combination of an exciting story and happy childhood memories is why the book still matters to me.","phrases":[{"en":"wizard","meaning":"A character in stories who can use magic."},{"en":"completed another chapter","meaning":"Finished reading one more section of a book."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-065.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A comedy TV series that you watch","prompt":"Describe a comedy TV series that you watch.\nYou should say:\nWhat TV series is it?\nWho are the main characters there?\nDescribe an interesting event from the series.","answer":"I'm going to talk about Friends, which is probably the comedy series I've rewatched more than anything else in my life. It's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an American sitcom</strong> from the nineties, set in New York, and even though it finished long before I was watching it properly, it's still one of those shows that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never really gets old</strong>.\n\nThere are six main characters, and the whole show revolves around their friendship group. There's Ross, who's a bit awkward and obsessed with dinosaurs, his sister Monica, who's incredibly tidy and competitive about everything, Chandler, who can't say a sentence without making a joke, Joey, who's not the sharpest but has the biggest heart, and then Rachel and Phoebe, who are both hilarious in completely different ways — Rachel's more sarcastic, and Phoebe's just wonderfully strange.\n\nOne episode that really sticks in my mind is the one where Ross tries to move a sofa up a narrow staircase with Rachel and Chandler, and he keeps shouting 'pivot!' over and over while they get it stuck at every single turn. It sounds like such a small, silly thing, but the timing and the way the actors play off each other <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had me in stitches</strong>, and it's become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a running joke</strong> among my own friends now whenever we're moving furniture.\n\nI think what makes the series so rewatchable is that the humour doesn't really rely on anything dated — it's mostly just how the characters <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bounce off one another</strong>. Whenever I've had a rough day, I put an episode on in the background, and it never fails to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cheer me up</strong>.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"an American sitcom","context":"It's an American sitcom from the nineties, set in New York, and even though it finished long before I was watching it properly, it's still one of those shows that never really gets old."},{"en":"never really gets old","context":"It's an American sitcom from the nineties, set in New York, and even though it finished long before I was watching it properly, it's still one of those shows that never really gets old."},{"en":"had me in stitches","context":"It sounds like such a small, silly thing, but the timing and the way the actors play off each other had me in stitches, and it's become a running joke among my own friends now whenever we're moving furniture."},{"en":"a running joke","context":"It sounds like such a small, silly thing, but the timing and the way the actors play off each other had me in stitches, and it's become a running joke among my own friends now whenever we're moving furniture."},{"en":"bounce off one another","context":"I think what makes the series so rewatchable is that the humour doesn't really rely on anything dated — it's mostly just how the characters bounce off one another."},{"en":"cheer me up","context":"Whenever I've had a rough day, I put an episode on in the background, and it never fails to cheer me up."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a comedy TV series that you watch","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-066","_displayNo":"#045","outline":{"topic":"Friends (TV sitcom)","intro":"Friends, an American sitcom from the 90s, rewatched more than anything else, never really gets old","body":["six main characters: Ross, Monica, Chandler, Joey, Rachel, Phoebe, each a very different personality","memorable episode: Ross moving a sofa up the stairs shouting 'pivot!', had me in stitches","become a running joke with my own friends whenever we're moving furniture","humour doesn't rely on anything dated, mostly just how the characters bounce off one another"],"conclusion":"put an episode on when I've had a rough day, it never fails to cheer me up"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-066.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A comedy series I enjoy is Friends, an American programme about six friends living in New York. It was made in the 1990s, but I still find it entertaining and often watch episodes again.\n\nEach character has a different personality. Ross is rather awkward, Monica likes everything clean, Chandler makes jokes, Joey is friendly, and Rachel and Phoebe add their own kinds of humour. Their different ways of thinking create many funny situations.\n\nOne episode I remember clearly involves Ross trying to move a sofa up a narrow staircase with Rachel and Chandler. He keeps giving instructions, but they cannot get the sofa around the corners. The problem is ordinary, yet the actors make it extremely funny. My friends and I still mention that scene when we move furniture. I like the series because the humour mainly comes from the relationships between the characters. After a difficult day, watching a short episode helps me relax and usually puts me in a better mood.","html":"A comedy series I enjoy is Friends, an American programme about six friends living in New York. It was made in the 1990s, but I still find it entertaining and often watch episodes again.\n\nEach character has a different personality. Ross is rather <strong class=\"key-phrase\">awkward</strong>, Monica likes everything clean, Chandler makes jokes, Joey is friendly, and Rachel and Phoebe add their own kinds of humour. Their different ways of thinking create many funny situations.\n\nOne episode I remember clearly involves Ross trying to move a sofa up a narrow staircase with Rachel and Chandler. He keeps giving instructions, but they cannot get the sofa around the corners. The problem is ordinary, yet the actors make it extremely funny. My friends and I still mention that scene when we move furniture. I like the series because the humour mainly comes from the relationships between the characters. After a difficult day, watching a short episode helps me relax and usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">puts me in a better mood</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"awkward","meaning":"Uncomfortable or unsure how to behave in social situations."},{"en":"puts me in a better mood","meaning":"Makes me feel happier or more relaxed."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-066.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A film that you did not enjoy","prompt":"Describe a film that you did not enjoy.\nYou should say:\nWhat the film was\nWhen you watched it\nWho you watched it with\nAnd explain why you did not enjoy this film","answer":"I'm going to tell you about the worst film I've ever sat through — Deadpool. I watched it about a year ago with my best friend, Linh, on a lazy Friday afternoon when neither of us had any plans.\n\nWe'd been wandering around town with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nothing to do</strong>, and then we spotted the poster outside the cinema and thought, why not. Fifteen minutes later we were in our seats with a box of popcorn, completely unprepared for what we were about to watch. Looking back, it was one of the worst decisions I've made in ages.\n\nThe plot follows a man whose body is experimented on to try to cure his cancer, and it ends up giving him superhuman healing powers, but the film itself is packed with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">over-the-top violence</strong> — constant fighting, blood everywhere, and jokes I found more crude than funny. While Linh was practically crying with laughter next to me, I ended up with a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">splitting headache</strong> and was seriously tempted to walk out about a third of the way through. I only stayed because she kept nudging me to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give it a chance</strong>.\n\nI know plenty of people, especially younger viewers, absolutely love it, and it clearly did well at the box office. It's just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not my kind of humour</strong>, and I couldn't get past how casually cruel it was, even toward the villains. Linh's already asked if I want to see the sequel with her, but honestly, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">once was more than enough</strong> for me.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"nothing to do","context":"We'd been wandering around town with nothing to do, and then we spotted the poster outside the cinema and thought, why not."},{"en":"over-the-top violence","context":"The plot follows a man whose body is experimented on to try to cure his cancer, and it ends up giving him superhuman healing powers, but the film itself is packed with over-the-top violence — constant fighting, blood everywhere, and jokes I found more crude than funny."},{"en":"splitting headache","context":"While Linh was practically crying with laughter next to me, I ended up with a splitting headache and was seriously tempted to walk out about a third of the way through."},{"en":"give it a chance","context":"I only stayed because she kept nudging me to give it a chance."},{"en":"not my kind of humour","context":"It's just not my kind of humour, and I couldn't get past how casually cruel it was, even toward the villains."},{"en":"once was more than enough","context":"Linh's already asked if I want to see the sequel with her, but honestly, once was more than enough for me."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a film that you did not enjoy","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-067","_displayNo":"#046","outline":{"topic":"Deadpool","intro":"worst film I've sat through, Deadpool, watched with best friend Linh, lazy Friday afternoon","body":["wandering around town w/ nothing to do, saw the poster, went in on a whim","plot: man experimented on to cure cancer, gets healing powers, but film full of over-the-top violence","Linh loved it, I got a splitting headache, nearly walked out a third of the way through","not my kind of humour, casually cruel even towards the villains"],"conclusion":"Linh wants to watch the sequel, but once was more than enough for me"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-067.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A film I did not enjoy was Deadpool. I watched it with my best friend, Linh, about a year ago. We had no plans on a Friday afternoon, saw a poster outside a cinema and decided to buy tickets.\n\nThe story follows a man who receives unusual healing powers after medical experiments. I expected an exciting superhero film, but there was much more violence than I wanted. The fighting and blood made me uncomfortable, and I did not enjoy many of the jokes.\n\nLinh found the film very funny, while I developed a headache and considered leaving early. I stayed because she wanted me to give it a chance. I understand that many people like this kind of humour and action, but it did not suit my taste. The experience reminded me that friends can enjoy very different things. Next time, I would check a film's style more carefully before choosing it. Linh has suggested watching the next film, but I think I would prefer a different cinema trip.","html":"A film I did not enjoy was Deadpool. I watched it with my best friend, Linh, about a year ago. We had no plans on a Friday afternoon, saw a poster outside a cinema and decided to buy tickets.\n\nThe story follows a man who receives unusual healing powers after medical experiments. I expected an exciting superhero film, but there was much more violence than I wanted. The fighting and blood made me uncomfortable, and I did not enjoy many of the jokes.\n\nLinh found the film very funny, while I developed a headache and considered leaving early. I stayed because she wanted me to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give it a chance</strong>. I understand that many people like this kind of humour and action, but it did not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suit my taste</strong>. The experience reminded me that friends can enjoy very different things. Next time, I would check a film's style more carefully before choosing it. Linh has suggested watching the next film, but I think I would prefer a different cinema trip.","phrases":[{"en":"give it a chance","meaning":"Try something properly before deciding whether you like it."},{"en":"suit my taste","meaning":"Match the kind of thing I personally enjoy."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-067.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A film that you would like to see in the future","prompt":"Describe a film that you would like to see in the future.\nYou should say:\n- what the film is called\n- what it is about\n- how you heard about this film\n- and why you would like to see it.","answer":"I'm going to talk about a film I'd love to see once it comes out later this year — the new James Bond film, Spectre. I saw in the news that Spectre is the name of the imaginary criminal organisation that Bond has to defeat in this one.\n\nSince it hasn't been released yet, I don't know exactly what it'll be about, but I can guess that Bond's going to be on a mission to save the world from some arch-criminal, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as usual</strong>. I'm sure it'll be similar to most of the previous films, with a plot full of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">twists and turns</strong>, and Bond using his skills to outsmart his nemesis right at the end.\n\nI first heard about it while I was watching the news a couple of months back, and since then I've seen the official trailer on TV. There have also been several newspaper articles about which actors have been cast to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">play supporting roles in</strong> it. Bond films are always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">big news</strong> here in the UK, probably because Bond's an English character.\n\nI'd like to catch Spectre at the cinema because I'm sure it'll be full of action scenes and special effects, and I love how Bond's missions always take place in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spectacular locations</strong> all over the world. Bond films are completely unrealistic, ridiculous even, but they're always fast, entertaining and fun — a bit like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a roller-coaster ride</strong>.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"as usual","context":"Since it hasn't been released yet, I don't know exactly what it'll be about, but I can guess that Bond's going to be on a mission to save the world from some arch-criminal, as usual."},{"en":"twists and turns","context":"I'm sure it'll be similar to most of the previous films, with a plot full of twists and turns, and Bond using his skills to outsmart his nemesis right at the end."},{"en":"play supporting roles in","context":"There have also been several newspaper articles about which actors have been cast to play supporting roles in it."},{"en":"big news","context":"Bond films are always big news here in the UK, probably because Bond's an English character."},{"en":"spectacular locations","context":"I'd like to catch Spectre at the cinema because I'm sure it'll be full of action scenes and special effects, and I love how Bond's missions always take place in spectacular locations all over the world."},{"en":"a roller-coaster ride","context":"Bond films are completely unrealistic, ridiculous even, but they're always fast, entertaining and fun — a bit like a roller-coaster ride."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a film that you would like to see in the future","id":"speaking-part-2-068","_displayNo":"#047","outline":{"topic":"Spectre","intro":"film like see comes out later year","body":["It's new James Bond film saw news it's going called Spectre’","sure similar previous Bond films w/ plot line involving lots twists","James Bond films always big news UK probably bc Bond himself","like see Spectre’ cinema bc sure full action scenes special effects"],"conclusion":"they're always fast entertaining fun bit like roller-coaster ride"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-068.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A film I would like to see is Spectre, one of the James Bond films. I first heard about it in the news and later saw a trailer on television. The name refers to a criminal organisation in the story.\n\nI have not watched the film, so I do not know every detail of the plot. However, I expect Bond to face a dangerous enemy and complete a difficult mission. I enjoy the surprises, action scenes and unusual equipment that appear in these films. The international locations are another attraction because they allow me to see places I might never visit myself.\n\nI would prefer to watch it at the cinema. A large screen and strong sound would make the action and special effects more exciting. I know James Bond stories are not very realistic, but that does not stop me enjoying them. Sometimes I want a film that is fast and entertaining rather than serious. I hope Spectre gives me that kind of enjoyable escape for a couple of hours.","html":"A film I would like to see is Spectre, one of the James Bond films. I first heard about it in the news and later saw a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trailer</strong> on television. The name refers to a criminal organisation in the story.\n\nI have not watched the film, so I do not know every detail of the plot. However, I expect Bond to face a dangerous enemy and complete a difficult mission. I enjoy the surprises, action scenes and unusual equipment that appear in these films. The international locations are another attraction because they allow me to see places I might never visit myself.\n\nI would prefer to watch it at the cinema. A large screen and strong sound would make the action and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">special effects</strong> more exciting. I know James Bond stories are not very realistic, but that does not stop me enjoying them. Sometimes I want a film that is fast and entertaining rather than serious. I hope Spectre gives me that kind of enjoyable escape for a couple of hours.","phrases":[{"en":"trailer","meaning":"A short video showing parts of a film to advertise it."},{"en":"special effects","meaning":"Images or sounds created to show things that would be difficult to film normally."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-068.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A foreign food you would like to try","prompt":"Describe a foreign food you would like to try.\nYou should say:\nWhat it is\nWhere you can eat it\nHow you know about this food\nAnd explain why you would like to try it","answer":"Today I'd like to tell you about crepes — one of the most popular French street foods, and something I've been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">longing to try</strong> for ages. It's basically a thin pancake, and depending on whether you go sweet or savoury, it gets filled with anything from chocolate and banana to ham and cheese.\n\nI first got interested in it after watching a TV programme about food from different cultures, maybe a year or so ago. The episode went into a lot of detail about how crepes are made, what fillings are typical, and why they're such a big part of French <strong class=\"key-phrase\">street food culture</strong>. I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completely hooked</strong>, and I ended up on my laptop straight after the show researching recipes.\n\nApparently if you walk around Paris, you'll find street stalls selling crepes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on almost every corner</strong>, and it's the kind of snack people grab in the afternoon without ruining their appetite for dinner. There are a few places back home in Hanoi that do a decent version, but I doubt it comes close to the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">authentic recipe</strong> you'd get from an actual French street stall.\n\nEver since watching that programme, walking down a street in Paris with a warm crepe in my hand has been right at the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">top of my wish list</strong>. I've actually started putting a bit of money aside recently, hoping I'll get the chance to make that happen sometime soon.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"longing to try","context":"Today I'd like to tell you about crepes — one of the most popular French street foods, and something I've been longing to try for ages."},{"en":"street food culture","context":"The episode went into a lot of detail about how crepes are made, what fillings are typical, and why they're such a big part of French street food culture."},{"en":"completely hooked","context":"I was completely hooked, and I ended up on my laptop straight after the show researching recipes."},{"en":"on almost every corner","context":"Apparently if you walk around Paris, you'll find street stalls selling crepes on almost every corner, and it's the kind of snack people grab in the afternoon without ruining their appetite for dinner."},{"en":"authentic recipe","context":"There are a few places back home in Hanoi that do a decent version, but I doubt it comes close to the authentic recipe you'd get from an actual French street stall."},{"en":"top of my wish list","context":"Ever since watching that programme, walking down a street in Paris with a warm crepe in my hand has been right at the top of my wish list."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a foreign food you would like to try","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-069","_displayNo":"#048","outline":{"topic":"crepes","intro":"crepes one popular French street foods longing try","body":["show gave detailed description prepare dish ingredients required different fillings used","watching w/ utmost interest attention immediately jumped onto computer research after","It's said walking around Paris locate street stands selling crepes everywhere","not hard find crepe shop Hanoi believe taste cannot compared authentic"],"conclusion":"watching programme idea walking down street Paris enjoying delicious crepe"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-069.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A foreign food I would like to try is a crepe, a thin French pancake. It can have sweet fillings, such as chocolate and banana, or savoury ones, such as ham and cheese. I like the idea of having several choices in one simple dish.\n\nI became interested after watching a television programme about food from different cultures about a year ago. It showed how crepes are made and why they are popular as street food in France. Afterwards, I looked up recipes online because I wanted to learn more.\n\nI know there are places in Hanoi selling crepes, but I would especially like to try one from a street stall in Paris. Walking around with a warm pancake would make the experience feel more special than simply ordering it at home. I have started saving a little money towards a future trip. Of course, there would be many things to see in Paris, but trying a freshly made crepe is one of the small experiences I would enjoy most.","html":"A foreign food I would like to try is a crepe, a thin French pancake. It can have sweet fillings, such as chocolate and banana, or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">savoury</strong> ones, such as ham and cheese. I like the idea of having several choices in one simple dish.\n\nI became interested after watching a television programme about food from different cultures about a year ago. It showed how crepes are made and why they are popular as street food in France. Afterwards, I looked up recipes online because I wanted to learn more.\n\nI know there are places in Hanoi selling crepes, but I would especially like to try one from a street stall in Paris. Walking around with a warm pancake would make the experience feel more special than simply ordering it at home. I have started saving a little money towards a future trip. Of course, there would be many things to see in Paris, but trying a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">freshly made</strong> crepe is one of the small experiences I would enjoy most.","phrases":[{"en":"savoury","meaning":"Having a salty or spicy taste rather than a sweet one."},{"en":"freshly made","meaning":"Prepared recently, just before being served or used."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-069.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A kind of music or a song","prompt":"Describe a kind of music or a song\nYou should say:\nWhat kind of music it is\nWhere you usually listen to it\nWhy you like it\nAnd say what kind of mood this music puts you in","answer":"I like to think of myself as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a lover of music</strong> that's kind of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sentimental and romantic</strong>. I listen to loads of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">different music genres</strong>, but the one I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">particularly enjoy</strong> most is ballads — that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">slow tempo</strong>, sad, heart-breaking kind of song.\n\nI mostly listen on my phone with my headphones in, so honestly you'll catch me playing ballads pretty much anywhere — on the bus, doing chores, lying in bed before I fall asleep. I'm quite a sentimental person by nature, so whenever I'm feeling low, I'll just put one on and let the melody say what I can't.\n\nBallads give me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of nostalgia</strong> — they bring back memories of my childhood, old friends, past relationships, all of it. Every time a particular ballad comes on, I'm suddenly back at some specific moment, like messing around with my friends after school or a long car ride with my family. It's like the genre <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unlocks my past</strong> for me.\n\nThe mood it puts me in is a strange mix of sad and comforting at the same time. I feel a bit wistful that those days are behind me, but I also find myself looking forward to whatever's still ahead. And if I happen to fall asleep with a ballad playing, I sometimes dream about those exact memories, which honestly leaves me smiling when I wake up. It really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ticks every box</strong> I look for in a genre of music.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"a lover of music","context":"I like to think of myself as a lover of music that's kind of sentimental and romantic. I listen to loads of different music genres, but the one I particularly enjoy most is ballads — that slow tempo, sad, heart-breaking kind of song."},{"en":"sentimental and romantic","context":"I like to think of myself as a lover of music that's kind of sentimental and romantic. I listen to loads of different music genres, but the one I particularly enjoy most is ballads — that slow tempo, sad, heart-breaking kind of song."},{"en":"different music genres","context":"I like to think of myself as a lover of music that's kind of sentimental and romantic. I listen to loads of different music genres, but the one I particularly enjoy most is ballads — that slow tempo, sad, heart-breaking kind of song."},{"en":"particularly enjoy","context":"I like to think of myself as a lover of music that's kind of sentimental and romantic. I listen to loads of different music genres, but the one I particularly enjoy most is ballads — that slow tempo, sad, heart-breaking kind of song."},{"en":"slow tempo","context":"I like to think of myself as a lover of music that's kind of sentimental and romantic. I listen to loads of different music genres, but the one I particularly enjoy most is ballads — that slow tempo, sad, heart-breaking kind of song."},{"en":"a sense of nostalgia","context":"Ballads give me a sense of nostalgia — they bring back memories of my childhood, old friends, past relationships, all of it."},{"en":"unlocks my past","context":"It's like the genre unlocks my past for me."},{"en":"ticks every box","context":"It really ticks every box I look for in a genre of music."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a kind of music or a song","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-070","_displayNo":"#049","outline":{"topic":"ballads","intro":"love listen different music genres one particularly enjoy ballads","body":["usually listen kinds songs computer MP3 player w/ headphones basically see","time ballad played remember childhood sweet memories reckless moments w/ friends","genre key opens up door past","feel sad past days gone yet wonder future moments w/ special"],"conclusion":"everything seek comes music genre"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-070.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A kind of music I particularly enjoy is ballads. These are usually slower songs about love, sadness or important feelings. I listen to other styles too, but I return to ballads most often because they match my personality.\n\nI normally listen on my phone with headphones. I play them on the bus, while doing housework or before going to sleep. When I feel low, a familiar melody can help me express feelings that are difficult to explain.\n\nMany songs also bring back memories. A particular ballad might remind me of school friends, a family car journey or an old relationship. This can make me feel a little sad because that time has passed, but it is also comforting to remember it. Sometimes I fall asleep listening and wake up thinking about those happy moments. I like music that has an emotional connection with my own life. For me, ballads offer more than background sound; they help me relax and remember people and experiences that matter.","html":"A kind of music I particularly enjoy is ballads. These are usually slower songs about love, sadness or important feelings. I listen to other styles too, but I return to ballads most often because they match my personality.\n\nI normally listen on my phone with headphones. I play them on the bus, while doing housework or before going to sleep. When I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel low</strong>, a familiar melody can help me express feelings that are difficult to explain.\n\nMany songs also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bring back memories</strong>. A particular ballad might remind me of school friends, a family car journey or an old relationship. This can make me feel a little sad because that time has passed, but it is also comforting to remember it. Sometimes I fall asleep listening and wake up thinking about those happy moments. I like music that has an emotional connection with my own life. For me, ballads offer more than background sound; they help me relax and remember people and experiences that matter.","phrases":[{"en":"feel low","meaning":"Feel sad or unhappy."},{"en":"bring back memories","meaning":"Make someone remember things from the past."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-070.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A memorable story told by someone","prompt":"Describe a memorable story told by someone\nYou should say:\nWho told you the story?\nWhen you heard the story\nWhat the story was about\nWhy you found it memorable.","answer":"I'd like to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">share a funny story</strong> I heard from my friend Julie a couple of years ago, back when we were both in our second year at university. Our little group used to hang out most weekends at a coffee shop near campus, always at the same circular table by the window — it was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">our favourite spot</strong>.\n\nThat particular day, instead of ordering my usual cappuccino, I decided to try <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a completely new drink</strong> — some kind of mojito mixed with fruit, though I honestly can't remember which fruit anymore. It looked great, but the taste was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unbearable to me</strong>. Julie insisted on trying a sip anyway, and to my surprise she loved it — she said it tasted exactly like the toothpaste she used to eat as a kid.\n\nWe were all a bit stunned, so naturally we made her explain herself. She told us she'd genuinely thought toothpaste tasted amazing, and even though her parents banned her from eating it, she secretly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saved money to buy</strong> her own tube a few years later, hid it in her room, and finished the whole thing in one sitting. There was this dead silence for a couple of seconds while we all just stared at her, and then the whole table <strong class=\"key-phrase\">burst into laughter</strong>. I remember laughing so hard my eyes were watering.\n\nIt wasn't the first time I'd heard of someone enjoying the taste of toothpaste, but the secret saving-up-to-buy-it part was such an unexpected detail that it made the whole story hilarious. It's honestly become a running joke between the two of us ever since.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"share a funny story","context":"I'd like to share a funny story I heard from my friend Julie a couple of years ago, back when we were both in our second year at university."},{"en":"our favourite spot","context":"Our little group used to hang out most weekends at a coffee shop near campus, always at the same circular table by the window — it was our favourite spot."},{"en":"a completely new drink","context":"That particular day, instead of ordering my usual cappuccino, I decided to try a completely new drink — some kind of mojito mixed with fruit, though I honestly can't remember which fruit anymore."},{"en":"unbearable to me","context":"It looked great, but the taste was unbearable to me."},{"en":"saved money to buy","context":"She told us she'd genuinely thought toothpaste tasted amazing, and even though her parents banned her from eating it, she secretly saved money to buy her own tube a few years later, hid it in her room, and finished the whole thing in one sitting."},{"en":"burst into laughter","context":"There was this dead silence for a couple of seconds while we all just stared at her, and then the whole table burst into laughter."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a memorable story told by someone","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-071","_displayNo":"#050","outline":{"topic":"toothpaste story","intro":"like share funny story heard Julie friend long time ago","body":["day instead ordering cappuccino usually decided try completely new drink","recalled indeed thought taste toothpaste delicious banned eating parents secretly saved","added sneakily hid tube room finish whole thing one go","remember laughing hard tears ran down"],"conclusion":"course become inside joke between then"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-071.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A memorable story I heard was told by my friend Julie during our second year at university. Our group was sitting at our usual table in a cafe near campus. I had ordered an unfamiliar fruit drink instead of my normal coffee.\n\nI disliked the taste, but Julie tried it and said she loved it because it reminded her of toothpaste. We were surprised and asked what she meant. She explained that she had enjoyed eating toothpaste as a child. Her parents had stopped her, but later she secretly saved money, bought a tube and ate it in her room.\n\nFor a moment, everyone was silent. Then we all started laughing, and I laughed so much that my eyes watered. It was the unexpected detail about saving money that made the story especially funny. We knew eating toothpaste was not a good idea, but her way of telling this childhood memory was unforgettable. Years later, we still mention it as a private joke whenever we see a strange-looking drink.","html":"A memorable story I heard was told by my friend Julie during our second year at university. Our group was sitting at our usual table in a cafe near campus. I had ordered an unfamiliar fruit drink instead of my normal coffee.\n\nI disliked the taste, but Julie tried it and said she loved it because it reminded her of toothpaste. We were surprised and asked what she meant. She explained that she had enjoyed eating toothpaste as a child. Her parents had stopped her, but later she secretly saved money, bought a tube and ate it in her room.\n\nFor a moment, everyone was silent. Then we all started laughing, and I laughed so much that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">my eyes watered</strong>. It was the unexpected detail about saving money that made the story especially funny. We knew eating toothpaste was not a good idea, but her way of telling this childhood memory was unforgettable. Years later, we still mention it as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a private joke</strong> whenever we see a strange-looking drink.","phrases":[{"en":"my eyes watered","meaning":"Tears came into my eyes, for example from laughing a lot."},{"en":"a private joke","meaning":"Something funny understood mainly by a particular person or small group."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-071.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A movie you would like to watch again","prompt":"Describe a movie you would like to watch again.\nYou should say:\nWhat it was about\nWhen and where you watched it\nWho you watched it with\nAnd explain why you would like to watch it again.","answer":"The film I'd love to watch again is Love, Rosie — <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a romantic comedy starring</strong> Lily Collins and Sam Claflin. It's actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an adaptation of a book</strong> called Where Rainbows End, which was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an Irish best seller</strong> back in 2004.\n\nI remember watching it for the first time in 2014, not long after it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hit the big screen</strong>. I went with my best friend, who's just as much of a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">film buff</strong> as I am. We'd been scrolling through trailers online, and this one caught our attention straight away because of the cinematography and the soundtrack, so we booked tickets that same evening.\n\nIt ended up completely exceeding our expectations. The story follows two childhood friends who clearly have feelings for each other but keep missing their chance to say so, over years and years of near-misses. It does end happily, with them finally finding their way back to each other, but there are so many heartbreaking moments along the way that I was properly crying in the cinema by the end.\n\nThe main reason I'd watch it again is the dialogue — it's witty, but it's also full of these little lines about life and love that actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuck with me</strong>. I could relate to so much of what the characters said, and I think I'd pick up on even more of it on a second watch. That's why it's stayed one of my favourites.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"a romantic comedy starring","context":"The film I'd love to watch again is Love, Rosie — a romantic comedy starring Lily Collins and Sam Claflin. It's actually an adaptation of a book called Where Rainbows End, which was an Irish best seller back in 2004."},{"en":"an adaptation of a book","context":"The film I'd love to watch again is Love, Rosie — a romantic comedy starring Lily Collins and Sam Claflin. It's actually an adaptation of a book called Where Rainbows End, which was an Irish best seller back in 2004."},{"en":"an Irish best seller","context":"The film I'd love to watch again is Love, Rosie — a romantic comedy starring Lily Collins and Sam Claflin. It's actually an adaptation of a book called Where Rainbows End, which was an Irish best seller back in 2004."},{"en":"hit the big screen","context":"I remember watching it for the first time in 2014, not long after it hit the big screen."},{"en":"film buff","context":"I went with my best friend, who's just as much of a film buff as I am."},{"en":"stuck with me","context":"The main reason I'd watch it again is the dialogue — it's witty, but it's also full of these little lines about life and love that actually stuck with me."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a movie you would like to watch again","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-072","_displayNo":"#051","outline":{"topic":"Love, Rosie","intro":"film I'd watch again, Love Rosie, romantic comedy starring Lily Collins and Sam Claflin, adapted from Where Rainbows End","body":["watched it first in 2014 not long after release, went with my best friend (a film buff), trailer caught our attention","story: two childhood friends who keep missing their chance, happy ending but heartbreaking moments, cried in the cinema","main reason to rewatch: witty dialogue full of lines about life and love that stuck with me","think I'd notice even more of it on a second watch"],"conclusion":"stayed one of my favourite films"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-072.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A film I would like to watch again is Love, Rosie, a romantic comedy starring Lily Collins and Sam Claflin. It is based on the book Where Rainbows End. I first watched it at the cinema in 2014 with my best friend.\n\nWe chose it after seeing a trailer online. The pictures and music attracted us, so we booked tickets that evening. The story follows two childhood friends who have feelings for each other but repeatedly miss the chance to say so. They eventually find their way back together, although many sad things happen first.\n\nI enjoyed the film more than I expected and cried during some scenes. The main reason I want to see it again is the dialogue. Some conversations are funny, while others contain ideas about love and life that I understood personally. A second viewing might help me notice details I missed. I also like films that make me feel several emotions rather than only entertaining me. This one stayed in my mind long after leaving the cinema.","html":"A film I would like to watch again is Love, Rosie, a romantic comedy starring Lily Collins and Sam Claflin. It is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">based on</strong> the book Where Rainbows End. I first watched it at the cinema in 2014 with my best friend.\n\nWe chose it after seeing a trailer online. The pictures and music attracted us, so we booked tickets that evening. The story follows two childhood friends who have feelings for each other but repeatedly miss the chance to say so. They eventually find their way back together, although many sad things happen first.\n\nI enjoyed the film more than I expected and cried during some scenes. The main reason I want to see it again is the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dialogue</strong>. Some conversations are funny, while others contain ideas about love and life that I understood personally. A second viewing might help me notice details I missed. I also like films that make me feel several emotions rather than only entertaining me. This one stayed in my mind long after leaving the cinema.","phrases":[{"en":"based on","meaning":"Developed from an existing story or idea."},{"en":"dialogue","meaning":"The words spoken between characters in a film or story."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-072.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A photo you have taken","prompt":"Describe a photo you have taken\nYou should say:\n- Where you took it.\n- When you took the photo.\n- What/who is in the photo.\n- How you felt about the photo.","answer":"I'm going to talk about a photo I took a few years ago, back when I was around fourteen, on a family holiday to Phan Thiet beach. It's a photo that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">captured the memory of</strong> a really happy family, and it's honestly a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">priceless possession</strong> to me — I'd like to keep it with me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">for the rest of my life</strong>.\n\nI've looked at it hundreds of times, and it still gives me this <strong class=\"key-phrase\">indescribable warm feeling</strong>. Everyone in it is smiling — my dad's eyes are actually sparkling, with these little crinkles at the corners, which is rare because he's normally quite serious-looking in photos. My mum's standing right next to him, glowing with this really warm happiness. My younger brother and sister aren't exactly photogenic in it, though — they're both mid-laugh about something I can't even remember anymore.\n\nIn the background, the sun's just about to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dip below the horizon</strong>, and the whole sky's this mix of reds, oranges and yellows, with the water reflecting it all back. It's honestly a beautiful scene on its own, even without the family standing there in it.\n\nI'll admit I know next to nothing about the technical side of photography — I just take photos based on what looks good to me rather than doing anything a professional would do. But even though it wasn't shot professionally, it's still the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">most treasured photo</strong> I own.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"captured the memory of","context":"It's a photo that captured the memory of a really happy family, and it's honestly a priceless possession to me — I'd like to keep it with me for the rest of my life."},{"en":"priceless possession","context":"It's a photo that captured the memory of a really happy family, and it's honestly a priceless possession to me — I'd like to keep it with me for the rest of my life."},{"en":"for the rest of my life","context":"It's a photo that captured the memory of a really happy family, and it's honestly a priceless possession to me — I'd like to keep it with me for the rest of my life."},{"en":"indescribable warm feeling","context":"I've looked at it hundreds of times, and it still gives me this indescribable warm feeling."},{"en":"dip below the horizon","context":"In the background, the sun's just about to dip below the horizon, and the whole sky's this mix of reds, oranges and yellows, with the water reflecting it all back. It's honestly a beautiful scene on its own, even without the family standing there in it."},{"en":"most treasured photo","context":"But even though it wasn't shot professionally, it's still the most treasured photo I own."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a photo you have taken","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-073","_displayNo":"#052","outline":{"topic":"family photo at Phan Thiet Beach","intro":"photo that captured the memory of a happy family, taken around age 14 on a family holiday","body":["looked at it hundreds of times, dad's rare sparkling smile, mum glowing next to him","younger siblings not photogenic, both mid-laugh about something now forgotten","background: sun dipping below the horizon, sky red/orange/yellow, water reflecting it","know little about photography technically, took it based on instinct not professional skill"],"conclusion":"not professionally shot but still the most treasured photo I own"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-073.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A photo I treasure is one I took during a family holiday to Phan Thiet when I was about fourteen. It shows my parents and my younger brother and sister at the beach near sunset.\n\nEveryone looks happy in the picture. My father, who is usually serious in photographs, has a clear smile. My mother is standing beside him, and my brother and sister are laughing at something I can no longer remember. Behind them, the sky is red, orange and yellow, and the sea reflects those colours.\n\nI do not know much about professional photography. I simply took the picture because I liked what I saw. However, it became one of my most important possessions. Whenever I look at it, I remember how relaxed we all felt on that holiday. It is special because it records a real family moment rather than a carefully arranged pose. I hope to keep it for the rest of my life, even as other photos and memories become less clear.","html":"A photo I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treasure</strong> is one I took during a family holiday to Phan Thiet when I was about fourteen. It shows my parents and my younger brother and sister at the beach near sunset.\n\nEveryone looks happy in the picture. My father, who is usually serious in photographs, has a clear smile. My mother is standing beside him, and my brother and sister are laughing at something I can no longer remember. Behind them, the sky is red, orange and yellow, and the sea reflects those colours.\n\nI do not know much about professional photography. I simply took the picture because I liked what I saw. However, it became one of my most important possessions. Whenever I look at it, I remember how relaxed we all felt on that holiday. It is special because it records a real family moment rather than a carefully <strong class=\"key-phrase\">arranged pose</strong>. I hope to keep it for the rest of my life, even as other photos and memories become less clear.","phrases":[{"en":"treasure","meaning":"Value something very highly because it is important to you."},{"en":"arranged pose","meaning":"A position people deliberately take for a photograph."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-073.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A photograph of you that you like","prompt":"Describe a photograph of you that you like.\nYou should say:\n- where the photo was taken\n- what you were doing when it was taken\n- who took it\n- and explain why you like the photo.","answer":"The photograph that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">immediately comes to mind</strong> when I think of one I like is my university graduation photo. It was taken just before the ceremony that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">marked the end of my undergraduate degree</strong>. You can't really see anything in the background, but it was actually taken in the students' cafe next to the hall where the ceremony was held. In the photo, I'm wearing the traditional graduate outfit — a black gown and one of those flat academic caps, the mortarboard.\n\nI'm also holding my rolled-up degree certificate. It's obviously <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a formal portrait</strong>, so I'm just standing there, smiling, looking straight into the camera. A professional photographer took it — the university had hired him to set up in the cafe and photograph graduates before the ceremony started.\n\nHe'd brought a white screen backdrop, some lighting equipment, and an assistant who was taking payments and noting down everyone's details. The reason I love this photo so much is that it captures <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a turning point</strong> in my life. Getting handed that certificate at the ceremony was the payoff for four years of hard work, and it felt like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">closing one whole chapter</strong> of my life.\n\nI'd achieved what I set out to do and made my family proud, and I remember knowing that the next step was job-hunting. Whenever I look at that photo now, it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">brings back such happy memories</strong> — of my time at university, and of the relief and contentment I felt that day.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"immediately comes to mind","context":"The photograph that immediately comes to mind when I think of one I like is my university graduation photo."},{"en":"marked the end of my undergraduate degree","context":"It was taken just before the ceremony that marked the end of my undergraduate degree."},{"en":"a formal portrait","context":"It's obviously a formal portrait, so I'm just standing there, smiling, looking straight into the camera."},{"en":"a turning point","context":"The reason I love this photo so much is that it captures a turning point in my life."},{"en":"closing one whole chapter","context":"Getting handed that certificate at the ceremony was the payoff for four years of hard work, and it felt like closing one whole chapter of my life."},{"en":"brings back such happy memories","context":"Whenever I look at that photo now, it brings back such happy memories — of my time at university, and of the relief and contentment I felt that day."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a photograph of you that you like","id":"speaking-part-2-074","_displayNo":"#053","outline":{"topic":"uni graduation photo","intro":"photograph immediately comes mind think one like uni graduation photo","body":["photo wearing traditional clothing uni graduates black gown academic cap often","Obviously formal portrait photo standing smiling looking camera","hired uni set up cafe take photos graduates before ceremony","presented w/ degree certificate graduation ceremony culmination 4y hard work marked"],"conclusion":"Whenever look photo brings back happy memories uni days relief"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-074.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A photograph of myself that I like is my university graduation picture. It was taken just before the ceremony, in the student cafe beside the main hall. The university had hired a professional photographer to take portraits of the graduates.\n\nIn the photo, I am wearing a black graduation gown and a flat academic cap. I am holding a rolled-up certificate and smiling at the camera. The photographer used a white background and special lights, so there is not much else to see. An assistant organised payments and recorded everyone's details.\n\nI like this picture because it represents the end of four years of hard work. I had finished my degree and made my family proud. At the same time, I knew a new stage was beginning, including looking for a job. When I see the photo now, I remember the friendships and experiences of university, as well as the relief I felt that day. It is a simple portrait, but it captures an important achievement in my life.","html":"A photograph of myself that I like is my university graduation picture. It was taken just before the ceremony, in the student cafe beside the main hall. The university had hired a professional photographer to take portraits of the graduates.\n\nIn the photo, I am wearing a black <strong class=\"key-phrase\">graduation gown</strong> and a flat academic cap. I am holding a rolled-up certificate and smiling at the camera. The photographer used a white background and special lights, so there is not much else to see. An assistant organised payments and recorded everyone's details.\n\nI like this picture because it represents the end of four years of hard work. I had finished my degree and made my family proud. At the same time, I knew <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a new stage</strong> was beginning, including looking for a job. When I see the photo now, I remember the friendships and experiences of university, as well as the relief I felt that day. It is a simple portrait, but it captures an important achievement in my life.","phrases":[{"en":"graduation gown","meaning":"The long formal clothing worn at a ceremony for completing a degree."},{"en":"a new stage","meaning":"A different period of someone's life."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-074.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A piece of artwork that you have seen before","prompt":"Describe a piece of artwork that you have seen before.\nYou should say:\nWhere you saw it\nWhat it was about\nWhen you saw it\nAnd explain how you felt about it","answer":"Today I'd like to tell you about one of the most famous paintings in the world, and my personal favourite — the portrait of the Mona Lisa. It was painted by Leonardo da Vinci, the Italian artist, back in the fifteenth century.\n\nI first came across it in one of my high school art textbooks, when I was about fifteen, and I honestly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">couldn't take my eyes off it</strong> — there was something mysterious about it I couldn't quite explain. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">At first sight</strong>, all I really noticed was a long-haired woman with a gentle, slightly rounded face and no eyebrows at all, which I found a bit strange at the time. The longer I looked, though, the more it felt like she was actually smiling at me specifically, and I remember flicking back to the page more than once just to check I wasn't imagining it.\n\nSome people say Mona Lisa was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">purely imaginary</strong>, while others believe she was actually Leonardo's wife. Honestly, it doesn't really matter to me either way, because to me the Mona Lisa is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a masterpiece that attracts people</strong> with that one magical smile, and that's the main reason I'm such a fan of it.\n\nAs for the actual truth behind the portrait, I guess we'll <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never know for certain</strong>, but I think that mystery is exactly what makes the whole thing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">all the more intriguing</strong>, and it's probably why the painting still draws such huge crowds today.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"couldn't take my eyes off it","context":"I first came across it in one of my high school art textbooks, when I was about fifteen, and I honestly couldn't take my eyes off it — there was something mysterious about it I couldn't quite explain."},{"en":"At first sight","context":"At first sight, all I really noticed was a long-haired woman with a gentle, slightly rounded face and no eyebrows at all, which I found a bit strange at the time."},{"en":"purely imaginary","context":"Some people say Mona Lisa was purely imaginary, while others believe she was actually Leonardo's wife."},{"en":"a masterpiece that attracts people","context":"Honestly, it doesn't really matter to me either way, because to me the Mona Lisa is a masterpiece that attracts people with that one magical smile, and that's the main reason I'm such a fan of it."},{"en":"never know for certain","context":"As for the actual truth behind the portrait, I guess we'll never know for certain, but I think that mystery is exactly what makes the whole thing all the more intriguing, and it's probably why the painting still draws such huge crowds today."},{"en":"all the more intriguing","context":"As for the actual truth behind the portrait, I guess we'll never know for certain, but I think that mystery is exactly what makes the whole thing all the more intriguing, and it's probably why the painting still draws such huge crowds today."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a piece of artwork that you have seen before","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-075","_displayNo":"#054","outline":{"topic":"Mona Lisa","intro":"one of the most famous paintings in the world, personal favourite, Mona Lisa by Leonardo da Vinci","body":["first saw it in a high school art textbook around age 15, couldn't take my eyes off it","at first sight: long-haired woman, round face, no eyebrows, felt like she was smiling at me","some say purely imaginary, some say Leonardo's wife, doesn't matter to me either way","it's a masterpiece that attracts people with that one magical smile"],"conclusion":"truth behind the portrait we'll never know for certain, but that mystery makes it all the more intriguing"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-075.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A piece of art I like is the Mona Lisa, a portrait painted by Leonardo da Vinci. I first saw a picture of it in an art textbook when I was about fifteen. Even though it was only a printed image, it caught my attention.\n\nThe painting shows a woman with long hair and a calm face. At first, I mainly noticed her unusual expression. The more I looked, the more I wondered whether she was smiling. I kept turning back to the page because her expression seemed difficult to understand.\n\nThat mystery is the main reason I like the painting. People have different ideas about the woman and her smile, and I find those discussions interesting. I am not an art expert, so I cannot explain every detail of the technique. However, I think a good painting can make an ordinary viewer stop and feel curious. The Mona Lisa did that for me. It helped me understand that a picture does not need a dramatic scene to hold someone's interest.","html":"A piece of art I like is the Mona Lisa, a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">portrait</strong> painted by Leonardo da Vinci. I first saw a picture of it in an art textbook when I was about fifteen. Even though it was only a printed image, it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caught my attention</strong>.\n\nThe painting shows a woman with long hair and a calm face. At first, I mainly noticed her unusual expression. The more I looked, the more I wondered whether she was smiling. I kept turning back to the page because her expression seemed difficult to understand.\n\nThat mystery is the main reason I like the painting. People have different ideas about the woman and her smile, and I find those discussions interesting. I am not an art expert, so I cannot explain every detail of the technique. However, I think a good painting can make an ordinary viewer stop and feel curious. The Mona Lisa did that for me. It helped me understand that a picture does not need a dramatic scene to hold someone's interest.","phrases":[{"en":"portrait","meaning":"A painting or photograph of a person, especially their face."},{"en":"caught my attention","meaning":"Made me notice and become interested in something."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-075.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A piece of clothing that you like wearing","prompt":"Describe a piece of clothing that you like wearing.\nYou should say:\n- what the item of clothing is\n- where and when you bought it\n- when you wear it\n- and explain why you like wearing it.","answer":"I'm going to describe a coat I wear all the time at the moment. It's dark blue, and it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made out of</strong> a lightweight, waterproof material, which makes it perfect for this time of year here in the UK. It's also got <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a detachable hood</strong>, which I actually used just yesterday when it was raining, plus various pockets on the outside and the inside.\n\nI bought it last year, right at the beginning of autumn when the weather started <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turning colder</strong>. It was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an easy purchase</strong>, actually, because I'd had the exact same coat before — my old one was starting to look pretty worn out, so I just replaced it. Since I already knew my size, I found the best offer online, ordered it through a shopping website, and it turned up at my door in a box a few days later.\n\nLike I said, I'm wearing this coat constantly right now. It's my everyday coat — work, popping out to the shops, whatever else I'm doing. The reason I like it so much is that it's so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical and functional</strong>.\n\nIt's waterproof, it keeps me warm, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comes in handy for</strong> carrying things — my phone, wallet, loose change, a pen, even a notebook; I basically stuff my pockets with whatever I've got on me. It even folds up small enough to fit in my bag on days when I don't need it.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"made out of","context":"It's dark blue, and it's made out of a lightweight, waterproof material, which makes it perfect for this time of year here in the UK."},{"en":"a detachable hood","context":"It's also got a detachable hood, which I actually used just yesterday when it was raining, plus various pockets on the outside and the inside."},{"en":"turning colder","context":"I bought it last year, right at the beginning of autumn when the weather started turning colder."},{"en":"an easy purchase","context":"It was an easy purchase, actually, because I'd had the exact same coat before — my old one was starting to look pretty worn out, so I just replaced it."},{"en":"practical and functional","context":"Like I said, I'm wearing this coat constantly right now. It's my everyday coat — work, popping out to the shops, whatever else I'm doing. The reason I like it so much is that it's so practical and functional."},{"en":"comes in handy for","context":"It's waterproof, it keeps me warm, and it comes in handy for carrying things — my phone, wallet, loose change, a pen, even a notebook; I basically stuff my pockets with whatever I've got on me."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a piece of clothing that you like wearing","id":"speaking-part-2-076","_displayNo":"#055","outline":{"topic":"blue waterproof coat","intro":"going describe coat often wear moment","body":["coat dark blue colour it's made out lightweight waterproof material makes","easy buy bc exact same coat before old coat starting look","It's everyday coat going work go out shopping reason","It's waterproof keeps warm comes handy carrying things like phone wallet"],"conclusion":"folds up small enough fit bag not need it"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-076.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A piece of clothing I like is my dark blue coat. It is light, waterproof and warm enough for the cooler weather in the UK. It also has a hood that can be removed and several pockets inside and outside.\n\nI bought it online at the start of autumn last year. I had owned the same model before, but my old coat was worn out. Because I already knew the right size, ordering a replacement was easy. It arrived a few days later, and I began wearing it immediately.\n\nI use it almost every day, whether I am going to work or visiting the shops. My favourite thing is how practical it is. It keeps rain out, and the pockets hold my phone, wallet, keys and sometimes a small notebook. When the weather becomes warmer, I can fold it into my bag. It is not a special outfit for an important event, but it makes daily life easier. That is why it has become one of my most useful clothes.","html":"A piece of clothing I like is my dark blue coat. It is light, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">waterproof</strong> and warm enough for the cooler weather in the UK. It also has a hood that can be removed and several pockets inside and outside.\n\nI bought it online at the start of autumn last year. I had owned the same model before, but my old coat was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worn out</strong>. Because I already knew the right size, ordering a replacement was easy. It arrived a few days later, and I began wearing it immediately.\n\nI use it almost every day, whether I am going to work or visiting the shops. My favourite thing is how practical it is. It keeps rain out, and the pockets hold my phone, wallet, keys and sometimes a small notebook. When the weather becomes warmer, I can fold it into my bag. It is not a special outfit for an important event, but it makes daily life easier. That is why it has become one of my most useful clothes.","phrases":[{"en":"worn out","meaning":"Damaged or no longer useful after being used a lot."},{"en":"waterproof","meaning":"Designed to stop water passing through."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-076.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A piece of furniture that you own","prompt":"Describe a piece of furniture that you own.\nYou should say:\n- what it is\n- where you bought it\n- how you use it\n- and explain why you like it.","answer":"I'm going to talk about my favourite chair. It's a big, leather armchair that sits in my living room at home, just under my living room window. I didn't actually buy the armchair.\n\nOne of my friends was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">moving house</strong> and was going to buy some new furniture. I had always liked his leather armchair, so he said I could have it if I managed to move it. The chair wouldn't fit in my car, and it was tricky to get it out of my friend's apartment.\n\nI had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hire a small van</strong> to take the armchair home, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">took two of us</strong> just to carry it up the stairs to my flat. Obviously I use the armchair for sitting! It's my favourite place in the house to relax, read a book, watch TV or even do some work; the chair's arms are quite wide and flat, so my laptop fits nicely on either of them.\n\nLast night I fell asleep in my armchair while I was watching a film, which happens <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more often than I'd like to admit</strong>. The main reasons why I like this piece of furniture are that it's comfortable and it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the perfect position</strong> in my living room, right by the window <strong class=\"key-phrase\">where the light comes in</strong> during the afternoon. The only drawback might be that I enjoy sitting in the armchair too much, and sometimes I think it makes me lazy!","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"moving house","context":"One of my friends was moving house and was going to buy some new furniture. I had always liked his leather armchair, so he said I could have it if I managed to move it. The chair wouldn't fit in my car, and it was tricky to get it out of my friend's apartment."},{"en":"hire a small van","context":"I had to hire a small van to take the armchair home, and it took two of us just to carry it up the stairs to my flat."},{"en":"took two of us","context":"I had to hire a small van to take the armchair home, and it took two of us just to carry it up the stairs to my flat."},{"en":"more often than I'd like to admit","context":"Last night I fell asleep in my armchair while I was watching a film, which happens more often than I'd like to admit."},{"en":"in the perfect position","context":"The main reasons why I like this piece of furniture are that it's comfortable and it's in the perfect position in my living room, right by the window where the light comes in during the afternoon."},{"en":"where the light comes in","context":"The main reasons why I like this piece of furniture are that it's comfortable and it's in the perfect position in my living room, right by the window where the light comes in during the afternoon."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a piece of furniture that you own","id":"speaking-part-2-077","_displayNo":"#056","outline":{"topic":"leather armchair","intro":"I'm going talk favourite chair","body":["It's big leather armchair sits living room home living room window","chair wouldn't fit car tricky get out friend's apartment","It's favourite place house relax read book watch TV work chair's","main reasons like piece furniture it's comfortable it's perfect position living"],"conclusion":"drawback enjoy sitting armchair too sometimes think makes lazy"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-077.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A piece of furniture I own is a large leather armchair in my living room. It stands beside the window and is my favourite place to relax. A friend gave it to me when he moved house and decided to buy new furniture.\n\nGetting it home was harder than I expected. It would not fit in my car, so I hired a small van. We then needed two people to carry it upstairs to my flat. The effort was worth it because the chair is extremely comfortable.\n\nI use it for reading, watching television and sometimes working. Its wide arms can even support my laptop. The window lets in pleasant light during the afternoon, making it an especially nice place to sit. Last night, I fell asleep there while watching a film, which happens fairly often. I like the chair because it makes my home feel comfortable without costing me anything to buy. The only disadvantage is that once I sit down, I sometimes find it difficult to get up and do something useful.","html":"A piece of furniture I own is a large leather armchair in my living room. It stands beside the window and is my favourite place to relax. A friend gave it to me when he moved house and decided to buy new furniture.\n\nGetting it home was harder than I expected. It would not fit in my car, so I hired a small van. We then needed two people to carry it upstairs to my flat. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The effort was worth it</strong> because the chair is extremely comfortable.\n\nI use it for reading, watching television and sometimes working. Its wide arms can even <strong class=\"key-phrase\">support my laptop</strong>. The window lets in pleasant light during the afternoon, making it an especially nice place to sit. Last night, I fell asleep there while watching a film, which happens fairly often. I like the chair because it makes my home feel comfortable without costing me anything to buy. The only disadvantage is that once I sit down, I sometimes find it difficult to get up and do something useful.","phrases":[{"en":"The effort was worth it","meaning":"The result was good enough to justify the work involved."},{"en":"support my laptop","meaning":"Hold my laptop in place."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-077.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A piece of furniture you like. (in your home)","prompt":"Describe a piece of furniture you like. (in your home)\nYou should say:\n- What kind of furniture it is;\n- What it looks like;\n- Who bought it;\n- What the furniture are made of;\n- Why you like the furniture.","answer":"I'm going to describe my night lamp, which my dad gave me when I first left home to study. It's a medium-sized plastic lamp, maybe two or three times the size of a teddy bear, shaped like a panda, with the socket <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hidden at the bottom</strong> and the switch sitting neatly on its head.\n\nThere's a battery holder right beside the socket too, so you can still use it even if there's no plug socket nearby. It was the first time anyone had ever given me a night lamp — I'd never used one like it before, so I remember <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feeling really excited and grateful</strong> when I unwrapped it. My dad told me it would help me sleep better, and honestly, it actually worked. It gives off this <strong class=\"key-phrase\">soft, cosy yellow glow</strong>, and I sleep properly soundly with it on.\n\nI've kept it beside my bed every single night since the day I got it, and it's genuinely become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of my little treasures</strong>. I'm planning to hold onto it for years, so I'm careful to look after it and keep it in good condition.\n\nI like my night lamp for two main reasons. First, it's a gift that represents my dad's love for me, and I owe him a lot for that. Second, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">it also offers me a feeling of</strong> warmth and comfort whenever it's in my room, especially on nights when I'm away from home and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">missing my family</strong> a bit.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"hidden at the bottom","context":"It's a medium-sized plastic lamp, maybe two or three times the size of a teddy bear, shaped like a panda, with the socket hidden at the bottom and the switch sitting neatly on its head."},{"en":"feeling really excited and grateful","context":"It was the first time anyone had ever given me a night lamp — I'd never used one like it before, so I remember feeling really excited and grateful when I unwrapped it."},{"en":"soft, cosy yellow glow","context":"It gives off this soft, cosy yellow glow, and I sleep properly soundly with it on."},{"en":"one of my little treasures","context":"I've kept it beside my bed every single night since the day I got it, and it's genuinely become one of my little treasures. I'm planning to hold onto it for years, so I'm careful to look after it and keep it in good condition."},{"en":"it also offers me a feeling of","context":"Second, it also offers me a feeling of warmth and comfort whenever it's in my room, especially on nights when I'm away from home and missing my family a bit."},{"en":"missing my family","context":"Second, it also offers me a feeling of warmth and comfort whenever it's in my room, especially on nights when I'm away from home and missing my family a bit."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a piece of furniture you like. (in your home)","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-078","_displayNo":"#057","outline":{"topic":"panda night lamp","intro":"going describe night lamp father gave leave home studying","body":["There's battery holder beside socket always use battery cannot find outside","never used type lamp before felt excited grateful upon receiving it","sleeping w/ lamp beside bed night day received cute little thing","plan w/ long time trying keep best possible condition"],"conclusion":"Secondly offers feeling warmth comfort room"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-078.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An item in my room I like is a panda-shaped night lamp. My father gave it to me when I first left home to study. It is made of plastic, has a switch on its head and can use batteries when there is no power socket nearby.\n\nI remember being excited when I opened the present because I had never owned a lamp like it. My father said it would help me sleep better. Its soft yellow light makes the room feel warm and comfortable without being too bright.\n\nI have kept it beside my bed ever since. It is useful at night, but its emotional value is more important. Whenever I miss home, it reminds me that my father cares about me. Having something familiar in an unfamiliar room makes being away easier. I try to keep it in good condition because I want to use it for many years. Although it is a simple household object, it has become one of the things I value most.","html":"An item in my room I like is a panda-shaped night lamp. My father gave it to me when I first left home to study. It is made of plastic, has a switch on its head and can use batteries when there is no power socket nearby.\n\nI remember being excited when I opened the present because I had never owned a lamp like it. My father said it would help me sleep better. Its soft yellow light makes the room feel warm and comfortable without being too bright.\n\nI have kept it beside my bed ever since. It is useful at night, but its <strong class=\"key-phrase\">emotional value</strong> is more important. Whenever I miss home, it reminds me that my father cares about me. Having something familiar in an unfamiliar room makes being away easier. I try to keep it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in good condition</strong> because I want to use it for many years. Although it is a simple household object, it has become one of the things I value most.","phrases":[{"en":"emotional value","meaning":"Importance based on feelings or memories rather than money."},{"en":"in good condition","meaning":"Clean, working properly and not damaged."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-078.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A piece of good news you heard","prompt":"Describe a piece of good news you heard\nYou should say:\nwhat this news was\nwhen and where you heard it\nhow you heard it\nAnd explain why you think it was good news","answer":"I'm going to talk about the moment I got <strong class=\"key-phrase\">official confirmation</strong> of a scholarship to join an exchange programme in Japan, about three years ago now. It feels like a while back, but I can still remember that day really clearly.\n\nAt the time I was a second-year student at university, and one day I came across an advert for a two-month <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cultural exchange programme</strong> in Japan for students from Southeast Asia. I was really keen to apply, and luckily my application got accepted — I was even nominated for a scholarship on top of that.\n\nAbout two months later, I was sitting in the university library, half-heartedly checking my email between classes, when I saw a message from the Scholarship Committee saying I'd been chosen for a full scholarship that would cover all my fees and living costs for the whole programme. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">My heart skipped a beat</strong> the moment I saw the word 'Congratulations', and I actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gasped out loud</strong> right there in the library. I was so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">proud of myself</strong> for being picked out of hundreds of other candidates.\n\nIt was honestly the best news I got that whole year. If I hadn't won it, covering my living costs would've been a serious <strong class=\"key-phrase\">financial burden</strong> for my parents, but thanks to that scholarship, I got to enjoy my time in Japan without worrying about money at all. I still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep this lucky email</strong> sitting in my inbox to this day, just to remind me of that moment.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"official confirmation","context":"I'm going to talk about the moment I got official confirmation of a scholarship to join an exchange programme in Japan, about three years ago now. It feels like a while back, but I can still remember that day really clearly."},{"en":"cultural exchange programme","context":"At the time I was a second-year student at university, and one day I came across an advert for a two-month cultural exchange programme in Japan for students from Southeast Asia."},{"en":"My heart skipped a beat","context":"My heart skipped a beat the moment I saw the word 'Congratulations', and I actually gasped out loud right there in the library."},{"en":"gasped out loud","context":"My heart skipped a beat the moment I saw the word 'Congratulations', and I actually gasped out loud right there in the library."},{"en":"proud of myself","context":"I was so proud of myself for being picked out of hundreds of other candidates."},{"en":"financial burden","context":"If I hadn't won it, covering my living costs would've been a serious financial burden for my parents, but thanks to that scholarship, I got to enjoy my time in Japan without worrying about money at all."},{"en":"keep this lucky email","context":"I still keep this lucky email sitting in my inbox to this day, just to remind me of that moment."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a piece of good news you heard","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-194","_displayNo":"#058","outline":{"topic":"Japan exchange scholarship","intro":"the moment I got official confirmation of a scholarship to Japan, about three years ago","body":["2nd-year university student, saw an advert for a two-month cultural exchange programme for SE Asian students","2 months later, sitting in the university library checking email, saw the scholarship email","heart skipped a beat at the word 'Congratulations', gasped out loud, proud of myself among hundreds of candidates"],"conclusion":"would've been a financial burden for my parents otherwise; still keep this lucky email in my inbox"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-194.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A piece of good news I heard was receiving a full scholarship for an exchange programme in Japan about three years ago. I was a second-year university student and had applied for a two-month cultural programme for students from Southeast Asia.\n\nMy application was accepted, and I was also considered for financial support. About two months later, I was checking email in the university library between classes. A message from the scholarship committee said I had been chosen and that my fees and living costs would be covered.\n\nI was so surprised that I made a noise in the quiet library. Being selected from many applicants made me feel proud. The news also brought relief because paying for the trip would have been difficult for my parents. I could now enjoy the programme without adding that pressure to the family. I still keep the email as a reminder. It represents both an opportunity to experience another culture and a moment when my effort created a result better than I had expected.","html":"A piece of good news I heard was receiving a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">full scholarship</strong> for an exchange programme in Japan about three years ago. I was a second-year university student and had applied for a two-month cultural programme for students from Southeast Asia.\n\nMy application was accepted, and I was also considered for financial support. About two months later, I was checking email in the university library between classes. A message from the scholarship committee said I had been chosen and that my fees and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">living costs</strong> would be covered.\n\nI was so surprised that I made a noise in the quiet library. Being selected from many applicants made me feel proud. The news also brought relief because paying for the trip would have been difficult for my parents. I could now enjoy the programme without adding that pressure to the family. I still keep the email as a reminder. It represents both an opportunity to experience another culture and a moment when my effort created a result better than I had expected.","phrases":[{"en":"full scholarship","meaning":"Financial support that pays all the costs included in a study award."},{"en":"living costs","meaning":"Money needed for everyday needs such as food and accommodation."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-194.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A piece of technology (not computer—related) you like to use","prompt":"Describe a piece of technology (not computer—related) you like to use\nYou should say:\nWhat the technology is\nWhen it was invented\nWhat it is used for\nHow you feel about it","answer":"I'd call myself a bit of a technophile, so it's hard for me to get through a day without some kind of tech, especially my smartphone. Mine was a present from my parents for finishing high school, four years ago now, and it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">come in handy</strong> pretty much every single day since.\n\nSmartphones as we know them really only <strong class=\"key-phrase\">took off</strong> after the first iPhone came out back in 2007 — before that, phones could call and text, but not much else. Mine was one of the newer Android models when I got it, packed with apps and features, but no one really showed me how to use it properly, so I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taught myself through Google</strong> whenever something went wrong.\n\nIt's with me almost constantly, and even though I know staring at it for six-plus hours a day probably isn't great for my eyes, I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">use it on a daily basis</strong> anyway. It's had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a huge effect on my life</strong>, both good and bad. I don't really need a dictionary, a notebook, a camera, an MP3 player or even a TV any more, because my phone covers all of that on one screen.\n\nInstead of lugging around a heavy laptop, I can edit documents on my phone wherever I am, and online shopping alerts save me time and money too. The downside is that I've become pretty dependent on it — if the battery dies or I leave it at home by accident, I honestly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel a bit lost</strong>. It's helped me massively with work and study, but I'm trying to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cut down my screen time</strong> for the sake of my own wellbeing.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"come in handy","context":"Mine was a present from my parents for finishing high school, four years ago now, and it's come in handy pretty much every single day since."},{"en":"took off","context":"Smartphones as we know them really only took off after the first iPhone came out back in 2007 — before that, phones could call and text, but not much else."},{"en":"use it on a daily basis","context":"It's with me almost constantly, and even though I know staring at it for six-plus hours a day probably isn't great for my eyes, I use it on a daily basis anyway."},{"en":"a huge effect on my life","context":"It's had a huge effect on my life, both good and bad."},{"en":"feel a bit lost","context":"The downside is that I've become pretty dependent on it — if the battery dies or I leave it at home by accident, I honestly feel a bit lost."},{"en":"cut down my screen time","context":"It's helped me massively with work and study, but I'm trying to cut down my screen time for the sake of my own wellbeing."},{"en":"taught myself through Google","context":"Mine was one of the newer Android models when I got it, packed with apps and features, but no one really showed me how to use it properly, so I taught myself through Google whenever something went wrong."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a piece of technology (not computer—related) you like to use","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-079","_displayNo":"#059","outline":{"topic":"smartphone","intro":"technophile, hard to get through a day without tech, smartphone a gift for finishing high school 4 years ago","body":["smartphones really took off after the first iPhone in 2007, mine was a newer Android model, taught myself via Google","with me almost constantly, screen time 6+ hours a day, use it on a daily basis","replaces dictionary, notebook, camera, MP3 player, TV — a huge effect on my life, both good and bad","edit documents on the phone, online shopping saves time and money, but I've become dependent on it"],"conclusion":"helped a lot with work and study but trying to cut down my screen time for my wellbeing"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-079.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A piece of technology I like using is my smartphone. My parents gave it to me when I finished high school four years ago. It was a recent Android model at the time, and I learned most of its functions by trying them or searching online.\n\nI use it every day for communication, study and entertainment. It works as a dictionary, camera, notebook and music player. I can also edit documents without carrying a laptop and compare prices when shopping. Having all these tools in one small device is very convenient.\n\nHowever, there is a disadvantage. I have become so used to it that I feel rather lost when the battery runs out or I leave it at home. Spending many hours looking at the screen is also not a good habit. I am trying to reduce unnecessary use while keeping the useful parts. Overall, it has made work and study easier, but I want to control how I use it rather than checking it automatically all day.","html":"A piece of technology I like using is my smartphone. My parents gave it to me when I finished high school four years ago. It was a recent Android model at the time, and I learned most of its <strong class=\"key-phrase\">functions</strong> by trying them or searching online.\n\nI use it every day for communication, study and entertainment. It works as a dictionary, camera, notebook and music player. I can also edit documents without carrying a laptop and compare prices when shopping. Having all these tools in one small device is very convenient.\n\nHowever, there is a disadvantage. I have become so used to it that I feel rather lost when the battery <strong class=\"key-phrase\">runs out</strong> or I leave it at home. Spending many hours looking at the screen is also not a good habit. I am trying to reduce unnecessary use while keeping the useful parts. Overall, it has made work and study easier, but I want to control how I use it rather than checking it automatically all day.","phrases":[{"en":"functions","meaning":"The different jobs that a device can do."},{"en":"runs out","meaning":"Has none left, such as when a battery has no power remaining."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-079.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A plant, flower or tree that is famous or important","prompt":"Describe a plant, flower or tree that is famous or important\nYou should say:\n- what type of plant, flower or tree it is\n- where it grows or is usually seen\n- how it is used in your country\n- and why it is famous or important","answer":"I'm going to talk about oak trees, and I'll also describe a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">famous example</strong> called the Major Oak. This enormous tree stands in Sherwood Forest near Nottingham and is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thought to be around a thousand years old</strong>. I'd probably say it's the most impressive tree I've ever seen, mainly just because of its sheer size.\n\nOak trees are common in England. They grow in woodland, fields, parks and gardens, and their strong timber's traditionally been used for furniture and the structural beams of buildings. I've got several pieces of oak furniture at home myself, including the dining table we eat at every day, so the tree's also got <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a practical connection to everyday life</strong> for me.\n\nThe Major Oak's different because it's been preserved as a landmark rather than cut down for timber. Its trunk and branches are exceptionally broad, and visitors can immediately <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sense its age</strong> just standing next to it. I first saw it on a school trip when I was about ten, and I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">was struck by</strong> the idea that this exact living tree had survived through so many centuries of history, long before any of my own family's history even began.\n\nIt's also tied to the legend of Robin Hood, who's said to have used it as a meeting place for his men. Whether or not that story's literally true, the Major Oak's become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a powerful symbol of</strong> English history, strength and continuity.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"famous example","context":"I'm going to talk about oak trees, and I'll also describe a famous example called the Major Oak."},{"en":"thought to be around a thousand years old","context":"This enormous tree stands in Sherwood Forest near Nottingham and is thought to be around a thousand years old."},{"en":"a practical connection to everyday life","context":"I've got several pieces of oak furniture at home myself, including the dining table we eat at every day, so the tree's also got a practical connection to everyday life for me."},{"en":"sense its age","context":"Its trunk and branches are exceptionally broad, and visitors can immediately sense its age just standing next to it."},{"en":"was struck by","context":"I first saw it on a school trip when I was about ten, and I was struck by the idea that this exact living tree had survived through so many centuries of history, long before any of my own family's history even began."},{"en":"a powerful symbol of","context":"It's also tied to the legend of Robin Hood, who's said to have used it as a meeting place for his men. Whether or not that story's literally true, the Major Oak's become a powerful symbol of English history, strength and continuity."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a plant, flower or tree that is famous or important","id":"speaking-part-2-080","_displayNo":"#060","outline":{"topic":"Major Oak","intro":"oak trees describe famous example called Major Oak","body":["enormous tree stands Sherwood Forest near Nottingham thought around thousand years","grow woodland fields parks gardens strong timber traditionally used furniture structural","Major Oak different bc preserved landmark rather cut timber","1st saw school trip struck idea same living tree survived through"],"conclusion":"Whether not story literally true Major Oak become powerful symbol"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-080.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An important tree I know about is the oak, especially the Major Oak in Sherwood Forest near Nottingham. This famous tree is thought to be around a thousand years old. I first saw it on a school trip when I was about ten.\n\nIts trunk and branches are very wide, which made it seem enormous to me. I remember thinking about all the history that had happened while the same tree continued growing. It is also connected with stories about Robin Hood, who is said to have met his men there.\n\nOak trees have a practical importance too. Their strong wood is used for furniture and parts of buildings. My family has an oak dining table that we use every day. The Major Oak, however, is protected as a landmark rather than used for wood. I like it because it combines natural beauty with history. Standing beside something so old helped me think differently about time and why preserving important trees matters for future visitors.","html":"An important tree I know about is the oak, especially the Major Oak in Sherwood Forest near Nottingham. This famous tree is thought to be around a thousand years old. I first saw it on a school trip when I was about ten.\n\nIts <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trunk</strong> and branches are very wide, which made it seem enormous to me. I remember thinking about all the history that had happened while the same tree continued growing. It is also connected with stories about Robin Hood, who is said to have met his men there.\n\nOak trees have a practical importance too. Their strong wood is used for furniture and parts of buildings. My family has an oak dining table that we use every day. The Major Oak, however, is protected as a landmark rather than used for wood. I like it because it combines natural beauty with history. Standing beside something so old helped me think differently about time and why <strong class=\"key-phrase\">preserving</strong> important trees matters for future visitors.","phrases":[{"en":"trunk","meaning":"The main thick stem of a tree."},{"en":"preserving","meaning":"Protecting something so that it remains in good condition."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-080.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A prize that you would like to win","prompt":"Describe a prize that you would like to win.\nYou should say:\n- what the prize is for\n- how you know about it\n- what you would have to do to win it\n- and why you would like to win this prize.","answer":"I'm going to talk about a prize I'd love to win: the Employee of the Year award at the company I work for. Every December, the directors give it to one member of staff who has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made an outstanding contribution</strong> to the business over the previous twelve months. I first heard about it during my induction, just after I'd got the job three years ago.\n\nIt was early December, and a couple of my colleagues were chatting about who might win it that year. I was curious, so I asked them to explain the rules properly. As I said, it's given for outstanding contribution to the business, but there isn't an exact definition of what that actually means.\n\nFrom what I can tell, you need to hit the basics first: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">good attendance and punctuality</strong>, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meeting your targets and deadlines</strong>. On top of that, the winner usually needs to have done something genuinely creative — last year's winner, for instance, designed a completely new service for our clients, and the directors were clearly impressed.\n\nI'd love to win Employee of the Year because it would mean my work had finally been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recognised by the people at the top</strong>, and that would definitely help me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">move forward in my career</strong>. But honestly, beyond the practical side, it would just give me a real sense of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">personal achievement</strong> — proof that the extra effort hasn't gone unnoticed.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"made an outstanding contribution","context":"Every December, the directors give it to one member of staff who has made an outstanding contribution to the business over the previous twelve months."},{"en":"good attendance and punctuality","context":"From what I can tell, you need to hit the basics first: good attendance and punctuality, and meeting your targets and deadlines."},{"en":"meeting your targets and deadlines","context":"From what I can tell, you need to hit the basics first: good attendance and punctuality, and meeting your targets and deadlines."},{"en":"recognised by the people at the top","context":"I'd love to win Employee of the Year because it would mean my work had finally been recognised by the people at the top, and that would definitely help me move forward in my career."},{"en":"move forward in my career","context":"I'd love to win Employee of the Year because it would mean my work had finally been recognised by the people at the top, and that would definitely help me move forward in my career."},{"en":"personal achievement","context":"But honestly, beyond the practical side, it would just give me a real sense of personal achievement — proof that the extra effort hasn't gone unnoticed."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a prize that you would like to win","id":"speaking-part-2-081","_displayNo":"#061","outline":{"topic":"Employee of the Year award","intro":"prize I'd love to win Employee of the Year award company work for","body":["directors give it member staff made outstanding contribution business twelve months","heard about induction colleagues discussing who might win","need hit basics good attendance punctuality meeting targets deadlines","winner also needs done something creative last year's winner new service"],"conclusion":"love win because recognised move forward career personal achievement"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-081.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A prize I would like to win is Employee of the Year at my company. The directors give it to one staff member every December for making a particularly valuable contribution during the year. I learned about it soon after joining three years ago.\n\nSome colleagues were discussing possible winners, so I asked how the award worked. There are no completely fixed rules, but reliable attendance, arriving on time and meeting deadlines are important. Winners usually do something extra as well. For example, last year's winner created a new service for customers.\n\nI would like to receive the prize because it would show that senior managers appreciate my work. It might also help me progress in my career. More personally, it would make me feel that my effort had achieved something worthwhile. I know I would need to do more than simply finish my usual tasks. Thinking about the award encourages me to look for useful ideas and ways to support the company, rather than only focusing on my own daily workload.","html":"A prize I would like to win is Employee of the Year at my company. The directors give it to one staff member every December for making a particularly valuable <strong class=\"key-phrase\">contribution</strong> during the year. I learned about it soon after joining three years ago.\n\nSome colleagues were discussing possible winners, so I asked how the award worked. There are no completely fixed rules, but reliable attendance, arriving on time and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meeting deadlines</strong> are important. Winners usually do something extra as well. For example, last year's winner created a new service for customers.\n\nI would like to receive the prize because it would show that senior managers appreciate my work. It might also help me progress in my career. More personally, it would make me feel that my effort had achieved something worthwhile. I know I would need to do more than simply finish my usual tasks. Thinking about the award encourages me to look for useful ideas and ways to support the company, rather than only focusing on my own daily workload.","phrases":[{"en":"contribution","meaning":"Something useful that a person gives or does to help a group."},{"en":"meeting deadlines","meaning":"Completing work by the time it is due."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-081.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A rule at your school that you agree or disagree with","prompt":"Describe a rule at your school that you agree or disagree with.\nYou should say:\n- What the rule is;\n- What will happen when students break the rule;\n- Why school makes the rule;\n- Why you agree or not.","answer":"Vietnamese high schools are known for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">imposing a lot of strict rules on students</strong>, and mine was no exception. The one rule I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">couldn't stand</strong> was this: boys and girls weren't allowed to be seen together, or even sit next to each other, in class or during break time.\n\nIf you broke the rule, or you were just caught standing near someone of the opposite gender, you'd get a warning from the supervisor. If it happened again, you could be suspended for anything from three days to a week, so it wasn't taken lightly at all.\n\nI do know the reasoning behind it. The rumour was that a few couples used to skip school to meet up secretly, and ended up failing their university entrance exams because of it. The principal was worried that teenagers falling in love would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">neglect their studies</strong>, so the rule was there to stop students from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">developing an intimate relationship</strong> that might distract them.\n\nPersonally, though, I think the school <strong class=\"key-phrase\">took it way too far</strong>. I agree that studying should be our main priority, but there's nothing wrong with boys and girls simply spending time together — most of the time it's just friendship, nothing more. Boys and girls often have different strengths too; the boys in my class tended to be stronger at Maths and Physics, while the girls were better at Literature and languages, so if anything, mixing us up would've helped everyone learn faster.\n\nTo me, the whole separation was pointless. It didn't just stop us enjoying our school years properly, it also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caused constant friction</strong> between students and teachers. If the school really wanted to help us focus, a much better approach would've been to explain the consequences of poor results clearly, rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeping the genders apart</strong> like that.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"imposing a lot of strict rules on students","context":"Vietnamese high schools are known for imposing a lot of strict rules on students, and mine was no exception."},{"en":"couldn't stand","context":"The one rule I couldn't stand was this: boys and girls weren't allowed to be seen together, or even sit next to each other, in class or during break time."},{"en":"neglect their studies","context":"The principal was worried that teenagers falling in love would neglect their studies, so the rule was there to stop students from developing an intimate relationship that might distract them."},{"en":"developing an intimate relationship","context":"The principal was worried that teenagers falling in love would neglect their studies, so the rule was there to stop students from developing an intimate relationship that might distract them."},{"en":"took it way too far","context":"Personally, though, I think the school took it way too far."},{"en":"caused constant friction","context":"It didn't just stop us enjoying our school years properly, it also caused constant friction between students and teachers."},{"en":"keeping the genders apart","context":"If the school really wanted to help us focus, a much better approach would've been to explain the consequences of poor results clearly, rather than keeping the genders apart like that."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a rule at your school that you agree or disagree with","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-196","_displayNo":"#062","outline":{"topic":"school gender-separation rule","intro":"one rule couldn't stand boys girls not allowed seen together sit next each other","body":["break rule caught near opposite gender warning supervisor suspended three days week","rumour couples skipped school secretly failed entrance exams principal worried neglect studies","personally think took way too far agree study main priority nothing wrong spending time together","boys girls different strengths Maths Physics Literature languages mixing helped everyone learn faster"],"conclusion":"separation pointless caused constant friction better explain consequences than keeping genders apart"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-196.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A school rule I disagreed with was keeping boys and girls apart at my high school. They were not allowed to sit together or spend time together during breaks. Breaking the rule could lead to a warning and, if repeated, a short suspension.\n\nI understood that the school wanted students to focus on exams. Some teachers worried that romantic relationships could distract us from studying. However, I felt the rule treated every friendship as a problem. Boys and girls often simply wanted to talk, work on assignments or help each other understand a subject.\n\nIn my opinion, the school could have explained responsible behaviour without separating everyone. Students have different strengths, and working together can help them learn. The strict rule also caused arguments between students and teachers, which did not improve the atmosphere. I agreed that study should be important, but I disagreed with the method used. I would have preferred clear expectations about schoolwork and respectful behaviour. That would have taught responsibility more effectively than assuming all contact between boys and girls was harmful.","html":"A school rule I disagreed with was keeping boys and girls apart at my high school. They were not allowed to sit together or spend time together during breaks. Breaking the rule could lead to a warning and, if repeated, a short <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suspension</strong>.\n\nI understood that the school wanted students to focus on exams. Some teachers worried that romantic relationships could distract us from studying. However, I felt the rule treated every friendship as a problem. Boys and girls often simply wanted to talk, work on assignments or help each other understand a subject.\n\nIn my opinion, the school could have explained responsible behaviour without separating everyone. Students have different strengths, and working together can help them learn. The strict rule also caused arguments between students and teachers, which did not improve the atmosphere. I agreed that study should be important, but I disagreed with the method used. I would have preferred <strong class=\"key-phrase\">clear expectations</strong> about schoolwork and respectful behaviour. That would have taught responsibility more effectively than assuming all contact between boys and girls was harmful.","phrases":[{"en":"suspension","meaning":"A temporary period when a student is not allowed to attend school."},{"en":"clear expectations","meaning":"Easily understood ideas about what people are required to do."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-196.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A science subject you studied at school","prompt":"Describe a science subject you studied at school.\nYou should say:\n- what you learned about\n- what the teacher was like\n- what you thought of the teacher\n- and how you felt about it.","answer":"There were quite a few science subjects I studied years ago, but the one I'd like to talk about is Biology, which I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had the chance to learn</strong> at high school. This was about six years ago, right <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at the start of the school year</strong>, when I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">absolutely swamped</strong> with new subjects. Biology happened to be one of the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compulsory subjects</strong> that year, so I didn't really have a choice about taking it.\n\nWe covered things like how the human body works, cells, and later on genetics, which I found genuinely interesting once I got into it. My teacher was a woman called Linh, and she was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of the most respected teachers</strong> in the school — quite strict, but she always backed it up with real examples, like explaining digestion by walking us through what happens after we eat lunch, rather than just making us memorise diagrams.\n\nHonestly, at first I struggled. I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">under the impression that</strong> Biology would be complicated, and in the first few lessons I couldn't understand half of what she taught, which knocked my confidence and showed up badly in my mid-term results. But I didn't give up, and kept working at it day by day until it gradually started to click.\n\nBy the end of the term, I'd <strong class=\"key-phrase\">passed with flying colours</strong>, and I honestly think that came down to Linh's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical advice</strong> on how to study more effectively — she taught me to summarise each chapter in my own words instead of just re-reading the textbook. Looking back, Biology wouldn't have been an easy subject to pass without that kind of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">determination and perseverance</strong>, and it's actually one of the reasons I still enjoy reading about science now.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"had the chance to learn","context":"There were quite a few science subjects I studied years ago, but the one I'd like to talk about is Biology, which I had the chance to learn at high school."},{"en":"at the start of the school year","context":"This was about six years ago, right at the start of the school year, when I was absolutely swamped with new subjects."},{"en":"absolutely swamped","context":"This was about six years ago, right at the start of the school year, when I was absolutely swamped with new subjects."},{"en":"compulsory subjects","context":"Biology happened to be one of the compulsory subjects that year, so I didn't really have a choice about taking it."},{"en":"one of the most respected teachers","context":"My teacher was a woman called Linh, and she was one of the most respected teachers in the school — quite strict, but she always backed it up with real examples, like explaining digestion by walking us through what happens after we eat lunch, rather than just making us memorise diagrams."},{"en":"under the impression that","context":"I was under the impression that Biology would be complicated, and in the first few lessons I couldn't understand half of what she taught, which knocked my confidence and showed up badly in my mid-term results."},{"en":"passed with flying colours","context":"By the end of the term, I'd passed with flying colours, and I honestly think that came down to Linh's practical advice on how to study more effectively — she taught me to summarise each chapter in my own words instead of just re-reading the textbook."},{"en":"practical advice","context":"By the end of the term, I'd passed with flying colours, and I honestly think that came down to Linh's practical advice on how to study more effectively — she taught me to summarise each chapter in my own words instead of just re-reading the textbook."},{"en":"determination and perseverance","context":"Looking back, Biology wouldn't have been an easy subject to pass without that kind of determination and perseverance, and it's actually one of the reasons I still enjoy reading about science now."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a science subject you learned in your school","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-082","_displayNo":"#063","outline":{"topic":"Biology","intro":"science subjects years ago Biology chance learn high school six years ago start school year","body":["swamped new subjects Biology compulsory subject that year no choice","covered human body cells genetics teacher Linh respected strict real examples","struggled first thought would complicated first lessons couldn't understand knocked confidence","didn't give up kept working day by day gradually clicked passed flying colours"],"conclusion":"came down Linh's practical advice summarise chapters own words determination perseverance"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-082.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A science subject I studied at high school was Biology. I began learning it about six years ago because it was a compulsory subject. We studied topics such as cells, the human body and genetics.\n\nOur teacher, Linh, was strict but explained ideas using everyday examples. For instance, she described digestion by discussing what happens after we eat lunch. Even with her help, I found the first lessons difficult. I expected the subject to be complicated, and my poor mid-term result made me less confident.\n\nHowever, I continued practising. Linh advised me to summarise each chapter in my own words instead of only reading the textbook again. This helped me understand the ideas rather than just remember labels. By the end of the term, my result had improved greatly. I felt proud because I had worked through something difficult. Biology also made me more curious about how living things function. I still enjoy reading about science, partly because that class taught me that a challenging subject can become interesting with the right approach.","html":"A science subject I studied at high school was Biology. I began learning it about six years ago because it was a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compulsory subject</strong>. We studied topics such as cells, the human body and genetics.\n\nOur teacher, Linh, was strict but explained ideas using everyday examples. For instance, she described digestion by discussing what happens after we eat lunch. Even with her help, I found the first lessons difficult. I expected the subject to be complicated, and my poor mid-term result made me less confident.\n\nHowever, I continued practising. Linh advised me to summarise each chapter <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in my own words</strong> instead of only reading the textbook again. This helped me understand the ideas rather than just remember labels. By the end of the term, my result had improved greatly. I felt proud because I had worked through something difficult. Biology also made me more curious about how living things function. I still enjoy reading about science, partly because that class taught me that a challenging subject can become interesting with the right approach.","phrases":[{"en":"compulsory subject","meaning":"A subject that students are required to study."},{"en":"in my own words","meaning":"Using my own way of expressing an idea rather than copying it."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-082.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A service from a company or shop","prompt":"Describe a service from a company or shop\nYou should say:\nWhat the service was\nWhen and where you received the service\nWhom you were with\nAnd explain how you feel about it","answer":"I'm going to describe an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">excellent service</strong> that I received from Traveloka, an online travel company. I used it last summer when my mother and I decided to visit Taiwan and wanted to organise the trip ourselves instead of buying a package tour.\n\nThe service was mainly a booking service for flights and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hotel accommodation</strong>. I did the whole thing from my laptop at home over a couple of evenings. The website let us compare prices, check flight times and choose a hotel in a convenient location. What impressed me most was that the whole process was clear, fast and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easy to follow</strong>. Even though I hadn't booked an overseas trip online before, I didn't feel confused at any stage.\n\nAnother good point was that the company offered <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a variety of payment methods</strong> and sent confirmation emails immediately. When we needed to change one detail in the booking, the customer-service team replied quickly and explained the steps in simple language.\n\nOverall, I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completely satisfied with the service</strong> because it saved us time, reduced our stress and helped us arrange the trip <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at a reasonable price</strong>. I'd definitely use the same service again. Thinking about why it worked so well, I'd say the key was that the company <strong class=\"key-phrase\">anticipated the questions</strong> we were going to ask. Every screen told us what would happen next and what it would cost, so we never had to hunt for information or worry that a fee would appear at the end.\n\nMy mother isn't comfortable with technology at all, and she was able to follow what I was doing on the screen, which I thought was a good test of the design. Poor service usually means <strong class=\"key-phrase\">being left uncertain</strong>, and at no point during that booking were we uncertain about anything.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"excellent service","context":"I'm going to describe an excellent service that I received from Traveloka, an online travel company. I used it last summer when my mother and I decided to visit Taiwan and wanted to organise the trip ourselves instead of buying a package tour."},{"en":"hotel accommodation","context":"The service was mainly a booking service for flights and hotel accommodation."},{"en":"easy to follow","context":"What impressed me most was that the whole process was clear, fast and easy to follow."},{"en":"a variety of payment methods","context":"Another good point was that the company offered a variety of payment methods and sent confirmation emails immediately. When we needed to change one detail in the booking, the customer-service team replied quickly and explained the steps in simple language."},{"en":"completely satisfied with the service","context":"Overall, I was completely satisfied with the service because it saved us time, reduced our stress and helped us arrange the trip at a reasonable price."},{"en":"at a reasonable price","context":"Overall, I was completely satisfied with the service because it saved us time, reduced our stress and helped us arrange the trip at a reasonable price."},{"en":"anticipated the questions","context":"Thinking about why it worked so well, I'd say the key was that the company anticipated the questions we were going to ask."},{"en":"being left uncertain","context":"Poor service usually means being left uncertain, and at no point during that booking were we uncertain about anything."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a service from a company or shop","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-161","_displayNo":"#064","outline":{"topic":"Traveloka service","intro":"going describe excellent service received Traveloka online travel company","body":["used last summer mother decided visit Taiwan wanted organise trip ourselves","website allowed compare prices check flight times choose hotel convenient location","good point company offered variety payment methods sent confirmation emails immediately","needed change one detail booking customer-service team replied quickly explained steps","key = company anticipated questions we were going to ask","every screen told what happen next + what it cost no hidden fee","mother not comfortable w/ technology could still follow = good test of design","poor service = being left uncertain never uncertain at any point"],"conclusion":"poor service = being left uncertain never uncertain at any point"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-161.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A service I appreciated was Traveloka's online booking service. I used it last summer when my mother and I planned a trip to Taiwan. We wanted to organise flights and a hotel ourselves instead of joining a package tour.\n\nI made the bookings on my laptop over a couple of evenings. The site let us compare prices, flight times and hotel locations clearly. It also explained payment choices and sent confirmation emails quickly. When we needed to change one detail, customer service replied promptly with instructions I could understand.\n\nWhat impressed me most was the lack of confusion. Each stage showed what would happen next and what we would pay. My mother is not confident with technology, but she could still follow what I was doing. That made me feel the design was effective. The service saved time and reduced the pressure of planning an overseas holiday. I would use it again because good service, for me, means not being left uncertain or having to search repeatedly for basic information.","html":"A service I appreciated was Traveloka's online booking service. I used it last summer when my mother and I planned a trip to Taiwan. We wanted to organise flights and a hotel ourselves instead of joining a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">package tour</strong>.\n\nI made the bookings on my laptop over a couple of evenings. The site let us compare prices, flight times and hotel locations clearly. It also explained payment choices and sent <strong class=\"key-phrase\">confirmation emails</strong> quickly. When we needed to change one detail, customer service replied promptly with instructions I could understand.\n\nWhat impressed me most was the lack of confusion. Each stage showed what would happen next and what we would pay. My mother is not confident with technology, but she could still follow what I was doing. That made me feel the design was effective. The service saved time and reduced the pressure of planning an overseas holiday. I would use it again because good service, for me, means not being left uncertain or having to search repeatedly for basic information.","phrases":[{"en":"package tour","meaning":"A holiday sold with transport, accommodation and sometimes activities arranged together."},{"en":"confirmation emails","meaning":"Messages checking that a booking or payment has been completed."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-161.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A special cake you had","prompt":"Describe a special cake you had\nYou should say:\nWhat kind of cake it is\nWhen you ate the cake\nWho you ate with\nAnd explain why you think the cake is special","answer":"I want to talk about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a special occasion</strong> when a simple cake genuinely changed how I felt about an awful few months in my life. It happened right after I'd <strong class=\"key-phrase\">started high school</strong>, which was 80km from my hometown, so I had to rent a small flat and live there on my own. Like a lot of people in that situation, I found it hard to adjust, and for the first couple of months I barely made any friends at all.\n\nThen I got sick — properly sick, not just a cold. I couldn't even walk fifty metres without feeling dizzy, let alone go to school. Stubbornly, I convinced myself I'd get better on my own if I just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">waited it out</strong>, so after asking my teacher for a few days off, I spent three days basically just lying in bed, barely eating anything. If anything, I got worse.\n\nThen, one afternoon, I got a call from a classmate called Trang, asking if she could come and check on me. When she turned up, I forced myself downstairs, and I was completely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caught off guard</strong> — she was holding a homemade chocolate sponge cake, still slightly warm from the oven. She'd heard I was struggling and decided to bake it herself rather than just texting to ask if I was okay.\n\nThat small act nearly made me cry. We sat on the floor of my tiny room and ate the whole cake together that evening, and it's still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of the kindest things anyone's ever done for me</strong>. My body actually started recovering pretty quickly after that — maybe it was just good timing, or maybe I just needed to eat something again.\n\nFrom that day, Trang and I became close friends, and even though we've gone our separate ways since finishing school, we still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep in touch with</strong> each other and swap stories whenever we can. All these years later, I'm <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely thankful</strong> for a friendship that started with something as small as a cake.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"a special occasion","context":"I want to talk about a special occasion when a simple cake genuinely changed how I felt about an awful few months in my life."},{"en":"started high school","context":"It happened right after I'd started high school, which was 80km from my hometown, so I had to rent a small flat and live there on my own."},{"en":"waited it out","context":"Stubbornly, I convinced myself I'd get better on my own if I just waited it out, so after asking my teacher for a few days off, I spent three days basically just lying in bed, barely eating anything."},{"en":"caught off guard","context":"When she turned up, I forced myself downstairs, and I was completely caught off guard — she was holding a homemade chocolate sponge cake, still slightly warm from the oven."},{"en":"one of the kindest things anyone's ever done for me","context":"We sat on the floor of my tiny room and ate the whole cake together that evening, and it's still one of the kindest things anyone's ever done for me."},{"en":"keep in touch with","context":"From that day, Trang and I became close friends, and even though we've gone our separate ways since finishing school, we still keep in touch with each other and swap stories whenever we can."},{"en":"genuinely thankful","context":"All these years later, I'm genuinely thankful for a friendship that started with something as small as a cake."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a special cake you had","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-083","_displayNo":"#065","outline":{"topic":"cake from a friend","intro":"special occasion simple cake changed awful months life started high school 80km hometown rent flat alone","body":["got properly sick couldn't walk fifty metres waited it out three days lying bed worse","afternoon call classmate Trang check on me turned up caught off guard holding homemade chocolate sponge cake","small act nearly made cry sat floor room ate whole cake together evening body recovering quickly after","Trang close friends gone separate ways still keep touch swap stories"],"conclusion":"years later genuinely thankful friendship started something small as cake"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-083.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A special cake I remember was a chocolate sponge cake made by my classmate Trang. This happened soon after I started high school, about eighty kilometres from my hometown. I lived alone in a small flat and found it difficult to settle in.\n\nDuring the first few months, I became quite ill. I felt dizzy, missed school and stayed in bed for three days, hardly eating. Then Trang phoned and asked if she could visit. When I went downstairs, she was holding a homemade cake that was still warm.\n\nI was deeply touched because I had not expected anyone to make such an effort for me. We sat on the floor of my room and ate the cake together. I started feeling better afterwards, but the kindness mattered more than the food itself. From that day, we became close friends. Although our lives took different directions after school, we still keep in touch. I remember that cake because it made a lonely and difficult period feel less frightening.","html":"A special cake I remember was a chocolate sponge cake made by my classmate Trang. This happened soon after I started high school, about eighty kilometres from my hometown. I lived alone in a small flat and found it difficult to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">settle in</strong>.\n\nDuring the first few months, I became quite ill. I felt dizzy, missed school and stayed in bed for three days, hardly eating. Then Trang phoned and asked if she could visit. When I went downstairs, she was holding a homemade cake that was still warm.\n\nI was deeply touched because I had not expected anyone to make such an effort for me. We sat on the floor of my room and ate the cake together. I started feeling better afterwards, but the kindness mattered more than the food itself. From that day, we became close friends. Although our lives took different directions after school, we still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep in touch</strong>. I remember that cake because it made a lonely and difficult period feel less frightening.","phrases":[{"en":"settle in","meaning":"Become comfortable in a new place or situation."},{"en":"keep in touch","meaning":"Continue communicating with someone after being apart."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-083.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A subject that you think should be removed from school","prompt":"Describe a subject that you think should be removed from school\nYou should say:\n- what the subject is\n- why you think it is unnecessary for children to study it\n- and explain what you would replace it with.","answer":"If I had to pick one subject to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take off the school curriculum</strong>, I'd probably go with art. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">In my experience</strong>, art lessons mostly involve drawing, painting, and making collages out of paper, fabric and whatever else is lying around. There are a few reasons I think it's not really worth keeping.\n\nFor one thing, I don't think drawing and painting are skills most children actually need once they leave school. They might enjoy the lessons at the time, but it's pretty unlikely they'll use them in most jobs. On top of that, kids can draw, paint and make collages at home in their own time anyway — my parents used to sit and do exactly that with me on weekends, no school lesson required.\n\nAnd honestly, looking back at my own art classes, I don't think we picked up much real skill from them at all. The teachers mostly just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">left us to get on with it</strong>, without giving us much <strong class=\"key-phrase\">proper technical guidance</strong>, so it always felt more like free time than an actual lesson.\n\nInstead of art, I'd rather see that time go towards core subjects like maths, science or a language. Those are the subjects that are actually going to help children later on, once they're <strong class=\"key-phrase\">out looking for a job</strong>, and I think both students and teachers would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get more out of</strong> the extra time spent on them.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"take off the school curriculum","context":"If I had to pick one subject to take off the school curriculum, I'd probably go with art."},{"en":"In my experience","context":"In my experience, art lessons mostly involve drawing, painting, and making collages out of paper, fabric and whatever else is lying around."},{"en":"left us to get on with it","context":"The teachers mostly just left us to get on with it, without giving us much proper technical guidance, so it always felt more like free time than an actual lesson."},{"en":"proper technical guidance","context":"The teachers mostly just left us to get on with it, without giving us much proper technical guidance, so it always felt more like free time than an actual lesson."},{"en":"out looking for a job","context":"Those are the subjects that are actually going to help children later on, once they're out looking for a job, and I think both students and teachers would get more out of the extra time spent on them."},{"en":"get more out of","context":"Those are the subjects that are actually going to help children later on, once they're out looking for a job, and I think both students and teachers would get more out of the extra time spent on them."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a subject that you think should be removed from school","id":"speaking-part-2-084","_displayNo":"#066","outline":{"topic":"remove Art from school","intro":"pick one subject take off curriculum probably art in my experience drawing painting collages","body":["not think drawing painting skills children need leave school unlikely use most jobs","kids draw paint home own time anyway parents used sit do that weekends","looking back own art classes not picked up real skill teachers left us get on with it","instead art rather core subjects maths science language actually help later job"],"conclusion":"think both students teachers get more out of extra time spent them"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-084.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"If I had to choose a subject to remove from school, I would choose art as a compulsory lesson. In my own experience, we mainly drew pictures, painted and made collages from paper or fabric.\n\nMy first reason is that many students may not use these particular skills in their future jobs. Children who enjoy art can still draw and paint in their free time. My parents often did creative activities with me at weekends, so I did not rely only on school for that experience.\n\nAnother reason comes from the way my classes were taught. We often received little technical guidance and were simply left to finish a picture. It felt more like a break than a structured lesson. I would prefer extra time for maths, science or languages because those subjects seem more directly useful for many careers. This is based on my personal experience rather than a dislike of creativity itself. I understand that others enjoy art, but if timetable space were limited, I would give those core subjects priority.","html":"If I had to choose a subject to remove from school, I would choose art as a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compulsory lesson</strong>. In my own experience, we mainly drew pictures, painted and made collages from paper or fabric.\n\nMy first reason is that many students may not use these particular skills in their future jobs. Children who enjoy art can still draw and paint in their free time. My parents often did creative activities with me at weekends, so I did not rely only on school for that experience.\n\nAnother reason comes from the way my classes were taught. We often received little technical guidance and were simply left to finish a picture. It felt more like a break than a structured lesson. I would prefer extra time for maths, science or languages because those subjects seem more directly useful for many careers. This is based on my personal experience rather than a dislike of creativity itself. I understand that others enjoy art, but if timetable space were limited, I would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give those core subjects priority</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"compulsory lesson","meaning":"A class that all students must attend."},{"en":"give those core subjects priority","meaning":"Treat those main subjects as more important when deciding how to use time."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-084.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A subject you would like to study that you never had the opportunity to study","prompt":"Describe a subject you would like to study that you never had the opportunity to study.\nYou should say:\nWhat is it\nWhat this subject deals with\nWhat benefits you would get\nAnd explain why you want to study this subject.","answer":"If I had the chance, I'd love to study Japanese — it's something I've wanted to do since I was a kid. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Back in the day</strong>, I was obsessed with manga, Japanese comic books. I couldn't <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take my eyes off</strong> them; I read a new one practically every day, and I used to tell myself I'd study Japanese properly one day. Unfortunately, once I got older, I was buried in exams and then work, so that plan just never happened.\n\nThe subject itself would mainly involve learning to read and write the two phonetic alphabets, hiragana and katakana, plus a huge number of kanji characters, on top of grammar and speaking practice. It sounds like a lot, and it is, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">now and then</strong> I still think about that childhood dream, and I'd love to eventually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take part in</strong> a proper course.\n\nAs for the benefits, I think there'd be two main ones. The obvious one is access — a huge amount of Japanese material never gets translated, or gets translated badly, so reading it in the original would open up a whole side of the culture I currently miss out on. The other is more practical: plenty of Japanese companies operate here in Vietnam, and the language is studied far less than English or Chinese, so it would genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set me apart</strong> in the job market.\n\nSo really, it's a mix of an old childhood dream and a practical calculation, and being fluent would finally <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make that dream come true</strong>. I'd like to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">act on it</strong> before life gets any busier.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"Back in the day","context":"Back in the day, I was obsessed with manga, Japanese comic books."},{"en":"take my eyes off","context":"I couldn't take my eyes off them; I read a new one practically every day, and I used to tell myself I'd study Japanese properly one day."},{"en":"now and then","context":"It sounds like a lot, and it is, but now and then I still think about that childhood dream, and I'd love to eventually take part in a proper course."},{"en":"take part in","context":"It sounds like a lot, and it is, but now and then I still think about that childhood dream, and I'd love to eventually take part in a proper course."},{"en":"set me apart","context":"The other is more practical: plenty of Japanese companies operate here in Vietnam, and the language is studied far less than English or Chinese, so it would genuinely set me apart in the job market."},{"en":"make that dream come true","context":"So really, it's a mix of an old childhood dream and a practical calculation, and being fluent would finally make that dream come true. I'd like to act on it before life gets any busier."},{"en":"act on it","context":"So really, it's a mix of an old childhood dream and a practical calculation, and being fluent would finally make that dream come true. I'd like to act on it before life gets any busier."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a subject you would like to study that you never had the opportunity to study","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-085","_displayNo":"#067","outline":{"topic":"learn Japanese","intro":"chance love study Japanese wanted since kid back day obsessed manga comic books","body":["couldn't take eyes off read every day told myself study Japanese one day grew up buried exams work","subject deals hiragana katakana kanji characters grammar speaking practice","now then think childhood dream take part proper course","benefits access untranslated material professional advantage Japanese companies Vietnam less commonly studied"],"conclusion":"mix childhood dream practical calculation act on it before life busier"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-085.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A subject I would like to study is Japanese. I became interested in it as a child because I loved reading manga, or Japanese comic books. I often imagined reading the original versions one day, but exams and later work kept delaying my plans.\n\nStudying Japanese would involve learning the hiragana and katakana writing systems, as well as kanji characters. I would also need grammar lessons and regular speaking practice. It sounds challenging, but I would enjoy joining a proper course instead of only learning occasional words online.\n\nThere are two main benefits for me. First, I could understand books and other material that may not be translated well. This would help me learn more about Japanese culture. Second, Japanese companies operate in Vietnam, so the language could be useful for future work. It might help me stand out when applying for jobs. For me, this subject combines an old personal interest with a practical goal. I hope to begin before my life becomes even busier.","html":"A subject I would like to study is Japanese. I became interested in it as a child because I loved reading manga, or Japanese comic books. I often imagined reading the original versions one day, but exams and later work kept <strong class=\"key-phrase\">delaying my plans</strong>.\n\nStudying Japanese would involve learning the hiragana and katakana writing systems, as well as kanji characters. I would also need grammar lessons and regular speaking practice. It sounds challenging, but I would enjoy joining a proper course instead of only learning occasional words online.\n\nThere are two main benefits for me. First, I could understand books and other material that may not be translated well. This would help me learn more about Japanese culture. Second, Japanese companies operate in Vietnam, so the language could be useful for future work. It might help me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stand out</strong> when applying for jobs. For me, this subject combines an old personal interest with a practical goal. I hope to begin before my life becomes even busier.","phrases":[{"en":"delaying my plans","meaning":"Making the things I intend to do happen later."},{"en":"stand out","meaning":"Be noticed as different or especially suitable."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-085.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A toy that was special to you when you were a child","prompt":"Describe a toy that was special to you when you were a child.\nYou should say:\n- when you got it\n- what it looked like\n- who gave it to you","answer":"One special toy that I remember getting was a Lego car. It was a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">birthday present from my parents</strong>. I can't remember exactly how old I was, but I was probably about 10 or 11. The special thing about this car was that I had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">build it myself</strong> out of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hundreds of pieces of Lego</strong>. The pieces came in a box with a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">picture of the finished car</strong> on the front, and I had to follow step-by-step instructions to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put all the pieces together</strong> in the correct way.\n\nThis wasn't an easy task because the car even had an engine, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">movable seats and gears</strong>. It took me a day or two to make, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">required a lot of concentration</strong>. When the car was finished it looked great, and I felt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of accomplishment</strong>. I seem to remember that I didn't play with the car very much; the fun part had been the process of building it.\n\nLooking back, I think that is exactly why my parents chose it. They knew I got bored with toys that did everything for you, and they wanted something that would keep me occupied for more than an afternoon. My father actually sat with me for the first hour, sorting the pieces into piles by colour, and that is probably the part I remember most fondly now — not the car itself, but the two of us working through the instructions together at the kitchen table.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"birthday present from my parents","context":"It was a birthday present from my parents."},{"en":"build it myself","context":"The special thing about this car was that I had to build it myself out of hundreds of pieces of Lego."},{"en":"hundreds of pieces of Lego","context":"The special thing about this car was that I had to build it myself out of hundreds of pieces of Lego."},{"en":"picture of the finished car","context":"The pieces came in a box with a picture of the finished car on the front, and I had to follow step-by-step instructions to put all the pieces together in the correct way."},{"en":"put all the pieces together","context":"The pieces came in a box with a picture of the finished car on the front, and I had to follow step-by-step instructions to put all the pieces together in the correct way."},{"en":"movable seats and gears","context":"This wasn't an easy task because the car even had an engine, movable seats and gears."},{"en":"required a lot of concentration","context":"It took me a day or two to make, and required a lot of concentration."},{"en":"a sense of accomplishment","context":"When the car was finished it looked great, and I felt a sense of accomplishment."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a toy that was special to you when you were a child","id":"speaking-part-2-086","_displayNo":"#068","outline":{"topic":"Lego car","intro":"One special toy remember getting Lego car","body":["can't remember exactly old probably 10 11","pieces came box w/ picture finished car front follow step-by-step instructions","wasn't easy task bc car engine movable seats gears","seem remember didn't play w/ car fun part process building it","father sat w/ me 1st hour sorting pieces piles by colour","remember most fondly = us working through instructions kitchen table"],"conclusion":"remember most fondly = us working through instructions kitchen table"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-086.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A special childhood toy was a Lego car my parents gave me for my birthday when I was about ten or eleven. It came in a box containing hundreds of small pieces and instructions showing how to build the finished model.\n\nThe car was quite complicated. It had an engine, movable seats and gears, so I needed to concentrate carefully. Building it took me a day or two. I felt proud when I finally completed it, although I did not play with it very much afterwards. The enjoyable part had been making it.\n\nMy father helped during the first hour. We sat at the kitchen table, sorted pieces by colour and followed the instructions together. Looking back, that shared time is my strongest memory of the toy. I think my parents chose it because they knew I liked solving problems and could become bored with simpler toys. It taught me patience and gave me something useful to do for more than one afternoon. More importantly, it gave my father and me a project to enjoy together.","html":"A special childhood toy was a Lego car my parents gave me for my birthday when I was about ten or eleven. It came in a box containing hundreds of small pieces and instructions showing how to build the finished model.\n\nThe car was quite complicated. It had an engine, movable seats and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gears</strong>, so I needed to concentrate carefully. Building it took me a day or two. I felt proud when I finally completed it, although I did not play with it very much afterwards. The enjoyable part had been making it.\n\nMy father helped during the first hour. We sat at the kitchen table, sorted pieces by colour and followed the instructions together. Looking back, that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shared time</strong> is my strongest memory of the toy. I think my parents chose it because they knew I liked solving problems and could become bored with simpler toys. It taught me patience and gave me something useful to do for more than one afternoon. More importantly, it gave my father and me a project to enjoy together.","phrases":[{"en":"gears","meaning":"Parts with teeth that turn together to move a machine or mechanism."},{"en":"shared time","meaning":"Time spent doing something together with another person."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-086.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A toy that you received when you were a child","prompt":"Describe a toy that you received when you were a child.\nYou should say:\n- What the toy was.\n- Who gave it to you.\n- How you used it.\n- An explanation of how you felt when you got this toy.","answer":"The first <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sentimental item</strong> that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">came to mind</strong> when I saw this topic was a stuffed toy called Marsupilami. It was a birthday present from my parents, and I can't remember my exact age, but I was probably around five or six. I'd been asking for it for weeks after seeing the character on TV, so when I finally unwrapped it, I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely thrilled</strong>.\n\nIt was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">identical to the character</strong> from the cartoon, so it felt like he'd actually stepped out of the screen — a black-spotted yellow creature, a bit like a monkey crossed with a dog, with this incredibly long tail that in the show could do almost anything, from swinging between trees to picking things up.\n\nI carried Marsupilami around the house constantly, and for the first few weeks I wouldn't even let my cousins touch him when they came over. He sat on my pillow every single day, and I actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">got into the habit of</strong> telling him about my day before I went to sleep, the way some kids talk to a diary. When I started kindergarten not long after, he even came with me in my school bag for the first week, until my mother gently convinced me to leave him at home.\n\nLooking back, that toy is tied up with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">some of my happiest early memories</strong>, and even now, whenever I see a Marsupilami toy in a shop, it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">takes me straight back to</strong> being five years old and utterly convinced he was real.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"sentimental item","context":"The first sentimental item that came to mind when I saw this topic was a stuffed toy called Marsupilami."},{"en":"came to mind","context":"The first sentimental item that came to mind when I saw this topic was a stuffed toy called Marsupilami."},{"en":"genuinely thrilled","context":"I'd been asking for it for weeks after seeing the character on TV, so when I finally unwrapped it, I was genuinely thrilled."},{"en":"identical to the character","context":"It was identical to the character from the cartoon, so it felt like he'd actually stepped out of the screen — a black-spotted yellow creature, a bit like a monkey crossed with a dog, with this incredibly long tail that in the show could do almost anything, from swinging between trees to picking t..."},{"en":"got into the habit of","context":"He sat on my pillow every single day, and I actually got into the habit of telling him about my day before I went to sleep, the way some kids talk to a diary."},{"en":"some of my happiest early memories","context":"Looking back, that toy is tied up with some of my happiest early memories, and even now, whenever I see a Marsupilami toy in a shop, it takes me straight back to being five years old and utterly convinced he was real."},{"en":"takes me straight back to","context":"Looking back, that toy is tied up with some of my happiest early memories, and even now, whenever I see a Marsupilami toy in a shop, it takes me straight back to being five years old and utterly convinced he was real."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a toy that you received when you were a child","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-087","_displayNo":"#069","outline":{"topic":"Marsupilami toy","intro":"first sentimental item came mind saw topic stuffed toy Marsupilami birthday present parents age five six","body":["asked for weeks after seeing character TV unwrapped genuinely thrilled","identical character cartoon black-spotted yellow creature monkey dog long tail","carried around house constantly wouldn't let cousins touch sat pillow every day","got into habit telling him about day school bag first week kindergarten"],"conclusion":"tied up happiest early memories takes me straight back being five convinced real"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-087.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A toy I received as a child was a soft toy called Marsupilami. My parents gave it to me for my birthday when I was about five or six. I had seen the character on television and asked for the toy for several weeks.\n\nIt looked just like the cartoon creature: yellow with black spots and an extremely long tail. In the programme, the tail could help it swing through trees and pick things up. When I opened the present, I was delighted because the character seemed almost real.\n\nI carried it around the house and kept it on my pillow at night. I even told it about my day before sleeping. For the first week of kindergarten, I took it in my school bag until my mother encouraged me to leave it at home. The toy made unfamiliar situations feel more comfortable. I no longer treat toys like living friends, of course, but seeing a Marsupilami in a shop still reminds me of that happy period of childhood.","html":"A toy I received as a child was a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">soft toy</strong> called Marsupilami. My parents gave it to me for my birthday when I was about five or six. I had seen the character on television and asked for the toy for several weeks.\n\nIt looked just like the cartoon creature: yellow with black spots and an extremely long tail. In the programme, the tail could help it swing through trees and pick things up. When I opened the present, I was delighted because the character seemed almost real.\n\nI carried it around the house and kept it on my pillow at night. I even told it about my day before sleeping. For the first week of kindergarten, I took it in my school bag until my mother encouraged me to leave it at home. The toy made <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unfamiliar situations</strong> feel more comfortable. I no longer treat toys like living friends, of course, but seeing a Marsupilami in a shop still reminds me of that happy period of childhood.","phrases":[{"en":"soft toy","meaning":"A toy animal made from fabric and filled with soft material."},{"en":"unfamiliar situations","meaning":"Experiences or places that are new and not yet comfortable."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-087.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A traditional product in your country","prompt":"Describe a traditional product in your country.\nYou should say:\n- What it is;\n- When was the first time you tried it;\n- How to make it;\n- How you like it.","answer":"I'd love to talk about rice, which is probably <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the most important agricultural plant</strong> in my country. I genuinely can't remember the first time I tried it, because it's the first solid food most Vietnamese babies eat — my mother says I was having soft rice porridge before I could even sit up properly, so it's been part of my life since before I have any real memories.\n\nAs for how it's made, it obviously starts in the paddy fields — farmers plant the seedlings by hand, flood the fields, and then harvest and dry the grain months later. At home, though, making it is much simpler: you just wash the rice a couple of times until the water runs clear, then <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cook it in a rice cooker</strong> with the right amount of water. My grandmother always insisted on doing it the traditional way, in a pot on the stove, because she said the rice cooker version never had quite the same texture.\n\nRice turns up in almost every meal in my house — usually a bowl of plain white rice alongside fish, vegetables and a bit of fish sauce. What I love is that it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">goes with almost anything</strong>; it's simple, but it never gets boring no matter what you serve alongside it.\n\nBeyond the dinner table, though, rice really is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">woven into the culture</strong> here. Millions of families still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depend on growing rice to make a living</strong>, and Vietnam has become one of the world's biggest rice exporters. A lot of traditional festivals and ceremonies still centre around farmers hoping for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a good harvest</strong>, and children here <strong class=\"key-phrase\">grow up eating rice</strong> from the moment they're weaned, so it's genuinely one of the strongest symbols of Vietnamese life.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"the most important agricultural plant","context":"I'd love to talk about rice, which is probably the most important agricultural plant in my country."},{"en":"cook it in a rice cooker","context":"At home, though, making it is much simpler: you just wash the rice a couple of times until the water runs clear, then cook it in a rice cooker with the right amount of water."},{"en":"goes with almost anything","context":"What I love is that it goes with almost anything; it's simple, but it never gets boring no matter what you serve alongside it."},{"en":"woven into the culture","context":"Beyond the dinner table, though, rice really is woven into the culture here."},{"en":"depend on growing rice to make a living","context":"Millions of families still depend on growing rice to make a living, and Vietnam has become one of the world's biggest rice exporters."},{"en":"a good harvest","context":"A lot of traditional festivals and ceremonies still centre around farmers hoping for a good harvest, and children here grow up eating rice from the moment they're weaned, so it's genuinely one of the strongest symbols of Vietnamese life."},{"en":"grow up eating rice","context":"A lot of traditional festivals and ceremonies still centre around farmers hoping for a good harvest, and children here grow up eating rice from the moment they're weaned, so it's genuinely one of the strongest symbols of Vietnamese life."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a traditional product in your country","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-088","_displayNo":"#070","outline":{"topic":"rice","intro":"rice most important agricultural plant country can't remember first time tried it first solid food babies eat","body":["how made starts paddy fields farmers plant seedlings flood harvest dry grain months later","home simpler wash rice cook rice cooker grandmother insisted traditional way pot stove","turns up almost every meal plain white rice fish vegetables fish sauce goes with almost anything","beyond dinner table woven into culture millions families depend growing rice make living Vietnam leading exporter"],"conclusion":"festivals centre farmers hoping good harvest children grow up eating rice strongest symbols Vietnamese life"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-088.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A traditional product that is important in Vietnam is rice. It has been part of my diet for as long as I can remember. My mother says I ate soft rice porridge when I was a baby, before I could eat normal family meals.\n\nRice begins in the fields, where farmers grow and later harvest the plants. At home, we usually wash the grains and put them in a rice cooker with water. My grandmother prefers cooking rice in a pot on the stove because she likes the texture better.\n\nIn my family, we eat plain rice with fish, vegetables and other dishes. I enjoy it because it goes well with many different foods. Rice is important beyond our kitchen too. Many families earn their living by growing it, and it appears in traditional celebrations connected with farming and harvests. To me, it represents everyday Vietnamese life. It may seem like a simple food, but it connects family meals, work and traditions across the country.","html":"A traditional product that is important in Vietnam is rice. It has been part of my diet for as long as I can remember. My mother says I ate soft rice porridge when I was a baby, before I could eat normal family meals.\n\nRice begins in the fields, where farmers grow and later harvest the plants. At home, we usually wash the grains and put them in a rice cooker with water. My grandmother prefers cooking rice in a pot on the stove because she likes the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">texture</strong> better.\n\nIn my family, we eat plain rice with fish, vegetables and other dishes. I enjoy it because it goes well with many different foods. Rice is important beyond our kitchen too. Many families <strong class=\"key-phrase\">earn their living</strong> by growing it, and it appears in traditional celebrations connected with farming and harvests. To me, it represents everyday Vietnamese life. It may seem like a simple food, but it connects family meals, work and traditions across the country.","phrases":[{"en":"texture","meaning":"The way food feels in your mouth or when you touch it."},{"en":"earn their living","meaning":"Make the money they need for everyday life."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-088.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A TV programme you enjoy","prompt":"Describe a TV programme you enjoy\nYou should say:\nWhat it is\nWhen you watch it\nWho you watch it with\nAnd explain why you like to watch it.","answer":"I'd like to talk about The Walking Dead, an American drama series I've recently become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completely hooked on</strong>. It's set in the world after a zombie apocalypse, where society has collapsed and there's no government left to look after anyone.\n\nI usually watch it late on Friday nights, once my flatmate Duc gets home from work — we've turned it into a bit of a ritual, actually, with a big bowl of instant noodles each and the lights off for the full effect. He got me into it in the first place, and now neither of us can imagine a Friday without an episode or two.\n\nThe show has a brilliant cast, but for me, Andrew Lincoln, who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">plays the leading role</strong> of a police officer trying to hold his group together, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaves the deepest impression</strong>. His acting feels completely genuine, especially in the quieter scenes where he's just trying to keep everyone calm.\n\nWhat I find most fascinating, though, is the make-up work on the zombies — every single one goes through an incredibly detailed process to look <strong class=\"key-phrase\">properly rotten and realistic</strong> on screen, and I still don't fully understand how they pull it off.\n\nI've been drawn to zombie stories since I was a kid, if I'm honest, so this show was always going to appeal to me. On top of that, the plot has plenty of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unexpected twists</strong>, which keeps things genuinely tense. I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">can't get enough of this show</strong>, and I'm always counting down the days until the next season starts.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"completely hooked on","context":"I'd like to talk about The Walking Dead, an American drama series I've recently become completely hooked on. It's set in the world after a zombie apocalypse, where society has collapsed and there's no government left to look after anyone."},{"en":"plays the leading role","context":"The show has a brilliant cast, but for me, Andrew Lincoln, who plays the leading role of a police officer trying to hold his group together, leaves the deepest impression."},{"en":"leaves the deepest impression","context":"The show has a brilliant cast, but for me, Andrew Lincoln, who plays the leading role of a police officer trying to hold his group together, leaves the deepest impression."},{"en":"properly rotten and realistic","context":"What I find most fascinating, though, is the make-up work on the zombies — every single one goes through an incredibly detailed process to look properly rotten and realistic on screen, and I still don't fully understand how they pull it off."},{"en":"unexpected twists","context":"On top of that, the plot has plenty of unexpected twists, which keeps things genuinely tense."},{"en":"can't get enough of this show","context":"I can't get enough of this show, and I'm always counting down the days until the next season starts."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a TV programme you enjoy","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-089","_displayNo":"#071","outline":{"topic":"The Walking Dead","intro":"Walking Dead American drama series completely hooked on zombie apocalypse society collapsed no government","body":["usually watch late Friday nights flatmate Duc home work ritual noodles lights off","brilliant cast Andrew Lincoln plays leading role police officer leaves deepest impression genuine acting","fascinating makeup work zombies detailed process properly rotten realistic screen","drawn zombie stories since kid plot unexpected twists keeps things tense"],"conclusion":"can't get enough show always counting down days next season starts"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-089.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A television programme I enjoy is The Walking Dead, an American drama about people trying to survive after society has been destroyed by zombies. My flatmate Duc introduced me to it, and we now watch together on Friday nights.\n\nAfter he returns from work, we usually make instant noodles and put on an episode or two. It has become a regular activity that we both look forward to. I particularly like Andrew Lincoln's performance as a police officer trying to keep his group together. He makes the quieter emotional scenes feel believable.\n\nThe make-up is another impressive part. The zombies look frighteningly realistic, and I often wonder how much work each one requires. I have liked this type of story since childhood, so the subject already appealed to me. However, it is the unexpected events and difficult choices that keep me interested. The programme can be tense, but discussing it with Duc makes watching enjoyable. I am always curious to find out what will happen to the characters next.","html":"A television programme I enjoy is The Walking Dead, an American drama about people trying to survive after society has been destroyed by zombies. My flatmate Duc introduced me to it, and we now watch together on Friday nights.\n\nAfter he returns from work, we usually make instant noodles and put on an episode or two. It has become a regular activity that we both look forward to. I particularly like Andrew Lincoln's performance as a police officer trying to keep his group together. He makes the quieter emotional scenes feel believable.\n\nThe make-up is another impressive part. The zombies look frighteningly realistic, and I often wonder how much work each one requires. I have liked this type of story since childhood, so the subject already <strong class=\"key-phrase\">appealed to me</strong>. However, it is the unexpected events and difficult choices that keep me interested. The programme can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tense</strong>, but discussing it with Duc makes watching enjoyable. I am always curious to find out what will happen to the characters next.","phrases":[{"en":"appealed to me","meaning":"Seemed interesting or enjoyable to me."},{"en":"tense","meaning":"Making you feel worried or excited about what might happen."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-089.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A TV programme or channel that you enjoy","prompt":"Describe a TV programme or channel that you enjoy\nYou should say:\n- What it is called and what it was about?\n- When do you usually watch it\n- Why do you prefer it to other programmes or channels?","answer":"I rarely watch <strong class=\"key-phrase\">traditional television channels</strong> nowadays; I much prefer searching for interesting programmes or videos online. So, although it isn't a conventional TV channel, I'd like to talk about TED, a website that functions like a modern on-demand channel. TED <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stands for</strong> Technology, Entertainment and Design, and it features short talks on subjects ranging from science and education to health and social change.\n\nThe main advantage is that I can watch the talks whenever I want. I've got the website saved on my phone, and I usually check it every few days. I often watch one presentation during my train journey to work because most talks are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">concise enough</strong> to fit into a short commute.\n\nI prefer TED to ordinary television because I learn something useful almost every time. Its slogan, “<strong class=\"key-phrase\">ideas worth spreading</strong>”, captures its appeal perfectly. Instead of spending an hour on a repetitive talk show, I can watch a specialist explain a breakthrough in medicine, a new teaching method or an unfamiliar social issue in ten or fifteen minutes. The talk I remember best was one on how sleep affects memory. The speaker was a neuroscientist, but she explained the research so clearly that I changed my own habits within a week, and I've never gone back to working late the way I used to.\n\nIf I had to name a drawback, it'd be that the format encourages simplification. A complicated subject compressed into twelve minutes inevitably loses its qualifications, and the confident delivery can make an idea sound <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more settled than it really is</strong>. So I treat the talks as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a starting point rather than the final word</strong>, and I look things up afterwards if a claim seems important.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"traditional television channels","context":"I rarely watch traditional television channels nowadays; I much prefer searching for interesting programmes or videos online."},{"en":"stands for","context":"TED stands for Technology, Entertainment and Design, and it features short talks on subjects ranging from science and education to health and social change."},{"en":"concise enough","context":"I often watch one presentation during my train journey to work because most talks are concise enough to fit into a short commute."},{"en":"ideas worth spreading","context":"Its slogan, “ideas worth spreading”, captures its appeal perfectly."},{"en":"more settled than it really is","context":"A complicated subject compressed into twelve minutes inevitably loses its qualifications, and the confident delivery can make an idea sound more settled than it really is."},{"en":"a starting point rather than the final word","context":"So I treat the talks as a starting point rather than the final word, and I look things up afterwards if a claim seems important."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a TV programme or channel that you enjoy","id":"speaking-part-2-090","_displayNo":"#072","outline":{"topic":"TED","intro":"not conventional TV channel like talk TED website functions like","body":["TED stands Technology Entertainment Design features short talks subjects ranging science","website saved phone usually check few days","often watch one presentation during train journey work bc talks concise","slogan ideas worth spreading captures appeal perfectly","talk remember best = sleep affects memory neuroscientist","changed own habits within a week never went back working late","drawback = format encourages simplification loses qualifications","treat talks as starting point not final word look things up after"],"conclusion":"treat talks as starting point not final word look things up after"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-090.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A programme source I enjoy is TED. It is a website rather than a normal television channel, but I use it to watch short talks whenever I want. The talks cover subjects such as science, education, health and society.\n\nI have the website saved on my phone and check it every few days. A short presentation often fits well into my train journey to work. I like hearing an expert explain an unfamiliar idea in clear language. One talk about sleep and memory encouraged me to stop working so late at night, so it had a practical effect on my routine.\n\nThe main advantage is learning something useful in a short time. However, a brief talk cannot explain every side of a complicated issue. I try to remember that a confident speaker may still be simplifying the subject. If an idea seems important, I read more afterwards. For me, TED is a good way to discover questions and interests, rather than a replacement for studying a topic in greater detail.","html":"A programme source I enjoy is TED. It is a website rather than a normal television channel, but I use it to watch short talks whenever I want. The talks cover subjects such as science, education, health and society.\n\nI have the website saved on my phone and check it every few days. A short presentation often fits well into my train journey to work. I like hearing an expert explain an unfamiliar idea in clear language. One talk about sleep and memory encouraged me to stop working so late at night, so it had a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical effect</strong> on my routine.\n\nThe main advantage is learning something useful in a short time. However, a brief talk cannot explain every side of a complicated issue. I try to remember that a confident speaker may still be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">simplifying the subject</strong>. If an idea seems important, I read more afterwards. For me, TED is a good way to discover questions and interests, rather than a replacement for studying a topic in greater detail.","phrases":[{"en":"practical effect","meaning":"A change that influences real actions or everyday life."},{"en":"simplifying the subject","meaning":"Making a topic easier to explain by leaving out some details."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-090.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A TV series you enjoy watching","prompt":"Describe a TV series you enjoy watching\nYou should say:\nWhat it is\nWhat it's about\nWhen you first watched it\nAnd explain why you like watching it","answer":"The TV series I want to talk about is Friends, the American sitcom about a group of six friends living in New York. I know it finished decades ago, but I only discovered it myself about two years ago, during the exam stress of my final year at school, when I badly needed something light to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">switch my brain off</strong> with.\n\nIt follows six friends — Ross, Rachel, Monica, Chandler, Joey and Phoebe — through their twenties and thirties, mostly hanging out in Monica's apartment or the coffee shop downstairs, dealing with ordinary things like jobs, break-ups and family drama, but always with a joke close behind. There isn't really one big storyline; it's more about watching the same group grow up together over ten years.\n\nI first watched it completely by accident. A classmate had it running in the background during a study session, and within about two episodes I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completely hooked</strong>. I ended up watching the entire first season that same week instead of revising, which I probably shouldn't admit.\n\nThe reason I love it so much is that it's just endlessly comforting. Every episode <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wraps up neatly</strong> in about twenty minutes, so it's perfect when I'm tired and don't want to commit to anything complicated. Chandler is definitely my favourite character — his <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sarcastic one-liners</strong> genuinely make me laugh out loud, even on a rewatch, and I think that's rare for a show this old.\n\nWhat surprises me most is how well it's held up. The fashion is obviously dated, but the friendships and the humour still feel completely relevant, and I think that's exactly why it's stayed so popular with people my age who weren't even born when it first <strong class=\"key-phrase\">came out</strong>. Whenever I've had a bad day, I still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put an episode on</strong> just to feel better, and it works pretty much every time.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"switch my brain off","context":"I know it finished decades ago, but I only discovered it myself about two years ago, during the exam stress of my final year at school, when I badly needed something light to switch my brain off with."},{"en":"completely hooked","context":"A classmate had it running in the background during a study session, and within about two episodes I was completely hooked."},{"en":"wraps up neatly","context":"Every episode wraps up neatly in about twenty minutes, so it's perfect when I'm tired and don't want to commit to anything complicated."},{"en":"sarcastic one-liners","context":"Chandler is definitely my favourite character — his sarcastic one-liners genuinely make me laugh out loud, even on a rewatch, and I think that's rare for a show this old."},{"en":"came out","context":"The fashion is obviously dated, but the friendships and the humour still feel completely relevant, and I think that's exactly why it's stayed so popular with people my age who weren't even born when it first came out."},{"en":"put an episode on","context":"Whenever I've had a bad day, I still put an episode on just to feel better, and it works pretty much every time."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a TV series you enjoy watching","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-091","_displayNo":"#073","outline":{"topic":"Friends (TV sitcom)","intro":"TV series Friends American sitcom six friends New York discovered two years ago exam stress final year","body":["follows six friends Ross Rachel Monica Chandler Joey Phoebe twenties thirties apartment coffee shop","first watched by accident classmate had running background study session two episodes completely hooked","love it endlessly comforting episode wraps up neatly twenty minutes Chandler favourite sarcastic one-liners","surprises how well held up fashion dated friendships humour still feel relevant popular people my age"],"conclusion":"bad day still put episode on feel better works pretty much every time"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-091.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A television series I enjoy is Friends. It follows six people in New York as they deal with jobs, relationships and family problems. Much of the story takes place in an apartment or a nearby coffee shop.\n\nI discovered it about two years ago, during a stressful exam period at school. A classmate played it while we were studying, and after a couple of episodes I wanted to see more. I watched much of the first season that week, although I probably should have spent more time revising.\n\nI like the programme because it is easy and comforting to watch. Episodes last about twenty minutes, so I can enjoy one without following a complicated plot. Chandler is my favourite character because his quick jokes often make me laugh, even when I have seen the episode before. Some clothes and objects look old now, but the friendship still feels familiar. After a bad day, watching the group support each other usually helps me feel better. It is a simple way to relax without too much effort.","html":"A television series I enjoy is Friends. It follows six people in New York as they deal with jobs, relationships and family problems. Much of the story takes place in an apartment or a nearby coffee shop.\n\nI discovered it about two years ago, during a stressful exam period at school. A classmate played it while we were studying, and after a couple of episodes I wanted to see more. I watched much of the first season that week, although I probably should have spent more time <strong class=\"key-phrase\">revising</strong>.\n\nI like the programme because it is easy and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comforting</strong> to watch. Episodes last about twenty minutes, so I can enjoy one without following a complicated plot. Chandler is my favourite character because his quick jokes often make me laugh, even when I have seen the episode before. Some clothes and objects look old now, but the friendship still feels familiar. After a bad day, watching the group support each other usually helps me feel better. It is a simple way to relax without too much effort.","phrases":[{"en":"revising","meaning":"Studying material again to prepare for an exam."},{"en":"comforting","meaning":"Making someone feel calmer, safer or less unhappy."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-091.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A useful website that you often visit","prompt":"Describe a useful website that you often visit.\nYou should say:\nWhat the contents of this website are\nHow you first found this website\nHow often you visit this website\nAnd explain how this website helps you.","answer":"The website I want to talk about is called Cookpad, a recipe-sharing site where home cooks upload their own dishes with step-by-step photos. I'm not a confident cook at all, so it's become something I check almost every time I'm planning what to make for dinner.\n\nI first found it through my friend Minh, who's a genuinely good cook. I was complaining that everything I made tasted bland, and she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sent me a link to</strong> a recipe for braised fish in caramel sauce that turned out perfectly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on the first try</strong>. I was honestly shocked it worked, and I've been using the site ever since.\n\nThe content is mostly ordinary home recipes rather than anything fancy — things like what to do with leftover vegetables, or how my grandmother's generation used to make certain dishes, written by real people rather than professional chefs. Each recipe usually comes with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">clear step-by-step photos</strong> and a comments section where other users mention any changes they made, which is often more useful than the recipe itself.\n\nI probably check it three or four times a week, usually on my way home from work when I'm trying to decide what to cook. It's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely useful</strong> because it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saved me from</strong> ordering takeaway more times than I can count, and it's slowly made me a much more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">confident cook</strong>. My family has definitely noticed the difference — my mother even asked me for a recipe last month, which has never happened before.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"sent me a link to","context":"I was complaining that everything I made tasted bland, and she sent me a link to a recipe for braised fish in caramel sauce that turned out perfectly on the first try."},{"en":"on the first try","context":"I was complaining that everything I made tasted bland, and she sent me a link to a recipe for braised fish in caramel sauce that turned out perfectly on the first try."},{"en":"clear step-by-step photos","context":"Each recipe usually comes with clear step-by-step photos and a comments section where other users mention any changes they made, which is often more useful than the recipe itself."},{"en":"genuinely useful","context":"It's genuinely useful because it's saved me from ordering takeaway more times than I can count, and it's slowly made me a much more confident cook."},{"en":"saved me from","context":"It's genuinely useful because it's saved me from ordering takeaway more times than I can count, and it's slowly made me a much more confident cook."},{"en":"confident cook","context":"I'm not a confident cook at all, so it's become something I check almost every time I'm planning what to make for dinner."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a useful website that you often visit","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-092","_displayNo":"#074","outline":{"topic":"Cookpad recipe website","intro":"website Cookpad recipe-sharing site home cooks upload dishes step-by-step photos not confident cook","body":["first found through friend Minh good cook sent link braised fish caramel sauce worked first try","content ordinary home recipes leftover vegetables grandmother generation real people not professional chefs","clear step-by-step photos comments section users mention changes made often useful","check three four times week way home work deciding what cook"],"conclusion":"genuinely useful saved from takeaway confident cook family noticed mother asked recipe"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-092.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A useful website I often visit is Cookpad. It allows ordinary people to share recipes, usually with photographs showing each stage. I am not a very confident cook, so I use it when deciding what to make for dinner.\n\nMy friend Minh first sent me a link after I complained that my meals had little flavour. It was a recipe for fish cooked in caramel sauce, and it worked well on my first attempt. Since then, I have checked the site three or four times a week, often on my way home from work.\n\nI like the everyday recipes, including ideas for using leftover vegetables. The photos make the instructions easier to follow, and comments from other users often suggest useful changes. The site has helped me cook more instead of ordering takeaway. My family has noticed the improvement, and my mother even asked me for a recipe recently. That made me proud. Cookpad is useful because it turns a daily problem into an opportunity to learn something practical.","html":"A useful website I often visit is Cookpad. It allows ordinary people to share recipes, usually with photographs showing each stage. I am not a very confident cook, so I use it when deciding what to make for dinner.\n\nMy friend Minh first sent me a link after I complained that my meals had little flavour. It was a recipe for fish cooked in caramel sauce, and it worked well on my first attempt. Since then, I have checked the site three or four times a week, often on my way home from work.\n\nI like the everyday recipes, including ideas for using <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leftover vegetables</strong>. The photos make the instructions easier to follow, and comments from other users often suggest useful changes. The site has helped me cook more instead of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ordering takeaway</strong>. My family has noticed the improvement, and my mother even asked me for a recipe recently. That made me proud. Cookpad is useful because it turns a daily problem into an opportunity to learn something practical.","phrases":[{"en":"leftover vegetables","meaning":"Vegetables remaining after a previous meal or cooking session."},{"en":"ordering takeaway","meaning":"Buying prepared food to eat somewhere else, often at home."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-092.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A vehicle you would like to buy in the future","prompt":"Describe a vehicle you would like to buy in the future.\nYou should say:\n- what kind of vehicle it is\n- what you would use it for\n- and why you would like to buy it.","answer":"I would like to buy my own helicopter. I recently saw a TV programme about someone who flew around in his own helicopter, and I remember thinking that it would be great to have one. The helicopter on the programme was a small, blue, two-seater and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">seemed quite easy to fly</strong> - that's the kind of thing I'd like to buy.\n\nIdeally, I would use my helicopter instead of a car. First, I would need to learn to fly it, but then I would use it to go on short trips or holidays. It would be fantastic to be able to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">avoid traffic jams</strong>, and get everywhere <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in a fraction of the time</strong> it normally takes.\n\nI would take friends and family sightseeing, over cities or mountain tops, and maybe I could even use it to do the shopping! The reason I would like to buy a helicopter is that you can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">park one almost anywhere</strong>. You do not need to find a runway or an airport like you would for a plane.\n\nSo I would be able to park my helicopter in my garden - if I had a bigger garden. Also, I have always been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">intrigued by</strong> those helipads on the tops of tall buildings - I think it would be fun to land on top of a building. A helicopter probably isn't <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a realistic choice of vehicle</strong>, but you never know - I might win the lottery!","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"seemed quite easy to fly","context":"The helicopter on the programme was a small, blue, two-seater and it seemed quite easy to fly - that's the kind of thing I'd like to buy."},{"en":"avoid traffic jams","context":"It would be fantastic to be able to avoid traffic jams, and get everywhere in a fraction of the time it normally takes."},{"en":"in a fraction of the time","context":"It would be fantastic to be able to avoid traffic jams, and get everywhere in a fraction of the time it normally takes."},{"en":"park one almost anywhere","context":"The reason I would like to buy a helicopter is that you can park one almost anywhere."},{"en":"intrigued by","context":"Also, I have always been intrigued by those helipads on the tops of tall buildings - I think it would be fun to land on top of a building."},{"en":"a realistic choice of vehicle","context":"A helicopter probably isn't a realistic choice of vehicle, but you never know - I might win the lottery!"}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a vehicle you would like to buy in the future","id":"speaking-part-2-093","_displayNo":"#075","outline":{"topic":"helicopter","intro":"like buy own helicopter","body":["helicopter programme small blue two-seater seemed easy fly - that's kind","fantastic able avoid traffic jams get everywhere fraction time normally takes","reason like buy helicopter park one almost anywhere","always intrigued helipads tops tall buildings - think fun land top"],"conclusion":"helicopter probably not realistic choice vehicle never know - win"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-093.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An unusual vehicle I would like to buy is a small helicopter. The idea came from a television programme about someone who owned one. His helicopter was blue and had two seats, which is the kind I imagine having.\n\nOf course, I would first need proper training and permission to fly. If I could use one, I would enjoy taking short trips and seeing places from the air. Flying above cities or mountains with a family member would be a memorable experience. Avoiding long traffic jams is another part of the attraction.\n\nI am also interested in helicopter landing areas on top of tall buildings. Landing there seems very different from arriving by car. However, I realise owning a helicopter is not a realistic plan for me now. It would be expensive, and finding suitable places to land would require care. I treat it mainly as a dream rather than a serious shopping decision. Still, if I ever had enough money, perhaps through winning the lottery, I would love to explore the possibility.","html":"An unusual vehicle I would like to buy is a small helicopter. The idea came from a television programme about someone who owned one. His helicopter was blue and had two seats, which is the kind I imagine having.\n\nOf course, I would first need proper training and permission to fly. If I could use one, I would enjoy taking short trips and seeing places from the air. Flying above cities or mountains with a family member would be a memorable experience. Avoiding long traffic jams is another part of the attraction.\n\nI am also interested in helicopter landing areas on top of tall buildings. Landing there seems very different from arriving by car. However, I realise owning a helicopter is not a realistic plan for me now. It would be expensive, and finding <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suitable places</strong> to land would require care. I treat it mainly as a dream rather than a serious shopping decision. Still, if I ever had enough money, perhaps through winning the lottery, I would love to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">explore the possibility</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"suitable places","meaning":"Locations that are appropriate for a particular purpose."},{"en":"explore the possibility","meaning":"Find out whether something could actually be done."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-093.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An advertisement that you saw or heard or liked","prompt":"Describe an advertisement that you saw or heard or liked\nYou should say:\nWhere you saw or heard it\nWhat kind of advertisement it was\nWhat the content of the advertisement was (or, what product /service was\nadvertised)\nAnd explain how you felt about this advertisement/ why you liked it.","answer":"I want to tell you about my all-time favourite advert, for a paint called Dulux Easy Clean. I first saw it years ago, during a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">commercial break</strong> on TV, and I've loved it ever since.\n\nIt starts with a group of children covering the walls of their house in paint, mud and powder, having <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the time of their lives</strong>. Then their mother gets home after a long day at work, walks in the front door, and finds her little boy happily drawing all over the newly painted wall with his crayons. Instead of getting angry, which is what you'd expect, she just looks at him and smiles.\n\nThat's the whole point of the advert — because the walls are painted with Dulux Easy Clean, any mark can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wiped off in seconds</strong>. It ends with a slogan that works two ways at once: with Dulux Easy Clean, forgiveness comes easily, because the mess itself is never really a problem.\n\nI love this ad for a few reasons. First, the kids act exactly like real kids — mischievous but completely innocent — which I find genuinely sweet. I can't <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take my eyes off</strong> the screen whenever it comes on. The song playing over it, “I'm Sorry”, fits the mood perfectly too. But honestly, it's that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">clever slogan</strong> that stuck with me the most, and I think it's exactly why the campaign did such a good job of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">building brand awareness</strong> — you remember the ad long after you've forgotten most others.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"commercial break","context":"I want to tell you about my all-time favourite advert, for a paint called Dulux Easy Clean. I first saw it years ago, during a commercial break on TV, and I've loved it ever since."},{"en":"the time of their lives","context":"It starts with a group of children covering the walls of their house in paint, mud and powder, having the time of their lives."},{"en":"wiped off in seconds","context":"That's the whole point of the advert — because the walls are painted with Dulux Easy Clean, any mark can be wiped off in seconds."},{"en":"take my eyes off","context":"I can't take my eyes off the screen whenever it comes on."},{"en":"clever slogan","context":"But honestly, it's that clever slogan that stuck with me the most, and I think it's exactly why the campaign did such a good job of building brand awareness — you remember the ad long after you've forgotten most others."},{"en":"building brand awareness","context":"But honestly, it's that clever slogan that stuck with me the most, and I think it's exactly why the campaign did such a good job of building brand awareness — you remember the ad long after you've forgotten most others."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an advertisement that you saw or heard or liked","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-094","_displayNo":"#076","outline":{"topic":"Dulux Easy Clean ad","intro":"all-time favourite advert paint Dulux Easy Clean first saw years ago commercial break loved ever since","body":["starts children covering walls paint mud powder having time of their lives","mother gets home long day work finds boy drawing crayons newly painted wall smiles instead angry","point advert Dulux Easy Clean marks wiped off seconds slogan works two ways forgiveness comes easily","love ad few reasons kids act like real kids mischievous innocent genuinely sweet"],"conclusion":"clever slogan stuck with me most campaign building brand awareness remember ad long after forgotten others"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-094.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An advertisement I like is for Dulux Easy Clean paint. I first saw it during a television commercial break several years ago. It shows children making a mess on the walls of their home with paint, mud and crayons.\n\nWhen their mother returns from work, she finds her little boy drawing on a newly painted wall. Instead of becoming angry, she smiles. The message is that marks can be wiped off easily because of the paint, so the children's mistakes do not become a major problem.\n\nI like the advertisement because the children seem playful and natural. The song, I'm Sorry, also suits the story. Most importantly, the idea connects the product with an ordinary family experience. It explains the benefit without giving a long technical description. I still remember the message after many years, which suggests that the advertisement worked well. It made the brand familiar to me and showed how a practical product could be presented through a warm, funny story rather than just a list of features.","html":"An advertisement I like is for Dulux Easy Clean paint. I first saw it during a television <strong class=\"key-phrase\">commercial break</strong> several years ago. It shows children making a mess on the walls of their home with paint, mud and crayons.\n\nWhen their mother returns from work, she finds her little boy drawing on a newly painted wall. Instead of becoming angry, she smiles. The message is that marks can be wiped off easily because of the paint, so the children's mistakes do not become a major problem.\n\nI like the advertisement because the children seem playful and natural. The song, I'm Sorry, also suits the story. Most importantly, the idea connects the product with an ordinary family experience. It explains the benefit without giving a long technical description. I still remember the message after many years, which suggests that the advertisement worked well. It made the brand familiar to me and showed how a practical product could be presented through a warm, funny story rather than just a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">list of features</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"commercial break","meaning":"A pause in a television programme when advertisements are shown."},{"en":"list of features","meaning":"A description of the functions or qualities of a product."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-094.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An advertisement you have seen recently","prompt":"Describe an advertisement you have seen recently.\nYou should say:\n- When and where you saw it;\n- What kind of advertisement it was;\n- What the contents of the advertisements were;\n- How you felt about it.","answer":"I want to talk about an advert for Blue Electronics, one of the biggest <strong class=\"key-phrase\">electronics brands</strong> in Vietnam. I saw the latest one just last week, scrolling through Facebook late at night, though I'd already seen versions of it on TV plenty of times before.\n\nThe advert shows people dressed up as blue household appliances — a fridge, a washing machine, a rice cooker — smiling and dancing to a really catchy, slightly annoying tune, while staring almost too intensely at the camera. I think the whole point is to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">create a big impression</strong> and make the products feel more appealing, maybe even a bit more like friendly characters than just machines.\n\nHonestly, I think they're also trying to get the tune <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuck in your head</strong> on purpose, so that whenever you hear it again, you automatically think of Blue Electronics. It's a bit of a strange approach, but I have to admit it's clever, because I still remember the tune weeks later, even though I never wanted to.\n\nWhat's interesting is that I don't think this kind of ad works for everyone. My parents find it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a bit over the top</strong> and much prefer <strong class=\"key-phrase\">straightforward, informative ads</strong> that just explain the product clearly. People my age, though, tend to find it funny rather than annoying — I think we're just more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">used to this kind of advertising</strong> online.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"electronics brands","context":"I want to talk about an advert for Blue Electronics, one of the biggest electronics brands in Vietnam. I saw the latest one just last week, scrolling through Facebook late at night, though I'd already seen versions of it on TV plenty of times before."},{"en":"create a big impression","context":"I think the whole point is to create a big impression and make the products feel more appealing, maybe even a bit more like friendly characters than just machines."},{"en":"stuck in your head","context":"Honestly, I think they're also trying to get the tune stuck in your head on purpose, so that whenever you hear it again, you automatically think of Blue Electronics."},{"en":"a bit over the top","context":"My parents find it a bit over the top and much prefer straightforward, informative ads that just explain the product clearly."},{"en":"straightforward, informative ads","context":"My parents find it a bit over the top and much prefer straightforward, informative ads that just explain the product clearly."},{"en":"used to this kind of advertising","context":"People my age, though, tend to find it funny rather than annoying — I think we're just more used to this kind of advertising online."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an advertisement you have seen recently","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-095","_displayNo":"#077","outline":{"topic":"Blue Electronics ad","intro":"advert Blue Electronics biggest electronics brands Vietnam saw latest just last week scrolling Facebook late night","body":["shows people dressed blue household appliances fridge washing machine rice cooker smiling dancing catchy tune","point create big impression make products feel appealing friendly characters not just machines","trying get tune stuck in your head on purpose whenever hear again think Blue Electronics","parents find bit over top prefer straightforward informative ads people my age find funny not annoying"],"conclusion":"think we're just more used to this kind of advertising online"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-095.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An advertisement I saw recently was for Blue Electronics. I came across it on Facebook last week, although I had seen similar versions on television before. It showed people dressed as blue household machines, including a fridge and a washing machine.\n\nThey smiled and danced while a short tune played repeatedly. The style was unusual and very noticeable. I think the company wanted viewers to remember the colour, the music and the brand together. It certainly worked on me because I could still remember the tune afterwards.\n\nMy feelings about it are mixed. I find it quite funny, but the song can also be annoying when repeated too often. My parents prefer advertisements that explain products clearly and think this one is too dramatic. People my age may be more used to this kind of online humour. I would not say it taught me much about the machines themselves, but it created a strong impression. It shows that an advertisement can be memorable even when viewers do not all enjoy it.","html":"An advertisement I saw recently was for Blue Electronics. I came across it on Facebook last week, although I had seen similar versions on television before. It showed people dressed as blue <strong class=\"key-phrase\">household machines</strong>, including a fridge and a washing machine.\n\nThey smiled and danced while a short tune played repeatedly. The style was unusual and very noticeable. I think the company wanted viewers to remember the colour, the music and the brand together. It certainly worked on me because I could still remember the tune afterwards.\n\nMy feelings about it are mixed. I find it quite funny, but the song can also be annoying when repeated too often. My parents prefer advertisements that explain products clearly and think this one is too dramatic. People my age may be more used to this kind of online humour. I would not say it taught me much about the machines themselves, but it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">created a strong impression</strong>. It shows that an advertisement can be memorable even when viewers do not all enjoy it.","phrases":[{"en":"household machines","meaning":"Devices used for tasks in the home."},{"en":"created a strong impression","meaning":"Made people notice and remember it clearly."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-095.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An article you read about healthy life","prompt":"Describe an article you read about healthy life\nYou should say:\nwhere you read it from\nwhen you read it\nwhat the article was about\nAnd explain what you thought about this article","answer":"I want to talk about an article called “Ten Ways to Live Longer”, which I read a few months ago in the Guardian, a well-known British newspaper. I came across it almost by accident, scrolling through news online late one night when I probably should have been asleep, and it ended up being one of the more useful things I've read all year.\n\nThe article opens with a surprising fact: the country with the longest life expectancy, for both men and women, is Japan. It explains that people there tend to have regular health check-ups and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pay close attention to</strong> hygiene in daily life. It also points out that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">avoiding a sedentary lifestyle</strong> is one of the simplest rules for good health, even though plenty of countries still struggle with it — basically, get moving instead of sitting around all day.\n\nAnother point that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuck with me</strong> was about blood pressure — the article says you should get it checked regularly, since high blood pressure is one of the biggest single causes of early death and years of poor health, and a lot of people don't even realise they have it.\n\nThe ten tips themselves were pretty simple: things like not smoking, eating more vegetables instead of junk food, drinking in moderation, and managing stress, which all sound obvious but can genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">add years to your life</strong> if you actually stick to them. You don't need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">intense workouts</strong> either — the article suggested small things like carrying your own shopping bags instead of using a trolley, just to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improve your muscle strength</strong> a couple of days a week.\n\nWhat I liked most about it was how <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical and realistic</strong> the advice was — nothing extreme, just small daily habits. It made me rethink a few of my own habits, and I've actually started walking to work twice a week since reading it.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"pay close attention to","context":"It explains that people there tend to have regular health check-ups and pay close attention to hygiene in daily life."},{"en":"avoiding a sedentary lifestyle","context":"It also points out that avoiding a sedentary lifestyle is one of the simplest rules for good health, even though plenty of countries still struggle with it — basically, get moving instead of sitting around all day."},{"en":"stuck with me","context":"Another point that stuck with me was about blood pressure — the article says you should get it checked regularly, since high blood pressure is one of the biggest single causes of early death and years of poor health, and a lot of people don't even realise they have it."},{"en":"add years to your life","context":"The ten tips themselves were pretty simple: things like not smoking, eating more vegetables instead of junk food, drinking in moderation, and managing stress, which all sound obvious but can genuinely add years to your life if you actually stick to them."},{"en":"intense workouts","context":"You don't need intense workouts either — the article suggested small things like carrying your own shopping bags instead of using a trolley, just to improve your muscle strength a couple of days a week."},{"en":"improve your muscle strength","context":"You don't need intense workouts either — the article suggested small things like carrying your own shopping bags instead of using a trolley, just to improve your muscle strength a couple of days a week."},{"en":"practical and realistic","context":"What I liked most about it was how practical and realistic the advice was — nothing extreme, just small daily habits. It made me rethink a few of my own habits, and I've actually started walking to work twice a week since reading it."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an article you read about healthy life","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-096","_displayNo":"#078","outline":{"topic":"Ten Ways to Live Longer","intro":"article Ten Ways Live Longer read few months ago Guardian British newspaper came across accident scrolling news late night","body":["surprising fact country longest life expectancy Japan regular health check-ups pay close attention hygiene","avoiding sedentary lifestyle simple rule good health get moving instead sitting around","point stuck with me blood pressure checked regularly biggest cause early death poor health","ten tips simple not smoking vegetables junk food drinking moderation managing stress add years life"],"conclusion":"liked most practical realistic advice small daily habits started walking work twice week since"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-096.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An article about healthy living that I remember was called Ten Ways to Live Longer. I read it online in the Guardian a few months ago after finding it while looking through the news late at night.\n\nThe article discussed everyday habits connected with health. It mentioned regular check-ups, being physically active, avoiding smoking and eating more vegetables. It also explained why people should pay attention to things such as blood pressure rather than waiting until they feel unwell. The suggestions were presented as ordinary habits, rather than an extreme fitness plan.\n\nWhat I liked was how practical the advice seemed. The article encouraged readers to make small changes instead of assuming that healthy living requires hours in a gym. It made me think about how much time I spent sitting and how often I chose convenience over movement. After reading it, I began walking to work twice a week. I cannot judge every claim in the article myself, but it gave me a useful reason to look more carefully at my routine.","html":"An article about healthy living that I remember was called Ten Ways to Live Longer. I read it online in the Guardian a few months ago after finding it while looking through the news late at night.\n\nThe article discussed everyday habits connected with health. It mentioned <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regular check-ups</strong>, being <strong class=\"key-phrase\">physically active</strong>, avoiding smoking and eating more vegetables. It also explained why people should pay attention to things such as blood pressure rather than waiting until they feel unwell. The suggestions were presented as ordinary habits, rather than an extreme fitness plan.\n\nWhat I liked was how practical the advice seemed. The article encouraged readers to make small changes instead of assuming that healthy living requires hours in a gym. It made me think about how much time I spent sitting and how often I chose convenience over movement. After reading it, I began walking to work twice a week. I cannot judge every claim in the article myself, but it gave me a useful reason to look more carefully at my routine.","phrases":[{"en":"regular check-ups","meaning":"Health examinations carried out from time to time."},{"en":"physically active","meaning":"Moving the body regularly rather than spending most of the time sitting."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-096.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An educational television programme that you liked","prompt":"Describe an educational television programme that you liked.\nYou should say:\n- what the programme was about\n- when and where you saw it\n- how you found out about this programme\n- and why you liked it.","answer":"I'm going to talk about one of my favourite educational TV programmes, a nature documentary called Planet Earth. It has eleven episodes, and each one covers a different habitat, like mountains, caves, deserts and jungles, using <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spectacular footage</strong> of animals, plants and landscapes from all over the world. I first saw it years ago, when it was broadcast on the BBC.\n\nI think it went out every Sunday evening, which was honestly the perfect slot for a programme like that. A few years later, someone gave me the whole box set on DVD for Christmas, so I ended up watching every episode again from the start. I actually found out about it before it aired, because the trailers were shown constantly in the weeks leading up to the first episode, and they were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">impossible to ignore</strong> — beautiful shots of nature set to dramatic music.\n\nI also recognised the narrator's voice straight away — it's David Attenborough, and I don't think there's a nature programme from the last twenty years he hasn't narrated. It was those trailers that had me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hooked from the first episode</strong>. What I liked most, though, was the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stunning photography</strong> and how the series covered the whole planet rather than just one place. In the Fresh Water episode, for example, they filmed giant salamanders in Japan, crocodiles in the Nile, and river dolphins in the Amazon.\n\nI remember being amazed that a film crew could get access to locations like that. I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">picked up all sorts of facts</strong> about animals I'd never heard of, and the programme genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">opened my eyes to</strong> how much of the planet I hadn't seen.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"spectacular footage","context":"It has eleven episodes, and each one covers a different habitat, like mountains, caves, deserts and jungles, using spectacular footage of animals, plants and landscapes from all over the world."},{"en":"impossible to ignore","context":"I actually found out about it before it aired, because the trailers were shown constantly in the weeks leading up to the first episode, and they were impossible to ignore — beautiful shots of nature set to dramatic music."},{"en":"hooked from the first episode","context":"It was those trailers that had me hooked from the first episode."},{"en":"stunning photography","context":"What I liked most, though, was the stunning photography and how the series covered the whole planet rather than just one place."},{"en":"picked up all sorts of facts","context":"I remember being amazed that a film crew could get access to locations like that. I picked up all sorts of facts about animals I'd never heard of, and the programme genuinely opened my eyes to how much of the planet I hadn't seen."},{"en":"opened my eyes to","context":"I remember being amazed that a film crew could get access to locations like that. I picked up all sorts of facts about animals I'd never heard of, and the programme genuinely opened my eyes to how much of the planet I hadn't seen."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an educational television programme that you liked","id":"speaking-part-2-097","_displayNo":"#079","outline":{"topic":"Planet Earth","intro":"one favourite educational TV programmes nature documentary called Planet Earth’","body":["aim Planet Earth’ series take viewer habitats using spectacular footage interesting","few years ago somebody bought DVD box set whole series Christmas","recognised voice narrator programme well-known naturalist David Attenborough","example Fresh Water episode showed giant salamander Japan crocodiles river Nile"],"conclusion":"enjoyed learning strange animal species programme opened eyes hidden wonders"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-097.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An educational programme I enjoyed was Planet Earth, a nature documentary shown on the BBC. It has eleven episodes about different environments, including mountains, deserts, caves and forests. I first watched it years ago after seeing its beautiful television trailers.\n\nThe programme was narrated by David Attenborough, whose voice I recognised from other nature documentaries. Later, someone gave me the DVD collection for Christmas, so I watched the whole series again. I enjoyed being able to return to my favourite episodes.\n\nThe photography impressed me most. The crew filmed animals and landscapes in places I had never seen. In the episode about freshwater, for example, there were animals from Japan, the Nile and the Amazon. I wondered how the team managed to reach such locations and film so clearly. The series taught me about unfamiliar species and showed how varied the planet is. It was entertaining without being only entertainment. After watching, I wanted to learn more about nature and the places where these animals live.","html":"An educational programme I enjoyed was Planet Earth, a nature documentary shown on the BBC. It has eleven episodes about different environments, including mountains, deserts, caves and forests. I first watched it years ago after seeing its beautiful television trailers.\n\nThe programme was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">narrated</strong> by David Attenborough, whose voice I recognised from other nature documentaries. Later, someone gave me the DVD collection for Christmas, so I watched the whole series again. I enjoyed being able to return to my favourite episodes.\n\nThe photography impressed me most. The crew filmed animals and landscapes in places I had never seen. In the episode about freshwater, for example, there were animals from Japan, the Nile and the Amazon. I wondered how the team managed to reach such locations and film so clearly. The series taught me about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unfamiliar species</strong> and showed how varied the planet is. It was entertaining without being only entertainment. After watching, I wanted to learn more about nature and the places where these animals live.","phrases":[{"en":"narrated","meaning":"Explained by a speaker whose voice is heard during a programme."},{"en":"unfamiliar species","meaning":"Types of animals or plants that someone does not already know about."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-097.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An electronic machine you want to buy","prompt":"An electronic machine you want to buy\nYou should say:\nWhat it is\nWhen you know this machine\nWhat specific features you want\nAnd explain why you want this machine.","answer":"Let me tell you about an air conditioner I'm hoping to buy in the next few weeks. It's an Aqua — the brand's Vietnamese, but the units are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">manufactured under licence from</strong> a big Japanese company, so I'd expect the quality to be decent. I first noticed it about two weeks ago: I was watching a film online, an advert for it popped up before the video started, and I was struck straight away by how sleek it looked.\n\nI made up my mind pretty much <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on the spot</strong> to buy one, mainly because it's the middle of summer here and my old fan just isn't cutting it any more. There's just one problem — I'm <strong class=\"key-phrase\">short on cash</strong> right now, since I overspent on clothes last month, and this air conditioner isn't cheap. I might have to borrow a bit from my mother and cut back on takeaway food for a week or two to afford it, but I've more or less decided it's worth it.\n\nAs for the features, what really sold me is the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">inverter technology</strong>, which is supposed to cut electricity use quite a lot compared with older models — that matters because my bill practically doubles every summer. It's also got an auto-adjust function, so it can sense the temperature in the room and regulate itself instead of just blasting cold air at one fixed setting all day.\n\nHonestly, it's not a glamorous purchase, but a good air conditioner <strong class=\"key-phrase\">makes a real difference to</strong> how well I sleep and work once the heat sets in properly, so I think it'll be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">money well spent</strong> in the long run.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"manufactured under licence from","context":"It's an Aqua — the brand's Vietnamese, but the units are manufactured under licence from a big Japanese company, so I'd expect the quality to be decent."},{"en":"on the spot","context":"I made up my mind pretty much on the spot to buy one, mainly because it's the middle of summer here and my old fan just isn't cutting it any more."},{"en":"short on cash","context":"There's just one problem — I'm short on cash right now, since I overspent on clothes last month, and this air conditioner isn't cheap."},{"en":"inverter technology","context":"As for the features, what really sold me is the inverter technology, which is supposed to cut electricity use quite a lot compared with older models — that matters because my bill practically doubles every summer."},{"en":"makes a real difference to","context":"Honestly, it's not a glamorous purchase, but a good air conditioner makes a real difference to how well I sleep and work once the heat sets in properly, so I think it'll be money well spent in the long run."},{"en":"money well spent","context":"Honestly, it's not a glamorous purchase, but a good air conditioner makes a real difference to how well I sleep and work once the heat sets in properly, so I think it'll be money well spent in the long run."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"An electronic machine you want to buy","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-098","_displayNo":"#080","outline":{"topic":"Aqua air conditioner","intro":"air conditioner want to buy next few weeks, Aqua brand, Vietnamese but under licence from a Japanese company","body":["first noticed it 2 weeks ago, ad before an online video, struck by how sleek it looked","made up mind on the spot, summer heat + old fan not enough, but short on cash after overspending on clothes","features: inverter technology cuts electricity use, auto-adjust senses room temperature"],"conclusion":"not glamorous but makes a real difference to sleep/work in the heat, money well spent"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-098.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An electronic device I want to buy is an Aqua air conditioner. I first noticed it in an advertisement before an online film about two weeks ago. I liked its simple, modern appearance and began looking at its features.\n\nThe main reason I want it is the summer heat. My old fan is no longer enough to keep my room comfortable. The model I am considering uses inverter technology, which is advertised as using less electricity than older models. It can also adjust its operation according to the room temperature.\n\nThe difficulty is the price. I spent too much on clothes last month, so I would need to save more, reduce spending on takeaway food or ask my mother for some help. I have not bought it yet because I want to make sure I can afford it. Still, I think a comfortable room would help me sleep and work better during hot weather. It is a practical purchase rather than an exciting luxury, but it could improve daily life considerably.","html":"An electronic device I want to buy is an Aqua air conditioner. I first noticed it in an advertisement before an online film about two weeks ago. I liked its simple, modern appearance and began looking at its <strong class=\"key-phrase\">features</strong>.\n\nThe main reason I want it is the summer heat. My old fan is no longer enough to keep my room comfortable. The model I am considering uses inverter technology, which is advertised as using less electricity than older models. It can also adjust its operation according to the room temperature.\n\nThe difficulty is the price. I spent too much on clothes last month, so I would need to save more, reduce spending on takeaway food or ask my mother for some help. I have not bought it yet because I want to make sure I can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">afford it</strong>. Still, I think a comfortable room would help me sleep and work better during hot weather. It is a practical purchase rather than an exciting luxury, but it could improve daily life considerably.","phrases":[{"en":"features","meaning":"The useful functions or qualities of a product."},{"en":"afford it","meaning":"Have enough money to buy it without financial difficulty."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-098.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An exciting book you have read","prompt":"Describe an exciting book you have read.\nYou should say:\n- When you read it;\n- What type/kind of book it is;\n- What it is about;\n- Why you read it /Why it is exciting.","answer":"The book I want to talk about is a thriller called \"Gone Girl\" by Gillian Flynn, which I read last summer during a two-week break from work.\n\nIt's a psychological thriller about a woman who disappears on her fifth wedding anniversary, and the story is told from two points of view — the husband, who quickly becomes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the main suspect</strong>, and the wife, through diary entries written before she vanished. Without giving too much away, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nothing is quite what it seems</strong>, and the story completely changes direction about halfway through.\n\nI <strong class=\"key-phrase\">picked it up</strong> because a friend told me she'd finished it in two days and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">couldn't stop talking about it</strong>, which made me curious enough to try it myself.\n\nWhat made it so exciting for me was that I genuinely couldn't predict where it was going. Most thrillers give you a few obvious clues, but this one kept <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pulling the rug out from under me</strong> every few chapters. I actually stayed up until three in the morning on two separate nights just to find out what happened next, which almost never happens with me and books.\n\nIt also made me think about how differently two people can describe exactly the same relationship, since the husband and wife's versions of events barely match at all. That's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stayed with me</strong> longer than the plot twist itself, if I'm honest.\n\nI'd definitely recommend it to anyone who enjoys a story that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeps you guessing until the very last page</strong>.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"the main suspect","context":"It's a psychological thriller about a woman who disappears on her fifth wedding anniversary, and the story is told from two points of view — the husband, who quickly becomes the main suspect, and the wife, through diary entries written before she vanished."},{"en":"nothing is quite what it seems","context":"Without giving too much away, nothing is quite what it seems, and the story completely changes direction about halfway through."},{"en":"picked it up","context":"I picked it up because a friend told me she'd finished it in two days and couldn't stop talking about it, which made me curious enough to try it myself."},{"en":"couldn't stop talking about it","context":"I picked it up because a friend told me she'd finished it in two days and couldn't stop talking about it, which made me curious enough to try it myself."},{"en":"pulling the rug out from under me","context":"Most thrillers give you a few obvious clues, but this one kept pulling the rug out from under me every few chapters."},{"en":"stayed with me","context":"It also made me think about how differently two people can describe exactly the same relationship, since the husband and wife's versions of events barely match at all. That's stayed with me longer than the plot twist itself, if I'm honest."},{"en":"keeps you guessing until the very last page","context":"I'd definitely recommend it to anyone who enjoys a story that keeps you guessing until the very last page."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an exciting book you have read","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-099","_displayNo":"#081","outline":{"topic":"Gone Girl","intro":"thriller 'Gone Girl' by Gillian Flynn, read last summer on 2-week break","body":["woman disappears on wedding anniversary, told from husband + wife's viewpoints","picked it up bc friend finished it in 2 days, couldn't stop talking about it","exciting bc unpredictable, stayed up till 3am twice to keep reading","made me think how differently 2 people can describe same relationship"],"conclusion":"would recommend to anyone who likes being kept guessing till the last page"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-099.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An exciting book I read is Gone Girl by Gillian Flynn. I read it last summer during a two-week break from work. A friend recommended it after finishing it in just two days, which made me curious.\n\nThe story begins when a woman disappears on her fifth wedding anniversary. Her husband becomes a suspect. We hear his account and also read the wife's earlier diary entries. As the story continues, the reader discovers that the situation is not as simple as it first appears.\n\nI enjoyed it because I could not easily predict what would happen. Several times, a new detail changed my understanding of the characters. On two nights, I stayed awake until three in the morning because I wanted to keep reading. The book also made me think about how two people can describe the same relationship very differently. That idea stayed with me after the mystery ended. I would recommend it to someone who likes an unpredictable story, especially if they enjoy questioning what the characters tell them.","html":"An exciting book I read is Gone Girl by Gillian Flynn. I read it last summer during a two-week break from work. A friend recommended it after finishing it in just two days, which made me curious.\n\nThe story begins when a woman disappears on her fifth wedding anniversary. Her husband becomes a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suspect</strong>. We hear his account and also read the wife's earlier diary entries. As the story continues, the reader discovers that the situation is not as simple as it first appears.\n\nI enjoyed it because I could not easily predict what would happen. Several times, a new detail changed my understanding of the characters. On two nights, I stayed awake until three in the morning because I wanted to keep reading. The book also made me think about how two people can describe the same relationship very differently. That idea stayed with me after the mystery ended. I would recommend it to someone who likes an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unpredictable story</strong>, especially if they enjoy questioning what the characters tell them.","phrases":[{"en":"suspect","meaning":"A person who may have committed a crime."},{"en":"unpredictable story","meaning":"A story whose events or ending are difficult to guess."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-099.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An important letter you received","prompt":"Describe an important letter you received.\nYou should say:\n- who sent the letter\n- what it was about\n- why it was important\n- and explain how you felt about it.","answer":"I'd like to talk about a handwritten letter I received from my grandmother, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">arrived a few months before</strong> she died.\n\nShe'd sent it by post, which was unusual even then, because everyone in the family used the phone by that point. The letter itself was quite short — two sides of thin paper in very careful handwriting, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">her eyesight was failing</strong>. Most of it was ordinary news about the garden and the neighbours, but the last paragraph was different. She wrote that she'd watched me worry over decisions for years, and that in her experience almost <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nothing turned out to be as permanent as it felt</strong> at the time.\n\nIt became important for reasons that had nothing to do with what she'd intended. At the time I read it quickly and put it in a drawer. After she died I found it again, and it was suddenly the only thing I had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in her own words</strong>, rather than just my memory of her voice.\n\nI felt slightly guilty, if I'm honest, because I never replied properly — I phoned instead, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">isn't the same thing</strong>. But I also felt grateful that she'd bothered. A text would've just disappeared into a phone somewhere, whereas that letter's still in my desk, and I take it out occasionally.\n\nIt taught me something practical too: if something matters, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">write it down</strong>, because the person receiving it might need it much later than you'd expect.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"arrived a few months before","context":"I'd like to talk about a handwritten letter I received from my grandmother, which arrived a few months before she died."},{"en":"her eyesight was failing","context":"The letter itself was quite short — two sides of thin paper in very careful handwriting, because her eyesight was failing."},{"en":"nothing turned out to be as permanent as it felt","context":"She wrote that she'd watched me worry over decisions for years, and that in her experience almost nothing turned out to be as permanent as it felt at the time."},{"en":"in her own words","context":"After she died I found it again, and it was suddenly the only thing I had in her own words, rather than just my memory of her voice."},{"en":"isn't the same thing","context":"I felt slightly guilty, if I'm honest, because I never replied properly — I phoned instead, which isn't the same thing."},{"en":"write it down","context":"It taught me something practical too: if something matters, write it down, because the person receiving it might need it much later than you'd expect."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an important letter you received","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-100","_displayNo":"#082","outline":{"topic":"grandmother's handwritten letter","intro":"handwritten letter from grandmother arrived few months before she died","body":["sent by post unusual everyone used phone two sides thin paper careful handwriting","mostly ordinary news garden neighbours but last paragraph different","wrote nothing turns out as permanent as it feels at the time","read quickly put in drawer after she died = only thing in her own words","felt guilty never replied properly phoned instead not same thing"],"conclusion":"if something matters write it down receiver may need it much later"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-100.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An important letter I received was a handwritten one from my grandmother. She sent it a few months before she died. It was unusual because by then our family normally spoke on the phone rather than writing letters.\n\nThe letter covered two pages in careful handwriting. Most of it was news about her garden and neighbours. However, the final paragraph offered advice. She said she had noticed how much I worried about decisions and reminded me that situations often change more than we expect.\n\nAt first, I read it quickly and put it in a drawer. After she died, I found it again and understood its value differently. It was something she had written herself, in her own words. I felt a little guilty that I had phoned instead of sending a proper reply, but I was grateful to have it. I still keep it in my desk and read it occasionally. It reminds me of her care and of the importance of recording things that someone may need much later.","html":"An important letter I received was a handwritten one from my grandmother. She sent it a few months before she died. It was unusual because by then our family normally spoke on the phone rather than writing letters.\n\nThe letter covered two pages in careful handwriting. Most of it was news about her garden and neighbours. However, the final paragraph offered advice. She said she had noticed how much I worried about decisions and reminded me that situations often change more than we expect.\n\nAt first, I read it quickly and put it in a drawer. After she died, I found it again and understood its value differently. It was something she had written herself, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in her own words</strong>. I felt a little guilty that I had phoned instead of sending a proper reply, but I was grateful to have it. I still keep it in my desk and read it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">occasionally</strong>. It reminds me of her care and of the importance of recording things that someone may need much later.","phrases":[{"en":"in her own words","meaning":"Expressed exactly as she chose to say it."},{"en":"occasionally","meaning":"Sometimes, but not very often."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-100.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An important plant in your country","prompt":"Describe an important plant in your country.\nYou should say:\n- what the plant is\n- how you know about it\n- why it is important\n- and explain how you feel about this plant.","answer":"The plant I've chosen to talk about is bamboo, probably the most useful plant in Vietnam after rice, though it gets far less attention.\n\nI've known about it since I was small because it grows everywhere — around villages, along riverbanks and at the edge of almost every field. My grandparents' house had a bamboo hedge that my grandfather planted as a windbreak, and I remember being told never to cut it, because it was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">holding the soil together</strong>.\n\nIt's important for two quite different reasons. The practical one is that almost anything can be made from it: scaffolding, furniture, baskets, fencing, chopsticks, even food, since bamboo shoots are a normal ingredient. It grows extraordinarily fast, so a household could cut what it needed and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the plant would replace itself</strong> within a couple of years. Before cheap steel and plastic became widely available, a rural family <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relied on it constantly</strong>.\n\nThe second reason is cultural. Bamboo turns up throughout Vietnamese literature as a symbol of resilience, because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bends a long way in a storm without snapping</strong> and then straightens again. That image gets used so often it's almost become a cliche, but I think it's a fair one.\n\nI'm quite fond of it, partly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">out of habit</strong>. It's not a spectacular plant, and nobody photographs it, but it's one of those things <strong class=\"key-phrase\">you would notice immediately if it disappeared</strong>.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"holding the soil together","context":"My grandparents' house had a bamboo hedge that my grandfather planted as a windbreak, and I remember being told never to cut it, because it was holding the soil together."},{"en":"the plant would replace itself","context":"It grows extraordinarily fast, so a household could cut what it needed and the plant would replace itself within a couple of years."},{"en":"relied on it constantly","context":"Before cheap steel and plastic became widely available, a rural family relied on it constantly."},{"en":"bends a long way in a storm without snapping","context":"Bamboo turns up throughout Vietnamese literature as a symbol of resilience, because it bends a long way in a storm without snapping and then straightens again."},{"en":"out of habit","context":"I'm quite fond of it, partly out of habit. It's not a spectacular plant, and nobody photographs it, but it's one of those things you would notice immediately if it disappeared."},{"en":"you would notice immediately if it disappeared","context":"I'm quite fond of it, partly out of habit. It's not a spectacular plant, and nobody photographs it, but it's one of those things you would notice immediately if it disappeared."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an important plant in your country","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-101","_displayNo":"#083","outline":{"topic":"bamboo","intro":"bamboo most useful plant after rice gets far less attention","body":["known since small grows everywhere villages riverbanks edge of fields","grandfather planted bamboo hedge as windbreak told never cut = holding soil","practical: scaffolding furniture baskets fencing chopsticks food shoots","grows fast household cut what needed plant replaces itself couple yrs","cultural: symbol of resilience bends in storm w/o snapping straightens again"],"conclusion":"not spectacular nobody photographs it but would notice if it disappeared"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-101.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An important plant in Vietnam is bamboo. I have known it since childhood because it grows around villages, fields and rivers. My grandfather planted a bamboo hedge beside his house to reduce wind and help hold the soil in place.\n\nBamboo has many practical uses. People make baskets, furniture, fences and chopsticks from it. Young bamboo shoots are also used in cooking. Because it grows quickly, it has long been a useful material for families, especially before metal and plastic became so common.\n\nIt is important in culture as well. In Vietnamese stories and writing, bamboo often represents the ability to stay strong during difficult times. It can bend in bad weather and then return upright, which makes that comparison easy to understand. I like bamboo partly because it is so familiar. It is not always treated as a beautiful plant, but it quietly supports everyday life in many ways. If it disappeared from villages, both the scenery and people's practical routines would feel very different.","html":"An important plant in Vietnam is bamboo. I have known it since childhood because it grows around villages, fields and rivers. My grandfather planted a bamboo hedge beside his house to reduce wind and help hold the soil in place.\n\nBamboo has many practical uses. People make baskets, furniture, fences and chopsticks from it. Young <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bamboo shoots</strong> are also used in cooking. Because it grows quickly, it has long been a useful material for families, especially before metal and plastic became so common.\n\nIt is important in culture as well. In Vietnamese stories and writing, bamboo often represents the ability to stay strong during difficult times. It can bend in bad weather and then <strong class=\"key-phrase\">return upright</strong>, which makes that comparison easy to understand. I like bamboo partly because it is so familiar. It is not always treated as a beautiful plant, but it quietly supports everyday life in many ways. If it disappeared from villages, both the scenery and people's practical routines would feel very different.","phrases":[{"en":"bamboo shoots","meaning":"The young new stems of bamboo that can be prepared as food."},{"en":"return upright","meaning":"Move back into a straight, standing position."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-101.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An important skill which cannot be learned at school","prompt":"Describe an important skill which cannot be learned at school\nYou should say:\n- What the skill is\n- Why it cannot be learned at school\n- Why it is important\n- How we can learn it","answer":"The skill I want to talk about is public speaking — specifically, giving presentations — which schools don't really teach, even though almost every job needs it eventually. I picked up most of it from a mentor, Jimmy, who ran presentation classes at the English centre I studied at a few years ago.\n\nSchools teach facts and grammar rules, but they rarely put you in front of a room and force you to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hold people's attention</strong>, which is really the only way to improve. I remember my first attempt under Jimmy's guidance: I'd written out my whole talk and read it off the page in a shaky voice, and about a minute in I completely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lost my place</strong> and stood there blushing while twenty classmates waited. It was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">excruciatingly awkward</strong>, but Jimmy stopped the session, pointed out I'd lost eye contact the second I got nervous, and had me try again looking only at him.\n\nIt matters because in almost any job you eventually have to stand up and explain something to a room full of people, whether that's a project update or convincing a client to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sign off on an idea</strong>. I've used it constantly since — I now present quarterly figures at work, and I still use Jimmy's trick of picking three friendly faces in the room and speaking mainly to them.\n\nThere's no real shortcut to learning it — you have to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put yourself in front of people</strong> repeatedly and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get feedback each time</strong>, whether that's a course, a Toastmasters group, or recording yourself and watching it back. It took about a year of doing that regularly before speaking in front of a group stopped making my hands shake.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"hold people's attention","context":"Schools teach facts and grammar rules, but they rarely put you in front of a room and force you to hold people's attention, which is really the only way to improve."},{"en":"lost my place","context":"I remember my first attempt under Jimmy's guidance: I'd written out my whole talk and read it off the page in a shaky voice, and about a minute in I completely lost my place and stood there blushing while twenty classmates waited."},{"en":"excruciatingly awkward","context":"It was excruciatingly awkward, but Jimmy stopped the session, pointed out I'd lost eye contact the second I got nervous, and had me try again looking only at him."},{"en":"sign off on an idea","context":"It matters because in almost any job you eventually have to stand up and explain something to a room full of people, whether that's a project update or convincing a client to sign off on an idea."},{"en":"put yourself in front of people","context":"There's no real shortcut to learning it — you have to put yourself in front of people repeatedly and get feedback each time, whether that's a course, a Toastmasters group, or recording yourself and watching it back."},{"en":"get feedback each time","context":"There's no real shortcut to learning it — you have to put yourself in front of people repeatedly and get feedback each time, whether that's a course, a Toastmasters group, or recording yourself and watching it back."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an important skill which cannot be learned at school","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-128","_displayNo":"#084","outline":{"topic":"public speaking / presentation skills","intro":"public speaking, not taught properly at school, learned mostly from mentor Jimmy at an English centre","body":["school teaches facts, not how to hold a room's attention","first attempt: read from a script, lost my place, stood blushing — excruciatingly awkward","Jimmy stopped the session, pointed out I'd lost eye contact, had me try again looking at him","important bc every job eventually needs presenting — now present quarterly figures at work","trick: pick 3 friendly faces in the room and speak mainly to them"],"conclusion":"no shortcut — put yourself in front of people repeatedly, get feedback, took about a year"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-128.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An important skill I did not learn properly at school is public speaking. I developed it later through presentation classes at an English centre. A mentor called Jimmy helped me understand how to speak more confidently in front of people.\n\nDuring my first attempt, I read from a complete script in a nervous voice. After about a minute, I lost my place and stopped. Jimmy noticed that I had avoided looking at anyone. He asked me to try again while looking at him, which made the task feel more manageable.\n\nI now use presentations at work, including when explaining quarterly results. I still follow his advice by choosing a few friendly faces in the room and speaking towards them. Learning took about a year of regular practice and feedback. I think this skill matters because knowing information is not enough if you cannot explain it clearly to others. Books can offer advice, but real improvement comes from actually speaking, noticing problems and trying again. It is a practical ability I continue to develop.","html":"An important skill I did not learn properly at school is public speaking. I developed it later through presentation classes at an English centre. A mentor called Jimmy helped me understand how to speak more confidently in front of people.\n\nDuring my first attempt, I read from a complete script in a nervous voice. After about a minute, I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lost my place</strong> and stopped. Jimmy noticed that I had avoided looking at anyone. He asked me to try again while looking at him, which made the task feel more manageable.\n\nI now use presentations at work, including when explaining quarterly results. I still follow his advice by choosing a few friendly faces in the room and speaking towards them. Learning took about a year of regular practice and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feedback</strong>. I think this skill matters because knowing information is not enough if you cannot explain it clearly to others. Books can offer advice, but real improvement comes from actually speaking, noticing problems and trying again. It is a practical ability I continue to develop.","phrases":[{"en":"lost my place","meaning":"Forgot which part of a text or speech I had reached."},{"en":"feedback","meaning":"Comments that help someone understand and improve their performance."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-128.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An impressive story you heard from other people","prompt":"Describe an impressive story you heard from other people.\nYou should say:\n- what the story was\n- when you heard it\n- who told you the story\n- and explain why you were impressed by it.","answer":"The story that's stayed with me longest is one a former colleague told me about her father, and I heard it over lunch about two years ago.\n\nHer father had trained as a teacher, but when <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the family's finances collapsed</strong> he took work as a long-distance lorry driver, which he ended up doing for nineteen years. What she only found out after he'd retired was that he'd spent almost every night of those years reading in the cab under a little clip-on light, and had quietly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worked his way through</strong> a correspondence degree in history, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">submitting the assignments from post offices along his route</strong>.\n\nHe never told anyone while he was doing it — not even her mother knew until years later. He didn't use the qualification either; he was fifty-eight by the time he finished, and he had no intention of changing careers at that point. When she asked him why he'd bothered, he apparently said that the driving was something he did, and the reading was who he was, and he hadn't wanted to lose the second one.\n\nI was impressed for two reasons. The obvious one is the persistence: nineteen years of study with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no deadline, no classmates and no reward at the end</strong>. But what struck me more was the reason. We usually assume people study in order to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get something out of it</strong> — a promotion, a new career, a certificate to hang on a wall — and he'd done it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">purely to remain himself</strong>, which I found unexpectedly moving.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"the family's finances collapsed","context":"Her father had trained as a teacher, but when the family's finances collapsed he took work as a long-distance lorry driver, which he ended up doing for nineteen years."},{"en":"worked his way through","context":"What she only found out after he'd retired was that he'd spent almost every night of those years reading in the cab under a little clip-on light, and had quietly worked his way through a correspondence degree in history, submitting the assignments from post offices along his route."},{"en":"submitting the assignments from post offices along his route","context":"What she only found out after he'd retired was that he'd spent almost every night of those years reading in the cab under a little clip-on light, and had quietly worked his way through a correspondence degree in history, submitting the assignments from post offices along his route."},{"en":"no deadline, no classmates and no reward at the end","context":"The obvious one is the persistence: nineteen years of study with no deadline, no classmates and no reward at the end."},{"en":"get something out of it","context":"We usually assume people study in order to get something out of it — a promotion, a new career, a certificate to hang on a wall — and he'd done it purely to remain himself, which I found unexpectedly moving."},{"en":"purely to remain himself","context":"We usually assume people study in order to get something out of it — a promotion, a new career, a certificate to hang on a wall — and he'd done it purely to remain himself, which I found unexpectedly moving."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an impressive story you heard from other people","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-102","_displayNo":"#085","outline":{"topic":"colleague's father studied while driving","intro":"story former colleague told about her father heard over lunch 2 yrs ago","body":["trained as teacher finances collapsed took work long-distance lorry driver 19 yrs","found out only after retired: read in cab every night completed correspondence degree history","submitted assignments from post offices along route never told anyone","never used qualification 58 when finished no intention changing careers","said driving = what he did reading = who he was didn't want to lose second"],"conclusion":"impressed by persistence but more by reason: studied purely to remain himself"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-102.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An impressive story I heard was about a former colleague's father. She told me over lunch about two years ago. He had trained as a teacher, but financial difficulties forced him to become a long-distance lorry driver, a job he did for nineteen years.\n\nWhat surprised her was discovering that he had continued studying throughout that period. At night, he read in his cab under a small light. He completed a history degree by studying at a distance and posting assignments from places along his driving route. He did this quietly, without telling the family at first.\n\nHe finished at fifty-eight and did not plan to change jobs. When asked why he had studied, he explained that learning was an important part of who he was. I found this moving because we often connect education with money or promotion. He had continued for personal reasons, despite tiredness and a difficult schedule. His patience impressed me, but his love of learning impressed me even more. The story changed how I think about the purpose of education.","html":"An impressive story I heard was about a former colleague's father. She told me over lunch about two years ago. He had trained as a teacher, but financial difficulties forced him to become a long-distance lorry driver, a job he did for nineteen years.\n\nWhat surprised her was discovering that he had continued studying throughout that period. At night, he read in his <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cab</strong> under a small light. He completed a history degree by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">studying at a distance</strong> and posting assignments from places along his driving route. He did this quietly, without telling the family at first.\n\nHe finished at fifty-eight and did not plan to change jobs. When asked why he had studied, he explained that learning was an important part of who he was. I found this moving because we often connect education with money or promotion. He had continued for personal reasons, despite tiredness and a difficult schedule. His patience impressed me, but his love of learning impressed me even more. The story changed how I think about the purpose of education.","phrases":[{"en":"cab","meaning":"The part of a lorry where the driver sits."},{"en":"studying at a distance","meaning":"Learning away from a college, using materials and assignments sent remotely."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-102.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting advertisement","prompt":"Describe an interesting advertisement\nYou should say:\nWhere you saw it\nWhat it's about\nWhy you think it was an interesting advertisement","answer":"I want to talk about an airline safety advert I saw on a flight a few months ago, made by Air New Zealand.\n\nInstead of the usual, quite boring safety demonstration, this one was filmed like a proper action film trailer, with dramatic music, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">quick cuts between scenes</strong>, and the flight attendants dressed as adventurers running through jungle sets while still somehow explaining seatbelts and oxygen masks. It was about four minutes long, and I watched the entire thing, which never normally happens with safety videos.\n\nI saw it on the plane itself, right before take-off, on the little screen on the seat in front of me.\n\nI found it interesting mainly because of how well it solved a genuinely hard problem: nobody wants to watch a safety briefing, but airlines are legally required to show one on every single flight. Instead of fighting that, they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned a boring requirement into entertainment</strong>, and by doing that they probably got far more passengers to actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pay attention to</strong> safety information that could matter in an emergency.\n\nIt also made me look at the airline differently. Before that flight I'd never really thought about Air New Zealand's brand at all, but the advert left me with the impression that they're a company willing to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take a creative risk</strong> rather than just doing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the bare minimum</strong> the regulations require.\n\nI think that's what separates a genuinely interesting advert from a forgettable one — it has to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make you feel something</strong> about the brand, not just inform you.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"quick cuts between scenes","context":"Instead of the usual, quite boring safety demonstration, this one was filmed like a proper action film trailer, with dramatic music, quick cuts between scenes, and the flight attendants dressed as adventurers running through jungle sets while still somehow explaining seatbelts and oxygen masks."},{"en":"turned a boring requirement into entertainment","context":"Instead of fighting that, they turned a boring requirement into entertainment, and by doing that they probably got far more passengers to actually pay attention to safety information that could matter in an emergency."},{"en":"pay attention to","context":"Instead of fighting that, they turned a boring requirement into entertainment, and by doing that they probably got far more passengers to actually pay attention to safety information that could matter in an emergency."},{"en":"take a creative risk","context":"Before that flight I'd never really thought about Air New Zealand's brand at all, but the advert left me with the impression that they're a company willing to take a creative risk rather than just doing the bare minimum the regulations require."},{"en":"the bare minimum","context":"Before that flight I'd never really thought about Air New Zealand's brand at all, but the advert left me with the impression that they're a company willing to take a creative risk rather than just doing the bare minimum the regulations require."},{"en":"make you feel something","context":"I think that's what separates a genuinely interesting advert from a forgettable one — it has to make you feel something about the brand, not just inform you."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting advertisement","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-103","_displayNo":"#086","outline":{"topic":"Air New Zealand safety advert","intro":"airline safety advert by Air New Zealand, seen on a flight a few months ago","body":["filmed like action trailer, flight attendants as adventurers, still explains safety rules","saw it on the seat-back screen right before take-off","interesting bc turned a legally-required boring briefing into entertainment people watch","changed how I see the airline's brand - creative, not just doing the minimum"],"conclusion":"a genuinely interesting advert has to make you feel something about the brand"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-103.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An interesting advertisement I saw was an Air New Zealand safety video. I watched it on the seat screen before a flight a few months ago. Instead of a normal safety demonstration, it looked like a short adventure film.\n\nThere was dramatic music, quick changes between scenes and flight attendants moving through a jungle setting. At the same time, they explained seatbelts, oxygen masks and other safety information. It lasted about four minutes, and I watched the whole thing. I do not usually pay such close attention to these videos.\n\nI found it clever because it made a familiar message interesting. Passengers have often heard the instructions before, so it can be difficult to keep their attention. This video used entertainment to help them listen. It also changed my impression of the airline. I felt the company had put effort into doing an ordinary task creatively. For me, an effective advertisement does more than repeat a name. It gives people a clear reason to remember the company afterwards.","html":"An interesting advertisement I saw was an Air New Zealand safety video. I watched it on the seat screen before a flight a few months ago. Instead of a normal safety demonstration, it looked like a short adventure film.\n\nThere was dramatic music, quick changes between scenes and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">flight attendants</strong> moving through a jungle setting. At the same time, they explained seatbelts, oxygen masks and other safety information. It lasted about four minutes, and I watched the whole thing. I do not usually pay such close attention to these videos.\n\nI found it clever because it made a familiar message interesting. Passengers have often heard the instructions before, so it can be difficult to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep their attention</strong>. This video used entertainment to help them listen. It also changed my impression of the airline. I felt the company had put effort into doing an ordinary task creatively. For me, an effective advertisement does more than repeat a name. It gives people a clear reason to remember the company afterwards.","phrases":[{"en":"flight attendants","meaning":"Staff who help and look after passengers on a plane."},{"en":"keep their attention","meaning":"Continue making people interested enough to watch or listen."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-103.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting song you like","prompt":"Describe an interesting song you like\nYou should say:\nWhat it is\nWhich country the song comes from\nWhat story the song tells\nAnd explain why you think it is interesting.","answer":"One of my favourite songs is \"Bonjour Vietnam\", by a French singer called Pham Quynh Anh — she was born in Belgium to Vietnamese parents, and the song's in French, though it's really about Vietnam. It came out years ago, but it's still sitting in my favourites playlist on my phone.\n\nI first <strong class=\"key-phrase\">came across this song</strong> back in secondary school, when it was suddenly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">discussed all over the media</strong> — it's pretty rare for a song about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Vietnamese identity</strong> to get that kind of attention. It's about a Vietnamese woman living abroad who's desperate to learn more about her roots, and who dreams of going to Vietnam herself one day to see whether it's really like the country her parents and the media had always described.\n\nThe first time I heard it, I didn't understand a single word of French, but I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">was hooked by</strong> the melody and the singer's voice straight away. That's actually what pushed me to start learning French properly — I wanted to know exactly what the lyrics meant. Once I understood them, the song became even more beautiful to me, because it made me realise there was still so much about my own country I hadn't discovered yet.\n\nI suppose you could say my love for Vietnam <strong class=\"key-phrase\">was rekindled</strong> by a song written by someone who'd never really lived here. I still put it on sometimes when I'm feeling a bit homesick, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">does the job</strong> every time.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"came across this song","context":"I first came across this song back in secondary school, when it was suddenly discussed all over the media — it's pretty rare for a song about Vietnamese identity to get that kind of attention."},{"en":"discussed all over the media","context":"I first came across this song back in secondary school, when it was suddenly discussed all over the media — it's pretty rare for a song about Vietnamese identity to get that kind of attention."},{"en":"Vietnamese identity","context":"I first came across this song back in secondary school, when it was suddenly discussed all over the media — it's pretty rare for a song about Vietnamese identity to get that kind of attention."},{"en":"was hooked by","context":"The first time I heard it, I didn't understand a single word of French, but I was hooked by the melody and the singer's voice straight away."},{"en":"was rekindled","context":"I suppose you could say my love for Vietnam was rekindled by a song written by someone who'd never really lived here. I still put it on sometimes when I'm feeling a bit homesick, and it does the job every time."},{"en":"does the job","context":"I suppose you could say my love for Vietnam was rekindled by a song written by someone who'd never really lived here. I still put it on sometimes when I'm feeling a bit homesick, and it does the job every time."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting song you like","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-104","_displayNo":"#087","outline":{"topic":"Bonjour Vietnam (song)","intro":"Bonjour Vietnam by Pham Quynh Anh, French singer born in Belgium to Vietnamese parents, song is in French","body":["came across it in secondary school when it was all over the media","tells the story of a Vietnamese woman abroad wanting to learn about her roots, dreams of visiting Vietnam","didn't understand French at first but was hooked by the melody and voice","motivated me to learn French properly to understand the lyrics"],"conclusion":"love for Vietnam was rekindled by the song, still puts it on when feeling homesick"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-104.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A song I like is Bonjour Vietnam, sung by Pham Quynh Anh. She was born in Belgium to Vietnamese parents, and the song is in French. I first heard it at secondary school when it received a lot of attention in the media.\n\nThe song describes someone living abroad who wants to understand Vietnam and her family background. She imagines visiting the country she has heard about from her parents and other sources. Although I did not understand French at first, I loved the melody and the singer's voice.\n\nWanting to understand the words encouraged me to begin learning French. Once I understood the meaning, the song became even more special. It reminded me that there was still much about my own country that I had not explored. I keep it in my phone's favourite playlist and sometimes listen when I miss home. I like how a gentle song can connect language, family and a sense of belonging. It helped me appreciate Vietnam through the feelings of someone looking at it from far away.","html":"A song I like is Bonjour Vietnam, sung by Pham Quynh Anh. She was born in Belgium to Vietnamese parents, and the song is in French. I first heard it at secondary school when it received a lot of attention in the media.\n\nThe song describes someone living abroad who wants to understand Vietnam and her <strong class=\"key-phrase\">family background</strong>. She imagines visiting the country she has heard about from her parents and other sources. Although I did not understand French at first, I loved the melody and the singer's voice.\n\nWanting to understand the words encouraged me to begin learning French. Once I understood the meaning, the song became even more special. It reminded me that there was still much about my own country that I had not explored. I keep it in my phone's favourite playlist and sometimes listen when I miss home. I like how a gentle song can connect language, family and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of belonging</strong>. It helped me appreciate Vietnam through the feelings of someone looking at it from far away.","phrases":[{"en":"family background","meaning":"The history and origins of a person's family."},{"en":"a sense of belonging","meaning":"A feeling of being connected to a place or group."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-104.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An invention that changed people's lives","prompt":"Describe an invention that changed people's lives\nYou should say:\nWhat it was\nWho invented it\nWhen it was invented\nAnd explain how it changed people's lives","answer":"The invention I want to describe is the telephone, which I think is one of the most life-changing inventions there's ever been, even though we barely think about it these days because it's just always there.\n\nAs far as I know, it was Alexander Graham Bell who invented it, back in 1876, after years of experimenting with ways to send sound down a wire. Interestingly, another inventor, Elisha Gray, filed a similar patent on almost the exact same day, so the two of them were basically <strong class=\"key-phrase\">racing each other</strong> without even knowing it.\n\nThe reason it changed people's lives so completely is obvious once you think about what came before it. My grandmother used to tell me that when she was young, if a relative in another city fell ill, the family found out by letter, and by the time it arrived, things had often already changed completely. A phone call turned something that used to take days into something that takes seconds.\n\nIt's changed how I live too, though in a completely different way from my grandmother's generation. I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rely on my phone</strong> for almost everything now — calling my parents every Sunday, sorting out problems at work <strong class=\"key-phrase\">before they get worse</strong>, or letting a friend know I'm running late. It's strange that all of that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">traces back to</strong> one man in a lab in Boston over a century ago.\n\nWhat impresses me most is how something so simple — turning sound into an electrical signal and back again — ended up <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reshaping how the world communicates</strong>. Almost every invention that's come after it, from the mobile phone to video calls, is just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a more advanced version of</strong> the same basic idea.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"racing each other","context":"Interestingly, another inventor, Elisha Gray, filed a similar patent on almost the exact same day, so the two of them were basically racing each other without even knowing it."},{"en":"rely on my phone","context":"I rely on my phone for almost everything now — calling my parents every Sunday, sorting out problems at work before they get worse, or letting a friend know I'm running late."},{"en":"before they get worse","context":"I rely on my phone for almost everything now — calling my parents every Sunday, sorting out problems at work before they get worse, or letting a friend know I'm running late."},{"en":"traces back to","context":"It's strange that all of that traces back to one man in a lab in Boston over a century ago."},{"en":"reshaping how the world communicates","context":"What impresses me most is how something so simple — turning sound into an electrical signal and back again — ended up reshaping how the world communicates."},{"en":"a more advanced version of","context":"Almost every invention that's come after it, from the mobile phone to video calls, is just a more advanced version of the same basic idea."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an invention that changed people's lives","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-105","_displayNo":"#088","outline":{"topic":"the telephone","intro":"telephone, one of the most life-changing inventions, barely think about it bc it's always there","body":["Alexander Graham Bell invented it in 1876; Elisha Gray filed a near-identical patent the same day","grandmother's era: news of illness travelled by letter, took days; phone made it instant","changed my own life too — rely on my phone for calling parents, sorting work problems","strange that all of it traces back to one man in a lab in Boston"],"conclusion":"impressive how something so simple reshaped how the whole world communicates"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-105.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An invention that changed people's lives is the telephone. It made it possible to speak to someone far away instead of waiting for a letter. Alexander Graham Bell is closely associated with its development in the nineteenth century.\n\nMy grandmother used to explain how slowly family news travelled when she was young. If someone in another city became ill, a letter could take days to arrive. A telephone call allowed people to share important information almost immediately. This made a major difference to both family life and work.\n\nThe telephone affects my own life every day. I call my parents, solve problems with colleagues and tell friends when I am running late. Modern mobile phones do many other things, but direct communication remains one of their most useful functions. What impresses me is that a basic idea about sending sound became something people depend on around the world. We often take it for granted because it is so familiar, but life would be much less convenient without the ability to hear another person's voice from a distance.","html":"An invention that changed people's lives is the telephone. It made it possible to speak to someone far away instead of waiting for a letter. Alexander Graham Bell is closely associated with its development in the nineteenth century.\n\nMy grandmother used to explain how slowly family news travelled when she was young. If someone in another city became ill, a letter could take days to arrive. A telephone call allowed people to share important information almost immediately. This made a major difference to both family life and work.\n\nThe telephone affects my own life every day. I call my parents, solve problems with colleagues and tell friends when I am <strong class=\"key-phrase\">running late</strong>. Modern mobile phones do many other things, but direct communication remains one of their most useful functions. What impresses me is that a basic idea about sending sound became something people depend on around the world. We often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take it for granted</strong> because it is so familiar, but life would be much less convenient without the ability to hear another person's voice from a distance.","phrases":[{"en":"running late","meaning":"Expecting to arrive after the planned time."},{"en":"take it for granted","meaning":"Fail to notice or appreciate something because it seems normal."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-105.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Something important that you learned in a maths lesson at primary school","prompt":"Describe something important that you learned in a maths lesson at primary school\nYou should say:\n- what it was\n- when you learned it\n- how you learned it\n- and explain how it became important in your future life.","answer":"Let me talk about something I learned in a maths lesson at primary school: the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">multiplication tables</strong> for the numbers 1 to 12. I remember doing this in the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">penultimate year of primary school</strong>, when I was maybe nine years old. As children, we just called them 'times tables'. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">As far as I remember</strong>, we started with the number 2 and worked through multiplying it by every number from 1 to 12. Our teacher had us go through the tables <strong class=\"key-phrase\">speaking aloud</strong> — one times two is two, two times two is four, three times two is six, and so on. We <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gradually worked our way through</strong> the tables for every number, until we knew the answers by heart.\n\nI think our teacher also gave us regular tests to check we'd learned them properly. I'm not sure I really understood at the time why learning times tables by rote mattered so much, but I remember enjoying the challenge, and I was keen to get good marks — partly because I liked the teacher and wanted to do well for her.\n\nYears later, I realise how useful it is to have those tables <strong class=\"key-phrase\">just sitting in my head</strong>. It means I can do quick calculations without having to think. The other day, for instance, I needed to multiply six by seven for some reason I can't remember, and the answer, forty-two, just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">popped into my head</strong>. Another example is whenever I'm working out bill payments or savings over a twelve-month period — I find myself reaching for the twelve times table without noticing.\n\nIn situations like that, I'm genuinely glad my primary school teachers were so insistent about learning to multiply by heart.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"multiplication tables","context":"Let me talk about something I learned in a maths lesson at primary school: the multiplication tables for the numbers 1 to 12."},{"en":"penultimate year of primary school","context":"I remember doing this in the penultimate year of primary school, when I was maybe nine years old."},{"en":"As far as I remember","context":"As far as I remember, we started with the number 2 and worked through multiplying it by every number from 1 to 12."},{"en":"speaking aloud","context":"Our teacher had us go through the tables speaking aloud — one times two is two, two times two is four, three times two is six, and so on."},{"en":"gradually worked our way through","context":"We gradually worked our way through the tables for every number, until we knew the answers by heart."},{"en":"just sitting in my head","context":"Years later, I realise how useful it is to have those tables just sitting in my head."},{"en":"popped into my head","context":"The other day, for instance, I needed to multiply six by seven for some reason I can't remember, and the answer, forty-two, just popped into my head."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"something important that you learned in a maths lesson at primary school","id":"speaking-part-2-199","_displayNo":"#089","outline":{"topic":"multiplication tables","intro":"learning multiplication tables numbers 1 12 remember doing penultimate year","body":["teacher taught go through tables speaking aloud example 1 times 2","think teacher gave regular tests make sure learned tables properly","years later realise important basic multiplication tables ingrained mind","instance think calculating bill payments earnings savings 12-month period find myself"],"conclusion":"situations glad primary school teachers insistent learning multiply heart"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-199.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Something important I learned in primary school maths was the multiplication tables from one to twelve. I was about nine, in the year before my final primary year. We called them times tables.\n\nOur teacher began with the twos and had us repeat the answers aloud. We gradually practised the other numbers until we could remember them without working out each calculation again. There were regular tests, and I wanted good marks partly because I liked the teacher. At the time, I did not fully understand why memorising the tables mattered so much.\n\nNow I use them in ordinary situations. For example, I can quickly work out that six times seven is forty-two. The twelve times table is also helpful when calculating monthly payments or savings over a year. Having the answers ready in my mind saves time and makes larger calculations easier. Looking back, I am grateful that we practised so often. It was a basic lesson, but it gave me a skill I have continued using long after leaving the classroom.","html":"Something important I learned in primary school maths was the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">multiplication tables</strong> from one to twelve. I was about nine, in the year before my final primary year. We called them times tables.\n\nOur teacher began with the twos and had us repeat the answers aloud. We gradually practised the other numbers until we could remember them without working out each calculation again. There were regular tests, and I wanted good marks partly because I liked the teacher. At the time, I did not fully understand why memorising the tables mattered so much.\n\nNow I use them in ordinary situations. For example, I can quickly work out that six times seven is forty-two. The twelve times table is also helpful when <strong class=\"key-phrase\">calculating</strong> monthly payments or savings over a year. Having the answers ready in my mind saves time and makes larger calculations easier. Looking back, I am grateful that we practised so often. It was a basic lesson, but it gave me a skill I have continued using long after leaving the classroom.","phrases":[{"en":"multiplication tables","meaning":"Lists showing the results of multiplying numbers together."},{"en":"calculating","meaning":"Working out a result using numbers."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-199.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Something special that you saved money to buy","prompt":"Describe something special that you saved money to buy\nYou should say:\n- What was this thing.\n- Why was it special to you.\n- How much did it cost you.\n- How you felt after you bought it.","answer":"I want to tell you about a clock necklace I bought for my best friend, Julie, at the end of grade ten, when she was leaving to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">study abroad</strong>. I wanted something she'd think of me by, so I spent weeks going shop to shop to find the right one.\n\nJulie's always been more like a sister than a friend, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an ordinary present</strong> wasn't going to cut it — I didn't want something pretty but pointless, or useful but with no feeling behind it. Then one day I found a vintage necklace with a little clock pendant and knew it was right. It suited her style, and because it was custom-made, I could choose the numbers and have a message engraved on the back.\n\nThe detail I'm proudest of is that I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set the clock to</strong> Vietnamese time before I gave it to her, even though she'd be living on the other side of the world. That way, whenever she checked the time and it looked wrong, she'd have to stop and remember why — which meant thinking of home, and of me, if only for a second.\n\nIt only cost about twenty dollars — tiny, but as a high schooler <strong class=\"key-phrase\">living on pocket money</strong>, it was a small fortune. I skipped breakfast for a month to afford it, though Julie never found out.\n\nWhen I gave it to her, she loved it, and I felt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">properly proud of myself</strong> — not just for the gift, but because I'd managed to give my best friend something that meant something, on a budget that shouldn't have stretched that far. I hope that little clock <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeps us connected</strong>, even with an ocean between us.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"study abroad","context":"I want to tell you about a clock necklace I bought for my best friend, Julie, at the end of grade ten, when she was leaving to study abroad. I wanted something she'd think of me by, so I spent weeks going shop to shop to find the right one."},{"en":"an ordinary present","context":"Julie's always been more like a sister than a friend, so an ordinary present wasn't going to cut it — I didn't want something pretty but pointless, or useful but with no feeling behind it."},{"en":"set the clock to","context":"The detail I'm proudest of is that I set the clock to Vietnamese time before I gave it to her, even though she'd be living on the other side of the world."},{"en":"living on pocket money","context":"It only cost about twenty dollars — tiny, but as a high schooler living on pocket money, it was a small fortune. I skipped breakfast for a month to afford it, though Julie never found out."},{"en":"properly proud of myself","context":"When I gave it to her, she loved it, and I felt properly proud of myself — not just for the gift, but because I'd managed to give my best friend something that meant something, on a budget that shouldn't have stretched that far."},{"en":"keeps us connected","context":"I hope that little clock keeps us connected, even with an ocean between us."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"something special that you saved money to buy","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-106","_displayNo":"#090","outline":{"topic":"clock necklace for Julie","intro":"clock necklace bought for best friend Julie, end of grade 10, she was leaving to study abroad","body":["wanted something she'd think of me by, weeks of shopping around, an ordinary present wouldn't cut it","found vintage necklace w/ clock pendant, custom-made — could choose numbers + engrave a message","set the clock to Vietnamese time so she'd think of home whenever it looked wrong to her","cost about $20 — a small fortune as a high schooler living on pocket money, skipped breakfast a month"],"conclusion":"she loved it, felt properly proud of myself, hope it keeps us connected"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-106.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Something special I saved for was a clock necklace for my best friend Julie. I bought it at the end of grade ten, before she left to study abroad. I wanted a gift that would remind her of our friendship.\n\nAfter visiting several shops, I found a vintage-style necklace with a small clock pendant. It could be customised, so I chose details and added a message on the back. Before giving it to her, I set the clock to Vietnamese time. I hoped that seeing the time back home would make her think of her family and friends.\n\nThe necklace cost about twenty dollars. That was a lot for me as a school student relying on pocket money, so saving enough took a month. When she opened it, she was delighted. I felt proud because I had chosen something personal rather than simply buying an expensive-looking object. The gift represented our connection even though we would be far apart. I hoped it would remind her that she still had someone thinking about her at home.","html":"Something special I saved for was a clock necklace for my best friend Julie. I bought it at the end of grade ten, before she left to study abroad. I wanted a gift that would remind her of our friendship.\n\nAfter visiting several shops, I found a vintage-style necklace with a small clock <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pendant</strong>. It could be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">customised</strong>, so I chose details and added a message on the back. Before giving it to her, I set the clock to Vietnamese time. I hoped that seeing the time back home would make her think of her family and friends.\n\nThe necklace cost about twenty dollars. That was a lot for me as a school student relying on pocket money, so saving enough took a month. When she opened it, she was delighted. I felt proud because I had chosen something personal rather than simply buying an expensive-looking object. The gift represented our connection even though we would be far apart. I hoped it would remind her that she still had someone thinking about her at home.","phrases":[{"en":"pendant","meaning":"A small decorative object hanging from a necklace."},{"en":"customised","meaning":"Made or changed according to a particular person's choices."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-106.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Something you bought according to an advertisement you saw","prompt":"Describe something you bought according to an advertisement you saw.\nYou should say:\n- What it is;\n- When and where you see the advertisement;\n- Why you want to buy it;\n- and how you felt about it.","answer":"About a couple of months ago I came across an advert on the internet for a rather unusual pair of running shoes. It was the picture that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caught my attention</strong> first, because they looked completely different from any running shoe I'd seen before.\n\nThey're made from some kind of special material, with a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">synthetic sole</strong> that's moulded onto the upper so the whole shoe <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fits like a glove</strong>. The sole itself isn't too different from a normal running shoe underneath — it still grips well and protects my feet properly — but the way the upper's built is what really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stood out to me</strong> in the photo.\n\nThe advert itself also had a short video, maybe three or four minutes long, showing a runner going up a rocky hillside. It explained how the shoes let you feel more of the ground under your feet and grip better, because of the way the sole flexes with each step.\n\nIt was more of an informative ad than anything flashy or creative, but it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worked on me</strong> — I hadn't seen shoes built quite like that before, and I wanted to try a pair for myself, so I ordered them in my size. I've worn them a handful of times now and they've genuinely impressed me. They cost a bit more than an ordinary pair of running shoes, but they're <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worth every penny</strong> as far as I'm concerned — running in them just feels completely different, and honestly, I prefer it.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"caught my attention","context":"About a couple of months ago I came across an advert on the internet for a rather unusual pair of running shoes. It was the picture that caught my attention first, because they looked completely different from any running shoe I'd seen before."},{"en":"synthetic sole","context":"They're made from some kind of special material, with a synthetic sole that's moulded onto the upper so the whole shoe fits like a glove."},{"en":"fits like a glove","context":"They're made from some kind of special material, with a synthetic sole that's moulded onto the upper so the whole shoe fits like a glove."},{"en":"stood out to me","context":"The sole itself isn't too different from a normal running shoe underneath — it still grips well and protects my feet properly — but the way the upper's built is what really stood out to me in the photo."},{"en":"worked on me","context":"It was more of an informative ad than anything flashy or creative, but it worked on me — I hadn't seen shoes built quite like that before, and I wanted to try a pair for myself, so I ordered them in my size."},{"en":"worth every penny","context":"They cost a bit more than an ordinary pair of running shoes, but they're worth every penny as far as I'm concerned — running in them just feels completely different, and honestly, I prefer it."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"something you bought according to an advertisement you saw","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-107","_displayNo":"#091","outline":{"topic":"running shoes","intro":"picture caught attention 1st bc shoes looked different normal running","body":["made special material synthetic sole grafted onto body fit like glove","Speaking advertisement video maybe 3 4 minutes long showed runner going","informative ad anything arty creative made want buy shoes bc hadn't","cost little bit normal running shoes think they're worth extra money"],"conclusion":"running experience different normal personally prefer it"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-107.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Something I bought after seeing an advertisement was a pair of running shoes. I noticed the advert online a couple of months ago. The photograph caught my attention because the shoes looked different from the ones I normally wore.\n\nThe upper part fitted closely around the foot, and the flexible sole was designed to move with each step. A short video showed someone running up a rocky hill. It explained how the shoes could help the runner feel the ground while still providing grip and protection.\n\nThe advertisement was fairly simple and informative. It did not tell a dramatic story, but it explained the design clearly enough to make me curious. I ordered a pair in my size and have worn them several times. They were more expensive than ordinary running shoes, but I find them comfortable and enjoy the different feeling when I run. I am pleased with the purchase because the actual experience matched what interested me in the advertisement. In this case, learning about a product's design was more convincing than a flashy promotion.","html":"Something I bought after seeing an advertisement was a pair of running shoes. I noticed the advert online a couple of months ago. The photograph caught my attention because the shoes looked different from the ones I normally wore.\n\nThe upper part fitted closely around the foot, and the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">flexible sole</strong> was designed to move with each step. A short video showed someone running up a rocky hill. It explained how the shoes could help the runner feel the ground while still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">providing grip</strong> and protection.\n\nThe advertisement was fairly simple and informative. It did not tell a dramatic story, but it explained the design clearly enough to make me curious. I ordered a pair in my size and have worn them several times. They were more expensive than ordinary running shoes, but I find them comfortable and enjoy the different feeling when I run. I am pleased with the purchase because the actual experience matched what interested me in the advertisement. In this case, learning about a product's design was more convincing than a flashy promotion.","phrases":[{"en":"flexible sole","meaning":"The bottom part of a shoe that can bend easily."},{"en":"providing grip","meaning":"Helping something hold onto a surface without slipping."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-107.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Something you bought recently that made you happy","prompt":"Describe something you bought recently that made you happy.\nYou should say:\n- What it is.\n- When and where you bought it.\n- How it was used/what is was used for.\n- Why it made you happy.","answer":"My laptop is what I want to talk about — I bought this Dell about three years ago, in my final year of university, and it's still the thing I use every single day.\n\nAt the time, my old laptop had basically given up — the fan was so loud it sounded like it was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">about to take off</strong>, and it kept freezing halfway through writing my thesis, which was terrifying given the deadline. I asked around, read a stack of reviews, and picked out a Dell from an electronics shop downtown, because it gave me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">solid performance</strong> without the price tag of brands like ASUS or Lenovo, which can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cost an arm and a leg</strong>.\n\nI use it for pretty much everything — finishing my thesis was the original reason, but since then it's been assignments, job applications, watching films, and gaming late at night when I can't sleep. It's the one object I'd grab first if there were ever a fire.\n\nWhat made me happy about it wasn't really the laptop itself, if I'm honest — it was what it let me do. I finished my thesis on it at four in the morning, three days before the deadline, and I remember just sitting there staring at the screen, completely relieved. It's also become my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">go-to way to unwind</strong> after a long day — putting a film on or playing a game <strong class=\"key-phrase\">helps me switch off</strong> from everything else.\n\nI'm planning to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hang onto it</strong> for as long as it'll survive, honestly, because it's been through a lot with me, and I'm genuinely fond of it in a way that probably sounds a bit silly for a piece of plastic and metal.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"about to take off","context":"At the time, my old laptop had basically given up — the fan was so loud it sounded like it was about to take off, and it kept freezing halfway through writing my thesis, which was terrifying given the deadline."},{"en":"solid performance","context":"I asked around, read a stack of reviews, and picked out a Dell from an electronics shop downtown, because it gave me solid performance without the price tag of brands like ASUS or Lenovo, which can cost an arm and a leg."},{"en":"cost an arm and a leg","context":"I asked around, read a stack of reviews, and picked out a Dell from an electronics shop downtown, because it gave me solid performance without the price tag of brands like ASUS or Lenovo, which can cost an arm and a leg."},{"en":"go-to way to unwind","context":"It's also become my go-to way to unwind after a long day — putting a film on or playing a game helps me switch off from everything else."},{"en":"helps me switch off","context":"It's also become my go-to way to unwind after a long day — putting a film on or playing a game helps me switch off from everything else."},{"en":"hang onto it","context":"I'm planning to hang onto it for as long as it'll survive, honestly, because it's been through a lot with me, and I'm genuinely fond of it in a way that probably sounds a bit silly for a piece of plastic and metal."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"something you bought recently that made you happy","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-108","_displayNo":"#092","outline":{"topic":"Dell laptop","intro":"Dell laptop bought ~3 yrs ago in final year of uni, still use it every day","body":["old laptop dying — loud fan, freezing mid-thesis — picked a Dell from a shop downtown, solid performance for the price vs ASUS/Lenovo","use it for everything now — thesis, job applications, films, gaming when I can't sleep","finished thesis on it at 4am 3 days before the deadline, huge relief","become go-to way to unwind — a film or a game helps me switch off"],"conclusion":"planning to hang onto it as long as possible, genuinely fond of it"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-108.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Something I bought that made me happy is my Dell laptop. I purchased it about three years ago, in my final year at university. My old laptop was noisy and often stopped working while I was writing my thesis, which made me anxious.\n\nI asked people for advice and read reviews before choosing a replacement from an electronics shop in the city centre. I wanted dependable performance at a price I could afford. The new laptop helped me continue writing without worrying about losing my work.\n\nMy happiest memory is finishing the thesis at four in the morning, three days before the deadline. I felt enormously relieved, and the laptop had helped make that possible. Since then, I have used it for assignments, job applications, films and games. It supports both serious tasks and relaxation after work. I know it is only a machine, but I have become attached to it because of the experiences connected with it. I plan to keep using it for as long as it works well.","html":"Something I bought that made me happy is my Dell laptop. I purchased it about three years ago, in my final year at university. My old laptop was noisy and often stopped working while I was writing my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thesis</strong>, which made me anxious.\n\nI asked people for advice and read reviews before choosing a replacement from an electronics shop in the city centre. I wanted <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dependable performance</strong> at a price I could afford. The new laptop helped me continue writing without worrying about losing my work.\n\nMy happiest memory is finishing the thesis at four in the morning, three days before the deadline. I felt enormously relieved, and the laptop had helped make that possible. Since then, I have used it for assignments, job applications, films and games. It supports both serious tasks and relaxation after work. I know it is only a machine, but I have become attached to it because of the experiences connected with it. I plan to keep using it for as long as it works well.","phrases":[{"en":"thesis","meaning":"A long piece of research writing completed for a university qualification."},{"en":"dependable performance","meaning":"Working well and reliably when needed."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-108.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Something you learned in a place or from someone","prompt":"Describe something you learned in a place or from someone\nYou should say:\nWhat it was\nWhere or who you got it\nWhat the situation was\nHow you felt","answer":"Something I learned from a woman in my neighbourhood, Ms. Lan, is the value of doing voluntary work — and doing it properly, not just for show.\n\nMs. Lan was born during <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of the most turbulent periods</strong> in Vietnam's modern history, and now, in her late fifties, she runs her own small volunteer group. A handful of young people, including me for a while, used to help her visit elderly residents in nursing homes around the town who didn't get many visitors.\n\nI remember her explaining why she does it, sitting on a plastic stool outside one of the nursing homes: 'Spending time with the elderly matters — a lot of them don't have relatives who visit, so I try to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fill that gap</strong>. I've always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had a rapport with</strong> older people; I'm patient, and I actually enjoy sitting and talking with them.'\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Working alongside her</strong> for those few months taught me something I hadn't expected — that volunteering isn't really about what you're giving up, it's about what you get back. I went in thinking I was doing the residents a favour, but I came away having <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gained more confidence</strong> talking to people I didn't know, and I'd made a couple of friends I still see now.\n\nWhat stayed with me most, though, was realising how much you can learn from someone who's lived through far more than you have. Ms. Lan never once made it sound like a chore, and I think that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rubbed off on</strong> me — I still volunteer occasionally now, even though I'm nowhere near as dedicated as she is.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"one of the most turbulent periods","context":"Lan was born during one of the most turbulent periods in Vietnam's modern history, and now, in her late fifties, she runs her own small volunteer group."},{"en":"fill that gap","context":"I remember her explaining why she does it, sitting on a plastic stool outside one of the nursing homes: 'Spending time with the elderly matters — a lot of them don't have relatives who visit, so I try to fill that gap."},{"en":"had a rapport with","context":"I've always had a rapport with older people; I'm patient, and I actually enjoy sitting and talking with them.'"},{"en":"Working alongside her","context":"Working alongside her for those few months taught me something I hadn't expected — that volunteering isn't really about what you're giving up, it's about what you get back."},{"en":"gained more confidence","context":"I went in thinking I was doing the residents a favour, but I came away having gained more confidence talking to people I didn't know, and I'd made a couple of friends I still see now."},{"en":"rubbed off on","context":"Lan never once made it sound like a chore, and I think that rubbed off on me — I still volunteer occasionally now, even though I'm nowhere near as dedicated as she is."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"something you learned in a place or from someone","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-201","_displayNo":"#093","outline":{"topic":"volunteering with Ms Lan","intro":"learned the value of volunteering properly, not for show, from a neighbour, Ms Lan","body":["Ms Lan born during a turbulent period in Vietnam's history, now late 50s, runs her own small volunteer group","used to help her visit elderly residents in nursing homes who didn't get many visitors","in her own words: fills the gap for elderly with no visiting relatives, has a rapport with older people","working alongside her taught me volunteering is about what you get back, not just give up","gained more confidence talking to strangers, made a couple of friends I still see"],"conclusion":"learned a lot from someone who's lived through more than me, still volunteer occasionally now"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-201.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Something I learned from another person was the value of volunteering. A woman in my neighbourhood, Ms Lan, runs a small group that visits older people in local nursing homes. I helped her for a few months.\n\nShe explained that some residents rarely received visitors and needed someone willing to sit and listen. She was patient and genuinely enjoyed their company. Watching her helped me understand that the work was about building a human connection, not simply completing a good deed for others to notice.\n\nAt first, I thought mainly about what I was giving up, such as free afternoons. Later, I realised how much I gained. I became more confident speaking with unfamiliar people and learned from residents whose lives had been very different from mine. I also made friends. Ms Lan never treated the visits as an unpleasant duty, and her attitude influenced me. I still volunteer occasionally, although not as often as she does. She taught me that useful service can benefit both the person receiving support and the person offering it.","html":"Something I learned from another person was the value of volunteering. A woman in my neighbourhood, Ms Lan, runs a small group that visits older people in local <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nursing homes</strong>. I helped her for a few months.\n\nShe explained that some residents rarely received visitors and needed someone willing to sit and listen. She was patient and genuinely enjoyed their company. Watching her helped me understand that the work was about building a human connection, not simply completing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a good deed</strong> for others to notice.\n\nAt first, I thought mainly about what I was giving up, such as free afternoons. Later, I realised how much I gained. I became more confident speaking with unfamiliar people and learned from residents whose lives had been very different from mine. I also made friends. Ms Lan never treated the visits as an unpleasant duty, and her attitude influenced me. I still volunteer occasionally, although not as often as she does. She taught me that useful service can benefit both the person receiving support and the person offering it.","phrases":[{"en":"nursing homes","meaning":"Places where older or unwell people live and receive care."},{"en":"a good deed","meaning":"A kind or helpful action."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-201.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Something you made yourself","prompt":"Describe something you made yourself.\nYou should say:\nWhat you made.\nWhy you make it.\nHow you make it.\nAnd explain how you felt about it.","answer":"A couple of years ago I tried to make a Tiramisu cake for my mother's birthday, and that's what I want to talk about — though I'll admit, it wasn't exactly a success.\n\nI'd first tasted Tiramisu at a restaurant in District 1 ages before that, and I loved it so much I decided it would make <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the perfect birthday present</strong>. I'd only learned to bake at all a few months earlier, after picking up a cookbook from a local bookshop, so this was ambitious for someone with about three cakes' worth of experience.\n\nOn the morning of her birthday, I got up early and bought all the ingredients from the supermarket, and everything was going fine until I got to the mascarpone cream. The recipe called for a stainless-steel bowl to whip it in, but I only had a porcelain one, so I used that instead — and it turned out that actually mattered. The cream refused to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hold its shape</strong>, stayed completely liquid, and the whole cake pretty quickly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned into a big mess</strong>.\n\nMy mother walked in right in the middle of this disaster, and I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">braced myself for</strong> her to be annoyed about the state of the kitchen. Instead she just smiled and said she was touched that I'd even tried to make her something myself. I cleaned everything up, and later that day I bought her a dress to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compensate for</strong> the cake I'd ruined.\n\nWe still had a lovely birthday together in the end, and I've actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had another go at</strong> that Tiramisu recipe since then — successfully, this time — so hopefully next year I'll finally get to give her the cake I originally had in mind.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"the perfect birthday present","context":"I'd first tasted Tiramisu at a restaurant in District 1 ages before that, and I loved it so much I decided it would make the perfect birthday present."},{"en":"hold its shape","context":"The cream refused to hold its shape, stayed completely liquid, and the whole cake pretty quickly turned into a big mess."},{"en":"turned into a big mess","context":"The cream refused to hold its shape, stayed completely liquid, and the whole cake pretty quickly turned into a big mess."},{"en":"braced myself for","context":"My mother walked in right in the middle of this disaster, and I braced myself for her to be annoyed about the state of the kitchen."},{"en":"compensate for","context":"I cleaned everything up, and later that day I bought her a dress to compensate for the cake I'd ruined."},{"en":"had another go at","context":"We still had a lovely birthday together in the end, and I've actually had another go at that Tiramisu recipe since then — successfully, this time — so hopefully next year I'll finally get to give her the cake I originally had in mind."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"something you made yourself","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-109","_displayNo":"#094","outline":{"topic":"Tiramisu cake for Mum's birthday","intro":"Tiramisu cake attempted for mother's birthday a couple of years ago, wasn't a success","body":["first tasted it at a restaurant in District 1, decided it'd make the perfect present","only recently learned to bake from a cookbook — an ambitious first attempt","used a porcelain bowl instead of stainless steel for the cream — wouldn't hold its shape, turned into a mess","mother walked in, wasn't annoyed, was touched I'd tried — bought her a dress to compensate"],"conclusion":"still a lovely birthday; have since had another go at the recipe successfully"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-109.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Something I made myself was a tiramisu cake for my mother's birthday a couple of years ago. I had enjoyed the dessert at a restaurant in District 1 and thought a homemade version would be a thoughtful present.\n\nI had only learned basic baking a few months earlier, so it was quite a challenge. On her birthday morning, I bought the ingredients and started following a recipe. Unfortunately, the cream did not become firm as expected. I had used a different bowl from the one suggested, and before long the cake had turned into a mess.\n\nWhen my mother came into the kitchen, I expected her to be annoyed. Instead, she smiled and said she appreciated the effort. I cleaned up and later bought her a dress to replace the failed cake. We still enjoyed her birthday together. I have tried the recipe again since then and succeeded. The experience taught me to practise before making an important gift, but it also showed me that my mother valued the care behind the present more than a perfect result.","html":"Something I made myself was a tiramisu cake for my mother's birthday a couple of years ago. I had enjoyed the dessert at a restaurant in District 1 and thought a homemade version would be a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thoughtful present</strong>.\n\nI had only learned basic baking a few months earlier, so it was quite a challenge. On her birthday morning, I bought the ingredients and started following a recipe. Unfortunately, the cream did not become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">firm</strong> as expected. I had used a different bowl from the one suggested, and before long the cake had turned into a mess.\n\nWhen my mother came into the kitchen, I expected her to be annoyed. Instead, she smiled and said she appreciated the effort. I cleaned up and later bought her a dress to replace the failed cake. We still enjoyed her birthday together. I have tried the recipe again since then and succeeded. The experience taught me to practise before making an important gift, but it also showed me that my mother valued the care behind the present more than a perfect result.","phrases":[{"en":"thoughtful present","meaning":"A gift chosen or made with care for the person receiving it."},{"en":"firm","meaning":"Solid enough to keep its shape rather than remaining liquid."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-109.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Your favourite flower","prompt":"Describe your favourite flower.\nYou should say:\nWhat it is\nWhere it is grown\nIf it is common in your country\nAnd explain why it is your favourite flower.","answer":"My favourite flower is the rose, which probably isn't the most original answer, but I've got a specific reason for choosing it — my grandmother grew them, and I've loved them since I was small because of her.\n\nShe had a small garden behind her house with maybe a dozen rose bushes, mostly red and a few pale pink ones, and every visit as a kid I'd get <strong class=\"key-phrase\">roped into helping her</strong> deadhead them or pull up weeds around the roots. I remember being warned constantly about the thorns, and getting <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pricked more than once</strong> before I learned to handle the stems properly.\n\nRoses are grown pretty much everywhere in Vietnam — you'll find them in home gardens, in flower farms up in Da Lat, where the cooler climate suits them well, and on every street corner around Tet and Valentine's Day, when florists <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sell out almost completely</strong>. So yes, they're extremely common here, not some rare or exotic flower at all.\n\nWhat makes the rose my favourite, though, is less about the flower itself and more about what it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reminds me of</strong>. My grandmother passed away a few years ago, but she's still growing roses in my memory — whenever I walk past a rose bush now, especially a red one, I think of her garden and those Sunday afternoons.\n\nI've actually started growing a few myself on my balcony, though nowhere near as successfully as she did — mine are a bit <strong class=\"key-phrase\">patchy and uneven</strong> compared to hers. Still, every time one <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comes into bloom</strong>, it feels like a small connection to her, which is really the whole reason I love roses more than any other flower.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"roped into helping her","context":"She had a small garden behind her house with maybe a dozen rose bushes, mostly red and a few pale pink ones, and every visit as a kid I'd get roped into helping her deadhead them or pull up weeds around the roots."},{"en":"pricked more than once","context":"I remember being warned constantly about the thorns, and getting pricked more than once before I learned to handle the stems properly."},{"en":"sell out almost completely","context":"Roses are grown pretty much everywhere in Vietnam — you'll find them in home gardens, in flower farms up in Da Lat, where the cooler climate suits them well, and on every street corner around Tet and Valentine's Day, when florists sell out almost completely."},{"en":"reminds me of","context":"What makes the rose my favourite, though, is less about the flower itself and more about what it reminds me of."},{"en":"patchy and uneven","context":"I've actually started growing a few myself on my balcony, though nowhere near as successfully as she did — mine are a bit patchy and uneven compared to hers."},{"en":"comes into bloom","context":"Still, every time one comes into bloom, it feels like a small connection to her, which is really the whole reason I love roses more than any other flower."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"your favourite flower","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-110","_displayNo":"#095","outline":{"topic":"rose (grandmother's garden)","intro":"favourite flower is the rose — not original, but tied to a specific memory of my grandmother","body":["grandmother's small garden, a dozen rose bushes, roped into helping deadhead/weed as a kid, got pricked by thorns","roses grown everywhere in Vietnam — home gardens, Da Lat flower farms, sell out around Tet/Valentine's","grandmother passed away a few years ago; roses remind me of her garden and those Sunday afternoons","now growing a few myself on the balcony, patchy compared to hers"],"conclusion":"every bloom feels like a small connection to her — the real reason I love roses most"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-110.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"My favourite flower is the rose because it reminds me of my grandmother. She had about twelve rose bushes in her small back garden, mostly red with a few light pink ones. I often helped her when I visited as a child.\n\nWe removed old flowers and weeds, and she taught me to be careful with the thorns. I did not always manage that and sometimes hurt my fingers. Still, I remember those afternoons as happy, quiet time together. Roses are common in Vietnam, including in home gardens and flower farms around Da Lat, but my connection with them is personal.\n\nMy grandmother died a few years ago. Now, whenever I pass a red rose bush, I think of her garden. I have started growing some roses on my balcony too. Mine are not as healthy or tidy as hers were, but seeing a flower open gives me pleasure. It feels like a small way of continuing something she enjoyed. That memory matters more to me than whether roses are rare or fashionable.","html":"My favourite flower is the rose because it reminds me of my grandmother. She had about twelve rose bushes in her small back garden, mostly red with a few light pink ones. I often helped her when I visited as a child.\n\nWe removed old flowers and weeds, and she taught me to be careful with the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thorns</strong>. I did not always manage that and sometimes hurt my fingers. Still, I remember those afternoons as happy, quiet time together. Roses are common in Vietnam, including in home gardens and flower farms around Da Lat, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">my connection with them is personal</strong>.\n\nMy grandmother died a few years ago. Now, whenever I pass a red rose bush, I think of her garden. I have started growing some roses on my balcony too. Mine are not as healthy or tidy as hers were, but seeing a flower open gives me pleasure. It feels like a small way of continuing something she enjoyed. That memory matters more to me than whether roses are rare or fashionable.","phrases":[{"en":"thorns","meaning":"Sharp points growing on the stems of some plants."},{"en":"my connection with them is personal","meaning":"They matter to me because of my own experiences and feelings."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-110.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Your favourite piece of clothing","prompt":"Describe your favourite piece of clothing\nYou should say:\nWhere you got it\nWhen you got it\nHow often you wear it\nAnd explain why it is your favourite piece of clothing.","answer":"A pair of ripped jeans is honestly my favourite item in my entire wardrobe, even though they're years old now and a bit <strong class=\"key-phrase\">past their best</strong>.\n\nI picked them up for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">next to nothing</strong> in a second-hand clothing store back when I was still at school, right when ripped jeans were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">really popular</strong> and everyone I knew wanted a pair. I remember wearing them on my first day back after the holidays and having three different classmates ask me where I'd got them, which felt like a pretty big deal at fifteen.\n\nTrends moved on eventually, the way they always do, but I never really stopped wearing mine. I wear them almost everywhere — round the house, to school, running errands — and I only swap them out for something smarter if it's an actual formal event, like a wedding or a ceremony. They're honestly so comfortable that I've fallen asleep in them more than once without even meaning to.\n\nThere are a few reasons I'm still this attached to a single pair of jeans. For one, they go with almost anything, so they're incredibly easy to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mix and match</strong> — I can throw on nearly any shirt without worrying I'll look ridiculous. They're also just tough; denim doesn't wear out the way cheaper fabrics do, so I've never had to think about replacing them, which has probably <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saved me a fair bit of money</strong> over the years.\n\nAll in all, I can't really imagine getting through a normal week without them. If I'm honest, I think ripped jeans deserve to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">come back into fashion</strong> properly, because younger people today don't seem to rate them the way we did.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"past their best","context":"A pair of ripped jeans is honestly my favourite item in my entire wardrobe, even though they're years old now and a bit past their best."},{"en":"next to nothing","context":"I picked them up for next to nothing in a second-hand clothing store back when I was still at school, right when ripped jeans were really popular and everyone I knew wanted a pair."},{"en":"really popular","context":"I picked them up for next to nothing in a second-hand clothing store back when I was still at school, right when ripped jeans were really popular and everyone I knew wanted a pair."},{"en":"mix and match","context":"For one, they go with almost anything, so they're incredibly easy to mix and match — I can throw on nearly any shirt without worrying I'll look ridiculous."},{"en":"saved me a fair bit of money","context":"They're also just tough; denim doesn't wear out the way cheaper fabrics do, so I've never had to think about replacing them, which has probably saved me a fair bit of money over the years."},{"en":"come back into fashion","context":"All in all, I can't really imagine getting through a normal week without them. If I'm honest, I think ripped jeans deserve to come back into fashion properly, because younger people today don't seem to rate them the way we did."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"your favourite piece of clothing","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-111","_displayNo":"#096","outline":{"topic":"ripped jeans","intro":"pair of ripped jeans, favourite item in the wardrobe, years old now but past their best","body":["bought for next to nothing second-hand when ripped jeans were really popular at school","specific memory: classmates asking where I got them on the first day back after the holidays","wear them almost everywhere, only swap for something smarter for formal events, fallen asleep in them","easy to mix and match with anything, denim tough — never had to replace them, saved money"],"conclusion":"can't imagine a week without them; think ripped jeans deserve to come back into fashion"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-111.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"My favourite piece of clothing is a pair of ripped jeans. I bought them cheaply in a second-hand shop when I was still at school. At that time, they were fashionable, and I felt pleased when classmates asked where I had found them.\n\nAlthough fashions have changed, I still wear the jeans regularly. They are comfortable enough for relaxing at home or doing everyday jobs outside. I choose something smarter for formal events such as weddings, but these jeans suit most ordinary days.\n\nI like them because they go well with different shirts, so getting dressed is easy. The denim has also lasted a long time, which has saved me from buying replacements. They are now old and no longer look new, but that does not bother me much. I have worn them through many different periods of my life, so they feel familiar. For me, useful clothes do not have to follow the latest trend. Comfort, durability and the ease of wearing them matter more than looking fashionable every season.","html":"My favourite piece of clothing is a pair of ripped jeans. I bought them cheaply in a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">second-hand shop</strong> when I was still at school. At that time, they were fashionable, and I felt pleased when classmates asked where I had found them.\n\nAlthough fashions have changed, I still wear the jeans regularly. They are comfortable enough for relaxing at home or doing everyday jobs outside. I choose something smarter for formal events such as weddings, but these jeans suit most ordinary days.\n\nI like them because they go well with different shirts, so getting dressed is easy. The denim has also lasted a long time, which has saved me from buying replacements. They are now old and no longer look new, but that does not bother me much. I have worn them through many different periods of my life, so they feel familiar. For me, useful clothes do not have to follow the latest trend. Comfort, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">durability</strong> and the ease of wearing them matter more than looking fashionable every season.","phrases":[{"en":"second-hand shop","meaning":"A shop selling things that have already belonged to someone else."},{"en":"durability","meaning":"The ability to last a long time without breaking or wearing out."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-111.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A type of weather you like","prompt":"Describe a type of weather you like\nYou should say:\nwhat kind of weather it is\nwhen this weather usually occurs\nwhat you usually do during this weather\nAnd explain why you like this type of weather","answer":"I was born in Hanoi and I've lived here my whole life. The city's known for a lot of things — the street food, the mad <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rush-hour traffic</strong> — but it's also famous for how unpredictable the weather can be. In summer the mornings can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">scorching hot</strong>, the kind where you don't want to leave the house, and then out of nowhere it'll be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raining cats and dogs</strong> by the afternoon. After more than twenty years here, I've worked out exactly when it's worth planning a picnic or a bike ride with friends: late autumn.\n\nThe last couple of weeks of October are pretty much ideal for anything outdoors. There's a clear blue sky most days, and after a brutally hot summer, it's such <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a change of scenery</strong> to sit outside in a gentle breeze without sweating through your shirt. People here genuinely say Hanoi looks its best during this stretch of the year, and I'd agree.\n\nIt's also when I make a real effort to spend time with my parents and the neighbours. We usually get a few families together and have a barbecue out on the street. My dad drags out this ancient cassette player he's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">oddly proud of</strong> — probably because nobody else in the neighbourhood still owns one — and he sits there listening to the football results with his mates over a beer, while the neighbours <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turn on some music</strong> and sing along. It's a bit chaotic, honestly, like a small Viking feast, but I love being part of it.\n\nLiving somewhere the weather's this harsh and unpredictable for most of the year, I think two or three weeks of genuinely perfect weather, spent properly enjoying time with family and neighbours, is something <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worth appreciating</strong>.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"scorching hot","context":"In summer the mornings can be scorching hot, the kind where you don't want to leave the house, and then out of nowhere it'll be raining cats and dogs by the afternoon."},{"en":"raining cats and dogs","context":"In summer the mornings can be scorching hot, the kind where you don't want to leave the house, and then out of nowhere it'll be raining cats and dogs by the afternoon."},{"en":"a change of scenery","context":"There's a clear blue sky most days, and after a brutally hot summer, it's such a change of scenery to sit outside in a gentle breeze without sweating through your shirt."},{"en":"turn on some music","context":"My dad drags out this ancient cassette player he's oddly proud of — probably because nobody else in the neighbourhood still owns one — and he sits there listening to the football results with his mates over a beer, while the neighbours turn on some music and sing along."},{"en":"rush-hour traffic","context":"The city's known for a lot of things — the street food, the mad rush-hour traffic — but it's also famous for how unpredictable the weather can be."},{"en":"oddly proud of","context":"My dad drags out this ancient cassette player he's oddly proud of — probably because nobody else in the neighbourhood still owns one — and he sits there listening to the football results with his mates over a beer, while the neighbours turn on some music and sing along."},{"en":"worth appreciating","context":"Living somewhere the weather's this harsh and unpredictable for most of the year, I think two or three weeks of genuinely perfect weather, spent properly enjoying time with family and neighbours, is something worth appreciating."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a type of weather you like","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-197","_displayNo":"#097","outline":{"topic":"late-autumn weather in Hanoi","intro":"born Hanoi lived whole life city known street food rush-hour traffic weather unpredictable","body":["summer mornings scorching hot afternoons raining cats dogs 20+ yrs here best time picnic bike ride late autumn","last 2 weeks Oct ideal outdoors clear blue sky change of scenery after hot summer Hanoi looks best","spend time parents neighbours street barbecue dad's old cassette player oddly proud of listens football neighbours turn on music"],"conclusion":"weather harsh unpredictable most of year few weeks perfect weather w/ family neighbours worth appreciating"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-197.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A type of weather I like is late autumn in Hanoi, especially the last few weeks of October. I have lived here all my life and find the weather difficult during much of the year. Summer can be extremely hot and then suddenly rainy.\n\nIn late autumn, the sky is often blue and there is a gentle breeze. It becomes comfortable to sit outside or go cycling without feeling too hot. That makes me more willing to plan picnics and spend time outdoors with friends.\n\nIt is also a time when my family and neighbours sometimes gather for a barbecue. My father brings out his old cassette player, people talk about football, and someone usually starts playing music. The gathering can be noisy, but I enjoy the friendly atmosphere. What makes this weather special is partly the contrast with hotter or wetter months. A few pleasant weeks encourage everyone to leave the house and enjoy being together. For me, comfortable weather is not only about temperature; it creates opportunities for the kinds of shared evenings I remember happily.","html":"A type of weather I like is late autumn in Hanoi, especially the last few weeks of October. I have lived here all my life and find the weather difficult during much of the year. Summer can be extremely hot and then suddenly rainy.\n\nIn late autumn, the sky is often blue and there is a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gentle breeze</strong>. It becomes comfortable to sit outside or go cycling without feeling too hot. That makes me more willing to plan picnics and spend time outdoors with friends.\n\nIt is also a time when my family and neighbours sometimes gather for a barbecue. My father brings out his old cassette player, people talk about football, and someone usually starts playing music. The gathering can be noisy, but I enjoy the friendly atmosphere. What makes this weather special is partly the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">contrast</strong> with hotter or wetter months. A few pleasant weeks encourage everyone to leave the house and enjoy being together. For me, comfortable weather is not only about temperature; it creates opportunities for the kinds of shared evenings I remember happily.","phrases":[{"en":"gentle breeze","meaning":"A light, pleasant wind."},{"en":"contrast","meaning":"A clear difference between two things."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-197.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An environment law","prompt":"Describe an environment law\nYou should say:\nwhat it is\nhow you first learned about it\nwho benefits from it\nAnd explain how you feel about this law","answer":"I'd like to talk about a rule the government introduced a few years ago that's had a genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">positive effect on</strong> how people in Vietnam deal with rubbish. I don't remember its exact official name, but basically it requires households to sort their waste into three separate bins — organic waste, general waste, and anything recyclable — and the city's had to install thousands of these <strong class=\"key-phrase\">colour-coded bins</strong> in public areas to make it work.\n\nI first found out about it through a poster campaign near my apartment, and then later through my building's group chat, which kept sending reminders about which colour bin was for what. Everyone benefits from it, really, but I'd say it's mostly the local environment and the waste collectors themselves — before this, a lot of collectors had to sort through mixed, often quite unpleasant rubbish by hand.\n\nBefore the law, waste disposal was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a nightmare to manage</strong> for local authorities, mainly because people had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">very little environmental awareness</strong> — rubbish just got dumped into rivers, left on pavements, or thrown wherever was convenient. It did <strong class=\"key-phrase\">real damage to the environment</strong>, on top of making a lot of neighbourhoods look and smell awful.\n\nSince the law came in, and with a fair bit of campaigning alongside it, there's been a real shift in how people think about rubbish. There's now <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an effective and practical solution</strong> for getting rid of everyday waste, and people's awareness of the whole issue has grown a lot over the years. I'm honestly quite happy about how this law's helped tackle the waste problem here, rather than everyone just talking about it without anything actually changing.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"positive effect on","context":"I'd like to talk about a rule the government introduced a few years ago that's had a genuinely positive effect on how people in Vietnam deal with rubbish."},{"en":"colour-coded bins","context":"I don't remember its exact official name, but basically it requires households to sort their waste into three separate bins — organic waste, general waste, and anything recyclable — and the city's had to install thousands of these colour-coded bins in public areas to make it work."},{"en":"a nightmare to manage","context":"Before the law, waste disposal was a nightmare to manage for local authorities, mainly because people had very little environmental awareness — rubbish just got dumped into rivers, left on pavements, or thrown wherever was convenient."},{"en":"very little environmental awareness","context":"Before the law, waste disposal was a nightmare to manage for local authorities, mainly because people had very little environmental awareness — rubbish just got dumped into rivers, left on pavements, or thrown wherever was convenient."},{"en":"real damage to the environment","context":"It did real damage to the environment, on top of making a lot of neighbourhoods look and smell awful."},{"en":"an effective and practical solution","context":"There's now an effective and practical solution for getting rid of everyday waste, and people's awareness of the whole issue has grown a lot over the years."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an environment law","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-198","_displayNo":"#098","outline":{"topic":"waste-sorting law","intro":"govt rule few yrs ago positive effect on how people deal w/ rubbish 3 bins organic general recyclable colour-coded","body":["first found out poster campaign apartment building group chat everyone benefits environment waste collectors","before law disposal nightmare to manage little environmental awareness dumped rivers pavements detrimental impact","since law campaigning real shift effective practical solution awareness grown over years"],"conclusion":"happy w/ how law helped tackle waste problem rather than just talking about it w/o change"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-198.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An environmental rule I am interested in concerns sorting household waste. I first learned about it through posters near my flat and reminders in our building's group chat. The message was to separate organic waste, general rubbish and recyclable materials.\n\nThe system uses different bins so that people know where to put each type. Before learning about it, I had not thought carefully about how difficult mixed rubbish can be for collectors to handle. Separating it at home seemed like a practical way for residents to help.\n\nI like the idea because it connects a large environmental problem with something people can do every day. Public bins and clear instructions are important; simply telling people to care is not enough. The campaign around the rule also encouraged conversations about waste in my building. I do not remember the exact official name, but its main purpose is clear to me. It aims to make disposal more organised and help people take responsibility for the rubbish they produce, rather than leaving the whole problem to someone else.","html":"An environmental rule I am interested in concerns sorting household waste. I first learned about it through posters near my flat and reminders in our building's group chat. The message was to separate organic waste, general rubbish and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recyclable materials</strong>.\n\nThe system uses different bins so that people know where to put each type. Before learning about it, I had not thought carefully about how difficult mixed rubbish can be for collectors to handle. Separating it at home seemed like a practical way for residents to help.\n\nI like the idea because it connects a large environmental problem with something people can do every day. Public bins and clear instructions are important; simply telling people to care is not enough. The campaign around the rule also encouraged conversations about waste in my building. I do not remember the exact official name, but its main purpose is clear to me. It aims to make <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disposal</strong> more organised and help people take responsibility for the rubbish they produce, rather than leaving the whole problem to someone else.","phrases":[{"en":"recyclable materials","meaning":"Things that can be processed and used to make new products."},{"en":"disposal","meaning":"The process of getting rid of unwanted things or waste."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-198.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Something that makes you excited","prompt":"Talk about something that makes you excited.\nYou should say:\n- What it is\n- How it is exciting\n- Why you feel excited about it","answer":"Honestly, there isn't a huge amount of excitement in my life at the moment, but if I have to pick one thing, it'd be the weekly coffee date I have with my best friend — that's genuinely what I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look forward to</strong> most.\n\nThe reason it means so much is that we barely get the chance to talk during the week. Back in school the two of us were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">like the white on rice</strong>, completely inseparable, and I could spend entire evenings just gossiping with her without it ever feeling like enough. Since we graduated, though, we've both been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">buried in work</strong>, so staying in regular contact has got a lot harder. Weekends are really the only time we can meet up properly now, and just thinking about it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never fails to excite me</strong>.\n\nWe do all sorts of things together, honestly. Most weeks we just pick a nice cafe and talk for hours, but sometimes we'll go shopping instead, or have brunch somewhere a bit fancier. What we actually do doesn't matter that much to me — it's really just about seeing her. I always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel so much lighter</strong> after I've confided in her about whatever's stressing me out, because I know she'll cheer me up and give me genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">useful advice</strong>.\n\nSo yes, out of everything going on in my life right now, that weekly coffee date is probably the one thing that excites me the most.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"look forward to","context":"Honestly, there isn't a huge amount of excitement in my life at the moment, but if I have to pick one thing, it'd be the weekly coffee date I have with my best friend — that's genuinely what I look forward to most."},{"en":"like the white on rice","context":"Back in school the two of us were like the white on rice, completely inseparable, and I could spend entire evenings just gossiping with her without it ever feeling like enough."},{"en":"buried in work","context":"Since we graduated, though, we've both been buried in work, so staying in regular contact has got a lot harder."},{"en":"never fails to excite me","context":"Weekends are really the only time we can meet up properly now, and just thinking about it never fails to excite me."},{"en":"feel so much lighter","context":"I always feel so much lighter after I've confided in her about whatever's stressing me out, because I know she'll cheer me up and give me genuinely useful advice."},{"en":"useful advice","context":"I always feel so much lighter after I've confided in her about whatever's stressing me out, because I know she'll cheer me up and give me genuinely useful advice."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"something that makes you excited","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-200","_displayNo":"#099","outline":{"topic":"weekly coffee date w/ best friend","intro":"not much excitement in life at moment but weekly coffee date w/ best friend is what I look forward to most","body":["barely talk during week school like white on rice inseparable since graduated both buried in work","weekends only time meet up properly never fails to excite me","do all sorts things together cafe shopping brunch what we do doesn't matter just about seeing her"],"conclusion":"feel so much lighter after confiding in her useful advice one thing excites me most"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-200.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Something that makes me excited is my weekly coffee meeting with my best friend. We were extremely close at school and could spend hours talking without getting bored. Since graduating, however, work has made it harder to meet during the week.\n\nWe now try to keep time free at the weekend. Usually, we choose a cafe and talk for a few hours. Sometimes we go shopping or have brunch instead. The exact activity is less important than knowing we will have a proper chance to see each other.\n\nI look forward to it because I can speak honestly about whatever is worrying me. She listens, makes me laugh and often gives useful advice. I normally feel lighter afterwards, even if the problem itself has not disappeared. The meeting also helps us keep our friendship strong while our lives become busier. It may seem like a small thing to be excited about, but familiar relationships matter a lot to me. Having this regular plan gives me something enjoyable to think about during a difficult working week.","html":"Something that makes me excited is my weekly coffee meeting with my best friend. We were extremely close at school and could spend hours talking without getting bored. Since graduating, however, work has made it harder to meet during the week.\n\nWe now try to keep time free at the weekend. Usually, we choose a cafe and talk for a few hours. Sometimes we go shopping or have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">brunch</strong> instead. The exact activity is less important than knowing we will have a proper chance to see each other.\n\nI look forward to it because I can speak honestly about whatever is worrying me. She listens, makes me laugh and often gives useful advice. I normally <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel lighter</strong> afterwards, even if the problem itself has not disappeared. The meeting also helps us keep our friendship strong while our lives become busier. It may seem like a small thing to be excited about, but familiar relationships matter a lot to me. Having this regular plan gives me something enjoyable to think about during a difficult working week.","phrases":[{"en":"brunch","meaning":"A meal eaten late in the morning, combining breakfast and lunch."},{"en":"feel lighter","meaning":"Feel less worried or emotionally burdened."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-200.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Your favourite season of the year","prompt":"Describe your favourite season of the year.\nYou should say:\n- What the weather is like.\n- Why you like this season.\n- What activities you usually do during this season.\n- How different kinds of weather in this season affect people.","answer":"My favourite season has to be spring. In Vietnam it usually starts around the end of December and runs through to about the middle of March, and honestly, it's the one time of year when the weather doesn't really bother me either way — it's not roasting like summer or freezing like winter, just genuinely comfortable, day and night.\n\nWhat I love most about spring is how much nature changes. Cherry and apricot trees are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in full blossom</strong>, and you start seeing daffodils and marigolds everywhere in the parks near my flat. The mornings are full of birdsong too, which you barely notice the rest of the year. And of course, spring is when we celebrate Tet, which for my family means <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a proper reunion</strong> — my cousins come back from wherever they're studying or working, and every meal for about a week is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">full of delicious traditional dishes</strong>.\n\nActivity-wise, this is the season I actually get outside the most. My friends and I usually go on at least one <strong class=\"key-phrase\">flower-viewing trip</strong> to somewhere just outside the city, and I try to get back into cycling once the weather's mild enough not to make it miserable. It's also just a nicer season for walking around in the evenings without either sweating or shivering.\n\nAs for how the weather affects people, I've definitely noticed that the extreme seasons <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hit people harder</strong> than spring does — my grandmother, for instance, really struggles in both the height of summer and the depths of winter, but during spring she's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">noticeably more comfortable and active</strong>. It's a small thing, but it's part of why spring's my favourite time of year in Vietnam.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"in full blossom","context":"Cherry and apricot trees are in full blossom, and you start seeing daffodils and marigolds everywhere in the parks near my flat."},{"en":"a proper reunion","context":"And of course, spring is when we celebrate Tet, which for my family means a proper reunion — my cousins come back from wherever they're studying or working, and every meal for about a week is full of delicious traditional dishes."},{"en":"full of delicious traditional dishes","context":"And of course, spring is when we celebrate Tet, which for my family means a proper reunion — my cousins come back from wherever they're studying or working, and every meal for about a week is full of delicious traditional dishes."},{"en":"flower-viewing trip","context":"My friends and I usually go on at least one flower-viewing trip to somewhere just outside the city, and I try to get back into cycling once the weather's mild enough not to make it miserable."},{"en":"hit people harder","context":"As for how the weather affects people, I've definitely noticed that the extreme seasons hit people harder than spring does — my grandmother, for instance, really struggles in both the height of summer and the depths of winter, but during spring she's noticeably more comfortable and active."},{"en":"noticeably more comfortable and active","context":"As for how the weather affects people, I've definitely noticed that the extreme seasons hit people harder than spring does — my grandmother, for instance, really struggles in both the height of summer and the depths of winter, but during spring she's noticeably more comfortable and active."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"your favourite season of the year","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-203","_displayNo":"#100","outline":{"topic":"spring in Vietnam","intro":"favourite season spring Vietnam end Dec to mid-March weather not roasting or freezing comfortable day and night","body":["nature changes cherry apricot trees in full blossom daffodils marigolds birdsong Tet family reunion traditional dishes","activities flower-viewing trip outside city cycling once mild evening walks","extreme seasons hit people harder grandmother struggles summer/winter but more comfortable active in spring"],"conclusion":"small thing but part of why spring is favourite time of year in Vietnam"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-203.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"My favourite season in Vietnam is spring. I enjoy the mild weather because it is more comfortable than the hottest summer days or the coldest winter ones. It is a pleasant time to spend longer outdoors.\n\nI especially like seeing flowers and fresh growth in parks and gardens. Peach and apricot blossoms are closely connected with the season for me. Spring also brings Tet, when my family gathers and relatives return from work or study elsewhere. We share traditional meals and have more time together than usual.\n\nMy friends and I often plan a trip to see flowers outside the city. I also return to cycling and enjoy evening walks without feeling too hot or cold. The comfortable weather seems to help my grandmother too. She struggles more in extreme temperatures but is noticeably more active during spring. That is another reason I appreciate it. For me, the season combines pleasant surroundings, family traditions and opportunities to be outside. It makes ordinary days feel more cheerful without needing a special event every time.","html":"My favourite season in Vietnam is spring. I enjoy the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mild weather</strong> because it is more comfortable than the hottest summer days or the coldest winter ones. It is a pleasant time to spend longer outdoors.\n\nI especially like seeing flowers and fresh growth in parks and gardens. Peach and apricot blossoms are closely connected with the season for me. Spring also brings Tet, when my family gathers and relatives return from work or study elsewhere. We share traditional meals and have more time together than usual.\n\nMy friends and I often plan a trip to see flowers outside the city. I also return to cycling and enjoy evening walks without feeling too hot or cold. The comfortable weather seems to help my grandmother too. She struggles more in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">extreme temperatures</strong> but is noticeably more active during spring. That is another reason I appreciate it. For me, the season combines pleasant surroundings, family traditions and opportunities to be outside. It makes ordinary days feel more cheerful without needing a special event every time.","phrases":[{"en":"mild weather","meaning":"Weather that is not very hot or very cold."},{"en":"extreme temperatures","meaning":"Conditions that are unusually hot or cold."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-203.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Your favourite season in your country","prompt":"Describe your favourite season in your country.\nYou should say:\n- when that season is\n- what the weather is like at that time of year\n- how that season is different from other seasons\n- and why it is your favourite season.","answer":"My favourite season in England is winter. A typical English winter would last around 4 or 5 months between November and March, but when I say that I like the winter, I'm really thinking about the coldest month or two each year. During those really wintry months, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the temperature drops below zero</strong> and it often snows. Over the last few years we've had periods of quite <strong class=\"key-phrase\">heavy snowfall</strong>, to the extent that roads have been blocked and schools have had to close. Last winter I remember looking out of the window one morning and seeing everything <strong class=\"key-phrase\">covered in a layer of white</strong>. Obviously the snow is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what differentiates winter from the other seasons</strong>, but when it doesn't snow there isn't much difference between winter, autumn and spring; winter is just a bit colder. The reason I've chosen winter as my favourite season is that I like the snow. I live near the countryside, and the landscape looks beautiful when everything is covered in white. We also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">celebrate Christmas and New Year</strong> in the winter, and these are my favourite festivals. I'll be honest and say that winter has its drawbacks too. The days are extremely short — it can be dark by four in the afternoon — and a lot of people find that genuinely difficult by about February. When it's grey and wet rather than crisp and snowy, which is most of the time, it's not a season anyone would choose.\n\nBut on the good days it's unbeatable. There is a particular kind of bright, still, freezing morning where the sky is completely clear and the ground is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hard underfoot</strong>, and for me that is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worth putting up with</strong> the rest of it.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"the temperature drops below zero","context":"During those really wintry months, the temperature drops below zero and it often snows."},{"en":"heavy snowfall","context":"Over the last few years we've had periods of quite heavy snowfall, to the extent that roads have been blocked and schools have had to close."},{"en":"covered in a layer of white","context":"Last winter I remember looking out of the window one morning and seeing everything covered in a layer of white."},{"en":"what differentiates winter from the other seasons","context":"Obviously the snow is what differentiates winter from the other seasons, but when it doesn't snow there isn't much difference between winter, autumn and spring; winter is just a bit colder."},{"en":"celebrate Christmas and New Year","context":"We also celebrate Christmas and New Year in the winter, and these are my favourite festivals."},{"en":"worth putting up with","context":"But on the good days it's unbeatable. There is a particular kind of bright, still, freezing morning where the sky is completely clear and the ground is hard underfoot, and for me that is worth putting up with the rest of it."},{"en":"hard underfoot","context":"But on the good days it's unbeatable. There is a particular kind of bright, still, freezing morning where the sky is completely clear and the ground is hard underfoot, and for me that is worth putting up with the rest of it."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"your favourite season in your country","id":"speaking-part-2-204","_displayNo":"#101","outline":{"topic":"English winter","intro":"favourite season England winter","body":["typical English winter last around 4 5 mos between November March","Last winter remember looking out window one morning seeing everything covered","Obviously snow differentiates winter seasons not snow not difference between winter","drawbacks days extremely short dark by 4pm difficult by February","grey and wet rather than crisp snowy most of the time","good days unbeatable bright still freezing morning clear sky hard ground"],"conclusion":"good days unbeatable bright still freezing morning clear sky hard ground"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-204.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"My favourite season in England is winter, especially the coldest weeks when there is snow. I live near the countryside, and the fields and trees look beautiful under a layer of white.\n\nOne morning last winter, I looked outside and saw the whole area covered in snow. The familiar landscape seemed completely different. I enjoy clear, freezing mornings when the ground feels hard and the air is still. Winter also includes Christmas and New Year, which are my favourite celebrations because of the time spent with family and friends.\n\nOf course, the season has disadvantages. Snow can block roads and close schools, while short days mean it may be dark by four in the afternoon. Much of the weather is grey and wet rather than bright and snowy, and I understand why some people dislike it. Still, the good days make it special to me. I would not claim that every winter afternoon is enjoyable, but the combination of snowy scenery and important celebrations gives the season a feeling I do not get at other times of year.","html":"My favourite season in England is winter, especially the coldest weeks when there is snow. I live near the countryside, and the fields and trees look beautiful under a layer of white.\n\nOne morning last winter, I looked outside and saw the whole area covered in snow. The <strong class=\"key-phrase\">familiar landscape</strong> seemed completely different. I enjoy clear, freezing mornings when the ground feels hard and the air is still. Winter also includes Christmas and New Year, which are my favourite celebrations because of the time spent with family and friends.\n\nOf course, the season has disadvantages. Snow can block roads and close schools, while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">short days</strong> mean it may be dark by four in the afternoon. Much of the weather is grey and wet rather than bright and snowy, and I understand why some people dislike it. Still, the good days make it special to me. I would not claim that every winter afternoon is enjoyable, but the combination of snowy scenery and important celebrations gives the season a feeling I do not get at other times of year.","phrases":[{"en":"familiar landscape","meaning":"The scenery of a place you know well."},{"en":"short days","meaning":"Days with relatively few hours of daylight."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-204.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A businessman that you admire","prompt":"Describe a businessman that you admire.\nYou should say:\n- Who this person is;\n- How you got to know this person;\n- What this person does;\n- How you think of this person.","answer":"Today I'm going to talk about a businessman that I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">really admire and respect</strong>. I live on a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">beautiful and peaceful street</strong>, and most of my neighbours work for companies or run their own businesses, but out of all of them, I'd pick Mr Vincent as the most admirable businessman I've ever met.\n\nI got to know him about three years ago, not long after he and his family moved in two doors down from us. We got chatting over the garden fence one weekend, and once he found out I was interested in business, he was happy to tell me about what he does, and we've stayed close ever since.\n\nMr Vincent is only thirty, but he <strong class=\"key-phrase\">established his first company</strong> when he was just twenty-six. His company trades medical equipment, and it's called Viet Sun. He's built up a reputation as an incredibly hard-working person — he leaves for work early and gets home late, and even on weekends, instead of hanging out with friends, he's still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">glued to his computer screen</strong>.\n\nHe's nearly ten years older than me but looks really young and always seems full of energy. The fact that he built all this up from nothing by the age of twenty-six is honestly what I find most inspiring about him.\n\nAnother thing I love about him is his advice — whenever I'm feeling discouraged, I'll go and talk to him, and I've learned a lot from him, not just about business, but about his <strong class=\"key-phrase\">attitudes to life</strong>.\n\nTo me, he's not just a businessman I happen to live near — he's someone I can share things with and learn from. I really owe him a lot.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"really admire and respect","context":"Today I'm going to talk about a businessman that I really admire and respect."},{"en":"beautiful and peaceful street","context":"I live on a beautiful and peaceful street, and most of my neighbours work for companies or run their own businesses, but out of all of them, I'd pick Mr Vincent as the most admirable businessman I've ever met."},{"en":"established his first company","context":"Mr Vincent is only thirty, but he established his first company when he was just twenty-six."},{"en":"glued to his computer screen","context":"He's built up a reputation as an incredibly hard-working person — he leaves for work early and gets home late, and even on weekends, instead of hanging out with friends, he's still glued to his computer screen."},{"en":"attitudes to life","context":"Another thing I love about him is his advice — whenever I'm feeling discouraged, I'll go and talk to him, and I've learned a lot from him, not just about business, but about his attitudes to life."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a businessman that you admire","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-001","_displayNo":"#102","outline":{"topic":"Mr Vincent","intro":"businessman admire respect, neighbour got to know him ~3 years ago","body":["moved in two doors down, met chatting over garden fence, stayed close since","Mr. Vincent 30 y/o established 1st company at 26, trades medical equipment (Viet Sun)","reputation as hard-working, early to work late home, weekends still on computer","not many have passion/skills to chase goals - built company from nothing at 26, inspiring"],"conclusion":"love his advice, learned about business and attitudes to life, owe him a lot"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-001.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A businessman I admire is my neighbour, Mr Vincent. His family moved into our street about three years ago. We first spoke over the garden fence, and we became friends because I was interested in his work.\n\nHe is thirty now and started a medical equipment company called Viet Sun when he was twenty-six. He works very hard. He usually leaves home early and returns late, and he often uses his weekends to work on his computer. Although he is nearly ten years older than me, he always seems young and full of energy.\n\nI admire him because he built his company from nothing. He also makes time to help me. Whenever I feel worried about my future, I talk to him, and he gives me useful advice about business and life. To me, he is both a successful businessman and a good friend. His experience encourages me to keep working towards my own goals.","html":"A businessman I admire is my neighbour, Mr Vincent. His family moved into our street about three years ago. We first spoke over the garden fence, and we became friends because I was interested in his work.\n\nHe is thirty now and started a medical equipment company called Viet Sun when he was twenty-six. He works very hard. He usually leaves home early and returns late, and he often uses his weekends to work on his computer. Although he is nearly ten years older than me, he always seems young and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">full of energy</strong>.\n\nI admire him because he <strong class=\"key-phrase\">built his company from nothing</strong>. He also makes time to help me. Whenever I feel worried about my future, I talk to him, and he gives me useful advice about business and life. To me, he is both a successful businessman and a good friend. His experience encourages me to keep working towards my own goals.","phrases":[{"en":"full of energy","meaning":"Feeling active and ready to do things."},{"en":"built his company from nothing","meaning":"Started and developed his business without having an established company already."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-001.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A couple you know who have a happy marriage","prompt":"Describe a couple you know who have a happy marriage\nYou should say:\nWho they are\nHow you know them\nWhat they usually do together\nAnd how you feel about their marriage.","answer":"Well, I'd like to talk about my own parents, whose marriage is, without a doubt, a happy one. They've been married for over thirty years now, and to me they're the perfect couple.\n\nMy mum and dad are both in their sixties, and they're still really busy with their nine-to-five jobs at a government office, though they're both planning to retire this year. Obviously, I've known them my whole life!\n\nWhat I love is that they still do simple things together — they go for a walk around the neighbourhood most evenings after dinner, and on Sundays they cook a big family meal together, my dad chopping vegetables while my mum handles the stove. It's nothing fancy, but they clearly look forward to it.\n\nOver the years, my parents have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">been through thick and thin</strong> together but have never lost their love for each other. They <strong class=\"key-phrase\">show their affection</strong> simply by always being honest with each other and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sharing their true feelings</strong>. Their <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mutual understanding</strong> is so strong that sometimes it only <strong class=\"key-phrase\">takes one look to realise</strong> what the other is thinking.\n\nOf course, they still have minor tiffs <strong class=\"key-phrase\">now and then</strong> — things like whose turn it is to walk the dog, or who's hogging the TV remote — but my dad's always first to give in and comfort my mum. I think little things like that are actually the key to their long-lasting relationship.\n\nTo me, my parents are a perfect match, and I admire their love for each other so much. Hopefully, one day, I'll have a partner who cares for me just as much as they care for each other.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"been through thick and thin","context":"Over the years, my parents have been through thick and thin together but have never lost their love for each other."},{"en":"show their affection","context":"They show their affection simply by always being honest with each other and sharing their true feelings."},{"en":"sharing their true feelings","context":"They show their affection simply by always being honest with each other and sharing their true feelings."},{"en":"mutual understanding","context":"Their mutual understanding is so strong that sometimes it only takes one look to realise what the other is thinking."},{"en":"takes one look to realise","context":"Their mutual understanding is so strong that sometimes it only takes one look to realise what the other is thinking."},{"en":"now and then","context":"Of course, they still have minor tiffs now and then — things like whose turn it is to walk the dog, or who's hogging the TV remote — but my dad's always first to give in and comfort my mum."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a couple you know who have a happy marriage","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-002","_displayNo":"#103","outline":{"topic":"my own parents","intro":"own parents marriage without doubt happy one, known them my whole life","body":["mum dad in sixties busy w/ nine-to-five govt jobs, planning to retire this year","do simple things together daily - evening walk, Sunday family meal cooked together","been through thick and thin, show affection by honesty and sharing true feelings","mutual understanding so strong takes one look to realise what other thinking","minor tiffs now and then e.g. dog walking/TV remote, dad first to give in and comfort"],"conclusion":"perfect match, admire their love, hope for a partner who cares as much"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-002.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"I'd like to talk about my parents, who have been happily married for over thirty years. They are both in their sixties and work at a government office, although they plan to retire this year.\n\nThey enjoy doing ordinary things together. After dinner, they often walk around our neighbourhood. On Sundays, they prepare a family meal, with my father cutting vegetables while my mother cooks. These small activities give them time to talk and enjoy each other's company.\n\nTheir life has not always been easy, but they have supported each other through difficult times. They are honest about their feelings and understand each other well. Of course, they sometimes argue about little things, such as the television remote. However, my father usually makes peace first. I think their kindness and willingness to listen explain why their marriage has lasted. I admire their relationship, and I hope to have the same kind of loving partnership in the future.","html":"I'd like to talk about my parents, who have been happily married for over thirty years. They are both in their sixties and work at a government office, although they plan to retire this year.\n\nThey enjoy doing ordinary things together. After dinner, they often walk around our neighbourhood. On Sundays, they prepare a family meal, with my father cutting vegetables while my mother cooks. These small activities give them time to talk and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">enjoy each other's company</strong>.\n\nTheir life has not always been easy, but they have supported each other through difficult times. They are honest about their feelings and understand each other well. Of course, they sometimes argue about little things, such as the television remote. However, my father usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">makes peace</strong> first. I think their kindness and willingness to listen explain why their marriage has lasted. I admire their relationship, and I hope to have the same kind of loving partnership in the future.","phrases":[{"en":"enjoy each other's company","meaning":"Like spending time together."},{"en":"makes peace","meaning":"Helps people stop arguing and become friendly again."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-002.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A family member that made you proud","prompt":"Describe a family member that made you proud.\nYou should say:\nWho the person is\nHow close you are\nWhat he/she did that made you proud\nAnd explain why they made you proud.","answer":"There's no one else in this world I hold more respect for than my mother. She's my guardian, my friend, and honestly my best friend too — we still talk on the phone most days, even now that I've moved out.\n\nMy mother's well into her forties now, but she still manages to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">maintain a youthful appearance</strong>. She's an extremely compassionate, kind-hearted person who's always looked after her children, and she's both a mother and a friend to me — she gives me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">valuable advice</strong> and is always there for me, no matter what. She drives me a bit crazy sometimes, because she's such a perfectionist, but I still hold huge pride and respect for her.\n\nThe reason I'm so proud of her is that she's such a strong-willed person. Ever since she was young, she's had to be independent, because my grandparents were always so busy, so she had to take care of almost everything herself — her younger brother and the household chores. Challenges like that shaped her into the strong woman she is today.\n\nWhat made me proudest, though, was when she reached her thirties and decided she wanted to run her own business, a clothing store. Pretty much the whole family tried to talk her out of it, mainly because of all the competition from other shops nearby. But she went ahead anyway, and through sheer <strong class=\"key-phrase\">determination and perseverance</strong>, she's managed to make a real success out of it. It's honestly something for all of us to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look up to</strong>, and I keep reminding myself every day to try to be as strong as my mother — a real-life \"wonder woman\".","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"maintain a youthful appearance","context":"My mother's well into her forties now, but she still manages to maintain a youthful appearance."},{"en":"valuable advice","context":"She's an extremely compassionate, kind-hearted person who's always looked after her children, and she's both a mother and a friend to me — she gives me valuable advice and is always there for me, no matter what."},{"en":"determination and perseverance","context":"But she went ahead anyway, and through sheer determination and perseverance, she's managed to make a real success out of it."},{"en":"look up to","context":"It's honestly something for all of us to look up to, and I keep reminding myself every day to try to be as strong as my mother — a real-life \"wonder woman\"."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a family member that made you proud","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-003","_displayNo":"#104","outline":{"topic":"my mother","intro":"no one else in world I hold more respect for than my mother, best friend, talk daily","body":["well into 40s, maintains youthful appearance, compassionate kind-hearted attentive personality","mother and friend, gives valuable advice, there through ups and downs, drives me crazy (perfectionist)","strong-willed since young - grandparents busy, she had to be independent, raised brother/chores herself","proudest moment: opened clothing store in her 30s despite family trying to talk her out of it"],"conclusion":"determination and perseverance made it a success, something to look up to, try to be as strong as her"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-003.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"The family member who makes me proud is my mother. She is in her forties and still looks young. She is kind and caring, and I can talk to her like a close friend. Even though I have moved out, we speak on the phone most days.\n\nShe became independent quite early. My grandparents were busy, so she had to help with the housework and look after her younger brother. This taught her to deal with problems on her own.\n\nI felt especially proud when she decided to open a clothing shop in her thirties. Most of our family thought it was too risky because there were already many shops nearby. However, she believed in her idea and worked hard to make it successful. She did not give up when things were difficult. Her shop showed me what she could achieve through patience and effort. Whenever I face a challenge, I think about her example and try to be as determined as she is.","html":"The family member who makes me proud is my mother. She is in her forties and still looks young. She is kind and caring, and I can talk to her like a close friend. Even though I have moved out, we speak on the phone most days.\n\nShe became independent quite early. My grandparents were busy, so she had to help with the housework and look after her younger brother. This taught her to deal with problems <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on her own</strong>.\n\nI felt especially proud when she decided to open a clothing shop in her thirties. Most of our family thought it was too risky because there were already many shops nearby. However, she believed in her idea and worked hard to make it successful. She <strong class=\"key-phrase\">did not give up</strong> when things were difficult. Her shop showed me what she could achieve through patience and effort. Whenever I face a challenge, I think about her example and try to be as determined as she is.","phrases":[{"en":"on her own","meaning":"Without help from other people."},{"en":"did not give up","meaning":"Continued trying even when something was difficult."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-003.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A family member who has had an important influence on you","prompt":"Describe a family member who has had an important influence on you\nYou should say:\nWho the person is;\nWhat kind of person he or she is;\nWhat this person has done to influence you;\nAnd explain why you think this person is important to you.","answer":"I'm going to talk about my mother, who's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">played an essential role in my life</strong>. She's my guardian, my friend, and my role model, and if I'm honest, most of the person I've become is down to her.\n\nMy mother's in her forties now, but she still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">maintains a youthful look</strong>. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">In terms of personality</strong>, she's an extremely compassionate and kind-hearted person who always takes care of her children. She's really a mother and a friend to me, as she always gives me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">valuable advice</strong> and is willing to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stand by me</strong> <strong class=\"key-phrase\">through all the ups and downs</strong> of my life. Although she sometimes drives me crazy because she's such a perfectionist, I still hold so much pride and respect for her.\n\nThe clearest way she's influenced me is through her strong will, and there's one moment I always come back to. When I was about sixteen, I wanted to quit learning the piano because I wasn't improving fast enough and I found it boring. My mother sat me down and told me that giving up the moment something got difficult would become a habit if I let it, and she made me promise to stick with it for just one more year. I did, and by the end of that year I'd actually started to enjoy it — and that lesson about not quitting when things get hard has stuck with me ever since, through exams, job applications, everything.\n\nHer determination and perseverance are really something for us to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look up to</strong>, and that piano lesson is exactly why I keep reminding myself every day to be as strong as my mother — a real-life \"wonder woman\".","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"played an essential role in my life","context":"I'm going to talk about my mother, who's played an essential role in my life. She's my guardian, my friend, and my role model, and if I'm honest, most of the person I've become is down to her."},{"en":"maintains a youthful look","context":"My mother's in her forties now, but she still maintains a youthful look."},{"en":"In terms of personality","context":"In terms of personality, she's an extremely compassionate and kind-hearted person who always takes care of her children."},{"en":"valuable advice","context":"She's really a mother and a friend to me, as she always gives me valuable advice and is willing to stand by me through all the ups and downs of my life."},{"en":"stand by me","context":"She's really a mother and a friend to me, as she always gives me valuable advice and is willing to stand by me through all the ups and downs of my life."},{"en":"through all the ups and downs","context":"She's really a mother and a friend to me, as she always gives me valuable advice and is willing to stand by me through all the ups and downs of my life."},{"en":"look up to","context":"Her determination and perseverance are really something for us to look up to, and that piano lesson is exactly why I keep reminding myself every day to be as strong as my mother — a real-life \"wonder woman\"."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a family member who has had an important influence on you","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-004","_displayNo":"#105","outline":{"topic":"my mother","intro":"mother played essential role in life, most of who I am is down to her","body":["in 40s, maintains youthful look, compassionate kind-hearted, gives valuable advice, stands by me","clearest influence: piano story age 16 - wanted to quit, mother made me stick it out a year","learned not to quit when things get hard - stuck with me since, through exams and jobs"],"conclusion":"determination and perseverance something to look up to, piano lesson reminds me to be as strong as her"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-004.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"My mother has had the biggest influence on me. She is in her forties and is a very kind person. She takes good care of our family and always listens when I need advice. Sometimes she expects everything to be perfect, but I know she wants the best for us.\n\nOne experience explains her influence clearly. When I was about sixteen, I wanted to stop learning the piano. I thought I was improving too slowly, and the lessons had become boring. My mother asked me to continue for just one more year. She explained that stopping whenever something became difficult could turn into a bad habit.\n\nI followed her advice. By the end of that year, I was playing better and actually enjoying the piano. That experience taught me to be patient. Since then, I have remembered her advice during exams and job applications. She has helped me understand that progress takes time, and this lesson still affects my decisions today.","html":"My mother has had the biggest influence on me. She is in her forties and is a very kind person. She takes good care of our family and always listens when I need advice. Sometimes she expects everything to be perfect, but I know she wants the best for us.\n\nOne experience explains her influence clearly. When I was about sixteen, I wanted to stop learning the piano. I thought I was improving too slowly, and the lessons had become boring. My mother asked me to continue for just one more year. She explained that stopping whenever something became difficult could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turn into a bad habit</strong>.\n\nI followed her advice. By the end of that year, I was playing better and actually enjoying the piano. That experience taught me to be patient. Since then, I have remembered her advice during exams and job applications. She has helped me understand that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">progress takes time</strong>, and this lesson still affects my decisions today.","phrases":[{"en":"turn into a bad habit","meaning":"Become an unhelpful action that you repeat regularly."},{"en":"progress takes time","meaning":"Improving usually happens gradually rather than immediately."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-004.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A famous athlete you know","prompt":"Describe a famous athlete you know\nYou should say:\nWho he or she is\nHow you know him or her\nWhat he or she has achieved\nWhy he is famous","answer":"<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Being a big fan of</strong> basketball, I've long idolised the legend of the sport, Michael Jordan, also known as MJ. I first <strong class=\"key-phrase\">got curious about</strong> his life when I saw the Jumpman logo on a pair of Nike trainers, and once I started reading about him, his story <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be</strong> genuinely fascinating.\n\nHe played shooting guard for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of the most renowned teams</strong> in the sport, the Chicago Bulls, and he was famous for his <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exceptional leaping ability</strong> — he could take off from around the free-throw line and still finish with a slam dunk. Even back in college at the University of North Carolina, he hit the winning shot in the 1982 national championship game as a freshman. But it wasn't just raw talent that made him great, it was his dedication — he'd spend hours studying footage of his opponents just to sharpen his defence.\n\nThroughout his career, he led the Chicago Bulls to six championships and was named NBA Most Valuable Player five times. He's also one of the very few players in history to have won both the Defensive Player of the Year award and the MVP award. Honestly, none of the praise that journalists gave him feels like an overstatement.\n\nAlthough Michael Jordan retired back in 2003, his skills and his legacy are still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a great inspiration</strong> for any basketball player. He's stayed active in charity work too — his clothing line, the Jordan Brand, has made donations to organisations across America. It's genuinely hard to put into words how good he was; you really just have to watch a few clips to understand it for yourself.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"Being a big fan of","context":"Being a big fan of basketball, I've long idolised the legend of the sport, Michael Jordan, also known as MJ."},{"en":"got curious about","context":"I first got curious about his life when I saw the Jumpman logo on a pair of Nike trainers, and once I started reading about him, his story turned out to be genuinely fascinating."},{"en":"turned out to be","context":"I first got curious about his life when I saw the Jumpman logo on a pair of Nike trainers, and once I started reading about him, his story turned out to be genuinely fascinating."},{"en":"one of the most renowned teams","context":"He played shooting guard for one of the most renowned teams in the sport, the Chicago Bulls, and he was famous for his exceptional leaping ability — he could take off from around the free-throw line and still finish with a slam dunk."},{"en":"exceptional leaping ability","context":"He played shooting guard for one of the most renowned teams in the sport, the Chicago Bulls, and he was famous for his exceptional leaping ability — he could take off from around the free-throw line and still finish with a slam dunk."},{"en":"a great inspiration","context":"Although Michael Jordan retired back in 2003, his skills and his legacy are still a great inspiration for any basketball player."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a famous athlete you know","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-005","_displayNo":"#106","outline":{"topic":"MJ","intro":"big fan of basketball, long idolised Michael Jordan, got curious via Nike Jumpman logo","body":["shooting guard Chicago Bulls, exceptional leaping ability, slam dunks from free-throw line","even in college at UNC, hit winning shot in 1982 championship game as a freshman","not just talent but dedication - studied opponents' footage, pushed himself harder in practice","career: six championships, five-time MVP, one of few to also win Defensive Player of the Year"],"conclusion":"retired 2003, still a great inspiration, active in charity work through his clothing line"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-005.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A famous athlete I admire is Michael Jordan, the basketball player also known as MJ. I first became interested in him after seeing the Jumpman logo on a pair of Nike shoes. I then read about his life and watched videos of his games.\n\nHe played for the Chicago Bulls and was famous for jumping very high and scoring difficult shots. He also scored the winning basket in a national college championship while studying at the University of North Carolina. What impressed me was his preparation. He spent hours studying other players so he could defend better.\n\nDuring his career, he helped the Bulls win six championships and received the NBA Most Valuable Player award five times. Although he retired in 2003, his performances still inspire basketball fans like me. He has also supported charity work through his brand. I admire his talent, but I respect his hard work even more. His story reminds me that natural ability alone is not enough to succeed.","html":"A famous athlete I admire is Michael Jordan, the basketball player also known as MJ. I first became interested in him after seeing the Jumpman logo on a pair of Nike shoes. I then read about his life and watched videos of his games.\n\nHe played for the Chicago Bulls and was famous for jumping very high and scoring difficult shots. He also scored the winning basket in a national college championship while studying at the University of North Carolina. What impressed me was his preparation. He spent hours studying other players so he could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">defend better</strong>.\n\nDuring his career, he helped the Bulls win six championships and received the NBA Most Valuable Player award five times. Although he retired in 2003, his performances still inspire basketball fans like me. He has also supported charity work through his brand. I admire his talent, but I respect his hard work even more. His story reminds me that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">natural ability</strong> alone is not enough to succeed.","phrases":[{"en":"defend better","meaning":"Stop the other team from scoring more effectively."},{"en":"natural ability","meaning":"A skill that someone seems to have without much training."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-005.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A famous person that you are interested in","prompt":"Describe a famous person that you are interested in\nYou should say:\n- Who this person is.\n- How you know about this\nperson.\n- What this person is famous for.\n- Why you are interested in this person.","answer":"A famous person I'm genuinely interested in is My Tam. To me, she's the best pop singer in Vietnam, and she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">embarked on her singing career</strong> back in 1999. She's originally from Da Nang, and she recently became Vietnam's first-ever artist to appear on Billboard's World Albums chart, after releasing her ninth studio album, 'Tam 9'.\n\nI first came across her by accident. Some years ago, I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caught a glimpse of</strong> her on TV, singing live, and I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">immediately hooked</strong> by how effortless she made it look. I've been her loyal fan ever since. What I love most is the way she sings — she doesn't just hit the notes, she really seems to feel every line of the song, especially in the slower, more emotional tracks. I've watched her live once, at a concert here in the city, and even from the back of the crowd her voice carried the whole room.\n\nShe's also just as admirable as a person. She's known to all her fans for being <strong class=\"key-phrase\">down-to-earth</strong> and for having a great sense of humour in interviews, which isn't always the case with singers at her level of fame. On top of that, she's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">devoted her time to</strong> organising free concerts for university students nationwide, and together with her fans, she's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">quietly done a lot of charity work</strong> over the years to help the poor, the sick and the homeless.\n\nHonestly, it's her combination of talent and genuine kindness that makes her my biggest idol, and I don't think that's going to change any time soon.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"embarked on her singing career","context":"To me, she's the best pop singer in Vietnam, and she embarked on her singing career back in 1999."},{"en":"caught a glimpse of","context":"Some years ago, I caught a glimpse of her on TV, singing live, and I was immediately hooked by how effortless she made it look."},{"en":"immediately hooked","context":"Some years ago, I caught a glimpse of her on TV, singing live, and I was immediately hooked by how effortless she made it look."},{"en":"down-to-earth","context":"She's known to all her fans for being down-to-earth and for having a great sense of humour in interviews, which isn't always the case with singers at her level of fame."},{"en":"devoted her time to","context":"On top of that, she's devoted her time to organising free concerts for university students nationwide, and together with her fans, she's quietly done a lot of charity work over the years to help the poor, the sick and the homeless."},{"en":"quietly done a lot of charity work","context":"On top of that, she's devoted her time to organising free concerts for university students nationwide, and together with her fans, she's quietly done a lot of charity work over the years to help the poor, the sick and the homeless."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a famous person that you are interested in","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-006","_displayNo":"#107","outline":{"topic":"My Tam","intro":"famous person genuinely interested in: My Tam, best pop singer in Vietnam, career since 1999","body":["Da Nang-born, recently became Vietnam's first artist on Billboard's World Albums chart, album 'Tam 9'","came across her by accident years ago, caught a glimpse on TV, immediately hooked","watched her live once at a concert, voice carried the whole room","down-to-earth, great sense of humour, devotes time to free concerts for students"],"conclusion":"quietly does a lot of charity work, combination of talent and kindness makes her my biggest idol"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-006.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A famous person I am interested in is My Tam, a Vietnamese pop singer from Da Nang. She started her singing career in 1999. I first saw her performing on television several years ago and immediately liked her voice.\n\nSince then, I have listened to many of her songs. I especially enjoy her slower songs because she expresses the feelings clearly. I also saw her at a concert in my city. Even though I was standing near the back, her singing made the performance feel special. Her album Tam 9 also helped her reach listeners outside Vietnam.\n\nI admire her personality as well as her music. In interviews, she seems friendly and funny. She has organised free concerts for university students and worked with her fans to help people who are poor, ill or homeless. I think this combination of talent and kindness explains why so many people respect her. She makes me feel that a successful artist can also have a positive effect on others.","html":"A famous person I am interested in is My Tam, a Vietnamese pop singer from Da Nang. She started her singing career in 1999. I first saw her performing on television several years ago and immediately liked her voice.\n\nSince then, I have listened to many of her songs. I especially enjoy her slower songs because she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expresses the feelings</strong> clearly. I also saw her at a concert in my city. Even though I was standing near the back, her singing made the performance feel special. Her album Tam 9 also helped her reach listeners outside Vietnam.\n\nI admire her personality as well as her music. In interviews, she seems friendly and funny. She has organised free concerts for university students and worked with her fans to help people who are poor, ill or homeless. I think this combination of talent and kindness explains why so many people respect her. She makes me feel that a successful artist can also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have a positive effect</strong> on others.","phrases":[{"en":"expresses the feelings","meaning":"Shows the emotions in something clearly."},{"en":"have a positive effect","meaning":"Make a situation or someone's life better."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-006.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A helpful person at work or school","prompt":"Describe a helpful person at work or school\nYou should say:\nWho it was\nWhat this person did\nHow this person helped\nAnd explain how you felt after this person helped you.","answer":"I'd like to tell you about one of my teachers from high school, who had a huge influence on me and was honestly the reason I managed to get into university. Her name's Linh, and she taught me English for all three years of high school — she was my form teacher too. She was in her late forties at the time, but she looked young for her age.\n\nI was really influenced by the way she taught. Her <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thoroughness in teaching</strong> is what first got me interested in English, even though I hadn't cared much for the subject before that. We never felt bored in her classes — she'd tell us interesting stories about British and American culture, and she'd organise games so we could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">learn new vocabulary and grammar effortlessly</strong> instead of just memorising word lists.\n\nWhat I remember most, though, is the three months before the university entrance exam, which was easily the most important exam of my life. She ran free extra classes after school for students who weren't quite up to standard, including me, and she patiently <strong class=\"key-phrase\">guided us through difficult questions</strong> and gave us loads of useful tips for the test. Thanks to her teaching and her dedication, I ended up passing the exam with an almost perfect score in English — I remember calling my parents the second I saw the results, almost too relieved to speak.\n\nI've graduated now and I'm studying in Hanoi, but I still visit her whenever I'm back home for the holidays, and she's always ready to give me advice whenever I need it. I'm <strong class=\"key-phrase\">forever grateful</strong> to have had Mrs Linh in my life.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"thoroughness in teaching","context":"Her thoroughness in teaching is what first got me interested in English, even though I hadn't cared much for the subject before that."},{"en":"learn new vocabulary and grammar effortlessly","context":"We never felt bored in her classes — she'd tell us interesting stories about British and American culture, and she'd organise games so we could learn new vocabulary and grammar effortlessly instead of just memorising word lists."},{"en":"guided us through difficult questions","context":"She ran free extra classes after school for students who weren't quite up to standard, including me, and she patiently guided us through difficult questions and gave us loads of useful tips for the test."},{"en":"forever grateful","context":"I've graduated now and I'm studying in Hanoi, but I still visit her whenever I'm back home for the holidays, and she's always ready to give me advice whenever I need it. I'm forever grateful to have had Mrs Linh in my life."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a helpful person at work or school","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-007","_displayNo":"#108","outline":{"topic":"Mrs Linh","intro":"teacher from high school, huge influence, reason I got into university - Linh, English, form teacher","body":["thoroughness in teaching got me interested in English, even though I hadn't cared before","never bored in class - stories about British/American culture, games to learn vocab and grammar effortlessly","3 months before entrance exam: free extra classes, guided through difficult questions, useful tips","passed exam with almost perfect score - remember calling parents, relieved and proud"],"conclusion":"graduated, studying in Hanoi, still visit on holidays, forever grateful to have had Mrs Linh"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-007.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A helpful person I remember is Mrs Linh, my high school English teacher. She taught me for three years and was also responsible for our class. She was in her late forties, but she looked younger.\n\nBefore meeting her, I was not very interested in English. However, she made lessons enjoyable by telling us about British and American culture. We also played games to practise vocabulary and grammar, so learning did not feel like simply remembering lists.\n\nShe helped me most during the three months before the university entrance exam. I was struggling with some questions, so she invited me to free extra classes after school. She explained difficult points patiently and taught us useful exam techniques. Thanks to her support, I passed with an almost perfect English score. I was so relieved that I called my parents straight away. I now study in Hanoi, but I still visit her during holidays. I will always be grateful for the time and care she gave me.","html":"A helpful person I remember is Mrs Linh, my high school English teacher. She taught me for three years and was also responsible for our class. She was in her late forties, but she looked younger.\n\nBefore meeting her, I was not very interested in English. However, she made lessons enjoyable by telling us about British and American culture. We also played games to practise vocabulary and grammar, so learning did not feel like simply remembering lists.\n\nShe helped me most during the three months before the university entrance exam. I was struggling with some questions, so she invited me to free <strong class=\"key-phrase\">extra classes</strong> after school. She explained difficult points patiently and taught us useful exam techniques. Thanks to her support, I passed with an almost perfect English score. I was so relieved that I called my parents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">straight away</strong>. I now study in Hanoi, but I still visit her during holidays. I will always be grateful for the time and care she gave me.","phrases":[{"en":"extra classes","meaning":"Additional lessons outside the normal timetable."},{"en":"straight away","meaning":"Immediately, without waiting."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-007.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A leader or politician who you admire","prompt":"Describe a leader or politician who you admire.\nYou should say:\nWho is he/ she?\nWhat is so appealing about him?\nWhat type of leader is he/ she\nAnd explain why do you admire him /her.","answer":"I'd like to talk about Mr David Cameron, the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">former Prime Minister</strong> of the UK, and I honestly think he's one of the most professional people I've ever come across.\n\nTo <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get the ball rolling</strong>, I should probably explain how I actually met him. It was about five years ago, more or less, when I travelled to England and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had a chance to meet</strong> him in front of Number 10 Downing Street — I was there as a tourist, and he happened to walk past and stopped to shake a few hands.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">On the subject of his appearance</strong>, I have to admit I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">was struck by</strong> how sharp and presentable he looked the moment I saw him. He came across as so charismatic and confident that everyone around him seemed to want to listen, even when he was debating political issues. What stood out even more, though, was how calm he stayed in every situation — it always felt like he'd <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weighed up the pros and cons</strong> before making any decision, rather than jumping to conclusions.\n\nThere are a couple of reasons I really admire him. For one, he led his party in a pretty democratic way — he'd listen to everyone's opinions and comment fairly on them rather than shutting people down. He also played a real part in improving things like healthcare and social welfare while he was in office.\n\nFor all these reasons, I'd say David Cameron is the most honourable leader I've ever met, and if I ever get the chance, I'd love to go back to the UK and meet him again.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"former Prime Minister","context":"I'd like to talk about Mr David Cameron, the former Prime Minister of the UK, and I honestly think he's one of the most professional people I've ever come across."},{"en":"get the ball rolling","context":"To get the ball rolling, I should probably explain how I actually met him."},{"en":"had a chance to meet","context":"It was about five years ago, more or less, when I travelled to England and had a chance to meet him in front of Number 10 Downing Street — I was there as a tourist, and he happened to walk past and stopped to shake a few hands."},{"en":"On the subject of his appearance","context":"On the subject of his appearance, I have to admit I was struck by how sharp and presentable he looked the moment I saw him."},{"en":"was struck by","context":"On the subject of his appearance, I have to admit I was struck by how sharp and presentable he looked the moment I saw him."},{"en":"weighed up the pros and cons","context":"What stood out even more, though, was how calm he stayed in every situation — it always felt like he'd weighed up the pros and cons before making any decision, rather than jumping to conclusions."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a leader or politician who you admire","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-008","_displayNo":"#109","outline":{"topic":"Mr David Cameron","intro":"David Cameron, former Prime Minister of the UK, one of the most professional people I've met","body":["how I met him - about 5 years ago, tourist in England, had a chance to meet him at Number 10","on his appearance - struck by how sharp and presentable, charismatic and confident, everyone wanted to listen","stayed calm in every situation, always seemed to have weighed up the pros and cons first","why I admire him - led party democratically, listened to everyone, improved healthcare and social welfare"],"conclusion":"most honourable leader I've ever met, would love to go back to the UK and meet him again"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-008.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A leader I admire is David Cameron, a former Prime Minister of the United Kingdom. I met him briefly during a trip to England about five years ago. I was visiting Number 10 Downing Street when he walked past and stopped to shake hands with some people.\n\nMy first impression was that he looked professional and confident. He also seemed calm, even when discussing difficult political questions. From what I saw and read about him, I felt that he considered different choices carefully before making decisions.\n\nAnother reason I admired him was his approach to working with other people. He appeared willing to listen to different opinions instead of immediately refusing them. I also appreciated his interest in public services such as healthcare and support for people in need. Although our meeting was short, it was memorable because I had never met such a well-known politician before. I would enjoy visiting the UK again and learning more about its political life.","html":"A leader I admire is David Cameron, a former Prime Minister of the United Kingdom. I met him briefly during a trip to England about five years ago. I was visiting Number 10 Downing Street when he walked past and stopped to shake hands with some people.\n\nMy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">first impression</strong> was that he looked professional and confident. He also seemed calm, even when discussing difficult political questions. From what I saw and read about him, I felt that he considered different choices carefully before making decisions.\n\nAnother reason I admired him was his approach to working with other people. He appeared willing to listen to different opinions instead of immediately refusing them. I also appreciated his interest in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">public services</strong> such as healthcare and support for people in need. Although our meeting was short, it was memorable because I had never met such a well-known politician before. I would enjoy visiting the UK again and learning more about its political life.","phrases":[{"en":"first impression","meaning":"The opinion you form when you first meet someone."},{"en":"public services","meaning":"Services provided for people, often by the government."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-008.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A new friend you have made recently","prompt":"Describe a new friend you`ve made recently\nYou should say:\nWho the person is\nWhat kind of person he/she is\nHow you met this person\nAnd explain why you made friends with him/her","answer":"I'm going to talk about Mrs Kim, a lovely lady I've recently become friends with.\n\nI started college three months ago, and on my very first day I couldn't find my way to the university at all. Google Maps wasn't much help, and asking the locals didn't get me very far either. So, after <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wandering around</strong> for what felt like hours, I finally <strong class=\"key-phrase\">resorted to asking</strong> a lady who was selling noodles from a food cart near where I was standing. I asked her if she knew the way to the HCMC University of Education, and she just smiled and pointed at the huge five-storey building right across the road — turns out I'd been standing there the whole time! After thanking her, we ended up chatting for a bit, and I found out she's also from Ninh Thuan, which is my hometown too. We hit it off straight away, and now her food cart is my favourite lunch spot whenever I'm at university.\n\nI think the main reason Mrs Kim and I get on so well is that she's just a genuinely sweet person. The more I've got to know her, the more I've realised she's had a pretty tough life, yet she's managed to hold on to a warm, gentle personality that plenty of other people would have lost after going through what she has. I guess the other reason we click is that we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">share the same hometown</strong>, and I tend to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get on well</strong> with people from back home. Sometimes I think the best friendships are the ones you never see coming.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"wandering around","context":"So, after wandering around for what felt like hours, I finally resorted to asking a lady who was selling noodles from a food cart near where I was standing."},{"en":"resorted to asking","context":"So, after wandering around for what felt like hours, I finally resorted to asking a lady who was selling noodles from a food cart near where I was standing."},{"en":"share the same hometown","context":"I guess the other reason we click is that we share the same hometown, and I tend to get on well with people from back home."},{"en":"get on well","context":"I guess the other reason we click is that we share the same hometown, and I tend to get on well with people from back home."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a new friend you`ve made recently","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-009","_displayNo":"#110","outline":{"topic":"Mrs Kim","intro":"Mrs Kim, a lovely lady I've recently become friends with","body":["started college 3 months ago, first day couldn't find the way, Google Maps not much help","wandering around, resorted to asking a lady selling noodles, pointed at the building across the road","chatted, found out she's from Ninh Thuan too, hit it off, food cart now my favourite lunch spot","main reason we get on so well - genuinely sweet, tough life but stayed warm and gentle"],"conclusion":"also share the same hometown, best friendships are the ones you never see coming"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-009.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A new friend I have made is Mrs Kim, who sells noodles near my university. We met on my first day of college about three months ago. I was looking for the HCMC University of Education, but I could not find it, even with Google Maps.\n\nI finally asked Mrs Kim at her food cart. She smiled and pointed to the large building across the road. I felt embarrassed because I had been so close all along. We started talking, and I discovered that she came from Ninh Thuan, my hometown too. Now I often eat lunch at her cart.\n\nI enjoy talking to her because she is warm and friendly. She has had a difficult life, but she still treats people kindly. Coming from the same place also gives us plenty to talk about. Our friendship was unexpected, and that makes it special to me. A stressful first morning at university ended with meeting someone who now helps me feel more at home.","html":"A new friend I have made is Mrs Kim, who sells noodles near my university. We met on my first day of college about three months ago. I was looking for the HCMC University of Education, but I could not find it, even with Google Maps.\n\nI finally asked Mrs Kim at her food cart. She smiled and pointed to the large building across the road. I felt embarrassed because I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had been so close all along</strong>. We started talking, and I discovered that she came from Ninh Thuan, my hometown too. Now I often eat lunch at her cart.\n\nI enjoy talking to her because she is warm and friendly. She has had a difficult life, but she still treats people kindly. Coming from the same place also gives us plenty to talk about. Our friendship was unexpected, and that makes it special to me. A stressful first morning at university ended with meeting someone who now helps me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel more at home</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"had been so close all along","meaning":"Had been near something for the whole time."},{"en":"feel more at home","meaning":"Feel more comfortable and relaxed in a place."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-009.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A person who does well in work","prompt":"Describe a person who does well in work\nYou should say:\nWho the person is\nHow do you know this person\nWhat kinds of work this person does\nAnd explain why this person can do well","answer":"Recently I took a job as a maths teacher at an international school, and when I think of someone who genuinely does well at work, the first person who comes to mind is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the head of the Maths department</strong> at my school, Mr Lawrence, or Laurie, as everyone calls him. I got to know him properly one lunchtime <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the school cafeteria</strong>, over coffee, when we ended up talking about his role as head of Maths.\n\nLaurie told me he's been a maths teacher since 1985, so that's over thirty-five years <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the trade</strong>. He clearly loves the job — he joked that he'd happily keep teaching maths for another five hundred years if old age didn't get in the way first. To be honest, I was quite <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taken aback by</strong> that comment, because I usually feel something between admiration and sympathy for teachers who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">commit almost every aspect of their life to their work</strong>. Admiration for their dedication to their students; sympathy for how much they've had to give up along the way.\n\nWhat actually convinced me he does well at work, though, wasn't just what he said — it was watching him with a group of students who were struggling with algebra before their final exams. He didn't just repeat the same explanation louder; he found three different ways to explain the same idea until it clicked, and by the end of the session, every one of them looked relieved rather than lost.\n\nIt's precisely this quality that explains why Laurie's so good at his job. His work and his life are intimately linked, and if Laurie wants to lead a good life, that's almost synonymous with being a good teacher.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"the head of the Maths department","context":"Recently I took a job as a maths teacher at an international school, and when I think of someone who genuinely does well at work, the first person who comes to mind is the head of the Maths department at my school, Mr Lawrence, or Laurie, as everyone calls him."},{"en":"in the school cafeteria","context":"I got to know him properly one lunchtime in the school cafeteria, over coffee, when we ended up talking about his role as head of Maths."},{"en":"in the trade","context":"Laurie told me he's been a maths teacher since 1985, so that's over thirty-five years in the trade."},{"en":"taken aback by","context":"To be honest, I was quite taken aback by that comment, because I usually feel something between admiration and sympathy for teachers who commit almost every aspect of their life to their work."},{"en":"commit almost every aspect of their life to their work","context":"To be honest, I was quite taken aback by that comment, because I usually feel something between admiration and sympathy for teachers who commit almost every aspect of their life to their work."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a person who does well in work","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-010","_displayNo":"#111","outline":{"topic":"Mr Lawrence (Laurie)","intro":"recently took job as maths teacher, head of Maths dept Mr Lawrence/Laurie comes to mind","body":["got to know him over coffee in the school cafeteria, talking about his role","maths teacher since 1985, over 35 years in the trade, loves the job, joked about 500 more years","taken aback - feel admiration and sympathy for teachers who commit their whole life to work","convinced by watching him help students struggling with algebra using three different explanations"],"conclusion":"explains why Laurie's so good at his job - work and life intimately linked"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-010.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Someone who does well at work is Mr Lawrence, the head of the Maths department at the international school where I recently started teaching. Everyone calls him Laurie. I got to know him during a conversation over coffee in the school cafeteria.\n\nHe has taught maths since 1985 and clearly loves his job. He once joked that he would continue for another five hundred years if he could. I was surprised by how much he enjoyed teaching after so many years.\n\nWhat impressed me most was watching him help students with algebra before their final exams. When they did not understand, he did not simply repeat himself. He explained the same idea in three different ways until everyone understood it. By the end, the students looked much more confident. I think this patience is why he is such a good teacher. He cares about whether students learn, rather than just finishing a lesson. As a new teacher, I can learn a lot from him.","html":"Someone who does well at work is Mr Lawrence, the head of the Maths department at the international school where I recently started teaching. Everyone calls him Laurie. I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">got to know him</strong> during a conversation over coffee in the school cafeteria.\n\nHe has taught maths since 1985 and clearly loves his job. He once joked that he would continue for another five hundred years if he could. I was surprised by how much he enjoyed teaching after so many years.\n\nWhat impressed me most was watching him help students with algebra before their final exams. When they did not understand, he did not simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">repeat himself</strong>. He explained the same idea in three different ways until everyone understood it. By the end, the students looked much more confident. I think this patience is why he is such a good teacher. He cares about whether students learn, rather than just finishing a lesson. As a new teacher, I can learn a lot from him.","phrases":[{"en":"got to know him","meaning":"Learned more about him by spending time together."},{"en":"repeat himself","meaning":"Say the same thing again."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-010.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A person who gave a clever solution to a problem","prompt":"Describe a person who gave a clever solution to a problem\nYou should say:\nWho the person is\nWhen and where it happened\nWhat the problem was\nAnd explain why it was a clever solution","answer":"There's a phrase that always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comes to mind</strong> whenever a tricky problem crops up: you need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">think outside of the box</strong>. Not everyone manages to find a solution, but there's one person who never fails to surprise me with his cleverness, and how he can always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">come up with a solution to</strong> even <strong class=\"key-phrase\">some of the toughest problems</strong>. His name's Nam, and he's my older brother.\n\nMy mother always says that even though we're brothers, we're about as different as two people can be. I'm quite an active, impulsive person and I tend to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make very quick decisions</strong>, while my brother's extremely calm and usually solves problems in a level-headed way — which is exactly why he's been a kind of saviour to me on more than one occasion.\n\nThe clearest example was the day of my university entrance exam, which I still think of as a huge landmark in my life. Like my mother, I suffer from hypotension, which is low blood pressure, and it sometimes makes me dizzy if I'm hungry or overtired. Normally a couple of sweets are enough to sort it out, but I'm quite absent-minded, so I usually forget to carry any with me.\n\nThat morning, as the exam got closer, the nerves and the fact I hadn't eaten properly must have caught up with me — I felt light-headed and my hands started to shake. Then, out of nowhere, I found a couple of tiny chocolate bars in my pocket, and they genuinely saved me. It turned out my brother had slipped them in there without a word, just in case. He was so thoughtful, and honestly, so smart to think of it.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"think outside of the box","context":"There's a phrase that always comes to mind whenever a tricky problem crops up: you need to think outside of the box."},{"en":"comes to mind","context":"There's a phrase that always comes to mind whenever a tricky problem crops up: you need to think outside of the box."},{"en":"come up with a solution to","context":"Not everyone manages to find a solution, but there's one person who never fails to surprise me with his cleverness, and how he can always come up with a solution to even some of the toughest problems."},{"en":"some of the toughest problems","context":"Not everyone manages to find a solution, but there's one person who never fails to surprise me with his cleverness, and how he can always come up with a solution to even some of the toughest problems."},{"en":"make very quick decisions","context":"I'm quite an active, impulsive person and I tend to make very quick decisions, while my brother's extremely calm and usually solves problems in a level-headed way — which is exactly why he's been a kind of saviour to me on more than one occasion."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a person who gave a clever solution to a problem","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-011","_displayNo":"#112","outline":{"topic":"Nam","intro":"'think outside of the box' comes to mind - one person, my older brother Nam","body":["mother says we're as different as two people can be - I'm impulsive/quick decisions, he's calm/level-headed","clearest example: day of my university entrance exam, biggest landmark - I suffer from hypotension","normally sweets fix it, but I'm absent-minded and usually forget to carry any","that morning felt light-headed, hands shaking, found chocolate bars - brother had slipped them in my pocket"],"conclusion":"so thoughtful and smart of him to think of it"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-011.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A person who found a clever solution to a problem is my older brother, Nam. He is calm and careful, while I often act quickly without thinking. Because of this, he has helped me out several times.\n\nOne example happened on the day of my university entrance exam. Like my mother, I have low blood pressure. If I am hungry or very tired, I sometimes feel dizzy. I usually carry sweets to help with this, but I often forget them. That morning, I was nervous and had not eaten properly. Just before the exam, I started feeling weak and my hands were shaking.\n\nThen I found two small chocolate bars in my pocket. My brother had put them there without telling me, in case I needed them. I ate them and felt better. I was grateful because he had thought about a problem before it happened. His simple action helped me feel ready for a very important exam and showed how much he cared.","html":"A person who found a clever solution to a problem is my older brother, Nam. He is calm and careful, while I often act quickly without thinking. Because of this, he has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">helped me out</strong> several times.\n\nOne example happened on the day of my university entrance exam. Like my mother, I have low blood pressure. If I am hungry or very tired, I sometimes feel dizzy. I usually carry sweets to help with this, but I often forget them. That morning, I was nervous and had not eaten properly. Just before the exam, I started feeling weak and my hands were shaking.\n\nThen I found two small chocolate bars in my pocket. My brother had put them there without telling me, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in case</strong> I needed them. I ate them and felt better. I was grateful because he had thought about a problem before it happened. His simple action helped me feel ready for a very important exam and showed how much he cared.","phrases":[{"en":"helped me out","meaning":"Gave me help when I needed it."},{"en":"in case","meaning":"Because something might happen and you want to be prepared."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-011.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A person who has apologised to you","prompt":"Describe a person who has apologised to you\nYou should say:\nwhen it was\nwho the person was\nwhy this person apologised\nand explain how you felt about it.","answer":"I think we all make mistakes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">now and then</strong> in life, sometimes on purpose and sometimes completely by accident, but we usually end up regretting them afterwards. I always try to apologise when I've done something wrong, and naturally, I expect the same from other people.\n\nSo, last month, my friend Duy borrowed my car for the day. He knows perfectly well how possessive I am about it, so I asked him to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take good care of it</strong>, especially since he's not the greatest driver — it's actually the reason his own dad won't buy him a car of his own.\n\nHe promised he'd drive carefully, but when he brought it back that evening he looked really down. He told me someone had hit the car from behind while he was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuck in traffic</strong>, which pushed him straight into the car in front. He <strong class=\"key-phrase\">felt terrible about it</strong> and apologised over and over, and he promised he'd get it fixed first thing the next morning.\n\nI did feel sorry for him, though I was also pretty gutted about the damage, but somewhere in the back of my mind I knew it wasn't really his fault. I usually lose my temper fairly easily, but his apology was so sincere that I just couldn't stay angry with him for long.\n\nThe next day, he came with me to the mechanic, and luckily the damage wasn't too bad or expensive to fix. He apologised again and promised he'd be more careful in future. We're still good friends now, and honestly, I think receiving an apology and being willing to forgive someone is one of the best ways to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep a friendship strong</strong>.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"now and then","context":"I think we all make mistakes now and then in life, sometimes on purpose and sometimes completely by accident, but we usually end up regretting them afterwards."},{"en":"take good care of it","context":"He knows perfectly well how possessive I am about it, so I asked him to take good care of it, especially since he's not the greatest driver — it's actually the reason his own dad won't buy him a car of his own."},{"en":"stuck in traffic","context":"He told me someone had hit the car from behind while he was stuck in traffic, which pushed him straight into the car in front."},{"en":"felt terrible about it","context":"He felt terrible about it and apologised over and over, and he promised he'd get it fixed first thing the next morning."},{"en":"keep a friendship strong","context":"We're still good friends now, and honestly, I think receiving an apology and being willing to forgive someone is one of the best ways to keep a friendship strong."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a person who has apologised to you","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-012","_displayNo":"#113","outline":{"topic":"my friend Duy","intro":"we all make mistakes now and then, I always try to apologise and expect the same","body":["last month friend Duy borrowed my car, asked him to take good care of it, not the best driver","promised to drive carefully, brought it back looking down - hit from behind while stuck in traffic","felt terrible, apologised over and over, promised to get it fixed","felt sorry but gutted about damage, knew it wasn't his fault, sincere apology so couldn't stay angry"],"conclusion":"next day to the mechanic, damage not too bad, still good friends, apology keeps a friendship strong"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-012.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Someone who apologised to me was my friend Duy. Last month, he borrowed my car for a day. I care a lot about my car, so I asked him to drive carefully. He promised that he would.\n\nWhen he returned that evening, he looked upset. He explained that another driver had hit the back of my car in traffic. This pushed it into the vehicle in front. He said sorry several times and offered to take it to a mechanic the next morning.\n\nAt first, I felt unhappy about the damage. However, I understood that Duy had not caused the accident, and his apology was clearly honest. The next day, he came with me to get the car repaired. Luckily, the damage was not serious or very expensive. I accepted his apology, and we stayed friends. This experience reminded me that accidents can happen even when someone tries to be careful. Being honest and helping to solve the problem matter more than simply getting angry.","html":"Someone who apologised to me was my friend Duy. Last month, he borrowed my car for a day. I care a lot about my car, so I asked him to drive carefully. He promised that he would.\n\nWhen he returned that evening, he looked upset. He explained that another driver had hit the back of my car in traffic. This pushed it into the vehicle in front. He said sorry several times and offered to take it to a mechanic the next morning.\n\nAt first, I felt unhappy about the damage. However, I understood that Duy had not caused the accident, and his apology was clearly honest. The next day, he came with me to get the car repaired. Luckily, the damage was not serious or very expensive. I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accepted his apology</strong>, and we stayed friends. This experience reminded me that accidents can happen even when someone tries to be careful. Being honest and helping to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">solve the problem</strong> matter more than simply getting angry.","phrases":[{"en":"accepted his apology","meaning":"Agreed to forgive him after he said sorry."},{"en":"solve the problem","meaning":"Find a way to deal with something that has gone wrong."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-012.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A person who knows a lot","prompt":"Describe a person who knows a lot/is knowledgeable.\nYou should say:\n- Who the person is;\n- What the person does;\n- What kinds of things he knows;\n- What you learn from him/her.","answer":"I'd like to tell you about my grandmother, who is easily the most knowledgeable person I know. She's in her late seventies now, and she spent most of her working life as a pharmacist, but what strikes me is how much she knows well beyond her own field.\n\nShe reads constantly — newspapers in the morning, then whatever book she's currently working through, and she still does the crossword every single day without fail. Ask her about history, plants, traditional medicine or even current politics, and she'll usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have a well-informed opinion</strong> rather than just a vague guess.\n\nWhat I find most impressive is that she never shows off about it. She only brings up what she knows when it's actually useful, like when my mother was ill and my grandmother could explain exactly what the doctor's diagnosis meant and which questions we should ask.\n\nI've learned a huge amount from her over the years, mostly about staying curious. She still says that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the day you stop learning</strong> is the day you start getting old, and she genuinely lives by that. Whenever I visit, she'll have some new fact or story ready, usually something she picked up from a book or a documentary, and she always explains it in a way that's easy to follow rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">talking down to you</strong>.\n\nHonestly, she's the reason I still try to read something new every week myself. Knowledge like hers doesn't come from one qualification; it comes from decades of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuine curiosity about the world</strong>.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"have a well-informed opinion","context":"Ask her about history, plants, traditional medicine or even current politics, and she'll usually have a well-informed opinion rather than just a vague guess."},{"en":"the day you stop learning","context":"She still says that the day you stop learning is the day you start getting old, and she genuinely lives by that."},{"en":"talking down to you","context":"Whenever I visit, she'll have some new fact or story ready, usually something she picked up from a book or a documentary, and she always explains it in a way that's easy to follow rather than talking down to you."},{"en":"genuine curiosity about the world","context":"Honestly, she's the reason I still try to read something new every week myself. Knowledge like hers doesn't come from one qualification; it comes from decades of genuine curiosity about the world."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a person who knows a lot/is knowledgeable","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-013","_displayNo":"#114","outline":{"topic":"grandmother","intro":"grandmother late 70s most knowledgeable person I know, former pharmacist","body":["reads constantly newspapers books crossword daily, knows history plants medicine politics","never shows off, only shares knowledge when useful e.g. explaining a diagnosis","taught me to stay curious - day you stop learning is day you get old","explains things simply, never talks down to you"],"conclusion":"she's why I still try to read something new every week myself"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-013.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A person who knows a lot is my grandmother. She is in her late seventies and used to work as a pharmacist. However, her knowledge is not limited to medicine. She can also talk about history, plants and things happening in the world.\n\nShe reads the newspaper every morning and always has a book nearby. She also does a crossword every day. What I like is that she does not try to impress people with difficult words. She explains things simply when her knowledge can help. For example, when my mother was ill, she helped us understand the doctor's explanation and think of useful questions to ask.\n\nMy grandmother has taught me to stay curious. Whenever I visit, she shares something new from a book or documentary. She believes people should continue learning at every age. Her example encourages me to read something new each week. I admire her because she shows that learning can be a lifelong habit, rather than something that ends after school.","html":"A person who knows a lot is my grandmother. She is in her late seventies and used to work as a pharmacist. However, her knowledge is not limited to medicine. She can also talk about history, plants and things happening in the world.\n\nShe reads the newspaper every morning and always has a book nearby. She also does a crossword every day. What I like is that she does not try to impress people with difficult words. She explains things simply when her knowledge can help. For example, when my mother was ill, she helped us understand the doctor's explanation and think of useful questions to ask.\n\nMy grandmother has taught me to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay curious</strong>. Whenever I visit, she shares something new from a book or documentary. She believes people should continue learning at every age. Her example encourages me to read something new each week. I admire her because she shows that learning can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a lifelong habit</strong>, rather than something that ends after school.","phrases":[{"en":"stay curious","meaning":"Keep wanting to learn and understand new things."},{"en":"a lifelong habit","meaning":"Something you do regularly throughout your life."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-013.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A person you know","prompt":"Describe a person you know\nYou should say:\n- Who this person is.\n- How long you have known each other.\n- How you got to know him/her.\n- How you feel about this person.","answer":"I'm going to talk about a friend I met in college, who goes by the name Moon.\n\nI met her during our freshman orientation, and I honestly think I was really lucky to have been introduced to her. She's a Chinese student who pretty much everybody adores. I've known her for three years now, and in all that time, I don't think I've ever once been bored by her company. She's always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">energetic and fun to be with</strong>. We had loads of classes together in our first year, and she'd often come round to help me with my homework at the weekends. I got to know her properly through revising for exams together and hanging out after class.\n\nAs cheerful as she seemed on the surface, she was actually under a lot of pressure from her parents about getting good grades and getting into a decent graduate school. I really admire the way she always managed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay positive despite all the stress</strong> she was under — she never let it show in how she treated the people around her.\n\nNow that we're in our final year, I don't see her nearly as often. But <strong class=\"key-phrase\">once in a while</strong> she'll send me a message just to check how I'm getting on with my schoolwork, and I really appreciate that about her. Either way, I know I'll always cherish the time we spent together, and whether or not we manage to stay in each other's lives after this, I'll always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look back on</strong> our college years with real fondness.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"energetic and fun to be with","context":"She's always energetic and fun to be with."},{"en":"stay positive despite all the stress","context":"I really admire the way she always managed to stay positive despite all the stress she was under — she never let it show in how she treated the people around her."},{"en":"once in a while","context":"But once in a while she'll send me a message just to check how I'm getting on with my schoolwork, and I really appreciate that about her."},{"en":"look back on","context":"Either way, I know I'll always cherish the time we spent together, and whether or not we manage to stay in each other's lives after this, I'll always look back on our college years with real fondness."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a person you know","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-014","_displayNo":"#115","outline":{"topic":"Moon","intro":"friend met in college, goes by the name Moon, met during freshman orientation","body":["Chinese student everybody adores, known her 3 years, always energetic and fun to be with","loads of classes together, she'd help with homework at weekends, got closer through revising and hangouts","cheerful on surface but under a lot of parental pressure, admire how she stayed positive despite stress","final year now, don't see her as often, once in a while she messages to check in"],"conclusion":"I'll always look back on our college years with real fondness"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-014.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"I'd like to describe my friend Moon, a Chinese student I met during the welcome activities at college. We have known each other for three years. During our first year, we shared many classes and often studied together before exams.\n\nMoon is energetic and enjoyable to be around. She sometimes came to my home at weekends to help with homework. We also spent time together after class, and I never found her company boring. Although she seemed cheerful, her parents expected very high grades and wanted her to enter a good graduate school. This put her under a lot of pressure.\n\nI admired the way she stayed positive and continued treating others kindly despite her worries. We are now in our final year, so we do not meet as often. However, she still messages me to ask how my studies are going. I appreciate these small signs of care. Whatever happens after college, I will remember our friendship and the happy time we spent learning together.","html":"I'd like to describe my friend Moon, a Chinese student I met during the welcome activities at college. We have known each other for three years. During our first year, we shared many classes and often studied together before exams.\n\nMoon is energetic and enjoyable to be around. She sometimes came to my home at weekends to help with homework. We also spent time together after class, and I never found her company boring. Although she seemed cheerful, her parents expected very high grades and wanted her to enter a good graduate school. This put her <strong class=\"key-phrase\">under a lot of pressure</strong>.\n\nI admired the way she stayed positive and continued treating others kindly despite her worries. We are now in our final year, so we do not meet as often. However, she still messages me to ask how my studies are going. I appreciate these small <strong class=\"key-phrase\">signs of care</strong>. Whatever happens after college, I will remember our friendship and the happy time we spent learning together.","phrases":[{"en":"under a lot of pressure","meaning":"Feeling strong expectations or demands to do well."},{"en":"signs of care","meaning":"Small actions that show someone is interested in your well-being."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-014.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A person you know who is beautiful or handsome","prompt":"Describe a person you know who is beautiful or handsome.\nYou should say:\nWho the person is\nWhen you met the person at the first time\nWhat the person's character\nAnd explain how you feel about the person.","answer":"I'd like to tell you about my older brother, who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">used to be a role model for me</strong> <strong class=\"key-phrase\">when I was growing up</strong>. He's eight years older than me, and we've gone down completely different paths. I can't really say when I first met him, since he's my brother — but I remember the moment I first started looking up to him. I was about seven, and I watched him walk into an argument between two much bigger boys and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">talk his way out of it</strong> without throwing a single punch. That's the moment I decided I wanted to be like him.\n\nHe's tall, strong and ridiculously confident, and he looked a bit like Superman to me when I was small. At school he wasn't particularly academic — maths and physics just weren't his thing — but he was brilliant at every sport he tried, which was the complete opposite of me. I was small and skinny, but I could pick up new ideas and solve problems a bit faster than most of my classmates. Still, it was always my brother that people noticed first.\n\nHe used to run around with a group of older boys, and yes, they got into a fair few fights, though they never caused any real trouble in our <strong class=\"key-phrase\">local community</strong>. My dad, who's a secondary school teacher, hated it and was constantly punishing him, hoping he'd turn out a bit more like me, without realising I secretly admired every bit of it.\n\nHe didn't really change until he got badly hurt in a fight, and that seemed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">knock some sense into him</strong>. He's got his own family now, a wife and a son, and if you met him today you'd never guess he used to be that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reckless teenager</strong>. I still look up to him, for completely different reasons now — his <strong class=\"key-phrase\">calmness under pressure</strong>, and the steady, dependable man he's grown into.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"used to be a role model for me","context":"I'd like to tell you about my older brother, who used to be a role model for me when I was growing up."},{"en":"when I was growing up","context":"I'd like to tell you about my older brother, who used to be a role model for me when I was growing up."},{"en":"talk his way out of it","context":"I was about seven, and I watched him walk into an argument between two much bigger boys and talk his way out of it without throwing a single punch."},{"en":"local community","context":"He used to run around with a group of older boys, and yes, they got into a fair few fights, though they never caused any real trouble in our local community."},{"en":"knock some sense into him","context":"He didn't really change until he got badly hurt in a fight, and that seemed to knock some sense into him."},{"en":"reckless teenager","context":"He's got his own family now, a wife and a son, and if you met him today you'd never guess he used to be that reckless teenager."},{"en":"calmness under pressure","context":"I still look up to him, for completely different reasons now — his calmness under pressure, and the steady, dependable man he's grown into."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a person you know who is beautiful or handsome","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-015","_displayNo":"#116","outline":{"topic":"my older brother","intro":"older brother, used to be a role model growing up; can't say exactly 'when I met him' (he's my brother) so describe the moment I started looking up to him instead","body":["tall, strong, confident, not academic but brilliant at sport - opposite of me","ran with older boys, got into fights, never caused real trouble locally; dad (a teacher) disapproved","didn't change until badly hurt in a fight - knocked some sense into him"],"conclusion":"now has his own family, still look up to him but for his calmness and steadiness now, not the old reckless side"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-015.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A person I think is handsome is my older brother. He is eight years older than me, tall and strong, and he has always seemed very confident. When I was small, I wanted to be like him.\n\nI remember seeing him deal with an argument between two bigger boys when I was about seven. He managed to end it by talking, without fighting. At school, he was good at sport, while I was better at subjects such as maths. However, he also spent time with some older boys and sometimes got into fights. My father, a secondary school teacher, worried about his behaviour.\n\nAfter being badly hurt in one fight, my brother became more careful. He now has a wife and a son, and he is much calmer and more responsible. I still admire his appearance, but his personality matters more to me now. He is someone our family can depend on. I am pleased that he has grown into a person who handles pressure sensibly.","html":"A person I think is handsome is my older brother. He is eight years older than me, tall and strong, and he has always seemed very confident. When I was small, I wanted to be like him.\n\nI remember seeing him deal with an argument between two bigger boys when I was about seven. He managed to end it by talking, without fighting. At school, he was good at sport, while I was better at subjects such as maths. However, he also spent time with some older boys and sometimes got into fights. My father, a secondary school teacher, worried about his behaviour.\n\nAfter being badly hurt in one fight, my brother became more careful. He now has a wife and a son, and he is much calmer and more responsible. I still admire his appearance, but his personality matters more to me now. He is someone our family can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depend on</strong>. I am pleased that he has grown into a person who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">handles pressure sensibly</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"depend on","meaning":"Trust someone to help or do what is needed."},{"en":"handles pressure sensibly","meaning":"Deals with difficult situations in a calm and reasonable way."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-015.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A polite person you met","prompt":"Describe a polite person you met\nYou should say:\nWho the person is\nHow you know the person\nWhat the person looks like\nAnd explain why you think the person is polite.","answer":"Well, the person I'd like to talk about is a very good friend of mine, Mai. She has to be one of the nicest and most polite people I know. We've basically known each other since forever, because we were in the same class from high school right through to college, and we've stayed close ever since.\n\nIn terms of what she looks like, Mai's quite tall, with straight brown hair and these deep, dark brown eyes, and she's honestly pretty gorgeous — she gets a fair bit of attention wherever we go. She's always dressed in whatever's trendiest, too, though none of that ever seems to go to her head, which says a lot about her <strong class=\"key-phrase\">down-to-earth personality</strong>.\n\nThe first time I met her, she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">left a deep impression on me</strong>, because on the first day of school I got completely lost trying to find my classroom, and she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">went out of her way to help me</strong>. As luck would have it, the teacher then sat us together as desk-mates, and that was that.\n\nThe reason I think she's so polite is simply that she's got really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">good manners</strong>, and it never feels put on. I've noticed she always thanks the security guard at our building by name, and she'll let older people go through a door first even when she's in a rush herself. What really struck me, though, was watching her deal with a rude customer once, back when she had a part-time job at a cafe — she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stayed calm and polite</strong> the whole time, even though the woman was being pretty unreasonable. And whenever classmates used to ask her for help with homework, she'd <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never once complain</strong> or make them feel like a bother.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"down-to-earth personality","context":"She's always dressed in whatever's trendiest, too, though none of that ever seems to go to her head, which says a lot about her down-to-earth personality."},{"en":"left a deep impression on me","context":"The first time I met her, she left a deep impression on me, because on the first day of school I got completely lost trying to find my classroom, and she went out of her way to help me."},{"en":"went out of her way to help me","context":"The first time I met her, she left a deep impression on me, because on the first day of school I got completely lost trying to find my classroom, and she went out of her way to help me."},{"en":"good manners","context":"The reason I think she's so polite is simply that she's got really good manners, and it never feels put on."},{"en":"stayed calm and polite","context":"What really struck me, though, was watching her deal with a rude customer once, back when she had a part-time job at a cafe — she stayed calm and polite the whole time, even though the woman was being pretty unreasonable."},{"en":"never once complain","context":"And whenever classmates used to ask her for help with homework, she'd never once complain or make them feel like a bother."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a polite person you met","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-016","_displayNo":"#117","outline":{"topic":"Mai","intro":"good friend Mai, one of the nicest and most polite people I know, known since high school","body":["what she looks like: tall, straight brown hair, dark brown eyes, gorgeous, dresses in trendy fashion, gets attention but stays down-to-earth about it","how met: first day of school, got lost, she helped, became desk-mates","why polite: good manners that feel genuine - thanks security guard by name, lets elders go first, stayed calm with a rude customer at her cafe job, never complains about helping classmates"],"conclusion":"manners feel genuine, not put on - that's why she stands out as a polite person"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-016.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A polite person I know is my friend Mai. We first met at high school and later attended the same college. On the first day of school, I could not find my classroom. She stopped to help me, and we were then asked to sit together.\n\nMai is tall, with straight brown hair and dark brown eyes. She dresses well, but she never acts as if she is better than other people. Her good manners seem natural. For example, she always thanks the security guard by name and lets older people go through a doorway first, even when she is busy.\n\nOne thing I remember clearly happened when she worked part time in a cafe. A customer was rude to her, but Mai stayed calm and spoke politely throughout the conversation. She also helped classmates with homework without making them feel uncomfortable. I admire her because she shows respect through everyday actions. Being around her reminds me to be more patient and considerate too.","html":"A polite person I know is my friend Mai. We first met at high school and later attended the same college. On the first day of school, I could not find my classroom. She stopped to help me, and we were then asked to sit together.\n\nMai is tall, with straight brown hair and dark brown eyes. She dresses well, but she never acts as if she is better than other people. Her <strong class=\"key-phrase\">good manners</strong> seem natural. For example, she always thanks the security guard by name and lets older people go through a doorway first, even when she is busy.\n\nOne thing I remember clearly happened when she worked part time in a cafe. A customer was rude to her, but Mai stayed calm and spoke politely throughout the conversation. She also helped classmates with homework without making them feel uncomfortable. I admire her because she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shows respect</strong> through everyday actions. Being around her reminds me to be more patient and considerate too.","phrases":[{"en":"good manners","meaning":"Polite and respectful ways of behaving."},{"en":"shows respect","meaning":"Treats someone as a person whose feelings and needs matter."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-016.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A sports person you admire","prompt":"Describe a sports person you admire.\nYou should say:\n- who this person is\n- what they have achieved\n- how you found out about them\n- and explain why you admire this person.","answer":"The sportsperson I most admire is the marathon runner Eliud Kipchoge, partly because of what he's achieved and partly because of how he talks about it.\n\nHe is a Kenyan distance runner who has won the Olympic marathon twice and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">held the world record</strong>, and in 2019 he became the first person to run the distance in under two hours, although that particular run was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">staged under special conditions</strong> and doesn't count officially.\n\nI first heard about him through a documentary a friend recommended, and what surprised me was how ordinary the training looked. There weren't any dramatic scenes. He lives in a simple training camp, shares the chores with the other runners, and he's followed almost exactly the same weekly routine for years.\n\nI admire him for two reasons. The first is the discipline, which isn't the exciting kind — it's the willingness to do <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the same unglamorous session</strong> on a wet Tuesday morning for a decade. The second is his attitude to limits. He's said repeatedly that the barrier is usually mental rather than physical, and unlike most people who say that, he actually demonstrated it in front of a stopwatch.\n\nWhat I find useful is that his approach transfers. Very few of us will run a marathon, but almost anything worth doing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rewards showing up consistently</strong> rather than working intensely for a fortnight.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"held the world record","context":"He is a Kenyan distance runner who has won the Olympic marathon twice and held the world record, and in 2019 he became the first person to run the distance in under two hours, although that particular run was staged under special conditions and doesn't count officially."},{"en":"staged under special conditions","context":"He is a Kenyan distance runner who has won the Olympic marathon twice and held the world record, and in 2019 he became the first person to run the distance in under two hours, although that particular run was staged under special conditions and doesn't count officially."},{"en":"the same unglamorous session","context":"The first is the discipline, which isn't the exciting kind — it's the willingness to do the same unglamorous session on a wet Tuesday morning for a decade."},{"en":"rewards showing up consistently","context":"What I find useful is that his approach transfers. Very few of us will run a marathon, but almost anything worth doing rewards showing up consistently rather than working intensely for a fortnight."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a sports person you admire","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-017","_displayNo":"#118","outline":{"topic":"Eliud Kipchoge","intro":"marathon runner Kipchoge admire what achieved + how he talks about it","body":["Kenyan distance runner won Olympic marathon twice held world record","2019 1st person under 2 hrs staged special conditions doesn't count officially","heard through documentary friend recommended surprised how ordinary training looked","simple training camp shares chores same weekly routine for years","discipline not exciting kind = same session wet Tuesday morning for a decade","attitude to limits barrier mental not physical demonstrated in front of stopwatch"],"conclusion":"approach transfers almost anything rewards showing up consistently"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-017.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A sportsperson I admire is Eliud Kipchoge, a marathon runner from Kenya. I first learned about him through a documentary recommended by a friend. He has won two Olympic marathon titles and has held the world record.\n\nIn 2019, he completed the marathon distance in less than two hours. That run used special conditions, so it was not an official world record, but I still found it impressive. What surprised me more was his ordinary daily life. He stays in a simple training camp, helps with chores and follows a regular training plan.\n\nI admire his discipline. He continues training even when the weather is unpleasant or the session is repetitive. He also believes that the way we think can affect what we achieve. His example is useful beyond sport. I am unlikely to run a marathon, but I can apply his attitude to studying or work. He shows that regular effort over a long time is often more helpful than working extremely hard for just a short period.","html":"A sportsperson I admire is Eliud Kipchoge, a marathon runner from Kenya. I first learned about him through a documentary recommended by a friend. He has won two Olympic marathon titles and has held the world record.\n\nIn 2019, he completed the marathon distance in less than two hours. That run used special conditions, so it was not an official world record, but I still found it impressive. What surprised me more was his ordinary daily life. He stays in a simple training camp, helps with chores and follows a regular training plan.\n\nI admire his discipline. He continues training even when the weather is unpleasant or the session is repetitive. He also believes that the way we think can affect what we achieve. His example is useful <strong class=\"key-phrase\">beyond sport</strong>. I am unlikely to run a marathon, but I can apply his attitude to studying or work. He shows that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regular effort</strong> over a long time is often more helpful than working extremely hard for just a short period.","phrases":[{"en":"regular effort","meaning":"Work that you keep doing steadily over time."},{"en":"beyond sport","meaning":"In other areas of life as well as in sport."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-017.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A visitor in your home","prompt":"Describe a visitor in your home\nYou should say:\n- Who this visitor was.\n- When he/she visited your home.\n- Why he/she came to visit.\n- Whether you liked him/her or not","answer":"Today I'd like to talk about one of the guests who visited my family and left a great impression on me. His name was Armane, a Frenchman and my brother's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">business partner</strong> for more than two years. I heard from my mother that my brother <strong class=\"key-phrase\">happened to meet</strong> him at a yoga and meditation class. He came to our house last week to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">say goodbye before returning to</strong> Paris.\n\nArmane had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a masculine look</strong>, with large blue eyes, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a wide forehead</strong> and a sharp, straight nose. Although he's European, he seemed to have a genuinely deep understanding of Vietnamese culture — the way he used chopsticks and rolled vegetables in rice paper told me he was well aware of our table manners. I still remember him insisting on trying every single dish at dinner, even the strongest fermented shrimp paste, just so he wouldn't seem rude — you could tell he was struggling not to pull a face! He even admitted that at first he'd found it pretty stressful trying to follow such a minefield of social do's and don'ts.\n\nWithin a week of the visit, Armane surprised us with a thank-you card he'd made himself, by hand, which really touched us all. My parents are usually pretty understanding when foreigners aren't familiar with Vietnamese customs, but they were genuinely impressed that Armane had picked up so many of our traditions. He <strong class=\"key-phrase\">earned our utmost respect</strong>, and I'd say he was easily one of the most likeable guests we've ever had to stay.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"business partner","context":"His name was Armane, a Frenchman and my brother's business partner for more than two years."},{"en":"happened to meet","context":"I heard from my mother that my brother happened to meet him at a yoga and meditation class."},{"en":"say goodbye before returning to","context":"He came to our house last week to say goodbye before returning to Paris."},{"en":"a masculine look","context":"Armane had a masculine look, with large blue eyes, a wide forehead and a sharp, straight nose."},{"en":"a wide forehead","context":"Armane had a masculine look, with large blue eyes, a wide forehead and a sharp, straight nose."},{"en":"earned our utmost respect","context":"He earned our utmost respect, and I'd say he was easily one of the most likeable guests we've ever had to stay."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a visitor in your home","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-018","_displayNo":"#119","outline":{"topic":"Armane","intro":"one guests visited family left great impression me","body":["name Armane Frenchman brother's business partner 2y","Armane masculine look w/ large blue eyes wide forehead","way used chopsticks rolled vegetables w/ rice paper told deeply aware","happy hear Armane"],"conclusion":"earned utmost respect"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-018.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A memorable visitor to my home was Armane, a French business partner of my brother. They had worked together for more than two years after meeting at a yoga and meditation class. He visited us last week to say goodbye before returning to Paris.\n\nArmane had large blue eyes and a strong-looking face. During dinner, I noticed that he understood many Vietnamese customs. He used chopsticks well and knew how to roll vegetables in rice paper. He even tried the strong shrimp paste because he did not want to seem impolite. Later, he admitted that learning our table manners had been difficult at first.\n\nAfter the visit, he sent us a thank-you card that he had made by hand. My whole family was touched. We do not expect visitors from other countries to know all our traditions, but we appreciated his effort. I remember him as a friendly and thoughtful guest. His visit showed me how learning a little about another culture can make people feel respected.","html":"A memorable visitor to my home was Armane, a French business partner of my brother. They had worked together for more than two years after meeting at a yoga and meditation class. He visited us last week to say goodbye before returning to Paris.\n\nArmane had large blue eyes and a strong-looking face. During dinner, I noticed that he understood many Vietnamese customs. He used chopsticks well and knew how to roll vegetables in rice paper. He even tried the strong shrimp paste because he did not want to seem impolite. Later, he admitted that learning our <strong class=\"key-phrase\">table manners</strong> had been difficult at first.\n\nAfter the visit, he sent us a thank-you card that he had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made by hand</strong>. My whole family was touched. We do not expect visitors from other countries to know all our traditions, but we appreciated his effort. I remember him as a friendly and thoughtful guest. His visit showed me how learning a little about another culture can make people feel respected.","phrases":[{"en":"table manners","meaning":"Polite ways of behaving while eating with others."},{"en":"made by hand","meaning":"Created personally rather than produced by a machine."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-018.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An adventurous person who you know","prompt":"Describe an adventurous person who you know.\nYou should say:\nWho the person is\nHow you know this person\nWhat this person does that is adventurous\nAnd explain why you think this person likes to take risks.","answer":"Although I don't know this person personally, I'm going to talk about Bear Grylls, a famous adventurer with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">his own survival show</strong> on the National Geographic channel. I first came across him years ago, completely by accident, flicking through channels late one night, and I've been hooked on his programmes ever since.\n\nYou don't really need much explanation once people hear the name — Bear Grylls is pretty much <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the embodiment of adventure and courage</strong>. In his show, he goes to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remote places with harsh conditions</strong> and has to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">try to survive</strong> for several days, finding food and water in the most unlikely places, sometimes hunting wild animals or eating insects just to get by. I don't think there's much this guy's afraid to do — he's drunk his own urine and eaten a sheep's eye on camera, and he didn't even flinch.\n\nI think the reason he takes so many risks is that he's just incredibly confident in his own survival skills, built up from years of military training before he ever got a TV show. There's also a genuine purpose behind it — the show's meant to teach people what to do if they're ever stuck in a similar situation, so in a way he has to push himself to those extremes to make the lessons convincing.\n\nI'm honestly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in awe of</strong> the guy. I can't even manage eating insects myself, so I really can't imagine going through half of what he puts himself through. He's getting a bit older now, so I don't know how much longer the show will carry on, but either way, he's a tough one.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"his own survival show","context":"Although I don't know this person personally, I'm going to talk about Bear Grylls, a famous adventurer with his own survival show on the National Geographic channel."},{"en":"the embodiment of adventure and courage","context":"You don't really need much explanation once people hear the name — Bear Grylls is pretty much the embodiment of adventure and courage."},{"en":"remote places with harsh conditions","context":"In his show, he goes to remote places with harsh conditions and has to try to survive for several days, finding food and water in the most unlikely places, sometimes hunting wild animals or eating insects just to get by."},{"en":"try to survive","context":"In his show, he goes to remote places with harsh conditions and has to try to survive for several days, finding food and water in the most unlikely places, sometimes hunting wild animals or eating insects just to get by."},{"en":"in awe of","context":"I'm honestly in awe of the guy."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an adventurous person who you know","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-019","_displayNo":"#120","outline":{"topic":"Bear Grylls","intro":"don't know him personally - famous adventurer with his own survival show; first came across him by accident on TV, hooked ever since","body":["embodiment of adventure and courage - goes to remote places, hunts/eats bugs, drank his own urine, ate a sheep's eye","why he takes risks: confident in survival skills built up from military training; also a genuine purpose - the show teaches people what to do in similar situations"],"conclusion":"in awe of him, can't imagine going through what he does; a tough guy even as he gets older"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-019.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An adventurous person I know about is Bear Grylls. I do not know him personally, but I have watched his survival programmes for years. I first saw one by chance while changing television channels late at night.\n\nIn his shows, he visits remote places where conditions are difficult. He demonstrates ways to find water, get food and survive outdoors. Sometimes he eats insects or other unusual things. I find these scenes hard to watch, but they show how challenging life can be in those environments.\n\nI think he is willing to take risks because of his training and experience. He had military training before becoming a television presenter, so he has developed useful survival skills. His programmes also aim to explain what someone might do in a difficult outdoor situation. I admire his confidence and courage, although I would not want to copy the dangerous activities myself. Watching him makes me realise how much knowledge and preparation are needed for an adventure in a wild place.","html":"An adventurous person I know about is Bear Grylls. I do not know him personally, but I have watched his survival programmes for years. I first saw one <strong class=\"key-phrase\">by chance</strong> while changing television channels late at night.\n\nIn his shows, he visits remote places where conditions are difficult. He demonstrates ways to find water, get food and survive outdoors. Sometimes he eats insects or other unusual things. I find these scenes hard to watch, but they show how challenging life can be in those environments.\n\nI think he is willing to take risks because of his training and experience. He had military training before becoming a television presenter, so he has developed useful <strong class=\"key-phrase\">survival skills</strong>. His programmes also aim to explain what someone might do in a difficult outdoor situation. I admire his confidence and courage, although I would not want to copy the dangerous activities myself. Watching him makes me realise how much knowledge and preparation are needed for an adventure in a wild place.","phrases":[{"en":"by chance","meaning":"Without planning or expecting something."},{"en":"survival skills","meaning":"Abilities that help a person stay alive in difficult conditions."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-019.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An artist or entertainer you admire","prompt":"Describe an artist or entertainer you admire\nYou should say:\n- who they are and what they do\n- how they became successful\n- how you found out about them\n- and why you admire them","answer":"I'd like to talk about Ed Sheeran, a British singer-songwriter who performs mainly with his voice, an acoustic guitar and a loop pedal. I first became aware of him when a friend played “The A Team” for me, and I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">was struck by</strong> how a simple arrangement could carry such a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strong emotional message</strong>.\n\nHe became successful gradually rather than through a single manufactured publicity campaign. Before he was internationally known, he performed in small venues, released music independently and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">built an audience</strong> through constant live work. His breakthrough came when his songs reached a much wider online audience and attracted <strong class=\"key-phrase\">support from established musicians</strong>.\n\nI admire him for two reasons. First, he writes much of his own material, so his music feels personal rather than interchangeable. Second, his live performances show technical control and creativity: he can build a complete arrangement alone by recording rhythms and harmonies in real time. Even though his music is commercially successful, the core of it still depends on songwriting and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sustained practice</strong>, which makes his career feel earned. I'd add that what I admire is not really the fame but the working method behind it. He's apparently played several hundred gigs in a single year at one stage, many of them to almost empty rooms, and he's said in interviews that he simply treated songwriting as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a skill to be practised</strong> rather than as something you wait to feel inspired about.\n\nThat's a useful attitude well beyond music, and it's the main reason I find him interesting. Plenty of performers are more technically gifted, but very few of them are as visibly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the product of their own effort</strong>.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"was struck by","context":"I first became aware of him when a friend played “The A Team” for me, and I was struck by how a simple arrangement could carry such a strong emotional message."},{"en":"strong emotional message","context":"I first became aware of him when a friend played “The A Team” for me, and I was struck by how a simple arrangement could carry such a strong emotional message."},{"en":"built an audience","context":"Before he was internationally known, he performed in small venues, released music independently and built an audience through constant live work."},{"en":"support from established musicians","context":"His breakthrough came when his songs reached a much wider online audience and attracted support from established musicians."},{"en":"sustained practice","context":"Even though his music is commercially successful, the core of it still depends on songwriting and sustained practice, which makes his career feel earned."},{"en":"a skill to be practised","context":"He's apparently played several hundred gigs in a single year at one stage, many of them to almost empty rooms, and he's said in interviews that he simply treated songwriting as a skill to be practised rather than as something you wait to feel inspired about."},{"en":"the product of their own effort","context":"That's a useful attitude well beyond music, and it's the main reason I find him interesting. Plenty of performers are more technically gifted, but very few of them are as visibly the product of their own effort."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an artist or entertainer you admire","id":"speaking-part-2-020","_displayNo":"#121","outline":{"topic":"Ed Sheeran","intro":"Ed Sheeran British singer-songwriter performs mainly w/ voice acoustic guitar","body":["1st became aware friend played Team struck simple arrangement carry strong","Before internationally known performed small venues released music independently built audience","writes own material music feels personal rather interchangeable","live performances show technical control creativity build complete arrangement alone recording","admire not fame but working method behind it","played several hundred gigs in a yr many to almost empty rooms","treated songwriting as skill to be practised not waiting for inspiration","useful attitude well beyond music visibly product of own effort"],"conclusion":"useful attitude well beyond music visibly product of own effort"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-020.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An entertainer I admire is Ed Sheeran, a British singer and songwriter. A friend first played his song The A Team for me. I liked how a simple song could express such strong feelings.\n\nHe often performs with just a guitar and a loop pedal. This device lets him record short sounds and repeat them while he plays other parts. In this way, he can build a full musical performance on his own. I find that both clever and enjoyable to watch.\n\nI also respect the effort behind his success. Before becoming famous, he played in small places, sometimes to very few people, and released his own music. He kept practising songwriting instead of waiting for a perfect idea. Many of his songs feel personal because he writes them himself. I admire his working attitude as much as his music. His career reminds me that a skill develops through regular practice, and that starting with a small audience does not mean you cannot achieve something bigger later.","html":"An entertainer I admire is Ed Sheeran, a British singer and songwriter. A friend first played his song The A Team for me. I liked how a simple song could express such strong feelings.\n\nHe often performs with just a guitar and a loop pedal. This device lets him record short sounds and repeat them while he plays other parts. In this way, he can build a full musical performance <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on his own</strong>. I find that both clever and enjoyable to watch.\n\nI also respect the effort behind his success. Before becoming famous, he played in small places, sometimes to very few people, and released his own music. He kept practising songwriting instead of waiting for a perfect idea. Many of his songs feel personal because he writes them himself. I admire his <strong class=\"key-phrase\">working attitude</strong> as much as his music. His career reminds me that a skill develops through regular practice, and that starting with a small audience does not mean you cannot achieve something bigger later.","phrases":[{"en":"on his own","meaning":"Without other people helping him perform."},{"en":"working attitude","meaning":"The way someone thinks about and approaches their work."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-020.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting neighbour you know","prompt":"Describe an interesting neighbour you know.\nYou should say:\nWho this person is\nHow you know this person\nWhat this person likes to do\nAnd explain why you think this neighbour is interesting.","answer":"I must say, I'm extremely fortunate to have such a wonderful neighbour, Duy, who's really friendly and always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">willing to assist me</strong> whenever I'm in trouble. Duy and I live in the same building, and our flats are right next to each other. We got to know each other when the power to the whole building was cut off one evening — I'd <strong class=\"key-phrase\">run out of candles</strong> and my torch was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nowhere to be found</strong>, so I had to go and knock on the apartment next door for help, and that's when I first met him.\n\nHe was so helpful and cheerful about it — he even asked me to hold his torch while he lit candles in his own flat. I was really grateful, so a few days later I made him some snacks as a thank-you, and we've been pretty close friends ever since. The more I've got to know Duy, the more I've come to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look up to</strong> him. He's well into his fifties and lives alone, since he lost his family in a car accident a while back, but he still somehow manages to see life through rose coloured glasses — his motto is simply to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep moving forward</strong>. He works and studies at the same time, and every weekend he travels out to an orphanage to teach the kids there maths, completely unpaid.\n\nHe really is a wonderful neighbour, and I know I could call on him any time I needed to. We look out for each other whenever we can, and since we both live on our own, it's really important to have a good friend that close by.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"willing to assist me","context":"I must say, I'm extremely fortunate to have such a wonderful neighbour, Duy, who's really friendly and always willing to assist me whenever I'm in trouble."},{"en":"run out of candles","context":"We got to know each other when the power to the whole building was cut off one evening — I'd run out of candles and my torch was nowhere to be found, so I had to go and knock on the apartment next door for help, and that's when I first met him."},{"en":"nowhere to be found","context":"We got to know each other when the power to the whole building was cut off one evening — I'd run out of candles and my torch was nowhere to be found, so I had to go and knock on the apartment next door for help, and that's when I first met him."},{"en":"look up to","context":"The more I've got to know Duy, the more I've come to look up to him."},{"en":"keep moving forward","context":"He's well into his fifties and lives alone, since he lost his family in a car accident a while back, but he still somehow manages to see life through rose coloured glasses — his motto is simply to keep moving forward."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting neighbour you know","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-021","_displayNo":"#122","outline":{"topic":"Duy","intro":"say extremely fortunate wonderful neighbour Duy friendly always willing assist","body":["Unfortunately run out candles torch nowhere found go apartment next door","extremely helpful enthusiastic asked hold torch lit candles apartment","well fifties lives own lost family car accident manages see life","works studies same time weekends travels orphanage teach maths kids there"],"conclusion":"always help out bc live own it's important good friend"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-021.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An interesting neighbour I know is Duy. Our flats are next to each other. We first met during a power cut one evening. I could not find my torch and had no candles, so I knocked on his door for help.\n\nHe was friendly and immediately helped me. A few days later, I made some snacks to thank him, and we became close friends. Duy is in his fifties and lives alone because his family died in a car accident. Despite this terrible experience, he has remained positive about life.\n\nHe works and studies at the same time. At weekends, he also visits an orphanage to teach children maths without payment. I admire both his kindness and his determination to continue helping others. Since we both live alone, it is comforting to have a friend so close by. We look after each other, and I know I can ask him for help when I need it. He makes our building feel like a community.","html":"An interesting neighbour I know is Duy. Our flats are next to each other. We first met during a power cut one evening. I could not find my torch and had no candles, so I knocked on his door for help.\n\nHe was friendly and immediately helped me. A few days later, I made some snacks to thank him, and we became close friends. Duy is in his fifties and lives alone because his family died in a car accident. Despite this terrible experience, he has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remained positive</strong> about life.\n\nHe works and studies at the same time. At weekends, he also visits an orphanage to teach children maths without payment. I admire both his kindness and his determination to continue helping others. Since we both live alone, it is comforting to have a friend so close by. We <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look after each other</strong>, and I know I can ask him for help when I need it. He makes our building feel like a community.","phrases":[{"en":"remained positive","meaning":"Continued to feel hopeful despite difficulties."},{"en":"look after each other","meaning":"Help and care for one another."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-021.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting person from another country","prompt":"Describe an interesting person from another country.\nYou should say:\n- Who this person is;\n- How you know this person;\n- What this person does;\n- Why this person is interesting.","answer":"Today I'd like to talk about an interesting person from another country who I really admire and respect — my English teacher, Mr Vincent. I'm currently studying at ZIM English Centre, where there are quite a few foreign teachers, but out of all of them, Vincent's easily the one I've grown closest to.\n\nHe's American, and he's been living and teaching in Vietnam for about two years now. He's got a real reputation at the school for being switched-on and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely dedicated</strong> — I often see him arriving early and still there marking homework long after everyone else has gone home. Since he joined a year ago, he's more or less <strong class=\"key-phrase\">won everyone over</strong>, students and staff alike.\n\nWhat I find most interesting about him, though, is how hard he's worked to fit into Vietnamese culture rather than just expecting everyone else to adapt to him. He's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">picked up enough Vietnamese</strong> to haggle at the market, he always takes his shoes off without being asked, and he even learned to make Vietnamese baguette sandwiches from a stall owner near his flat, purely because he wanted to understand the culture better rather than just live alongside it. He's also incredibly patient — whenever a student messages him at midnight panicking about homework, he'll actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reply properly</strong> instead of brushing them off till the next morning.\n\nOn top of all that, he's got really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">good manners</strong> — small things like remembering the name of every student he's ever taught, or always thanking the canteen staff by name when he grabs lunch. It's that combination of curiosity about our culture and genuine warmth towards people that makes him stand out to me, and it's exactly why he's built such a good reputation here.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"genuinely dedicated","context":"He's got a real reputation at the school for being switched-on and genuinely dedicated — I often see him arriving early and still there marking homework long after everyone else has gone home."},{"en":"won everyone over","context":"Since he joined a year ago, he's more or less won everyone over, students and staff alike."},{"en":"picked up enough Vietnamese","context":"He's picked up enough Vietnamese to haggle at the market, he always takes his shoes off without being asked, and he even learned to make Vietnamese baguette sandwiches from a stall owner near his flat, purely because he wanted to understand the culture better rather than just live alongside it."},{"en":"reply properly","context":"He's also incredibly patient — whenever a student messages him at midnight panicking about homework, he'll actually reply properly instead of brushing them off till the next morning."},{"en":"good manners","context":"On top of all that, he's got really good manners — small things like remembering the name of every student he's ever taught, or always thanking the canteen staff by name when he grabs lunch."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting person from another country","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-022","_displayNo":"#123","outline":{"topic":"Mr Vincent","intro":"American English teacher at ZIM English Centre, the foreign teacher I've grown closest to","body":["living/teaching in Vietnam ~2 years, reputation for being switched-on and dedicated, arrives early stays late","what makes him interesting: works hard to fit into Vietnamese culture - learned enough Vietnamese to haggle, takes shoes off, learned to make banh mi, replies properly to students even at midnight","good manners - remembers every student's name, thanks canteen staff"],"conclusion":"combination of cultural curiosity and warmth is why he's built such a good reputation"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-022.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An interesting person from another country is Mr Vincent, my teacher at ZIM English Centre. He is American and has lived and taught in Vietnam for about two years. He joined our centre a year ago.\n\nHe is a hardworking teacher. I often see him arriving early or staying late to mark homework. He also explains things patiently when students need help. However, what interests me most is his effort to understand Vietnamese life. He has learned enough Vietnamese to bargain at markets, remembers to remove his shoes indoors and even learned to make Vietnamese baguette sandwiches from a local seller.\n\nThese actions show that he wants to be part of the culture around him. He is also polite and remembers students' names, as well as the names of people working in the canteen. I enjoy his classes because he combines good teaching with a friendly personality. He has shown me that learning about another country includes respecting the people and their everyday habits.","html":"An interesting person from another country is Mr Vincent, my teacher at ZIM English Centre. He is American and has lived and taught in Vietnam for about two years. He joined our centre a year ago.\n\nHe is a hardworking teacher. I often see him arriving early or staying late to mark homework. He also explains things patiently when students need help. However, what interests me most is his effort to understand Vietnamese life. He has learned enough Vietnamese to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bargain at markets</strong>, remembers to remove his shoes indoors and even learned to make Vietnamese baguette sandwiches from a local seller.\n\nThese actions show that he wants to be part of the culture around him. He is also polite and remembers students' names, as well as the names of people working in the canteen. I enjoy his classes because he combines good teaching with a friendly personality. He has shown me that learning about another country includes respecting the people and their <strong class=\"key-phrase\">everyday habits</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"bargain at markets","meaning":"Discuss a price with a seller to try to pay less."},{"en":"everyday habits","meaning":"Ordinary things that people do regularly."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-022.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An old friend you enjoy talking with","prompt":"Describe an old friend you enjoy talking with\nYou should say:\nWho this person is\nHow you knew him/her\nWhat you usually talk about\nAnd explain why you want to talk to this person","answer":"I'd like to talk about Nguyen, one of my university friends, who's probably the most approachable and funny guy I've ever met. We weren't classmates and we weren't even in the same clubs — I actually met him purely by chance, when we ended up as rival candidates in a citywide singing contest back in our second year.\n\nAt the time, he was just an average-looking guy with a silly grin permanently on his face, so if I'm honest, he didn't make much of an impression on me then. It wasn't until we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bumped into each other</strong> again at school a few months later that we actually started talking properly, and we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hit it off</strong> almost straight away.\n\nWe talk about pretty much everything, but music's probably our favourite subject — we're both into guitar, and we'll argue for hours about which bands are actually any good. Beyond that, it's mostly university gossip, our families, and increasingly, where our lives are heading now we've both graduated. He's also one of the only people I can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">properly honest with</strong> about relationships — he never judges, he just listens.\n\nIf there's one thing that makes Nguyen so easy to talk to, it's his voice — he's got this warm, playful way of speaking, always slipping in a joke, and it somehow <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cracks me up</strong> even when I'm in a foul mood. We once spent an entire day, from seven in the morning until ten at night, just lying on the floor of a quiet cafe talking about nothing in particular, and I never once got bored.\n\nThese days he's abroad, finishing off his <strong class=\"key-phrase\">further education</strong>, so I don't see him nearly as often as I'd like — but he's still, without question, the person I most enjoy talking to.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"bumped into each other","context":"It wasn't until we bumped into each other again at school a few months later that we actually started talking properly, and we hit it off almost straight away."},{"en":"hit it off","context":"It wasn't until we bumped into each other again at school a few months later that we actually started talking properly, and we hit it off almost straight away."},{"en":"properly honest with","context":"He's also one of the only people I can be properly honest with about relationships — he never judges, he just listens."},{"en":"cracks me up","context":"If there's one thing that makes Nguyen so easy to talk to, it's his voice — he's got this warm, playful way of speaking, always slipping in a joke, and it somehow cracks me up even when I'm in a foul mood."},{"en":"further education","context":"These days he's abroad, finishing off his further education, so I don't see him nearly as often as I'd like — but he's still, without question, the person I most enjoy talking to."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an old friend you enjoy talking with","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-023","_displayNo":"#124","outline":{"topic":"Nguyen","intro":"university friend Nguyen, approachable and funny, met by chance at a citywide singing contest","body":["didn't make much impression at first, only became close after bumping into each other again at school","what we talk about: music/guitar mostly, also uni gossip, family, where our lives are heading, relationships (he never judges)","why I enjoy talking to him: his warm, playful voice and jokes always crack me up"],"conclusion":"he's abroad finishing further education now so we don't talk as often, but still the person I most enjoy talking to"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-023.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An old friend I enjoy talking to is Nguyen. We met during a city singing competition in our second year at university. We were competing against each other, but we became friends after meeting again at school a few months later.\n\nWe both play guitar, so music is our favourite subject. We talk about bands, university life, our families and our plans for the future. I can also speak honestly about relationships because he listens without judging me. He has a warm voice and often makes jokes, which helps when I am in a bad mood.\n\nOnce, we spent almost a whole day talking in a quiet cafe, from seven in the morning until ten at night. I never felt bored. He is now studying abroad, so we do not meet as often. However, I still enjoy every conversation we have. I value our friendship because talking to him feels easy, and I can be myself rather than worrying about saying the perfect thing.","html":"An old friend I enjoy talking to is Nguyen. We met during a city singing competition in our second year at university. We were competing against each other, but we became friends after meeting again at school a few months later.\n\nWe both play guitar, so music is our favourite subject. We talk about bands, university life, our families and our plans for the future. I can also speak honestly about relationships because he listens <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without judging me</strong>. He has a warm voice and often makes jokes, which helps when I am in a bad mood.\n\nOnce, we spent almost a whole day talking in a quiet cafe, from seven in the morning until ten at night. I never felt bored. He is now studying abroad, so we do not meet as often. However, I still enjoy every conversation we have. I value our friendship because talking to him feels easy, and I can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">be myself</strong> rather than worrying about saying the perfect thing.","phrases":[{"en":"without judging me","meaning":"Without deciding that I am wrong or a bad person."},{"en":"be myself","meaning":"Behave naturally without pretending to be different."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-023.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"One of your best friends","prompt":"Describe one of your best friends\nYou should say:\n- What this person looks like\n- When and where you met this person\n- What you do when you are together\nAnd explain why he/she is one of your best friends","answer":"The person I'd like to talk about is my good friend Mai. She has to be one of the nicest and most down-to-earth people I know. Mai's gorgeous — tall, with straight brown hair and deep brown eyes — and she attracts a fair amount of attention wherever we go, which makes standing next to her a little embarrassing! She <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pays a lot of attention to her appearance</strong> too, and always dresses in whatever's trendiest — I've never once seen her in a plain old T-shirt.\n\nWe've basically known each other since forever, because we were in the same class from high school through to college. The first time I met her, she left a deep impression on me — on the first day of school I got completely lost trying to find my classroom, and she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be my saviour</strong>. As luck would have it, we were then put together as desk-mates by the teacher.\n\nThese days we try to see each other weekly — shopping on a Saturday, or just grabbing bubble tea and talking for hours about nothing. We're also both obsessed with the same drama series, so we'll watch an episode together and message each other our theories afterwards.\n\nHonestly, the main reason we're best friends is that we just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hit it off</strong> from the very start, and we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">see eye to eye</strong> on nearly everything, from fashion to sport to video games. What I admire most is how protective she is. She's not perfect, mind you — she's never on time, and she can be a bit absent-minded, so people sometimes assume she doesn't take things seriously. But she's genuinely got a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strong sense of responsibility</strong>, and I know I can always rely on her.\n\nFriendship's the most important thing in the world to me, and I'm proud to say I've got a friend as loyal and as much fun as Mai — she really is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one in a million</strong>.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"pays a lot of attention to her appearance","context":"She pays a lot of attention to her appearance too, and always dresses in whatever's trendiest — I've never once seen her in a plain old T-shirt."},{"en":"turned out to be my saviour","context":"The first time I met her, she left a deep impression on me — on the first day of school I got completely lost trying to find my classroom, and she turned out to be my saviour."},{"en":"hit it off","context":"Honestly, the main reason we're best friends is that we just hit it off from the very start, and we see eye to eye on nearly everything, from fashion to sport to video games."},{"en":"see eye to eye","context":"Honestly, the main reason we're best friends is that we just hit it off from the very start, and we see eye to eye on nearly everything, from fashion to sport to video games."},{"en":"strong sense of responsibility","context":"But she's genuinely got a strong sense of responsibility, and I know I can always rely on her."},{"en":"one in a million","context":"Friendship's the most important thing in the world to me, and I'm proud to say I've got a friend as loyal and as much fun as Mai — she really is one in a million."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"one of your best friends","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-024","_displayNo":"#125","outline":{"topic":"Mai","intro":"good friend Mai, down-to-earth, gorgeous and tall, pays attention to her appearance","body":["known since high school, met on the first day when I got lost and she helped, became desk-mates","what we do together: shopping on Saturdays, bubble tea, watching a drama series together weekly","why best friend: hit it off from the start, see eye to eye on things, protective, has shortcomings (late, absent-minded) but a strong sense of responsibility"],"conclusion":"friendship matters most to me, proud to have a friend as loyal as Mai"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-024.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"One of my best friends is Mai. We met on the first day of high school when I was lost and she helped me find our classroom. The teacher then put us at the same desk. We later attended the same college and have stayed close.\n\nMai is tall, with straight brown hair and brown eyes. She likes fashionable clothes, but she is friendly and easy to talk to. We try to meet every week, either to shop or have bubble tea. We also enjoy the same television drama and often discuss what might happen next.\n\nWe have similar interests, including fashion, sport and games. Although Mai is sometimes late and forgets small things, she takes our friendship seriously. She is protective and helps me when I need support. I know I can rely on her. I value having someone who understands me so well and also makes ordinary afternoons enjoyable. To me, a loyal friend like Mai is more important than having a large number of friends.","html":"One of my best friends is Mai. We met on the first day of high school when I was lost and she helped me find our classroom. The teacher then put us at the same desk. We later attended the same college and have stayed close.\n\nMai is tall, with straight brown hair and brown eyes. She likes fashionable clothes, but she is friendly and easy to talk to. We try to meet every week, either to shop or have bubble tea. We also enjoy the same television drama and often discuss what might happen next.\n\nWe have similar interests, including fashion, sport and games. Although Mai is sometimes late and forgets small things, she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">takes our friendship seriously</strong>. She is protective and helps me when I need support. I know I can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rely on her</strong>. I value having someone who understands me so well and also makes ordinary afternoons enjoyable. To me, a loyal friend like Mai is more important than having a large number of friends.","phrases":[{"en":"rely on her","meaning":"Trust her to help or support me."},{"en":"takes our friendship seriously","meaning":"Treats our friendship as something important."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-024.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Someone who is a good parent","prompt":"Describe someone who is a good parent.\nYou should say:\n- Who the person is.\n- How you know him/her.\n- What the person does.\n- Why you think he/she is good.","answer":"Speaking of a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">role model in parenting</strong>, I'd like to talk about my best friend's father. He's a doctor, and because of his <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tight working schedule</strong>, I don't actually get that many chances to talk to him directly, even though I'm round at their house pretty often. Still, I feel like I know him really well through the stories my best friend tells me — and honestly, how well-rounded and grounded she turned out proves to me that he's done a brilliant job raising her.\n\nVietnamese parents get a reputation for being “tiger parents”, but he's very much the exception. He respects his daughter's decisions and never forces her into anything against her will. Unlike a lot of doctors, who'd probably expect their kid to follow them into medicine, he's always pushed her to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pursue her dream</strong> of becoming a singer instead of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">following the family line of work</strong>. Even when she messes up and comes home with bad grades, she's never afraid to tell him, because to her he isn't just a parent; he's a genuine friend who gives her honest advice and helps her figure out how to do better.\n\nBeing a doctor is nowhere near <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a nine-to-five job</strong> — he's on night shifts several times a week — but he still manages to take brilliant care of his daughter. He's clearly worked out how to keep a proper work-life balance: he clears his entire weekend for family time, teaching her cooking and basic survival skills, and sometimes taking her to the cinema so they can chat about the film afterwards and whatever it made them think about.\n\nAll in all, he's exactly the kind of parent I hope to become myself one day.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"role model in parenting","context":"Speaking of a role model in parenting, I'd like to talk about my best friend's father."},{"en":"tight working schedule","context":"He's a doctor, and because of his tight working schedule, I don't actually get that many chances to talk to him directly, even though I'm round at their house pretty often."},{"en":"pursue her dream","context":"Unlike a lot of doctors, who'd probably expect their kid to follow them into medicine, he's always pushed her to pursue her dream of becoming a singer instead of following the family line of work."},{"en":"following the family line of work","context":"Unlike a lot of doctors, who'd probably expect their kid to follow them into medicine, he's always pushed her to pursue her dream of becoming a singer instead of following the family line of work."},{"en":"a nine-to-five job","context":"Being a doctor is nowhere near a nine-to-five job — he's on night shifts several times a week — but he still manages to take brilliant care of his daughter."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"someone who is a good parent","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-025","_displayNo":"#126","outline":{"topic":"my best friend's father","intro":"Speaking role model parenting like talk best friend's father","body":["working doctor due tight working schedule not chances talk come house","Unlike doctors always encourages pursue dream singer rather follow family line","causes trouble receives bad grades school not afraid tell father bc","allocates whole weekend apart work social life spend time w/ family"],"conclusion":"type parent wish become future"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-025.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Someone I think is a good parent is my best friend's father. He is a doctor, so he has a busy schedule and often works at night. I visit their home quite often, although I usually learn about him through my friend's stories.\n\nWhat I admire is the way he respects his daughter's choices. He does not expect her to become a doctor just because he is one. Instead, he supports her dream of being a singer. When she receives poor grades, she can tell him honestly because she knows he will help her improve rather than simply get angry.\n\nHe also makes time for family despite his work. At weekends, he teaches her cooking and practical skills. Sometimes they go to the cinema and talk about the film afterwards. I think these conversations help them understand each other. He acts as both a parent and a trusted friend. His example has taught me that good parenting means offering guidance while allowing a child to develop their own interests.","html":"Someone I think is a good parent is my best friend's father. He is a doctor, so he has a busy schedule and often works at night. I visit their home quite often, although I usually learn about him through my friend's stories.\n\nWhat I admire is the way he respects his daughter's choices. He does not expect her to become a doctor just because he is one. Instead, he supports her dream of being a singer. When she receives poor grades, she can tell him honestly because she knows he will help her improve rather than simply get angry.\n\nHe also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">makes time for family</strong> despite his work. At weekends, he teaches her cooking and practical skills. Sometimes they go to the cinema and talk about the film afterwards. I think these conversations help them understand each other. He acts as both a parent and a trusted friend. His example has taught me that good parenting means <strong class=\"key-phrase\">offering guidance</strong> while allowing a child to develop their own interests.","phrases":[{"en":"makes time for family","meaning":"Arranges time to spend with relatives despite being busy."},{"en":"offering guidance","meaning":"Giving advice and support to help someone make decisions."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-025.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Someone you admire who is much older than you","prompt":"Describe someone you admire who is much older than you\nYou should say:\nwho this person is\nhow long you have known him/ her\nwhat qualities he/ she has\nAnd say why you admire him/ her.","answer":"The first person who comes to mind is my grandmother. She's almost ninety now, and she's been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a huge influence on</strong> me for as long as I can remember — literally, since I've known her my whole life. She actually moved in with us not long after my grandfather passed away, so I got to spend basically my entire childhood and my teenage years growing up right alongside her.\n\nShe's kind and wise, which is exactly why I still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turn to her for advice</strong> whenever I'm stuck on something. I remember one time that really impressed me — a neighbour's house caught fire late one night, and while everyone else just stood outside panicking, my grandmother, who was about seventy at the time, ran straight back in to help drag out an elderly man who couldn't walk on his own. She never makes a big deal of it, but I still think about how <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without a second thought</strong> she just went in.\n\nShe raised four children of her own, one of whom is my father, and she's still the person the whole family turns to when something's wrong. When I failed an important exam a couple of years ago and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">felt like a total failure</strong>, she just made a pot of tea, sat with me on the porch, and told me how she'd failed her own teacher-training exam twice before finally passing on the third attempt — and somehow, by the time she'd finished, it all felt a lot less enormous than it had five minutes earlier.\n\nI could talk about her for hours, but if I had to pick one reason I admire her most, it's that her love for me has never once had any conditions attached. She's the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">closest thing I have to a mentor</strong>, and even now, talking to her genuinely calms me down when everything else feels chaotic. I think she's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shaped my whole outlook on life</strong>, and there's a real bond between us that I don't think will ever fade.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"a huge influence on","context":"She's almost ninety now, and she's been a huge influence on me for as long as I can remember — literally, since I've known her my whole life."},{"en":"turn to her for advice","context":"She's kind and wise, which is exactly why I still turn to her for advice whenever I'm stuck on something."},{"en":"without a second thought","context":"She never makes a big deal of it, but I still think about how without a second thought she just went in."},{"en":"felt like a total failure","context":"When I failed an important exam a couple of years ago and felt like a total failure, she just made a pot of tea, sat with me on the porch, and told me how she'd failed her own teacher-training exam twice before finally passing on the third attempt — and somehow, by the time she'd finished, it all..."},{"en":"closest thing I have to a mentor","context":"She's the closest thing I have to a mentor, and even now, talking to her genuinely calms me down when everything else feels chaotic."},{"en":"shaped my whole outlook on life","context":"I think she's shaped my whole outlook on life, and there's a real bond between us that I don't think will ever fade."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"someone you admire who is much older than you","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-026","_displayNo":"#127","outline":{"topic":"my grandmother","intro":"grandmother, almost 90, a huge influence my whole life, moved in with us after grandfather died","body":["kind and wise - turn to her for advice; specific example: ran into a neighbour's burning house at 70 to help pull a man out, without a second thought","raised 4 kids incl. my father; specific example: comforted me after I failed an exam by telling me about failing her own teacher-training exam twice before passing"],"conclusion":"admire her unconditional love, she's the closest thing I have to a mentor and has shaped my whole outlook on life"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-026.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An older person I admire is my grandmother, who is almost ninety. She moved in with us after my grandfather died, so I spent much of my childhood with her. She is kind and wise, and I still ask her for advice.\n\nOne event showed how brave she is. When a neighbour's house caught fire, she helped bring out an elderly man who could not walk alone. She was about seventy herself, but she acted while other people were still panicking.\n\nShe is also very good at helping people feel better. When I failed an important exam, she sat with me over tea and explained that she had failed her teacher-training exam twice before passing. Hearing this helped me see my own problem differently. She raised four children and is still the person our family turns to for support. I admire her because her love does not depend on my success. Spending time with her makes me calmer and reminds me that one failure is not the end.","html":"An older person I admire is my grandmother, who is almost ninety. She moved in with us after my grandfather died, so I spent much of my childhood with her. She is kind and wise, and I still ask her for advice.\n\nOne event showed how brave she is. When a neighbour's house caught fire, she helped bring out an elderly man who could not walk alone. She was about seventy herself, but she acted while other people were still panicking.\n\nShe is also very good at helping people feel better. When I failed an important exam, she sat with me over tea and explained that she had failed her teacher-training exam twice before passing. Hearing this helped me see my own problem differently. She raised four children and is still the person our family <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turns to for support</strong>. I admire her because her love <strong class=\"key-phrase\">does not depend on my success</strong>. Spending time with her makes me calmer and reminds me that one failure is not the end.","phrases":[{"en":"turns to for support","meaning":"Goes to someone when help or comfort is needed."},{"en":"does not depend on my success","meaning":"Is there whether I succeed or fail."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-026.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Two people from the same family","prompt":"Describe two people from the same family\nYou should say:\nWho they are\nHow you know them\nIf they look a-like\nAnd if you like spending time with them.","answer":"I'd like to describe twin sisters who live in the apartment next to mine. I met them about a year ago during a power cut, when I knocked on their door to borrow some candles, feeling a bit awkward about bothering total strangers. They invited me in until the electricity came back, and we've been friends ever since.\n\nAlthough they're twins, they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look nothing alike</strong>. The older sister has short hair, a mischievous smile and a very outgoing personality. She's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a real social butterfly</strong> and can start a conversation with almost anyone. Her younger sister has long hair and is much <strong class=\"key-phrase\">quieter and more reserved</strong>. She usually listens carefully before speaking, but she's got a dry sense of humour that makes me laugh, even when I'm least expecting it.\n\nI enjoy spending time with them because their personalities <strong class=\"key-phrase\">balance each other out</strong>. The older one brings energy to any gathering, while the younger one notices details and gives thoughtful advice. We often have film nights at my apartment, order food and talk until late. Because they see situations differently, we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never run out of things to talk about</strong>.\n\nWhat I value most is that they're both dependable. If I need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical help</strong>, there'll usually be one of them around, and they know they can rely on me too. I hope we'll stay close even if one of us eventually moves away, which feels more likely now that the older one's talking about a job abroad.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"look nothing alike","context":"Although they're twins, they look nothing alike."},{"en":"a real social butterfly","context":"She's a real social butterfly and can start a conversation with almost anyone."},{"en":"quieter and more reserved","context":"Her younger sister has long hair and is much quieter and more reserved."},{"en":"balance each other out","context":"I enjoy spending time with them because their personalities balance each other out."},{"en":"never run out of things to talk about","context":"Because they see situations differently, we never run out of things to talk about."},{"en":"practical help","context":"If I need practical help, there'll usually be one of them around, and they know they can rely on me too."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"two people from the same family","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-027","_displayNo":"#128","outline":{"topic":"twin sisters","intro":"like describe twin sisters live apartment next mine","body":["met year ago during power cut knocked door borrow candles","usually listens carefully before speaking dry sense humour makes laugh","older one brings energy gathering younger one notices details gives thoughtful","bc see situations differently never run out things talk about"],"conclusion":"hope remain close one eventually moves away"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-027.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Two people from the same family I know are twin sisters who live next door. We met about a year ago during a power cut. I asked to borrow candles, and they invited me in while we waited for the electricity to return.\n\nAlthough they are twins, they look and behave differently. The older sister has short hair and is very outgoing. She can start a conversation with almost anyone. The younger one has long hair and is quieter. She listens carefully and sometimes makes unexpected jokes.\n\nWe often watch films at my flat, order food and talk until late. Their different personalities make these evenings interesting. One brings energy to the conversation, while the other gives thoughtful advice. They are also reliable, and we help each other with practical problems. I enjoy knowing them both because I feel comfortable asking for support. The older sister is considering a job abroad, so we may not always be neighbours, but I hope our friendship continues wherever we live.","html":"Two people from the same family I know are twin sisters who live next door. We met about a year ago during a power cut. I asked to borrow candles, and they invited me in while we waited for the electricity to return.\n\nAlthough they are twins, they look and behave differently. The older sister has short hair and is very <strong class=\"key-phrase\">outgoing</strong>. She can start a conversation with almost anyone. The younger one has long hair and is quieter. She listens carefully and sometimes makes unexpected jokes.\n\nWe often watch films at my flat, order food and talk until late. Their different personalities make these evenings interesting. One brings energy to the conversation, while the other gives thoughtful advice. They are also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reliable</strong>, and we help each other with practical problems. I enjoy knowing them both because I feel comfortable asking for support. The older sister is considering a job abroad, so we may not always be neighbours, but I hope our friendship continues wherever we live.","phrases":[{"en":"outgoing","meaning":"Friendly and comfortable meeting and talking to people."},{"en":"reliable","meaning":"Someone you can trust to help or do what they promise."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-027.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A beautiful garden that you like","prompt":"Describe a beautiful garden that you like.\nYou should say:\n- where it is\n- what you can see there\n- what people do there\n- and explain why you like it.","answer":"Today I'd like to describe my home garden, which I think is a beautiful one and which I like very much. The garden is part of my home's backyard, and my house is located in the suburbs, about a 1-hour drive from the city of San Francisco. There is a big tree right in the middle of my garden, which is surrounded by a hot tub, a small water fountain, several bushes, a section for vegetables, and different kinds of flowers.\n\nThis garden was actually one of the reasons why my wife and I chose to buy our house because it had been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">beautifully maintained</strong> by the previous owner's gardener and landscaper. It's been only 1 year since we bought the house and now we're taking care of the garden ourselves. Even though we're not experts in gardening, we still try our best to plant things that we like, such as roses and tulips, and we even grow our own tomatoes.\n\nWhenever family and friends come to visit us, we invite them to have a relaxing time in our hot tub, and enjoy the view. So in other words, our garden has also become a kind of resort! It definitely requires lots of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hard work to properly maintain the garden</strong>, but it gives us <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a great sense of accomplishment</strong> to \"decorate\" it the way we like.\n\nAll in all, the garden is one of the best features of my home and it's a place where I can relax after a long day of work or at weekends.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"beautifully maintained","context":"This garden was actually one of the reasons why my wife and I chose to buy our house because it had been beautifully maintained by the previous owner's gardener and landscaper."},{"en":"hard work to properly maintain the garden","context":"It definitely requires lots of hard work to properly maintain the garden, but it gives us a great sense of accomplishment to \"decorate\" it the way we like."},{"en":"a great sense of accomplishment","context":"It definitely requires lots of hard work to properly maintain the garden, but it gives us a great sense of accomplishment to \"decorate\" it the way we like."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a beautiful garden that you like","id":"speaking-part-2-028","_displayNo":"#129","outline":{"topic":"my home garden","intro":"I'd like describe home garden think beautiful one like much","body":["big tree right middle garden surrounded hot tub small water fountain","garden actually one reasons wife chose buy house bc beautifully maintained","not experts gardening try best plant things like roses tulips grow","definitely requires lots hard work properly maintain garden gives great sense"],"conclusion":"garden one best features home place relax after long day"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-028.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A beautiful garden I like is the one behind my home. My house is in the suburbs, about an hour's drive from San Francisco. There is a large tree in the middle of the garden, along with a hot tub, a small fountain, bushes, flowers and a vegetable area.\n\nMy wife and I bought the house a year ago. The garden was one of the main reasons we chose it because the previous owner had kept it in excellent condition. We now look after it ourselves. We are not experienced gardeners, but we enjoy growing roses, tulips and tomatoes.\n\nWhen friends and relatives visit, we invite them to relax in the hot tub and enjoy the view. Looking after the garden takes time and hard work, but seeing the plants grow gives us a sense of achievement. It is also my favourite place to rest after work. I like having a peaceful outdoor space that we can gradually make our own.","html":"A beautiful garden I like is the one behind my home. My house is in the suburbs, about an hour's drive from San Francisco. There is a large tree in the middle of the garden, along with a hot tub, a small fountain, bushes, flowers and a vegetable area.\n\nMy wife and I bought the house a year ago. The garden was one of the main reasons we chose it because the previous owner had kept it in excellent condition. We now look after it ourselves. We are not experienced gardeners, but we enjoy growing roses, tulips and tomatoes.\n\nWhen friends and relatives visit, we invite them to relax in the hot tub and enjoy the view. Looking after the garden takes time and hard work, but seeing the plants grow gives us <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of achievement</strong>. It is also my favourite place to rest after work. I like having a peaceful outdoor space that we can gradually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make our own</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"a sense of achievement","meaning":"A pleased feeling because you have completed something successfully."},{"en":"make our own","meaning":"Change something so it reflects our personal choices."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-028.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A cafe that you have been to","prompt":"Describe a cafe that you have been to.\nYou should say:\n- where it is\n- why you went there\n- what you ate or drank\n- and explain why you liked or disliked the cafe.","answer":"I am going to describe a cafe that I go to maybe once a week. It's just down the road from where I live, near Manchester, so it's really convenient for me to walk down there for a coffee or a bite to eat, and to use the wifi. The last time I went to this local cafe was on Friday afternoon. I was trying to do some work at home, but I found myself getting distracted by little jobs around the house.\n\nSo I decided to have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a change of scenery</strong>; I put my laptop in my bag and made the short walk to the cafe, where I spent the next few hours getting all of my work done. With a nice cup of coffee and just the right level of background noise, I found it much easier to concentrate. Over the course of the afternoon, I drank two cups of coffee and a glass or two of water. The cafe owner takes a lot of pride in making the best \"flat white\" coffee in the area, so that's what I tend to drink.\n\nIf I remember correctly, the second coffee that I ordered was a decaf, because I try to avoid drinking too much caffeine in the afternoon. I didn't have anything to eat on that occasion, but I do sometimes have one of the home-made cakes that they sell. There are a few reasons why I like going to my local cafe. I suppose the main reasons are the coffee, the free wifi and the friendly staff.\n\nHowever, on Friday I also managed to sit at my favourite table near one of the windows, and the cafe wasn't very busy. So <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the atmosphere and noise level</strong> were just right, and I was able to focus on my work without distractions for most of the afternoon.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"a change of scenery","context":"So I decided to have a change of scenery; I put my laptop in my bag and made the short walk to the cafe, where I spent the next few hours getting all of my work done."},{"en":"the atmosphere and noise level","context":"However, on Friday I also managed to sit at my favourite table near one of the windows, and the cafe wasn't very busy. So the atmosphere and noise level were just right, and I was able to focus on my work without distractions for most of the afternoon."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a cafe that you have been to","id":"speaking-part-2-029","_displayNo":"#130","outline":{"topic":"cafe near my home","intro":"going describe caf go maybe once week","body":["It's down road live near Manchester it's convenient walk down coffee","decided change scenery put laptop bag made short walk caf spent","caf owner takes lot pride making best flat white’ coffee area","However Friday managed sit favourite table near one windows caf not"],"conclusion":"atmosphere noise level right able focus work w/o distractions afternoon"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-029.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A cafe I visit regularly is near my home, outside Manchester. It is close enough to walk to, and I go there about once a week. My last visit was on Friday afternoon, when I needed somewhere to work.\n\nAt home, I kept noticing housework instead of concentrating. I took my laptop to the cafe and found a seat by the window. The room was not very busy, and the quiet background noise helped me focus. I stayed for several hours and finished my work. During that time, I had two coffees and some water. The second coffee was decaffeinated because I did not want too much caffeine late in the day.\n\nThe owner is proud of the flat white coffee, which is what I usually order. I sometimes buy a homemade cake too. I like the friendly staff, free Wi-Fi and comfortable atmosphere. On that Friday, the cafe gave me exactly what I needed: a pleasant place where I could work without constant interruptions.","html":"A cafe I visit regularly is near my home, outside Manchester. It is close enough to walk to, and I go there about once a week. My last visit was on Friday afternoon, when I needed somewhere to work.\n\nAt home, I kept noticing housework instead of concentrating. I took my laptop to the cafe and found a seat by the window. The room was not very busy, and the quiet <strong class=\"key-phrase\">background noise</strong> helped me focus. I stayed for several hours and finished my work. During that time, I had two coffees and some water. The second coffee was decaffeinated because I did not want too much caffeine late in the day.\n\nThe owner is proud of the flat white coffee, which is what I usually order. I sometimes buy a homemade cake too. I like the friendly staff, free Wi-Fi and comfortable atmosphere. On that Friday, the cafe gave me exactly what I needed: a pleasant place where I could work <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without constant interruptions</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"background noise","meaning":"Sounds around you that are not the main thing you are listening to."},{"en":"without constant interruptions","meaning":"Without things repeatedly stopping what you are doing."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-029.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A cafe you like or dislike","prompt":"Describe a cafe you like or dislike\nYou should say:\nWhere it was\nWhen you went there\nwhat you ate or drank\nAnd explain why you liked or disliked the cafe.","answer":"<strong class=\"key-phrase\">The fast pace of modern life</strong> in the city can be too much for an introvert like myself, and sometimes I just need somewhere to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">retreat into my shell</strong> for an hour or two. Fortunately, I found the perfect place, a spot called \"Secret Garden,\" which I'd like to talk about now.\n\n\"Secret Garden\" is a garden-themed coffee shop on the rooftop of a building in the centre of Hanoi. It's well known for its floral, botanical-inspired design, which gives the whole place a real sense of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">peace and tranquillity</strong> — you almost forget you're still in the middle of a busy city.\n\nI first found it about two years ago, when a colleague dragged me along after a rough week at work, and I've been going back every couple of weekends ever since. I usually order chamomile tea and a slice of their lemon drizzle cake, and I'll sit there for a couple of hours with a book, half-reading and half just watching the plants. If a friend comes along instead, we'll share a pot of tea and a plate of biscuits and chat quietly in the corner.\n\nWhat keeps me going back, though, isn't really the food or drink — it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the mood of the place</strong>. Even when every seat is taken, nobody raises their voice, nobody plays music from their phone, and the staff move around so quietly you barely notice them refilling your cup. It's the kind of calm that's hard to find anywhere else in a city as loud and crowded as Hanoi, and that's exactly why I always tell my friends to go there whenever they need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a break from the chaos</strong> outside.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"The fast pace of modern life","context":"The fast pace of modern life in the city can be too much for an introvert like myself, and sometimes I just need somewhere to retreat into my shell for an hour or two."},{"en":"retreat into my shell","context":"The fast pace of modern life in the city can be too much for an introvert like myself, and sometimes I just need somewhere to retreat into my shell for an hour or two."},{"en":"peace and tranquillity","context":"It's well known for its floral, botanical-inspired design, which gives the whole place a real sense of peace and tranquillity — you almost forget you're still in the middle of a busy city."},{"en":"the mood of the place","context":"What keeps me going back, though, isn't really the food or drink — it's the mood of the place."},{"en":"a break from the chaos","context":"It's the kind of calm that's hard to find anywhere else in a city as loud and crowded as Hanoi, and that's exactly why I always tell my friends to go there whenever they need a break from the chaos outside."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a cafe you like or dislike","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-030","_displayNo":"#131","outline":{"topic":"Secret Garden cafe","intro":"fast pace modern life introvert need retreat into shell found Secret Garden","body":["garden-themed coffee shop rooftop centre Hanoi floral botanical design peace tranquillity","found it 2 years ago colleague dragged along after rough week going back every couple weekends","order chamomile tea lemon drizzle cake sit with book or share tea biscuits w/ friend","what keeps me back isn't food it's mood of place nobody loud staff move quietly"],"conclusion":"kind of calm hard to find elsewhere always tell friends need break from chaos"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-030.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A cafe I like is Secret Garden in central Hanoi. It is on the roof of a building and has a garden design, with plenty of plants and flowers. Although the streets below are busy, the cafe feels peaceful.\n\nA colleague first took me there about two years ago after a difficult week at work. Since then, I have returned every few weekends. I usually order chamomile tea and lemon cake, then spend a couple of hours reading. If I go with a friend, we share tea and biscuits and have a quiet conversation.\n\nThe main reason I like it is the atmosphere. Even when the cafe is full, people speak softly and do not play loud music on their phones. The staff are calm and helpful too. I am quite a quiet person, so I appreciate having somewhere to take a break from the city. After a visit, I normally feel more relaxed and ready to deal with work again. It is a simple place that makes a big difference to my mood.","html":"A cafe I like is Secret Garden in central Hanoi. It is on the roof of a building and has a garden design, with plenty of plants and flowers. Although the streets below are busy, the cafe feels peaceful.\n\nA colleague first took me there about two years ago after a difficult week at work. Since then, I have returned every few weekends. I usually order chamomile tea and lemon cake, then spend a couple of hours reading. If I go with a friend, we share tea and biscuits and have a quiet conversation.\n\nThe main reason I like it is the atmosphere. Even when the cafe is full, people speak softly and do not play loud music on their phones. The staff are calm and helpful too. I am quite a quiet person, so I appreciate having somewhere to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take a break</strong> from the city. After a visit, I normally feel more relaxed and ready to deal with work again. It is a simple place that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">makes a big difference</strong> to my mood.","phrases":[{"en":"take a break","meaning":"Stop an activity for a while to rest."},{"en":"makes a big difference","meaning":"Has a strong or noticeable effect."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-030.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A city or country where you would most like to live in the future","prompt":"Describe a city or country where you would most like to live in the future.\nYou should say:\n- where it is\n- what you know about it\n- what you would do there\n- and explain why you would like to live there.","answer":"I'd like to tell you about Tokyo, the city I want to live in the most in the future. It's the capital city of Japan and the most populous metropolitan area in the world.\n\nThe main reason I'd love to settle down in Tokyo is the co-existence of modern and traditional values. It's the kind of city where the red gates of a centuries-old Shinto shrine may well be found just next door to a glass skyscraper showcasing the finest 21st-century technology. Or where a quiet green lane lined with wooden low-rise houses and an old-school tofu shop might sit just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a short stroll from</strong> a neon-lit square packed with flickering billboards and rainbow-bright street fashion.\n\nIf I actually lived there, I think I'd spend most weekends just wandering — a different neighbourhood each time, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no real plan</strong>, just seeing where the back streets take me. I'd want to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get properly good at</strong> Japanese rather than just knowing enough to order food, maybe take an evening class, and I'd definitely try to learn how to make ramen properly instead of always eating it out.\n\nThe hectic pace of life in Tokyo is also an irresistible attraction for someone like me. Tokyo's railway system seems like it was designed to win world records — it's hard to find a location in the metropolitan area that can't be reached with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a train ride and a short walk</strong>. And you could easily spend hours <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drooling over</strong> the elaborate pastries and picture-perfect sushi rolls in the basement of any department store.\n\nI feel like the city's always moving towards the future rather than looking back, and that's exactly the kind of energy I want around me every day. I honestly believe Tokyo would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never stop surprising me</strong>.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"a short stroll from","context":"Or where a quiet green lane lined with wooden low-rise houses and an old-school tofu shop might sit just a short stroll from a neon-lit square packed with flickering billboards and rainbow-bright street fashion."},{"en":"no real plan","context":"If I actually lived there, I think I'd spend most weekends just wandering — a different neighbourhood each time, no real plan, just seeing where the back streets take me."},{"en":"get properly good at","context":"I'd want to get properly good at Japanese rather than just knowing enough to order food, maybe take an evening class, and I'd definitely try to learn how to make ramen properly instead of always eating it out."},{"en":"a train ride and a short walk","context":"Tokyo's railway system seems like it was designed to win world records — it's hard to find a location in the metropolitan area that can't be reached with a train ride and a short walk."},{"en":"drooling over","context":"And you could easily spend hours drooling over the elaborate pastries and picture-perfect sushi rolls in the basement of any department store."},{"en":"never stop surprising me","context":"I feel like the city's always moving towards the future rather than looking back, and that's exactly the kind of energy I want around me every day. I honestly believe Tokyo would never stop surprising me."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a city or country you want to live the most in the future","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-032","_displayNo":"#132","outline":{"topic":"Tokyo","intro":"Tokyo capital Japan most populous metropolitan area want live future","body":["co-existence modern traditional values shrine next door skyscraper short stroll from","if lived there would spend weekends wandering different neighbourhood no real plan","want get properly good at Japanese learn make ramen properly","hectic pace life irresistible attraction railway system train ride short walk food"],"conclusion":"city always moving towards future energy want around me Tokyo never stop surprising"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-032.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A city I would like to live in is Tokyo, the capital of Japan. What attracts me most is the way modern buildings and traditional places exist together. You might see an old shrine beside a glass tower, or a quiet street close to a busy shopping area.\n\nIf I lived there, I would spend weekends exploring different neighbourhoods. I would also take Japanese classes so I could have real conversations rather than just order food. Learning to cook ramen would be another enjoyable goal.\n\nI like busy cities, and Tokyo seems to offer plenty to do. Its railway network would make travelling around convenient. I would enjoy looking at the food in department stores, especially the cakes and sushi. Living there would certainly bring challenges, including learning the language, but that is part of the attraction. I think Tokyo would give me a chance to experience a different way of life while discovering something new almost every day.","html":"A city I would like to live in is Tokyo, the capital of Japan. What attracts me most is the way modern buildings and traditional places exist together. You might see an old shrine beside a glass tower, or a quiet street close to a busy shopping area.\n\nIf I lived there, I would spend weekends exploring different neighbourhoods. I would also take Japanese classes so I could have real conversations rather than just order food. Learning to cook ramen would be another enjoyable goal.\n\nI like busy cities, and Tokyo seems to offer plenty to do. Its <strong class=\"key-phrase\">railway network</strong> would make travelling around convenient. I would enjoy looking at the food in department stores, especially the cakes and sushi. Living there would certainly bring challenges, including learning the language, but that is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">part of the attraction</strong>. I think Tokyo would give me a chance to experience a different way of life while discovering something new almost every day.","phrases":[{"en":"railway network","meaning":"A system of connected train routes."},{"en":"part of the attraction","meaning":"One of the reasons something seems interesting or appealing."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-032.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A city or town you have been to","prompt":"Describe a city or town you have been to\nYou should say:\nWhere it is\nWhen you visited it\nWhat you did there\nAnd explain why you like it","answer":"I'd like to talk about Singapore, one of several cities I've visited and really enjoyed. I went there on holiday last summer with my sister, Min, and we stayed for 5 days.\n\nSingapore's a city-state just to the southeast of Vietnam, and it only took us about 2 hours to fly there from Hanoi.\n\nWhen I arrived in Singapore, I was quite impressed by how clean the city looked. I could walk around all day and come back without a speck of dirt on my shoes. While I was there, I did some shopping and visited a few famous landmarks, like the Merlion statue, Singapore Zoo, and the botanical gardens.\n\nFor me, Singapore's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">well worth visiting</strong> for a few reasons. One of the main ones is that the public transport system there is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">extremely reliable</strong> — you don't have to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spend hours waiting for buses</strong> or trains, and the staff are always friendly and helpful. What I miss most about Singapore, though, is how multicultural it is. Over the 5 days I was there, I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bumped into people</strong> from all sorts of different countries. English is the first language, so it was great to get the chance to practise my English-speaking skills with locals and other tourists alike. Five days in Singapore passed by so fast that I wish I'd stayed a bit longer.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"well worth visiting","context":"For me, Singapore's well worth visiting for a few reasons."},{"en":"extremely reliable","context":"One of the main ones is that the public transport system there is extremely reliable — you don't have to spend hours waiting for buses or trains, and the staff are always friendly and helpful."},{"en":"spend hours waiting for buses","context":"One of the main ones is that the public transport system there is extremely reliable — you don't have to spend hours waiting for buses or trains, and the staff are always friendly and helpful."},{"en":"bumped into people","context":"Over the 5 days I was there, I bumped into people from all sorts of different countries."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a city or town you have been to","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-033","_displayNo":"#133","outline":{"topic":"Singapore","intro":"Singapore one several cities visited enjoyed","body":["vacationed wonderful city last summer w/ sister Min stayed 5 days","During stay Singapore shopping visited famous landmarks like Merlion statue Singapore","People not spend hours waiting buses trains staff friendly helpful","English 1st language country great get chance practice English-speaking skills w/"],"conclusion":"Five days Singapore passed fast wish stayed bit longer"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-033.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A city I have visited is Singapore. I went there last summer with my sister, Min. We flew from Hanoi, which took about two hours, and stayed for five days.\n\nMy first impression was how clean the streets were. We spent a lot of time walking around, shopping and visiting famous places. These included the Merlion statue, the zoo and the botanical gardens. I particularly enjoyed seeing different parts of the city rather than staying in just one area.\n\nAnother thing I liked was the public transport. The buses and trains were easy to use, and the staff were helpful when we needed directions. I also enjoyed meeting people from different countries. Because English was widely used, I had plenty of opportunities to practise speaking with local people and other visitors. The five days passed very quickly. I wished we had stayed longer because there were still places to explore. It was a convenient and enjoyable holiday, and I would happily return.","html":"A city I have visited is Singapore. I went there last summer with my sister, Min. We flew from Hanoi, which took about two hours, and stayed for five days.\n\nMy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">first impression</strong> was how clean the streets were. We spent a lot of time walking around, shopping and visiting famous places. These included the Merlion statue, the zoo and the botanical gardens. I particularly enjoyed seeing different parts of the city rather than staying in just one area.\n\nAnother thing I liked was the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">public transport</strong>. The buses and trains were easy to use, and the staff were helpful when we needed directions. I also enjoyed meeting people from different countries. Because English was widely used, I had plenty of opportunities to practise speaking with local people and other visitors. The five days passed very quickly. I wished we had stayed longer because there were still places to explore. It was a convenient and enjoyable holiday, and I would happily return.","phrases":[{"en":"first impression","meaning":"Your first opinion of a person or place."},{"en":"public transport","meaning":"Buses, trains and other services that many people can use."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-033.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A crowded place","prompt":"Describe a crowded place\nYou should say:\nWhere it was\nWhen you went there\nWhat you did there\nAnd explain how you felt about the crowded place.","answer":"I'd like to talk about a trip to Ba Den Pagoda that I still remember vividly, mainly because of the sheer number of visitors I ran into while I was there.\n\nSo usually, during the Tet holiday, my family drives out to Tay Ninh province to visit my grandfather, and last year we decided to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take a short trip to</strong> Ba Den Pagoda on Ba Den Mountain.\n\nBefore we even got on the cable car up to the pagoda, I had a feeling there'd be a lot of Buddhist worshippers there, since it's a sacred place that draws huge crowds all year round — let alone during Tet, which is the biggest holiday in Vietnam. And I was right. There were people carrying flowers and candles everywhere you looked. In one corner, there was a whole queue of people trying to light incense sticks, and inside the pagoda itself people were kneeling shoulder to shoulder, praying with their eyes closed. It was so packed that we could barely find room to stand, let alone move.\n\nIn the end, my mum and I pushed our way through the crowd and managed to get close enough to light some incense of our own and say a quick prayer, but my grandfather stayed outside because he <strong class=\"key-phrase\">can't stand</strong> all the smoke and the lack of oxygen in there. Honestly, by the time we got back to the car, I felt completely drained — it wasn't fear exactly, more just that sense of being crushed on every side for almost an hour.\n\nI understand why so many Vietnamese people love visiting pagodas and temples on these special days, to wish for good luck and happiness in the year ahead, but after that experience, I don't think I'll be rushing back to do it again anytime soon — maybe I'll visit on a quieter day instead, when there's actually room to breathe.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"take a short trip to","context":"So usually, during the Tet holiday, my family drives out to Tay Ninh province to visit my grandfather, and last year we decided to take a short trip to Ba Den Pagoda on Ba Den Mountain."},{"en":"can't stand","context":"In the end, my mum and I pushed our way through the crowd and managed to get close enough to light some incense of our own and say a quick prayer, but my grandfather stayed outside because he can't stand all the smoke and the lack of oxygen in there."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a crowded place","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-034","_displayNo":"#134","outline":{"topic":"Ba Den Pagoda","intro":"trip Ba Den Pagoda remember vividly sheer number visitors ran into","body":["during Tet holiday family drives Tay Ninh province visit grandfather took short trip","cable car up pagoda expected lot worshippers sacred place draws huge crowds Tet","people flowers candles everywhere queue light incense kneeling shoulder to shoulder praying","mum pushed through crowd lit incense prayed grandfather stayed outside can't stand smoke"],"conclusion":"felt drained crushed on every side understand why Vietnamese visit pagodas but won't rush back"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-034.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A crowded place I remember is Ba Den Pagoda in Tay Ninh province. My family visited during the Tet holiday last year, while we were staying with my grandfather. We took a cable car up Ba Den Mountain to reach it.\n\nI expected plenty of visitors, but the crowd was larger than I imagined. People were carrying flowers and candles, waiting to light incense and praying inside. The room was so full that moving around was difficult. My mother and I eventually reached a place where we could light incense and say a short prayer. My grandfather stayed outside because the smoke and crowded space made him uncomfortable.\n\nBy the time we returned to the car, I felt tired. I understood why people wanted to visit a religious place and wish for a good year, but I found the experience stressful. I would like to see the pagoda again on a quieter day. That would give me more time to enjoy the surroundings without feeling pushed by people on every side.","html":"A crowded place I remember is Ba Den Pagoda in Tay Ninh province. My family visited during the Tet holiday last year, while we were staying with my grandfather. We took a cable car up Ba Den Mountain to reach it.\n\nI expected plenty of visitors, but the crowd was larger than I imagined. People were carrying flowers and candles, waiting to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">light incense</strong> and praying inside. The room was so full that moving around was difficult. My mother and I eventually reached a place where we could light incense and say a short prayer. My grandfather stayed outside because the smoke and crowded space made him uncomfortable.\n\nBy the time we returned to the car, I felt tired. I understood why people wanted to visit a religious place and wish for a good year, but I found the experience stressful. I would like to see the pagoda again on a quieter day. That would give me more time to enjoy the surroundings without feeling pushed by people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on every side</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"light incense","meaning":"Set a scented stick burning, often as part of a religious practice."},{"en":"on every side","meaning":"All around someone or something."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-034.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A dream house or apartment you want to live in","prompt":"Describe a dream house or apartment you want to live in.\nYou should say:\n- Where it is\n- What your dream home is like\n- Why you like this dream home\n- What you would do to get it.","answer":"I'd like to talk about my vision of an ideal home — the place I really want to live in one day.\n\nMy long-term goal is to settle down in one of the villas in District 7, or somewhere similarly upscale in HCMC. I don't want to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">settle for anything less</strong> when it comes to housing, and District 7 has great access to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">infrastructure facilities</strong> — good schools, hospitals, roads, and plenty of shopping centres.\n\nThere's actually a specific villa in that area I have in mind: three storeys, a big garden, and a small pond right next to an old tree, which I'd stock with a few fish. I'd plant all sorts of trees and flowers around the garden and let my cats and dogs run around out there — I love animals, so there'd definitely be a few of those. Inside, I'd paint the walls in bright colours like yellow and white, my favourites, and hang up paintings from local artists.\n\nPart of the reason I want a villa specifically, rather than an apartment, is that I want a big family one day. I'd bring my parents to live with me and my wife, and together we'd <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bring up my future children</strong> right there in that garden.\n\nAs for actually getting there, I know it won't just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fall into my lap</strong>. I'm saving a fixed amount from my salary every month, and hoping a promotion at work in the next couple of years will <strong class=\"key-phrase\">speed things up considerably</strong>. Longer term, I'm also thinking about investing in a smaller property first, as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a stepping stone</strong>, rather than trying to buy the villa outright in one go.\n\nThis villa is going to be the symbol of everything I've worked for, and I honestly believe that if I stay focused on it, I can make it happen.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"settle for anything less","context":"I don't want to settle for anything less when it comes to housing, and District 7 has great access to infrastructure facilities — good schools, hospitals, roads, and plenty of shopping centres."},{"en":"infrastructure facilities","context":"I don't want to settle for anything less when it comes to housing, and District 7 has great access to infrastructure facilities — good schools, hospitals, roads, and plenty of shopping centres."},{"en":"bring up my future children","context":"Part of the reason I want a villa specifically, rather than an apartment, is that I want a big family one day. I'd bring my parents to live with me and my wife, and together we'd bring up my future children right there in that garden."},{"en":"fall into my lap","context":"As for actually getting there, I know it won't just fall into my lap."},{"en":"speed things up considerably","context":"I'm saving a fixed amount from my salary every month, and hoping a promotion at work in the next couple of years will speed things up considerably."},{"en":"a stepping stone","context":"Longer term, I'm also thinking about investing in a smaller property first, as a stepping stone, rather than trying to buy the villa outright in one go."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a dream house or apartment you want to live in","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-035","_displayNo":"#135","outline":{"topic":"ideal villa in District 7","intro":"vision ideal home want live one day","body":["settle District 7 upscale HCMC infrastructure schools hospitals roads shopping","specific villa in mind 3-storey garden pond old tree stock pond plant trees pets","want big family bring parents live with wife bring up future children","getting there saving salary monthly hoping promotion investing smaller property stepping stone"],"conclusion":"villa symbol everything worked for believe if stay focused can make happen"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-035.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"My dream home would be a villa in District 7 of Ho Chi Minh City. I like that area because it has good roads, schools, hospitals and shopping centres. It would be a convenient place for a family.\n\nI imagine a three-storey house with a large garden. There would be an old tree and a small pond with fish beside it. I would grow flowers and give my cats and dogs space to play. Inside, I would choose yellow and white walls and hang paintings by local artists.\n\nI want a house rather than a flat because I hope to have a large family. My parents could live with my wife, our future children and me. Buying a villa would be expensive, so I am saving part of my salary every month. I also hope for a promotion and may buy a smaller property first. This home represents a long-term goal for me: somewhere comfortable where the people I love can live together.","html":"My dream home would be a villa in District 7 of Ho Chi Minh City. I like that area because it has good roads, schools, hospitals and shopping centres. It would be a convenient place for a family.\n\nI imagine a three-storey house with a large garden. There would be an old tree and a small pond with fish beside it. I would grow flowers and give my cats and dogs space to play. Inside, I would choose yellow and white walls and hang paintings by local artists.\n\nI want a house rather than a flat because I hope to have a large family. My parents could live with my wife, our future children and me. Buying a villa would be expensive, so I am saving part of my salary every month. I also hope for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a promotion</strong> and may buy a smaller property first. This home represents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a long-term goal</strong> for me: somewhere comfortable where the people I love can live together.","phrases":[{"en":"a promotion","meaning":"A move to a higher position at work."},{"en":"a long-term goal","meaning":"Something you hope to achieve over a period of years."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-035.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A garden or park you have visited","prompt":"Describe a garden/park you have visited\nYou should say:\n- Where it was\n- What it looked like\n- What you did there\n- Why you liked it there.","answer":"I'd like to talk about Tao Dan Park, which is right in the centre of Ho Chi Minh City, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">just a ten-minute walk from</strong> where I work. It's actually one of the oldest parks in the city — it used to be part of the grounds of the old Governor's Palace back in the French colonial period, so some of the trees there are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">well over a hundred years old</strong>.\n\nAs for what it looks like, there are huge, shady trees everywhere, so even at midday it stays cool underneath them, which is rare in a city this hot. Stone paths wind between the flower beds, there are a couple of small lakes with turtles in them, and an old bandstand in the middle that gets used for events now and then. Early in the morning the whole place smells of frangipani, mixed in with birdsong and traffic noise from the streets outside.\n\nI go there most mornings <strong class=\"key-phrase\">before it gets too hot</strong>, usually around six, to run a couple of laps before work. By that time the park's already full — groups of older residents doing tai chi <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in perfect unison</strong>, a few men playing chess under the trees, and other joggers weaving between them like me. I remember one morning last year, right before a big exam, sitting on a bench watching an elderly woman feed the pigeons, and it somehow <strong class=\"key-phrase\">calmed me down</strong> more than any amount of revision had.\n\nThat's really why I keep going back — the park does something to your mood that's hard to explain. However stressed I am before I get there, I always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leave feeling lighter</strong>. It costs nothing, it's open to everyone, and unlike a gym or a cafe, nobody expects anything from you — you can just sit there for twenty minutes and nobody minds. That's what makes Tao Dan special, in a city that otherwise never really slows down.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"just a ten-minute walk from","context":"I'd like to talk about Tao Dan Park, which is right in the centre of Ho Chi Minh City, just a ten-minute walk from where I work."},{"en":"well over a hundred years old","context":"It's actually one of the oldest parks in the city — it used to be part of the grounds of the old Governor's Palace back in the French colonial period, so some of the trees there are well over a hundred years old."},{"en":"before it gets too hot","context":"I go there most mornings before it gets too hot, usually around six, to run a couple of laps before work."},{"en":"in perfect unison","context":"By that time the park's already full — groups of older residents doing tai chi in perfect unison, a few men playing chess under the trees, and other joggers weaving between them like me."},{"en":"calmed me down","context":"I remember one morning last year, right before a big exam, sitting on a bench watching an elderly woman feed the pigeons, and it somehow calmed me down more than any amount of revision had."},{"en":"leave feeling lighter","context":"However stressed I am before I get there, I always leave feeling lighter."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a garden/park you have visited","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-036","_displayNo":"#136","outline":{"topic":"Tao Dan Park","intro":"Tao Dan Park centre HCMC ten-minute walk from work old French colonial park","body":["huge shady trees everywhere stays cool stone paths small lakes turtles old bandstand","go most mornings around six run laps before work park full tai chi chess joggers","one morning before big exam watched elderly woman feed pigeons calmed me down","park does something to mood however stressed always leave feeling lighter"],"conclusion":"costs nothing open to everyone nobody expects anything special place city never slows down"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-036.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A park I visit is Tao Dan Park in central Ho Chi Minh City. It is about ten minutes' walk from my workplace. It is an old park, with very large trees that provide shade even on hot days.\n\nThere are stone paths, flower beds, small lakes and a space used for events. I usually go at about six in the morning and run a few laps before work. At that time, other people are already exercising, playing chess or doing tai chi. I enjoy seeing so many different activities in one place.\n\nOne morning before an important exam, I sat on a bench and watched an older woman feeding pigeons. The quiet moment helped me feel much calmer. That is the main reason I keep returning. The park gives me a break from traffic and work without costing anything. Sometimes I exercise there, and sometimes I simply sit under a tree. Either way, I usually leave in a better mood than when I arrived.","html":"A park I visit is Tao Dan Park in central Ho Chi Minh City. It is about ten minutes' walk from my workplace. It is an old park, with very large trees that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">provide shade</strong> even on hot days.\n\nThere are stone paths, flower beds, small lakes and a space used for events. I usually go at about six in the morning and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">run a few laps</strong> before work. At that time, other people are already exercising, playing chess or doing tai chi. I enjoy seeing so many different activities in one place.\n\nOne morning before an important exam, I sat on a bench and watched an older woman feeding pigeons. The quiet moment helped me feel much calmer. That is the main reason I keep returning. The park gives me a break from traffic and work without costing anything. Sometimes I exercise there, and sometimes I simply sit under a tree. Either way, I usually leave in a better mood than when I arrived.","phrases":[{"en":"provide shade","meaning":"Keep direct sunlight off an area."},{"en":"run a few laps","meaning":"Run around the same route several times."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-036.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A good place where people go to swim","prompt":"Describe a good place where people go to swim.\nYou should say:\nWhat is this place?\nWhere is it situated?\nWhy is it so popular?","answer":"To be honest, I'm pretty obsessed with swimming — it's a great way for me to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">build up my stamina</strong>. So I'd like to tell you about the Central Pool in District 4, which I go to most weekends.\n\nFirst, let me mention the size of the place, because it's what everyone talks about. It's the biggest swimming pool in HCM City, around 50 metres wide and 100 metres long, and it's split into two separate areas — one for adults and one for children.\n\nThe pool's open 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, and right next to it there's a huge restaurant specialising in Vietnamese and Japanese food. It's the perfect spot to unwind with something to eat after a long training session in the water. On top of that, the staff and lifeguards there are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">properly professional</strong>, and — more importantly to me — they're <strong class=\"key-phrase\">always punctual</strong>, which gives everyone swimming there a real sense of respect and satisfaction.\n\nFor all these reasons, the pool's always packed at weekends, so you have to book tickets in advance if you want any chance of getting in. I actually bought <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a season ticket</strong> for the whole year so I can just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gain access to the pool</strong> whenever I feel like it. If I had a bit more free time, I'd definitely invite more friends along so we could all train together.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"build up my stamina","context":"To be honest, I'm pretty obsessed with swimming — it's a great way for me to build up my stamina. So I'd like to tell you about the Central Pool in District 4, which I go to most weekends."},{"en":"properly professional","context":"On top of that, the staff and lifeguards there are properly professional, and — more importantly to me — they're always punctual, which gives everyone swimming there a real sense of respect and satisfaction."},{"en":"always punctual","context":"On top of that, the staff and lifeguards there are properly professional, and — more importantly to me — they're always punctual, which gives everyone swimming there a real sense of respect and satisfaction."},{"en":"a season ticket","context":"I actually bought a season ticket for the whole year so I can just gain access to the pool whenever I feel like it."},{"en":"gain access to the pool","context":"I actually bought a season ticket for the whole year so I can just gain access to the pool whenever I feel like it."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a good place where people go to swim","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-037","_displayNo":"#137","outline":{"topic":"Central Pool","intro":"completely honest w/ incredibly obsessed w/ swimming good way enhance","body":["name suggests biggest swimming pool HCM city 80m wide 120m long","pool open 24 hrs day 7 days week","reasons pool always crowded weekends people book tickets advance want chance"],"conclusion":"spare time definitely invite friends come w/ work out together"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-037.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A good place to swim is the Central Pool in District 4 of Ho Chi Minh City. I enjoy swimming because it helps me become fitter, so I go there most weekends.\n\nThe pool is very large, with separate areas for adults and children. The staff and lifeguards are professional, and I appreciate the way they manage the place. There is also a large restaurant nearby serving Vietnamese and Japanese food. After swimming for a while, it is pleasant to sit down and have something to eat.\n\nThe pool is open throughout the day and night, which makes visiting convenient. However, it gets particularly busy at weekends, so visitors often need to book tickets ahead of time. I bought a yearly ticket because I wanted to go regularly. I like having somewhere I can exercise and relax in the same visit. If I had more free time, I would invite friends to come with me so we could swim together rather than always training alone.","html":"A good place to swim is the Central Pool in District 4 of Ho Chi Minh City. I enjoy swimming because it helps me become fitter, so I go there most weekends.\n\nThe pool is very large, with separate areas for adults and children. The staff and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lifeguards</strong> are professional, and I appreciate the way they manage the place. There is also a large restaurant nearby serving Vietnamese and Japanese food. After swimming for a while, it is pleasant to sit down and have something to eat.\n\nThe pool is open throughout the day and night, which makes visiting convenient. However, it gets particularly busy at weekends, so visitors often need to book tickets <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ahead of time</strong>. I bought a yearly ticket because I wanted to go regularly. I like having somewhere I can exercise and relax in the same visit. If I had more free time, I would invite friends to come with me so we could swim together rather than always training alone.","phrases":[{"en":"lifeguards","meaning":"Trained people who help keep swimmers safe."},{"en":"ahead of time","meaning":"Before something happens or before it is needed."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-037.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A leisure facility (cinema, theatre, sports centre) you would like to have in your hometown","prompt":"Describe a leisure facility (cinema, theatre, sports centre) you would like to have in your hometown.\nYou should say:\n- What it is;\n- Why you would like to have this facility in your hometown;\n- When will you go there;\n- How the facility changes your life;\n- How you felt about it.","answer":"I'd like to talk about something my hometown doesn't have yet but really should: a proper indoor sports and climbing centre.\n\nRight now, if you want to do any indoor sport where I live, your only real option is a small, old badminton hall that's usually fully booked. There's nowhere to climb, no proper indoor running track for when it rains, and no space for group fitness classes. I'd love to see a modern centre built with a climbing wall, a couple of multi-purpose courts, and a running track around the edge of the building.\n\nIf it existed, I'd probably go most weekday evenings after work, since that's really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the only free time I get</strong> during the week, plus a longer session on Saturday mornings before the place gets busy.\n\nThe reason I think this matters is that our town has a lot of young people with plenty of energy and nowhere really to put it. At the moment, most of us either drive to the next city to use their facilities, which takes almost an hour, or we just don't exercise regularly at all. A <strong class=\"key-phrase\">well-designed centre</strong> close to home would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remove that barrier completely</strong>.\n\nI also think it would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bring the community together</strong> in a way we're currently missing. Sports centres are somewhere people of different ages naturally mix — kids after school, working adults in the evening, older residents in the daytime — and that kind of casual social contact is hard to create any other way.\n\nIf the local government ever built something like this, I honestly think it would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">transform how people spend their free time</strong> here, and it would probably keep quite a few young people from moving away simply to have better facilities. I'd honestly be first in the queue on opening day.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"remove that barrier completely","context":"A well-designed centre close to home would remove that barrier completely."},{"en":"bring the community together","context":"I also think it would bring the community together in a way we're currently missing."},{"en":"transform how people spend their free time","context":"If the local government ever built something like this, I honestly think it would transform how people spend their free time here, and it would probably keep quite a few young people from moving away simply to have better facilities."},{"en":"the only free time I get","context":"If it existed, I'd probably go most weekday evenings after work, since that's really the only free time I get during the week, plus a longer session on Saturday mornings before the place gets busy."},{"en":"well-designed centre","context":"A well-designed centre close to home would remove that barrier completely."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a leisure facility (cinema, theatre, sports centre) you would like to have in your hometown","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-038","_displayNo":"#138","outline":{"topic":"indoor sports centre","intro":"hometown lacks proper indoor sports centre - only option is old badminton hall","body":["would want climbing wall, multi-purpose courts, indoor running track","would go weekday evenings after work, longer session Saturday mornings","currently drive an hour to next city or don't exercise at all","would remove that barrier, give young people somewhere to put their energy","would bring community together - different ages naturally mixing there"],"conclusion":"would transform free time here, might stop young people moving away, genuinely excited about it"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-038.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A facility I would like in my hometown is an indoor sports and climbing centre. At the moment, we only have a small, old badminton hall, and it is usually fully booked. For better facilities, people often travel almost an hour to another city.\n\nMy ideal centre would have a climbing wall, courts for different sports and an indoor running track. It would also offer group exercise classes. I would probably go after work during the week and spend longer there on Saturday mornings.\n\nI think it would help many people exercise more regularly. Rain would no longer stop us from training, and travelling there would be easy. It could also bring local people together. Children could attend after school, adults could come in the evening, and older residents could use it during the day. Our town has many young people who need enjoyable activities nearby. I would be excited if the local government built a place like this, and I would certainly try it as soon as it opened.","html":"A facility I would like in my hometown is an indoor sports and climbing centre. At the moment, we only have a small, old badminton hall, and it is usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fully booked</strong>. For better facilities, people often travel almost an hour to another city.\n\nMy ideal centre would have a climbing wall, courts for different sports and an indoor running track. It would also offer group exercise classes. I would probably go after work during the week and spend longer there on Saturday mornings.\n\nI think it would help many people exercise more regularly. Rain would no longer stop us from training, and travelling there would be easy. It could also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bring local people together</strong>. Children could attend after school, adults could come in the evening, and older residents could use it during the day. Our town has many young people who need enjoyable activities nearby. I would be excited if the local government built a place like this, and I would certainly try it as soon as it opened.","phrases":[{"en":"fully booked","meaning":"Having no spaces available because all have been reserved."},{"en":"bring local people together","meaning":"Give people from the area opportunities to meet and interact."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-038.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A modern building","prompt":"Describe a modern building.\nYou should say:\n- where it is\n- what it is used for\n- and why you like/dislike it.","answer":"I'm going to describe a modern building in Manchester. It's called the Beetham Tower, and it's the tallest building in the city, with about 50 floors. Although it's called the Beetham Tower, most people know this building as the Hilton Hotel. In fact, the bottom half of the tower is the hotel and the top half is apartments. The apartments are expensive because the location and views make them very desirable.\n\nI'm not sure if I like the design of the building — it's just a huge glass tower — but it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">definitely stands out</strong>. It has become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a famous landmark</strong> in the city. You can see it as you approach Manchester, and it's an easy place to meet people because it's so distinctive and easy to find.\n\nThe most interesting thing about the Beetham Tower is that there's a bar and restaurant on the 23rd floor with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spectacular views</strong> of the city. It's definitely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the best vantage point</strong> in Manchester, because there are no walls, only huge windows, so you can look out over the city in any direction. I actually went up there for a friend's birthday last year, and I remember just standing at the window for ages, completely forgetting about the party behind me — on a clear day you can apparently see all the way out to the hills beyond the city, which I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wasn't expecting at all</strong>. I'd recommend anyone who visits Manchester to go there and experience the view for themselves.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"a famous landmark","context":"It has become a famous landmark in the city."},{"en":"spectacular views","context":"The most interesting thing about the Beetham Tower is that there's a bar and restaurant on the 23rd floor with spectacular views of the city."},{"en":"definitely stands out","context":"I'm not sure if I like the design of the building — it's just a huge glass tower — but it definitely stands out."},{"en":"the best vantage point","context":"It's definitely the best vantage point in Manchester, because there are no walls, only huge windows, so you can look out over the city in any direction."},{"en":"wasn't expecting at all","context":"I actually went up there for a friend's birthday last year, and I remember just standing at the window for ages, completely forgetting about the party behind me — on a clear day you can apparently see all the way out to the hills beyond the city, which I wasn't expecting at all."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a modern building","id":"speaking-part-2-039","_displayNo":"#139","outline":{"topic":"Beetham Tower","intro":"I'm going describe modern building Manchester","body":["It's called Beetham Tower it's tallest building city w/ 50 floors","bottom half tower hotel top half apartments","not sure like design but definitely stands out become famous landmark easy to find","bar/restaurant 23rd floor spectacular views best vantage point no walls huge windows","went up for a friend's birthday last year stood at window forgetting party hills beyond wasn't expecting"],"conclusion":"I'd recommend anyone visits Manchester go experience view"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-039.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A modern building I know is the Beetham Tower in Manchester. Many people call it the Hilton Hotel because the lower part of the tower is a hotel. The upper floors contain flats. It has about fifty floors and is easy to recognise from a distance.\n\nThe building is mainly glass, so its design is quite simple. I am not completely sure whether I like its appearance, but it certainly stands out. Because it is so easy to find, it is also a useful meeting point.\n\nThe part I like most is the bar and restaurant on the twenty-third floor. It has large windows with views across the city. I went there for a friend's birthday last year and spent a long time looking outside. On a clear day, you can see the hills beyond Manchester. The view surprised me and made the visit memorable. I would recommend going upstairs to visitors who want to see the city from a different angle.","html":"A modern building I know is the Beetham Tower in Manchester. Many people call it the Hilton Hotel because the lower part of the tower is a hotel. The upper floors contain flats. It has about fifty floors and is easy to recognise from a distance.\n\nThe building is mainly glass, so its design is quite simple. I am not completely sure whether I like its appearance, but it certainly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stands out</strong>. Because it is so easy to find, it is also a useful meeting point.\n\nThe part I like most is the bar and restaurant on the twenty-third floor. It has large windows with views across the city. I went there for a friend's birthday last year and spent a long time looking outside. On a clear day, you can see the hills beyond Manchester. The view surprised me and made the visit memorable. I would recommend going upstairs to visitors who want to see the city <strong class=\"key-phrase\">from a different angle</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"stands out","meaning":"Is easy to notice because it looks different."},{"en":"from a different angle","meaning":"From a new position or point of view."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-039.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A newly-opened shop","prompt":"Describe a newly-opened shop\nYou should say:\nWhere the shop is\nWhen it opened\nWhat it sells\nAnd how you feel about the shop.","answer":"I'm going to talk about a newly-opened convenience store in my neighbourhood called Circle K. Actually, Circle K's an extremely popular chain from America, and it's got multiple outlets around Hanoi now.\n\nBut the branch I'm talking about opened just up the road from where I live about four months ago, and I noticed it going up almost as soon as they started building it. It's also got this <strong class=\"key-phrase\">eye-catching logo</strong> — a giant red letter K inside a white circle — so it's pretty hard to miss.\n\nI think the main reason I became <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a regular customer</strong> there was one particular item they sell. I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on cloud nine</strong> when I discovered it — my favourite drink, Vodka Cruiser. They only contain about 4.5% alcohol, which is perfect for a lightweight like me.\n\nAfter that discovery, Circle K pretty much became a regular hangout spot for me and my mates. We'd sometimes meet up and sit outside the store with a couple of Cruisers, just chatting about life. It's actually a bit embarrassing to admit, but it's also become the place I go to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">let off some steam</strong> after a break-up, or whenever there's some other drama going on in my life.\n\nSo yeah, as long as Circle K keeps selling Vodka Cruisers, it'll always be my favourite shop.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"a regular customer","context":"I think the main reason I became a regular customer there was one particular item they sell. I was on cloud nine when I discovered it — my favourite drink, Vodka Cruiser. They only contain about 4.5% alcohol, which is perfect for a lightweight like me."},{"en":"eye-catching logo","context":"It's also got this eye-catching logo — a giant red letter K inside a white circle — so it's pretty hard to miss."},{"en":"on cloud nine","context":"I think the main reason I became a regular customer there was one particular item they sell. I was on cloud nine when I discovered it — my favourite drink, Vodka Cruiser. They only contain about 4.5% alcohol, which is perfect for a lightweight like me."},{"en":"let off some steam","context":"It's actually a bit embarrassing to admit, but it's also become the place I go to let off some steam after a break-up, or whenever there's some other drama going on in my life."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a newly-opened shop","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-040","_displayNo":"#140","outline":{"topic":"Circle K","intro":"newly-opened convenience store neighbourhood called Circle K popular chain from America","body":["branch opened just up road about four months ago eye-catching logo hard to miss","became regular customer bc Vodka Cruiser discovery on cloud nine 4.5% alcohol lightweight","became regular hangout spot me mates sit outside couple Cruisers chatting life","bit embarrassing also place let off steam after break-up other drama"],"conclusion":"long Circle K keeps selling Vodka Cruisers it'll always be favourite shop"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-040.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A newly opened shop in my neighbourhood is Circle K. It is a convenience store, part of an American chain with several branches in Hanoi. The branch near my home opened about four months ago.\n\nI noticed it while it was being built because it is just up the road. Its red K logo inside a white circle is easy to recognise. After it opened, I went in and discovered that it sold my favourite drink, Vodka Cruiser. This made me start visiting more regularly.\n\nSince then, the shop has also become a place where I meet friends. We sometimes sit outside and talk about our lives. I find these relaxed conversations helpful after a stressful day or a personal problem. Of course, the shop is convenient for everyday purchases too, but the social side is what makes it memorable for me. I like having a familiar place nearby where my friends and I can meet without arranging a formal evening out.","html":"A newly opened shop in my neighbourhood is Circle K. It is a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">convenience store</strong>, part of an American chain with several branches in Hanoi. The branch near my home opened about four months ago.\n\nI noticed it while it was being built because it is just up the road. Its red K logo inside a white circle is easy to recognise. After it opened, I went in and discovered that it sold my favourite drink, Vodka Cruiser. This made me start visiting more regularly.\n\nSince then, the shop has also become a place where I meet friends. We sometimes sit outside and talk about our lives. I find these relaxed conversations helpful after a stressful day or a personal problem. Of course, the shop is convenient for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">everyday purchases</strong> too, but the social side is what makes it memorable for me. I like having a familiar place nearby where my friends and I can meet without arranging a formal evening out.","phrases":[{"en":"convenience store","meaning":"A small shop selling everyday items, often open for long hours."},{"en":"everyday purchases","meaning":"Ordinary things that people buy regularly."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-040.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A place in your city you want to go to","prompt":"Describe a place in your city you want to go to\nYou should say:\n- What this place is.\n- Where this place is.\n- Why you like this place.\n- What do you want to do there.","answer":"Ho Chi Minh City is a popular <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tourist destination</strong> thanks to its fascinating culture, classic French architecture, sleek skyscrapers, and ornate temples and pagodas. Because of that, it's pretty difficult to pick out just one place among the millions of tourist attractions here.\n\nPersonally, I think Bitexco Financial Tower is the most impressive skyscraper. It's right in the heart of the city's business and entertainment district, and when it was completed in 2010, it was actually the tallest building in Vietnam — at least until January 2011.\n\nThe <strong class=\"key-phrase\">creative mastermind behind</strong> Bitexco drew inspiration for the skyscraper's unique shape from Vietnam's national flower, the lotus, which to Vietnamese people is a symbol of purity, commitment, and optimism. The 68-storey building's full of offices, shops, and restaurants, but the main attraction is the Sky Deck, where you can enjoy dramatic views right across the bustling city below.\n\nThere are lots of places I like to go in Ho Chi Minh City, depending on my mood, but if you want that hectic, modern buzz, Bitexco is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an ideal attraction to consider</strong>.\n\nAs for what I'd actually like to do there, my main reason for wanting to go back is the Sky Deck at sunset. I've only ever been during the day, and I'm told that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">watching the light change</strong> over the river and the rooftops is a completely different experience. I'd also love to take my parents, since neither of them has seen the city from above, and I think they'd be genuinely surprised by how much it's grown since they moved here.\n\nAfter that, I'd probably just stay for a coffee upstairs and watch the traffic, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">from that height</strong> stops looking stressful and starts looking almost orderly.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"tourist destination","context":"Ho Chi Minh City is a popular tourist destination thanks to its fascinating culture, classic French architecture, sleek skyscrapers, and ornate temples and pagodas."},{"en":"creative mastermind behind","context":"The creative mastermind behind Bitexco drew inspiration for the skyscraper's unique shape from Vietnam's national flower, the lotus, which to Vietnamese people is a symbol of purity, commitment, and optimism."},{"en":"an ideal attraction to consider","context":"There are lots of places I like to go in Ho Chi Minh City, depending on my mood, but if you want that hectic, modern buzz, Bitexco is an ideal attraction to consider."},{"en":"watching the light change","context":"I've only ever been during the day, and I'm told that watching the light change over the river and the rooftops is a completely different experience."},{"en":"from that height","context":"After that, I'd probably just stay for a coffee upstairs and watch the traffic, which from that height stops looking stressful and starts looking almost orderly."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a place in your city you want to go to","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-041","_displayNo":"#141","outline":{"topic":"Bitexco Financial Tower","intro":"Ho Chi Minh City popular tourist destination due fascinating culture","body":["result pretty difficult pick out one particular place among millions tourist","completion 2010 became tallest building Vietnam until January 2011","68-storey building features offices shops restaurants","main attraction tower Sky Deck visitors enjoy dramatic views across bustling","want to do = Sky Deck at sunset only been during day","take parents neither seen city from above surprised how much grown","coffee upper floor watch traffic from height looks almost orderly"],"conclusion":"coffee upper floor watch traffic from height looks almost orderly"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-041.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A place in Ho Chi Minh City I want to visit again is the Bitexco Financial Tower. It is in the central business and entertainment area. The tower has sixty-eight floors, with offices, shops and restaurants inside.\n\nIts shape was inspired by the lotus flower, which makes it different from many other tall buildings. The main attraction for me is the Sky Deck, where visitors can look across the city. I have been during the day, but next time I would like to go at sunset and watch the light change over the river and buildings.\n\nI would take my parents because they have never seen the city from that height. I think they would be surprised by how much it has grown. Afterwards, we could have coffee upstairs and watch the traffic below. From far above, the busy roads look less stressful. I like this idea because it combines a new view with time spent together as a family.","html":"A place in Ho Chi Minh City I want to visit again is the Bitexco Financial Tower. It is in the central business and entertainment area. The tower has sixty-eight floors, with offices, shops and restaurants inside.\n\nIts shape <strong class=\"key-phrase\">was inspired by</strong> the lotus flower, which makes it different from many other tall buildings. The main attraction for me is the Sky Deck, where visitors can look across the city. I have been during the day, but next time I would like to go <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at sunset</strong> and watch the light change over the river and buildings.\n\nI would take my parents because they have never seen the city from that height. I think they would be surprised by how much it has grown. Afterwards, we could have coffee upstairs and watch the traffic below. From far above, the busy roads look less stressful. I like this idea because it combines a new view with time spent together as a family.","phrases":[{"en":"was inspired by","meaning":"Was created using an idea taken from something else."},{"en":"at sunset","meaning":"At the time when the sun goes down in the evening."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-041.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A place of entertainment (e.g. a theatre, cinema or sports centre) that you would like to have near your home","prompt":"Describe a place of entertainment (e.g. a theatre, cinema or sports centre) that you would like to have near your home.\nYou should say:\n- what this place would be\n- where it should be\n- and why you think this place of entertainment is needed","answer":"The place of entertainment I wish we had closer to where I live is a sports centre. My ideal one would have a gym, a swimming pool, indoor and outdoor football pitches, courts for tennis, badminton and squash, and an all-purpose area for things like gymnastics and circuit training. I can actually picture the perfect spot for it too — there's a patch of disused land just a few minutes' walk from my house, where an old warehouse got knocked down a while back.\n\nIt's close to the main road and the train station, so it'd be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easy for people to get to</strong>. There are plans to build houses on that plot instead, but I honestly think a sports centre would do a lot more for local residents. The great thing about a sports centre, after all, is that literally everyone can use it.\n\nThere could be lessons and clubs for children and teenagers — things like swimming lessons and football practice sessions. I'm sure plenty of adults round here would join the gym, use the pool, or book the courts for a game of tennis or badminton. The centre could even host sports events, like local competitions, that would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">attract spectators as well as participants</strong>.\n\nI think we genuinely need something like this where I live, because at the moment people have to travel all the way to the next town for most of the facilities, activities, lessons and clubs I've just mentioned. I also think it'd give young people, in particular, something fun and productive to do in the evenings and at weekends, rather than nothing at all. Playing sport keeps people healthy and makes them happier, so a sports centre like this would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">brighten up my local area</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improve our quality of life</strong>.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"easy for people to get to","context":"It's close to the main road and the train station, so it'd be easy for people to get to."},{"en":"attract spectators as well as participants","context":"The centre could even host sports events, like local competitions, that would attract spectators as well as participants."},{"en":"brighten up my local area","context":"Playing sport keeps people healthy and makes them happier, so a sports centre like this would brighten up my local area and improve our quality of life."},{"en":"improve our quality of life","context":"Playing sport keeps people healthy and makes them happier, so a sports centre like this would brighten up my local area and improve our quality of life."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a place of entertainment (e.g., theatre, cinema)","id":"speaking-part-2-042","_displayNo":"#142","outline":{"topic":"sports centre","intro":"place entertainment wish close live sports centre","body":["ideal sports centre gym swimming pool indoor outdoor football pitches courts","sports lessons clubs children teenagers - things like swimming lessons football","sure adults local area join gym use pool book courts games","think need good sports centre live bc people currently travel next"],"conclusion":"Playing sport keeps people healthy makes feel happy think sports"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-042.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A place I would like near my home is a sports centre. I imagine a building with a gym, swimming pool, football pitches and courts for sports such as tennis and badminton. A shared exercise area could also be used for fitness classes.\n\nThere is an empty piece of land a few minutes' walk from my house where an old warehouse used to stand. It is near a main road and a train station, so people could reach it easily. Although houses are planned for the site, I think sports facilities would also be useful.\n\nAt present, residents have to travel to another town for many activities. A local centre could provide swimming lessons for children, exercise sessions for adults and competitions for the whole community. It would give young people something enjoyable to do after school and at weekends. I would use it regularly because being close to home would make exercise easier to fit into my routine. I believe it could improve both health and social life in our area.","html":"A place I would like near my home is a sports centre. I imagine a building with a gym, swimming pool, football pitches and courts for sports such as tennis and badminton. A shared exercise area could also be used for fitness classes.\n\nThere is an empty piece of land a few minutes' walk from my house where an old warehouse used to stand. It is near a main road and a train station, so people could reach it easily. Although houses are planned for the site, I think sports facilities would also be useful.\n\nAt present, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">residents</strong> have to travel to another town for many activities. A local centre could provide swimming lessons for children, exercise sessions for adults and competitions for the whole community. It would give young people something enjoyable to do after school and at weekends. I would use it regularly because being close to home would make exercise easier to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fit into my routine</strong>. I believe it could improve both health and social life in our area.","phrases":[{"en":"residents","meaning":"People who live in a particular area."},{"en":"fit into my routine","meaning":"Be possible to include in my usual daily activities."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-042.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A place people can listen to music. (such as a theatre or a music hall)","prompt":"Describe a place people can listen to music. (such as a theatre or a music hall)\nYou should say:\n- Where it is;\n- What kind of music is performed there;\n- What types of people go there;\n- Why people go there;\n- and how you felt about it.","answer":"There are plenty of places in my city where you can go to enjoy live music, but the one I'd recommend is the Hoa Binh Performing Arts Centre — most people just call it Hoa Binh Theatre, and it's probably the best-known one in the city.\n\nIt's in Ho Chi Minh City, close to the city centre, and I got the chance to visit about a year ago. From what I remember, the whole building's shaped like a circle, which is apparently done deliberately to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improve the acoustics</strong>.\n\nAll kinds of performances happen there, one after another throughout the year, and plenty of well-known orchestras and troupes, both local and international, have performed on that stage. When you go, you can hear all sorts of music, from pop and rock right through to traditional folk songs, and a lot of it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">doesn't cost a thing</strong>.\n\nAs for who goes there, it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a real mix</strong>. I noticed families with young kids, groups of university students, and quite a few older couples who clearly went for the classical performances. Some people go purely for the music, others just to people-watch or take photos of the building, and plenty go because it's one of the few places in the city where you can see something cultural without spending a lot of money.\n\nThe musicians themselves are seriously good — I remember sitting there completely still, with my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mind blown</strong> by how skilful some of the performers were. The theatre's also open for general sightseeing even when there's nothing on, so I'd strongly recommend a visit whether or not you catch an actual show. It's still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worth going</strong> just to appreciate the building's design or check out whatever art exhibition happens to be on at the time.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"improve the acoustics","context":"It's in Ho Chi Minh City, close to the city centre, and I got the chance to visit about a year ago. From what I remember, the whole building's shaped like a circle, which is apparently done deliberately to improve the acoustics."},{"en":"doesn't cost a thing","context":"When you go, you can hear all sorts of music, from pop and rock right through to traditional folk songs, and a lot of it doesn't cost a thing."},{"en":"a real mix","context":"As for who goes there, it's a real mix."},{"en":"mind blown","context":"The musicians themselves are seriously good — I remember sitting there completely still, with my mind blown by how skilful some of the performers were."},{"en":"worth going","context":"It's still worth going just to appreciate the building's design or check out whatever art exhibition happens to be on at the time."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a place people can listen to music. (such as a theatre or a music hall)","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-043","_displayNo":"#143","outline":{"topic":"Hoa Binh Performing Arts Centre","intro":"plenty places city enjoy live music recommend Hoa Binh Performing Arts Centre best-known","body":["close to city centre visited year ago building shaped circle improve acoustics","all kinds performances orchestras troupes local international pop rock folk mostly free","who goes real mix families young kids students older couples classical fans","musicians seriously good mind blown skilful performers"],"conclusion":"theatre open sightseeing even no show worth going appreciate design art exhibitions"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-043.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A place where people can listen to music is Hoa Binh Performing Arts Centre in Ho Chi Minh City. It is also known as Hoa Binh Theatre and is close to the city centre. I visited it about a year ago.\n\nI remember its round shape and the way sound carried clearly inside. The theatre presents different kinds of performances, including pop, rock, classical music and traditional songs. Both Vietnamese and international performers appear there. The audience is varied too. I saw families, students and older couples, all enjoying the same cultural space.\n\nWhat impressed me most was the skill of the musicians. Watching them perform live felt very different from listening to a recording. Some events are free, so people can enjoy music without spending much money. The building itself is also interesting to look around, and it sometimes has art exhibitions. I would recommend it to visitors who want to experience the city's cultural life. Even a short visit could introduce them to music they have not heard before.","html":"A place where people can listen to music is Hoa Binh Performing Arts Centre in Ho Chi Minh City. It is also known as Hoa Binh Theatre and is close to the city centre. I visited it about a year ago.\n\nI remember its round shape and the way sound carried clearly inside. The theatre presents different kinds of performances, including pop, rock, classical music and traditional songs. Both Vietnamese and international performers appear there. The <strong class=\"key-phrase\">audience</strong> is varied too. I saw families, students and older couples, all enjoying the same cultural space.\n\nWhat impressed me most was the skill of the musicians. Watching them perform live felt very different from listening to a recording. Some events are free, so people can enjoy music without spending much money. The building itself is also interesting to look around, and it sometimes has art exhibitions. I would recommend it to visitors who want to experience the city's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cultural life</strong>. Even a short visit could introduce them to music they have not heard before.","phrases":[{"en":"audience","meaning":"The people who watch or listen to a performance."},{"en":"cultural life","meaning":"The music, art and other creative activities of a place."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-043.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A place where people go to listen to music","prompt":"Describe a place where people go to listen to music\nYou should say:\nWhat kind of music is performed there\nWhat type of people go there\nWhy people go there\nExplain your impressions of this place","answer":"I'm going to talk about a music pub I go to pretty often, called Polygon. It's tucked away in a hidden corner of Cat Linh Street, which somehow makes it feel even better when you find it.\n\nI first found out about it through a close friend of mine, who's a massive fan of live music. Instead of sticking to just one genre, the place offers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a wide variety of</strong> styles depending on whoever's playing that night. Most of the bands play western pop, but on special occasions there are jazz nights or ballad nights too.\n\nFrom what I can tell, it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a real hotspot</strong> for younger people who are into band music. Some are just there for a drink with friends while nodding along to the music, some want a place to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dance the night away</strong>, and honestly, I'm mostly there to watch the artists <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sing their hearts out</strong> on stage — that's what I love most about it.\n\nAs for my impressions of the place, I'd describe it as somewhere that feels genuinely free. It's the kind of place where total strangers end up talking to each other just because of the music. The audience can even get up on stage if they fancy it, and the artists will happily <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improvise a tune</strong> if someone shouts out a request. I'd honestly recommend it to anyone who's into live band music.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"a wide variety of","context":"Instead of sticking to just one genre, the place offers a wide variety of styles depending on whoever's playing that night."},{"en":"a real hotspot","context":"From what I can tell, it's a real hotspot for younger people who are into band music."},{"en":"dance the night away","context":"Some are just there for a drink with friends while nodding along to the music, some want a place to dance the night away, and honestly, I'm mostly there to watch the artists sing their hearts out on stage — that's what I love most about it."},{"en":"sing their hearts out","context":"Some are just there for a drink with friends while nodding along to the music, some want a place to dance the night away, and honestly, I'm mostly there to watch the artists sing their hearts out on stage — that's what I love most about it."},{"en":"improvise a tune","context":"The audience can even get up on stage if they fancy it, and the artists will happily improvise a tune if someone shouts out a request."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a place where people go to listen to music","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-044","_displayNo":"#144","outline":{"topic":"Polygon","intro":"famous music pub frequently visit","body":["Instead playing one genre pub offers wide variety styles depending artists","bands mostly play western pop special occasions jazz ballad nights","personally love seeing talented artists singing heart out stage","audience take stage want artists improvise tune someone makes request"],"conclusion":"highly recommend pub anyone interested band music"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-044.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A place I like for live music is Polygon, a music pub on Cat Linh Street. It is in a fairly hidden corner, so I might not have found it without a close friend who recommended it.\n\nThe music changes depending on the band performing. Western pop is common, but there are also jazz and ballad evenings. Many younger people go there with friends. Some have a drink and listen, while others dance. I mainly enjoy watching the musicians perform because they put so much feeling into the songs.\n\nThe atmosphere is friendly and relaxed. People who have never met sometimes start talking because they like the same music. Audience members can even join the performers on stage, and the musicians may play a requested song. This makes each evening feel a little different. I like Polygon because the audience is involved rather than simply watching quietly. It is a good place to spend time with friends and enjoy the energy of a live band.","html":"A place I like for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">live music</strong> is Polygon, a music pub on Cat Linh Street. It is in a fairly hidden corner, so I might not have found it without a close friend who recommended it.\n\nThe music changes depending on the band performing. Western pop is common, but there are also jazz and ballad evenings. Many younger people go there with friends. Some have a drink and listen, while others dance. I mainly enjoy watching the musicians perform because they put so much feeling into the songs.\n\nThe atmosphere is friendly and relaxed. People who have never met sometimes start talking because they like the same music. Audience members can even join the performers on stage, and the musicians may play a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">requested song</strong>. This makes each evening feel a little different. I like Polygon because the audience is involved rather than simply watching quietly. It is a good place to spend time with friends and enjoy the energy of a live band.","phrases":[{"en":"live music","meaning":"Music performed by musicians in front of an audience."},{"en":"requested song","meaning":"A song that someone asks the performers to play."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-044.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A place where you can read and write","prompt":"Describe a place where you can read and write.\nYou should say:\nWhere this place is\nHow do you know this place\nWhat do you do there\nAnd explain why you think it's a good place for reading and writing.","answer":"I'm going to talk about the City Library, which is one of the largest libraries in Ho Chi Minh City. I went there for the first time about 3 years ago, when I was in my final year of university. It was right in the middle of my final exams, so I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">absolutely swamped</strong> with homework and revision. That's why I decided to try <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to ease my mind</strong> after all that stress by reading a few new books, and that's how I ended up visiting this library.\n\nThe library's roughly 5 kilometres from my house, in downtown Ho Chi Minh City, which is a pretty bustling area with loads of people, noise and traffic. It still attracts a huge number of visitors every day, mostly because of its enormous range of fascinating books.\n\nThis library's well known for how peaceful it is — it's basically heaven for anyone with a real passion for books, especially novels and comics. It's got these long tables and it's properly air-conditioned, so I ended up writing a lot of my essays there, since it gives you access to such <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a great range of learning materials</strong>. At first I thought the place was kinda boring, because I wasn't much of a reader back then. But after that first visit, it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be</strong> genuinely wonderful, and I've actually become a big reader ever since.\n\nEvery time I read there, I feel like I can shake off everyday stress and just switch off after a hard day. On top of that, the books and other materials there help me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expand my horizons</strong> and learn all sorts of new things. That's really why I like this building so much.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"absolutely swamped","context":"It was right in the middle of my final exams, so I was absolutely swamped with homework and revision."},{"en":"to ease my mind","context":"That's why I decided to try to ease my mind after all that stress by reading a few new books, and that's how I ended up visiting this library."},{"en":"a great range of learning materials","context":"It's got these long tables and it's properly air-conditioned, so I ended up writing a lot of my essays there, since it gives you access to such a great range of learning materials."},{"en":"turned out to be","context":"But after that first visit, it turned out to be genuinely wonderful, and I've actually become a big reader ever since."},{"en":"expand my horizons","context":"On top of that, the books and other materials there help me expand my horizons and learn all sorts of new things."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a place where you can read and write","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-045","_displayNo":"#145","outline":{"topic":"City Library","intro":"went 1st time like 3y ago senior college","body":["City Library one largest libraries Ho Chi Minh city","library less 5 kilometers away house located downtown Ho Chi Minh","essays written bc library provides w/ great range learning materials","Besides books materials library enable expand horizons learn lots new things"],"conclusion":"That's like building"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-045.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A place where I can read and write is the City Library in central Ho Chi Minh City. It is about five kilometres from my home. I first visited around three years ago, during my final university exams.\n\nAt that time, I had a lot of revision and homework. I wanted to relax with a book, so I decided to try the library. Although the surrounding streets are noisy, the rooms inside are peaceful. There are long tables, air conditioning and a large collection of books. I later began writing essays there because useful study materials were easy to find.\n\nBefore my first visit, I was not a keen reader and thought libraries might be boring. However, this one changed my opinion. I discovered novels, comics and books about new subjects. Reading there helps me put everyday worries aside for a while. I enjoy having somewhere quiet where I can either study seriously or simply read for pleasure. It has helped make reading a regular part of my life.","html":"A place where I can read and write is the City Library in central Ho Chi Minh City. It is about five kilometres from my home. I first visited around three years ago, during my final university exams.\n\nAt that time, I had a lot of revision and homework. I wanted to relax with a book, so I decided to try the library. Although the surrounding streets are noisy, the rooms inside are peaceful. There are long tables, air conditioning and a large collection of books. I later began writing essays there because useful <strong class=\"key-phrase\">study materials</strong> were easy to find.\n\nBefore my first visit, I was not a keen reader and thought libraries might be boring. However, this one changed my opinion. I discovered novels, comics and books about new subjects. Reading there helps me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put everyday worries aside</strong> for a while. I enjoy having somewhere quiet where I can either study seriously or simply read for pleasure. It has helped make reading a regular part of my life.","phrases":[{"en":"study materials","meaning":"Books and other resources used for learning."},{"en":"put everyday worries aside","meaning":"Stop thinking about ordinary problems for a while."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-045.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A place where you often visit with your friends or family","prompt":"Describe a place where you often visit with your friends or family\nYou should say:\nWhere the place is\nHow you know the place\nHow you go there\nWhy you want to visit","answer":"I'd like to talk about Freedom Park, which is right in the heart of District 4, where I've lived for about five years now.\n\nI first got to know the park just after I moved into the area. I didn't know anyone nearby yet, so one evening I just went out for a walk to explore, and I stumbled across it almost by accident. It's actually only a five-minute walk from my flat, so these days I just head there on foot whenever I feel like some fresh air.\n\nWhat I love about it is that early in the morning, before the traffic builds up, it's wonderfully quiet. Locals go there <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to take a stroll</strong> or work out before starting their day, and the park's got a decent range of public <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sports facilities</strong> that people use to keep fit and boost their <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mental wellness</strong>. There's also a little cafe right at the top of the hill that does a simple breakfast — fried rice, sausages, that sort of thing — so you can grab something to eat once you've finished exercising.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">If my memory serves me right</strong>, the first time I actually spent real time there, rather than just passing through, was about two years ago, when I went one evening with my girlfriend. It was incredibly peaceful — the sun was going down, the park lights had just come on, and we walked hand in hand along the little paths that wind through the trees. It's honestly one of my favourite memories of that whole year.\n\nThat's really why Freedom Park always comes out on top whenever I want to get back to nature and just switch off for a while. I don't get there as often as I'd like these days, so I'm hoping that once things settle down a bit, I can start going back regularly with my family and friends.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"to take a stroll","context":"Locals go there to take a stroll or work out before starting their day, and the park's got a decent range of public sports facilities that people use to keep fit and boost their mental wellness."},{"en":"sports facilities","context":"Locals go there to take a stroll or work out before starting their day, and the park's got a decent range of public sports facilities that people use to keep fit and boost their mental wellness."},{"en":"mental wellness","context":"Locals go there to take a stroll or work out before starting their day, and the park's got a decent range of public sports facilities that people use to keep fit and boost their mental wellness."},{"en":"If my memory serves me right","context":"If my memory serves me right, the first time I actually spent real time there, rather than just passing through, was about two years ago, when I went one evening with my girlfriend."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a place where you often visit with your friends or family","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-046","_displayNo":"#146","outline":{"topic":"Freedom Park","intro":"Freedom Park heart District 4, lived there 5 years","body":["1st got know park just after moved area, stumbled across it exploring evening","5 min walk from flat, head there foot fresh air, quiet mornings before traffic","locals stroll/work out, sports facilities boost mental wellness, cafe top hill breakfast","2 years ago went evening w/ girlfriend, hand in hand along paths, favourite memory"],"conclusion":"why always top list, get back to nature switch off, hope go back regularly w/ family friends"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-046.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A place I visit with people close to me is Freedom Park in District 4. I have lived in the area for about five years, and the park is only a five-minute walk from my flat. I discovered it while exploring the neighbourhood after moving in.\n\nIt is especially pleasant early in the morning, before the traffic becomes busy. People walk, exercise and use the public sports equipment. There is also a small cafe on a hill where visitors can have a simple breakfast after exercising.\n\nMy favourite memory there is an evening walk with my girlfriend about two years ago. The sun was setting and the park lights had just come on. We walked together along paths between the trees, and everything felt peaceful. That memory is one reason the park remains special to me. I do not visit as often as I would like now, but I hope to go more regularly with family and friends. It is a convenient place to enjoy fresh air and slow down.","html":"A place I visit with people close to me is Freedom Park in District 4. I have lived in the area for about five years, and the park is only a five-minute walk from my flat. I discovered it while exploring the neighbourhood after moving in.\n\nIt is especially pleasant early in the morning, before the traffic becomes busy. People walk, exercise and use the public sports equipment. There is also a small cafe on a hill where visitors can have a simple breakfast after exercising.\n\nMy favourite memory there is an evening walk with my girlfriend about two years ago. The sun was setting and the park lights had just come on. We walked together along paths between the trees, and everything felt peaceful. That memory is one reason the park remains special to me. I do not visit as often as I would like now, but I hope to go more regularly with family and friends. It is a convenient place to enjoy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fresh air</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">slow down</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"fresh air","meaning":"Air outside, especially somewhere open and pleasant."},{"en":"slow down","meaning":"Become less busy or hurried."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-046.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A place where you want to work","prompt":"Describe a place where you want to work\nYou should say:\n- where it is\n- what kind of work you would like to do\n- when you would like to work there\nAnd explain why you want to work in this place.","answer":"Well, the place I've always wanted to work, even though I haven't actually been there yet, is Tokyo, the capital of Japan.\n\nI first fell for the idea after watching a travel and lifestyle programme about it a few years back. It's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a major cosmopolitan city</strong>, obviously, but what struck me was how well it manages to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">preserve the cultural aspect</strong> of Japan at the same time — you'll see a beautiful old temple tucked right in among the skyscrapers, or women walking to work in kimonos, and it just looks amazing. On top of that, Hanoi, where I live now, has a pretty serious <strong class=\"key-phrase\">air pollution</strong> problem, so honestly, the idea of working somewhere with cleaner air is appealing on its own.\n\nAs for the kind of work, I'd like to go into marketing, ideally somewhere around Ginza, which is one of the busiest and most fashionable streets in Tokyo. There are quite a few agencies now that help graduates find internships in Japan, so I've actually been in touch with one, and if everything works out, I should be starting as a marketing intern there in about six months.\n\nI'm genuinely excited about it. Fingers crossed it all comes together, because it feels like it could be the start of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a completely new phase in my life</strong>.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"a major cosmopolitan city","context":"It's a major cosmopolitan city, obviously, but what struck me was how well it manages to preserve the cultural aspect of Japan at the same time — you'll see a beautiful old temple tucked right in among the skyscrapers, or women walking to work in kimonos, and it just looks amazing."},{"en":"preserve the cultural aspect","context":"It's a major cosmopolitan city, obviously, but what struck me was how well it manages to preserve the cultural aspect of Japan at the same time — you'll see a beautiful old temple tucked right in among the skyscrapers, or women walking to work in kimonos, and it just looks amazing."},{"en":"air pollution","context":"On top of that, Hanoi, where I live now, has a pretty serious air pollution problem, so honestly, the idea of working somewhere with cleaner air is appealing on its own."},{"en":"a completely new phase in my life","context":"I'm genuinely excited about it. Fingers crossed it all comes together, because it feels like it could be the start of a completely new phase in my life."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a place where you want to work","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-047","_displayNo":"#147","outline":{"topic":"Tokyo","intro":"always wanted to work in Tokyo, haven't been there yet","body":["1st fell for idea watching travel/lifestyle programme, cosmopolitan but preserves culture, temples/kimonos","Hanoi's air pollution problem, cleaner air appealing on its own","kind of work: marketing, Ginza, busy fashionable street, agencies help graduates find internships","in touch w/ agency, if works out starting as marketing intern in ~6 months"],"conclusion":"excited, fingers crossed, completely new phase in life"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-047.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A place where I would like to work is Tokyo, the capital of Japan. I have not been there yet, but a travel and lifestyle programme first made me interested in it a few years ago.\n\nWhat appealed to me was the combination of modern city life and Japanese traditions. There were tall office buildings near old temples, and people seemed to respect their culture while living in a busy international city. I currently live in Hanoi, where air pollution can be a problem, so the possibility of cleaner surroundings also attracts me.\n\nI would like to work in marketing, ideally near Ginza, a busy shopping and business area. I have contacted an agency that helps graduates find internships in Japan. If my plans work out, I may begin a marketing placement in about six months. I know moving abroad would require preparation and adjustment, but I feel excited. It would be a chance to gain work experience, learn about another culture and become more independent.","html":"A place where I would like to work is Tokyo, the capital of Japan. I have not been there yet, but a travel and lifestyle programme first made me interested in it a few years ago.\n\nWhat appealed to me was the combination of modern city life and Japanese traditions. There were tall office buildings near old temples, and people seemed to respect their culture while living in a busy international city. I currently live in Hanoi, where air pollution can be a problem, so the possibility of cleaner surroundings also attracts me.\n\nI would like to work in marketing, ideally near Ginza, a busy shopping and business area. I have contacted an agency that helps graduates find <strong class=\"key-phrase\">internships</strong> in Japan. If my plans <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work out</strong>, I may begin a marketing placement in about six months. I know moving abroad would require preparation and adjustment, but I feel excited. It would be a chance to gain work experience, learn about another culture and become more independent.","phrases":[{"en":"internships","meaning":"Short work placements that help people gain practical experience."},{"en":"work out","meaning":"Develop or finish successfully."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-047.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A place you visited","prompt":"Describe a place you visited\nYou should say:\nwhere it was (or is)\nwhen you visited it\nwhat it looked like\nAnd explain why you visited it.","answer":"I'd like to describe a small mountain town called Sapa, in the north of Vietnam, which I visited two years ago with my university friends.\n\nWe went in October, just before the rice terraces turn from green to gold, and stayed for three days. Sapa sits high up in the mountains, so the town itself is full of narrow, sloping streets, and it's surrounded by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">layer upon layer of terraced fields</strong> cut into the hillsides. Early in the morning, the whole valley is usually covered in mist, and it only clears a couple of hours later.\n\nI decided to visit mainly because a colleague had shown me photos of the terraces and I couldn't quite believe a landscape like that existed in Vietnam. We spent most of our time trekking between villages, chatting with local Hmong and Dao families, and eating in tiny family-run restaurants that served food I'd never tried before.\n\nWhat struck me most was how <strong class=\"key-phrase\">different the pace of life</strong> felt compared to Hanoi. Nobody seemed to be in a rush, and even the market only really got busy for a couple of hours each morning. On our last evening we watched the sunset from a hill just outside town, and the whole valley turned a deep orange colour, which is still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of the most beautiful sights</strong> I've ever seen.\n\nI'd love to go back one day, ideally in a different season, just to see how much the landscape changes throughout the year.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"layer upon layer of terraced fields","context":"Sapa sits high up in the mountains, so the town itself is full of narrow, sloping streets, and it's surrounded by layer upon layer of terraced fields cut into the hillsides."},{"en":"different the pace of life","context":"What struck me most was how different the pace of life felt compared to Hanoi."},{"en":"one of the most beautiful sights","context":"On our last evening we watched the sunset from a hill just outside town, and the whole valley turned a deep orange colour, which is still one of the most beautiful sights I've ever seen."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a place you visited","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-048","_displayNo":"#148","outline":{"topic":"Sapa","intro":"mountain town Sapa, north Vietnam, visited 2 years ago w/ uni friends","body":["went October before rice terraces turn gold, stayed 3 days, misty mornings","visited bc colleague's photos of terraces, couldn't believe landscape existed in Vietnam","trekked between villages, met Hmong/Dao families, tried new food","pace of life much slower than Hanoi, sunset on last evening unforgettable"],"conclusion":"would love to go back in a different season to see it change"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-048.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A place I visited is Sapa, a mountain town in northern Vietnam. I went there with university friends two years ago. We travelled in October and stayed for three days after a colleague showed me beautiful photos of the area.\n\nThe town has narrow streets on hillsides and is surrounded by fields built in steps along the mountains. In the morning, mist covered much of the valley before slowly clearing. We spent our days walking between villages, talking with Hmong and Dao families and trying food in small family restaurants.\n\nThe main difference from Hanoi was the slower pace of life. People seemed less hurried, and even the market was busy for only part of the morning. On our last evening, we watched the sunset from a nearby hill. The valley turned orange, and the view was wonderful. I enjoyed both the scenery and the opportunity to experience local life. I would like to return in another season to see how the fields and mountains change throughout the year.","html":"A place I visited is Sapa, a mountain town in northern Vietnam. I went there with university friends two years ago. We travelled in October and stayed for three days after a colleague showed me beautiful photos of the area.\n\nThe town has narrow streets on hillsides and is surrounded by fields built in steps along the mountains. In the morning, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mist</strong> covered much of the valley before slowly clearing. We spent our days walking between villages, talking with Hmong and Dao families and trying food in small family restaurants.\n\nThe main difference from Hanoi was the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">slower pace of life</strong>. People seemed less hurried, and even the market was busy for only part of the morning. On our last evening, we watched the sunset from a nearby hill. The valley turned orange, and the view was wonderful. I enjoyed both the scenery and the opportunity to experience local life. I would like to return in another season to see how the fields and mountains change throughout the year.","phrases":[{"en":"mist","meaning":"A thin cloud of tiny water drops near the ground."},{"en":"slower pace of life","meaning":"A way of living with less hurry and pressure."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-048.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A popular place","prompt":"Describe a popular place\nYou should say:\nWhere is this place?\nWhen did you first visit this place?\nHow often do you go to this place?\nAnd explain why it is so popular.","answer":"So today I want to talk about a swimming pool called Four Seasons, which I'd say is probably the most popular pool in the city — it's packed pretty much every single day.\n\nFour Seasons is an indoor public pool in my local sports complex. It's called Four Seasons because it's heated, so people can go and enjoy warm water all year round, even in winter. Swimming's become a really popular <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recreational activity</strong> here in Hanoi recently, for people of all ages, and I actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">took swimming lessons</strong> back when I was at school, so I've always felt comfortable in the water.\n\nI only started going there regularly about a year ago, when my best friend, who'd been a member for ages, finally dragged me along one weekend — and I've been hooked ever since. These days I try to go two or three times a week, usually early in the morning before work, because that's when it's quietest.\n\nI think Four Seasons is popular for a couple of reasons, really. Safety's clearly their top priority, for one — there's always a well-trained lifeguard on duty, ready to enforce the pool rules and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">perform any water rescues</strong> if something goes wrong. And the facilities are excellent too: the water's kept at a nice temperature and regularly sanitised, and the changing rooms are clean, with proper showers and big lockers for your things. Honestly, I think the pool totally <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deserves its great reputation</strong>.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"recreational activity","context":"Swimming's become a really popular recreational activity here in Hanoi recently, for people of all ages, and I actually took swimming lessons back when I was at school, so I've always felt comfortable in the water."},{"en":"took swimming lessons","context":"Swimming's become a really popular recreational activity here in Hanoi recently, for people of all ages, and I actually took swimming lessons back when I was at school, so I've always felt comfortable in the water."},{"en":"perform any water rescues","context":"Safety's clearly their top priority, for one — there's always a well-trained lifeguard on duty, ready to enforce the pool rules and perform any water rescues if something goes wrong."},{"en":"deserves its great reputation","context":"Honestly, I think the pool totally deserves its great reputation."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a popular place","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-049","_displayNo":"#149","outline":{"topic":"Four Seasons swimming pool","intro":"Four Seasons pool, probably most popular pool in the city, packed daily","body":["indoor public pool local sports complex, heated, warm water year round","swimming popular recreational activity in Hanoi, took swimming lessons at school","started going regularly ~1 year ago, best friend dragged me along, now go 2-3x/week","popular bc: safety/lifeguard/water rescues, and excellent clean facilities"],"conclusion":"totally deserves its great reputation"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-049.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A popular place in Hanoi is the Four Seasons swimming pool at my local sports complex. It is an indoor pool with heated water, so people can use it comfortably throughout the year, including winter.\n\nI learned to swim at school, but I only started visiting this pool regularly about a year ago. My best friend invited me one weekend, and I enjoyed it so much that I kept going. Now I try to swim two or three mornings a week before work, when it is quieter.\n\nI think the pool is popular because it is clean and well managed. A trained lifeguard is always there, and staff take safety seriously. The changing rooms have good showers and large lockers, which make the visit convenient. I also appreciate the comfortable water temperature. Swimming helps me exercise without feeling too hot or tired afterwards. Although the pool can be crowded, I think its good reputation is deserved. It has become an enjoyable and useful part of my weekly routine.","html":"A popular place in Hanoi is the Four Seasons swimming pool at my local <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sports complex</strong>. It is an indoor pool with heated water, so people can use it comfortably throughout the year, including winter.\n\nI learned to swim at school, but I only started visiting this pool regularly about a year ago. My best friend invited me one weekend, and I enjoyed it so much that I kept going. Now I try to swim two or three mornings a week before work, when it is quieter.\n\nI think the pool is popular because it is clean and well managed. A trained lifeguard is always there, and staff take safety seriously. The changing rooms have good showers and large lockers, which make the visit convenient. I also appreciate the comfortable water temperature. Swimming helps me exercise without feeling too hot or tired afterwards. Although the pool can be crowded, I think its <strong class=\"key-phrase\">good reputation</strong> is deserved. It has become an enjoyable and useful part of my weekly routine.","phrases":[{"en":"sports complex","meaning":"A group of facilities where people can do different sports."},{"en":"good reputation","meaning":"A positive opinion that many people have about something."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-049.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A public place that you think needs improvements","prompt":"Describe a public place that you think needs improvements.\nYou should say:\n- What the place is.\n- When you visit the place.\n- What do you like and dislike about the public place","answer":"A public place that needs improvement is Dam Sen Park in Ho Chi Minh City. It's a large amusement park that many families visit at weekends, and parts of it still feel like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a green oasis</strong> in a crowded city.\n\nI went there recently with my nieces and nephews. The children enjoyed the rides, but I noticed that several areas had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fallen into disrepair</strong>. There weren't enough rubbish bins, some of the walls were covered in moss, and weeds had grown up around equipment nobody uses any more. The thing that really worried me, though, was finding discarded needles near a quiet corner of the park — that's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a serious safety hazard</strong>, especially for kids in thin sandals.\n\nI don't think the authorities need to redesign the whole park. They should just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carry out basic maintenance</strong> first — clear away anything dangerous, fix the broken paths, improve the lighting, and put more bins in the busy areas. Regular cleaning and a visible security presence would also stop people littering or messing about. Longer term, they could renovate the old toilets and the buildings that have just been left abandoned.\n\nI still like the park — it's affordable and it's a great spot for a family day out — but people won't keep coming back if they don't feel safe. A fairly modest programme of cleaning and repairs could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">restore public confidence</strong> and make the park feel welcoming again.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"a green oasis","context":"A public place that needs improvement is Dam Sen Park in Ho Chi Minh City. It's a large amusement park that many families visit at weekends, and parts of it still feel like a green oasis in a crowded city."},{"en":"fallen into disrepair","context":"The children enjoyed the rides, but I noticed that several areas had fallen into disrepair."},{"en":"a serious safety hazard","context":"The thing that really worried me, though, was finding discarded needles near a quiet corner of the park — that's a serious safety hazard, especially for kids in thin sandals."},{"en":"carry out basic maintenance","context":"They should just carry out basic maintenance first — clear away anything dangerous, fix the broken paths, improve the lighting, and put more bins in the busy areas."},{"en":"restore public confidence","context":"A fairly modest programme of cleaning and repairs could restore public confidence and make the park feel welcoming again."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a public place that you think needs improvements","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-050","_displayNo":"#150","outline":{"topic":"Dam Sen Park","intro":"large amusement park families visit weekends parts feel like green","body":["public place needs improvement Dam Sen Park Ho Chi Minh City","too few rubbish bins walls covered moss weeds grown around unused","1st carry out basic maintenance remove dangerous waste repair damaged paths","relatively modest programme cleaning repairs restore public confidence make park attractive"],"conclusion":"relatively modest programme cleaning repairs restore public confidence make park"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-050.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A public place that needs improvement is Dam Sen Park in Ho Chi Minh City. It is a large amusement park, and I recently went there with my nieces and nephews. They enjoyed the rides, but I noticed some problems.\n\nSeveral areas needed cleaning and repairs. There were not enough rubbish bins, some walls were covered in moss, and unused equipment had weeds around it. Most worrying was some discarded needles in a quiet corner. This made me concerned about children walking nearby.\n\nI do not think the whole park needs to be rebuilt. Basic maintenance would make a big difference. Staff should remove dangerous rubbish, repair paths, improve lighting and add bins in busy places. Regular cleaning and security would also help. Later, the old toilets and empty buildings could be repaired. I still like the park because it is an affordable place for families to spend a day. However, visitors need to feel safe. A cleaner, better-maintained park would encourage people like me to come back.","html":"A public place that needs improvement is Dam Sen Park in Ho Chi Minh City. It is a large amusement park, and I recently went there with my nieces and nephews. They enjoyed the rides, but I noticed some problems.\n\nSeveral areas needed cleaning and repairs. There were not enough rubbish bins, some walls were covered in moss, and unused equipment had weeds around it. Most worrying was some <strong class=\"key-phrase\">discarded needles</strong> in a quiet corner. This made me concerned about children walking nearby.\n\nI do not think the whole park needs to be rebuilt. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Basic maintenance</strong> would make a big difference. Staff should remove dangerous rubbish, repair paths, improve lighting and add bins in busy places. Regular cleaning and security would also help. Later, the old toilets and empty buildings could be repaired. I still like the park because it is an affordable place for families to spend a day. However, visitors need to feel safe. A cleaner, better-maintained park would encourage people like me to come back.","phrases":[{"en":"Basic maintenance","meaning":"Regular simple work to keep a place safe and in good condition."},{"en":"discarded needles","meaning":"Used or unwanted needles that have been thrown away."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-050.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A school that you went to when you were a child","prompt":"Describe a school that you went to when you were a child.\nYou should say:\n- where the school was\n- when you went there\n- what the school and the teachers were like\n- and explain whether you enjoyed your time there.","answer":"I'd like to talk about the primary school I went to in the town where I grew up. It was a small state school <strong class=\"key-phrase\">within walking distance</strong> of my family home, so between the ages of five and eleven, I walked there with my older sister almost every single day, rain or shine.\n\nThe school itself wasn't anything fancy — just six classrooms, one for each year group, plus a small library, a sports field and a big assembly hall where we all gathered on Monday mornings. At the time it felt huge to me, but when I went back and had a look years later, I realised how compact it actually was. The teachers were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caring but firm</strong>; they never let us get away with being lazy, but they also made the classroom feel safe enough that we weren't afraid to put our hands up and get things wrong.\n\nWhat I remember most clearly, though, is taking part in the school play in my final year. I had a small speaking part, just a handful of lines, but I had to say them in front of the whole school and all the parents, and I was terrified beforehand. Somehow I didn't forget a single word, and walking off stage afterwards gave me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a genuine sense of achievement</strong> that I still remember now.\n\nLooking back, I've got really fond memories of that school. It gave me some close friendships and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a strong academic foundation</strong>, but more than that, it taught me that confidence isn't something you're born with — it's something you build gradually, usually by doing things that scare you a bit first.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"within walking distance","context":"It was a small state school within walking distance of my family home, so between the ages of five and eleven, I walked there with my older sister almost every single day, rain or shine."},{"en":"caring but firm","context":"The teachers were caring but firm; they never let us get away with being lazy, but they also made the classroom feel safe enough that we weren't afraid to put our hands up and get things wrong."},{"en":"a genuine sense of achievement","context":"Somehow I didn't forget a single word, and walking off stage afterwards gave me a genuine sense of achievement that I still remember now."},{"en":"a strong academic foundation","context":"It gave me some close friendships and a strong academic foundation, but more than that, it taught me that confidence isn't something you're born with — it's something you build gradually, usually by doing things that scare you a bit first."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a school that you went to when you were a child","id":"speaking-part-2-052","_displayNo":"#151","outline":{"topic":"my childhood primary school","intro":"going describe primary school attended town grew up","body":["small state school within walking distance family home walked almost day","teachers caring firm created atmosphere children felt safe enough ask questions","remember clearly taking part school play during final year","fond memories school bc gave close friendships strong academic foundation"],"conclusion":"importantly taught confidence developed gradually through supportive challenges"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-052.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A school I attended as a child was a small state primary school in my hometown. It was close to our house, so I walked there with my older sister almost every day from the age of five to eleven.\n\nThe school had six classrooms, a small library, a sports field and a large hall for assemblies. It seemed enormous when I was young, although it looked much smaller when I returned years later. The teachers were caring but expected us to work hard. We felt comfortable asking questions, even when our answers were wrong.\n\nMy clearest memory is taking part in a school play in my final year. I only had a few lines, but speaking in front of the school and our parents frightened me. Luckily, I remembered everything. Afterwards, I felt proud and more confident. The school gave me useful knowledge and good friendships, but that performance taught me another lesson: confidence can grow when you try something that feels difficult at first.","html":"A school I attended as a child was a small <strong class=\"key-phrase\">state primary school</strong> in my hometown. It was close to our house, so I walked there with my older sister almost every day from the age of five to eleven.\n\nThe school had six classrooms, a small library, a sports field and a large hall for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">assemblies</strong>. It seemed enormous when I was young, although it looked much smaller when I returned years later. The teachers were caring but expected us to work hard. We felt comfortable asking questions, even when our answers were wrong.\n\nMy clearest memory is taking part in a school play in my final year. I only had a few lines, but speaking in front of the school and our parents frightened me. Luckily, I remembered everything. Afterwards, I felt proud and more confident. The school gave me useful knowledge and good friendships, but that performance taught me another lesson: confidence can grow when you try something that feels difficult at first.","phrases":[{"en":"state primary school","meaning":"A school for young children funded by the government."},{"en":"assemblies","meaning":"Meetings where many or all students in a school gather together."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-052.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A shop that has opened in your hometown","prompt":"Describe a shop that has opened in your hometown\nYou should say:\nWhat shop it is\nWhat it sells (or, what you usually buy there)\nWhen you first started going to this shop (or, how you came to know about this\nshop)\nAnd explain why you like (to go to) this shop","answer":"Well, a shop that's just opened in my hometown that I want to talk about is Tranquil Cafe, which I'd say is one of the best book-and-coffee shops in Hanoi.\n\nIt's decorated in an old, traditional Hanoi style, with bookshelves everywhere, and it actually attracts quite a few foreign tourists too. They do lovely coffee — I always go for a black coffee with condensed milk — plus cakes and a few light snacks, and there's a whole wall of books you're welcome to borrow while you sit there.\n\nI still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">vividly remember</strong> the first time I went, about three months ago, which happened to be the opening day. It was completely by chance — I'd read an article online about a new spot everyone in Hanoi was starting to talk about, and I was curious enough to go and check it out. The place <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caught my eye</strong> the second I walked in. The whole space was so unique, with gorgeous paintings on the walls and quirky little touches everywhere, and it somehow made me feel completely at home.\n\nThe main reason I love it, though, is the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">warm ambience</strong> — I haven't found that anywhere else in the city. Hanoi's such a busy, noisy place, and I'm always a bit wound up after fighting through the traffic, so I often go there as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a hideaway</strong>, just to get away from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the hustle and bustle</strong> for an hour. A coffee and a good book there is honestly one of the most soothing things I know. On Saturday nights, one of the staff sometimes plays live acoustic music too, which makes it even better. And the waiters remember their regulars — they always give me a little discount now, which is a nice touch.\n\nSo yeah, for all those reasons, I know I'll be going back <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the long run</strong>.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"vividly remember","context":"I still vividly remember the first time I went, about three months ago, which happened to be the opening day."},{"en":"caught my eye","context":"The place caught my eye the second I walked in."},{"en":"warm ambience","context":"The main reason I love it, though, is the warm ambience — I haven't found that anywhere else in the city."},{"en":"a hideaway","context":"Hanoi's such a busy, noisy place, and I'm always a bit wound up after fighting through the traffic, so I often go there as a hideaway, just to get away from the hustle and bustle for an hour."},{"en":"the hustle and bustle","context":"Hanoi's such a busy, noisy place, and I'm always a bit wound up after fighting through the traffic, so I often go there as a hideaway, just to get away from the hustle and bustle for an hour."},{"en":"in the long run","context":"So yeah, for all those reasons, I know I'll be going back in the long run."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a shop that has opened in your hometown","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-053","_displayNo":"#152","outline":{"topic":"Tranquil Cafe","intro":"shop just opened in hometown, Tranquil Cafe, one of best book/coffee shops in Hanoi","body":["old traditional Hanoi style, bookshelves, attracts tourists; sells coffee/cakes/snacks, books to borrow","1st went ~3 months ago, opening day, by chance read article online, caught my eye walking in","warm ambience main reason love it, Hanoi busy/noisy, hideaway from hustle bustle, coffee+book soothing","Saturday nights live music, waiters remember regulars, small discount"],"conclusion":"keep going back in the long run"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-053.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A shop that recently opened in my hometown is Tranquil Cafe, a book-and-coffee shop in Hanoi. It has traditional-style decoration, paintings and shelves full of books that customers can read during their visit.\n\nI first went there about three months ago after reading an online article. By chance, my visit was on opening day. I liked the place immediately because it felt warm and comfortable. It serves coffee, cakes and light snacks. I normally order coffee with condensed milk and choose a book from the shelves.\n\nThe main reason I return is the peaceful atmosphere. Hanoi's traffic can leave me feeling stressed, so sitting with a drink and a book is a welcome break. On some Saturday evenings, a member of staff plays acoustic music, which adds to the experience. The staff also remember regular customers and sometimes give me a small discount. I like having a place where reading and relaxing are equally welcome, and I expect to keep visiting it for a long time.","html":"A shop that recently opened in my hometown is Tranquil Cafe, a book-and-coffee shop in Hanoi. It has traditional-style decoration, paintings and shelves full of books that customers can read during their visit.\n\nI first went there about three months ago after reading an online article. By chance, my visit was on opening day. I liked the place immediately because it felt warm and comfortable. It serves coffee, cakes and light snacks. I normally order coffee with condensed milk and choose a book from the shelves.\n\nThe main reason I return is the peaceful atmosphere. Hanoi's traffic can leave me feeling stressed, so sitting with a drink and a book is a welcome break. On some Saturday evenings, a member of staff plays <strong class=\"key-phrase\">acoustic music</strong>, which adds to the experience. The staff also remember <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regular customers</strong> and sometimes give me a small discount. I like having a place where reading and relaxing are equally welcome, and I expect to keep visiting it for a long time.","phrases":[{"en":"acoustic music","meaning":"Music mainly played with instruments that do not need electronic sound effects."},{"en":"regular customers","meaning":"People who visit and buy things from the same business often."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-053.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A shopping street you would like to go","prompt":"Describe a shopping street you would like to go.\nYou should say:\nWhat is this place?\nWhere is it situated?\nWhy is it so popular?","answer":"Right, today I want to talk about Nguyen Trai Street, one of the busiest shopping streets in Ho Chi Minh City.\n\nIt's in District 5, about twenty minutes by scooter from where I live. I first went there years ago, in my first year at university, right after a brutal exam period. A couple of my flatmates practically dragged me out of the house <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to chill out</strong> and clear my head, and we ended up just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wandering around</strong> the neighbourhood with no real plan — that's how we stumbled on it.\n\nHonestly, I wasn't expecting much. I'd always found busy, crowded streets a bit overwhelming, so I assumed I'd want to leave after ten minutes. It <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be</strong> the opposite — there were stalls selling grilled corn and fried spring rolls right next to little shops full of cheap, trendy clothes, and the whole street had this buzzing, festival-like energy I hadn't expected at all. I think it's especially popular with students, since everything's so cheap and most stalls stay open really late.\n\nThese days I don't get there anywhere near as often as I'd like — maybe once or twice a year — but whenever exams or work get on top of me, it's still the first place that comes to mind <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to relieve everyday stress</strong>. There's something about eating street food and browsing stalls with no agenda that clears my head better than anything else. I'd really like to take my younger cousins there next time they visit, actually, just to see it through their eyes.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"to chill out","context":"A couple of my flatmates practically dragged me out of the house to chill out and clear my head, and we ended up just wandering around the neighbourhood with no real plan — that's how we stumbled on it."},{"en":"wandering around","context":"A couple of my flatmates practically dragged me out of the house to chill out and clear my head, and we ended up just wandering around the neighbourhood with no real plan — that's how we stumbled on it."},{"en":"turned out to be","context":"It turned out to be the opposite — there were stalls selling grilled corn and fried spring rolls right next to little shops full of cheap, trendy clothes, and the whole street had this buzzing, festival-like energy I hadn't expected at all."},{"en":"to relieve everyday stress","context":"These days I don't get there anywhere near as often as I'd like — maybe once or twice a year — but whenever exams or work get on top of me, it's still the first place that comes to mind to relieve everyday stress."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a shopping street you would like to go","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-054","_displayNo":"#153","outline":{"topic":"Nguyen Trai Street","intro":"Nguyen Trai Street, one of busiest shopping streets in HCMC, District 5","body":["~20 min by scooter, 1st went uni 1st year after exams, flatmates dragged me out to chill/wander","wasn't expecting much, crowded streets usually overwhelming, assumed I'd leave after 10 min","turned out opposite: grilled corn/spring rolls next to cheap trendy clothes, festival energy, popular w/ students","don't go often now (once/twice a yr), still 1st place I think of to relieve stress, food+browsing no agenda"],"conclusion":"want to take younger cousins next time, see it through their eyes"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-054.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A shopping street I would like to visit again is Nguyen Trai Street in District 5 of Ho Chi Minh City. It is about twenty minutes from my home by scooter. I first discovered it during my first year at university.\n\nI had just finished a difficult exam period when my flatmates persuaded me to go out. We walked around without a plan and found the street. At first, I thought the crowds would make me uncomfortable. Instead, I enjoyed the lively atmosphere. There were stalls selling grilled corn and spring rolls beside shops with inexpensive, fashionable clothes.\n\nThe street is popular with students because prices are affordable and many places stay open late. I now go only once or twice a year, but I still think of it when I need a break from work or study. Browsing shops and eating street food helps me relax. Next time my younger cousins visit, I would like to take them there and share a place that has given me many enjoyable afternoons.","html":"A shopping street I would like to visit again is Nguyen Trai Street in District 5 of Ho Chi Minh City. It is about twenty minutes from my home by scooter. I first discovered it during my first year at university.\n\nI had just finished a difficult exam period when my flatmates persuaded me to go out. We walked around without a plan and found the street. At first, I thought the crowds would make me uncomfortable. Instead, I enjoyed the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lively atmosphere</strong>. There were stalls selling grilled corn and spring rolls beside shops with inexpensive, fashionable clothes.\n\nThe street is popular with students because prices are affordable and many places stay open late. I now go only once or twice a year, but I still think of it when I need a break from work or study. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Browsing shops</strong> and eating street food helps me relax. Next time my younger cousins visit, I would like to take them there and share a place that has given me many enjoyable afternoons.","phrases":[{"en":"lively atmosphere","meaning":"A feeling of activity, energy and enjoyment in a place."},{"en":"Browsing shops","meaning":"Looking around shops without necessarily planning to buy something."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-054.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A street you know well","prompt":"Describe a street you know well\nYou should say:\nWhere it is\nWhat the main buildings are\nHow often you go there\nAnd explain how you feel about this street","answer":"Well, there are quite a few streets I could talk about, but the one I most enjoy going to is Ly Thai To Street, which is one of the most popular tourist areas in Hanoi.\n\nIt's right in the centre of the city, close to Hoan Kiem Lake, about fifteen to twenty minutes' drive from where I live, so I normally just hop on my scooter and go. In terms of buildings, it's mostly lined with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">colonial-style architecture</strong> — old French villas with high ceilings and shuttered windows — and it's only a short walk from the Hanoi Opera House, which is easily <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the grandest building</strong> in that part of town.\n\nAs for how often I go, honestly, probably only once or twice a month. I know that's not a lot, but I just don't have the time to go as often as I'd like.\n\nMost of the time I go for the street food, if I'm being honest — there are loads of stalls doing really good barbecued squid, bacon sandwiches, chicken wings, that kind of thing, so that's basically my main reason for going. There are also a few little shops selling souvenirs and handicrafts, but I don't spend long in them because everything's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wildly overpriced</strong> — a pack of postcards there will probably <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set you back</strong> about 30,000 dong, whereas the same thing in any bookshop would only cost about a third of that.\n\nBut in terms of why I actually like the street, it's mainly the food, like I said, plus the fact that it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">closed off to traffic</strong>, so you've got loads of space to walk around without worrying about getting hit by a scooter. And because most of the buildings are so old, the whole street's just got this really nice, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">old-world charm</strong> to it.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"colonial-style architecture","context":"In terms of buildings, it's mostly lined with colonial-style architecture — old French villas with high ceilings and shuttered windows — and it's only a short walk from the Hanoi Opera House, which is easily the grandest building in that part of town."},{"en":"the grandest building","context":"In terms of buildings, it's mostly lined with colonial-style architecture — old French villas with high ceilings and shuttered windows — and it's only a short walk from the Hanoi Opera House, which is easily the grandest building in that part of town."},{"en":"wildly overpriced","context":"There are also a few little shops selling souvenirs and handicrafts, but I don't spend long in them because everything's wildly overpriced — a pack of postcards there will probably set you back about 30,000 dong, whereas the same thing in any bookshop would only cost about a third of that."},{"en":"set you back","context":"There are also a few little shops selling souvenirs and handicrafts, but I don't spend long in them because everything's wildly overpriced — a pack of postcards there will probably set you back about 30,000 dong, whereas the same thing in any bookshop would only cost about a third of that."},{"en":"closed off to traffic","context":"But in terms of why I actually like the street, it's mainly the food, like I said, plus the fact that it's closed off to traffic, so you've got loads of space to walk around without worrying about getting hit by a scooter."},{"en":"old-world charm","context":"And because most of the buildings are so old, the whole street's just got this really nice, old-world charm to it."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a street you know well","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-055","_displayNo":"#154","outline":{"topic":"Ly Thai To Street","intro":"few streets could talk about, one enjoy most is Ly Thai To, popular tourist area Hanoi","body":["centre of city, near Hoan Kiem Lake, 15-20 min drive, go by scooter","main buildings: old French colonial villas, short walk from Hanoi Opera House (grandest building)","how often: once/twice a month, don't have more time","mainly go for street food (squid/bacon sandwiches/chicken wings), souvenir shops overpriced"],"conclusion":"like it bc food, closed off to traffic (space to walk), old buildings give old-world charm"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-055.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A street I know well is Ly Thai To Street in central Hanoi, near Hoan Kiem Lake. It takes me about fifteen to twenty minutes to get there by scooter. I usually visit once or twice a month.\n\nThe street has many attractive old buildings, including French-style villas with high ceilings and shutters. It is also near the Hanoi Opera House. I enjoy walking there and looking at the architecture, but my main reason for visiting is the food. Stalls sell things such as grilled squid, chicken wings and sandwiches.\n\nThere are souvenir and craft shops too, although I rarely buy anything because prices seem high. For example, postcards can cost much more than in an ordinary bookshop. What I appreciate most is having space to walk when the street is closed to traffic. I can enjoy the food and old buildings without constantly watching for scooters. The combination of good snacks and a relaxed walk makes this one of my favourite parts of the city.","html":"A street I know well is Ly Thai To Street in central Hanoi, near Hoan Kiem Lake. It takes me about fifteen to twenty minutes to get there by scooter. I usually visit once or twice a month.\n\nThe street has many attractive old buildings, including French-style villas with high ceilings and shutters. It is also near the Hanoi Opera House. I enjoy walking there and looking at the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">architecture</strong>, but my main reason for visiting is the food. Stalls sell things such as grilled squid, chicken wings and sandwiches.\n\nThere are souvenir and craft shops too, although I rarely buy anything because prices seem high. For example, postcards can cost much more than in an ordinary bookshop. What I appreciate most is having space to walk when the street is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">closed to traffic</strong>. I can enjoy the food and old buildings without constantly watching for scooters. The combination of good snacks and a relaxed walk makes this one of my favourite parts of the city.","phrases":[{"en":"architecture","meaning":"The design and style of buildings."},{"en":"closed to traffic","meaning":"Not allowing cars, motorbikes or other vehicles to enter."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-055.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A tall building in your hometown you like or dislike","prompt":"Describe a tall building in your hometown you like or dislike.\nYou should say:\nWhere it is\nWhat it is used for\nWhat it looks like\nAnd explain why you like or dislike it.","answer":"I'm going to talk about the Bitexco Financial Tower, which is probably the most recognisable tall building in Ho Chi Minh City.\n\nIt's right in the centre of District 1, close to the Saigon River, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">towers over</strong> pretty much everything else nearby, so you can spot it from most parts of downtown. It's mainly an office building — a lot of banks and financial companies have their headquarters there — but what it's really famous for is the Saigon Skydeck, an observation deck on the 49th floor that's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">open to the public</strong>, plus a helipad sticking out from one side like a little disc.\n\nAs for what it looks like, the whole design is meant to represent a lotus bud, which is Vietnam's national flower, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tapers into a spiral shape</strong> as it rises. Though if I'm honest, I've always thought it looks more like a stack of coins, or corn on the cob, than a flower.\n\nI like it because of one particular memory. A few years back, my uncle flew in from Australia, and I took him up to the Skydeck one evening right as the sun was setting. Neither of us said much for the first few minutes — you can see the whole city spread out below, with the river cutting straight through the middle of it, and it really is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a breathtaking view</strong>. Now, whenever I spot the building's outline on the skyline, from wherever I happen to be in the city, it takes me straight back to that evening, and that's really why I've <strong class=\"key-phrase\">grown so fond of</strong> it.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"towers over","context":"It's right in the centre of District 1, close to the Saigon River, and it towers over pretty much everything else nearby, so you can spot it from most parts of downtown."},{"en":"open to the public","context":"It's mainly an office building — a lot of banks and financial companies have their headquarters there — but what it's really famous for is the Saigon Skydeck, an observation deck on the 49th floor that's open to the public, plus a helipad sticking out from one side like a little disc."},{"en":"tapers into a spiral shape","context":"As for what it looks like, the whole design is meant to represent a lotus bud, which is Vietnam's national flower, and it tapers into a spiral shape as it rises."},{"en":"a breathtaking view","context":"Neither of us said much for the first few minutes — you can see the whole city spread out below, with the river cutting straight through the middle of it, and it really is a breathtaking view."},{"en":"grown so fond of","context":"Now, whenever I spot the building's outline on the skyline, from wherever I happen to be in the city, it takes me straight back to that evening, and that's really why I've grown so fond of it."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a tall building in your hometown you like or dislike","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-056","_displayNo":"#155","outline":{"topic":"Bitexco Financial Tower","intro":"Bitexco Financial Tower, most recognisable tall building in HCMC","body":["District 1, near Saigon River, towers over everything, visible from downtown","mainly office building (banks/finance), famous for Saigon Skydeck (49th floor) + helipad","looks like: lotus-bud design, tapers into spiral shape, honestly looks more like coins/corn","like it bc of memory: took visiting uncle to Skydeck at sunset, breathtaking view"],"conclusion":"seeing the building on the skyline now always brings back that evening"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-056.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A tall building I like is the Bitexco Financial Tower in District 1 of Ho Chi Minh City. It is near the Saigon River and is easy to recognise from many places downtown. The building contains offices, including those of financial companies.\n\nIt is known for its unusual design, which is intended to look like a lotus bud. There is also a landing area for helicopters projecting from one side. Visitors can go to the Skydeck on the forty-ninth floor to look over the city.\n\nMy favourite memory is taking my uncle there when he visited from Australia a few years ago. We arrived around sunset and stood quietly by the windows for several minutes. We could see the river and buildings spread out below us. The view was much more impressive than I had expected. Now, whenever I see the tower, I remember that evening. I like it partly for its appearance, but mainly because it reminds me of a special experience with a family member.","html":"A tall building I like is the Bitexco Financial Tower in District 1 of Ho Chi Minh City. It is near the Saigon River and is easy to recognise from many places downtown. The building contains offices, including those of financial companies.\n\nIt is known for its unusual design, which is intended to look like a lotus bud. There is also a landing area for helicopters <strong class=\"key-phrase\">projecting from one side</strong>. Visitors can go to the Skydeck on the forty-ninth floor to look over the city.\n\nMy favourite memory is taking my uncle there when he visited from Australia a few years ago. We arrived around sunset and stood quietly by the windows for several minutes. We could see the river and buildings <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spread out below us</strong>. The view was much more impressive than I had expected. Now, whenever I see the tower, I remember that evening. I like it partly for its appearance, but mainly because it reminds me of a special experience with a family member.","phrases":[{"en":"projecting from one side","meaning":"Sticking out from the side of something."},{"en":"spread out below us","meaning":"Extending over a large area beneath where we were standing."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-056.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An ideal house","prompt":"Describe an ideal house\nYou should say:\nwhere it is\nwhat it looks like\nwhen you would like to live in\nAnd explain why you would like to live in it","answer":"OK, so my ideal house isn't really a palace or anything grand — I'd actually like somewhere fairly small, but in the right location: up in the hills just outside Da Lat, where it's cool and quiet all year round, away from the noise of the city.\n\nI picture a single-storey house with big windows all along one side, so there's plenty of natural light and good <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cross-ventilation</strong> without needing the air-con on constantly. It'd be simply furnished — wooden floors, a small reading corner by the window — with a garden out front full of flowers and a couple of fruit trees, just enough space to sit outside with a coffee in the morning.\n\nAs for when I'd want to live there, honestly, not any time soon. I'd say once I've got a stable career going and I'm not tied to the city for work any more — maybe in my forties. It's more of a long-term goal than something I'm planning next year.\n\nThe main reason I want it is just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">peace and quiet</strong>. My job right now is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pretty full-on</strong>, and by the time the weekend comes I'm usually exhausted, so the idea of waking up somewhere cool and silent, with nothing to do but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">potter around the garden</strong> or read a book, genuinely sounds like the perfect way to unwind. I don't need anything fancy — just somewhere I can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">properly switch off</strong>.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"cross-ventilation","context":"I picture a single-storey house with big windows all along one side, so there's plenty of natural light and good cross-ventilation without needing the air-con on constantly."},{"en":"peace and quiet","context":"The main reason I want it is just peace and quiet."},{"en":"pretty full-on","context":"My job right now is pretty full-on, and by the time the weekend comes I'm usually exhausted, so the idea of waking up somewhere cool and silent, with nothing to do but potter around the garden or read a book, genuinely sounds like the perfect way to unwind."},{"en":"potter around the garden","context":"My job right now is pretty full-on, and by the time the weekend comes I'm usually exhausted, so the idea of waking up somewhere cool and silent, with nothing to do but potter around the garden or read a book, genuinely sounds like the perfect way to unwind."},{"en":"properly switch off","context":"I don't need anything fancy — just somewhere I can properly switch off."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an ideal house","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-057","_displayNo":"#156","outline":{"topic":"ideal house in the hills near Da Lat","intro":"not a palace, somewhere small, right location: hills outside Da Lat, cool quiet","body":["single-storey, big windows, natural light, cross-ventilation, wooden floors, reading corner, garden","when: not any time soon, once career stable and not tied to city, maybe in 40s, long-term goal","why: peace and quiet, job pretty full-on, weekends exhausted, want to potter around garden/read"],"conclusion":"don't need anything fancy, just somewhere to properly switch off"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-057.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"My ideal house would be a small home in the hills outside Da Lat. I would choose that location because the weather is cool and the surroundings are quieter than the city. I do not need a very large or expensive-looking building.\n\nI imagine one floor, wooden floors inside and large windows along one side. These would let in daylight and fresh air. There would be a reading corner by a window and a garden with flowers and a few fruit trees. I would like somewhere to sit with coffee in the morning.\n\nThis is a long-term plan rather than something I expect to do soon. Perhaps I could move there in my forties, when my career is stable and I do not need to work in the city every day. My current job is busy, so the idea of a quiet home is attractive. I would enjoy reading, looking after plants and resting properly. For me, comfort and peace matter more than having a grand house.","html":"My ideal house would be a small home in the hills outside Da Lat. I would choose that location because the weather is cool and the surroundings are quieter than the city. I do not need a very large or expensive-looking building.\n\nI imagine one floor, wooden floors inside and large windows along one side. These would let in daylight and fresh air. There would be a reading corner by a window and a garden with flowers and a few fruit trees. I would like somewhere to sit with coffee in the morning.\n\nThis is a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long-term plan</strong> rather than something I expect to do soon. Perhaps I could move there in my forties, when <strong class=\"key-phrase\">my career is stable</strong> and I do not need to work in the city every day. My current job is busy, so the idea of a quiet home is attractive. I would enjoy reading, looking after plants and resting properly. For me, comfort and peace matter more than having a grand house.","phrases":[{"en":"long-term plan","meaning":"A plan for something you hope to do years in the future."},{"en":"my career is stable","meaning":"My working life feels secure and well established."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-057.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An important river/ area of water in your country","prompt":"Describe an important river/ area of water in your country\nYou should say:\nHow long the river is\nWhere it is\nWhat it looks like\nWhy it is important","answer":"I'm going to talk about the Mekong River, one of the biggest and most important rivers in Vietnam, flowing through the southwestern part of the country before it reaches the sea.\n\nIt's about 4,300 kilometres long in total, which makes it one of the longest rivers in Asia, though most of that length is actually in other countries before it even reaches Vietnam. Locally, people call it the River of the Nine Dragons, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">since time immemorial</strong>, its smaller branches have been linked to the image of nine dragons — it even shows up in poems and songs because of that.\n\nWhat it looks like really depends where you are. Up close, in the Delta, it's an incredible <strong class=\"key-phrase\">patchwork of green</strong> — rice paddies, orchards and little canals everywhere — with houses, markets and boats floating along waterways that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">criss-cross the landscape</strong> almost like veins. I went on a boat trip through part of it with my family a couple of summers ago, and I still remember drifting past a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">floating market</strong> at sunrise, with vendors calling out prices from their boats.\n\nIt's important mainly because of agriculture and fishing — the Delta's often called the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rice bowl</strong> of the whole country, and it's Vietnam's most important region for freshwater fishing too. Honestly, without the Mekong, a huge part of Vietnam's food supply just wouldn't exist.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"since time immemorial","context":"Locally, people call it the River of the Nine Dragons, because since time immemorial, its smaller branches have been linked to the image of nine dragons — it even shows up in poems and songs because of that."},{"en":"patchwork of green","context":"Up close, in the Delta, it's an incredible patchwork of green — rice paddies, orchards and little canals everywhere — with houses, markets and boats floating along waterways that criss-cross the landscape almost like veins."},{"en":"criss-cross the landscape","context":"Up close, in the Delta, it's an incredible patchwork of green — rice paddies, orchards and little canals everywhere — with houses, markets and boats floating along waterways that criss-cross the landscape almost like veins."},{"en":"floating market","context":"I went on a boat trip through part of it with my family a couple of summers ago, and I still remember drifting past a floating market at sunrise, with vendors calling out prices from their boats."},{"en":"rice bowl","context":"It's important mainly because of agriculture and fishing — the Delta's often called the rice bowl of the whole country, and it's Vietnam's most important region for freshwater fishing too."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an important river/ area of water in your country","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-058","_displayNo":"#157","outline":{"topic":"Mekong River","intro":"Mekong River, one of biggest/most important rivers in Vietnam, flows through southwest","body":["~4,300 km, one of longest in Asia; locally called River of Nine Dragons, since time immemorial, poems/songs","looks like: patchwork of green in the Delta, rice paddies/orchards/canals, boats/houses/markets criss-cross","personal: boat trip w/ family couple summers ago, floating market at sunrise, vendors calling prices","important bc: agriculture/rice bowl of country, most important fishing region"],"conclusion":"without Mekong, huge part of Vietnam's food supply wouldn't exist"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-058.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An important river in Vietnam is the Mekong. It passes through several countries before reaching southwestern Vietnam and flowing into the sea. In Vietnam, it is also associated with the name River of the Nine Dragons.\n\nThe part I know best is the Delta. There are green rice fields, fruit gardens and many small canals. Boats travel between homes and markets along the waterways. I visited with my family a couple of summers ago and went on a boat trip. My clearest memory is passing a floating market at sunrise, with sellers calling to customers from their boats.\n\nThe river is important because so many people depend on it for farming and fishing. The Delta produces a large amount of rice and freshwater fish. It is also part of local culture, appearing in songs and stories. Visiting helped me understand that it is more than a beautiful view. It supports everyday life and work for many communities, which is why I consider it such an important river.","html":"An important river in Vietnam is the Mekong. It passes through several countries before reaching southwestern Vietnam and flowing into the sea. In Vietnam, it is also associated with the name River of the Nine Dragons.\n\nThe part I know best is the Delta. There are green rice fields, fruit gardens and many small canals. Boats travel between homes and markets along the waterways. I visited with my family a couple of summers ago and went on a boat trip. My clearest memory is passing a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">floating market</strong> at sunrise, with sellers calling to customers from their boats.\n\nThe river is important because so many people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depend on it</strong> for farming and fishing. The Delta produces a large amount of rice and freshwater fish. It is also part of local culture, appearing in songs and stories. Visiting helped me understand that it is more than a beautiful view. It supports everyday life and work for many communities, which is why I consider it such an important river.","phrases":[{"en":"floating market","meaning":"A market where goods are sold from boats."},{"en":"depend on it","meaning":"Need it in order to live or work successfully."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-058.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting house or apartment that you have visited","prompt":"Describe an interesting house or apartment that you have visited.\nYou should say:\n- where the house or apartment was\n- who lived there\n- what it looked like on the outside and/or inside\n- and explain why you found this house or apartment interesting.","answer":"I'm going to describe an apartment that I recently visited in the city centre of Manchester. The apartment was on the 10th floor of a brand new block that had just been built. This building's on the edge of the main commercial area of the city, and the apartment overlooks several of Manchester's busiest shopping streets. Nobody was living in the apartment when I went there, because it had only just been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put on the market</strong>.\n\nI went to see it with a friend who's thinking of moving to Manchester; he wanted to view a few different properties, and I decided to go along and see what the newest apartments were like. From the outside, it was a tall, shiny glass building — nothing too remarkable. On the inside, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as far as I remember</strong>, there weren't many different colours: the walls were an off-white colour, the floors were done in whitish tiles, and the kitchen appliances were all silver metal coloured. I think the kitchen surfaces were made from dark grey granite.\n\nThe main feature, in terms of the look of the apartment, was the huge <strong class=\"key-phrase\">floor-to-ceiling window</strong> in the living room, which let the sunlight flood in. Although the white and silver decor wasn't really to my taste, I did like the fact that the apartment seemed so cool, clean and spacious. I particularly liked the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">open-plan kitchen</strong> and living room, which had all sorts of gadgets you could operate through a mobile phone app. For example, I learned how to use the app to control the lighting, heating, blinds, security cameras, even the cooker.\n\nI found it fascinating that the apartment contained so much <strong class=\"key-phrase\">up-to-the-minute technology</strong>, and that everything seemed to be connected via wifi. It felt like I was getting a glimpse into the future.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"as far as I remember","context":"On the inside, as far as I remember, there weren't many different colours: the walls were an off-white colour, the floors were done in whitish tiles, and the kitchen appliances were all silver metal coloured."},{"en":"put on the market","context":"Nobody was living in the apartment when I went there, because it had only just been put on the market."},{"en":"floor-to-ceiling window","context":"The main feature, in terms of the look of the apartment, was the huge floor-to-ceiling window in the living room, which let the sunlight flood in."},{"en":"open-plan kitchen","context":"I particularly liked the open-plan kitchen and living room, which had all sorts of gadgets you could operate through a mobile phone app."},{"en":"up-to-the-minute technology","context":"I found it fascinating that the apartment contained so much up-to-the-minute technology, and that everything seemed to be connected via wifi. It felt like I was getting a glimpse into the future."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting house or apartment that you have visited","id":"speaking-part-2-059","_displayNo":"#158","outline":{"topic":"Manchester apartment","intro":"going describe apartment recently visited city centre Manchester","body":["building edge main commercial area city apartment overlooks several Manchester's busiest","went see w/ friend thinking moving Manchester wanted view different properties","main feature terms look apartment huge floor-to-ceiling window living room allowed","particularly liked open-plan kitchen living room sorts gadgets operated mobile phone"],"conclusion":"felt like taking glimpse future"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-059.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An interesting flat I visited was on the tenth floor of a new building in central Manchester. I went with a friend who was considering moving there. Nobody lived in it yet because it had only recently been offered for sale.\n\nThe building looked modern from outside, with lots of glass. Inside, the flat had pale walls, light tiles, silver kitchen equipment and dark grey work surfaces. A huge window covered most of the living-room wall, allowing plenty of sunlight inside. The kitchen and living room shared one open space.\n\nWhat interested me most was the technology. A phone app could control the lights, heating, blinds and even some kitchen equipment. There were also connected security cameras. Although the white and silver decoration was not exactly my style, I liked how bright, clean and spacious the flat felt. Seeing so many things controlled through one app was new to me. The visit made me curious about how technology might change ordinary homes in the future.","html":"An interesting flat I visited was on the tenth floor of a new building in central Manchester. I went with a friend who was considering moving there. Nobody lived in it yet because it had only recently been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">offered for sale</strong>.\n\nThe building looked modern from outside, with lots of glass. Inside, the flat had pale walls, light tiles, silver kitchen equipment and dark grey <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work surfaces</strong>. A huge window covered most of the living-room wall, allowing plenty of sunlight inside. The kitchen and living room shared one open space.\n\nWhat interested me most was the technology. A phone app could control the lights, heating, blinds and even some kitchen equipment. There were also connected security cameras. Although the white and silver decoration was not exactly my style, I liked how bright, clean and spacious the flat felt. Seeing so many things controlled through one app was new to me. The visit made me curious about how technology might change ordinary homes in the future.","phrases":[{"en":"offered for sale","meaning":"Made available for people to buy."},{"en":"work surfaces","meaning":"Flat areas in a kitchen used for preparing food."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-059.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting place that few tourists visit","prompt":"Describe an interesting place that few tourists visit.\nYou should say:\n- Where it is;\n- When you went there;\n- What you can see there;\n- Why there is a few people to go.","answer":"To be honest, it's really hard to think of somewhere that's genuinely interesting but that hardly anyone knows about. So today I'm going to talk about one of my favourite restaurants — it's out in the suburbs of my hometown, and it doesn't even have a proper name. Everyone just calls it \"Grandma May's\", after the owner, who's also the cook — an elderly woman called May who only ever serves lunch.\n\nI've been going there since I was at primary school, actually, because it's right next to my old school gate, so my classmates and I used to pop in after class. It's on a busy little street, small and a bit chaotic inside, and from the outside it honestly doesn't look like much — a bit <strong class=\"key-phrase\">run-down</strong>, if I'm being honest. Inside, though, there are rows of old square wooden tables, each with four mismatched chairs, and it's always full of regulars.\n\nThere's no menu at all — you just walk up to the kitchen, see what May's cooked that day, and point at whatever <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catches your eye</strong>. Everything's delicious, but the best dishes go fast, so if you turn up too late, you'll miss out. Whenever I've got some free time back home, I still go there with my friends or family to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tuck into</strong> my favourite dishes.\n\nEven though it's tiny, the food is good enough that everyone who's ever eaten there ends up <strong class=\"key-phrase\">falling in love with it</strong>. Honestly, in my mind it's one of the best restaurants anywhere. But hardly anyone's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">heard of it</strong>, probably because it's never had a proper name, and my hometown's just too small for word to spread very far.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"run-down","context":"It's on a busy little street, small and a bit chaotic inside, and from the outside it honestly doesn't look like much — a bit run-down, if I'm being honest."},{"en":"catches your eye","context":"There's no menu at all — you just walk up to the kitchen, see what May's cooked that day, and point at whatever catches your eye."},{"en":"tuck into","context":"Whenever I've got some free time back home, I still go there with my friends or family to tuck into my favourite dishes."},{"en":"falling in love with it","context":"Even though it's tiny, the food is good enough that everyone who's ever eaten there ends up falling in love with it."},{"en":"heard of it","context":"But hardly anyone's heard of it, probably because it's never had a proper name, and my hometown's just too small for word to spread very far."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting place that few tourists visit","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-060","_displayNo":"#159","outline":{"topic":"\"Grandma May's\" restaurant","intro":"hard to think of somewhere interesting hardly anyone knows about; favourite restaurant, suburbs, no proper name","body":["been going since primary school, right next to old school gate, popped in after class","busy little street, small/chaotic, run-down outside, old wooden tables/mismatched chairs inside, full of regulars","no menu, walk up to kitchen and point at what catches your eye, best dishes go fast","tiny but food good enough everyone falls in love with it, best restaurant in my mind"],"conclusion":"hardly anyone's heard of it, never had a proper name, hometown too small for word to spread"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-060.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An interesting place that few tourists know is a restaurant people call Grandma May's. It is in the suburbs of my hometown, beside my old primary school. May is the elderly owner and cook, and she only serves lunch.\n\nI have eaten there since I was a child. From outside, it looks small and rather old. Inside, there are wooden tables and chairs that do not all match. There is no printed menu. Customers look at the food May has prepared and choose what they would like. The most popular dishes sell out quickly, so arriving early helps.\n\nWhenever I return home, I try to go with friends or family. I enjoy both the food and the familiar atmosphere. Although the place is simple, the cooking is excellent. I think few tourists visit because the restaurant has no clear business name and my hometown is not a major destination. To me, that makes it feel like a local secret: an ordinary-looking place with food that is worth remembering.","html":"An interesting place that few tourists know is a restaurant people call Grandma May's. It is in the suburbs of my hometown, beside my old primary school. May is the elderly owner and cook, and she only serves lunch.\n\nI have eaten there since I was a child. From outside, it looks small and rather old. Inside, there are wooden tables and chairs that do not all match. There is no printed menu. Customers look at the food May has prepared and choose what they would like. The most popular dishes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sell out</strong> quickly, so arriving early helps.\n\nWhenever I return home, I try to go with friends or family. I enjoy both the food and the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">familiar atmosphere</strong>. Although the place is simple, the cooking is excellent. I think few tourists visit because the restaurant has no clear business name and my hometown is not a major destination. To me, that makes it feel like a local secret: an ordinary-looking place with food that is worth remembering.","phrases":[{"en":"sell out","meaning":"Have none left because everything has been bought."},{"en":"familiar atmosphere","meaning":"A feeling of comfort because a place is well known to you."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-060.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An old building that you like","prompt":"Describe an old building that you like.\nYou should say:\n- where it is\n- what it is used for\n- and why you like it.","answer":"I'm going to describe a really famous building in New York: the Empire State Building. People might not think of it as an old building, but it was actually built back in 1931, so I don't think it really counts as new any more. It's right in the middle of Manhattan, and it's probably the most <strong class=\"key-phrase\">distinctive and recognisable</strong> building on the whole New York skyline.\n\nAs far as I know, it's mainly an office building, but visitors can also go up to an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">observation deck</strong> on the 102nd floor, right at the top. There's also a 360-degree viewing area on the 86th floor, which is actually the one most tourists go to. Apparently, the building makes more money these days from ticket sales to those observation floors than it does from renting out office space, which I find quite funny given it's meant to be an office building first.\n\nI think the Empire State Building's still one of the most impressive skyscrapers in the world. It's such an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">iconic structure</strong>, and it's kind of amazing to think it's still standing strong nearly a hundred years after it was built. The best part is definitely the viewing deck on top, which gives you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spectacular views</strong> of the whole city — it's genuinely the best <strong class=\"key-phrase\">vantage point</strong> in New York, in my opinion.\n\nI'd recommend it to anyone visiting the city — just go up there once, even if it's touristy, because the view alone makes it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">totally worth it</strong>.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"distinctive and recognisable","context":"It's right in the middle of Manhattan, and it's probably the most distinctive and recognisable building on the whole New York skyline."},{"en":"observation deck","context":"As far as I know, it's mainly an office building, but visitors can also go up to an observation deck on the 102nd floor, right at the top."},{"en":"iconic structure","context":"It's such an iconic structure, and it's kind of amazing to think it's still standing strong nearly a hundred years after it was built."},{"en":"spectacular views","context":"The best part is definitely the viewing deck on top, which gives you spectacular views of the whole city — it's genuinely the best vantage point in New York, in my opinion."},{"en":"vantage point","context":"The best part is definitely the viewing deck on top, which gives you spectacular views of the whole city — it's genuinely the best vantage point in New York, in my opinion."},{"en":"totally worth it","context":"I'd recommend it to anyone visiting the city — just go up there once, even if it's touristy, because the view alone makes it totally worth it."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an old building that you like","id":"speaking-part-2-061","_displayNo":"#160","outline":{"topic":"Empire State Building","intro":"I'm going describe famous building New York Empire State Building","body":["People not think old building Empire State Building built 1931 don't","far know Empire State Building office building visitors go up observation","Apparently building makes money sale tickets observation floors office rentals","best thing building viewing deck top floor offers spectacular views city"],"conclusion":"I'd recommend anyone visits city go experience view"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-061.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An old building I like is the Empire State Building in New York. It stands in Manhattan and was completed in 1931. Although many people think of skyscrapers as modern, this one has been part of the city for many decades.\n\nMuch of the building is used for offices, but visitors can also go to viewing areas. There is a famous outdoor viewing floor on the eighty-sixth level and another area higher up. From there, people can see the city in many directions.\n\nI like the building because its shape is so easy to recognise. It has appeared in many photographs and films, so it feels closely connected with New York. I also admire the fact that a building constructed so long ago still attracts so much attention. The views are the main reason I would recommend it to a visitor. Even if it is a popular tourist attraction, seeing the streets and towers from above would make the visit worthwhile. It combines an important piece of the city's past with a striking view of its present.","html":"An old building I like is the Empire State Building in New York. It stands in Manhattan and was completed in 1931. Although many people think of skyscrapers as modern, this one has been part of the city for many decades.\n\nMuch of the building is used for offices, but visitors can also go to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">viewing areas</strong>. There is a famous outdoor viewing floor on the eighty-sixth level and another area higher up. From there, people can see the city in many directions.\n\nI like the building because its shape is so easy to recognise. It has appeared in many photographs and films, so it feels closely connected with New York. I also admire the fact that a building constructed so long ago still attracts so much attention. The views are the main reason I would recommend it to a visitor. Even if it is a popular tourist attraction, seeing the streets and towers from above would make the visit <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worthwhile</strong>. It combines an important piece of the city's past with a striking view of its present.","phrases":[{"en":"viewing areas","meaning":"Places designed for people to look out over the surroundings."},{"en":"worthwhile","meaning":"Good or useful enough to deserve the time, effort or money spent."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-061.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An open-air or street market which you enjoyed","prompt":"Describe an open-air or street market which you enjoyed\nYou should say:\n- where the market is\n- what the market sells\n- how big the market is\n- and explain why you enjoyed visiting it.","answer":"I'm going to describe a street market that I've really enjoyed visiting, many times now, in Manchester. It's the Manchester Christmas Market, and it comes to the city for about a month from the end of November every year. The market stalls are spread across several sites in the city centre, but the centrepiece is the large European market in Albert Square, next to the Town Hall.\n\nThe Christmas Market stalls sell <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an array of Christmas gifts</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mouth-watering food and drink</strong> from all over Europe. It's a great place to find handmade crafts such as jewellery, ornaments and wooden toys, but it's the food and drink that seem to be most popular. Probably the biggest seller is the 'Gluhwein', a hot, sweet wine sold in a souvenir mug that you get to keep.\n\nThe market was originally quite small, occupying just one of the central squares, but it's grown quickly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in recent years</strong>, spilling over into maybe five other pedestrian streets and a few more squares. Apparently there are over 200 stalls now, so it's become a genuinely huge event that pulls in thousands of visitors. The main reason I keep going back is the atmosphere, honestly.\n\nWhen I went last December, it didn't matter how cold or wet it was — Manchester just seemed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">come alive</strong> the moment the market opened. The streets were packed, and there was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a real festive feel</strong> to the whole city.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"an array of Christmas gifts","context":"The Christmas Market stalls sell an array of Christmas gifts and mouth-watering food and drink from all over Europe."},{"en":"mouth-watering food and drink","context":"The Christmas Market stalls sell an array of Christmas gifts and mouth-watering food and drink from all over Europe."},{"en":"in recent years","context":"The market was originally quite small, occupying just one of the central squares, but it's grown quickly in recent years, spilling over into maybe five other pedestrian streets and a few more squares."},{"en":"come alive","context":"When I went last December, it didn't matter how cold or wet it was — Manchester just seemed to come alive the moment the market opened. The streets were packed, and there was a real festive feel to the whole city."},{"en":"a real festive feel","context":"When I went last December, it didn't matter how cold or wet it was — Manchester just seemed to come alive the moment the market opened. The streets were packed, and there was a real festive feel to the whole city."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an open-air or street market which you enjoyed","id":"speaking-part-2-062","_displayNo":"#161","outline":{"topic":"Manchester Christmas Market","intro":"going describe street market enjoyed visiting times Manchester","body":["market stalls spread across several sites city centre centrepiece large European","It's great place find handmade crafts jewellery ornaments wooden toys souvenirs","market originally small occupying one central squares Manchester it's grown quickly","Apparently 200 stalls now it's become big event attracting thousands visitors"],"conclusion":"went last December not matter cold wet weather Manchester seemed"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-062.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An outdoor market I enjoy is the Manchester Christmas Market. It takes place in the city centre for about a month from late November. The main area is in Albert Square, beside the Town Hall, but stalls also fill nearby streets and squares.\n\nVisitors can buy handmade jewellery, decorations, wooden toys and Christmas gifts. There is also food and drink from different parts of Europe. A popular drink is warm, sweet wine served in a souvenir mug that customers can keep. I enjoy looking around even when I am not planning to buy anything.\n\nThe market has grown a lot from its small beginnings and now attracts many visitors. My favourite thing is the atmosphere. When I went last December, the weather was cold and wet, but the streets still felt lively and cheerful. People were eating, shopping and spending time together. The lights and busy stalls made the city feel different from an ordinary winter evening. That festive feeling is why I keep returning.","html":"An outdoor market I enjoy is the Manchester Christmas Market. It takes place in the city centre for about a month from late November. The main area is in Albert Square, beside the Town Hall, but stalls also fill nearby streets and squares.\n\nVisitors can buy handmade jewellery, decorations, wooden toys and Christmas gifts. There is also food and drink from different parts of Europe. A popular drink is warm, sweet wine served in a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">souvenir mug</strong> that customers can keep. I enjoy looking around even when I am not planning to buy anything.\n\nThe market has grown a lot from its small beginnings and now attracts many visitors. My favourite thing is the atmosphere. When I went last December, the weather was cold and wet, but the streets still felt lively and cheerful. People were eating, shopping and spending time together. The lights and busy stalls made the city feel different from an ordinary winter evening. That <strong class=\"key-phrase\">festive feeling</strong> is why I keep returning.","phrases":[{"en":"souvenir mug","meaning":"A drinking cup kept as a reminder of a visit or event."},{"en":"festive feeling","meaning":"A cheerful mood connected with a celebration."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-062.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A change in your life","prompt":"Describe a change in your life.\nYou should say:\nWhat the change was\nWhen it happened\nWhy it happened\nAnd explain how you have benefited from this change","answer":"I'd like to talk about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of the greatest transformations in my life</strong>, which happened when I was seventeen, in my final year of high school. Back then I weighed around sixty-five kilos, and after about six months of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">continuous dieting and working out</strong>, I lost fifteen kilos and got down to a weight I actually felt comfortable with.\n\nThe reason it happened was that my eating habits had got completely out of control. As a teenager I mainly lived on fast food, things like fried chicken, burgers and crisps, and because I had so many exams to prepare for, I'd often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay up late</strong> revising and snacking at the same time. Unsurprisingly, I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put on weight</strong> fairly quickly, and one morning I stepped on the bathroom scale and it read sixty-five kilos. That's when I decided enough was enough.\n\nAfter talking to my aunt, who's a nutritionist, I realised that losing weight really just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comes down to diet and exercise</strong>, there's no shortcut. So I cleared my fridge of junk food, started cooking homemade meals, and swapped soft drinks for at least two litres of water a day. I also signed up at my local gym, trained three times a week, and got into the habit of going to bed early and waking up at five to cycle before school.\n\nAfter six months of sticking to that routine, my weight had dropped to fifty kilos, but honestly, the number on the scale wasn't even the best part. I felt stronger and happier than I had in years, and I stopped getting ill all the time. Most importantly, I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">got my confidence back</strong>. I used to run and hide whenever someone pulled out a camera, but now I don't mind being photographed at all. Best of all, I can finally fit into my old dresses and skinny jeans again.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"one of the greatest transformations in my life","context":"I'd like to talk about one of the greatest transformations in my life, which happened when I was seventeen, in my final year of high school."},{"en":"continuous dieting and working out","context":"Back then I weighed around sixty-five kilos, and after about six months of continuous dieting and working out, I lost fifteen kilos and got down to a weight I actually felt comfortable with."},{"en":"stay up late","context":"As a teenager I mainly lived on fast food, things like fried chicken, burgers and crisps, and because I had so many exams to prepare for, I'd often stay up late revising and snacking at the same time."},{"en":"put on weight","context":"Unsurprisingly, I put on weight fairly quickly, and one morning I stepped on the bathroom scale and it read sixty-five kilos."},{"en":"comes down to diet and exercise","context":"After talking to my aunt, who's a nutritionist, I realised that losing weight really just comes down to diet and exercise, there's no shortcut."},{"en":"got my confidence back","context":"Most importantly, I got my confidence back."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a change in your life","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-152","_displayNo":"#162","outline":{"topic":"lose 15 kg in 6 mos","intro":"one greatest transformations life","body":["used weigh around 65 kilos after 6 mos continuous dieting working","cleaned out fridge favourite unhealthy foods began cooking homemade meals","After 6 mos working out regularly resisting junk food weight dropped","used run hide cameras now comfortable photographed"],"conclusion":"best thing now able finally wear old dresses skinny jeans"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-152.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A change I made in my life was becoming more active and improving my eating habits at seventeen. I was in my final year of high school and spent many evenings revising while eating fast food and snacks. Over time, I gained weight and felt less comfortable.\n\nI spoke to my aunt, who is a nutritionist, and began making changes. I cooked more meals at home, drank water instead of soft drinks and joined a local gym. I also tried to sleep earlier and sometimes cycled before school. Keeping the routine took effort because it was different from how I had lived before.\n\nOver six months, my weight changed from around sixty-five to fifty kilos. For me, though, the most important result was feeling stronger and more confident. I stopped avoiding photographs and felt happier wearing clothes I already owned. This was a personal experience rather than a plan for anyone else. It taught me that lasting change depends on daily habits and patience, not simply wanting a quick result.","html":"A change I made in my life was becoming more active and improving my eating habits at seventeen. I was in my final year of high school and spent many evenings revising while eating fast food and snacks. Over time, I gained weight and felt less comfortable.\n\nI spoke to my aunt, who is a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nutritionist</strong>, and began making changes. I cooked more meals at home, drank water instead of soft drinks and joined a local gym. I also tried to sleep earlier and sometimes cycled before school. Keeping the routine took effort because it was different from how I had lived before.\n\nOver six months, my weight changed from around sixty-five to fifty kilos. For me, though, the most important result was feeling stronger and more confident. I stopped avoiding photographs and felt happier wearing clothes I already owned. This was a personal experience rather than a plan for anyone else. It taught me that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lasting change</strong> depends on daily habits and patience, not simply wanting a quick result.","phrases":[{"en":"nutritionist","meaning":"Someone trained to advise people about food and nutrition."},{"en":"lasting change","meaning":"A difference that continues for a long time."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-152.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A change that you would like to see in your local area","prompt":"Describe a change that you would like to see in your local area.\nYou should say:\n- what the change would be\n- who would be involved in making the change\n- how it would be done\n- and why you would like to see the change happen.","answer":"The change that I would like to see in my local neighbourhood is the pedestrianisation of the main shopping street in the town centre. I would like to see traffic diverted around this area in order to make it safer and more attractive for visitors and shoppers. Most of the traffic that clogs up the main street is just passing through the town, but it's the local people who have to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put up with the congestion</strong>, noise and pollution. It's the job of the local council to make changes like the one I am suggesting.\n\nBut before the council does anything, there usually needs to be some pressure from local people to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bring a problem to light</strong>. So, perhaps we need a campaign by local residents who want to see this change. Then the council might contract a town planner or an architect to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">come up with some possible designs</strong> for a new road system. I'm not sure exactly how the process of creating a pedestrian street would work, but I imagine it would require detailed planning.\n\nPerhaps a bypass would need to be built around the town centre first. This would give drivers a faster alternative to driving through the town, and it would allow the main shopping street to be blocked off and pedestrianised. I would like to see this change because it would really improve <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the quality of life</strong> of residents in the town where I live. I can imagine the new pedestrian street having cafes and restaurants with outdoor seating.\n\nIt would be a safer and more pleasant place for families to go shopping, and I'm sure this would have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a positive knock-on effect</strong> on local shops and businesses.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"put up with the congestion","context":"Most of the traffic that clogs up the main street is just passing through the town, but it's the local people who have to put up with the congestion, noise and pollution."},{"en":"bring a problem to light","context":"But before the council does anything, there usually needs to be some pressure from local people to bring a problem to light."},{"en":"come up with some possible designs","context":"Then the council might contract a town planner or an architect to come up with some possible designs for a new road system."},{"en":"the quality of life","context":"I would like to see this change because it would really improve the quality of life of residents in the town where I live."},{"en":"a positive knock-on effect","context":"It would be a safer and more pleasant place for families to go shopping, and I'm sure this would have a positive knock-on effect on local shops and businesses."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a change that you would like to see in your local area","id":"speaking-part-2-153","_displayNo":"#163","outline":{"topic":"pedestrianise main shopping street","intro":"change like see local neighbourhood pedestrianisation main shopping street town","body":["traffic clogs up main street passing through town it's local people","council contract town planner architect come up w/ possible designs new","give drivers faster alternative driving through town allow main shopping street","imagine new pedestrian street cafes restaurants w/ outdoor seating"],"conclusion":"safer pleasant place families go shopping sure positive knock-on effect"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-153.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A change I would like in my local area is making the main shopping street pedestrian-only. At present, many vehicles pass through the town centre even when their drivers are not stopping there. This creates noise, pollution and traffic for local people.\n\nI would like the council to move this traffic onto another route. It might require a bypass or changes to nearby roads, so careful planning would be necessary. Residents could begin by discussing the problem and asking the council to consider possible designs.\n\nIf the street were closed to normal traffic, it could become safer and more pleasant for shopping. Cafes might have outdoor tables, and families could walk without constantly watching for cars. I think local shops could benefit too, because people might stay longer in the area. I understand that deliveries and access would need to be considered, but the idea seems worth exploring. A town centre should be a place people enjoy using, rather than simply a road they have to cross quickly.","html":"A change I would like in my local area is making the main shopping street <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pedestrian-only</strong>. At present, many vehicles pass through the town centre even when their drivers are not stopping there. This creates noise, pollution and traffic for local people.\n\nI would like the council to move this traffic onto another route. It might require a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bypass</strong> or changes to nearby roads, so careful planning would be necessary. Residents could begin by discussing the problem and asking the council to consider possible designs.\n\nIf the street were closed to normal traffic, it could become safer and more pleasant for shopping. Cafes might have outdoor tables, and families could walk without constantly watching for cars. I think local shops could benefit too, because people might stay longer in the area. I understand that deliveries and access would need to be considered, but the idea seems worth exploring. A town centre should be a place people enjoy using, rather than simply a road they have to cross quickly.","phrases":[{"en":"pedestrian-only","meaning":"Reserved for people walking rather than normal vehicle traffic."},{"en":"bypass","meaning":"A road taking traffic around a town or busy area."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-153.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A decision that you disagreed with","prompt":"Describe a decision that you disagreed with\nYou should say:\nWho made this decision\nWhat it was\nHow you told him/her\nAnd explain why you disagreed with the decision","answer":"I'd like to talk about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an important milestone in my life</strong>, which was the first real argument I ever had with my parents. I've always been a bit of a mummy's boy, especially with my mother, who's definitely the stricter of the two. She's so direct that it's usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">her way or the highway</strong>, and she's always been incredibly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">protective of me</strong>, to the point where she'd never once let me stay over at a friend's house.\n\nThis all came to a head about three weeks ago, when my best friend invited me to his birthday sleepover. I was thrilled, it would have been my first time staying at a friend's place overnight, but my excitement didn't last long, because the moment I mentioned it to my mum, she said no straight away.\n\nFilled with frustration, I actually raised my voice at her for the first time in my life and said, \"Mum, I'm sixteen, not six, I can look after myself for one night.\" She was so taken aback by my tone that she told me off even harder at first, but I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stood my ground</strong>. I felt it was about time I told her how I really felt, that I was old enough to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">be responsible for</strong> myself and that she needed to loosen the reins a little.\n\nI disagreed with her decision simply because I didn't think it was fair to treat me like a little kid when all my friends were already allowed to do this kind of thing. In the end, she understood where I was coming from and let me go. I'll never forget that evening, not just because it was the night I finally stood up to my mother, but because it taught me that speaking up for myself, calmly but firmly, is something I still need to work on.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"an important milestone in my life","context":"I'd like to talk about an important milestone in my life, which was the first real argument I ever had with my parents."},{"en":"her way or the highway","context":"She's so direct that it's usually her way or the highway, and she's always been incredibly protective of me, to the point where she'd never once let me stay over at a friend's house."},{"en":"protective of me","context":"She's so direct that it's usually her way or the highway, and she's always been incredibly protective of me, to the point where she'd never once let me stay over at a friend's house."},{"en":"be responsible for","context":"I felt it was about time I told her how I really felt, that I was old enough to be responsible for myself and that she needed to loosen the reins a little."},{"en":"stood my ground","context":"Filled with frustration, I actually raised my voice at her for the first time in my life and said, \"Mum, I'm sixteen, not six, I can look after myself for one night.\" She was so taken aback by my tone that she told me off even harder at first, but I stood my ground."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a decision that you disagreed with","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-154","_displayNo":"#164","outline":{"topic":"1st argument w/ parents over a sleepover","intro":"important milestone in life, 1st real argument w/ parents, mummy's boy, strict mother","body":["best friend's birthday sleepover invite, mother said no straight away","raised voice at mother for 1st time, stood ground, felt old enough be responsible for self","disagreed bc treated like a kid when friends allowed, mother came round, let me go"],"conclusion":"taught me to speak up for myself calmly but firmly"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-154.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A decision I disagreed with was my mother's refusal to let me attend a friend's birthday sleepover. This happened about three weeks ago. I was sixteen and had never stayed overnight at a friend's home because my parents were very protective.\n\nWhen my best friend invited me, I was excited. However, my mother immediately said no. I became frustrated and raised my voice, telling her I was old enough to look after myself for one night. She was surprised and initially became even stricter.\n\nAfterwards, I explained why I thought the decision was unfair. My friends were allowed to attend, and I wanted a chance to show that I could behave responsibly. Eventually, she understood my point and gave permission. I was pleased, but I also recognised that shouting had not helped. The experience mattered because it was one of the first times I clearly expressed a different opinion to my parents. It taught me that speaking up is important, but doing so calmly makes it easier for someone else to listen.","html":"A decision I disagreed with was my mother's refusal to let me attend a friend's birthday <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sleepover</strong>. This happened about three weeks ago. I was sixteen and had never stayed overnight at a friend's home because my parents were very protective.\n\nWhen my best friend invited me, I was excited. However, my mother immediately said no. I became frustrated and raised my voice, telling her I was old enough to look after myself for one night. She was surprised and initially became even stricter.\n\nAfterwards, I explained why I thought the decision was unfair. My friends were allowed to attend, and I wanted a chance to show that I could behave responsibly. Eventually, she understood my point and gave permission. I was pleased, but I also recognised that shouting had not helped. The experience mattered because it was one of the first times I clearly expressed a different opinion to my parents. It taught me that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">speaking up</strong> is important, but doing so calmly makes it easier for someone else to listen.","phrases":[{"en":"sleepover","meaning":"A visit where someone stays overnight at a friend's home."},{"en":"speaking up","meaning":"Expressing your opinion instead of remaining silent."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-154.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A difficult choice that you made","prompt":"Describe a difficult choice that you made.\nYou should say:\nwhat the choice was\nwhen this happened\nwhat choice you made\nAnd explain why the choice was a difficult one.","answer":"One time when I found it really difficult to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make up my mind</strong> was back when I was a child, about ten years ago. My parents were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not getting on well with each other</strong>, and the atmosphere at home became pretty unbearable. In the end they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">got divorced</strong>, and I was left not knowing which of them I should go and live with.\n\nThat was a genuinely difficult period for me, because so much changed at once, and mostly not for the better. Choosing who to live with felt almost impossible, since I loved both of my parents equally and we'd built up so many memories together as a family. In the end, we sat down as a family and spent ages talking it through, trying to work out what would actually be best for me.\n\nI finally decided to stay with my mother, mainly because she'd be able to take better care of me day to day, and because I felt she needed my support more after the divorce. My father was upset at first, but he came round and agreed it was the right choice, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">for my sake</strong>. Looking back, I think it wasn't just a hard decision for me, it was hard for both of my parents too.\n\nPart of what made it so difficult was that I'd always wanted to live <strong class=\"key-phrase\">under the same roof</strong> as both of my parents, so having to pick just one felt like a kind of loss in itself. Not getting to see my father every single day made me quite sad for a long time, and even now I'd say it's one of the toughest choices I've ever had to make.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"make up my mind","context":"One time when I found it really difficult to make up my mind was back when I was a child, about ten years ago."},{"en":"not getting on well with each other","context":"My parents were not getting on well with each other, and the atmosphere at home became pretty unbearable."},{"en":"got divorced","context":"In the end they got divorced, and I was left not knowing which of them I should go and live with."},{"en":"for my sake","context":"My father was upset at first, but he came round and agreed it was the right choice, for my sake."},{"en":"under the same roof","context":"Part of what made it so difficult was that I'd always wanted to live under the same roof as both of my parents, so having to pick just one felt like a kind of loss in itself."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a difficult choice that you made","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-155","_displayNo":"#165","outline":{"topic":"choose mum after parents split","intro":"going talk situation found difficult make up mind","body":["child ten years ago parents not getting well w/ made atmosphere","difficult time choose live w/ bc loved parents unforgettable memories together","chose stay w/ mother bc take better care needed support after","always want live same roof parents choosing one person live w/"],"conclusion":"Not able see father day made feel sad one toughest"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-155.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A difficult choice I made was deciding which parent to live with after their divorce. This happened when I was a child, about ten years ago. They had been arguing, and life at home had become very uncomfortable.\n\nI loved both of them, so choosing felt almost impossible. I did not want either parent to think I cared less about them. We spent a long time discussing what would be best for me. In the end, I decided to stay with my mother because she could look after me day to day, and I felt she needed support after the separation.\n\nMy father was upset at first, but he eventually accepted the choice. I think it was painful for everyone, not just me. The hardest part was losing the everyday experience of living with both parents under one roof. I missed seeing my father daily for a long time. Looking back, I understand why the decision was necessary, but it remains one of the most emotionally difficult choices I have ever faced.","html":"A difficult choice I made was deciding which parent to live with after their divorce. This happened when I was a child, about ten years ago. They had been arguing, and life at home had become very uncomfortable.\n\nI loved both of them, so choosing felt almost impossible. I did not want either parent to think I cared less about them. We spent a long time discussing what would be best for me. In the end, I decided to stay with my mother because she could look after me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">day to day</strong>, and I felt she needed support after the separation.\n\nMy father was upset at first, but he eventually accepted the choice. I think it was painful for everyone, not just me. The hardest part was losing the everyday experience of living with both parents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">under one roof</strong>. I missed seeing my father daily for a long time. Looking back, I understand why the decision was necessary, but it remains one of the most emotionally difficult choices I have ever faced.","phrases":[{"en":"day to day","meaning":"In ordinary daily life."},{"en":"under one roof","meaning":"Living together in the same home."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-155.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A difficult decision that you once made","prompt":"Describe a difficult decision that you once made\nYou should say:\n- What the decision was\n- How you made your decision\n- What the results of the decision were\n- Why it was difficult to make.","answer":"I've made plenty of difficult decisions in my life, but the one I'd like to talk about today is choosing which university to study at.\n\nIt was about two years ago, right after I'd graduated from high school. I remember being incredibly busy at the time, filling in application forms for the university entrance exam while also trying to revise for it, so on top of all that stress, I also had to make this huge decision about my future. I was genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at a crossroads</strong>: my main interest was English language, but that subject was taught at several different universities in Hanoi, each with its own reputation, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tuition fees</strong> and location, so it wasn't obvious which one to pick. Some of my friends were choosing based on which school their friends were going to, which I didn't think was a good enough reason for such an important decision.\n\nFortunately, my older brother, who'd graduated from the University of Pedagogy a few years earlier, gave me some really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sound advice</strong>. We sat down together one evening and he helped me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weigh up the pros and cons</strong> of each option, things like teaching quality, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">job prospects</strong> after graduating and how far each campus was from home. In the end, he encouraged me to go for the university that specialised in my subject rather than the one with the flashier campus, and after thinking it over carefully, I applied there.\n\nAt present, I feel really satisfied with the learning environment at this university, and I'm constantly thankful for that decision, and for my brother's advice. Without his guidance, I honestly don't think I'd be anywhere near as content with my university life as I am now.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"at a crossroads","context":"I was genuinely at a crossroads: my main interest was English language, but that subject was taught at several different universities in Hanoi, each with its own reputation, tuition fees and location, so it wasn't obvious which one to pick."},{"en":"sound advice","context":"Fortunately, my older brother, who'd graduated from the University of Pedagogy a few years earlier, gave me some really sound advice."},{"en":"weigh up the pros and cons","context":"We sat down together one evening and he helped me weigh up the pros and cons of each option, things like teaching quality, job prospects after graduating and how far each campus was from home."},{"en":"tuition fees","context":"I was genuinely at a crossroads: my main interest was English language, but that subject was taught at several different universities in Hanoi, each with its own reputation, tuition fees and location, so it wasn't obvious which one to pick."},{"en":"job prospects","context":"We sat down together one evening and he helped me weigh up the pros and cons of each option, things like teaching quality, job prospects after graduating and how far each campus was from home."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a difficult decision that you once made","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-156","_displayNo":"#166","outline":{"topic":"choose a uni","intro":"made difficult decisions life today like talk decision choosing uni","body":["time terribly busy prepare application form uni entrance exam","time crossroads decide uni apply for","weighed up pros cons school encouraged choose uni specialising subject"],"conclusion":"w/o guidance probably wouldn't content w/ uni life am"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-156.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A difficult decision I made was choosing a university about two years ago, after finishing high school. I wanted to study English, but several universities in Hanoi offered the subject. Their fees, locations and reputations were different, so deciding was not easy.\n\nAt the same time, I was completing applications and preparing for the entrance exam. Some friends were choosing schools mainly to stay together, but I wanted a reason connected with my own future. Fortunately, my older brother helped me think through the options. He had graduated from the University of Pedagogy and understood the process.\n\nWe compared teaching quality, distance from home and possible jobs after graduation. He advised me to choose a university with a strong focus on my subject rather than the most impressive-looking campus. After considering this, I applied to the specialised option. I am now happy with the learning environment. The experience taught me to ask for advice without letting others make the whole decision. I am grateful that my brother helped me focus on what mattered most.","html":"A difficult decision I made was choosing a university about two years ago, after finishing high school. I wanted to study English, but several universities in Hanoi offered the subject. Their fees, locations and reputations were different, so deciding was not easy.\n\nAt the same time, I was completing applications and preparing for the entrance exam. Some friends were choosing schools mainly to stay together, but I wanted a reason connected with my own future. Fortunately, my older brother helped me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">think through the options</strong>. He had graduated from the University of Pedagogy and understood the process.\n\nWe compared teaching quality, distance from home and possible jobs after graduation. He advised me to choose a university with a strong focus on my subject rather than the most impressive-looking campus. After considering this, I applied to the specialised option. I am now happy with the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">learning environment</strong>. The experience taught me to ask for advice without letting others make the whole decision. I am grateful that my brother helped me focus on what mattered most.","phrases":[{"en":"think through the options","meaning":"Consider the available choices carefully."},{"en":"learning environment","meaning":"The conditions and atmosphere in which someone studies."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-156.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A funny situation that made you laugh","prompt":"Describe a funny situation that made you laugh.\nYou should say:\n- when this situation took place\n- what happened\n- how you reacted and why you found the situation funny.","answer":"I'm going to talk about a funny thing that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">happened to</strong> me a couple of weeks ago. I think it was a Saturday morning, and I was sitting having a coffee in a cafe near where I live. I was on my own so I decided to read the newspaper while drinking my coffee.\n\nI must have been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">quite engrossed in</strong> what I was reading because the time passed quickly, and I suddenly realised that I needed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get going</strong>. What I didn't realise was that I had been sitting with my legs crossed, and one of my legs had completely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gone to sleep</strong>. As I stood up to leave the cafe, I quickly became aware that my left leg was 'dead', but it was too late; I started to fall.\n\nI thought I could catch myself on the table, but the table tipped over and I fell to the floor <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in front of everyone</strong> in the cafe! I can remember being on my knees in the middle of the cafe, looking up at the staff and customers around me. I felt really embarrassed and I expected the other people to find it funny, but to my surprise nobody was laughing.\n\nThey were all worried that something really bad had happened to me! As I got up from the floor, I had to explain to the whole cafe that I was fine. I was embarrassed at the time, but I laughed about it later!","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"happened to","context":"I'm going to talk about a funny thing that happened to me a couple of weeks ago. I think it was a Saturday morning, and I was sitting having a coffee in a cafe near where I live. I was on my own so I decided to read the newspaper while drinking my coffee."},{"en":"quite engrossed in","context":"I must have been quite engrossed in what I was reading because the time passed quickly, and I suddenly realised that I needed to get going."},{"en":"get going","context":"I must have been quite engrossed in what I was reading because the time passed quickly, and I suddenly realised that I needed to get going."},{"en":"gone to sleep","context":"What I didn't realise was that I had been sitting with my legs crossed, and one of my legs had completely gone to sleep."},{"en":"in front of everyone","context":"I thought I could catch myself on the table, but the table tipped over and I fell to the floor in front of everyone in the cafe!"}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a funny situation that made you laugh","id":"speaking-part-2-157","_displayNo":"#167","outline":{"topic":"fall in cafe bc numb leg","intro":"think Saturday morning sitting coffee caf near live","body":["engrossed reading bc time passed quickly suddenly realised needed get going","stood up leave caf quickly became aware left leg dead' too","remember knees middle caf looking up staff customers around me","got up floor explain whole caf fine"],"conclusion":"embarrassed time laughed later"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-157.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A funny situation happened in a cafe near my home a couple of weeks ago. It was a Saturday morning, and I was alone, reading a newspaper while drinking coffee. I became so interested that I did not notice how long I had been sitting.\n\nWhen I realised I needed to leave, I stood up quickly. Unfortunately, I had kept my legs crossed, and my left leg had gone numb. It could not support me properly, so I began to fall. I tried to hold the table, but it tipped over too.\n\nI ended up on the floor in front of everyone. I felt extremely embarrassed and expected people to laugh. Instead, they looked worried because they thought something serious had happened. I had to explain that I was fine and that my leg had simply fallen asleep. At the time, I wanted to leave as quickly as possible. Later, however, I could laugh about it. The kindness of the other customers made the memory less embarrassing than it might have been.","html":"A funny situation happened in a cafe near my home a couple of weeks ago. It was a Saturday morning, and I was alone, reading a newspaper while drinking coffee. I became so interested that I did not notice how long I had been sitting.\n\nWhen I realised I needed to leave, I stood up quickly. Unfortunately, I had kept my legs crossed, and my left leg had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gone numb</strong>. It could not support me properly, so I began to fall. I tried to hold the table, but it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tipped over</strong> too.\n\nI ended up on the floor in front of everyone. I felt extremely embarrassed and expected people to laugh. Instead, they looked worried because they thought something serious had happened. I had to explain that I was fine and that my leg had simply fallen asleep. At the time, I wanted to leave as quickly as possible. Later, however, I could laugh about it. The kindness of the other customers made the memory less embarrassing than it might have been.","phrases":[{"en":"gone numb","meaning":"Lost normal feeling temporarily."},{"en":"tipped over","meaning":"Turned onto its side or fell from its normal position."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-157.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A positive change that you made to your life","prompt":"Describe a positive change that you made to your life.\nYou should say:\n- When it happened\n- Where it happened\n- What the change was\nAnd explain how you have benefited from this change\n(or, explain how you feel about this change)","answer":"I'd like to tell you about a life-changing decision I made a while back: switching my entire career path. About six months ago I was still working as a real-estate agent. I'd been in that job for about a year and a half and was making decent progress, but the more I got into it, the more I realised it just didn't suit me. When I pictured myself five years down the line still doing the same thing, I honestly couldn't see myself being happy, so that's why I quit and decided to try my luck in a completely unrelated field: teaching.\n\nAs I looked into it, I discovered that teaching IELTS in Hanoi could actually offer quite a lucrative income, so I started learning absolutely everything I could about the exam. It was arduous at first, I felt overwhelmed by all the self-study materials I found online, and I knew straight away that I couldn't manage it alone, so I asked a few friends for help. One of them turned out to want exactly the same thing, so we ended up studying together.\n\nMainly what we did was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep each other motivated and disciplined</strong>: we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set out</strong> a few ground rules, and whoever broke one had to face some kind of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">harsh punishment</strong>, like buying the other coffee for a week. After about two months of that, we sat the test on the same day, and now we're both working as IELTS teachers.\n\nOf course, as a new teacher I still have to figure a lot of things out, and I've had to do a lot of research to build up a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deep understanding</strong> of different teaching methods, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in retrospect</strong>, I'd say I made the right move. The job's a lot of fun, and it's opened doors to plenty of opportunities for self-improvement, so all in all, this has genuinely been a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">positive change in my life</strong>.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"keep each other motivated and disciplined","context":"Mainly what we did was keep each other motivated and disciplined: we set out a few ground rules, and whoever broke one had to face some kind of harsh punishment, like buying the other coffee for a week."},{"en":"set out","context":"Mainly what we did was keep each other motivated and disciplined: we set out a few ground rules, and whoever broke one had to face some kind of harsh punishment, like buying the other coffee for a week."},{"en":"harsh punishment","context":"Mainly what we did was keep each other motivated and disciplined: we set out a few ground rules, and whoever broke one had to face some kind of harsh punishment, like buying the other coffee for a week."},{"en":"deep understanding","context":"Of course, as a new teacher I still have to figure a lot of things out, and I've had to do a lot of research to build up a deep understanding of different teaching methods, but in retrospect, I'd say I made the right move."},{"en":"in retrospect","context":"Of course, as a new teacher I still have to figure a lot of things out, and I've had to do a lot of research to build up a deep understanding of different teaching methods, but in retrospect, I'd say I made the right move."},{"en":"positive change in my life","context":"The job's a lot of fun, and it's opened doors to plenty of opportunities for self-improvement, so all in all, this has genuinely been a positive change in my life."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a positive change that you made to your life","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-158","_displayNo":"#168","outline":{"topic":"change career from real estate","intro":"life-changing decision made lately","body":["mean pictured myself 5y not think wanted salesman anymore","searching realised job teaching IELTS Hanoi offers lucrative income started learning","Mainly keep motivated disciplined set out ground rules follow whoever broke","new teacher figure out lot things lot research develop deep understanding"],"conclusion":"positive change life"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-158.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A positive change I made was moving from real estate into teaching. About six months ago, I was still working as a property agent. I had done that job for around a year and a half, but I realised it did not suit me.\n\nI became interested in teaching IELTS and began studying the exam. At first, the amount of material felt overwhelming, so I asked friends for help. One friend had the same goal, and we decided to prepare together. We set study rules and encouraged each other to keep working. After about two months, we took the test on the same day.\n\nWe are now both working as IELTS teachers. I still need to learn more about lesson planning and teaching methods, but I enjoy the work much more than my previous job. It gives me opportunities to improve my own knowledge while helping students. Changing careers involved uncertainty, yet I feel it was the right decision. I learned that progress in a job is not enough if I cannot imagine being happy doing it for years.","html":"A positive change I made was moving from real estate into teaching. About six months ago, I was still working as a property agent. I had done that job for around a year and a half, but I realised it did not suit me.\n\nI became interested in teaching IELTS and began studying the exam. At first, the amount of material felt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overwhelming</strong>, so I asked friends for help. One friend had the same goal, and we decided to prepare together. We set study rules and encouraged each other to keep working. After about two months, we took the test on the same day.\n\nWe are now both working as IELTS teachers. I still need to learn more about lesson planning and teaching methods, but I enjoy the work much more than my previous job. It gives me opportunities to improve my own knowledge while helping students. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Changing careers</strong> involved uncertainty, yet I feel it was the right decision. I learned that progress in a job is not enough if I cannot imagine being happy doing it for years.","phrases":[{"en":"overwhelming","meaning":"Feeling too large or difficult to manage comfortably."},{"en":"Changing careers","meaning":"Moving into a different type of work."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-158.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A positive experience that you had as a teenager","prompt":"Describe a positive experience that you had as a teenager.\nYou should say:\n- what the positive experience was\n- where you were\n- who you were with\n- and explain why you found the experience positive.","answer":"I'm going to describe the first time I visited London, the capital of England, on a school trip when I was a teenager. I'd never been before, and it was brilliant to share that experience with my school friends rather than go with family.\n\nThe positive experience wasn't confined to any one place, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as far as I remember</strong>, I enjoyed pretty much the whole trip, from the coach journey down to the visits to all the different tourist attractions. We got off the coach right near the Houses of Parliament, so one of my first memories is looking up at the famous Big Ben clock tower and just thinking how much bigger it was than it looked on TV. We also visited the Tower of London, Buckingham Palace and Trafalgar Square, and I remember being fascinated by the guards standing so still outside the palace gates.\n\nAs I said, it was a school trip, so there were around thirty of us altogether, including two teachers, and I was with a group of close friends, which definitely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made the experience more enjoyable</strong>. We were constantly laughing and pointing things out to each other, which you wouldn't get on a trip with your parents.\n\nWhat really struck me about London was that it felt historic and modern at the same time, lively, fashionable and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">properly cosmopolitan</strong>. Coming from a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relatively small</strong> town myself, the whole experience left me really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keen to visit</strong> more big capital cities in the future.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"as far as I remember","context":"The positive experience wasn't confined to any one place, as far as I remember, I enjoyed pretty much the whole trip, from the coach journey down to the visits to all the different tourist attractions."},{"en":"made the experience more enjoyable","context":"As I said, it was a school trip, so there were around thirty of us altogether, including two teachers, and I was with a group of close friends, which definitely made the experience more enjoyable."},{"en":"relatively small","context":"Coming from a relatively small town myself, the whole experience left me really keen to visit more big capital cities in the future."},{"en":"properly cosmopolitan","context":"What really struck me about London was that it felt historic and modern at the same time, lively, fashionable and properly cosmopolitan."},{"en":"keen to visit","context":"Coming from a relatively small town myself, the whole experience left me really keen to visit more big capital cities in the future."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a positive experience that you had as a teenager","id":"speaking-part-2-159","_displayNo":"#169","outline":{"topic":"London school trip","intro":"never London before great share experience w/ school friends","body":["far remember enjoyed whole trip coach journey visits various tourist attractions","got off coach near Houses Parliament one 1st memories seeing famous","said earlier school trip think around thirty including two teachers","struck London historic modern thriving same time"],"conclusion":"Coming relatively small town experience made keen visit capital cities"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-159.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A positive experience I had as a teenager was my first school trip to London. There were around thirty students and two teachers. I went with several close friends, which made the coach journey and visits especially enjoyable.\n\nWe got off near the Houses of Parliament. I remember looking up at the Big Ben clock tower and being surprised by how much larger it seemed than on television. We also visited Buckingham Palace, the Tower of London and Trafalgar Square. At the palace, I was fascinated by the guards standing so still.\n\nComing from a small town, I found London exciting. It had historic buildings but also felt modern, busy and international. My friends and I kept pointing things out and laughing together, so the experience was about more than seeing famous sights. It gave us shared memories outside the classroom. The trip made me want to explore other capital cities and showed me that travelling with school could be both educational and fun. I remember the whole day positively rather than only one particular attraction.","html":"A positive experience I had as a teenager was my first school trip to London. There were around thirty students and two teachers. I went with several close friends, which made the coach journey and visits especially enjoyable.\n\nWe got off near the Houses of Parliament. I remember looking up at the Big Ben clock tower and being surprised by how much larger it seemed than on television. We also visited Buckingham Palace, the Tower of London and Trafalgar Square. At the palace, I was fascinated by the guards standing so still.\n\nComing from a small town, I found London exciting. It had historic buildings but also felt modern, busy and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">international</strong>. My friends and I kept pointing things out and laughing together, so the experience was about more than seeing famous sights. It gave us <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shared memories</strong> outside the classroom. The trip made me want to explore other capital cities and showed me that travelling with school could be both educational and fun. I remember the whole day positively rather than only one particular attraction.","phrases":[{"en":"international","meaning":"Including or connected with people from many countries."},{"en":"shared memories","meaning":"Experiences that several people remember having together."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-159.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A recent change in your life","prompt":"Describe a recent change in your life\nYou should say:\nWhat the change was\nWhen it happened\nHow you felt about it\nAnd explain if it was a positive or negative change for you","answer":"I suppose <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of the biggest turning points in my life</strong> was when I graduated from university just a couple of months ago. I started job-hunting straight away and began working full-time almost immediately, so I haven't really had time to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take a deep breath and reflect on</strong> such a big change.\n\nPeople always say university is one of the best periods of your life and that you'll miss it once you're working, but that's not true for me at all. I was honestly relieved when I finally got my degree. I didn't feel like I learned much there, and I was constantly irritated by how poor the staff's attitude was. All I wanted for those four years was to get a job so I could support myself, and eventually my family too, and now that's finally come true.\n\nI feel much more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in my element</strong> working than I ever did studying, mainly because I'm constantly learning new things and having to deal with fresh challenges every week. But honestly, the biggest change is that I can now take care of myself financially and don't have to rely on my family any more. I did work part-time during university, but what I earned then wasn't nearly enough to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">save for a rainy day</strong>, let alone help support my family.\n\nSo although the job <strong class=\"key-phrase\">isn't all plain sailing</strong>, I'm still really glad I've made it this far, and personally, I feel this change has had a very positive impact on my life.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"one of the biggest turning points in my life","context":"I suppose one of the biggest turning points in my life was when I graduated from university just a couple of months ago."},{"en":"take a deep breath and reflect on","context":"I started job-hunting straight away and began working full-time almost immediately, so I haven't really had time to take a deep breath and reflect on such a big change."},{"en":"in my element","context":"I feel much more in my element working than I ever did studying, mainly because I'm constantly learning new things and having to deal with fresh challenges every week."},{"en":"save for a rainy day","context":"I did work part-time during university, but what I earned then wasn't nearly enough to save for a rainy day, let alone help support my family."},{"en":"isn't all plain sailing","context":"So although the job isn't all plain sailing, I'm still really glad I've made it this far, and personally, I feel this change has had a very positive impact on my life."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a recent change in your life","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-160","_displayNo":"#170","outline":{"topic":"graduate + start full-time work","intro":"suppose one biggest turning points life graduated uni couple months","body":["looked job began working full-time right after not time take deep","not get learn lot school always irritated poor attitude staff there","element working bc constantly learn new things deal w/ new challenges","already worked part-time uni made not nearly enough save rainy day"],"conclusion":"Personally feel change positive impact me"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-160.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A recent change in my life was graduating from university and starting full-time work a couple of months ago. I began looking for jobs immediately and found one quickly, so the move from student life happened quite fast.\n\nUnlike some people, I was relieved to leave university. I had often felt disappointed by the course and wanted to become financially independent. Although I worked part time while studying, the income was not enough to save much or help my family.\n\nWorking full time has suited me better. I learn new things regularly and deal with practical challenges, which makes me feel more involved. Earning enough to cover my own needs is especially important. I no longer have to depend on my parents in the same way, and I hope to support them more in the future. The job is not always easy, and I am still adjusting to the routine. Even so, I see this as a positive change because it has given me greater responsibility, confidence and control over my own life.","html":"A recent change in my life was graduating from university and starting full-time work a couple of months ago. I began looking for jobs immediately and found one quickly, so the move from student life happened quite fast.\n\nUnlike some people, I was relieved to leave university. I had often felt disappointed by the course and wanted to become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">financially independent</strong>. Although I worked part time while studying, the income was not enough to save much or help my family.\n\nWorking full time has suited me better. I learn new things regularly and deal with practical challenges, which makes me feel more involved. Earning enough to cover my own needs is especially important. I no longer have to depend on my parents in the same way, and I hope to support them more in the future. The job is not always easy, and I am still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">adjusting to</strong> the routine. Even so, I see this as a positive change because it has given me greater responsibility, confidence and control over my own life.","phrases":[{"en":"financially independent","meaning":"Able to pay for your needs without relying on someone else's money."},{"en":"adjusting to","meaning":"Gradually becoming comfortable with a new situation."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-160.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A recent development in your city","prompt":"Describe a recent development in your city\nYou should say:\n- What this development is.\n- How it could help people.\n- What benefits it could bring.\n- Whether it changes your life or not.","answer":"One of the newest additions to my city that I've noticed is that the public parks are now equipped with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">communal exercise equipment</strong>, things like outdoor <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pull-up bars</strong>, sit-up benches and pedal machines.\n\nI've watched them being installed gradually over the last few months, and now they're common enough that even the small park near my flat has a set. I think they're going to be really helpful for a lot of different people. Whatever your situation, most of us could do with a bit more exercise, and this equipment gives people an extra, completely free way to get it. Anyone who wants to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work out</strong> but wouldn't normally pay for a gym membership now has a way to access proper equipment without it costing them any time travelling or any money at all.\n\nIt should also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">serve as an encouragement</strong> for people who don't normally think about exercise to start paying it more attention. Because the machines are free to try, people can test out different routines and see what actually suits them before committing to anything. More importantly, I think this kind of thing helps people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get used to</strong> treating exercise as a normal part of daily life rather than something separate you have to plan for.\n\nI know it's already benefited me personally. I spend a lot of time in the park anyway, walking my dog most evenings, and now I stop off at the pull-up bars on the way, so that time feels a lot more productive and it's doing my health some good too.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"communal exercise equipment","context":"One of the newest additions to my city that I've noticed is that the public parks are now equipped with communal exercise equipment, things like outdoor pull-up bars, sit-up benches and pedal machines."},{"en":"serve as an encouragement","context":"It should also serve as an encouragement for people who don't normally think about exercise to start paying it more attention."},{"en":"get used to","context":"More importantly, I think this kind of thing helps people get used to treating exercise as a normal part of daily life rather than something separate you have to plan for."},{"en":"work out","context":"Anyone who wants to work out but wouldn't normally pay for a gym membership now has a way to access proper equipment without it costing them any time travelling or any money at all."},{"en":"pull-up bars","context":"One of the newest additions to my city that I've noticed is that the public parks are now equipped with communal exercise equipment, things like outdoor pull-up bars, sit-up benches and pedal machines."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a recent development in your city","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-051","_displayNo":"#171","outline":{"topic":"outdoor gym equipment in parks","intro":"seen installed gradually last few months now common enough small","body":["One newest additions city noticed public parks now equipped w/ communal","People want exercise normally not go gym now new way access","now try out machines no cost themselves help gauge interest different","importantly helps people get used idea exercise necessary part life"],"conclusion":"normally spend lot time park helpful allowing spend time productively"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-051.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A recent development in my city is the addition of free exercise equipment in public parks. Over the last few months, I have noticed workers installing pull-up bars, benches for exercises and machines with pedals. Even the small park near my flat now has some.\n\nI think this is useful because not everyone can afford a gym membership. People can try the equipment without paying or travelling far. They can also discover which activities they enjoy before deciding on a regular exercise plan.\n\nAnother advantage is that the equipment makes exercise more visible. Someone who only came for a walk may decide to try a machine. This could help physical activity become an ordinary part of daily life. It has already changed my own routine. I usually walk my dog in the park in the evening, and now I stop at the pull-up bars too. I like being able to combine a normal walk with a little extra exercise, and I hope more local people benefit in the same way.","html":"A recent development in my city is the addition of free exercise equipment in public parks. Over the last few months, I have noticed workers installing pull-up bars, benches for exercises and machines with pedals. Even the small park near my flat now has some.\n\nI think this is useful because not everyone can afford a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gym membership</strong>. People can try the equipment without paying or travelling far. They can also discover which activities they enjoy before deciding on a regular exercise plan.\n\nAnother advantage is that the equipment makes exercise more visible. Someone who only came for a walk may decide to try a machine. This could help <strong class=\"key-phrase\">physical activity</strong> become an ordinary part of daily life. It has already changed my own routine. I usually walk my dog in the park in the evening, and now I stop at the pull-up bars too. I like being able to combine a normal walk with a little extra exercise, and I hope more local people benefit in the same way.","phrases":[{"en":"gym membership","meaning":"An arrangement that lets someone use a gym, usually for a fee."},{"en":"physical activity","meaning":"Movement that uses the body's energy, such as walking or exercising."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-051.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A situation that made you angry","prompt":"Describe a situation that made you angry.\nYou should say:\n- when it happened\n- what happened\n- how you felt","answer":"A recent situation that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made me angry</strong> was getting stuck in a traffic jam on the way to meet some friends. It happened at lunchtime last Sunday, and I hadn't expected the roads to be busy since most people weren't working.\n\nEverything was going smoothly until I suddenly saw a long queue of cars ahead. I had no choice but to join it and wait to get past whatever was causing the delay. Eventually, I discovered that roadworks had reduced the road to a single lane, and it took me an extra thirty minutes to get past them.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Traffic congestion</strong> doesn't usually make me angry, but this time I felt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">frustrated and powerless</strong>. I knew my friends were waiting for me, yet there was nothing I could do and no way of knowing how long the delay would last. When I finally got past the roadworks, I was relieved to be moving again, but I was still stressed because I was already half an hour late. What annoyed me most, thinking about it afterwards, wasn't the delay itself but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the lack of any warning</strong>. There was no sign at the junction where I'd turned in, and no notification on the map app I was using, even though the roadworks had apparently been there for several days already. Ten seconds of information would have let me take a completely different route.\n\nMy friends were totally relaxed about it when I finally arrived, which made me feel slightly foolish for having been so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wound up</strong>. Since then I've made a point of checking the traffic before I set off, and it's saved me from the same situation more than once.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"made me angry","context":"A recent situation that made me angry was getting stuck in a traffic jam on the way to meet some friends. It happened at lunchtime last Sunday, and I hadn't expected the roads to be busy since most people weren't working."},{"en":"Traffic congestion","context":"Traffic congestion doesn't usually make me angry, but this time I felt frustrated and powerless."},{"en":"frustrated and powerless","context":"Traffic congestion doesn't usually make me angry, but this time I felt frustrated and powerless."},{"en":"the lack of any warning","context":"What annoyed me most, thinking about it afterwards, wasn't the delay itself but the lack of any warning."},{"en":"wound up","context":"My friends were totally relaxed about it when I finally arrived, which made me feel slightly foolish for having been so wound up. Since then I've made a point of checking the traffic before I set off, and it's saved me from the same situation more than once."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a situation that made you angry","id":"speaking-part-2-162","_displayNo":"#172","outline":{"topic":"traffic jam","intro":"happened lunchtime last Sunday not expected roads busy bc people","body":["recent situation made angry getting stuck traffic jam way meet friends","Eventually discovered roadworks reduced road single lane took extra thirty minutes","knew friends waiting yet nothing no way knowing long delay last","finally reached roadworks relieved moving again stressed bc already half hour","annoyed most = not delay itself but lack of warning","no sign at junction no notification on map app roadworks there days","friends relaxed when arrived felt foolish for being wound up","since then check traffic before setting off"],"conclusion":"since then check traffic before setting off"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-162.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A situation that made me angry was getting stuck in traffic while going to meet friends last Sunday. It was lunchtime, and I had expected quiet roads because many people were not working.\n\nAt first, the journey was easy. Then I reached a long queue of cars and could not turn away. Roadworks had reduced the road to one lane, and getting past them took an extra thirty minutes. I felt frustrated because my friends were waiting and I did not know how long the delay would last.\n\nWhat annoyed me most was the lack of warning. There had been no sign where I entered the road, and my map app had not mentioned the work. With earlier information, I could have chosen another route. When I finally arrived, my friends were relaxed about the delay. Their reaction made me realise that getting so angry had not helped. Since then, I have tried to check traffic conditions before leaving and allow more time for unexpected problems. It is a small habit that makes journeys less stressful.","html":"A situation that made me angry was getting stuck in traffic while going to meet friends last Sunday. It was lunchtime, and I had expected quiet roads because many people were not working.\n\nAt first, the journey was easy. Then I reached a long queue of cars and could not turn away. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Roadworks</strong> had reduced the road to one lane, and getting past them took an extra thirty minutes. I felt frustrated because my friends were waiting and I did not know how long the delay would last.\n\nWhat annoyed me most was the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lack of warning</strong>. There had been no sign where I entered the road, and my map app had not mentioned the work. With earlier information, I could have chosen another route. When I finally arrived, my friends were relaxed about the delay. Their reaction made me realise that getting so angry had not helped. Since then, I have tried to check traffic conditions before leaving and allow more time for unexpected problems. It is a small habit that makes journeys less stressful.","phrases":[{"en":"Roadworks","meaning":"Repairs or construction taking place on a road."},{"en":"lack of warning","meaning":"Not being told beforehand that a problem may happen."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-162.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A situation when someone made noise","prompt":"Describe a situation when someone made noise\nYou should say:\nwhen it happened\nwho made the noise\nwhere it was\nAnd explain how you felt about it.","answer":"I'm going to talk about the time my neighbour <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made a lot of noise</strong>, renovating his house a few months ago. The sound of drills, hammers and other <strong class=\"key-phrase\">power tools</strong> going all day was honestly one of the most annoying things I've dealt with in ages.\n\nA new family had bought the house right next to mine, and because of some problems with water leaking through the walls, they'd decided to gut the place and redo it properly. From that point on, the noise of hammers, saws and drills seemed completely endless around my house, all day, every day, even at weekends. My neighbour clearly wanted to move in as soon as possible, so the workers would turn up and start banging away by about seven every morning, which meant my whole family ended up waking up much earlier than we wanted to.\n\nDuring that time I felt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">properly exhausted</strong>, and there was one particularly bad day when I had an important video call for work and had to keep apologising because the drilling kept drowning out my voice. I really wanted to make a complaint so my neighbour would do something about it, but since the renovation was completely legal, there wasn't actually anything I could do. Hearing that noise constantly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drove me crazy</strong> and had a genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">negative impact</strong> on my work, and some nights I was so wound up by it all that I could barely sleep.\n\nBut luckily, after about a month, my neighbour finished the renovations and the construction noise finally stopped. I was so relieved when things went quiet again, I actually noticed how peaceful my own flat could be.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"made a lot of noise","context":"I'm going to talk about the time my neighbour made a lot of noise, renovating his house a few months ago. The sound of drills, hammers and other power tools going all day was honestly one of the most annoying things I've dealt with in ages."},{"en":"power tools","context":"I'm going to talk about the time my neighbour made a lot of noise, renovating his house a few months ago. The sound of drills, hammers and other power tools going all day was honestly one of the most annoying things I've dealt with in ages."},{"en":"negative impact","context":"Hearing that noise constantly drove me crazy and had a genuinely negative impact on my work, and some nights I was so wound up by it all that I could barely sleep."},{"en":"drove me crazy","context":"Hearing that noise constantly drove me crazy and had a genuinely negative impact on my work, and some nights I was so wound up by it all that I could barely sleep."},{"en":"properly exhausted","context":"During that time I felt properly exhausted, and there was one particularly bad day when I had an important video call for work and had to keep apologising because the drilling kept drowning out my voice."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a situation when someone made noise","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-163","_displayNo":"#173","outline":{"topic":"neighbour's renovation noise","intro":"going talk neighbour made lot noise renovated house few months","body":["heard newcomer bought house next bc problems w/ water leakage made","neighbour wanted live house soon possible start work early morning made","Hearing noise time drove crazy negative impact work","luckily after month neighbour finished renovations terrible construction noises ceased"],"conclusion":"finally felt relieved"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-163.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A noisy situation I experienced was a neighbour's house renovation a few months ago. A new family had bought the house next to mine and needed to repair water damage before moving in.\n\nFor about a month, workers used drills, hammers and saws almost every day, including weekends. They often began around seven in the morning. This woke my family earlier than we wanted and made it hard to relax at home. One particularly difficult moment was an important work video call. The drilling was so loud that people struggled to hear me, and I kept apologising.\n\nI understood that the family needed the repairs completed, but the constant noise still left me tired and stressed. Sometimes I even found it difficult to sleep because I could not relax properly in the evening. When the work finally ended, I felt enormous relief. I noticed how peaceful my own home could be without machinery next door. The experience made me more aware that building work can affect neighbours even when the reason for doing it is understandable.","html":"A noisy situation I experienced was a neighbour's house <strong class=\"key-phrase\">renovation</strong> a few months ago. A new family had bought the house next to mine and needed to repair water damage before moving in.\n\nFor about a month, workers used drills, hammers and saws almost every day, including weekends. They often began around seven in the morning. This woke my family earlier than we wanted and made it hard to relax at home. One particularly difficult moment was an important work video call. The drilling was so loud that people struggled to hear me, and I kept apologising.\n\nI understood that the family needed the repairs completed, but the constant noise still left me tired and stressed. Sometimes I even found it difficult to sleep because I could not relax properly in the evening. When the work finally ended, I felt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">enormous relief</strong>. I noticed how peaceful my own home could be without machinery next door. The experience made me more aware that building work can affect neighbours even when the reason for doing it is understandable.","phrases":[{"en":"renovation","meaning":"Work to repair or improve a building."},{"en":"enormous relief","meaning":"A very strong feeling of comfort when a difficult situation ends."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-163.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A situation when you had to be polite","prompt":"Describe a situation when you had to be polite\nYou should say:\n- What situation it was\n- Who were there\n- Why you had to be polite\n- How you showed politeness","answer":"I'd like to talk about a time when I gave up my bus seat for a pregnant woman.\n\nIt was a rainy day about three years ago, when I was still a freshman at university. I was on a bus from my dormitory into the city centre to go to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a music festival</strong>, and partway through the journey, a heavily pregnant woman got on at one of the stops. The bus was already <strong class=\"key-phrase\">swarming with</strong> students by that point, so many of them were standing because there weren't enough seats to go round. In that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuffy atmosphere</strong>, I noticed her face suddenly turn pale, and she had to grip the hand rail tightly, with sweat rolling down her forehead.\n\nWithout really thinking about it, I stood up straight away and offered her my seat, then leaned over and opened the window to let some air in. After taking a few deep breaths she seemed to feel a lot better, and we ended up chatting for the rest of the journey. She told me she was on her way to visit her aunt in hospital and hadn't wanted to wait for the next bus in case she missed the visiting hours. She thanked me warmly, saying that if she'd had to stand any longer, she might actually have fainted.\n\nI did it simply because it felt like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the right thing to do</strong>, it wasn't really a decision, more just instinct. My mother has always told me to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pay close attention to</strong> the people around me and help out whenever I can, because small moments like that can matter more than we realise. By the end of that day I felt genuinely thrilled, knowing I'd helped not just one person, but two, her and the baby.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"a music festival","context":"I was on a bus from my dormitory into the city centre to go to a music festival, and partway through the journey, a heavily pregnant woman got on at one of the stops."},{"en":"swarming with","context":"The bus was already swarming with students by that point, so many of them were standing because there weren't enough seats to go round."},{"en":"stuffy atmosphere","context":"In that stuffy atmosphere, I noticed her face suddenly turn pale, and she had to grip the hand rail tightly, with sweat rolling down her forehead."},{"en":"pay close attention to","context":"My mother has always told me to pay close attention to the people around me and help out whenever I can, because small moments like that can matter more than we realise."},{"en":"the right thing to do","context":"I did it simply because it felt like the right thing to do, it wasn't really a decision, more just instinct."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a situation when you had to be polite","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-164","_displayNo":"#174","outline":{"topic":"give bus seat to pregnant woman","intro":"time gave up bus seat for a pregnant woman","body":["rainy day 3 years ago, freshman, bus to city centre for a music festival, pregnant woman got on, bus swarming and stuffy, she turned pale","stood up, offered seat, opened window, she felt better, chatted - going to visit aunt in hospital","did it bc felt like the right thing to do, instinct, mother's advice to pay attention to others"],"conclusion":"felt thrilled, helped two people - her and the baby"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-164.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I behaved politely was when I offered my bus seat to a pregnant woman. This happened on a rainy day about three years ago, during my first year at university. I was travelling from my dormitory to a music festival in the city centre.\n\nThe bus was already crowded when she got on. After a short time, she looked pale and held the rail tightly. I stood up and offered her my seat, then opened the window to let in some fresh air. She seemed more comfortable after sitting down.\n\nWe talked for the rest of the journey. She was visiting her aunt in hospital and had not wanted to wait for another bus. She thanked me warmly because she had been feeling faint. I did not think of the action as anything special; it simply seemed right. My mother has always encouraged me to notice when people need help. The experience made me feel pleased because a small, easy action had made someone else's journey much more comfortable.","html":"A time I behaved politely was when I offered my bus seat to a pregnant woman. This happened on a rainy day about three years ago, during my first year at university. I was travelling from my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dormitory</strong> to a music festival in the city centre.\n\nThe bus was already crowded when she got on. After a short time, she looked pale and held the rail tightly. I stood up and offered her my seat, then opened the window to let in some fresh air. She seemed more comfortable after sitting down.\n\nWe talked for the rest of the journey. She was visiting her aunt in hospital and had not wanted to wait for another bus. She thanked me warmly because she had been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feeling faint</strong>. I did not think of the action as anything special; it simply seemed right. My mother has always encouraged me to notice when people need help. The experience made me feel pleased because a small, easy action had made someone else's journey much more comfortable.","phrases":[{"en":"dormitory","meaning":"A building where students live while attending school or university."},{"en":"feeling faint","meaning":"Feeling weak or dizzy, as if you might lose consciousness."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-164.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A situation where you have to be polite","prompt":"Describe a situation where you have to be polite\nYou should say:\n- What it is.\n- What you do to be polite.\n- Why you have to be polite.\n- and how you felt about it.","answer":"I always try to be polite in most situations, that's just how I was brought up, but I'll admit there are some people out there who genuinely seem to enjoy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feeding on negative energy</strong>, and this is a story about one of them.\n\nIt happened when I had to work in a team with some university classmates on an assignment for Economic Studies. There was one guy in our group who I really couldn't stand, he always had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">half-baked knowledge</strong> about everything but acted like he knew it all, and his attitude wound the rest of us up constantly. Meetings would drag on because he'd argue over small details, and the whole project felt a lot more stressful than it needed to be.\n\nThe reason I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had to be polite</strong> was simple: we were stuck working together for about four months, and getting a good grade depended on the group actually functioning. So instead of arguing back, I made a point of staying calm in meetings, actually listening to his ideas before disagreeing, and giving him credit whenever he did contribute something useful, even when it was hard to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bite my tongue</strong>. It was difficult at first, mainly because he always assumed he was right, but by sticking with that approach, things gradually improved.\n\nBy the end of the project he wasn't nearly as difficult as he'd been at the start, and he'd actually become a lot more willing to collaborate with the rest of us. That experience taught me that you can't shield yourself from negative people completely, so the best you can do is learn how to handle them calmly and grow a slightly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thicker skin</strong>.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"feeding on negative energy","context":"I always try to be polite in most situations, that's just how I was brought up, but I'll admit there are some people out there who genuinely seem to enjoy feeding on negative energy, and this is a story about one of them."},{"en":"had to be polite","context":"The reason I had to be polite was simple: we were stuck working together for about four months, and getting a good grade depended on the group actually functioning."},{"en":"half-baked knowledge","context":"There was one guy in our group who I really couldn't stand, he always had half-baked knowledge about everything but acted like he knew it all, and his attitude wound the rest of us up constantly."},{"en":"bite my tongue","context":"So instead of arguing back, I made a point of staying calm in meetings, actually listening to his ideas before disagreeing, and giving him credit whenever he did contribute something useful, even when it was hard to bite my tongue."},{"en":"thicker skin","context":"That experience taught me that you can't shield yourself from negative people completely, so the best you can do is learn how to handle them calmly and grow a slightly thicker skin."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a situation where you have to be polite","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-165","_displayNo":"#175","outline":{"topic":"stay polite during team conflict","intro":"like start saying always try polite situations bc brought up","body":["happened work team w/ uni friends assignment Economic Studies","group one person disliked bc always half-baked knowledge everything perception knew","difficult 1st bc personality fact always thought right best ideas eventually","experience nice someone disliked"],"conclusion":"incident helped understand impossible shield yourself negativity things learn deal"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-165.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A situation where I needed to remain polite was a university group assignment for Economic Studies. We worked together for about four months. One member was difficult because he often acted as if he knew everything and argued over small details.\n\nAt first, his attitude annoyed the rest of us and made meetings longer than necessary. However, our final grade depended on working as a group, so arguing back would not have helped. I tried to stay calm, listen to his ideas and explain disagreements respectfully. When he suggested something useful, I made sure to recognise it.\n\nThis was not easy because I sometimes wanted to interrupt or complain. Gradually, though, he became more willing to cooperate. By the end, our discussions were more productive. The experience taught me that politeness can be practical, not just a matter of manners. I cannot always choose the people I work with, but I can control how I respond. Staying calm gave us a better chance of completing the assignment than turning every disagreement into a personal argument.","html":"A situation where I needed to remain polite was a university group assignment for Economic Studies. We worked together for about four months. One member was difficult because he often acted as if he knew everything and argued over small details.\n\nAt first, his attitude annoyed the rest of us and made meetings longer than necessary. However, our final grade depended on working as a group, so arguing back would not have helped. I tried to stay calm, listen to his ideas and explain disagreements respectfully. When he suggested something useful, I made sure to recognise it.\n\nThis was not easy because I sometimes wanted to interrupt or complain. Gradually, though, he became more willing to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cooperate</strong>. By the end, our discussions were more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">productive</strong>. The experience taught me that politeness can be practical, not just a matter of manners. I cannot always choose the people I work with, but I can control how I respond. Staying calm gave us a better chance of completing the assignment than turning every disagreement into a personal argument.","phrases":[{"en":"cooperate","meaning":"Work together to achieve something."},{"en":"productive","meaning":"Producing useful work or progress."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-165.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A success in your life","prompt":"Describe a success in your life\nYou should say:\nwhen it was\nwhat it was\nwhy you chose it\nhow you achieved.","answer":"The success I'd like to talk about today is my part in a bit of local activism that happened about five years ago, when I was just a freshman in college. I still feel really proud of it, because I genuinely think I helped do something good for my community.\n\nAt the time, the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">local authorities</strong> in my hometown had announced plans to knock down a public park, Thong Nhat Park, and build a shopping mall in its place. I noticed workers had already started cutting down some of the older trees, so I got a group of friends together and we decided to protest, even though I knew it wasn't going to be a simple fight against the local council.\n\nThe reason I got involved was that this park was the only real <strong class=\"key-phrase\">green space</strong> in the area. People came every single morning to jog, do tai chi or just sit and get some fresh air, and my own grandmother used to walk there daily too. It's also home to quite a few wild birds, which felt like something genuinely worth protecting rather than losing to another shopping centre.\n\nI knew that going up against the authorities wasn't going to be easy, but I felt I had to try, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">for the sake of</strong> the whole neighbourhood. We put together a petition, collected a few hundred signatures, and reached out to a couple of local news outlets to help <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make some noise about it</strong>. In the end, our campaign worked, the mall project was cancelled, and honestly, that whole experience still feels like a real <strong class=\"key-phrase\">success in my life</strong>.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"local authorities","context":"At the time, the local authorities in my hometown had announced plans to knock down a public park, Thong Nhat Park, and build a shopping mall in its place."},{"en":"success in my life","context":"In the end, our campaign worked, the mall project was cancelled, and honestly, that whole experience still feels like a real success in my life."},{"en":"green space","context":"The reason I got involved was that this park was the only real green space in the area."},{"en":"for the sake of","context":"I knew that going up against the authorities wasn't going to be easy, but I felt I had to try, for the sake of the whole neighbourhood."},{"en":"make some noise about it","context":"We put together a petition, collected a few hundred signatures, and reached out to a couple of local news outlets to help make some noise about it."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a success in your life","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-166","_displayNo":"#176","outline":{"topic":"social welfare activity","intro":"feel proud achievement believe done something good society","body":["success like talk today contribution social welfare activity occurred 5y back","taken notice cutting down trees gathered friends protest complicated situation","came day exercise enjoy fresh air order maintain improve health","made plans contact media go local authorities express view opinion end"],"conclusion":"made plans contact media go local authorities express view opinion"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-166.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A success I am proud of was helping protect Thong Nhat Park in my hometown about five years ago. I was a first-year college student when I heard plans to replace the park with a shopping centre.\n\nSome trees were already being cut down, so I gathered a group of friends who shared my concern. The park was the area's main green space. Residents used it for walking, jogging and tai chi, and my grandmother went there daily. It was also home to birds, which made losing it feel even worse.\n\nWe organised a petition and collected several hundred signatures. We also contacted local news organisations to explain why residents wanted the park kept. Eventually, the shopping centre plan was cancelled. I felt proud because our effort had helped protect something valuable for the community. The experience showed me that students can contribute to local decisions when they work together and explain their concerns clearly. I still see it as a success because the benefit was shared by many people, rather than belonging only to me.","html":"A success I am proud of was helping protect Thong Nhat Park in my hometown about five years ago. I was a first-year college student when I heard plans to replace the park with a shopping centre.\n\nSome trees were already being cut down, so I gathered a group of friends who shared my concern. The park was the area's main green space. Residents used it for walking, jogging and tai chi, and my grandmother went there daily. It was also home to birds, which made losing it feel even worse.\n\nWe organised a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">petition</strong> and collected several hundred signatures. We also contacted local news organisations to explain why residents wanted the park kept. Eventually, the shopping centre plan was cancelled. I felt proud because our effort had helped protect something valuable for the community. The experience showed me that students can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">contribute to local decisions</strong> when they work together and explain their concerns clearly. I still see it as a success because the benefit was shared by many people, rather than belonging only to me.","phrases":[{"en":"petition","meaning":"A written request that many people sign to show support."},{"en":"contribute to local decisions","meaning":"Help influence choices that affect the area where people live."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-166.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time that you arrived early","prompt":"Describe a time that you arrived early\nYou should say:\nWhen it was;\nHow early you arrived;\nWhy you arrived early;\nAnd explain how you felt about it.","answer":"So last week I actually had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">show up early</strong> for my best friend's wedding. As her <strong class=\"key-phrase\">maid of honour</strong>, I felt it was my responsibility to help her have the best day possible, so on the morning of the wedding I decided to turn up early and help with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">all the preparations</strong>.\n\nI think it was about five in the morning when I arrived at her front door. I'm honestly quite the night owl, so it was pretty tough for me to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get up that early</strong>, but the second I saw the huge smile on her face, I was wide awake and got straight to work. The to-do list was seriously long, decorating the room with flowers, washing up dishes, helping prepare the food, so I was really glad I'd made the effort to come early.\n\nBy about nine, once everything was finally sorted, the ceremony began in such a warm, sincere atmosphere. Watching the bride and groom <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exchanging vows</strong> was genuinely moving, I even teared up a little, and I felt so happy for her that she'd finally found the right person.\n\nAfter the party, even though I was completely exhausted, I also felt relieved and really delighted, because I like to think my help was part of what made her day so special.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"show up early","context":"So last week I actually had to show up early for my best friend's wedding."},{"en":"all the preparations","context":"As her maid of honour, I felt it was my responsibility to help her have the best day possible, so on the morning of the wedding I decided to turn up early and help with all the preparations."},{"en":"get up that early","context":"I'm honestly quite the night owl, so it was pretty tough for me to get up that early, but the second I saw the huge smile on her face, I was wide awake and got straight to work."},{"en":"exchanging vows","context":"Watching the bride and groom exchanging vows was genuinely moving, I even teared up a little, and I felt so happy for her that she'd finally found the right person."},{"en":"maid of honour","context":"As her maid of honour, I felt it was my responsibility to help her have the best day possible, so on the morning of the wedding I decided to turn up early and help with all the preparations."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time that you arrived early","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-167","_displayNo":"#177","outline":{"topic":"arrive early for friend's wedding","intro":"bestie think it's responsibility help best wedding ever wedding day","body":["think 5 a.m. arrived front door house","Actually night owl little difficult get up early once saw big","9am after everything thoroughly done wedding ceremony began sincere happy atmosphere","after wedding party despite exhausted relieved delighted bc think help one"],"conclusion":"after wedding party despite exhausted relieved delighted bc think help"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-167.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I arrived early was my best friend's wedding last week. I was her maid of honour, so I wanted to help with preparations and make the morning easier for her. I reached her home at about five o'clock.\n\nGetting up that early was difficult because I normally stay awake late. However, seeing her smile made me glad I had made the effort. There was plenty to do. We arranged flowers, washed dishes and helped prepare food. Starting early meant we had time to finish without rushing at the last moment.\n\nBy about nine, everything was ready and the ceremony began. Watching the couple make their promises was emotional, and I felt very happy for my friend. After the celebration, I was exhausted but also pleased. I knew my help had contributed to a special day. The experience reminded me that arriving early can be more than simply avoiding lateness. Sometimes it gives you a chance to support someone when they are busiest and show that an important event matters to you too.","html":"A time I arrived early was my best friend's wedding last week. I was her <strong class=\"key-phrase\">maid of honour</strong>, so I wanted to help with preparations and make the morning easier for her. I reached her home at about five o'clock.\n\nGetting up that early was difficult because I normally stay awake late. However, seeing her smile made me glad I had made the effort. There was plenty to do. We arranged flowers, washed dishes and helped prepare food. Starting early meant we had time to finish without rushing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at the last moment</strong>.\n\nBy about nine, everything was ready and the ceremony began. Watching the couple make their promises was emotional, and I felt very happy for my friend. After the celebration, I was exhausted but also pleased. I knew my help had contributed to a special day. The experience reminded me that arriving early can be more than simply avoiding lateness. Sometimes it gives you a chance to support someone when they are busiest and show that an important event matters to you too.","phrases":[{"en":"maid of honour","meaning":"The bride's main female helper at a wedding."},{"en":"at the last moment","meaning":"Just before something begins or must be completed."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-167.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when someone helped you","prompt":"Describe a time when someone helped you\nYou should say:\nWho helped you\nWhen it was\nWhy s/he helped you\nAnd describe how you felt about it.","answer":"About a month ago I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">received support from a stranger</strong>, in a moment that's stuck with me ever since. I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on my way back home from work</strong> quite late at night. Everything was fine until <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sudden torrential rain poured down</strong>, and to make things worse, my raincoat was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nowhere to be found</strong> in my bag.\n\nI couldn't find any shop or house along the road with any kind of cover where I could shelter for a bit, so I just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">kept moving on</strong>, resigning myself to arriving home looking like a drowned rat.\n\nThen, in my rear-view mirror, I noticed a young woman on a motorbike trying to catch up with me. When she got closer, she called out loudly enough for me to hear that she wanted to give me her spare raincoat. I was a bit sceptical at first, since my mother's always warned me not to trust strangers on the street, but one look at her face told me her offer was completely genuine. I took the raincoat with both hands and thanked her as sincerely as I could. She just smiled warmly, told me to keep it, and rode off into the distance before I could even ask her name.\n\nI was still soaked by the time I got home, but it was the kindness of that woman that really warmed me up from the inside. She was a complete stranger who had no reason to help me at all, and yet she did, and that small act of humanity is something I'll always remember.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"received support from a stranger","context":"About a month ago I received support from a stranger, in a moment that's stuck with me ever since."},{"en":"on my way back home from work","context":"I was on my way back home from work quite late at night."},{"en":"a sudden torrential rain poured down","context":"Everything was fine until a sudden torrential rain poured down, and to make things worse, my raincoat was nowhere to be found in my bag."},{"en":"nowhere to be found","context":"Everything was fine until a sudden torrential rain poured down, and to make things worse, my raincoat was nowhere to be found in my bag."},{"en":"kept moving on","context":"I couldn't find any shop or house along the road with any kind of cover where I could shelter for a bit, so I just kept moving on, resigning myself to arriving home looking like a drowned rat."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when someone helped you","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-168","_displayNo":"#178","outline":{"topic":"stranger gave me a raincoat","intro":"time received support stranger. back month ago way back home","body":["Everything fine until sudden torrential rain poured down raincoat nowhere found","rather skeptical 1st mother always warns not trust anyone street after","received raincoat w/ hands looked w/ deepest appreciation","However kindness woman warmed inside heart"],"conclusion":"Obviously no one ever met before willing help no reward"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-168.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time someone helped me was during a journey home from work about a month ago. It was late at night when heavy rain suddenly began. I searched my bag but realised I had forgotten my raincoat.\n\nThere was nowhere nearby to shelter, so I continued riding and expected to arrive completely wet. Then a young woman on a motorbike caught up with me. She called out and offered me a spare raincoat. At first, I was unsure because she was a stranger, but her manner seemed kind and honest.\n\nI accepted the raincoat and thanked her. She smiled, told me to keep it and rode away before I could ask her name. I was still wet when I reached home, but I felt much better emotionally. She had no responsibility to help and received nothing in return. That made the action especially memorable. The experience reminded me how much a small act of kindness can matter at the right time. I hope I would notice a similar opportunity to help someone else.","html":"A time someone helped me was during a journey home from work about a month ago. It was late at night when heavy rain suddenly began. I searched my bag but realised I had forgotten my raincoat.\n\nThere was nowhere nearby to shelter, so I continued riding and expected to arrive completely wet. Then a young woman on a motorbike caught up with me. She called out and offered me a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spare raincoat</strong>. At first, I was unsure because she was a stranger, but her manner seemed kind and honest.\n\nI accepted the raincoat and thanked her. She smiled, told me to keep it and rode away before I could ask her name. I was still wet when I reached home, but I felt much better emotionally. She had no responsibility to help and received nothing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in return</strong>. That made the action especially memorable. The experience reminded me how much a small act of kindness can matter at the right time. I hope I would notice a similar opportunity to help someone else.","phrases":[{"en":"spare raincoat","meaning":"An extra coat used for protection from rain."},{"en":"in return","meaning":"As something given back after receiving help or a gift."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-168.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when someone visited your home","prompt":"Describe a time when someone visited your home\nYou should say:\n- Who he or she was\n- When it happened\n- What you did\n- How you felt about the visit","answer":"It was around four months ago that I invited one of my closest friends round for dinner. It happened to be Christmas Eve, and since he's an expat living far from his own family, I didn't want him spending it alone, so I asked him to join us for a proper family dinner instead.\n\nMy friend's American, and since he'd missed out on turkey that Thanksgiving, my mother decided to cook turkey specially for him that night instead, as a kind of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">belated treat</strong>.\n\nOne of the funniest things about that evening was that almost every cooking appliance in our house runs on electricity, even the stove, and of course we had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a blackout</strong> exactly an hour before dinner was due to start. By the time my friend arrived, none of the food was anywhere near ready. I was mortified, but he was completely relaxed about the whole thing. We lit candles all over the kitchen, my father dashed out to buy some bread and ham, and we ended up sitting round talking and snacking while we waited for the power to come back on. Honestly, we had an amazing time discussing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the cultural differences</strong> between our countries, how hospitable Vietnamese people are, and how <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the language barrier</strong> never stopped him from getting to know the locals.\n\nWe finally sat down to the turkey after a two-hour delay, and even though my mother's version turned out quite differently from how his family makes it back home, he genuinely loved it. My parents absolutely adored him after that night, and he's been coming round much more often ever since, he's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practically part of the family</strong> now.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"a blackout","context":"One of the funniest things about that evening was that almost every cooking appliance in our house runs on electricity, even the stove, and of course we had a blackout exactly an hour before dinner was due to start."},{"en":"the cultural differences","context":"Honestly, we had an amazing time discussing the cultural differences between our countries, how hospitable Vietnamese people are, and how the language barrier never stopped him from getting to know the locals."},{"en":"the language barrier","context":"Honestly, we had an amazing time discussing the cultural differences between our countries, how hospitable Vietnamese people are, and how the language barrier never stopped him from getting to know the locals."},{"en":"belated treat","context":"My friend's American, and since he'd missed out on turkey that Thanksgiving, my mother decided to cook turkey specially for him that night instead, as a kind of belated treat."},{"en":"practically part of the family","context":"My parents absolutely adored him after that night, and he's been coming round much more often ever since, he's practically part of the family now."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when someone visited your home","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-169","_displayNo":"#179","outline":{"topic":"Thanksgiving dinner","intro":"around 4 mos ago invited one close friends come house","body":["not want spend Christmas alone asked join family special family dinner","One funny things remember night pretty cooking appliances house electrical stove","absolutely amazing time talking cultural differences hospitable Vietnamese people language barrier","mother made differently way friend's family enjoyed it"],"conclusion":"coming often becoming close friend family"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-169.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A memorable home visit was when I invited an American friend for dinner on Christmas Eve about four months ago. He lived far from his family, and I did not want him to spend the evening alone. My mother decided to cook turkey because he had missed it at Thanksgiving.\n\nUnfortunately, the electricity failed an hour before dinner. Most of our cooking equipment needed power, so the food was not ready when he arrived. I felt embarrassed, but he stayed relaxed. We lit candles, and my father bought bread and ham to eat while we waited.\n\nWe spent the delay talking about differences between our countries and his experience of living in Vietnam. The conversation was so enjoyable that the problem mattered less than I expected. We finally ate turkey two hours late. It tasted different from his family's version, but he said he loved it. My parents enjoyed his company, and he has visited more often since then. The evening showed me that a warm welcome matters more than a perfectly organised meal.","html":"A memorable home visit was when I invited an American friend for dinner on Christmas Eve about four months ago. He lived far from his family, and I did not want him to spend the evening alone. My mother decided to cook turkey because he had missed it at Thanksgiving.\n\nUnfortunately, the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">electricity failed</strong> an hour before dinner. Most of our cooking equipment needed power, so the food was not ready when he arrived. I felt embarrassed, but he stayed relaxed. We lit candles, and my father bought bread and ham to eat while we waited.\n\nWe spent the delay talking about differences between our countries and his experience of living in Vietnam. The conversation was so enjoyable that the problem mattered less than I expected. We finally ate turkey two hours late. It tasted different from his family's version, but he said he loved it. My parents enjoyed his company, and he has visited more often since then. The evening showed me that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a warm welcome</strong> matters more than a perfectly organised meal.","phrases":[{"en":"electricity failed","meaning":"The supply of electrical power stopped working."},{"en":"a warm welcome","meaning":"Friendly and kind treatment when someone arrives."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-169.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when you had a problem with a piece of equipment","prompt":"Describe a time when you had a problem with a piece of equipment\nYou should say:\n- What equipment it was\n- What problem you had\n- When you had this problem\nAnd explain what you did about this problem.","answer":"I'm usually pretty quick when it comes to technology, but I want to tell you about the one time I completely panicked over what turned out to be a fake problem with my phone.\n\nThis story <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dates back to</strong> when I got my very first smartphone, about seven or eight years ago. Back then, most students still had a basic phone, or what we used to call a dumbphone, that could barely do more than call and text, so turning up to school with a brand-new smartphone was a huge deal, and I instantly became the target of quite a bit of jealousy.\n\nAt first it was actually pretty nice being the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">centre of attention</strong>, but after a while I noticed my classmates kept borrowing it to download apps onto it behind my back. Later, one of them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">let slip</strong> that a boy in our class had secretly installed something that would drain the battery fast, and sure enough, right after that, the phone just switched itself off. I was horrified, it was literally the first day I'd owned it, and I had no idea how to explain it to my parents, especially since I was too broke to get it fixed at a shop.\n\nI sat there in silence, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on the verge of tears</strong>, convinced my life as I knew it was over. My desk-mate noticed and asked what was wrong, and when I told her, instead of comforting me, she just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">burst out laughing</strong> until her face went red. Confused, I asked what was so funny, and she just told me to turn the phone back on. It worked instantly, my classmates had fooled me the whole time.\n\nSo that's how I \"fixed\" the huge technical problem with my phone, by pressing one button.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"dates back to","context":"This story dates back to when I got my very first smartphone, about seven or eight years ago."},{"en":"centre of attention","context":"At first it was actually pretty nice being the centre of attention, but after a while I noticed my classmates kept borrowing it to download apps onto it behind my back."},{"en":"let slip","context":"Later, one of them let slip that a boy in our class had secretly installed something that would drain the battery fast, and sure enough, right after that, the phone just switched itself off."},{"en":"on the verge of tears","context":"I sat there in silence, on the verge of tears, convinced my life as I knew it was over."},{"en":"burst out laughing","context":"My desk-mate noticed and asked what was wrong, and when I told her, instead of comforting me, she just burst out laughing until her face went red."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when you had a problem with a piece of equipment","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-170","_displayNo":"#180","outline":{"topic":"1st smartphone technical problem","intro":"actually quick comes technology devices going tell one time couldn't","body":["story dates back got 1st smartphone. time 7 – 8y ago","not know tell mother father incident bc sure kill 2nd found","thought going say something cheer up no laughed hard until whole","stopped crying looked w/ curiosity told turn phone on"],"conclusion":"That's fixed problem w/ phone surprisingly easy way"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-170.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A problem I thought I had with equipment involved my first smartphone about seven or eight years ago. Most classmates still had basic phones, so my new one attracted a lot of attention on its first day at school.\n\nSeveral people asked to borrow it and added apps. Later, someone told me another student had secretly installed something that would damage the battery. Soon afterwards, the phone switched off. I panicked because it was brand new, and I did not know how I could explain a repair bill to my parents.\n\nMy desk mate noticed how upset I was and asked what had happened. When I explained, she started laughing and told me to press the power button. The phone turned on immediately. My classmates had been playing a joke, and there had been no serious technical problem at all. I felt relieved and rather embarrassed. Looking back, the situation is funny because I imagined the worst before checking the simplest explanation. I learned to stay calmer and investigate before assuming equipment is broken.","html":"A problem I thought I had with equipment involved my first smartphone about seven or eight years ago. Most classmates still had basic phones, so my new one attracted a lot of attention on its first day at school.\n\nSeveral people asked to borrow it and added apps. Later, someone told me another student had secretly installed something that would damage the battery. Soon afterwards, the phone switched off. I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">panicked</strong> because it was brand new, and I did not know how I could explain a repair bill to my parents.\n\nMy desk mate noticed how upset I was and asked what had happened. When I explained, she started laughing and told me to press the power button. The phone turned on immediately. My classmates had been playing a joke, and there had been no serious technical problem at all. I felt relieved and rather embarrassed. Looking back, the situation is funny because I imagined the worst before checking the simplest explanation. I learned to stay calmer and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">investigate</strong> before assuming equipment is broken.","phrases":[{"en":"panicked","meaning":"Suddenly felt very frightened and unable to think calmly."},{"en":"investigate","meaning":"Look carefully for information to understand what happened."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-170.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when you had to do something in a hurry","prompt":"Describe a time when you had to do something in a hurry.\nYou should say:\n- what you were doing\n- when this was\n- why you had to hurry\n- and explain how you felt at that time","answer":"About five years ago, I had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get ready in a great hurry</strong> for a work trip, and it's a morning I still cringe about. A few colleagues and I had to catch an early flight to a conference in Da Nang, and a senior colleague had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">offered to collect me from home</strong> at half past five in the morning.\n\nThe problem was that I'd overslept. I'd set an alarm the night before, but I must have switched it off in my sleep without ever properly waking up. At exactly 5.30, the doorbell rang, and I looked at the clock in complete shock, I hadn't even got out of bed yet. I jumped up, got dressed in what must have been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">record time</strong>, splashed water on my face, brushed my teeth in about ten seconds and shoved my shoes on without bothering to tie the laces properly.\n\nFortunately, I'd packed my suitcase the night before, so at least I didn't have to think about that. I grabbed it, apologised to my colleague about six times on the way down, and jumped into the car. We ended up reaching the airport <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with only minutes to spare</strong>, and since the rest of the group had already been standing around waiting for me, I felt absolutely mortified walking up to them.\n\nThe whole incident taught me a pretty practical lesson. Before any early journey now, I always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set two independent alarms</strong>, and I make sure to leave my phone far enough from the bed that I physically have to get up to switch it off.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"get ready in a great hurry","context":"About five years ago, I had to get ready in a great hurry for a work trip, and it's a morning I still cringe about."},{"en":"offered to collect me from home","context":"A few colleagues and I had to catch an early flight to a conference in Da Nang, and a senior colleague had offered to collect me from home at half past five in the morning."},{"en":"with only minutes to spare","context":"We ended up reaching the airport with only minutes to spare, and since the rest of the group had already been standing around waiting for me, I felt absolutely mortified walking up to them."},{"en":"set two independent alarms","context":"The whole incident taught me a pretty practical lesson. Before any early journey now, I always set two independent alarms, and I make sure to leave my phone far enough from the bed that I physically have to get up to switch it off."},{"en":"record time","context":"I jumped up, got dressed in what must have been record time, splashed water on my face, brushed my teeth in about ten seconds and shoved my shoes on without bothering to tie the laces properly."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when you had to do something in a hurry","id":"speaking-part-2-171","_displayNo":"#181","outline":{"topic":"oversleep before work trip","intro":"time get ready great hurry work trip","body":["senior colleague offered collect home 5.30 morning","jumped out bed dressed record time splashed water face brushed teeth","grabbed apologised repeatedly colleague got car","Before early journey now set two independent alarms place phone far"],"conclusion":"Before early journey now set two independent alarms place phone"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-171.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I had to hurry was before a work trip about five years ago. My colleagues and I had an early flight to a conference in Da Nang. A senior colleague had agreed to collect me at half past five in the morning.\n\nI had set an alarm, but apparently switched it off without waking properly. When the doorbell rang at five thirty, I was still in bed. I jumped up, got dressed, washed quickly and brushed my teeth in a rush. Luckily, my suitcase was already packed from the night before.\n\nI apologised repeatedly and ran to the car. We reached the airport with very little time left, and the rest of the group had been waiting. I felt embarrassed because my mistake had affected other people, not only me. Since then, I have used two separate alarms before early journeys. I also leave my phone away from the bed so I must stand up to turn it off. It was an unpleasant morning, but it helped me become more careful about preparation and punctuality.","html":"A time I had to hurry was before a work trip about five years ago. My colleagues and I had an early flight to a conference in Da Nang. A senior colleague had agreed to collect me at half past five in the morning.\n\nI had set an alarm, but apparently switched it off without waking properly. When the doorbell rang at five thirty, I was still in bed. I jumped up, got dressed, washed quickly and brushed my teeth <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in a rush</strong>. Luckily, my suitcase was already packed from the night before.\n\nI apologised repeatedly and ran to the car. We reached the airport with very little time left, and the rest of the group had been waiting. I felt embarrassed because my mistake had affected other people, not only me. Since then, I have used two separate alarms before early journeys. I also leave my phone away from the bed so I must stand up to turn it off. It was an unpleasant morning, but it helped me become more careful about preparation and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">punctuality</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"in a rush","meaning":"Doing something quickly because there is little time."},{"en":"punctuality","meaning":"The habit of arriving or doing things at the correct time."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-171.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when you had to work hard to achieve a goal","prompt":"Describe a time when you had to work hard to achieve a goal\nYou should say:\nwhat the goal was\nwhen this happened\nwhat you did to achieve this goal\nand explain why the goal was important to you.","answer":"I'm the type of person who's never liked relying on luck, I believe everything worthwhile can only be achieved <strong class=\"key-phrase\">through hard work and determination</strong>, and my final year of high school was one of the times I really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spared no effort</strong> to reach a goal.\n\nBack then, like every other senior, my main goal was to pass the university entrance exam and get into my dream university, but the pressure felt even higher for me because I'd set my sights on Hanoi Medical University, one of the top universities in the country. Thousands of students sit that exam every year, so it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">highly competitive</strong>, to put it mildly.\n\nTo keep up with the other candidates, I'd spend hours in the library every single day, reading as many textbooks as I could get my hands on, and even after school I'd stay up late going back over everything I'd covered that day. It was an exhausting few months, but I didn't feel tired at all, becoming a doctor was one of my biggest dreams, and I was determined not to let anything get in the way of it.\n\nIn the end, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the hard work paid off</strong>: I passed the entrance exam with results I was genuinely proud of and got a place as a medical student. When the results came out, I was so happy I actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">burst into tears</strong>. Looking back, that whole year of effort helped me reach one of the biggest goals I've set myself so far.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"through hard work and determination","context":"I'm the type of person who's never liked relying on luck, I believe everything worthwhile can only be achieved through hard work and determination, and my final year of high school was one of the times I really spared no effort to reach a goal."},{"en":"spared no effort","context":"I'm the type of person who's never liked relying on luck, I believe everything worthwhile can only be achieved through hard work and determination, and my final year of high school was one of the times I really spared no effort to reach a goal."},{"en":"highly competitive","context":"Thousands of students sit that exam every year, so it's highly competitive, to put it mildly."},{"en":"the hard work paid off","context":"In the end, the hard work paid off: I passed the entrance exam with results I was genuinely proud of and got a place as a medical student."},{"en":"burst into tears","context":"When the results came out, I was so happy I actually burst into tears."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when you had to work hard to achieve a goal","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-172","_displayNo":"#182","outline":{"topic":"enter Hanoi Medical Uni","intro":"type person never likes rely luck","body":["believe everything achieved through hard work determination final year high school","order compete w/ candidates day spend hours library read books possible","long process w/ lot hard work not feel tired bc becoming","end hard work paid off passed entrance exam w/ outstanding results"],"conclusion":"hard work year helped achieve one big goals life far"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-172.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I worked hard for a goal was my final year of high school. I wanted to enter Hanoi Medical University because becoming a doctor had been an important dream for me. I knew the entrance exam was highly competitive.\n\nTo prepare, I spent many hours in the library reading textbooks and practising questions. After school, I reviewed the work from that day and tried to understand anything I had found difficult. The routine lasted several months and required me to give up some free time.\n\nAlthough I felt tired at times, thinking about my goal helped me continue. I did not want to depend only on luck when so many other students were preparing seriously. In the end, I passed with results I was proud of and received a place at the university. I was so happy that I cried when I saw the news. The achievement mattered because it showed me what sustained effort could do. Looking back, the preparation was difficult, but reaching a goal that meant so much made the work feel worthwhile.","html":"A time I worked hard for a goal was my final year of high school. I wanted to enter Hanoi Medical University because becoming a doctor had been an important dream for me. I knew the entrance exam was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">highly competitive</strong>.\n\nTo prepare, I spent many hours in the library reading textbooks and practising questions. After school, I reviewed the work from that day and tried to understand anything I had found difficult. The routine lasted several months and required me to give up some free time.\n\nAlthough I felt tired at times, thinking about my goal helped me continue. I did not want to depend only on luck when so many other students were preparing seriously. In the end, I passed with results I was proud of and received a place at the university. I was so happy that I cried when I saw the news. The achievement mattered because it showed me what <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sustained effort</strong> could do. Looking back, the preparation was difficult, but reaching a goal that meant so much made the work feel worthwhile.","phrases":[{"en":"highly competitive","meaning":"Difficult to succeed in because many people are trying for limited places."},{"en":"sustained effort","meaning":"Work that continues steadily over a long period."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-172.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when you received good service in a restaurant or cafe","prompt":"Describe a time when you received good service in a restaurant or cafe\nYou should say:\n- when this was\n- what kind of restaurant or cafe you were in\n- why you were there\n- and why you thought the service was good","answer":"I'll tell you about the service we got at a restaurant when a friend of mine, Duc, celebrated his birthday last Saturday evening. He'd booked a table at a small, family-run Italian place near his flat, and I wasn't expecting much from somewhere so tiny, but it turned out to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">all the more welcoming</strong> for being so small and personal.\n\nThere were about ten of us altogether, mostly people I've known since university, and it was great to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catch up with</strong> everyone and hear what they'd been up to since we'd last got together. We were there for Duc's birthday, but also because he'd been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raving about the place</strong> for weeks, so the rest of us were curious to try it for ourselves.\n\nWhat made the service stand out was that the staff didn't just take our order and disappear - they actually made an effort to make us feel at home. The waiters were attentive without hovering, and whenever anyone needed something, it was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no trouble at all</strong> for them. At one point they brought out a cake with candles and sang 'Happy Birthday', even though nobody had asked them to, which was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a really nice touch</strong>. The owner also came round a couple of times just to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">check we were happy with everything</strong>, and it didn't feel like a routine question - he genuinely seemed to care.\n\nAll in all, it was those little extra touches, more than the food itself, that made the evening so memorable. I'd definitely go back, and I've already <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recommended it to</strong> a couple of friends.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"all the more welcoming","context":"He'd booked a table at a small, family-run Italian place near his flat, and I wasn't expecting much from somewhere so tiny, but it turned out to be all the more welcoming for being so small and personal."},{"en":"catch up with","context":"There were about ten of us altogether, mostly people I've known since university, and it was great to catch up with everyone and hear what they'd been up to since we'd last got together."},{"en":"raving about the place","context":"We were there for Duc's birthday, but also because he'd been raving about the place for weeks, so the rest of us were curious to try it for ourselves."},{"en":"no trouble at all","context":"The waiters were attentive without hovering, and whenever anyone needed something, it was no trouble at all for them."},{"en":"a really nice touch","context":"At one point they brought out a cake with candles and sang 'Happy Birthday', even though nobody had asked them to, which was a really nice touch."},{"en":"check we were happy with everything","context":"The owner also came round a couple of times just to check we were happy with everything, and it didn't feel like a routine question - he genuinely seemed to care."},{"en":"recommended it to","context":"All in all, it was those little extra touches, more than the food itself, that made the evening so memorable. I'd definitely go back, and I've already recommended it to a couple of friends."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when you received good service in a restaurant or","id":"speaking-part-2-173","_displayNo":"#183","outline":{"topic":"Italian restaurant birthday service","intro":"friend Duc birthday last Saturday small family-run Italian place near his flat","body":["ten of us mostly people known since uni catch up w/ everyone raving about place","staff didn't just take order disappear made effort feel at home attentive w/o hovering","brought out cake candles sang Happy Birthday nobody asked nice touch","owner came round check happy everything didn't feel routine genuinely cared"],"conclusion":"little extra touches more than food made evening memorable recommended to friends"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-173.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I received good restaurant service was at my friend Duc's birthday last Saturday. He booked a table at a small Italian restaurant near his flat. About ten of us attended, mostly friends from university.\n\nI did not expect much before arriving, but the small, family-run place felt very welcoming. The waiters noticed when we needed something without standing too close or interrupting our conversation. The owner also checked that we were enjoying ourselves and seemed interested in our answers.\n\nThe nicest moment was when staff brought a cake with candles and sang Happy Birthday, although we had not asked them to. It made Duc feel special and gave everyone something to remember. Of course, the food mattered too, but the personal attention was what made the evening stand out. We were treated as guests rather than simply another order to complete. I would happily return, and I have already recommended it to friends. The experience showed me that good service often comes from small actions that make customers feel noticed and welcome.","html":"A time I received good restaurant service was at my friend Duc's birthday last Saturday. He booked a table at a small Italian restaurant near his flat. About ten of us attended, mostly friends from university.\n\nI did not expect much before arriving, but the small, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">family-run</strong> place felt very welcoming. The waiters noticed when we needed something without standing too close or interrupting our conversation. The owner also checked that we were enjoying ourselves and seemed interested in our answers.\n\nThe nicest moment was when staff brought a cake with candles and sang Happy Birthday, although we had not asked them to. It made Duc feel special and gave everyone something to remember. Of course, the food mattered too, but the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">personal attention</strong> was what made the evening stand out. We were treated as guests rather than simply another order to complete. I would happily return, and I have already recommended it to friends. The experience showed me that good service often comes from small actions that make customers feel noticed and welcome.","phrases":[{"en":"family-run","meaning":"Managed by members of one family."},{"en":"personal attention","meaning":"Care given to someone's individual needs or experience."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-173.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when you saw an interesting animal","prompt":"Describe a time when you saw an interesting animal\nYou should say:\n- What animal it was\n- When and Where you saw it\nAnd explain how you feel about this animal","answer":"I'd like to talk about the time I saw a troop of wild monkeys on a school trip when I was about fourteen. We'd gone to climb a mountain on the outskirts of Hanoi with a few classmates, and about halfway up, a group of small monkeys suddenly appeared in the middle of the path ahead of us.\n\nAt first we all froze - I hadn't expected to see any wildlife at all, let alone a dozen monkeys just sitting there watching us. They came a bit closer and started <strong class=\"key-phrase\">staring us down</strong>, almost as if they were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sizing us up</strong> to see whether we had any food. One of my friends had a bag of biscuits, so we ended up <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tossing a few scraps</strong> their way, and within seconds they were fighting each other over the crumbs.\n\nWhat struck me most was how human their hands looked - small and wrinkled, almost like a tiny version of our own. It made them seem oddly familiar rather than wild, and I remember thinking that must be part of why people find them so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">endlessly entertaining</strong> to watch. At the same time, I quickly learned they're not as harmless as they look. Later on the same trip, I saw a monkey <strong class=\"key-phrase\">snatch a bag of crisps</strong> straight out of a tourist's hand and disappear into the trees before anyone could react, so there's clearly a cheeky, opportunistic side to them too.\n\nLooking back, it's one of those small experiences that's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuck with me</strong> ever since. It genuinely made me curious about how closely we're related to them, and I ended up reading a bit about evolution afterwards, just because of that one encounter on the mountain.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"staring us down","context":"They came a bit closer and started staring us down, almost as if they were sizing us up to see whether we had any food."},{"en":"sizing us up","context":"They came a bit closer and started staring us down, almost as if they were sizing us up to see whether we had any food."},{"en":"tossing a few scraps","context":"One of my friends had a bag of biscuits, so we ended up tossing a few scraps their way, and within seconds they were fighting each other over the crumbs."},{"en":"endlessly entertaining","context":"It made them seem oddly familiar rather than wild, and I remember thinking that must be part of why people find them so endlessly entertaining to watch."},{"en":"snatch a bag of crisps","context":"Later on the same trip, I saw a monkey snatch a bag of crisps straight out of a tourist's hand and disappear into the trees before anyone could react, so there's clearly a cheeky, opportunistic side to them too."},{"en":"stuck with me","context":"Looking back, it's one of those small experiences that's stuck with me ever since."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when you saw an interesting animal","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-174","_displayNo":"#184","outline":{"topic":"wild monkeys on a mountain school trip","intro":"saw troop wild monkeys school trip age fourteen mountain outskirts Hanoi w/ classmates","body":["halfway up group small monkeys appeared path ahead froze staring us down sizing us up","friend had biscuits tossed few scraps monkeys fought over crumbs","hands looked human small wrinkled mini version our own made them seem familiar endlessly entertaining","later monkey snatched bag crisps straight tourist's hand disappeared into trees"],"conclusion":"experience stuck with me made curious how closely related read about evolution after"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-174.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I saw an interesting animal was on a mountain trip near Hanoi when I was about fourteen. I was with classmates, and halfway up the path we suddenly met a group of wild monkeys.\n\nThere were around a dozen, and at first we stopped in surprise. They watched us and moved closer, apparently interested in whether we had food. One friend threw a few biscuit pieces, and the monkeys quickly gathered around them. Later, we saw one take a bag of crisps from a tourist and disappear into the trees.\n\nWhat interested me most was their hands. They looked surprisingly similar to small human hands, with fingers and wrinkles. The animals were entertaining to watch, but the stolen food reminded me that they were still wild and could behave unexpectedly. I had not expected the walk to include any wildlife, so the encounter stayed in my memory. Afterwards, I read a little about evolution because I wanted to understand our connection with monkeys better. A brief meeting on a path had created a new curiosity.","html":"A time I saw an interesting animal was on a mountain trip near Hanoi when I was about fourteen. I was with classmates, and halfway up the path we suddenly met a group of wild monkeys.\n\nThere were around a dozen, and at first we stopped in surprise. They watched us and moved closer, apparently interested in whether we had food. One friend threw a few biscuit pieces, and the monkeys quickly gathered around them. Later, we saw one take a bag of crisps from a tourist and disappear into the trees.\n\nWhat interested me most was their hands. They looked surprisingly similar to small human hands, with fingers and wrinkles. The animals were entertaining to watch, but the stolen food reminded me that they were still wild and could behave unexpectedly. I had not expected the walk to include any wildlife, so the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">encounter</strong> stayed in my memory. Afterwards, I read a little about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">evolution</strong> because I wanted to understand our connection with monkeys better. A brief meeting on a path had created a new curiosity.","phrases":[{"en":"encounter","meaning":"An unexpected meeting or experience."},{"en":"evolution","meaning":"The gradual development and change of living things over many generations."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-174.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when you were looking at the sky","prompt":"Describe a time when you were looking at the sky\nYou should say:\nWhen and where you looked at the sky\nWho you looked at sky with\nHow you felt when looking at it\nAnd explain why you looked at the sky","answer":"A couple of summers ago I had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a great opportunity</strong> to escape the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">scorching heat</strong> of a Hanoi summer and visit London with my mother. I remember looking up at the sky properly, really noticing it, on the drive from the airport to our hotel - something I don't think I'd ever consciously done back home.\n\nWe'd landed on a rare sunny day, which apparently isn't typical for London at all - I'd been told to expect grey skies almost <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the whole time</strong>. So when we stepped out of the airport into bright sunshine, I was pleasantly surprised, and I remember opening the taxi window just to feel the breeze on my face.\n\nBy the time we were halfway to the hotel, though, the sky had completely changed. Thick grey clouds had rolled in from nowhere, and it looked ready to pour down any moment. I kept glancing up, half fascinated and half worried we'd get soaked before we even checked in - which, sure enough, we did, the second we stepped out of the taxi.\n\nThat evening my mother and I sat by the hotel window for a long while, watching the rain <strong class=\"key-phrase\">come down in sheets</strong> and the sky <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turn a heavy shade of grey</strong>. Neither of us said much; we just watched it together, which was actually a nice way to end an odd first day. I think I kept looking up mainly out of disbelief - it was hard to square that dull, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overcast and grey</strong> sky with the postcard pictures I'd seen of London. It wasn't unpleasant exactly, more a quiet kind of disappointment, one of those small moments that somehow <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sums up the whole trip</strong>.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"a great opportunity","context":"A couple of summers ago I had a great opportunity to escape the scorching heat of a Hanoi summer and visit London with my mother."},{"en":"scorching heat","context":"A couple of summers ago I had a great opportunity to escape the scorching heat of a Hanoi summer and visit London with my mother."},{"en":"the whole time","context":"We'd landed on a rare sunny day, which apparently isn't typical for London at all - I'd been told to expect grey skies almost the whole time."},{"en":"come down in sheets","context":"That evening my mother and I sat by the hotel window for a long while, watching the rain come down in sheets and the sky turn a heavy shade of grey."},{"en":"turn a heavy shade of grey","context":"That evening my mother and I sat by the hotel window for a long while, watching the rain come down in sheets and the sky turn a heavy shade of grey."},{"en":"overcast and grey","context":"I think I kept looking up mainly out of disbelief - it was hard to square that dull, overcast and grey sky with the postcard pictures I'd seen of London."},{"en":"sums up the whole trip","context":"It wasn't unpleasant exactly, more a quiet kind of disappointment, one of those small moments that somehow sums up the whole trip."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when you were looking at the sky","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-175","_displayNo":"#185","outline":{"topic":"watching London's sky change with my mother","intro":"couple summers ago great opportunity escape Hanoi heat visit London w/ mother noticed sky drive airport hotel","body":["landed rare sunny day surprised expected grey skies drizzle whole time opened taxi window","halfway hotel sky changed thick grey clouds rolled in about pour down","soaked moment stepped out taxi","evening mother sat hotel window watching rain come down sheets sky turn heavy grey"],"conclusion":"kept looking up out of disbelief odd quiet disappointment sums up whole trip"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-175.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I paid close attention to the sky was during a trip to London with my mother a couple of summers ago. We were travelling from the airport to our hotel after leaving the intense summer heat of Hanoi.\n\nWhen we first arrived, the sky was bright and sunny. I had expected grey weather, so I was pleasantly surprised. I opened the taxi window to enjoy the cool air. However, halfway through the journey, thick clouds appeared and the light changed quickly. By the time we stepped out at the hotel, it was raining heavily.\n\nThat evening, my mother and I sat by the window and watched the rain. The sky looked dark and grey, very different from the sunny photographs I had imagined before the trip. I felt a little disappointed, but the quiet time together was still pleasant. I kept looking because I was surprised by how quickly the weather had changed. It became one of those ordinary travel memories that stay with you, even though nothing particularly dramatic happened.","html":"A time I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">paid close attention</strong> to the sky was during a trip to London with my mother a couple of summers ago. We were travelling from the airport to our hotel after leaving the intense summer heat of Hanoi.\n\nWhen we first arrived, the sky was bright and sunny. I had expected grey weather, so I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pleasantly surprised</strong>. I opened the taxi window to enjoy the cool air. However, halfway through the journey, thick clouds appeared and the light changed quickly. By the time we stepped out at the hotel, it was raining heavily.\n\nThat evening, my mother and I sat by the window and watched the rain. The sky looked dark and grey, very different from the sunny photographs I had imagined before the trip. I felt a little disappointed, but the quiet time together was still pleasant. I kept looking because I was surprised by how quickly the weather had changed. It became one of those ordinary travel memories that stay with you, even though nothing particularly dramatic happened.","phrases":[{"en":"paid close attention","meaning":"Watched or listened very carefully."},{"en":"pleasantly surprised","meaning":"Pleased because something was better than expected."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-175.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when you were not allowed to use your cell phone","prompt":"Describe a time when you were not allowed to use your cell phone\nYou should say:\nWhere you were\nWhen it happened\nWhat you were doing\nAnd why you were not allowed to use your cell phone","answer":"Mobile phones have basically become the must-have item for everyone these days - we can't seem to live without them. But I still remember one time I had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">be apart from my phone</strong> completely: during my high school graduation exam two years ago, and it honestly felt quite uncomfortable.\n\nThat exam is compulsory in Vietnam - you have to sit it at the end of grade 12 to get your diploma, and your score also decides whether you can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get into university</strong>. It's one of the biggest events in a student's life, really, because it doesn't just shape your future, it's also a way of proving to yourself what you're capable of.\n\nNaturally, phones aren't allowed anywhere near the exam room, so nobody can get <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an unfair advantage</strong> by cheating. If your phone even makes a noise, or if you're caught using it at any point, including during breaks, you can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disqualified on the spot</strong>, and your results get cancelled, no exceptions.\n\nI'll admit I had a bit of a habit of checking my phone whenever I was doing practice tests at home, just to see if I'd got the right answers. So during the actual exam, I kept feeling this urge to reach for it, but obviously I couldn't, so I just had to sit with the uncertainty. Not knowing whether I'd got things right was honestly more stressful than the exam itself, and I didn't really relax until I'd got home and checked everything against the answer key.\n\nLooking back, that experience made me realise I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rely too much on my phone</strong> for reassurance. It's a brilliant tool for studying, but I think it also taught me to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trust my own judgement</strong> a bit more, instead of needing to double-check everything straight away.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"be apart from my phone","context":"But I still remember one time I had to be apart from my phone completely: during my high school graduation exam two years ago, and it honestly felt quite uncomfortable."},{"en":"get into university","context":"That exam is compulsory in Vietnam - you have to sit it at the end of grade 12 to get your diploma, and your score also decides whether you can get into university."},{"en":"an unfair advantage","context":"Naturally, phones aren't allowed anywhere near the exam room, so nobody can get an unfair advantage by cheating."},{"en":"disqualified on the spot","context":"If your phone even makes a noise, or if you're caught using it at any point, including during breaks, you can be disqualified on the spot, and your results get cancelled, no exceptions."},{"en":"rely too much on my phone","context":"Looking back, that experience made me realise I rely too much on my phone for reassurance. It's a brilliant tool for studying, but I think it also taught me to trust my own judgement a bit more, instead of needing to double-check everything straight away."},{"en":"trust my own judgement","context":"Looking back, that experience made me realise I rely too much on my phone for reassurance. It's a brilliant tool for studying, but I think it also taught me to trust my own judgement a bit more, instead of needing to double-check everything straight away."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when you were not allowed to use your cell phone","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-176","_displayNo":"#186","outline":{"topic":"phone ban during high school graduation exam","intro":"mobile phones must-have item can't live without remember time apart from phone graduation exam two years ago","body":["exam compulsory Vietnam sit end grade 12 diploma score decides get into university","phones not allowed near exam room prevent unfair advantage cheating","phone noise caught using any point incl breaks disqualified on spot results cancelled","habit checking phone practice tests home during exam kept urge reach for it couldn't"],"conclusion":"experience made realise rely too much on phone taught trust own judgement more"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-176.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I was not allowed to use my phone was during my high school graduation exam two years ago. It was an important test because it affected both finishing school and my future university plans.\n\nPhones were not permitted in the exam room because they could be used to gain an unfair advantage. I understood the reason, but being without mine still felt uncomfortable. When practising at home, I had developed a habit of checking answers on my phone whenever I felt unsure.\n\nDuring the real exam, I sometimes wanted to reach for it automatically. Instead, I had to trust the answers I had worked out myself. I remained uncertain about some questions until I returned home and checked the answer key. Looking back, the experience taught me something useful about my habits. A phone can support learning, but relying on it for immediate reassurance can reduce confidence. I realised I needed to become more comfortable making decisions without checking everything at once. The rule was sensible, even though following it made me notice my dependence on the device.","html":"A time I was not allowed to use my phone was during my high school graduation exam two years ago. It was an important test because it affected both finishing school and my future university plans.\n\nPhones were not permitted in the exam room because they could be used to gain an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unfair advantage</strong>. I understood the reason, but being without mine still felt uncomfortable. When practising at home, I had developed a habit of checking answers on my phone whenever I felt unsure.\n\nDuring the real exam, I sometimes wanted to reach for it automatically. Instead, I had to trust the answers I had worked out myself. I remained uncertain about some questions until I returned home and checked the answer key. Looking back, the experience taught me something useful about my habits. A phone can support learning, but relying on it for immediate <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reassurance</strong> can reduce confidence. I realised I needed to become more comfortable making decisions without checking everything at once. The rule was sensible, even though following it made me notice my dependence on the device.","phrases":[{"en":"unfair advantage","meaning":"Help that makes a competition or test unequal."},{"en":"reassurance","meaning":"Something that makes a person feel less worried or uncertain."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-176.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when you were not satisfied with a service","prompt":"Describe a time when you were not satisfied with a service.\nYou should say:\n- what the service was\n- who provided it\n- what happened\n- and explain why you were unhappy with the service.","answer":"I'll tell you about a time I got poor service from staff in a restaurant. It happened a couple of months ago, when some family members who live abroad came to stay with us. We'd decided to visit a nearby town, and at some point everyone got a little hungry, so we thought we'd grab a bite to eat. The first restaurant we came across looked perfect.\n\nIt had plenty of empty tables - we hadn't booked in advance - and we were all happy with the menu. But as soon as we walked through the door, things <strong class=\"key-phrase\">started to go wrong</strong>, and we weren't at all impressed with the staff's attitude. After standing around for a few minutes waiting for someone to greet us, I finally managed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get the attention of</strong> a waitress, who told me we'd come in through the wrong door. So I went round to the main entrance, only to be told by the manager that there weren't any tables free for a party our size - there were eight of us.\n\nI could see several free tables with four seats each, so I suggested we simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">push a couple of them together</strong>. But the manager was adamant that this was impossible; our only option, apparently, was to split the family across two separate tables. By that point some of my family were getting fed up - the restaurant was half empty, yet we were being treated as though they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">couldn't be bothered with us</strong>.\n\nIn the end we decided we didn't want to sit apart or stay somewhere we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">felt unwelcome</strong>, so we left, feeling pretty disappointed by how dismissive the manager had been. Fortunately, we found a much friendlier place just around the corner, and the whole episode <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made us appreciate</strong> good service a lot more.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"started to go wrong","context":"But as soon as we walked through the door, things started to go wrong, and we weren't at all impressed with the staff's attitude."},{"en":"get the attention of","context":"After standing around for a few minutes waiting for someone to greet us, I finally managed to get the attention of a waitress, who told me we'd come in through the wrong door."},{"en":"push a couple of them together","context":"I could see several free tables with four seats each, so I suggested we simply push a couple of them together."},{"en":"couldn't be bothered with us","context":"By that point some of my family were getting fed up - the restaurant was half empty, yet we were being treated as though they couldn't be bothered with us."},{"en":"felt unwelcome","context":"In the end we decided we didn't want to sit apart or stay somewhere we felt unwelcome, so we left, feeling pretty disappointed by how dismissive the manager had been."},{"en":"made us appreciate","context":"Fortunately, we found a much friendlier place just around the corner, and the whole episode made us appreciate good service a lot more."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when you were not satisfied with a service","id":"speaking-part-2-177","_displayNo":"#187","outline":{"topic":"poor restaurant service on a family visit","intro":"time got poor service staff restaurant couple months ago family abroad came to stay","body":["visited nearby town everyone hungry first restaurant looked perfect plenty empty tables","walked through door things started go wrong waitress said wrong door manager no tables for eight","saw free tables four seats suggested push together manager adamant impossible split family","decided didn't want sit apart felt unwelcome left found friendlier place around corner"],"conclusion":"whole episode made us appreciate good service a lot more"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-177.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I was dissatisfied with a service was during a restaurant visit a couple of months ago. Some relatives from abroad were staying with us, and eight of us went to a nearby town. We became hungry and chose a restaurant with several empty tables.\n\nAfter waiting for someone to greet us, a waitress said we had used the wrong entrance. At the main door, the manager then told us there was no table for eight. I suggested joining two empty four-person tables, but he refused and said we would have to sit separately.\n\nWe felt unwelcome because the restaurant was not busy and nobody seemed interested in finding a solution. In the end, we decided to leave. Fortunately, another restaurant around the corner welcomed us warmly, and we enjoyed our meal there. I understand that restaurants have practical limits, but the way staff explain a problem matters. A polite explanation or an effort to help would have changed our experience. Instead, the manager's attitude made us feel that our family was an inconvenience.","html":"A time I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dissatisfied</strong> with a service was during a restaurant visit a couple of months ago. Some relatives from abroad were staying with us, and eight of us went to a nearby town. We became hungry and chose a restaurant with several empty tables.\n\nAfter waiting for someone to greet us, a waitress said we had used the wrong entrance. At the main door, the manager then told us there was no table for eight. I suggested joining two empty four-person tables, but he refused and said we would have to sit separately.\n\nWe felt unwelcome because the restaurant was not busy and nobody seemed interested in finding a solution. In the end, we decided to leave. Fortunately, another restaurant around the corner welcomed us warmly, and we enjoyed our meal there. I understand that restaurants have practical limits, but the way staff explain a problem matters. A polite explanation or an effort to help would have changed our experience. Instead, the manager's attitude made us feel that our family was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an inconvenience</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"dissatisfied","meaning":"Unhappy because something is not as good as expected."},{"en":"an inconvenience","meaning":"Something or someone causing extra trouble or difficulty."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-177.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when you were really busy","prompt":"Describe a time when you were really busy.\nYou should say:\n- when this was\n- why you were so busy\n- how you managed the situation\n- and explain how you felt at the time.","answer":"I want to tell you about the time I left a university assignment until the very last minute and had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work flat out</strong> to get it finished. This was about ten years ago, during my Master's degree. We had several 3,000-word assignments to write for different modules, but there was one in particular that I really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">struggled with</strong>.\n\nI'd been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">procrastinating and putting it off</strong> for weeks, if I'm honest, and in the end I left myself just two days to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get the whole thing done</strong> - finding and reading relevant books and articles, deciding on my thesis and title, and producing a final draft to hand in before the deadline. Looking back, I don't know what I was thinking, because for most of those two days I was in a genuine <strong class=\"key-phrase\">state of panic</strong>, convinced I wouldn't make it in time.\n\nOddly enough, though, that pressure ended up forcing me to work in a much more efficient way than I normally would. My first step was narrowing down the general area I wanted to write about, then I limited myself to just ten sources instead of trying to read everything. I printed the most relevant pages, highlighted the key paragraphs, and jotted down notes to summarise the ideas I found useful. After that I just started writing the main body straightaway, leaving the title and introduction until last.\n\nI did hand it in on time, and I actually got a decent mark, but I remember feeling completely drained afterwards - more relieved than proud, really. It taught me that I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work best under pressure</strong>, but also that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cutting it that fine</strong> isn't something I want to repeat too often.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"work flat out","context":"I want to tell you about the time I left a university assignment until the very last minute and had to work flat out to get it finished."},{"en":"struggled with","context":"We had several 3,000-word assignments to write for different modules, but there was one in particular that I really struggled with."},{"en":"procrastinating and putting it off","context":"I'd been procrastinating and putting it off for weeks, if I'm honest, and in the end I left myself just two days to get the whole thing done - finding and reading relevant books and articles, deciding on my thesis and title, and producing a final draft to hand in before the deadline."},{"en":"get the whole thing done","context":"I'd been procrastinating and putting it off for weeks, if I'm honest, and in the end I left myself just two days to get the whole thing done - finding and reading relevant books and articles, deciding on my thesis and title, and producing a final draft to hand in before the deadline."},{"en":"state of panic","context":"Looking back, I don't know what I was thinking, because for most of those two days I was in a genuine state of panic, convinced I wouldn't make it in time."},{"en":"work best under pressure","context":"It taught me that I work best under pressure, but also that cutting it that fine isn't something I want to repeat too often."},{"en":"cutting it that fine","context":"It taught me that I work best under pressure, but also that cutting it that fine isn't something I want to repeat too often."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when you were really busy","id":"speaking-part-2-178","_displayNo":"#188","outline":{"topic":"last-minute Master's assignment","intro":"time left uni assignment last minute work flat out ten years ago Master's degree","body":["several 3000-word assignments modules one particular struggled with procrastinating weeks left two days","state of panic convinced wouldn't make it pressure forced work efficient way","narrowed general area limited ten sources printed highlighted pages jotted notes summarise","started writing main body straightaway left title introduction until last"],"conclusion":"handed in on time drained more relieved than proud taught work best under pressure"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-178.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I was extremely busy was during my Master's degree about ten years ago. I had several assignments, including one three-thousand-word essay that I found particularly difficult. Unfortunately, I kept delaying it until only two days remained.\n\nI then had to do everything quickly: read sources, choose my argument and write a final version. I felt worried that I would miss the deadline. To manage, I limited myself to ten useful sources instead of trying to read too much. I highlighted relevant ideas, made short notes and began writing the main sections before the introduction.\n\nThe pressure forced me to work in a more organised way, and I submitted the assignment on time. I even received a reasonable mark. However, I was exhausted afterwards and felt more relieved than proud. The experience showed me that I could focus under pressure, but it also revealed the cost of leaving work too late. I would rather use the efficient methods I discovered while starting earlier, instead of depending on another stressful two-day rush.","html":"A time I was extremely busy was during my Master's degree about ten years ago. I had several assignments, including one three-thousand-word essay that I found particularly difficult. Unfortunately, I kept <strong class=\"key-phrase\">delaying it</strong> until only two days remained.\n\nI then had to do everything quickly: read sources, choose my argument and write a final version. I felt worried that I would miss the deadline. To manage, I limited myself to ten useful sources instead of trying to read too much. I highlighted <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relevant ideas</strong>, made short notes and began writing the main sections before the introduction.\n\nThe pressure forced me to work in a more organised way, and I submitted the assignment on time. I even received a reasonable mark. However, I was exhausted afterwards and felt more relieved than proud. The experience showed me that I could focus under pressure, but it also revealed the cost of leaving work too late. I would rather use the efficient methods I discovered while starting earlier, instead of depending on another stressful two-day rush.","phrases":[{"en":"delaying it","meaning":"Putting off doing the task until later."},{"en":"relevant ideas","meaning":"Information connected with the subject being discussed."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-178.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when you worked in a team","prompt":"Describe a time when you worked in a team\nYou should say:\nwhat the activity was\nwho you worked with you\nhow easy or difficult it was\nAnd explain how you felt about being part of this team.","answer":"I'd like to talk about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a team project</strong> I worked on during my 'British and American Culture' class at the Foreign Trade University in Hanoi. There were six of us on the team, and our task was to identify the differences in the travelling habits of British and American tourists. We had to produce a report and give a presentation on our findings.\n\nThe first thing we did was split into two groups of three. We had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">conduct a survey</strong>, so three of us spent time designing the questionnaire while the other three drafted an outline for the report. One of the trickiest parts was finding enough respondents, since we needed fifty British and fifty American participants. Thankfully, Facebook came to the rescue - we managed to reach enough people from both countries who were willing to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fill in the online survey</strong> for us.\n\nIn the end, I think it turned out to be a genuinely successful project, because we were able to work out the main travel patterns of people from the US and the UK, and our presentation went down really well with the class.\n\nLooking back, it was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a memorable experience</strong> for all of us, and I felt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a real sense of accomplishment</strong> once we'd handed in the report and delivered the presentation. What I was proudest of, though, was how well we worked together as a team. There were a few minor disagreements along the way, as there always are, but overall everyone showed a good level of responsibility, and I think that's what made the whole thing work.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"a team project","context":"I'd like to talk about a team project I worked on during my 'British and American Culture' class at the Foreign Trade University in Hanoi."},{"en":"conduct a survey","context":"We had to conduct a survey, so three of us spent time designing the questionnaire while the other three drafted an outline for the report."},{"en":"fill in the online survey","context":"Thankfully, Facebook came to the rescue - we managed to reach enough people from both countries who were willing to fill in the online survey for us."},{"en":"a memorable experience","context":"Looking back, it was a memorable experience for all of us, and I felt a real sense of accomplishment once we'd handed in the report and delivered the presentation."},{"en":"a real sense of accomplishment","context":"Looking back, it was a memorable experience for all of us, and I felt a real sense of accomplishment once we'd handed in the report and delivered the presentation."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when you worked in a team","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-179","_displayNo":"#189","outline":{"topic":"British and American culture travel-habits project","intro":"team project worked during British American Culture class Foreign Trade University Hanoi six on team","body":["task identify differences travelling habits British American tourists produce report presentation","split two groups three conduct survey design questionnaire draft outline report","trickiest part finding enough respondents fifty British fifty American Facebook came to rescue","successful project worked out main travel patterns US UK presentation went down well"],"conclusion":"proudest of how well worked together as team despite few minor disagreements"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-179.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I worked in a team was for a British and American Culture class at the Foreign Trade University in Hanoi. There were six of us. We had to compare the travel habits of British and American tourists, then produce a report and presentation.\n\nWe divided into two groups of three. One group designed a questionnaire, and the other prepared the report outline. Finding enough participants was difficult because we needed fifty people from each country. Eventually, we used Facebook to reach people who were willing to complete the survey online.\n\nOnce we had the responses, we compared them and identified the main patterns. Our presentation went well, and we felt pleased when the work was finished. There were a few disagreements, but everyone took responsibility for their part. I was proud not just of the final result, but of how we solved problems together. The experience showed me that dividing tasks is only the beginning of teamwork. People also need to communicate and help each other when one part of the project becomes difficult.","html":"A time I worked in a team was for a British and American Culture class at the Foreign Trade University in Hanoi. There were six of us. We had to compare the travel habits of British and American tourists, then produce a report and presentation.\n\nWe divided into two groups of three. One group designed a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">questionnaire</strong>, and the other prepared the report outline. Finding enough <strong class=\"key-phrase\">participants</strong> was difficult because we needed fifty people from each country. Eventually, we used Facebook to reach people who were willing to complete the survey online.\n\nOnce we had the responses, we compared them and identified the main patterns. Our presentation went well, and we felt pleased when the work was finished. There were a few disagreements, but everyone took responsibility for their part. I was proud not just of the final result, but of how we solved problems together. The experience showed me that dividing tasks is only the beginning of teamwork. People also need to communicate and help each other when one part of the project becomes difficult.","phrases":[{"en":"questionnaire","meaning":"A set of questions used to collect information from people."},{"en":"participants","meaning":"People who take part in an activity or study."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-179.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time you felt happy that you used your cellphone","prompt":"Describe a time you felt happy that you used your cellphone.\nYou should say:\n- What you do;\n- When, where you do it;\n- Who you do it with;\n- Why you felt happy using your cell phone.","answer":"I'd like to talk about the day my mother gave me my first smartphone, an iPhone, as a reward for getting into university. I'd just found out I'd passed the entrance exam, which felt like a huge milestone at the time, and she surprised me with it that same evening.\n\nI remember being completely fascinated by it at first - I spent almost the whole night just exploring the settings and apps, working out what everything did. Before that I'd had a really basic phone that could barely do more than make calls and send texts, so having something that could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep me connected</strong> to everyone, my friends, my family, even my teachers, felt like a huge upgrade.\n\nThe thing I appreciated most, though, wasn't really the phone calls. It was being able to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay on top of</strong> my university group chats, which back then were mostly on Facebook Messenger, and never missing an update about lecture changes or assignment deadlines. My mother used to joke that I'd <strong class=\"key-phrase\">become glued to</strong> the screen, and honestly, she wasn't wrong - I even started using it to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">revise for exams</strong> on the bus, which I'd never have bothered doing with textbooks.\n\nWhat made me happiest, though, was something smaller: I used it to video call my grandmother, who lives a few hours away, almost every weekend after that. Before the phone, we mostly only spoke when I visited in person, so being able to see her face on a screen and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">check in with</strong> her so easily made a real difference. It's funny looking back - a gift that started out as a reward for an exam ended up being far more valuable for the little everyday connections it gave me.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"keep me connected","context":"Before that I'd had a really basic phone that could barely do more than make calls and send texts, so having something that could keep me connected to everyone, my friends, my family, even my teachers, felt like a huge upgrade."},{"en":"stay on top of","context":"It was being able to stay on top of my university group chats, which back then were mostly on Facebook Messenger, and never missing an update about lecture changes or assignment deadlines."},{"en":"become glued to","context":"My mother used to joke that I'd become glued to the screen, and honestly, she wasn't wrong - I even started using it to revise for exams on the bus, which I'd never have bothered doing with textbooks."},{"en":"revise for exams","context":"My mother used to joke that I'd become glued to the screen, and honestly, she wasn't wrong - I even started using it to revise for exams on the bus, which I'd never have bothered doing with textbooks."},{"en":"check in with","context":"Before the phone, we mostly only spoke when I visited in person, so being able to see her face on a screen and check in with her so easily made a real difference."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time you felt happy that you used your cellphone","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-180","_displayNo":"#190","outline":{"topic":"smartphone gift from mum after the entrance exam","intro":"day mother gave first smartphone iPhone reward getting into university passed entrance exam","body":["fascinated at first spent whole night exploring settings apps previously had basic phone","appreciated staying on top of uni group chats Facebook Messenger deadlines lecture changes","mother joked become glued to screen started using it to revise for exams on bus","video call grandmother lives few hours away almost every weekend check in with her"],"conclusion":"gift started as exam reward ended up valuable for everyday connections it gave"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-180.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time using my phone made me happy began when my mother gave me my first iPhone. It was a reward for passing the university entrance exam. I had used only a basic phone before, so exploring the new settings and apps felt exciting.\n\nAt university, it became useful for group messages about lecture changes and assignment deadlines. I could also review study material on the bus instead of carrying several textbooks. These functions made daily life more convenient, although my mother sometimes joked that I spent too long looking at the screen.\n\nThe happiest use, however, was video calling my grandmother. She lived several hours away, and before that we mainly spoke when I visited. With the phone, I could see her face and talk almost every weekend. Those small conversations helped us feel closer despite the distance. Looking back, the present became more valuable for the relationships it supported than for its technical features. It started as a reward for one exam, but it gave me many ordinary moments of connection afterwards.","html":"A time using my phone made me happy began when my mother gave me my first iPhone. It was a reward for passing the university entrance exam. I had used only a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">basic phone</strong> before, so exploring the new settings and apps felt exciting.\n\nAt university, it became useful for group messages about lecture changes and assignment deadlines. I could also review study material on the bus instead of carrying several textbooks. These functions made daily life more convenient, although my mother sometimes joked that I spent too long looking at the screen.\n\nThe happiest use, however, was video calling my grandmother. She lived several hours away, and before that we mainly spoke when I visited. With the phone, I could see her face and talk almost every weekend. Those small conversations helped us feel closer <strong class=\"key-phrase\">despite the distance</strong>. Looking back, the present became more valuable for the relationships it supported than for its technical features. It started as a reward for one exam, but it gave me many ordinary moments of connection afterwards.","phrases":[{"en":"basic phone","meaning":"A phone mainly used for calls and texts, with few extra functions."},{"en":"despite the distance","meaning":"Even though people are far apart."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-180.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An achievement that you are proud of","prompt":"Describe an achievement that you are proud of.\nYou should say:\n- What you do;\n- When, where, and how you do it;\n- How difficult it is;\n- Why you are proud of it.","answer":"I'd like to talk about one of my personal achievements that I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take pride in</strong> - passing my university entrance exam. It's known to be nerve-wracking and pretty challenging, especially if you're hoping to get into one of the more prestigious schools.\n\nTo be honest, back in high school I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not much of</strong> a diligent student. I didn't really appreciate hard work or self-improvement - all I wanted was to graduate and be done with it. But in my final year, I had a Literature teacher who completely changed that. The way she taught each lesson was genuinely thought-provoking, and she sort of woke up this desire in me to actually improve myself. Around the same time, I'd also started thinking seriously about becoming a teacher myself one day.\n\nI knew it wouldn't be easy for someone with my academic record to get into the University of Education, but once I'd set my heart on something, I tend to go to great lengths to get it. The exam covered three core subjects - Maths, Literature and English. Languages came naturally to me, but Maths definitely didn't, so I ended up studying around the clock, doing extra algebra exercises and constantly going to my teachers with questions. There were plenty of evenings when I was exhausted and ready to give up, but their encouragement kept me going.\n\nIn the end, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the hard work paid off</strong>. I was over the moon when I found out I'd been accepted. I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">proud of myself</strong>, not just for the result, but for the determination it took to step out of my comfort zone and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make my dream come true</strong>.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"take pride in","context":"I'd like to talk about one of my personal achievements that I take pride in - passing my university entrance exam. It's known to be nerve-wracking and pretty challenging, especially if you're hoping to get into one of the more prestigious schools."},{"en":"not much of","context":"To be honest, back in high school I was not much of a diligent student."},{"en":"the hard work paid off","context":"In the end, the hard work paid off. I was over the moon when I found out I'd been accepted. I was proud of myself, not just for the result, but for the determination it took to step out of my comfort zone and make my dream come true."},{"en":"proud of myself","context":"In the end, the hard work paid off. I was over the moon when I found out I'd been accepted. I was proud of myself, not just for the result, but for the determination it took to step out of my comfort zone and make my dream come true."},{"en":"make my dream come true","context":"In the end, the hard work paid off. I was over the moon when I found out I'd been accepted. I was proud of myself, not just for the result, but for the determination it took to step out of my comfort zone and make my dream come true."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an achievement that you are proud of","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-181","_displayNo":"#191","outline":{"topic":"pass uni entrance exam","intro":"one personal achievements take pride passing uni entrance examination","body":["exam known nerve-wracking challenging especially wish attend prestigious schools town","know not easy student w/ bad academic results like get uni","started study around clock took active role learning doing extra algebra","times exhausted disheartened encouragements teachers helpful keeping motivated"],"conclusion":"proud myself determination effort took step out comfort zone make"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-181.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An achievement I am proud of is passing my university entrance exam. Earlier in high school, I was not a particularly hardworking student. I mainly wanted to finish school, but my final-year Literature teacher changed my attitude.\n\nHer lessons made me interested in learning and encouraged me to consider becoming a teacher. I decided to apply to the University of Education, although my earlier results meant I would need to improve. The entrance exam covered Maths, Literature and English. Languages were easier for me, but Maths required a lot of practice.\n\nI completed extra algebra exercises and often asked teachers to explain problems. Some evenings, I felt tired and wanted to stop, but their encouragement helped me continue. When I learned that I had been accepted, I felt extremely happy. The result mattered, but I was also proud of changing my habits and working towards a difficult goal. It showed me that my earlier performance did not have to decide my future. With effort and help, I could move towards something I genuinely wanted.","html":"An achievement I am proud of is passing my university entrance exam. Earlier in high school, I was not a particularly hardworking student. I mainly wanted to finish school, but my final-year Literature teacher changed my attitude.\n\nHer lessons made me interested in learning and encouraged me to consider becoming a teacher. I decided to apply to the University of Education, although my earlier results meant I would need to improve. The entrance exam covered Maths, Literature and English. Languages were easier for me, but Maths required a lot of practice.\n\nI completed extra <strong class=\"key-phrase\">algebra</strong> exercises and often asked teachers to explain problems. Some evenings, I felt tired and wanted to stop, but their encouragement helped me continue. When I learned that I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had been accepted</strong>, I felt extremely happy. The result mattered, but I was also proud of changing my habits and working towards a difficult goal. It showed me that my earlier performance did not have to decide my future. With effort and help, I could move towards something I genuinely wanted.","phrases":[{"en":"algebra","meaning":"A type of maths that uses letters and symbols to represent numbers."},{"en":"had been accepted","meaning":"Had been given permission to join the university or course."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-181.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An enjoyable experience you had in the countryside","prompt":"Describe an enjoyable experience you had in the countryside.\nYou should say:\nWhen and where you went\nWho you went with\nWhat you did there\nAnd explain why you think it was enjoyable.","answer":"I'd like to talk about last summer, when I had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an unforgettable opportunity</strong> to visit my hometown - a small village <strong class=\"key-phrase\">located in the north of Vietnam</strong>.\n\nMy whole family, my parents, my younger sister and I, decided to spend the day driving out there together. For my parents, it was a chance to see relatives they hadn't seen in ages, and for my sister and me, it was more about getting a closer look at rural life and just spending time together as a family.\n\nWe hit the road really early, around 5 a.m., and after about four hours of driving we arrived at this peaceful, tranquil little village. I remember being genuinely surprised by how different the scenery was from where I live - we were surrounded by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rolling green paddy fields</strong>, winding dirt paths, and small houses standing right next to each other.\n\nOnce we arrived, we went to see our relatives, who were incredibly warm and welcoming and greeted us like they hadn't seen us in years, which, to be fair, they hadn't. We spent the whole morning just chatting and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reminiscing about old times</strong>. At noon we had this amazing home-cooked meal, all made with local, organically grown ingredients - honestly some of the best food I've had in ages. In the afternoon, one of our relatives took us out to see the paddy fields and the village's little lake, and my sister and I even got to try fishing and feeding the chickens, which neither of us had ever done before.\n\nWe were all in such <strong class=\"key-phrase\">high spirits</strong> the whole day. It really was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a once-in-a-lifetime experience</strong>, and it's the kind of memory that's going to stay with me for a long time.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"an unforgettable opportunity","context":"I'd like to talk about last summer, when I had an unforgettable opportunity to visit my hometown - a small village located in the north of Vietnam."},{"en":"located in the north of Vietnam","context":"I'd like to talk about last summer, when I had an unforgettable opportunity to visit my hometown - a small village located in the north of Vietnam."},{"en":"rolling green paddy fields","context":"I remember being genuinely surprised by how different the scenery was from where I live - we were surrounded by rolling green paddy fields, winding dirt paths, and small houses standing right next to each other."},{"en":"reminiscing about old times","context":"We spent the whole morning just chatting and reminiscing about old times."},{"en":"high spirits","context":"We were all in such high spirits the whole day. It really was a once-in-a-lifetime experience, and it's the kind of memory that's going to stay with me for a long time."},{"en":"a once-in-a-lifetime experience","context":"We were all in such high spirits the whole day. It really was a once-in-a-lifetime experience, and it's the kind of memory that's going to stay with me for a long time."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an enjoyable experience you had in the countryside","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-182","_displayNo":"#192","outline":{"topic":"family day trip to hometown village","intro":"last summer unforgettable opportunity visit hometown small village located north Vietnam","body":["whole family parents sister decided spend day driving out together chance see relatives","hit road early 5am four hours driving arrived peaceful tranquil village rolling green paddy fields","visited relatives warm welcoming spent morning chatting reminiscing old times noon home-cooked meal","afternoon relative took see paddy fields village lake sister tried fishing feeding chickens"],"conclusion":"high spirits whole day once-in-a-lifetime experience memory stay with me long time"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-182.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An enjoyable countryside experience was a family visit to my hometown village in northern Vietnam last summer. My parents, younger sister and I left at about five in the morning and arrived after a four-hour drive.\n\nThe village felt very different from the city. There were green rice fields, narrow dirt paths and small houses close together. My parents were excited to see relatives they had not met for a long time. We spent the morning talking, and at noon our relatives served a delicious meal made with local ingredients.\n\nIn the afternoon, they showed us the fields and a small lake. My sister and I tried fishing and helped feed chickens, which were new activities for us. I enjoyed being able to take part rather than only looking around. The whole family seemed cheerful and relaxed throughout the day. The trip helped me understand more about rural life and gave my parents time with people they missed. It remains special because we shared simple experiences together without the usual noise and pressure of the city.","html":"An enjoyable countryside experience was a family visit to my hometown village in northern Vietnam last summer. My parents, younger sister and I left at about five in the morning and arrived after a four-hour drive.\n\nThe village felt very different from the city. There were green rice fields, narrow dirt paths and small houses close together. My parents were excited to see relatives they had not met for a long time. We spent the morning talking, and at noon our relatives served a delicious meal made with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">local ingredients</strong>.\n\nIn the afternoon, they showed us the fields and a small lake. My sister and I tried fishing and helped feed chickens, which were new activities for us. I enjoyed being able to take part rather than only looking around. The whole family seemed cheerful and relaxed throughout the day. The trip helped me understand more about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rural life</strong> and gave my parents time with people they missed. It remains special because we shared simple experiences together without the usual noise and pressure of the city.","phrases":[{"en":"local ingredients","meaning":"Food items grown or produced in the nearby area."},{"en":"rural life","meaning":"Everyday life in the countryside."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-182.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An experience that you had when you left home","prompt":"Describe an experience that you had when you left home\nYou should say:\n- What is it\n- When did it happen\n- And how do you feel about that experience","answer":"I'm going to talk about the time I got lost in a forest about eighty kilometres from home. This was around a year ago, on a two-day company trip to a resort near Hanoi.\n\nThe first evening was great fun - eating, drinking and singing with my colleagues until late, and most of us had had a few too many drinks, which is really the only reason what happened next made any sense. Our campsite was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">right next to</strong> a huge forest, and in that state I announced what seemed like a brilliant idea: 'Let's go and explore it.' Ridiculous as it sounds, everyone who could still walk straight was surprisingly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">up for it</strong>.\n\nSo about ten of us set off, using our phone torches to see. At first, while we could still see the campfire behind us, it felt exciting, even though I was leading the way with no idea where I was heading. After twenty minutes of wandering, we found ourselves <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the middle of nowhere</strong>, and worse, none of us could remember the way back. Further on we found a huge banyan tree with a small temple beside it, lit in an eerie reddish glow, and right then a colleague chose that exact moment to tell a ghost story about a haunted temple back home - creepy enough that someone screamed.\n\nWe <strong class=\"key-phrase\">instantly regretted</strong> the whole idea, but there wasn't much to do except call the people back at camp. Luckily a few were still awake, and they found the caretakers, who knew exactly where that tree was and told us to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay put</strong>. So we huddled by the temple, feeling equal parts scared and stupid, until they turned up about fifteen minutes later and walked us back. We were exhausted and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more than a little embarrassed</strong>, but somehow stayed up half the night laughing about it. I don't think I'll ever forget that trip - one of the most ridiculous nights of my life, and, oddly, one of the best.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"right next to","context":"Our campsite was right next to a huge forest, and in that state I announced what seemed like a brilliant idea: 'Let's go and explore it.' Ridiculous as it sounds, everyone who could still walk straight was surprisingly up for it."},{"en":"up for it","context":"Our campsite was right next to a huge forest, and in that state I announced what seemed like a brilliant idea: 'Let's go and explore it.' Ridiculous as it sounds, everyone who could still walk straight was surprisingly up for it."},{"en":"in the middle of nowhere","context":"After twenty minutes of wandering, we found ourselves in the middle of nowhere, and worse, none of us could remember the way back."},{"en":"instantly regretted","context":"We instantly regretted the whole idea, but there wasn't much to do except call the people back at camp."},{"en":"stay put","context":"Luckily a few were still awake, and they found the caretakers, who knew exactly where that tree was and told us to stay put."},{"en":"more than a little embarrassed","context":"We were exhausted and more than a little embarrassed, but somehow stayed up half the night laughing about it."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an experience that you had when you left home","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-183","_displayNo":"#193","outline":{"topic":"getting lost in a forest on a company trip","intro":"time got lost forest eighty km from home year ago two-day company trip resort near Hanoi","body":["first evening fun eating drinking singing colleagues few too many drinks campsite right next to forest","came up with idea explore forest everyone up for it set off using phone torches","twenty minutes wandering found ourselves in the middle of nowhere none remembered way back","banyan tree temple eerie glow ghost story someone screamed instantly regretted whole idea"],"conclusion":"caretakers found us told us stay put walked back exhausted embarrassed laughing never forget trip"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-183.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An experience I had away from home was getting lost in a forest on a company trip about a year ago. We were staying near Hanoi, around eighty kilometres from home, for two days.\n\nAfter an evening of food, singing and too many drinks, I suggested exploring the forest beside our campsite. About ten colleagues agreed. We used phone lights and walked until the campfire was no longer visible. After roughly twenty minutes, nobody knew the way back. Finding a large banyan tree and a small temple made the situation feel even stranger, especially when someone began telling a ghost story.\n\nWe became frightened and called colleagues who had stayed at camp. They contacted the caretakers, who knew the location and told us to wait. About fifteen minutes later, they arrived and guided us back. We felt relieved and embarrassed. Later, we laughed about the evening, but I realised how foolish the decision had been. The experience taught me not to confuse an exciting idea with a sensible one, particularly when exploring an unfamiliar place at night.","html":"An experience I had away from home was getting lost in a forest on a company trip about a year ago. We were staying near Hanoi, around eighty kilometres from home, for two days.\n\nAfter an evening of food, singing and too many drinks, I suggested exploring the forest beside our <strong class=\"key-phrase\">campsite</strong>. About ten colleagues agreed. We used phone lights and walked until the campfire was no longer visible. After roughly twenty minutes, nobody knew the way back. Finding a large banyan tree and a small temple made the situation feel even stranger, especially when someone began telling a ghost story.\n\nWe became frightened and called colleagues who had stayed at camp. They contacted the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caretakers</strong>, who knew the location and told us to wait. About fifteen minutes later, they arrived and guided us back. We felt relieved and embarrassed. Later, we laughed about the evening, but I realised how foolish the decision had been. The experience taught me not to confuse an exciting idea with a sensible one, particularly when exploring an unfamiliar place at night.","phrases":[{"en":"campsite","meaning":"A place where people stay in tents or camp outdoors."},{"en":"caretakers","meaning":"People responsible for looking after a place or property."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-183.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An experience that you taught a friend or a relative","prompt":"Describe an experience that you taught a friend or a relative.\nYou should say:\n- When it happened and what it was;\n- Who was taught by you;\n- Why you taught someone something;\n- How difficult it was for you.","answer":"I'd like to talk about the time I taught my mother how to use Facebook to send messages and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep in touch with</strong> people. My mum's in her late forties, and she never went to university - she started working straight after high school, which was pretty normal for her generation. Ever since she got married, her whole world has basically revolved around the family; she's always made sure my dad and I are well looked after, the house is tidy, all that.\n\nBecause of that, she never really had time to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep up with</strong> new technology, and she'd get a bit flustered whenever she had to deal with smartphones, laptops or smart TVs. Last year she asked me whether I knew how to post photos and updates on Facebook, since a lot of her friends had started using it, along with apps like Zalo, to stay in touch.\n\nOf course I said yes, and I sat down with her one afternoon to show her the basics - how to open the app, log in, and use the main features. She was so thorough about it that she actually wrote everything down step by step in a notebook, as if she were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taking notes for an exam</strong>. After a few practice runs, she could manage it completely on her own, and I remember the huge grin on her face when she posted her first photo.\n\nHonestly, I didn't <strong class=\"key-phrase\">find it difficult</strong> to teach her at all - she picked it up faster than I expected. What mattered more was that it let her <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catch up with</strong> her old friends and stay a little more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the loop</strong> with what's going on in the world, which I think made her genuinely happier.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"keep in touch with","context":"I'd like to talk about the time I taught my mother how to use Facebook to send messages and keep in touch with people."},{"en":"keep up with","context":"Because of that, she never really had time to keep up with new technology, and she'd get a bit flustered whenever she had to deal with smartphones, laptops or smart TVs."},{"en":"taking notes for an exam","context":"She was so thorough about it that she actually wrote everything down step by step in a notebook, as if she were taking notes for an exam."},{"en":"find it difficult","context":"Honestly, I didn't find it difficult to teach her at all - she picked it up faster than I expected."},{"en":"catch up with","context":"What mattered more was that it let her catch up with her old friends and stay a little more in the loop with what's going on in the world, which I think made her genuinely happier."},{"en":"in the loop","context":"What mattered more was that it let her catch up with her old friends and stay a little more in the loop with what's going on in the world, which I think made her genuinely happier."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an experience that you taught a friend or a relative","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-184","_displayNo":"#194","outline":{"topic":"teaching mum to use Facebook","intro":"time taught mother use Facebook send messages keep in touch with people mum late forties","body":["never went university started working after high school normal her generation family focus","never had time keep up with technology flustered smartphones laptops smart TVs","last year asked knew how post photos Facebook friends using Zalo stay in touch","sat down afternoon show basics open app log in wrote everything down notebook"],"conclusion":"didn't find difficult teach her picked up fast mattered catch up with friends in the loop"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-184.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An experience of teaching a relative was showing my mother how to use Facebook last year. She is in her late forties and had spent much of her life working and looking after our family. New technology often made her nervous.\n\nMany of her friends had begun using Facebook and Zalo, so she wanted to learn how to send messages, share photographs and see their updates. We sat together one afternoon, and I showed her how to open the app, log in and use the main functions. She carefully wrote the steps in a notebook.\n\nAfter practising a few times, she could manage without my help. I clearly remember her smile when she posted her first photo. Teaching her was easier than I had expected because she paid close attention and was willing to try. The most important result was that she could reconnect with old friends and feel more involved in their lives. I was pleased to give something back to someone who had spent so much time teaching and supporting me.","html":"An experience of teaching a relative was showing my mother how to use Facebook last year. She is in her late forties and had spent much of her life working and looking after our family. New technology often made her nervous.\n\nMany of her friends had begun using Facebook and Zalo, so she wanted to learn how to send messages, share photographs and see their updates. We sat together one afternoon, and I showed her how to open the app, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">log in</strong> and use the main functions. She carefully wrote the steps in a notebook.\n\nAfter practising a few times, she could manage without my help. I clearly remember her smile when she posted her first photo. Teaching her was easier than I had expected because she paid close attention and was willing to try. The most important result was that she could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reconnect with old friends</strong> and feel more involved in their lives. I was pleased to give something back to someone who had spent so much time teaching and supporting me.","phrases":[{"en":"log in","meaning":"Enter an account using the required name, password or other details."},{"en":"reconnect with old friends","meaning":"Start communicating again with friends from the past."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-184.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An experience when you spent time with a child","prompt":"Describe an experience when you spent time with a child.\nYou should say:\nWhen you spent time with this child\nWho this child was\nWhat you did together\nAnd how you felt about the experience.","answer":"I'd like to tell you about the time I looked after a young child, my two-year-old nephew. I was about fifteen at the time, and it happened one weekend when I was visiting my brother, his wife and their son. My brother's wife was at work that day, and he needed to nip out to the shops for groceries. Instead of all of us going together, he decided it'd be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">much more convenient</strong> if he went alone and I stayed home to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look after my nephew</strong>.\n\nAt first I was honestly a bit shocked he'd even suggest it - I'd never looked after a small child alone before, and I didn't have a clue what to do if something went wrong. But he reassured me he'd only be gone about half an hour, and that I should call him for any emergency. So, reluctantly, I agreed.\n\nOnce he'd left, I put on one of my nephew's favourite cartoons to try to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep him occupied</strong>, but that only bought me about fifteen minutes before he got bored and wanted something else. So I brought all his toys out and tipped them onto the lounge floor, and we made a proper mess playing with those for a while, though that only lasted about ten minutes too. After that I found some of his favourite snacks in the kitchen, and we sat back down to watch more cartoons while he ate. About five minutes later, to my relief, he actually fell asleep, and stayed asleep until my brother got back.\n\nAll in all, it turned out to be a pretty <strong class=\"key-phrase\">painless experience</strong>. I was relieved nothing had gone wrong, and it taught me that looking after small kids isn't nearly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as daunting as it seems</strong>.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"much more convenient","context":"Instead of all of us going together, he decided it'd be much more convenient if he went alone and I stayed home to look after my nephew."},{"en":"look after my nephew","context":"Instead of all of us going together, he decided it'd be much more convenient if he went alone and I stayed home to look after my nephew."},{"en":"keep him occupied","context":"Once he'd left, I put on one of my nephew's favourite cartoons to try to keep him occupied, but that only bought me about fifteen minutes before he got bored and wanted something else."},{"en":"painless experience","context":"All in all, it turned out to be a pretty painless experience. I was relieved nothing had gone wrong, and it taught me that looking after small kids isn't nearly as daunting as it seems."},{"en":"as daunting as it seems","context":"All in all, it turned out to be a pretty painless experience. I was relieved nothing had gone wrong, and it taught me that looking after small kids isn't nearly as daunting as it seems."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an experience when you spent time with a child","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-185","_displayNo":"#195","outline":{"topic":"babysit my 2 y/o nephew","intro":"like tell experience spent time w/ kid","body":["brother's wife work day go out shops pick-up groceries cook dinner","brother reassured gone 30 minutes everything fine needed contact emergency call","After around 15 minutes got bored wanted something else went got","after went kitchen found favourite snacks sat back down watch cartoons"],"conclusion":"relieved nothing bad happened realised not difficult look after small"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-185.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I spent with a child was when I looked after my two-year-old nephew. I was about fifteen and visiting my brother's family for the weekend. His wife was at work, and he needed to go shopping for groceries.\n\nHe asked me to stay with his son for about half an hour. I was nervous because I had never cared for a small child alone. However, he said I could phone if there was a problem. Once he left, I played my nephew's favourite cartoon, which kept him interested for about fifteen minutes.\n\nThen we played with toys on the living-room floor. When he became bored again, I found some snacks and we watched more cartoons. Soon afterwards, he fell asleep and stayed asleep until my brother returned. I felt relieved that everything had gone well. The experience was easier than I had feared, although I noticed how quickly a young child needs a new activity. It gave me more confidence and a better understanding of the attention involved in looking after children.","html":"A time I spent with a child was when I looked after my two-year-old nephew. I was about fifteen and visiting my brother's family for the weekend. His wife was at work, and he needed to go shopping for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">groceries</strong>.\n\nHe asked me to stay with his son for about half an hour. I was nervous because I had never cared for a small child alone. However, he said I could phone if there was a problem. Once he left, I played my nephew's favourite cartoon, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">kept him interested</strong> for about fifteen minutes.\n\nThen we played with toys on the living-room floor. When he became bored again, I found some snacks and we watched more cartoons. Soon afterwards, he fell asleep and stayed asleep until my brother returned. I felt relieved that everything had gone well. The experience was easier than I had feared, although I noticed how quickly a young child needs a new activity. It gave me more confidence and a better understanding of the attention involved in looking after children.","phrases":[{"en":"groceries","meaning":"Food and other everyday items bought for the home."},{"en":"kept him interested","meaning":"Continued to hold his attention."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-185.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An experience where you were late for an event","prompt":"Describe an experience where you were late for an event\nYou should say:\n- When did this happen.\n- Why were you late.\n- What consequence have you caused.\n- How you felt afterwards.","answer":"I like to think I'm <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an organised person</strong> and that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tardiness is not my problem</strong> - but I'd be lying if I said I'm never late.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">As a matter of fact</strong>, there was one time I was late for a job interview. It was just a couple of months ago, when I was looking for a part-time job to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gain experience in</strong> my field. There was a position at a company whose job description suited me perfectly, and I really wanted it. I filled out the application online the moment I heard about the vacancy, and to my surprise, they called me for an interview the very next day, at 9 a.m.\n\nI was thrilled, obviously, and I spent the evening before getting everything ready, picking out what to wear, ironing my clothes to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leave a good impression</strong>, even packing a notebook and pen in case I needed to take notes. I set my alarm for 7 a.m. so I wouldn't have to rush breakfast before heading over.\n\nThe problem was, I'd forgotten to charge my phone, and it died overnight, so the alarm never went off. I woke up in a panic at 9 a.m., exactly when the interview was supposed to start. I decided I'd rather just skip it than turn up looking like a disorganised latecomer. I did think about calling to ask if they'd reschedule, but I couldn't come up with a decent excuse, so I just let the whole thing go. In the end, I missed out on a job I'd genuinely wanted, and I felt pretty ashamed of myself for such a silly mistake. Still, I learned from it - I'm much more careful about that sort of thing now.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"an organised person","context":"I like to think I'm an organised person and that tardiness is not my problem - but I'd be lying if I said I'm never late."},{"en":"tardiness is not my problem","context":"I like to think I'm an organised person and that tardiness is not my problem - but I'd be lying if I said I'm never late."},{"en":"As a matter of fact","context":"As a matter of fact, there was one time I was late for a job interview."},{"en":"gain experience in","context":"It was just a couple of months ago, when I was looking for a part-time job to gain experience in my field."},{"en":"leave a good impression","context":"I was thrilled, obviously, and I spent the evening before getting everything ready, picking out what to wear, ironing my clothes to leave a good impression, even packing a notebook and pen in case I needed to take notes."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an experience where you were late for an event","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-186","_displayNo":"#196","outline":{"topic":"late for a job interview","intro":"organised person tardiness not problem","body":["couple months ago search new part-time job gain experience career","chose wear ironed set clothes leave good impression interviewer put notebook","everything done set alarm 7am wouldn't rush breakfast headed firm","wonder ask postpone interview couldn't think reasonable excuse let go"],"conclusion":"now careful comes similar situations"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-186.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An experience of being late was missing a job interview a couple of months ago. I had applied online for a part-time role that matched my interests, and the company invited me to an interview at nine the next morning.\n\nI was excited and prepared carefully that evening. I chose and ironed my clothes, packed a notebook and pen, and set an alarm for seven. I expected to have plenty of time for breakfast and travel. Unfortunately, I forgot to charge my phone, and its battery died overnight.\n\nThe alarm did not ring. When I woke, it was already nine, exactly when the interview should have begun. I panicked and decided not to go. I considered calling to ask for another appointment, but I felt too embarrassed and let the opportunity pass. Looking back, I should have contacted them instead of disappearing. I lost a job chance because of one small preparation mistake and my poor response to it. Since then, I have checked both my alarm and my phone battery before an important early appointment.","html":"An experience of being late was missing a job interview a couple of months ago. I had applied online for a part-time role that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">matched my interests</strong>, and the company invited me to an interview at nine the next morning.\n\nI was excited and prepared carefully that evening. I chose and ironed my clothes, packed a notebook and pen, and set an alarm for seven. I expected to have plenty of time for breakfast and travel. Unfortunately, I forgot to charge my phone, and its battery died overnight.\n\nThe alarm did not ring. When I woke, it was already nine, exactly when the interview should have begun. I panicked and decided not to go. I considered calling to ask for another appointment, but I felt too embarrassed and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">let the opportunity pass</strong>. Looking back, I should have contacted them instead of disappearing. I lost a job chance because of one small preparation mistake and my poor response to it. Since then, I have checked both my alarm and my phone battery before an important early appointment.","phrases":[{"en":"matched my interests","meaning":"Was suitable for the things I wanted to do or learn."},{"en":"let the opportunity pass","meaning":"Failed to use a chance that was available."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-186.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An important stage of your life","prompt":"Describe an important stage of your life\nYou should say:\nWhat part it is\nWhere you were then\nWhat memorable things you did\nAnd explain why this is the most enjoyable part / stage in your life.","answer":"I want to talk about the time I was preparing for my university entrance exam, which is still probably the most <strong class=\"key-phrase\">important stage of my life</strong> so far. It happened about five years ago, but the whole experience is still so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fresh in my memory</strong> that it could've been last year.\n\nIn Vietnam, that exam is basically treated as the most important test you'll ever sit, and mine was no exception. Back then, we had to cover three full years of high school content in one exam, which honestly felt like too much for anyone to handle - I remember <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pulling all-nighters</strong> almost every night in the final month. The competition was fierce too; you simply wouldn't get into a decent university unless your score was genuinely high.\n\nOn top of the academic pressure, I also had to figure out which major to choose, which stressed me out just as much as the studying itself. Some of my classmates seemed to know exactly what they wanted to do, but I had absolutely no idea, so I spent that whole period feeling completely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on edge</strong>.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">In hindsight</strong>, I wouldn't call it an enjoyable time exactly, but it's without doubt one of my most precious memories. It was the first experience that ever had that much of an impact on my life, and it forced me to actually think about who I wanted to become, rather than just drifting along. Looking back now, I can even laugh about those exhausting late nights with my old classmates - we still bring them up whenever we meet. It wouldn't be an exaggeration to say I properly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">grew up</strong> during that period, and that's really why it still matters so much to me.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"important stage of my life","context":"I want to talk about the time I was preparing for my university entrance exam, which is still probably the most important stage of my life so far."},{"en":"fresh in my memory","context":"It happened about five years ago, but the whole experience is still so fresh in my memory that it could've been last year."},{"en":"pulling all-nighters","context":"Back then, we had to cover three full years of high school content in one exam, which honestly felt like too much for anyone to handle - I remember pulling all-nighters almost every night in the final month."},{"en":"on edge","context":"Some of my classmates seemed to know exactly what they wanted to do, but I had absolutely no idea, so I spent that whole period feeling completely on edge."},{"en":"In hindsight","context":"In hindsight, I wouldn't call it an enjoyable time exactly, but it's without doubt one of my most precious memories."},{"en":"grew up","context":"It wouldn't be an exaggeration to say I properly grew up during that period, and that's really why it still matters so much to me."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an important stage of your life","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-187","_displayNo":"#197","outline":{"topic":"preparing for the university entrance exam","intro":"time preparing uni entrance exam still important stage of life five years ago fresh in memory","body":["Vietnam exam most important test cover three years content one exam pulling all-nighters","competition fierce wouldn't get decent uni unless score high","also had figure out which major stressed classmates knew idea felt on edge","hindsight not enjoyable but precious memory first big impact forced think who wanted become"],"conclusion":"can laugh about exhausting nights with old classmates now grew up during that period"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-187.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An important stage of my life was preparing for the university entrance exam about five years ago. The test covered a large amount of high school work, and I felt strong pressure to achieve a good result.\n\nDuring the final month, I spent many late evenings revising. I worried about remembering enough material and competing with other students. At the same time, I had to decide which subject to study at university. Some classmates already had clear plans, but I was still uncertain, which made the period more stressful.\n\nIt was not a particularly enjoyable time, but it was important for my development. I had to think seriously about my future rather than simply follow the next school timetable. I also learned that important goals require preparation and personal decisions. Now, when I meet old classmates, we can laugh about some of those difficult evenings. The pressure feels smaller from a distance. I remember the stage because it helped me become more independent and think about who I wanted to be, not only which exam I wanted to pass.","html":"An important stage of my life was preparing for the university entrance exam about five years ago. The test covered a large amount of high school work, and I felt strong pressure to achieve a good result.\n\nDuring the final month, I spent many late evenings <strong class=\"key-phrase\">revising</strong>. I worried about remembering enough material and competing with other students. At the same time, I had to decide which subject to study at university. Some classmates already had clear plans, but I was still uncertain, which made the period more stressful.\n\nIt was not a particularly enjoyable time, but it was important for my development. I had to think seriously about my future rather than simply follow the next school timetable. I also learned that important goals require preparation and personal decisions. Now, when I meet old classmates, we can laugh about some of those difficult evenings. The pressure feels smaller from a distance. I remember the stage because it helped me become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more independent</strong> and think about who I wanted to be, not only which exam I wanted to pass.","phrases":[{"en":"revising","meaning":"Studying something again in preparation for a test."},{"en":"more independent","meaning":"Better able to make decisions and manage life without constant help."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-187.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An occasion that someone or something made noise","prompt":"Describe an occasion that someone or something made noise\nYou should say:\n- When this happened\n- Who this person was/what this thing was\n- What kinds of noise was\nAnd explain what you did when you heard the noise.","answer":"I remember a period last year when the apartment above mine was being renovated. Work started at about seven every morning, and the loudest sound came from an electric drill on the concrete floor. Since I was preparing lessons from home at the time, the noise <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made it almost impossible</strong> to concentrate.\n\nAt first I tried to put up with it, telling myself renovation work doesn't last forever. But after several days the drilling was still going on well outside the building's permitted hours, so I decided to speak to the neighbour myself, calmly, rather than confronting the workers directly. I explained the noise was disrupting my work and my sleep, and asked if the loudest jobs could be kept to the middle of the day.\n\nHe was genuinely apologetic and arranged a clearer schedule with the contractor. From then on, I'd work from a library during the two-hour drilling window, and the workers stopped starting so early. What I took from it was that a complaint lands better when it comes with a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reasonable solution instead of anger</strong>, and that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">addressing a problem directly</strong> beats letting frustration build up until you snap. Looking back, what really made the conversation work wasn't even what I said, it was the timing - I waited until a Saturday morning, when he wasn't about to rush off anywhere, and framed it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as a practical scheduling question</strong> rather than a personal complaint.\n\nI suspect if I'd gone up there on the third morning, tired and irritated, it would've turned into an argument instead. We still say hello to each other now, which almost certainly wouldn't be the case if I'd handled it badly - and when you share a wall with someone, that matters more than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">winning the argument</strong>.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"made it almost impossible","context":"Since I was preparing lessons from home at the time, the noise made it almost impossible to concentrate."},{"en":"reasonable solution instead of anger","context":"What I took from it was that a complaint lands better when it comes with a reasonable solution instead of anger, and that addressing a problem directly beats letting frustration build up until you snap."},{"en":"addressing a problem directly","context":"What I took from it was that a complaint lands better when it comes with a reasonable solution instead of anger, and that addressing a problem directly beats letting frustration build up until you snap."},{"en":"as a practical scheduling question","context":"Looking back, what really made the conversation work wasn't even what I said, it was the timing - I waited until a Saturday morning, when he wasn't about to rush off anywhere, and framed it as a practical scheduling question rather than a personal complaint."},{"en":"winning the argument","context":"We still say hello to each other now, which almost certainly wouldn't be the case if I'd handled it badly - and when you share a wall with someone, that matters more than winning the argument."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an occasion that someone or something made noise","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-188","_displayNo":"#198","outline":{"topic":"upstairs renovation noise","intro":"work began seven morning loudest sound came electric drill used","body":["bc preparing lessons home noise made almost impossible concentrate","After several days however drilling continued outside building's permitted hours spoke","explained sound disrupting work sleep asked whether noisiest tasks limited middle","worked library during two-hour drilling period workers avoided early mornings","what made conversation work = timing waited Saturday morning not rushing out","brought up as practical scheduling question not personal complaint","if gone up 3rd morning tired irritated = argument nothing changed","still greet each other now matters more than winning argument"],"conclusion":"still greet each other now matters more than winning argument"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-188.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An occasion involving noise was when the flat above mine was renovated last year. Work began around seven each morning, and drilling into the floor was particularly loud. I was preparing lessons at home, so concentrating became difficult.\n\nAt first, I tried to tolerate it. However, after several days, the work continued outside the building's permitted hours. I chose a quiet Saturday morning to speak to the neighbour rather than complain angrily to the workers. I explained how the noise affected my work and sleep and asked whether the loudest jobs could happen in the middle of the day.\n\nHe apologised and agreed on a clearer timetable with the contractor. I then worked at a library during the two-hour drilling period, and the early starts stopped. The solution helped both of us, and we remained friendly. I learned that a complaint is easier to accept when it includes a reasonable suggestion. Choosing the right moment and speaking calmly worked better than letting frustration build until the conversation became an argument.","html":"An occasion involving noise was when the flat above mine was renovated last year. Work began around seven each morning, and drilling into the floor was particularly loud. I was preparing lessons at home, so concentrating became difficult.\n\nAt first, I tried to tolerate it. However, after several days, the work continued outside the building's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">permitted hours</strong>. I chose a quiet Saturday morning to speak to the neighbour rather than complain angrily to the workers. I explained how the noise affected my work and sleep and asked whether the loudest jobs could happen in the middle of the day.\n\nHe apologised and agreed on a clearer timetable with the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">contractor</strong>. I then worked at a library during the two-hour drilling period, and the early starts stopped. The solution helped both of us, and we remained friendly. I learned that a complaint is easier to accept when it includes a reasonable suggestion. Choosing the right moment and speaking calmly worked better than letting frustration build until the conversation became an argument.","phrases":[{"en":"permitted hours","meaning":"The times when an activity is officially allowed."},{"en":"contractor","meaning":"A person or company hired to carry out work, such as building repairs."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-188.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An occasion when you helped a person","prompt":"Describe an occasion when you helped a person.\nYou should say:\n- What the situation was\n- Who you helped\n- How you helped\n- How you felt after giving help.","answer":"There are plenty of times I've asked myself what it actually takes to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make a difference</strong> for someone else. But it wasn't until I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">got a heartfelt thank-you</strong> from a complete stranger, a little boy, that I realised how simple helping someone can actually be. Funnily enough, I helped him <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without even meaning to</strong>.\n\nI can't remember the exact date, but it was on a day off work, and I came across a young boy selling balloons and toys at a street fair, looking pretty dejected because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nobody was paying him any attention</strong>. When I walked over, his whole face lit up. I bought a couple of toys from him and asked a few questions, and from what he told me, it was clear he was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">going through a rough patch</strong>: his father had left the family <strong class=\"key-phrase\">out of the blue</strong>, and his mother hadn't been coping well since, so he'd more or less had to start earning money himself to support her and his younger sister.\n\nAfter I got home, I posted a photo of his toys on Facebook along with a short caption about his situation, mentioned roughly which street he sold on, and asked my friends to buy from him if they got the chance. I honestly hadn't expected much, but it's amazing what a bit of social media can do, a lot of people ended up going to find him and buying his stuff, or just offering to help however they could.\n\nA few months later I went back to see him. He and his mother were incredibly grateful and told me that one small gesture had genuinely changed things for them. It wasn't much on my part, but knowing it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meant the world to</strong> them made me happier than almost anything else I've done.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"make a difference","context":"There are plenty of times I've asked myself what it actually takes to make a difference for someone else."},{"en":"got a heartfelt thank-you","context":"But it wasn't until I got a heartfelt thank-you from a complete stranger, a little boy, that I realised how simple helping someone can actually be."},{"en":"without even meaning to","context":"Funnily enough, I helped him without even meaning to."},{"en":"nobody was paying him any attention","context":"I can't remember the exact date, but it was on a day off work, and I came across a young boy selling balloons and toys at a street fair, looking pretty dejected because nobody was paying him any attention."},{"en":"going through a rough patch","context":"I bought a couple of toys from him and asked a few questions, and from what he told me, it was clear he was going through a rough patch: his father had left the family out of the blue, and his mother hadn't been coping well since, so he'd more or less had to start earning money himself to support..."},{"en":"out of the blue","context":"I bought a couple of toys from him and asked a few questions, and from what he told me, it was clear he was going through a rough patch: his father had left the family out of the blue, and his mother hadn't been coping well since, so he'd more or less had to start earning money himself to support..."},{"en":"meant the world to","context":"It wasn't much on my part, but knowing it meant the world to them made me happier than almost anything else I've done."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an occasion when you helped a person","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-148","_displayNo":"#199","outline":{"topic":"helping a boy selling balloons at a street fair","intro":"asked myself what takes make a difference someone got heartfelt thank-you stranger little boy realised simple","body":["day off work saw boy selling balloons toys street fair nobody paying him any attention","walked over face lit up bought toys asked questions going through rough patch father left out of the blue","mother not coping well since had to earn money support her younger sister","posted photo Facebook caption mentioned street asked friends buy people came to help"],"conclusion":"went back months later grateful told changed things meant the world to them"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-148.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An occasion when I helped someone began at a street fair on a day off. I noticed a young boy selling balloons and toys. He looked unhappy because few people were stopping, so I bought a couple of items and spoke to him.\n\nHe explained that his father had left and his mother was having a difficult time. He was trying to earn money for his family, including his younger sister. His situation stayed in my mind after I returned home.\n\nI posted a photo of the toys on Facebook and told friends about his stall, asking them to buy something if they passed. I did not expect a large response, but several people visited or offered help. A few months later, I saw him again. He and his mother thanked me and said the extra support had made a difference. I felt happy because the action had taken little effort from me but had been useful to them. It reminded me that helping does not always require a complicated plan or a large amount of money.","html":"An occasion when I helped someone began at a street fair on a day off. I noticed a young boy selling balloons and toys. He looked unhappy because few people were stopping, so I bought a couple of items and spoke to him.\n\nHe explained that his father had left and his mother was having a difficult time. He was trying to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">earn money</strong> for his family, including his younger sister. His situation stayed in my mind after I returned home.\n\nI posted a photo of the toys on Facebook and told friends about his stall, asking them to buy something if they passed. I did not expect a large response, but several people visited or offered help. A few months later, I saw him again. He and his mother thanked me and said the extra support had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made a difference</strong>. I felt happy because the action had taken little effort from me but had been useful to them. It reminded me that helping does not always require a complicated plan or a large amount of money.","phrases":[{"en":"earn money","meaning":"Receive money by doing work or selling something."},{"en":"made a difference","meaning":"Changed a situation in a useful or noticeable way."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-148.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An occasion where somebody gave you positive suggestions","prompt":"Describe an occasion where somebody gave you positive suggestions.\nYou should say:\n- When you received\n- Who gave you the suggestions\n- What you did\n- Why the suggestions were positive/how you felt.","answer":"I'd like to talk about the time my best friend gave me some genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">positive suggestions</strong> about how to beat procrastination, advice that, looking back, probably saved my degree.\n\nThis was about three years ago, in my final year at university, right before my dissertation was due. I had a serious procrastination problem back then, my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Word document</strong> was still completely blank even though the deadline was only a month away. Weirdly, I wasn't even that worried about it; I was overconfident, telling myself I'd somehow pull it together at the last minute, the way I always had with smaller assignments.\n\nLuckily, my best friend, who was doing his dissertation on a similar topic, could see exactly what I was doing and refused to just let it slide. He sat me down one evening and suggested <strong class=\"key-phrase\">breaking the dissertation down into</strong> smaller chunks, with a proper deadline for each section, the literature review one week, the methodology the next, and so on. He even offered to check in with me every few days to make sure I was actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeping to it</strong>.\n\nI did exactly what he suggested, and it honestly transformed how I worked. Instead of one terrifying deadline looming in the distance, I had a series of manageable ones I could actually picture finishing. I ended up submitting everything on time and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">graduated with flying colours</strong>, which felt almost unbelievable given where I'd started. I still use that same approach at work now, breaking big projects into smaller stages with their own deadlines, and it's proved useful again and again. I'm genuinely grateful for that advice, without it, I dread to think how differently things might have turned out.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"positive suggestions","context":"I'd like to talk about the time my best friend gave me some genuinely positive suggestions about how to beat procrastination, advice that, looking back, probably saved my degree."},{"en":"Word document","context":"I had a serious procrastination problem back then, my Word document was still completely blank even though the deadline was only a month away."},{"en":"breaking the dissertation down into","context":"He sat me down one evening and suggested breaking the dissertation down into smaller chunks, with a proper deadline for each section, the literature review one week, the methodology the next, and so on."},{"en":"keeping to it","context":"He even offered to check in with me every few days to make sure I was actually keeping to it."},{"en":"graduated with flying colours","context":"I ended up submitting everything on time and graduated with flying colours, which felt almost unbelievable given where I'd started."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an occasion where somebody gave you positive suggestions","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-189","_displayNo":"#200","outline":{"topic":"friend's advice on beating procrastination","intro":"best friend gave positive suggestions beat procrastination three years ago final year before dissertation","body":["serious procrastination problem Word document blank month before deadline overconfident","best friend doing similar topic saw what doing refused to let it slide","suggested breaking dissertation down into smaller chunks deadline each section offered check in","did exactly suggested transformed how worked manageable deadlines instead one terrifying"],"conclusion":"submitted on time graduated flying colours still use approach at work now grateful"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-189.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An occasion when someone gave me useful suggestions was in my final university year about three years ago. My dissertation deadline was a month away, but I had hardly started. I kept telling myself I would manage at the last moment.\n\nMy best friend recognised the problem because he was working on a similar topic. He suggested dividing the dissertation into smaller sections and giving each one its own deadline. For example, I could complete the review of previous research first, then move to the methods section. He also offered to check my progress every few days.\n\nI followed the plan, and it changed how the task felt. Instead of one enormous job, I had smaller pieces that seemed possible to finish. I submitted on time and graduated successfully. I still use the same method at work when a large project feels difficult to begin. I am grateful because his advice was practical and supportive rather than simply critical. He helped me change a habit that could have caused a serious problem, and the benefit continued after university.","html":"An occasion when someone gave me useful suggestions was in my final university year about three years ago. My <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dissertation</strong> deadline was a month away, but I had hardly started. I kept telling myself I would manage at the last moment.\n\nMy best friend recognised the problem because he was working on a similar topic. He suggested dividing the dissertation into smaller sections and giving each one its own deadline. For example, I could complete the review of previous research first, then move to the methods section. He also offered to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">check my progress</strong> every few days.\n\nI followed the plan, and it changed how the task felt. Instead of one enormous job, I had smaller pieces that seemed possible to finish. I submitted on time and graduated successfully. I still use the same method at work when a large project feels difficult to begin. I am grateful because his advice was practical and supportive rather than simply critical. He helped me change a habit that could have caused a serious problem, and the benefit continued after university.","phrases":[{"en":"dissertation","meaning":"A long research project written for a university qualification."},{"en":"check my progress","meaning":"See how much of the work I had completed."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-189.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An occasion where you received a good service from a company or shop","prompt":"Describe an occasion where you received a good service from a company or shop\nYou should say:\n- What the service was.\n- When and where it happened.\n- Whom you were with.\n- How you felt about it.\n- Explain why you think it was good service.","answer":"I'm going to talk about how impressed I was with the service I got at a music shop a couple of months ago.\n\nI'd just watched a really inspiring concert with my flatmate, and by the end of it I'd decided, completely on a whim, that I wanted to learn guitar. So the following weekend, my flatmate came with me into town to help me find a shop, since he actually plays a bit himself and knew roughly what to look for. As we were walking along one of the main shopping streets, a window full of guitars and other instruments <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caught my eye</strong>, so we went in for a look.\n\nA young shop assistant came over almost straight away and greeted us with a genuine smile. After a bit of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">friendly small talk</strong>, I explained that I'd never played guitar in my life and had no idea what actually separated one acoustic guitar from another. He talked us through the different makes and models, whether they were factory-made or handmade, the type of wood used, how that affected the sound, and, of course, the price range. I'll admit I felt a little <strong class=\"key-phrase\">out of my depth</strong> as a total beginner, but I really appreciated how patient he was about it.\n\nHonestly, it's rare these days to come across such attentive customer service. This assistant clearly knew his stuff, and more importantly, he took the time to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">actually listen to what I needed</strong> rather than just pushing the most expensive option. My flatmate ended up being just as impressed, and we both agreed, walking out, that we'd <strong class=\"key-phrase\">definitely be back</strong> once I'd learned enough to know what I was doing.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"caught my eye","context":"As we were walking along one of the main shopping streets, a window full of guitars and other instruments caught my eye, so we went in for a look."},{"en":"friendly small talk","context":"After a bit of friendly small talk, I explained that I'd never played guitar in my life and had no idea what actually separated one acoustic guitar from another."},{"en":"out of my depth","context":"I'll admit I felt a little out of my depth as a total beginner, but I really appreciated how patient he was about it."},{"en":"actually listen to what I needed","context":"This assistant clearly knew his stuff, and more importantly, he took the time to actually listen to what I needed rather than just pushing the most expensive option."},{"en":"definitely be back","context":"My flatmate ended up being just as impressed, and we both agreed, walking out, that we'd definitely be back once I'd learned enough to know what I was doing."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an occasion where you received a good service from a company or shop","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-190","_displayNo":"#201","outline":{"topic":"buying a first guitar with help from a music shop","intro":"impressed service music shop couple months ago inspired by concert decided learn guitar","body":["flatmate came help find shop plays bit himself knew what to look for window caught my eye","assistant greeted friendly small talk explained never played guitar before","talked through makes models factory handmade wood sound price range felt out of my depth","rare attentive customer service actually listened to what needed not pushing expensive option"],"conclusion":"flatmate impressed too agreed definitely be back once learned enough"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-190.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An occasion when I received good service was at a music shop a couple of months ago. After watching an inspiring concert, I decided to learn guitar. The following weekend, my flatmate came with me to help choose an instrument because he already played a little.\n\nWe noticed a shop window full of guitars and went inside. A young assistant greeted us warmly. I explained that I was a complete beginner and did not understand the differences between acoustic guitars. He patiently described the models, materials, prices and the way different types of wood affected the sound.\n\nI appreciated that he listened to what I needed instead of immediately recommending the most expensive guitar. There was a lot to understand, but he did not make me feel foolish for asking basic questions. My flatmate was impressed too. We left agreeing that we would return when I had a clearer idea of what to buy. For me, the good service came from sharing knowledge honestly and giving a new customer time to make a sensible choice.","html":"An occasion when I received good service was at a music shop a couple of months ago. After watching an inspiring concert, I decided to learn guitar. The following weekend, my flatmate came with me to help choose an instrument because he already played a little.\n\nWe noticed a shop window full of guitars and went inside. A young assistant greeted us warmly. I explained that I was a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">complete beginner</strong> and did not understand the differences between acoustic guitars. He patiently described the models, materials, prices and the way different types of wood affected the sound.\n\nI appreciated that he listened to what I needed instead of immediately recommending the most expensive guitar. There was a lot to understand, but he did not make me feel foolish for asking basic questions. My flatmate was impressed too. We left agreeing that we would return when I had a clearer idea of what to buy. For me, the good service came from sharing knowledge honestly and giving a new customer time to make a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sensible choice</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"complete beginner","meaning":"Someone who has no previous experience of an activity."},{"en":"sensible choice","meaning":"A practical and reasonable decision."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-190.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Something you did that made you feel proud of yourself","prompt":"Describe something you did that made you feel proud of yourself.\nYou should say:\n- what you did\n- when you did it\n- why you felt proud of what you had done\n- and explain how you felt when this happened.","answer":"One of my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">proudest achievements</strong> was completing my undergraduate degree. The moment I remember most clearly is opening my final results and realising I'd done better than I'd expected.\n\nI graduated about ten years ago after several genuinely demanding years of study. The course was full of difficult assignments, presentations and group projects, so that final result meant a lot more than just passing a set of exams, it showed I'd actually developed the discipline to manage long-term work and keep going even when progress felt painfully slow.\n\nMy family supported me the whole way through, but I still had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take responsibility for my own schedule</strong> and motivation. During the final term especially, I spent countless evenings in the library, and that's really where I learned to break large tasks down into smaller, more manageable stages.\n\nI felt proud because that degree was the first major goal I'd ever achieved <strong class=\"key-phrase\">through sustained effort</strong>, rather than luck or last-minute cramming. It also opened doors, both for further study and for work, so its value wasn't just personal, it was practical too. The moment itself, though, was oddly quiet, not at all what I'd imagined. I'd pictured some big celebration, but when the results actually came up on screen, I just sat there for a minute before calling my parents. My mum ended up crying, which honestly surprised me more than the grade itself did.\n\nWhat I really took away from it, in the end, wasn't the qualification at all. It was discovering that I could commit to something for three whole years <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without any guarantee it would work out</strong>, and still see it through. I've drawn on that more than once since, any time I've started something where the outcome was uncertain and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">progress was slow</strong>.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"proudest achievements","context":"One of my proudest achievements was completing my undergraduate degree. The moment I remember most clearly is opening my final results and realising I'd done better than I'd expected."},{"en":"take responsibility for my own schedule","context":"My family supported me the whole way through, but I still had to take responsibility for my own schedule and motivation."},{"en":"through sustained effort","context":"I felt proud because that degree was the first major goal I'd ever achieved through sustained effort, rather than luck or last-minute cramming."},{"en":"without any guarantee it would work out","context":"It was discovering that I could commit to something for three whole years without any guarantee it would work out, and still see it through."},{"en":"progress was slow","context":"I've drawn on that more than once since, any time I've started something where the outcome was uncertain and progress was slow."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"something you did that made you feel proud of yourself","id":"speaking-part-2-191","_displayNo":"#202","outline":{"topic":"complete undergraduate degree","intro":"moment remember clearly receiving final examination results discovering performed better","body":["graduated ten years ago after several demanding years study","course included difficult assignments presentations group projects final result represented far","During final term spent evenings library learned divide large tasks smaller","opened door further study employment value personal practical","moment itself oddly quiet imagined celebrating just sat looking at screen","phoned parents mother cried surprised me more than the mark","took from it = could commit 3 yrs w/o guarantee and still finish","used that knowledge since when result uncertain progress slow"],"conclusion":"used that knowledge since when result uncertain progress slow"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-191.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Something I did that made me proud was completing my undergraduate degree about ten years ago. The clearest moment was opening my final results and discovering that I had done better than I expected.\n\nThe course involved difficult essays, presentations and group projects. My family supported me, but I still had to manage my own time and motivation. During the final term, I spent many evenings in the library and learned to divide large assignments into smaller stages. That made the work easier to handle.\n\nWhen I saw the result, I did not immediately celebrate loudly. I sat quietly for a moment and then called my parents. My mother cried, which made me realise how much the achievement meant to her too. I felt proud because the degree came from years of effort rather than luck or one successful exam. It also created opportunities for further study and employment. Most importantly, I learned that I could continue working towards something uncertain. I still remember that lesson when I begin a long project and progress feels slow.","html":"Something I did that made me proud was completing my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">undergraduate degree</strong> about ten years ago. The clearest moment was opening my final results and discovering that I had done better than I expected.\n\nThe course involved difficult essays, presentations and group projects. My family supported me, but I still had to manage my own time and motivation. During the final term, I spent many evenings in the library and learned to divide large assignments into smaller stages. That made the work easier to handle.\n\nWhen I saw the result, I did not immediately celebrate loudly. I sat quietly for a moment and then called my parents. My mother cried, which made me realise how much the achievement meant to her too. I felt proud because the degree came from years of effort rather than luck or one successful exam. It also created opportunities for further study and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">employment</strong>. Most importantly, I learned that I could continue working towards something uncertain. I still remember that lesson when I begin a long project and progress feels slow.","phrases":[{"en":"undergraduate degree","meaning":"A first university qualification, usually completed before a Master's degree."},{"en":"employment","meaning":"Paid work or the state of having a job."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-191.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Game shows and whether adverts get noticed","prompt":"Do you think there are too many game shows on TV nowadays and why?\nDo you think that people pay attention to adverts on TV and why?","answer":"Q: Do you think there are too many game shows on TV nowadays and why?\nA: Yes, there are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far too many</strong> game shows on TV for my liking. I suppose the channels show these programmes because they're popular, and they must be cheap to make compared with drama, which is why they've <strong class=\"key-phrase\">multiplied so quickly</strong>. A good example is 'Who Wants to Be a Millionaire?', which has been sold to broadcasters across the world and repeated in dozens of local versions. Personally, I'm not a fan, and I'd much rather watch a film or an original drama series. That said, I'd make an exception for the handful that test real knowledge rather than luck, since at least those <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reward something more than guessing</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you think that people pay attention to adverts on TV and why?\nA: Yes, I think we're all influenced <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to some extent</strong> by television advertising; otherwise companies wouldn't spend so much money on it. Advertisers create memorable images and link products with desirable lifestyles, so a brand starts to feel familiar before you've even used it. Even when viewers don't buy immediately, an advert can plant a seed and affect a later decision — that's the whole point of a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long-running campaign</strong>. How closely people pay attention obviously varies, though, and most of us skip adverts whenever we can, which is exactly why advertisers have moved towards sponsorship and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">product placement</strong>, since you can't skip what's built into the programme itself.","topic":"Advertising","phrases":[{"en":"far too many","context":"Yes, there are far too many game shows on TV for my liking."},{"en":"multiplied so quickly","context":"I suppose the channels show these programmes because they're popular, and they must be cheap to make compared with drama, which is why they've multiplied so quickly."},{"en":"reward something more than guessing","context":"That said, I'd make an exception for the handful that test real knowledge rather than luck, since at least those reward something more than guessing."},{"en":"to some extent","context":"Yes, I think we're all influenced to some extent by television advertising; otherwise companies wouldn't spend so much money on it."},{"en":"long-running campaign","context":"Even when viewers don't buy immediately, an advert can plant a seed and affect a later decision — that's the whole point of a long-running campaign."},{"en":"product placement","context":"How closely people pay attention obviously varies, though, and most of us skip adverts whenever we can, which is exactly why advertisers have moved towards sponsorship and product placement, since you can't skip what's built into the programme itself."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Advertising","id":"speaking-part-3-001","_displayNo":"#001","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-001-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-001-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, I think there are too many. Game shows are popular and cheaper to make than dramas, so channels keep producing similar versions. For example, Who Wants to Be a Millionaire? has appeared in many countries. I'd rather watch a film or a new drama, although I do enjoy some shows that test knowledge rather than depend mainly on luck.","phrases":[{"en":"depend mainly on luck","meaning":"Be decided mostly by chance rather than ability."}],"question":"Do you think there are too many game shows on TV nowadays and why?","html":"Yes, I think there are too many. Game shows are popular and cheaper to make than dramas, so channels keep producing similar versions. For example, Who Wants to Be a Millionaire? has appeared in many countries. I&#x27;d rather watch a film or a new drama, although I do enjoy some shows that test knowledge rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depend mainly on luck</strong>."},{"text":"Yes, at least to some degree. Adverts make products familiar and connect them with a lifestyle people want. We may not buy immediately, but remember the name later. Many viewers skip adverts, so companies also pay to show products inside programmes. That way, the message can reach people even when they are not actively watching an advert.","phrases":[{"en":"to some degree","meaning":"Partly, although not completely."}],"question":"Do you think that people pay attention to adverts on TV and why?","html":"Yes, at least <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to some degree</strong>. Adverts make products familiar and connect them with a lifestyle people want. We may not buy immediately, but remember the name later. Many viewers skip adverts, so companies also pay to show products inside programmes. That way, the message can reach people even when they are not actively watching an advert."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-001-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-001-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Whether adverts change what people buy","prompt":"Do you think advertisements influence what people buy?\nWhat criteria should be used to assess a good advertisement?","answer":"Q: Do you think advertisements influence what people buy?\nA: Yes, though not in the direct way people imagine. Very few adverts make somebody buy something immediately. What they do is build familiarity, so when you're standing in front of two similar products, one <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feels known and therefore safer</strong>. That's a subtle advantage but a valuable one, which is why brands spend so much simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeping their name in front of you</strong>. Advertising also attaches products to an image — a car sold as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">evidence of success</strong> rather than as transport — and that works even on people who insist it doesn't. The exception is a genuinely new product with no reputation yet; there, word of mouth often does more than the advert itself.\n\nQ: What criteria should be used to assess a good advertisement?\nA: Whether it's memorable is the first test, since an advert nobody recalls has failed regardless of how elegant it is. Clarity comes next: you should know what's being sold and why it might matter to you. But the criterion I'd weight most heavily is truthfulness. An advertisement that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exaggerates may work once</strong>, and then the customer discovers the product doesn't match the claim, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">damages the brand far more</strong> than the sale was worth. Occasionally a company gets away with mild exaggeration for years, so it isn't an absolute rule — but it's a risk that rarely pays off in the long run.","topic":"Advertising","phrases":[{"en":"feels known and therefore safer","context":"What they do is build familiarity, so when you're standing in front of two similar products, one feels known and therefore safer."},{"en":"keeping their name in front of you","context":"That's a subtle advantage but a valuable one, which is why brands spend so much simply keeping their name in front of you."},{"en":"evidence of success","context":"Advertising also attaches products to an image — a car sold as evidence of success rather than as transport — and that works even on people who insist it doesn't."},{"en":"exaggerates may work once","context":"An advertisement that exaggerates may work once, and then the customer discovers the product doesn't match the claim, which damages the brand far more than the sale was worth."},{"en":"damages the brand far more","context":"An advertisement that exaggerates may work once, and then the customer discovers the product doesn't match the claim, which damages the brand far more than the sale was worth."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Advertising","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-002","_displayNo":"#002","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-002-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-002-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, but they usually work slowly. Seeing a brand repeatedly makes it familiar, so it may feel safer than another product when we shop. Adverts can also suggest that owning something shows success. However, for a completely new product, people may trust recommendations from friends more than the advert itself.","phrases":[{"en":"recommendations","meaning":"Suggestions about what someone should try or choose."}],"question":"Do you think advertisements influence what people buy?","html":"Yes, but they usually work slowly. Seeing a brand repeatedly makes it familiar, so it may feel safer than another product when we shop. Adverts can also suggest that owning something shows success. However, for a completely new product, people may trust <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recommendations</strong> from friends more than the advert itself."},{"text":"A good advert should be easy to remember and explain clearly what the product offers. Most importantly, it should be honest. An exciting promise may attract a buyer once, but people lose trust if the product disappoints them. Some exaggeration might succeed for a while, but it can damage the business in the long term.","phrases":[{"en":"in the long term","meaning":"Over a long period rather than immediately."}],"question":"What criteria should be used to assess a good advertisement?","html":"A good advert should be easy to remember and explain clearly what the product offers. Most importantly, it should be honest. An exciting promise may attract a buyer once, but people lose trust if the product disappoints them. Some exaggeration might succeed for a while, but it can damage the business <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the long term</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-002-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-002-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Popular forms of advertising, and working in the industry","prompt":"What's the most popular form of advertising in your country?\nWould you like to work for an advertising company?\nDo you agree that advertisements for smoking should be banned?","answer":"Q: What's the most popular form of advertising in your country?\nA: Social media advertising, without much doubt, and short video particularly. It's overtaken television because it's far cheaper, it can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">targeted at very specific groups</strong>, and people share it themselves, which effectively makes the advertising free. Influencer marketing is a large part of it, where a recommendation looks like a personal opinion rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a paid promotion</strong>. That's precisely what makes it effective, and also what makes a lot of people uncomfortable about it — you often can't tell whether someone genuinely likes a product or was simply paid to say so.\n\nQ: Would you like to work for an advertising company?\nA: Possibly, depending on the clients. The work itself looks genuinely interesting — it combines research into how people think, writing and visual design, and you see results quickly. What would put me off is the type of product. I'd have no difficulty advertising something useful, but a good deal of the industry works by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">creating insecurity and then selling the remedy</strong> — skincare that promises to fix a flaw you didn't know you had, for instance — and I don't think I could keep doing that for long. That said, plenty of agencies work purely on brand image or public information campaigns, so it wouldn't necessarily be every client.\n\nQ: Do you agree that advertisements for smoking should be banned?\nA: Yes, and the evidence supports it fairly clearly. Tobacco's unusual in that there's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no safe level of use</strong>, and the public health costs fall on everybody through the health system. Advertising's main effect was normalising it and recruiting new smokers, who are almost always teenagers. Countries that banned it saw rates fall within a few years. It does restrict commercial freedom, certainly, and I wouldn't apply that argument to every product. But with tobacco the harm's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">severe enough to justify it</strong>.","topic":"Advertising","phrases":[{"en":"targeted at very specific groups","context":"It's overtaken television because it's far cheaper, it can be targeted at very specific groups, and people share it themselves, which effectively makes the advertising free."},{"en":"a paid promotion","context":"Influencer marketing is a large part of it, where a recommendation looks like a personal opinion rather than a paid promotion."},{"en":"creating insecurity and then selling the remedy","context":"I'd have no difficulty advertising something useful, but a good deal of the industry works by creating insecurity and then selling the remedy — skincare that promises to fix a flaw you didn't know you had, for instance — and I don't think I could keep doing that for long."},{"en":"no safe level of use","context":"Tobacco's unusual in that there's no safe level of use, and the public health costs fall on everybody through the health system."},{"en":"severe enough to justify it","context":"But with tobacco the harm's severe enough to justify it."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Advertising","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-003","_displayNo":"#003","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-003-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-003-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-003-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I'd say social media adverts, especially short videos, are very common. They cost less than television adverts and can reach particular groups. People may also share them with friends. Influencers are important too, but their recommendations can be confusing because viewers may not know whether the opinion is honest or paid for.","phrases":[{"en":"particular groups","meaning":"Specific types of people with shared interests or characteristics."}],"question":"What's the most popular form of advertising in your country?","html":"I&#x27;d say social media adverts, especially short videos, are very common. They cost less than television adverts and can reach <strong class=\"key-phrase\">particular groups</strong>. People may also share them with friends. Influencers are important too, but their recommendations can be confusing because viewers may not know whether the opinion is honest or paid for."},{"text":"Possibly, if I believed in the products. The mix of research, writing and design sounds interesting, and you can see the results quickly. However, I would dislike making people feel bad about themselves just to sell something. Advertising useful products or sharing public information would feel more suitable for me.","phrases":[{"en":"believed in","meaning":"Trusted or thought something had real value."}],"question":"Would you like to work for an advertising company?","html":"Possibly, if I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">believed in</strong> the products. The mix of research, writing and design sounds interesting, and you can see the results quickly. However, I would dislike making people feel bad about themselves just to sell something. Advertising useful products or sharing public information would feel more suitable for me."},{"text":"Yes, I think they should be banned because smoking causes serious harm. Adverts can make it seem normal or attractive, especially to young people. The health costs also affect society, not only smokers. Although businesses normally need freedom to advertise, I think protecting people from this level of harm is more important in this case.","phrases":[{"en":"banned","meaning":"Officially not allowed."}],"question":"Do you agree that advertisements for smoking should be banned?","html":"Yes, I think they should be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">banned</strong> because smoking causes serious harm. Adverts can make it seem normal or attractive, especially to young people. The health costs also affect society, not only smokers. Although businesses normally need freedom to advertise, I think protecting people from this level of harm is more important in this case."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-003-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-003-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-003-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Advice from friends or from family","prompt":"Is it better to get advice from a friend or from a family member?\nWhat would you say are the characteristics of a good adviser?\nShould people make their own work and career decisions, or is it a good idea to ask for advice about this?","answer":"Q: Is it better to get advice from a friend or from a family member?\nA: I think it depends on the kind of advice that you need. Parents and grandparents probably have more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">life experience</strong> than a friend, so you might get a wiser, more considered answer from them. Friends, on the other hand, are less likely to get anxious if you go to them with a problem — I probably wouldn't want to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">burden my parents with</strong> a financial worry, for instance, when a friend would just help me think it through calmly. It's not a hard rule, though; some parents are far more relaxed than some friends, so it really comes down to the individual person, not the category they fall into.\n\nQ: What would you say are the characteristics of a good adviser?\nA: I'd say the most important quality is being <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a good listener</strong> — someone who takes the time to actually understand the situation instead of jumping straight to a solution. That matters because most people who ask for advice have already half-decided what they want to hear, so a good adviser needs to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ask the right questions</strong> rather than just handing out opinions. My aunt's like this: she almost never tells you what to do, she just asks questions until you work it out yourself, which is far more useful than being told outright. The one limitation is that even a good adviser can only draw on their own experience, so it's worth being wary of anyone who's certain their solution fits every situation.\n\nQ: Should people make their own work and career decisions, or is it a good idea to ask for advice about this?\nA: I'd say it's a combination of both. Most people benefit from discussing career choices with family, teachers or experienced colleagues, because someone outside the situation often notices risks or opportunities you've missed yourself. I know someone who almost turned down a job abroad because her parents were nervous about the move, and it turned out to be the best decision she's made. That said, the final decision has to rest with the individual, who must <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take ultimate responsibility for the consequences</strong>. Advice is also worth weighing by where it comes from — parents often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prize security above everything</strong>, which is understandable but can just as easily steer someone away from a risk that was actually worth taking.","topic":"Advice","phrases":[{"en":"life experience","context":"Parents and grandparents probably have more life experience than a friend, so you might get a wiser, more considered answer from them."},{"en":"burden my parents with","context":"Friends, on the other hand, are less likely to get anxious if you go to them with a problem — I probably wouldn't want to burden my parents with a financial worry, for instance, when a friend would just help me think it through calmly."},{"en":"a good listener","context":"I'd say the most important quality is being a good listener — someone who takes the time to actually understand the situation instead of jumping straight to a solution."},{"en":"ask the right questions","context":"That matters because most people who ask for advice have already half-decided what they want to hear, so a good adviser needs to ask the right questions rather than just handing out opinions."},{"en":"take ultimate responsibility for the consequences","context":"That said, the final decision has to rest with the individual, who must take ultimate responsibility for the consequences."},{"en":"prize security above everything","context":"Advice is also worth weighing by where it comes from — parents often prize security above everything, which is understandable but can just as easily steer someone away from a risk that was actually worth taking."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Advice","id":"speaking-part-3-004","_displayNo":"#004","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-004-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-004-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-004-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"It depends on the problem and the person. Older relatives may have useful experience, while a friend might discuss a worry more calmly without becoming too anxious. For example, I might discuss a money problem with a friend first. Still, some parents are very relaxed, so I would choose someone I trust rather than follow a fixed rule.","phrases":[{"en":"a fixed rule","meaning":"An instruction that is expected to apply in every situation."}],"question":"Is it better to get advice from a friend or from a family member?","html":"It depends on the problem and the person. Older relatives may have useful experience, while a friend might discuss a worry more calmly without becoming too anxious. For example, I might discuss a money problem with a friend first. Still, some parents are very relaxed, so I would choose someone I trust rather than follow <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fixed rule</strong>."},{"text":"A good adviser listens carefully before suggesting a solution. They ask questions to understand the situation and help you think for yourself. My aunt does this: she asks questions until I work out my own answer. They should also accept that their experience may not fit every problem, instead of acting certain about everything.","phrases":[{"en":"work out my own answer","meaning":"Find a solution by thinking for myself."}],"question":"What would you say are the characteristics of a good adviser?","html":"A good adviser listens carefully before suggesting a solution. They ask questions to understand the situation and help you think for yourself. My aunt does this: she asks questions until I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work out my own answer</strong>. They should also accept that their experience may not fit every problem, instead of acting certain about everything."},{"text":"People should ask for advice, but make the final choice themselves. Family or colleagues may notice a risk or opportunity they have missed. However, advice can reflect the adviser's priorities, such as wanting a secure job. The person choosing must consider that and take responsibility, because they will live with the result.","phrases":[{"en":"take responsibility","meaning":"Accept that you are answerable for a decision and its effects."}],"question":"Should people make their own work and career decisions, or is it a good idea to ask for advice about this?","html":"People should ask for advice, but make the final choice themselves. Family or colleagues may notice a risk or opportunity they have missed. However, advice can reflect the adviser&#x27;s priorities, such as wanting a secure job. The person choosing must consider that and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take responsibility</strong>, because they will live with the result."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-004-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-004-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-004-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Learning from other people's experience","prompt":"Does that person still give you advice?\nIs it helpful to learn from other people's experiences?\nWhat kind of advice do parents give to their children?","answer":"Q: Does that person still give you advice?\nA: Yes, though the nature of it's changed completely. When I was younger I'd ask what to do and just expect an answer. Now I ask more for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">perspective rather than instructions</strong> — when I was deciding whether to change jobs last year, for example, I didn't want to be told what to pick, I wanted to know what they'd be worried about, or what I might not be seeing. That's more useful, because the decision stays mine. It doesn't always work, though — sometimes I still just want someone to tell me the answer, and perspective isn't much comfort when you're exhausted and need a decision made quickly. But mostly they've adapted too, offering far less <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unsolicited advice</strong> than they used to.\n\nQ: Is it helpful to learn from other people's experiences?\nA: Definitely, because otherwise everyone has to make every mistake personally, which is a slow and expensive way to learn. Somebody who's already done what you're attempting can warn you about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the predictable problems</strong> — a friend who'd already done a master's abroad warned me about visa delays I'd never have thought to plan for. The limitation is that circumstances differ: advice that worked twenty years ago in a different economy might not transfer at all, and people tend to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">over-generalise from their own case</strong>. So I treat experience as evidence rather than instruction.\n\nQ: What kind of advice do parents give to their children?\nA: Mostly about education, money, relationships and personal safety — the areas where a mistake has lasting consequences. What I've noticed is that good advice changes shape as children grow. With a young child it's fairly direct instruction, because they can't assess risk themselves — 'don't cross without looking,' not a discussion. With a teenager the same parent has to shift towards <strong class=\"key-phrase\">asking questions rather than issuing rules</strong>, otherwise it simply gets ignored. That shift doesn't happen at the same pace for every family, though — some teenagers still want, and get, fairly direct guidance on the big decisions, like which subject to study.","topic":"Advice","phrases":[{"en":"perspective rather than instructions","context":"Now I ask more for perspective rather than instructions — when I was deciding whether to change jobs last year, for example, I didn't want to be told what to pick, I wanted to know what they'd be worried about, or what I might not be seeing."},{"en":"unsolicited advice","context":"But mostly they've adapted too, offering far less unsolicited advice than they used to."},{"en":"the predictable problems","context":"Somebody who's already done what you're attempting can warn you about the predictable problems — a friend who'd already done a master's abroad warned me about visa delays I'd never have thought to plan for."},{"en":"over-generalise from their own case","context":"The limitation is that circumstances differ: advice that worked twenty years ago in a different economy might not transfer at all, and people tend to over-generalise from their own case."},{"en":"asking questions rather than issuing rules","context":"With a teenager the same parent has to shift towards asking questions rather than issuing rules, otherwise it simply gets ignored."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Advice","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-005","_displayNo":"#005","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-005-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-005-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-005-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, but I ask for a different kind of help now. When I was younger, I wanted instructions. Now I want another opinion about risks I might have missed, such as when I considered changing jobs. That leaves the choice with me. Sometimes I still want a quick answer, but usually discussion is more helpful.","phrases":[{"en":"another opinion","meaning":"A different person's view of a situation."}],"question":"Does that person still give you advice?","html":"Yes, but I ask for a different kind of help now. When I was younger, I wanted instructions. Now I want <strong class=\"key-phrase\">another opinion</strong> about risks I might have missed, such as when I considered changing jobs. That leaves the choice with me. Sometimes I still want a quick answer, but usually discussion is more helpful."},{"text":"Yes, it can save us from repeating mistakes. Someone who has already done something can warn us about problems; a friend studying abroad, for example, might explain visa delays. However, their situation may differ from ours. I would use their experience as useful information, rather than assume I must do exactly what they did.","phrases":[{"en":"assume","meaning":"Believe something is true without checking it fully."}],"question":"Is it helpful to learn from other people's experiences?","html":"Yes, it can save us from repeating mistakes. Someone who has already done something can warn us about problems; a friend studying abroad, for example, might explain visa delays. However, their situation may differ from ours. I would use their experience as useful information, rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">assume</strong> I must do exactly what they did."},{"text":"Parents often advise children about school, money, relationships and safety because these affect their future. Young children need simple instructions, such as looking before crossing a road. As children grow, parents can explain choices and ask questions instead. The right approach depends on the child, since some teenagers still want direct help with important decisions.","phrases":[{"en":"direct help","meaning":"Clear guidance about what to do."}],"question":"What kind of advice do parents give to their children?","html":"Parents often advise children about school, money, relationships and safety because these affect their future. Young children need simple instructions, such as looking before crossing a road. As children grow, parents can explain choices and ask questions instead. The right approach depends on the child, since some teenagers still want <strong class=\"key-phrase\">direct help</strong> with important decisions."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-005-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-005-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-005-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"How humans differ from animals, and what animals are for","prompt":"In what ways are humans different from other animals?\nWhat are the main roles of animals in your country?","answer":"Q: In what ways are humans different from other animals?\nA: The fundamental difference is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cumulative culture</strong>. Other animals learn, and some use tools, but each generation largely starts again, whereas humans pass down knowledge in a form that accumulates, so we build on what previous generations worked out — nobody has to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reinvent the wheel</strong> every generation. Complex language is what makes that possible. Long-term planning is the other major difference: humans will plant an orchard they won't see fruit from, or save for a pension decades away, which almost no other species does <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in any meaningful sense</strong> — though a few animals, like some whales, do pass on narrow local traditions of their own, so the line isn't quite absolute.\n\nQ: What are the main roles of animals in your country?\nA: Agriculture's still the largest, providing meat, eggs, milk and fish, though mechanisation has removed most of the working roles that buffalo and horses once had. Then there's the ecological function, which people rarely think about — pollination, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pest control</strong>, maintaining soil — and agriculture depends on it entirely. The fastest-growing role is companionship: urban families increasingly keep pets, and in smaller households the animal's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treated much like a family member</strong>. It's a slightly uneven picture, though — people who'd never eat their own dog give barely a thought to the animals raised for their dinner, which shows how much the \"role\" of an animal depends on which category we've mentally put it in.","topic":"Animals","phrases":[{"en":"cumulative culture","context":"The fundamental difference is cumulative culture."},{"en":"reinvent the wheel","context":"Other animals learn, and some use tools, but each generation largely starts again, whereas humans pass down knowledge in a form that accumulates, so we build on what previous generations worked out — nobody has to reinvent the wheel every generation."},{"en":"in any meaningful sense","context":"Long-term planning is the other major difference: humans will plant an orchard they won't see fruit from, or save for a pension decades away, which almost no other species does in any meaningful sense — though a few animals, like some whales, do pass on narrow local traditions of their own, so th..."},{"en":"pest control","context":"Then there's the ecological function, which people rarely think about — pollination, pest control, maintaining soil — and agriculture depends on it entirely."},{"en":"treated much like a family member","context":"The fastest-growing role is companionship: urban families increasingly keep pets, and in smaller households the animal's treated much like a family member."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Animals","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-006","_displayNo":"#006","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-006-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-006-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"One important difference is how humans share knowledge across generations. We use complex language to record discoveries, so each generation can build on earlier work. We also plan far into the future, such as saving for retirement. Some animals learn traditions too, so the difference is not complete, but human knowledge develops on a much larger scale.","phrases":[{"en":"across generations","meaning":"From older people to younger people over time."}],"question":"In what ways are humans different from other animals?","html":"One important difference is how humans share knowledge <strong class=\"key-phrase\">across generations</strong>. We use complex language to record discoveries, so each generation can build on earlier work. We also plan far into the future, such as saving for retirement. Some animals learn traditions too, so the difference is not complete, but human knowledge develops on a much larger scale."},{"text":"Animals provide food, including meat, eggs, milk and fish. They also support farming and nature by spreading pollen and controlling pests. Machines have replaced many jobs once done by working animals. Meanwhile, more families keep pets for company. People often care deeply about pets while thinking less about farm animals, which shows that our attitudes can be inconsistent.","phrases":[{"en":"inconsistent","meaning":"Not following the same ideas or standards in different situations."}],"question":"What are the main roles of animals in your country?","html":"Animals provide food, including meat, eggs, milk and fish. They also support farming and nature by spreading pollen and controlling pests. Machines have replaced many jobs once done by working animals. Meanwhile, more families keep pets for company. People often care deeply about pets while thinking less about farm animals, which shows that our attitudes can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">inconsistent</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-006-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-006-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Caring for pets and changing attitudes to animals","prompt":"What is involved in taking care of a pet?\nHow do you think people's attitudes towards animals will change in the future?\nWhat do you think should be done about poaching endangered animals?","answer":"Q: What is involved in taking care of a pet?\nA: More than most people expect when they take one on. There's the daily routine — feeding, exercise, cleaning up — which sounds simple enough but has to happen every single day for years on end. Then vet care, which is genuinely expensive and unpredictable. Beyond the practical side, animals need attention and company, since a dog left alone for ten hours becomes distressed. What people underestimate most is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the length of the commitment</strong>. That's not true of every animal, though — a cat manages considerably better on its own, which is one reason cats have overtaken dogs as the more popular pet in a lot of cities.\n\nQ: How do you think people's attitudes towards animals will change in the future?\nA: I'd expect concern about welfare to keep growing, particularly around intensive farming, which is already driving legislation on cage sizes and transport in several countries. At the same time, pets are increasingly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treated as family members</strong> rather than animals, with specialised food and even insurance. Those two trends coexist a little uncomfortably, since people who adore their own dog will still eat industrially farmed pork <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without much reflection</strong> about the difference — it's not that they don't care, it's that the connection just doesn't occur to them at the supermarket.\n\nQ: What do you think should be done about poaching endangered animals?\nA: Enforcement matters, and rangers need proper funding and equipment, since they're often outmatched by organised criminal groups. But enforcement alone doesn't work — you have to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce demand at the other end</strong>, through prosecution and campaigns in the countries actually buying the products. And crucially, local communities need a reason to protect wildlife: if an elephant destroys your crops and brings you nothing, protecting it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">asking a great deal</strong>. It's not a quick fix in any case; even where all three strands work together, populations of the worst-affected species can take decades to recover.","topic":"Animals","phrases":[{"en":"the length of the commitment","context":"What people underestimate most is the length of the commitment."},{"en":"treated as family members","context":"At the same time, pets are increasingly treated as family members rather than animals, with specialised food and even insurance."},{"en":"without much reflection","context":"Those two trends coexist a little uncomfortably, since people who adore their own dog will still eat industrially farmed pork without much reflection about the difference — it's not that they don't care, it's that the connection just doesn't occur to them at the supermarket."},{"en":"reduce demand at the other end","context":"But enforcement alone doesn't work — you have to reduce demand at the other end, through prosecution and campaigns in the countries actually buying the products."},{"en":"asking a great deal","context":"And crucially, local communities need a reason to protect wildlife: if an elephant destroys your crops and brings you nothing, protecting it is asking a great deal."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Animals","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-007","_displayNo":"#007","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-007-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-007-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-007-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"A pet needs food, cleaning, exercise and medical care, and those needs continue every day for years. Vet bills can also be expensive. Beyond basic care, animals need attention and company. People should think about the long commitment before getting a pet. Different animals have different needs, though; some cope better alone than others.","phrases":[{"en":"long commitment","meaning":"A responsibility that you agree to continue for a long time."}],"question":"What is involved in taking care of a pet?","html":"A pet needs food, cleaning, exercise and medical care, and those needs continue every day for years. Vet bills can also be expensive. Beyond basic care, animals need attention and company. People should think about the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long commitment</strong> before getting a pet. Different animals have different needs, though; some cope better alone than others."},{"text":"I think people will care more about how animals are treated, including animals on large farms. Pets may also receive more special food, insurance and attention as families treat them like members of the household. However, people may still care more about their own pets than farm animals because they do not connect the two situations.","phrases":[{"en":"how animals are treated","meaning":"The way people care for, handle or behave towards animals."}],"question":"How do you think people's attitudes towards animals will change in the future?","html":"I think people will care more about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">how animals are treated</strong>, including animals on large farms. Pets may also receive more special food, insurance and attention as families treat them like members of the household. However, people may still care more about their own pets than farm animals because they do not connect the two situations."},{"text":"We need trained, well-equipped people to protect endangered animals and enforce the law. But we also need to reduce demand for animal products and help local communities benefit from protecting wildlife. Otherwise, people may see the animals only as a threat to crops or income. These steps should work together, and recovery may still take many years.","phrases":[{"en":"reduce demand","meaning":"Make fewer people want to buy something."}],"question":"What do you think should be done about poaching endangered animals?","html":"We need trained, well-equipped people to protect endangered animals and enforce the law. But we also need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce demand</strong> for animal products and help local communities benefit from protecting wildlife. Otherwise, people may see the animals only as a threat to crops or income. These steps should work together, and recovery may still take many years."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-007-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-007-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-007-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Reading at speed, and jobs that involve heavy reading","prompt":"Are there any occasions when reading at speed is a useful skill?\nAre there any jobs in which people need to read a lot? Which ones?\nDo you think reading novels is more interesting than reading factual books, and why?","answer":"Q: Are there any occasions when reading at speed is a useful skill?\nA: Certainly, though I'd separate it from proper reading. Skimming's genuinely useful when you need one specific fact, or want to know whether a document's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worth reading at all</strong> — bills, timetables, long work emails, the opening pages of a report. What it doesn't suit is anything you actually want to understand or enjoy. If a book's good, racing through it seems to me to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">defeat the entire point</strong> of having picked it up in the first place.\n\nQ: Are there any jobs in which people need to read a lot? Which ones?\nA: Lawyers are the obvious example, since a single case can involve thousands of pages and missing one clause has real consequences. Researchers and academics read constantly, as do journalists and anyone working in policy. What's less obvious is that most ordinary office jobs now involve enormous amounts of reading — emails, reports, specifications. Nobody describes it as a reading job, but people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spend hours a day doing exactly that</strong>, which says something about how narrowly we still define what counts as real work. It's just not the kind of reading anyone finds relaxing, which is probably why it doesn't get counted.\n\nQ: Do you think reading novels is more interesting than reading factual books, and why?\nA: I don't think either's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">inherently more interesting</strong>; they satisfy different appetites. A good novel creates a world convincing enough that the characters feel like people you know, and that's an experience non-fiction can't really provide. A well-written factual book gives you something quite different — <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a genuine insight into how something works</strong>, whether that's the human brain or an ancient civilisation. Which one appeals depends entirely on my mood, and on a stressful week I'll admit the novel usually wins, since fact-heavy books take more concentration than I've got left by the evening.","topic":"Books & Reading","phrases":[{"en":"worth reading at all","context":"Skimming's genuinely useful when you need one specific fact, or want to know whether a document's worth reading at all — bills, timetables, long work emails, the opening pages of a report."},{"en":"defeat the entire point","context":"If a book's good, racing through it seems to me to defeat the entire point of having picked it up in the first place."},{"en":"spend hours a day doing exactly that","context":"Nobody describes it as a reading job, but people spend hours a day doing exactly that, which says something about how narrowly we still define what counts as real work."},{"en":"inherently more interesting","context":"I don't think either's inherently more interesting; they satisfy different appetites."},{"en":"a genuine insight into how something works","context":"A well-written factual book gives you something quite different — a genuine insight into how something works, whether that's the human brain or an ancient civilisation."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Books & Reading","id":"speaking-part-3-008","_displayNo":"#008","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-008-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-008-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-008-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, when you need to find one fact quickly or decide whether a document is useful. For example, you might scan a timetable, bill or long email. However, fast reading is less useful when you need a deep understanding. If I enjoy a book, I would rather take my time than rush through it.","phrases":[{"en":"take my time","meaning":"Do something without hurrying."}],"question":"Are there any occasions when reading at speed is a useful skill?","html":"Yes, when you need to find one fact quickly or decide whether a document is useful. For example, you might scan a timetable, bill or long email. However, fast reading is less useful when you need a deep understanding. If I enjoy a book, I would rather <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take my time</strong> than rush through it."},{"text":"Yes, lawyers, researchers and journalists read a great deal. They need to understand documents accurately because missing a detail can cause problems. Many office workers also spend hours reading emails and reports, although we don't usually call theirs reading jobs. It is often necessary reading rather than something they do to relax.","phrases":[{"en":"accurately","meaning":"Correctly and with attention to important details."}],"question":"Are there any jobs in which people need to read a lot? Which ones?","html":"Yes, lawyers, researchers and journalists read a great deal. They need to understand documents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accurately</strong> because missing a detail can cause problems. Many office workers also spend hours reading emails and reports, although we don&#x27;t usually call theirs reading jobs. It is often necessary reading rather than something they do to relax."},{"text":"Neither is always more interesting; they offer different experiences. A novel lets you imagine a world and understand its characters. A factual book explains real subjects, such as history or how the brain works. My choice depends on my mood. After a stressful week, I often choose a novel because facts can require more concentration.","phrases":[{"en":"factual book","meaning":"A book about real information rather than an invented story."}],"question":"Do you think reading novels is more interesting than reading factual books, and why?","html":"Neither is always more interesting; they offer different experiences. A novel lets you imagine a world and understand its characters. A <strong class=\"key-phrase\">factual book</strong> explains real subjects, such as history or how the brain works. My choice depends on my mood. After a stressful week, I often choose a novel because facts can require more concentration."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-008-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-008-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-008-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"The books people read and encouraging children to read","prompt":"What kind of books do you read?\nWhat types of books do children like?\nShould we encourage children to read books and why?","answer":"Q: What kind of books do you read?\nA: Mostly non-fiction — psychology, education and technology are the areas I keep coming back to — with a novel now and then. The non-fiction's partly practical, since I want ideas I can actually use. But I've come to think fiction does something non-fiction can't: spending four hundred pages <strong class=\"key-phrase\">inside somebody else's reasoning</strong> is genuinely different from reading about people from the outside. I try to keep a balance for that reason, though I'll admit some months the non-fiction pile wins completely and I don't touch a novel at all, which probably isn't the healthiest ratio.\n\nQ: What types of books do children like?\nA: It changes fast with age. Young children want pictures, repetition and humour, and will happily hear the same story fifty times without getting bored of it. Slightly older ones move to adventure and anything a little frightening. Teenagers tend towards fantasy and mystery, or books connected to films and games they already know. What holds across all ages is that children read far more when they've <strong class=\"key-phrase\">chosen the book themselves</strong>, even if the choice seems unambitious to an adult — a graphic novel or a joke book still counts as reading, and insisting on something more \"worthy\" usually just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">kills the habit</strong>.\n\nQ: Should we encourage children to read books and why?\nA: Yes, but how you encourage matters enormously. Reading builds vocabulary and concentration in a way screens generally don't, and children who read well find every other subject easier. The mistake is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turning it into an obligation</strong> — comprehension tests, required pages, reading logs — because that reliably makes children associate books with work rather than pleasure. What works is keeping books within reach, reading alongside them, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">letting them abandon anything they are not enjoying</strong> without any sense of failure.","topic":"Books & Reading","phrases":[{"en":"inside somebody else's reasoning","context":"But I've come to think fiction does something non-fiction can't: spending four hundred pages inside somebody else's reasoning is genuinely different from reading about people from the outside."},{"en":"chosen the book themselves","context":"What holds across all ages is that children read far more when they've chosen the book themselves, even if the choice seems unambitious to an adult — a graphic novel or a joke book still counts as reading, and insisting on something more \"worthy\" usually just kills the habit."},{"en":"kills the habit","context":"What holds across all ages is that children read far more when they've chosen the book themselves, even if the choice seems unambitious to an adult — a graphic novel or a joke book still counts as reading, and insisting on something more \"worthy\" usually just kills the habit."},{"en":"turning it into an obligation","context":"The mistake is turning it into an obligation — comprehension tests, required pages, reading logs — because that reliably makes children associate books with work rather than pleasure."},{"en":"letting them abandon anything they are not enjoying","context":"What works is keeping books within reach, reading alongside them, and letting them abandon anything they are not enjoying without any sense of failure."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Books & Reading","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-009","_displayNo":"#009","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-009-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-009-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-009-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I mainly read factual books about psychology, education and technology because they give me useful ideas. I also read novels sometimes. Stories help me understand a person's thoughts from the inside, which is different from reading explanations about people. I try to include both, although in some months I read almost only non-fiction.","phrases":[{"en":"non-fiction","meaning":"Writing about real people, events or information."}],"question":"What kind of books do you read?","html":"I mainly read factual books about psychology, education and technology because they give me useful ideas. I also read novels sometimes. Stories help me understand a person&#x27;s thoughts from the inside, which is different from reading explanations about people. I try to include both, although in some months I read almost only <strong class=\"key-phrase\">non-fiction</strong>."},{"text":"It depends on age. Young children often enjoy pictures, funny stories and repetition. Older children may prefer adventures, while teenagers choose fantasy or mysteries. Across these ages, children usually read more when they can choose for themselves. Even a joke book or comic can help; forcing a more serious choice may reduce their interest.","phrases":[{"en":"repetition","meaning":"Something happening or being said again several times."}],"question":"What types of books do children like?","html":"It depends on age. Young children often enjoy pictures, funny stories and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">repetition</strong>. Older children may prefer adventures, while teenagers choose fantasy or mysteries. Across these ages, children usually read more when they can choose for themselves. Even a joke book or comic can help; forcing a more serious choice may reduce their interest."},{"text":"Yes, reading helps children build vocabulary and concentrate, which supports other school subjects too. However, we should make it enjoyable rather than another duty. Keeping books nearby, reading together and letting children choose are useful approaches. If they dislike a book, allowing them to try another may help them keep enjoying reading.","phrases":[{"en":"vocabulary","meaning":"The words a person knows and uses."}],"question":"Should we encourage children to read books and why?","html":"Yes, reading helps children build <strong class=\"key-phrase\">vocabulary</strong> and concentrate, which supports other school subjects too. However, we should make it enjoyable rather than another duty. Keeping books nearby, reading together and letting children choose are useful approaches. If they dislike a book, allowing them to try another may help them keep enjoying reading."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-009-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-009-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-009-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Why people write books, and books against films","prompt":"Why do you think some people write books?\nWhat type of books do you read often?\nDo you prefer to read a book or watch a movie on the same subject and why?","answer":"Q: Why do you think some people write books?\nA: Different motives, and they rarely overlap much. Some write to tell a story they can't stop thinking about. Others want to preserve knowledge or influence an argument, which accounts for most non-fiction. A great deal of writing is also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a way of working something out</strong> — people write about a difficult period of their life partly in order to understand it, the way keeping a diary forces you to name what you're actually feeling. What almost none of them do it for is money, though, since very few books earn much at all.\n\nQ: What type of books do you read often?\nA: Mostly research-based non-fiction, particularly on education, psychology and technology, because I want ideas I can apply to what I'm doing. But I read novels regularly too, and I've come to value them differently. Non-fiction tells you about people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">from the outside</strong>, whereas fiction puts you inside someone's reasoning for several hundred pages — a novel about a difficult marriage will teach you something a relationship self-help book simply can't, because you feel the decision being made rather than being told the theory behind it. I'd find it hard to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give up either</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you prefer to read a book or watch a movie on the same subject and why?\nA: The book, generally, if I want to understand something properly. A novel can spend a whole chapter inside a character's thinking, whereas a film has to convey the same thing through an expression lasting two seconds. Films also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compress ruthlessly</strong> — a five-hundred-page plot becomes two hours, and most of the internal reasoning simply can't survive that cut. That said, film does things prose can't: landscape, music and performance carry <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an immediate emotional force</strong> that writing has to work considerably harder to achieve, so I wouldn't say one form is simply better than the other.","topic":"Books & Reading","phrases":[{"en":"a way of working something out","context":"A great deal of writing is also a way of working something out — people write about a difficult period of their life partly in order to understand it, the way keeping a diary forces you to name what you're actually feeling."},{"en":"from the outside","context":"Non-fiction tells you about people from the outside, whereas fiction puts you inside someone's reasoning for several hundred pages — a novel about a difficult marriage will teach you something a relationship self-help book simply can't, because you feel the decision being made rather than being t..."},{"en":"give up either","context":"I'd find it hard to give up either."},{"en":"compress ruthlessly","context":"Films also compress ruthlessly — a five-hundred-page plot becomes two hours, and most of the internal reasoning simply can't survive that cut."},{"en":"an immediate emotional force","context":"That said, film does things prose can't: landscape, music and performance carry an immediate emotional force that writing has to work considerably harder to achieve, so I wouldn't say one form is simply better than the other."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Books & Reading","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-010","_displayNo":"#010","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-010-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-010-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-010-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"People write for different reasons. Some have a story they want to tell; others want to share knowledge or change people's opinions. Writing can also help someone understand a difficult experience, much like a diary does. Money is not the main reward for most writers, because relatively few books earn a large amount.","phrases":[{"en":"reward","meaning":"Something valuable or satisfying you get from an activity."}],"question":"Why do you think some people write books?","html":"People write for different reasons. Some have a story they want to tell; others want to share knowledge or change people&#x27;s opinions. Writing can also help someone understand a difficult experience, much like a diary does. Money is not the main <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reward</strong> for most writers, because relatively few books earn a large amount."},{"text":"I often read books based on research, especially about education, psychology and technology. I want ideas I can use. I also value novels because they let me experience a character's thoughts and decisions, rather than just read advice about behaviour. Both types teach me something, so I would find it difficult to stop reading either.","phrases":[{"en":"based on research","meaning":"Using information gathered through careful study."}],"question":"What type of books do you read often?","html":"I often read books <strong class=\"key-phrase\">based on research</strong>, especially about education, psychology and technology. I want ideas I can use. I also value novels because they let me experience a character&#x27;s thoughts and decisions, rather than just read advice about behaviour. Both types teach me something, so I would find it difficult to stop reading either."},{"text":"I generally prefer a book when I want to understand something in detail. It has more time to explain a character's thoughts, while a film must shorten the story. However, films use music, scenery and acting to create feelings very quickly. So I prefer books for depth, but I don't think films are always less valuable.","phrases":[{"en":"in detail","meaning":"With a lot of information about the different parts."}],"question":"Do you prefer to read a book or watch a movie on the same subject and why?","html":"I generally prefer a book when I want to understand something <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in detail</strong>. It has more time to explain a character&#x27;s thoughts, while a film must shorten the story. However, films use music, scenery and acting to create feelings very quickly. So I prefer books for depth, but I don&#x27;t think films are always less valuable."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-010-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-010-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-010-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Why parties matter, and why some people avoid them","prompt":"What types of parties do people have, and why are parties important?\nWhy do you think some people like parties but others hate them?\nDo you think parties will become more popular in the future?","answer":"Q: What types of parties do people have, and why are parties important?\nA: People have parties to celebrate special occasions like birthdays, weddings, or the start of a new year. I think it's important to mark these things because they're landmarks in our lives — a wedding, for instance, is really the one time an entire scattered family ends up in the same room. Parties are a good way to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bring people together</strong>, and they're an opportunity to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">let off some steam</strong>. Without occasions like these, families and old friends would probably drift apart, since everyone's scattered across different cities and nobody would otherwise make the effort. That said, not everyone enjoys them equally — plenty of people find a big, noisy gathering more draining than joyful.\n\nQ: Why do you think some people like parties but others hate them?\nA: Most people like parties because they have a good time - eating a nice meal, chatting to friends, or having a dance. People who don't like them might find social situations difficult because they're shy, or maybe they don't enjoy having to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make small talk with</strong> people they don't know. I'd also say a lot depends on the size of the party. Plenty of people who dread a room of two hundred strangers are perfectly happy at a dinner with six friends.\n\nQ: Do you think parties will become more popular in the future?\nA: I don't think parties will necessarily become more popular; they've always been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a basic form of social life</strong>, so there isn't really room for them to grow much further. The format may well change, but people will keep celebrating and socialising together one way or another. If anything, I'd expect gatherings to get smaller and less formal — a barbecue with ten friends rather than a hall booked for two hundred. Big parties are expensive and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take a great deal of organising</strong>, and younger people I know increasingly prefer seeing a few friends regularly to hosting one enormous event a year.","topic":"Celebrations","phrases":[{"en":"bring people together","context":"Parties are a good way to bring people together, and they're an opportunity to let off some steam."},{"en":"let off some steam","context":"Parties are a good way to bring people together, and they're an opportunity to let off some steam."},{"en":"make small talk with","context":"People who don't like them might find social situations difficult because they're shy, or maybe they don't enjoy having to make small talk with people they don't know."},{"en":"a basic form of social life","context":"I don't think parties will necessarily become more popular; they've always been a basic form of social life, so there isn't really room for them to grow much further."},{"en":"take a great deal of organising","context":"Big parties are expensive and take a great deal of organising, and younger people I know increasingly prefer seeing a few friends regularly to hosting one enormous event a year."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Celebrations","id":"speaking-part-3-011","_displayNo":"#011","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-011-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-011-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-011-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"People hold parties for birthdays, weddings and other important occasions. They bring family and friends together, including people who normally live far apart. They also give everyone a chance to relax and have fun. Without these occasions, people might meet less often. However, large, noisy parties can be tiring for people who prefer quieter gatherings.","phrases":[{"en":"gatherings","meaning":"Events where people meet and spend time together."}],"question":"What types of parties do people have, and why are parties important?","html":"People hold parties for birthdays, weddings and other important occasions. They bring family and friends together, including people who normally live far apart. They also give everyone a chance to relax and have fun. Without these occasions, people might meet less often. However, large, noisy parties can be tiring for people who prefer quieter <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gatherings</strong>."},{"text":"Some people enjoy the food, conversation and dancing. Others feel shy or dislike talking to strangers, so the same party may feel stressful. Size matters too: someone who dislikes a crowded hall may enjoy dinner with six close friends. I think the type of party is as important as whether a person enjoys socialising.","phrases":[{"en":"socialising","meaning":"Spending time with people for enjoyment."}],"question":"Why do you think some people like parties but others hate them?","html":"Some people enjoy the food, conversation and dancing. Others feel shy or dislike talking to strangers, so the same party may feel stressful. Size matters too: someone who dislikes a crowded hall may enjoy dinner with six close friends. I think the type of party is as important as whether a person enjoys <strong class=\"key-phrase\">socialising</strong>."},{"text":"I don't expect a big increase because parties are already common. People will continue celebrating, but gatherings may become smaller and more casual. A meal or barbecue with a few friends costs less and is easier to organise than a huge event. People may prefer meeting regularly instead of hosting one large party each year.","phrases":[{"en":"casual","meaning":"Relaxed and not very formal."}],"question":"Do you think parties will become more popular in the future?","html":"I don&#x27;t expect a big increase because parties are already common. People will continue celebrating, but gatherings may become smaller and more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">casual</strong>. A meal or barbecue with a few friends costs less and is easier to organise than a huge event. People may prefer meeting regularly instead of hosting one large party each year."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-011-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-011-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-011-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Festivals in the working year, and new ones","prompt":"Do you think new festivals will be introduced in the future?\nWhy do you think festivals are important in the working year?\nWould you agree that the original significance of the festivals is often lost today?","answer":"Q: Do you think new festivals will be introduced in the future?\nA: Almost certainly, because it's happened continuously throughout history — most traditions people assume are ancient were actually invented at a specific point. I'd expect new ones around environmental themes and around local identity, as places try to distinguish themselves. Many will begin commercially, like Black Friday, which was straightforwardly invented to sell things. Whether any of them becomes genuinely meaningful depends on whether communities <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take them over and add their own customs</strong>, which is exactly how the older festivals came about in the first place. Plenty will just fade out again, though — most invented celebrations never catch on beyond a marketing campaign.\n\nQ: Why do you think festivals are important in the working year?\nA: They interrupt routine, which matters more than it sounds. Without fixed occasions people simply don't stop, since there's always a reason to keep working. A festival gives everyone permission to rest <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at the same time</strong>, which is the key point, because a holiday is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far less valuable</strong> if everyone you'd want to see is stuck at work instead. They also bring extended families together, often the only time all year it happens. Not everyone gets that rest equally, though — people in hospitals, transport and hospitality often work through the festival precisely so everyone else can enjoy it.\n\nQ: Would you agree that the original significance of the festivals is often lost today?\nA: Partly, though I'd be careful with the word \"lost\". Commercial promotion certainly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overshadows the original meaning</strong> — plenty of people now think of a festival mainly in terms of the sales and the food rather than the event it commemorates. But traditions have always changed, and the version people call authentic was itself a modification of something earlier. What survives in practice is usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the social function</strong> of bringing families together, and that seems remarkably resistant to commercialisation, even when everything wrapped around it has become thoroughly commercial.","topic":"Celebrations","phrases":[{"en":"take them over and add their own customs","context":"Whether any of them becomes genuinely meaningful depends on whether communities take them over and add their own customs, which is exactly how the older festivals came about in the first place."},{"en":"at the same time","context":"A festival gives everyone permission to rest at the same time, which is the key point, because a holiday is far less valuable if everyone you'd want to see is stuck at work instead."},{"en":"far less valuable","context":"A festival gives everyone permission to rest at the same time, which is the key point, because a holiday is far less valuable if everyone you'd want to see is stuck at work instead."},{"en":"overshadows the original meaning","context":"Commercial promotion certainly overshadows the original meaning — plenty of people now think of a festival mainly in terms of the sales and the food rather than the event it commemorates."},{"en":"the social function","context":"What survives in practice is usually the social function of bringing families together, and that seems remarkably resistant to commercialisation, even when everything wrapped around it has become thoroughly commercial."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Celebrations","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-012","_displayNo":"#012","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-012-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-012-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-012-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, new festivals will probably appear because traditions have always developed over time. Some may focus on the environment or local identity; others may begin as ways to sell products. To last, a festival needs people to give it their own customs and meaning. Many new celebrations will disappear if communities never become interested in them.","phrases":[{"en":"local identity","meaning":"The qualities and traditions that make a place and its people distinctive."}],"question":"Do you think new festivals will be introduced in the future?","html":"Yes, new festivals will probably appear because traditions have always developed over time. Some may focus on the environment or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">local identity</strong>; others may begin as ways to sell products. To last, a festival needs people to give it their own customs and meaning. Many new celebrations will disappear if communities never become interested in them."},{"text":"Festivals give people a shared break from work. That matters because free time is more enjoyable when family and friends are available too. They also help relatives meet when they live far apart. However, not everyone can rest: hospital, transport and restaurant workers may need to work so that others can enjoy the celebration.","phrases":[{"en":"a shared break","meaning":"Time off that people can enjoy at the same time."}],"question":"Why do you think festivals are important in the working year?","html":"Festivals give people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a shared break</strong> from work. That matters because free time is more enjoyable when family and friends are available too. They also help relatives meet when they live far apart. However, not everyone can rest: hospital, transport and restaurant workers may need to work so that others can enjoy the celebration."},{"text":"Partly. Shopping and advertising can receive more attention than the event a festival originally remembered. However, traditions have always changed, so change does not necessarily mean everything valuable is lost. Families still come together, and that social purpose often remains strong. I think the original meaning may become weaker while some important benefits continue.","phrases":[{"en":"original meaning","meaning":"The purpose or importance something had when it began."}],"question":"Would you agree that the original significance of the festivals is often lost today?","html":"Partly. Shopping and advertising can receive more attention than the event a festival originally remembered. However, traditions have always changed, so change does not necessarily mean everything valuable is lost. Families still come together, and that social purpose often remains strong. I think the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">original meaning</strong> may become weaker while some important benefits continue."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-012-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-012-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-012-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Parties in your country and what happens at them","prompt":"What types of parties are popular in your country?\nWhat sort of activities do people do at parties?\nWhat do you think of attending parties?","answer":"Q: What types of parties are popular in your country?\nA: Weddings are the biggest by some distance — they're often enormous, with hundreds of guests, and families save for years to hold them. Birthday parties are common for children, rather less so for adults. Then there are family gatherings around New Year and traditional festivals, which matter more than birthdays to most people. Companies hold year-end celebrations too, which are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">half social and half obligation</strong> — you're expected to show up even when you'd rather not. Younger people increasingly prefer small informal gatherings to large formal ones, partly because the enormous weddings are getting harder to afford.\n\nQ: What sort of activities do people do at parties?\nA: Eating's central, since most gatherings are built around a meal and the food's often the point rather than an accompaniment. Beyond that, people talk, take an enormous number of photographs, and there's usually music, sometimes karaoke. At weddings there are speeches and formal rituals too. The best parties in my experience have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">just enough structure</strong> to stop people standing around awkwardly, without scheduling every minute so tightly that nobody can simply sit and talk. Get that balance wrong in either direction and people start <strong class=\"key-phrase\">checking their phones</strong>.\n\nQ: What do you think of attending parties?\nA: I enjoy them when they're small enough for actual conversation, perhaps ten or fifteen people. Large parties I find harder work: the music's too loud to talk properly, and you end up having <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the same brief exchange twenty times</strong> without really speaking to anyone. I'm glad I went, usually, but I often leave feeling I've <strong class=\"key-phrase\">been social rather than connected with anybody</strong> in particular, which is a slightly hollow feeling. Small gatherings don't have that problem, so it really comes down to numbers rather than parties as a whole.","topic":"Celebrations","phrases":[{"en":"half social and half obligation","context":"Companies hold year-end celebrations too, which are half social and half obligation — you're expected to show up even when you'd rather not."},{"en":"just enough structure","context":"The best parties in my experience have just enough structure to stop people standing around awkwardly, without scheduling every minute so tightly that nobody can simply sit and talk."},{"en":"checking their phones","context":"Get that balance wrong in either direction and people start checking their phones."},{"en":"the same brief exchange twenty times","context":"Large parties I find harder work: the music's too loud to talk properly, and you end up having the same brief exchange twenty times without really speaking to anyone."},{"en":"been social rather than connected with anybody","context":"I'm glad I went, usually, but I often leave feeling I've been social rather than connected with anybody in particular, which is a slightly hollow feeling."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Celebrations","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-013","_displayNo":"#013","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-013-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-013-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-013-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Weddings are especially important and can have hundreds of guests. Children's birthdays, New Year gatherings and company celebrations are common too. However, younger people often prefer small, relaxed events. Large parties, particularly weddings, can cost a lot, so meeting a few friends may be a more practical choice for them.","phrases":[{"en":"practical choice","meaning":"An option that works well with the time, money or resources available."}],"question":"What types of parties are popular in your country?","html":"Weddings are especially important and can have hundreds of guests. Children&#x27;s birthdays, New Year gatherings and company celebrations are common too. However, younger people often prefer small, relaxed events. Large parties, particularly weddings, can cost a lot, so meeting a few friends may be a more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical choice</strong> for them."},{"text":"Most parties involve a meal, conversation, photos and music, sometimes including karaoke. Weddings may also have speeches and traditional activities. I think a good party needs some organisation so guests feel comfortable, but enough free time for them to talk. If every minute is planned, people may not really enjoy being together.","phrases":[{"en":"free time","meaning":"Time without a fixed task or organised activity."}],"question":"What sort of activities do people do at parties?","html":"Most parties involve a meal, conversation, photos and music, sometimes including karaoke. Weddings may also have speeches and traditional activities. I think a good party needs some organisation so guests feel comfortable, but enough <strong class=\"key-phrase\">free time</strong> for them to talk. If every minute is planned, people may not really enjoy being together."},{"text":"I enjoy smaller parties where I can have a proper conversation, perhaps with ten or fifteen people. At a huge party, loud music and many short introductions make it harder to connect. I may be glad I attended, but still feel that I barely spoke to anyone. So the size matters more than the party itself.","phrases":[{"en":"connect","meaning":"Build a meaningful or friendly relationship with someone."}],"question":"What do you think of attending parties?","html":"I enjoy smaller parties where I can have a proper conversation, perhaps with ten or fifteen people. At a huge party, loud music and many short introductions make it harder to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">connect</strong>. I may be glad I attended, but still feel that I barely spoke to anyone. So the size matters more than the party itself."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-013-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-013-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-013-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Why older people resist change","prompt":"Why do some older people resist change?\nIs technological change always positive?","answer":"Q: Why do some older people resist change?\nA: I think it's less about age than about competence. Someone who's spent forty years becoming genuinely skilled at something has a great deal to lose when the method changes overnight — they go from being the expert to being the person who needs help, and that's uncomfortable at any age. It's also often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">handled badly</strong>: new systems get introduced with no training and no explanation of why the old way was inadequate, which naturally feels like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a judgement on everything they have done</strong>. Age plays some part too, admittedly — younger people have simply had more practice adapting to new tools throughout their lives, so it isn't purely about who's more competent.\n\nQ: Is technological change always positive?\nA: No, and I think we're too quick to assume it is. Technology's clearly improved healthcare, communication and access to information — nobody wants to give up video calls with family abroad. But it's also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">eliminated jobs faster than people can retrain</strong>, weakened privacy considerably, and created dependencies we don't fully understand. The test should be the outcome and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">who benefits from it</strong>, not simply whether something's new. And the people who benefit are rarely the people who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carry the cost</strong>, which is why the argument gets so heated.","topic":"Change","phrases":[{"en":"handled badly","context":"It's also often handled badly: new systems get introduced with no training and no explanation of why the old way was inadequate, which naturally feels like a judgement on everything they have done."},{"en":"a judgement on everything they have done","context":"It's also often handled badly: new systems get introduced with no training and no explanation of why the old way was inadequate, which naturally feels like a judgement on everything they have done."},{"en":"eliminated jobs faster than people can retrain","context":"But it's also eliminated jobs faster than people can retrain, weakened privacy considerably, and created dependencies we don't fully understand."},{"en":"who benefits from it","context":"The test should be the outcome and who benefits from it, not simply whether something's new."},{"en":"carry the cost","context":"And the people who benefit are rarely the people who carry the cost, which is why the argument gets so heated."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Change","id":"speaking-part-3-014","_displayNo":"#014","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-014-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-014-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"They may worry about losing skills they have spent years developing. When a familiar method changes, an expert suddenly needs help, which can feel uncomfortable. Changes are also harder when nobody provides training or explains the reason. Younger people may have more practice with new technology, but resistance is not simply about being old.","phrases":[{"en":"resistance","meaning":"Unwillingness to accept or support a change."}],"question":"Why do some older people resist change?","html":"They may worry about losing skills they have spent years developing. When a familiar method changes, an expert suddenly needs help, which can feel uncomfortable. Changes are also harder when nobody provides training or explains the reason. Younger people may have more practice with new technology, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">resistance</strong> is not simply about being old."},{"text":"No. Technology has improved healthcare and made it easier to contact family abroad, but it also creates problems. People can lose jobs before learning new skills, and privacy may become weaker. We should ask whether a change improves life and who pays the cost, rather than assume everything new is automatically better.","phrases":[{"en":"privacy","meaning":"Control over your personal information and private life."}],"question":"Is technological change always positive?","html":"No. Technology has improved healthcare and made it easier to contact family abroad, but it also creates problems. People can lose jobs before learning new skills, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">privacy</strong> may become weaker. We should ask whether a change improves life and who pays the cost, rather than assume everything new is automatically better."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-014-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-014-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Toys for boys and girls, and what children should try","prompt":"Do you think it is important for boys to play with boys' toys and for girls to only play with girls' toys?\nDo you think it is good if parents only buy educational toys for their children?","answer":"Q: Do you think it is important for boys to play with boys' toys and for girls to only play with girls' toys?\nA: I think boys do <strong class=\"key-phrase\">naturally seem to prefer</strong> playing with \"boys' toys\" and girls with \"girls' toys\", though how much of that is innate and how much is just modelled by parents, adverts and other children is genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hard to untangle</strong>. When I was young I had toy cars, trucks and toy guns, whereas my sister always wanted dolls, and neither of us was ever pushed towards those choices that I remember. I don't suppose it really matters if a boy plays with a doll or a girl plays with a toy car, but children pick up very quickly what they're \"supposed\" to like, and that's the part I'd want to loosen up.\n\nQ: Do you think it is good if parents only buy educational toys for their children?\nA: No. Educational toys can be useful in moderation, but children also learn a great deal through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unstructured play</strong>. If every toy's chosen for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an obvious academic purpose</strong>, play can turn into another lesson rather than a chance to experiment, invent rules and just enjoy themselves. Children lose interest fast, too, once they sense a toy's really a test in disguise. In my experience the things they come back to are simple and open-ended, like building blocks or a box of old clothes, because those can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">become something different every time</strong> — a purpose-built \"educational\" toy usually can't offer that same flexibility.","topic":"Children","phrases":[{"en":"naturally seem to prefer","context":"I think boys do naturally seem to prefer playing with \"boys' toys\" and girls with \"girls' toys\", though how much of that is innate and how much is just modelled by parents, adverts and other children is genuinely hard to untangle."},{"en":"hard to untangle","context":"I think boys do naturally seem to prefer playing with \"boys' toys\" and girls with \"girls' toys\", though how much of that is innate and how much is just modelled by parents, adverts and other children is genuinely hard to untangle."},{"en":"unstructured play","context":"Educational toys can be useful in moderation, but children also learn a great deal through unstructured play."},{"en":"an obvious academic purpose","context":"If every toy's chosen for an obvious academic purpose, play can turn into another lesson rather than a chance to experiment, invent rules and just enjoy themselves."},{"en":"become something different every time","context":"In my experience the things they come back to are simple and open-ended, like building blocks or a box of old clothes, because those can become something different every time — a purpose-built \"educational\" toy usually can't offer that same flexibility."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Children","id":"speaking-part-3-015","_displayNo":"#015","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-015-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-015-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"No, I don't think those limits are necessary. Boys and girls may show different preferences, but parents, adverts and other children can influence them. I had cars and my sister liked dolls, yet that does not mean everyone should choose the same way. Children should feel free to try toys without worrying about what they are supposed to like.","phrases":[{"en":"preferences","meaning":"The things people like or choose more than others."}],"question":"Do you think it is important for boys to play with boys' toys and for girls to only play with girls' toys?","html":"No, I don&#x27;t think those limits are necessary. Boys and girls may show different <strong class=\"key-phrase\">preferences</strong>, but parents, adverts and other children can influence them. I had cars and my sister liked dolls, yet that does not mean everyone should choose the same way. Children should feel free to try toys without worrying about what they are supposed to like."},{"text":"No. Educational toys can help, but children also need play without a fixed lesson. Simple things such as blocks or old clothes can become many different games. If every toy feels like a test, children may lose interest. I would offer a mixture and let them experiment, imagine and enjoy themselves.","phrases":[{"en":"experiment","meaning":"Try different things to discover what happens or works."}],"question":"Do you think it is good if parents only buy educational toys for their children?","html":"No. Educational toys can help, but children also need play without a fixed lesson. Simple things such as blocks or old clothes can become many different games. If every toy feels like a test, children may lose interest. I would offer a mixture and let them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">experiment</strong>, imagine and enjoy themselves."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-015-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-015-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Encouraging children to try new things","prompt":"Do you think children today are encouraged to try new things?\nWhat can people do to make the best use of their life?\nWhat is the most important event for a teenager?","answer":"Q: Do you think children today are encouraged to try new things?\nA: Less than they should be, I'd say. Schools have become quite exam-focused, so anything that isn't assessed gets squeezed out, and parents are more anxious about safety than they were a generation ago. There's also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fear-of-failure problem</strong>: children praised for being clever often avoid anything they might be bad at, in case it proves them wrong. What works is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">low-stakes encouragement</strong> — letting a child try something with no expectation of being immediately good at it. Some children need none of this pushed on them, of course; a naturally curious kid will try new things regardless of how cautiously they're raised.\n\nQ: What can people do to make the best use of their life?\nA: That's a large question, but I'd start with health, because everything else depends on it and it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far easier to protect than to recover</strong> once it's gone. Then close relationships — the long-running research is fairly consistent that these <strong class=\"key-phrase\">predict life satisfaction better than income does</strong>; someone with a huge salary and nobody to share dinner with usually isn't the happier one. And work that gives some sense of direction, which needn't be a grand purpose. None of this is a fixed order for everyone, though — someone in real financial hardship reasonably has to prioritise income for a while before any of the rest becomes possible.\n\nQ: What is the most important event for a teenager?\nA: There's no single event that applies to everybody, but finishing school and making the first real educational choice is significant for most. It's usually the first decision with genuine long-term consequences that a young person makes largely alone. What makes it difficult is that they're asked to choose <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with very little information</strong> about what any of those paths actually involve day to day — nobody really knows what studying engineering is like until they're two years into the degree. For others the defining event is more personal, like a first job or moving away from home for the first time, so it really varies by what a particular teenager's life looks like.","topic":"Children","phrases":[{"en":"a fear-of-failure problem","context":"There's also a fear-of-failure problem: children praised for being clever often avoid anything they might be bad at, in case it proves them wrong."},{"en":"low-stakes encouragement","context":"What works is low-stakes encouragement — letting a child try something with no expectation of being immediately good at it."},{"en":"far easier to protect than to recover","context":"That's a large question, but I'd start with health, because everything else depends on it and it's far easier to protect than to recover once it's gone."},{"en":"predict life satisfaction better than income does","context":"Then close relationships — the long-running research is fairly consistent that these predict life satisfaction better than income does; someone with a huge salary and nobody to share dinner with usually isn't the happier one."},{"en":"with very little information","context":"What makes it difficult is that they're asked to choose with very little information about what any of those paths actually involve day to day — nobody really knows what studying engineering is like until they're two years into the degree."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Children","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-016","_displayNo":"#016","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-016-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-016-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-016-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Not enough, in my opinion. Exams can leave little room for other activities, and adults may worry about safety or failure. Children need chances to try something without having to be good immediately. Praising effort can help. Of course, some children are naturally curious and will explore even without much encouragement.","phrases":[{"en":"encouragement","meaning":"Support that helps someone feel willing or confident to try."}],"question":"Do you think children today are encouraged to try new things?","html":"Not enough, in my opinion. Exams can leave little room for other activities, and adults may worry about safety or failure. Children need chances to try something without having to be good immediately. Praising effort can help. Of course, some children are naturally curious and will explore even without much <strong class=\"key-phrase\">encouragement</strong>."},{"text":"I'd start with health, close relationships and work that gives life some direction. These can make everyday life more satisfying than simply earning more money. However, people have different needs. Someone struggling to pay for food and housing may reasonably focus on income first, before they have the freedom to think about other goals.","phrases":[{"en":"income","meaning":"Money received from work or other sources."}],"question":"What can people do to make the best use of their life?","html":"I&#x27;d start with health, close relationships and work that gives life some direction. These can make everyday life more satisfying than simply earning more money. However, people have different needs. Someone struggling to pay for food and housing may reasonably focus on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">income</strong> first, before they have the freedom to think about other goals."},{"text":"Finishing school and choosing further education is a major event for many teenagers. It can affect their future, although they often know little about what each course or career is really like. For others, a first job or moving away matters more. I don't think one event is equally important for every young person.","phrases":[{"en":"further education","meaning":"Study or training after finishing school."}],"question":"What is the most important event for a teenager?","html":"Finishing school and choosing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">further education</strong> is a major event for many teenagers. It can affect their future, although they often know little about what each course or career is really like. For others, a first job or moving away matters more. I don&#x27;t think one event is equally important for every young person."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-016-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-016-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-016-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Children's activities in a city and getting them interested","prompt":"What kinds of children's activities are there available in your city?\nWhat do you need to do to interest a child in such activities?\nHow do you think adults should improve their relations with children?","answer":"Q: What kinds of children's activities are there available in your city?\nA: Quite a lot — sports clubs, swimming pools, music and art classes, libraries with children's sections, and public parks. Larger cities also have science museums and coding workshops, which have become popular recently. The problem isn't availability but access, though: plenty of the good ones charge fees a low-income family can't manage, and they're <strong class=\"key-phrase\">concentrated in central districts</strong>, so a child in an outer suburb <strong class=\"key-phrase\">faces a long journey</strong> just to get there, on top of the fee itself.\n\nQ: What do you need to do to interest a child in such activities?\nA: Start with something connected to what they already like, rather than what you think they should enjoy — a child obsessed with dinosaurs will happily sit through a museum trip that a \"get some culture\" lecture would never achieve. Then remove the pressure, because a child who feels they're being assessed will usually refuse, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">praising effort rather than results</strong> matters a great deal, since a child praised for being talented stops attempting anything difficult. It doesn't always work straightaway, though — plenty of children need to see a friend enjoying something first before they're willing to try it themselves.\n\nQ: How do you think adults should improve their relations with children?\nA: The most effective thing is listening properly — not just waiting for your turn to reply, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taking what they say seriously</strong>, including complaints that seem trivial to an adult. Explaining rules helps too, since \"because I said so\" only produces compliance while an adult's actually watching. And keeping promises matters, because children remember broken ones for years. None of this means removing boundaries, though; <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consistent limits</strong> are part of what makes a child feel secure in the first place.","topic":"Children","phrases":[{"en":"concentrated in central districts","context":"The problem isn't availability but access, though: plenty of the good ones charge fees a low-income family can't manage, and they're concentrated in central districts, so a child in an outer suburb faces a long journey just to get there, on top of the fee itself."},{"en":"faces a long journey","context":"The problem isn't availability but access, though: plenty of the good ones charge fees a low-income family can't manage, and they're concentrated in central districts, so a child in an outer suburb faces a long journey just to get there, on top of the fee itself."},{"en":"praising effort rather than results","context":"Then remove the pressure, because a child who feels they're being assessed will usually refuse, and praising effort rather than results matters a great deal, since a child praised for being talented stops attempting anything difficult."},{"en":"taking what they say seriously","context":"The most effective thing is listening properly — not just waiting for your turn to reply, but taking what they say seriously, including complaints that seem trivial to an adult."},{"en":"consistent limits","context":"None of this means removing boundaries, though; consistent limits are part of what makes a child feel secure in the first place."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Children","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-017","_displayNo":"#017","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-017-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-017-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-017-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"There are sports clubs, swimming pools, art and music classes, libraries and parks. Some larger cities also have science museums and computer workshops. However, not every child can use them. Fees may be too high, and many activities are far from outer neighbourhoods, so travel time and money can limit access.","phrases":[{"en":"access","meaning":"The opportunity or ability to use something."}],"question":"What kinds of children's activities are there available in your city?","html":"There are sports clubs, swimming pools, art and music classes, libraries and parks. Some larger cities also have science museums and computer workshops. However, not every child can use them. Fees may be too high, and many activities are far from outer neighbourhoods, so travel time and money can limit <strong class=\"key-phrase\">access</strong>."},{"text":"I'd begin with something the child already enjoys. A child interested in dinosaurs may like a museum more than a general lecture. It also helps to remove pressure and praise trying rather than winning. Some children need time, or may become interested only after seeing a friend enjoy the same activity.","phrases":[{"en":"remove pressure","meaning":"Stop making someone feel that they must succeed or perform."}],"question":"What do you need to do to interest a child in such activities?","html":"I&#x27;d begin with something the child already enjoys. A child interested in dinosaurs may like a museum more than a general lecture. It also helps to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remove pressure</strong> and praise trying rather than winning. Some children need time, or may become interested only after seeing a friend enjoy the same activity."},{"text":"Adults should listen seriously, explain rules and keep promises. Something that seems small to an adult may be important to a child, so dismissing it can damage trust. Listening does not mean allowing everything, though. Clear, consistent limits also help children feel safe because they know what to expect.","phrases":[{"en":"consistent limits","meaning":"Rules that stay clear and are applied in a similar way each time."}],"question":"How do you think adults should improve their relations with children?","html":"Adults should listen seriously, explain rules and keep promises. Something that seems small to an adult may be important to a child, so dismissing it can damage trust. Listening does not mean allowing everything, though. Clear, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consistent limits</strong> also help children feel safe because they know what to expect."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-017-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-017-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-017-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"City life against rural life, and making cities easier","prompt":"How is life different in cities compared to rural areas?\nHow do you think life in big cities could be made easier?\nWhat do you think cities will be like in 50 years time?","answer":"Q: How is life different in cities compared to rural areas?\nA: I'd point to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the cost of living</strong> first — housing alone is far more expensive in cities than in rural areas, and pretty much everything else costs more too. There's also more competition, for jobs and for space generally, simply because there are more people chasing the same things. Rural life moves a lot slower, people know their neighbours, and there's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of community</strong> that's harder to find in a big, anonymous city. It's not all one-sided, though — rural areas usually have far fewer services, from hospitals to decent internet, and isolation and lack of opportunity are real problems there too, just quieter ones than city crime.\n\nQ: How do you think life in big cities could be made easier?\nA: In my opinion cities need to be well-planned, above all. Good public transport can definitely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make life easier</strong>, because with so many people around, just getting from one side of the city to the other can be genuinely stressful otherwise. Public parks and green spaces matter too, since people need somewhere to actually relax rather than just commute and work. Affordable housing has to be part of it as well, because when ordinary workers can't live anywhere near their jobs, everything else gets harder. None of this is quick or cheap, though — a proper transit system takes decades and enormous budgets to build, so most cities are stuck living with compromises for a long time before things genuinely improve.\n\nQ: What do you think cities will be like in 50 years time?\nA: I imagine cities will be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less polluted</strong> because we'll have electric cars and far better public transport by then. More people might work from home too, so maybe cities won't feel quite so packed during the day. But I think there'll probably still be real problems, because more and more people are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">migrating to cities</strong> in search of work, so I'm not especially optimistic about issues like crime, homelessness and unemployment going away on their own. I'd also expect cities to be a good deal hotter, which is something planners are only just beginning to design for.","topic":"Cities","phrases":[{"en":"the cost of living","context":"I'd point to the cost of living first — housing alone is far more expensive in cities than in rural areas, and pretty much everything else costs more too."},{"en":"a sense of community","context":"Rural life moves a lot slower, people know their neighbours, and there's a sense of community that's harder to find in a big, anonymous city."},{"en":"make life easier","context":"Good public transport can definitely make life easier, because with so many people around, just getting from one side of the city to the other can be genuinely stressful otherwise."},{"en":"less polluted","context":"I imagine cities will be less polluted because we'll have electric cars and far better public transport by then."},{"en":"migrating to cities","context":"But I think there'll probably still be real problems, because more and more people are migrating to cities in search of work, so I'm not especially optimistic about issues like crime, homelessness and unemployment going away on their own."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Cities","id":"speaking-part-3-018","_displayNo":"#018","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-018-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-018-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-018-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"City life is usually more expensive and competitive, especially for housing and jobs. Rural areas often feel slower and more connected because neighbours know one another. However, they may have fewer hospitals, jobs and reliable internet services. Both places have difficulties; country problems may simply be less visible than those in a crowded city.","phrases":[{"en":"Rural areas","meaning":"Places in the countryside, outside towns and cities."}],"question":"How is life different in cities compared to rural areas?","html":"City life is usually more expensive and competitive, especially for housing and jobs. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Rural areas</strong> often feel slower and more connected because neighbours know one another. However, they may have fewer hospitals, jobs and reliable internet services. Both places have difficulties; country problems may simply be less visible than those in a crowded city."},{"text":"Cities need good public transport, green spaces and housing that ordinary workers can afford. These make travel easier and give people somewhere to relax. Better planning can bring homes and jobs closer together. However, major improvements cost a lot and take years, so residents may have to live with imperfect solutions for some time.","phrases":[{"en":"can afford","meaning":"Have enough money to pay for something."}],"question":"How do you think life in big cities could be made easier?","html":"Cities need good public transport, green spaces and housing that ordinary workers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">can afford</strong>. These make travel easier and give people somewhere to relax. Better planning can bring homes and jobs closer together. However, major improvements cost a lot and take years, so residents may have to live with imperfect solutions for some time."},{"text":"I expect cleaner vehicles and better transport, and perhaps more people working from home. That could reduce some pollution and crowding. However, growing populations may mean that homelessness, unemployment and crime remain problems. Cities may also become hotter, so I think future planning will need to deal with both population growth and heat.","phrases":[{"en":"unemployment","meaning":"The situation of people being unable to find work."}],"question":"What do you think cities will be like in 50 years time?","html":"I expect cleaner vehicles and better transport, and perhaps more people working from home. That could reduce some pollution and crowding. However, growing populations may mean that homelessness, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unemployment</strong> and crime remain problems. Cities may also become hotter, so I think future planning will need to deal with both population growth and heat."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-018-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-018-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-018-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"What makes a city worth living in","prompt":"What makes a city a good place to live?\nHow can crowded cities improve residents' living standards?","answer":"Q: What makes a city a good place to live?\nA: The basics come first — <strong class=\"key-phrase\">affordable housing</strong>, safe streets, transport that actually runs on time, and decent access to work and healthcare. Beyond that, I'd say the best cities manage to feel convenient without feeling anonymous. Somewhere with green space, local shops and places where people bump into each other tends to feel like a community rather than just a collection of buildings, and that's what makes people want to stay long-term rather than just pass through. There's often a trade-off, though — the neighbourhoods with the strongest sense of community are frequently the ones where housing's least affordable, precisely because everyone wants to live there.\n\nQ: How can crowded cities improve residents' living standards?\nA: The most effective investment is usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mass transit</strong>, because it determines how far people can live from work without spending three hours a day commuting. After that I'd prioritise <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mixed-use neighbourhoods</strong>, so shops, schools and workplaces are all within walking distance, along with public housing and local parks. The underlying principle should be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spreading opportunity across every district</strong> rather than concentrating everything in one expensive centre — otherwise you just push the problem outwards instead of solving it. It's rarely painless, though: better transport and new parks tend to raise property prices nearby, so the very investment meant to help existing residents can end up <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pricing some of them out</strong>.","topic":"Cities","phrases":[{"en":"affordable housing","context":"The basics come first — affordable housing, safe streets, transport that actually runs on time, and decent access to work and healthcare."},{"en":"mass transit","context":"The most effective investment is usually mass transit, because it determines how far people can live from work without spending three hours a day commuting."},{"en":"mixed-use neighbourhoods","context":"After that I'd prioritise mixed-use neighbourhoods, so shops, schools and workplaces are all within walking distance, along with public housing and local parks."},{"en":"spreading opportunity across every district","context":"The underlying principle should be spreading opportunity across every district rather than concentrating everything in one expensive centre — otherwise you just push the problem outwards instead of solving it."},{"en":"pricing some of them out","context":"It's rarely painless, though: better transport and new parks tend to raise property prices nearby, so the very investment meant to help existing residents can end up pricing some of them out."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Cities","id":"speaking-part-3-019","_displayNo":"#019","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-019-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-019-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"It needs affordable homes, safe streets, reliable transport, jobs and healthcare. Beyond those basics, local shops, parks and meeting places help people feel part of a community. A city should be convenient without making everyone feel like strangers. Unfortunately, the most attractive neighbourhoods often become expensive because so many people want to live there.","phrases":[{"en":"community","meaning":"People connected by living in the same place or sharing interests."}],"question":"What makes a city a good place to live?","html":"It needs affordable homes, safe streets, reliable transport, jobs and healthcare. Beyond those basics, local shops, parks and meeting places help people feel part of a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">community</strong>. A city should be convenient without making everyone feel like strangers. Unfortunately, the most attractive neighbourhoods often become expensive because so many people want to live there."},{"text":"Better public transport lets people reach work without very long journeys. Cities also need neighbourhoods where shops, schools and parks are close to homes, including affordable housing. Improvements should reach different districts rather than only the centre. However, new parks or stations can raise rents, so existing residents may need protection from being forced to move.","phrases":[{"en":"residents","meaning":"People who live in a particular place."}],"question":"How can crowded cities improve residents' living standards?","html":"Better public transport lets people reach work without very long journeys. Cities also need neighbourhoods where shops, schools and parks are close to homes, including affordable housing. Improvements should reach different districts rather than only the centre. However, new parks or stations can raise rents, so existing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">residents</strong> may need protection from being forced to move."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-019-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-019-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Migration from the countryside to the cities","prompt":"Do you think large-scale migration from the countryside to cities is positive or negative?\nDo you think that the possibility of working from home via the internet will lead to many people going back to the countryside?\nIn what ways do the new megacities of Asia, Africa, and South America differ from older ones such as London or New York?","answer":"Q: Do you think large-scale migration from the countryside to cities is positive or negative?\nA: On balance, positive for the individuals, and difficult for the places at both ends. People move because cities offer work, wages and schooling that simply don't exist in rural areas, and it'd be patronising to say they shouldn't. The problem's speed: when a city grows faster than its housing and transport can keep up, migrants end up in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">informal settlements</strong> with poor sanitation, and rural areas lose exactly the young workers they needed to keep going.\n\nQ: Do you think that the possibility of working from home via the internet will lead to many people going back to the countryside?\nA: Some, but not the large-scale reversal people predicted. Remote work does let a certain group — office workers with decent salaries — choose cheaper housing and more space, and there was a visible movement in that direction just after the pandemic. But it stalls for practical reasons: schools, hospitals, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">patchy internet</strong>, and the fact that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">most jobs still cannot be done remotely</strong>. There's also isolation, which people underestimate until they've actually experienced it, so the shift looks real but partial rather than any kind of wholesale return to rural life.\n\nQ: In what ways do the new megacities of Asia, Africa, and South America differ from older ones such as London or New York?\nA: The main difference is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the speed of growth relative to infrastructure</strong>. London expanded over centuries, so its transport and sanitation had time to develop alongside the population. Cities like Lagos or Dhaka have added millions of people within a few decades, so a large proportion of residents end up in informal housing that the city never really planned for. Older cities have serious inequality too, and it'd be wrong to romanticise them — but they generally do possess <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mature institutions</strong> and transport networks that the newer megacities are still very much in the middle of building.","topic":"Cities","phrases":[{"en":"informal settlements","context":"The problem's speed: when a city grows faster than its housing and transport can keep up, migrants end up in informal settlements with poor sanitation, and rural areas lose exactly the young workers they needed to keep going."},{"en":"patchy internet","context":"But it stalls for practical reasons: schools, hospitals, patchy internet, and the fact that most jobs still cannot be done remotely."},{"en":"most jobs still cannot be done remotely","context":"But it stalls for practical reasons: schools, hospitals, patchy internet, and the fact that most jobs still cannot be done remotely."},{"en":"the speed of growth relative to infrastructure","context":"The main difference is the speed of growth relative to infrastructure."},{"en":"mature institutions","context":"Older cities have serious inequality too, and it'd be wrong to romanticise them — but they generally do possess mature institutions and transport networks that the newer megacities are still very much in the middle of building."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Cities","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-020","_displayNo":"#020","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-020-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-020-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-020-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"It can benefit individuals because cities offer better jobs, wages and education. However, rapid movement puts pressure on both places. Cities may not build homes and services quickly enough, while rural areas lose young workers. I think moving is a reasonable choice, but governments need to manage the speed of growth and support the communities affected.","phrases":[{"en":"puts pressure on","meaning":"Creates extra demands that are difficult to manage."}],"question":"Do you think large-scale migration from the countryside to cities is positive or negative?","html":"It can benefit individuals because cities offer better jobs, wages and education. However, rapid movement <strong class=\"key-phrase\">puts pressure on</strong> both places. Cities may not build homes and services quickly enough, while rural areas lose young workers. I think moving is a reasonable choice, but governments need to manage the speed of growth and support the communities affected."},{"text":"Some people will move back, but probably not most. Online work can give office workers cheaper homes and more space. However, many jobs require people to be present, and rural areas may lack schools, hospitals or good internet. Living far from others can also be lonely. So remote work creates more choice, rather than a complete return to country life.","phrases":[{"en":"remote work","meaning":"Work done away from a usual workplace, often using the internet."}],"question":"Do you think that the possibility of working from home via the internet will lead to many people going back to the countryside?","html":"Some people will move back, but probably not most. Online work can give office workers cheaper homes and more space. However, many jobs require people to be present, and rural areas may lack schools, hospitals or good internet. Living far from others can also be lonely. So <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remote work</strong> creates more choice, rather than a complete return to country life."},{"text":"Many newer megacities have grown much faster than their roads, transport and basic services. Cities such as Lagos or Dhaka have gained millions of residents in a short time. Older cities developed more gradually, giving systems longer to grow. They still have inequality, but usually have more established institutions and transport networks.","phrases":[{"en":"basic services","meaning":"Essential facilities such as clean water, sanitation and public transport."}],"question":"In what ways do the new megacities of Asia, Africa, and South America differ from older ones such as London or New York?","html":"Many newer megacities have grown much faster than their roads, transport and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">basic services</strong>. Cities such as Lagos or Dhaka have gained millions of residents in a short time. Older cities developed more gradually, giving systems longer to grow. They still have inequality, but usually have more established institutions and transport networks."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-020-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-020-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-020-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Collections and what they may become worth","prompt":"Do you think you will ever have the biggest collection in the world?\nHow valuable might your collection become in the future?\nWhy do you think some people collect things?","answer":"Q: Do you think you will ever have the biggest collection in the world?\nA: No, and honestly that's never been the appeal. Once size becomes the objective you end up buying things indiscriminately just to push the count up, and the collection stops meaning anything. What I enjoy is the opposite — finding one item that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fills an obvious gap</strong>, or that comes with a story about how I got it. I'll admit that's a fairly personal definition of success, and a few serious collectors genuinely do build <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exhaustive collections</strong> that have real historical value — mine was just never going to be one of them.\n\nQ: How valuable might your collection become in the future?\nA: Genuinely impossible to predict, and I'd be suspicious of anyone who claims otherwise. Collectors' markets depend entirely on taste, and things that were valuable a generation ago can become almost worthless once the people who wanted them stop buying. So I treat any financial return as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a bonus rather than a plan</strong> — if I'd wanted a proper investment, I'd have bought something with a proven track record instead. That said, a handful of narrow categories, first editions of certain authors for instance, have held their value remarkably well, so it isn't pure guesswork.\n\nQ: Why do you think some people collect things?\nA: A few different motives, really. Nostalgia is a common one — people collect objects from their childhood, and what they're really holding onto is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a period rather than the objects</strong>. Others enjoy the systematic side: researching, organising, becoming genuinely expert in something narrow, and that need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">complete a set</strong> can be satisfying in itself. There's a social element too, since collectors form strong communities. It becomes a problem when it turns compulsive and people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spend money they do not have</strong> chasing the next item.","topic":"Collecting","phrases":[{"en":"fills an obvious gap","context":"What I enjoy is the opposite — finding one item that fills an obvious gap, or that comes with a story about how I got it."},{"en":"exhaustive collections","context":"I'll admit that's a fairly personal definition of success, and a few serious collectors genuinely do build exhaustive collections that have real historical value — mine was just never going to be one of them."},{"en":"a bonus rather than a plan","context":"So I treat any financial return as a bonus rather than a plan — if I'd wanted a proper investment, I'd have bought something with a proven track record instead."},{"en":"a period rather than the objects","context":"Nostalgia is a common one — people collect objects from their childhood, and what they're really holding onto is a period rather than the objects."},{"en":"complete a set","context":"Others enjoy the systematic side: researching, organising, becoming genuinely expert in something narrow, and that need to complete a set can be satisfying in itself."},{"en":"spend money they do not have","context":"It becomes a problem when it turns compulsive and people spend money they do not have chasing the next item."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Collecting","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-021","_displayNo":"#021","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-021-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-021-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-021-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"No, and that is not my goal. I enjoy finding an item that fills a gap or has a personal story, rather than buying anything just to increase the number. Some very large collections do have historical value. Mine is mainly about personal interest, so becoming the biggest would not make it more enjoyable for me.","phrases":[{"en":"fills a gap","meaning":"Provides something that was missing from a group or collection."}],"question":"Do you think you will ever have the biggest collection in the world?","html":"No, and that is not my goal. I enjoy finding an item that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fills a gap</strong> or has a personal story, rather than buying anything just to increase the number. Some very large collections do have historical value. Mine is mainly about personal interest, so becoming the biggest would not make it more enjoyable for me."},{"text":"It is difficult to know because prices depend on what future collectors want. An object valued today may become unpopular later. I would see any profit as an extra benefit, not the main purpose. Some special items, such as certain first-edition books, keep their value well, but I would not assume that my whole collection will do the same.","phrases":[{"en":"keep their value","meaning":"Continue to be worth a similar amount of money."}],"question":"How valuable might your collection become in the future?","html":"It is difficult to know because prices depend on what future collectors want. An object valued today may become unpopular later. I would see any profit as an extra benefit, not the main purpose. Some special items, such as certain first-edition books, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep their value</strong> well, but I would not assume that my whole collection will do the same."},{"text":"Some people collect reminders of childhood. Others enjoy researching a subject, arranging objects or completing a set. Collecting can also introduce them to people with the same interests. These benefits can be enjoyable, but problems start if the desire for another item becomes too strong and people spend money they cannot afford.","phrases":[{"en":"completing a set","meaning":"Obtaining all the items that belong to a particular group."}],"question":"Why do you think some people collect things?","html":"Some people collect reminders of childhood. Others enjoy researching a subject, arranging objects or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completing a set</strong>. Collecting can also introduce them to people with the same interests. These benefits can be enjoyable, but problems start if the desire for another item becomes too strong and people spend money they cannot afford."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-021-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-021-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-021-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Ways of communicating, and how technology changed them","prompt":"What are the different ways people communicate?\nDo you prefer to write a letter to a friend or send an email and why?\nHow has technology affected the way people communicate?","answer":"Q: What are the different ways people communicate?\nA: The obvious ones are face-to-face conversation, phone calls, messaging apps, email and video calls, plus formal written documents at work. What interests me is that people have developed quite specific unwritten rules about which to use. A message is fine for arranging something, a phone call's for when it's urgent, and anything difficult or emotional really needs a face-to-face conversation. Using <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the wrong channel</strong> — resigning by email, for instance — is read as a statement in itself. Those rules aren't fixed, though: video calls have blurred the line, and what counts as acceptable <strong class=\"key-phrase\">varies a lot by generation</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you prefer to write a letter to a friend or send an email and why?\nA: Email, for anything ordinary, because it's instant and I can find it again months later. But I'd write an actual letter for something significant, like a condolence or properly thanking someone. The reason is that a letter takes effort, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the effort is part of the message</strong> — the person knows you sat down and gave them twenty minutes, not two. An email can carry that too, if you genuinely slow down and write it properly, but most of us don't, which is exactly why the letter still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stands out</strong>.\n\nQ: How has technology affected the way people communicate?\nA: It's made communication constant and global, which is largely positive, since families spread across countries can speak every day. The costs are subtler, though. We've become less patient, because anything slower than instant feels broken, and a lot of communication has moved to text, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strips out tone</strong> completely — so misunderstandings happen constantly over messages that would've been perfectly clear <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spoken aloud</strong>. Difficult conversations still need a real voice or a face, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no amount of messaging</strong> replaces that.","topic":"Communication","phrases":[{"en":"the wrong channel","context":"Using the wrong channel — resigning by email, for instance — is read as a statement in itself."},{"en":"varies a lot by generation","context":"Those rules aren't fixed, though: video calls have blurred the line, and what counts as acceptable varies a lot by generation."},{"en":"the effort is part of the message","context":"The reason is that a letter takes effort, and the effort is part of the message — the person knows you sat down and gave them twenty minutes, not two."},{"en":"stands out","context":"An email can carry that too, if you genuinely slow down and write it properly, but most of us don't, which is exactly why the letter still stands out."},{"en":"strips out tone","context":"We've become less patient, because anything slower than instant feels broken, and a lot of communication has moved to text, which strips out tone completely — so misunderstandings happen constantly over messages that would've been perfectly clear spoken aloud."},{"en":"spoken aloud","context":"We've become less patient, because anything slower than instant feels broken, and a lot of communication has moved to text, which strips out tone completely — so misunderstandings happen constantly over messages that would've been perfectly clear spoken aloud."},{"en":"no amount of messaging","context":"Difficult conversations still need a real voice or a face, and no amount of messaging replaces that."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Communication","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-022","_displayNo":"#022","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-022-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-022-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-022-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"People communicate in person, by phone, messages, email and video calls, as well as through formal documents. They usually choose according to the situation: a text for plans, or a call for something urgent. Difficult conversations may need a face-to-face meeting. However, these habits vary between generations and change as new technology becomes normal.","phrases":[{"en":"face-to-face","meaning":"With people physically present together."}],"question":"What are the different ways people communicate?","html":"People communicate in person, by phone, messages, email and video calls, as well as through formal documents. They usually choose according to the situation: a text for plans, or a call for something urgent. Difficult conversations may need a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">face-to-face</strong> meeting. However, these habits vary between generations and change as new technology becomes normal."},{"text":"For ordinary news, I prefer email because it is quick and easy to find later. For something important, like expressing sympathy or giving special thanks, I might write a letter. The time and care involved make it feel more personal. An email can also be thoughtful, but people often write those much more quickly.","phrases":[{"en":"expressing sympathy","meaning":"Showing care for someone who is experiencing sadness or difficulty."}],"question":"Do you prefer to write a letter to a friend or send an email and why?","html":"For ordinary news, I prefer email because it is quick and easy to find later. For something important, like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expressing sympathy</strong> or giving special thanks, I might write a letter. The time and care involved make it feel more personal. An email can also be thoughtful, but people often write those much more quickly."},{"text":"Technology lets people communicate instantly across the world, which helps families stay close. However, people may expect immediate replies and become less patient. Written messages also hide tone, so misunderstandings are easier. For something sensitive or difficult, hearing a voice or seeing a face is still valuable; sending more messages may not solve the problem.","phrases":[{"en":"tone","meaning":"The feeling or attitude shown by how someone speaks."}],"question":"How has technology affected the way people communicate?","html":"Technology lets people communicate instantly across the world, which helps families stay close. However, people may expect immediate replies and become less patient. Written messages also hide <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tone</strong>, so misunderstandings are easier. For something sensitive or difficult, hearing a voice or seeing a face is still valuable; sending more messages may not solve the problem."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-022-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-022-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-022-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Competition in the classroom and prizes for children","prompt":"Why do you think some school teachers use competitions as class activities?\nIs it a good thing to give prizes to children who do well at school and why?\nWould you say that schools for young children have become more or less competitive since you were that age and why?","answer":"Q: Why do you think some school teachers use competitions as class activities?\nA: I think teachers use competitions mainly to motivate the children in their classes. I'm sure teachers try all kinds of activities to engage their pupils, and competitions might be one of the best ways to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep children interested</strong> or get them excited, because children love winning things and it changes the whole atmosphere of a lesson. A class that's gone completely quiet will suddenly come alive if you turn the same exercise into a team game, even though the content's identical. It doesn't always work, though — a child who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never wins</strong> can switch off just as quickly as one who's fired up by it.\n\nQ: Is it a good thing to give prizes to children who do well at school and why?\nA: It might be a good idea to reward children for doing well in games or sports, but I don't think we should give prizes for their <strong class=\"key-phrase\">academic work</strong>. Children need to learn that the point of studying is to learn useful things that'll help them later in life, not to collect prizes. I've seen what happens when rewards become routine: a child who's used to getting a sticker every time stops trying the moment the stickers disappear, because the work itself was never the point. Occasional, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">low-key recognition</strong> for effort still seems far less risky than a formal prize system, though.\n\nQ: Would you say that schools for young children have become more or less competitive since you were that age and why?\nA: Schools for young children do seem more competitive than they were in the past. Testing begins earlier, parents compare results more closely, and some children start extra classes before they've even developed a proper attitude to learning — I've seen five-year-olds doing maths worksheets after school simply because a neighbour's child is doing the same. That pressure can improve short-term performance, but it often comes at the cost of curiosity. Parents feel it too: they see other families paying for extra classes and worry their own child will <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fall behind</strong>, and that anxiety spreads fast, so it's very hard for one family to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">opt out alone</strong>.","topic":"Competition","phrases":[{"en":"keep children interested","context":"I'm sure teachers try all kinds of activities to engage their pupils, and competitions might be one of the best ways to keep children interested or get them excited, because children love winning things and it changes the whole atmosphere of a lesson."},{"en":"never wins","context":"It doesn't always work, though — a child who never wins can switch off just as quickly as one who's fired up by it."},{"en":"academic work","context":"It might be a good idea to reward children for doing well in games or sports, but I don't think we should give prizes for their academic work."},{"en":"low-key recognition","context":"Occasional, low-key recognition for effort still seems far less risky than a formal prize system, though."},{"en":"fall behind","context":"Parents feel it too: they see other families paying for extra classes and worry their own child will fall behind, and that anxiety spreads fast, so it's very hard for one family to opt out alone."},{"en":"opt out alone","context":"Parents feel it too: they see other families paying for extra classes and worry their own child will fall behind, and that anxiety spreads fast, so it's very hard for one family to opt out alone."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Competition","id":"speaking-part-3-023","_displayNo":"#023","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-023-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-023-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-023-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Competitions can make a lesson more exciting and encourage children to join in. The same exercise may feel more enjoyable as a team game because pupils want to win. However, teachers need to be careful. Children who repeatedly lose may stop trying, so competition should support learning rather than make some students feel incapable.","phrases":[{"en":"join in","meaning":"Take part in an activity with other people."}],"question":"Why do you think some school teachers use competitions as class activities?","html":"Competitions can make a lesson more exciting and encourage children to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">join in</strong>. The same exercise may feel more enjoyable as a team game because pupils want to win. However, teachers need to be careful. Children who repeatedly lose may stop trying, so competition should support learning rather than make some students feel incapable."},{"text":"Prizes may be useful in games or sport, but I would be careful about rewarding every good piece of schoolwork. Children should value learning itself. If they expect a prize each time, they may stop trying when rewards disappear. Occasional praise for effort seems more helpful than making academic work depend on winning something.","phrases":[{"en":"effort","meaning":"The energy and care you put into doing something."}],"question":"Is it a good thing to give prizes to children who do well at school and why?","html":"Prizes may be useful in games or sport, but I would be careful about rewarding every good piece of schoolwork. Children should value learning itself. If they expect a prize each time, they may stop trying when rewards disappear. Occasional praise for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">effort</strong> seems more helpful than making academic work depend on winning something."},{"text":"I think they have become more competitive. Testing starts earlier, parents compare results and children attend extra classes. This may improve marks for a while, but it can reduce curiosity and create anxiety. Parents also worry when other families do more, so it is difficult for one family to avoid the pressure alone.","phrases":[{"en":"curiosity","meaning":"The desire to learn or understand new things."}],"question":"Would you say that schools for young children have become more or less competitive since you were that age and why?","html":"I think they have become more competitive. Testing starts earlier, parents compare results and children attend extra classes. This may improve marks for a while, but it can reduce <strong class=\"key-phrase\">curiosity</strong> and create anxiety. Parents also worry when other families do more, so it is difficult for one family to avoid the pressure alone."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-023-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-023-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-023-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Entering competitions","prompt":"Do you enjoy entering competitions?\nHave you entered any other competitions?","answer":"Q: Do you enjoy entering competitions?\nA: I do, provided the competition <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tests a genuine skill</strong> and gives you something back for entering. What I like most is the deadline, because it forces a level of preparation I'd never manage on my own — I once signed up for a local photography competition and it was the only thing that got me out shooting every weekend for a month. I'm much less keen on events decided largely by luck, a prize draw, say, since there's nothing to learn from the result either way. For me the point should be that you're <strong class=\"key-phrase\">better afterwards than you were before</strong>, not simply where you finished.\n\nQ: Have you entered any other competitions?\nA: Yes, mostly academic and language competitions when I was at school — essay writing and public speaking, that sort of thing. Honestly, I didn't win very often, but the preparation was where the real value lay. I learned to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">plan my time properly</strong>, to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">perform under pressure</strong>, and to look at a poor result and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work out what actually went wrong</strong> instead of simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feeling discouraged</strong>. Those habits turned out to matter far more than any of the results ever did, though I'll admit it would've been nice to win occasionally too.","topic":"Competition","phrases":[{"en":"tests a genuine skill","context":"I do, provided the competition tests a genuine skill and gives you something back for entering."},{"en":"better afterwards than you were before","context":"For me the point should be that you're better afterwards than you were before, not simply where you finished."},{"en":"plan my time properly","context":"I learned to plan my time properly, to perform under pressure, and to look at a poor result and work out what actually went wrong instead of simply feeling discouraged."},{"en":"perform under pressure","context":"I learned to plan my time properly, to perform under pressure, and to look at a poor result and work out what actually went wrong instead of simply feeling discouraged."},{"en":"work out what actually went wrong","context":"I learned to plan my time properly, to perform under pressure, and to look at a poor result and work out what actually went wrong instead of simply feeling discouraged."},{"en":"feeling discouraged","context":"I learned to plan my time properly, to perform under pressure, and to look at a poor result and work out what actually went wrong instead of simply feeling discouraged."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Competition","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-024","_displayNo":"#024","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-024-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-024-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, when a competition tests a skill and helps me improve. A deadline makes me prepare more seriously. For example, a photography competition once encouraged me to practise every weekend. I am less interested in events decided by luck because there is little to learn. Improving matters more to me than my final position.","phrases":[{"en":"deadline","meaning":"The time by which something must be finished."}],"question":"Do you enjoy entering competitions?","html":"Yes, when a competition tests a skill and helps me improve. A <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deadline</strong> makes me prepare more seriously. For example, a photography competition once encouraged me to practise every weekend. I am less interested in events decided by luck because there is little to learn. Improving matters more to me than my final position."},{"text":"Yes, I entered school competitions such as essay writing and public speaking. I did not often win, but preparing taught me to organise my time, handle pressure and learn from mistakes. Those skills have been more useful than the results themselves, although winning occasionally would also have been nice.","phrases":[{"en":"handle pressure","meaning":"Stay effective when a situation is demanding or stressful."}],"question":"Have you entered any other competitions?","html":"Yes, I entered school competitions such as essay writing and public speaking. I did not often win, but preparing taught me to organise my time, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">handle pressure</strong> and learn from mistakes. Those skills have been more useful than the results themselves, although winning occasionally would also have been nice."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-024-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-024-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Local goods against imported ones","prompt":"Why do some people prefer domestically produced goods to imported products?","answer":"Q: Why do some people prefer domestically produced goods to imported products?\nA: There are a few reasons. The most common is a sense of responsibility towards <strong class=\"key-phrase\">local employment</strong> — people feel that buying domestic keeps money circulating in the national economy and protects jobs in their own community. Trust is another factor, particularly with food: consumers understand their own country's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">safety standards</strong> and know that a problem can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">traced back to a specific farm or factory</strong>, which isn't always the case with imports. There's an emotional element too — for some people buying local is simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a form of national pride</strong>, regardless of price or quality. It isn't always the rational choice, though: a domestic product is sometimes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more expensive for no real gain</strong> in quality, and buyers who insist on it anyway are paying for the principle, not the product.","topic":"Consumerism & Possessions","phrases":[{"en":"local employment","context":"The most common is a sense of responsibility towards local employment — people feel that buying domestic keeps money circulating in the national economy and protects jobs in their own community."},{"en":"safety standards","context":"Trust is another factor, particularly with food: consumers understand their own country's safety standards and know that a problem can be traced back to a specific farm or factory, which isn't always the case with imports."},{"en":"traced back to a specific farm or factory","context":"Trust is another factor, particularly with food: consumers understand their own country's safety standards and know that a problem can be traced back to a specific farm or factory, which isn't always the case with imports."},{"en":"a form of national pride","context":"There's an emotional element too — for some people buying local is simply a form of national pride, regardless of price or quality."},{"en":"more expensive for no real gain","context":"It isn't always the rational choice, though: a domestic product is sometimes more expensive for no real gain in quality, and buyers who insist on it anyway are paying for the principle, not the product."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Consumerism & Possessions","id":"speaking-part-3-025","_displayNo":"#025","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-025-q01.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Some people buy local products to support jobs and keep money in their own economy. They may also trust familiar safety standards, especially for food, or feel proud of their country. However, local does not always mean better. If a product costs more without offering extra quality, the buyer may be paying for a belief rather than a practical benefit.","phrases":[{"en":"local products","meaning":"Goods made in your own area or country."}],"question":"Why do some people prefer domestically produced goods to imported products?","html":"Some people buy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">local products</strong> to support jobs and keep money in their own economy. They may also trust familiar safety standards, especially for food, or feel proud of their country. However, local does not always mean better. If a product costs more without offering extra quality, the buyer may be paying for a belief rather than a practical benefit."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-025-q01.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Possessions as a mark of status","prompt":"In your country, what possessions show a higher status or position in society?\nDo you think we place too much importance on possessions?\nDo you think things were different 50 years ago?","answer":"Q: In your country, what possessions show a higher status or position in society?\nA: Property first — where you live and whether you own it says more than anything else. Then cars, luxury watches and designer clothing, and until recently the latest phone, though phones have become so universal now that they don't signal much any more. What these have in common is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">visibility</strong>: a possession only works as a status marker if other people can see it and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recognise what it cost</strong>, which is exactly why the specific items shift so quickly — today's must-have brand is often forgotten in five years.\n\nQ: Do you think we place too much importance on possessions?\nA: Yes, though I'd say the pressure's manufactured rather than natural. Advertising works precisely by linking products to identity, so you're not buying a jacket but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a version of yourself</strong>, and social media has widened the comparison enormously. It's hardest on teenagers, who face <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuine social consequences</strong> for having the wrong brand — being excluded over a pair of trainers is a real thing — and whose families often can't afford to prevent it. Not everyone falls for it equally, though; I know plenty of people who've simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">opted out of the whole game</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you think things were different 50 years ago?\nA: Considerably. People owned far fewer things and got them repaired instead of replacing them, because goods were expensive relative to income and credit was much harder to obtain — you generally couldn't buy what you hadn't saved for. My grandfather still talks about having his shoes resoled rather than buying new ones, which sounds almost unthinkable now. Advertising existed then too, but it reached you a few times a day rather than constantly. Materialism isn't new, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the cycle of upgrading</strong> has accelerated beyond anything he'd recognise, which says as much about marketing as it does about human nature.","topic":"Consumerism & Possessions","phrases":[{"en":"visibility","context":"What these have in common is visibility: a possession only works as a status marker if other people can see it and recognise what it cost, which is exactly why the specific items shift so quickly — today's must-have brand is often forgotten in five years."},{"en":"recognise what it cost","context":"What these have in common is visibility: a possession only works as a status marker if other people can see it and recognise what it cost, which is exactly why the specific items shift so quickly — today's must-have brand is often forgotten in five years."},{"en":"a version of yourself","context":"Advertising works precisely by linking products to identity, so you're not buying a jacket but a version of yourself, and social media has widened the comparison enormously."},{"en":"genuine social consequences","context":"It's hardest on teenagers, who face genuine social consequences for having the wrong brand — being excluded over a pair of trainers is a real thing — and whose families often can't afford to prevent it."},{"en":"opted out of the whole game","context":"Not everyone falls for it equally, though; I know plenty of people who've simply opted out of the whole game."},{"en":"the cycle of upgrading","context":"Materialism isn't new, but the cycle of upgrading has accelerated beyond anything he'd recognise, which says as much about marketing as it does about human nature."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Consumerism & Possessions","id":"speaking-part-3-026","_displayNo":"#026","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-026-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-026-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-026-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Homes and land are the clearest signs, followed by expensive cars, watches and designer clothes. These things show status because other people can see them and recognise their price. Phones used to do this too, but they are now so common that they show less. The particular products change as fashions and ideas of success change.","phrases":[{"en":"status","meaning":"A person's social position or level of respect in society."}],"question":"In your country, what possessions show a higher status or position in society?","html":"Homes and land are the clearest signs, followed by expensive cars, watches and designer clothes. These things show <strong class=\"key-phrase\">status</strong> because other people can see them and recognise their price. Phones used to do this too, but they are now so common that they show less. The particular products change as fashions and ideas of success change."},{"text":"Yes, I think adverts and social media encourage us to connect possessions with who we are. We compare ourselves with more people than before, and teenagers may feel pressure to wear certain brands. That can be hard for families with less money. Still, some people choose to ignore this competition and focus on what they actually need.","phrases":[{"en":"possessions","meaning":"The things a person owns."}],"question":"Do you think we place too much importance on possessions?","html":"Yes, I think adverts and social media encourage us to connect <strong class=\"key-phrase\">possessions</strong> with who we are. We compare ourselves with more people than before, and teenagers may feel pressure to wear certain brands. That can be hard for families with less money. Still, some people choose to ignore this competition and focus on what they actually need."},{"text":"Yes. People owned less and often repaired things because new goods were expensive and borrowing money was harder. My grandfather remembers repairing his shoes instead of replacing them. Advertising was also less constant. People still cared about possessions, but buying newer versions happened more slowly than it often does now.","phrases":[{"en":"replacing","meaning":"Getting something new to take the place of an older item."}],"question":"Do you think things were different 50 years ago?","html":"Yes. People owned less and often repaired things because new goods were expensive and borrowing money was harder. My grandfather remembers repairing his shoes instead of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">replacing</strong> them. Advertising was also less constant. People still cared about possessions, but buying newer versions happened more slowly than it often does now."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-026-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-026-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-026-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Valuable possessions and changing attitudes to keeping things","prompt":"What are the valuable personal possessions in your country?\nHow has your attitude towards keeping personal possessions changed?\nWhy do you think people keep valuable things?","answer":"Q: What are the valuable personal possessions in your country?\nA: Property's by far the most valuable thing most families own — a house or a piece of land usually represents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a lifetime's savings</strong>. Beyond that, gold and jewellery are traditionally important, partly because older generations trust them more than banks, and they're often given as wedding gifts. Vehicles matter too, and increasingly phones and laptops, though those are valued for what they let you do rather than as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">assets in themselves</strong>. That said, younger people often rate a good laptop above gold, so what counts as valuable is starting to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">split along generational lines</strong>.\n\nQ: How has your attitude towards keeping personal possessions changed?\nA: I've become far more selective. When I was younger I kept almost everything, partly on the assumption that it might be useful one day and partly because throwing things away felt wasteful. What changed my mind was moving house a few times and realising how much I was carrying around that I'd never even touched. Now I keep two categories only: things I actually use, and things that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carry real personal meaning</strong>, like photographs or gifts from family. I'll admit I still break my own rule occasionally — I've kept a jacket I never wear simply because I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">can't quite part with it</strong>.\n\nQ: Why do you think people keep valuable things?\nA: Several reasons, and they're quite different from one another. Some people keep valuables purely as an investment, since gold and property hold their worth when currency doesn't. Others keep <strong class=\"key-phrase\">family heirlooms</strong>, where the point isn't the price at all but the connection to a grandparent or a particular period of family history — my mother still has her mother's wedding ring and would never sell it whatever it was worth. And then there's status: an expensive watch signals success to other people. I'd say the emotional and financial motives are strongest and they often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overlap</strong>, though buying purely for status, things you'll never actually use, is a less healthy version of the same instinct.","topic":"Consumerism & Possessions","phrases":[{"en":"a lifetime's savings","context":"Property's by far the most valuable thing most families own — a house or a piece of land usually represents a lifetime's savings."},{"en":"assets in themselves","context":"Vehicles matter too, and increasingly phones and laptops, though those are valued for what they let you do rather than as assets in themselves."},{"en":"split along generational lines","context":"That said, younger people often rate a good laptop above gold, so what counts as valuable is starting to split along generational lines."},{"en":"carry real personal meaning","context":"Now I keep two categories only: things I actually use, and things that carry real personal meaning, like photographs or gifts from family."},{"en":"can't quite part with it","context":"I'll admit I still break my own rule occasionally — I've kept a jacket I never wear simply because I can't quite part with it."},{"en":"family heirlooms","context":"Others keep family heirlooms, where the point isn't the price at all but the connection to a grandparent or a particular period of family history — my mother still has her mother's wedding ring and would never sell it whatever it was worth."},{"en":"overlap","context":"I'd say the emotional and financial motives are strongest and they often overlap, though buying purely for status, things you'll never actually use, is a less healthy version of the same instinct."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Consumerism & Possessions","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-027","_displayNo":"#027","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-027-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-027-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-027-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"For most families, a home or land is the most valuable possession. Gold and jewellery also matter, often as savings or wedding gifts. Vehicles, phones and laptops are useful too. Younger people may value a good computer more than gold because of the work it allows them to do, so priorities differ between generations.","phrases":[{"en":"priorities","meaning":"The things people consider most important."}],"question":"What are the valuable personal possessions in your country?","html":"For most families, a home or land is the most valuable possession. Gold and jewellery also matter, often as savings or wedding gifts. Vehicles, phones and laptops are useful too. Younger people may value a good computer more than gold because of the work it allows them to do, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">priorities</strong> differ between generations."},{"text":"I keep fewer things now. When I was younger, I saved almost everything in case it became useful. Moving house showed me how much I never used. Now I mainly keep useful items and things with personal meaning, such as family gifts. I am not perfect, though; I still have an old jacket I cannot quite give away.","phrases":[{"en":"personal meaning","meaning":"Importance connected with your own memories or feelings."}],"question":"How has your attitude towards keeping personal possessions changed?","html":"I keep fewer things now. When I was younger, I saved almost everything in case it became useful. Moving house showed me how much I never used. Now I mainly keep useful items and things with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">personal meaning</strong>, such as family gifts. I am not perfect, though; I still have an old jacket I cannot quite give away."},{"text":"People may keep valuable things as savings, as family memories or to show success. A family ring, for example, can matter because of who owned it rather than its price. These emotional and financial reasons often overlap. Buying things only to impress others seems less useful to me, especially if the owner never actually uses them.","phrases":[{"en":"overlap","meaning":"Be partly connected or have something in common."}],"question":"Why do you think people keep valuable things?","html":"People may keep valuable things as savings, as family memories or to show success. A family ring, for example, can matter because of who owned it rather than its price. These emotional and financial reasons often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overlap</strong>. Buying things only to impress others seems less useful to me, especially if the owner never actually uses them."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-027-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-027-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-027-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"What shows status now and in your grandparents' time","prompt":"What kinds of possessions show status in your country?\nDo you think it was different for your grandparents?\nModern society is often called 'materialistic'. Why do you think this is?","answer":"Q: What kinds of possessions show status in your country?\nA: Property first, then cars, then branded clothing, watches and the latest phone. What's interesting is that the signal only works because everyone agrees it does — an expensive watch keeps time no better than a cheap one, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the entire value is social</strong>. The specific items shift quite quickly, too: something that signalled success ten years ago can look dated now, which is precisely what keeps people buying replacements they don't really need. That's starting to shift a little, though — plenty of younger buyers now signal status through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">experiences instead of objects</strong>, a good holiday rather than a good watch.\n\nQ: Do you think it was different for your grandparents?\nA: Yes, considerably. Their generation valued things that lasted — land, gold, solid furniture — partly because goods were genuinely scarce and partly because owning something durable meant security. Status came from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what you had accumulated over a lifetime</strong>. Now it's much more visible and much faster-moving, largely because social media displays everyone's purchases continuously: my grandparents' neighbours knew what they owned, and that was the whole audience, whereas <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the audience is effectively unlimited</strong> now. I wouldn't call their era better exactly, though — they also had far less choice and far less means of fixing a bad purchase once they'd made it.\n\nQ: Modern society is often called 'materialistic'. Why do you think this is?\nA: Partly because economies now depend on continuous consumption — if people stopped replacing things, a huge amount of employment would disappear, so there's enormous pressure to keep buying. Advertising then does the work of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">linking products with identity</strong>, so you're not purchasing a phone but a version of yourself. Comparison has widened, too: people once measured themselves against neighbours, and now it's against everyone they follow online. A minority of people do consciously <strong class=\"key-phrase\">resist that pressure</strong>, though, and minimalism as a lifestyle has grown partly in response to it.","topic":"Consumerism & Possessions","phrases":[{"en":"the entire value is social","context":"What's interesting is that the signal only works because everyone agrees it does — an expensive watch keeps time no better than a cheap one, so the entire value is social."},{"en":"experiences instead of objects","context":"That's starting to shift a little, though — plenty of younger buyers now signal status through experiences instead of objects, a good holiday rather than a good watch."},{"en":"what you had accumulated over a lifetime","context":"Status came from what you had accumulated over a lifetime."},{"en":"the audience is effectively unlimited","context":"Now it's much more visible and much faster-moving, largely because social media displays everyone's purchases continuously: my grandparents' neighbours knew what they owned, and that was the whole audience, whereas the audience is effectively unlimited now."},{"en":"linking products with identity","context":"Advertising then does the work of linking products with identity, so you're not purchasing a phone but a version of yourself."},{"en":"resist that pressure","context":"A minority of people do consciously resist that pressure, though, and minimalism as a lifestyle has grown partly in response to it."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Consumerism & Possessions","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-028","_displayNo":"#028","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-028-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-028-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-028-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Homes, cars, expensive watches, branded clothes and new phones can all show status. They work only because other people recognise them as signs of success. A costly watch may not tell the time better, for example. These signals change quickly, and some younger people now prefer showing experiences, such as holidays, rather than possessions.","phrases":[{"en":"branded clothes","meaning":"Clothing sold under a recognised company or designer name."}],"question":"What kinds of possessions show status in your country?","html":"Homes, cars, expensive watches, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">branded clothes</strong> and new phones can all show status. They work only because other people recognise them as signs of success. A costly watch may not tell the time better, for example. These signals change quickly, and some younger people now prefer showing experiences, such as holidays, rather than possessions."},{"text":"Yes. My grandparents valued things that lasted, such as land, gold and strong furniture, because possessions were harder to obtain and offered security. Today, people replace things faster and show purchases online to a much wider audience. Their generation had less pressure of that kind, but also fewer choices, so I would not say everything was better.","phrases":[{"en":"security","meaning":"A feeling of safety and protection from future difficulties."}],"question":"Do you think it was different for your grandparents?","html":"Yes. My grandparents valued things that lasted, such as land, gold and strong furniture, because possessions were harder to obtain and offered <strong class=\"key-phrase\">security</strong>. Today, people replace things faster and show purchases online to a much wider audience. Their generation had less pressure of that kind, but also fewer choices, so I would not say everything was better."},{"text":"Modern economies depend on people continuing to buy things, so businesses strongly encourage spending. Adverts link products with identity, while social media lets us compare ourselves with many others. This can make possessions seem unusually important. However, some people resist by owning less and choosing carefully, so not everyone accepts the same values.","phrases":[{"en":"identity","meaning":"The qualities and ideas that form who a person is."}],"question":"Modern society is often called 'materialistic'. Why do you think this is?","html":"Modern economies depend on people continuing to buy things, so businesses strongly encourage spending. Adverts link products with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">identity</strong>, while social media lets us compare ourselves with many others. This can make possessions seem unusually important. However, some people resist by owning less and choosing carefully, so not everyone accepts the same values."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-028-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-028-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-028-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"The advantages of country living, and how it has changed","prompt":"What advantages does living in the countryside have, compared to living in a city?\nHave areas of countryside in your country changed since you were a child?\nDo you think that visits to the countryside should be something that schools offer to their children?","answer":"Q: What advantages does living in the countryside have, compared to living in a city?\nA: I think there are some clear advantages to living in the countryside. With the clean air and less traffic or industry, the countryside's generally a much healthier place to live than the average city, and there's a stronger sense of community too — in a small village everyone tends to know everyone else, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the pace of life</strong> is slower, with people more laid back and friendly, compared to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the hustle and bustle</strong> of city life. It's not without its downsides, though: jobs are scarcer, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">basic services</strong> like hospitals or good schools are often a long drive away.\n\nQ: Have areas of countryside in your country changed since you were a child?\nA: Less than I expected, in the area I know best, because a good deal of it's protected from development — the hills, footpaths and woodland are still pretty much as I remember them. What has changed is the infrastructure around the villages: better roads, a good many more cafes and guesthouses, and considerably more visitors at weekends than there used to be. That's brought welcome income to places that were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">losing their young people</strong>, though a few villages now feel arranged for tourists rather than actually lived in.\n\nQ: Do you think that visits to the countryside should be something that schools offer to their children?\nA: Yes, I think it's a great idea for schools to take children on trips to the countryside, especially if those children live in a large town or city. A visit like that can be both relaxing and educational — children get to walk in the fresh air, and see animals and plants they might never have come across otherwise. If schools don't offer this, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">some children will never see life outside the city</strong>. The main obstacle is usually cost, though: a proper residential trip isn't cheap, and schools in poorer areas often can't manage more than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a single day out</strong> a year.","topic":"Countryside","phrases":[{"en":"the pace of life","context":"With the clean air and less traffic or industry, the countryside's generally a much healthier place to live than the average city, and there's a stronger sense of community too — in a small village everyone tends to know everyone else, and the pace of life is slower, with people more laid back an..."},{"en":"the hustle and bustle","context":"With the clean air and less traffic or industry, the countryside's generally a much healthier place to live than the average city, and there's a stronger sense of community too — in a small village everyone tends to know everyone else, and the pace of life is slower, with people more laid back an..."},{"en":"basic services","context":"It's not without its downsides, though: jobs are scarcer, and basic services like hospitals or good schools are often a long drive away."},{"en":"losing their young people","context":"That's brought welcome income to places that were losing their young people, though a few villages now feel arranged for tourists rather than actually lived in."},{"en":"some children will never see life outside the city","context":"If schools don't offer this, some children will never see life outside the city."},{"en":"a single day out","context":"The main obstacle is usually cost, though: a proper residential trip isn't cheap, and schools in poorer areas often can't manage more than a single day out a year."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Countryside","id":"speaking-part-3-029","_displayNo":"#029","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-029-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-029-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-029-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"The countryside usually offers cleaner air, less traffic and a slower way of life. In small villages, neighbours often know each other, which can create a friendly community. However, it also has disadvantages. Jobs may be harder to find, and good schools or hospitals can be far away, so the benefits depend on a person's needs.","phrases":[{"en":"way of life","meaning":"The usual habits and conditions in which people live."}],"question":"What advantages does living in the countryside have, compared to living in a city?","html":"The countryside usually offers cleaner air, less traffic and a slower <strong class=\"key-phrase\">way of life</strong>. In small villages, neighbours often know each other, which can create a friendly community. However, it also has disadvantages. Jobs may be harder to find, and good schools or hospitals can be far away, so the benefits depend on a person&#x27;s needs."},{"text":"The protected hills, woods and paths I know have changed less than I expected. However, villages now have better roads, more cafes and guesthouses, and more weekend visitors. Tourism provides useful income, but some villages feel designed mainly for visitors rather than local families. So the landscape may stay familiar while daily life changes around it.","phrases":[{"en":"landscape","meaning":"The natural features and appearance of an area."}],"question":"Have areas of countryside in your country changed since you were a child?","html":"The protected hills, woods and paths I know have changed less than I expected. However, villages now have better roads, more cafes and guesthouses, and more weekend visitors. Tourism provides useful income, but some villages feel designed mainly for visitors rather than local families. So the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">landscape</strong> may stay familiar while daily life changes around it."},{"text":"Yes, especially for children who mainly know city life. They can exercise outdoors and learn about animals and plants directly. Some might never experience the countryside otherwise. The difficulty is cost, particularly for longer trips. Schools with limited money may only manage a short visit, so the opportunity is not equally available to everyone.","phrases":[{"en":"directly","meaning":"Through personal experience rather than through someone else's description."}],"question":"Do you think that visits to the countryside should be something that schools offer to their children?","html":"Yes, especially for children who mainly know city life. They can exercise outdoors and learn about animals and plants <strong class=\"key-phrase\">directly</strong>. Some might never experience the countryside otherwise. The difficulty is cost, particularly for longer trips. Schools with limited money may only manage a short visit, so the opportunity is not equally available to everyone."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-029-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-029-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-029-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Customer service and how companies could improve it","prompt":"How important is customer service for you?\nWhat can companies do to improve their customer service?\nWhy do you think employees sometimes fail to provide good customer service?","answer":"Q: How important is customer service for you?\nA: Customer service is really important for me because I like to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel welcome and valued</strong> as a customer. If companies want us to use their products or services, I believe they should treat us well and therefore encourage us to return. For example, I have a favourite cafe where I like to go, and the friendly staff are the main reason I've become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a loyal customer</strong>. Price matters, obviously, but I'll pay slightly more to be treated well.\n\nQ: What can companies do to improve their customer service?\nA: I think managers need to train their employees to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deliver great customer service</strong>, because good intentions alone don't achieve much without real skills behind them. There are lots of things staff can do, like greeting customers properly, handling problems quickly, and making sure they're <strong class=\"key-phrase\">asked for feedback</strong>. I called my phone provider recently with a billing problem, and the difference was obvious: the first person I spoke to just read from a script, while the second actually solved it in five minutes because she took real ownership of the problem instead of passing it on. Training only goes so far, though, if staff are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stretched too thin</strong> — even the best-trained call-handler can't do much when they're rushing through fifty calls an hour.\n\nQ: Why do you think employees sometimes fail to provide good customer service?\nA: When employees' treatment of customers isn't good, it's usually because they're unhappy doing the job itself. That might be because they're treated badly by their own managers, or because they've never been encouraged to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take pride in</strong> their work. I remember my first ever job — I didn't enjoy the work I'd been given, and I probably wasn't as thoughtful or attentive as I should've been when I had to deal with customers. It isn't always about the person, though; sometimes it really is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">just one bad day</strong>, not a sign of how they usually work.","topic":"Customer Service","phrases":[{"en":"feel welcome and valued","context":"Customer service is really important for me because I like to feel welcome and valued as a customer."},{"en":"a loyal customer","context":"For example, I have a favourite cafe where I like to go, and the friendly staff are the main reason I've become a loyal customer."},{"en":"deliver great customer service","context":"I think managers need to train their employees to deliver great customer service, because good intentions alone don't achieve much without real skills behind them."},{"en":"asked for feedback","context":"There are lots of things staff can do, like greeting customers properly, handling problems quickly, and making sure they're asked for feedback."},{"en":"stretched too thin","context":"Training only goes so far, though, if staff are stretched too thin — even the best-trained call-handler can't do much when they're rushing through fifty calls an hour."},{"en":"take pride in","context":"That might be because they're treated badly by their own managers, or because they've never been encouraged to take pride in their work."},{"en":"just one bad day","context":"It isn't always about the person, though; sometimes it really is just one bad day, not a sign of how they usually work."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Customer Service","id":"speaking-part-3-030","_displayNo":"#030","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-030-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-030-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-030-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"It matters a lot because I want to feel welcome and respected. Friendly service makes me more likely to return. For example, I regularly visit one cafe largely because of its staff. Price still matters, but I am willing to pay a little more when I know I will be treated well.","phrases":[{"en":"willing to","meaning":"Ready or happy to do something."}],"question":"How important is customer service for you?","html":"It matters a lot because I want to feel welcome and respected. Friendly service makes me more likely to return. For example, I regularly visit one cafe largely because of its staff. Price still matters, but I am <strong class=\"key-phrase\">willing to</strong> pay a little more when I know I will be treated well."},{"text":"Companies should train staff to welcome people, listen and solve problems. Employees also need enough freedom to take responsibility rather than pass a complaint around. When I called about a bill, one worker solved it quickly while another only followed a script. However, training cannot fix everything if there are too few staff and everyone is rushed.","phrases":[{"en":"take responsibility","meaning":"Accept the job of dealing with something properly."}],"question":"What can companies do to improve their customer service?","html":"Companies should train staff to welcome people, listen and solve problems. Employees also need enough freedom to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take responsibility</strong> rather than pass a complaint around. When I called about a bill, one worker solved it quickly while another only followed a script. However, training cannot fix everything if there are too few staff and everyone is rushed."},{"text":"Sometimes employees dislike their work because managers treat them badly or give them little support. That can affect how carefully they deal with customers. I was less attentive in my first job because I did not enjoy it. Still, poor service does not always show a permanent attitude; someone may simply be having a difficult day.","phrases":[{"en":"attentive","meaning":"Paying careful attention to people's needs."}],"question":"Why do you think employees sometimes fail to provide good customer service?","html":"Sometimes employees dislike their work because managers treat them badly or give them little support. That can affect how carefully they deal with customers. I was less <strong class=\"key-phrase\">attentive</strong> in my first job because I did not enjoy it. Still, poor service does not always show a permanent attitude; someone may simply be having a difficult day."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-030-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-030-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-030-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Popular dances and attitudes to dancing","prompt":"What dances are popular in your country?\nWhat is the general attitude towards dancing in your country?\nDo young people dance any differently from 10 years ago?","answer":"Q: What dances are popular in your country?\nA: There's quite a range, and it splits fairly clearly by age. Traditional dances still appear at festivals and weddings, and older people take them seriously as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">part of cultural heritage</strong>. Among young people, styles picked up from social media dominate — K-pop choreography especially, which spread incredibly fast because anyone can learn it from a video. Then there's dance purely as exercise: Zumba and similar classes are popular with adults who'd never call themselves dancers at all. The traditional forms are the ones losing ground, though — fewer young people are learning them properly, and a couple are kept alive mainly by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">festival performances</strong> rather than everyday practice.\n\nQ: What is the general attitude towards dancing in your country?\nA: It's become noticeably more relaxed. Most people now see dancing as either entertainment or a form of exercise, and dance classes are common in cities. There's still some shyness about dancing in public, especially among men of my parents' generation, who'd feel <strong class=\"key-phrase\">embarrassed doing it in front of others</strong>. Among younger people that's largely disappeared, partly because posting dance videos online has made being seen doing it completely normal. That relaxed attitude is really a city thing, though; in smaller towns <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the old self-consciousness</strong> hasn't shifted nearly as much.\n\nQ: Do young people dance any differently from 10 years ago?\nA: Yes, in two ways. First, they learn from global platforms rather than from family or local teachers, so the styles are far more international and they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mix influences freely</strong>. Second, a lot of dancing's now done for the camera rather than for a room of people, which changes the movements — routines are shorter, more repetitive, and designed to look good in a fifteen-second clip. Something's gained in creativity, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the link to local tradition</strong> has weakened, and that's a genuine loss, not just a stylistic shift.","topic":"Dance","phrases":[{"en":"part of cultural heritage","context":"Traditional dances still appear at festivals and weddings, and older people take them seriously as part of cultural heritage."},{"en":"festival performances","context":"The traditional forms are the ones losing ground, though — fewer young people are learning them properly, and a couple are kept alive mainly by festival performances rather than everyday practice."},{"en":"embarrassed doing it in front of others","context":"There's still some shyness about dancing in public, especially among men of my parents' generation, who'd feel embarrassed doing it in front of others."},{"en":"the old self-consciousness","context":"That relaxed attitude is really a city thing, though; in smaller towns the old self-consciousness hasn't shifted nearly as much."},{"en":"mix influences freely","context":"First, they learn from global platforms rather than from family or local teachers, so the styles are far more international and they mix influences freely."},{"en":"the link to local tradition","context":"Something's gained in creativity, but the link to local tradition has weakened, and that's a genuine loss, not just a stylistic shift."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Dance","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-031","_displayNo":"#031","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-031-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-031-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-031-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Traditional dances appear at weddings and festivals, while younger people often learn international styles such as K-pop online. Adults also attend dance exercise classes like Zumba. Traditional forms seem less secure because fewer young people learn them fully. Some now survive mainly through festival performances rather than being part of ordinary community life.","phrases":[{"en":"Traditional forms","meaning":"Styles passed down through a culture over time."}],"question":"What dances are popular in your country?","html":"Traditional dances appear at weddings and festivals, while younger people often learn international styles such as K-pop online. Adults also attend dance exercise classes like Zumba. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Traditional forms</strong> seem less secure because fewer young people learn them fully. Some now survive mainly through festival performances rather than being part of ordinary community life."},{"text":"People seem more relaxed about dancing now, particularly in cities. They see it as entertainment or exercise, and classes are common. Some older people still feel embarrassed dancing in public. Younger people are often more comfortable because online dance videos have made it familiar, although smaller towns may remain more reserved.","phrases":[{"en":"reserved","meaning":"Not showing feelings or behaviour very openly."}],"question":"What is the general attitude towards dancing in your country?","html":"People seem more relaxed about dancing now, particularly in cities. They see it as entertainment or exercise, and classes are common. Some older people still feel embarrassed dancing in public. Younger people are often more comfortable because online dance videos have made it familiar, although smaller towns may remain more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reserved</strong>."},{"text":"Yes. Young people now learn styles from around the world through online videos, rather than mainly from local teachers. Many routines are also designed for short recordings, so movements are brief and repeated. This can encourage creativity, but it may weaken the connection to local dance traditions. I think there are gains and losses.","phrases":[{"en":"routines","meaning":"Planned sequences of movements in a dance."}],"question":"Do young people dance any differently from 10 years ago?","html":"Yes. Young people now learn styles from around the world through online videos, rather than mainly from local teachers. Many <strong class=\"key-phrase\">routines</strong> are also designed for short recordings, so movements are brief and repeated. This can encourage creativity, but it may weaken the connection to local dance traditions. I think there are gains and losses."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-031-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-031-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-031-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Choices at different stages of life","prompt":"What are the typical choices people make at different stages of their lives?\nShould important choices be made by parents rather than by young adults?\nWhy do some people like to discuss choices with other people?","answer":"Q: What are the typical choices people make at different stages of their lives?\nA: They follow a fairly predictable sequence. Children choose their friends and how much effort to put into school, which seems minor but shapes a lot more than people realise. Young adults face the biggest decisions — what to study, which career to enter, whether to move away — and they make these with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the least experience</strong> of anyone. Later choices, around housing, children and retirement, are financially bigger but usually much better informed, simply because by then people have some sense of how their own judgement performs. That's not a fixed rule, though: some people are still making decisions <strong class=\"key-phrase\">just as badly informed</strong> at fifty as they were at twenty.\n\nQ: Should important choices be made by parents rather than by young adults?\nA: Parents should advise, and their experience is genuinely valuable, but the final decision has to belong to the young person. There's a practical reason for this: someone pushed into a career they didn't choose tends to resent it — I know someone who did medicine because his parents insisted, and he's spent a decade being competent at a job he never wanted. Making a decision and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">living with the consequences</strong> is how judgement develops, and without that you get adults who've never really practised deciding anything. The exception is anything genuinely dangerous or irreversible, where <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a parent's veto</strong> is reasonable whatever the teenager says.\n\nQ: Why do some people like to discuss choices with other people?\nA: Partly because talking forces you to actually articulate the reasoning, and you often notice a flaw the moment you say it out loud. Another person also brings options you hadn't considered and questions <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the assumptions you'd stopped noticing</strong> — a friend once pointed out I was choosing a flat based purely on rent, having completely forgotten how long the commute would be. There's an emotional element too, since a decision feels less frightening once someone else has heard it. The risk is asking people who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have a stake in the outcome</strong>, because their advice will quietly serve their own interests rather than yours.","topic":"Decisions","phrases":[{"en":"the least experience","context":"Young adults face the biggest decisions — what to study, which career to enter, whether to move away — and they make these with the least experience of anyone."},{"en":"just as badly informed","context":"That's not a fixed rule, though: some people are still making decisions just as badly informed at fifty as they were at twenty."},{"en":"living with the consequences","context":"Making a decision and living with the consequences is how judgement develops, and without that you get adults who've never really practised deciding anything."},{"en":"a parent's veto","context":"The exception is anything genuinely dangerous or irreversible, where a parent's veto is reasonable whatever the teenager says."},{"en":"the assumptions you'd stopped noticing","context":"Another person also brings options you hadn't considered and questions the assumptions you'd stopped noticing — a friend once pointed out I was choosing a flat based purely on rent, having completely forgotten how long the commute would be."},{"en":"have a stake in the outcome","context":"The risk is asking people who have a stake in the outcome, because their advice will quietly serve their own interests rather than yours."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Decisions","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-032","_displayNo":"#032","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-032-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-032-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-032-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Children choose friends and how much effort to give school. Young adults make major choices about education, careers and moving away. Later, people decide about homes, children and retirement. Older adults often have more experience, but that does not guarantee better judgement. The order also varies, so not everyone follows exactly the same life path.","phrases":[{"en":"life path","meaning":"The course a person's life takes through choices and events."}],"question":"What are the typical choices people make at different stages of their lives?","html":"Children choose friends and how much effort to give school. Young adults make major choices about education, careers and moving away. Later, people decide about homes, children and retirement. Older adults often have more experience, but that does not guarantee better judgement. The order also varies, so not everyone follows exactly the same <strong class=\"key-phrase\">life path</strong>."},{"text":"Parents should offer advice, but young adults usually need to make the final choice. Being forced into a career can create lasting unhappiness. Deciding for yourself also develops judgement. However, parents should still step in when a choice is seriously dangerous or cannot easily be reversed, rather than leave a young person without protection.","phrases":[{"en":"reversed","meaning":"Changed back or undone."}],"question":"Should important choices be made by parents rather than by young adults?","html":"Parents should offer advice, but young adults usually need to make the final choice. Being forced into a career can create lasting unhappiness. Deciding for yourself also develops judgement. However, parents should still step in when a choice is seriously dangerous or cannot easily be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reversed</strong>, rather than leave a young person without protection."},{"text":"Talking helps people explain their reasoning and notice mistakes. Someone else may suggest an option they missed; a friend once reminded me to consider travel time, not just a flat's rent. Discussion can also make a decision feel less frightening. However, advice may be biased if the other person benefits from a particular result.","phrases":[{"en":"biased","meaning":"Unfairly influenced by one person's interests or preferences."}],"question":"Why do some people like to discuss choices with other people?","html":"Talking helps people explain their reasoning and notice mistakes. Someone else may suggest an option they missed; a friend once reminded me to consider travel time, not just a flat&#x27;s rent. Discussion can also make a decision feel less frightening. However, advice may be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">biased</strong> if the other person benefits from a particular result."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-032-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-032-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-032-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Why decisions matter and how to make good ones","prompt":"Why is decision-making important in our lives?\nHow can we make good decisions?\nDo you think parents should allow their children to make their own decisions?","answer":"Q: Why is decision-making important in our lives?\nA: Because the shape of your life is essentially <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the accumulation of decisions</strong> — what you study, where you work, who you spend time with, how you look after your health. Individually most of them seem small, but they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compound over decades</strong>; someone who exercises three times a week for twenty years ends up in a completely different place physically from someone who always meant to start next month. Good decision-making doesn't guarantee a good outcome, since luck plays an enormous part, but it improves the odds and reduces the particular kind of regret that comes from never having thought about it at all.\n\nQ: How can we make good decisions?\nA: A few things help consistently. Being clear about what you're actually trying to achieve, because a lot of bad decisions come from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">answering the wrong question</strong> — optimising for the cheapest option when what you actually wanted was reliability, for example. Gathering information from sources that don't all agree with each other helps too, and so does deliberately asking what could go wrong, uncomfortable as that is. For anything major I'd add two more: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sleep on it</strong>, and ask someone with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no stake in the outcome</strong>. None of this removes risk entirely, though — even a carefully made decision can still turn out badly.\n\nQ: Do you think parents should allow their children to make their own decisions?\nA: Yes, but gradually, with the stakes matched to the age. A young child can choose their own clothes or how to spend their pocket money — if the choice is a poor one, the cost is small and the lesson's real. A teenager can choose their subjects and manage their own time. What parents shouldn't do is either decide everything, which produces adults who've never practised deciding anything, or step back entirely on things that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely cannot be undone</strong> — a tattoo, say, or dropping out of school at sixteen.","topic":"Decisions","phrases":[{"en":"the accumulation of decisions","context":"Because the shape of your life is essentially the accumulation of decisions — what you study, where you work, who you spend time with, how you look after your health."},{"en":"compound over decades","context":"Individually most of them seem small, but they compound over decades; someone who exercises three times a week for twenty years ends up in a completely different place physically from someone who always meant to start next month."},{"en":"answering the wrong question","context":"Being clear about what you're actually trying to achieve, because a lot of bad decisions come from answering the wrong question — optimising for the cheapest option when what you actually wanted was reliability, for example."},{"en":"sleep on it","context":"For anything major I'd add two more: sleep on it, and ask someone with no stake in the outcome."},{"en":"no stake in the outcome","context":"For anything major I'd add two more: sleep on it, and ask someone with no stake in the outcome."},{"en":"genuinely cannot be undone","context":"What parents shouldn't do is either decide everything, which produces adults who've never practised deciding anything, or step back entirely on things that genuinely cannot be undone — a tattoo, say, or dropping out of school at sixteen."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Decisions","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-033","_displayNo":"#033","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-033-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-033-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-033-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Our lives develop through many decisions about work, relationships and health. Small choices can have large effects when repeated for years, such as exercising regularly. Making good decisions cannot remove bad luck, but it improves our chances. It also helps avoid the regret of realising we never properly considered something important.","phrases":[{"en":"regret","meaning":"Sadness about a choice or action you wish had been different."}],"question":"Why is decision-making important in our lives?","html":"Our lives develop through many decisions about work, relationships and health. Small choices can have large effects when repeated for years, such as exercising regularly. Making good decisions cannot remove bad luck, but it improves our chances. It also helps avoid the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regret</strong> of realising we never properly considered something important."},{"text":"First, be clear about your goal. Then gather information from different sources and consider what could go wrong. For an important choice, it helps to wait a night and ask someone who has no personal interest in the result. These steps do not guarantee success, but they make the decision better informed.","phrases":[{"en":"better informed","meaning":"Based on more useful or reliable knowledge."}],"question":"How can we make good decisions?","html":"First, be clear about your goal. Then gather information from different sources and consider what could go wrong. For an important choice, it helps to wait a night and ask someone who has no personal interest in the result. These steps do not guarantee success, but they make the decision <strong class=\"key-phrase\">better informed</strong>."},{"text":"Yes, with more freedom as they grow. A small child can choose clothes or spend pocket money, where mistakes are not very costly. Teenagers can make larger choices about study and time. Parents should neither control everything nor ignore serious risks. The level of responsibility needs to match the child's age and ability.","phrases":[{"en":"pocket money","meaning":"A small amount of money regularly given to a child to spend."}],"question":"Do you think parents should allow their children to make their own decisions?","html":"Yes, with more freedom as they grow. A small child can choose clothes or spend <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pocket money</strong>, where mistakes are not very costly. Teenagers can make larger choices about study and time. Parents should neither control everything nor ignore serious risks. The level of responsibility needs to match the child&#x27;s age and ability."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-033-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-033-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-033-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"What dreams tell us and whether to interpret them","prompt":"What can dreams tell us about ourselves?\nWhy are dreams considered important in many cultures?\nDo you think it is helpful to interpret someone's dreams for them?","answer":"Q: What can dreams tell us about ourselves?\nA: Something, but less than people would like. Dreams clearly draw on recent experience — you dream about whatever you've been worrying about, so they can point you towards a concern you haven't consciously admitted to yourself. I once had the same anxiety dream about missing a flight every night for a week before a big exam, which wasn't exactly subtle. What I'm sceptical about is the idea of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fixed symbols</strong>, where a particular object always means one thing — there's no real evidence for that. That said, I wouldn't dismiss dreams completely; one that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeps coming back</strong> is at least worth paying attention to.\n\nQ: Why are dreams considered important in many cultures?\nA: Because they feel significant. A dream is vivid, emotionally intense and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completely outside your control</strong>, so it's natural to assume it came from somewhere else rather than from your own mind. Historically, many cultures treated dreams as messages from ancestors or warnings about the future, and important decisions, going to war, choosing a leader, were sometimes taken on that basis. They also served a social function, giving communities a shared language for fears that were difficult to state directly. Modern psychology takes a far more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">modest view</strong> of what dreams can actually tell us, but the emotional pull hasn't gone away just because the science caught up.\n\nQ: Do you think it is helpful to interpret someone's dreams for them?\nA: It can be, but it depends entirely on how it's done. If it opens a conversation about what someone's anxious about, that's useful, and the dream's simply the way in. What I'd avoid is presenting an interpretation as fact, because telling somebody confidently what their dream means can create <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a false certainty</strong> they then act on — deciding to leave a job because a dream book said water means change, say. A therapist explores it together with the person; <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fixed reading from a book</strong> does the opposite.","topic":"Dreams","phrases":[{"en":"fixed symbols","context":"What I'm sceptical about is the idea of fixed symbols, where a particular object always means one thing — there's no real evidence for that."},{"en":"keeps coming back","context":"That said, I wouldn't dismiss dreams completely; one that keeps coming back is at least worth paying attention to."},{"en":"completely outside your control","context":"A dream is vivid, emotionally intense and completely outside your control, so it's natural to assume it came from somewhere else rather than from your own mind."},{"en":"modest view","context":"Modern psychology takes a far more modest view of what dreams can actually tell us, but the emotional pull hasn't gone away just because the science caught up."},{"en":"a false certainty","context":"What I'd avoid is presenting an interpretation as fact, because telling somebody confidently what their dream means can create a false certainty they then act on — deciding to leave a job because a dream book said water means change, say."},{"en":"a fixed reading from a book","context":"A therapist explores it together with the person; a fixed reading from a book does the opposite."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Dreams","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-034","_displayNo":"#034","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-034-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-034-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-034-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Dreams may show what we have recently experienced or worried about. Before an exam, for example, I repeatedly dreamed about missing a flight. That suggested anxiety rather than a secret prediction. I don't think every object in a dream has one fixed meaning, but a repeated dream may still encourage us to consider our feelings.","phrases":[{"en":"anxiety","meaning":"A feeling of worry or nervousness."}],"question":"What can dreams tell us about ourselves?","html":"Dreams may show what we have recently experienced or worried about. Before an exam, for example, I repeatedly dreamed about missing a flight. That suggested <strong class=\"key-phrase\">anxiety</strong> rather than a secret prediction. I don&#x27;t think every object in a dream has one fixed meaning, but a repeated dream may still encourage us to consider our feelings."},{"text":"Dreams can feel very real and emotional, so people may think they have a special source or message. Many cultures have treated them as warnings or messages from ancestors. They can also give people a way to discuss fears. Modern explanations are usually more cautious, but the strong feelings that dreams create still matter to people.","phrases":[{"en":"ancestors","meaning":"Family members or people from earlier generations."}],"question":"Why are dreams considered important in many cultures?","html":"Dreams can feel very real and emotional, so people may think they have a special source or message. Many cultures have treated them as warnings or messages from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ancestors</strong>. They can also give people a way to discuss fears. Modern explanations are usually more cautious, but the strong feelings that dreams create still matter to people."},{"text":"It can help if it starts a conversation about the person's feelings or worries. However, I would not claim that one interpretation is certainly true. Someone might make an important decision based on a meaning that is only a guess. Exploring possibilities together is safer than telling them a dream has a single fixed message.","phrases":[{"en":"interpretation","meaning":"One possible explanation of what something means."}],"question":"Do you think it is helpful to interpret someone's dreams for them?","html":"It can help if it starts a conversation about the person&#x27;s feelings or worries. However, I would not claim that one <strong class=\"key-phrase\">interpretation</strong> is certainly true. Someone might make an important decision based on a meaning that is only a guess. Exploring possibilities together is safer than telling them a dream has a single fixed message."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-034-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-034-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-034-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"What makes a good lesson, and teaching yourself","prompt":"What do you think makes a good lesson?\nDo you think it is better to have a teacher or to teach yourself?\nDo you think the traditional classroom will disappear in the future?","answer":"Q: What do you think makes a good lesson?\nA: I think a good lesson is one that's interesting and engaging. By 'engaging' I mean the students should feel actually involved, not just present — they should feel they're learning something <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relevant to them</strong>. A lot depends on how the teacher <strong class=\"key-phrase\">delivers the content</strong>, too, in a way students actually respond to. My favourite teacher at university used to get us to teach parts of the lessons ourselves, which worked brilliantly for confident students, though it left the quieter students visibly uncomfortable.\n\nQ: Do you think it is better to have a teacher or to teach yourself?\nA: Well, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">there's no substitute for</strong> a good teacher, in most cases. You can teach yourself, but you'll usually learn a lot faster <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with the guidance of a teacher</strong> who can see your mistakes as you make them. When you're learning a language, for instance, you really need someone to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">correct your mistakes</strong> — books can give you grammar and vocabulary, but they can't tell you where you're actually going wrong as you speak. A teacher also keeps you working when your motivation drops, which is usually what defeats people studying entirely alone. The exception's a highly motivated adult learner with a specific, narrow goal — someone cramming for a work exam can often manage perfectly well with self-study materials alone.\n\nQ: Do you think the traditional classroom will disappear in the future?\nA: The <strong class=\"key-phrase\">traditional classroom</strong> won't disappear, but it may become less dominant. Students can learn factual content online now and use classroom time for discussion, feedback and practical work instead, so the teacher's role may shift from delivering information to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">guiding and evaluating learning</strong>. Teaching moved online during the pandemic and that made the limits fairly clear: students could reach the material perfectly well, but attainment still fell, because most people need the structure of a fixed time, a room, and somebody who notices when they've stopped trying. The exception's a small minority of highly self-directed adult learners, for whom online-only study genuinely does work.","topic":"Education","phrases":[{"en":"relevant to them","context":"By 'engaging' I mean the students should feel actually involved, not just present — they should feel they're learning something relevant to them."},{"en":"delivers the content","context":"A lot depends on how the teacher delivers the content, too, in a way students actually respond to."},{"en":"there's no substitute for","context":"Well, there's no substitute for a good teacher, in most cases."},{"en":"with the guidance of a teacher","context":"You can teach yourself, but you'll usually learn a lot faster with the guidance of a teacher who can see your mistakes as you make them."},{"en":"correct your mistakes","context":"When you're learning a language, for instance, you really need someone to correct your mistakes — books can give you grammar and vocabulary, but they can't tell you where you're actually going wrong as you speak."},{"en":"traditional classroom","context":"Do you think the traditional classroom will disappear in the future?"},{"en":"guiding and evaluating learning","context":"Students can learn factual content online now and use classroom time for discussion, feedback and practical work instead, so the teacher's role may shift from delivering information to guiding and evaluating learning."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Education","id":"speaking-part-3-035","_displayNo":"#035","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-035-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-035-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-035-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"A good lesson makes students interested and actively involved. They should understand why the topic is useful, and the teacher needs to explain it in a way they can follow. My university teacher sometimes asked us to teach parts ourselves. That worked well for confident students, but quieter students needed a different way to participate.","phrases":[{"en":"actively involved","meaning":"Taking part rather than only watching or listening."}],"question":"What do you think makes a good lesson?","html":"A good lesson makes students interested and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">actively involved</strong>. They should understand why the topic is useful, and the teacher needs to explain it in a way they can follow. My university teacher sometimes asked us to teach parts ourselves. That worked well for confident students, but quieter students needed a different way to participate."},{"text":"Usually a good teacher helps people learn faster by noticing and correcting mistakes. Language learners especially benefit from feedback on their speaking. Teachers also provide structure when motivation falls. However, an adult with a clear goal and strong motivation may study successfully alone, particularly for a specific exam. So it depends partly on the learner.","phrases":[{"en":"motivation","meaning":"The desire or reason that makes you want to do something."}],"question":"Do you think it is better to have a teacher or to teach yourself?","html":"Usually a good teacher helps people learn faster by noticing and correcting mistakes. Language learners especially benefit from feedback on their speaking. Teachers also provide structure when <strong class=\"key-phrase\">motivation</strong> falls. However, an adult with a clear goal and strong motivation may study successfully alone, particularly for a specific exam. So it depends partly on the learner."},{"text":"I don't think it will disappear. Students may learn facts online and use class time for discussion, practice and feedback. However, many still need a set time, a teacher and a shared space to keep working steadily. Online-only study can suit independent adults, but a mixture is likely to work better for many learners.","phrases":[{"en":"independent adults","meaning":"Adults who can organise and manage their learning without much help."}],"question":"Do you think the traditional classroom will disappear in the future?","html":"I don&#x27;t think it will disappear. Students may learn facts online and use class time for discussion, practice and feedback. However, many still need a set time, a teacher and a shared space to keep working steadily. Online-only study can suit <strong class=\"key-phrase\">independent adults</strong>, but a mixture is likely to work better for many learners."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-035-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-035-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-035-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"How many subjects schools teach, and changes to the system","prompt":"Do you think too many subjects are taught at school?\nHave there been any major changes in the education system in your country?\nShould everyone be entitled to go to university?","answer":"Q: Do you think too many subjects are taught at school?\nA: In some systems, yes. There's a genuine tension here: breadth lets children discover what they're actually good at, but an overcrowded timetable means nothing gets studied in real depth, and students end up memorising for exams and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">forgetting immediately afterwards</strong>. I'd rather see fewer subjects taught properly — the core ones, language, maths and science, need enough time for real understanding, and I'd protect those before adding anything new. I wouldn't cut breadth completely, though; a child who never tries art or music simply never finds out whether <strong class=\"key-phrase\">that's where their talent lies</strong>.\n\nQ: Have there been any major changes in the education system in your country?\nA: Several. The most obvious is technology, since classrooms use digital resources routinely now and students have access to information their teachers never had. Assessment's also become far more central, with much more frequent testing, which has advantages but does <strong class=\"key-phrase\">narrow what teachers cover</strong>, since anything not tested tends to get squeezed out. There's far more international influence too: English is taught earlier, international curricula have spread, and studying abroad's now a realistic ambition for many more families than it was a generation ago. Not every family benefits equally, though — that international option still depends heavily on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">household income</strong>.\n\nQ: Should everyone be entitled to go to university?\nA: Everyone should have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fair opportunity</strong>, in the sense that money should never be the barrier. But entitlement's a different thing from everyone actually going. A degree isn't the right path for every person, and countries that pushed participation very high often ended up with graduates stuck in jobs that never needed a degree in the first place, carrying <strong class=\"key-phrase\">student debt</strong> for it. Strong vocational training deserves equal status — <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a skilled electrician is not a failed academic</strong>.","topic":"Education","phrases":[{"en":"forgetting immediately afterwards","context":"There's a genuine tension here: breadth lets children discover what they're actually good at, but an overcrowded timetable means nothing gets studied in real depth, and students end up memorising for exams and forgetting immediately afterwards."},{"en":"that's where their talent lies","context":"I wouldn't cut breadth completely, though; a child who never tries art or music simply never finds out whether that's where their talent lies."},{"en":"narrow what teachers cover","context":"Assessment's also become far more central, with much more frequent testing, which has advantages but does narrow what teachers cover, since anything not tested tends to get squeezed out."},{"en":"household income","context":"Not every family benefits equally, though — that international option still depends heavily on household income."},{"en":"a fair opportunity","context":"Everyone should have a fair opportunity, in the sense that money should never be the barrier."},{"en":"student debt","context":"A degree isn't the right path for every person, and countries that pushed participation very high often ended up with graduates stuck in jobs that never needed a degree in the first place, carrying student debt for it."},{"en":"a skilled electrician is not a failed academic","context":"Strong vocational training deserves equal status — a skilled electrician is not a failed academic."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Education","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-036","_displayNo":"#036","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-036-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-036-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-036-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Sometimes there are so many subjects that students only memorise enough to pass tests. I would give key subjects such as languages, maths and science enough time for real understanding. However, schools should still offer some variety. Without trying art or music, children may never discover that they have a talent for them.","phrases":[{"en":"talent","meaning":"A natural or developed ability to do something well."}],"question":"Do you think too many subjects are taught at school?","html":"Sometimes there are so many subjects that students only memorise enough to pass tests. I would give key subjects such as languages, maths and science enough time for real understanding. However, schools should still offer some variety. Without trying art or music, children may never discover that they have a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">talent</strong> for them."},{"text":"Technology is a major change: students now use digital resources and can find information easily. There is also more testing, which can push teachers towards exam content. English and international education have become more important too. However, these opportunities are unequal, since studying abroad or following certain programmes still depends strongly on family income.","phrases":[{"en":"digital resources","meaning":"Learning materials available through computers or the internet."}],"question":"Have there been any major changes in the education system in your country?","html":"Technology is a major change: students now use <strong class=\"key-phrase\">digital resources</strong> and can find information easily. There is also more testing, which can push teachers towards exam content. English and international education have become more important too. However, these opportunities are unequal, since studying abroad or following certain programmes still depends strongly on family income."},{"text":"Everyone should have a fair chance if university suits them, and lack of money should not block that. However, a degree is not the best route for everyone. Some graduates take jobs that do not need one while still owing money. Strong practical training deserves equal respect because skilled work is valuable too.","phrases":[{"en":"practical training","meaning":"Education that teaches the skills needed for a particular kind of work."}],"question":"Should everyone be entitled to go to university?","html":"Everyone should have a fair chance if university suits them, and lack of money should not block that. However, a degree is not the best route for everyone. Some graduates take jobs that do not need one while still owing money. Strong <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical training</strong> deserves equal respect because skilled work is valuable too."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-036-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-036-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-036-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"The classroom of the future and what makes a good teacher","prompt":"How will classrooms change in the future?\nWhat qualities make a good teacher?","answer":"Q: How will classrooms change in the future?\nA: I expect a mixture rather than a revolution. Digital tools will handle the routine parts — drilling vocabulary, marking exercises, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">adjusting difficulty to each student</strong> — because software can do that better and faster than one teacher with thirty pupils. But the classroom itself won't disappear: remote teaching during the pandemic showed fairly clearly that most children don't learn well without a teacher physically present, and that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the weakest students suffer most</strong> when that structure disappears. It's not universal, though — a small number of highly motivated older students genuinely thrive with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">self-paced online courses</strong>, so the mix will probably look different by age group.\n\nQ: What qualities make a good teacher?\nA: Three things stand out for me. First, they know the subject properly, because you can only explain something simply if you actually understand it deeply yourself. Second, they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">notice when a student is lost</strong>, which sounds obvious but is surprisingly rare in practice. And third, they set high expectations while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">making it safe to be wrong</strong> — if pupils are afraid of looking foolish, they stop asking questions, and the learning stops right along with it. None of that requires charisma particularly; it requires sustained attention to the individual student, which is exactly why <strong class=\"key-phrase\">class size matters</strong> so much — even the best teacher can't give thirty-five children that kind of attention.","topic":"Education","phrases":[{"en":"adjusting difficulty to each student","context":"Digital tools will handle the routine parts — drilling vocabulary, marking exercises, adjusting difficulty to each student — because software can do that better and faster than one teacher with thirty pupils."},{"en":"the weakest students suffer most","context":"But the classroom itself won't disappear: remote teaching during the pandemic showed fairly clearly that most children don't learn well without a teacher physically present, and that the weakest students suffer most when that structure disappears."},{"en":"self-paced online courses","context":"It's not universal, though — a small number of highly motivated older students genuinely thrive with self-paced online courses, so the mix will probably look different by age group."},{"en":"notice when a student is lost","context":"Second, they notice when a student is lost, which sounds obvious but is surprisingly rare in practice."},{"en":"making it safe to be wrong","context":"And third, they set high expectations while making it safe to be wrong — if pupils are afraid of looking foolish, they stop asking questions, and the learning stops right along with it."},{"en":"class size matters","context":"None of that requires charisma particularly; it requires sustained attention to the individual student, which is exactly why class size matters so much — even the best teacher can't give thirty-five children that kind of attention."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Education","id":"speaking-part-3-037","_displayNo":"#037","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-037-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-037-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I expect more digital tools for routine practice, marking and activities matched to each student's level. Teachers will still matter because many children need guidance and a clear structure. Older, highly motivated students may prefer more independent online study. So classrooms will probably combine technology and personal teaching, with the balance depending on students' ages and needs.","phrases":[{"en":"guidance","meaning":"Help and advice about how to do something."}],"question":"How will classrooms change in the future?","html":"I expect more digital tools for routine practice, marking and activities matched to each student&#x27;s level. Teachers will still matter because many children need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">guidance</strong> and a clear structure. Older, highly motivated students may prefer more independent online study. So classrooms will probably combine technology and personal teaching, with the balance depending on students&#x27; ages and needs."},{"text":"A good teacher understands the subject, notices when students are confused and expects effort without making mistakes feel shameful. Students need to feel safe asking questions. These qualities require attention to each person, not just an entertaining personality. That is why class size matters: even an excellent teacher struggles to support too many learners individually.","phrases":[{"en":"individually","meaning":"Separately, with attention to each person."}],"question":"What qualities make a good teacher?","html":"A good teacher understands the subject, notices when students are confused and expects effort without making mistakes feel shameful. Students need to feel safe asking questions. These qualities require attention to each person, not just an entertaining personality. That is why class size matters: even an excellent teacher struggles to support too many learners <strong class=\"key-phrase\">individually</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-037-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-037-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Showing anger, and whether men and women differ","prompt":"Do you think it is good to show your emotions when you are angry?\nIn your opinion, do women show their emotions more than men?\nWhy do you think men tend to show their emotions less than women?","answer":"Q: Do you think it is good to show your emotions when you are angry?\nA: It's better than concealing it, but how you show it decides everything. Suppressing anger doesn't remove it — it usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">resurfaces later as resentment</strong>, or comes out in some completely unrelated argument a week later over something trivial like the washing-up. Saying clearly what caused the problem, while you're still able to speak calmly, generally resolves it. What doesn't work is shouting or insulting somebody, because the other person stops listening to the complaint and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">starts defending themselves against the attack</strong> instead — at which point nothing productive is actually being said.\n\nQ: In your opinion, do women show their emotions more than men?\nA: Outwardly, often yes, but I'd question whether they actually feel more. The difference looks largely learned to me — in most cultures girls are permitted <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a wider range of emotional expression</strong>, while boys are steered towards a narrower one from a young age. So the same feeling gets displayed differently rather than experienced differently. The variation between individuals is far larger than the variation between the sexes, though; I know men who are highly expressive and women who reveal almost nothing, my own brother and sister being a perfect example of exactly that.\n\nQ: Why do you think men tend to show their emotions less than women?\nA: Mostly upbringing, I'd say. Boys are told very early not to cry, often by well-meaning adults, and they quickly learn that visible distress <strong class=\"key-phrase\">invites mockery from other boys</strong>. That becomes self-reinforcing: if nobody in the group admits to struggling, admitting it feels like being the only weak one. It has real consequences too, since men are noticeably less likely to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ask for help when they need it</strong>. That's shifting a bit in younger generations, though, where <strong class=\"key-phrase\">talking openly</strong> about mental health has become far more normal among male friend groups than it was even ten years ago.","topic":"Emotions","phrases":[{"en":"starts defending themselves against the attack","context":"What doesn't work is shouting or insulting somebody, because the other person stops listening to the complaint and starts defending themselves against the attack instead — at which point nothing productive is actually being said."},{"en":"resurfaces later as resentment","context":"Suppressing anger doesn't remove it — it usually resurfaces later as resentment, or comes out in some completely unrelated argument a week later over something trivial like the washing-up."},{"en":"a wider range of emotional expression","context":"The difference looks largely learned to me — in most cultures girls are permitted a wider range of emotional expression, while boys are steered towards a narrower one from a young age."},{"en":"invites mockery from other boys","context":"Boys are told very early not to cry, often by well-meaning adults, and they quickly learn that visible distress invites mockery from other boys."},{"en":"ask for help when they need it","context":"It has real consequences too, since men are noticeably less likely to ask for help when they need it."},{"en":"talking openly","context":"That's shifting a bit in younger generations, though, where talking openly about mental health has become far more normal among male friend groups than it was even ten years ago."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Emotions","id":"speaking-part-3-038","_displayNo":"#038","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-038-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-038-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-038-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, if you explain the anger calmly. Hiding it does not necessarily solve the problem and may lead to another argument later. Saying what upset you can help. Shouting or insulting someone usually makes them defend themselves instead of listen, though. The important thing is expressing the feeling clearly without attacking the other person.","phrases":[{"en":"defend themselves","meaning":"Try to protect themselves from criticism or blame."}],"question":"Do you think it is good to show your emotions when you are angry?","html":"Yes, if you explain the anger calmly. Hiding it does not necessarily solve the problem and may lead to another argument later. Saying what upset you can help. Shouting or insulting someone usually makes them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">defend themselves</strong> instead of listen, though. The important thing is expressing the feeling clearly without attacking the other person."},{"text":"Women may sometimes show emotion more openly, but that does not prove they feel more. Children often learn different rules about expressing feelings: girls may be allowed more freedom, while boys are discouraged. Individual differences are large, though. I know expressive men and quiet women, so I would avoid judging a person mainly by gender.","phrases":[{"en":"expressive","meaning":"Showing thoughts and feelings openly."}],"question":"In your opinion, do women show their emotions more than men?","html":"Women may sometimes show emotion more openly, but that does not prove they feel more. Children often learn different rules about expressing feelings: girls may be allowed more freedom, while boys are discouraged. Individual differences are large, though. I know <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expressive</strong> men and quiet women, so I would avoid judging a person mainly by gender."},{"text":"Boys are often taught not to cry and may fear being laughed at if they admit difficulties. When no one in a group talks openly, asking for help can feel like weakness. However, this is changing among some younger men as discussing mental health becomes more normal. I think upbringing and social expectations matter more than a simple natural difference.","phrases":[{"en":"upbringing","meaning":"The way a person is taught and cared for while growing up."}],"question":"Why do you think men tend to show their emotions less than women?","html":"Boys are often taught not to cry and may fear being laughed at if they admit difficulties. When no one in a group talks openly, asking for help can feel like weakness. However, this is changing among some younger men as discussing mental health becomes more normal. I think <strong class=\"key-phrase\">upbringing</strong> and social expectations matter more than a simple natural difference."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-038-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-038-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-038-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"What excites people, and staying calm","prompt":"What generally makes people in your country excited?\nAre people in your country generally calm, or do they get excited easily?\nHow do you think people show their excitement?","answer":"Q: What generally makes people in your country excited?\nA: Sport's probably the biggest one — a national football victory genuinely brings people out onto the streets in a way almost nothing else does. Beyond that, family celebrations like weddings and New Year, and personal milestones such as exam results or a first job. Announcements about things people have been waiting for, a concert finally being confirmed, say, also generate a surprising amount of excitement. The common thread is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">anticipation combined with emotional investment</strong>; excitement seems to need both. It's not universal, though — plenty of people find big national events <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completely underwhelming</strong>, and get far more excited by something purely personal instead.\n\nQ: Are people in your country generally calm, or do they get excited easily?\nA: It depends enormously on the setting, which I suspect is true of most cultures. In formal situations, at work, with people they don't know well, dealing with officials, people are quite restrained, and showing strong emotion would seem <strong class=\"key-phrase\">out of place</strong>. But at a festival, a football match or a family gathering, exactly the same people can be extremely expressive. So it's less about temperament than about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">where the culture permits emotion</strong>. That's not the whole story, though; some individuals stay reserved wherever they are, festival or office, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">personality still matters</strong> on top of context.\n\nQ: How do you think people show their excitement?\nA: The physical signs are fairly universal — speaking faster and louder, smiling, gesturing more, and an obvious need to tell somebody immediately. That last part's interesting, because excitement seems to need an audience in a way other emotions don't. Online it looks different, expressed through messages and emojis, but I'd say it reads as more deliberate, more composed than the real thing. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Face-to-face reactions are harder to fake</strong> because they happen before you've had time to think about how you want to look.","topic":"Emotions","phrases":[{"en":"anticipation combined with emotional investment","context":"The common thread is anticipation combined with emotional investment; excitement seems to need both."},{"en":"completely underwhelming","context":"It's not universal, though — plenty of people find big national events completely underwhelming, and get far more excited by something purely personal instead."},{"en":"out of place","context":"In formal situations, at work, with people they don't know well, dealing with officials, people are quite restrained, and showing strong emotion would seem out of place."},{"en":"where the culture permits emotion","context":"So it's less about temperament than about where the culture permits emotion."},{"en":"personality still matters","context":"That's not the whole story, though; some individuals stay reserved wherever they are, festival or office, so personality still matters on top of context."},{"en":"Face-to-face reactions are harder to fake","context":"Face-to-face reactions are harder to fake because they happen before you've had time to think about how you want to look."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Emotions","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-039","_displayNo":"#039","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-039-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-039-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-039-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"National football victories, weddings, New Year celebrations and personal achievements often create excitement. People also get excited about events they have waited for, such as a concert. Usually they need both expectation and a personal reason to care. However, interests vary: someone may ignore a huge national event but feel thrilled about getting their first job.","phrases":[{"en":"personal achievements","meaning":"Goals or successes that matter in your own life."}],"question":"What generally makes people in your country excited?","html":"National football victories, weddings, New Year celebrations and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">personal achievements</strong> often create excitement. People also get excited about events they have waited for, such as a concert. Usually they need both expectation and a personal reason to care. However, interests vary: someone may ignore a huge national event but feel thrilled about getting their first job."},{"text":"It depends on the setting. People may control their feelings at work or around strangers but show much more excitement at a match or family celebration. Culture affects when strong emotion seems acceptable. Personality matters too, though, because some people remain quiet in almost every situation. I would not describe a whole country in only one way.","phrases":[{"en":"setting","meaning":"The place or situation in which something happens."}],"question":"Are people in your country generally calm, or do they get excited easily?","html":"It depends on the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">setting</strong>. People may control their feelings at work or around strangers but show much more excitement at a match or family celebration. Culture affects when strong emotion seems acceptable. Personality matters too, though, because some people remain quiet in almost every situation. I would not describe a whole country in only one way."},{"text":"People often smile, move their hands more and speak faster or louder. They also want to share their news immediately, as if excitement needs someone to hear it. Online, people use messages and emojis instead. Those reactions may be more carefully chosen, while a face-to-face response often happens before someone has time to plan it.","phrases":[{"en":"response","meaning":"What someone says or does after something happens."}],"question":"How do you think people show their excitement?","html":"People often smile, move their hands more and speak faster or louder. They also want to share their news immediately, as if excitement needs someone to hear it. Online, people use messages and emojis instead. Those reactions may be more carefully chosen, while a face-to-face <strong class=\"key-phrase\">response</strong> often happens before someone has time to plan it."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-039-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-039-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-039-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Human effects on wild animals and protecting wildlife","prompt":"What effects do you think humans have on wild animals?\nWhat measures could we take to protect wildlife?\nIs it the responsibility of schools to teach children about protecting wildlife?","answer":"Q: What effects do you think humans have on wild animals?\nA: Habitat destruction's the largest by a long way — clearing forests and draining wetlands for agriculture removes the places animals actually live, and no amount of protection helps a species with nowhere left to go. Beyond that, pollution, overfishing and climate change all take a heavy toll. There's a subtler effect too: roads and cities <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fragment habitats into isolated patches</strong>, so populations can no longer migrate or interbreed, and small isolated groups tend to die out gradually without anyone really noticing. It's not universally bad news, though — a handful of adaptable species, foxes and certain birds, have actually thrived alongside humans by exploiting <strong class=\"key-phrase\">urban environments</strong> most others can't use.\n\nQ: What measures could we take to protect wildlife?\nA: Protecting habitat's the foundation, since everything else is secondary to having somewhere to live. That means properly funded national parks with actual rangers, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not simply lines drawn on a map</strong>. Enforcing anti-poaching law matters too, along with reducing demand in the countries that buy wildlife products. And local communities have to benefit financially from it, because conservation imposed on people who lose crops or land to it will always be resisted, whatever the law says on paper — a farmer who's lost his harvest to elephants isn't going to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel any goodwill</strong> towards the park next door.\n\nQ: Is it the responsibility of schools to teach children about protecting wildlife?\nA: Schools have a clear role, though I wouldn't say the responsibility's theirs alone. Children who learn about ecosystems and endangered species early tend to keep that concern into adulthood, and they often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">influence their parents' behaviour at home</strong>, which is a genuinely useful side effect. What works better than classroom lessons, though, is direct contact: a single trip to a forest or a nature reserve tends to do more than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a term of textbook material</strong>, simply because it's harder to care about a species you've never actually seen.","topic":"Environment & Nature","phrases":[{"en":"fragment habitats into isolated patches","context":"There's a subtler effect too: roads and cities fragment habitats into isolated patches, so populations can no longer migrate or interbreed, and small isolated groups tend to die out gradually without anyone really noticing."},{"en":"urban environments","context":"It's not universally bad news, though — a handful of adaptable species, foxes and certain birds, have actually thrived alongside humans by exploiting urban environments most others can't use."},{"en":"not simply lines drawn on a map","context":"That means properly funded national parks with actual rangers, not simply lines drawn on a map."},{"en":"feel any goodwill","context":"And local communities have to benefit financially from it, because conservation imposed on people who lose crops or land to it will always be resisted, whatever the law says on paper — a farmer who's lost his harvest to elephants isn't going to feel any goodwill towards the park next door."},{"en":"influence their parents' behaviour at home","context":"Children who learn about ecosystems and endangered species early tend to keep that concern into adulthood, and they often influence their parents' behaviour at home, which is a genuinely useful side effect."},{"en":"a term of textbook material","context":"What works better than classroom lessons, though, is direct contact: a single trip to a forest or a nature reserve tends to do more than a term of textbook material, simply because it's harder to care about a species you've never actually seen."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Environment & Nature","id":"speaking-part-3-040","_displayNo":"#040","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-040-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-040-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-040-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Humans destroy habitats by clearing forests or changing land for farming and building. Pollution, fishing and climate change cause further harm. Roads also separate animal groups, making movement and breeding harder. Some adaptable animals do well in cities, but many cannot use those environments. Protecting a species is difficult if it no longer has a suitable place to live.","phrases":[{"en":"habitats","meaning":"The natural places where particular animals or plants live."}],"question":"What effects do you think humans have on wild animals?","html":"Humans destroy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">habitats</strong> by clearing forests or changing land for farming and building. Pollution, fishing and climate change cause further harm. Roads also separate animal groups, making movement and breeding harder. Some adaptable animals do well in cities, but many cannot use those environments. Protecting a species is difficult if it no longer has a suitable place to live."},{"text":"We should protect habitats, support parks properly and enforce laws against illegal hunting. Reducing demand for wildlife products is also important. Local people need benefits from conservation, especially if animals damage their crops or land use is restricted. Protection is more likely to succeed when communities have a reason to support it, rather than only being told what not to do.","phrases":[{"en":"conservation","meaning":"Protecting nature and using its resources carefully."}],"question":"What measures could we take to protect wildlife?","html":"We should protect habitats, support parks properly and enforce laws against illegal hunting. Reducing demand for wildlife products is also important. Local people need benefits from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">conservation</strong>, especially if animals damage their crops or land use is restricted. Protection is more likely to succeed when communities have a reason to support it, rather than only being told what not to do."},{"text":"Schools should help, but they are not the only ones responsible. Learning about nature can build concern that lasts into adulthood, and children may influence family habits too. Visits to forests or nature reserves can make the subject more meaningful than textbooks alone. It is easier to care about something after seeing and experiencing it.","phrases":[{"en":"nature reserves","meaning":"Areas protected so that wildlife and natural habitats can survive."}],"question":"Is it the responsibility of schools to teach children about protecting wildlife?","html":"Schools should help, but they are not the only ones responsible. Learning about nature can build concern that lasts into adulthood, and children may influence family habits too. Visits to forests or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nature reserves</strong> can make the subject more meaningful than textbooks alone. It is easier to care about something after seeing and experiencing it."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-040-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-040-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-040-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Water transport compared with other forms","prompt":"How does water transport compare with other forms of transport?\nWhat are the advantages and disadvantages of water transport?\nHow important is it for a town or city to be located near a river or the sea?","answer":"Q: How does water transport compare with other forms of transport?\nA: It occupies a specific niche. It's far slower than air freight — weeks rather than hours — but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the cost per tonne is a fraction</strong>, which is why almost all international trade in bulk goods goes by sea. Compared with road transport it's much less flexible, because you're tied to ports and navigable rivers, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">something else has to cover the final leg</strong> of the journey to the actual destination. So it suits bulk, low-value cargo far better than anything urgent or door-to-door.\n\nQ: What are the advantages and disadvantages of water transport?\nA: The advantages are capacity and fuel efficiency: a single container ship carries what would otherwise need thousands of lorries, using far less fuel per tonne, and it keeps freight traffic off the roads. The disadvantages are speed, inflexible routes and environmental risk, since an oil spill causes damage lasting decades and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">building ports destroys coastal habitat</strong>. Shipping also burns <strong class=\"key-phrase\">some of the dirtiest fuel available</strong>, which is only now being regulated properly — it's not that the industry can't clean itself up, it simply hasn't had much incentive to until now.\n\nQ: How important is it for a town or city to be located near a river or the sea?\nA: Historically it was decisive, since almost every major old city sits on water because that was the only practical way to trade and to obtain fresh water. Today it matters far less for transport, as roads and air freight have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduced the dependence</strong>, but it hasn't disappeared — river and coastal cities still do well out of tourism and industry. The complication is climate: coastal cities now face flooding, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the location that created them has become a liability</strong>, and today's advantage can quietly turn into tomorrow's expensive problem.","topic":"Environment & Nature","phrases":[{"en":"the cost per tonne is a fraction","context":"It's far slower than air freight — weeks rather than hours — but the cost per tonne is a fraction, which is why almost all international trade in bulk goods goes by sea."},{"en":"something else has to cover the final leg","context":"Compared with road transport it's much less flexible, because you're tied to ports and navigable rivers, and something else has to cover the final leg of the journey to the actual destination."},{"en":"building ports destroys coastal habitat","context":"The disadvantages are speed, inflexible routes and environmental risk, since an oil spill causes damage lasting decades and building ports destroys coastal habitat."},{"en":"some of the dirtiest fuel available","context":"Shipping also burns some of the dirtiest fuel available, which is only now being regulated properly — it's not that the industry can't clean itself up, it simply hasn't had much incentive to until now."},{"en":"reduced the dependence","context":"Today it matters far less for transport, as roads and air freight have reduced the dependence, but it hasn't disappeared — river and coastal cities still do well out of tourism and industry."},{"en":"the location that created them has become a liability","context":"The complication is climate: coastal cities now face flooding, so the location that created them has become a liability, and today's advantage can quietly turn into tomorrow's expensive problem."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Environment & Nature","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-041","_displayNo":"#041","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-041-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-041-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-041-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Water transport is much slower than flying but usually cheaper for heavy goods. That makes it useful for large shipments that are not urgent. It is less flexible than road transport because boats depend on ports and rivers. Goods often need a truck for the final journey, so shipping is not ideal for direct, fast deliveries.","phrases":[{"en":"shipments","meaning":"Large amounts of goods sent from one place to another."}],"question":"How does water transport compare with other forms of transport?","html":"Water transport is much slower than flying but usually cheaper for heavy goods. That makes it useful for large <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shipments</strong> that are not urgent. It is less flexible than road transport because boats depend on ports and rivers. Goods often need a truck for the final journey, so shipping is not ideal for direct, fast deliveries."},{"text":"Ships carry huge loads using less fuel per tonne than many smaller road vehicles, and they reduce lorry traffic. However, they are slow and follow limited routes. They can also cause serious pollution, especially through fuel emissions or oil spills, while ports may damage coastal habitats. So being efficient does not mean water transport has no environmental costs.","phrases":[{"en":"fuel emissions","meaning":"Polluting gases released when fuel is burned."}],"question":"What are the advantages and disadvantages of water transport?","html":"Ships carry huge loads using less fuel per tonne than many smaller road vehicles, and they reduce lorry traffic. However, they are slow and follow limited routes. They can also cause serious pollution, especially through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fuel emissions</strong> or oil spills, while ports may damage coastal habitats. So being efficient does not mean water transport has no environmental costs."},{"text":"It used to be extremely important because water provided trade routes and a supply of fresh water. Roads and air transport have reduced that dependence, although rivers and coasts still support tourism and industry. However, flooding is a growing risk for some coastal cities. A location that once created wealth may now require expensive protection.","phrases":[{"en":"dependence","meaning":"The need to rely on someone or something."}],"question":"How important is it for a town or city to be located near a river or the sea?","html":"It used to be extremely important because water provided trade routes and a supply of fresh water. Roads and air transport have reduced that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dependence</strong>, although rivers and coasts still support tourism and industry. However, flooding is a growing risk for some coastal cities. A location that once created wealth may now require expensive protection."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-041-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-041-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-041-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"How aware people are of environmental problems","prompt":"How aware do you think people are nowadays about environmental issues?","answer":"Q: How aware do you think people are nowadays about environmental issues?\nA: Awareness is far higher than it was twenty years ago. Climate change, plastic waste and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">air quality</strong> are covered constantly in the news, and most schools now teach the subject, so even young children can explain why recycling matters. What I'd question is how much that awareness actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changes behaviour</strong>. People know that flying frequently and eating a lot of meat are damaging, but they carry on doing both, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the sustainable option</strong> is usually more expensive or less convenient. So I'd say we've largely solved <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the information problem</strong>, but not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the incentive problem</strong>. It isn't that people need convincing any more; it's that doing the right thing still costs more than doing the easy thing.","topic":"Environment & Nature","phrases":[{"en":"air quality","context":"Climate change, plastic waste and air quality are covered constantly in the news, and most schools now teach the subject, so even young children can explain why recycling matters."},{"en":"changes behaviour","context":"What I'd question is how much that awareness actually changes behaviour."},{"en":"the sustainable option","context":"People know that flying frequently and eating a lot of meat are damaging, but they carry on doing both, because the sustainable option is usually more expensive or less convenient."},{"en":"the information problem","context":"So I'd say we've largely solved the information problem, but not the incentive problem."},{"en":"the incentive problem","context":"So I'd say we've largely solved the information problem, but not the incentive problem."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Environment & Nature","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-042","_displayNo":"#042","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-042-q01.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"People know much more about climate change, plastic waste and dirty air because schools and news discuss them regularly. However, knowing is different from changing habits. People may continue flying often or making other harmful choices because greener options cost more or take extra effort. I think making better choices practical and affordable is now as important as providing information.","phrases":[{"en":"affordable","meaning":"Not too expensive for people to pay for."}],"question":"How aware do you think people are nowadays about environmental issues?","html":"People know much more about climate change, plastic waste and dirty air because schools and news discuss them regularly. However, knowing is different from changing habits. People may continue flying often or making other harmful choices because greener options cost more or take extra effort. I think making better choices practical and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">affordable</strong> is now as important as providing information."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-042-q01.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Human effects on the environment and what can be done","prompt":"What are the effects of human activities on the environment?\nWhat can be done to protect the environment?\nWhat would be the impact of global warming on the environment?","answer":"Q: What are the effects of human activities on the environment?\nA: The largest are deforestation, burning fossil fuels, intensive agriculture and waste. Between them they've altered habitats and the climate itself, and the consequences are visible: species disappearing at <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an unprecedented rate</strong>, air quality in major cities that measurably shortens lives, and rivers carrying plastic and agricultural runoff. What concerns me most is the change in weather patterns, because that affects food production, and food security affects almost everything else in a society. Not all of this is equally permanent, though — <strong class=\"key-phrase\">air quality can bounce back within years</strong> once emissions drop, whereas a lost species or a collapsed fishery doesn't come back at all.\n\nQ: What can be done to protect the environment?\nA: The decisive action has to come from governments, because individuals can't buy clean options that don't exist. That means enforceable emissions standards, investment in public transport and renewable energy, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">making polluters pay for the damage they cause</strong> rather than passing it to the public. Individual behaviour matters and shouldn't be dismissed, but I'm impatient with campaigns that place the whole responsibility on consumers while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the underlying system stays identical</strong>.\n\nQ: What would be the impact of global warming on the environment?\nA: More intense heatwaves, rising sea levels, longer droughts in some regions and heavier flooding in others. Ecosystems will be disrupted because species <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot relocate as fast as their habitats change</strong>. The part that receives less attention is the human distribution of it: the countries that will suffer worst contributed least to the emissions and have the fewest resources to adapt. That's what makes it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a question of fairness</strong>, not only of science. It isn't uniformly bad news, either — a handful of colder regions may see longer growing seasons, though that's a small and temporary upside next to the disruption everywhere else.","topic":"Environment & Nature","phrases":[{"en":"an unprecedented rate","context":"Between them they've altered habitats and the climate itself, and the consequences are visible: species disappearing at an unprecedented rate, air quality in major cities that measurably shortens lives, and rivers carrying plastic and agricultural runoff."},{"en":"air quality can bounce back within years","context":"Not all of this is equally permanent, though — air quality can bounce back within years once emissions drop, whereas a lost species or a collapsed fishery doesn't come back at all."},{"en":"making polluters pay for the damage they cause","context":"That means enforceable emissions standards, investment in public transport and renewable energy, and making polluters pay for the damage they cause rather than passing it to the public."},{"en":"the underlying system stays identical","context":"Individual behaviour matters and shouldn't be dismissed, but I'm impatient with campaigns that place the whole responsibility on consumers while the underlying system stays identical."},{"en":"cannot relocate as fast as their habitats change","context":"Ecosystems will be disrupted because species cannot relocate as fast as their habitats change."},{"en":"a question of fairness","context":"That's what makes it a question of fairness, not only of science."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Environment & Nature","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-043","_displayNo":"#043","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-043-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-043-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-043-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Cutting down forests, burning fossil fuels, intensive farming and waste damage both nature and the climate. They can destroy habitats, pollute rivers and make the air unhealthy. Changing weather is especially worrying because it affects food production. Some damage can improve when pollution falls, but losing a species may be permanent, so prevention matters.","phrases":[{"en":"prevention","meaning":"Taking action to stop a problem before it happens."}],"question":"What are the effects of human activities on the environment?","html":"Cutting down forests, burning fossil fuels, intensive farming and waste damage both nature and the climate. They can destroy habitats, pollute rivers and make the air unhealthy. Changing weather is especially worrying because it affects food production. Some damage can improve when pollution falls, but losing a species may be permanent, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prevention</strong> matters."},{"text":"Governments need to lead by setting pollution limits and investing in clean energy and public transport. People cannot choose a greener option if none is available. Companies should also pay for the damage they cause. Individual habits matter, but asking consumers to solve everything while the wider system stays the same is not enough.","phrases":[{"en":"consumers","meaning":"People who buy or use goods and services."}],"question":"What can be done to protect the environment?","html":"Governments need to lead by setting pollution limits and investing in clean energy and public transport. People cannot choose a greener option if none is available. Companies should also pay for the damage they cause. Individual habits matter, but asking <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consumers</strong> to solve everything while the wider system stays the same is not enough."},{"text":"It can cause stronger heatwaves, rising seas, droughts and floods. Animals and plants may struggle because their habitats change faster than they can move. Poorer countries may suffer most despite contributing less to the problem, so fairness matters too. Some cold areas might briefly benefit from warmer growing conditions, but that does not balance the wider damage.","phrases":[{"en":"droughts","meaning":"Long periods with too little rain."}],"question":"What would be the impact of global warming on the environment?","html":"It can cause stronger heatwaves, rising seas, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">droughts</strong> and floods. Animals and plants may struggle because their habitats change faster than they can move. Poorer countries may suffer most despite contributing less to the problem, so fairness matters too. Some cold areas might briefly benefit from warmer growing conditions, but that does not balance the wider damage."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-043-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-043-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-043-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Whether life is becoming safer or more dangerous","prompt":"In what ways is life becoming safer, and in what ways is it becoming more dangerous?\nShould people always avoid dangers, or is it a good idea to take a risk?\nWhat risk should people try to avoid?","answer":"Q: In what ways is life becoming safer, and in what ways is it becoming more dangerous?\nA: It's become dramatically safer in the most fundamental respects. Medicine has eliminated diseases that once routinely killed children, building and fire regulations have reduced accidents enormously, and workplace deaths are now a small fraction of what they were. The new dangers are different in character — they're <strong class=\"key-phrase\">systemic rather than personal</strong>. Cybercrime, climate instability and highly connected supply chains mean a failure in one place can spread globally within days. The catch is that this kind of danger is much harder to protect yourself from individually; <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no amount of personal caution</strong> stops a bank's systems going down or a supply chain breaking.\n\nQ: Should people always avoid dangers, or is it a good idea to take a risk?\nA: Avoiding all risk is neither possible nor desirable. Nothing worth doing — starting a business, moving abroad, learning something in public — comes without the possibility of failure, and a person who avoids all of it ends up with a very narrow life. The useful distinction is between <strong class=\"key-phrase\">informed and reckless risk</strong>. Informed means you understand the downside and you've decided it's worth taking; reckless means you simply never looked at it in the first place. That said, the line <strong class=\"key-phrase\">isn't always obvious in the moment</strong> — plenty of reckless decisions felt informed right up until they went wrong.\n\nQ: What risk should people try to avoid?\nA: The ones where the potential harm is permanent and the benefit is small — that's the test I'd apply. Dangerous driving is the obvious example, since you save a few minutes and risk your life. High-interest debt belongs in the same category, as does ignoring a serious medical symptom. If <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the worst realistic outcome cannot be undone</strong>, the reward has to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely substantial</strong> to justify it, whereas a risk you can recover from is worth taking more freely.","topic":"Experiences","phrases":[{"en":"systemic rather than personal","context":"The new dangers are different in character — they're systemic rather than personal."},{"en":"no amount of personal caution","context":"The catch is that this kind of danger is much harder to protect yourself from individually; no amount of personal caution stops a bank's systems going down or a supply chain breaking."},{"en":"informed and reckless risk","context":"The useful distinction is between informed and reckless risk."},{"en":"isn't always obvious in the moment","context":"That said, the line isn't always obvious in the moment — plenty of reckless decisions felt informed right up until they went wrong."},{"en":"the worst realistic outcome cannot be undone","context":"If the worst realistic outcome cannot be undone, the reward has to be genuinely substantial to justify it, whereas a risk you can recover from is worth taking more freely."},{"en":"genuinely substantial","context":"If the worst realistic outcome cannot be undone, the reward has to be genuinely substantial to justify it, whereas a risk you can recover from is worth taking more freely."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Experiences","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-044","_displayNo":"#044","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-044-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-044-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-044-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Medicine, safer buildings and workplace rules have reduced many traditional dangers. However, new risks such as cybercrime and climate problems can affect whole systems. A failure in one country may disrupt supplies elsewhere very quickly. These dangers are harder to avoid personally because being careful cannot protect you from every failure in a bank or transport network.","phrases":[{"en":"disrupt","meaning":"Interrupt something and stop it working normally."}],"question":"In what ways is life becoming safer, and in what ways is it becoming more dangerous?","html":"Medicine, safer buildings and workplace rules have reduced many traditional dangers. However, new risks such as cybercrime and climate problems can affect whole systems. A failure in one country may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disrupt</strong> supplies elsewhere very quickly. These dangers are harder to avoid personally because being careful cannot protect you from every failure in a bank or transport network."},{"text":"People should not avoid every risk. Starting a business, moving abroad or learning a new skill all involve possible failure. What matters is understanding the downside and deciding whether the benefit is worth it. Acting without considering consequences is different. Even careful decisions can go wrong, but that is not a reason to avoid all opportunities.","phrases":[{"en":"downside","meaning":"The negative part or possible disadvantage of something."}],"question":"Should people always avoid dangers, or is it a good idea to take a risk?","html":"People should not avoid every risk. Starting a business, moving abroad or learning a new skill all involve possible failure. What matters is understanding the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">downside</strong> and deciding whether the benefit is worth it. Acting without considering consequences is different. Even careful decisions can go wrong, but that is not a reason to avoid all opportunities."},{"text":"I would avoid risks with lasting harm and very little benefit. Dangerous driving is a clear example: saving a few minutes is not worth risking a life. Taking on very expensive debt or ignoring a serious health problem can also have major consequences. Risks are easier to accept when a mistake can be repaired or recovered from.","phrases":[{"en":"consequences","meaning":"The results or effects of an action."}],"question":"What risk should people try to avoid?","html":"I would avoid risks with lasting harm and very little benefit. Dangerous driving is a clear example: saving a few minutes is not worth risking a life. Taking on very expensive debt or ignoring a serious health problem can also have major <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consequences</strong>. Risks are easier to accept when a mistake can be repaired or recovered from."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-044-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-044-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-044-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Family likeness, and family against friends","prompt":"In what ways can a family be similar to each other?\nIn terms of her personality, are people more influenced by their family or by their friends?\nDo you think daughters are always more similar to their mothers than to male relatives?","answer":"Q: In what ways can a family be similar to each other?\nA: The obvious similarities are physical — height, features, the way people walk. But what strikes me more is the behavioural side: families share a sense of humour, particular expressions, how loudly they talk and how they handle disagreement. Some of that's genetic, but a great deal is simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">absorbed from years of proximity</strong>. Values around money, work and hospitality also pass down remarkably reliably, usually without anyone deliberately teaching them and often without the family noticing it's happening. It doesn't hold for every trait, though — <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a strong-willed child in a quiet family</strong> can turn out nothing like the people who raised them.\n\nQ: In terms of her personality, are people more influenced by their family or by their friends?\nA: It shifts with age, which I think is the key point. Family dominates early childhood, setting the basic values and habits, and those tend to persist. During adolescence friends become far more influential — teenagers adjust their language, clothing and often their ambitions <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to match their group</strong>. In adulthood people usually settle somewhere between the two, keeping the family's underlying values while having acquired much of their manner and outlook from their peers. It's not a clean split, though; <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a strong personality can resist both influences</strong> and stay fairly constant from childhood onward.\n\nQ: Do you think daughters are always more similar to their mothers than to male relatives?\nA: No, and I think that assumption comes from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expectation rather than observation</strong>. Genetics doesn't work that way — a daughter can easily inherit her father's temperament or her grandfather's build. What may create an appearance of similarity is that mothers and daughters often spend more time together, so mannerisms get copied. But plenty of families would tell you that the daughter is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unmistakably like her father</strong>, and nobody in those families finds it surprising. The one place the stereotype has some basis is early childcare, since whoever spends the most hours with a baby shapes it a little more than the other parent does.","topic":"Family","phrases":[{"en":"absorbed from years of proximity","context":"Some of that's genetic, but a great deal is simply absorbed from years of proximity."},{"en":"a strong-willed child in a quiet family","context":"It doesn't hold for every trait, though — a strong-willed child in a quiet family can turn out nothing like the people who raised them."},{"en":"to match their group","context":"During adolescence friends become far more influential — teenagers adjust their language, clothing and often their ambitions to match their group."},{"en":"a strong personality can resist both influences","context":"It's not a clean split, though; a strong personality can resist both influences and stay fairly constant from childhood onward."},{"en":"expectation rather than observation","context":"No, and I think that assumption comes from expectation rather than observation."},{"en":"unmistakably like her father","context":"But plenty of families would tell you that the daughter is unmistakably like her father, and nobody in those families finds it surprising."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Family","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-045","_displayNo":"#045","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-045-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-045-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-045-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Families can share physical features, such as height or facial appearance. They also copy habits, expressions, humour and ways of handling arguments after years together. Ideas about money and work can pass between generations without formal teaching. However, family influence is not complete; a child may develop a very different personality from the people who raised them.","phrases":[{"en":"physical features","meaning":"Visible parts of a person's appearance, such as face shape or height."}],"question":"In what ways can a family be similar to each other?","html":"Families can share <strong class=\"key-phrase\">physical features</strong>, such as height or facial appearance. They also copy habits, expressions, humour and ways of handling arguments after years together. Ideas about money and work can pass between generations without formal teaching. However, family influence is not complete; a child may develop a very different personality from the people who raised them."},{"text":"It changes with age. Family shapes early habits and values, while friends become especially influential during the teenage years. Adults often keep family values but adopt ways of speaking or thinking from friends. Some people resist both influences strongly, though. I think personality develops through a mixture rather than coming entirely from one group.","phrases":[{"en":"influential","meaning":"Having a strong effect on what people think or do."}],"question":"In terms of her personality, are people more influenced by their family or by their friends?","html":"It changes with age. Family shapes early habits and values, while friends become especially <strong class=\"key-phrase\">influential</strong> during the teenage years. Adults often keep family values but adopt ways of speaking or thinking from friends. Some people resist both influences strongly, though. I think personality develops through a mixture rather than coming entirely from one group."},{"text":"No. A daughter can resemble her father or another male relative in appearance or personality. Mothers and daughters may copy each other's habits if they spend a lot of time together, but that is not a rule. The person providing most early care may have a stronger influence, regardless of whether they are male or female.","phrases":[{"en":"resemble","meaning":"Look or behave like someone else."}],"question":"Do you think daughters are always more similar to their mothers than to male relatives?","html":"No. A daughter can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">resemble</strong> her father or another male relative in appearance or personality. Mothers and daughters may copy each other&#x27;s habits if they spend a lot of time together, but that is not a rule. The person providing most early care may have a stronger influence, regardless of whether they are male or female."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-045-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-045-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-045-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Celebrities now and celebrities in the past","prompt":"Are there any differences between the types of people who were seen as celebrities in the past and those who are celebrities nowadays?","answer":"Q: Are there any differences between the types of people who were seen as celebrities in the past and those who are celebrities nowadays?\nA: Yes, I think <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the route to fame</strong> has changed completely. In the past, people became well known because of their position or their ability — monarchs, generals, politicians, or exceptional writers and performers. Then film, radio and television created a new category of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">international star</strong>, but those people still had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a recognised craft</strong> behind them. What's different now is that social media allows anyone to become famous without any particular skill. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Visibility itself has become the achievement</strong>, and in some cases controversy is what generates the audience, which would've been unthinkable a generation ago. That's not true across the board, though — plenty of today's biggest names, in music or sport especially, still built their following on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">years of genuine skill</strong> before social media ever came into it.","topic":"Famous People","phrases":[{"en":"the route to fame","context":"Yes, I think the route to fame has changed completely."},{"en":"international star","context":"Then film, radio and television created a new category of international star, but those people still had a recognised craft behind them."},{"en":"a recognised craft","context":"Then film, radio and television created a new category of international star, but those people still had a recognised craft behind them."},{"en":"Visibility itself has become the achievement","context":"Visibility itself has become the achievement, and in some cases controversy is what generates the audience, which would've been unthinkable a generation ago."},{"en":"years of genuine skill","context":"That's not true across the board, though — plenty of today's biggest names, in music or sport especially, still built their following on years of genuine skill before social media ever came into it."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Famous People","id":"speaking-part-3-046","_displayNo":"#046","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-046-q01.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, the way people become famous has changed. In the past, position or recognised skills often mattered most. Film and television then created international stars. Social media now lets people gain attention through visibility or controversy, sometimes without an obvious skill. However, many modern athletes and musicians still become famous through years of serious practice and achievement.","phrases":[{"en":"controversy","meaning":"Strong public disagreement about a person or issue."}],"question":"Are there any differences between the types of people who were seen as celebrities in the past and those who are celebrities nowadays?","html":"Yes, the way people become famous has changed. In the past, position or recognised skills often mattered most. Film and television then created international stars. Social media now lets people gain attention through visibility or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">controversy</strong>, sometimes without an obvious skill. However, many modern athletes and musicians still become famous through years of serious practice and achievement."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-046-q01.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Role models for children, and being a child","prompt":"Is it important for young children to have a role model?\nWhat are the advantages and disadvantages of being a child?\nHow do you imagine traditional relationships changing in the future?","answer":"Q: Is it important for young children to have a role model?\nA: Yes, because abstract values mean very little to a child until they see them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">embodied in a person</strong>. Watching someone they admire work hard or behave decently teaches far more than simply being told to. What I'd argue against is having a single role model, particularly a celebrity, because every public figure eventually disappoints. Several is healthier, and the most useful ones are usually people children actually know, such as a teacher or a relative — someone whose <strong class=\"key-phrase\">flaws are visible too</strong>, not just the polished version a celebrity presents.\n\nQ: What are the advantages and disadvantages of being a child?\nA: The advantages are freedom from responsibility and an enormous amount of time to learn — a child can spend a year becoming good at something with no financial pressure at all. The disadvantage is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">powerlessness</strong>. Children don't choose where they live, which school they attend or how they're treated, and their well-being depends almost entirely on the adults around them. That's why a stable home matters so much — though it's worth saying that within a stable home, plenty of children would happily trade some of that freedom for a bit more say in decisions that affect them.\n\nQ: How do you imagine traditional relationships changing in the future?\nA: I'd expect them to become more equal and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less defined by fixed roles</strong> — who earns, who cares for the children, who makes decisions. That's already well advanced in many places. Families are also more geographically scattered, so relationships increasingly have to be maintained across distance, which technology makes possible without quite replicating the real thing. What families will need to protect is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unstructured time together</strong>, since messaging conspicuously fails to provide it.","topic":"Famous People","phrases":[{"en":"embodied in a person","context":"Yes, because abstract values mean very little to a child until they see them embodied in a person."},{"en":"flaws are visible too","context":"Several is healthier, and the most useful ones are usually people children actually know, such as a teacher or a relative — someone whose flaws are visible too, not just the polished version a celebrity presents."},{"en":"powerlessness","context":"The disadvantage is powerlessness."},{"en":"less defined by fixed roles","context":"I'd expect them to become more equal and less defined by fixed roles — who earns, who cares for the children, who makes decisions."},{"en":"unstructured time together","context":"What families will need to protect is unstructured time together, since messaging conspicuously fails to provide it."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Famous People","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-047","_displayNo":"#047","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-047-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-047-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-047-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, because children understand values better when they see someone practising them. A teacher or relative who works hard and treats people well can be a useful example. I would encourage several role models rather than one perfect hero. Seeing ordinary people's weaknesses also gives children a more realistic picture than a carefully managed celebrity image.","phrases":[{"en":"role models","meaning":"People whose behaviour others admire and may try to copy."}],"question":"Is it important for young children to have a role model?","html":"Yes, because children understand values better when they see someone practising them. A teacher or relative who works hard and treats people well can be a useful example. I would encourage several <strong class=\"key-phrase\">role models</strong> rather than one perfect hero. Seeing ordinary people&#x27;s weaknesses also gives children a more realistic picture than a carefully managed celebrity image."},{"text":"Children usually have fewer financial responsibilities and more time to learn. However, they have little control over where they live, their school or how adults treat them. Their happiness depends heavily on the home around them. A stable family can make childhood enjoyable, but children may still want more say in decisions affecting their lives.","phrases":[{"en":"more say","meaning":"Have a greater opportunity to influence a decision."}],"question":"What are the advantages and disadvantages of being a child?","html":"Children usually have fewer financial responsibilities and more time to learn. However, they have little control over where they live, their school or how adults treat them. Their happiness depends heavily on the home around them. A stable family can make childhood enjoyable, but children may still want <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more say</strong> in decisions affecting their lives."},{"text":"I expect family roles to become more equal, with less fixed ideas about who earns money or cares for children. Families may also live further apart and rely more on technology to stay connected. However, messages cannot fully replace relaxed time together. Protecting that time will remain important even as relationships become more flexible.","phrases":[{"en":"flexible","meaning":"Able to change to suit different needs or situations."}],"question":"How do you imagine traditional relationships changing in the future?","html":"I expect family roles to become more equal, with less fixed ideas about who earns money or cares for children. Families may also live further apart and rely more on technology to stay connected. However, messages cannot fully replace relaxed time together. Protecting that time will remain important even as relationships become more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">flexible</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-047-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-047-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-047-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"How films have changed and the future of cinemas","prompt":"Do you think films have changed since you were a child?\nAs the technology for home viewing improves, do you think people will stop going to the cinema in future?","answer":"Q: Do you think films have changed since you were a child?\nA: No, I don't think films have changed all that much since I was a child. When I was younger I enjoyed watching action films, and the Hollywood formula for this type of film seems to be the same today. For example, I liked the original 'Superman' films, and superheroes are still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a popular subject</strong> for film-makers. What's changed is the technology rather than the stories: the effects are far more convincing now, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a franchise runs for a decade</strong> instead of producing one sequel and moving on.\n\nQ: As the technology for home viewing improves, do you think people will stop going to the cinema in future?\nA: No, I don't think that people will stop going to the cinema altogether. People can already buy fantastic <strong class=\"key-phrase\">home viewing equipment</strong>, but it still feels more special to share the experience of watching a new film with a theatre full of people. I don't think that technology will ever fully <strong class=\"key-phrase\">replicate that cinema atmosphere</strong>. There's also the fact that going out is an occasion in a way that staying in simply isn't — you leave the house, you can't pause it, and you aren't checking your phone every ten minutes. Cinemas will have to work harder for that loyalty, though; <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ticket prices and dull scheduling</strong> are exactly the kind of thing that pushes people back towards their sofas.","topic":"Film & Television","phrases":[{"en":"a popular subject","context":"For example, I liked the original 'Superman' films, and superheroes are still a popular subject for film-makers."},{"en":"a franchise runs for a decade","context":"What's changed is the technology rather than the stories: the effects are far more convincing now, and a franchise runs for a decade instead of producing one sequel and moving on."},{"en":"home viewing equipment","context":"People can already buy fantastic home viewing equipment, but it still feels more special to share the experience of watching a new film with a theatre full of people."},{"en":"replicate that cinema atmosphere","context":"I don't think that technology will ever fully replicate that cinema atmosphere."},{"en":"ticket prices and dull scheduling","context":"Cinemas will have to work harder for that loyalty, though; ticket prices and dull scheduling are exactly the kind of thing that pushes people back towards their sofas."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Film & Television","id":"speaking-part-3-048","_displayNo":"#048","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-048-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-048-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"The technology has changed more than the basic stories, I think. I enjoyed superhero action films such as Superman as a child, and similar films are still popular. Effects now look more realistic, and successful stories may continue for many films. However, the familiar heroes and basic story patterns often remain much the same.","phrases":[{"en":"realistic","meaning":"Looking or feeling close to real life."}],"question":"Do you think films have changed since you were a child?","html":"The technology has changed more than the basic stories, I think. I enjoyed superhero action films such as Superman as a child, and similar films are still popular. Effects now look more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">realistic</strong>, and successful stories may continue for many films. However, the familiar heroes and basic story patterns often remain much the same."},{"text":"No, cinemas offer a shared atmosphere that home equipment cannot fully replace. Going out for a new film feels special, and people may concentrate more without phones or pauses. However, cinemas still need attractive films and reasonable prices. If visiting becomes too expensive or disappointing, people will naturally choose the comfort of home more often.","phrases":[{"en":"shared atmosphere","meaning":"The feeling created by experiencing something with other people."}],"question":"As the technology for home viewing improves, do you think people will stop going to the cinema in future?","html":"No, cinemas offer a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shared atmosphere</strong> that home equipment cannot fully replace. Going out for a new film feels special, and people may concentrate more without phones or pauses. However, cinemas still need attractive films and reasonable prices. If visiting becomes too expensive or disappointing, people will naturally choose the comfort of home more often."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-048-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-048-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Television for education or entertainment","prompt":"Do you think most people watch TV for education or for entertainment?\nShould TV play a role in educating children; how?\nHow do you think TV viewing habits change as people get older?","answer":"Q: Do you think most people watch TV for education or for entertainment?\nA: I think people watch TV primarily for entertainment. There are far more entertainment programmes than educational ones, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in my experience</strong> most people treat television as a form of relaxation in the evening. If I think about the most popular TV programmes in the UK, such as talent shows like 'X Factor' or soap operas like 'Eastenders', the focus is definitely on entertainment rather than education. That's not the whole picture, though — people do pick up a great deal by accident from documentaries and news programmes, so the education is often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">incidental rather than intended</strong>.\n\nQ: Should TV play a role in educating children; how?\nA: In my opinion, yes — TV should definitely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">play a role in</strong> educating children. Good children's TV programmes should tell stories that contain some kind of lesson about how to behave or what's morally right and wrong. Many of the traditional fairy tales, such as 'Cinderella', have been made into TV programmes, and there's always a positive message in those stories. Programmes about science and the natural world do much the same job for older children, and they often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spark an interest that lasts</strong>. It can't replace an adult explaining things, though — a five-year-old watching alone will pick up the story but often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">miss the lesson underneath it</strong>.\n\nQ: How do you think TV viewing habits change as people get older?\nA: TV viewing habits obviously change a lot as we get older. While toddlers might watch programmes about talking animals, teenagers prefer action and adventure or sports, and as adults we start taking more of an interest in news and politics. My own preferences, for example, have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changed over the years</strong> — I'd never have watched news programmes when I was younger. That's not true for everyone, though; plenty of adults keep the same taste in cartoons or action films they had as teenagers, and I don't think that says anything bad about them.","topic":"Film & Television","phrases":[{"en":"in my experience","context":"There are far more entertainment programmes than educational ones, and in my experience most people treat television as a form of relaxation in the evening."},{"en":"incidental rather than intended","context":"That's not the whole picture, though — people do pick up a great deal by accident from documentaries and news programmes, so the education is often incidental rather than intended."},{"en":"play a role in","context":"Should TV play a role in educating children; how?"},{"en":"spark an interest that lasts","context":"Programmes about science and the natural world do much the same job for older children, and they often spark an interest that lasts."},{"en":"miss the lesson underneath it","context":"It can't replace an adult explaining things, though — a five-year-old watching alone will pick up the story but often miss the lesson underneath it."},{"en":"changed over the years","context":"My own preferences, for example, have changed over the years — I'd never have watched news programmes when I was younger."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Film & Television","id":"speaking-part-3-049","_displayNo":"#049","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-049-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-049-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-049-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Mostly for entertainment. After work, people often want to relax with talent shows, dramas or other enjoyable programmes. There are many more of these than programmes mainly designed to teach. However, viewers can still learn from documentaries and news without making education their main goal, so entertainment and learning are not always completely separate.","phrases":[{"en":"entertainment","meaning":"Activities or programmes people enjoy in their free time."}],"question":"Do you think most people watch TV for education or for entertainment?","html":"Mostly for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">entertainment</strong>. After work, people often want to relax with talent shows, dramas or other enjoyable programmes. There are many more of these than programmes mainly designed to teach. However, viewers can still learn from documentaries and news without making education their main goal, so entertainment and learning are not always completely separate."},{"text":"Yes. Stories can help children think about behaviour and right and wrong, while science programmes can build interest in the world. Fairy tales such as Cinderella can carry useful messages. However, young children may follow the story without understanding the lesson, so an adult discussing it with them can make the experience more valuable.","phrases":[{"en":"carry useful messages","meaning":"Communicate ideas that people can learn from."}],"question":"Should TV play a role in educating children; how?","html":"Yes. Stories can help children think about behaviour and right and wrong, while science programmes can build interest in the world. Fairy tales such as Cinderella can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carry useful messages</strong>. However, young children may follow the story without understanding the lesson, so an adult discussing it with them can make the experience more valuable."},{"text":"Young children may watch talking animals, while teenagers often prefer action, sport or adventure. Adults may become more interested in news and politics; that happened to me. However, these are general patterns, not fixed rules. Some adults still enjoy cartoons or action films, and keeping those interests does not mean there is anything wrong with them.","phrases":[{"en":"general patterns","meaning":"Common tendencies that do not apply to every individual."}],"question":"How do you think TV viewing habits change as people get older?","html":"Young children may watch talking animals, while teenagers often prefer action, sport or adventure. Adults may become more interested in news and politics; that happened to me. However, these are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">general patterns</strong>, not fixed rules. Some adults still enjoy cartoons or action films, and keeping those interests does not mean there is anything wrong with them."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-049-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-049-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-049-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Whether cinema is gaining or losing ground","prompt":"Do you think the cinema has increased or decreased in popularity in recent years?\nIn your opinion, will this trend continue into the future?\nWhat are the advantages and disadvantages of making films of real-life events?","answer":"Q: Do you think the cinema has increased or decreased in popularity in recent years?\nA: Overall it's declined, and streaming is the obvious reason — for the price of one ticket you get a month of viewing at home. But it hasn't collapsed either. Big spectacle films still fill cinemas, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">premium formats</strong> with large screens and proper sound have actually grown. What's changed is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the occasion</strong>: going to the cinema used to be the default way to see a film, and now it's a deliberate outing that people plan.\n\nQ: In your opinion, will this trend continue into the future?\nA: I think so. Streaming will remain dominant for everyday viewing, because convenience nearly always wins. Cinemas will survive by offering what a living room genuinely can't — scale, sound, and the experience of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">watching with an audience</strong>, which does change how a comedy or a horror film feels. I'd expect fewer cinemas overall but better ones, used increasingly for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">live broadcasts and re-releases</strong> rather than only for new films on their opening weekend. Smaller independent cinemas are the ones I'd worry about, since they can't easily compete on either price or spectacle.\n\nQ: What are the advantages and disadvantages of making films of real-life events?\nA: The advantage is reach. A well-made film about a historical event will be seen by far more people than any book on the subject, and it can make something abstract feel immediate. The problem is that drama <strong class=\"key-phrase\">requires simplification</strong> — you need clear heroes, a narrative arc and often invented dialogue. Audiences then <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remember the film version as fact</strong>, and that's considerably more serious when the events are recent and the people involved are still alive to be misrepresented. It's not all bad, though — a film that gets a few details wrong can still send people off to read the real history afterwards, which a textbook alone rarely manages.","topic":"Film & Television","phrases":[{"en":"premium formats","context":"Big spectacle films still fill cinemas, and premium formats with large screens and proper sound have actually grown."},{"en":"the occasion","context":"What's changed is the occasion: going to the cinema used to be the default way to see a film, and now it's a deliberate outing that people plan."},{"en":"watching with an audience","context":"Cinemas will survive by offering what a living room genuinely can't — scale, sound, and the experience of watching with an audience, which does change how a comedy or a horror film feels."},{"en":"live broadcasts and re-releases","context":"I'd expect fewer cinemas overall but better ones, used increasingly for live broadcasts and re-releases rather than only for new films on their opening weekend."},{"en":"requires simplification","context":"The problem is that drama requires simplification — you need clear heroes, a narrative arc and often invented dialogue."},{"en":"remember the film version as fact","context":"Audiences then remember the film version as fact, and that's considerably more serious when the events are recent and the people involved are still alive to be misrepresented."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Film & Television","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-050","_displayNo":"#050","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-050-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-050-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-050-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I think cinema has become less common for everyday viewing because streaming offers many films for a low monthly cost. However, big films and large screens still attract audiences. The cinema has changed from the usual way to watch any film into a special outing that people choose and plan more carefully.","phrases":[{"en":"outing","meaning":"A trip away from home for enjoyment."}],"question":"Do you think the cinema has increased or decreased in popularity in recent years?","html":"I think cinema has become less common for everyday viewing because streaming offers many films for a low monthly cost. However, big films and large screens still attract audiences. The cinema has changed from the usual way to watch any film into a special <strong class=\"key-phrase\">outing</strong> that people choose and plan more carefully."},{"text":"Probably. Streaming is convenient, while cinemas will need to offer excellent sound, large screens and a shared experience. There may be fewer cinemas, but more special screenings and live broadcasts. Smaller independent cinemas could struggle because they cannot easily compete on either price or expensive technology, even if some audiences value their different choices.","phrases":[{"en":"independent cinemas","meaning":"Cinemas run separately from large commercial chains."}],"question":"In your opinion, will this trend continue into the future?","html":"Probably. Streaming is convenient, while cinemas will need to offer excellent sound, large screens and a shared experience. There may be fewer cinemas, but more special screenings and live broadcasts. Smaller <strong class=\"key-phrase\">independent cinemas</strong> could struggle because they cannot easily compete on either price or expensive technology, even if some audiences value their different choices."},{"text":"Films can introduce a historical event to many people and make it feel real. However, a clear dramatic story often needs shortened events or invented conversations. Viewers may then remember fiction as fact, which can unfairly affect real people. Still, an interesting film may encourage viewers to read the actual history afterwards, so it can start useful learning.","phrases":[{"en":"invented conversations","meaning":"Dialogue created by a writer rather than taken from a real record."}],"question":"What are the advantages and disadvantages of making films of real-life events?","html":"Films can introduce a historical event to many people and make it feel real. However, a clear dramatic story often needs shortened events or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">invented conversations</strong>. Viewers may then remember fiction as fact, which can unfairly affect real people. Still, an interesting film may encourage viewers to read the actual history afterwards, so it can start useful learning."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-050-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-050-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-050-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Feeding a guest, and popular food in your country","prompt":"If you invite a friend, what meal are you likely to offer them?\nWhat kinds of food are popular in your country?\nWhat are people's eating habits like in your country?","answer":"Q: If you invite a friend, what meal are you likely to offer them?\nA: Something shared, usually — grilled meat or tofu, rice, a couple of vegetable dishes and a soup, all placed in the middle for people to help themselves. That style works well because everyone simply takes what they want, so it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accommodates different tastes</strong> without my having to ask anybody in advance. It also means I'm not stuck in the kitchen all evening, which rather defeats the purpose of inviting somebody. The one time it doesn't work is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a serious allergy or dietary restriction</strong>, where I do still have to ask ahead rather than leave it to chance.\n\nQ: What kinds of food are popular in your country?\nA: Rice and noodle dishes are the foundation of almost every meal, and street food is enormously popular because it's cheap, fast and often better than what restaurants serve, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regional specialities</strong> matter a great deal too; people are quite proud about which province prepares a particular dish properly. Convenience food and international chains have grown steadily, especially among younger people, but traditional dishes still dominate because they're affordable and tied closely to cultural identity.\n\nQ: What are people's eating habits like in your country?\nA: They're changing in two opposite directions at once. On one side, people in cities eat far more processed food and order delivery constantly, mainly because working hours <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leave no time to cook</strong>. On the other, a growing group pays close attention to ingredients, buying organic produce and avoiding sugar. So you find health food shops opening on the same street as fast food outlets, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">both doing well</strong> at the same time. Older generations sit slightly outside both trends, mind, since plenty of grandparents cook the same simple, unprocessed meals they always have, largely untouched by either shift.","topic":"Food","phrases":[{"en":"accommodates different tastes","context":"That style works well because everyone simply takes what they want, so it accommodates different tastes without my having to ask anybody in advance."},{"en":"a serious allergy or dietary restriction","context":"The one time it doesn't work is a serious allergy or dietary restriction, where I do still have to ask ahead rather than leave it to chance."},{"en":"regional specialities","context":"Rice and noodle dishes are the foundation of almost every meal, and street food is enormously popular because it's cheap, fast and often better than what restaurants serve, and regional specialities matter a great deal too; people are quite proud about which province prepares a particular dish pr..."},{"en":"leave no time to cook","context":"On one side, people in cities eat far more processed food and order delivery constantly, mainly because working hours leave no time to cook."},{"en":"both doing well","context":"So you find health food shops opening on the same street as fast food outlets, and both doing well at the same time."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Food","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-051","_displayNo":"#051","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-051-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-051-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-051-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I'd offer shared dishes such as grilled meat or tofu, rice, vegetables and soup. Guests can choose what they enjoy, and I can spend time with them instead of cooking all evening. However, I would ask about serious allergies or dietary needs beforehand. A flexible meal is useful, but it does not replace checking important restrictions.","phrases":[{"en":"dietary needs","meaning":"Particular requirements about what a person can or should eat."}],"question":"If you invite a friend, what meal are you likely to offer them?","html":"I&#x27;d offer shared dishes such as grilled meat or tofu, rice, vegetables and soup. Guests can choose what they enjoy, and I can spend time with them instead of cooking all evening. However, I would ask about serious allergies or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dietary needs</strong> beforehand. A flexible meal is useful, but it does not replace checking important restrictions."},{"text":"Rice and noodles are central, and street food is popular because it is quick, affordable and often very good. Different regions also take pride in their special dishes. International chains and convenience foods are growing, especially among younger people. Even so, traditional meals remain common because they are affordable and closely connected with local culture.","phrases":[{"en":"take pride in","meaning":"Feel pleased and proud about something connected with you."}],"question":"What kinds of food are popular in your country?","html":"Rice and noodles are central, and street food is popular because it is quick, affordable and often very good. Different regions also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take pride in</strong> their special dishes. International chains and convenience foods are growing, especially among younger people. Even so, traditional meals remain common because they are affordable and closely connected with local culture."},{"text":"Some city residents eat more processed meals and order food because work leaves little time to cook. Others are becoming more careful about ingredients and avoiding too much sugar. Both trends can happen in the same area. Many older people, meanwhile, continue preparing the same simple meals they have always eaten, outside either new trend.","phrases":[{"en":"processed meals","meaning":"Food changed or prepared in a factory before being sold."}],"question":"What are people's eating habits like in your country?","html":"Some city residents eat more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">processed meals</strong> and order food because work leaves little time to cook. Others are becoming more careful about ingredients and avoiding too much sugar. Both trends can happen in the same area. Many older people, meanwhile, continue preparing the same simple meals they have always eaten, outside either new trend."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-051-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-051-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-051-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"The qualities that matter in a friend","prompt":"What do you think are the most important qualities for friends to have?\nHow important do you think it is for a person to spend some time alone?","answer":"Q: What do you think are the most important qualities for friends to have?\nA: The most important qualities are honesty, reliability and mutual respect. Shared interests make a friendship enjoyable, but trust is what makes it durable. A close friend should be able to listen, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tell the truth tactfully</strong> and remain dependable when circumstances become difficult. That last part is really the test: plenty of people are good company while everything's going well, but a genuine friend is the one who still answers the phone when you're the one with the problem. That doesn't mean every friendship needs to survive a crisis to count, though — <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a lighter, purely social friendship</strong> is still perfectly valid, just a different kind of relationship.\n\nQ: How important do you think it is for a person to spend some time alone?\nA: Spending some time alone is essential because it allows people to process experiences and recover from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">constant social demands</strong>. Even a short walk or quiet coffee can create space for reflection. Solitude becomes unhealthy only when it turns into <strong class=\"key-phrase\">persistent isolation</strong>. I'd add that people differ enormously in how much they need: some <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recover their energy in company</strong> and find being alone draining, whereas others can't think clearly until the room's quiet. Neither one is a flaw; the mistake is assuming everyone works the same way.","topic":"Friendship","phrases":[{"en":"tell the truth tactfully","context":"A close friend should be able to listen, tell the truth tactfully and remain dependable when circumstances become difficult."},{"en":"a lighter, purely social friendship","context":"That doesn't mean every friendship needs to survive a crisis to count, though — a lighter, purely social friendship is still perfectly valid, just a different kind of relationship."},{"en":"constant social demands","context":"Spending some time alone is essential because it allows people to process experiences and recover from constant social demands."},{"en":"persistent isolation","context":"Solitude becomes unhealthy only when it turns into persistent isolation."},{"en":"recover their energy in company","context":"I'd add that people differ enormously in how much they need: some recover their energy in company and find being alone draining, whereas others can't think clearly until the room's quiet."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Friendship","id":"speaking-part-3-052","_displayNo":"#052","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-052-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-052-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Honesty, reliability and respect matter most. Similar interests make a friendship enjoyable, but trust helps it last. A close friend listens and supports you when things are difficult, not just when life is fun. However, lighter friendships still have value. Not every person we enjoy spending time with needs to become someone we rely on in a crisis.","phrases":[{"en":"reliability","meaning":"The quality of being trustworthy and dependable."}],"question":"What do you think are the most important qualities for friends to have?","html":"Honesty, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reliability</strong> and respect matter most. Similar interests make a friendship enjoyable, but trust helps it last. A close friend listens and supports you when things are difficult, not just when life is fun. However, lighter friendships still have value. Not every person we enjoy spending time with needs to become someone we rely on in a crisis."},{"text":"Some time alone helps people rest, understand their experiences and think clearly. A quiet walk or coffee may be enough. However, being alone too much can become unhealthy isolation. People also need different amounts: some recover energy with friends, while others need silence. I think the right balance matters more than expecting everyone to prefer the same thing.","phrases":[{"en":"isolation","meaning":"Being separated from other people or having very little contact."}],"question":"How important do you think it is for a person to spend some time alone?","html":"Some time alone helps people rest, understand their experiences and think clearly. A quiet walk or coffee may be enough. However, being alone too much can become unhealthy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">isolation</strong>. People also need different amounts: some recover energy with friends, while others need silence. I think the right balance matters more than expecting everyone to prefer the same thing."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-052-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-052-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"How people meet friends, and who finds it easier","prompt":"How do people usually meet new friends where you live?\nIs it easier for adults or children to make new friends and why?\nHow are friendships different from relationships at work or college?","answer":"Q: How do people usually meet new friends where you live?\nA: School and university are where most lasting friendships start, simply because you spend years in the same place with the same people. After that it's work, mutual friends and shared activities such as sports clubs or classes, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shared activity</strong> works particularly well because you're not obliged to make conversation from nothing; there's an obvious thing to talk about. Online communities matter too, though those friendships usually need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">move offline to last</strong> — a group chat on its own rarely turns into a close friendship.\n\nQ: Is it easier for adults or children to make new friends and why?\nA: Children, clearly. They spend <strong class=\"key-phrase\">repeated unstructured time</strong> with the same group, which is exactly the condition friendship needs, and they haven't yet learned to be cautious — a child can meet someone in a park and be friends within an hour. Adults have the opposite situation: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fixed schedules and established social circles</strong>, plus a self-consciousness about appearing to need people. So adult friendship generally requires deliberate, repeated effort, and a great many people simply never get round to making it.\n\nQ: How are friendships different from relationships at work or college?\nA: The essential difference is that friendship is voluntary and has no other purpose. At work you're placed with people you didn't choose, and there's a structure around the relationship — someone evaluates someone else, and there are things you can't say honestly. Colleagues can absolutely become genuine friends, but usually only once <strong class=\"key-phrase\">that structure is removed</strong>, or after one of you leaves the organisation. Until then there's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a natural ceiling on the trust</strong>.","topic":"Friendship","phrases":[{"en":"shared activity","context":"After that it's work, mutual friends and shared activities such as sports clubs or classes, and shared activity works particularly well because you're not obliged to make conversation from nothing; there's an obvious thing to talk about."},{"en":"move offline to last","context":"Online communities matter too, though those friendships usually need to move offline to last — a group chat on its own rarely turns into a close friendship."},{"en":"repeated unstructured time","context":"They spend repeated unstructured time with the same group, which is exactly the condition friendship needs, and they haven't yet learned to be cautious — a child can meet someone in a park and be friends within an hour."},{"en":"fixed schedules and established social circles","context":"Adults have the opposite situation: fixed schedules and established social circles, plus a self-consciousness about appearing to need people."},{"en":"that structure is removed","context":"Colleagues can absolutely become genuine friends, but usually only once that structure is removed, or after one of you leaves the organisation."},{"en":"a natural ceiling on the trust","context":"Until then there's a natural ceiling on the trust."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Friendship","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-053","_displayNo":"#053","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-053-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-053-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-053-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Many friendships begin at school or university because people spend a lot of time together. Adults also meet through work, mutual friends and shared activities such as classes. An activity gives them an easy subject to discuss. Online groups can help too, although regular personal contact often helps those connections become closer and last longer.","phrases":[{"en":"mutual friends","meaning":"People who are friends with both of you."}],"question":"How do people usually meet new friends where you live?","html":"Many friendships begin at school or university because people spend a lot of time together. Adults also meet through work, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mutual friends</strong> and shared activities such as classes. An activity gives them an easy subject to discuss. Online groups can help too, although regular personal contact often helps those connections become closer and last longer."},{"text":"Usually children, because they have repeated free time together and worry less about making an impression. Adults have work schedules, existing friendship groups and sometimes feel embarrassed about needing company. Making new friends as an adult therefore takes deliberate effort and repeated meetings. It is possible, but the right conditions do not happen as automatically.","phrases":[{"en":"making an impression","meaning":"Trying to affect how other people see or judge you."}],"question":"Is it easier for adults or children to make new friends and why?","html":"Usually children, because they have repeated free time together and worry less about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">making an impression</strong>. Adults have work schedules, existing friendship groups and sometimes feel embarrassed about needing company. Making new friends as an adult therefore takes deliberate effort and repeated meetings. It is possible, but the right conditions do not happen as automatically."},{"text":"Friendship is chosen freely, while work or college relationships often begin because people are placed together. At work, responsibilities and power differences may limit what people can say honestly. Colleagues can become close friends, but it may be easier after changing jobs. Until then, professional roles can create a limit on openness and trust.","phrases":[{"en":"openness","meaning":"Willingness to share thoughts and feelings honestly."}],"question":"How are friendships different from relationships at work or college?","html":"Friendship is chosen freely, while work or college relationships often begin because people are placed together. At work, responsibilities and power differences may limit what people can say honestly. Colleagues can become close friends, but it may be easier after changing jobs. Until then, professional roles can create a limit on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">openness</strong> and trust."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-053-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-053-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-053-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Presents children ask for, and advertising to children","prompt":"What kinds of presents do children ask for today?\nWhat effect has advertising had on children's preferences?\nIs there a case for banning advertising directed at children?","answer":"Q: What kinds of presents do children ask for today?\nA: Mostly electronics and things connected to them — phones, tablets, games and in-game purchases, which parents often find harder to understand than a physical toy. Branded clothing and trainers matter a great deal to older children. What's changed is where the requests come from: children now ask for specific items they've seen an influencer using, so the demand arrives <strong class=\"key-phrase\">already attached to a brand</strong> rather than to a general idea of what they'd enjoy. It's not universal, though — plenty of younger children still just want <strong class=\"key-phrase\">whatever toy a friend happens to have</strong>, brand aside.\n\nQ: What effect has advertising had on children's preferences?\nA: It's made them extraordinarily brand-aware — young children can identify logos before they can read. The more serious effect concerns younger children specifically, because below about eight they don't reliably understand that an advertisement is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trying to sell them something</strong>. They take the claims as straightforward fact. Repeated exposure then turns something they'd never heard of into something they feel they need, and that pressure lands on the parents. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Older children are more resistant</strong>, since by their teens most can recognise persuasion for what it is, even if they don't always act on that awareness.\n\nQ: Is there a case for banning advertising directed at children?\nA: There's a strong case for restricting it, particularly for unhealthy food and for games using <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gambling-like mechanisms</strong>. The argument is straightforward: advertising assumes an audience capable of evaluating a claim, and small children aren't. A total ban would be difficult to enforce given how much content crosses borders online, so I'd focus regulation on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the youngest age groups</strong> and the most harmful categories rather than attempting to prohibit everything.","topic":"Gifts","phrases":[{"en":"already attached to a brand","context":"What's changed is where the requests come from: children now ask for specific items they've seen an influencer using, so the demand arrives already attached to a brand rather than to a general idea of what they'd enjoy."},{"en":"whatever toy a friend happens to have","context":"It's not universal, though — plenty of younger children still just want whatever toy a friend happens to have, brand aside."},{"en":"trying to sell them something","context":"The more serious effect concerns younger children specifically, because below about eight they don't reliably understand that an advertisement is trying to sell them something."},{"en":"Older children are more resistant","context":"Older children are more resistant, since by their teens most can recognise persuasion for what it is, even if they don't always act on that awareness."},{"en":"gambling-like mechanisms","context":"There's a strong case for restricting it, particularly for unhealthy food and for games using gambling-like mechanisms."},{"en":"the youngest age groups","context":"A total ban would be difficult to enforce given how much content crosses borders online, so I'd focus regulation on the youngest age groups and the most harmful categories rather than attempting to prohibit everything."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Gifts","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-054","_displayNo":"#054","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-054-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-054-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-054-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Many ask for phones, tablets, games and online game items. Older children may want branded clothes or trainers. Influencers can create demand for a particular brand instead of just a type of toy. However, younger children often still want something simply because a friend has it, so technology and branding do not explain every request.","phrases":[{"en":"demand","meaning":"The desire to buy or obtain a product."}],"question":"What kinds of presents do children ask for today?","html":"Many ask for phones, tablets, games and online game items. Older children may want branded clothes or trainers. Influencers can create <strong class=\"key-phrase\">demand</strong> for a particular brand instead of just a type of toy. However, younger children often still want something simply because a friend has it, so technology and branding do not explain every request."},{"text":"Adverts make children recognise brands early and can turn an unfamiliar product into something they feel they need. Younger children may believe claims without understanding that someone is trying to sell to them. This creates pressure on parents. Older children usually recognise advertising more easily, though knowing its purpose does not always stop it influencing them.","phrases":[{"en":"claims","meaning":"Statements saying that something is true or has a particular benefit."}],"question":"What effect has advertising had on children's preferences?","html":"Adverts make children recognise brands early and can turn an unfamiliar product into something they feel they need. Younger children may believe <strong class=\"key-phrase\">claims</strong> without understanding that someone is trying to sell to them. This creates pressure on parents. Older children usually recognise advertising more easily, though knowing its purpose does not always stop it influencing them."},{"text":"I support strong limits, especially for unhealthy food and games that encourage gambling-like spending. Young children cannot judge advertising claims as well as adults. A complete ban would be difficult online because content comes from different countries. I would focus on the youngest audiences and the most harmful products, where protection is most clearly needed.","phrases":[{"en":"limits","meaning":"Rules controlling how much something can happen or what is allowed."}],"question":"Is there a case for banning advertising directed at children?","html":"I support strong <strong class=\"key-phrase\">limits</strong>, especially for unhealthy food and games that encourage gambling-like spending. Young children cannot judge advertising claims as well as adults. A complete ban would be difficult online because content comes from different countries. I would focus on the youngest audiences and the most harmful products, where protection is most clearly needed."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-054-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-054-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-054-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"What the past teaches, and what may come next","prompt":"What do you think we can learn by studying events of the past?\nWhat important events do you think might take place in the future?","answer":"Q: What do you think we can learn by studying events of the past?\nA: I think we can learn a lot by studying history. Just as individual people learn from their mistakes, societies can learn from the mistakes made by previous governments or leaders. For example, from what I've read in the newspapers, many economists are looking back to the time of the Great Depression, around 80 years ago, in order to understand the financial crisis that is currently affecting many countries around the world. Even if we don't always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">learn from mistakes</strong>, I think it's fascinating to study history because it gives us <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an insight into who we are</strong> and where we come from.\n\nQ: What important events do you think might take place in the future?\nA: It's really difficult to predict what will happen in the future; most of the big, historic events of the past would have been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">impossible to foresee</strong>. For example, I don't think that anyone living 100 years ago could have imagined that people would one day walk on the moon. That's not true of every kind of change, though — <strong class=\"key-phrase\">slow trends like an ageing population</strong> are fairly predictable decades in advance; it's the sudden breakthroughs and shocks that catch everyone out. If I had to guess, I'd like to think that scientists will <strong class=\"key-phrase\">invent cures for diseases</strong> like cancer, and we'll all live longer.","topic":"History","phrases":[{"en":"learn from mistakes","context":"Even if we don't always learn from mistakes, I think it's fascinating to study history because it gives us an insight into who we are and where we come from."},{"en":"an insight into who we are","context":"Even if we don't always learn from mistakes, I think it's fascinating to study history because it gives us an insight into who we are and where we come from."},{"en":"impossible to foresee","context":"It's really difficult to predict what will happen in the future; most of the big, historic events of the past would have been impossible to foresee."},{"en":"slow trends like an ageing population","context":"That's not true of every kind of change, though — slow trends like an ageing population are fairly predictable decades in advance; it's the sudden breakthroughs and shocks that catch everyone out."},{"en":"invent cures for diseases","context":"If I had to guess, I'd like to think that scientists will invent cures for diseases like cancer, and we'll all live longer."}],"subtype":"","summary":"History","id":"speaking-part-3-055","_displayNo":"#055","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-055-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-055-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"History helps us understand earlier mistakes and how societies developed. Governments can compare past financial crises or conflicts with present problems, rather than assume everything is new. We do not always use the lessons well, but studying them still gives useful context. History also helps explain who we are and how our communities came to be.","phrases":[{"en":"context","meaning":"Background information that helps explain a situation."}],"question":"What do you think we can learn by studying events of the past?","html":"History helps us understand earlier mistakes and how societies developed. Governments can compare past financial crises or conflicts with present problems, rather than assume everything is new. We do not always use the lessons well, but studying them still gives useful <strong class=\"key-phrase\">context</strong>. History also helps explain who we are and how our communities came to be."},{"text":"It is very hard to predict major events. People in the distant past could not easily imagine later achievements such as walking on the moon. Slow changes, like an ageing population, are easier to expect than sudden discoveries or crises. I hope future science will find better treatments for serious diseases and help people live longer.","phrases":[{"en":"an ageing population","meaning":"A population in which older people form a growing share."}],"question":"What important events do you think might take place in the future?","html":"It is very hard to predict major events. People in the distant past could not easily imagine later achievements such as walking on the moon. Slow changes, like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an ageing population</strong>, are easier to expect than sudden discoveries or crises. I hope future science will find better treatments for serious diseases and help people live longer."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-055-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-055-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Whether studying history prevents repeated mistakes","prompt":"Studying history can help countries to avoid repeating past mistakes. Do you agree or not?\nHow can studying your own country's history help you understand present-day situations?\nHow important do you consider it to study the history of other countries?","answer":"Q: Studying history can help countries to avoid repeating past mistakes. Do you agree or not?\nA: I agree in principle, though the record suggests it works less often than it should. History does reveal patterns — how financial crises build, how conflicts escalate, what happens when a country isolates itself economically. The difficulty is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recognising which past situation actually resembles the present one</strong>, because leaders tend to select the analogy that supports what they'd already decided to do anyway. So the knowledge only helps where there's a genuine willingness to be corrected.\n\nQ: How can studying your own country's history help you understand present-day situations?\nA: It explains things that otherwise look arbitrary — why institutions are organised as they are, why particular regions are wealthier, why certain political arguments recur every generation. Without that background, current events seem to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">come from nowhere</strong>. The important condition is that it's taught critically, including the uncomfortable episodes. A national history presented purely as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a patriotic story</strong> doesn't explain the present; it simply produces people who are easy to mislead about it.\n\nQ: How important do you consider it to study the history of other countries?\nA: Very important, because no country developed on its own. Trade, migration and war connected societies long before anyone used the word globalisation, and you can't understand your own country's position without that. There's a second benefit as well: reading how another society understands the same events is the fastest cure for assuming that your own version is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">simply the truth</strong> rather than one account among several. Nobody can study every country in depth, of course, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the practical priority</strong> should be the histories most entangled with your own — former colonisers, close neighbours, major trading partners — rather than an impossible attempt at everything.","topic":"History","phrases":[{"en":"recognising which past situation actually resembles the present one","context":"The difficulty is recognising which past situation actually resembles the present one, because leaders tend to select the analogy that supports what they'd already decided to do anyway."},{"en":"come from nowhere","context":"Without that background, current events seem to come from nowhere."},{"en":"a patriotic story","context":"A national history presented purely as a patriotic story doesn't explain the present; it simply produces people who are easy to mislead about it."},{"en":"simply the truth","context":"There's a second benefit as well: reading how another society understands the same events is the fastest cure for assuming that your own version is simply the truth rather than one account among several."},{"en":"the practical priority","context":"Nobody can study every country in depth, of course, so the practical priority should be the histories most entangled with your own — former colonisers, close neighbours, major trading partners — rather than an impossible attempt at everything."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"History","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-056","_displayNo":"#056","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-056-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-056-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-056-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I agree that it can help, but only if people use it honestly. History shows how crises and conflicts develop. The difficulty is deciding whether a past situation really matches the present. Leaders may choose only examples supporting their existing plans. Lessons are useful when people are willing to question their own views, not just defend them.","phrases":[{"en":"existing plans","meaning":"Plans that have already been made."}],"question":"Studying history can help countries to avoid repeating past mistakes. Do you agree or not?","html":"I agree that it can help, but only if people use it honestly. History shows how crises and conflicts develop. The difficulty is deciding whether a past situation really matches the present. Leaders may choose only examples supporting their <strong class=\"key-phrase\">existing plans</strong>. Lessons are useful when people are willing to question their own views, not just defend them."},{"text":"It explains why institutions, regions and political arguments are the way they are. Without that background, current events can seem to appear from nowhere. However, history needs to include mistakes and uncomfortable events, not only achievements. A story designed just to make people proud may hide information they need to understand their country today.","phrases":[{"en":"institutions","meaning":"Established organisations or systems that shape public life."}],"question":"How can studying your own country's history help you understand present-day situations?","html":"It explains why <strong class=\"key-phrase\">institutions</strong>, regions and political arguments are the way they are. Without that background, current events can seem to appear from nowhere. However, history needs to include mistakes and uncomfortable events, not only achievements. A story designed just to make people proud may hide information they need to understand their country today."},{"text":"It is very important because trade, migration and war have connected countries for centuries. Another country's account can also challenge our own assumptions about the same event. Nobody can study every place deeply, so I would start with neighbours, major trading partners and countries closely linked to our history. That makes learning both useful and manageable.","phrases":[{"en":"assumptions","meaning":"Ideas accepted as true without being fully checked."}],"question":"How important do you consider it to study the history of other countries?","html":"It is very important because trade, migration and war have connected countries for centuries. Another country&#x27;s account can also challenge our own <strong class=\"key-phrase\">assumptions</strong> about the same event. Nobody can study every place deeply, so I would start with neighbours, major trading partners and countries closely linked to our history. That makes learning both useful and manageable."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-056-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-056-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-056-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"How the design of homes will change","prompt":"How do you think the design of homes will change in the future?","answer":"Q: How do you think the design of homes will change in the future?\nA: I think the exterior will stay fairly conventional, because people are quite conservative about how a house should look, but the inside will change considerably. The main driver will be energy costs, so better insulation, solar panels and heat pumps will become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">standard rather than optional</strong>. Space will also be used more flexibly, with rooms that serve as an office during the day and something else in the evening, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the shift to home working</strong> has made necessary. And with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ageing populations</strong>, I'd expect far more single-storey layouts and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">step-free access</strong>. New builds will lead the way; the millions of older houses already standing won't get any of this unless someone pays to retrofit them, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">retrofitting is far more expensive</strong> than designing it in from the start.","topic":"Housing & Architecture","phrases":[{"en":"standard rather than optional","context":"The main driver will be energy costs, so better insulation, solar panels and heat pumps will become standard rather than optional."},{"en":"the shift to home working","context":"Space will also be used more flexibly, with rooms that serve as an office during the day and something else in the evening, which the shift to home working has made necessary."},{"en":"ageing populations","context":"And with ageing populations, I'd expect far more single-storey layouts and step-free access."},{"en":"step-free access","context":"And with ageing populations, I'd expect far more single-storey layouts and step-free access."},{"en":"retrofitting is far more expensive","context":"New builds will lead the way; the millions of older houses already standing won't get any of this unless someone pays to retrofit them, and retrofitting is far more expensive than designing it in from the start."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Housing & Architecture","id":"speaking-part-3-057","_displayNo":"#057","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-057-q01.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Homes may look familiar outside but change more inside. Higher energy costs could encourage better insulation and clean heating, while rooms may serve several purposes, such as work and relaxation. Older populations may need fewer stairs and easier entrances. New homes can include these features from the start; improving existing houses may be slower because it costs more.","phrases":[{"en":"insulation","meaning":"Material that helps stop heat escaping or entering a building."}],"question":"How do you think the design of homes will change in the future?","html":"Homes may look familiar outside but change more inside. Higher energy costs could encourage better <strong class=\"key-phrase\">insulation</strong> and clean heating, while rooms may serve several purposes, such as work and relaxation. Older populations may need fewer stairs and easier entrances. New homes can include these features from the start; improving existing houses may be slower because it costs more."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-057-q01.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Renting or buying, and leaving the family home","prompt":"Do you think it is better to rent or to buy a place to live in?\nDo you agree that there is a right age for young adults to stop living with their parents?","answer":"Q: Do you think it is better to rent or to buy a place to live in?\nA: I think it's better to buy your own home if you can afford to do that. Buying your own home is a better investment <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the long term</strong>, because you can sell the home and perhaps even <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make a profit on</strong> it. The alternative is to pay rent to a landlord who can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ask you to leave at any moment</strong>. The difficulty is that deposits have become enormous relative to wages, so a great many people can't realistically buy however sensible it might be.\n\nQ: Do you agree that there is a right age for young adults to stop living with their parents?\nA: There's no universal age at which young adults should leave home. Financial independence, housing costs, education and family culture all matter. The important point is that adults gradually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take responsibility for</strong> money and daily decisions, whether they live alone at eighteen or remain with their parents longer. It's also worth saying that staying at home much longer is completely normal in many cultures, and nobody there regards it as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a failure to grow up</strong>. That judgement is quite specific to certain Western countries.","topic":"Housing & Architecture","phrases":[{"en":"in the long term","context":"Buying your own home is a better investment in the long term, because you can sell the home and perhaps even make a profit on it."},{"en":"make a profit on","context":"Buying your own home is a better investment in the long term, because you can sell the home and perhaps even make a profit on it."},{"en":"ask you to leave at any moment","context":"The alternative is to pay rent to a landlord who can ask you to leave at any moment."},{"en":"take responsibility for","context":"The important point is that adults gradually take responsibility for money and daily decisions, whether they live alone at eighteen or remain with their parents longer."},{"en":"a failure to grow up","context":"It's also worth saying that staying at home much longer is completely normal in many cultures, and nobody there regards it as a failure to grow up."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Housing & Architecture","id":"speaking-part-3-058","_displayNo":"#058","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-058-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-058-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I prefer buying if the cost is manageable. It gives more long-term control, and the home may keep or increase its value. Renting means paying an owner and having less certainty about staying. However, buying requires a large deposit, which many wages cannot support. So although ownership appeals to me, it is not a realistic choice for everyone.","phrases":[{"en":"deposit","meaning":"An initial payment made before paying the rest of a purchase price."}],"question":"Do you think it is better to rent or to buy a place to live in?","html":"I prefer buying if the cost is manageable. It gives more long-term control, and the home may keep or increase its value. Renting means paying an owner and having less certainty about staying. However, buying requires a large <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deposit</strong>, which many wages cannot support. So although ownership appeals to me, it is not a realistic choice for everyone."},{"text":"No, there is no right age for everyone. Money, education, housing costs and culture all affect the decision. More importantly, young adults should gradually learn to manage responsibilities and make choices. They can do that while living with parents too. In many cultures, staying home longer is normal rather than a sign that someone has failed to mature.","phrases":[{"en":"mature","meaning":"Become more responsible and able to manage adult life."}],"question":"Do you agree that there is a right age for young adults to stop living with their parents?","html":"No, there is no right age for everyone. Money, education, housing costs and culture all affect the decision. More importantly, young adults should gradually learn to manage responsibilities and make choices. They can do that while living with parents too. In many cultures, staying home longer is normal rather than a sign that someone has failed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mature</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-058-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-058-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Protecting historic buildings and what they bring an area","prompt":"How do people in your country feel about protecting historic buildings?\nDo you think an area can benefit from having an interesting historic place locally, and in what way?\nWhat do you think will happen to historic places or buildings in the future?","answer":"Q: How do people in your country feel about protecting historic buildings?\nA: Most people support it in principle, since nobody argues that heritage should simply be demolished. The disagreement starts when there's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a cost attached</strong>. Restoration is expensive, and when public money is involved, or when protection blocks housing that people badly need, opinion divides quickly. What I've noticed is that support is much stronger where old buildings remain in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">everyday use</strong> as cafes, offices or homes, rather than being preserved as untouchable monuments nobody enters.\n\nQ: Do you think an area can benefit from having an interesting historic place locally, and in what way?\nA: Yes, in several ways. The economic benefit is obvious: visitors spend money on food, accommodation and transport, and that supports jobs which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot easily be moved elsewhere</strong>. There's a less measurable benefit too — a distinctive local landmark gives residents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">something to identify with</strong>, particularly in places that have lost their traditional industry. The important condition is that some of the tourist revenue goes back into maintaining the site and the surrounding streets, which doesn't always happen.\n\nQ: What do you think will happen to historic places or buildings in the future?\nA: I'd expect the picture to divide. Famous sites will be better protected than ever, because they generate income and attract political attention, though over-tourism will force them to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">limit visitor numbers</strong>. Less celebrated buildings are the vulnerable ones, since ordinary old housing gets demolished quietly because nobody campaigns for it. Digital recording will improve enormously, but a detailed scan <strong class=\"key-phrase\">documents a building rather than preserving it</strong>, and no virtual model replaces standing inside the real thing.","topic":"Housing & Architecture","phrases":[{"en":"a cost attached","context":"The disagreement starts when there's a cost attached."},{"en":"everyday use","context":"What I've noticed is that support is much stronger where old buildings remain in everyday use as cafes, offices or homes, rather than being preserved as untouchable monuments nobody enters."},{"en":"cannot easily be moved elsewhere","context":"The economic benefit is obvious: visitors spend money on food, accommodation and transport, and that supports jobs which cannot easily be moved elsewhere."},{"en":"something to identify with","context":"There's a less measurable benefit too — a distinctive local landmark gives residents something to identify with, particularly in places that have lost their traditional industry."},{"en":"limit visitor numbers","context":"Famous sites will be better protected than ever, because they generate income and attract political attention, though over-tourism will force them to limit visitor numbers."},{"en":"documents a building rather than preserving it","context":"Digital recording will improve enormously, but a detailed scan documents a building rather than preserving it, and no virtual model replaces standing inside the real thing."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Housing & Architecture","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-059","_displayNo":"#059","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-059-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-059-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-059-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Most support protecting them until the cost becomes clear. Repairing old buildings can be expensive, and protection may compete with the need for new homes. Support often grows when historic buildings remain useful as cafes, offices or houses. I think people find it easier to value heritage when it is still part of everyday life.","phrases":[{"en":"heritage","meaning":"Buildings, traditions or objects inherited from the past."}],"question":"How do people in your country feel about protecting historic buildings?","html":"Most support protecting them until the cost becomes clear. Repairing old buildings can be expensive, and protection may compete with the need for new homes. Support often grows when historic buildings remain useful as cafes, offices or houses. I think people find it easier to value <strong class=\"key-phrase\">heritage</strong> when it is still part of everyday life."},{"text":"Yes, visitors spend money locally, supporting jobs in food, accommodation and transport. A historic place can also give residents a shared identity and something to feel proud of. However, some of the income needs to return to the site and surrounding area. Otherwise, the place attracts visitors without receiving enough care to remain enjoyable or safe.","phrases":[{"en":"accommodation","meaning":"A place where people stay or live."}],"question":"Do you think an area can benefit from having an interesting historic place locally, and in what way?","html":"Yes, visitors spend money locally, supporting jobs in food, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accommodation</strong> and transport. A historic place can also give residents a shared identity and something to feel proud of. However, some of the income needs to return to the site and surrounding area. Otherwise, the place attracts visitors without receiving enough care to remain enjoyable or safe."},{"text":"Famous places will probably receive more protection because they attract money and attention, although visitor numbers may need limits. Ordinary old buildings are at greater risk because fewer people campaign for them. Digital records can preserve information, but a scan cannot replace a real building. I expect better recording alongside an uneven picture of actual protection.","phrases":[{"en":"uneven picture","meaning":"A situation in which some things improve or receive support more than others."}],"question":"What do you think will happen to historic places or buildings in the future?","html":"Famous places will probably receive more protection because they attract money and attention, although visitor numbers may need limits. Ordinary old buildings are at greater risk because fewer people campaign for them. Digital records can preserve information, but a scan cannot replace a real building. I expect better recording alongside an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">uneven picture</strong> of actual protection."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-059-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-059-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-059-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Why holidays matter, and why some people stay at home","prompt":"Do you think that it is important for people to go on holiday?\nWhy do you think some people prefer not to go abroad on holiday?","answer":"Q: Do you think that it is important for people to go on holiday?\nA: Yes, I think we all need to go on holiday at least once or twice a year. It isn't healthy to work all year round without some time off to relax; we all need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take a break</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recharge our batteries</strong> from time to time. Last summer, for example, I went on holiday to France for a couple of weeks, and it was great to leave all of my usual responsibilities behind me — I came home feeling properly refreshed. It doesn't have to mean going abroad, though; <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a quiet week at home</strong> away from work emails can do almost the same job for a fraction of the cost.\n\nQ: Why do you think some people prefer not to go abroad on holiday?\nA: Cost is usually the biggest factor — it's simply more expensive to fly somewhere and stay in a hotel than to holiday at home, especially for a family. There's also the stress of a foreign country where you don't speak the language and can't easily <strong class=\"key-phrase\">integrate with the locals</strong>, which some people would rather avoid altogether. And plenty genuinely just love where they live and don't feel any pull to leave it. That's not the full story for everyone, though — some people avoid flying specifically because of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the environmental impact</strong>, which has very little to do with cost or comfort at all.","topic":"Leisure & Holidays","phrases":[{"en":"take a break","context":"It isn't healthy to work all year round without some time off to relax; we all need to take a break and recharge our batteries from time to time."},{"en":"recharge our batteries","context":"It isn't healthy to work all year round without some time off to relax; we all need to take a break and recharge our batteries from time to time."},{"en":"a quiet week at home","context":"It doesn't have to mean going abroad, though; a quiet week at home away from work emails can do almost the same job for a fraction of the cost."},{"en":"integrate with the locals","context":"There's also the stress of a foreign country where you don't speak the language and can't easily integrate with the locals, which some people would rather avoid altogether."},{"en":"the environmental impact","context":"That's not the full story for everyone, though — some people avoid flying specifically because of the environmental impact, which has very little to do with cost or comfort at all."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Leisure & Holidays","id":"speaking-part-3-060","_displayNo":"#060","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-060-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-060-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, people need breaks to rest rather than work all year. A holiday can remove normal responsibilities and help us return with more energy; a trip to France did that for me. However, it does not have to mean travelling abroad. A quiet week at home without work messages can provide many of the same benefits for less money.","phrases":[{"en":"responsibilities","meaning":"Duties or tasks you are expected to manage."}],"question":"Do you think that it is important for people to go on holiday?","html":"Yes, people need breaks to rest rather than work all year. A holiday can remove normal <strong class=\"key-phrase\">responsibilities</strong> and help us return with more energy; a trip to France did that for me. However, it does not have to mean travelling abroad. A quiet week at home without work messages can provide many of the same benefits for less money."},{"text":"Cost is one reason, especially for families paying for travel and hotels. Some people also dislike unfamiliar languages or the stress of a foreign place. Others genuinely enjoy their local area and feel no need to leave. Environmental concerns can matter too, so staying home is not always only about money or comfort.","phrases":[{"en":"Environmental concerns","meaning":"Worries about damage to nature or the climate."}],"question":"Why do you think some people prefer not to go abroad on holiday?","html":"Cost is one reason, especially for families paying for travel and hotels. Some people also dislike unfamiliar languages or the stress of a foreign place. Others genuinely enjoy their local area and feel no need to leave. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Environmental concerns</strong> can matter too, so staying home is not always only about money or comfort."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-060-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-060-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Leisure and the economy","prompt":"What is the relationship between leisure and the economy?\nHow does the economy benefit from people's leisure activities?\nDo men and women enjoy the same type of leisure activities?","answer":"Q: What is the relationship between leisure and the economy?\nA: Well, people spend a huge amount of money on all sorts of leisure activities, so I'd say leisure is a genuinely important part of the economy in most countries. Leisure could be anything people do in their free time — eating out, going to the cinema, watching a football match, or staying in a hotel — and millions of people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make a living from</strong> these industries. What I'd add, though, is that leisure spending is one of the first things households cut when money's tight, so it's also one of the more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exposed parts of the economy</strong>.\n\nQ: How does the economy benefit from people's leisure activities?\nA: The leisure industry makes a huge contribution to the economy. It keeps millions of people in employment, and those employees pay tax and spend their wages on other goods and services, so the money keeps moving round the economy rather than sitting still. Tourism works the same way but on a bigger scale, because it pulls in money from outside the country altogether. The one qualification I'd make is that a lot of leisure jobs are seasonal or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">poorly paid</strong>, so the economy benefits even when the people doing the work don't benefit all that much.\n\nQ: Do men and women enjoy the same type of leisure activities?\nA: Yes and no. I think both men and women enjoy things like eating in restaurants or going to the cinema, but I'm sure there are other activities that are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more popular with one gender</strong>. For example, I think more men than women go to football matches. I'd be careful about generalising, though, because those differences seem to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">narrowing</strong>. Women's football has grown enormously in the last few years, and a lot of the differences people assume are natural turn out to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mostly habit</strong>.","topic":"Leisure & Holidays","phrases":[{"en":"make a living from","context":"Leisure could be anything people do in their free time — eating out, going to the cinema, watching a football match, or staying in a hotel — and millions of people make a living from these industries."},{"en":"exposed parts of the economy","context":"What I'd add, though, is that leisure spending is one of the first things households cut when money's tight, so it's also one of the more exposed parts of the economy."},{"en":"poorly paid","context":"The one qualification I'd make is that a lot of leisure jobs are seasonal or poorly paid, so the economy benefits even when the people doing the work don't benefit all that much."},{"en":"more popular with one gender","context":"I think both men and women enjoy things like eating in restaurants or going to the cinema, but I'm sure there are other activities that are more popular with one gender."},{"en":"narrowing","context":"I'd be careful about generalising, though, because those differences seem to be narrowing."},{"en":"mostly habit","context":"Women's football has grown enormously in the last few years, and a lot of the differences people assume are natural turn out to be mostly habit."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Leisure & Holidays","id":"speaking-part-3-061","_displayNo":"#061","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-061-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-061-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-061-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Leisure is an important part of the economy because people spend money on restaurants, films, sport and holidays. Those activities provide many jobs. However, this spending can fall quickly when families have less money, since entertainment is easier to cut than food or housing. So leisure supports the economy but can also be especially vulnerable during difficult periods.","phrases":[{"en":"vulnerable","meaning":"Easily affected or harmed by a problem."}],"question":"What is the relationship between leisure and the economy?","html":"Leisure is an important part of the economy because people spend money on restaurants, films, sport and holidays. Those activities provide many jobs. However, this spending can fall quickly when families have less money, since entertainment is easier to cut than food or housing. So leisure supports the economy but can also be especially <strong class=\"key-phrase\">vulnerable</strong> during difficult periods."},{"text":"Leisure businesses employ people who then spend their wages and pay taxes, so the money supports other parts of the economy too. Tourism also brings income from visitors outside the country. However, some jobs are seasonal or poorly paid. The industry may create wealth without giving every worker a secure or comfortable living.","phrases":[{"en":"seasonal","meaning":"Available or happening only during certain parts of the year."}],"question":"How does the economy benefit from people's leisure activities?","html":"Leisure businesses employ people who then spend their wages and pay taxes, so the money supports other parts of the economy too. Tourism also brings income from visitors outside the country. However, some jobs are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">seasonal</strong> or poorly paid. The industry may create wealth without giving every worker a secure or comfortable living."},{"text":"They share many interests, such as restaurants and cinema, although some activities attract more of one gender. Football matches have traditionally been an example. However, those differences are changing, and habits do not prove a natural preference. I would consider the individual rather than assume men and women must enjoy separate kinds of leisure.","phrases":[{"en":"gender","meaning":"Being male, female or another gender identity."}],"question":"Do men and women enjoy the same type of leisure activities?","html":"They share many interests, such as restaurants and cinema, although some activities attract more of one <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gender</strong>. Football matches have traditionally been an example. However, those differences are changing, and habits do not prove a natural preference. I would consider the individual rather than assume men and women must enjoy separate kinds of leisure."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-061-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-061-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-061-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Leisure time in the past and hobbies in the future","prompt":"Did people have more or less leisure time in the past?\nDo you think hobbies will change much in the future?","answer":"Q: Did people have more or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less leisure time</strong> in the past?\nA: When my grandparents were young, I think they generally had less leisure time than we do now. They didn't have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the gadgets we take for granted</strong>, so ordinary tasks simply took longer — cooking a meal from scratch instead of using a microwave, washing clothes by hand, that sort of thing. Most people also worked six days a week, so whatever free time they had was mostly spent resting rather than doing anything organised. I'd say that's a bit of a generalisation, though, because it depended enormously on money — a wealthier household with servants would have had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">plenty of leisure</strong> even back then.\n\nQ: Do you think hobbies will change much in the future?\nA: No, I don't think hobbies will change all that much in the future — I'm sure people will still play games and sports much as they always have. Where I do think things are shifting is the Internet, which is changing the way we spend our free time. What I suspect will happen is that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the old hobbies survive</strong> but move partly online: people still cook, garden and play music, but now they learn it from videos and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">share the results with strangers</strong>, which is genuinely new. The hobbies themselves probably won't change; how we learn and show them off will.","topic":"Leisure & Holidays","phrases":[{"en":"less leisure time","context":"Did people have more or less leisure time in the past?"},{"en":"the gadgets we take for granted","context":"They didn't have the gadgets we take for granted, so ordinary tasks simply took longer — cooking a meal from scratch instead of using a microwave, washing clothes by hand, that sort of thing."},{"en":"plenty of leisure","context":"I'd say that's a bit of a generalisation, though, because it depended enormously on money — a wealthier household with servants would have had plenty of leisure even back then."},{"en":"the old hobbies survive","context":"What I suspect will happen is that the old hobbies survive but move partly online: people still cook, garden and play music, but now they learn it from videos and share the results with strangers, which is genuinely new."},{"en":"share the results with strangers","context":"What I suspect will happen is that the old hobbies survive but move partly online: people still cook, garden and play music, but now they learn it from videos and share the results with strangers, which is genuinely new."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Leisure & Holidays","id":"speaking-part-3-062","_displayNo":"#062","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-062-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-062-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Many people in my grandparents' generation had less free time. Household jobs took longer without modern appliances, and working six days a week was common. They often used the remaining time simply to rest. However, money made a large difference: wealthy families with paid help could enjoy much more leisure, so the past was not the same for everyone.","phrases":[{"en":"appliances","meaning":"Machines used for household tasks, such as washing or cooking."}],"question":"Did people have more or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less leisure time</strong> in the past?","html":"Many people in my grandparents&#x27; generation had less free time. Household jobs took longer without modern <strong class=\"key-phrase\">appliances</strong>, and working six days a week was common. They often used the remaining time simply to rest. However, money made a large difference: wealthy families with paid help could enjoy much more leisure, so the past was not the same for everyone."},{"text":"The basic hobbies may stay similar: people will still cook, garden, play music and enjoy sport. The internet is changing how they learn and share those activities. Someone can now follow a video lesson or show a result to people worldwide. So I expect familiar hobbies to continue, but with different tools and social connections.","phrases":[{"en":"social connections","meaning":"Relationships or links with other people."}],"question":"Do you think hobbies will change much in the future?","html":"The basic hobbies may stay similar: people will still cook, garden, play music and enjoy sport. The internet is changing how they learn and share those activities. Someone can now follow a video lesson or show a result to people worldwide. So I expect familiar hobbies to continue, but with different tools and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">social connections</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-062-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-062-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Morning or evening people, and what evenings are for","prompt":"Are you a morning or evening type of person?\nWhat activities do people often do in the evening in your country?\nIs evening a good time for doing exercises?","answer":"Q: Are you a morning or evening type of person?\nA: Definitely a morning person. My concentration's sharpest in the first few hours after waking, before messages start arriving and the day fills up, so that's when I try to do anything demanding. By the evening I'm fine for routine tasks but not for anything requiring real thought — I've tried working late plenty of times and it never lasts more than a few days, because I get the hours in but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the quality drops noticeably</strong>. I know plenty of genuine night owls whose brains work the opposite way, though, so I wouldn't say it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a universal rule</strong>.\n\nQ: What activities do people often do in the evening in your country?\nA: Eating together is probably the main one — dinner's still a family occasion in most households, far more so than lunch. After that it varies quite a bit. Loads of people exercise, either at a gym or walking and jogging in parks, which stay busy surprisingly late. Younger people meet friends at cafes, and shopping streets are often livelier in the evening than during the day. For working adults in big cities, evening is realistically <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the only personal time they get</strong>, though that's starting to change now that more people work from home and can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">grab an hour during the day</strong> instead.\n\nQ: Is evening a good time for doing exercises?\nA: For most people, yes — often it's the only time available, and exercise you actually do beats exercise you theoretically should do at six in the morning before work. The one caution is intensity. Hard training raises your body temperature and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaves you too alert to sleep</strong>, so I'd finish anything intense at least an hour or two before bed. Anything moderate, like a walk or gentle swim, is fine right up until bedtime, but leave a proper gap to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wind down</strong> afterwards.","topic":"Leisure & Holidays","phrases":[{"en":"the quality drops noticeably","context":"By the evening I'm fine for routine tasks but not for anything requiring real thought — I've tried working late plenty of times and it never lasts more than a few days, because I get the hours in but the quality drops noticeably."},{"en":"a universal rule","context":"I know plenty of genuine night owls whose brains work the opposite way, though, so I wouldn't say it's a universal rule."},{"en":"the only personal time they get","context":"For working adults in big cities, evening is realistically the only personal time they get, though that's starting to change now that more people work from home and can grab an hour during the day instead."},{"en":"grab an hour during the day","context":"For working adults in big cities, evening is realistically the only personal time they get, though that's starting to change now that more people work from home and can grab an hour during the day instead."},{"en":"leaves you too alert to sleep","context":"Hard training raises your body temperature and leaves you too alert to sleep, so I'd finish anything intense at least an hour or two before bed."},{"en":"wind down","context":"Anything moderate, like a walk or gentle swim, is fine right up until bedtime, but leave a proper gap to wind down afterwards."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Leisure & Holidays","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-063","_displayNo":"#063","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-063-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-063-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-063-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I'm a morning person because I concentrate best soon after waking, before messages and other demands build up. I try to do difficult work then and leave simpler tasks for the evening. Working late gives me more hours but poorer results. However, some people genuinely work better at night, so my routine would not suit everyone.","phrases":[{"en":"demands","meaning":"Things requiring your time, effort or attention."}],"question":"Are you a morning or evening type of person?","html":"I&#x27;m a morning person because I concentrate best soon after waking, before messages and other <strong class=\"key-phrase\">demands</strong> build up. I try to do difficult work then and leave simpler tasks for the evening. Working late gives me more hours but poorer results. However, some people genuinely work better at night, so my routine would not suit everyone."},{"text":"Many families eat dinner together, then people exercise, walk in parks or meet friends at cafes. Shopping areas can also become busy. For many workers, evening is their main personal time after a full day. However, people working from home may have more freedom to take breaks earlier, so patterns depend on work and household arrangements.","phrases":[{"en":"household arrangements","meaning":"The way people organise living and responsibilities at home."}],"question":"What activities do people often do in the evening in your country?","html":"Many families eat dinner together, then people exercise, walk in parks or meet friends at cafes. Shopping areas can also become busy. For many workers, evening is their main personal time after a full day. However, people working from home may have more freedom to take breaks earlier, so patterns depend on work and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">household arrangements</strong>."},{"text":"Yes, if it is the time people can manage regularly. Doing exercise in the evening is more useful than planning a morning session that never happens. However, very hard training close to bedtime may make it difficult to relax. I would leave time to calm down afterwards and choose gentler activity when it is already late.","phrases":[{"en":"calm down","meaning":"Become more relaxed and less physically or mentally excited."}],"question":"Is evening a good time for doing exercises?","html":"Yes, if it is the time people can manage regularly. Doing exercise in the evening is more useful than planning a morning session that never happens. However, very hard training close to bedtime may make it difficult to relax. I would leave time to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">calm down</strong> afterwards and choose gentler activity when it is already late."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-063-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-063-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-063-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Television as relaxation, and leisure against stress","prompt":"Is watching television a good way of relaxing?\nWhat leisure activities would you recommend as a way of combating stress?\nDo young people get enough physical exercise these days?","answer":"Q: Is watching television a good way of relaxing?\nA: It can be, particularly after a mentally demanding day when you want something that requires no effort. But I'd distinguish between choosing a programme and just leaving the television on. Deliberate viewing for an hour is genuinely restful, whereas <strong class=\"key-phrase\">automatic viewing</strong> for four hours tends to leave people feeling worse — you've lost the evening and you're not actually rested. The same applies to scrolling on a phone, which has largely replaced it for younger people, so the habit hasn't disappeared, it's just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">moved to a smaller screen</strong>.\n\nQ: What leisure activities would you recommend as a way of combating stress?\nA: Anything physical first of all — walking, swimming, cycling — because exercise <strong class=\"key-phrase\">works on the body's stress response directly</strong> rather than merely distracting you from it. Beyond that, I'd add anything that needs enough concentration to occupy your mind, like cooking, gardening or playing an instrument, plus anything social, since isolation makes stress considerably worse. The most important thing, though, is that it's something the person will actually keep doing every week rather than in theory, because an activity that feels like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a chore</strong> rarely survives a busy week.\n\nQ: Do young people get enough physical exercise these days?\nA: Most don't, and I don't think it's mainly laziness. Study takes longer than it used to, cities aren't really built for walking or cycling safely, and screens are simply more available. The activity that used to happen automatically — walking to school, playing outside — has been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">designed out of daily life</strong>. So it now has to be provided deliberately, and schools that only offer competitive team sports <strong class=\"key-phrase\">miss most of the pupils who need it</strong>. The exception is schools that also offer non-competitive options like dance or climbing — they tend to reach far more of the class.","topic":"Leisure & Holidays","phrases":[{"en":"automatic viewing","context":"Deliberate viewing for an hour is genuinely restful, whereas automatic viewing for four hours tends to leave people feeling worse — you've lost the evening and you're not actually rested."},{"en":"moved to a smaller screen","context":"The same applies to scrolling on a phone, which has largely replaced it for younger people, so the habit hasn't disappeared, it's just moved to a smaller screen."},{"en":"works on the body's stress response directly","context":"Anything physical first of all — walking, swimming, cycling — because exercise works on the body's stress response directly rather than merely distracting you from it."},{"en":"a chore","context":"The most important thing, though, is that it's something the person will actually keep doing every week rather than in theory, because an activity that feels like a chore rarely survives a busy week."},{"en":"designed out of daily life","context":"The activity that used to happen automatically — walking to school, playing outside — has been designed out of daily life."},{"en":"miss most of the pupils who need it","context":"So it now has to be provided deliberately, and schools that only offer competitive team sports miss most of the pupils who need it."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Leisure & Holidays","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-064","_displayNo":"#064","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-064-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-064-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-064-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"It can be helpful after a tiring day, especially if you choose a programme you enjoy. However, watching deliberately for an hour is different from leaving the TV on all evening. Long, automatic viewing may waste time without leaving you rested. The same problem can happen with phones, so the way we use screens matters more than the device.","phrases":[{"en":"deliberately","meaning":"With a clear purpose or conscious choice."}],"question":"Is watching television a good way of relaxing?","html":"It can be helpful after a tiring day, especially if you choose a programme you enjoy. However, watching <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deliberately</strong> for an hour is different from leaving the TV on all evening. Long, automatic viewing may waste time without leaving you rested. The same problem can happen with phones, so the way we use screens matters more than the device."},{"text":"Physical activities such as walking, swimming and cycling can help. Cooking, gardening or music may also work because they hold your attention, while social activities reduce loneliness. The best choice is something you actually enjoy and will repeat. If it feels like another duty, it is less likely to continue when life becomes busy.","phrases":[{"en":"loneliness","meaning":"The unhappy feeling of lacking the company or connection you want."}],"question":"What leisure activities would you recommend as a way of combating stress?","html":"Physical activities such as walking, swimming and cycling can help. Cooking, gardening or music may also work because they hold your attention, while social activities reduce <strong class=\"key-phrase\">loneliness</strong>. The best choice is something you actually enjoy and will repeat. If it feels like another duty, it is less likely to continue when life becomes busy."},{"text":"Many do not, but it is not simply because they are lazy. Study, unsafe streets and easy access to screens reduce natural opportunities to move. Schools need to provide activity deliberately. Offering only competitive team games leaves some pupils out, so choices such as dance or climbing may help more young people find something they enjoy.","phrases":[{"en":"competitive","meaning":"Involving an effort to win or perform better than others."}],"question":"Do young people get enough physical exercise these days?","html":"Many do not, but it is not simply because they are lazy. Study, unsafe streets and easy access to screens reduce natural opportunities to move. Schools need to provide activity deliberately. Offering only <strong class=\"key-phrase\">competitive</strong> team games leaves some pupils out, so choices such as dance or climbing may help more young people find something they enjoy."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-064-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-064-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-064-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Superstition and what people believe","prompt":"Do you think people in your country are superstitious?\nCan you describe a superstition that they believe in?\nDo you think people are less superstitious than they used to be?","answer":"Q: Do you think people in your country are superstitious?\nA: To a degree, yes, though it varies a lot by generation. Older people tend to take certain beliefs seriously, particularly around dates, numbers and important decisions, whereas younger people tend to treat the same customs as tradition rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">literal belief</strong> — they'll follow them at a wedding without genuinely thinking that ignoring them would cause misfortune. It's not strictly an age thing, though; what I've noticed is that superstition strengthens whenever the stakes are high and the outcome's outside your control, whatever generation you're from.\n\nQ: Can you describe a superstition that they believe in?\nA: The most widespread one's probably to do with numbers. Certain numbers are considered lucky and others unlucky, largely because of how they sound in the language, and it has real practical consequences: people will pay significantly more for a phone number or a house number with the right digits, and buildings sometimes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">skip unlucky floor numbers</strong> entirely. What's interesting is that plenty of the people who do this will happily admit there's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no rational basis</strong> for it — they just don't want to take the risk.\n\nQ: Do you think people are less superstitious than they used to be?\nA: Less so in the literal sense, yes — education and access to scientific explanation mean fewer people genuinely believe a ritual will change an outcome. But it hasn't disappeared so much as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changed function</strong>; it survives as cultural habit and emotional comfort more than actual belief. You see it clearly with students before exams, or athletes with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pre-match routines</strong>. They don't really believe it works; it just reduces anxiety when they can't control what happens next.","topic":"Luck & Superstition","phrases":[{"en":"literal belief","context":"Older people tend to take certain beliefs seriously, particularly around dates, numbers and important decisions, whereas younger people tend to treat the same customs as tradition rather than literal belief — they'll follow them at a wedding without genuinely thinking that ignoring them would cau..."},{"en":"skip unlucky floor numbers","context":"Certain numbers are considered lucky and others unlucky, largely because of how they sound in the language, and it has real practical consequences: people will pay significantly more for a phone number or a house number with the right digits, and buildings sometimes skip unlucky floor numbers ent..."},{"en":"no rational basis","context":"What's interesting is that plenty of the people who do this will happily admit there's no rational basis for it — they just don't want to take the risk."},{"en":"changed function","context":"But it hasn't disappeared so much as changed function; it survives as cultural habit and emotional comfort more than actual belief."},{"en":"pre-match routines","context":"You see it clearly with students before exams, or athletes with pre-match routines."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Luck & Superstition","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-065","_displayNo":"#065","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-065-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-065-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-065-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Some are, especially around dates, numbers and important decisions. Older people may take these beliefs more literally, while younger people often follow them as traditions. However, superstition can affect any age when the result matters and feels outside our control. In those moments, a familiar custom may provide comfort even when someone doubts that it really works.","phrases":[{"en":"superstition","meaning":"A belief that certain actions or objects bring luck without a clear logical basis."}],"question":"Do you think people in your country are superstitious?","html":"Some are, especially around dates, numbers and important decisions. Older people may take these beliefs more literally, while younger people often follow them as traditions. However, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">superstition</strong> can affect any age when the result matters and feels outside our control. In those moments, a familiar custom may provide comfort even when someone doubts that it really works."},{"text":"Beliefs about lucky and unlucky numbers are common. Their sounds can suggest positive or negative meanings in the language. People may pay extra for a particular phone number or avoid certain floor numbers. Some admit there is no logical evidence, but still prefer following the custom rather than feeling they have taken an unnecessary chance.","phrases":[{"en":"logical evidence","meaning":"Facts or reasoning that clearly support an idea."}],"question":"Can you describe a superstition that they believe in?","html":"Beliefs about lucky and unlucky numbers are common. Their sounds can suggest positive or negative meanings in the language. People may pay extra for a particular phone number or avoid certain floor numbers. Some admit there is no <strong class=\"key-phrase\">logical evidence</strong>, but still prefer following the custom rather than feeling they have taken an unnecessary chance."},{"text":"Probably fewer people believe a ritual directly changes an outcome, because education offers more scientific explanations. However, the customs have not disappeared. They may now provide comfort or a sense of tradition. Students and athletes can follow routines before important events without fully believing in them, simply because doing so makes them feel less anxious.","phrases":[{"en":"ritual","meaning":"A repeated action performed in a particular way for a special reason."}],"question":"Do you think people are less superstitious than they used to be?","html":"Probably fewer people believe a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ritual</strong> directly changes an outcome, because education offers more scientific explanations. However, the customs have not disappeared. They may now provide comfort or a sense of tradition. Students and athletes can follow routines before important events without fully believing in them, simply because doing so makes them feel less anxious."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-065-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-065-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-065-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Why saving matters and teaching children about money","prompt":"Why is it important for people to save some of their money?\nIn your view, should children be taught how to manage money?\nDo you think that girls are better at saving money than boys?","answer":"Q: Why is it important for people to save some of their money?\nA: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">In the long term</strong> I think it's important to save for retirement, but it's also a good idea to have money set aside so you can deal with unexpected problems sooner than that. For example, I've learned to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put some money aside</strong> for car repairs, and that came in useful recently when I needed my car's brake pads changed — if I hadn't had savings, the bill could have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sent me into the red</strong>. The exception, I think, is people on very low incomes, where telling them to save doesn't help much if there's nothing left over at the end of the month.\n\nQ: In your view, should children be taught how to manage money?\nA: Not at school, no — I think it's mainly the job of parents to teach children that gradually, and a lot of it they pick up naturally just by growing up around money. I remember being given money for sweets or an ice cream as a kid, and I soon learned <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what I could afford</strong> and what I couldn't. Where I'd make an exception is the more technical stuff — interest, credit, pensions — because that genuinely is easier to learn properly in a classroom than to pick up by trial and error.\n\nQ: Do you think that girls are better at saving money than boys?\nA: I've never really thought about that before, and I don't see any obvious reason why girls would be better savers than boys, or the other way round. I suppose it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depends more on</strong> the individual's personality than their gender — a child who likes cheap toys might not think about saving at all, whereas one who wants something expensive, like a bike or a games console, might <strong class=\"key-phrase\">summon the willpower</strong> to save up! If there is a pattern, I'd guess it's more about how parents treat sons and daughters differently around money than anything to do with the children themselves.","topic":"Money","phrases":[{"en":"In the long term","context":"In the long term I think it's important to save for retirement, but it's also a good idea to have money set aside so you can deal with unexpected problems sooner than that."},{"en":"put some money aside","context":"For example, I've learned to put some money aside for car repairs, and that came in useful recently when I needed my car's brake pads changed — if I hadn't had savings, the bill could have sent me into the red."},{"en":"sent me into the red","context":"For example, I've learned to put some money aside for car repairs, and that came in useful recently when I needed my car's brake pads changed — if I hadn't had savings, the bill could have sent me into the red."},{"en":"what I could afford","context":"I remember being given money for sweets or an ice cream as a kid, and I soon learned what I could afford and what I couldn't."},{"en":"depends more on","context":"I suppose it depends more on the individual's personality than their gender — a child who likes cheap toys might not think about saving at all, whereas one who wants something expensive, like a bike or a games console, might summon the willpower to save up!"},{"en":"summon the willpower","context":"I suppose it depends more on the individual's personality than their gender — a child who likes cheap toys might not think about saving at all, whereas one who wants something expensive, like a bike or a games console, might summon the willpower to save up!"}],"subtype":"","summary":"Money","id":"speaking-part-3-066","_displayNo":"#066","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-066-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-066-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-066-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Savings help with retirement and unexpected expenses, such as car repairs. Having money ready can prevent a sudden bill from becoming debt; that helped me when my brakes needed work. However, saving requires some money left after essentials. People on very low incomes may not have that choice, so telling them to save is not always useful.","phrases":[{"en":"unexpected expenses","meaning":"Costs you did not know or plan would arise."}],"question":"Why is it important for people to save some of their money?","html":"Savings help with retirement and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unexpected expenses</strong>, such as car repairs. Having money ready can prevent a sudden bill from becoming debt; that helped me when my brakes needed work. However, saving requires some money left after essentials. People on very low incomes may not have that choice, so telling them to save is not always useful."},{"text":"Yes, but I think parents should teach most everyday money habits gradually. Pocket money can show children what they can afford and what they need to save for. Schools are more useful for technical subjects such as interest, borrowing and pensions. Those are better explained clearly than learned only by making expensive mistakes later.","phrases":[{"en":"interest","meaning":"Extra money paid for borrowing money or earned on some savings."}],"question":"In your view, should children be taught how to manage money?","html":"Yes, but I think parents should teach most everyday money habits gradually. Pocket money can show children what they can afford and what they need to save for. Schools are more useful for technical subjects such as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">interest</strong>, borrowing and pensions. Those are better explained clearly than learned only by making expensive mistakes later."},{"text":"I don't see a clear reason why either group would naturally save better. Personality and goals seem more important. A child wanting an expensive bicycle may save carefully, while another has no reason to wait. If boys and girls behave differently, that may also reflect how adults teach or treat them around money.","phrases":[{"en":"reflect","meaning":"Show or be influenced by something."}],"question":"Do you think that girls are better at saving money than boys?","html":"I don&#x27;t see a clear reason why either group would naturally save better. Personality and goals seem more important. A child wanting an expensive bicycle may save carefully, while another has no reason to wait. If boys and girls behave differently, that may also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reflect</strong> how adults teach or treat them around money."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-066-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-066-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-066-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Reacting to a situation and accounting for it","prompt":"Do you think other people would feel the same in that situation?\nHave you had a similar experience since then?\nHow would you account for the popularity of credit cards?","answer":"Q: Do you think other people would feel the same in that situation?\nA: Most people would, I think — the embarrassment comes from it happening in front of others rather than from the money itself. Being unable to pay in a queue makes you feel judged, even though the cause is usually something trivial, like a technical fault, rather than someone actually being broke. What varies is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">how long the feeling lasts</strong>, though: if it's a one-off, people forget it within the day, but if it reflects <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a genuine financial difficulty</strong>, the anxiety doesn't go away nearly as quickly.\n\nQ: Have you had a similar experience since then?\nA: Minor versions, yes — a card that wouldn't read, or a payment app failing when I had no cash on me as a backup. Nothing serious, but enough to change my habits a little. I now carry a small amount of cash and keep <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a second payment method</strong> on me, which probably sounds excessive in a country where almost everyone pays by phone these days. It's less about the money, really, than about not wanting to be caught out in front of a queue of people.\n\nQ: How would you account for the popularity of credit cards?\nA: Convenience, mainly — they're accepted almost everywhere, work internationally, and mean you don't have to carry cash around. The protections matter too: if a purchase goes wrong, or a card's used fraudulently, you can usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get the money back</strong>, which you can't with cash, and rewards or cashback add a further incentive on top. The danger is that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">separating the purchase from the payment</strong> makes spending feel less real, and interest on unpaid balances is genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">punishing</strong>.","topic":"Money","phrases":[{"en":"how long the feeling lasts","context":"What varies is how long the feeling lasts, though: if it's a one-off, people forget it within the day, but if it reflects a genuine financial difficulty, the anxiety doesn't go away nearly as quickly."},{"en":"a genuine financial difficulty","context":"What varies is how long the feeling lasts, though: if it's a one-off, people forget it within the day, but if it reflects a genuine financial difficulty, the anxiety doesn't go away nearly as quickly."},{"en":"a second payment method","context":"I now carry a small amount of cash and keep a second payment method on me, which probably sounds excessive in a country where almost everyone pays by phone these days."},{"en":"get the money back","context":"The protections matter too: if a purchase goes wrong, or a card's used fraudulently, you can usually get the money back, which you can't with cash, and rewards or cashback add a further incentive on top."},{"en":"separating the purchase from the payment","context":"The danger is that separating the purchase from the payment makes spending feel less real, and interest on unpaid balances is genuinely punishing."},{"en":"punishing","context":"The danger is that separating the purchase from the payment makes spending feel less real, and interest on unpaid balances is genuinely punishing."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Money","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-067","_displayNo":"#067","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-067-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-067-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-067-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I think most people would feel embarrassed if they could not pay while others waited. The problem may be a technical fault rather than lack of money, but it can still feel as if people are judging you. A one-off incident is soon forgotten; if it connects with real money worries, the anxiety may last much longer.","phrases":[{"en":"one-off incident","meaning":"Something that happens once rather than repeatedly."}],"question":"Do you think other people would feel the same in that situation?","html":"I think most people would feel embarrassed if they could not pay while others waited. The problem may be a technical fault rather than lack of money, but it can still feel as if people are judging you. A <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one-off incident</strong> is soon forgotten; if it connects with real money worries, the anxiety may last much longer."},{"text":"Yes, small payment problems have happened again, such as a card or app not working when I had no cash. They were not serious, but they changed my habits. I now carry some cash and another way to pay. It is mainly to avoid feeling stuck in front of a queue, rather than a concern about the amount.","phrases":[{"en":"another way to pay","meaning":"A different payment method available if the first fails."}],"question":"Have you had a similar experience since then?","html":"Yes, small payment problems have happened again, such as a card or app not working when I had no cash. They were not serious, but they changed my habits. I now carry some cash and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">another way to pay</strong>. It is mainly to avoid feeling stuck in front of a queue, rather than a concern about the amount."},{"text":"Credit cards are convenient, widely accepted and useful abroad. Some also offer protection if a purchase goes wrong, along with rewards or cashback. However, paying later can make spending feel less real. If users leave balances unpaid, interest can become expensive. Their popularity is understandable, but the convenience works best when spending remains under control.","phrases":[{"en":"under control","meaning":"Managed so it does not become excessive or harmful."}],"question":"How would you account for the popularity of credit cards?","html":"Credit cards are convenient, widely accepted and useful abroad. Some also offer protection if a purchase goes wrong, along with rewards or cashback. However, paying later can make spending feel less real. If users leave balances unpaid, interest can become expensive. Their popularity is understandable, but the convenience works best when spending remains <strong class=\"key-phrase\">under control</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-067-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-067-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-067-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Museums and art galleries, and recommending one","prompt":"Do you like museums/ art galleries?\nWould you recommend this museum to other people?","answer":"Q: Do you like museums/ art galleries?\nA: I do, particularly ones that explain the context rather than just displaying objects behind glass. A case of pottery doesn't mean much to me on its own, but if the exhibition explains who made it, why, and what daily life was like at the time, an abstract historical period suddenly becomes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a tangible human story</strong>. I find I remember those visits years afterwards. The ones I enjoy least, though, are the sort that assume you already know the period and don't bother explaining the basics.\n\nQ: Would you recommend this museum to other people?\nA: Yes, I would. The collection's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">well organised</strong> and the labelling's written for non-specialists, so you don't need any <strong class=\"key-phrase\">background knowledge</strong> to follow it. It also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strikes a good balance</strong> — there's enough depth for someone genuinely interested, but it's not so dense that an ordinary visitor leaves feeling <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overwhelmed</strong>. I'd suggest going on a weekday morning, though, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">it gets extremely crowded</strong> at weekends, and two hours is about right for a first visit if you go when it's quiet.","topic":"Museums","phrases":[{"en":"a tangible human story","context":"A case of pottery doesn't mean much to me on its own, but if the exhibition explains who made it, why, and what daily life was like at the time, an abstract historical period suddenly becomes a tangible human story."},{"en":"well organised","context":"The collection's well organised and the labelling's written for non-specialists, so you don't need any background knowledge to follow it."},{"en":"background knowledge","context":"The collection's well organised and the labelling's written for non-specialists, so you don't need any background knowledge to follow it."},{"en":"strikes a good balance","context":"It also strikes a good balance — there's enough depth for someone genuinely interested, but it's not so dense that an ordinary visitor leaves feeling overwhelmed."},{"en":"overwhelmed","context":"It also strikes a good balance — there's enough depth for someone genuinely interested, but it's not so dense that an ordinary visitor leaves feeling overwhelmed."},{"en":"it gets extremely crowded","context":"I'd suggest going on a weekday morning, though, because it gets extremely crowded at weekends, and two hours is about right for a first visit if you go when it's quiet."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Museums","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-068","_displayNo":"#068","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-068-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-068-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, especially when exhibitions explain the people and daily life behind objects. A pot becomes much more interesting if I understand who made it and why. That turns a distant historical period into a human story. I enjoy museums less when labels assume visitors already know the background and leave basic questions unanswered.","phrases":[{"en":"background","meaning":"The information needed to understand a subject or event."}],"question":"Do you like museums/ art galleries?","html":"Yes, especially when exhibitions explain the people and daily life behind objects. A pot becomes much more interesting if I understand who made it and why. That turns a distant historical period into a human story. I enjoy museums less when labels assume visitors already know the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">background</strong> and leave basic questions unanswered."},{"text":"Yes. The displays are organised clearly and explained for ordinary visitors, so you do not need specialist knowledge. There is enough detail to learn something without feeling overwhelmed. I would suggest a weekday morning because weekends can be crowded. About two hours would give a first-time visitor a useful introduction without becoming too tiring.","phrases":[{"en":"overwhelmed","meaning":"Feeling that there is too much to deal with or understand."}],"question":"Would you recommend this museum to other people?","html":"Yes. The displays are organised clearly and explained for ordinary visitors, so you do not need specialist knowledge. There is enough detail to learn something without feeling <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overwhelmed</strong>. I would suggest a weekday morning because weekends can be crowded. About two hours would give a first-time visitor a useful introduction without becoming too tiring."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-068-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-068-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Music young people like, and what technology changed","prompt":"What kinds of music are popular with young people in your country?\nHow has technology affected the music that is popular with young people?\nWhat do you think influences a young person's taste in music?","answer":"Q: What kinds of music are popular with young people in your country?\nA: Pop and hip-hop dominate, along with electronic dance music and Korean pop, which has got <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an enormous following</strong> here. What strikes me is how international the taste's become. My parents' generation listened almost entirely to domestic music because that's what was available, whereas young people now discover things through streaming and short videos, so a song from anywhere can become popular here within days. Local music's still strong, but it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">competes globally now</strong> in a way it never used to.\n\nQ: How has technology affected the music that is popular with young people?\nA: In two significant ways, really. Production first — you can record something decent on a laptop these days, so artists don't need a record label to be heard, and that's opened things up to people who'd never have got through the old system. The second is discovery: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recommendation algorithms</strong> decide a huge amount of what people actually hear, and because they keep suggesting what resembles what you already like, taste can narrow even while choice technically expands.\n\nQ: What do you think influences a young person's taste in music?\nA: Friends most of all, especially in the teenage years, because music's partly a way of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">signalling which group you belong to</strong>. Family matters earlier on, since people often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep an attachment to</strong> whatever was played at home. Beyond that, films, games and online platforms introduce a lot too. But there's something genuinely personal in it as well: people attach songs to particular periods of their life, and that connection <strong class=\"key-phrase\">has nothing to do with what anybody else thinks</strong>.","topic":"Music","phrases":[{"en":"an enormous following","context":"Pop and hip-hop dominate, along with electronic dance music and Korean pop, which has got an enormous following here."},{"en":"competes globally now","context":"Local music's still strong, but it competes globally now in a way it never used to."},{"en":"recommendation algorithms","context":"The second is discovery: recommendation algorithms decide a huge amount of what people actually hear, and because they keep suggesting what resembles what you already like, taste can narrow even while choice technically expands."},{"en":"signalling which group you belong to","context":"Friends most of all, especially in the teenage years, because music's partly a way of signalling which group you belong to."},{"en":"keep an attachment to","context":"Family matters earlier on, since people often keep an attachment to whatever was played at home."},{"en":"has nothing to do with what anybody else thinks","context":"But there's something genuinely personal in it as well: people attach songs to particular periods of their life, and that connection has nothing to do with what anybody else thinks."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Music","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-069","_displayNo":"#069","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-069-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-069-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-069-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Pop, hip-hop, electronic dance music and K-pop are very popular. Young people discover songs through streaming and short videos, so their choices are much more international than my parents' were. A song from another country can become familiar quickly. Local music still matters, but it now competes with a much wider range of music.","phrases":[{"en":"streaming","meaning":"Listening to or watching online content without downloading the whole file first."}],"question":"What kinds of music are popular with young people in your country?","html":"Pop, hip-hop, electronic dance music and K-pop are very popular. Young people discover songs through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">streaming</strong> and short videos, so their choices are much more international than my parents&#x27; were. A song from another country can become familiar quickly. Local music still matters, but it now competes with a much wider range of music."},{"text":"Technology makes it easier to create music on a laptop, so artists do not always need a record company. It also changes discovery through recommendations made by apps. These can help listeners find songs, but often suggest similar music repeatedly. As a result, people may have more choice available while actually exploring fewer different styles.","phrases":[{"en":"recommendations","meaning":"Suggestions about things you might like or find useful."}],"question":"How has technology affected the music that is popular with young people?","html":"Technology makes it easier to create music on a laptop, so artists do not always need a record company. It also changes discovery through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recommendations</strong> made by apps. These can help listeners find songs, but often suggest similar music repeatedly. As a result, people may have more choice available while actually exploring fewer different styles."},{"text":"Friends strongly influence teenagers because music helps show which group they belong to. Family introduces music earlier, while films, games and online platforms add new choices. Personal memories matter too. A song linked to an important period can remain special even when friends dislike it, so musical taste is not only a result of outside influence.","phrases":[{"en":"musical taste","meaning":"The types of music a person enjoys."}],"question":"What do you think influences a young person's taste in music?","html":"Friends strongly influence teenagers because music helps show which group they belong to. Family introduces music earlier, while films, games and online platforms add new choices. Personal memories matter too. A song linked to an important period can remain special even when friends dislike it, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">musical taste</strong> is not only a result of outside influence."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-069-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-069-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-069-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Whether newspapers still matter","prompt":"In your opinion, are newspapers important?","answer":"Q: In your opinion, are newspapers important?\nA: Yes, though I'd say it's professional journalism that matters rather than the printed paper itself. Newspapers employ reporters, editors and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fact-checkers</strong>, and they fund investigations that can take months and might ultimately produce nothing usable. An individual posting on social media simply can't do that. The other thing they provide is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accountability</strong>: if a newspaper prints something false, it can be sued and forced to correct it, whereas an anonymous account <strong class=\"key-phrase\">faces no consequences at all</strong>. I wouldn't pretend it's a perfect system, though — plenty of papers are owned by people with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an obvious agenda</strong>, and that clearly shapes what gets covered. Even so, the format may well disappear, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the process behind it</strong> is something we'd genuinely struggle to replace.","topic":"News & Media","phrases":[{"en":"fact-checkers","context":"Newspapers employ reporters, editors and fact-checkers, and they fund investigations that can take months and might ultimately produce nothing usable."},{"en":"accountability","context":"The other thing they provide is accountability: if a newspaper prints something false, it can be sued and forced to correct it, whereas an anonymous account faces no consequences at all."},{"en":"faces no consequences at all","context":"The other thing they provide is accountability: if a newspaper prints something false, it can be sued and forced to correct it, whereas an anonymous account faces no consequences at all."},{"en":"an obvious agenda","context":"I wouldn't pretend it's a perfect system, though — plenty of papers are owned by people with an obvious agenda, and that clearly shapes what gets covered."},{"en":"the process behind it","context":"Even so, the format may well disappear, but the process behind it is something we'd genuinely struggle to replace."}],"subtype":"","summary":"News & Media","id":"speaking-part-3-070","_displayNo":"#070","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-070-q01.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, but the reporting process matters more than printed paper. Newspapers support reporters, editors and investigations that take time and money. They can also be held responsible for false reporting, unlike many anonymous online accounts. Some owners influence coverage unfairly, so they are not perfect. However, losing professional journalism would mean losing something difficult to replace.","phrases":[{"en":"professional journalism","meaning":"News reporting carried out through trained staff and editorial checks."}],"question":"In your opinion, are newspapers important?","html":"Yes, but the reporting process matters more than printed paper. Newspapers support reporters, editors and investigations that take time and money. They can also be held responsible for false reporting, unlike many anonymous online accounts. Some owners influence coverage unfairly, so they are not perfect. However, losing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">professional journalism</strong> would mean losing something difficult to replace."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-070-q01.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Newspapers against radio and television for news","prompt":"In what ways are newspapers better for learning about the news than radio or television?\nDo you think newspapers should be completely free to say whatever they want?\nWhat do people enjoy reading in your country?","answer":"Q: In what ways are newspapers better for learning about the news than radio or television?\nA: The main advantage is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">control over the pace</strong>. With a broadcast you move at the presenter's speed, whereas reading lets you slow down on something complicated, go back over a paragraph and check where a claim actually came from. Print also has more room, so a newspaper can give you the background to a story rather than just the headline, and it's easier to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tell reporting from opinion</strong>, because the two are physically separated on the page. The trade-off is immediacy, though — if something's breaking right now, a newspaper that's printed once a day can't compete with television or a news app.\n\nQ: Do you think newspapers should be completely free to say whatever they want?\nA: No, and I don't think any country actually allows that in practice. A free press is essential, since without it governments aren't <strong class=\"key-phrase\">held to account</strong>. But that freedom has to sit alongside laws on defamation, privacy and incitement, because publishing a false accusation can destroy someone who has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no comparable platform to respond</strong>. The distinction I'd draw is between restricting what the press may investigate, which is dangerous, and holding it responsible for what it actually prints, which isn't the same thing at all.\n\nQ: What do people enjoy reading in your country?\nA: News and sport are probably the most widely followed, along with entertainment and celebrity coverage. Business and property attract a lot of attention too, especially among people my age. What's changed is that people no longer read a whole publication — they follow individual topics through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">social media feeds</strong> instead, so someone might read enormously about football and almost nothing else. That's convenient, but it means most people never encounter <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a story they did not already care about</strong>.","topic":"News & Media","phrases":[{"en":"control over the pace","context":"The main advantage is control over the pace."},{"en":"tell reporting from opinion","context":"Print also has more room, so a newspaper can give you the background to a story rather than just the headline, and it's easier to tell reporting from opinion, because the two are physically separated on the page."},{"en":"held to account","context":"A free press is essential, since without it governments aren't held to account."},{"en":"no comparable platform to respond","context":"But that freedom has to sit alongside laws on defamation, privacy and incitement, because publishing a false accusation can destroy someone who has no comparable platform to respond."},{"en":"social media feeds","context":"What's changed is that people no longer read a whole publication — they follow individual topics through social media feeds instead, so someone might read enormously about football and almost nothing else."},{"en":"a story they did not already care about","context":"That's convenient, but it means most people never encounter a story they did not already care about."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"News & Media","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-071","_displayNo":"#071","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-071-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-071-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-071-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Reading lets you choose your own pace, return to difficult passages and check the source of a claim. Newspapers also have room for background and usually separate opinion from reporting clearly. Their weakness is speed: a daily printed paper cannot update as quickly as television or an app when an event is happening right now.","phrases":[{"en":"choose your own pace","meaning":"Decide how quickly or slowly to do something."}],"question":"In what ways are newspapers better for learning about the news than radio or television?","html":"Reading lets you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">choose your own pace</strong>, return to difficult passages and check the source of a claim. Newspapers also have room for background and usually separate opinion from reporting clearly. Their weakness is speed: a daily printed paper cannot update as quickly as television or an app when an event is happening right now."},{"text":"No. A free press is important for questioning governments, but it should still respect privacy and rules against harmful false accusations. Publishing something untrue can seriously damage someone who cannot respond equally. I would distinguish preventing a newspaper from investigating from making it responsible for what it publishes. Freedom and responsibility should work together.","phrases":[{"en":"false accusations","meaning":"Untrue statements saying someone has done something wrong."}],"question":"Do you think newspapers should be completely free to say whatever they want?","html":"No. A free press is important for questioning governments, but it should still respect privacy and rules against harmful <strong class=\"key-phrase\">false accusations</strong>. Publishing something untrue can seriously damage someone who cannot respond equally. I would distinguish preventing a newspaper from investigating from making it responsible for what it publishes. Freedom and responsibility should work together."},{"text":"News, sport, business and celebrity stories attract many readers. People also follow property and other personal interests. Increasingly, they find individual stories through social media rather than read a whole newspaper. That is convenient, but it can narrow their knowledge: someone following only football may rarely encounter important stories about anything else.","phrases":[{"en":"encounter","meaning":"Come across or experience something."}],"question":"What do people enjoy reading in your country?","html":"News, sport, business and celebrity stories attract many readers. People also follow property and other personal interests. Increasingly, they find individual stories through social media rather than read a whole newspaper. That is convenient, but it can narrow their knowledge: someone following only football may rarely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">encounter</strong> important stories about anything else."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-071-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-071-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-071-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Where people get news, and the future of newspapers","prompt":"Which do you prefer for news: newspapers, television or radio? Why?\nWhat will happen to newspapers as the internet becomes more dominant?","answer":"Q: Which do you prefer for news: newspapers, television or radio? Why?\nA: I prefer reading, mainly because I can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">control the pace</strong>. I can slow down on something complicated, check where a claim came from and tell reporting from opinion, which is much harder when a broadcast's moving at its own speed. That said, television's better for live events where you genuinely need to see what's happening, and radio suits me when I'm driving or cooking — but for actually understanding something properly, I'd still choose <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the written version</strong> every time.\n\nQ: What will happen to newspapers as the internet becomes more dominant?\nA: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Printed circulation</strong> will keep falling, and I don't think anything reverses that now. But journalism itself won't disappear, because the demand for reliable information's arguably higher than ever. The stronger organisations have already moved into <strong class=\"key-phrase\">digital subscriptions</strong>, newsletters and podcasts, and the ones that survive will be the ones that keep their <strong class=\"key-phrase\">editorial standards</strong> rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">chasing clicks</strong>. What worries me is local news, though, because those papers don't have a realistic subscription model, and plenty of towns now have nobody reporting on the council at all.","topic":"News & Media","phrases":[{"en":"control the pace","context":"I prefer reading, mainly because I can control the pace."},{"en":"the written version","context":"That said, television's better for live events where you genuinely need to see what's happening, and radio suits me when I'm driving or cooking — but for actually understanding something properly, I'd still choose the written version every time."},{"en":"Printed circulation","context":"Printed circulation will keep falling, and I don't think anything reverses that now."},{"en":"digital subscriptions","context":"The stronger organisations have already moved into digital subscriptions, newsletters and podcasts, and the ones that survive will be the ones that keep their editorial standards rather than chasing clicks."},{"en":"editorial standards","context":"The stronger organisations have already moved into digital subscriptions, newsletters and podcasts, and the ones that survive will be the ones that keep their editorial standards rather than chasing clicks."},{"en":"chasing clicks","context":"The stronger organisations have already moved into digital subscriptions, newsletters and podcasts, and the ones that survive will be the ones that keep their editorial standards rather than chasing clicks."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"News & Media","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-072","_displayNo":"#072","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-072-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-072-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I prefer written news because I can slow down, reread and check where information comes from. It helps me understand complicated issues. Television is better for seeing a live event, and radio works while driving or cooking. Each has a use, but for careful understanding I would usually choose to read.","phrases":[{"en":"reread","meaning":"Read something again."}],"question":"Which do you prefer for news: newspapers, television or radio? Why?","html":"I prefer written news because I can slow down, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reread</strong> and check where information comes from. It helps me understand complicated issues. Television is better for seeing a live event, and radio works while driving or cooking. Each has a use, but for careful understanding I would usually choose to read."},{"text":"Printed newspapers will probably keep losing readers, but reliable journalism will still be needed. Strong organisations can use subscriptions, newsletters and podcasts while keeping careful reporting standards. I worry more about local newspapers, which may struggle to earn enough online. If they disappear, some communities lose the people checking what local officials are doing.","phrases":[{"en":"subscriptions","meaning":"Regular payments for continued access to a service or publication."}],"question":"What will happen to newspapers as the internet becomes more dominant?","html":"Printed newspapers will probably keep losing readers, but reliable journalism will still be needed. Strong organisations can use <strong class=\"key-phrase\">subscriptions</strong>, newsletters and podcasts while keeping careful reporting standards. I worry more about local newspapers, which may struggle to earn enough online. If they disappear, some communities lose the people checking what local officials are doing."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-072-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-072-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Taking photographs and looking at other people's","prompt":"Are you a keen photographer?\nDo you like looking at other people's photographs?\nDo you prefer to take a photo or film and why?","answer":"Q: Are you a keen photographer?\nA: I take photographs fairly regularly, but I wouldn't call myself a photographer in any serious sense — it's mostly travel and family occasions. What's changed is that I've become far more selective. When phones made it free to take a thousand pictures, I did exactly that, and then never looked at any of them again. Now I take far fewer and actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">choose between them afterwards</strong>, though I'll admit that discipline slips the moment something exciting happens and I end up back at fifty shots of the same view.\n\nQ: Do you like looking at other people's photographs?\nA: It depends entirely on how they're presented. If someone's chosen twenty images <strong class=\"key-phrase\">that tell a story</strong>, I'm genuinely interested, especially photographs from an earlier period, because you can see how people dressed and what places looked like before they changed. What's difficult is being shown four hundred <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unedited pictures</strong> from someone's holiday, because the problem isn't the photographs themselves, it's that no selection's been made, so nothing stands out. The one exception is close family — I'll happily sit through all four hundred of those, because even a handful of good ones makes it worthwhile.\n\nQ: Do you prefer to take a photo or film and why?\nA: Photographs, generally. A still image's quick to revisit — you take it in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at a glance</strong> — and it leaves something to the imagination, because you don't know what happened just before or after. Video <strong class=\"key-phrase\">captures movement and voices</strong>, though, which is irreplaceable for recordings of relatives in particular — that's the one area where I'd choose it every time. But it demands time to watch and a lot of storage, and I've noticed that filming an event can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stop you experiencing it</strong> properly.","topic":"Photography","phrases":[{"en":"choose between them afterwards","context":"Now I take far fewer and actually choose between them afterwards, though I'll admit that discipline slips the moment something exciting happens and I end up back at fifty shots of the same view."},{"en":"that tell a story","context":"If someone's chosen twenty images that tell a story, I'm genuinely interested, especially photographs from an earlier period, because you can see how people dressed and what places looked like before they changed."},{"en":"unedited pictures","context":"What's difficult is being shown four hundred unedited pictures from someone's holiday, because the problem isn't the photographs themselves, it's that no selection's been made, so nothing stands out."},{"en":"at a glance","context":"A still image's quick to revisit — you take it in at a glance — and it leaves something to the imagination, because you don't know what happened just before or after."},{"en":"captures movement and voices","context":"Video captures movement and voices, though, which is irreplaceable for recordings of relatives in particular — that's the one area where I'd choose it every time."},{"en":"stop you experiencing it","context":"But it demands time to watch and a lot of storage, and I've noticed that filming an event can stop you experiencing it properly."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Photography","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-073","_displayNo":"#073","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-073-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-073-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-073-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I take photos regularly, mainly of trips and family events, but I am not a serious photographer. I used to take huge numbers and never look at them. Now I try to take fewer and choose the best afterwards. That habit still disappears sometimes when I get excited and take many shots of the same thing.","phrases":[{"en":"shots","meaning":"Individual photographs taken with a camera."}],"question":"Are you a keen photographer?","html":"I take photos regularly, mainly of trips and family events, but I am not a serious photographer. I used to take huge numbers and never look at them. Now I try to take fewer and choose the best afterwards. That habit still disappears sometimes when I get excited and take many <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shots</strong> of the same thing."},{"text":"Yes, when they have been chosen carefully and tell a story. Older photos are especially interesting because they show how people and places have changed. Hundreds of similar holiday pictures are harder to enjoy because nothing stands out. I make an exception for close family, though, since even ordinary pictures of them can mean something to me.","phrases":[{"en":"stands out","meaning":"Is more noticeable or memorable than the things around it."}],"question":"Do you like looking at other people's photographs?","html":"Yes, when they have been chosen carefully and tell a story. Older photos are especially interesting because they show how people and places have changed. Hundreds of similar holiday pictures are harder to enjoy because nothing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stands out</strong>. I make an exception for close family, though, since even ordinary pictures of them can mean something to me."},{"text":"Usually photos, because they are quick to look at again and leave room for imagination. Video is better for saving voices and movement, especially of relatives. However, it takes more storage and time to watch. Filming can also distract me from enjoying an event, so I choose it more carefully than taking a few pictures.","phrases":[{"en":"storage","meaning":"Space available for keeping digital files."}],"question":"Do you prefer to take a photo or film and why?","html":"Usually photos, because they are quick to look at again and leave room for imagination. Video is better for saving voices and movement, especially of relatives. However, it takes more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">storage</strong> and time to watch. Filming can also distract me from enjoying an event, so I choose it more carefully than taking a few pictures."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-073-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-073-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-073-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Showing politeness, at home and outside it","prompt":"In your country's culture, how do you show that you are being polite?\nAre we less polite with members of our families than with people we do not know?","answer":"Q: In your country's culture, how do you show that you are being polite?\nA: Politeness in the UK's often shown through indirect language, personal space and frequent use of 'please', 'thank you' and 'sorry'. People normally say something like, 'Could we have the bill, please?' rather than giving a direct command — the wording <strong class=\"key-phrase\">softens the request</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">signals respect</strong>. Queuing's another one; people take it far more seriously than visitors often expect, and pushing in front of somebody is one of the few things that'll genuinely annoy an otherwise reserved crowd. The downside is that visitors from more direct-speaking cultures sometimes read all that indirectness as insincere, when it's really just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the local convention</strong>.\n\nQ: Are we less polite with members of our families than with people we do not know?\nA: I suppose it's normal to be a bit <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more relaxed about politeness</strong> with family members. Most people tend to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">speak in a more informal way</strong> at home — in the UK we still say 'please' and 'thanks', but it's fine to use colloquial language and nicknames you'd never use with someone you didn't know. I'd say the politeness doesn't actually disappear, though; it just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changes form</strong> — within a family it shows up as remembering things and doing small favours rather than as careful wording.","topic":"Politeness","phrases":[{"en":"softens the request","context":"People normally say something like, 'Could we have the bill, please?' rather than giving a direct command — the wording softens the request and signals respect."},{"en":"signals respect","context":"People normally say something like, 'Could we have the bill, please?' rather than giving a direct command — the wording softens the request and signals respect."},{"en":"the local convention","context":"The downside is that visitors from more direct-speaking cultures sometimes read all that indirectness as insincere, when it's really just the local convention."},{"en":"more relaxed about politeness","context":"I suppose it's normal to be a bit more relaxed about politeness with family members."},{"en":"speak in a more informal way","context":"Most people tend to speak in a more informal way at home — in the UK we still say 'please' and 'thanks', but it's fine to use colloquial language and nicknames you'd never use with someone you didn't know."},{"en":"changes form","context":"I'd say the politeness doesn't actually disappear, though; it just changes form — within a family it shows up as remembering things and doing small favours rather than as careful wording."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Politeness","id":"speaking-part-3-074","_displayNo":"#074","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-074-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-074-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"In the UK, people often use polite questions, say please and thank you, and respect personal space. Waiting your turn in a queue is important too. These habits show consideration rather than direct demands. However, people from more direct-speaking cultures may misunderstand the careful wording as dishonest, when it is simply the usual local style.","phrases":[{"en":"consideration","meaning":"Care for other people's feelings, needs or comfort."}],"question":"In your country's culture, how do you show that you are being polite?","html":"In the UK, people often use polite questions, say please and thank you, and respect personal space. Waiting your turn in a queue is important too. These habits show <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consideration</strong> rather than direct demands. However, people from more direct-speaking cultures may misunderstand the careful wording as dishonest, when it is simply the usual local style."},{"text":"We usually speak more casually at home, using nicknames and language we might avoid with strangers. That does not mean respect disappears. In a family, politeness may show through remembering someone's needs or doing small favours rather than choosing formal words. I think the form changes because people know each other better, while the care can remain.","phrases":[{"en":"favours","meaning":"Helpful actions done for someone without expecting payment."}],"question":"Are we less polite with members of our families than with people we do not know?","html":"We usually speak more casually at home, using nicknames and language we might avoid with strangers. That does not mean respect disappears. In a family, politeness may show through remembering someone&#x27;s needs or doing small <strong class=\"key-phrase\">favours</strong> rather than choosing formal words. I think the form changes because people know each other better, while the care can remain."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-074-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-074-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Managing risk and the impact of natural hazards","prompt":"How important is risk management?\nWhat can be done to prevent natural disasters?\nWhat are the impacts of natural hazards on the surrounding areas?","answer":"Q: How important is risk management?\nA: It's essential in any situation where failure would be expensive or dangerous. The basic idea's simple enough — you identify what could go wrong, rank each possibility by how likely it is and how serious it'd be, and then put safeguards in place <strong class=\"key-phrase\">proportionate to that</strong>. What people underestimate is the cost of getting it wrong: most major industrial accidents were preceded by warnings that somebody had already flagged and nobody had acted on. The danger is doing it badly, though — a box-ticking risk assessment that lists hazards nobody then fixes is arguably worse than having none at all, because it creates <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a false sense</strong> that the problem's been handled.\n\nQ: What can be done to prevent natural disasters?\nA: Strictly speaking you can't prevent the natural event itself, since an earthquake or a typhoon's going to happen regardless. What you can prevent is the disaster, which is really a question of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">how exposed people are</strong>. That means not building on floodplains, enforcing construction standards so buildings don't collapse, installing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">early-warning systems</strong> and, crucially, teaching people what to do. Japan's the obvious example, where the same earthquake causes far fewer deaths than it would elsewhere — though even Japan doesn't get it right every time, since a big enough event can still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overwhelm defences</strong> built for anything smaller.\n\nQ: What are the impacts of natural hazards on the surrounding areas?\nA: The immediate damage to homes and infrastructure is what gets reported, but the longer-term effects are often worse. Livelihoods disappear when farmland's contaminated or boats destroyed, children lose a year or more of schooling, and health services collapse exactly when they're needed most. There's also displacement, since people move away and frequently never return. That's why recovery has to mean far more than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rebuilding the physical structures</strong>. The one qualification is that reconstruction aid sometimes leaves a place with better infrastructure than it had before, though that's small comfort to the people who lost everything to get there.","topic":"Risk & Safety","phrases":[{"en":"proportionate to that","context":"The basic idea's simple enough — you identify what could go wrong, rank each possibility by how likely it is and how serious it'd be, and then put safeguards in place proportionate to that."},{"en":"a false sense","context":"The danger is doing it badly, though — a box-ticking risk assessment that lists hazards nobody then fixes is arguably worse than having none at all, because it creates a false sense that the problem's been handled."},{"en":"how exposed people are","context":"What you can prevent is the disaster, which is really a question of how exposed people are."},{"en":"early-warning systems","context":"That means not building on floodplains, enforcing construction standards so buildings don't collapse, installing early-warning systems and, crucially, teaching people what to do."},{"en":"overwhelm defences","context":"Japan's the obvious example, where the same earthquake causes far fewer deaths than it would elsewhere — though even Japan doesn't get it right every time, since a big enough event can still overwhelm defences built for anything smaller."},{"en":"rebuilding the physical structures","context":"That's why recovery has to mean far more than rebuilding the physical structures."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Risk & Safety","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-075","_displayNo":"#075","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-075-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-075-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-075-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"It is essential when mistakes could cause serious harm or expense. We need to identify possible problems, judge their likelihood and seriousness, then take suitable precautions. Simply writing a list is not enough. If warnings are recorded but ignored, a risk assessment may create false confidence instead of improving safety. The actions following the assessment matter most.","phrases":[{"en":"precautions","meaning":"Steps taken in advance to reduce danger or prevent problems."}],"question":"How important is risk management?","html":"It is essential when mistakes could cause serious harm or expense. We need to identify possible problems, judge their likelihood and seriousness, then take suitable <strong class=\"key-phrase\">precautions</strong>. Simply writing a list is not enough. If warnings are recorded but ignored, a risk assessment may create false confidence instead of improving safety. The actions following the assessment matter most."},{"text":"We cannot usually stop an earthquake or storm, but we can reduce the harm. Avoiding risky building locations, using strong construction, providing warnings and teaching people what to do all help. Countries with good preparation can lose fewer lives. However, a very large event may still exceed the protection planned, so preparation reduces risk rather than removing it completely.","phrases":[{"en":"exceed","meaning":"Be greater than an expected amount or limit."}],"question":"What can be done to prevent natural disasters?","html":"We cannot usually stop an earthquake or storm, but we can reduce the harm. Avoiding risky building locations, using strong construction, providing warnings and teaching people what to do all help. Countries with good preparation can lose fewer lives. However, a very large event may still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exceed</strong> the protection planned, so preparation reduces risk rather than removing it completely."},{"text":"They damage homes and roads, but the effects continue much longer. People may lose farms or businesses, children miss school and health services struggle. Some residents move away permanently. Recovery therefore needs jobs, services and community support, not just new buildings. Rebuilding may sometimes improve facilities, but that does not cancel the suffering people have experienced.","phrases":[{"en":"Recovery","meaning":"The process of returning to a safer or more normal situation after harm."}],"question":"What are the impacts of natural hazards on the surrounding areas?","html":"They damage homes and roads, but the effects continue much longer. People may lose farms or businesses, children miss school and health services struggle. Some residents move away permanently. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Recovery</strong> therefore needs jobs, services and community support, not just new buildings. Rebuilding may sometimes improve facilities, but that does not cancel the suffering people have experienced."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-075-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-075-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-075-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Whether the seasons still shape behaviour","prompt":"Do you think the seasons still influence people's behaviour?","answer":"Q: Do you think the seasons still influence people's behaviour?\nA: Yes, though much less than they used to. People still adjust their clothing, routines and free time according to daylight and temperature — in summer we eat outdoors and travel more, whereas in winter people stay in and socialise at home. Where the influence's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weakened considerably</strong> is food and work: supermarkets sell strawberries in December, and most people now work indoors under <strong class=\"key-phrase\">artificial light</strong>, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the farming calendar</strong> that once governed everything has almost no effect on daily life. I'd say the seasons now shape <strong class=\"key-phrase\">our mood and habits</strong> far more than our economy, though that's arguably a bigger effect than people realise — plenty of low mood in winter genuinely does trace back to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a lack of daylight</strong>.","topic":"Seasons","phrases":[{"en":"weakened considerably","context":"Where the influence's weakened considerably is food and work: supermarkets sell strawberries in December, and most people now work indoors under artificial light, so the farming calendar that once governed everything has almost no effect on daily life."},{"en":"artificial light","context":"Where the influence's weakened considerably is food and work: supermarkets sell strawberries in December, and most people now work indoors under artificial light, so the farming calendar that once governed everything has almost no effect on daily life."},{"en":"the farming calendar","context":"Where the influence's weakened considerably is food and work: supermarkets sell strawberries in December, and most people now work indoors under artificial light, so the farming calendar that once governed everything has almost no effect on daily life."},{"en":"our mood and habits","context":"I'd say the seasons now shape our mood and habits far more than our economy, though that's arguably a bigger effect than people realise — plenty of low mood in winter genuinely does trace back to a lack of daylight."},{"en":"a lack of daylight","context":"I'd say the seasons now shape our mood and habits far more than our economy, though that's arguably a bigger effect than people realise — plenty of low mood in winter genuinely does trace back to a lack of daylight."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Seasons","id":"speaking-part-3-076","_displayNo":"#076","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-076-q01.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, seasons still change clothes, mood and leisure habits. People spend more time outside in summer and socialise indoors in winter. However, food and work depend less on seasons now because of supermarkets and indoor jobs. The influence has become less directly economic, but limited winter daylight can still have a real effect on how people feel and behave.","phrases":[{"en":"daylight","meaning":"Natural light during the daytime."}],"question":"Do you think the seasons still influence people's behaviour?","html":"Yes, seasons still change clothes, mood and leisure habits. People spend more time outside in summer and socialise indoors in winter. However, food and work depend less on seasons now because of supermarkets and indoor jobs. The influence has become less directly economic, but limited winter <strong class=\"key-phrase\">daylight</strong> can still have a real effect on how people feel and behave."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-076-q01.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Shopping for clothes and judging people by them","prompt":"Do you enjoy shopping for clothes?\nWhat kind of clothes do you feel least comfortable in?\nHow much do you judge a person by their appearance?","answer":"Q: Do you enjoy shopping for clothes?\nA: Only when I know what I'm looking for. If I need a particular thing, I quite enjoy the process of finding something well made that'll last. What I don't enjoy is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">browsing with no purpose</strong> — I find it exhausting, and that's exactly when I end up buying things I never wear. I also prefer buying clothes in person rather than online, because sizing's so inconsistent between brands that ordering blind's usually a waste of time. That said, for basics like socks or plain T-shirts, where fit barely matters, I'll happily buy online instead.\n\nQ: What kind of clothes do you feel least comfortable in?\nA: Anything too tight or too formal, really. I dislike stiff, heavy fabrics, and I'm uncomfortable in clothes that are wrong for the weather — being too hot in a suit is genuinely distracting. There's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a psychological element</strong> too: if I'm dressed very differently from everyone around me, I spend the whole time aware of it. For me, comfortable means being able to move naturally and then <strong class=\"key-phrase\">forget about what I am wearing</strong>. The exception's somewhere like a wedding or an interview, where I'll happily put up with a few uncomfortable hours because looking right matters more that day than feeling right.\n\nQ: How much do you judge a person by their appearance?\nA: I'd be dishonest if I said not at all, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">first impressions</strong> form automatically and appearance's a large part of that. What I try to do is treat it as information rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">evidence of character</strong>. Someone's clothing usually tells you about their job, their culture or simply what they could afford that month — it doesn't tell you who they actually are. I've been wrong about people often enough to have learned that judging quickly mostly just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reinforces stereotypes</strong>.","topic":"Shopping","phrases":[{"en":"browsing with no purpose","context":"What I don't enjoy is browsing with no purpose — I find it exhausting, and that's exactly when I end up buying things I never wear."},{"en":"a psychological element","context":"There's a psychological element too: if I'm dressed very differently from everyone around me, I spend the whole time aware of it."},{"en":"forget about what I am wearing","context":"For me, comfortable means being able to move naturally and then forget about what I am wearing."},{"en":"first impressions","context":"I'd be dishonest if I said not at all, because first impressions form automatically and appearance's a large part of that."},{"en":"evidence of character","context":"What I try to do is treat it as information rather than evidence of character."},{"en":"reinforces stereotypes","context":"I've been wrong about people often enough to have learned that judging quickly mostly just reinforces stereotypes."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Shopping","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-077","_displayNo":"#077","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-077-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-077-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-077-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Only when I have a clear purpose. I enjoy finding a well-made item that will last, but browsing without a plan is tiring and can lead to unnecessary purchases. I prefer trying most clothes in person because sizes vary. For basic things such as socks or plain T-shirts, though, ordering online is convenient enough.","phrases":[{"en":"browsing","meaning":"Looking around without necessarily deciding to buy something."}],"question":"Do you enjoy shopping for clothes?","html":"Only when I have a clear purpose. I enjoy finding a well-made item that will last, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">browsing</strong> without a plan is tiring and can lead to unnecessary purchases. I prefer trying most clothes in person because sizes vary. For basic things such as socks or plain T-shirts, though, ordering online is convenient enough."},{"text":"I dislike tight, very formal clothes and heavy fabrics, especially in hot weather. I also feel uncomfortable if I am dressed very differently from everyone else. Ideally, I want to move freely and forget about my clothes. For a wedding or interview, however, I accept some discomfort because dressing appropriately matters more on that occasion.","phrases":[{"en":"appropriately","meaning":"In a way that suits the situation or occasion."}],"question":"What kind of clothes do you feel least comfortable in?","html":"I dislike tight, very formal clothes and heavy fabrics, especially in hot weather. I also feel uncomfortable if I am dressed very differently from everyone else. Ideally, I want to move freely and forget about my clothes. For a wedding or interview, however, I accept some discomfort because dressing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">appropriately</strong> matters more on that occasion."},{"text":"Appearance affects first impressions, so I cannot honestly say it has no influence. However, clothing may reflect someone's work, culture or budget rather than character. I try to treat it as limited information and get to know the person. Judging quickly can simply repeat stereotypes, and I have been wrong about people often enough to remember that.","phrases":[{"en":"stereotypes","meaning":"Fixed general ideas about groups that may not fit individuals."}],"question":"How much do you judge a person by their appearance?","html":"Appearance affects first impressions, so I cannot honestly say it has no influence. However, clothing may reflect someone&#x27;s work, culture or budget rather than character. I try to treat it as limited information and get to know the person. Judging quickly can simply repeat <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stereotypes</strong>, and I have been wrong about people often enough to remember that."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-077-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-077-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-077-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Natural ability against developing a skill","prompt":"Could you comment on the idea that children shouldn't waste their time trying to develop a skill if they lack natural ability?\nIs it important to learn a skill?\nWhat do you think are the most important skills for children to learn in the modern world?","answer":"Q: Could you comment on the idea that children shouldn't waste their time trying to develop a skill if they lack natural ability?\nA: I disagree fairly strongly. Natural ability affects how quickly someone improves, not whether they can improve at all, and most people vastly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overestimate how much talent explains</strong>. A child who practises consistently will overtake a naturally gifted one who doesn't, in almost any field. There's a wider point too: the value of learning an instrument isn't only the music, it's discovering that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sustained effort</strong> produces results, and that lesson transfers to everything else a child attempts. I'd make one exception, though — right at the elite level, in top-tier sport or music, natural ability probably does start to matter more, but that's a tiny fraction of children and no reason to hold the rest back.\n\nQ: Is it important to learn a skill?\nA: Yes, and the process matters more than the specific skill itself. Learning something difficult teaches you what your own progress actually looks like — that it's slow, uneven, and that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">plateaus are normal rather than a signal to stop</strong>. People who've been through that once are usually much harder to discourage later on, in completely unrelated areas. The particular skill may well become obsolete — nobody needs my childhood recorder-playing now — but the habit of deliberate practice stays useful for a lifetime.\n\nQ: What do you think are the most important skills for children to learn in the modern world?\nA: The foundations haven't changed — reading, writing and maths still determine what everything else is built on. Beyond that I'd prioritise <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the ability to evaluate information</strong>, because children now encounter enormous amounts of it with no filter. Communication and working with others matter too, since almost nothing significant gets done alone, and increasingly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">learning how to learn</strong>, since most of them will change occupation several times over a career. The one thing I'd push back on is treating these as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one-off modules</strong> — they only really stick when they're built into ordinary lessons.","topic":"Skills","phrases":[{"en":"overestimate how much talent explains","context":"Natural ability affects how quickly someone improves, not whether they can improve at all, and most people vastly overestimate how much talent explains."},{"en":"sustained effort","context":"There's a wider point too: the value of learning an instrument isn't only the music, it's discovering that sustained effort produces results, and that lesson transfers to everything else a child attempts."},{"en":"plateaus are normal rather than a signal to stop","context":"Learning something difficult teaches you what your own progress actually looks like — that it's slow, uneven, and that plateaus are normal rather than a signal to stop."},{"en":"the ability to evaluate information","context":"Beyond that I'd prioritise the ability to evaluate information, because children now encounter enormous amounts of it with no filter."},{"en":"learning how to learn","context":"Communication and working with others matter too, since almost nothing significant gets done alone, and increasingly learning how to learn, since most of them will change occupation several times over a career."},{"en":"one-off modules","context":"The one thing I'd push back on is treating these as one-off modules — they only really stick when they're built into ordinary lessons."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Skills","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-078","_displayNo":"#078","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-078-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-078-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-078-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I disagree. Natural ability can affect how quickly a child improves, but practice still makes a difference. A talented child who never works may progress less than someone who keeps trying. Learning also teaches that effort produces results, which helps elsewhere. Talent may matter more at the very highest level, but that is no reason to discourage most children.","phrases":[{"en":"Natural ability","meaning":"A skill or strength someone appears to have without much training."}],"question":"Could you comment on the idea that children shouldn't waste their time trying to develop a skill if they lack natural ability?","html":"I disagree. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Natural ability</strong> can affect how quickly a child improves, but practice still makes a difference. A talented child who never works may progress less than someone who keeps trying. Learning also teaches that effort produces results, which helps elsewhere. Talent may matter more at the very highest level, but that is no reason to discourage most children."},{"text":"Yes, because learning a skill teaches us how improvement works. Progress can be slow or uneven, and a difficult period does not mean we should stop. Once people understand that, they may handle challenges better in other areas. The original skill might become less useful, but the habit of careful, repeated practice can stay valuable throughout life.","phrases":[{"en":"repeated practice","meaning":"Doing an activity many times to improve at it."}],"question":"Is it important to learn a skill?","html":"Yes, because learning a skill teaches us how improvement works. Progress can be slow or uneven, and a difficult period does not mean we should stop. Once people understand that, they may handle challenges better in other areas. The original skill might become less useful, but the habit of careful, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">repeated practice</strong> can stay valuable throughout life."},{"text":"Reading, writing and maths remain basic. Children also need to judge information carefully, communicate and work with others. Learning how to learn matters because their jobs may change several times. These skills should be practised throughout ordinary lessons rather than taught once in a separate activity, so children can use them in different real situations.","phrases":[{"en":"judge information","meaning":"Decide how trustworthy, useful or accurate information is."}],"question":"What do you think are the most important skills for children to learn in the modern world?","html":"Reading, writing and maths remain basic. Children also need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">judge information</strong> carefully, communicate and work with others. Learning how to learn matters because their jobs may change several times. These skills should be practised throughout ordinary lessons rather than taught once in a separate activity, so children can use them in different real situations."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-078-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-078-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-078-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Why sport matters and sportspeople as role models","prompt":"Why do you think sport is important?\nDo you think famous sportspeople are good role models for children?\nDo you agree that sports stars earn too much money?","answer":"Q: Why do you think sport is important?\nA: I think sport's important for a few different reasons. For me, doing a sport's mainly about having fun — when I play football, for example, I forget about everything else and just enjoy myself. It also helps you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep fit</strong> and healthy, and it's a good way to socialise and make friends. There's a discipline side too: training regularly teaches you that progress is slow and uneven, and that lesson transfers to almost everything else you do. That's assuming it stays enjoyable, though — plenty of people are put off sport for life by being forced into competitive games at school that they never actually liked.\n\nQ: Do you think famous sportspeople are good role models for children?\nA: I think that sportspeople should be good role models. Children <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look up to</strong> their favourite football players, like David Beckham for example, so I think that these people have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an enormous responsibility</strong>. They should try to be a good influence, and behave in the right way. That said, I think it's unfair to expect them to be perfect. They're usually young people under enormous public pressure, and we ask far more of them than we would of anyone else.\n\nQ: Do you agree that sports stars earn too much money?\nA: In my opinion, it's fair that the best sportspeople earn a lot of money. Being a top sportsperson <strong class=\"key-phrase\">requires hours of practice</strong>, and there are millions of sports fans who are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">willing to pay</strong> to see them play. If we don't want sportspeople to earn so much money, we shouldn't go to watch them. What does seem unfair is the gap within the sport itself. A handful of players earn extraordinary sums while most professionals earn an ordinary wage and retire in their thirties.","topic":"Sports & Exercise","phrases":[{"en":"keep fit","context":"It also helps you keep fit and healthy, and it's a good way to socialise and make friends."},{"en":"look up to","context":"Children look up to their favourite football players, like David Beckham for example, so I think that these people have an enormous responsibility."},{"en":"an enormous responsibility","context":"Children look up to their favourite football players, like David Beckham for example, so I think that these people have an enormous responsibility."},{"en":"requires hours of practice","context":"Being a top sportsperson requires hours of practice, and there are millions of sports fans who are willing to pay to see them play."},{"en":"willing to pay","context":"Being a top sportsperson requires hours of practice, and there are millions of sports fans who are willing to pay to see them play."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Sports & Exercise","id":"speaking-part-3-079","_displayNo":"#079","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-079-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-079-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-079-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Sport is enjoyable, helps fitness and creates chances to make friends. Training also teaches patience because improvement is not always quick. When I play football, I can forget other worries and enjoy the moment. However, these benefits depend partly on enjoying the activity. Forcing someone into a game they dislike may put them off sport for years.","phrases":[{"en":"put them off","meaning":"Make them dislike or avoid something."}],"question":"Why do you think sport is important?","html":"Sport is enjoyable, helps fitness and creates chances to make friends. Training also teaches patience because improvement is not always quick. When I play football, I can forget other worries and enjoy the moment. However, these benefits depend partly on enjoying the activity. Forcing someone into a game they dislike may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put them off</strong> sport for years."},{"text":"They should try to be because children admire them and copy their behaviour. Famous players therefore have a responsibility to act fairly and set a useful example. However, expecting perfection is unfair. Many are young and under constant pressure, so we should recognise their mistakes without assuming they must be ideal people in every part of life.","phrases":[{"en":"admire","meaning":"Respect someone and think highly of them."}],"question":"Do you think famous sportspeople are good role models for children?","html":"They should try to be because children <strong class=\"key-phrase\">admire</strong> them and copy their behaviour. Famous players therefore have a responsibility to act fairly and set a useful example. However, expecting perfection is unfair. Many are young and under constant pressure, so we should recognise their mistakes without assuming they must be ideal people in every part of life."},{"text":"I think high earnings can be fair because top athletes train hard and many fans pay to watch them. The money reflects that demand. What concerns me more is the difference within sport: a few stars earn enormous sums while many professionals have ordinary pay and short careers. That gap seems harder to justify than success itself.","phrases":[{"en":"justify","meaning":"Give a convincing reason why something is reasonable or fair."}],"question":"Do you agree that sports stars earn too much money?","html":"I think high earnings can be fair because top athletes train hard and many fans pay to watch them. The money reflects that demand. What concerns me more is the difference within sport: a few stars earn enormous sums while many professionals have ordinary pay and short careers. That gap seems harder to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">justify</strong> than success itself."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-079-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-079-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-079-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Attending sporting events and funding sport","prompt":"Do you often go to sporting events?\nAre you very competitive?\nDo you agree or disagree that governments should spend large sums on major sporting events?","answer":"Q: Do you often go to sporting events?\nA: Not often, but I go when I can for the bigger ones. What television genuinely can't reproduce is the atmosphere — thousands of people reacting simultaneously, and the sense that you're part of something rather than just watching it. You actually see less of the detail than you would at home, but you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel far more</strong>. The obstacle's cost, though: between tickets, travel and food, a single match's become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an expensive day out</strong>.\n\nQ: Are you very competitive?\nA: I'd say I'm competitive with myself rather than with other people. I like measuring progress — running slightly faster than last month, doing something better than I did before — and that keeps me motivated without much downside. Competing directly with others I find less useful, because it turns everything into <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a question of status</strong>, and there's always somebody better than you. Comparing yourself with your previous self is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the version you can actually win</strong>.\n\nQ: Do you agree or disagree that governments should spend large sums on major sporting events?\nA: I partly agree, though I'm sceptical. The arguments for it are real — infrastructure gets built, the country gets attention, and there's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuine national pride</strong>. But the costs regularly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">run far over budget</strong>, and cities are left with stadiums nobody uses. It's very hard to defend when hospitals are short of staff. If a government does bid, I'd say the spending should be transparent and every venue should have a clear use afterwards, not just for the two weeks of the event itself.","topic":"Sports & Exercise","phrases":[{"en":"feel far more","context":"You actually see less of the detail than you would at home, but you feel far more."},{"en":"an expensive day out","context":"The obstacle's cost, though: between tickets, travel and food, a single match's become an expensive day out."},{"en":"a question of status","context":"Competing directly with others I find less useful, because it turns everything into a question of status, and there's always somebody better than you."},{"en":"the version you can actually win","context":"Comparing yourself with your previous self is the version you can actually win."},{"en":"genuine national pride","context":"The arguments for it are real — infrastructure gets built, the country gets attention, and there's genuine national pride."},{"en":"run far over budget","context":"But the costs regularly run far over budget, and cities are left with stadiums nobody uses."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Sports & Exercise","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-080","_displayNo":"#080","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-080-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-080-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-080-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Not regularly, but I sometimes attend major events. The shared reaction of thousands of fans creates an atmosphere television cannot copy, even if I see less detail than at home. However, tickets, travel and food can make one match expensive. So I enjoy the experience, but cost limits how often I can go.","phrases":[{"en":"limits","meaning":"Restricts the amount or frequency of something."}],"question":"Do you often go to sporting events?","html":"Not regularly, but I sometimes attend major events. The shared reaction of thousands of fans creates an atmosphere television cannot copy, even if I see less detail than at home. However, tickets, travel and food can make one match expensive. So I enjoy the experience, but cost <strong class=\"key-phrase\">limits</strong> how often I can go."},{"text":"I mainly compete with my previous performance. Running faster or doing a task better than last month motivates me without making everything about beating others. Direct comparison is less useful because someone will always be better. Personal progress gives me a clearer and more realistic target, although I still enjoy challenging myself.","phrases":[{"en":"challenging myself","meaning":"Trying something difficult to test or improve my ability."}],"question":"Are you very competitive?","html":"I mainly compete with my previous performance. Running faster or doing a task better than last month motivates me without making everything about beating others. Direct comparison is less useful because someone will always be better. Personal progress gives me a clearer and more realistic target, although I still enjoy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">challenging myself</strong>."},{"text":"I partly agree because major events can improve facilities and create national pride. However, costs often rise beyond the plan, and stadiums may be unused afterwards. That is difficult to accept when essential services need money. Governments should explain spending openly and show how every venue will benefit the public long after the event.","phrases":[{"en":"venue","meaning":"Places where events or activities are held."}],"question":"Do you agree or disagree that governments should spend large sums on major sporting events?","html":"I partly agree because major events can improve facilities and create national pride. However, costs often rise beyond the plan, and stadiums may be unused afterwards. That is difficult to accept when essential services need money. Governments should explain spending openly and show how every <strong class=\"key-phrase\">venue</strong> will benefit the public long after the event."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-080-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-080-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-080-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Exercise and health, and compulsory sport at school","prompt":"How can regular exercise improve your health?\nShould schools make sport compulsory?\nHow have the types of exercise people do changed in recent years?","answer":"Q: How can regular exercise improve your health?\nA: Exercise improves health in two ways, and people usually only think about one of them. Physically, it's well documented — it strengthens the heart and circulation, preserves muscle and bone density, and cuts the risk of diabetes and several cancers quite substantially. What people underestimate is the mental side: it's one of the most effective treatments there is for mild anxiety, and it improves sleep, which then affects everything else you do that day. The catch is that it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consistency rather than intensity</strong> that actually delivers these benefits — someone who walks briskly most days for years ends up healthier than someone who trains hard for a month and then <strong class=\"key-phrase\">burns out and gives up</strong>.\n\nQ: Should schools make sport compulsory?\nA: I think so, up to a point. Left to choose, the children who most need physical activity are exactly the ones who'll <strong class=\"key-phrase\">opt out</strong>, so making it compulsory reaches them. What I'd change is the range on offer, though. Traditional school sport means competitive team games, which suits maybe half the pupils and actively puts off the rest. If schools offered swimming, cycling or dance as alternatives too, compulsion would be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far easier to justify</strong>.\n\nQ: How have the types of exercise people do changed in recent years?\nA: Exercise has become much more individual and much more measured. Gym training, running and yoga have grown at the expense of organised team sports, largely because they fit around unpredictable working hours in a way a Tuesday-night five-a-side match doesn't. Apps and watches have added <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a tracking element</strong> that didn't exist before, which motivates some people and makes others slightly obsessive about the numbers. The underlying reason is that work has become sedentary, so movement now has to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deliberately scheduled</strong> rather than happening naturally.","topic":"Sports & Exercise","phrases":[{"en":"consistency rather than intensity","context":"The catch is that it's consistency rather than intensity that actually delivers these benefits — someone who walks briskly most days for years ends up healthier than someone who trains hard for a month and then burns out and gives up."},{"en":"burns out and gives up","context":"The catch is that it's consistency rather than intensity that actually delivers these benefits — someone who walks briskly most days for years ends up healthier than someone who trains hard for a month and then burns out and gives up."},{"en":"opt out","context":"Left to choose, the children who most need physical activity are exactly the ones who'll opt out, so making it compulsory reaches them."},{"en":"far easier to justify","context":"If schools offered swimming, cycling or dance as alternatives too, compulsion would be far easier to justify."},{"en":"a tracking element","context":"Apps and watches have added a tracking element that didn't exist before, which motivates some people and makes others slightly obsessive about the numbers."},{"en":"deliberately scheduled","context":"The underlying reason is that work has become sedentary, so movement now has to be deliberately scheduled rather than happening naturally."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Sports & Exercise","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-081","_displayNo":"#081","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-081-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-081-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-081-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Regular exercise supports the heart, muscles and bones, and can help sleep and mood. These mental benefits matter as well as physical fitness. The key is keeping it up over time, rather than training extremely hard for a short period and stopping. A manageable activity done regularly is more useful than an ambitious plan someone cannot maintain.","phrases":[{"en":"keeping it up","meaning":"Continuing an activity or habit over time."}],"question":"How can regular exercise improve your health?","html":"Regular exercise supports the heart, muscles and bones, and can help sleep and mood. These mental benefits matter as well as physical fitness. The key is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeping it up</strong> over time, rather than training extremely hard for a short period and stopping. A manageable activity done regularly is more useful than an ambitious plan someone cannot maintain."},{"text":"Yes, to some extent, because children who most need activity might otherwise avoid it. However, schools should offer more than competitive team games. Swimming, cycling or dance may appeal to different pupils. Requiring movement is easier to support when children have a reasonable choice and do not feel forced into a sport they hate.","phrases":[{"en":"appeal to","meaning":"Seem interesting or attractive to someone."}],"question":"Should schools make sport compulsory?","html":"Yes, to some extent, because children who most need activity might otherwise avoid it. However, schools should offer more than competitive team games. Swimming, cycling or dance may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">appeal to</strong> different pupils. Requiring movement is easier to support when children have a reasonable choice and do not feel forced into a sport they hate."},{"text":"More people choose individual activities such as gym training, running or yoga because these fit changing work schedules. Apps and watches also make progress easy to measure. That motivates some people but makes others worry too much about numbers. Since work now involves more sitting, exercise often needs to be planned rather than happening naturally during the day.","phrases":[{"en":"work schedules","meaning":"The times and arrangements for when people work."}],"question":"How have the types of exercise people do changed in recent years?","html":"More people choose individual activities such as gym training, running or yoga because these fit changing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work schedules</strong>. Apps and watches also make progress easy to measure. That motivates some people but makes others worry too much about numbers. Since work now involves more sitting, exercise often needs to be planned rather than happening naturally during the day."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-081-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-081-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-081-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"How games have changed since childhood","prompt":"How have games changed since you were a child?\nDo you think this has been a positive change and why?\nWhy do you think children like playing games?","answer":"Q: How have games changed since you were a child?\nA: The shift is from unstructured outdoor play to structured digital games. When I was young, children largely invented their own games outside, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">negotiating the rules as they went</strong>. Now the rules come built in, the environment's designed by somebody else, and most play happens on a screen. The games themselves are far more sophisticated and often genuinely creative, so it's not simply a step down. What's been lost, though, is the physical movement and the face-to-face negotiation that used to come with it — a screen doesn't teach a child how to talk another kid out of changing the rules halfway through.\n\nQ: Do you think this has been a positive change and why?\nA: It's genuinely mixed. Digital games develop real skills — strategy, quick decision-making, cooperating with people in other countries — and for children who aren't athletic, they offer somewhere to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">be good at something</strong>. The problems are about displacement rather than the games themselves: hours spent playing are hours not spent moving or sleeping. So I'd <strong class=\"key-phrase\">judge it by proportion rather than by principle</strong> — a child who plays for an hour and also goes outside is perfectly fine, whereas one who does nothing else clearly isn't.\n\nQ: Why do you think children like playing games?\nA: Several things at once. Games provide a clear challenge with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">immediate feedback</strong>, which school often doesn't, since you know instantly whether you succeeded. There's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">steady, visible progress</strong>, which is satisfying in a way that long-term effort at school usually isn't. And they're safe: you can lose, fail and try again <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without any real consequence</strong>, which is quite rare in a child's life. Add playing with friends, and it's a powerful combination — though that safety net is also exactly why games can't teach a child how to handle a failure that actually matters.","topic":"Sports & Exercise","phrases":[{"en":"negotiating the rules as they went","context":"When I was young, children largely invented their own games outside, negotiating the rules as they went."},{"en":"be good at something","context":"Digital games develop real skills — strategy, quick decision-making, cooperating with people in other countries — and for children who aren't athletic, they offer somewhere to be good at something."},{"en":"judge it by proportion rather than by principle","context":"So I'd judge it by proportion rather than by principle — a child who plays for an hour and also goes outside is perfectly fine, whereas one who does nothing else clearly isn't."},{"en":"immediate feedback","context":"Games provide a clear challenge with immediate feedback, which school often doesn't, since you know instantly whether you succeeded."},{"en":"steady, visible progress","context":"There's steady, visible progress, which is satisfying in a way that long-term effort at school usually isn't."},{"en":"without any real consequence","context":"And they're safe: you can lose, fail and try again without any real consequence, which is quite rare in a child's life."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Sports & Exercise","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-082","_displayNo":"#082","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-082-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-082-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-082-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"More play now happens in digital games with rules already designed for the player. In my childhood, children often played outside and invented rules together. Modern games can be creative and complex, so the change is not entirely negative. However, some physical movement and face-to-face discussion have been lost when play moves mainly to screens.","phrases":[{"en":"face-to-face discussion","meaning":"Talking together while physically in the same place."}],"question":"How have games changed since you were a child?","html":"More play now happens in digital games with rules already designed for the player. In my childhood, children often played outside and invented rules together. Modern games can be creative and complex, so the change is not entirely negative. However, some physical movement and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">face-to-face discussion</strong> have been lost when play moves mainly to screens."},{"text":"It has both good and bad sides. Digital games can develop planning, quick decisions and teamwork, including with people abroad. They also give less sporty children a chance to succeed. Problems arise when gaming replaces sleep, exercise or other activities. I would judge the balance in a child's life rather than treat all games as either good or bad.","phrases":[{"en":"teamwork","meaning":"Working together with others to achieve something."}],"question":"Do you think this has been a positive change and why?","html":"It has both good and bad sides. Digital games can develop planning, quick decisions and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">teamwork</strong>, including with people abroad. They also give less sporty children a chance to succeed. Problems arise when gaming replaces sleep, exercise or other activities. I would judge the balance in a child&#x27;s life rather than treat all games as either good or bad."},{"text":"Games offer clear goals, quick feedback and visible progress. Children can fail and try again without serious consequences, which makes experimenting enjoyable. Playing with friends adds to that appeal. However, this safe setting has limits: learning to recover from a lost game is not exactly the same as facing a real-world failure with lasting effects.","phrases":[{"en":"feedback","meaning":"Information showing how well you did and what could improve."}],"question":"Why do you think children like playing games?","html":"Games offer clear goals, quick <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feedback</strong> and visible progress. Children can fail and try again without serious consequences, which makes experimenting enjoyable. Playing with friends adds to that appeal. However, this safe setting has limits: learning to recover from a lost game is not exactly the same as facing a real-world failure with lasting effects."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-082-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-082-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-082-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"How people keep fit and why exercise matters","prompt":"What do most people in your country do to keep fit?\nHow important is it for people to do some regular physical exercise?\nWhy do you think that modern lifestyles are not healthy?","answer":"Q: What do most people in your country do to keep fit?\nA: Walking is the most common by a distance — parks and lakesides are busy from early morning, particularly with older people. Gym membership has grown enormously among younger adults in cities, and badminton and football are the popular team sports. There's also been a big rise in following workout videos at home, which took off during the pandemic and never really went away. What the popular activities share is that they're cheap and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fit around a working day</strong> — though that's much truer in the cities than in rural areas, where gyms and classes simply aren't as available.\n\nQ: How important is it for people to do some regular physical exercise?\nA: Extremely important, and more so than it used to be. Work and travel once involved a great deal of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">incidental movement</strong>, and modern life has removed almost all of it — we sit to work, sit to travel and sit to relax. So exercise now has to be deliberately scheduled or it simply doesn't happen. Beyond the cardiovascular benefits, it largely determines whether people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay independent in old age</strong>. It isn't <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a cure-all</strong>, though — it won't undo a genuinely poor diet or make up for years of bad sleep, so it has to work alongside the other things, not instead of them.\n\nQ: Why do you think that modern lifestyles are not healthy?\nA: It's an accumulation of small things rather than one large cause. People sit for eight or nine hours, sleep irregularly because screens keep them up, eat processed food because it's quick and cheap, and live under constant low-level stress from being permanently contactable. Any one of these alone would be manageable. The problem is that they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reinforce one another</strong> — poor sleep makes you eat badly, and eating badly makes you inactive, so it becomes a cycle that's hard to break from just one direction.","topic":"Sports & Exercise","phrases":[{"en":"fit around a working day","context":"What the popular activities share is that they're cheap and fit around a working day — though that's much truer in the cities than in rural areas, where gyms and classes simply aren't as available."},{"en":"incidental movement","context":"Work and travel once involved a great deal of incidental movement, and modern life has removed almost all of it — we sit to work, sit to travel and sit to relax."},{"en":"a cure-all","context":"It isn't a cure-all, though — it won't undo a genuinely poor diet or make up for years of bad sleep, so it has to work alongside the other things, not instead of them."},{"en":"stay independent in old age","context":"Beyond the cardiovascular benefits, it largely determines whether people stay independent in old age."},{"en":"reinforce one another","context":"The problem is that they reinforce one another — poor sleep makes you eat badly, and eating badly makes you inactive, so it becomes a cycle that's hard to break from just one direction."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Sports & Exercise","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-083","_displayNo":"#083","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-083-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-083-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-083-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Walking is very common, especially around parks and lakes early in the day. Younger city residents also use gyms, play badminton or football, and follow workout videos at home. Popular activities tend to be affordable and easy to fit around work. Rural areas may have fewer gyms and classes, though, so available choices differ by location.","phrases":[{"en":"fit around work","meaning":"Be arranged at times that do not conflict with work."}],"question":"What do most people in your country do to keep fit?","html":"Walking is very common, especially around parks and lakes early in the day. Younger city residents also use gyms, play badminton or football, and follow workout videos at home. Popular activities tend to be affordable and easy to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fit around work</strong>. Rural areas may have fewer gyms and classes, though, so available choices differ by location."},{"text":"It is extremely important because normal life now involves so much sitting. People often need to plan movement or it never happens. Exercise supports health and helps maintain independence later in life. However, it does not solve everything: poor food habits or lack of sleep still matter. It should work alongside other healthy habits, not replace them.","phrases":[{"en":"independence","meaning":"The ability to manage your life without needing constant help."}],"question":"How important is it for people to do some regular physical exercise?","html":"It is extremely important because normal life now involves so much sitting. People often need to plan movement or it never happens. Exercise supports health and helps maintain <strong class=\"key-phrase\">independence</strong> later in life. However, it does not solve everything: poor food habits or lack of sleep still matter. It should work alongside other healthy habits, not replace them."},{"text":"Several habits build on one another. People sit for long hours, stay up with screens, choose quick processed food and feel constantly available for work. Poor sleep can then make eating and activity habits worse. It becomes a cycle rather than one isolated problem. Improving health may therefore require several manageable changes instead of one dramatic solution.","phrases":[{"en":"a cycle","meaning":"A repeated pattern in which effects lead back to similar causes."}],"question":"Why do you think that modern lifestyles are not healthy?","html":"Several habits build on one another. People sit for long hours, stay up with screens, choose quick processed food and feel constantly available for work. Poor sleep can then make eating and activity habits worse. It becomes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a cycle</strong> rather than one isolated problem. Improving health may therefore require several manageable changes instead of one dramatic solution."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-083-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-083-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-083-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Exercise among young people and changing attitudes","prompt":"What forms of exercise are popular among young people?\nHave people's attitudes towards exercise changed in your country in recent years?","answer":"Q: What forms of exercise are popular among young people?\nA: Gym training is probably the most popular now, along with running, football, badminton and yoga. What's changed is how people find these activities — social media has driven a lot of interest towards anything that produces <strong class=\"key-phrase\">visible results</strong> and can be tracked with an app, so people share their progress in a way they never used to. Team sports remain popular, but individual training that fits <strong class=\"key-phrase\">your own schedule</strong> has grown fastest — the one thing it can't really replace, though, is the social side that comes automatically with playing on a team.\n\nQ: Have people's attitudes towards exercise changed in your country in recent years?\nA: Yes, considerably. Exercise used to be associated mainly with competitive sport, whereas people now connect it with mental health, appearance and general lifestyle, which has brought in a lot of people who were never sporty as children. That's a positive shift overall. The one drawback is that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">heavily edited images</strong> online set standards nobody can realistically meet, and that puts <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unnecessary pressure</strong> on younger people in particular. Gyms have become a good deal more welcoming to beginners, though, which helps <strong class=\"key-phrase\">balance that out</strong>.","topic":"Sports & Exercise","phrases":[{"en":"visible results","context":"What's changed is how people find these activities — social media has driven a lot of interest towards anything that produces visible results and can be tracked with an app, so people share their progress in a way they never used to."},{"en":"your own schedule","context":"Team sports remain popular, but individual training that fits your own schedule has grown fastest — the one thing it can't really replace, though, is the social side that comes automatically with playing on a team."},{"en":"heavily edited images","context":"The one drawback is that heavily edited images online set standards nobody can realistically meet, and that puts unnecessary pressure on younger people in particular."},{"en":"unnecessary pressure","context":"The one drawback is that heavily edited images online set standards nobody can realistically meet, and that puts unnecessary pressure on younger people in particular."},{"en":"balance that out","context":"Gyms have become a good deal more welcoming to beginners, though, which helps balance that out."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Sports & Exercise","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-084","_displayNo":"#084","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-084-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-084-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Gym training, running, football, badminton and yoga are popular. Social media encourages activities with visible results and apps make progress easy to share. Individual exercise has grown because people can choose their own schedule. However, it does not automatically provide the friendships and shared experience that come with playing regularly in a team.","phrases":[{"en":"visible results","meaning":"Improvements that people can see or recognise."}],"question":"What forms of exercise are popular among young people?","html":"Gym training, running, football, badminton and yoga are popular. Social media encourages activities with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">visible results</strong> and apps make progress easy to share. Individual exercise has grown because people can choose their own schedule. However, it does not automatically provide the friendships and shared experience that come with playing regularly in a team."},{"text":"Yes. Exercise is now connected with mental well-being, appearance and everyday health, not just competition. This attracts people who did not enjoy sport at school. Online images can create unrealistic expectations, particularly for young people, which is a drawback. However, many gyms have also become more welcoming to beginners, making it easier for them to start.","phrases":[{"en":"unrealistic expectations","meaning":"Hopes or standards that are not reasonably achievable."}],"question":"Have people's attitudes towards exercise changed in your country in recent years?","html":"Yes. Exercise is now connected with mental well-being, appearance and everyday health, not just competition. This attracts people who did not enjoy sport at school. Online images can create <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unrealistic expectations</strong>, particularly for young people, which is a drawback. However, many gyms have also become more welcoming to beginners, making it easier for them to start."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-084-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-084-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"The most important invention, and whether technology complicates life","prompt":"What is the most important invention of the last century?\nCan technology make life harder?","answer":"Q: What is the most important invention of the last century?\nA: I'd say the internet, though the transistor's a reasonable answer too, since nothing else would exist without it. The reason I'd choose the internet is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the sheer breadth of what it changed</strong> — communication, commerce, education, entertainment and access to information all work in a fundamentally different way now. It's stopped being a product and become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">basic infrastructure</strong>, in much the same way as electricity or the road network. The honest problem with an answer this broad, though, is that it's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a bit of a cheat</strong> — you could just as easily break it down into the specific technologies underneath it, like fibre optics or mobile networks, and credit those instead.\n\nQ: Can technology make life harder?\nA: Definitely. The obvious problem is distraction, since a device designed to save time ends up consuming your attention unless you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set firm boundaries</strong> around it. Beyond that, there's the expectation of being permanently available, which has quietly extended the working day for a great many people. And there's a dependency issue: when a system fails, we often discover we've <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lost the ability to manage without it</strong>. The technology itself is fairly neutral, though; it's really the habits people build around it that matter.","topic":"Technology","phrases":[{"en":"the sheer breadth of what it changed","context":"The reason I'd choose the internet is the sheer breadth of what it changed — communication, commerce, education, entertainment and access to information all work in a fundamentally different way now."},{"en":"a bit of a cheat","context":"The honest problem with an answer this broad, though, is that it's a bit of a cheat — you could just as easily break it down into the specific technologies underneath it, like fibre optics or mobile networks, and credit those instead."},{"en":"basic infrastructure","context":"It's stopped being a product and become basic infrastructure, in much the same way as electricity or the road network."},{"en":"set firm boundaries","context":"The obvious problem is distraction, since a device designed to save time ends up consuming your attention unless you set firm boundaries around it."},{"en":"lost the ability to manage without it","context":"And there's a dependency issue: when a system fails, we often discover we've lost the ability to manage without it."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Technology","id":"speaking-part-3-085","_displayNo":"#085","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-085-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-085-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I would choose the internet because it has changed communication, shopping, learning and entertainment so widely. It now supports daily life like roads or electricity. However, that is a broad answer, since the internet depends on many other inventions, such as transistors and communication networks. Those deserve credit too, even if the internet is the change people notice most.","phrases":[{"en":"deserve credit","meaning":"Should be recognised for making an important contribution."}],"question":"What is the most important invention of the last century?","html":"I would choose the internet because it has changed communication, shopping, learning and entertainment so widely. It now supports daily life like roads or electricity. However, that is a broad answer, since the internet depends on many other inventions, such as transistors and communication networks. Those <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deserve credit</strong> too, even if the internet is the change people notice most."},{"text":"Yes. Devices can distract us, and being reachable all the time can extend the working day. We may also depend on systems so much that a failure leaves us unable to manage basic tasks. Technology can still be helpful, though. Clear boundaries and sensible habits often decide whether it saves time or makes life more complicated.","phrases":[{"en":"boundaries","meaning":"Limits that protect your time, privacy or well-being."}],"question":"Can technology make life harder?","html":"Yes. Devices can distract us, and being reachable all the time can extend the working day. We may also depend on systems so much that a failure leaves us unable to manage basic tasks. Technology can still be helpful, though. Clear <strong class=\"key-phrase\">boundaries</strong> and sensible habits often decide whether it saves time or makes life more complicated."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-085-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-085-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Life then and now, and the effect of electrical equipment","prompt":"What are the main differences between life in the past and life today?\nWhat are the impacts of electrical equipment on our life?\nHow will technology change our life in future?","answer":"Q: What are the main differences between life in the past and life today?\nA: The clearest difference is speed and choice. Information arrives instantly, travel takes hours rather than days, and household technology has removed a great deal of the physical labour that used to fill people's time. What people had in the past was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a stronger local routine</strong> — you knew your neighbours and life followed a predictable rhythm. The trade-off is that they had far fewer options, and everyday tasks were exhausting in a way that's hard to imagine now.\n\nQ: What are the impacts of electrical equipment on our life?\nA: Household appliances have transformed daily life more than people usually acknowledge, particularly for women, since washing and cooking once occupied most of the day. Refrigeration alone changed diet and health enormously by making it safe to store food. The costs are energy consumption and a kind of dependence — very few of us could manage a week without electricity. There's also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the repair problem</strong>: modern appliances are sealed units, so when one fails, you replace it rather than fix it.\n\nQ: How will technology change our life in future?\nA: I'd expect automation to take over most routine decisions — scheduling, purchasing, basic diagnosis, the kind of thing a call-centre worker or a data-entry clerk does today — and services to become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far more personalised</strong>. The genuine questions aren't technical, though. One is employment: if systems handle routine analytical work, many middle-income jobs disappear <strong class=\"key-phrase\">faster than people can retrain</strong>. The other is control. When a system decides who gets a loan or an interview, somebody needs to be able to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">explain and challenge that decision</strong>.","topic":"Technology","phrases":[{"en":"a stronger local routine","context":"What people had in the past was a stronger local routine — you knew your neighbours and life followed a predictable rhythm."},{"en":"the repair problem","context":"There's also the repair problem: modern appliances are sealed units, so when one fails, you replace it rather than fix it."},{"en":"far more personalised","context":"I'd expect automation to take over most routine decisions — scheduling, purchasing, basic diagnosis, the kind of thing a call-centre worker or a data-entry clerk does today — and services to become far more personalised."},{"en":"faster than people can retrain","context":"One is employment: if systems handle routine analytical work, many middle-income jobs disappear faster than people can retrain."},{"en":"explain and challenge that decision","context":"When a system decides who gets a loan or an interview, somebody needs to be able to explain and challenge that decision."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Technology","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-086","_displayNo":"#086","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-086-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-086-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-086-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Life is faster and offers more choices now. Information arrives quickly, travel takes less time and machines do much household work. In the past, people often had stronger local routines and knew neighbours better. However, they also had fewer options and spent more time on tiring tasks. So I see benefits and losses rather than one period being completely better.","phrases":[{"en":"household work","meaning":"Tasks needed to run a home, such as cooking and cleaning."}],"question":"What are the main differences between life in the past and life today?","html":"Life is faster and offers more choices now. Information arrives quickly, travel takes less time and machines do much <strong class=\"key-phrase\">household work</strong>. In the past, people often had stronger local routines and knew neighbours better. However, they also had fewer options and spent more time on tiring tasks. So I see benefits and losses rather than one period being completely better."},{"text":"Appliances have saved enormous time and effort, particularly for those who did most cooking and washing. Refrigerators also make food storage safer and reduce waste. However, these machines use energy and make us dependent on electricity. Another problem is repair: some modern products are difficult to fix, so people replace them instead, creating extra cost and waste.","phrases":[{"en":"dependent on","meaning":"Needing something in order to function or manage."}],"question":"What are the impacts of electrical equipment on our life?","html":"Appliances have saved enormous time and effort, particularly for those who did most cooking and washing. Refrigerators also make food storage safer and reduce waste. However, these machines use energy and make us <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dependent on</strong> electricity. Another problem is repair: some modern products are difficult to fix, so people replace them instead, creating extra cost and waste."},{"text":"I expect more routine tasks to become automatic and services to suit individual users more closely. The main difficulties may concern jobs and control rather than the technology itself. Workers may lose roles before retraining, and people need ways to question important automated decisions. A system choosing someone for a loan or interview should not be impossible to challenge.","phrases":[{"en":"automated decisions","meaning":"Choices made by a computer system with little or no direct human involvement."}],"question":"How will technology change our life in future?","html":"I expect more routine tasks to become automatic and services to suit individual users more closely. The main difficulties may concern jobs and control rather than the technology itself. Workers may lose roles before retraining, and people need ways to question important <strong class=\"key-phrase\">automated decisions</strong>. A system choosing someone for a loan or interview should not be impossible to challenge."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-086-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-086-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-086-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Inventions of the last fifty years and mobile phones","prompt":"What do you think are the most important inventions of the last 50 years?\nHow have mobile phones made communications easier?\nWhich household appliances do you think people need most?","answer":"Q: What do you think are the most important inventions of the last 50 years?\nA: The smartphone's the obvious answer, because it collapsed a dozen separate devices into one — telephone, camera, map, bank, library and music player. For much of the world it was also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the first computer people ever owned</strong>. I'd add the internet itself, since the phone would be useless without it, and medical imaging, which transformed diagnosis. The smartphone stands out mainly because its effects reached almost everybody, including in poorer countries — though that's not quite true even now, since plenty of people still can't afford one or don't have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reliable coverage</strong>, so the benefits are less universal than the answer makes them sound.\n\nQ: How have mobile phones made communications easier?\nA: They removed the two constraints that used to govern communication: location and cost. You can reach someone anywhere, instantly, and international calls that once cost a fortune are effectively free. For emergencies, and for families separated by migration, that's genuinely transformative in a way older generations find hard to imagine. The side effect is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an expectation of immediate response</strong> — because people know you have the phone with you, not replying quickly is now read as rudeness.\n\nQ: Which household appliances do you think people need most?\nA: The refrigerator, without much question. It changed what people could safely eat, cut food waste enormously and reduced how often households had to shop. After that the washing machine, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">returned hours of every week</strong> to whoever was doing the laundry. Cooking appliances too, obviously. What these three share is that they replaced <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely heavy labour</strong> rather than merely adding convenience — though which one you'd rank first really depends on your circumstances, and someone hand-washing clothes for a big family would probably put the washing machine ahead of everything else.","topic":"Technology","phrases":[{"en":"the first computer people ever owned","context":"For much of the world it was also the first computer people ever owned."},{"en":"reliable coverage","context":"The smartphone stands out mainly because its effects reached almost everybody, including in poorer countries — though that's not quite true even now, since plenty of people still can't afford one or don't have reliable coverage, so the benefits are less universal than the answer makes them sound."},{"en":"an expectation of immediate response","context":"The side effect is an expectation of immediate response — because people know you have the phone with you, not replying quickly is now read as rudeness."},{"en":"returned hours of every week","context":"After that the washing machine, which returned hours of every week to whoever was doing the laundry."},{"en":"genuinely heavy labour","context":"What these three share is that they replaced genuinely heavy labour rather than merely adding convenience — though which one you'd rank first really depends on your circumstances, and someone hand-washing clothes for a big family would probably put the washing machine ahead of everything else."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Technology","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-087","_displayNo":"#087","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-087-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-087-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-087-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Smartphones stand out because they combine a phone, camera, map and many other tools. For many people, they were also the first computer they owned. I would include the internet and medical scanning too. However, the benefits are not universal: some people still lack affordable devices or a dependable connection, so these inventions have not reached everyone equally.","phrases":[{"en":"universal","meaning":"Applying to or available to everyone."}],"question":"What do you think are the most important inventions of the last 50 years?","html":"Smartphones stand out because they combine a phone, camera, map and many other tools. For many people, they were also the first computer they owned. I would include the internet and medical scanning too. However, the benefits are not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">universal</strong>: some people still lack affordable devices or a dependable connection, so these inventions have not reached everyone equally."},{"text":"They let us contact people quickly from almost anywhere, usually for much less than international calls once cost. This is especially useful in emergencies and for families living in different countries. However, easy contact creates pressure for immediate replies. People may see a slow response as rude because they assume the phone is always nearby.","phrases":[{"en":"immediate replies","meaning":"Answers sent straight away, without delay."}],"question":"How have mobile phones made communications easier?","html":"They let us contact people quickly from almost anywhere, usually for much less than international calls once cost. This is especially useful in emergencies and for families living in different countries. However, easy contact creates pressure for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">immediate replies</strong>. People may see a slow response as rude because they assume the phone is always nearby."},{"text":"I'd put the refrigerator first because it keeps food safe, reduces waste and cuts shopping trips. Washing machines and cooking equipment are also essential because they replace heavy daily work. The order depends on the household, though. Someone washing clothes by hand for a large family might reasonably consider the washing machine the most important.","phrases":[{"en":"essential","meaning":"Extremely important or necessary."}],"question":"Which household appliances do you think people need most?","html":"I&#x27;d put the refrigerator first because it keeps food safe, reduces waste and cuts shopping trips. Washing machines and cooking equipment are also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">essential</strong> because they replace heavy daily work. The order depends on the household, though. Someone washing clothes by hand for a large family might reasonably consider the washing machine the most important."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-087-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-087-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-087-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"How the internet will change our lives","prompt":"How do you think the internet will affect our lives in the future?","answer":"Q: How do you think the internet will affect our lives in the future?\nA: I think it'll become almost invisible, in the sense that it'll be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">built into everything</strong> rather than something we consciously log into. Healthcare, education, transport and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">government services</strong> are all moving online, and systems will increasingly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">anticipate what we need</strong> before we ask — a fridge that reorders milk before you run out, or a health app that flags a problem before you'd have noticed the symptoms yourself. The benefits are obvious, but there are two things societies will have to manage. One is privacy, because prediction requires collecting enormous quantities of personal data. The other is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dependence</strong> — if everything runs through a single network, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a failure or an attack</strong> becomes far more damaging than it would once have been.","topic":"Technology","phrases":[{"en":"built into everything","context":"I think it'll become almost invisible, in the sense that it'll be built into everything rather than something we consciously log into."},{"en":"anticipate what we need","context":"Healthcare, education, transport and government services are all moving online, and systems will increasingly anticipate what we need before we ask — a fridge that reorders milk before you run out, or a health app that flags a problem before you'd have noticed the symptoms yourself."},{"en":"dependence","context":"The other is dependence — if everything runs through a single network, a failure or an attack becomes far more damaging than it would once have been."},{"en":"a failure or an attack","context":"The other is dependence — if everything runs through a single network, a failure or an attack becomes far more damaging than it would once have been."},{"en":"government services","context":"Healthcare, education, transport and government services are all moving online, and systems will increasingly anticipate what we need before we ask — a fridge that reorders milk before you run out, or a health app that flags a problem before you'd have noticed the symptoms yourself."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Technology & Internet","id":"speaking-part-3-088","_displayNo":"#088","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-088-q01.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I think the internet will become built into more ordinary services, so people notice it less. Systems may order supplies automatically or warn of health problems earlier. That could be useful, but it requires collecting personal information. Greater dependence also means a breakdown or attack could affect more of life at once. Privacy and reliable systems will therefore become increasingly important.","phrases":[{"en":"breakdown","meaning":"A failure that stops a machine or system working."}],"question":"How do you think the internet will affect our lives in the future?","html":"I think the internet will become built into more ordinary services, so people notice it less. Systems may order supplies automatically or warn of health problems earlier. That could be useful, but it requires collecting personal information. Greater dependence also means a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">breakdown</strong> or attack could affect more of life at once. Privacy and reliable systems will therefore become increasingly important."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-088-q01.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Advertising online and how it changes what we buy","prompt":"What role does advertising play on the internet?\nHow do you think the internet will affect buying patterns in the future?\nWhat effect has the internet had on the way people generally communicate with each other?","answer":"Q: What role does advertising play on the internet?\nA: It funds almost everything people use for free — search, social media, email, video. That's the bargain, though it's not always made explicit: you're not the customer, you're what's being sold. Targeting has become extraordinarily precise, which advertisers value and which makes many users uncomfortable, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the line between relevance and surveillance</strong> is genuinely thin. What I'd want is real control over the data rather than a consent box nobody reads — though I'll admit most people, myself included, still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">click accept without reading it</strong>, so the market clearly isn't demanding that control very loudly.\n\nQ: How do you think the internet will affect buying patterns in the future?\nA: Routine purchasing will become largely automatic — subscriptions and systems that reorder things before you notice you need them. For anything considered, people will rely more on reviews and comparison, which shifts power towards the consumer, though <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fake reviews undermine that considerably</strong>. Physical shops won't disappear, but their role will change into places where you test or try something before buying it more cheaply elsewhere, which is a very difficult business model to sustain.\n\nQ: What effect has the internet had on the way people generally communicate with each other?\nA: It's made communication immediate, cheap and global, which has obvious benefits. Two effects concern me, though. Messages have become shorter, so a lot of nuance goes missing and people misread tone constantly. And a great deal of communication is now <strong class=\"key-phrase\">public performance rather than conversation</strong> — people write for an audience, which changes what they're willing to say. So we communicate far more often while arguably <strong class=\"key-phrase\">understanding each other less well</strong>.","topic":"Technology","phrases":[{"en":"the line between relevance and surveillance","context":"Targeting has become extraordinarily precise, which advertisers value and which makes many users uncomfortable, because the line between relevance and surveillance is genuinely thin."},{"en":"click accept without reading it","context":"What I'd want is real control over the data rather than a consent box nobody reads — though I'll admit most people, myself included, still click accept without reading it, so the market clearly isn't demanding that control very loudly."},{"en":"fake reviews undermine that considerably","context":"For anything considered, people will rely more on reviews and comparison, which shifts power towards the consumer, though fake reviews undermine that considerably."},{"en":"public performance rather than conversation","context":"And a great deal of communication is now public performance rather than conversation — people write for an audience, which changes what they're willing to say."},{"en":"understanding each other less well","context":"So we communicate far more often while arguably understanding each other less well."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Technology & Internet","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-089","_displayNo":"#089","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-089-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-089-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-089-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Advertising pays for many free services, such as search, email and videos. Companies value being able to reach very specific users, but this often involves collecting personal data. I would like people to have more meaningful control over that information. However, many of us accept conditions without reading them, so we do not always act on our concerns.","phrases":[{"en":"personal data","meaning":"Information connected with an identifiable individual."}],"question":"What role does advertising play on the internet?","html":"Advertising pays for many free services, such as search, email and videos. Companies value being able to reach very specific users, but this often involves collecting <strong class=\"key-phrase\">personal data</strong>. I would like people to have more meaningful control over that information. However, many of us accept conditions without reading them, so we do not always act on our concerns."},{"text":"Everyday purchases may become more automatic through subscriptions or systems that reorder items. For bigger purchases, reviews and comparisons can help buyers, although fake reviews create problems. Physical shops may become places to try products before ordering elsewhere. That gives consumers options, but makes it harder for shops to earn enough from providing the experience.","phrases":[{"en":"comparisons","meaning":"Ways of examining different options to understand their advantages and disadvantages."}],"question":"How do you think the internet will affect buying patterns in the future?","html":"Everyday purchases may become more automatic through subscriptions or systems that reorder items. For bigger purchases, reviews and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comparisons</strong> can help buyers, although fake reviews create problems. Physical shops may become places to try products before ordering elsewhere. That gives consumers options, but makes it harder for shops to earn enough from providing the experience."},{"text":"It makes contact quicker, cheaper and global, which is very useful. However, short messages can lose meaning or tone and create misunderstandings. Online communication is also sometimes written for an audience rather than one person, making people less open. We may send far more messages without necessarily understanding each other better, especially in sensitive conversations.","phrases":[{"en":"sensitive conversations","meaning":"Discussions where feelings or personal issues need careful handling."}],"question":"What effect has the internet had on the way people generally communicate with each other?","html":"It makes contact quicker, cheaper and global, which is very useful. However, short messages can lose meaning or tone and create misunderstandings. Online communication is also sometimes written for an audience rather than one person, making people less open. We may send far more messages without necessarily understanding each other better, especially in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sensitive conversations</strong>."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-089-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-089-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-089-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Public transport against the car","prompt":"Do you prefer travelling by public transport or by car?\nWhat are the benefits of travelling by public transport?\nIn your opinion, in what ways can governments encourage people to use public transport?","answer":"Q: Do you prefer travelling by public transport or by car?\nA: Public transport, when it's reliable — and that qualification matters. The main advantage is that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the time is usable</strong>: I can read or work rather than concentrating on traffic, so an hour on a train isn't really lost. It's also cheaper once you count fuel, parking and maintenance. I'd choose a car for journeys poorly served by public routes, or when I'm carrying things, or travelling late at night, or when I'm heading somewhere with a young child and a lot of luggage, since the flexibility of a car just makes that kind of trip easier.\n\nQ: What are the benefits of travelling by public transport?\nA: The main one is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">efficiency of space</strong>. A single bus replaces perhaps fifty cars, so congestion, pollution and the amount of land given over to roads and parking all fall dramatically. There's a household benefit too, since running a car is one of the largest expenses most families have. And it provides <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuine independence</strong> to people who can't drive — the elderly, teenagers and anyone with a disability — which is easy to overlook if you've always had a car. None of this works, though, if the service itself is unreliable — the efficiency is only theoretical if the bus never turns up on time.\n\nQ: In your opinion, in what ways can governments encourage people to use public transport?\nA: By making it genuinely competitive, which means frequency above all, since people will accept a great deal if they never have to check a timetable, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">integrated ticketing</strong> across buses and trains matters, as does safety, particularly at night. Beyond that, some restriction on driving is usually necessary, such as congestion charges. But imposing the restriction first, before the alternative's actually good, simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">makes people resentful</strong> and turns the whole policy into a political liability.","topic":"Transport","phrases":[{"en":"the time is usable","context":"The main advantage is that the time is usable: I can read or work rather than concentrating on traffic, so an hour on a train isn't really lost."},{"en":"efficiency of space","context":"The main one is efficiency of space."},{"en":"genuine independence","context":"And it provides genuine independence to people who can't drive — the elderly, teenagers and anyone with a disability — which is easy to overlook if you've always had a car."},{"en":"integrated ticketing","context":"By making it genuinely competitive, which means frequency above all, since people will accept a great deal if they never have to check a timetable, and integrated ticketing across buses and trains matters, as does safety, particularly at night."},{"en":"makes people resentful","context":"But imposing the restriction first, before the alternative's actually good, simply makes people resentful and turns the whole policy into a political liability."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Transport","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-090","_displayNo":"#090","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-090-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-090-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-090-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"I prefer public transport when it is reliable. I can read or work instead of concentrating on driving, and it often costs less after parking and maintenance are included. However, a car is more practical for poorly served places, late journeys or carrying a child and lots of luggage. My choice depends on the actual trip.","phrases":[{"en":"maintenance","meaning":"Work needed to keep something in good condition."}],"question":"Do you prefer travelling by public transport or by car?","html":"I prefer public transport when it is reliable. I can read or work instead of concentrating on driving, and it often costs less after parking and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">maintenance</strong> are included. However, a car is more practical for poorly served places, late journeys or carrying a child and lots of luggage. My choice depends on the actual trip."},{"text":"Public transport moves many people using less road and parking space. It can reduce traffic and pollution, save households money and provide independence for people who cannot drive. However, those benefits depend on the service working properly. A bus that is unreliable may look efficient on paper but will not persuade people to leave their cars behind.","phrases":[{"en":"on paper","meaning":"In theory or according to a plan, rather than in real practice."}],"question":"What are the benefits of travelling by public transport?","html":"Public transport moves many people using less road and parking space. It can reduce traffic and pollution, save households money and provide independence for people who cannot drive. However, those benefits depend on the service working properly. A bus that is unreliable may look efficient <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on paper</strong> but will not persuade people to leave their cars behind."},{"text":"First, make services frequent, safe and easy to use, with tickets that work across different transport types. People need a realistic alternative before restrictions on driving are introduced. Charges may help afterwards, but applying them first can make people angry without changing travel habits. Good service should be the main reason to switch, not simply punishment for driving.","phrases":[{"en":"switch","meaning":"Change from one choice or method to another."}],"question":"In your opinion, in what ways can governments encourage people to use public transport?","html":"First, make services frequent, safe and easy to use, with tickets that work across different transport types. People need a realistic alternative before restrictions on driving are introduced. Charges may help afterwards, but applying them first can make people angry without changing travel habits. Good service should be the main reason to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">switch</strong>, not simply punishment for driving."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-090-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-090-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-090-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Getting to work, and how transport has improved","prompt":"Do you think getting to work is easier or more difficult than it used to be?\nHow have methods of transport improved?\nAre there any problems that have been created by new transport systems?","answer":"Q: Do you think getting to work is easier or more difficult than it used to be?\nA: It depends almost entirely on where you live. In cities with new metro lines it's genuinely easier — you can check live times, plan a route on your phone and know within a few minutes when you'll arrive, which removed a lot of the old uncertainty. But congestion has grown faster than the infrastructure in most places, and cities have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spread outwards</strong>, so people now commute much further than their parents did. My honest impression is that the technology improved while the journey itself got longer.\n\nQ: How have methods of transport improved?\nA: Safety is the clearest one — cars have airbags, crumple zones and automatic braking, and road deaths have fallen substantially in most developed countries despite far more traffic. Vehicles are also cleaner, with electric buses replacing diesel in a lot of cities. The less visible improvement is coordination: digital ticketing, live arrival information and route planning have made journeys <strong class=\"key-phrase\">predictable in a way they simply were not</strong> twenty years ago. Comfort hasn't improved anywhere near as fast, but on the essentials, transport keeps getting better.\n\nQ: Are there any problems that have been created by new transport systems?\nA: Yes, and some are counter-intuitive. Building new roads tends to generate additional traffic rather than relieving it, because easier driving encourages more of it — engineers call this <strong class=\"key-phrase\">induced demand</strong>. Major roads also cut neighbourhoods in half, and the communities affected are usually the poorest. Ride-hailing has had a similar effect, adding vehicles to congested streets while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drawing passengers away from public transport</strong>. So a project that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">looks like progress on paper</strong> — a wider road, a new app — can still leave a city worse off once you look at the wider effects rather than just journey times.","topic":"Transport","phrases":[{"en":"spread outwards","context":"But congestion has grown faster than the infrastructure in most places, and cities have spread outwards, so people now commute much further than their parents did."},{"en":"predictable in a way they simply were not","context":"The less visible improvement is coordination: digital ticketing, live arrival information and route planning have made journeys predictable in a way they simply were not twenty years ago."},{"en":"induced demand","context":"Building new roads tends to generate additional traffic rather than relieving it, because easier driving encourages more of it — engineers call this induced demand."},{"en":"drawing passengers away from public transport","context":"Ride-hailing has had a similar effect, adding vehicles to congested streets while drawing passengers away from public transport."},{"en":"looks like progress on paper","context":"So a project that looks like progress on paper — a wider road, a new app — can still leave a city worse off once you look at the wider effects rather than just journey times."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Transport","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-091","_displayNo":"#091","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-091-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-091-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-091-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"It depends on the place. New metro lines and live travel information can make commuting easier to plan. However, traffic has grown and cities have spread, so many people travel further than their parents did. My impression is that planning tools have improved while the journey itself may still take longer, especially where transport investment has not kept up.","phrases":[{"en":"commuting","meaning":"Regular travel between home and work or study."}],"question":"Do you think getting to work is easier or more difficult than it used to be?","html":"It depends on the place. New metro lines and live travel information can make <strong class=\"key-phrase\">commuting</strong> easier to plan. However, traffic has grown and cities have spread, so many people travel further than their parents did. My impression is that planning tools have improved while the journey itself may still take longer, especially where transport investment has not kept up."},{"text":"Vehicles have better safety features, such as airbags and automatic braking, and many are becoming cleaner. Digital tickets, route planning and live information also reduce uncertainty about a journey. Comfort may not have improved as quickly, but knowing how to travel and when to arrive is much easier. I think safety and coordination are the biggest gains.","phrases":[{"en":"coordination","meaning":"Organising different parts so they work well together."}],"question":"How have methods of transport improved?","html":"Vehicles have better safety features, such as airbags and automatic braking, and many are becoming cleaner. Digital tickets, route planning and live information also reduce uncertainty about a journey. Comfort may not have improved as quickly, but knowing how to travel and when to arrive is much easier. I think safety and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">coordination</strong> are the biggest gains."},{"text":"Yes. More roads can encourage extra driving instead of permanently reducing traffic. Large roads may also divide neighbourhoods, often affecting poorer communities. Ride-hailing can add vehicles and draw people away from buses. So a system that seems convenient for one journey may create wider problems. Governments need to examine the overall effects, not only individual travel times.","phrases":[{"en":"Ride-hailing","meaning":"Using an app or service to request a car and driver."}],"question":"Are there any problems that have been created by new transport systems?","html":"Yes. More roads can encourage extra driving instead of permanently reducing traffic. Large roads may also divide neighbourhoods, often affecting poorer communities. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Ride-hailing</strong> can add vehicles and draw people away from buses. So a system that seems convenient for one journey may create wider problems. Governments need to examine the overall effects, not only individual travel times."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-091-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-091-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-091-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Recent changes in transport and the future of public transport","prompt":"How has transport changed in recent decades?\nWill public transport become more popular?","answer":"Q: How has transport changed in recent decades?\nA: The biggest changes are speed and information. Journeys that once took a day now take a couple of hours, and more importantly you can compare routes, check live delays and book everything from a phone, which has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">removed much of the uncertainty</strong> that used to come with travelling. The drawback is that private car ownership has risen enormously over the same period, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">urban congestion</strong> in most cities is considerably worse than it was thirty years ago.\n\nQ: Will public transport become more popular?\nA: It will, but only where it's genuinely good. People switch when the service is frequent, clean and cheaper than driving — that's <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the deciding factor</strong>, not environmental concern. Cities such as Vienna and Singapore have shown that if you invest properly, people use it willingly. Where governments simply raise parking charges without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improving the alternative</strong>, drivers complain and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carry on driving</strong> anyway. Reliability matters more than speed, in my experience — people will accept a slower journey if they can predict it.","topic":"Transport","phrases":[{"en":"removed much of the uncertainty","context":"Journeys that once took a day now take a couple of hours, and more importantly you can compare routes, check live delays and book everything from a phone, which has removed much of the uncertainty that used to come with travelling."},{"en":"urban congestion","context":"The drawback is that private car ownership has risen enormously over the same period, so urban congestion in most cities is considerably worse than it was thirty years ago."},{"en":"the deciding factor","context":"People switch when the service is frequent, clean and cheaper than driving — that's the deciding factor, not environmental concern."},{"en":"improving the alternative","context":"Where governments simply raise parking charges without improving the alternative, drivers complain and carry on driving anyway."},{"en":"carry on driving","context":"Where governments simply raise parking charges without improving the alternative, drivers complain and carry on driving anyway."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Transport","id":"speaking-part-3-092","_displayNo":"#092","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-092-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-092-q02.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Travel has become faster, and information is much easier to get. People can compare routes, check delays and book through a phone, making trips more predictable. However, private car ownership has also increased. That means city traffic can be worse despite improvements in vehicles and planning tools, so progress has not removed every difficulty.","phrases":[{"en":"predictable","meaning":"Able to be expected or planned with reasonable confidence."}],"question":"How has transport changed in recent decades?","html":"Travel has become faster, and information is much easier to get. People can compare routes, check delays and book through a phone, making trips more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">predictable</strong>. However, private car ownership has also increased. That means city traffic can be worse despite improvements in vehicles and planning tools, so progress has not removed every difficulty."},{"text":"Yes, where services are frequent, clean, affordable and dependable. People usually change because transport works well for daily life, not just because it is greener. Higher parking fees alone will not be enough if the alternative is poor. In my experience, a journey people can predict may matter more than a faster one that is often delayed.","phrases":[{"en":"dependable","meaning":"Able to be trusted to work as expected."}],"question":"Will public transport become more popular?","html":"Yes, where services are frequent, clean, affordable and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dependable</strong>. People usually change because transport works well for daily life, not just because it is greener. Higher parking fees alone will not be enough if the alternative is poor. In my experience, a journey people can predict may matter more than a faster one that is often delayed."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-092-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-092-q02.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Recommending a place and sightseeing in general","prompt":"Would you recommend the place you described to other people?\nDo you enjoy sightseeing in general?\nHave you visited a similar place before?","answer":"Q: Would you recommend the place you described to other people?\nA: Yes, but with a couple of conditions. I'd recommend it strongly to anyone interested in culture and landscape rather than nightlife, because that's really what it offers. I'd also tell them to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">avoid the peak season</strong> if they possibly can — it gets so crowded that you lose the very thing you went for. And I'd mention respecting local customs, since places like that are damaged quite quickly when visitor numbers rise faster than the infrastructure can cope with.\n\nQ: Do you enjoy sightseeing in general?\nA: I do, though my approach has changed a great deal. I used to try to see everything, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">working through a list</strong> and photographing each item, and honestly I remember very little of it now. These days I'd rather visit three places properly than fifteen in a rush. I like reading some background beforehand so I understand what I'm looking at, and I try to leave time simply to walk around and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get a feel for</strong> how people actually live there — though I know plenty of people who prefer the list approach, and there's nothing wrong with that if it's genuinely what they enjoy.\n\nQ: Have you visited a similar place before?\nA: Yes, a few with comparable scenery, but what struck me was how different they felt despite the surface similarity. The scale was different, the food was different, and above all the way local people related to visitors varied enormously — some places felt genuinely welcoming, others clearly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">resented the tourism</strong>. Comparing them was useful, because it made me notice what made each one distinctive rather than treating them as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">interchangeable destinations</strong> — though a comparison only takes you so far, and I try not to let one trip set my expectations for the next.","topic":"Travel & Tourism","phrases":[{"en":"avoid the peak season","context":"I'd also tell them to avoid the peak season if they possibly can — it gets so crowded that you lose the very thing you went for."},{"en":"working through a list","context":"I used to try to see everything, working through a list and photographing each item, and honestly I remember very little of it now."},{"en":"get a feel for","context":"I like reading some background beforehand so I understand what I'm looking at, and I try to leave time simply to walk around and get a feel for how people actually live there — though I know plenty of people who prefer the list approach, and there's nothing wrong with that if it's genuinely what..."},{"en":"resented the tourism","context":"The scale was different, the food was different, and above all the way local people related to visitors varied enormously — some places felt genuinely welcoming, others clearly resented the tourism."},{"en":"interchangeable destinations","context":"Comparing them was useful, because it made me notice what made each one distinctive rather than treating them as interchangeable destinations — though a comparison only takes you so far, and I try not to let one trip set my expectations for the next."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Travel & Tourism","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-093","_displayNo":"#093","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-093-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-093-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-093-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Yes, especially to people interested in culture and scenery rather than nightlife. I would suggest avoiding the busiest season because crowds can reduce the experience. Visitors should also respect local customs. If tourism grows faster than facilities can manage, a place can lose the qualities that made it attractive in the first place.","phrases":[{"en":"scenery","meaning":"The natural views and appearance of a place."}],"question":"Would you recommend the place you described to other people?","html":"Yes, especially to people interested in culture and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">scenery</strong> rather than nightlife. I would suggest avoiding the busiest season because crowds can reduce the experience. Visitors should also respect local customs. If tourism grows faster than facilities can manage, a place can lose the qualities that made it attractive in the first place."},{"text":"Yes, but I now prefer seeing fewer places properly. I used to rush through a long list and remembered very little. Reading some background and leaving time to walk around helps me understand how people live. Others enjoy a faster checklist approach, which is fine, but slower visits are more meaningful for me.","phrases":[{"en":"meaningful","meaning":"Having personal value or importance."}],"question":"Do you enjoy sightseeing in general?","html":"Yes, but I now prefer seeing fewer places properly. I used to rush through a long list and remembered very little. Reading some background and leaving time to walk around helps me understand how people live. Others enjoy a faster checklist approach, which is fine, but slower visits are more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meaningful</strong> for me."},{"text":"Yes, I have visited places with similar scenery, but they felt different because of the scale, food and local attitudes towards visitors. Comparing them helped me notice each place's character. However, I try not to let one trip set every expectation for the next. Similar-looking destinations can still offer quite different experiences.","phrases":[{"en":"destinations","meaning":"Places people travel to."}],"question":"Have you visited a similar place before?","html":"Yes, I have visited places with similar scenery, but they felt different because of the scale, food and local attitudes towards visitors. Comparing them helped me notice each place&#x27;s character. However, I try not to let one trip set every expectation for the next. Similar-looking <strong class=\"key-phrase\">destinations</strong> can still offer quite different experiences."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-093-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-093-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-093-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Technology at work and whether it costs jobs","prompt":"Which technologies are commonly used in workplaces?\nDoes technology make work easier?\nDoes technology cause people to lose their jobs?","answer":"Q: Which technologies are commonly used in workplaces?\nA: Most workplaces run on a fairly standard set of equipment — computers, phones, printers and photocopiers, with fax machines still hanging on in a few offices. The computer's probably the most <strong class=\"key-phrase\">essential piece of equipment</strong> because we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rely on it for almost everything</strong>: email, reports, organising data, finding information online. Video calls have become just as essential now that teams are spread across different offices, and shared documents in the cloud have largely replaced sending files back and forth. The one exception is anything hands-on — a mechanic or an electrician still depends far more on physical tools than on a laptop.\n\nQ: Does technology make work easier?\nA: It does, on balance, because it removes a lot of friction from ordinary tasks. Email alone means a report or a query reaches a colleague in a different office, or a different country, in seconds rather than days. On the other hand, technology can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make life more difficult</strong> too, especially when it goes wrong — a dead internet connection or a crashed computer can bring an entire office to a stop for an afternoon. It also means work follows you home, because the same tools that let you work anywhere let your employer reach you anywhere.\n\nQ: Does technology cause people to lose their jobs?\nA: I think technology's often responsible for people losing their jobs. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Machines have replaced people</strong> in areas like manufacturing and agriculture, and whenever a new technology's introduced, there are redundancies. At the same time, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">jobs might be created</strong> thanks to a new technology — there'd be no computer programmers if the computer hadn't been invented. The real problem is timing. Jobs disappear quickly and new ones appear slowly, and they rarely go to the same people, so an individual can be displaced permanently even while total employment holds up.","topic":"Work","phrases":[{"en":"essential piece of equipment","context":"The computer's probably the most essential piece of equipment because we rely on it for almost everything: email, reports, organising data, finding information online."},{"en":"rely on it for almost everything","context":"The computer's probably the most essential piece of equipment because we rely on it for almost everything: email, reports, organising data, finding information online."},{"en":"make life more difficult","context":"On the other hand, technology can make life more difficult too, especially when it goes wrong — a dead internet connection or a crashed computer can bring an entire office to a stop for an afternoon."},{"en":"Machines have replaced people","context":"Machines have replaced people in areas like manufacturing and agriculture, and whenever a new technology's introduced, there are redundancies."},{"en":"jobs might be created","context":"At the same time, jobs might be created thanks to a new technology — there'd be no computer programmers if the computer hadn't been invented."}],"subtype":"","summary":"Work","id":"speaking-part-3-094","_displayNo":"#094","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-094-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-094-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-094-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Computers, phones, printers and shared online documents are common in offices. Computers support emails, reports, data and research, while video calls connect teams in different locations. However, needs vary by job. A mechanic or electrician still depends mainly on physical tools, so an office's most important technology is not necessarily essential in every workplace.","phrases":[{"en":"shared online documents","meaning":"Files that several people can access or edit through the internet."}],"question":"Which technologies are commonly used in workplaces?","html":"Computers, phones, printers and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shared online documents</strong> are common in offices. Computers support emails, reports, data and research, while video calls connect teams in different locations. However, needs vary by job. A mechanic or electrician still depends mainly on physical tools, so an office&#x27;s most important technology is not necessarily essential in every workplace."},{"text":"Overall, yes. Technology makes ordinary tasks quicker, such as sending a report to another country in seconds. However, an internet failure or computer crash can stop work completely. It also allows employers to contact staff at home. So it improves efficiency but can bring dependence and make it harder to separate work from personal time.","phrases":[{"en":"efficiency","meaning":"Doing work with less wasted time or effort."}],"question":"Does technology make work easier?","html":"Overall, yes. Technology makes ordinary tasks quicker, such as sending a report to another country in seconds. However, an internet failure or computer crash can stop work completely. It also allows employers to contact staff at home. So it improves <strong class=\"key-phrase\">efficiency</strong> but can bring dependence and make it harder to separate work from personal time."},{"text":"Yes, machines and software can replace jobs in areas such as farming and manufacturing. New technology also creates roles, including programming. The problem is that these changes do not happen at the same speed or help the same people. A worker may lose a job long before they can train for one of the new opportunities.","phrases":[{"en":"replace jobs","meaning":"Perform work that people were previously employed to do."}],"question":"Does technology cause people to lose their jobs?","html":"Yes, machines and software can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">replace jobs</strong> in areas such as farming and manufacturing. New technology also creates roles, including programming. The problem is that these changes do not happen at the same speed or help the same people. A worker may lose a job long before they can train for one of the new opportunities."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-094-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-094-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-094-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"Preparing for an interview, and dangerous jobs","prompt":"What is the best way to prepare for a job interview?\nWhich jobs do you think are the most dangerous?\nHow can employers keep their staff happy?","answer":"Q: What is the best way to prepare for a job interview?\nA: Research is the foundation — understanding what the organisation actually does and what the role involves day to day, not just the job title. Beyond that, I'd prepare specific examples rather than general claims: saying you work well under pressure means nothing, whereas describing a situation where you did is convincing. Some rehearsal helps with nerves, but there's a risk of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sounding memorised</strong>, so I'd practise the examples rather than scripting entire answers.\n\nQ: Which jobs do you think are the most dangerous?\nA: Mining and deep-sea fishing are consistently at the top of the statistics, along with construction, logging and firefighting. The common factors are heavy machinery, unstable environments and long shifts, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fatigue is where most accidents begin</strong>. What's often overlooked is that danger isn't always immediate, though — jobs involving chemicals, dust or constant noise can cause serious illness twenty years later, and those cases never make it into the annual accident figures that get all the attention.\n\nQ: How can employers keep their staff happy?\nA: Fair pay is the starting point, but it doesn't buy satisfaction on its own — it just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">removes a reason to leave</strong>. What matters more is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a manageable workload</strong>, being treated with respect, and having some genuine control over how you do the work. Opportunities to develop matter too, because people want to feel they're getting better at something. Superficial perks definitely don't work, though — <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a table-tennis table cannot compensate for a bad manager</strong>.","topic":"Work","phrases":[{"en":"sounding memorised","context":"Some rehearsal helps with nerves, but there's a risk of sounding memorised, so I'd practise the examples rather than scripting entire answers."},{"en":"fatigue is where most accidents begin","context":"The common factors are heavy machinery, unstable environments and long shifts, since fatigue is where most accidents begin."},{"en":"removes a reason to leave","context":"Fair pay is the starting point, but it doesn't buy satisfaction on its own — it just removes a reason to leave."},{"en":"a manageable workload","context":"What matters more is a manageable workload, being treated with respect, and having some genuine control over how you do the work."},{"en":"a table-tennis table cannot compensate for a bad manager","context":"Superficial perks definitely don't work, though — a table-tennis table cannot compensate for a bad manager."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Work","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-095","_displayNo":"#095","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-095-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-095-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-095-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Research the organisation and the actual work first. Then prepare specific examples showing your skills, rather than simply saying you are good under pressure. Practice can reduce nerves, but learning whole answers by heart may sound unnatural. I would rehearse explaining real examples clearly while leaving enough freedom to answer the question actually asked.","phrases":[{"en":"rehearse","meaning":"Practise something before a real event or performance."}],"question":"What is the best way to prepare for a job interview?","html":"Research the organisation and the actual work first. Then prepare specific examples showing your skills, rather than simply saying you are good under pressure. Practice can reduce nerves, but learning whole answers by heart may sound unnatural. I would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rehearse</strong> explaining real examples clearly while leaving enough freedom to answer the question actually asked."},{"text":"Mining, fishing, construction and firefighting can be very dangerous because of machinery, difficult conditions and tiredness from long shifts. However, danger is not always immediate. Dust, chemicals or constant noise can cause illness much later. Those slower harms may receive less attention than accidents, even though they can seriously affect workers' lives.","phrases":[{"en":"long shifts","meaning":"Extended periods spent working before a break or time off."}],"question":"Which jobs do you think are the most dangerous?","html":"Mining, fishing, construction and firefighting can be very dangerous because of machinery, difficult conditions and tiredness from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long shifts</strong>. However, danger is not always immediate. Dust, chemicals or constant noise can cause illness much later. Those slower harms may receive less attention than accidents, even though they can seriously affect workers&#x27; lives."},{"text":"Fair pay matters, but people also need respect, a manageable workload and some control over how they work. Chances to learn and improve can make a difference too. Small perks cannot make up for poor management. A workplace is more satisfying when everyday conditions are good, rather than when it offers entertainment while ignoring staff's real problems.","phrases":[{"en":"manageable workload","meaning":"An amount of work someone can reasonably complete."}],"question":"How can employers keep their staff happy?","html":"Fair pay matters, but people also need respect, a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">manageable workload</strong> and some control over how they work. Chances to learn and improve can make a difference too. Small perks cannot make up for poor management. A workplace is more satisfying when everyday conditions are good, rather than when it offers entertainment while ignoring staff&#x27;s real problems."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-095-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-095-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-095-q03.mp3"]},{"section":"speaking-part-3","title":"How computers changed writing, and the future of it","prompt":"What effect have computers had on the way we write?\nWhat are the advantages and disadvantages of email?\nWill writing remain an important skill in the future?","answer":"Q: What effect have computers had on the way we write?\nA: The biggest change is that writing became revisable. On paper, restructuring a piece meant copying the whole thing out again, so people planned carefully before they started. Now you can rearrange anything instantly, which is liberating but does encourage <strong class=\"key-phrase\">writing before thinking</strong>. Sharing and searching have transformed collaboration as well. The loss is at the level of detail, though — with autocorrect handling spelling, a lot of people have simply stopped noticing their own errors.\n\nQ: What are the advantages and disadvantages of email?\nA: It's fast, essentially free, and creates <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a searchable record</strong>, which is genuinely valuable when you need to establish what was agreed. The disadvantages are mostly about tone and volume. Written messages strip out the signals that stop a conversation going wrong, so something meant neutrally reads as cold. And because sending costs nothing, people get copied into everything, so important messages <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get lost among dozens</strong> that never needed to exist in the first place.\n\nQ: Will writing remain an important skill in the future?\nA: More important, not less, I'd say. Artificial intelligence can produce <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a competent draft</strong>, but somebody still has to judge whether it's accurate, whether the argument holds and whether it says what was actually intended — a manager still has to notice when an AI-drafted report gets a figure wrong or misses the actual point, and you can't judge that if you can't write yourself. There's a deeper point too: writing something down is how you discover whether you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely understand it</strong>. That said, for routine writing — a quick email, a simple summary — nobody's going to miss doing it by hand, so the skill that'll matter is judgement, not typing practice.","topic":"Writing","phrases":[{"en":"writing before thinking","context":"Now you can rearrange anything instantly, which is liberating but does encourage writing before thinking."},{"en":"a searchable record","context":"It's fast, essentially free, and creates a searchable record, which is genuinely valuable when you need to establish what was agreed."},{"en":"a competent draft","context":"Artificial intelligence can produce a competent draft, but somebody still has to judge whether it's accurate, whether the argument holds and whether it says what was actually intended — a manager still has to notice when an AI-drafted report gets a figure wrong or misses the actual point, and you..."},{"en":"get lost among dozens","context":"And because sending costs nothing, people get copied into everything, so important messages get lost among dozens that never needed to exist in the first place."},{"en":"genuinely understand it","context":"There's a deeper point too: writing something down is how you discover whether you genuinely understand it."}],"subtype":"Part 3","summary":"Writing","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-3-096","_displayNo":"#096","qaAudio":["audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-096-q01.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-096-q02.mp3","audio/ava-us/qa/speaking-part-3-096-q03.mp3"],"simpleAnswers":[{"text":"Computers make editing and reorganising writing much easier. People no longer need to copy everything again to change a structure. Sharing and searching also help people work together. However, easy editing may encourage starting before thinking, and automatic spelling correction can make people less aware of mistakes. The tools help, but careful attention is still needed.","phrases":[{"en":"editing","meaning":"Changing written work to improve or correct it."}],"question":"What effect have computers had on the way we write?","html":"Computers make <strong class=\"key-phrase\">editing</strong> and reorganising writing much easier. People no longer need to copy everything again to change a structure. Sharing and searching also help people work together. However, easy editing may encourage starting before thinking, and automatic spelling correction can make people less aware of mistakes. The tools help, but careful attention is still needed."},{"text":"Email is fast, cheap and creates a record that can be searched later. This is useful for checking agreements. However, written tone can be misunderstood, and too many unnecessary messages can hide important information. Copying everyone into every conversation increases that problem. Email works best when people think about both the wording and who actually needs the message.","phrases":[{"en":"wording","meaning":"The particular words chosen to express something."}],"question":"What are the advantages and disadvantages of email?","html":"Email is fast, cheap and creates a record that can be searched later. This is useful for checking agreements. However, written tone can be misunderstood, and too many unnecessary messages can hide important information. Copying everyone into every conversation increases that problem. Email works best when people think about both the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wording</strong> and who actually needs the message."},{"text":"Yes, because people still need to judge whether writing is clear and accurate, even when AI produces a first draft. Someone must notice a wrong number or weak argument. Writing also helps us discover whether we understand an idea ourselves. Routine typing may become less important, but the ability to organise and evaluate meaning will remain valuable.","phrases":[{"en":"evaluate","meaning":"Examine something carefully to judge its quality or usefulness."}],"question":"Will writing remain an important skill in the future?","html":"Yes, because people still need to judge whether writing is clear and accurate, even when AI produces a first draft. Someone must notice a wrong number or weak argument. Writing also helps us discover whether we understand an idea ourselves. Routine typing may become less important, but the ability to organise and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">evaluate</strong> meaning will remain valuable."}],"simpleQaAudio":["audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-096-q01.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-096-q02.mp3","audio/simple/speaking-part-3/speaking-part-3-096-q03.mp3"]}],"counts":{"writing-general":29,"writing-academic":69,"writing-task-2":214,"speaking-part-1":90,"speaking-part-2":222,"speaking-part-3":96},"sections":["writing-general","writing-academic","writing-task-2","speaking-part-1","speaking-part-2","speaking-part-3"],"sourceNote":"Public IELTSquirrel build: English-only interface, privacy-safe Core Stories, three themes, and the original IELTS practice bank retained."};
;
